(1458, 'posts', 1, 'en', NULL, 'What is music? How did the art of music come into existence? The history of different civilizations regarding the origin of music! The most influential art among the six arts for expressing emotions and feelings!', NULL, 'Music has been one of the pieces of the puzzle of human life since ancient times. This magical remedy was nature’s gift to humanity. The first sparks of music were ignited in the human mind by listening to the sounds of nature. Over time, music also penetrated into religions, and people would even perform their prayers to the gods in rhythmic and musical tones.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '</p><img class=\"wp-image-2316 size-large\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/music_history_1-576x1024.jpg\" alt=\"کیتارا یکی از آلات موسیقی در عهد باستان\" width=\"576\" height=\"1024\" /> Kithara, an ancient instrument from the harp family[/caption]\n\n<p> </p>\n\n<p>Where was the first music played? In different nations, legends about the origin of music in their lands have been passed down orally for centuries. From Hermes creating the “lyre” using Apollo’s intestines and a tortoise shell, to the Japanese sun goddess hiding in a cave, and even the Chinese Emperor Huang Ti ordering the invention of music and imitating the songs of birds.</p>\n\n<p>A tune or any pleasant and appealing sound that can transform a person or living being is called a song. Music means expressing human emotions through sounds. It is an art formed from sound and silence. Providing an academic definition of music has been a subject of debate and discussion among scholars for centuries. Even today, there is no single definition of music that is accepted by all <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d8%b2%d9%86%d8%af%da%af%db%8c%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%85%d9%87-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%86/\">musicians</a>. According to one of the most widely accepted definitions, music refers to sounds that are consciously produced.</p>\n\n<p>The word “music” comes from the Greek term “Mousika,” derived from “Muse,” the name of the ancient Greek goddess who protected poetry, literature, and music.</p><img class=\"wp-image-2317 size-large\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/music_history_2-576x1024.jpg\" alt=\"مجسمه مرمری آپولو\" width=\"576\" height=\"1024\" /> Marble statue of Apollo[/caption]\n\n<p>Music has been described as the art of expressing emotions through tones, with sound and rhythm as its most important elements. It is also called the science of combining sounds in a way that is pleasing and brings joy and emotional excitement to the soul.</p>\n\n<p>Our ancestors defined music as: “The knowledge of melodies and that which heals and completes them.”</p>\n\n<p>Aristotle considered music to be one of the branches of mathematics, and Islamic philosophers also accepted this view. For example, Avicenna (Ibn Sina) discussed music in the mathematics section of his book “The Cure.” However, because not all features of music are as fixed and unchangeable as mathematics — and the taste and creativity of the composer and performer play a major role — music is also regarded as an art. In any case, today’s music is a vast field of knowledge and art with many specialized branches.</p>\n\n<h2>The First Artistic Expressions of Human Beings</h2>\n\n<p>About four and a half billion years have passed since the formation of the Earth, and around two billion years since the first signs of life appeared on this planet. Scientists believe that the first human-like creatures began their life on Earth between 250,000 and one million years ago, while modern humans have existed for approximately 20,000 years. Through evolution, humans became intelligent, thoughtful, articulate, creative, and productive beings. These qualities created an immense gap between humans and other living creatures.</p>\n\n<p>Morteza Ravandi: What humans have gained through evolution are a large brain and delicate, dexterous hands. The same brain that gave them the power of thought and speech, and the same hands that turned their thoughts into action. This highly complex brain is a masterpiece that enabled humans to free themselves from the constraints of nature and take control of their own destiny, rising from the animal world to the human world.</p>\n\n<p>Art is one of humanity’s greatest achievements and a product of this liberation. After gaining mastery over nature and making efficient use of it, humans could obtain food more easily and store the surplus. This brought them relative comfort, allowing them to spend part of their time on activities other than gathering food.</p>\n\n<p>According to Morteza Ravandi and the opinion of “Anthoney White,” among the greatest victories humanity achieved over the course of a million years are the invention of speech, the discovery of fire, and agriculture. He writes:</p>\n\n<p>“Language, fire, and agriculture are humanity’s greatest inventions. In comparison, spears, spear-throwers, bows and arrows, needles, pottery, houses, boats, weaving, and even steamships, television, and splitting the atom are nothing. The foundations of human civilization and culture rest upon these three, which are the achievements of Neolithic man (the New Stone Age) — a human being whose very existence we doubted until just a hundred years ago.”</p>\n\n<p>He continues: “First, consider language: What is a human being? Without language, a person with even the largest brain and tallest stature is no more than an animal. Without language, humans cannot communicate or understand one another, cannot collaborate on great endeavors, and cannot even think, because we think with the help of words and within the framework of language.</p>\n\n<p>Second, consider fire: Without fire, humans could not eat cooked food, could not live in cold regions, could only work during daylight, and could not melt metals. As a result, they would have remained bound to stone, bone, and wood forever.</p>\n\n<p>Finally, consider agriculture: Agriculture is the mother of civilization. Without agriculture, there would be no construction or buildings. Without buildings, there would be no urban life, and without cities, there would be no civilization. Agriculture allows humans to produce food. The assurance of stored food increases the human population and brings ease and comfort, because a small number of people can provide food for many, while the rest can use their minds and strength in other ways. Some become skilled and agile, while others think and invent things for the comfort of others.</p>\n\n<p>Most importantly, agriculture ties humans to the land. Generation after generation, people live in one place, and this is when the seeds of art begin to sprout. As long as humans lived as nomadic hunters, they had little inclination to create beauty and refinement. Remember that Cro-Magnon man was a great artist while living in caves and staying in one place, but once he began migrating, his art died.”</p>\n\n<p>When humans constantly moved from place to place every day and every season, what use would painting or sculpture have? When people had to abandon their homes, what was the point of building beautiful and pleasant houses? But once people had food reserves, they stayed in one place, developed their surroundings, and art and industry flourished. Religion played a greater role in life, temples rose toward the sky, paintings and statues were created, writing emerged, and cities were born.</p>\n\n<p>We saw that when “Anthoney White” spoke about human artistic activity, he only mentioned visual and tangible arts such as sculpture, architecture, and painting, which have a physical and lasting presence. These arts can be “seen,” and humans can observe them across different eras.</p>\n\n<p>Paintings, sculptures, buildings, and decorative objects created by artists of ancient times have survived thanks to their visible and material nature. They show us how humans thousands of years ago took their first steps in creating such arts and with what mindset. But what can we say about the first musical creations of humanity?</p>\n\n<p>Unlike painting, sculpture, and architecture, music is not a material or permanent product. It cannot be touched or seen. It is an art that disappears the moment it is created and cannot be heard again in exactly the same way. Therefore, we can compare arts such as painting, sculpture, and architecture to the body, and music to the soul.</p>\n\n<p>For this reason, it is impossible to know exactly how and with what quality the first musical productions of humans were created — productions that undoubtedly originated from the human throat. However, what we are certain of is that humanity’s first artistic act was undoubtedly musical, even before painting or sculpting, and even before speech. Humans were able to produce the first musical sounds from their throats — however primitive and unrefined — long before reaching the final stages of evolution. They tried to imitate the sounds of nature around them, such as birdsong, the howling of animals, the sound of dripping water, and more.</p>\n\n<p>Mehdi Forough: “There is no doubt that among the six arts, music is the most effective and eloquent means of expressing human inner emotions and desires. Therefore, the history of its emergence should be considered the same as the history of the appearance of feeling human beings.</p>\n\n<p>Many scholars have correctly placed the invention of music before the invention of spoken language. They argue that from the very beginning of creation, to express and describe various spiritual states such as longing, excitement, ecstasy, need, sorrow, joy, and the like — all manifestations of love, desire, and hope — humans found themselves in need of music even before spoken language. They turned to the melody of their own voice because music and singing were the most natural and ready means to express these states.”</p>\n\n<p>Naturally, humanity’s first reaction when facing attacks by wild animals, terrifying natural phenomena, or bullying by stronger individuals was nothing but crying, shouting, and wailing. The fear caused by the unknowns of the surrounding environment gradually led humans to sing meaningless sounds to overcome fear and calm themselves. These primitive lamentations later became the foundation of religious music in subsequent eras.</p>\n\n<p>Alec Robertson: These people (the first inhabitants of Mesopotamia), who were among the earliest farmers, were animists. To them, all of nature appeared alive and conscious. Their god of thunder, “Ramman,” destroyed their fields and crops, and “Ea,” the god of the depths, sent floods upon them. However, they believed it was possible to appease and calm these gods through the sound of the human voice and musical instruments. These mental concepts, probably inherited from even earlier prehistoric times, formed the basis of their religious ceremonies in the temples.</p>\n\n<h2>How Did Music Come Into Existence?</h2>\n\n<p>There is no evidence that Neanderthals were familiar with music. However, historians attribute the invention of the drum and flute to the Andongones people. They lived in both cold and hot regions, often near reed beds. In warm areas, hollow trees grew. When the wind passed over the reeds, it produced pleasant sounds that attracted human attention. Humans then made the first musical instrument from a reed. Woodpeckers, by pecking trees with their beaks, also produced pleasant sounds. Later, humans learned that striking a small stick on a larger hollow piece of wood — especially hollow trees — could create pleasing sounds.</p>\n\n<p>In the early stages, humans used the drum as a warning signal in emergencies and during hunting. Later, they invented different ways of playing the drum to indicate various dangers or situations. On dark nights, they would dance, sing, and clap to stay awake. In this way, their experience with playing the flute and drum increased during the nights. Eventually, over 50,000 to 70,000 years, this music evolved into its modern form.</p>\n\n<h2>Legends of Different Nations About the Origin of Music</h2>\n\n<p>Every nation has expressed the story of this divine gift in its own special way through legends and tales. It can be said that all nations and ethnic groups have created such stories and passed them down orally for thousands of years.</p><img class=\"wp-image-2318 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/music_history_3.jpg\" alt=\"کیسار ( نوعی تنبور) مشابه چنگ قرن نوزدهمی\" width=\"810\" height=\"810\" /> A kissar (a type of tanbur) similar to a 19th-century harp in the Kunsthistorisches Museum in Vienna[/caption]\n\n<h3>Greece</h3>\n\n<p>The Greeks praised Hermes for bestowing music upon them. However, Hermes was the god of commerce and merchants, so it may seem unlikely that he had anything to do with music. Yet we should not forget that he was also the messenger and herald of the other gods.</p>\n\n<p>According to legends, he was born in a cave on Mount Cyllene in Arcadia. Zeus impregnated Maia in the darkness of night while all the gods were asleep. At a wondrous dawn, Hermes was born. Maia wrapped him in swaddling clothes and quickly fell asleep. However, Hermes freed himself from the swaddling and escaped to Thessaly. He stole his brother Apollo’s cattle, killed them, and used their intestines to make the first lyre (the same instrument mentioned earlier). He then returned to the cave and went back into his swaddling clothes.</p>\n\n<p>When Apollo discovered the theft, he complained to Maia that Hermes had stolen his cattle. Maia looked at Hermes and said it was impossible since he was still wrapped in his swaddling. At this point, mighty Zeus intervened. He had been watching Hermes the entire time and ordered him to return the cattle to Apollo, but did not punish him.</p>\n\n<p>While the argument continued, Hermes began playing the lyre. At first, this angered Apollo, but after hearing the sound of the lyre, his opinion changed. He took the lyre in exchange for his cattle. Later, Apollo became a master of this string instrument and made it one of his own symbols.</p>\n\n<h3>China</h3>\n\n<p>The Chinese were familiar with music long before this. Four thousand five hundred years ago, the powerful emperor Huang Ti ordered the invention of music and instructed the wise men of his country to take the songs of birds and the murmur of streams as their model.</p><img class=\"wp-image-2319 size-large\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/music_history_4-1024x576.jpg\" alt=\"انواع ساز های به کار رفته در موسیقی باستانی چین\" width=\"1024\" height=\"576\" /> A view of various instruments used in ancient Chinese music[/caption]\n\n<h3>Japan</h3>\n\n<p>In Japan, there was a time when the sun goddess became angry and hid in her cave. No matter how hard people tried, the only trick they could think of was to sing continuous and successive melodies to encourage her to come out. Even today in Japan, during a solar eclipse, people sing extremely ancient songs.</p>\n\n<h3>North America</h3>\n\n<p>People of one of the North American tribes believe that music was granted to them by a god named Tskanipuka. They think he built a bridge from turtles and whales, and when singing and playing music on it, the sun would rise.</p>\n\n<h3>Abyssinia (Ethiopia)</h3>\n\n<p>The Abyssinians also have a god in the form of a dove. According to them, this god taught them reading and writing and bestowed music upon them.</p><img class=\"wp-image-2320 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/music_history_5.jpg\" alt=\"ساز قدیمی لیر رومی در مصر باستان\" width=\"810\" height=\"810\" /> An ancient Roman lyre in ancient Egypt[/caption]\n\n<h3>Germanic</h3>\n\n<p>In Germanic legends, Heimdall, the gatekeeper of the heavens, has a gigantic horn that he has buried under the Tree of Life. Whenever he blows it, the Day of Judgment will begin. On that day, he will take all singers and musicians under his protection and, since he himself was a musician and singer, he will intercede for them.</p>\n\n<p>In this way, all ancient nations have mixed the history and story of the emergence of music with tales and legends. Even in the Middle Ages, it was believed that music was invented by Jubal, the son of Cain.</p>\n\n<p>What has been said so far is a history of the beliefs and legends of ancient nations or tribes living at lower stages of civilization. However, we — as people living in the age of natural sciences — do not look upon these ancient legends with contempt. Instead, we strive to understand the beauty and wisdom hidden within these stories in the best possible way.</p>\n\n<h2>Religious Music</h2>\n\n<p>Six thousand years ago, the first advanced civilization emerged in Mesopotamia, between the Tigris and Euphrates rivers. This land later became the homeland of us Iranians, and our ancestors established great dynasties across this vast region. In all societies and civilizations such as Mesopotamia, Egypt, India, and others, music from the very beginning served the religious needs of society and was under the control of priests and religious institutions.</p>\n\n<p>Alec Robertson: What is clear is that singing and playing music (in Mesopotamia) was the responsibility of specific individuals […] just as in medieval Europe this task was assigned to those who lived in places adjacent to and dependent on the temples.</p>\n\n<p>Mehdi Forough: Herodotus says about the way ancient Iranians worshipped: “The Magi sing and chant during prayer and worship.” In the Zoroastrian religion, prayer to the one God, Ahura Mazda, was generally accompanied by singing. In the Vendidad, Fargard III, the first paragraph states: “O holy Creator of the material world, where is the happiest place on Earth?” Ahura Mazda replies: “O Spitama Zarathustra, the happiest place on Earth is where a righteous man, holding sacred wood and barsom, milk and mortar in his hands, raises his voice with pleasant religious melody and praises Mithra, the god of the plains and meadows…”</p>\n\n<p>When humans reached the stage where they could refine and soften the harsh and unpleasant sounds of their throats, they moved beyond that stage. They learned to create the first “high and low” tones by using two sounds and produced the very first and most primitive melody of their own creation.</p>\n\n<p>Zigmund Spath: A perfectly logical question arises here: “To create a melody, how many tones are at least needed?” The answer can be: “Two tones,” because one of the oldest melodies in the world can be considered the “cuckoo,” which consists of only two tones. This same melody served as a means for early humans to call and address one another until they reached urban life.</p>\n\n<p>As humans passed this stage, they were able to create melodies using several tones with more ups and downs, which naturally produced a more pleasant and appealing result.</p>\n\n<p>Alec Robertson: In the fourth millennium BC (in Mesopotamia), only one tune or one religious hymn (psalm) was sung in honor of one or more gods. Religious ceremonies generally had the form of lamentation and mourning.</p>\n\n<p>Mehdi Forough: We see that poetry and music were an inseparable part of the lives of our Aryan ancestors at least six and a half centuries before Christ.</p>\n\n<p>Alongside the development of vocal music, wind and percussion instruments were gradually invented, followed by string instruments. These were placed at the service of vocal music so that religious music could be performed more magnificently and with higher quality.</p>\n\n<p>Alec Robertson: Vocal pieces were called “ersema,” meaning hymn, and were accompanied by a reed instrument with a tongue called “sem.” Other instruments used in this period included a primitive horizontal flute placed in front of the mouth called “tig ba tigi” […] and a hand-held frame drum called “adapa” […]. In the third millennium BC and the beginning of the first kingdoms in the twenty-eighth century BC, our information about Mesopotamian instruments increases. […] From this period onward, in addition to percussion instruments, we encounter a type of “lyre” called “algar” and two special types of harp.</p>\n\n<p>Some time later, religious hymns and melodies related to prayers became more diverse, and each found a specific application.</p>\n\n<p>Alec Robertson: Around 1830 BC […] the form and content of religious chanting had completely transformed and become more complete. The number of religious hymns reached twenty-seven types, and the instruments also evolved accordingly.</p>\n\n<h2>Secular Music</h2>\n\n<p>About six centuries after this period, music gradually stepped out of the exclusive domain of religion and found a place in worldly gatherings. At this time, musicians and singers emerged whose activities were not in the spiritual and divine realm, but related to the material and everyday life of the people.</p>\n\n<p>Alec Robertson: “Along with linguistic and religious worlds, music gradually connects with ordinary people’s lives and blends with work, livelihood, and the social world of people. Undoubtedly, only priests and temple workers were masters of musical knowledge and preserved its records, and only they knew the role of music in worship. However, during the Assyrian period (from about 1270 to 606 BC), secular music first gained importance and then dominance.”</p>\n\n<p>Musicians found their way into royal courts, and their presence became an essential part of every celebration and ceremony. Court musicians gained special privileges. Gradually, court musicians began to leave the palaces and perform for the general public in open spaces. In fact, these musicians also served earthly and material purposes. Such conditions undoubtedly opened the way for music to reach popular and social aspects as well.</p>\n\n<p>Apparently, at first, this new class of popular musicians were the same performers of religious music who, taking advantage of the tolerance of religious authorities, were also able to appear in this new role. These artists had a dual position comparable to the situation of some singers during the Qajar period. We know that some of these singers spent most of the year in private, often hedonistic gatherings, singing ghazals accompanied by instruments. But when the months of Muharram and Safar arrived, they would practice piety and perform ta’zieh and noheh in religious gatherings.</p>\n\n<p>Javad Badie Zadeh: From my early youth, when I had a place in gatherings and parties, I was, so to speak, like a frog that lives in two worlds — or like a chick that was killed both at funerals and at weddings. In fact, I was a singer who was accepted in celebration and joy gatherings, private parties, rawda and mourning sessions, and even in circles where scholars and clerics gathered. However, in private parties I sang with instruments, and in gatherings where instruments were not needed or where the attendees displayed religious appearances, I would still have a place and would recite ghazals or Molavi’s Masnavi without instruments.</p>\n\n<p>Hassan Mashhoun himself witnessed how much Sheikh Taher Khorasani, known as Zia’ al-Zakerin and a man of the pulpit familiar with religious sciences, was skilled in singing old tasnifs:</p>\n\n<p>Hassan Mashhoun: “One day when Zia’ al-Zakerin was supposed to come to my house, I ran into Mirza Abdollah Khan Davachi (Davami) — who is now the only master familiar with the radif of singing, rhythmic melodies, and old tasnifs — in the street and asked him to join our gathering as well. After we entered the house, Zia’ al-Zakerin and his talented son Mohammad Resayi had arrived.</p>\n\n<p>The conversation turned to music, melodies, and old tasnifs. Mirza Abdollah Khan would ask Zia about his mistakes in performing old melodies and tasnifs, and he would perform the desired melody without hesitation and resolve the doubts of Master Davami. The point was to show his mastery and vast knowledge of tasnif melodies. Abolhasan Saba and Gholam Hossein Banan greatly benefited from the late Zia’ al-Zakerin’s knowledge of music.”</p>\n\n<p>It is worth mentioning that court ta’zieh sessions during the Qajar period, contrary to popular belief, were not limited to religious music and mourning performances. There were always sections in these ceremonies where entertaining and humorous shows, and even secular music, were performed by the “amaleh tarab” (entertainers). Etemad al-Saltaneh wrote in his memoirs about Muharram in the year 1306 AH (1267 solar):</p>\n\n<p>“Yesterday it was decided that I would go to the court house in the morning after the rawda. I heard that last night the ta’zieh of ‘Deir-e Soleiman’ was performed in Tekyeh Dowlat, and the British and Italian ambassadors with their retinues had come to watch. After the ta’zieh ended, Ismail Bazzaz, the famous imitator, entered the tekkiyeh with about two hundred imitators and entertainers with white and fake beards and various European, Roman, and Iranian costumes, performing obscene movements. The ta’zieh assembly became worse than a theater.”</p>\n\n<p>Here is another account on the subject:</p>\n\n<p>Javad Badie Zadeh: “In the old ta’zieh performances, programs were not performed solely to make people cry. In those old ta’zieh, there were programs that often made the audience laugh so loudly that people outside the tekkiyeh or the place where the ta’zieh was held would involuntarily start laughing without even knowing what funny act had been performed.”</p>\n\n<h2>The Emergence of Musical Instruments</h2>\n\n<p>The most natural means of singing is the human chest, throat, and tongue, and the simplest way to create rhythm is with the hands. Humans kept rhythm by clapping and accompanied melodies and tunes with it. Later, they invented wind instruments such as horns and flutes from animal horns and plants they had at hand. Using their sense of hearing, they made holes in them according to astronomical beliefs and sacred numbers. These have now evolved into various forms.</p>\n\n<p>They also invented various drums and percussion instruments and worked on their development.</p>\n\n<p>Archaeological excavations have uncovered wind and string instruments such as harps and flutes dating back several thousand years before Christ. In the ancient site of Kul-e Farah, there are also carvings of daf and harp belonging to the Elamite civilization. By inventing instruments and musical devices, humans imitated the melodies and tunes that created pleasant and appealing feelings in their minds and continuously took steps toward their perfection.</p>\n\n<p>Along with progress in music and learning to produce beautiful and attractive sounds, humans have always sought ways to record and preserve their music and have made great efforts in the field of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a7%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%ae%da%86%d9%87-%d9%86%d8%aa-%d8%ae%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">musical notation and note reading</a>.</p>\n\n<p>In 2012, flutes made of bird bone and mammoth ivory were discovered in a cave in southern Germany. Using carbon dating, scientists have shown that these flutes are between 42,000 and 43,000 years old. These instruments are considered the oldest musical tools discovered in the world.</p><img class=\"wp-image-2321 size-large\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/music_history_6-1024x576.jpg\" alt=\"قدیمی ترین ساز جهان در غار آلمان\" width=\"1024\" height=\"576\" /> The oldest musical instrument in the world found in a German cave[/caption]\n\n<p>Knowing the origin and source of music is extremely useful for us because discovering this point greatly helps in understanding its nature. That is why scholars and philosophers have proposed many theories on this subject, among which we should mention Spencer, Nietzsche, Hausegger, and Herder.</p>\n\n<p>In recent periods, scholars and researchers have chosen a more reliable path by studying the conditions of primitive (savage and semi-savage) nations.</p>\n\n<p>The French were the first to study this subject. First, <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%DA%98%d8%a7%d9%86-%DA%98%d8%a7%da%a9_%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%b3%d9%88\">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</a> in his work “Dictionary of Music” in 1767, and after him, de Laborde in his book “Essay on Ancient and Modern Music” in 1780 raised this discussion. The travel-loving people of England also conducted research on this topic after the French, and recently the Germans have given these studies great depth under the title of comparative musicology.</p>\n\n<h2>Music from a Scientific Perspective</h2>\n\n<p>From the viewpoint of music theory, different and connected sounds are called music when they can create a connection between minds and are not limited by an abstract boundary.</p>\n\n<p>From the perspective of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%85-%d8%af%d8%b1-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">rhythmology</a>, music is divided into two categories:</p>\n\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Metric (with beat)</li>\n<li>Non-metric (without beat)</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>Metric music refers to music that has a regular meter, such as pieces composed for dance, pishdaramad, marches, and so on.</p>\n\n<p>Non-metric music is that which does not have a fixed and known meter. The best example of this is traditional singing in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a>. However, this type of singing should not be considered without rhythm; these avaz have their own special <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%88%d8%b2%d9%86-%d8%ae%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">meter reading</a> and rhythm reading, which should be studied in the context of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music theory</a> and the various forms of this music.</p>\n\n<p>Today, music is examined within the framework of music theory based on the three elements of rhythm, melody, and harmony.</p>\n\n<p><strong>To learn about the guitar and piano and get familiar with important and practical details and tips for buying these instruments, please read the articles <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%86%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%da%af%db%8c%d8%aa%d8%a7%d8%b1/\">Comprehensive Guide to Buying a Guitar</a> and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%86%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d9%be%db%8c%d8%a7%d9%86%d9%88/\">Comprehensive Guide to Buying a Piano</a>.</strong></p>\n\n<h3>Rhythm</h3>\n\n<p>In general, rhythm can be described as an organized and orderly collection of sounds and silences with specific durations or time values. For example, the tick-tock of a clock and the beating of the pulse or heart are all combinations of sound and silence that reach our ears with equal time intervals. <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%85-%d8%ae%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%b1-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">Rhythm reading</a> refers to the science and training related to reading these sounds and silences and observing their time values.</p>\n\n<p>Rhythm is the factor that creates order and coordination in musical pieces and gives shape and structure to the melody. The importance of rhythm in music is so great that it can be said that no piece can be composed without rhythm.</p>\n\n<h3>Melody</h3>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%84%d9%88%d8%af%db%8c/\">Melody</a> is actually the feeling that arises from the combination and connection of high and low notes along with rhythm or beat. After rhythm, melody holds a very special place in the world of music.</p>\n\n<h3>Harmony</h3>\n\n<p dir=\"RTL\"><span lang=\"FA\">In <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">music theory</a>, harmony refers to the process in which different sounds are created at the same moment in time and are heard and analyzed in coordination with each other. These sounds can be played simultaneously by performing different notes on one instrument or with the help of several different instruments in a harmonious way.</span></p>\n\n<p dir=\"RTL\"><span lang=\"FA\">The knowledge of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d9%88%d9%86%db%8c/\">harmony</a> or coordination is one of the main and fundamental topics in music theory.</span></p>\n\n<p>Please share your opinions or questions about this article in the comment section to help improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2025-04-22 07:13:20', 0, '2025-04-22 07:13:20', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1459, 'posts', 2, 'en', NULL, 'History of Iranian Music Part 1', NULL, 'The history of Iranian music reflects the country’s culture, history, and social transformations. Music evolves under the influence of historical events, cultural interactions, and changing lifestyles. Iranian music has undergone continuous transformation while preserving its identity. Its study requires examining cultural context, musical structure, key figures, and transmission methods. This history is divided into pre-Islamic and post-Islamic periods, evolving from modal (maqami) to the radif-based system. Despite changes, Iranian music has significantly influenced neighboring regions and remains an essential expression of national identity and artistic heritage.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>Before beginning the examination of the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">history of Iranian music</span></strong>, we must first explore the history of the land of Iran, its culture, customs, and traditions, because they have a direct impact on the auditory culture of its people and the type of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/music-what-is-it/\">music</a> they produce. Continue with us...</p>\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>Excerpt from the Introduction of the Book “A Look at Music” by Ruhollah Khaleqi Regarding the Examination of the History of Iranian Music</h2>\n<p>Music is the representative of humanity’s innate taste and must correspond to human feelings and translate human imagination. Since taste, talent, and feeling do not share a common standard among the people of different nations, every country has its own special music that carries the memories of past days and reflects the feelings and emotions of the people who were raised and lived in that land. There is no doubt that the antiquity of civilization, the quality of life in different periods, revolutions, events, historical incidents, joyful memories, and sorrowful times of the past have all come together to shape today’s music and the history of Iranian music.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, because world civilization has brought nations closer and exposed humanity to various feelings, the arts of every nation also undergo change and transformation through interaction with other nations and take on a fresh color and flavor in every historical period.</p>\n<p>For example, just as the language and script of Iran — which are the most important signs of our nationality — have changed due to interaction with other peoples, and our arts of painting, sculpture, and architecture have taken on a special appearance in every era, our customs, habits, and way of life have also changed. Is it conceivable that our feelings, emotions, and tastes — of which the history of Iranian music is also a result — would continue on their previous path without change?</p>\n<p>Naturally, our music, like our other arts, has not belonged solely to us. Every melody has emerged from somewhere, mixed with our national melodies, and, under the influence of our feelings, has undergone a long period of transformation until it has taken the form of today’s tunes. Although we cannot definitively and specifically determine the origin and main reasons for the creation of every melody, we still consider all of them, at present, as part of the history of our Iranian music, because our taste and talent have nurtured them, and these melodies are the result of various events of past periods and, in short, the thoughts, beliefs, and essence of our feelings and emotions.</p>\n<p>Throughout the history of Iranian music, if we have adopted a melody from others, there is no doubt that our taste and talent have not been without effect on it. We have changed it according to our level of understanding and perception and have shaped it in such a way that it cannot be called the music of others; rather, it has become part of the history of Iranian music and belongs to our nation.</p>\n<p>Preserving the works of the past and investigating the quality of the sciences, customs, and industries of our predecessors not only ensures the continuity and strength of a nation’s identity but also helps the progress of world civilization and the discovery of historical unknowns of other nations. Today, archaeologists search with great seriousness and effort in the ruins of past eras, and although the artifacts they find often hold little value in the eyes of non-experts, because they are historically significant, they are all kept in museums, and foreign tourists examine them with utmost care and discover historical unknowns from these very artifacts.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, scholars and men of learning find books written by Iranians in Persian and Arabic — whose manuscripts are often rare and unique — in libraries, copy them, and translate the Arabic versions into Persian, thereby becoming familiar with the way of thinking and beliefs of the people who lived in this country in former times.</p>\n<p>Similarly, men of poetry and literature find rare collections of poetry and publish them, making them accessible to those of taste. All this effort is made so that the works of the past — which are the scientific and literary foundation and the basis of this country’s civilization — are not lost and destroyed but remain eternal forever.</p>\n<p>Music is also one of the fine arts that, as mentioned, has a special appearance in every country, and we must be aware of how it has changed and transformed in the different periods of the history of Iranian music. Although most of the old melodies of the history of Iranian music have been lost and only their names remain, the tunes that are now in our hands — and with which our nation’s taste has a special interest and connection — must be preserved.</p>\n<p>Of course, we should not be satisfied with this alone; we must strive to complete and elevate this art. As mentioned, it is our duty to nurture it according to today’s needs and, as much as possible, to work on recording and preserving it, because music is also one of the greatest signs of our nation’s civilization and the representative of the taste of artists in the history of Iranian music — a land that has been attached to this art for more than two thousand years. Wherever our history has been discovered, music has also been the comforter of the soul and the solace of the spirit, and the history of Iranian music is as old as the history of the land of Iran.</p>\n<p>Of course, to preserve <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/iranian-music/\">Iranian music</a>, merely writing the melodies of the history of Iranian music is not enough. Gradually, more performers and composers must become familiar with the scientific rules and principles of music so that they can find the way to preserve it, write the melodies they compose for various instruments in a style common in the world, promote them, and gradually familiarize the ears of people in other countries of the world with Iranian music and the history of Iranian music.</p>\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>From What Angles Should the History of Iranian Music and the Music of Other Parts of the World Be Examined?</h2>\n<p>In a principled and precise examination to find the reasons and roots of the creation of different musical structures and the similarities between these structures in the history of music of various lands — and specifically in this article, the history of Iranian music — different characteristics and parameters must be considered and examined. Therefore, below we refer to some of these characteristics that should be studied:</p>\n<p><strong>1. The Relationship of Music with History, Culture, Customs, and Traditions:</strong> The influence of culture and history on the growth of a nation’s music, its connection with the social, cultural, and political conditions of society, and also the impact of musical works on various societies and cultures are discussed and examined in this category.</p>\n<p><strong>2. The History of Music Production:</strong> This history includes the time of the emergence of playing and singing, as well as the history and evolution of different musical styles.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Characteristics of Music:</strong> It pays attention to the architecture of sound and rhythmic structure, songwriting, musical terms, and other technical and artistic features of music. (This actually refers to <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/what-is-music-theory/\">the science of music theory</a> and its important sections for examining music, namely <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/rhythm-in-music/\">rhythm</a>, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/melody/\">melody</a>, and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/harmony-in-music/\">harmony</a>.)</p>\n<p><strong>4. Prominent and Inspiring Personalities:</strong> The artistic <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/musicians-biography/\">biographies</a> of some great creators of music (musicians, composers, instrumentalists, and singers) and their outstanding works that were effective in changing the music of their time.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Methods of Music Transmission:</strong> Finally, methods of recording, broadcasting, and disseminating musical pieces — such as various formats, radio, television, concerts, festivals, and the internet — are examined in this category.</p>\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>The History, Culture, and Customs of the Land of Iran</h2>\n<p>In examining the history of Iranian music, we must first realize that ancient Iran was a vast country that approximately included about 30 modern countries and naturally had diverse ethnic groups, religions, and cultures. Therefore, we should not expect a uniform and unified culture, because these ethnic groups progressed and evolved together with the help of each other’s achievements, not each one alone. Thus, all the achievements of this great land can be claimed by each of these ethnic groups.</p>\n<p>In the period after the conquest of Iran by the Arabs and the conversion of Iranians to Islam, because the Arabs did not have a dominant culture, they were culturally conquered by the Iranians and accepted the superior Iranian culture. They worked with other conquered peoples to flourish it and, from this Iranian culture, created Islamic culture, whose foundation and basis was the same ancient Iranian culture.</p>\n<p>In the first centuries of Islamic culture — that is, until approximately the Abbasid Caliphate — a unified culture existed throughout these Islamic lands. From that time onward, the conquered lands became independent, and each added its own regional and ethnic color and flavor to this mother culture according to its common cultural roots. It is for this reason that there are many common cultural and artistic aspects among Muslim countries. Most Orientalists mistakenly consider these common features to have been taken from Arab culture, which is due to a superficial view of the history and culture of these lands.</p><img class=\"wp-image-3475 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/250px-Sassanid_Music_Plate_7thcentury.jpg\" alt=\"The influence of ancient music on the industries of that time\" width=\"250\" height=\"247\" /> The influence of ancient music on the industries of that time[/caption]\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>Different Historical Periods for Examining the History of Iranian Music</h2>\n<p>Considering this very important event in the history of the land of Iran — namely, the Arab invasion — the history of Iranian music can be divided into two main sections:</p>\n<p>1. <strong><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/ancient-iranian-music/\">Ancient Period (Pre-Islamic)</a></strong><br>\n2. <strong><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/iranian-music-after-islam/\">Post-Islamic Period</a></strong></p>\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>The Structure of Iranian Music Throughout History</h2>\n<p>Various events and bitter and sweet incidents in the history of every land affect the culture, customs, traditions, and beliefs of its people. This mutual influence and being influenced inevitably occurs in life and brings about many changes. When these changes happen gradually over time, they alter the culture, customs, and traditions of a society with a gentle slope. However, parts of these customs and traditions always remain constant and enduring throughout the centuries.</p>\n<p>In music as well, as an important part of the culture and tradition of a society, we will witness structural changes many times throughout history. When we look at the history of Iranian music, we observe changes in the structure and names of musical melodies that have arisen according to the demands of time and historical events.</p>\n<p>When we examine the history of Iranian music, we arrive at modal music that existed from the Achaemenid period and even earlier. This music reached its peak of flourishing during the Sassanid period, especially during the reign of King Khosrow Parviz, and even spread to other countries in the region. Today, when we look at the music of neighboring countries, we clearly see the historical traces of Iranian music in their melodies and music.</p><img class=\"wp-image-3476 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/images-2.jpg\" alt=\"An example of ancient stone reliefs depicting a harp player\" width=\"183\" height=\"276\" /> An example of ancient stone reliefs depicting a harp player[/caption]\n\n<p>As mentioned, Iranian music became the foundation of the music of neighboring countries to such an extent that in the current modal music of Egypt, Syria, and Lebanon — which includes 52 maqams — 30 maqams have Persian names. Of the 15 common maqams in Iraq, 8 also have Persian names.</p>\n<p>Similarly, the modal music (dastgahi) of Azerbaijan, which includes 7 dastgahs, has been influenced by the history of Iranian music. The name of one of the dastgahs in Azerbaijani modal music is “Shur,” which is also the most widespread dastgah in the current modal music of Iran.</p>\n<p>As you have realized by now, Iranian music in the past was modal, and during the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Safavid_dynasty\">Safavid</a> and Qajar periods, that modal music gave way to dastgahi music. The foundation and basis of the structure of today’s <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/radif/\">radif</a> is the same modal music of the past.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions about this article on the history of Iranian music, please share them in the comments section to help improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-04-23 13:00:21', 0, '2024-08-30 19:41:37', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1460, 'posts', 3, 'en', NULL, 'History of Iranian Music Part 2, Music of Ancient Iran (Pre-Islamic)', NULL, 'Ancient Iranian music has deep roots, with evidence tracing back to early civilizations such as Elam. During the Achaemenid era, music played roles in religious rituals and warfare, while in the Sasanian period it flourished, notably through figures like Barbad. This music was largely improvisational, modal, and closely tied to poetry and spiritual practices. Following the advent of Islam, restrictions emerged, yet the tradition persisted. Historically, music was also studied as a theoretical science مرتبط با mathematics. Despite transformations over time, many of its عناصر and concepts remain present in contemporary Persian traditional music.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>As mentioned in the previous article <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/history-of-iranian-music/\">History of Iranian Music – Part 1</a>, Iranian music from a historical perspective can be examined in two main sections: <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Ancient Iranian Music</span></strong> (pre-Islamic) and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/iranian-music-after-islam/\">Iranian Music After Islam</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>History of Ancient Iranian Music</h2><img class=\"wp-image-2357 size-large\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/music_history_before_islam_2-1024x1024.jpg\" alt=\"Statue of an oud player from the Parthian Empire\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" /> Statue of an oud player from the Parthian Empire[/caption]\n\n<p>After the arrival of the Aryans, because dance and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/music-what-is-it/\">music</a> were not used or highly valued in performing Aryan religious rituals, these two arts did not develop among them as they should have, and left almost no trace in the works and writings of that era.</p>\n\n<p>The term “Ancient Iranian Khonyā” is the pure transliteration of Iranian words, meaning “Music of Ancient Iran” or, in other words, “Traditional Iranian Music.” This Iranian term is now well understood, but it is rarely used compared to the more common term “Authentic Music,” which carries the same meaning.</p>\n\n<p>Using excavated evidence, such as the statue discovered in Susa, the history of ancient Iranian music clearly dates back to the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elam\">Elamite Empire (2500–644 BCE)</a>. Obviously, very little information is available about the music of this period. The only exception is the surviving musical instruments such as guitars, lutes, and flutes that were invented and played. It is said that musical instruments like the “barbat” originated in this era, around 800 BCE.</p>\n\n<p>Herodotus states that during the Achaemenid Empire, also known as the “Persian Empire,” music played an important role, especially in court ceremonies. He says that in ancient Iran, music was essential for religious ceremonies of worshiping God.</p>\n\n<h3>Music in the Achaemenid Period</h3>\n<p>We do not have sufficient information about the music of this period. However, a nation that considered itself a rival to Greece, had historical relations with its people, and left highly valuable industrial works such as the carvings of Persepolis, was undoubtedly not devoid of music. Moreover, since the Achaemenid state succeeded the civilized governments of its time, it adopted their industries to the extent that they matched Iranian taste. Here are references made by two famous Greek historians on this matter:</p>\n\n<p>Herodotus (484–406 BCE) writes in his history about the Iranians:</p>\n<blockquote style=\"padding-right: 40px;\">\n<p>“The Iranians have no altar for offering sacrifices to God and the sacred, they do not light sacred fire, they do not pour wine on graves, but one of the Magi is present and recites one of the sacred religious hymns.”</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>From Herodotus’ statement, it appears that the Iranians at that time had special music for religious ceremonies, and the hymn he refers to may have been one of the hymns from the Avesta, which we will mention later.</p>\n\n<p>Xenophon (435–355 BCE), the Greek historian, in his book on the characteristics of Cyrus the Great (<em>Cyropaedia</em>, which has also been translated into Persian), mentions several sentences from which conclusions can be drawn. In one place he writes:</p>\n<blockquote style=\"padding-right: 40px;\">\n<p>“Cyrus the Great, when attacking the Assyrian army, according to his custom began a hymn that the soldiers sang loudly with great respect and discipline. When the hymn ended, the free men set off with equal steps and perfect order.”</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>In another place he says:</p>\n<blockquote style=\"padding-right: 40px;\">\n<p>“Cyrus ordered for the movement of the army that the sound of the trumpet would be the signal for departure, and as soon as the trumpet sounded, all soldiers must be ready and move.”</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>A few lines later he adds:</p>\n<blockquote style=\"padding-right: 40px;\">\n<p>“At midnight, when the trumpet of departure sounded, Cyrus ordered the army commander to stand with his companions at the front of the soldiers’ ranks. Then Cyrus said: ‘As soon as I reach the intended place and the attacks of the two armies draw near, I will sing the war hymn, and you must immediately respond to me.’ At the time of the attack, as he had said, Cyrus began the war hymn and the entire army sang with him.”</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>From the above, it appears that the Iranians of the Achaemenid period had a special type of music for war and battle, which undoubtedly had an effect on arousing the soldiers’ emotions. Their martial musical instruments, such as trumpets and drums, were also used at such times, and war hymns were a good means of arousing courage and bravery in the soldiers and ensuring their success on the battlefield. However, it is not known in what mode these hymns were composed or what poetry was sung with them.</p>\n\n<p>Furthermore, from Xenophon’s statements, it is clear that just as the Iranians had special music for the battlefield and conflict, they were undoubtedly familiar enough with music to have another type for leisure and entertainment. For example, the word “ney” (flute) is repeatedly mentioned in Herodotus’ book, and it seems that playing this instrument produced special effects.</p>\n\n<p>Regarding the existence of religious music in this period, the reference made by Herodotus — especially considering that the oldest part of the Avesta consists of hymns and prayers that were recited during worship with special ceremonies — leaves no doubt. From a musical point of view, one part of the book <em>Yasna</em>, known as the <em>Gathas</em>, is the oldest part of the Avesta and consists of effective hymns that probably had special melodies.</p>\n\n<p>The <em>Gathas</em> are located in the middle of the <em>Yasna</em> and consist of seventeen religious hymns. It is likely that these hymns belong to Zoroaster himself. The <em>Gathas</em> can be called poetry or at least rhymed prose, which proves that they were not devoid of melody and music.</p>\n\n<p>It should also be noted that the Iranian people, who originally separated from the Indians as one of the important branches of the Aryan race, had common characteristics from the beginning. Just as the Indian religious book known as the <em>Vedas</em>, a remnant of the ancient Sanskrit language, also contains religious and literary hymns and songs of the Aryan race, comparing the contents of the <em>Vedas</em> and the <em>Avesta</em> clearly shows that the Iranian and Indian peoples are children of one mother, and their feelings, ways of thinking, and tastes had common aspects. As mentioned, Iranian and Indian music also share many similarities.</p>\n\n<p>It should also be pointed out that if there had been any trace of musicians, singers, or musical instruments from this period — or at least if a carving, inscription, or relief had been found in one corner of historical monuments — it would have brought us much closer to understanding the subject. However, no such trace remains, and experts have not discovered anything related to this in the ruins of Achaemenid monuments.</p>\n\n<p>After the Achaemenid period, because Alexander and his successors were Greek, and after them the Parthians were also considered foreigners and showed no interest in Iranian civilization, just as Greek civilization entered Iran during this period, it is possible that some of their musical melodies were also adopted.</p>\n\n<p>For example, one of the Parthian kings named “Ardavan I” was fond of Greek literature, and in his assemblies Greek plays such as the tragedies of “Euripides” (the Greek poet whose sorrowful plays are famous, 480–406 BCE) were performed. Perhaps those who knew and performed Greek plays were not unfamiliar with Greek music and sang Greek songs when appropriate. However, due to the lack of sufficient documents and information, we cannot discuss this topic further at present.</p>\n\n<h3>Music in the Sassanid Period</h3>\n<p>Since the Sassanid kings were of Iranian descent and had a special interest in expanding and developing Iranian civilization, sciences and industries flourished during their time, and music also gained more popularity and prominence due to their encouragement and support. Ardashir the Sassanid divided the people into different classes, among which musicians formed a special class and held a specific position with him.</p>\n\n<p>The literary and musical taste of Bahram Gur is well known. It is written that he brought four hundred musicians and singers from India to Iran. During his time, musicians were given precedence over other classes of people, and he greatly favored them.</p>\n\n<p>Music in the Sassanid period, especially during the time of Khosrow Parviz, advanced even further. Barbad was his special musician, and stories about him are recounted in literary and historical books such as Ferdowsi’s <em>Shahnameh</em> and Nizami’s <em>Khosrow and Shirin</em>. It is said that Barbad invented a number of melodies and tunes that he played in the presence of Khosrow Parviz. He is famous for having composed 360 musical melodies for the days of the year and playing one of them each day, appropriate to the occasion, so that Khosrow would not tire of repeating the same tunes.</p>\n\n<p>His position with Khosrow was so high that anyone with a request would convey their matter to the king through him. For example, when the king’s favorite horse named Shabdiz died and no one dared to inform him, Barbad prepared a special melody on this subject and informed Khosrow of the incident. Nizami has listed the names of thirty melodies of Barbad that he composed for the thirty days of the month in <em>Khosrow and Shirin</em> and begins the matter with this verse:</p>\n<blockquote style=\"padding-right: 40px;\">\n<p>“Praise Barbad who gave voice to song, and gave melody to the organ’s sound.”</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>The names listed by Nizami in <em>Khosrow and Shirin</em> may not necessarily be Barbad’s original inventions, but because most of the names are pure Persian and others have mentioned the same names to a greater or lesser extent before him, one can reasonably assume with near certainty that these melodies are remnants of the Sassanid period. Examples include: Ganj-e Bad-Avar, Arayesh-e Khorshid, Meshg-daneh, Nimruz, Ramesh-e Jan, Nushin Badeh, Nowruz, Shabdiz, Kabk-e Dari, Kaykhosrowi, Sabzeh dar Sabzeh, Sarvestan, Mah, Shadervan-e Morvarid.</p>\n\n<p>* Shabdiz is probably the name of the melody that Barbad composed upon the death of Khosrow Parviz’s favorite horse, as previously mentioned.</p>\n\n<p>Another musician of this period is Nakisa. It is not exactly clear whether she was Iranian or Greek. She is known to have been skilled at playing the chang (harp), and her name is still on people’s lips.</p>\n\n<p>Other musicians that can be mentioned are Bamshad (Bamshaz), Ramtin (Ramin), Azad, and Archangi, who were also among the entertainers and singers of Khosrow Parviz’s court.</p>\n\n<p>Some Iranian and European historians and researchers have collected the names of ancient Iranian melodies and have discussed which of them belong to the Sassanid period. Among them, the late scholar Abbas Eqbal Ashtiani, through research and investigation in dictionaries and collections of poetry, gathered the names of a number of musical melodies and wrote an excellent article in the magazine <em>Kaveh</em>.</p>\n\n<p>Here, for example, we mention the names of a few more melodies from among those he found and considers related to the Sassanid period:</p>\n\n<p>Palizban – Sabz-e Bahar – Bagh-e Siavoshan – Rah-e Gol – Shad Bad – Takht-e Ardeshir – Ganj-e Sukhteh – Del Angizan – Chekavak – Khosrowani – Nowruz (Bozorg – Kuchak – Khara) – Jameh Daran – Naheft – Dar Gham – Golzar – Roshan Cheragh – Gol Nush – Zir Afkan.</p>\n\n<p>Among the names of these melodies, Chekavak, Khosrowani, Nowruz-e Bozorg, Nowruz-e Kuchak, Nowruz-e Khara, Jameh Daran, Zir Afkan, and Naheft still remain among the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/dastgah-recognition-and-voice-recognition-in-iranian-music/\">dastgahs of Iranian music</a>, but it is clear that they have undergone many changes. A melody whose tune was not written down has, after this long period, taken on a different form. All we can say is that it has probably lost all resemblance to what it originally was.</p>\n\n<p>Mr. Mehdi Mofatteh has also mentioned some of these melodies with poetic examples in several articles (see the magazine <em>Payam-e Novin</em>, first year, issues 2 to 8).</p>\n\n<p>Nevertheless, the luxury of the Sassanid court itself is another proof that music in that period had dignity, respect, popularity, and prestige — especially since some kings of this dynasty, such as Bahram Gur and Khosrow Parviz, were pleasure-loving, merrymaking, and enjoyment-seeking people.</p>\n\n<p>Another proof of the importance of music in the Sassanid period is that after the conquest of Iran, the Arabs adopted Iranian music exactly as it was. Among a people who were completely devoid of this art, musicians and singers appeared, and Iranian musicians gained great status and prestige during the time of the Caliphs and became the adornment of their assemblies. This will be mentioned later in the article on Iranian music after Islam.</p>\n\n<p>It is also worth noting that according to Mazdak, who brought a religion during the time of Qobad the Sassanid, God sits on a throne in the heavens like a king, and four forces — perception, intellect, memory, and joy — stand before Him. These four forces, with the help of six ministers, manage the affairs of the world, and the ministers move among twelve spirits, one of which is the “singer.” (Quoted from the history by the late Pirnia)</p>\n\n<p>The force of joy, which represents music, held the same importance in Mazdak’s view as the other three forces. This belief may have been influenced in Mazdak’s mind by the conditions of the Sassanid era. Just as the Sassanids, especially some kings of this dynasty, had established a special rank for the class of musicians and singers and honored them greatly, Mazdak also imagined one of the forces standing before God to be the force of joy and music.</p>\n\n<p>Regarding the instruments that were common in the Sassanid period, as can be understood from the content of stories, legends, poems, and writings on the subject, the nay, ghazhak, rabab, barbat, and chang were in use at that time.</p>\n\n<p>The ghazhak is an instrument still seen in the cities of Baluchistan, especially in Davar Panah and Iranshahr. The locals call it “ghichak.” It is similar to the kamancheh and is played with a bow made of several horsehair strings. Some hold the instrument in the left hand and draw the bow with the right hand, while others hold the instrument with the right hand and the bow with the left hand.</p>\n\n<p>Of course, since the musicians seen in those areas did not have great skill and were wandering street performers, their melodies were not particularly attractive or pleasing to experts. There is no doubt that the current shape of this instrument has changed greatly from its original form. It appears that even a few centuries ago it had no more than two strings, and as “Land” writes in his book on the investigation of Arab scales, it seems unlikely that this instrument was originally played with a bow.</p>\n\n<p>Farabi also does not mention its name in his book on music. Moreover, as can be understood from his statements, the rabab — which closely resembled the ghazhak and had two strings — was not played with a bow.</p>\n\n<p>The barbat or barbat is one of the ancient instruments of Iranian music. The Arabs adopted it from the Iranians. It is an instrument held against the side and played, which later evolved into the “oud.”</p>\n\n<p>Among the instruments mentioned, a sample of the chang can be seen in Taq-e Bostan. Some Sassanid kings had a special interest in hunting, and a hunting scene is visible in Taq-e Bostan. It appears that musicians accompanied the king even during hunts.</p>\n\n<p>From the hunting scene in Taq-e Bostan, it appears that the king and his companions are hunting in a marsh, and female chang players are sitting in a boat, providing entertainment through musical melodies. It is very surprising that this instrument, which is one of the oldest musical instruments of Iran and was known even before the Iranians by other countries such as the Egyptians, Sumerians, Elamites, and Assyrians, and was also common in the Sassanid period, gradually disappeared in Iran, while this instrument has now been perfected and is common in European orchestras.</p><img class=\"wp-image-2358 size-large\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/music_history_before_islam_3-1024x1024.jpg\" alt=\"Barbat or oud from the 7th century AD. Silver plate from Iran, Sassanid, circa 7th century.\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" /> Barbat or oud from the 7th century AD. Silver plate from Iran, Sassanid, circa 7th century.[/caption]\n\n<p>The term “Ancient Iranian Khonyā” (Music of Ancient Iran) is a term related to the post-Achaemenid era. The Persian or Parsi language used in the courts, along with the Pahlavi language, was the official language of the Sassanid Empire (224–642 CE) and included most of the same words and the same grammar as the Pahlavi language. Therefore, the word used for Iranian music during the Sassanid period and in the pure contemporary Persian language is actually the word “Khonyā.” Although the exact origin of the ancient Iranian music model is still unclear, research has revealed new aspects of it.</p>\n\n<p>Barbad, one of the musicians of the Sassanid imperial court, invented the first Middle Eastern musical system known by the royal name “Khosravani” and dedicated it to King Khosrow (Khosrowan). Also, many of the current names of musical modes existed in ancient Iranian music as well. The dastgahs have been passed down orally from ancient times to the present, although many modes and melodies were probably lost due to the Arab invasion, which considered music an immoral matter.</p><img class=\"wp-image-2228 size-large\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/Museum_of_Persepolis_Darafsh-1024x737.jpg\" alt=\"One of the instruments remaining from ancient Iranian music\" width=\"1024\" height=\"737\" /> One of the instruments remaining from ancient Iranian music[/caption]\n\n<p>Ancient Iranian music was actually a form of improvisation, based on a series of analogous models that had to be preserved. Students and masters had a traditional relationship that declined in the twentieth century in parallel with the movement of music education to universities and conservatories.</p>\n\n<p>A common performance of this music consists of a <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/form-pish-daram/\">pish-daram</a> (initial introduction), daramad (introduction), <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/form-tasnif/\">tasnif</a> (song with rhythm and singer), <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/chaharmezrab/\">chaharmezrab</a> (rhythmic), and a selected number of gushehs. Unconventionally, these parts can be changed or omitted. With the approach of the end of the Safavid period (1502–1736), the performance of complex 10-, 14-, and 16-beat gushehs was discontinued. Today, pieces are played in 6 or at most 7 beats, which is regrettable.</p>\n\n<p>Many melodies and modes of ancient Iranian music can be found in Turkish maqams and Arab music. To explain this, we must clearly state that after the Arabs invaded the Persian Empire, they introduced the conquered lands as the “Islamic world.” Although most Arab rulers banned music-related activities, others ordered musicians to compose pieces in book form, which in Arabic was called “Kitab al-Musīqī al-Kabīr,” meaning the comprehensive book of music. In addition to the influences of the Sassanid Empire, this is another reason why the traditional melodies of Turkey, Syria, Iraq, and Egypt include names of Iranian scales and modes.</p><img class=\"wp-image-2359 size-large\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/music_history_before_islam_4-1024x1024.jpg\" alt=\"The Great Book of Music, a study of Iranian music theory in its time.\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" /> The Great Book of Music, a study of Iranian music theory in its time.[/caption]\n\n<p>Throughout history, ancient Iranian music has been more associated with voice. Even singers played a central role in its creation and performance. They decided which state was appropriate to express and which dastgah was related to it. In many cases, the singer was also responsible for choosing the poem to be sung. If the program required a singer, the singer had to be accompanied by at least one wind or string instrument and at least one percussion instrument. Of course, it was possible to have a set of instruments together, but the main singer retained their role. Sometimes it was necessary for the instrumentalists to accompany the singer by playing several solo pieces.</p>\n\n<p>Traditionally, ancient Iranian music was performed while seated in places decorated with cushions and carpets. Sometimes candles were lit in these places. The group of musicians and the singer would determine the dastgah and which gushehs to perform according to the time, place, and other conditions.</p>\n\n<p>Before the Arab invasion in ancient Iranian music, melodies were hummed or played in which verses from the Avesta, the religious book of the prophet Zoroaster of the Mazdean religion, were recited or performed in a manner consistent with that atmosphere. The word “gah” has two meanings: in Pahlavi, it means both “Gath” (Avestan worshiper) and “time.” The states of “one gah, two gah, three gah, four gah, five gah, six gah, and seven gah” were used to express stories, and the Gaths were hummed from one to seven.</p>\n\n<p>It has recently been discovered that the “Rast” state was used to express stories about righteous people or correct actions, and “Shekasteh” was used to express stories about wrongdoers. The “Homayun” state was performed during the morning prayer. Most of these states that existed in ancient Iranian music, except possibly “six gah” and “seven gah,” are still used in the modern system. Despite these evidences, musicians still tend to directly attribute the meaning of “gah” to “time” or “place” of movement of a musical instrument.</p><img class=\"wp-image-2360 size-large\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/music_history_before_islam_5-1024x1024.jpg\" alt=\"Types of santur in the Achaemenid period of Iran and the Assyrian period of Mesopotamia\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" /> Types of santur in the Achaemenid period of Iran and the Assyrian period of Mesopotamia[/caption]\n\n<p>When Islam was accepted as the dominant religion of Iran, the decision of the rulers was to completely ban music and later to discourage people from humming Mazdean prayers in these modes. Ancient Iranian music or traditional Iranian music continued to be played in the courts until the twentieth century. During the rule of extremist Islamists in the Middle Ages, music was played secretly.</p>\n\n<p>It is worth noting that several movements are probably not considered ancient and are only very old. As has been the case throughout history, ancient Iranian music continued to function as a spiritual tool and was less viewed as a means of entertainment. Musical works had the possibility of extensive change from beginning to end and usually involved changes between low, thoughtful, and heroic performance sections called “tahrir.”</p>\n\n<p>The interaction of religious texts as ghazals replaced the great ghazals composed by Sufi poets of the Middle Ages, especially Hafez and Jalal al-Din Rumi (Mowlavi). In addition, ancient Iranian music is a perception of the pains, sufferings, and joys of a fragmented yet unified civilization, many of whose remnants, such as gushehs (Hosseini, Jameh Daran, Shabdiz, etc.), testify to this claim.</p><img class=\"wp-image-2361 size-large\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/music_history_before_islam_6-1024x1024.jpg\" alt=\"Image of pre-Islamic instruments\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" /> Image of pre-Islamic instruments[/caption]\n\n<h2>Ancient Iranian Music</h2>\n<p>Books written in the past about Iranian music have mostly considered the scientific and theoretical aspects of music, because our predecessors considered music as one of the branches of mathematical wisdom. In music books, sounds were determined according to mathematical ratios. As Avicenna says in his book <em>Dānishnāmeh-ye Alā’ī</em>:</p>\n\n<blockquote>\n<p>“The art of music has two parts. One is composition, and its subject is melodies (notes), and they look at their agreement and disagreement. The second is iqa‘ (rhythm and duration of notes), and its subject is the times that occur between notes and the beats that succeed one another, and they look at their weight and lack of weight. The ultimate goal in both is to create melodies. Thus, the art of music is a theoretical science.”</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>In the old terminology, the word “naghmah” was used instead of musical sound, and “lahn” was used in contrast to <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/melody/\">melody</a>. However, in modern terminology, these two words are synonymous.</p>\n\n<p>Sound is produced by the collision of two things, and this collision creates a movement in the air. The transfer of air particles affects our eardrum, and the sound is perceived. Musical sound, which in old terminology was called “naghmah,” has pitch and must be pleasing to the ear. The purpose is that its high and low degree can be distinguished relative to another sound, and its duration and extension can be determined.</p>\n\n<p>The ancients determined the pitch of sounds by the shortness and length of the strings of instruments. The instrument used for experimentation was the oud, which is one of the ancient instruments of Iranian music. It appears that this instrument is the same as the Iranian barbat, which later changed its name, because it is very similar in shape and construction.</p>\n\n<h2>Musical Intervals</h2>\n<p>In the terminology of the ancients, the distance between two musical sounds (<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/musical-interval/\">musical interval</a>) was called “bu’d” (dimension). They divided the intervals, as is still done today, into two classes: pleasant and unpleasant. However, instead of these two terms, they used the words “malayem” and “motanāfer” or “ettefāq” and “tanāfor.”</p>\n\n<p>Melodies are formed by combining intervals, provided that the different intervals follow one another in a suitable and pleasing manner so that the listener derives pleasure from their totality. Some have added the condition that musical melodies must be accompanied by appropriate words and poetry to arouse emotions. Of course, the poetry must also be arranged according to the rules of prosody in terms of meter.</p>\n\n<p>They divided the intervals into three categories:</p>\n<p>1) <strong>‘Azām</strong>: An interval that exceeds an octave, which is what is called a compound interval in modern musical terminology. However, it should be noted that not every interval larger than an octave is considered ‘azām; only the following four intervals are meant:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Perfect octave, which in old terminology was called “dhū al-kull.”</li>\n  <li>Perfect eleventh, which was called “dhū al-kull wa al-arba‘.”</li>\n  <li>Perfect twelfth, which was called “dhū al-kull wa al-khams.”</li>\n  <li>Perfect fifteenth, or the interval between two octaves, which was called “dhū al-kull marratayn.”</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>2) <strong>Awsāt</strong>: They called the perfect fourth “dhū al-arba‘” and the perfect fifth “dhū al-khams,” and these two intervals were called “awsāt.”</p>\n\n<p>The six intervals mentioned above were pleasant intervals, which in old books were called “al-bu‘d al-ettefāq.”</p>\n\n<p>3) <strong>Saghār</strong>: These are smaller intervals, also called melodic intervals, which are not very pleasing to the ear and for this reason were called unpleasant or, in old terminology, “ab‘ād al-tanāfor.” Among the small intervals, the most important is the “tanīnī” interval, which today is called the major second (one whole tone) and was considered the most pleasant of the melodic intervals.</p>\n\n<p>In old music books, intervals were also determined by numerical ratios, as is still done today. For example, the intervals mentioned earlier have ratios as follows:</p>\n\n<table style=\"text-align: justify; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Old name of interval</th>\n      <th>Modern name of interval</th>\n      <th>Ratio representing the interval</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>dhū al-kull marratayn</td>\n      <td>perfect fifteenth</td>\n      <td>4</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>dhū al-kull wa al-khams</td>\n      <td>perfect twelfth</td>\n      <td>3</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>dhū al-kull wa al-arba‘</td>\n      <td>perfect eleventh</td>\n      <td>3/2 + 2</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>dhū al-kull</td>\n      <td>perfect octave</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>dhū al-khams</td>\n      <td>perfect fifth</td>\n      <td>2/1 + 1</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>dhū al-arba‘</td>\n      <td>perfect fourth</td>\n      <td>3/1 + 1</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>tanīnī</td>\n      <td>major second</td>\n      <td>8/1 + 1</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<p>Apart from the intervals mentioned above, old music books mention many other intervals and determine their ratios, but mentioning all of them would make the text too long. In reducing intervals, they went to great lengths, to the point that distinguishing the interval between two sounds becomes difficult. For example, they said that an interval with the ratio 201/200 is difficult or even impossible to perceive.</p>\n\n<p>The ‘azām intervals, i.e., the pleasant intervals, were called the noblest intervals. However, to form melodies, one cannot limit oneself to them, because, as they said, repeating them causes boredom.</p>\n\n<p>For this reason, the dhū al-kull (octave) is divided into smaller intervals, and the saghār intervals are also used to form melodies, especially so that singing them is easy.</p>\n\n<p>The dhū al-kull (octave) is composed of one dhū al-khams (perfect fifth) and one dhū al-arba‘ (perfect fourth), or consists of two dhū al-arba‘ and one tanīnī (two perfect fourths and one major second). Therefore, depending on the precedence or succession of dhū al-arba‘, dhū al-khams, and tanīnī, different types appear. Thus, one dhū al-kull can be formed in the following ways:</p>\n\n<p>Do — Fa — Do (1)<br>\nDo — Sol — Do (2)<br>\nDo — Fa — Sol — Do (3)<br>\nDo — Fa — Si♭ — Do (4)</p>\n\n<p>In number (1), the octave is formed by one perfect fourth and one perfect fifth.<br>\nNumber (2) is the reverse of number (1).<br>\nIn number (3), one tanīnī occurs between two perfect fourths.<br>\nIn number (4), after two perfect fourths, one tanīnī is placed.</p>\n\n<h2>Ancient Musical Instruments</h2>\n<p>The ancients considered the human throat and larynx to be the noblest of musical instruments, because the human voice is natural and close to words. Since the words combined with melodies contain meanings, the human voice is more effective than other instruments (which are artificial and man-made) and this perception is completely correct and appropriate.</p>\n\n<p>Musical instruments were of two types: “dhawāt al-awtār” or stringed and plucked instruments, and “dhawāt al-nafkh” or wind instruments.</p>\n\n<p>Dhawāt al-awtār were of two kinds: one in which, like the chang, qanun, and santur, one string was tied for each sound and the fingers did not press on the strings; the second type was those on which the fingers of the left hand pressed on the strings, each of which was called a “watar,” such as the oud, rabab, and tanbur.</p>\n\n<p>In other words, one type was those whose melody was produced from open strings (mutlaq), and the other type was those whose melody was produced from both open strings and stopped strings (maghrūf).</p>\n\n<p>The ancients called the open string of any instrument “mutlaq.”</p>\n\n<p>When one of the fingers of the left hand pressed on a string, it was called “maghrūf” in old terminology.</p>\n\n<p>The rabab was an instrument with two strings. It is believed that it later evolved into the kamancheh.</p>\n\n<p>The tanbur existed in two forms: Khorasani and Baghdadi. They also had two strings and were plucked instruments. Unlike the oud, which had a short and curved neck, the neck of the tanbur was long and straight. These instruments, like the tar, were fretted, and Farabi in his book on music has described in detail how they were fretted.</p>\n\n<p>Dhawāt al-nafkh are wind instruments whose sound is produced by human breath, such as the sornā, nay, and halq (throat). Therefore, the throat was also considered among the wind instruments, and after that, the nay or ney, which had a natural and pleasant sound, was considered the best musical instrument.</p>\n\n<h3>Oud</h3>\n<p>This instrument, which was originally the same as the Iranian barbat, is one of the very old instruments. Farabi was the first to describe it in detail. Because he considered it more complete than the other common instruments of his time, he used it for experimentation, and others followed him.</p>\n\n<p>In the old terminology, the oud had four strings. Later, a fifth string was added to it. Initially, the strings of the oud were single, but later, to make its sound stronger, each of its strings was tied in pairs (like the strings of the tar). The strings of this instrument were tuned in perfect fourths, and the names of its strings from low to high were as follows:</p>\n\n<p>1 – Bam (lowest)<br>\n2 – Mathnā (second)<br>\n3 – Mathlath (third)<br>\n4 – Zīr (highest)<br>\n5 – Ḥādd (added later)</p>\n\n<p>Because the oud originally had four strings and the strings were counted from the highest, the second string was called “mathnā” and the third string “mathlath” (in Persian “do tā” and “se tā”).</p>\n\n<p>So that beginners would know how to finger the oud, frets were tied on this instrument. In the old terminology, a fret was called “dastān,” and the act of fretting was called “dastān neshānī.”</p>\n\n<p>On the other hand, there was disagreement among musicians about the placement of the dastāns. Different frets were tied from top to bottom on the strings, and the last fret with the open string (mutlaq) of the string formed a perfect fourth interval. The frets were also named after the fingers, as follows:</p>\n\n<p>1 – Mutlaq (open string)<br>\n2 – Sabbābah or first finger<br>\n3 – Wustā or second finger<br>\n4 – Binsar or third finger<br>\n5 – Khinsar or fourth finger (little finger)</p>\n\n<p>Between the mutlaq and the sabbābah, two other frets called “zā’id” and “majannab” were tied, and between the sabbābah and the binsar, two frets called “wustā” were placed: one higher called the Iranian wustā and the other lower called the Zalzal wustā, which had a slight difference in pitch from each other. (The intervals of these frets relative to the open string will be mentioned later.)</p>\n\n<p>The Iranian wustā fret was called “wustā al-furs” or “old wustā” in music books.</p>\n\n<p>The Zalzal fret was tied on the oud by Mansur ibn Ja‘far Zalzal, who lived about a century and a half before Farabi and was one of the famous Iranian musicians. This fret became known by his name.</p>\n\n<h3>Divisions of the Perfect Fourth (Dhū al-Arba‘)</h3>\n<p>Earlier, we briefly described the divisions of the perfect fourth. Now we add that in the old terminology, the set of three intervals that formed the perfect fourth was called a “jins,” and from the succession of two jins (“two perfect fourths”) with one interval placed between them, maqams or adwār were created. It was also possible to form each maqam from the succession of one perfect fourth and one perfect fifth.</p>\n\n<p>In ancient music, six types of perfect fourth were common, as follows:</p>\n\n<table style=\"text-align: justify; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>No.</th>\n      <th>Abbreviated signs</th>\n      <th>Abjad letters</th>\n      <th>Equivalent modern notes</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>ṭ ṭ b</td>\n      <td>a d z ḥ</td>\n      <td>Do – Re – Mi – Fa</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>2</td>\n      <td>ṭ b ṭ</td>\n      <td>a d h ḥ</td>\n      <td>Do – Re – Mi♭ – Fa</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>b ṭ ṭ</td>\n      <td>a b h ḥ</td>\n      <td>Do – Re♭ – Mi♭ – Fa</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>ṭ j j</td>\n      <td>a d w ḥ</td>\n      <td>Do – Re – Mi♯ – Fa</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>j j ṭ</td>\n      <td>a j h ḥ</td>\n      <td>Do – Re♯ – Mi♭ – Fa</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>6</td>\n      <td>j ṭ j</td>\n      <td>a j w ḥ</td>\n      <td>Do – Re♯ – Mi♯ – Fa</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<p>Considering the explanation given earlier about strong fourth combinations, we can now say that of the six fourths above, the first three are strong (because two tanīnī intervals are used in their formation) and the second three are soft (because only one tanīnī interval exists in them).</p>\n\n<p>They have also mentioned another perfect fourth that is composed of three majannab and one baqiyyah. In this case, of course, the “baqiyyah” would be a quarter tone and not a half tone.</p>\n\n<p>In order to easily calculate fourths and fifths, it should be known that since a perfect fourth consists of two and a half tones, i.e., five half tones or ten quarter tones, every perfect fourth is equal to the fraction 10/4, and the perfect fifth, which has one more tone, is equal to 14/4.</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, a fourth that is formed from three majannab and one baqiyyah, its baqiyyah will be a quarter tone.</p>\n\n<h3>Types of Perfect Fifth (Dhū al-Khams)</h3>\n<p>There were twelve types of perfect fifth. By comparing their intervals with the frets of the oud, it appears that six of them had slight differences that could not be shown with the old Abjad letter notation. Therefore, we first mention the six perfect fifths whose intervals are indisputable, and then explain the other six perfect fifths.</p>\n\n<p>The first six perfect fifths are as follows:</p>\n\n<table style=\"text-align: justify; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>No.</th>\n      <th>Abbreviated signs</th>\n      <th>Abjad letters</th>\n      <th>Equivalent modern notes</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>ṭ ṭ b ṭ</td>\n      <td>ḥ or yd yh yḥ</td>\n      <td>Fa – Sol – La – Si♭ – Do</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>2</td>\n      <td>ṭ b ṭ ṭ</td>\n      <td>ḥ or yb yh yḥ</td>\n      <td>Fa – Sol – La♭ – Si♭ – Do</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>b ṭ ṭ ṭ</td>\n      <td>ḥ ṭ yb yh yḥ</td>\n      <td>Fa – Sol♭ – La♭ – Si♭ – Do</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>ṭ j j ṭ</td>\n      <td>ḥ or yj yh yḥ</td>\n      <td>Fa – Sol – La♯ – Si♭ – Do</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>j j ṭ ṭ</td>\n      <td>ḥ y yb yh yḥ</td>\n      <td>Fa – Sol♯ – La♭ – Si♭ – Do</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>6</td>\n      <td>j ṭ j ṭ</td>\n      <td>ḥ y yj yh yḥ</td>\n      <td>Fa – Sol♯ – La♯ – Si♭ – Do</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<p>The six perfect fifths mentioned above are formed by adding one tanīnī interval (major second) to the end of the six perfect fourths.</p>\n\n<p>Among the other six perfect fifths that we must mention, two of them that are written with Abjad letters have no error; it should only be noted that the baqiyyah interval in these two perfect fifths is smaller than a half tone and equal to a quarter tone, which must be calculated as the fraction 1/4 when counting.</p>\n\n<p>Also, these two perfect fifths, unlike the others, instead of being formed from four intervals as per the rule, consist of five intervals:</p>\n\n<table style=\"text-align: justify; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>No.</th>\n      <th>Abbreviated signs</th>\n      <th>Abjad letters</th>\n      <th>Equivalent modern notes</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>7</td>\n      <td>ṭ j j j b</td>\n      <td>ḥ or yj yh yz yḥ</td>\n      <td>Fa – Sol – La♯ – Si♭ – Si♯ – Do</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>8</td>\n      <td>j ṭ j j b</td>\n      <td>ḥ y yj yh yz yḥ</td>\n      <td>Fa – Sol♯ – La♯ – Si♭ – Si♯ – Do</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<p>In the next four perfect fifths, several points must be kept in mind:</p>\n\n<p>1 – The baqiyyah interval in numbers 9, 10, and 11 is equal to a quarter tone.</p>\n<p>2 – In number 9, the sign “yd” is written instead of “La♯,” whereas this note is between “yd” and “yh.” Since there was no sign for it, they were satisfied with this method. There is also no fret at this interval on the frets of the oud. One can guess that when playing, the musicians either did not notice this difference, which is only a quarter tone, or they moved the “yd” fret, which was the binsar of the mathlath string, slightly toward the khinsar.</p>\n<p>3 – In numbers 10, 11, and 12, the sign “yw” is used instead of Si♯, whereas this note is between “yh” and “yw.” Since there was no sign for it, they wrote it this way. On the other hand, because there was also no fret at this interval on the frets of the oud, either the musicians moved the “yw” fret, which was the zā’id of the mathnā string, slightly toward the mutlaq, or they simply did not pay attention to this difference. Since they named and numbered the strings of the oud sequentially, they only discussed it from a scientific point of view.</p>\n\n<table style=\"text-align: justify; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>No.</th>\n      <th>Abbreviated signs</th>\n      <th>Abjad letters</th>\n      <th>Equivalent modern notes</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>9</td>\n      <td>j j j ṭ b</td>\n      <td>ḥ y yb yd yz yḥ</td>\n      <td>Fa – Sol♯ – La♭ – La♯ – Si♯ – Do</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>10</td>\n      <td>j b ṭ j j</td>\n      <td>ḥ y yā yd yw yḥ</td>\n      <td>Fa – Sol♯ – Sol♮ – La – Si♯ – Do</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>11</td>\n      <td>j j b ṭ j</td>\n      <td>ḥ y yb yj yw yḥ</td>\n      <td>Fa – Sol♯ – La♭ – La♯ – Si♯ – Do</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>12</td>\n      <td>ṭ j ṭ j</td>\n      <td>ḥ yā yj yw yḥ</td>\n      <td>Fa – Sol – La♯ – Si♯ – Do</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<p>Some have also written another type of perfect fifth composed of two tanīnī and two majannab, thus making thirteen perfect fifths.</p>\n\n<h2>Maqams of Ancient Iranian Music</h2>\n<p>As mentioned earlier, maqams of music can be formed from the succession of one perfect fourth and one perfect fifth. Therefore, each of the seven perfect fourths, when combined once with one of the thirteen perfect fifths, creates one maqam. In other words, from multiplying seven by thirteen, ninety-one different maqams appear, which they called ninety-one circles. Of these ninety-one maqams, they identified twelve as pleasant and called them the main maqams. They are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>‘Ushshāq – Navā – Būsalīk – Rāst – ‘Irāq – Iṣfahān – Zīr Afkand – Buzurg – Zangūlah – Rāhāvī – Ḥusaynī – Ḥijāzī.</p>\n\n<p>Now we can write the twelve maqams above with Abjad letters as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Maqam — Abjad letters</p>\n\n<p>1 – ‘Ushshāq a . . d . . z ḥ . . yā . . yd yh . . yḥ<br>\n2 – Navā a . . d h . . . ḥ . . yā yb . . yh . . yḥ<br>\n3 – Būsalīk a b . . h . . ḥ ṭ . . yb . . yh . . yḥ<br>\n4 – Rāst a . . d . w . ḥ . . yā . yj . yh . . yḥ<br>\n5 – ‘Irāq a . j . . w . ḥ . y . . yj . yh . . yḥ<br>\n6 – Iṣfahān a . j . h . . ḥ . y . yb yj . . yw . yḥ<br>\n7 – Zīr Afkand a . j . h . . ḥ . y . yb yj . . yw . yḥ<br>\n8 – Buzurg a . j . . w . ḥ . y yā . . yd . yw . yḥ<br>\n9 – Zangūlah a . . d . w . ḥ . y . . yj . yh . . yḥ<br>\n10 – Rāhāvī a . j . . w . ḥ . y . yb . . yh . . yḥ<br>\n11 – Ḥusaynī a . j . h . . ḥ . y . yb . . yh . . yḥ<br>\n12 – Ḥijāzī a . j . h . . ḥ . y . . yj . yh . . yḥ</p>\n\n<p>The dots placed between the letters indicate frets that are not used in forming that maqam. For example, between “alif” and “dāl,” two dots are placed, meaning “b” and “j” or the zā’id and majannab frets of the lowest string are not used in this maqam.</p>\n\n<p>Qutb al-Din Mahmud Shirazi in his book <em>Durrat al-Tāj</em> has also considered a few other circles pleasant and introduced them with the following names:</p>\n\n<p>Zangbār – Golestān – Bahār – Būstān – ‘Uzrī – Vāmeq.</p>\n\n<p>Some have also added six more maqams called āvāzah to the twelve maqams as follows: Gosht – Gardāniyah – Nowruz – Salmak – Māyah – Shahnāz.</p>\n\n<p>Some have also formed two branches from each of the twelve maqams, thus creating twenty-four branches and given them the following names:</p>\n\n<p>Zābolī – Awj – Nowruz-e Khārā – Māhūr – ‘Ushayrān – Nowruz-e Ṣabā – Mubarraq – Panjgāh – Maghlūb – Rāhāvī – Nīrīz – Nīshābūr – Rahāb – Bayātī – Homāyūn – Naheft – Segāh – Ḥeṣār – Chahārgāh – ‘Uzzāl – Nowruz-e ‘Arab – Nowruz-e ‘Ajam – Dogāh – Muḥayyar.</p>\n\n<h3>Names of the Degrees of the Scale in Ancient Iranian Music</h3>\n<p>As we said, the ancient musical scale consisted of eighteen notes and had seventeen intervals. Now we add that the ancients gave a name to each degree, and most of these names are now the names of melodies and gushehs, and even some of them, as will be mentioned later, are the names of today’s Iranian dastgahs. The names of the degrees of the scale, in order, were as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Yegāh – Nīm Bayātī – Nīm Ḥeṣār – ‘Ushayrān – Nīm ‘Ajam – ‘Irāq – Nīm Māhūr – Rāst – Zangūlah – Rāhāvī – Dogāh – Nahāvand – Segāh – Būsalīk – Chahārgāh – Ṣabā – ‘Uzzāl – Navā.</p>\n\n<p>Because the first note of the scale had no name in Persian, it was possible to call it Yegāh and use it instead of the word “tonic.”</p>\n\n<h2>The Effect of Melodies</h2>\n<p>In the books of ancient Iranian music, detailed discussion has been made about the effect of melodies and musical maqams. They attributed a specific effect to each maqam on human temperament. Therefore, it is not inappropriate to briefly state the opinion of the ancients on this subject:</p>\n\n<p>In the <em>Qābūs-nāmeh</em>, Chapter 36 on the etiquette of musicianship (printed version by Khaqan), composed by Kaykāvūs ibn Eskandar ibn Qābūs ibn Vashmgīr ibn Ziyār in 475 AH, it is stated:</p>\n\n<blockquote style=\"padding-right: 40px;\">\n<p>“When you sit in an assembly, observe: if the listener has a ruddy and sanguine complexion, play more on the low strings; if he has a yellowish complexion and is bilious, play more on the high strings; if he is dark-skinned, thin, and melancholic, play more on the third string; and if he is fair-skinned, fat, and phlegmatic, play more on the low strings (here it seems that either the author has made a mistake or this error has occurred due to the writer’s unfamiliarity, because instead of “bam” it should have been “mathnā”). These strings have been made for the four temperaments of people. Just as the sages of Rome and the scholars of the art of music have constructed this craft according to the four temperaments of people.”</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>There were also different opinions about the fact that each fret should be played at a specific time of the day and night. Some said that Ḥusaynī should be played at the end of the day, Rahāvī in the morning, Rāst at sunrise, Būsalīk in the afternoon, ‘Ushshāq at sunset, Navā before the evening prayer, and Ḥijāzī at midnight.</p>\n\n<p>There were also different opinions on this part.</p>\n\n<p>Some said that the above melodies are useful in treating diseases. For example, in the Persian translation of <em>Mujmal al-Ḥikmah</em>, which is a summary of the treatises of the Ikhwān al-Ṣafā, it is written as follows:</p>\n\n<blockquote style=\"padding-right: 40px;\">\n<p>“Thus, among the sages and philosophers, music has great benefit, and they have used it in many places, such as in the mihrabs for the acceptance of prayer and supplication, just as Prophet David would play the barbat in the mihrab and set his singing to it. And as in hospitals at dawn they would play it so that the patients would fall asleep and be relieved of their pains. And as they placed it in monasteries, and when the common people came for pilgrimage, the residents would play it so that the common people would turn to the path of repentance. And if today in some religious laws these are prohibited, it is because they use it in forbidden things and worldly pleasures, not what was the purpose of the sages.”</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>“Handwritten copy belonging to the late scholar Eqbal Ashtiani”</p>\n\n<p>The translator of <em>Mujmal al-Ḥikmah</em> says in another place:</p>\n\n<blockquote style=\"padding-right: 40px;\">\n<p>“A balanced voice between high and low keeps all temperaments in their proper place, and a voice that is out of balance disturbs all humors. When it exceeds the limit, it is like lightning and the like, which brings sudden death. Balanced, rhythmic, and equal voices moderate the temperament, move the nature, and may bring ecstasy. And every disease that exists in the body and in the soul has a type in music that brings it back to health.”</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>And in confirmation of the same matter, he says:</p>\n\n<blockquote style=\"padding-right: 40px;\">\n<p>“And when they saw that voice and what is set to voice has a complete effect on the soul, and the soul dominates the body, they established this science and practice so that the soul would receive effect from it and give it to the body and change the body from its state, so that if someone were skilled in this science and knew natural sciences well, he could remove any disease he wished from the body by means of a type of music.”</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>Regarding the states of the frets, they have also said:</p>\n\n<blockquote style=\"padding-right: 40px;\">\n<p>“‘Ushshāq, Būsalīk, and Navā produce courage and bravery. ‘Irāq, Iṣfahān, Nowruz, and Rāst cause moderate expansion and for this reason bring a delicate pleasure. Some other melodies cause weak expansion and excite the soul so that listening to them produces a state similar to madness, weakness, and contraction, such as Buzurg, Rahāvī, Zīr Afkand, Zangūlah, and Ḥusaynī. But one must choose poetry whose meanings are appropriate to the effect of that fret so that it causes complete effect.”</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>“Quoted from <em>Nafā’is al-Funūn fī ‘Arā’is al-‘Uyūn</em>”</p>\n\n<h2>Iqa‘ (Rhythm)</h2>\n<p>In the terminology of ancient music, rhythm was called “iqa‘.” Farabi has defined rhythm as follows:</p>\n\n<blockquote style=\"padding-right: 40px;\">\n<p>“Iqa‘ is the division of sounds into times that are limited in quantity.”</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>Safi al-Din Urmawi has given a more comprehensive definition and says:</p>\n\n<blockquote style=\"padding-right: 40px;\">\n<p>“Iqa‘ is the succession of a number of beats (naqrah in old terminology was used instead of “ḍarb”) that are separated from each other by times that have a limited quantity, and the times have a special arrangement in equal iqa‘ cycles that the healthy nature perceives as equal.”</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>If the above definition seems a bit complicated and difficult, it will become clear after becoming familiar with the old terms and explaining the following points.</p>\n\n<p>It should be noted that although the ancients mentioned various rhythms and worked with great precision on this subject, it is not possible to show the old rhythms with today’s notation style, because the division of rhythm into equal meters, which is now established, was not common in the past, and the matter was expressed differently. As they said:</p>\n\n<p>“When we pronounce two moving letters (such as ta-na), for the pronunciation of each of the two letters, the tongue strikes the roof of the mouth once, and there is a time between the two movements of the tongue.</p>\n\n<p>Now, if we consider each of these two letters as one beat or, in today’s terminology, one ḍarb, there is a time between them that separates the two. Of course, this time may be short, long, or medium. A short time makes it difficult to distinguish two sounds from each other, and a long time eliminates the effect of the first sound after hearing the second sound. Therefore, the time between two beats must be medium, but of course, while being medium, it may be short or long.</p>\n\n<p>The shortest common time between two beats was called the first time or iqa‘ time or time unit, and other times are measured by it. They expressed this matter in such a way that if another beat cannot be placed between two beats, the time between them is the shortest time and is considered the time unit.</p>\n\n<p>As can be seen, contrary to today, when the time unit is equal to the whole note and the largest duration or extension, the ancients considered the time unit as the shortest duration.</p>\n\n<p>The time unit is indicated by the letter alif, and if the times are longer than that, they are indicated with Abjad letters as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Time unit — alif<br>\nTwice the time unit — b<br>\nThree times the time unit — j<br>\nFour times the time unit — d<br>\nFive times the time unit — h</p>\n\n<p>This point should be kept in mind that instead of considering the beats as having a specific duration and forming the rhythm from their succession in a specific order, the ancients imagined the matter in reverse and gave the duration to the time between the beats. In practice, however, it becomes clear that what they meant by the time between beats is not silence and pause, but their real purpose from the time between beats is to determine the different durations of the beats. For example, if we want to interpret what they said and bring it into today’s notation style, we must use the following method.</p>\n\n<p>If the time between two beats is equal to the letter “alif,” i.e., the time unit, we show those two beats with an eighth note.</p>\n\n<p>If the time between two beats is equal to the letter “b,” we show the beats with two quarter notes.</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, if the time between two beats is equal to “j,” the beats will be dotted quarter notes, and if the time between them is equal to “h,” the beats will be written in the form of today’s half notes, and in all the above shapes, there will be no silence or pause between the two beats.</p>\n\n<h3>Types of Iqa‘</h3>\n<p>Iqa‘ is of two types: connected iqa‘ (mawṣil) and separated iqa‘ (mufaṣṣal).</p>\n\n<p>Connected iqa‘ is when the beats follow one another without pause or stop, and this type of iqa‘ is usually called “hazaj” iqa‘.</p>\n\n<p>Separated iqa‘ is when a silence occurs between every few beats. In this case, the silence between the beats is called “fāṣilah,” and every few beats that are separated from each other by one fāṣilah is called a “dawr.”</p>\n\n<p>Separated iqa‘, which in the old terminology had unequal times between the beats because the fāṣilah between the dawrs eliminates the equality of times, is of four types as follows:</p>\n\n<p>1 – Fast hazaj, in which the time between the beats is equal to the time unit.<br>\n2 – Light hazaj, in which the time between the beats is twice the time unit.<br>\n3 – Light-heavy hazaj, in which the time between the beats is three times the time unit.<br>\n4 – Heavy hazaj, in which the time between the beats is four times the time unit.</p>\n\n<p>Of course, in the four types of meter above, there is no interval between the beats. So the purpose of “fast” is that the duration of the notes is less, and gradually their duration increases until the heavy iqa‘, in which the notes take the form of dotted half notes, i.e., four times the fast beats.</p>\n\n<p>Separated iqa‘, depending on how many beats each dawr consists of, appears in the following forms:</p>\n\n<p>1 – If each dawr consists of two beats, it is called “mufaṣṣal al-awwal,” which is like duple meter.<br>\n2 – If each dawr consists of three beats, it is called “mufaṣṣal al-thānī,” which is like triple meter.</p>\n\n<p>In triple meter, it is possible for the beats of one dawr to be equal to each other, as in the example above, and it is also possible for them to be unequal.</p>\n\n<p>3 – If each dawr consists of four beats, it is called “mufaṣṣal al-thālith,” which is like quadruple meter.</p>\n\n<p>In this case as well, it is possible for the beats of each dawr to be unequal.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Note</strong></p>\n<p>Separated meters also appear in various forms. For example, the mufaṣṣal al-awwal, in which each dawr consists of two beats, can be presented in four different shapes.</p>\n\n<h3>Famous Iqa‘s</h3>\n<p>The famous rhythms mentioned in old music books are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>1 – <strong>Hazaj</strong>: In this meter, as mentioned, the beats follow one another without interval.</p>\n<p>2 – <strong>Light Ramal</strong>: A meter in which each dawr consists of two equal beats, or in other words, its beats succeed two by two.</p>\n<p>3 – <strong>Ramal</strong> or <strong>Heavy Ramal</strong>: Each dawr of which consists of one heavy beat and two light beats.</p>\n<p>4 – <strong>Heavy Second</strong>: Which, unlike ramal, each dawr consists of two heavy beats and one light beat.</p>\n<p>5 – <strong>Light-Heavy Second</strong>: Unlike the heavy second, each dawr consists of two light beats and one heavy beat, and it is also called “mākhūrī.”</p>\n<p>6 – <strong>Heavy First</strong>: Each dawr consists of three heavy beats.</p>\n<p>7 – <strong>Light-Heavy First</strong>: Each dawr consists of three light beats.</p>\n\n<p>Each of these seven meters, due to the shortness or length of the interval that occurs between each of its dawrs, can also appear in different forms, mentioning all of which would make the text too long.</p>\n\n<p>Now, for example, we compare two old rhythms of Iranian music with their poetic meter.</p>\n\n<p>Among them is a meter that was previously called <strong>heavy ramal</strong>, and its poetic meter was of two kinds: one equal to four fa‘ilātun (fa‘ilātun fa‘ilātun fa‘ilātun fa‘ilātun), and the other meter was as follows:</p>\n\n<p>fa‘ilun fa‘ilun maf‘ūlun maf‘ūlun fa‘ilun</p>\n\n<p>And its musical meter was written in the following form:</p>\n\n<p>ta-na-na-na-na-na tan tan tan tan tan tan ta-na-na</p>\n\n<p>In the past, prosodic meters were written differently in music. For example, instead of “fa‘ilun” they would say “ta-na-na,” and instead of “maf‘ūlun” they would write “tan tan tan,” or in the terminology of prosody, instead of the heavy sabab “tan,” and instead of the combined watad “ta-nan,” and instead of the small fāṣilah “ta-na-nan.”</p>\n\n<p>Today in notation, we also place an eighth note instead of one moving syllable, and we have placed a quarter note instead of a light sabab (or one moving and one quiescent syllable). So the eighth note is placed instead of a short syllable, and the quarter note replaces a long syllable.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions about this article on ancient Iranian music, please share them in the comments section to help improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-04-24 15:16:40', 0, '2024-08-30 19:41:58', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1461, 'posts', 4, 'en', NULL, 'History of Iranian Music Part 3, Iranian Music After Islam', NULL, 'After the advent of Islam, Persian music experienced significant changes due to historical events such as the fall of the Sassanian Empire and the Arab conquest. However, it did not disappear and continued to evolve over time. Persian music gradually spread to Arab regions and had a profound influence on the musical traditions of the Islamic world. During the Umayyad and Abbasid periods, Iranian musicians played a key role in developing and enriching this art. Moreover, with the contributions of scholars like Farabi and Avicenna, music transformed from a practical art into a theoretical science. Throughout different historical periods such as the Samanids, Safavids, and Qajar era, Persian music underwent various changes, yet it remained a fundamental part of Iranian cultural identity.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>As mentioned earlier, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/history-of-iranian-music/\">Iranian music from a historical perspective</a> can be examined in two main periods: <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/ancient-iranian-music/\">pre-Islamic Iranian music (ancient Iran)</a> and <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Iranian music after Islam</span></strong>. In this article, we examine the history of Iranian music after Islam. We first discuss how Iranian music entered Arab-speaking countries, introduce some prominent musicians of that era, and then divide post-Islamic Iranian music into different periods and examine each one separately. Stay with us...</p>\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>History of Iranian Music After Islam</h2>\n<p>Iranian music after Islam was heavily influenced by the Arab invasion and the fall of the Sassanid Empire. For a long time, music in Iran did not receive the attention it deserved. After the fall of the Sassanid dynasty and the establishment of the Islamic Caliphate, along with the events and upheavals resulting from this transformation, there was little opportunity to focus on music for a considerable period. However, the rich musical heritage and its widespread practice during the Sassanid era — especially during the reign of Khosrow Parviz — prevented Iranian music from being completely forgotten or destroyed after Islam.</p><img class=\"wp-image-2365 size-large\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/music_history_after_islam_1-1024x1024.jpg\" alt=\"Barbad (bottom right) playing in the court of Bahram Gur; a 17th-century illustrated manuscript of the Shahnameh.\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" /> Barbad (bottom right) playing in the court of Bahram Gur; a 17th-century illustrated manuscript of the Shahnameh.[/caption]\n\n<p>The singers and musicians who were scattered throughout Iran in the final years of the Sassanid Empire did not abandon their art despite all the hardships and turmoil. What was related to <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/music-what-is-it/\">music</a> and its terminology was passed down orally from generation to generation. When the opportunity arose during the Umayyad Caliphate, as we shall see, they began to spread and promote it. Thus, Iranian music after Islam flourished during the Umayyad period.</p><img class=\"wp-image-2366 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/music_history_after_islam_2.jpg\" alt=\"Hammered santur with Mesopotamian origin\" width=\"1920\" height=\"1920\" /> Hammered santur with Mesopotamian origin[/caption]\n\n<h2>Arabs’ Familiarity with Music Before Islam and the Beginning of the Islamic Era with Iranian Music</h2>\n<p>Martial musical melodies were common among the Arabs from ancient times, especially among their soldiers. These melodies were used to arouse the warriors’ emotions, and they would recite war poetry (rajaz) on the battlefield, which itself can be considered a form of music. They also had another type of melody, similar to rajaz and sung quickly, called nasib. However, apart from drums and horns, they apparently had no other musical instruments.</p>\n<p>The Arabs had melodies for entertainment that they called ghina (singing). Arab entertainment music was not very developed, and whatever little singing existed was unrestrained and in accordance with the rough and harsh habits and character of the desert-dwelling Bedouin Arabs.</p>\n<p>For this reason, Iranian music after Islam entered Arab countries and greatly influenced the music of Arab and Muslim nations, leading to significant changes and transformations in Arab music.</p>\n\n<h2>Music in the Islamic Era</h2>\n<p>After the Arab invasion and the fall of the Sassanid Empire, Iranian music also became common in Arab-speaking countries. The Arabs adopted music, like other sciences, customs, and industries, from Iran. The Arabs of the pre-Islamic Jahiliyyah period had little engagement with music. The only Arab poet before Islam who composed songs (taghanni) with his poetry was A’sha ibn Qays, a famous poet of the Jahiliyyah era.</p>\n<p>This poet frequented the courts of the kings of Hira and Iran. It is said that during the reign of Anushirvan he traveled to Ctesiphon (Mada’in), learned <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/iranian-music/\">Iranian music</a>, and became somewhat familiar with the Persian language. He even used Persian words and names of musical instruments such as nay (flute), sanj (harp), and barbat (oud) in his poems.</p>\n<p>Some say that the first time Iranians introduced their music to the Arabs was during the time of Abdullah ibn Zubayr, when he was repairing the Kaaba. Iranian architects and builders who were working on the project sang while working. The Arabs liked their singing, and some of the workers who knew music taught Iranian songs to the Arabs.</p>\n<p>According to the book Al-Aghani, the first Arab who learned Iranian melodies and sang Arabic poems with Iranian tunes was Sa’id ibn Misjah. He became fascinated with Iranian music, traveled to Iran for this purpose, and upon returning to his homeland, spread the Persian melodies he had learned in Mecca.</p>\n<p>Among the prominent Iranian musicians of this period was “Nashit al-Farisi” (died 80 AH), who was a companion of Abdullah ibn Ja’far ibn Abi Talib. Another was “Ibn Muhriz,” who lived during the time of Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik. “Yunus al-Katib,” who worked as a scribe in the Medina administration, also wrote books on music. Ibn al-Nadim in Al-Fihrist mentions that Yunus wrote books on music, including one titled “Kitab al-Ghanam,” though no copy of it has survived.</p>\n<p>Abu al-Faraj al-Isfahani also mentions Yunus’s music book in Al-Aghani. It appears that this was the first book written about music after Islam and served as a model and source for Abu al-Faraj’s Al-Aghani.</p>\n<p>Because Islam prohibited music for certain reasons, this art was almost abandoned in the early Islamic period, especially during the reign of the Rashidun Caliphs, who strictly enforced religious laws. However, after the Umayyad Caliphs turned the caliphate into a monarchy and relaxed the strictness of religious rulings, music entered their courts. Iranian musicians gained great favor and status. After learning Arabic, they sang Arabic poems with Persian melodies and received even more attention.</p>\n<p>Of course, not all Umayyad Caliphs held the same view toward music. Sulayman ibn Abd al-Malik was opposed to music and punished its practitioners. In contrast, Muawiyah and Yazid were both inclined toward it. Muawiyah brought a group of musicians to Damascus, and through this, Iranian music moved beyond the Hijaz and spread to other Islamic cities.</p>\n<p>In the Jahiliyyah period, Arab life was very simple and unpretentious. But after the advent of Islam, the conquest of Iran and other countries, and the formation of a great Islamic state, the Arabs became familiar with the luxurious lifestyle of the ancient kings of Iran and Rome. This was especially evident during the Abbasid period, when the caliphate took on an Iranian character in Arabic clothing.</p>\n<p>The Abbasid Caliphs completely transformed the caliphate into a monarchy. They appointed chamberlains and special servants for themselves. In the caliph’s harem, beautiful slave girls from various nations gathered. The caliphs spent their leisure time in pleasure and drinking. Enormous wealth flowed from all parts of the Islamic territories to the center of the caliphate. This lifestyle naturally changed the social, literary, and scientific atmosphere and greatly contributed to the advancement of the musical arts.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, the viziers of the Abbasid Caliphs, who were mostly from Iranian families — such as the Barmakids and the Banu Sahl — had a strong interest in reviving Iranian customs and traditions. Their material and spiritual power, wealth, and luxurious lifestyle also served as another incentive for nurturing artists.</p>\n<p>In short, it did not take long for Baghdad, the seat of the Abbasid Caliph, to become a center of science, literature, and a gathering place for the wealthy and pleasure-seekers. Naturally, ghazal writing, singing, and beautiful voices flourished greatly. Singers, musicians, and instrumentalists gathered around the caliphs, viziers, and the homes of the nobility. As can be seen from the book Al-Aghani, the atmosphere of the houses of Baghdad and the banks of the Tigris River at night became astonishing. From every direction, the sounds of instruments and singing could be heard, and poetry, singing, dance, and musical performance spread remarkably.</p>\n<p>There is no doubt that the Abbasid Caliphs initially had no strong inclination toward pleasure-seeking, as they had just come to power and had many enemies. Due to continuous wars, they had little opportunity for leisure. It is said that Abdullah al-Saffah, the first Abbasid Caliph, and his brother Mansur (the second caliph) were opposed to drinking and merrymaking. Saffah is even said not to have spent his free time in the harem.</p>\n<p>One day Mansur heard a loud noise and asked what was happening. He was told that one of his servants was playing the tanbur among the slave girls. Mansur went to the gathering; everyone scattered in fear. He then took the tanbur from the servant’s hand and struck him on the head with it until it broke. He sold the servant so he would never see his face again.</p>\n<p>Mahdi, the third Abbasid Caliph, loved the fine arts, including music, but he listened to the melodies and singers from behind a curtain. His son Ibrahim was passionate about this art; he learned the techniques of singing and entertainment at home, had a beautiful voice, and was considered one of the famous singers of that period. Since the caliph was not uninterested in the arts, poets and musicians strove to compose ghazals, describe wine and the beloved, and encourage people toward pleasure-seeking.</p>\n<p>At this time, the poems of “Bashar ibn Burd,” an Iranian ghazal poet, became widely popular. Without hesitation, he invited people to pleasure and drinking, to the point that people feared for the chastity of their relatives. Mahdi ordered that Bashar had no right to compose ghazals. However, Bashar continued to write poems secretly and did not abandon his style.</p>\n<p>Ultimately, Bashar’s support for Iranian ideas and beliefs, his interest in the Shu’ubiyyah movement, his excessive indulgence in the art of ghazal, his misuse of his delicate poetic taste, and his criticism of the Arabs (a common trait among Shu’ubiyyah supporters) worked against him. In 168 AH, he was killed by order of the Abbasid Caliph.</p>\n<p>Harun al-Rashid, the Abbasid Caliph, on one hand practiced extreme piety and asceticism, and on the other hand indulged heavily in pleasure, drinking, and merrymaking. His sons Amin and Ma’mun showed great affection toward musicians, instrumentalists, and singers. Ma’mun particularly loved singing and, just as he was the greatest patron of science and literature, he also supported musicians. During his reign, practitioners of this art gained great favor and high status.</p>\n<p>From the stories about singers and musicians mentioned in the book Al-Aghani, it is clear that during the Abbasid period — especially in the time of Harun and Ma’mun, which was the most brilliant era of the Abbasid Caliphate — the art of music advanced greatly, and the number of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/musicians-biography/\">musicians</a> increased significantly. Most of them were Iranian.</p>\n<p>Among the great music scholars of this period, two famous musicians stand out: Ibrahim and his son Ishaq. Their stories of playing and singing are described in detail in Al-Aghani. We will briefly mention them below.</p>\n\n<h3>Ibrahim al-Mawsili</h3>\n<p><strong>Ibrahim al-Mawsili</strong> (125–188 AH) (Ibrahim son of Mahan son of Bahman son of Nask, from the noble landowners of Arjan in Fars) was of noble and pure Iranian descent and was born in Kufa. He traveled to Ray and Baghdad and learned to play the oud and sing from masters of the art.</p>\n<p>Mahdi, the Abbasid Caliph, had a strong affection for him, and he was also respected by Harun, becoming his musician and companion. Through the gifts he received from Harun and other nobles, Ibrahim amassed great wealth and had many students to whom he taught music. Among them, he trained several young girls in the art of singing.</p>\n<p>Before him, women who knew the art of singing were usually dark-skinned slave girls. He was the first to train several beautiful, fair-skinned girls in singing, and their attractive appearance further increased the importance of this type of singer. Ibrahim had a very precise ear; among thirty girls playing the oud, he could immediately detect if even one string was out of tune.</p>\n<p>It is said that Ibrahim composed 900 musical melodies. His son Ishaq considered 300 of them masterpieces, 300 average, and the remaining 300 ordinary and commonplace. Ibrahim traveled to Ray and learned the ancient music of Iran from a Zoroastrian named Junuya.</p>\n\n<h3>Ishaq ibn Ibrahim</h3>\n<p><strong>Ishaq ibn Ibrahim</strong> (150–235 AH) (his mother “Shahak Razi” was a singer in Ray whom Ibrahim met and married) is very famous. He was a musician, singer, poet, and scholar. He learned music from his father and other masters such as “Mansur Zalzal,” who was Iranian.</p>\n<p>Like his father, Ishaq gained great wealth from the gifts he received. The author of Al-Aghani compared him to the sea and other musicians to streams and rivers. He is considered to have created his own unique style in music. It is written that Ishaq organized musical meters into a specific system and, in a book containing his compositions, classified the music he created according to meter.</p>\n<p>It is said that Ishaq had such precise knowledge of intervals and musical frets that he could play very difficult pieces on an untuned oud. This demonstrates the accuracy of his hearing, the skill of his fingers, and his deep familiarity with the oud.</p>\n<p>Unfortunately, none of the books he wrote have survived, but there is no doubt that they were important sources for Abu al-Faraj al-Isfahani’s Al-Aghani. After Ishaq’s death, the Abbasid Caliph said with regret that the caliph’s court would no longer have the same splendor without Ishaq.</p>\n<p>Ishaq had a student named Ziryab, who was the caliph’s companion and special singer. Ziryab was a famous composer and poet. He studied music with Ishaq in Baghdad, later became a singer at Harun’s court, and then traveled to Spain. Caliph Abd al-Rahman treated him with great kindness and respect, and he attained high status. It is said that when he passed through the streets, he was accompanied by a hundred servants.</p>\n<p>Since the book Al-Aghani was mentioned above, it is appropriate to also mention its author:</p>\n\n<h3>Abu al-Faraj al-Isfahani</h3>\n<p>Abu al-Faraj was born in Isfahan in 284 AH and died in Baghdad in 356 AH at the age of 73. The book he compiled is called Al-Aghani. It contains stories about musicians and especially provides an excellent description of the era of Harun al-Rashid and Ma’mun.</p>\n<p>The stories in Al-Aghani are delightful, and the book is also considered one of the best sources of Arabic literature. The author spent fifty years of his life compiling it. He presented a unique handwritten copy to Sayf al-Dawla al-Hamdani, who rewarded him with a thousand dinars.</p>\n<p>It is said that Adud al-Dawla al-Daylami always carried the book Al-Aghani with him on his travels. Although this book is the best source for the history of music during the brilliant period of the Abbasid Caliphate, one must also note that its content is not free from exaggeration and embellishment. Nevertheless, it is historically very important and has preserved the names of many poets, instrumentalists, singers, and composers that would otherwise have been lost.</p>\n\n<h2>The Scientific Movement in Music</h2>\n<p>Before Islam, Iranian music was a purely practical art. Musicians and composers did not seek to discover the rules governing the relationships between sounds and melodies. For example, Barbad, the greatest musician of the Sassanid period, is always introduced as a performer and composer. The melodies he created do not necessarily indicate that he had scientific knowledge of music, because even today in our country many musicians who compose melodies have no knowledge of the scientific principles of music and only create tunes according to their natural taste.</p>\n<p>As previously mentioned, Iranian music spread among the Arabs. Due to the movement of Arabs to Iran and the settlement of Iranian musicians in Islamic lands, Iranian music was adopted exactly as it was in Arab-speaking countries. In the early centuries of the Hijri calendar, no one examined music from a scientific perspective.</p>\n<p>As can be seen from the musical treatises written later, the Iranians, after becoming familiar with Greek philosophy, first adopted the scientific rules and laws of music from ancient Greek philosophers. In the books they wrote, they referred to these rules and gradually worked to perfect them. They adapted their own melodies, modes, and maqams to the general principles of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/sound-characteristics/\">acoustics and the physics of sound</a>, discussed the mathematical relationships and ratios of sounds, and wrote books on the subject that are scientifically significant.</p>\n<p>Thus, music, which had been only a practical skill with no written rules, became a scientific discipline, and its principles were documented in books. This development, like other sciences in the Islamic era, was carried out by Iranians, and gradually a scientific movement emerged in this art.</p>\n<p>It is surprising that although books were written on the art of music, these books never brought about any real change in our music, nor did they open a path for its progress. They never caused any transformation in the style of performance or composition.</p>\n<p>In truth, it can be said that the books written on this subject did not combine theory with practice. They only left a record that allows us to speak, albeit very simply and briefly, about the music of the past in this land. Because in none of these treatises is there any rule for notating musical melodies, nor is there any discussion of the style of composition or the combination of melodies.</p>\n<p>Moreover, the authors who wrote about music were mostly imitators of one another. Those who showed some originality only criticized the approach of previous writers and added very minor points to what their predecessors had written. Their writings had absolutely no influence on the performance style of musicians and composers.</p>\n<p>Now we will briefly discuss the authors who wrote works on music, mentioning only those whose contributions were more significant so as not to stray from the main purpose.</p>\n\n<h3>Abu Nasr al-Farabi</h3>\n<p><strong>Abu Nasr al-Farabi</strong> (259–339 AH) was the first person in the Islamic era to write a book on the art of music in which he discussed the laws of sound and the mathematical ratios of tones. His treatise has survived to this day. In his book <em>Al-Musīqī al-Kabīr</em> (The Great Book of Music), Farabi writes that he examined what the ancients and predecessors had written about music. Therefore, he adopted the basic rules and principles of music from Greek philosophers. Farabi succeeded so well in explaining the main principles of music that later writers who composed books on the subject accepted his writings as definitive and merely followed and expanded upon his ideas.</p>\n<p>A noteworthy point in Farabi’s book is his detailed description of the instruments that were common in his time. His writings represent the first serious inquiry into the science of organology. In his book on music, Farabi describes in detail the oud, the Khorasani and Baghdadi tanbur, the mizmar, and the rabab, explaining each one thoroughly. In particular, his comparison of the Baghdadi and Khorasani tanburs clearly shows that he was familiar with the various instruments in use at that time.</p>\n\n<h3>Abu Ali Hussein ibn Sina (Avicenna)</h3>\n<p><strong>Abu Ali Hussein ibn Sina</strong> (370–428 AH), also known as Avicenna, wrote two treatises on music. One is in Arabic and forms part of his book <em>Al-Shifā</em>. The other is in Persian and is part of <em>Dānishnāmeh-ye Alā’ī</em>, dedicated to Alā’ al-Dawla al-Hamadani, one of the Daylamite rulers. This is the first book on music written in Persian that has reached us.</p>\n<p>It is well known that because Alā’ al-Dawla could not understand Avicenna’s books written in Arabic, Avicenna wrote this book on various branches of wisdom in Persian so that it would be useful to him. Like Farabi, Avicenna learned the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/what-is-music-theory/\">rules of music</a> from studying Greek books and explained in different words the same points that Farabi had expressed. However, while Farabi was familiar with practical music and playing instruments and fully described the instruments of his time, Avicenna added nothing of his own and only referred to and confirmed what Farabi had written.</p>\n\n<h3>Safi al-Din Abd al-Mu’min Urmawi</h3>\n<p>The treatises of Farabi and Avicenna remained the only sources of information on scientific music for two or three centuries. During this long period, no Iranian or Arab writer composed anything significant on music until Safi al-Din Abd al-Mu’min Urmawi (died 656 AH), who lived during the time of the last Abbasid Caliph al-Musta’sim, wrote two books on the art of music: one titled <em>Risālah al-Sharafiyyah</em> and the other <em>Kitāb al-Adwār</em>.</p>\n<p>In <em>Risālah al-Sharafiyyah</em>, Safi al-Din states that the book contains principles adopted from Greek philosophers and the ancients. To show that his treatise is not merely an imitation of previous writings, he notes that it discusses matters that earlier authors did not mention.</p>\n<p>Of course, by “ancients” he means those who wrote books on music before him, such as Khalil ibn Ahmad (author of the science of prosody), the writers of the treatises of the Ikhwan al-Safa, Farabi, Avicenna, and the like.</p>\n\n<h3>Qutb al-Din Shirazi</h3>\n<p>Qutb al-Din Shirazi (died 710 AH) wrote a book on philosophy and sciences titled <em>Durrat al-Tāj li-Ghurrat al-Dubāj</em>, which includes a detailed section on music consisting of an introduction and five chapters.</p>\n<p>This book is written in Persian and contains useful discussions on sound and its attributes, the mathematical ratios of musical intervals, the relationship between sounds from a mathematical perspective, melodies, the frets of musical instruments, poetic meters, and music. The author presents the views of Farabi and Safi al-Din Urmawi on music, explains and clarifies their statements, and expresses great respect and admiration for Safi al-Din.</p>\n<p>He also drew diagrams for the relationships between intervals and melodies and provided explanations that demonstrate his complete precision in this art. In truth, his book is one of the most valuable works ever written on music.</p>\n\n<h3>Abd al-Qadir Maraghi</h3>\n<p>Abd al-Qadir Maraghi (died 838 AH) dedicated his music book titled <em>Maqāṣid al-Alḥān</em> to Sultan Murad II, one of the Ottoman sultans. He traveled from Samarkand to Asia Minor and presented the book to him in 826 AH. Abd al-Qadir later made another journey to Samarkand and spent time in the service of Sultan Shah Rukh, son of Timur, who was a patron of literature and the arts. He remained with him until the end of his life.</p>\n<p>Abd al-Qadir wrote two other books on music: one titled <em>Jāmi‘ al-Alḥān</em>, which he dedicated to Sultan Shah Rukh in 818 AH, and the other a commentary on Safi al-Din’s <em>Kitāb al-Adwār</em>, which appears to be his last work.</p>\n<p>From his writings, it appears that he first wrote a book called <em>Kanz al-Alḥān</em> and at the end of it notated the melodies he had composed using a special script. Unfortunately, no copy of this book has survived and it may have been lost.</p>\n<p>Of course, there were other individuals who also wrote works on music, but since the goal was to introduce the most prominent authors and important books, we have limited ourselves to mentioning the major treatises and works so as not to deviate from the main purpose. Almost all others added nothing new and merely repeated what their predecessors had written. For example, Mahmud Amuli in his book <em>Nafā’is al-Funūn fī ‘Arā’is al-‘Uyūn</em> included a discussion on music that is essentially a summary of Qutb al-Din Shirazi’s music section in <em>Durrat al-Tāj</em>, and he even copied many passages verbatim.</p>\n\n<h2>Great Iranian Musicians in Arabia and the Umayyad Court</h2>\n<p><strong>Muslim ibn Muhriz:</strong> Ibn Muhriz was originally Iranian; his father was the custodian of the Kaaba. He was one of the famous musicians of the Umayyad era. He was a master of both Iranian and Roman music. He spread Iranian music in Mecca and Medina. Abu al-Faraj al-Isfahani writes in <em>Al-Aghani</em> that he invented the ramal meter, which was a fast rhythmic melody. He established the first scientific school, properly organized melodies, created his own method in music, and worked to improve and perfect it.</p>\n<p>One of his achievements was forming groups of female singers (such groups had also existed in Iran during the Sassanid period). He formed a group of fifty female singers, led by Jamila, a famous female musician of that era, who sang the main melody.</p><img class=\"wp-image-2367 size-large\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/music_history_after_islam_3-1024x576.jpg\" alt=\"Muslim ibn Muhriz playing the six-stringed instrument\" width=\"1024\" height=\"576\" /> Muslim ibn Muhriz playing the six-stringed instrument[/caption]\n\n<h2>The Reflection of Sassanid Music Among Arab Speakers</h2>\n<p>Iranian music after Islam spread in Arab-speaking and Muslim countries. This music, which is now known in Western countries as Arab music, was founded by Iranians. As mentioned earlier, the Arabs initially paid little attention to civilization, industry, and the arts. Even after Islam, they considered practicing crafts beneath their dignity and left such work to the mawali (non-Arab Muslims). When they became accustomed to urban life and realized their need for sciences and industries, they had no choice but to turn to the mawali. Since most artists and craftsmen were Iranian, Iranian arts and industries — including music — spread among the Arabs and gradually developed.</p>\n<p>During the Abbasid Caliphate, Islamic music was based on the principles, rules, and methods of post-Islamic Iranian music. The maqams and melodies of ancient Iranian music replaced Arab music. Although, over time and due to local and social factors, the music of today’s Arab countries has acquired its own particular character, the influence of Iranian music is still clearly visible in it. Because its foundation was borrowed from Iranian music, the music of these countries is built upon Iranian music. For example, out of the 52 maqams commonly used in the music of Egypt, Syria, and Lebanon, 30 of them exist among the melodies and gushehs of the Iranian music radif.</p>\n<p>Continuing this research and comparison with neighboring countries also leads to the same conclusion: post-Islamic Iranian music is the root and foundation of the music of these nations.</p><img class=\"wp-image-2368 size-large\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/music_history_after_islam_4-1024x1024.jpg\" alt=\"A woman playing a stringed instrument, Qajar Iran, early 19th century\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" /> A woman playing a stringed instrument, Qajar Iran, early 19th century[/caption]\n\n<h2>Poetry and Iranian Music After Islam in the Abbasid Period</h2>\n<p>As mentioned earlier, after the fall of the Sassanid dynasty and the Arab conquest of Iran, the Iranians neither stopped composing poetry nor forgot their music. Music and poetry, which were closely interconnected, continued to exist, but they no longer enjoyed their former glory and gradually declined. This situation persisted until the Iranians succeeded in establishing semi-independent and independent governments.</p>\n<p>After the establishment of the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abbasid_Caliphate\">Abbasid Caliphate</a> with its capital in Baghdad, following the fall of the Umayyads, a relative freedom emerged. The attention of the Abbasid Caliphs to royal splendor and fine arts — in imitation of the Iranian kings — caused the market for fine arts such as calligraphy, painting, poetry, and music to flourish. Iranian artists, who despite adverse events had not lost the thread of their artistic unity, expressed their previous desires and intellectual and artistic creations in new forms. With the formation of semi-independent and independent governments in the early Abbasid period, they manifested their feelings in the new style of post-Islamic Iranian music.</p>\n<p>The two great writers of post-Islamic Iranian music in this period were Abu Nasr al-Farabi and Avicenna. Farabi’s famous book <em>Al-Musīqī al-Kabīr</em> is one of the greatest books ever written on Iranian music up to that time. Farabi was an oud player and skilled singer. The division of dangs into six dangs is attributed to Abu Nasr al-Farabi.</p><img class=\"wp-image-2369 size-large\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/music_history_after_islam_5-1024x1024.jpg\" alt=\"Al-Musīqī al-Kabīr by Abu Nasr al-Farabi\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" /> Al-Musīqī al-Kabīr by Abu Nasr al-Farabi[/caption]\n\n<h3>General Characteristics of Iranian Music After Islam in the Abbasid Period</h3>\n<p>1) Iranian music after Islam became noticeably dependent on poetry. Some poets performed their own poems with singing and instrumental accompaniment.</p>\n<p>2) The melodies and modes (dastans) attributed to Barbad from ancient times had a strong influence on musical works.</p>\n<p>3) The recitation of epic poems, especially Shahnameh reading, became very popular, and the meter of the Shahnameh became prominent in post-Islamic Iranian music during this period.</p>\n<p>4) Efforts began to notate musical melodies using special scripts.</p>\n<p>5) Various and diverse instruments were used in ensemble playing, and some new instruments such as the mughni (a type of oud) and the tarah (a type of qanun) were invented.</p>\n<p>6) Sufi music and sama‘ in khanqahs were performed with the usual instruments of that style, such as the ney, rabab, and sometimes the tanbur, and developed their own special melodies.</p>\n<p>7) In this period of post-Islamic Iranian music, the innovations of some musicians and performers led to the creation of new gushehs that were added to the Iranian music radif, such as Buselik (in Nava) and Ebrahimi in ‘Ushshaq, and others.</p><img class=\"wp-image-2370 size-large\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/music_history_after_islam_6-1024x576.jpg\" alt=\"Image of the qanun instrument\" width=\"1024\" height=\"576\" /> Image of the qanun instrument[/caption]\n\n<h2>Iranian Music After Islam in the Ilkhanid and Timurid Periods</h2>\n<p>There were many musicians in the Mongol courts, and literary texts from this period also indicate an interest in the science of music. After Abu Nasr al-Farabi and Avicenna, the notable theorist worth mentioning is Safi al-Din Abd al-Mu’min Urmawi, who lived during a critical time and served the last Abbasid Caliph in Baghdad. During the attack and massacre by Hulagu Khan in Baghdad, he saved his life by playing the oud for him. In Iran, Safi al-Din invented two musical instruments: the mughni, a type of oud, and a rectangular qanun-like instrument called the nuzha.</p>\n<p>Another important treatise from post-Islamic Iranian music that has survived is <em>Kanz al-Qasf</em>, written in the second half of the 8th century AH. The section describing musical instruments is particularly valuable. As can be seen from this book, the musical instruments of post-Islamic Iranian music used in banquets and dance were mainly two types of oud and rabab. Other instruments mentioned include the mughni, chang (harp), a bowed instrument called the ghichak, a small ney called the shabih, tanbur, dotar, a type of setar, a type of kamancheh, and percussion instruments such as the daf and dayereh.</p>\n<p>In the artistic works and paintings of this period, more instruments common in post-Islamic Iranian music appear than in previous eras. The instruments shown in Iranian paintings, used in banquets and on the battlefield, include: tanbur, chang, qanun, kamancheh, ney, sornā, trumpet, kus (large drum), and cymbals.</p>\n\n<h2>Iranian Music After Islam in the Samanid Period</h2>\n<p>After the arrival of Islam in Iran, the Samanid dynasty can be considered the era of the flourishing of art and culture in the land of Iran. The Samanid rulers modeled their court after the pre-Islamic kings and brought about a revolution in geography, prejudices, and the culture of the country’s people. Scientific and artistic works from regions such as Greece, Babylon, Egypt, Syria, and India were translated and used.</p>\n<p>The prominent artistic figures of this period are Rudaki, Farabi, and Abu al-Faraj al-Isfahani. Rudaki, known as the father of Persian poetry, was not only a poet but also a singer and played the chang (harp). Historical sources recount that Rudaki, by composing and singing the poem “Bu-ye Ju-ye Muliyan,” persuaded Amir Nasr Samani, the ruler of his time who intended to leave Bukhara, to return to the court.</p>\n<p>Farabi, titled “the Second Teacher,” was born in Farab in Khorasan. His father was one of the governors of the Samanid dynasty. He received his primary education in Farab and then traveled to Baghdad for further studies. After studying philosophy, logic, and wisdom in Baghdad, Farabi entered the court of Sayf al-Dawla al-Hamdani and began writing treatises. After composing numerous treatises, he wrote his famous book <em>Al-Musīqī al-Kabīr</em> in two volumes. This book included the biographies of past musicians and organology. His other famous book, <em>Iḥṣā’ al-‘Ulūm</em>, dedicates one chapter to music.</p>\n<p>Abu al-Faraj al-Isfahani also undertook a great task. During the Samanid era, he collected all the sounds, melodies, and tunes of post-Islamic Iranian music up to his time and wrote a book titled <em>Al-Aghānī al-Kabīr</em> in 24 volumes.</p>\n\n<h2>The Safavid Period: The Period of Stagnation in Theoretical Studies of Post-Islamic Iranian Music</h2>\n<p>During the Safavid period, significant transformations occurred in arts such as painting and metalwork, and specific designs were used in architecture. In music, however, unlike painting and tilework, no particular progress was made, especially in its theoretical aspects. No theorists like Farabi, Avicenna, or Safi al-Din Urmawi emerged. Due to religious beliefs and the prohibition of music, musicians were not welcomed. Moreover, music fell into the hands of illiterate individuals.</p>\n<p>In contrast to this situation in Iran during the Safavid period, Iranian music flourished in India. During the reign of the Mughal emperors after Amir Timur in India, some arts such as architecture and literature were influenced by Iran.</p>\n<p>One of the founders of the Safavid dynasty, Sheikh Haydar, is famous for inventing the chahartar. The rabab was perfected by Abdullah Faryadi, and the ghichak by Qoli Mohammad. In this period, foreign influence — especially from China — can be observed in the paintings of Isfahan, and it is not unlikely that it also had some effect on certain musical instruments.</p>\n\n<h3>General Characteristics of Iranian Music After Islam in the Safavid Period</h3>\n<p>1) In post-Islamic Iranian music during the Safavid period, the variety of instruments increased compared to the past, but some instruments gradually fell out of use. At least six instruments were commonly used in ensemble playing: oud, ney, tanbur, qanun, kamancheh, and daf.</p>\n<p>2) Music and singing became more of a form of entertainment and amusement. The high status that musicians enjoyed in the royal courts of the Sassanid period no longer existed. Instead, musicians in this period fell into the same category that in the Qajar era was called “amaleh tarab” (entertainers).</p>\n<p>3) Music was mostly a private art performed in intimate settings. The vocal range of melodies rarely exceeded two octaves.</p>\n<p>4) Singing was performed loudly and in a high register. Especially the high male voice, as in the past, was favored.</p>\n<p>5) Some group descents and soft singing in religious ceremonies became more common, in which children often sang an octave higher than the men’s voices simultaneously.</p>\n<p>6) The groundwork for the introduction of some European instruments was gradually prepared. Initially, these were limited to gifts from European monarchs to the Safavid court. This contact increased especially from the first half of the 11th century AH.</p>\n<p>7) Military music and the official naqqareh-khaneh ceremonies, performed at dawn, sunset, and during festivals, gained popularity.</p>\n\n<h2>Iranian Music After Islam in the Qajar Period</h2>\n<p>Compared to the Safavid period, more evidence and records exist about Iranian music after Islam in the Qajar period and its musicians. During the Qajar era, changes occurred in court music. Some instruments that were common in previous eras fell out of use and their playing was abandoned. These included the oud, rabab, chang (harp), and qanun. In their place, other instruments became more widespread, such as the tar and setar (which do not appear in earlier paintings), kamancheh, santur, ney, sornā, nafir (a type of horn made from animal horn or metal), kornā, dehl, tonbak, naqqareh, cymbals, ney anban, dayereh, and whistle.</p>\n<p>Some instruments were also invented in Iran during this period. For example, the ramuz was invented by a person from Hamadan in Tehran. One musician named Aqa Jan, father of Mirza Habib Sama‘ Hozur, invented a kamancheh with many strings and pegs and a long neck that was played while standing. He named it “Majles Ara” and played it for Naser al-Din Shah at night.</p>\n<p>According to some travelogues, playing music — especially during parties and while eating lunch — was common. In this type of chamber music, usually one percussion instrument and the tar accompanied the singing. In the later period mentioned, musicians and performers were generally not highly respected, and prominent individuals rarely learned music or played instruments. If they did, it was mostly for personal and private reasons. On the other hand, music and its performance fell into the hands of luti (street performers) and disreputable individuals.</p>\n<p>The travelogues of other travelers who mentioned Qajar-era music reflect more or less the same situation. The Englishman Edward Browne, in describing parties, notes that guests sat at the head of the table according to their rank, while musicians, singers, and dancers sat at the foot of the table. Mehdi Qoli Hedayat (Hajj Mokhber al-Saltaneh) writes: “Among the later generations, no one had a scientific position. Music was not pursued as it should have been, and in the end, practitioners (musicians) lost all status.”</p>\n<p>In the courts of governors, especially the Qajar kings, musicians and singers performed both privately and in appropriate ceremonies such as ash-pazan (cooking ash) ceremonies. Apart from the musicians who were mostly called “amaleh tarab,” other musicians — often called motreb (entertainers) — were used in parties and weddings. Village musicians, in addition to singing and using instruments such as the kamancheh, also used large drums and sornā. Groups of musicians and singers who worked together were usually called “dasteh” (ensemble).</p>\n<p>Among the most famous ensembles during the reign of Naser al-Din Shah, two groups can be mentioned, both led by blind musicians. One was known as the Momen Kor ensemble. Its leader was knowledgeable in the radif and had trained its members himself. His wife played the daf and dayereh, and his two daughters were also part of the group — one played the organette and the other was skilled in dance.</p>\n<p>The other group was led by Karim Kor, who himself played the tar and kamancheh, and his daughter also played the kamancheh. A singer named Hossein sang and kept the rhythm, while a person named Sadeq played the daf. Karim died in 1300 AH. The special court musicians, called “amaliyeh tarab khasseh,” served throughout the 50-year reign of Naser al-Din Shah. Their names include: Aqa Ali Akbar, Aqa Gholamhossein, and Mirza Abdollah (tar players); Santur Khan, Mohammad Sadeq Khan, and Mirza Habib Sama‘ Hozur (santur players); Khoshnavaz Khan, Matlab Khan, and Javad Khan Qazvini (kamancheh players).</p>\n<p>In addition to these, other musicians were also employed in Naser al-Din Shah’s court for specific occasions. For example, before the Shah went to sleep, musicians would perform for him. His bedroom had four doors opening to different sides, one of which led to the musicians’ room. Naqib al-Mamalek, who was skilled in storytelling, would sit in one of the rooms with the door slightly open so the Shah could hear his voice. Before that, one of the special musicians — Javad Khan Qazvini — would play a very small kamancheh, and Naqib al-Mamalek would recite a few verses softly.</p>\n<p>It appears that Naser al-Din Shah had some poetic taste and a relative familiarity with Iranian musical melodies. He encouraged masters of the art, and during his reign, famous musicians emerged, including: Aqa Ali Akbar Farahani, son of Shah Vali, who also had poetic talent. By order of Naser al-Din Shah, a watercolor painting was made of him and several of his students by Mirza Abu al-Hasan Khan Ghaffari (Sani‘ al-Molk) in 1273 AH.</p>\n<p>The French Count de Gobineau, who heard his playing in Tehran, wrote about him: “The most famous tar player is Ali Akbar. Europeans who had no interest in Eastern music were deeply moved when they heard him play, because he played the tar with great soul and sensitivity. However, according to some artists, he had a quick temper and one had to insist greatly for him to play.”</p>\n<p>Aqa Mohammad Sadeq Khan Sarvar al-Mamalek, the famous santur player of the Naserid era who had heard Aqa Ali Akbar’s playing, recounted: “When Aqa Ali Akbar touched the strings with his plectrum, I felt as if I were in another world and forgot my own playing.”</p>\n<p>Aqa Ali Akbar would place his tar beside his bed at night and sleep. Sometimes in the middle of the night he would wake up and play a new gusheh. One night, someone told him in a dream: “I will teach you the Rang-e Osul, an old piece that has been completely forgotten.” And so he did. Aqa Ali Akbar woke up in the middle of the night, took his tar from beside the bed, and played it. Two days later, he taught the rang to his nephew Aqa Gholamhossein, and later he taught it to his cousins Mirza Hossein Qoli and Aqa Mirza Abdollah.</p>\n<p>Aqa Ali Akbar had three sons: Mirza Hasan, a tar and setar player who died young, and his other two sons Mirza Abdollah and Aqa Hossein Qoli, who became famous tar players.</p>\n<p>Aqa Gholamhossein was the son of Mohammad Reza, nephew of Aqa Ali Akbar. He lived with his uncle and was one of the musicians of Naser al-Din Shah’s court. His fast and delicate plectrum technique was famous. In addition to his cousins Mirza Abdollah and Aqa Hossein Qoli, he trained other students such as Ne’matollah Atabaki Kermani and Mohammad Khan Mostowfi.</p>\n<p>Because Aqa Ali Akbar died young, his young sons Mirza Abdollah and Aqa Hossein Qoli could not benefit from their father’s teachings. Initially, Aqa Gholamhossein was reluctant to teach them, but due to the insistence of their mother — who after Aqa Ali Akbar’s death married Aqa Gholamhossein — he devoted himself to teaching the two boys and preserved the old melodies.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions about this article on Iranian music after Islam, please share them in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-04-25 14:54:42', 0, '2024-08-30 19:42:15', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
