(1462, 'posts', 5, 'en', NULL, 'What is music theory? Why and how did music theory come into existence? 5 Benefits of Learning Music Theory', NULL, 'Music theory is the study of the fundamental principles of music, including rhythm, melody, harmony, notation, and musical form. It developed over time as music became more complex, providing a system for organizing, preserving, and communicating musical ideas. Learning music theory helps individuals better understand the structure of musical pieces, improve performance and analytical skills, and enhance creativity in composition. Overall, it serves as the foundation for a deeper understanding and advancement in music.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>Music theory is the science that examines various aspects of music, including rhythm, harmony, melody, principles of music notation, different musical forms, and more.</p>\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>Why Did Music Theory Develop?</h2>\n<p>Imagine you go to a bookstore and buy five storybooks. When you get home, you place them on your desk or in a drawer.</p>\n<p>A few months later, you buy some textbooks and place them next to the storybooks.</p>\n<p>You subscribe to a monthly magazine and add each new issue to the pile.</p>\n<p>To prepare for a job exam, you acquire several more books. Now you need to organize your books and arrange them neatly on the same desk.</p>\n<p>Over the years, the number of books, magazines, and periodicals grows, making it increasingly difficult to find the one you want among so many items.</p>\n<p>Eventually, you have no choice but to get a bookshelf with multiple shelves so you can organize your books and magazines neatly and separately, allowing quick and easy access.</p>\n<p>Due to the passage of time and the increasing volume of material, your small desk has turned into a large library with many shelves.</p>\n<p>This example also applies to music theory and any other field. In ancient times, music theory was much simpler and more limited, and it was possible to memorize songs and pieces. However, as societies developed greater needs to express feelings, emotions, and social concerns through sound, the number of songs and musical sounds increased. The need to record and organize <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/music-what-is-it/\">music</a> became apparent, and that is how the science of music theory was born.</p>\n<p>This is why, from the past until now, various methods for writing music, songs, and pieces have been tested. Today, we have reached the system of writing music on five parallel lines (called staff lines, numbered from bottom to top), along with meter, time signatures, and rhythm — which is like the organized bookshelf in the example above.</p>\n<p>With these definitions, the importance of developing music theory for preserving the music of the past and creating new songs and artistic works becomes clear.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_2477\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"600\"]<img class=\"wp-image-2477 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/Rhythm2.jpg\" alt=\"Music theory and the science of notation\" width=\"600\" height=\"343\" /> Music theory and the science of notation have been common in Europe since ancient times[/caption]\n\n<h2>Notes, Music, and Music Theory</h2>\n<p>Music is made up of two components: sound and silence. These must be arranged regularly and according to the principles of music theory to create a beautiful and pleasant musical piece.</p>\n<p>In nature, sounds are divided into musical and non-musical sounds. Musical sounds are produced by the regular vibration of an object in the air — such as the regular vibration of strings on various instruments that create musical notes. Non-musical sounds result from irregular vibrations of objects.</p>\n<p>Notes can be considered the building blocks of the science of music. In fact, notes are the alphabet of music.</p>\n<p>The musical alphabet consists of seven notes: Do, Re, Mi, Fa, Sol, La, and Si.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_2245\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"376\"]<img class=\"wp-image-2245 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/music_theory_1.png\" alt=\"Musical notes\" width=\"376\" height=\"134\" /> The musical notes used are seven main notes.[/caption]\n\n<p>Music theory refers to the set of rules and terms created for performing music and explains how to compose and perform pieces and songs.</p>\n<p>Music theory is a branch of musicology that covers all the rules, symbols, and knowledge related to different sounds and musical notes, intervals, meter and time signatures, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/rhythm-reading-in-music/\">rhythm reading</a> and performance, scales, musical intervals, and more.</p>\n<p>In reality, the science of music theory includes all the efforts made throughout history to record, preserve, maintain, and create music.</p>\n&nbsp;\n\n<strong>For information about the guitar and important points to consider when buying one, read the article <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/comprehensive-guide-to-buying-guitar/\">Comprehensive Guide to Buying a Guitar</a>.</strong>\n\n<h2>Benefits of Learning Music Theory</h2>\n<p>Music theory explains the “why” and “how” hidden behind musical works.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Why do these notes sound good together?</li>\n  <li>How are musical pieces constructed and what makes certain works timeless?</li>\n  <li>What is the difference between strong and weak music?</li>\n  <li>What distinguishes different musical styles?</li>\n  <li>Why does music from different cultures and geographical regions have its own unique color and character?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Music theory can provide answers to all the above questions. Learning music theory helps you listen to pieces and songs with greater enjoyment, gain the ability to analyze them, and makes the movement, climaxes, and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/descents-in-music/\">descents</a> of music more tangible. Music takes on a whole new meaning for you. The fact that, through music theory, you can understand the musical structure, songs, speech patterns, and auditory traditions of different nations and cultures around the world brings great pleasure.</p>\n<p>In this series of articles, we aim to introduce you to the science of music theory in a simple and step-by-step manner.</p>\n<p>Music theory is the science that examines the fundamental principles and rules of music. People who study this science are called music theorists.</p>\n<p>Today’s music theory is the result of the efforts of theorists from <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_history\">ancient times</a> until the present day, and it becomes more complete and comprehensive year by year.</p>\n<p>Composition and arranging of musical pieces have an inseparable connection with music theory. If we imagine music as a building, music theory forms the foundation of that building — without these foundations, construction is impossible.</p>\n<p>Learning the rules and principles of music theory significantly helps a singer or instrumentalist progress faster. It gives the singer a higher understanding of their own performance and the instrumentalist a much better grasp of their playing technique and the techniques available for different instruments.</p>\n<p>In general, learning music theory has many advantages. Here are five of the most important ones:</p>\n<p><strong>1. Better understanding of musical structure and rules:</strong> Music theory helps you understand the structure and rules of music more deeply so you can perform them correctly.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Increased abilities for instrumentalists:</strong> By learning music theory, musicians can combine different notes with greater accuracy.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Improved creativity:</strong> Whether at the level of personal ideas, your own works, or collective projects, learning music theory will help you enhance your creativity.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Expanded listening range:</strong> You will hear and better understand different sonic sections and be able to identify various notes with greater precision.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Improved notation skills:</strong> With music theory knowledge, you will be able to notate your own works correctly using proper musical rules.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_2479\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"400\"]<img class=\"wp-image-2479 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/1.jpg\" alt=\"Music reading and notation\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" /> Music reading and notation are among the most important skills in music theory.[/caption]\n\n<h2>What Does Music Theory Include?</h2>\n<p>In general, music is examined through three main elements: <strong>rhythm</strong>, <strong><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/melody/\">melody</a></strong>, and <strong>harmony</strong>, which are the basic principles of music theory. We will examine each of these in more detail in future articles.</p>\n<p>Music theory generally includes the following topics:</p>\n<p><strong>1. Music Reading and Notation:</strong> One of the basic skills in music theory is music reading and notation. Notation means using lines and symbols to write and record music, while reading means interpreting those symbols and terms to perform the music — just as we write and read words. In fact, musical notes are the alphabet of music.</p>\n<p>In this skill, you must be able to recognize the rhythm of a piece by looking at the notes. When working in a group, you need to coordinate with other musicians. Naturally, those who read music better learn pieces faster and more effectively. This includes learning notes, their names, and how to interpret them.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Rhythm:</strong> <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/rhythm-in-music/\">Pulse or rhythm</a> refers to the sequence of beats used to make the musical line rhythmic. In other words, the repeated occurrence of a steady movement at specific times in music is called rhythm or meter. This includes learning beats and rhythmic patterns.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Harmony:</strong> <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/harmony-in-music/\">Harmony</a> comes from Greek words and in everyday language or philosophy means “proportion” or “agreement.” Etymologically, it means unity and coordination, expressing the oneness of parts that form a complete whole. Harmony is the process of combining individual sounds that are analyzed when heard simultaneously. The concept of harmony usually involves hearing frequencies, pitch range, tone color, notes, or chords at the same time. The science of harmony often refers to the vertical aspect of music and is distinct from horizontal and melodic movement.</p>\n<p>Harmony is the knowledge of making music polyphonic based on the characteristics of chords and the rules for connecting them to accompany the melody. It also considers tonality and textures derived from rhythm, resulting from connecting various chords. Unlike melodies, which deal with the pleasant succession of notes (not simultaneous), harmony adds depth and richness to the melody. A folk music creator (such as a singer using a guitar) experiments with different chords and chooses the ones that best match the mood of the melody. A chord alone cannot beautifully express a melodic idea; it is the successive connection of chords according to the rules of harmony that fully realizes a musical idea.</p>\n<p>This includes arranging sounds and different musical elements in a harmonic way.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Form:</strong> <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/musical-forms/\">Form</a> in music theory refers to the structure of a musical piece. The word “form” means shape or structure. Form describes a piece by breaking it down into its different parts. In other words, form is the architecture and order of a musical work and has concepts similar to those in architecture, painting, sculpture, and so on.</p>\n<p>Every artwork has a design, template, or pattern that contains its content. Form stands in contrast to content. In music, form represents the external shape, order, arrangement, avoidance of monotony, and the temporal dimension of the work. It has had different meanings in each historical period. This includes the structure of musical pieces and the relationship between their different parts.</p>\n<p><strong>5. History of Music:</strong> Understanding the origin and source of music is very important for comprehending music from different parts of the world, as it greatly helps in identifying the nature of music. That is why scholars and philosophers have proposed many theories on this subject. <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/music-history/\">History of Music</a> includes the study of musical history and developments in different regions of the world.</p>\n<p><strong>6. Philosophy of Music:</strong> The philosophy of music studies fundamental questions about the nature of music and our experience of it, examining core issues in the field of music.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>What is the definition of music?</li>\n  <li>What is the relationship between music and the human mind?</li>\n  <li>What information does the history of world music give us about different countries and cultures?</li>\n  <li>What is the relationship between music and human emotions?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These and similar questions are discussed in the philosophy of music. It includes examining the principles behind the creation of different musical structures around the world.</p>\n<p>Topics 1 and 2 can be placed under the category of rhythm mentioned earlier, and the topic of melody will be examined in the fourth section.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions about music theory or the topics discussed in this article, please share them in the comments section to help improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-04-26 12:43:13', 0, '2024-08-30 19:42:29', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1463, 'posts', 6, 'en', NULL, 'What does rhythm mean in music? Why is rhythm or beat the most important element of music?', NULL, 'This article explains the concept of rhythm in music in a simple way. Rhythm is defined as the regular repetition of patterns over time, and it exists not only in music but also in nature (such as heartbeat and natural cycles). In music theory, rhythm is a fundamental element, and without it, music loses its meaning.\n\nRhythm is created by combining different time values and can evoke various emotions in listeners. Different musical styles feature different rhythmic structures. Throughout history, composers like Ludwig van Beethoven and Igor Stravinsky introduced innovations in rhythm, bringing significant changes to music.\n\nLearning rhythm and rhythm reading is essential for musicians, as it improves performance accuracy and helps in understanding musical structure. Rhythm creates coordination among performers, organizes musical pieces, and enhances emotional impact.\n\nIn general, music can be divided into metered (with a clear beat) and non-metered types, and there is also free rhythm, which does not follow a fixed timing pattern. Ultimately, rhythm is closely connected to melody and harmony, and together they form a complete musical work.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we discuss the topic of <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">rhythm in music</span></strong> and provide simple definitions for it. We examine the importance of rhythm knowledge in advancing music education and explore different types of rhythm. We also talk about the role of rhythm in the melody and harmony of musical pieces. Continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>Understanding the Concept of Rhythm in Nature</h2>\n<p>Any action or movement that repeats periodically is called rhythm. In fact, rhythm is a pattern that repeats regularly and continuously. Rhythm is not only the main element of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/music-what-is-it/\">music</a> but is also considered the most important element in the entire system of existence. The beating of the heart, blood circulation, the rotation of planets around themselves and the sun — all follow a specific rhythm and order. If this order is disrupted, the entire system of existence becomes chaotic.</p>\n<p>In nature, any disruption in rhythm can create problems for creatures that depend on these rhythms for survival and interaction with their environment.</p>\n<p>For example, the growth and maturation of plants depend on a specific pattern of daily and seasonal rhythms. If this pattern is disturbed for any reason, plant growth and development will be impaired and may eventually lead to their death. Similarly, disruption in the rhythmic movement of animals can reduce their hunting or escaping ability, leading to failure against natural enemies. Therefore, preserving natural rhythms is extremely important.</p>\n<p>Music is also built upon this powerful foundation, and progress in music comes from practically understanding the concept of rhythm.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3097\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1920\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3097 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/whats_rythm1.jpg\" alt=\"Onion growth cycle\" width=\"1920\" height=\"1080\" /> The onion growth cycle that repeats regularly.[/caption]\n\n<h2>Definition of Rhythm from the Perspective of Music Theory</h2>\n<p>The most instinctive understanding we can have of musical structure is its pulse or rhythm. Rhythm is the fundamental element of music. For this reason, rhythm and rhythm reading are considered the first and most important topic in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/what-is-music-theory/\">music theory</a> and are taught before melody and harmony.</p>\n<p>Rhythm and pulse are essential for any song or musical piece. Without them, music loses its meaning. It is also one of the key factors that distinguish professional musicians from amateurs.</p>\n<p>Rhythm can exist without <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/melody/\">melody</a>, as it did in early and ancient musical pieces, but melody cannot exist without rhythm. For example, in percussion instruments, no specific pitch is played; the performer executes according to a specific rhythm and the techniques of the instrument.</p>\n<p>Rhythm in music can be periodic or continuous and may vary depending on the musical style. For instance, rhythm in classical music is often precise and continuous, while in jazz it is more flexible and free.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_2256\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"300\"]<img class=\"wp-image-2256 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/rhytm.png\" alt=\"What is rhythm\" width=\"300\" height=\"168\" /> Different weights in rhythm recognition in music[/caption]\n\n<h2>The Concept of Rhythm in Music</h2>\n<p>Rhythm is created by placing time durations of different lengths one after another. It is an inseparable part of music. Except for some traditional music styles in various countries that lack conventional meter-based rhythm, rhythm plays a very important role in music.</p>\n<p>Every melody has its own specific rhythm. If we perform the same melody with different rhythms, it will create completely different emotional effects because each rhythm gives the music a particular feeling and character. Works that have greater rhythmic variety and movement usually gain more popularity. Especially in pop and rock music, rhythm is the most important factor for attractiveness and dynamism.</p>\n<p>In Baroque music, rhythm has little variety and pieces from that period were performed with a fixed and almost uniform rhythm. In contrast, composers of the Classical period used more rhythmic diversity, though changes were still relatively limited.</p>\n<p>In the middle of the Classical period and then in the Classical-Romantic era, the famous German composer Ludwig van Beethoven, while making changes in musical forms, also took rhythms out of their usual patterns. He would create a rhythm and then shift the accents and swap the positions of strong and weak beats to generate great excitement in the music.</p>\n<p>This technique of using rhythm and shifting strong and weak beats gradually became common in the Romantic period, and composers began searching for new ways to create rhythmic variety.</p>\n<p>One effective method in this regard was placing different meters side by side, which was notably used by Igor Stravinsky in the early 20th century.</p>\n<p>Choosing the right rhythm for each melody creates more appeal. Additionally, certain <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/musical-forms/\">musical forms</a> have their own specific rhythms, such as the waltz, which is written and performed in 3/4 time at a moderate tempo.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3092\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1080\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3092 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/whatis_rythm2.jpg\" alt=\"Mathematics in music\" width=\"1080\" height=\"1080\" /> An image drawn by John Coltrane that clearly shows the application of mathematics in music.[/caption]\n\n<h2>What Impact Does Rhythm Knowledge Have on Learning Music?</h2>\n<p><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/rhythm-reading-in-music/\">Rhythm knowledge and rhythm reading</a> are extremely important and influential in learning and performing musical works. Learning and understanding rhythms through listening and analyzing rhythmic patterns helps musicians gain a better understanding of the structure and patterns of music. In this way, rhythm knowledge enables performers to play more accurately and reduce rhythmic errors.</p>\n<p>Furthermore, rhythm knowledge is very useful in analyzing and interpreting musical pieces. By recognizing different rhythms and their patterns, better analysis and description of musical structures and their meanings become possible.</p>\n<p>Rhythm knowledge also plays a significant role in improving the balance and coordination of instrumentalists and singers. With sufficient understanding of rhythms and how to distinguish them, performers can play and sing with greater balance and coordination, resulting in better performances.</p>\n<p>Rhythm has a profound effect on the listening experience and the emotions of listeners. It can create feelings of excitement, urgency, anxiety, calmness, or comfort. Depending on the musical style, rhythm can evoke energetic and passionate states or provide a sense of peace and tranquility.</p>\n<p>For example, the strong and fast rhythm in rock music creates a sense of energy, movement, and intensity in listeners, while the rhythm in classical music is often calm and slow, inducing feelings of relaxation and peace.</p>\n<p>Rhythm brings order and coordination to music and gives shape and structure to the melody. Its importance is so great that without rhythm, no musical piece can be created.</p>\n<p>In addition, rhythm helps listeners coordinate their body movements and dance with the music. Therefore, rhythm is very useful for <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dance\">dance</a> and physical activities.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3095\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3095 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/whatis_rythm5.jpg\" alt=\"Traditional Greek dance performance\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> Rhythm plays a very important role in the performance of traditional Greek dances.[/caption]\n\n<h2>Types of Music in Terms of Rhythm</h2>\n<p>Just as poems have meter, music also has meter, which is called rhythm or pulse. Rhythm in music can be divided into two categories:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Metric (Pulsatile) Music:</strong> This type of music has a clear meter and rhythm. Examples include dance music or <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/chaharmezrab/\">chaharmezrabs</a> in Iranian music.</li>\n  <li><strong>Non-Metric Music:</strong> This type does not have a fixed meter. The rhythm fluctuates and it is not possible to tap your foot steadily to it. Examples include traditional Iranian vocal radifs.</li>\n</ul>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3093\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1080\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3093 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/whatis_rythm3.jpg\" alt=\"Japanese Taiko drum\" width=\"1080\" height=\"1080\" /> The culture of Japanese Taiko drum music[/caption]\n\n<h2>What Does Free Rhythm Mean?</h2>\n<p>Free rhythm means the absence of a fixed reference for beats and time divisions in a musical piece. In this state, musicians and performers play freely without time restrictions and move away from fixed rhythmic patterns. In other words, this type of rhythm allows for a free execution in terms of timing and rhythm, which artists use to create variety and freedom in music. Free rhythm exists in many musical styles, including jazz, blues, rock, and most familiar to us, the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/radif/\">Iranian music radif</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>The Importance of the Relationship Between Rhythm and Melody</h2>\n<p>The connection between rhythm and melody in songs is very important, and these two elements work together in an integrated way. Rhythm can create repeating patterns in a piece, while melody consists of the playing of notes and groups of notes combined in a specific way.</p>\n<p>Rhythm can have a significant impact on melody. For example, simple rhythmic patterns help a melody become more memorable and repeatable, allowing listeners to learn and connect with the piece more easily.</p>\n<p>Moreover, rhythm can create specific emotions in listeners, and melody can reinforce those emotions. For instance, fast and energetic rhythmic patterns can evoke excitement and energy, which are enhanced when paired with a fast and passionate melody.</p>\n<p>Overall, the relationship between rhythm and melody is crucial and has a great influence on the listening experience and the emotions of the audience.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_2257\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"621\"]<img class=\"wp-image-2257 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/rhytm_in_1.png\" alt=\"Music rhythm\" width=\"621\" height=\"314\" /> Rhythm is the most important factor in the science of music[/caption]\n\n<h2>The Importance of the Relationship Between Rhythm and Harmony in Ensemble Playing</h2>\n<p>The connection between rhythm and harmony in group performance is very important and contributes to a successful execution. Harmony refers to a set of notes and sounds that occur simultaneously and are connected through rhythm.</p>\n<p>Rhythm helps ensemble players better understand harmony by repeating rhythmic patterns, allowing groups to perform pieces with greater coordination. It also enables musicians to express their notes and sounds accurately and in the right position relative to each other, resulting in the best possible performance of harmony.</p>\n<p>The presence of rhythm in a piece makes different instrumentalists in an ensemble play in sync, often causing the audience to start clapping together spontaneously.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/harmony-in-music/\">harmony</a> can help rhythm interact more effectively with melodic and harmonic structures, allowing rhythm to have a stronger impact on listeners. Harmony can also create a specific emotional atmosphere that strengthens feelings compatible with the rhythm, leading to a more powerful listening experience.</p>\n<p>In summary, the relationship between rhythm and harmony in ensemble playing is highly important and can provide listeners with unique emotions and an unparalleled auditory experience.</p>\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions about this article on rhythm in music, please share them in the comments section to help improve the quality of this educational content with the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com\">Sornaz Music School</a>.</p>', '2023-04-27 14:20:01', 0, '2024-08-30 19:42:45', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1464, 'posts', 7, 'en', NULL, 'What is melody? Why do melodies remain memorable? What are the 3 main characteristics of melodies?', NULL, 'This article explains the concept of melody in music as the most memorable and essential part of a musical piece—the part people usually hum. Melody is formed by a sequence of notes combined with rhythm and pitch, creating a continuous musical line that conveys emotions.\n\nFrom a music theory perspective, melody is a structured sequence of notes and rhythms that develops horizontally, in contrast to harmony, which is vertical. Its main elements include beat, rhythm, and pitch, while characteristics such as contour, range, scale, direction, and form help describe it.\n\nThe article also highlights the importance of rhythm and dynamics in shaping melody and influencing listeners’ emotions. Melodies can be created through creativity, experience, inspiration from existing works, or digital tools.\n\nFinally, the longevity of melodies depends on factors like simplicity, memorability, repetition, coordination with other musical elements, variation, and emotional impact. Practical steps for creating melodies include starting with an idea, choosing notes, developing rhythm, expanding the melody, and consistent practice.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">melody</span></strong> in music. We provide a simple definition of melody, explore its important characteristics, talk a little about how melodies are created and the process of melody writing, and finally discuss the secrets behind why some melodies become timeless and unforgettable. Continue with us in this article...</p>\n\n<h2>The Simplest Definition of Melody</h2>\n<p>Throughout the day — while driving, working, relaxing, or having fun — we often sing or hum part of a song we like.</p>\n<p>Sometimes, to check whether someone has heard a song, we hum a part of it for them.</p>\n<p>This part of the song that we hum is called the melody.</p>\n<p>When you hear a song, you usually remember the part that is often the most beautiful section and can hum it immediately.</p>\n<p>You remember many songs and hum them during the day. The part you can recall well is usually the melody of the song.</p>\n<p>These were the simplest definitions to help you understand the concept of melody.</p>\n<p>Melody can be called the main essence of a musical piece. The term “melody” comes from the Greek word “melos,” meaning “song” or “sequence.” In definition, it is a series of rhythmic and successive sounds that are heard in sequence. Singing, tunes, and musical notes arise from human inner emotions and express sorrow, pleasure, joy, excitement, and spiritual peace.</p>\n<p>For this reason, it is easy to distinguish a sad and mournful tune from a happy and cheerful melody, and to understand why hearing a melody can transfer the same feelings that the composer and performer experienced to the listener.</p>\n<p>A melody may be performed with an instrument or voice, in which case it is called “homophonic melody” (single-line texture). If the same melody is performed polyphonically — that is, with several different instruments and different notes — which forms the basis of the science of harmony and counterpoint, it is called “polyphonic melody.”</p>\n<p>To better understand musical textures such as monophonic, homophonic, and polyphonic, read the article <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/texture-in-music/\">“What is musical texture?”</a>.</p>\n<p>The simplest definition we can give for the concept of melody in music is: “Melody is another word for the tune or the tonal line of a musical piece.” If we want to make this definition a bit more scientific and precise, we can say: “Melody is created when you combine rhythm and the pitch of different sounds to build a part of a piece or song that you intend to sing or perform.”</p>\n<p>But how is melody defined in the science of music theory?</p>\n\n<h2>Melody from the Perspective of Music Theory</h2>\n<p>In <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/what-is-music-theory/\">music theory</a>, melody refers to a pattern of different notes and rhythms that are performed successively with rising and falling pitches. In fact, melody is generally the part of a song that makes it feel floating and beautiful, and is often recognized as an essential part of the song’s structure. Overall, a melody consists of a sequence of notes and rhythms performed continuously throughout the piece in a way that attracts the listener’s ear and creates a specific feeling.</p>\n<p>It is usually known as the part of a song or musical piece in which a main line of notes — most often performed vocally (as a lead vocal) or as a solo — stands out from the rest of the music.</p>\n<p>Just as every word plays a different and unique role in sentence construction, every note in a melody also has its own distinct role and character.</p>\n<p>Melody is the core of a song or piece. The composer then expands and develops this core using other techniques taught in music theory. In fact, the other parts of a song are its complements.</p>\n<p>In orchestras and ensemble playing, melody stands in contrast to <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/harmony-in-music/\">harmony</a>. Melody is the horizontal or linear development of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/music-what-is-it/\">music</a> — each instrument plays its own melody — while harmony refers to the simultaneous sounding of different instruments at one moment. Harmony deals with the vertical development of music and discusses the notes played simultaneously by different instruments and their pleasing combination.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_2268\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"815\"]<img class=\"wp-image-2268 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/l0doxdyviepo.jpeg\" alt=\"Song melody\" width=\"815\" height=\"420\" /> The melody is the most memorable part of a song.[/caption]\n\n<h2>What Characteristics Make Up a Melody?</h2>\n<p>Melody is made up of various musical elements, including:</p>\n<p><strong>Beat:</strong> The main pulse of the music that unifies it. The beat is the unit for measuring and dividing time in music and indicates the speed of performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Rhythm:</strong> The duration or length of notes — how short or long they are.</p>\n<p><strong>Pitch:</strong> How high or low the sound is.</p>\n<p>When all these elements are combined, a melody is created.</p>\n<p>In addition to the above, melody can sometimes be described using other terms. Here are some of them:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Melodic Contour:</strong> Refers to the shape and movement of a melody — its rises and falls. It can be thought of as the “shape” of the melody.</li>\n  <li><strong>Range:</strong> The distance between the highest and lowest notes of the melody.</li>\n  <li><strong>Scale:</strong> The pitches chosen for the melody.</li>\n  <li><strong>Style:</strong> The character of the melody, such as light, bouncy, dark, heavy, smooth, etc.</li>\n  <li><strong>Direction:</strong> The direction of the melody’s movement, indicating its climax and subsequent descents and other motions throughout the piece.</li>\n  <li><strong>Form:</strong> The form or forms in which the melody is constructed. A melody can be thought of as a puzzle made up of different sections. Read the article <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/form-in-music/\">“Form in Music”</a> for more information.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>What is Melodic Contour?</h3>\n<p>Melodic contour refers to the overall shape of a melody, often shown by connecting the main notes. For example, the melody played by a trumpet in ancient battles usually began with a leap of a perfect fifth (2.5 tones) and then descended within that interval. In melodic contour diagrams, these leaps and movements are shown with a continuous line.</p>\n<p>Read the article <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/musical-interval/\">“What is a musical interval?”</a> to learn about intervals and semitones in music.</p>\n<p>This contour or shape of the melody can be illustrated on paper with a continuous line that rises from low to high, curves softly, and continues to the end of the piece.</p>\n\n<h2>The Importance of Rhythm in Melody</h2>\n<p><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/rhythm-in-music/\">Rhythm</a> is one of the most important elements of music and plays a significant role in melody. Rhythm refers to the pattern of repeating beats that allows the performer to create a specific, flowing musical pattern by playing a sequence of related notes. These rhythmic patterns help the musician gain a better understanding of the melody and its various emotional dimensions.</p>\n<p>Using rhythm, a musician can create different emotions. For example, a fast and energetic rhythm can convey joy and excitement, while a slow and calm rhythm creates a sense of sadness and melancholy.</p>\n<p>Rhythm also helps the listener remember the melody more easily and connect with it. Therefore, rhythm and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/rhythm-reading-in-music/\">rhythm reading</a> are essential for creating various emotions, influencing the listener, and improving the quality of the melody.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_2269\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1000\"]<img class=\"wp-image-2269 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/Melody-Guide2.jpg\" alt=\"Music melody\" width=\"1000\" height=\"562\" /> Melody is the foundation for starting composition.[/caption]\n\n<h2>The Importance of Dynamics and Pitch in Melody</h2>\n<p>Dynamics in music refer to the intensity or volume with which notes are played — from soft and gentle to loud and powerful. Dynamics show the variations in sound intensity within a melody. For example, using high dynamics can give the listener a feeling of excitement and energy, while lower dynamics may create calmness, comfort, or sadness.</p>\n<p>Pitch also plays an important role in creating different emotions. By using rising and falling pitches, composers can influence feelings such as joy, sadness, fear, and sorrow.</p>\n<p>Therefore, dynamics and pitch are two key factors in music that can evoke various emotions in the listener and enhance the quality of the melody.</p>\n\n<h2>How Are Melodies Created?</h2>\n<p>First, the performer or composer looks for ideas to build a melody. These ideas can be inspired by social, cultural, mystical, emotional, political, or any other subjects.</p>\n<p>A melody may arise spontaneously in a moment, or in some cases, composers use musical patterns and rules to construct it.</p>\n<p>A composer might start with a small fragment of notes, experiment with different combinations, perform it, and then refine it.</p>\n<p>Melodies are created in various ways. The most important methods include:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Creativity of the performer or composer:</strong> One of the most well-known ways is using personal creativity. In this method, the musician creates the melody freely without strictly following rules.</li>\n  <li><strong>Experience and training:</strong> Professional musicians and composers learn various rules and techniques through experience and education. These include combining notes into specific patterns.</li>\n  <li><strong>Inspiration from existing pieces:</strong> Composers and performers often use existing works as models for creating new melodies.</li>\n  <li><strong>Using assistive tools:</strong> With technological advances, software and plugins are used to help create new melodies, often inspired by existing ones or based on musical rules.</li>\n</ol>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_2452\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"700\"]<img class=\"wp-image-2452 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/word-image-24049-1.webp\" alt=\"When writing the initial melody of pieces, music theory rules are usually not used.\" width=\"700\" height=\"406\" /> When writing the initial melody of pieces, music theory rules are usually not used.[/caption]\n\n<h2>Why Do Some Melodies Become Timeless?</h2>\n<p>Music and melodies have been among the oldest arts in all societies and historical periods. One of the main reasons for the longevity of melodies is their ability to create emotions and connect people. Melodies are often linked to positive and negative feelings and serve as a universal language for conveying emotions, culture, and ideas.</p>\n<p>Another reason is that melodies are repeated continuously and become imprinted in the listeners’ minds. This repetition and memorability cause them to be naturally stored in memory, so that recalling the melody instantly brings back the associated emotions and memories.</p>\n<p>Ultimately, melodies that have had the greatest impact on listeners become prominent works in music history and will always remain part of human culture and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_humanity\">civilization</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>What Is the Secret of Timeless Songs and Pieces?</h2>\n<p>Melody is recognized as one of the most important elements of music and plays a crucial role in forming a musical piece. It is the part that stays in the listener’s mind and attracts them.</p>\n<p>Determining the most important features of a melody is difficult because of the differences between various melodies and musical styles. However, some key characteristics of memorable melodies include:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Simplicity and accessibility:</strong> Simple melodies that are easy to understand and highly repeatable tend to be more memorable.</li>\n  <li><strong>Repeatability and memorability:</strong> A melody should be easy to repeat and stay in the listener’s mind. This allows musicians and composers to combine the piece in a way that is more accessible to the audience.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rhythm and flow:</strong> Proper rhythm and flow help the listener easily understand the sequence of notes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Interaction with other musical elements:</strong> Melody must harmonize well with the harmony and rhythm of the piece to create a coherent whole.</li>\n  <li><strong>Flexibility:</strong> A memorable melody is often a combination of repeating simple patterns with small variations in each repetition. This keeps the melody interesting and dynamic.</li>\n  <li><strong>Emotional impact and psychological effect:</strong> Melody can act as a psychological tool by evoking different emotions such as calmness, joy, excitement, or energy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Because of its strong influence on the listener, melody is considered one of the main reasons why certain songs and pieces become timeless.</p>\n\n<h2>How to Start Creating Melodies?</h2>\n<p>Creating a melody is a creative process that improves with repeated practice. Here are some steps to get started:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Start with an initial idea:</strong> Begin by playing several notes on your instrument or singing them to find a basic idea for the melody.</li>\n  <li><strong>Learn the principles of music:</strong> Familiarity with notes, chords, and different instruments helps you choose suitable notes and chords for your melodies.</li>\n  <li><strong>Choose appropriate notes:</strong> Select notes that harmonize well together. You can do this by experimenting on your instrument or using basic musical notes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Choose an instrument:</strong> The choice of instrument is one of the most important steps. It should match the style and emotion you want to convey (e.g., piano or violin for classical, guitar or electronic sounds for pop).</li>\n  <li><strong>Create a rhythmic pattern:</strong> A specific rhythmic pattern can give your melody a unique character and add movement and variety.</li>\n  <li><strong>Develop the melody:</strong> After creating the first few notes and the rhythm, expand the melody by combining notes and rhythmic patterns. You can make it more engaging by varying the pitch and range.</li>\n  <li><strong>Experiment and practice:</strong> Once you have a basic melody, review it and make changes until it feels right.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Finally, to develop the ability to create memorable melodies and songs, you must practice consistently. Remember that even the greatest musicians and composers in history started as beginners. With dedication and continuous practice, they were able to leave their names in the history of world music.</p>\n<p>If you have any comments or questions about this article on melody in music, please share them in the comments section to help improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-04-28 11:34:26', 0, '2024-08-30 19:43:00', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1465, 'posts', 8, 'en', NULL, 'What is harmony? Why should we learn harmony to become professional? 2 Important Methods in Musical Harmony', NULL, 'This article explores the concept of harmony in music, which refers to the simultaneous combination of sounds or instruments to create a cohesive musical result. A simple analogy compares harmony to teamwork in a football match, where coordinated effort leads to success.\n\nIn music theory, harmony is the combination of notes played together, typically forming chords. When three or more notes are played simultaneously, a chord is created. Harmony represents the vertical aspect of music, in contrast to melody, which is horizontal.\n\nThe article also discusses the historical development of harmony, from early church music and the emergence of polyphony to its evolution during the Renaissance, Baroque, and Classical periods. Two main approaches to multi-part music are highlighted:\n\nCounterpoint: combining independent melodic lines following strict rules\nHarmonic approach: using chords to create musical cohesion\n\nIt further explains how harmony works in an orchestra, where different instruments play individual notes that together form a unified chord.\n\nFinally, three main types of harmony are introduced:\n\nDiatonic harmony: based on notes within a single scale\nNon-diatonic harmony: includes notes outside the scale for added complexity\nAtonal harmony: lacks a tonal center and does not follow major/minor systems', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>This article is about <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">harmony</span></strong> and consonance in music. Harmony essentially refers to polyphony in music or the simultaneous sounding of several instruments or voices. Continue with us in this article...</p>\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>A Simple Example of Harmony</h2>\n<p>Imagine a football match in which 22 players, divided into two teams of 11, face each other. At any moment during the game, one team has possession of the ball and tries to score against the opposing team, while the other team strives to regain possession.</p>\n<p>In this process, the players of each team work with coordination and cooperation among their teammates, following pre-planned tactics practiced during training, to score goals or regain the ball.</p>\n<p>In this example, the players represent the musicians, the head coach plays the role of the composer of the group, and the teamwork and predetermined tactics practiced by the team represent harmony.</p>\n<p>In this game, the result of the match represents the musical piece, and the team that has a better composer and better musicians — and creates a piece with more beautiful harmony — will win the match.</p>\n<p>With this simple example, the concept of harmony can be easily understood.</p>\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>What Does Harmony Mean in Music?</h2>\n<p>The word “harmony” in music comes from a Greek term meaning “proportion” or “agreement” in music. Music can be analyzed by considering its various elements or sections either separately or in combination. The main elements of music include dynamics, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/texture-in-music/\">musical texture</a>, harmony, tone, timbre, melody, rhythm, and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/form-in-music/\">form</a>.</p>\n<p>In the following, we will explain this section from the perspective of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/what-is-music-theory/\">music theory</a>.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_2280\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1024\"]<img class=\"wp-image-2280 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/shakhes-153400-60-1024x282-1.jpg\" alt=\"Harmony in music\" width=\"1024\" height=\"282\" /> Harmony in music means the simultaneous sounding of several voices together at the same moment[/caption]\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>Harmony from the Perspective of Music Theory</h2>\n<p>As mentioned earlier, harmony (from the Greek word meaning “proportion”) in everyday language or philosophy refers to “agreement” or “balance.” Etymologically, harmony means unity and coordination, expressing oneness in the parts that form a complete whole. Harmony is the process of combining individual sounds that are analyzed when heard simultaneously. The concept of harmony usually occurs when hearing frequencies, pitch range, tone color, notes, or <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/chord/\">chords</a> at the same time.</p>\n<p>When three or more notes are played simultaneously, a chord is formed.</p>\n<p>The science of harmony often refers to the vertical aspect of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/music-what-is-it/\">music</a> and is distinct from horizontal and melodic movement. Its opposite is counterpoint, which describes the relationship between independent melodic lines and polyphony, and sometimes highlights the difference between the two.</p>\n<p>Throughout the history of Western music, two different methods have been used for making music polyphonic. The first method is the knowledge of counterpoint, which was created by interweaving several melodies while observing strict and rigid rules. In the Middle Ages, the concept of harmony referred to two voices, and in the Renaissance period, it referred to three voices heard simultaneously. These changes were based on a different division from the Pythagorean division.</p>\n<p>Historically and mathematically, harmony goes back to the work of Pythagoras and his followers. They were the first to experiment with strings of different lengths, relating the lengths of the strings to musical notes, experimenting with air columns containing liquid, and conducting other experiments in an attempt to develop their theory (the mathematical structure of the world).</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_2278\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1024\"]<img class=\"wp-image-2278 size-large\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/1200px-Quartal_chord-1024x572.png\" alt=\"Chord\" width=\"1024\" height=\"572\" /> Chords are the foundation of harmony[/caption]\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>The Formation of Harmony in Church Music</h2>\n<p>In the 9th and 10th centuries AD, religious-artistic figures supported by the church, after making progress in the areas of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/rhythm-in-music/\">rhythm</a> and melody in their musical pieces, gradually began experimenting with making music polyphonic and established simple rules for this purpose. At that time, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Church_(building)\">church</a> music was performed only vocally. Church musicians realized that the human voice could be divided into sections with different pitch ranges. For example, the voices of adult men and young boys differed in pitch range and tessitura.</p>\n<p>From the very beginning of polyphony, the various sections of the human voice, following the melodies written for them, were required to sing <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/melody/\">melodies</a> that were somewhat different from each other simultaneously. This method, with changes resulting from the passage of time and increased knowledge and understanding of music, continues to this day.</p>\n<p>After centuries of experience, it became clear that the most suitable vocal sections for polyphonic music in an educational program are four parts, as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Soprano</strong>: the high, bright female voice.</li>\n  <li><strong>Alto</strong>: the lower, richer female voice.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tenor</strong>: the high male voice.</li>\n  <li><strong>Bass</strong>: the low, deep male voice.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The ranges shown in the figure below are somewhat approximate. Since every person’s vocal range is not exactly the same as another’s, and there are differences even among high or low male voices, it is not possible to show the exact range and tessitura of each vocal section.</p>\n\n<h2>A Look at the Two Methods of Making Music Polyphonic</h2>\n<p>Almost from the beginning of polyphony, the church — following its own worldview — on one hand, and non-church musicians (those who performed instrumental music or instrumental and vocal music among the people) on the other, tried to make music polyphonic in two somewhat different ways.</p>\n<p>The first and historically older method, related to the church and religious music, was that the composer paid more attention to the individual melodic lines of each part and the independence of each part’s melody from the others.</p>\n<p>Another effort of church musicians was to make the melody of each part harmonize with the melodies of the other parts according to a set of strict and rigid rules. Later, this method was called “contrapuntal.”</p>\n<p>The second method, related to folk and non-church composers, was that the composer tried to create the most beautiful possible melody in each part by connecting chosen chords. This method is called “harmonic.” The harmonic method, of course, made polyphony simpler.</p>\n<p>Today, both methods are used with modifications resulting from various advances in composition techniques, and in many musical pieces, the two approaches are blended together simultaneously.</p>\n<p>In conclusion, it is essential to note that students of composition in music schools must become fully familiar with the rules and methods of both harmony and counterpoint. Since the knowledge of harmony is simpler, it is generally taught before counterpoint.</p>\n\n<h2>What is Counterpoint?</h2>\n<p>Counterpoint (from the Latin “punctus contra punctum” — point against point) means “playing against each other” and refers to one of the most important concepts in musical harmony. In fact, counterpoint is a type of harmony formed by using two or more independent melodic lines. In counterpoint, each melodic line is performed independently and harmonized with the other lines.</p>\n<p>In counterpoint, the separate melodic lines must be combined and played simultaneously. Since each melodic line is performed independently according to its own rhythm and notes, this technique is very important for creating greater variety and richness in music.</p>\n<p>Furthermore, counterpoint is one of the most essential skills required for learning composition and arranging music and is considered one of the fundamental topics in the teaching of music theory.</p>\n\n<h2>The Evolution of Harmony After the Renaissance</h2>\n<p>After the Renaissance, harmony evolved dramatically. With the development of Baroque music and later Classical music, new methods for using harmony in music were created.</p>\n<p>In the Baroque period, musicians used harmony to create more complex pieces. This was an era that showcased the power and richness of music and led the art of music toward further progress. During this period, more complex chords and new harmonies were employed, resulting in pieces with richer and more intricate content.</p>\n<p>Then, in the Classical period, harmony developed significantly. In this era, famous musicians such as Mozart and Haydn used harmony more extensively, and for this reason, it is known as the golden age of harmony in music history.</p>\n<p>In summary, after the Renaissance, harmony developed remarkably in Baroque and Classical music, and its use led to the creation of more complex musical pieces.</p>\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>How Does Harmony Occur in a Musical Piece or Orchestra?</h2>\n<p>Harmony is generally analyzed in the form of a series of chords within a musical piece. If we consider an orchestra, at any given moment, the viola player might be playing the note A, the violin player might perform C#, and the flute player might play F#. In this situation, the simultaneous sounding of these three notes creates an “F# minor” chord.</p>\n<p>As we can see, although each player in this imaginary orchestra is only playing one note, in reality they are all in coordination and proportion with one another and are performing an “F# minor” chord together. When all the instruments of the orchestra play a group of notes that are compatible with a specific chord, a complete chord is formed through the coordination of the instruments.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_4061\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1240\"]<img class=\"wp-image-4061 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/sornaz_harmoony_003.jpg\" alt=\"Harmony in an orchestra is created by playing the notes of a chord simultaneously on different instruments.\" width=\"1240\" height=\"698\" /> Harmony in an orchestra is created by playing the notes of a chord simultaneously on different instruments.[/caption]\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>Three Different Types of Harmony in Music</h2>\n<p>Usually, a musical piece revolves around a specific note called the tonic, which is heard more than the other notes throughout the piece. Therefore, the harmony of musical pieces also revolves mostly around tonic chords. Based on the chords used in the harmony of a piece, harmony in music can be divided into three different types:</p>\n\n<h3>1. Diatonic Harmony</h3>\n<p>Diatonic harmony can be observed in most musical pieces from ancient Greece (<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/church-modes/\">church modes</a>) to Renaissance and Baroque hymns and contemporary pop music. In this type, harmony is built using the notes of a single scale and around the tonic chord.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Non-Diatonic Harmony</h3>\n<p>In non-diatonic harmony, notes are used that are not all completely within one <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/scale-and-tonality/\">scale</a>. The composer or arranger goes beyond the seven notes of the chosen scale and also uses notes outside the scale when building chords. This form of harmony is most commonly seen in jazz music.</p>\n<p>Suppose you are in the key of A♭ major, which consists of the notes A♭, B♭, C, D♭, E♭, F, and G. You play a Bb chord consisting of the notes B♭, D, and F. As you can see, this chord contains the note D, which does not exist in the A♭ major scale. Although using this chord in A♭ major may seem a bit unusual, it still creates a memorable combination.</p>\n<p>Non-diatonic harmony is not a new concept in music. The preludes and fugues of the German composer Johann Sebastian Bach, even though they were written nearly 400 years ago, are still used as a primary source for teaching non-diatonic notes.</p>\n\n<h3>3. Atonal or Atonal Harmony</h3>\n<p>This type of harmony does not revolve around the tonal center of the music. In fact, it is not constructed within the framework of major and minor scales. For example, in the European Classical period, atonal music was usually found in the works of Arnold Schoenberg. However, Schoenberg himself did not use the term “atonal.” Instead, he introduced the twelve-tone scale (a scale that uses all 12 semitones) and gave equal importance to all 12 notes.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions about this article on harmony in music, please share them in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this educational content with the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com\">Sornaz Music School</a>.</p>', '2023-04-29 14:22:41', 0, '2024-08-30 19:43:15', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1466, 'posts', 9, 'en', NULL, 'Rhythm reading in music, Introduction to the main rules and concepts of timing in rhythm reading', NULL, 'This article introduces the concept of rhythm reading in music and explains its fundamental elements such as beat, measure, and time signature. It explores different types of meters, including simple, compound, and irregular meters. The article also discusses note values and their role in shaping rhythm. The main goal is to help learners better understand the timing structure of music and improve their rhythm reading skills.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>This article is about <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">rhythm reading</span></strong> in music. In it, we become familiar with the concepts of beat, measure, time signature (meter), and the different types of simple, compound, and irregular (asymmetric) meters in rhythm reading. Continue with us...</p>\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>Basic Concepts for Learning Rhythm and Rhythm Reading</h2>\n<p>To learn rhythm reading in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/music-what-is-it/\">music</a>, we need to become familiar with many concepts. In this article, we focus on the principles of timing in rhythm reading. In other articles, we will examine note reading on the staff, different methods of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/history-of-music-reading/\">music reading</a> and notation, and other related topics.</p>\n\n<h3>The Concept of Beat in Rhythm Reading</h3>\n<p>When a song or piece of music is played, if we ask the audience to clap along, they will clap together in unison without knowing anything about <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/rhythm-in-music/\">rhythm</a> or rhythm reading.</p>\n<p>Similarly, in religious ceremonies such as chest-beating or chain-beating during mourning processions, people strike their chests or hit the chains on their backs in perfect synchronization while reciting elegies, even though they have no knowledge of rhythm reading.</p>\n<p>This happens because of a force present at that particular moment in the music that compels the person to react. We call these moments “beats.”</p>\n<p>Therefore, we can conclude that rhythm creates an element called the beat. This type of beat that urges us to react is called a strong beat. When there is a strong beat that makes the listener want to clap, there must also be a weak beat so that the strong beat can have its own character and identity.</p>\n<p>For this reason, in rhythm reading we encounter both strong and weak beats.</p>\n\n<h3>The Concept of Measure (Bar) in Rhythm Reading</h3>\n<p>The intervals between the times when the listener claps are made up of strong and weak beats, and these intervals must be equal to each other so that the clapping remains organized and regular.</p>\n<p>In the end, we realize that clapping on the strong beat divides the progress of the music into equal and uniform time units. In <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/what-is-music-theory/\">music theory</a>, the distance between two strong beats (two claps) is called a measure (or bar).</p>\n<p>To identify rhythm and facilitate reading, measures are separated from each other by vertical lines.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_009_reading_rhythm/webp/sornaz_009_reading_rhythm_01.webp\" alt=\"Measure, time signature and common symbols in music rhythm reading\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 1- Measure, time signature and its common symbols in music rhythm reading[/caption]\n\n<h4>Simple Meters in Rhythm Reading</h4>\n<p>In rhythm reading, the simplest possible meter is one that has one strong beat and one weak beat. We call this two-beat meter a duple meter.</p>\n<p>Obviously, there is no such thing as a one-beat meter, because every measure must have at least one weak beat so that the strong beat can have its own distinct character.</p>\n<p>Sometimes a measure may have three beats, in which case the first beat is strong and the second and third beats are weak. This is called a triple meter (such as in a <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waltz\">waltz</a>), and the difference between the strong and weak beats is clearly noticeable.</p>\n<p>A measure can also be quadruple, in which the first beat is strong, the second is weak, the third is semi-strong, and the fourth is weak.</p>\n<p>In music theory, the simple forms of meters generally go up to four beats.</p>\n<p>To represent beats in music, specific symbols are used, which we will become familiar with.</p>\n\n<h4>Musical Beats (Notes or Durations) According to Their Time Value</h4>\n<p>In a general and comprehensive definition, rhythm is an organized sequence of sounds and silences with specific time values. For example, the tick-tock of a clock or the beating of a pulse and heart are all combinations of sound and silence that reach our ears at equal time intervals. In the following, we will discuss the shapes of notes and rests, and then explain how to count them.</p>\n<p>Musical notes can be displayed in different shapes, and their time duration is determined by their shape. These shapes include:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Whole note (semibreve)</li>\n  <li>Half note (minim)</li>\n  <li>Quarter note (crotchet)</li>\n  <li>Eighth note (quaver)</li>\n  <li>Sixteenth note (semiquaver)</li>\n  <li>Thirty-second note (demisemiquaver)</li>\n  <li>and so on...</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In rhythm reading, the whole note is used as the main unit of music, and the other notes are divided according to it. The time value of a whole note depends on the scale chosen by the composer, so we cannot say that every whole note lasts exactly one second or any fixed duration. If we consider the interval between our claps to be equal to one whole note.</p>\n<p>If we divide this time interval into two equal parts and count with each clap — one… two… — the distance between each number and the next (which is half the clap interval) can be considered equal to a half note, whose time value is half of a whole note.</p>\n<p>If we divide the time value of a half note into two equal parts, each new value is called a quarter note.</p>\n<p>If we divide the time value of a quarter note in half, we get an eighth note. The time value of a sixteenth note is half that of an eighth note, and this division continues similarly.</p>\n<p>Now, if we calculate the relative time value of each note compared to the whole note in rhythm reading, we get the following:</p>\n\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_009_reading_rhythm/webp/sornaz_009_reading_rhythm_02.webp\" alt=\"Division of musical notes (durations) and their time ratios in rhythm reading\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 2- Division of musical notes (durations) and their time ratios in rhythm reading[/caption]\n\n<p>If we place a dot after any note, the new time value becomes the original note’s value plus half of that value. For example, the time value of a dotted quarter note equals one quarter note plus one eighth note. (The note’s value is multiplied by 3/2.)</p>\n<p>If we place two dots after a note, the new time value equals the original note plus half plus a quarter of its value. For example, a double-dotted quarter note equals one quarter note + one eighth note + one sixteenth note.</p>\n\n<h4>Time Signature (Meter)</h4>\n<p>What is the use of these numbers in rhythm reading? At the beginning of a piece, the composer must indicate to the reader or performer how many beats are in each measure so that the performer does not get confused while reading the rhythm. In other words, the composer must specify which time value has been chosen as the beat unit.</p>\n<p>A duple meter piece can use any of the values mentioned above — it can have two half notes, two quarter notes, two eighth notes, etc., per measure.</p>\n<p>The choice of each time value depends on the composer’s taste and understanding of the piece. Generally, for slower music, larger note values are chosen.</p>\n<p>If the composer uses two half notes in each measure of a duple meter, they have chosen two units of 2/1.</p>\n<p>2/2 = 1/2 × 2</p>\n<p>So it is enough to write 2/2 at the beginning of the piece.</p>\n<p>Such a fraction written at the beginning of musical pieces is called a time signature (or meter).</p>\n<p>If quarter notes were used instead of half notes, the time signature would be 2/4, and if eighth notes were used, it would be 2/8.</p>\n<p>The same principle applies to triple and quadruple meters. For a triple meter, the composer can use 3/2 for three half notes, 3/4 for three quarter notes, or 3/8 for three eighth notes per measure.</p>\n<p>For a quadruple meter, the composer can use 4/2 for four half notes, 4/4 for four quarter notes, or 4/8 for four eighth notes per measure.</p>\n<p>A measure does not always consist of notes of the same shape. It is not necessary for all notes to be half notes, quarter notes, or eighth notes. It is sufficient that the total time value of the notes used in each measure equals the value indicated by the time signature. For example, instead of a 2/4 duple meter having two quarter notes per measure, it can contain “one half note,” “four eighth notes,” “one quarter note and two eighth notes,” or any other combination whose total time value equals two quarter notes.</p>\n<p>Note that in divisions, we usually group notes by connecting their flags (eighth notes, sixteenth notes, etc.) so that the divisions within the measure are clearly visible.</p>\n<p>In rhythm reading, instead of the 4/4 time signature, we often use the symbol C (common time).</p>\n\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_009_reading_rhythm/webp/sornaz_009_reading_rhythm_04.webp\" alt=\"Simple time signatures for different note values in music rhythm reading\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 3- Simple time signatures for different note values in music rhythm reading[/caption]\n\n<p><strong>For more information about the guitar and piano, and useful tips for buying these instruments, please read the articles <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/comprehensive-guide-to-buying-guitar/\">Comprehensive Guide to Buying a Guitar</a> and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/comprehensive-guide-to-buying-piano/\">Comprehensive Guide to Buying a Piano</a>.</strong></p>\n\n<h4>Compound Meters in Rhythm Reading</h4>\n<p>As mentioned earlier, in rhythm reading the simple forms of meters go up to four beats. Every simple meter can be turned into a compound meter. In other words, by dotting the beat unit of a simple meter, it can be converted into a compound meter. In this case, both the time signature and the beat unit of the piece change.</p>\n<p>Suppose we want to convert the simple meter 4/2 into a compound meter. As we know, 4/2 is a simple duple meter in which each beat is a quarter note. Now, according to the explanation above, we dot the beat unit of the simple meter, turning the quarter note into a dotted quarter note.</p>\n<p>Since we consider all divisions based on the whole note, and a whole note cannot be equally divided into dotted quarter notes, we have to choose another note as the new beat unit.</p>\n<p>If we take the quarter note as the beat unit, we would have three quarter notes per measure, which would make the time signature 3/4. However, since the original rhythm is duple and 3/4 belongs to simple triple meters, we are forced to use the eighth note as the beat unit. In this case, because there are six eighth notes per measure, the appropriate time signature for the piece becomes 6/8.</p>\n<p>To avoid confusing the number of beats in compound and simple meters, we use numbers larger than 4 for compound meters. Therefore, when converting a simple meter to a compound meter, the time value of the beat unit in the compound meter becomes half the time value of the beat unit in the corresponding simple meter.</p>\n<p>As you saw in the example above, the beat unit of the simple duple meter was a quarter note, while the beat unit of the corresponding compound duple meter was an eighth note. This process works the same way for other time signatures.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_009_reading_rhythm/webp/sornaz_009_reading_rhythm_03.webp\" alt=\"Division of dotted notes in music rhythm reading\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 4- Division of dotted notes (durations) in music rhythm reading[/caption]\n\n<p>According to the relationship above, by multiplying the simple time signature by 2/3, we can reach the corresponding compound time signature. In the figure below, you can see the corresponding simple and compound time signatures for duple, triple, and quadruple meters with half-note, quarter-note, and eighth-note beat units.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_009_reading_rhythm/webp/sornaz_009_reading_rhythm_05.webp\" alt=\"Compound time signatures for different note values in music rhythm reading\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 5- Compound time signatures for different note values in music rhythm reading[/caption]\n\n<h4>Irregular (Asymmetric) Meters in Rhythm Reading</h4>\n<p>Time signatures whose numerator is greater than 4 and is not divisible by 3 are called irregular (asymmetric) meters. For example, 5/4, which is actually the sum of two simple time signatures (3/4 + 2/4 = 5/4) or (2/4 + 3/4 = 5/4).</p>\n<p>The above definition may seem somewhat confusing. To clarify: according to previous definitions, simple meters are those with two, three, or four beats, so the numerator of simple time signatures is always equal to or smaller than 4.</p>\n<p>Compound time signatures are obtained by multiplying the corresponding simple time signature by 2/3, and their numerator will be one of the numbers 6, 9, or 12, all of which are divisible by 3.</p>\n<p>Therefore, if the numerator of a time signature is greater than 4 and is not divisible by 3, that time signature definitely belongs to an irregular meter.</p>\n<p>However, the above definition is incomplete for irregular meters!</p>\n<p>The first problem is that, for example, 15/8 according to the above definition is not considered irregular, while it actually belongs to an irregular meter and can be performed as 3+6+6 or any other combination.</p>\n<p>Instead of saying “not divisible by 3,” we should say the numerator is not one of the numbers 6, 9, or 12.</p>\n<p>The second issue concerns cases where a compound time signature is performed irregularly. For example, 12/8 can be performed as 3+5+4 or other combinations, in which case it belongs to a piece with an irregular rhythm.</p>\n<p>Usually, to specify irregular meters in rhythm reading, the internal combinations of the time signature are written, and within each measure, dashed vertical lines separate the internal simple and compound meters from each other.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_009_reading_rhythm/webp/sornaz_009_reading_rhythm_06.webp\" alt=\"Irregular time signatures and how to display them in music rhythm reading\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 6- Irregular time signatures and how to display them in music rhythm reading[/caption]\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any comments or questions about this educational article, please share them in the comments section to help us improve the quality of this content.</p>', '2023-04-30 14:49:43', 0, '2024-08-30 19:43:37', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1467, 'posts', 10, 'en', NULL, 'History of the invention of musical notation and note reading, Different clefs in note reading', NULL, 'This article explores the history and fundamentals of music notation and staff notation. It begins by explaining that before the invention of musical staff lines, simple symbols were used to help recall melodies, but over time, a more precise system for recording music was developed.\n\nMusic notation is an international language that allows musicians worldwide to read and perform compositions. Sounds are represented using seven basic notes (Do, Re, Mi, Fa, Sol, La, Si), which are named through two systems: syllabic and alphabetical. These notes repeat across different pitch ranges, and the distance between identical notes is called an octave.\n\nNotes are written on the staff, which consists of five parallel lines. The position of each note determines its pitch. To identify note names on the staff, clefs are used, mainly treble (G clef), bass (F clef), and alto/tenor (C clef).\n\nSince the staff has limited space, ledger lines are added above or below it to extend the range. For instruments with a wide range, such as the piano, a grand staff (combining treble and bass clefs) is used.\n\nOverall, the article emphasizes that mastering music notation across different staffs is essential for anyone learning music.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>Before the “staff” was generally used for <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">music reading</span></strong> and notation, recording music, and displaying the pitch and duration of sounds, simple signs were used — solely as a reminder of the melody — above the syllables of the text (poetry or verses from sacred books). Continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>What is music notation and why did it develop?</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/music-what-is-it/\">art of music</a> has the special feature that it can be recorded using a special script called “music notation.” This script, more or less like any other writing system, uses conventional signs and, like them, has constantly evolved. Therefore, those interested in learning music must become familiar with these signs and symbols so that, through persistent practice, they can gradually master reading music notation.</p>\n<p>In the art of ballet as well, dancers’ movements can be recorded on paper using a special script called “choreography,” but this script is not nearly as established or widespread as music notation, so it cannot be compared in scope and universality.</p>\n<p>Fortunately, unlike the various scripts of different languages around the world, music notation is an international script. Therefore, whenever, for example, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/iranian-music/\">Iranian music</a> or Japanese music is written using this notation, musicians from Iran, Japan, and everywhere else in the world can read it.</p>\n\n<h2>Names of musical notes in syllabic and alphabetic systems</h2>\n<p>For all musical sounds, from the lowest to the highest, there are only seven names. These seven names are pronounced in some Western countries with single-syllable words and in others alphabetically, as follows.</p>\n<p>Syllabic naming in music reading:</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_2311\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"361\"]<img class=\"wp-image-2311 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/nezam-hejayi.jpg\" alt=\"Syllabic system in music reading\" width=\"361\" height=\"176\" /> 1- Music reading and note naming in the syllabic system[/caption]\n<p>Alphabetic naming in music reading:</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_2310\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"342\"]<img class=\"wp-image-2310 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/nezam-Alefbayi.jpg\" alt=\"Alphabetic system in music reading\" width=\"342\" height=\"168\" /> 2- Music reading and note naming in the alphabetic system[/caption]\n<p>In older syllabic music reading systems, the syllable Ut was used instead of Do, and in some modern reading methods, the syllable Si is pronounced as Ti.</p>\n<p>The range of main musical sounds — that is, the span from the lowest to the highest pitches — covers more than 60 sounds, while the notes themselves have only seven names. In fact, within this wide range, after the seventh note, the names repeat from the beginning. In other words, throughout the range of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/sound-characteristics/\">musical sounds</a>, the note Si always comes before every Do, and the note Do always comes after every Si. This repetition continues until the end of the musical sounds. Of course, the frequency of each repeated note is twice that of the previous note with the same name. The interval between two notes with the same name is called an <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/musical-interval/\">octave</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>Historical roots of the syllabic and alphabetic systems</h2>\n<p>The syllabic system — the common and customary system used in Italy and France (and also in Iran, following the French system) — was originally based on <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/what-is-music-theory/\">medieval music theory</a> and consisted of six syllables.</p>\n<p>It should be noted, however, that in old Iranian treatises and books from the 3rd to the 8th centuries AH, music reading was done using the alphabetic system with Abjad letters.</p>\n<p>The six syllables were chosen by a French musician and priest living in Italy named <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guido_d%27Arezzo\">Guido d’Arezzo</a> (990–1050) from the following prayer-like poem, whose melody he probably composed himself. He taught it to his students and singers because it made it easier to memorize musical sounds and understand their relative pitches:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Ut</strong> queant laxis <strong>re</strong>sonare fibris <strong>Mi</strong>ra gestorum <strong>fa</strong>muli tuorum <strong>Sol</strong>ve polluti <strong>la</strong>bii reatum, Sancte Iohannes.</p>\n<p>In later centuries, as the six-note system expanded and reached the seven notes we use today, the syllable Si was added, and the syllable Ut — which was difficult to pronounce in reading — was changed to Do.</p>\n<p>Alphabetic music reading follows ancient Greek music theory and the tuning of instruments of that time. It starts with the note La, and the notes are named after the first seven letters of the Latin alphabet. <strong> </strong></p>\n<p>This system has been adopted and is used by English-speaking countries (England and the United States) and German-speaking countries (Germany and Austria). However, there are some differences in naming conventions between these two groups of countries.</p>\n\n<h2>The Staff</h2>\n<p>In order to write musical notes in the successive ranges shown earlier, lines called staves (or staffs) are used.</p>\n<p>In its simplest and most common form, the staff consists of five parallel lines with equal spacing, numbered from bottom to top. A five-line staff has eleven positions for placing musical notes: five on the lines, four in the spaces between the lines, one below the first line, and one above the fifth line. Thus, eleven notes can be placed on each staff.</p>\n<p>The relationship between note names and their positions on the staff is conventional. A starting point is chosen on one of the staff lines and one of the seven notes is assigned to it; the rest of the notes are then calculated from that starting point. For example, if we assume that the note Sol (G) is placed on the second line of the staff, the other notes will be named as shown in figure 4. To indicate this starting point, a sign called the treble clef (G clef) is written at the beginning of the staff on the second line, as shown below.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_2400\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"110\"]<img class=\"wp-image-2400 size-medium\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/soul-110x300.png\" alt=\"Treble clef in music reading\" width=\"110\" height=\"300\" /> 3- Treble clef (G clef) in music reading[/caption]\n<h2>Clef</h2>\n<p>As explained above, clefs are used to identify the names of notes on the staff lines. In the figure above, the treble clef is placed on the second line of the staff and gives its name to the note on that line. In addition to the treble clef, which is always placed on the second line, two other clefs are used in music reading: the “bass clef” (F clef) and the “C clef.” These clefs also give their name to the note on the line they are placed upon.</p>\n<p>For example, if the bass clef is placed on the fourth line, the note on the fourth line is read as “Fa” (F), and the rest of the notes are named accordingly.</p>\n<p>In music reading and notation, the “F” and “C” clefs — unlike the “G” clef — can be placed in more than one position on the staff lines.</p>\n\n<h2>Ledger Lines (Extra Lines)</h2>\n<p>It was mentioned that only eleven notes can be written on the staff lines, which is a very small number compared to the total number of musical notes (more than 60 notes). To overcome this limitation in music reading and notation, short lines are temporarily added below and above the staff wherever needed, thereby extending the range of the staff and the number of notes that can be written on it.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3527\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3527 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/sornaz_reading_note_05.webp\" alt=\"Maximum number of notes displayable on a 5-line staff with 4 ledger lines above and below\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 4- Maximum number of notes that can be displayed on a 5-line staff plus 4 ledger lines above and below[/caption]\n<p>Note: Ledger lines, whether added upward or downward, are only extended as far as the eye can easily recognize their number with a little experience.</p>\n<p>As seen in the figure above, by adding ledger lines (in this example up to four lines above and below), the number of representable notes increases from 11 to 27. Of course, the number of ledger lines is not limited. Ledger notes are also written following the same pattern as staff notes (alternating between lines and spaces).</p>\n<p>In terminology, the lines that pass through the middle of notes are called “half lines,” and the completing lines below or above the note are simply called “lines.” For example, the notes numbered 6 and 22 have “one and a half lines” (one full line plus one half line).</p>\n\n<h2>Other Staves and Clefs</h2>\n<p>As mentioned earlier: “The relationship between note names and their positions is conventional...” Now it should be known that besides the staff already described (with G placed on the second line, also called the “G staff”), there are other staves and clefs as well.</p>\n<p>All musical staves are located at different pitch levels relative to one another. In the image below, all musical staves are shown in such a way that the pitch relationship of each to the others is clear.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3528\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3528 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/sornaz_reading_note_06.webp\" alt=\"The seven musical staves and their relationship to each other\" width=\"1440\" height=\"1440\" /> 5- The seven musical staves and their relationship in reading musical pieces[/caption]\n<h3>Some notes about the image of different musical staves:</h3>\n<p>1) The “F” staff (on the fourth line, the lowest staff), which is still widely used today, is the lowest staff in music notation and is used for writing the lowest notes of musical instruments as well as the bass part of the piano.</p>\n<p>2) In reading on all seven staves, a total of three clefs are used: the “F” clef once on the fourth line and once on the third line; the “C” clef four times on the fourth, third, second, and first lines; and the “G” clef only on the second line. It is clear that the note-naming system on each of the seven staves is different from the other six.</p>\n<p>3) Notes connected by vertical lines are notes with the same name and the same pitch.</p>\n<p>4) In the past, each of the seven staves was assigned to one of the sections of the human voice (in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/vocal-form/\">vocal music</a>). Today, some of these staves are specific to one or more instruments.</p>\n<p>5) It is clear that the use of ledger lines on any staff for notation and reading — whether in the bass or treble register — has no limitation.</p>\n\n<h2>Grand Staff (Double Staff)</h2>\n<p>Some musical instruments (such as the pipe organ, piano, harp, etc.) have a very wide range (the pipe organ can produce more than 60, the piano more than 50, and the harp nearly 50 main notes).</p>\n<p>Earlier we saw that a five-line staff, even with the fairly sufficient use of ledger lines, can only show about 30 notes. Moreover, the aforementioned instruments are designed so that the fingers of both hands play a direct role in producing the sounds. Therefore, for these instruments, the “grand staff” (double staff) is used in notation and reading, by combining the lowest and highest of the seven staves.</p>\n<p>In notation and reading for keyboard instruments on the grand staff, notes written on the lower staff (F clef on the fourth line) are usually played with the left hand, and notes on the upper staff (G clef on the second line) with the right hand.</p>\n\n<h2>Relationship between notes on the two five-line staves in the grand staff</h2>\n<p>In the image of the seven staves, all the staves used in music are shown. It is clear that ledger lines can also be used on any of the seven staves and each can be extended as needed. The same principle applies to the grand staff — since there is only one note (with one “ledger half-line”) between its two five-line staves — and it can easily be extended. When the number of ledger lines between the two staves exceeds one, it indicates a note that can be written on the other staff without any ledger line.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3529\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3529 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/sornaz_reading_note_07.webp\" alt=\"Notes on the G clef (second line) and F clef (fourth line) staves and their shared notes\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 6- Notes on the G clef (second line) and F clef (fourth line) staves and the overlap between them in reading musical pieces[/caption]\n<p>On the other hand, with a little examination, in the figure below it can be seen that the “lines” and “half-lines” — except for one line called the middle “C” line — are actually the main lines of the other staff. In other words, the lines of the “G” staff are actually ledger lines for the higher register of the “F” staff, and the lines of the “F” staff are ledger lines for the lower register of the “G” staff.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3530\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3530 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/sornaz_reading_note_08.webp\" alt=\"Relationship between the G clef (second line) staff and the F clef (fourth line) staff\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 7- Relationship between the G clef (second line) staff and the F clef (fourth line) staff in reading musical pieces[/caption]\n<p>The shared notes shown in this example can be written on either of the two staves. Higher notes on the “G” staff and lower notes on the “F” staff can be written on the other staff using additional ledger lines. As a result, by understanding the relationship between the two staves shown above, one can conclude that the shared notes are identical in name and pitch.</p>\n<p>For piano notation and all keyboard instruments and harp, the grand staff is also used, where the upper staff is the “G” staff (the highest staff in music notation) and the lower staff is the “F” clef on the fourth line (the lowest staff in music notation).</p>\n<p>A music student should learn notation and reading on all seven staves — especially the more common ones — so well that they can read music on the more frequently used staves (in order of importance: G clef on the second line, F clef on the fourth line, and C clef on the fourth line) fluently and easily, and read the other four staves (F on the third line, C on the third, second, and first lines) with careful calculation. Although learning all seven staves may not seem very necessary at the beginning of music reading and notation studies, you will need this knowledge in the future.</p>\n&nbsp;\n\n&nbsp;\n\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any comments or questions about the history of the development of music notation, please share them in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this educational content with the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com\">Sornaz Music School</a>.</p>\n<p></p>', '2023-05-01 12:40:21', 0, '2024-08-30 19:43:52', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1468, 'posts', 11, 'en', NULL, 'What does meter and meter reading mean in music? And why should we learn meter reading? Training on using 2 methods: metronome and foot tapping for meter reading', NULL, 'This article discusses the fundamental concept of meter (rhythm) and meter reading in music. Meter refers to the organization of time in music and plays a crucial role in understanding rhythm, melody, and harmony. Just like in speech and poetry, where emphasis can change meaning, altering emphasis in music can affect the expression and interpretation of a piece.\n\nMeter reading involves performing the rhythmic values of notes without necessarily singing the melody. It is essential for both individual and ensemble performances, especially in orchestras, where coordination is key. Tools like the metronome or physical movements (such as foot tapping) are commonly used to maintain timing.\n\nMusical meters are mainly categorized into:\n\nSimple meters (duple, triple, quadruple), where each beat divides into two.\nCompound meters, where each beat divides into three using dotted notes.\nIrregular meters (briefly mentioned)\n\nIn simple meters, the hierarchy of strong and weak beats is important (e.g., in 4/4: strong, weak, medium, weak). Conducting patterns also vary depending on the meter, helping performers stay synchronized.\n\nOverall, meter reading is a fundamental skill for musicians, improving both their understanding of musical structure and performance accuracy.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article from the Sornaz Music School, we intend to address the fundamental and very important topic of <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">rhythm reading in music</span></strong>. First, we talk about the concepts of rhythm and rhythm reading in music. Then, we become familiar with different time values in music rhythm reading and explain two methods of rhythm reading using a metronome and foot tapping. In the continuation, we also discuss rhythm reading of simple measures in orchestra performances. So, continue with us in this article...</p>\n<h2>Rhythm and Rhythm Reading in Speech, Poetry, and Music</h2>\n<p>As you know, no melody can exist without rhythm or at least without emphasis on some <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%88%db%8c%da%98%da%af%db%8c-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%b5%d9%88%d8%aa/\">sounds</a>. In some melodies, sometimes the place of emphasis can be changed on the sounds of the melody, in which case the meaning of that melody will most likely change. This point can be better understood with an example.</p>\n<p>For example, consider the sentence “I got a good score in the Persian exam.” In this seven-word sentence, we can emphasize six or even all seven words in turn so that each time one of the words “I,” “in,” “exam,” “Persian,” “score,” “good,” and “got” is pronounced with more emphasis than the other words. For example, if in reading the rhythm of this sentence, we put the emphasis on “I,” we have “<strong>I</strong> got a good score in the Persian exam.” The meaning is that I, and not someone else, got a good score in the Persian exam.</p>\n<p>If we put the emphasis in reading the rhythm of this sentence on the word “Persian,” we will have “I got a good score in the <strong>Persian</strong> exam.” The meaning of this sentence is that I got a good score in the Persian exam and not in another exam. Similarly, if we put this emphasis on any other word in this sentence, we will change its meaning to some extent.</p>\n<p>In reading the rhythm of a melody, more or less the same can be done, and by changing the emphasis on different notes, the meaning of that melody can be changed.</p>\n<p>A piece of poetry, just like a melody, cannot be read and understood without feeling its rhythm and reading it correctly. Classical Iranian poetry has such various rhythms that, like musical rhythms, they can be classified. This classification is expressed in prosody books under the title “poetic meters (bahr).”</p>\n<p>Today’s poetry, contrary to the opinion of some of its opponents, has a very rich rhythm. It should only be said that the rhythms of today’s poetry and even prose are more complex and varied than the rhythms and meters of classical poetry.</p>\n<p>According to prosodists, the rhythm of every poem must be appropriate to its concept or purpose. An epic poem must have a different rhythm compared to a romantic ghazal. Perhaps one of the reasons for the complexity and variety of rhythm in today’s poetry is the complexity and variety of topics that occupy the mind of today’s poet.</p>\n<p>The same phenomenon has occurred in the history of music transformation regarding the change of styles. Great composers gradually became tired of the limitations and uniformity of existing rhythms. On the other hand, in order to find musical ideas, they turned to other sources besides what had been available to them until then. One of the most important of these sources was the local and folk melodies of various and distant lands, which occasionally seemed new and interesting to composers with their special elements. These melodies sometimes had more complex rhythms than traditional and historical music. In the same way, composers discovered new realms in more difficult rhythms.</p>\n<h2>Rhythm and Rhythm Reading in Music</h2>\n<p>Throughout the world, all musicians who perform <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">music</a> from notation usually, before performing any musical piece with an instrument, make movements with their right hand, which is called rhythm reading.</p>\n<p>During rhythm reading, there is no need for the desired melody to be sung; rather, it is sufficient to perform the time values of the notes with their specified time values.</p>\n<p>In group performances, one of the important tasks of the orchestra conductor is to maintain and show the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%85-%d8%af%d8%b1-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">rhythm</a> of each melody, which coordinates all members of the <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A7%D8%B1%DA%A9%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%B1\">orchestra</a> with both hands. These rhythmic hand movements have different shapes in pieces with different rhythms, and here we introduce the method of rhythm reading for simple duple, triple, and quadruple measures.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_2379\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"219\"]<img class=\"wp-image-2379 size-medium\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/ریتم-های-کاربردی-فرنان-فونتن-219x300.jpg\" alt=\"Rhythm and Practical Rhythms by Fernand Fontaine\" width=\"219\" height=\"300\" /> 1- Rhythm and Practical Rhythms by Fernand Fontaine[/caption]\n<h2>What Does Rhythm Mean in Music?</h2>\n<p>In music, rhythm refers to the division of musical time into equal or unequal sections based on the type and number of musical beats in each section. In other words, rhythm indicates how many and what type of beats exist in each section of the music.</p>\n<p>In general, rhythm in music determines the setting and rhythm of musical pieces and helps the listener understand the rhythm and deep feeling that the piece conveys. Also, rhythm in music is considered one of the principles of music and influential on musical pieces and plays an important role in creating <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d9%88%d9%86%db%8c/\">harmony</a> and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%84%d9%88%d8%af%db%8c/\">melody</a>.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_2381\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"262\"]<img class=\"wp-image-2381 size-medium\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/کتاب-وزن-خوانی-جلد-اول-اثر-علی-اکبر-شکارچی-262x300.jpg\" alt=\"Rhythm Reading by Ali Akbar Shokarchi\" width=\"262\" height=\"300\" /> 2- Rhythm Reading by Ali Akbar Shokarchi[/caption]\n<h3>Rhythm in Iranian Music Avazs</h3>\n<p>It has been said about Iranian music Avazs that: “… this music is without beat.”</p>\n<p>If the meaning of “beat” is measure, this claim can be considered correct to some extent and so far, because Avaz in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a> is basically written without measure, but whenever the meaning of “beat” is the rhythm of the Avaz, the above statement is by no means correct because music cannot exist without rhythm.</p>\n<p>“Every music has rhythm, and every rhythm can be written on paper with the help of special musical notation signs, that is, measure and time signature.” However, it should be known that the layers of rhythm and emphasis of strong, semi-strong, and weak beats in Iranian music Avazs are not as simple and clear as the measure factors of most Western music.</p>\n<p>The rhythm in Iranian Avaz is more or less the same as the monophonic music of ancient Greece and the religious chants of the early Middle Ages, which are based on the rhythm of the poetic words that were sung with the Avaz. Therefore, to discover the measure in this Avaz, it is necessary that we be completely familiar with the knowledge of prosody and poetic meters.</p>\n<h2>What Is Rhythm Reading in Music?</h2>\n<p>Rhythm reading (Meter) is one of the basic concepts in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">the science of music theory</a> that provides a kind of pattern of strong and semi-strong beats in the same way throughout the piece. In fact, rhythm reading is a kind of time division in which strong and semi-strong beats are divided into groups of equal length.</p>\n<p>Using rhythm reading in music helps the coordinated performance of works and provides the possibility of comparing different pieces for listeners. Also, rhythm reading gives a more emotional performance to music and can help the listener understand the piece better. Rhythm reading is also very useful for musicians in writing new pieces and improving their performances. In general, rhythm reading, as one of the basic principles of music, is very important in learning and performing any type of music.</p>\n<p>It should be noted that rhythm reading in simple, compound, and irregular measures is different from each other. Therefore, in the continuation, we examine the rhythm reading of each of these measure types:</p>\n<h3>Rhythm Reading of Simple Measures</h3>\n<p>In order to teach rhythm reading of simple measures, we must first become familiar with the definition of a simple measure. A simple measure in music refers to measure types in which the numerator of the time signature is equal to one of the numbers 2, 3, and 4. This means that simple measures exist in duple, triple, and quadruple forms. In simple time signatures, the upper number indicates the number of beats in each measure, and the lower number in the time signature indicates the type of beat in each measure.</p>\n<p>Now, to identify the type of beat in each measure, we must know the ratios of different time values to the basic time unit in music, that is, the whole note value. Therefore, in the following image, we have presented these time ratios.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_3662\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3662 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/sornaz_weight_01.webp\" alt=\"Time values of notes in music rhythm reading\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 3- Time values of notes in music rhythm reading[/caption]\n<p>By carefully looking at the above image, we conclude that if in a simple measure we want to use quarter notes, we must multiply the number of beats in each measure by 1/4 and mention the result as the time signature at the beginning of the piece. For example, if in each measure we had 2 beats of the quarter note type, we must multiply the number 2 by 1/4 and write the result, which is 2/4, as the time signature of the piece, and do the same for other time values.</p>\n<p>Therefore, in the image below, we have shown the simple time signatures for time values from “whole note” to “thirty-second note” and in duple, triple, and quadruple forms.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_3663\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3663 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/sornaz_weight_02.webp\" alt=\"Simple time signatures in music rhythm reading\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 4- Simple time signatures in music rhythm reading[/caption]\n<h4>Rhythm Reading of Simple Measures with Two Methods: Using a Metronome and Foot Tapping:</h4>\n<h5>Duple Measures</h5>\n<p>Duple measures refer to rhythms that have one strong beat and one weak beat in each measure, in such a way that the strong beat is the first beat of the measure and the weak beat is the second beat of the measure. According to this definition, we know that a duple measure consists of two sections or beats, and these two beats can be any of the specified time values in image number 3. As you can see in image number 4, the time signature or numerator related to each type of time value for the beats of a simple duple measure is specified.</p>\n<p>If we assume the quarter note as the beat unit, it means that in each measure we can place different notes equal to the time value of two quarter notes. For example, we can place one half note, 2 quarter notes, 4 eighth notes, 8 sixteenth notes, or any combination of these time values in the measure, for example, “one quarter note and two eighth notes” or “two eighth notes and 4 sixteenth notes” in one measure.</p>\n<p>In reading the rhythm of duple measures, if we consider the quarter note as the beat unit, we will have the time signature 2/4. Now, for rhythm reading in this rhythm, we must set the metronome to the 2/4 state with the quarter note unit and produce one quarter note for each metronome tick.</p>\n<p>This point should be noted that we must produce the first quarter note with more intensity because it is the strong beat of the measure, and we must play the next quarter note with less intensity. Of course, if the notes of the measure are not quarter notes, we must act in such a way that the notes of the first beat are played stronger and the notes of the second beat weaker.</p>\n<p>If the measure consisted of two quarter notes, we strike once on the string of a tar or guitar, or press one key of a piano, and if we assume the measure consists of one quarter note and two eighth notes, in this case, we must produce the first quarter note with the first tick of the metronome and produce the eighth notes at the time of the second tick with equal time intervals.</p>\n<p>One of the other methods in rhythm reading is foot tapping, in such a way that for each beat we bring the sole of the foot down from top to bottom and then return the foot from bottom to the previous state. In this state, in fact, we have performed one beat of a duple measure. To completely read the rhythm of a duple measure with the foot, we perform this movement twice.</p>\n<p>The time interval between lowering or raising the sole of the foot must be the same, in which case each beat is divided into two parts, and this means that each eighth note is equal to one lowering of the foot or one raising of the foot. Therefore, if we have two quarter notes in one measure, the first quarter note is produced with the lowering of the foot, and the time value of this note continues until the foot is raised, and the second quarter note is performed in the same way with the second beat.</p>\n<p>If the measure consists of one quarter note and two eighth notes, the first quarter note is performed as before, and the first eighth note is produced simultaneously with the lowering of the foot, and its time value continues until the moment the sole of the foot completely touches the ground, and the next eighth note is produced at the moment the foot moves upward and its time value continues until the foot returns to the initial state.</p>\n<h5>Triple and Quadruple Measures</h5>\n<p>So far, we have explained the rhythm reading of simple duple measures with a metronome and foot tapping. We use the same method for rhythm reading of simple triple and quadruple measures, with the difference that the time signature of triple measures with the quarter note beat unit is 3/4, and the time signature for simple quadruple measures with the quarter note beat shape is 4/4, which must be set on the metronome so that we hear the tick sound equal to the number of beats in each measure.</p>\n<p>In triple measures, the first beat of each measure is strong, and the second and third beats are weak, which must be observed in performance, and the notes that are in the time range of the first beat of the measure should be played with more intensity.</p>\n<p>In quadruple measures, the first beat of the measure is strong, the second beat is weak, the third beat is played semi-strong, and the fourth beat is also weak, which we must also observe in performance: we perform the first beat with high intensity, the second beat is played weakly, we perform the third beat in a strong way but with less intensity than the first beat, and finally, we also play the fourth beat weakly.</p>\n<p>The things that have been said so far about rhythm reading of simple duple, triple, and quadruple measures have been presented with the assumption of the quarter note as the beat unit. The same things apply in the same way for other time values such as half note, eighth note, sixteenth note, etc., and it was only used as an example in this text because the quarter note beat unit has high application in music.</p>\n<h4>Rhythm Reading of Simple Duple Measures in the Orchestra:</h4>\n<p>In duple rhythms such as 2/2, 2/4, 2/8, in which the beat unit is respectively the half note, quarter note, and eighth note, to show the first beat of the measure, the right hand moves from top to bottom, and then to show the second beat, it goes from bottom to top. As can be seen in the main image of the article.</p>\n<p>It should be noted that in duple measures, the first beat is always strong, and the second beat will be weak.</p>\n<h4>Rhythm Reading of Simple Triple Measures in the Orchestra:</h4>\n<p>In triple rhythms such as 3/2, 3/4, 3/8, in which the beat unit is respectively the half note, quarter note, and eighth note, to show the first beat of the measure, the right hand moves from top to bottom, to show the second beat, it moves horizontally from the previous location to the right side of the body, and finally, to show the third beat, it goes from that right point to the initial hand position upward. As can be seen in the main image of the article.</p>\n<p>It should be noted that in triple measures, the first beat is always strong, and the second and third beats are weak.</p>\n<h4>Rhythm Reading of Simple Quadruple Measures in the Orchestra:</h4>\n<p>In quadruple rhythms such as 4/2, 4/4, 4/8, in which the beat unit is respectively the half note, quarter note, and eighth note, to show the first beat of the measure, the right hand moves from top to bottom, to show the second beat, it moves horizontally from the previous location to the left side of the body, to show the third beat, it goes from the left side of the body to the right side of the body, and finally, to show the fourth beat, it goes from that right point to the initial hand position upward. As can be seen in the main image of the article.</p>\n<p>It should be noted that in quadruple measures, the first beat is always strong, the second beat is weak, the third beat is semi-strong, and the fourth beat is weak.</p>\n<h3>Rhythm Reading of Compound Measures</h3>\n<p>To recognize compound measures, we must first become familiar with how these measures are created. By carefully looking at image number 3, which shows the time values of different simple note values in music, we realize that the time value of each note is twice that of the smaller note than itself. For example, the time value of a half note is equal to the time value of two quarter notes.</p>\n<p>Now, if we put a dot in front of any note or time value, the time value of this new time value is equal to the time value of the main note plus half of its time value. For example, if we consider a dotted half note, its time value is equal to the time value of one half note plus half the time value of a half note, that is, the time value of a quarter note, meaning a dotted half note has a time value equal to 3 quarter notes.</p>\n<p>In image number 5, the time values of dotted whole note, dotted half note, dotted quarter note, and dotted eighth note are specified.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_4071\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"wp-image-4071 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/sornaz_weight_04.webp\" alt=\"Time values of dotted notes in music rhythm reading\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 5- Time values of dotted notes in music rhythm reading[/caption]\n<p>Now, if we carefully examine the relationship of dotted notes, we realize that the time value of each dotted note is equal to 3 times the time value of half the undotted note. For example, the time value of a dotted half note is equal to 3 times the time value of half a half note, which is the quarter note. If we consider this relationship mathematically, by multiplying the time value of each undotted note by the number 3/2, we reach the time value of the dotted note, meaning the time value of a half note multiplied by the number 3/2 is equal to the time value of a dotted half note.</p>\n<p>As previously mentioned about simple measures, these measures exist in duple, triple, and quadruple forms. So, if we multiply the time signatures related to simple measures by the number 3/2, we reach the time signature related to compound measures. Therefore, in image number 6, we have shown the compound time signatures for time values from “dotted whole note” to “dotted thirty-second note” and in duple, triple, and quadruple forms.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_3664\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3664 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/sornaz_weight_03.webp\" alt=\"Compound time signatures in music rhythm reading\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 6- Compound time signatures in music rhythm reading[/caption]\n<p>By carefully looking at the above time signatures, we realize that if the numerator is equal to the number 6, we have a compound duple measure, if it is 9, it is a compound triple measure, and finally, if the numerator is 12, it means we are facing a compound quadruple measure.</p>\n<p>Now, if we assume the numerator as one and multiply the denominator by the number 2, we reach the fractions that are the ratio of the time value of undotted notes to the basic time unit in music, which is the whole note value and is shown in image number 3, and we can say that the beat unit in this time signature is equal to this note in the dotted state.</p>\n<p>Now we explain these explanations better with an example. Consider the time signature 12/32. As we said, because the numerator is equal to 12, we have a compound quadruple measure.</p>\n<p>Now, to identify the type of beat unit in this time signature, we consider the numerator as the number one, in which case we will have the fraction 1/32. In the second step, we multiply the denominator of the fraction by the number 2, which brings us to the fraction 1/16, and then by carefully looking at image number 3, we realize that the fraction 1/16 is the ratio of the time value of a thirty-second note to the time value of a whole note. So, we conclude that the beat shape in this time signature is equal to a dotted thirty-second note.</p>\n<p>Finally, it can be said that the time signature 12/32 shows a compound quadruple measure in which the shape of each beat is a dotted thirty-second note.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding rhythm reading in music, please share it in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this educational content with us.</p>', '2023-05-07 11:19:11', 0, '2024-08-30 19:44:09', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1469, 'posts', 12, 'en', NULL, 'Iranian music, its divisions and various forms, The 7 Dastgah and 5 Avaz and their interrelations', NULL, 'This article provides an overview of Persian (Iranian) music, including its history, evolution, and structural forms. It begins with insights from Ruhollah Khaleghi, emphasizing that music reflects the culture, emotions, and historical experiences of a nation. Therefore, Iranian music carries a unique identity deeply rooted in the spirit of its people.\n\nThe article then traces the historical development of Iranian music from ancient times (Achaemenid and Sassanian periods) through the Islamic era, where new musical schools such as the Persian-Arabic and later Persian-Arabic-Turkish traditions emerged. Important theoretical works and influential musicians played a key role in shaping this evolution. During the Qajar period, traditional elements were preserved and reorganized into what is now known as the radif.\n\nThe main structural forms of Iranian music are introduced as:\n\nDastgahs (7 systems) such as Shur, Mahur, Homayun, etc.\nAvaz (5 branches) such as Abu Ata, Afshari, Dashti, etc.\n\nKey differences between Dastgah and Avaz include:\n\nDastgahs have a wider pitch range (4–6 dangs), while Avaz has a narrower range (2–3 dangs).\nStarting notes in Dastgahs are generally lower, while in Avaz they are higher.\nAvaz forms are more rooted in regional and folk traditions, whereas Dastgahs are used in more formal and classical performances.\n\nOverall, the article highlights that Iranian music is a product of historical continuity, cultural interaction, and artistic creativity, maintaining its identity while evolving over time.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article from the “Iranian Music” section, we examine <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Iranian music</span></strong></span>, its various divisions and forms, and the connections between these forms. Continue with us in this article...</p>\n<h2>Part of the Introduction to the Book “A Theoretical View of Music” by Ruhollah Khaleqi</h2>\n<p>The fine arts represent the feelings and spirits of every nation, and since the tastes of different nations differ, the arts of their people, which are the outpourings of their spirits, are also distinct from one another. Just as the customs and habits of two nations are different, it is not surprising if their music, which is the emanation of their souls, is also different.</p>\n<p>Geographical and natural conditions, spiritual and mental worlds, ways of thinking and beliefs, the quality of religious customs, the level of civilization, historical background, and material life needs are all effective factors in the difference between the music of two nations.</p>\n<p>Just as the spirit, taste, feelings, and emotions of the peoples of the East and West of the world do not have completely common aspects, for the same reason their arts also have different methods.</p>\n<p>No matter how familiar an Iranian is with world music and looks at his own music with indifference, if he finds a moment of leisure and becomes tired of the struggles of material life, he seeks the <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%AF%DB%8C%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%86_%D8%AD%D8%A7%D9%81%D8%B8\">Divan of Hafez</a> and the <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A8%D9%88%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%A7%D9%86_%D8%B3%D8%B9%D8%AF%DB%8C\">Bustan of Saadi</a> and reads a few verses of it with the melody of his national music, with which he has been raised. And if he does not find a book of poetry and does not even have a poem memorized by chance, he still hums to himself something that, without him being aware of it, is the melody of one of the tunes of the music of his homeland.</p>\n<p>Because he has been raised in the lap of a mother who sang him a lullaby with the melody of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%af%d8%b4%d8%aa%db%8c/\">Dashti</a>, he has heard the melodies of Abu Ata and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d9%81%d8%b4%d8%a7%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Afshari</a> from the mouths of every street performer in the alleys and bazaars, the muezzin and Quran reciter have soothed his ear and illuminated his heart with the melody of Hijaz, and if his friends and acquaintances knew music, they have played and sung the melodies of Shur and Shehnaz for him.</p>\n<p>The passing beggar has asked for help from him with the melody of Mansuri or another tune, he has heard the sound of the shepherd’s ney, and a dervish has read him a Masnavi, and finally, he has been raised in a land where the soul-burning lament of Dashti and the sorrowful melody of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b3%d9%87-%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">Segah</a>, the majestic melody of Homayun, the comforting melody of Nava, and the joyful melody of Mahur have been common and prevalent, and especially in recent times, he has repeatedly heard these melodies on the radio.</p>\n<p>How can someone who has become familiar and attached to these melodies and tunes console himself with another melody that does not affect his heart and does not fully agree with his taste and spirit, and enjoy it!</p>\n<p>At the time of sorrow and helplessness, people forget everything and involuntarily turn away from the manifestations of material life, and if in normal times they imagine themselves deluded by the melodies of others’ music through imitation and pretense, at this time they again seek a melody and tune that has penetrated the blood of their ancestors and is a reminder of their bitter and sweet past and their sorrowful and pleasurable memories.</p>\n<p>This is the same music that first came from the mother’s throat at the cradle and gave an effect to the child’s ear, which, even if determination and will try to erase it, still penetrates the depths of his heart and soul, and whenever the time comes, the heavenly melodies of it ring in the ear of consciousness and bring the matter to a point where, if someone does not have access to an instrument and singing, he sings the melodies of his national music with his own voice, which, no matter how bad it is, is pleasant and delightful to him.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_2458\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"200\"]<img class=\"size-medium wp-image-2458\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/images.jpg\" alt=\"A general view of Iranian dastgahs and Avazs\" width=\"200\" height=\"252\" /> A general view of Iranian dastgahs and Avazs[/caption]\n<h2>Continuity and Discontinuity of a Tradition</h2>\n<p>Although it is clear that Iranians have had advanced music since ancient times, it is better to see the history of Iranian music as successive waves and periods in which art and artistic sense have always taken on a different shape in confrontation with various cultures or in the wake of historical transformations, and ultimately new styles and forms have been born. In truth, if there is historical continuity in the scientific and artistic music of Iran, it should first of all be found in the intelligence and musical insight of Iranians, which they have shown in the formation of music in all periods.</p>\n<p>The excellence of Iranians in creating and perfecting instruments, in building the foundations of the science of music, in promoting music in the West and the East, and finally in creating new forms and styles is clear to us. First of all, it should be said that Iranian music has always been dependent on the court. For this reason, it has become customary to divide the history of music into periods and attribute each period to a dynasty of kings. But the point is that we cannot say that music suddenly changed shape when moving from one period to another.</p>\n<p>In the Achaemenid period, music was an important part of court ceremonies and entertainments. In ancient reliefs and stone carvings, singers and musicians with a three-cornered harp, along with the daf percussion instrument, as well as long-necked string instruments and double ney, are clearly visible.</p>\n<p>In the Sassanid period, the barbat (from which the modern oud is derived), various types of rebab that perhaps originated in eastern Khorasan and Transoxiana, and the tanbur were added to the instruments of that time. In the first century AH, with great musicians such as Barbad and Nakisa and other famous figures of Iranian music who are mentioned in history, the art of music reached its peak.</p>\n<p>With the advent of Islam, Baghdad became the central place of Iranian music, and for this reason, many musicians who are always considered Arabs actually had Iranian ancestry. In these conditions, Iranian music, in confrontation with Arab culture and the Near East, took on a different shape, so that from the third century AH, a new school was born that we can call the Iranian-Arab school.</p>\n<p>After the passage of several centuries, another music emerged that we can call the Iranian-Arab-Turkish school; the principles of this music are more or less the same from Egypt to Turkestan. In this splendid period, which continued until about the tenth century AH, important theoretical treatises on Iranian music were written, especially the treatises of Farabi, Avicenna, Safi al-Din Urmawi, Qutb al-Din Shirazi, and Abd al-Qadir Maraghi. The main instruments of Iranian music in this period are the barbat, chang, ney, kamancheh (old rebab or qichak), Central Asian rebab from the tar family with skin, dotar, setar or tanbur, and other plucked and qanun instruments.</p>\n<p>We see that from the eleventh to the twelfth century AH, Iranian music completely separates its path from its Arab, Turkish, Tajik, and Uzbek neighbors. One of the reasons for this transformation is perhaps the political instability and turmoil of Iran that occurred throughout the twelfth century, and therefore, with the political separation of Iran from Turkey and Central Asia, Iranian music treads its path alone and undergoes its own transformation.</p>\n<p>The new tradition that emerged in the Qajar period, despite the existence of non-professional music lovers or musicologist clerics or light and motrebi music groups, was able to preserve the ancient components of Iranian music.</p>\n<p>Old instruments such as the barbat, qanun, and chang disappeared and gave way to new types such as the rebab, tar, and also the santur, and the sound of silk and gut strings was replaced by shiny steel strings. In this period, the old music treatises and even the principles of Iranian music in the Safavid period lost their application in the context of the music of that time.</p>\n<p>In these treatises, old names were seen, but the content of their music had changed. Perhaps the modes that were not in harmony with the prevailing aesthetics of Iranians at that time (such as Saba, Rakb, or Nahavand) were set aside or changed. In this regard, we can say that many long rhythmic cycles (such as 10, 16, 24, and 32 beats, etc.) in Iranian music have disappeared, while these rhythms still remain and are performed in the music of neighboring countries.</p>\n<p>The modes and rhythms that survived more were those that existed in local music. On the other hand, local melodies, which were the heritage of various ethnic groups, entered the official radif of modes and gushehs and remained in the radif, and to some extent were spread in the Caucasus by professional ta’zieh singers who were from Isfahan. On the other hand, important components of Iranian musical dastgahs also existed in Herat (Afghanistan). Until fifty years ago, in this region, ghazals were also performed in the modes, dastgahs, and gushehs of Iranian music along with the tar.</p>\n<h2>Forms of Iranian Music and Its Divisions</h2>\n<p>Therefore, to become more familiar with and understand Iranian music, we must first introduce its various divisions in general. After becoming familiar with these divisions, we must research and examine each one separately and in greater depth, and finally, after introducing each section completely and becoming familiar with the obvious and hidden aspects of each, we must consider the connections and interconnections of the various sections with each other.</p>\n<p>In Iranian music, there are two general forms and structures that are called “Dastgah” and “Avaz.”</p>\n<p>The dastgahs of Iranian music are divided into 7 groups, which are:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b4%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Shur Dastgah</a></li>\n  <li>Nava Dastgah</li>\n  <li>Segah Dastgah</li>\n  <li>Chahargah Dastgah</li>\n  <li>Mahur Dastgah</li>\n  <li>Rast Panjgah Dastgah</li>\n  <li>Homayun Dastgah</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The Avazs of Iranian music are divided into 5 groups, which are:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%b9%d8%b7%d8%a7/\">Avaz of Abu Ata (Dastan al-Arab)</a></li>\n  <li>Avaz of Bayat-e Tork (Bayat-e Zand)</li>\n  <li>Avaz of Afshari</li>\n  <li>Avaz of Dashti</li>\n  <li>Avaz of Isfahan (Bayat-e Isfahan)</li>\n</ul>\n[caption id=\"attachment_2458\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"200\"]<img class=\"size-medium wp-image-2458\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/images.jpg\" alt=\"A general view of Iranian dastgahs and Avazs\" width=\"200\" height=\"252\" /> A general view of Iranian dastgahs and Avazs[/caption]\n<p>Of course, it should be noted that some Iranian musicians have considered other divisions for Iranian music, and what was stated regarding the divisions of dastgahs and Avazs of Iranian music is the most common and general division of Iranian music among the practitioners of this art.</p>\n<p>Now we must answer this important question:</p>\n<h2>What is the difference between Dastgah and Avaz in the divisions of Iranian music?</h2>\n<p>To answer this question, we mention 4 points regarding the differences and distinctions between the Dastgah form and the Avaz form in traditional Iranian music:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Sound range and scope: The most important mentionable difference in the Dastgah and Avaz forms is the difference in their sound range. The sound range of dastgahs is between 4 and 6 tetrachords, and the sound range of Avazs is limited to 2 to 3 tetrachords.</li>\n  <li>Starting note: In the Dastgah forms, the starting note is generally the note C in the second octave (frequency 131 Hz) or is in this range, but the starting note in the Avaz forms is in the third and fourth octaves.</li>\n  <li>Origin: Each of the Avazs belongs to a specific region and corner of the land of Iran and were prevalent in the environment of small cities and tribes and among the general public, and they have more social feeling and character, but the dastgahs were used for official ceremonies and solid performances.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the period of great figures such as Ali-Naqi Vaziri and Abolhassan Saba, the Avazs became more common, and more than 70 percent of the famous pieces were composed in Avaz forms.</p>\n<p>To mention the fourth difference in the Dastgah and Avaz forms of Iranian music, we must become a little more familiar with the concepts of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">the theory of Iranian music</a> and these forms.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p style=\"text-align: right;\">If you have any opinion or question regarding the article on Iranian music, please share it in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this educational content with the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>.</p>\n&nbsp;', '2023-05-13 11:05:48', 0, '2024-08-30 19:44:30', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1470, 'posts', 13, 'en', NULL, 'Iranian music theory, Examination of dangs and the 4 fundamental dangs in Iranian music', NULL, 'This article explores the theory of Persian music, focusing on its unique notation system and interval structure. In addition to standard musical symbols, Persian music uses two specific signs—Sori and Koron—introduced by Ali-Naqi Vaziri to represent quarter-tone intervals.\n\nThe article explains different types of intervals such as “Bish” (augmented by a quarter-tone), “Kam” (reduced by a quarter-tone), and “Nim” (half-reduced intervals). After eliminating overlapping interval types, seven essential intervals remain in Persian music theory.\n\nIt also introduces the concept of “Dāng” (tetrachords), identifying four primary ones: Shur, Dashti, Chahargah, and Mahur. These form the foundation of Persian musical modes (Dastgahs and Avaz) and collectively shape the Radif, the core repertoire of Persian classical music.\n\nOverall, the article highlights the distinct structure of intervals in Persian music and how it differs from Western music systems, emphasizing its microtonal complexity and cultural uniqueness.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article from the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>, we examine the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">theory of Iranian music</span></strong> and the specific signs and special intervals related to Iranian music. After that, we will introduce the 4 main tetrachords in the theory of Iranian music, on which the dastgahs and Avazs of Iranian music are based and which form the repertoire of Iranian music, that is, the radif.</p>\n<h2>Part of the Introduction to the Book “A Theoretical View of Music” by Ruhollah Khaleqi</h2>\n<p>Music is considered one of the fine arts, and in every land it has its own special color and fragrance, which recalls the memories of the past and bygone days and expresses the feelings, emotions, states, and spirits of the people of a land in different historical periods. We must be aware of the changes and transformations of the music of our country in different periods.</p>\n<p>One of the defects that has long plagued Iranian music is the lack of a specific and clear method for recording and documenting the melodies and works of past musicians. Although the literary works of our poets and writers have remained, due to the lack of a special script for writing music and beautiful melodies, the old music of Iran has not been recorded, and it has only been taught orally from master to student. Due to human forgetful memory, many of them have been forgotten or have not been transmitted to our era with their original quality, and misunderstandings have caused changes in the works of the past.</p>\n<p>As in the divans of poets such as Rudaki, Farrokhi, Ferdowsi, Manuchehri, and Nezami, the names of many of our musical melodies have been preserved, but the melodies of these various tunes, each of which was the product of the taste and talent of its creator, have been lost.</p>\n<p>For example, in the following few verses from Manuchehri, the names of 18 musical melodies are mentioned, of which only one or two remain today, and even those one or two, since they have passed from hand to hand among musicians from Manuchehri’s time until now — more than nine hundred years — and have not been written down, it is not known what changes they have undergone. But at least this much can be said: in this long period, with this method and manner, they have undoubtedly changed completely and perhaps now no longer resemble their original form.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">The minstrels hour by hour on high and low melodies                  Sometimes play <strong>Sarvestan</strong> today and sometimes <strong>Ashkaneh</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Sometimes the melody of <strong>Zir-e Qeysaran</strong> and sometimes <strong>Takht-e Ardeshir</strong>                         Sometimes <strong>Nowruz-e Bozorg</strong> and sometimes the melody of <strong>Basakaneh</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Sometimes the melody of <strong>Haft Ganj</strong> and sometimes the melody of <strong>Ganj-e Kav</strong>                       Sometimes the melody of <strong>Dif-e Rakhsh</strong> and sometimes the melody of <strong>Arjaneh</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">One turn <strong>Palizban</strong> and one turn <strong>Sarve Sehi</strong>                            One turn <strong>Roshan Cheragh</strong> and one turn <strong>Gavizaneh</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">One hour <strong>Sivar-e Tir</strong> and one hour <strong>Kabk-e Dari</strong>                       One hour <strong>Sarve Setah</strong> and one hour with <strong>Ruzineh</strong></p>\n<p>Although the musicologists of our country have written books about music since long ago, most of them have examined it from the point of view of the mathematical ratios of sounds and the connection between poetic meters and music, and if by chance some of them have used letters of the abjad alphabet in a light way for writing sounds, it has been very incomplete so that they could not completely write the melodies as was mentioned.</p>\n<p>Throughout these many centuries, this art has always been taught orally, whereas in the fourth century AH, a scientific movement emerged in the music of this country, and for the first time, Abu Nasr Farabi, the great Iranian philosopher and musician, examined the books that the Greeks had written about music and wrote treatises on it.</p>\n<p>After him, another famous Iranian philosopher, Avicenna, discussed and researched music from a scientific point of view and wrote a treatise on it.</p>\n<p>After these two philosophers, other people such as Safi al-Din Urmawi, Qutb al-Din Shirazi, Abd al-Qadir Maraghi, and many others, each of whom will be referred to in their own place, wrote books about music, but all these treatises lacked the quality of writing melodies and a script suitable for this work, whereas the European musical notation, which Europeans have invented for centuries and gradually perfected, is suitable for writing any kind of music, and if it has deficiencies for writing our special music, it is capable of correction.</p>\n<p>European musical notation became common from the time of the Frenchman <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A2%D9%84%D9%81%D8%B1%D8%AF_%D9%84%D9%88%D9%85%D8%B1\">Alfred Lemaire</a>, who came to Iran to organize military music, and initially it was only specific to military music bands, until gradually, due to Iranians’ familiarity with European music and the arrival of foreign musicians to Iran and the establishment of music schools, it became more widespread.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_2420\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"220\"]<img class=\"wp-image-2420 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/Alfred-Jean-Baptiste-Lemaire.jpg\" alt=\"Alfred Lemaire\" width=\"220\" height=\"345\" /> 1- The Frenchman Alfred Lemaire came to Iran to organize military music[/caption]\n<h2>Intervals of Notes in the Theory of Iranian Music</h2>\n<p>To examine the intervals in the theory of Iranian music, one must first read the article <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%a7%d8%b5%d9%84%d9%87-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%d8%a7%db%8c%db%8c-%d8%a8%d9%87-%da%86%d9%87-%d9%85%d8%b9%d9%86%d8%a7%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">“What does a musical interval mean?”</a>, because all its contents are valid in music theory, and in addition to the contents of this article, points related to quarter-tone intervals must be added.</p>\n<p>In addition to the signs mentioned in the above article, which have general application in world music, there are two other signs specific to the theory of Iranian music for displaying its quarter-tone intervals, which <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d9%86%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Ali-Naqi Vaziri</a> established:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Sori (</strong><strong>Sori</strong><strong>):</strong> This sign, whenever placed to the left of a note, raises it by a quarter step (makes the sound higher). For example, the note “C-sori” is placed between the notes “C” and “C-sharp.”</li>\n  <li><strong>Koron (</strong><strong>Koron</strong><strong>):</strong> This sign, whenever placed to the left of a note, lowers it by a quarter step (makes the sound lower). For example, the note “B koron” is placed between the notes “B-flat” and “B.”</li>\n</ul>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory/webp/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory_15.webp\" alt=\"Complete list of interval changing signs in Iranian and world music theory\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 2- Complete list of interval changing signs in Iranian and world music theory[/caption]\n<p>Regarding these two signs, two important points exist:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The signs “double sori” and “double koron” do not exist.</li>\n  <li>The “natural” sign is still effective for neutralizing these two signs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Intervals of Notes in Iranian Music</h3>\n<p>In the theory of Iranian music, due to the existence of quarter-tone signs, specific intervals are also created, which we will examine in the continuation of these special musical intervals in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a>:</p>\n<p>1- Augmented intervals: Whenever we add a quarter step to any of the perfect, major, minor, augmented, and diminished intervals, the name of the desired interval takes the prefix “augmented.”</p>\n<p>An example for converting a perfect fifth interval to an augmented perfect fifth:</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory/webp/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory_01.webp\" alt=\"Augmented perfect fifth interval compared to perfect fifth\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 3- Augmented perfect fifth interval compared to perfect fifth[/caption]\n<p>As seen in the above image, in the first part we have an interval from the note “F” to the note “C,” which is 3.5 steps. Now, to make this interval a quarter step more, we have two ways: first, to make the first note a quarter step lower, which we do by placing the “koron” sign before the note “F,” and the second way is to make the second note of the interval a quarter step higher, which happens by placing the “sori” sign before the note “C,” and with these two methods, we convert the perfect fifth interval in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">world music theory</a> to the augmented perfect fifth interval in Iranian music theory.</p>\n<p>An example for converting a major sixth interval to an augmented major sixth:</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory/webp/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory_02.webp\" alt=\"Augmented major sixth interval compared to major sixth\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 4- Augmented major sixth interval compared to major sixth[/caption]\n<p>To convert the 4.5-step major sixth interval to the augmented major sixth interval, as before, by placing the “koron” sign on the first note of the interval, we subtract a quarter step, or by placing the “sori” sign before the second note of the interval, we add a quarter step to it.</p>\n<p>An example for converting a minor third interval to an augmented minor third:</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory/webp/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory_03.webp\" alt=\"Augmented minor third interval compared to minor third\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 5- Augmented minor third interval compared to minor third[/caption]\n<p>An example for converting an augmented fourth interval to an augmented augmented fourth:</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory/webp/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory_04.webp\" alt=\"Augmented augmented fourth interval compared to augmented fourth\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 6- Augmented augmented fourth interval compared to augmented fourth[/caption]\n<p>An example for converting a diminished fifth interval to an augmented diminished fifth:</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory/webp/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory_05.webp\" alt=\"Augmented diminished fifth interval compared to diminished fifth\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 7- Augmented diminished fifth interval compared to diminished fifth[/caption]\n<p>2- Diminished intervals: Whenever we subtract a quarter step from any of the perfect, minor, augmented, and diminished intervals, the name of the desired interval takes the prefix “diminished.”</p>\n<p>An example for converting a perfect fourth interval to a diminished perfect fourth:</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory/webp/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory_06.webp\" alt=\"Diminished perfect fourth interval compared to perfect fourth\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 8- Diminished perfect fourth interval compared to perfect fourth[/caption]\n<p>As seen in the above image, in the first part we have an interval from the note “G” to the note “C,” which is 2.5 steps. Now, to make this interval a quarter step less, we have two ways: first, to make the first note a quarter step higher, which we do by placing the “sori” sign before the note “G,” and the second way is to make the second note of the interval a quarter step lower, which happens by placing the “koron” sign before the note “C,” and with these two methods, we convert the perfect fourth interval in world music theory to the diminished perfect fourth interval in Iranian music theory.</p>\n<p>An example for converting a minor seventh interval to a diminished minor seventh:</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory/webp/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory_07.webp\" alt=\"Diminished minor seventh interval compared to minor seventh\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 9- Diminished minor seventh interval compared to minor seventh[/caption]\n<p>An example for converting an augmented fourth interval to a diminished augmented fourth:</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory/webp/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory_08.webp\" alt=\"Diminished augmented fourth interval compared to augmented fourth\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 10- Diminished augmented fourth interval compared to augmented fourth[/caption]\n<p>An example for converting a diminished fifth interval to a diminished diminished fifth:</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory/webp/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory_09.webp\" alt=\"Diminished diminished fifth interval compared to diminished fifth\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 11- Diminished diminished fifth interval compared to diminished fifth[/caption]\n<p>3- Half intervals: Whenever we subtract a quarter step from major intervals, the name of the desired interval takes the prefix “half.”</p>\n<p>An example for converting a major sixth interval to a half major sixth:</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory/webp/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory_10.webp\" alt=\"Half major sixth interval compared to major sixth\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 12- Half major sixth interval compared to major sixth[/caption]\n<p>As seen in the above image, in the first part we have an interval from the note “G” to the note “E,” which is 4.5 steps. Now, to make this interval a quarter step less, we have two ways: first, to make the first note a quarter step higher, which we do by placing the “sori” sign before the note “G,” and the second way is to make the second note of the interval a quarter step lower, which happens by placing the “koron” sign before the note “E,” and with these two methods, we convert the major sixth interval in world music theory to the half major sixth interval in Iranian music theory.</p>\n<p>Now, by examining the above intervals more closely, we reach the conclusion that some of these intervals are the same, so in order to summarize the specific intervals in the theory of Iranian music, we remove the repetitive intervals. Therefore, we review the specific intervals in the theory of Iranian music once more in a summarized way and then remove the repetitive cases by mentioning the reason.</p>\n<p>In Iranian music, we have 3 words for determining quarter-step intervals:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Augmented</strong>: To add a quarter step to perfect, major, minor, augmented, and diminished intervals</li>\n  <li><strong>Diminished</strong>: To subtract a quarter step from perfect, minor, augmented, and diminished intervals</li>\n  <li><strong>Half</strong>: To subtract a quarter step from major intervals</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore, 10 specific intervals are created in the theory of Iranian music:</p>\n<p>Augmented perfect, diminished perfect, augmented major, half major, augmented minor, diminished minor, augmented augmented, diminished augmented, augmented diminished, and diminished diminished.</p>\n<p>Now we examine the identical intervals:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>The difference between major and minor intervals is a half step. If we subtract a quarter step from the major interval and add a quarter step to the minor interval, they coincide. Therefore, in the theory of Iranian music, the half major and augmented minor intervals are equal to each other, and for this reason, we remove the augmented minor interval.</li>\n  <li>The difference between major and augmented intervals is a half step, and if the major interval is increased by a quarter step and we subtract a quarter step from the augmented interval, they coincide. Therefore, in the theory of Iranian music, the augmented major and diminished augmented intervals are equal to each other, and for this reason, we remove the diminished augmented interval.</li>\n  <li>The difference between minor and diminished intervals is a half step, and if the diminished interval is increased by a quarter step and we subtract a quarter step from the minor interval, they coincide. Therefore, in the theory of Iranian music, the diminished minor and augmented diminished intervals are equal to each other, and for this reason, we remove the augmented diminished interval.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>With this accounting, 7 specific intervals remain in the theory of Iranian music, which are:</p>\n<p>Augmented perfect, augmented major, augmented augmented, diminished perfect, diminished minor, diminished diminished, and half major.</p>\n<p>The best way to remember the removed intervals is to pay attention to the contradiction between their words:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The augmented minor interval is one of these intervals, which has been removed due to the contradiction between its words, and instead, we have kept the half major interval.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The reason that for subtracting a quarter step from major intervals, the word “half” is used instead of the word “diminished” is perhaps this removal of the contradiction between the words “major” and “diminished.”</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The augmented diminished interval is also removed due to the contradiction between the words, and instead, we keep the diminished minor interval.</li>\n  <li>The diminished augmented interval is also removed due to the contradiction, and instead, we keep the augmented major interval.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>And finally, a better definition will be as follows:</p>\n<p>In the theory of Iranian music, we have 3 words for determining quarter-step intervals:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Augmented</strong>: To add a quarter step to perfect, major, and augmented intervals</li>\n  <li><strong>Diminished</strong>: To subtract a quarter step from perfect, minor, and diminished intervals</li>\n  <li><strong>Half</strong>: To subtract a quarter step from major intervals</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Classification of Tetrachords in the Theory of Iranian Music</h3>\n<p>In music theory, every octave (6 steps) is considered equal to 1200 cents, and therefore every tetrachord or dang (2.5 steps) will be equal to 500 cents.</p>\n<p>To examine the theory of Iranian music and the various dastgah and Avaz forms of it, one must first examine the tetrachords in Iranian music.</p>\n<p>All <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%88-%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2/\">Iranian dastgahs and Avazs</a> are built based on 4 main tetrachords in the theory of Iranian music, which are: the Shur tetrachord, the Dashti tetrachord, the Chahargah tetrachord, and the Mahur tetrachord.</p>\n<p>In one of the most common classifications of tetrachords, for each tetrachord, the intervals are divided based on the cent unit as follows.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li style=\"list-style-type: none;\">\n<ul>\n  <li>Shur tetrachord: 140 cents + 140 cents + 220 cents</li>\n  <li>Dashti tetrachord: 200 cents + 80 cents + 220 cents</li>\n  <li>Chahargah tetrachord: 140 cents + 240 cents + 120 cents</li>\n  <li>Mahur tetrachord: 200 cents + 180 cents + 120 cents</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>As you can see, in the theory of Iranian music, the intervals of whole step, half step, and quarter step are not the same, and for example, the whole-step intervals in the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%85%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Mahur</a> tetrachord are not equal to each other.</p>\n<p>For the simplicity of examining the theory of Iranian music, we define the following values for whole-step, half-step, quarter-step, and other intervals, and from now on, based on these definitions, we name the fretting of different intervals.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li style=\"list-style-type: none;\">\n<ul>\n  <li>We consider the values of 80 and 120 cents as half steps.</li>\n  <li>We consider the value of 140 cents as 4/3 steps.</li>\n  <li>We consider the values of 180, 200, and 220 cents as one whole step.</li>\n  <li>We consider the value of 240 cents as 1 and 1/4 steps.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In this way, the fretting of the tetrachords in the theory of Iranian music based on the above definitions is as follows.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Shur tetrachord</li>\n</ul>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory/webp/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory_11.webp\" alt=\"Intervals of the Shur tetrachord in the theory of Iranian music\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 13- Intervals of the Shur tetrachord in the theory of Iranian music[/caption]\n<ul>\n  <li>Dashti tetrachord</li>\n</ul>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory/webp/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory_12.webp\" alt=\"Intervals of the Dashti tetrachord in the theory of Iranian music\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 14- Intervals of the Dashti tetrachord in the theory of Iranian music[/caption]\n<ul>\n  <li>Chahargah tetrachord</li>\n</ul>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory/webp/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory_13.webp\" alt=\"Intervals of the Chahargah tetrachord in the theory of Iranian music\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 15- Intervals of the Chahargah tetrachord in the theory of Iranian music[/caption]\n<ul>\n  <li>Mahur tetrachord</li>\n</ul>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory/webp/sornaz_013_Iranian_music_theory_14.webp\" alt=\"Intervals of the Mahur tetrachord in the theory of Iranian music\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 16- Intervals of the Mahur tetrachord in the theory of Iranian music[/caption]\n<p>The dastgahs and Avazs of Iranian music are composed of two tetrachords and one whole step, which altogether equal one octave or 1200 cents, and in the continuation, in the examination of each of the dastgahs and Avazs, the tetrachords and their commonalities and differences will be examined completely and precisely.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding the theory of Iranian music, please share it in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this educational content with the Sornaz Music School.</p>', '2023-05-14 10:25:51', 0, '2024-08-30 19:19:06', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1471, 'posts', 14, 'en', NULL, 'What is the concept of musical scale and tonality? Types of scales in music', NULL, 'This article explores the concepts of musical scale and tonality and highlights the differences between them. A musical scale is a sequence of notes arranged in a specific order with defined intervals, starting from a note and ending at its octave. Scales are mainly divided into two categories: chromatic scales (with equal semitone intervals) and diatonic scales (with varying patterns of whole and half steps).\n\nIn diatonic scales, certain notes play more important roles, such as tonal notes (tonic, dominant, and subdominant), which establish the tonal center, and modal notes, which change between major and minor forms. The article also introduces other types of scales, including whole-tone scales, dodecaphonic (twelve-tone) scales, limited transposition scales, and natural scales like pentatonic and gypsy scales.\n\nAdditionally, it discusses scales with non-standard intervals, such as those used in Iranian music. Each type of scale has unique characteristics and applications across different musical styles. Overall, the article emphasizes the importance of interval structure in shaping scales and understanding music theory.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the concepts of tonality and the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">musical scale</span></strong> and examine the differences between the two. Continue with us in the following...</p>\n<h2>The Concept of a Musical Scale</h2>\n<p>A musical scale is a number of consecutive sounds that are placed after each other with certain and calculated intervals, and its last note is the upper octave (octave) of the first note. Therefore, in every scale, what is important is the number of consecutive sounds and also the intervals between them, and these factors determine the type of scale. The specific and consecutive sounds are called the degrees of the scale. Scales, no matter from which note they start, are divided into two general groups:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Chromatic scales</strong></li>\n  <li><strong>Diatonic scales</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Chromatic Scale</h3>\n<p>This musical scale, starting from any note, passes through the degrees at half-step intervals (both chromatic and diatonic) and gradually rises until, on the thirteenth sound, it reaches the octave of the starting sound. For example, if we start the chromatic scale from the note “C” or “G,” we will have such a diagram.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_014_scale_and_tonality/webp/sornaz_014_scale_and_tonality_01.webp\" alt=\"Chromatic scales of C and G\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 1- Chromatic scales of C and G[/caption]\n<p>Regarding the above figure, the question may arise as to which of the changing signs should be used in writing a chromatic scale?</p>\n<p>In this regard, it should be said that the use of flattened or sharpened notes does not make much difference in the practical process of music. A general point that can be added here is that:</p>\n<p>Whenever the musical chromatic scale rises upward, the ascending signs, that is, sharps (usually except “A-sharp,” which sometimes “B-flat” comes instead), are used, and whenever this scale comes downward, the descending signs, that is, flats (usually except “G-flat,” which sometimes “F-sharp” comes instead), are used.</p>\n<p>As mentioned, in the chromatic scale, all intervals between degrees are equal to half steps. Between the beginning and ending notes of a scale that are placed at the interval of one octave, there are 11 notes and 12 half steps. In fact, every chromatic scale, no matter from which note it starts, uses exactly the same notes that another chromatic scale with a different starting note has.</p>\n<p>Therefore, chromatic scales that start with different notes do not have an independent character relative to each other, and for this reason, the starting note in each of them lacks that “centrality state” that is seen in a diatonic musical scale (which we will explain in the continuation). The “centrality state” is a state similar to rest that is felt in one of the notes of a diatonic scale. In the figure below, three chromatic scales with starting notes “C,” “G,” and “D” are compared with each other.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_014_scale_and_tonality/webp/sornaz_014_scale_and_tonality_02.webp\" alt=\"Comparison of several chromatic scales with each other\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 2- Comparison of several chromatic scales with each other[/caption]\n<p>Chromatic scales (and similarly diatonic scales) can also have a descending movement, in which case they are notated as follows.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_014_scale_and_tonality/webp/sornaz_014_scale_and_tonality_03.webp\" alt=\"Descending chromatic scales of C and G\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 3- Descending chromatic scales of C and G[/caption]\n<p>Chromatic scales are also divided into two categories, which are:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Melodic chromatic</strong></li>\n  <li><strong>Harmonic chromatic</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The difference between the two is in the choice of sharp and flat signs for making whole-step intervals into half steps. In the melodic chromatic type, from one note to the next note at a whole-step interval, first a chromatic half step (a half step with the same name as the previous note and having a changing sign) comes, and then a diatonic half step (a half step with a different name from the previous note), while in the harmonic chromatic type, the order of chromatic and diatonic half steps is different; first the diatonic half step is placed, and after that the chromatic half step.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_014_scale_and_tonality/webp/sornaz_014_scale_and_tonality_04.webp\" alt=\"Harmonic chromatic scale of C in ascending and descending states\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 4- Harmonic chromatic scale of C in ascending and descending states[/caption]\n<h3>Diatonic Scale</h3>\n<p>Since the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%a7%d8%b5%d9%84%d9%87-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%d8%a7%db%8c%db%8c/\">musical interval</a> between degrees in the group of diatonic musical scales is not uniform and equal, and also the pattern of placing these intervals differs in each type from the other, this group includes various types. In a general classification, diatonic scales are divided into two main groups:</p>\n<p>The first group includes those musical scales in which the interval between consecutive degrees is composed of double whole-step and half-step intervals (in this group, it is usually assumed that each whole step is exactly twice each half step, and each octave is composed of 12 equal half steps in total).</p>\n<p>The second group consists of scales that, instead of one of the whole-step or half-step intervals (and sometimes up to one and a half steps), or in addition to these intervals, have other intervals between their degrees.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, because the second group of scales has become obsolete in today’s Western music, we will not discuss them in this article.</p>\n<p>In today’s music, the most common scales of the first group are <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d8%a7%da%98%d9%88%d8%b1/\">diatonic major scales (large)</a> and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%db%8c%d9%86%d9%88%d8%b1/\">diatonic minor scales (small)</a>.</p>\n<h2>Tonal and Modal Notes in a Musical Scale</h2>\n<p><strong>Tonal notes:</strong> Every musical scale, whether major or minor, has three notes that have more personality than the other notes in terms of stabilizing tonality:</p>\n<p>1) The tonic note (degree 1)</p>\n<p>2) The subdominant note (degree IV, in fact the tonic of the neighboring scale with one more flat or one less sharp)</p>\n<p>3) The dominant note (degree V, in fact the tonic of the neighboring scale with one more sharp or one less flat).</p>\n<p>These notes (degrees) are called tonal notes or degrees. Every major scale, when compared with its parallel minor scale (one can call every major scale that starts with a minor scale from the same note (namesake) — and vice versa — “parallel scales”), it is seen that they have the same tonal notes.</p>\n<p><strong>Modal notes:</strong> The notes that change when converting a major scale to its parallel minor scale, or vice versa, are called modal notes. Modal notes are placed on degrees VI, III (and in natural minor scales, degree VII).</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_014_scale_and_tonality/webp/sornaz_014_scale_and_tonality_05.webp\" alt=\"Difference in changing signs in parallel major and minor scales\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 5- Difference in changing signs in parallel major and minor scales[/caption]\n<p>In harmonic minor scales (and natural), the interval from the tonic note to each of degrees III and VI — and in natural minors also to degree VII — is always a chromatic half step less than the corresponding intervals in the major scale. In every major musical scale, there are 4 half steps between the tonic and degree II, 9 half steps between the tonic and degree VI (and 11 half steps to degree VII); while these numbers in the harmonic minor scale are respectively equal to 3 and 8 (and in natural minor: 10).</p>\n<h2>Modal Notes in Old Musical Scales</h2>\n<p>In past times, in addition to the two major and minor modes, there were other modes (for this reason, in those periods, the number of modal notes was more than today. More or less, it can be thought that every non-tonal note in the scale (that is, degrees VI, III, II, and VII) could be considered a modal note.</p>\n<p>In the continuation, we refer to a number of other musical scales that have been created by prominent <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d8%b2%d9%86%d8%af%da%af%db%8c%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%85%d9%87-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%86/\">musicians</a> for various reasons:</p>\n<h2>The Whole-Tone Scale</h2>\n<p>The whole-tone musical scale was created by the French Impressionist composer <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%DA%A9%D9%84%D9%88%D8%AF_%D8%AF%D8%A8%D9%88%D8%B3%DB%8C\">Claude Achille Debussy</a> (Claude-Achille DEBUSSY, Saint-Germain 1862 - Paris 1918), inspired by the Russian musical scale, in order to compose his works that were simultaneous and in line with the Impressionist style, and theorists called it the whole-tone scale. In this scale, the interval from the tonic to the octave is divided into six equal parts, and the scale has seven degrees instead of eight:</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_014_scale_and_tonality/webp/sornaz_014_scale_and_tonality_06.webp\" alt=\"Examples of whole-tone scales\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 6- Examples of whole-tone scales[/caption]\n<p>As is evident from examples “a” and “b,” all intervals between the degrees in this scale are whole steps; in this musical scale, there is no half-step interval, and it does not matter what name is chosen for this or that note (and degree). For example, in example “a,” instead of the names “C, D, E, F-sharp, G-sharp, A-sharp, C,” one can write: “C, D, E, G-flat, A-flat, B-flat, C,” and in example “b,” instead of the written notes, one can write this way: “C-sharp, D-sharp, F, G, A, B, C-sharp.”</p>\n<h2>The Whole-Half-Step Scale (Dodecaphonic)</h2>\n<p>The whole-half-step musical scale (dodecaphonic) is built based on the chromatic scale. This scale, until the beginning of the 20th century, was only discussed and examined in music theory books and was not independently used in composition.</p>\n<p>From the beginning of this century, some avant-garde composers, such as <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A2%D8%B1%D9%86%D9%88%D9%84%D8%AF_%D8%B4%D9%88%D9%86%D8%A8%D8%B1%DA%AF\">Arnold Schoenberg</a> (Arnold SCHOENBERG, Vienna 1874 – Los Angeles 1951) and two of his students, Theodor W. Adorno Alban Berg (Theodor W. Adorno Alban BERG, Vienna 1935) and Anton Webern (Anton WEBERN, Salzburg 1945), have benefited from the chromatic scale as a basis for setting the theory of their school (called “dodecaphonic”). These composers, by founding their school, have actually presented a translation of the Expressionist style in music.</p>\n<p>The basis of the dodecaphonic school (Dodecaphonique, composed of two words “Dodeca” (twelve) and “phone” (sound), meaning “twelve sounds”) is that, unlike diatonic scales, none of the twelve musical sounds in a piece has a share and personality higher and more than the other sounds, but rather “special methods” are what express various effects. These methods should be found in books related to the principles of dodecaphonic musical scale composition. There is no doubt that in this school, mathematical (and geometrical) ideas have a greater share than nature and feeling — especially “non-mathematical human feeling.”</p>\n<h3>Limited Transposition Scale</h3>\n<p>The limited transposition musical scale was proposed by one of the contemporary French composers, named <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A7%D9%84%DB%8C%D9%88%DB%8C%D9%87_%D9%85%D8%B3%DB%8C%D8%A7%D9%86\">Olivier Messiaen</a> (Olivier MESSIAEN, Avignon 1908 - ). In this scale, four whole-step intervals and four half-step intervals are placed alternately between the degrees, and in this way, a scale with 9 degrees is formed.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_014_scale_and_tonality/webp/sornaz_014_scale_and_tonality_07.webp\" alt=\"Intervals of the limited transposition scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 7- Intervals of the limited transposition scale[/caption]\n<p>One should not think that “artificial scales” — even the most important ones — are summarized in just these few examples above. The aforementioned examples have only been presented here so that the minds of students and those interested in music become familiar with the corners of music and, to some extent, with the diversity of possibilities of innovation in building a musical scale.</p>\n<h3>Natural Scales</h3>\n<p>The natural (traditional and emotional) musical scale has various types depending on different regions of the world. Each of these musical scales, because it is intertwined with the musical expression of its own region, also forms the basis of the music of that region. Therefore, with the performance of some of these scales, if the music of that region has been heard more, the memory of it will most likely come alive in the listener’s mind. Here, we suffice with introducing two examples of these scales.</p>\n<p><strong>1) Pentatonic scale (five sounds):</strong> As its name suggests (penta = five, tonic = sound note), this musical scale is composed of five sounds (with the repetition of the first sound in the upper octave). The intervals between the degrees are three whole steps and two one-and-a-half steps. This scale is the basis for the music of East Asia and especially the countries of China and Japan, but it seems that the local music of all regions of the world uses it more or less.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_014_scale_and_tonality/webp/sornaz_014_scale_and_tonality_08.webp\" alt=\"Two examples of the fretting of pentatonic musical scales\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 8- Two examples of the fretting of pentatonic musical scales[/caption]\n<p><strong>2) Gypsy scale:</strong> This musical scale is altogether, with the repetition of the first note in the upper octave, composed of eight notes, and the intervals between its degrees are: one whole step, four half steps, and two one-and-a-half steps.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_014_scale_and_tonality/webp/sornaz_014_scale_and_tonality_09.webp\" alt=\"Intervals of the gypsy musical scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 9- Intervals of the gypsy musical scale[/caption]\n<p>Regarding the gypsy musical scale, the Harvard Dictionary of Music says the following:</p>\n<p>““… It must be doubted that this scale belongs to the gypsies… rather, it seems that they were only performers of pieces that were built on the background of this scale…””</p>\n<p>Fortunately, Iranian musicologists have the possibility to clarify this doubt: the gypsies are scattered all over the world, including in Iran, and migrate to the whole world. Since the “gypsy musical scale,” with a little change and adjustment, is nothing but the “Chahargah dastgah” in Iranian music, it is very likely that they took the mentioned scale from this land.</p>\n<h2>Musical Scale with Different Intervals</h2>\n<p>There are scales in which some of the intervals between their degrees are not “half step” or “whole step,” but something in between. Or, in them, the “half step” is not exactly half of the “whole step.” It can be said that these scales cover the entire range of music — except classical Western music. These scales are also divided into two groups: natural and artificial:</p>\n<p><strong>1) Group of natural scales:</strong> The examples available to us are the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian musical scales</a>. The Iranian musical scale should be considered from the kind of scales in which the “half step” is not half of the “whole step.” The intervals of these musical scales should be found in the books of “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Theory of Iranian Music</a>.”</p>\n<p><strong>2) Group of artificial scales:</strong> Created by some contemporary musicians such as <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A2%D9%84%D9%88%DB%8C%D8%B3_%D9%87%D8%A7%D8%A8%D8%A7\">Alois Hába</a> (Alois HABA, Vizovice 1893 - Prague 1973), the Czech theorist and composer, who divided each whole step into 3, 4, 6, and even 12 pieces and took an effect from the mixture of the resulting sounds — invented.</p>\n<p>As was mentioned earlier, the main changing signs that are used to adjust the intervals in relation to the tonic note of each natural major and minor musical scale are always placed at the beginning of the staff, after the clef and before the time signature; but since these signs are only composed of “sharp” and “flat,” and as was said, the “natural” signs are never used in this situation (except in the middle of some pieces to change tonality).</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding the concept of tonality and the musical scale, please share it in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this educational content with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">us</a>.</p>', '2023-05-15 12:26:16', 0, '2024-08-30 19:19:21', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1472, 'posts', 15, 'en', NULL, 'What is a musical interval? How do you calculate the interval between two notes?', NULL, 'This article explains the concept of a musical interval, defined as the pitch difference between two notes. Intervals are named based on the number of notes they span (such as second, third, fifth, etc.), and in physics, they are also described by frequency ratios.\n\nIntervals are classified into simple (within one octave) and compound (beyond one octave). In music theory, seconds can be either whole tones (major seconds) or semitones (minor seconds), with a total of 12 semitones in an octave.\n\nAccidentals such as sharp, flat, double sharp, double flat, and natural are used to alter pitch. In Persian music, additional symbols like sori and koron indicate quarter-tone changes.\n\nSemitones are divided into diatonic and chromatic, and the concept of interval inversion shows that the sum of an interval and its inversion equals nine.\n\nFinally, the article demonstrates how intervals are structured on the piano keyboard, helping to visualize whole tones and semitones in practice.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">musical interval</span></strong>, its types, and the methods of calculating a musical interval. Continue with us in the following...</p>\n<p>Previously, we have spoken enough about the high and low of musical notes. Now we know that musical sounds are placed at different levels of high and low (read the article “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%88%db%8c%da%98%da%af%db%8c-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%b5%d9%88%d8%aa/\">The 4 Main Characteristics of Musical Sound and Sound</a>”), and we also know that musical notes on the staff lines become “higher” as they move upward and “lower” as they are placed lower.</p>\n<p>From this summary, one can understand that there is an interval between any two notes. It is clear that the farther these two notes are from each other in terms of high and low, the greater the musical interval between them.</p>\n<p>For example, in the figure below, the musical interval between two notes in part “a” is less than the interval between the notes in part “b,” and the interval between the two notes in part “c” becomes even greater, and finally the greatest interval is seen in part “e.”</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_015_distance/webp/sornaz_015_distance_01.webp\" alt=\"Interval of musical notes from each other\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 1- Interval of musical notes from each other[/caption]\n<p>Therefore, a musical interval is the distance between any two musical notes, or in other words, the difference between two notes in terms of high and low or the difference in their level on the staff lines.</p>\n<h2>Frequency Ratio</h2>\n<p>In acoustics, which is one of the topics of physics, the interval between two sounds is the ratio of their frequencies. For example, if the number of vibrations of the note “C” in one second is 264 times and the note “D” is 297 times, the interval between these two relative to each other will be 297/264 or 9/8. In the science of music theory, the size of a musical interval is calculated in another way, which we will explain in the continuation.</p>\n<h2>Measuring a Musical Interval</h2>\n<p>In the science of music theory, to measure the musical interval between two notes, it is necessary to count all the notes that form that interval and express the resulting number in ordinal form.</p>\n<p>For example, the musical interval between the two notes “C” and “D” is a second interval because this interval is composed of two notes, and the interval between the two notes “C” and “G” is a fifth interval (C, D, E, F, G). The interval shown in example “c” is of the eighth type, the interval between the notes in example “d” is a thirteenth, and the interval in example “e” is a nineteenth.</p>\n<p>The musical interval in example “f” is a ninth interval, but the place of the starting and ending notes of the musical interval has been swapped and the direction of counting the notes forming it has been reversed. It should be noted that in the science of music theory and in the ordinary state, the lower note of the musical interval is expressed before the higher note. Therefore, the form of example “f” is actually used very rarely in practice, and the purpose of showing such an example was only to mention this point.</p>\n<p>Therefore, to find the musical interval between two notes, we must start counting from the lower note and count the middle notes one by one (step by step) until we reach the higher note of the interval and add the latter note to the count as well, and mention the result in ordinal number form.</p>\n<p>In the continuation, we will become familiar with a more precise method of measuring a musical interval, which also gives a more precise result.</p>\n<h2>Simple Interval and Compound Interval</h2>\n<p>If the musical interval between two notes does not exceed the boundary of one octave, this interval is called simple, and if the two notes do not fall within the boundaries of one octave, that interval is called compound. In other words, the intervals of unison, second, third, fourth, fifth, sixth, and seventh are simple intervals.</p>\n<p>Regarding the musical interval of the eighth, which is actually the octave of the lower note of the interval, there are differences among music theorists; some consider this interval simple and others consider it compound.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_015_distance/webp/sornaz_015_distance_02.webp\" alt=\"Intervals from unison to octave in music\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 2- Intervals from unison to octave in music[/caption]\n<p>In the figure above, the hollow note is the lower note of the musical interval and the filled note is the higher note of the musical interval. It is clear that any other note can also be taken as the base of the interval instead of the note “C.”</p>\n<p>Intervals from the ninth upward are called compound intervals. In the science of music theory, sometimes it is necessary to convert a compound interval to a simple interval. Therefore, to convert a compound interval to a simple interval, it is sufficient to subtract the number 7 from the ordinal number of the compound interval as many times as needed until a number between 1 and 8 is obtained.</p>\n<p>For example, with the same instruction, we converted the compound intervals in the figure below to simple intervals.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_015_distance/webp/sornaz_015_distance_03.webp\" alt=\"Calculating the equivalent simple interval of compound intervals\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 3- Calculating the equivalent simple interval of compound intervals[/caption]\n<p>Example 1) The compound eleventh interval has been converted to the simple fourth interval.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\"> 4 = 7 – 11</p>\n<p>Example 2) The compound ninth interval has been converted to the simple second interval.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\"> 2 = 7 – 9</p>\n<p>Example 3) The compound nineteenth interval has been converted to the simple fifth interval.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\"> 4 = 7 – 12 &lt;&lt; 12 = 7 - 19</p>\n<p>Example 4) The compound twenty-second interval has been converted to the simple eighth interval.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\">8 = 7 – 15 &lt;&lt; 15 = 7 - 22</p>\n<h2>Whole Step and Half Step</h2>\n<p>We know that if we write the notes consecutively, the musical interval between every two consecutive notes is always a second interval. Although the second intervals are not always equal, and some notes are placed at a “whole step” interval from each other and some others at a “half step” interval. The whole-step second intervals are called “major second” and the half-step second intervals are called “minor second.”</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_015_distance/webp/sornaz_015_distance_04.webp\" alt=\"Whole-step and half-step second intervals in the C major scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 4- Whole-step and half-step second intervals in the C major scale[/caption]\n<p>With careful observation of the above figure, it is seen that in the sequence of notes, the minor second intervals are always placed between the notes “E – F” and “B – C,” and the major second intervals are always placed between the other notes.</p>\n<p>In the theory of Western music, it is assumed that every major second interval (a whole-step interval) can be divided into two equal half-step intervals. The half steps resulting from this division are in practice equal to the half-step intervals of the minor second.</p>\n<p>Therefore, if we divide all major second intervals into half-step pieces, we will have 12 half steps in each octave.</p>\n<p>In Western music, the half step is the smallest objective interval between two consecutive notes. Half steps have different types, which we will explain in the continuation.</p>\n<h2>Changing Signs of a Musical Interval</h2>\n<p>In <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">music</a>, there are signs whose role is to raise or lower notes by a half step (increasing or decreasing the musical interval) without changing the name of the note and its position on the staff line.</p>\n<p>In Western music, these signs can raise or lower any note by one or two half steps without changing its name. These signs are:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Sharp (Diesis): Whenever placed to the left of a note, it raises it by a half step (makes the note sharp). This sign can be placed in front of any musical note. If the musical interval between two consecutive notes is a whole step and the mentioned sign is placed to the left of the first note (the lower note), naturally the interval between them turns into a half step, and if the interval between two consecutive notes is a half step and the sharp sign is placed to the left of the second note (the higher note), the interval between them turns into a whole step.</li>\n</ul>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_015_distance/webp/sornaz_015_distance_05.webp\" alt=\"The sharp sign is used to make the sound a half step higher\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 5- The sharp sign is used to make the sound a half step higher[/caption]\n<p>2) Flat (Bemol): Whenever placed to the left of a note, it lowers it by a half step (makes it lower). This sign, like the sharp, can be placed in front of any musical note. In order for a whole-step musical interval (major second) to turn into a half step, the flat sign must be written before the higher note, and to make a half-step interval (minor second) into a whole step, this sign must be placed in front of the lower note.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_015_distance/webp/sornaz_015_distance_06.webp\" alt=\"The flat sign is used to make the sound a half step lower\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 6- The flat sign is used to make the sound a half step lower[/caption]\n<p>3) Double Sharp (Double-Diesis): Whenever placed to the left of a note, it raises that note twice, each time by a half step. With the help of this sign, one can raise the note “C-sharp” (or the note “C” that has been sharpened once) another half step and obtain the note “C double-sharp” as shown below.</p>\n<p>Although the note “C double-sharp” is practically in unison with the note “D” in Western music, it is still called the note “C double-sharp.” The note “E” with the “double sharp” sign in Western music is practically in unison with the note “F-sharp,” because the musical interval between the notes “E” and “F,” as was mentioned earlier, is a half step; if “E” is sharpened, it is practically in unison with the note “F,” and if it is sharpened twice, it is in unison with the note “F-sharp.”</p>\n<p>4) Double Flat (Double-Bemol): Whenever placed to the left of a note, it lowers it twice by a half step. If, for example, we want to lower the note “B-flat” (the note “B” that has been lowered once by “flat”) another half step, the note “B double-flat” will be obtained as shown below. The latter note in Western music is practically in unison with the note “A,” although it is still called “B double-flat.”</p>\n<p>5) Natural (Becarre): Whenever placed to the left of a note that has previously been sharpened or flattened, it neutralizes the effect of the previous sign. If the mentioned note has previously been “double-sharpened” or “double-flattened,” in order to neutralize one of the “sharps” or one of the “flats,” the “natural” sign must be used together with “sharp” or “flat.”</p>\n<p>Otherwise, that is, using the “natural” sign alone, causes the effect of the “double-sharp” or “double-flat” sign to be completely neutralized.</p>\n<p>The above signs are used in the music of all countries. In addition to the above signs, there are two other signs for changing the musical interval specific to <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a> that <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d9%86%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Ali-Naqi Vaziri</a> established:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Sori: This sign, whenever placed to the left of a note, raises it by a quarter step (makes the sound higher). For example, the note “C-sori” is placed between the notes “C” and “C-sharp.”</li>\n  <li>Koron: This sign, whenever placed to the left of a note, lowers it by a quarter step (makes the sound lower). For example, the note “B koron” is placed between the notes “B-flat” and “B.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Regarding these two signs, two important points exist:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The signs “double sori” and “double koron” do not exist.</li>\n  <li>The “natural” sign is still effective for neutralizing these two signs.</li>\n</ul>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_015_distance/webp/sornaz_015_distance_07.webp\" alt=\"List of musical interval changing signs\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 7- List of musical interval changing signs[/caption]\n<p>The changing signs are used in two situations. The first situation is that they are placed in a special order and for a specific purpose (in the topics related to <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">Western music scales</a>, it will be examined in detail), at the beginning of each musical piece and also at the beginning of each staff line, between the clef and the time signature. In this case, the change of notes is valid throughout the entire piece. In this situation, if in some place of the piece there is a need for the changed notes to be performed in their original state, the “natural” sign is used.</p>\n<p>However, it should not be forgotten that the use of the “natural” sign at the beginning of the piece is meaningless.</p>\n<p>As mentioned, the signs at the beginning of the piece must also be repeated at the beginning of each staff line.</p>\n<p>The double-sharp and double-flat signs are not used in this situation.</p>\n<p>The second situation for using the changing signs is that we use them at certain moments of the piece and for the purpose of making those moments more beautiful, or for another purpose, without that special order that was mentioned above without reference. In this case, their validity is preserved from the moment of use until the end of the same measure, and if we want to benefit from the same sign in the next measure as well, we must necessarily repeat the sign.</p>\n<h2>Types of Half Steps</h2>\n<p>In the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">science of music theory</a>, the half step has two types:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Diatonic half step (Diatonique): It is always placed between two non-namesake and, of course, consecutive notes. One or both notes forming the diatonic half step can be used with or without changing signs.</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n  <li>Chromatic half step (Chromatique): It is always placed between two namesake notes and in the same place on the staff. This half step is always created between a note and the same note that has been “sharpened” or “flattened.” In other words, at least one of the two notes forming the musical interval must have a changing sign.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%B5%D9%88%D8%AA%E2%80%8C%D8%B4%D9%86%D8%A7%D8%B3%DB%8C\">science of sound physics</a>, the two half steps “diatonic” and “chromatic” are not equal to each other. There, it is said that each whole step consists of 9 commas, and in dividing it into two diatonic and chromatic half steps, the diatonic half step is considered 4 commas and the chromatic half step 5 commas.</p>\n<p>In the science of Western music theory, this difference between diatonic and chromatic half steps also existed until the presentation of the tempered scale by Johann Sebastian Bach. For example, the notes “F-sharp” and “G-flat” were tuned with a difference of one comma.</p>\n<p>But after the presentation of the tempered scale, the difference between diatonic and chromatic half steps disappeared, and each half step was considered 4.5 commas. Therefore, at present, the notes “F-sharp” and “G-flat” are tuned the same.</p>\n<p>If a whole-step musical interval between consecutive notes is divided into two half steps, one of the half steps will be diatonic and the other chromatic. For example, between the notes “C” and “D,” the note “C-sharp” makes the first half step chromatic and the second half step diatonic, and the note “D-flat” makes the first half step diatonic and the second half step chromatic.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_015_distance/webp/sornaz_015_distance_08.webp\" alt=\"Types of half-step musical intervals\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 8- Types of half-step musical intervals[/caption]\n<p>As was mentioned earlier, the whole-step musical interval between consecutive notes is called a major second, and the half-step musical interval between two notes is called a minor second. In completing that, it should be said that the diatonic half-step interval is called “minor second” and the chromatic half-step interval is called “augmented unison.”</p>\n<p>Apart from the types of half steps mentioned above, there is another kind of this interval, which, because there is basically no need to use them in music, is not discussed in music theory.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_015_distance/webp/sornaz_015_distance_09.webp\" alt=\"Inverted half-step intervals in music\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 9- Inverted half-step intervals in music[/caption]\n<h2>Inversion of a Musical Interval</h2>\n<p>In music and more in theoretical discussions, every musical interval, whether simple or compound, is examined only in its simple form.</p>\n<p>Every simple interval can be inverted. If we raise the lower note of the interval by one octave, a new interval is obtained that is the inversion of the original interval; one can lower the higher note by one octave instead of raising the lower note of the interval.</p>\n<p>In any case, if the place of the first and second notes of the interval is swapped in such a way that the direction of the interval is still from bottom to top, the interval has been inverted. There is always a relationship between the ordinal number of each interval and the ordinal number of the inversion of the same interval. As seen in the figure below, the inversion of the third interval is always the sixth interval and vice versa, and the inversion of the fifth interval is the fourth interval and vice versa.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_015_distance/webp/sornaz_015_distance_10.webp\" alt=\"The major third interval and the minor sixth interval are inversions of each other\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 10- The major third interval and the minor sixth interval are inversions of each other[/caption]\n<p>In other words, the sum of the ordinal numbers of each interval and its inversion is always 9.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_015_distance/webp/sornaz_015_distance_11.webp\" alt=\"Sum of inverted intervals in music\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 11- Sum of inverted intervals in music[/caption]\n<h2>Piano Keys</h2>\n<p>By looking at the piano keys and examining the way the white and black keys are placed, we understand important points regarding the structure of intervals in this instrument.</p>\n<p>The piano keys are divided into two groups of white and black, in which the white keys create the main musical notes, namely “C,” “D,” “E,” “F,” “G,” “A,” and “B.” An ordinary piano has 52 white keys that are placed consecutively in a uniform manner.</p>\n<p>In each period of the seven white keys (from “C” to “B”), five black keys are placed in two groups of two and three.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"810\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_015_distance/webp/sornaz_015_distance_12.webp\" alt=\"Piano keys and their equivalent notes in one octave\" width=\"810\" height=\"810\" /> 12- Piano keys and their equivalent notes in one octave[/caption]\n<p>In the lower part of the above figure, the notes are drawn step by step from bottom to top and from left to right. In the seven intervals of the ladder, five steps, that is, steps number 1, 2, 4, 5, and 6 are large, and two steps 3 and 7 are small. The large steps, in accordance with the white keys of the piano, are in fact the same intervals between which the black keys are embedded.</p>\n<p>This musical interval is called a major interval, and we said that every major interval (a whole step between two consecutive notes) can be divided into two equal pieces. This work is done in the piano with the help of the black keys that are placed between two white keys with a whole step.</p>\n<p>In other words, the musical interval between two white keys that have a black key between them is a whole step, and the black key divides that interval into two equal half steps.</p>\n<p>In the row of piano keys, the half-step intervals are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>White keys that have no black key between them.</li>\n  <li>White keys with a black key before them.</li>\n  <li>White keys with a black key after them.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If we count the piano keys consecutively from any note (to the right or left), on number 13 we reach the note with the same name as the starting note of the count, of course in one higher or lower octave.</p>\n<p>Whole-step intervals can also be obtained by adding two half-step intervals; in the row of piano keys, the whole-step intervals are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Two white keys that have a black key between them.</li>\n  <li>Two black keys that have a white key between them.</li>\n  <li>One white key and one black key that have a white key between them.</li>\n  <li>One black key and one white key that have a white key between them.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The thirteen white and black keys in the above figure are respectively the creators of the following notes on the staff lines.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_015_distance/webp/sornaz_015_distance_13.webp\" alt=\"Notes of the C chromatic scale from low to high and from high to low and the equivalent number of each note with its related key\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 13- Notes of the C chromatic scale from low to high and from high to low and the equivalent number of each note with its related key[/caption]\n<p>The numbers that are seen above the double staves in the above figure are the same numbers that are placed on the piano keys in the piano fretting figure, and from their matching in the above figures, one can understand several points.</p>\n<p>For example, note number 2 (piano notes on staff lines a) is “C-sharp,” and the same number below (staff b) represents the note “D-flat.” From this matching, we understand that key number 2 (piano fretting figure) produces the sound of the notes “C-sharp” and “D-flat.” Also, the notes “D-sharp” and “E-flat,” “F-sharp” and “G-flat,” “A-sharp” and “B-flat” are in unison and have common black keys.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_015_distance/webp/sornaz_015_distance_14.webp\" alt=\"Piano keys and the name of the notes related to each key\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 14- Piano keys and the name of the notes related to each key[/caption]\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding the musical interval and how to calculate and its types, please share it in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this educational content with the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>.</p>', '2023-05-23 15:29:46', 0, '2024-08-30 19:19:33', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1473, 'posts', 16, 'en', NULL, 'Major scale, key signatures and recognizing types of scales, Differences between 3 different types of major scales', NULL, 'The major scale is one of the fundamental concepts in music theory, consisting of eight notes arranged in a fixed interval pattern (whole–whole–half–whole–whole–whole–half). It can start from any note, but accidentals (sharps and flats) are used to preserve this structure.\n\nMajor scales are built from two similar tetrachords, and their relationships are based on the interval of a perfect fifth. Moving through this system changes the number of sharps or flats in the key signature. There is also an inverse relationship between the order of sharps and flats.\n\nUnderstanding major scales and their key signatures is essential for music theory, melody creation, and musical analysis.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we talk about the <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">major scale</span></strong></span>, its key signatures, and the similarities and differences between the major scale and the minor scale. Continue with us in this article...</p>\n<h2>The Major Scale (Diatonic Major Scale – Large Scale)</h2>\n<p>The major scale, counting the last namesake note, has eight notes, and the intervals between the consecutive degrees of this scale consist of 5 whole steps and 2 half steps, arranged according to the following pattern.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_016_major_scale/webp/sornaz_016_major_scale_01.webp\" alt=\"Fretting pattern of major scales\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 1- Fretting pattern of major scales[/caption]\n<p>In major scales, the row of intervals between degrees always remains unchanged, meaning that whenever the places of whole steps and half steps change, the new pattern will not belong to the previous scale.</p>\n<p>The major scale and the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%db%8c%d9%86%d9%88%d8%b1/\">minor scale</a> are derived from the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%af%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%da%a9%d9%84%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%a7%db%8c%db%8c/\">church modes</a> (Ionian and Aeolian church modes).</p>\n<p>As mentioned in the article “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%a7%d8%b5%d9%84%d9%87-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%d8%a7%db%8c%db%8c/\">What does a musical interval mean?</a>,” if we go up from the note “C” degree by degree to its octave note, the intervals between the degrees will be as follows.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_016_major_scale/webp/sornaz_016_major_scale_02.webp\" alt=\"Interval pattern between the notes of the C major scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 2- Interval pattern between the notes of the C major scale[/caption]\n<p>Now if we compare the pattern of the major scale with this pattern, we notice that they are equal to each other.</p>\n<p>If we start from the note “C” on the piano keys and sound all the white keys up to the “C” note in the next octave, the order of the intervals between the keys will be exactly like the pattern of the major scale.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"810\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_016_major_scale/webp/sornaz_016_major_scale_03.webp\" alt=\"C major scale on piano keys\" width=\"810\" height=\"810\" /> 3- C major scale on piano keys[/caption]\n<p>The major scale, like any other <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">scale</a>, can start from any note, but in any case, the intervals between the degrees must be arranged according to the fixed pattern of major scales.</p>\n<p>For example, if we want to write the major scale from the note “D.” First, using the main notes, we write the notes from “D” to the next “D” octave, then we compare these notes with the fixed pattern of major scales.</p>\n<p>In this comparison, we see that the notes “D,” “E,” “G,” “A,” “B,” and “D” octave are in their correct places, and the notes “F” and “C” must be raised by a chromatic half step.</p>\n<p>Considering the discussion of changing signs in the article “What does a musical interval mean? How do we calculate the interval between 2 notes?,” we do this by adding the “sharp” sign to these notes. In this way, the “D major” scale is obtained as follows.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_016_major_scale/webp/sornaz_016_major_scale_04.webp\" alt=\"Note pattern of the D major scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 4- Note pattern of the D major scale[/caption]\n<p>One of the important applications of changing signs is to adjust the intervals between degrees in order to adapt them to the fixed pattern of different scales.</p>\n<p>Now let’s write the major scale from the note “F” this time. Like before, first we write the main notes in order from “F” to the “F” octave. Then we compare it with the major scale pattern.</p>\n<p>In this comparison, we see that the notes “F,” “G,” “A,” “C,” “D,” “E,” and “F” octave are in their correct positions, and only the note “B” must be lowered by a chromatic half step. Therefore, we do this using the “flat” sign. Finally, the “F major” scale is obtained as follows.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_016_major_scale/webp/sornaz_016_major_scale_05.webp\" alt=\"Note pattern of the F major scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 5- Note pattern of the F major scale[/caption]\n<h2>The System of Connections in the Major Scale</h2>\n<p>Whenever the pattern of the major scale is examined carefully, two identical and equal sections are seen in it.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_016_major_scale/webp/sornaz_016_major_scale_07.webp\" alt=\"Pattern of identical tetrachords in the major scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 6- Pattern of identical tetrachords in the major scale[/caption]\n<p>In the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">science of music theory</a>, each section is called a tetrachord (Tetrachord - dang).</p>\n<p>Now if we compare the “C major” scale with the “F major” scale in terms of tetrachords.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_016_major_scale/webp/sornaz_016_major_scale_09.webp\" alt=\"Comparison of the C major scale and the F major scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 7- Comparison of the C major scale and the F major scale[/caption]\n<p>From this comparison, we find that the first tetrachord of the “C major” scale completely matches the second tetrachord of the “F major” scale. With a simple calculation, one can understand the reason for this matching:</p>\n<p>1) Every major scale consists of two identical tetrachords with the pattern whole step – whole step – half step.</p>\n<p>2) The first tetrachord of any major scale can itself be the second tetrachord of another determined major scale, and vice versa.</p>\n<p>We know that the second tetrachord of the “C major” scale consists of the notes “G, A, B, and C”; this group of notes can be the first tetrachord of which major scale?</p>\n<p>It is clear that the first note of this tetrachord is the starting note of the new scale. That is, the mentioned group of notes belongs to the first tetrachord of the “G major” scale, and therefore the “C major” scale can be compared with the “G major” scale in terms of tetrachord.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_016_major_scale/webp/sornaz_016_major_scale_06.webp\" alt=\"Comparison of the C major scale and the G major scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 8- Comparison of the C major scale and the G major scale[/caption]\n<p>Now from the comparison and examination of the two figures above, we reach the following conclusions:</p>\n<p>1) Whenever we assume the first tetrachord of a given scale as the second tetrachord of another scale and write a first tetrachord for this new scale, after adjusting the intervals, we see that in the new scale, one changing sign (a lowering sign, flat) is found or one of the sharps of the given scale is reduced.</p>\n<p>2) Adding a flat (or reducing a sharp) affects the seventh degree of the given scale, which is actually the fourth degree of the found scale.</p>\n<p>Example: Apart from what was shown in the comparison of the “C major” and “F major” scales, that is, the given scale was “C major” and the found scale was “F major,” we saw that there the flat sign affected the seventh degree of the given scale or the fourth degree of the found scale, that is, the note “B.” This time, to better understand the matter, we show another example.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_016_major_scale/webp/sornaz_016_major_scale_08.webp\" alt=\"Comparison of the B-flat major scale and the F major scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 9- Comparison of the B-flat major scale and the F major scale[/caption]\n<p>The found scale is the “B-flat major” scale, and the added flat affected the seventh degree of the given scale or the fourth degree of the found scale — that is, the note “E.”</p>\n<p>3) If we reverse the order of the work, that is, assume the second tetrachord of a given scale as the first tetrachord of another major scale and write a “second tetrachord” for the new scale, in adjusting the intervals of the major scale pattern, we need an additional changing sign and in fact one “sharp” (or we must reduce one flat from the number of flats of the given scale).</p>\n<p>4) Adding a sharp (or reducing a flat) affects the fourth degree of the given scale (and in fact the seventh degree of the found scale).</p>\n<p>Example: Apart from what can be concluded from the previous figures about this, here we also show another example. The given scale is “D major.”</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_016_major_scale/webp/sornaz_016_major_scale_10.webp\" alt=\"Comparison of the A major scale and the D major scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 10- Comparison of the A major scale and the D major scale[/caption]\n<p>The found scale is “A major”; the added sharp affects the IV degree of the given scale, or the VII degree of the found scale — that is, the note “G.”</p>\n<p>In the same way, the number of changing signs, whether sharp or flat, can be increased. The “C major” scale lacks changing signs, and the tonic note of a scale that has one sharp is placed at the perfect fifth interval relative to the tonic note of the “C major” scale (the note “G,” the tonic of a scale that has one sharp, is at the fifth interval above the note “C”).</p>\n<p>The tonic of the “D major” scale is placed at the perfect fifth interval above the tonic of the “G major” scale, and relative to it, it has one more sharp. In the same way, the “A major” scale is at the fifth interval of the “D major” scale and relative to it, it again has one more sharp.</p>\n<p>The added sharps always raise the fourth degrees of each scale by a chromatic half step, and this note is always on the seventh degree in the new scale. The increase of sharps also goes beyond this limit. (This work continues until all natural notes are raised by a chromatic half step.) At present, only four major scales have been shown. The direction that the scales take toward increasing sharps (or reducing flats) is called the forward (or upward) direction.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_016_major_scale/webp/sornaz_016_major_scale_11.webp\" alt=\"Comparison of the direction of increasing sharp changing signs in the key signatures of major scales\" width=\"1440\" height=\"1440\" /> 11- Comparison of the direction of increasing sharp changing signs in the key signatures of major scales[/caption]\n<p>On the same basis but in the opposite direction, the number of flats is increased. The tonic note of a scale that has one flat (the “F major” scale), relative to the tonic note of the “C major” scale, which has no changing sign, is at a perfect fifth interval lower.</p>\n<p>Also, the tonic note of a scale that has two flats, the “B-flat major” scale, relative to the tonic of the “F major” scale (which has one flat), is also at a perfect fifth interval lower. From this connection, one can infer that the tonic note of a scale that has three flats must be at a perfect fifth interval lower than the tonic of the “B-flat major” scale (a scale with two flats). The connection of these four scales is seen in figure 12.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_016_major_scale/webp/sornaz_016_major_scale_12.webp\" alt=\"Comparison of the direction of increasing flat changing signs in the key signatures of major scales\" width=\"1440\" height=\"1440\" /> 12- Comparison of the direction of increasing flat changing signs in the key signatures of major scales[/caption]\n<p><strong>The direction that the scales travel toward increasing flats or reducing sharps is called the backward direction.</strong> Scales that differ by only one changing sign are called neighboring scales. At the end of the discussion “Changing signs of a musical interval” in the article “What does a musical interval mean? How do we calculate the interval between 2 notes?,” it was said that “changing signs” are used in two situations:</p>\n<p>“The first situation is that they are placed in a special order and for a specific purpose at the beginning of each musical <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">piece</a>, between the clef and the time signature.”</p>\n<p>Now, knowing these matters, we can understand what the concept of “a special order and for a specific purpose” means.</p>\n<p>It should be known that every musical piece, regardless of more or less modern pieces, is written in a certain scale. In musical pieces, the changing signs belonging to the pattern related to it are usually indicated at the beginning of each piece (and at the beginning of each staff line), between the clef and the time signature.</p>\n<p>In the explanation before figure 11, it was said that “the increase of sharps also goes beyond this limit.” Now let’s see how far the number of sharps can be increased. Increasing this number has no limit in theory and can go beyond the number of notes in the scale, but in practice, the number of sharps usually does not exceed seven, and scales that have more than seven sharps, or even those whose number of sharps is seven or close to seven, are written in “<a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%85%D8%AA%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%AF%D9%81_(%D9%85%D9%88%D8%B3%DB%8C%D9%82%DB%8C)\">enharmonic</a>” form. The above statements about flats are also true.</p>\n<p>We show the sequence of sharp scales up to seven sharps as follows.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_016_major_scale/webp/sornaz_016_major_scale_13.webp\" alt=\"Sequence of sharp major scales and their key signatures\" width=\"1440\" height=\"1440\" /> 13- Sequence of sharp major scales and their key signatures[/caption]\n<h2>The Relationship Between Sharps and the Starting Note of the Major Scale</h2>\n<p>1) As seen in figure 13 (and was mentioned earlier), in every major scale, the last sharp raises the seventh degree note by a chromatic half step (this note is shown as a hollow note on the staves in figure 13). The note seventh is placed on the fourth degree in one lower staff without a sign. For example, we consider staff 2.</p>\n<p>The seventh note of this major scale is the note “C,” which, with the help of the sharp sign, has been raised a chromatic half step to adapt to the major scale pattern. The note “C” in the lower staff (staff 1, “G major” scale) is on the fourth degree.</p>\n<p>2) Since the starting note of each scale is placed at the perfect fifth interval relative to the starting note of the scale lower than itself, therefore all degrees of the scale, each relative to the same degree note of the lower scale, are at the same interval, that is, the perfect fifth, and relative to the same degree note of the higher scale, they are at the perfect fifth interval lower. The seventh degrees also maintain the same interval with each other. As a result, the next sharps are also at the perfect fifth interval relative to the previous sharp, one by one.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_016_major_scale/webp/sornaz_016_major_scale_14.webp\" alt=\"Sequence of the appearance of sharp signs in sharp major scales\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 14- Sequence of the appearance of sharp signs in sharp major scales[/caption]\n<p>3) The eighth degree is placed at a diatonic half-step interval from the last sharp, and this degree is the namesake of the tonic and starting note of the major scale. Therefore, after knowing the number and order of the sharps, one can go up a diatonic half step (or a minor second interval) from the last sharp and reach the tonic note of the scale.</p>\n<p>Figure 15 shows the sequence of major scales toward increasing flats. In this figure, the relationship mentioned in figure 13 is extended among all scales (flat ones) until it reaches seven flats in the lowest staff (staff number 7). On the right side of figure 15, the sequence of increasing flats on the staff lines (from top to bottom) is shown.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_016_major_scale/webp/sornaz_016_major_scale_15.webp\" alt=\"Sequence of flat major scales and their key signatures\" width=\"1440\" height=\"1440\" /> 15- Sequence of flat major scales and their key signatures[/caption]\n<p>The order of flat signs is exactly the opposite of the order of sharp signs. Sharp major scales also have the same relationship with flat major scales, so that if we move from flat scales in the forward (and upward) direction, that is, in the direction of reducing flat signs, we reach sharp scales, and continuing the path in the same direction, the number of sharps will increase.</p>\n<p>The opposite of this phenomenon is also true.</p>\n<h2>The Relationship Between Flats and the Starting Note of the Major Scale</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li>In each of the scales in figure 15, the last flat always lowers the fourth degree of the scale by a chromatic half step (the note of this degree is shown as a hollow note in each of the scales in figure 15). This degree is placed on the seventh degree in the higher staff without a sign; for example, the fourth degree of staff number 3 (the “E-flat major” scale), that is, the note “A,” which has been lowered by a chromatic half step with the help of the flat sign, is placed naturally (without a sign) on the seventh degree in the higher staff.</li>\n  <li>Since the tonic note of each major scale is placed at the perfect fifth interval above the tonic note in the major scale after it (the lower scale), and at the perfect fifth interval below the tonic note of the scale before it (the higher scale), therefore all notes of the major scale, including the fourth note, are placed at the same ratio (that is, at the perfect fifth interval) higher than the major scale lower than itself; as a result, the flat signs also increase one by one with the same order.</li>\n</ul>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_016_major_scale/webp/sornaz_016_major_scale_16.webp\" alt=\"Sequence of the appearance of flat signs in flat major scales\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 16- Sequence of the appearance of flat signs in flat major scales[/caption]\n<p>3) The tonic note of each major scale matches the penultimate flat. For example, in staff 4 (figure 15, the “A-flat major” scale), the flat signs are in order: “B, E, A, D.” The tonic note is “A-flat.”</p>\n<p>If figures 14 and 16 are compared with each other, it is seen that sharps and flats are added in an opposite order:</p>\n<p>“Last sharp = first flat”</p>\n<p>“First sharp = last flat”</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_016_major_scale/webp/sornaz_016_major_scale_17.webp\" alt=\"Comparison of the sequence of appearance of flats and sharps in major scales\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 17- Comparison of the sequence of appearance of flats and sharps in major scales[/caption]\n<h2>The Sequence of Creating the Series of Sharps and Flats</h2>\n<p>With a little reflection on the C major scale and examining its intervals, we reach a very interesting point:</p>\n<p>C                D                E               F</p>\n<p>D                E                F               G</p>\n<p>E                F                G               A</p>\n<p><strong>F                G                A               B</strong></p>\n<p>G                A                B               C</p>\n<p>A                B                C               D</p>\n<p>B                C                D               E</p>\n<p>When we write a tetrachord from each note in the C major scale, we notice that the interval between the first and fourth notes of all tetrachords is 2.5 steps or a perfect fourth, except for the tetrachord that starts from the note F and ends on the note B.</p>\n<p>In order for the perfect fourth interval to be observed in this tetrachord, we can do two things:</p>\n<p><strong>1) Raise the note F by a half step using the changing sign #.</strong></p>\n<p>Now if we rewrite the tetrachords of the major scale with these changes, we will have:</p>\n<p><strong>C                D                E               #F</strong></p>\n<p>D                E                #F              G</p>\n<p>E                #F               G               A</p>\n<p>F                G                A               B#</p>\n<p>G                A                B               C</p>\n<p>A                B                C               D</p>\n<p>B                C                D               E</p>\n<p>Now if we again consider the intervals of the tetrachords, we notice that this time the tetrachord that starts from the note C and ends on the note F# has not observed the 2.5-step interval.</p>\n<p>This time, like the previous case, in order for the perfect fourth interval to be observed in this tetrachord of the major scale, we raise the starting note of the tetrachord by a half step using the changing sign # and rewrite the new tetrachords.</p>\n<p>C                D                E               #F#</p>\n<p>D                E                #F              G</p>\n<p>E                #F               G               A</p>\n<p>F                G                A               B#</p>\n<p><strong>G                A                B               #C</strong></p>\n<p>A                B                #C              D</p>\n<p>B                #C               D               E</p>\n<p>As you can see, again the tetrachord whose last note is sharpened has an augmented fourth interval, and by raising its starting note by a half step, the augmented fourth interval turns into a perfect fourth interval.</p>\n<p><strong>G                A                B               #C#</strong></p>\n<p>The new tetrachord that after these changes has an augmented fourth interval is:</p>\n<p><strong>D                E                #F              #G</strong></p>\n<p>If we continue the same process, the next tetrachords of the major scale will be as follows.</p>\n<p><strong>A                B                #C              #D</strong></p>\n<p><strong>E                #F               #G              #A</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B                #C               #D              #E</strong></p>\n<p>And finally, if we also turn the interval between the first and last notes of the last tetrachord into a perfect fourth, we will have:</p>\n<p><strong>B                #C               #D              #E#</strong></p>\n<p>Now if we examine the order of changing the signs, we will notice that the changing of the signs of the tetrachords has been done based on the sequence of sharps.</p>\n<p>F#          C#         G#         D#         A#         E#         B#</p>\n<p><strong>2) Lower the note B by a half step using the changing sign b.</strong></p>\n<p>Now if we rewrite the tetrachords of the major scale with these changes, we will have:</p>\n<p>A               <strong>b</strong>B               C               D</p>\n<p><strong>b</strong>B               C                D               E</p>\n<p>C                D                E               F</p>\n<p>D                E                F               G</p>\n<p>E                F                G               A</p>\n<p>F                G                A               <strong>b</strong>B</p>\n<p>G                A                <strong>b</strong>B            C</p>\n<p>Now if we again consider the intervals of the tetrachords, we notice that this time the tetrachord that starts from the note bB and ends on the note E has not observed the 2.5-step interval.</p>\n<p>This time, like the previous case, in order for the perfect fourth interval to be observed in this tetrachord of the major scale, we lower the ending note of the tetrachord by a half step using the changing sign b and rewrite the new tetrachords.</p>\n<p>A               <strong>b</strong>B               C               D</p>\n<p><strong>b</strong>B               C                D               <strong>b</strong>E</p>\n<p>C                D                <strong>b</strong>E             F</p>\n<p>D                <strong>b</strong>E               F               G</p>\n<p><strong>b</strong>E               F                G               A</p>\n<p>F                G                A               <strong>b</strong>B</p>\n<p>G                A                <strong>b</strong>B            C</p>\n<p>As you can see, again the tetrachord whose starting note is flattened has an augmented fourth interval, and by lowering its ending note by a half step, the augmented fourth interval turns into a perfect fourth interval.</p>\n<p><strong>b</strong>A               <strong>b</strong>B               C               D</p>\n<p>The new tetrachord that after these changes has an augmented fourth interval is:</p>\n<p><strong>b</strong>D               <strong>b</strong>E               F               G</p>\n<p>If we continue the same process, the next tetrachords will be as follows.</p>\n<p>G                <strong>b</strong>A               <strong>b</strong>B            C</p>\n<p><strong>b</strong>C               <strong>b</strong>D               <strong>b</strong>E             F</p>\n<p>F                <strong>b</strong>G               <strong>b</strong>A            <strong>b</strong>B</p>\n<p>And finally, if we also turn the interval between the first and last notes of the last tetrachord into a perfect fourth, we will have:</p>\n<p><strong>b</strong>F               <strong>b</strong>G               <strong>b</strong>A            <strong>b</strong>B</p>\n<p>Now if we examine the order of changing the signs, we will notice that the changing of the signs of the tetrachords has been done based on the sequence of flats.</p>\n<p>                 <strong>b</strong>B          <strong>b</strong>E          <strong>b</strong>A          <strong>b</strong>D          <strong>b</strong>G          <strong>b</strong>C          <strong>b</strong>F</p>\n<h2>List of Major Scale Sequences</h2>\n<p>If we write the list of major scales from 7 scales before the base scale (C major scale) to 7 scales after it, we reach interesting points. Now we write this list and then examine the results obtained from it.</p>\n<p><strong>b</strong>C          <strong>b</strong>D          <strong>b</strong>E      <strong>b</strong>F          <strong>b</strong>G          <strong>b</strong>A          <strong>b</strong>B          <strong>b</strong>C</p>\n<p><strong>b</strong>G         <strong>b</strong>A           <strong>b</strong>B      <strong>b</strong>C          <strong>b</strong>D          <strong>b</strong>E          F           <strong>b</strong>G</p>\n<p><strong>b</strong>D          <strong>b</strong>E           F       <strong>b</strong>G         <strong>b</strong>A           <strong>b</strong>B          C           <strong>b</strong>D</p>\n<p><strong>b</strong>A           <strong>b</strong>B           C       <strong>b</strong>D          <strong>b</strong>E          F           G           <strong>b</strong>A</p>\n<p><strong>b</strong>E           F            G       <strong>b</strong>A           <strong>b</strong>B          C           D           <strong>b</strong>E</p>\n<p><strong>b</strong>B           C            D       <strong>b</strong>E          F            G           A           <strong>b</strong>B</p>\n<p>F             G            A        <strong>b</strong>B          C            D           E           F</p>\n<p>C             D            E        F            G            A            B           C</p>\n<p>G             A            B        C            D            E            <strong>#</strong>F          G</p>\n<p>D             E            <strong>#</strong>F      G            A            B            <strong>#</strong>C         D</p>\n<p>A             B            <strong>#</strong>C      D            E            <strong>#</strong>F           <strong>#</strong>G         A</p>\n<p>E             <strong>#</strong>F           <strong>#</strong>G      A            B            <strong>#</strong>C           <strong>#</strong>D         E</p>\n<p>B             <strong>#</strong>C           <strong>#</strong>D      E            <strong>#</strong>F           <strong>#</strong>G           <strong>#</strong>A         B</p>\n<p>F             <strong>#</strong>G           <strong>#</strong>A      B            <strong>#</strong>C           <strong>#</strong>D           <strong>#</strong>E         <strong>#</strong>F#</p>\n<p>C             <strong>#</strong>D           <strong>#</strong>E      <strong>#</strong>F           <strong>#</strong>G           <strong>#</strong>A           <strong>#</strong>B         <strong>#</strong>C#</p>\n<p>The most important results obtained from the list of major scales are as follows:</p>\n<p>1) If we examine the same degrees of consecutive major scales from top to bottom, we notice that these degrees are based on the sequence of sharps, and in the change from the note B to the note F, the notes become a half step higher.</p>\n<p>2) If we examine the same degrees of consecutive major scales from bottom to top, we notice that these degrees are based on the sequence of flats, and in the change from the note F to the note B, the notes become a half step lower.</p>\n<p>3) 2.5 steps lower than the last flat sign is the name of the flat major scale.</p>\n<p>4) 0.5 steps higher than the last sharp sign is the name of the sharp major scale.</p>\n<p>5) The scales <strong>b</strong>C and B are enharmonic or equivalent scales.</p>\n<p>6) The scales <strong>b</strong>G and F# are enharmonic or equivalent scales.</p>\n<p>7) The scales <strong>b</strong>D and C# are enharmonic or equivalent scales.</p>\n<p><strong>Enharmonic scales:</strong> Scales are called enharmonic when all their degrees are enharmonic or equivalent notes (notes that are not the same name but the same in terms of frequency).</p>\n<h2>The Relationship Between Scales with a Chromatic Half Step Difference</h2>\n<p>In figure 13, we saw that the difference between the changing signs in the “C major” scale (the lowest staff) and the “C-sharp major” scale (the highest staff) is seven sharps. The reason for this phenomenon is easily understood: whenever we raise the tonic note of a major scale by a chromatic half step, the other notes, in order to preserve the major scale pattern, are all raised by a chromatic half step.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, in figure 15, we saw that the difference between the changing signs in the “C major” scale and the “C-flat major” scale (respectively the highest and lowest staff) is seven flats. Because whenever the tonic note of the major scale is lowered by a chromatic half step, the other notes must also be lowered by the same ratio in order to preserve the intervals in the major scale pattern. In the chromatic half-step change of the major scale, one of the three following states occurs:</p>\n<p><strong>First state:</strong> The “C major” scale (the scale without signs), in the chromatic half-step change upward, finds seven sharps, and in the same change downward, seven flats.</p>\n<p><strong>Second state:</strong> If we raise a sharp scale by a chromatic half step, a scale will be obtained whose number of sharps is seven more than the sharps of the previous scale. For example, the “D major” scale, which itself has two “sharps” (“F” and “C”), if raised by a chromatic half step, that is, turned into the “D-sharp major” scale, the number of sharps of the new scale will be 9; in this way, the notes “F-sharp” and “C-sharp” turn into the notes “F double-sharp” and “C double-sharp,” and the other notes will only take one sharp.</p>\n<p>If we lower a flat major scale by a chromatic half step, seven flats will be added to the previous flats (the previous natural and signless notes each take one flat, and the previously flattened notes each take one double flat — the previous flat plus one additional flat).</p>\n<p><strong>Third state:</strong> Whenever we raise a flat major scale by a chromatic half step, here too we must add seven sharps to the major scale. But some of the seven sharps (equal to the number of flats that existed in the flat scale) neutralize the flats and themselves disappear, and the new major scale only takes the remaining sharps.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_016_major_scale/webp/sornaz_016_major_scale_18.webp\" alt=\"Flat and sharp changing signs in flat major scales with a chromatic half step difference\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 18- Flat and sharp changing signs in flat major scales with a chromatic half step difference[/caption]\n<p>In figure 18, the selected scale is “A-flat major,” in which the notes “A,” “B,” “D,” and “E” are flat, and the notes “C,” “F,” and “G” are natural. Whereas in example b, the major scale with a chromatic half step difference is “A major,” in which the notes “A,” “B,” “D,” and “E” are natural, and the notes “C,” “F,” and “G” are sharp. In other words, every note that was flat in the previous major scale is natural in the new major scale, and all the previous natural notes have become sharp in the new scale.</p>\n<p>Now if we lower a sharp major scale by a chromatic half step, we must add seven flats to the major scale, some of which (equal to the sharps that existed in the previous sharp scale) are spent neutralizing the previous sharps and themselves disappear, and the new scale only takes the remaining flats.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_016_major_scale/webp/sornaz_016_major_scale_19.webp\" alt=\"Flat and sharp changing signs in sharp major scales and a scale with a chromatic half step difference\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 19- Flat and sharp changing signs in sharp major scales and a scale with a chromatic half step difference[/caption]\n<p>With a simple count, one can reach this conclusion that:</p>\n<p><strong>The number of sharps and flats of any two scales that are in the third state and have a chromatic half step difference relative to each other is the number 7.</strong></p>\n\"This article is taken from the book <strong>Fundamental Theory of Music by <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%BE%D8%B1%D9%88%DB%8C%D8%B2_%D9%85%D9%86%D8%B5%D9%88%D8%B1%DB%8C\">Parviz Mansouri</a></strong>.\"\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding major scales or the key signatures of major scales, please share it in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this educational content with the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>.</p>', '2023-05-27 05:27:28', 0, '2024-08-30 19:19:51', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1474, 'posts', 17, 'en', NULL, 'Minor scale, key signatures and recognizing types of scales, Differences between 3 different types of minor scales', NULL, 'This article explores the minor scale in music and presents it as derived from the major scale. The interval pattern of the minor scale (whole, half, whole, whole, half, whole, whole) differs from the major scale, creating a distinct emotional character.\n\nIt compares minor and major scales, showing that while some degrees are the same, the 3rd, 6th, and 7th degrees in the minor scale are lowered by a semitone. The article also explains how intervals influence melodic movement and highlights the importance of the 7th degree (leading tone) in resolving to the tonic.\n\nThree main types of minor scales are introduced:\n\nNatural minor: the basic form\nHarmonic minor: raised 7th degree to strengthen resolution\nMelodic minor: raised 6th and 7th in ascending motion\n\nFinally, the article discusses tonality, key signatures (sharps and flats), tetrachord structures, and relationships between minor scales, including enharmonic equivalents.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we talk about the <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">minor scale</span></strong></span>, its key signatures, and its similarities and differences with the major scale. Continue with us in the following...</p>\n<h2>The Minor Scale</h2>\n<p>Today, among the diatonic scales of Western music, there is another scale that is called the minor scale (in French Mode Mineur, which can be called “small scale” in Persian). The minor scale is in fact a derivative of the major scale.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_017_minor_scale/webp/sornaz_017_minor_scale_02.webp\" alt=\"Similarity of the patterns of the major scale and the minor scale with each other\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 1- Similarity of the patterns of the minor scale and the major scale with each other[/caption]\n<p>Looking at the figure above, if in the row of degrees of the major mode, starting from its VI degree, with the same order of intervals, we start another mode and advance it to the same degree in the next octave, this mode is called the minor mode. In the figure above, we can see that the order of intervals between the degrees of the minor mode is:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><strong>Whole step, half step, whole step, whole step, half step, whole step, whole step</strong></p>\n<p>The pattern of the minor scale is taken from the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%af%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%da%a9%d9%84%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%a7%db%8c%db%8c/\">Aeolian church mode</a>, and in this mode, which is called the “natural minor scale,” like the major scale, there are five whole steps and two half steps, with the difference that their places are not the same as in the major mode. This difference, of course, affects the character of these two scales, the major scale and the minor scale. The difference in the pattern between the two is shown below.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_017_minor_scale/webp/sornaz_017_minor_scale_03.webp\" alt=\"Comparison of the minor scale and the major scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 2- Comparison of the minor scale and the major scale[/caption]\n<p>In order for the comparison between the two minor and major scales to be made more simply, we have placed these two patterns one under the other in the figure above so that their degrees are exactly equal. In this case, we see that in addition to degrees I and of course VIII, degrees IV, II, and V are also aligned; and degrees III, VI, and VII in the minor scale are a chromatic half step lower than these degrees in the major mode.</p>\n<h2>The Effect of the Interval Between Degrees on the Melodic Movement of the Minor and Major Scale</h2>\n<p>As mentioned, the interval between the degrees of the minor and major scales consists of five whole steps and two half steps. The specific order of these intervals — which is not the same in the major scale and the minor scale — not only causes a change in the character of each of the two scales relative to the other scale, but also affects the character of the movement (melodic) of each two notes from the notes of this or that mode. In general, whenever the interval between two consecutive notes is a half step, the movement from one to the other is smoother than when there is a whole step between them. Also, in each scale, each of the degrees has a special personality.</p>\n<p>Some notes of the scale can manifest the characteristics of the scale more than other notes. Therefore, the notes or degrees of the scale must be considered unequal in terms of value and personality. The most important note of the scale is the “tonic,” and after that the “dominant”... and so on.</p>\n<p>From the combination of the two characteristics above, it appears that the strongest movement between the (consecutive) notes of a scale in a musical piece is the movement toward the tonic, especially if this movement starts from the VII degree, because on the one hand, the interval between degrees VII and VIII in major scales is a half step, and on the other hand, the destination of the movement is the “tonic” note (or “octave”) (the reason why the name of the VII degree is called the leading tone is also this).</p>\n<p>It is good to know that the name of this degree in English is “Leading note” and in German “Leitton,” which has a concept equal to a pulling note, “leading,” “guiding,” and the like.</p>\n<p>The movement from degree II to degree I can also be considered a more or less strong movement. The other half-step interval in the major scale is placed between degrees III and IV, and in any case, to some extent, the characteristic of sensitivity can be detected in the movement of each of these two notes to the other note and more from IV to III. But in the minor scale, the movement of each of the degrees to the note before and after it can be shown as follows.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_017_minor_scale/webp/sornaz_017_minor_scale_04.webp\" alt=\"The effect of the interval between degrees on the melodic movement of the minor scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 3- The effect of the interval between degrees on the melodic movement of the minor scale[/caption]\n<p>One of the discussable differences between the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d8%a7%da%98%d9%88%d8%b1/\">major scale</a> and the minor scale is the interval between degrees VII and VIII of the two, which is a half step in the major scale and a whole step in the minor scale. In the minor scale, the existence of a whole-step interval between degrees VII and VIII causes the special character of degree VII (being a leading tone, that is, a strong tendency of its movement toward degree VIII) to be weakened. For this reason, theorists have made artificial changes on degree VII, which we will discuss in the continuation.</p>\n<h2>Tonality</h2>\n<p>Whenever the notes of a minor or major scale are placed from degree I to VIII in order and consecutively or are performed consecutively, this way of performing the consecutive notes is called a <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">scale</a>; and if the notes of the scale are not performed consecutively with the intention of creating a melody, it is said that that melody is in the “tonality” of that minor or major scale. In the figure below, after showing a “G major” scale, a melody in the tonality of “G major” is presented.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_017_minor_scale/webp/sornaz_017_minor_scale_05.webp\" alt=\"Comparison of the G major scale and tonality\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 4- Comparison of the G major scale and tonality[/caption]\n<h2>The Harmonic Minor Scale</h2>\n<p>At the end of the discussion of the “natural minor scale,” it was said that “…the existence of a whole-step interval between degrees VII and VIII causes the special character of degree VII to be weakened. For this reason, theorists have made changes on degree VII…” The purpose of this change, in simple terms, is to make the interval between degrees VII and VIII a half step.</p>\n<p>It is clear that to achieve this goal, we cannot lower degree VIII and pull it toward degree VII, because in this case the interval between degree I and degree VIII (which is an octave interval and has 12 half steps) would be disrupted. For this reason, theorists have raised degree VII by a chromatic half step (read the article <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%a7%d8%b5%d9%84%d9%87-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%d8%a7%db%8c%db%8c/\">“What does a musical interval mean?”</a> to become familiar with the concepts of chromatic and diatonic half step) to turn the interval between degrees VII and VIII into a diatonic half step. The scale that is obtained in this way is called the harmonic minor scale.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_017_minor_scale/webp/sornaz_017_minor_scale_06.webp\" alt=\"A harmonic minor scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 5- A harmonic minor scale[/caption]\n<p>The harmonic minor scale has the following characteristics:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Unlike the diatonic scales we have seen so far, it has three half steps (between degrees II and III, degrees V and VI, degrees VII and VIII).</li>\n  <li>Unlike the diatonic scales we have seen so far, it has an augmented second interval (between degrees VI and VII).</li>\n  <li>Unlike the diatonic modes we have seen so far, it has only three whole-step intervals.</li>\n  <li>Unlike the diatonic modes we have seen so far, it has artificial changing signs. These signs are placed only where necessary, to the left of the seventh degree note of the scale.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The Melodic Minor Scale</h2>\n<p>The augmented second interval between degrees VI and VII in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">music</a> of the past was an unfamiliar interval (this interval is called an “augmented second”), and because in past centuries, writing unfamiliar intervals in melodies was not used and was outside the custom and rules of that period, theorists, in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%84%d9%88%d8%af%db%8c/\">melodies</a> that were composed in minor tonalities, also raised degree VI by a chromatic half step so that the mentioned augmented second interval would turn into a whole-step interval.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_017_minor_scale/webp/sornaz_017_minor_scale_07.webp\" alt=\"A melodic minor scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 6- A melodic minor scale[/caption]\n<p>Now if the pattern obtained above is compared with the pattern of the major scale, we see that there is a difference only on one of their degrees (degree III).</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_017_minor_scale/webp/sornaz_017_minor_scale_08.webp\" alt=\"Difference between the melodic minor scale and the major scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 7- Difference between the melodic minor scale and the major scale[/caption]\n<p>And if, for example, degree III does not appear in a short melody, it cannot be understood in which minor or major scale that melody is. To solve this problem, the melodic minor scale must be corrected according to the following analysis.</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis</strong>: <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%B1%D8%AF%D9%87:%D9%86%D8%B8%D8%B1%DB%8C%D9%87%E2%80%8C%D9%BE%D8%B1%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%B2%D8%A7%D9%86_%D9%85%D9%88%D8%B3%DB%8C%D9%82%DB%8C\">Music theorists</a> say that basically why do we need to change degree VII? Is it not that this degree has been raised by a half step in order to strengthen the leading tone character? So if the melodic process is descending (that is, the melodic movement is from VIII to VII), there is no longer a need to raise degree VII; also, whenever degree VII appears in its natural state in such a process, degree VI can also remain in its natural and unchanged state. Theorists of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">science of music theory</a> conclude from this analytical discussion that degrees VI and VII in the melodic minor scale must be raised by a half step in the ascending melodic process and in the descending process, the melody must return to its natural and unchanged state.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_017_minor_scale/webp/sornaz_017_minor_scale_09.webp\" alt=\"The melodic minor scale in ascending and descending states\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 8- The melodic minor scale in ascending and descending states[/caption]\n<p>It is clear that the three types of the minor scale (natural minor scale, harmonic minor scale, and melodic minor scale) can be set as relative minor scales against each of the major scales.</p>\n<h2>The Sequence of Creating the Series of Sharps and Flats</h2>\n<p>With a little reflection on the A minor scale and examining its intervals, we reach a very interesting point:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">A                B                C               D</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">B                C                D               E</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">C                D                E               F</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">D                E                F               G</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">E                F                G               A</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><strong>F                G                A               B</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">G                A                B               C</p>\n<p>When we write a tetrachord from each note in the A minor scale, we notice that the interval between the first and fourth notes of all tetrachords is 2.5 steps or a perfect fourth, except for the tetrachord that starts from the note F and ends on the note B.</p>\n<p>In order for the perfect fourth interval to be observed in this tetrachord, we can do two things:</p>\n<p><strong>1) Raise the note F by a half step using the changing sign #.</strong></p>\n<p>Now if we rewrite the tetrachords of the minor scale with these changes, we will have:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">A                B                C               D</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">B                C                D               E</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><strong>C                D                E               #F</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">D                E                #F              G</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">E                #F               G               A</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">F                G                A               B<strong>#</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">G                A                B               C</p>\n<p>Now if we again consider the intervals of the tetrachords of the minor scale, we notice that this time the tetrachord that starts from the note C and ends on the note F# has not observed the 2.5-step interval.</p>\n<p>This time, like the previous case, in order for the perfect fourth interval to be observed in this tetrachord of the minor scale, we raise the starting note of the tetrachord by a half step using the changing sign # and rewrite the new tetrachords.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">A                B                #C              D</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">B                #C               D               E</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">C                D                E               #F#</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">D                E                #F              G</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">E                #F               G               A</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">F                G                A               B#</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><strong>G                A                B               #C</strong></p>\n<p>As you can see, again the tetrachord whose last note is sharpened has an augmented fourth interval, and by raising its starting note by a half step, the augmented fourth interval turns into a perfect fourth interval.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><strong>G                A                B               #C#</strong></p>\n<p>The new tetrachord that after these changes has an augmented fourth interval is:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><strong>D                E                #F              #G</strong></p>\n<p>If we continue the same process, the next tetrachords of the minor scale will be as follows.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><strong>A                B                #C              #D</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><strong>E                #F               #G              #A</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><strong>B                #C               #D              #E</strong></p>\n<p>And finally, if we also turn the interval between the first and last notes of the last tetrachord into a perfect fourth, we will have:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><strong>B                #C               #D              #E#</strong></p>\n<p>Now if we examine the order of changing the signs, we will notice that the changing of the signs of the tetrachords has been done based on the sequence of sharps.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">F#          C#         G#         D#         A#         E#         B#</p>\n<p><strong>2) Lower the note B by a half step using the changing sign b.</strong></p>\n<p>Now if we rewrite the tetrachords of the minor scale with these changes, we will have:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">A               <strong>b</strong>B               C               D</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><strong>b</strong>B               C                D               E</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">C                D                E               F</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">D                E                F               G</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">E                F                G               A</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">F                G                A               <strong>b</strong>B</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">G                A                <strong>b</strong>B            C</p>\n<p>Now if we again consider the intervals of the tetrachords, we notice that this time the tetrachord that starts from the note bB and ends on the note E has not observed the 2.5-step interval.</p>\n<p>This time, like the previous case, in order for the perfect fourth interval to be observed in this tetrachord of the minor scale, we lower the ending note of the tetrachord by a half step using the changing sign b and rewrite the new tetrachords.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">A               <strong>b</strong>B               C               D</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><strong>b</strong>B               C                D               <strong>b</strong>E</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">C                D                <strong>b</strong>E             F</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">D                <strong>b</strong>E               F               G</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><strong>b</strong>E               F                G               A</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">F                G                A               <strong>b</strong>B</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">G                A                <strong>b</strong>B            C</p>\n<p>As you can see, again the tetrachord whose starting note is flattened has an augmented fourth interval, and by lowering its ending note by a half step, the augmented fourth interval turns into a perfect fourth interval.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><strong>b</strong>A               <strong>b</strong>B               C               D</p>\n<p>The new tetrachord that after these changes has an augmented fourth interval is:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><strong>b</strong>D               <strong>b</strong>E               F               G</p>\n<p>If we continue the same process, the next tetrachords will be as follows.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">G                <strong>b</strong>A               <strong>b</strong>B            C</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><strong>b</strong>C               <strong>b</strong>D               <strong>b</strong>E             F</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">F                <strong>b</strong>G               <strong>b</strong>A            <strong>b</strong>B</p>\n<p>And finally, if we also turn the interval between the first and last notes of the last tetrachord into a perfect fourth, we will have:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><strong>b</strong>F               <strong>b</strong>G               <strong>b</strong>A            <strong>b</strong>B</p>\n<p>Now if we examine the order of changing the signs, we will notice that the changing of the signs of the tetrachords has been done based on the sequence of flats.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><strong>b</strong>B          <strong>b</strong>E          <strong>b</strong>A          <strong>b</strong>D          <strong>b</strong>G          <strong>b</strong>C          <strong>b</strong>F</p>\n<h2>List of Minor Scale Sequences</h2>\n<p>If we write the list of minor scales from 7 scales before the base scale (A minor scale) to 7 scales after it, we reach interesting points. Now we write this list and then examine the results obtained from it.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><strong>b</strong>A           <strong>b</strong>B            <strong>b</strong>C           <strong>b</strong>D           <strong>b</strong>E           <strong>b</strong>F            <strong>b</strong>G           <strong>b</strong>A</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><strong>b</strong>E           F             <strong>b</strong>G           <strong>b</strong>A           <strong>b</strong>B           <strong>b</strong>C            <strong>b</strong>D           <strong>b</strong>E</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><strong>b</strong>B           C             <strong>b</strong>D           <strong>b</strong>E           F             <strong>b</strong>G            <strong>b</strong>A           <strong>b</strong>B</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">F             G             <strong>b</strong>A           <strong>b</strong>B           C             <strong>b</strong>D            <strong>b</strong>E           F</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">C             D             <strong>b</strong>E           F             G             <strong>b</strong>A            <strong>b</strong>B           C</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">G             A             <strong>b</strong>B           C             D             <strong>b</strong>E            F             G</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">D             E             F             G             A             <strong>b</strong>B            C             D</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">A             B             C             D             E             F              G             A</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">E             <strong>#</strong>F            G             A             B             C              D             E</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">B             <strong>#</strong>C            D             E             <strong>#</strong>F            G              A             B</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">F             <strong>#</strong>G            A             B             <strong>#</strong>C            D              E             <strong>#</strong>F#</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">C             <strong>#</strong>D            E             <strong>#</strong>F            <strong>#</strong>G            A              B             <strong>#</strong>C#</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">G             <strong>#</strong>A            B             <strong>#</strong>C            <strong>#</strong>D            E              <strong>#</strong>F            <strong>#</strong>G#</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">D             <strong>#</strong>E            <strong>#</strong>F            <strong>#</strong>G            <strong>#</strong>A            B              <strong>#</strong>C            <strong>#</strong>D#</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">A             <strong>#</strong>B            <strong>#</strong>C            <strong>#</strong>D            <strong>#</strong>E            <strong>#</strong>F             <strong>#</strong>G            <strong>#</strong>A#</p>\n<p>The most important results obtained from the list of minor scales are as follows:</p>\n<p>1) If we examine the same degrees of consecutive minor scales from top to bottom, we notice that these degrees are based on the sequence of sharps, and in the change from the note B to the note F, the notes become a half step higher.</p>\n<p>2) If we examine the same degrees of consecutive minor scales from bottom to top, we notice that these degrees are based on the sequence of flats, and in the change from the note F to the note B, the notes become a half step lower.</p>\n<p>3) 2 whole steps higher than the last flat sign is the name of the flat minor scale.</p>\n<p>4) 1 whole step lower than the last sharp sign is the name of the sharp minor scale.</p>\n<p>5) The scales <strong>b</strong>A and G# are enharmonic or equivalent scales.</p>\n<p>6) The scales <strong>b</strong>E and D# are enharmonic or equivalent scales.</p>\n<p>7) The scales <strong>b</strong>B and A# are enharmonic or equivalent scales.</p>\n<p>Enharmonic scales: Scales are called enharmonic when all their degrees are enharmonic or equivalent notes (notes that are not the same name but the same in terms of frequency).</p>\n\"This article is taken from the book <strong>Fundamental Theory of Music by <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%BE%D8%B1%D9%88%DB%8C%D8%B2_%D9%85%D9%86%D8%B5%D9%88%D8%B1%DB%8C\">Parviz Mansouri</a></strong>.\"\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding minor scales or the key signatures of minor scales, please share it in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this educational content with the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>.</p>', '2023-05-28 17:31:22', 0, '2024-08-30 19:20:10', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1475, 'posts', 18, 'en', NULL, 'Church modes in the Middle Ages, Introduction to the 8-fold and 12-fold church modes', NULL, 'This article explores church modes and their origins in ancient Greek music. It explains that early Christian churches lacked their own musical system and therefore adopted elements from Jewish and Greek musical traditions. Greek music, with its theoretical and mathematical foundations, became the basis for church music theory.\n\nIn ancient Greece, music was structured around tetrachords (groups of four notes), and different combinations of these created various modes. These Greek modes were later adapted by the church, forming the medieval church modes with some modifications.\n\nInitially, there were 8 church modes, each defined by a final note (Finalis) and an important secondary tone called the dominant. These modes were divided into authentic and plagal types, differing mainly in their range. In the 8th century, 4 additional modes were introduced, increasing the total to 12. Among them, the Ionian and Aeolian modes eventually evolved into the modern major and minor scales.\n\nThe article also discusses the concept of the leading tone and how, in some modes, the seventh degree was altered to strengthen melodic resolution. Over time, the modal system gradually declined and was replaced by the major-minor tonal system, although some modes remained in use until the time of composers like Bach.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">church modes</span></strong> and discuss their origin, that is, the modes of ancient Greece. Continue with us in this article...</p>\n<h2>Music in Ancient Greece</h2>\n<p>The Christian Church in the first and second centuries AD had no special musical culture of its own, and since it needed to give color and fragrance to its religious propaganda with the help of fine arts, it inevitably drew from cultures that it had previously called “the art and culture of pagans.” The art and culture of the <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%DB%8C%D9%87%D9%88%D8%AF%DB%8C%D8%A7%D9%86#:~:text=%DB%8C%D9%87%D9%88%D8%AF%DB%8C%D8%A7%D9%86%20(%D8%A8%D9%87%20%D8%B9%D8%A8%D8%B1%DB%8C%3A%20%D7%99%D7%94%D7%95%D7%93%D7%99%D7%9D%20),%D8%AA%D8%A7%20%D8%B9%D8%AF%D9%85%20%D8%B9%D9%85%D9%84%D8%8C%20%D9%85%D8%AA%D8%BA%DB%8C%D8%B1%20%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA.\">Jewish people</a> and the art and culture of <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%DB%8C%D9%88%D9%86%D8%A7%D9%86_%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%A7%D9%86#:~:text=%DB%8C%D9%88%D9%86%D8%A7%D9%86%D9%90%20%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%A7%D9%86%20%D8%A8%D9%87%20%D8%AA%D9%85%D8%AF%D9%86%DB%8C%20%DA%AF%D9%81%D8%AA%D9%87,%D9%88%20%D8%A7%D9%85%D9%BE%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%AA%D9%88%D8%B1%DB%8C%20%D8%B1%D9%88%D9%85%20%D8%B4%D8%B1%D9%82%DB%8C%20%D8%A8%D9%88%D8%AF.\">ancient Greece</a> provided the basic foundation for the culture of the Church.</p>\n<p>Jewish music more or less formed the entire religious and official chants of the Church, and ancient Greek music, which had complex mathematical and theoretical foundations, provided the basis for the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">music theory</a> of the Church. It is not bad to take a quick look here at a small part of the music theory of ancient Greece.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><em>“The main core of Gregorian music, which formed the majority of the religious and official musical literature of the Middle Ages, is Jewish rather than Greek before it.</em></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><em>A. Idelsohn proves that the melodies that are still sung today by Jews in lands outside the realm of Christianity (southern Arabia and Iran) have great similarity with the official solo singing of priests in the </em><em>“</em><em>Roman Catholic”</em> <em>Church.” </em></p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: left;\"><em>Quoted from the book</em><em> - Harvard Dictionary of Music</em><em>, section</em><em> Gregorian Chant.</em></p>\n<p>The theoretical foundation of the melodies of ancient Greek music was based on various descending modes, and each mode consisted of one to four tetrachords (in Greek Tetrachordon), that is, from four to thirteen notes. The first and fourth notes of most tetrachords were set at a precise perfect fourth interval, and the middle notes, in different modes, had various intervals among themselves.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_018_church_mode/webp/sornaz_018_church_mode_01.webp\" alt=\"Comparison of diatonic, chromatic, and enharmonic systems\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 1- Comparison of diatonic, chromatic, and enharmonic systems[/caption]\n<p>* The sign P is of course specific to <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a>, and since there is no sign to bring the note “F” closer to “E,” this sign was used.</p>\n<p>Whenever two tetrachords came consecutively in a continuous state, the interval between the beginning and ending notes did not reach one octave.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_018_church_mode/webp/sornaz_018_church_mode_02.webp\" alt=\"Continuous tetrachords\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 2- Continuous tetrachords[/caption]\n<p>If there was a “diatonic interval” between two tetrachords — the dependent state — the interval between the beginning and ending notes became one octave.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_018_church_mode/webp/sornaz_018_church_mode_03.webp\" alt=\"Non-continuous or dependent tetrachords\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 3- Non-continuous or dependent tetrachords[/caption]\n<p>The diatonic modes (those that, like figure 3, were composed of two dependent tetrachords) could start from various notes, each of which was given a name.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_018_church_mode/webp/sornaz_018_church_mode_04.webp\" alt=\"Dorian, Lydian, Phrygian, Mixolydian, Hypodorian, Hypophrygian, and Hypolydian modes in ancient Greek music\" width=\"1440\" height=\"1440\" /> 4- Dorian, Lydian, Phrygian, Mixolydian, Hypodorian, Hypophrygian, and Hypolydian modes in ancient Greek music[/caption]\n<p>Sometimes the mode was called <strong>harmonia</strong>. This word in Greek means “tuning” or “being in tune” (the interval between consecutive notes).</p>\n<p>The seven modes in figures 4 and 5 collectively had a range of about two octaves, and if we consider that each musical piece in a certain mode could extend up to two octaves (four tetrachords), therefore it was necessary for the range of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%88%db%8c%da%98%da%af%db%8c-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%b5%d9%88%d8%aa/\">musical sounds</a> (and the range of common instruments) to reach the full extent of this range, that is, four octaves, in order to perform all the modes. But the instruments, which only had the role of accompanying singing, were built with a range in the extent of the human voice (each instrument specific to one section of the human voice); and therefore, neither they nor any of the vocal sections could dominate the entire range of music and all the modes in performance.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, based on the statements of theorists, each mode had its own special character and quality, and the composer of each vocal section had to have the ability to perform all the modes in various occasions. For this reason, theorists, in solving this difficulty, resorted to a specific application of “changing signs,” that is, by chromatically changing one or two notes of this or that mode, they turned it into another mode, and in this way, in a smaller range, they obtained more modes. For example, if the note “F” in the “Hypophrygian” mode was raised a chromatic half step (“F sharp”), the “Lydian” mode was obtained.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_018_church_mode/webp/sornaz_018_church_mode_05.webp\" alt=\"Similarity of the Hypophrygian mode to the Lydian mode\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 5- Similarity of the Hypophrygian mode to the Lydian mode[/caption]\n<p>In this way, if in addition to the note “F,” the note “C” was also sharpened, it became possible to build all the modes in a limited range.</p>\n<p>Later, Christians took these same Greek musical modes as the theoretical basis for their work. The Greek modes became the foundation and basis of the church modes, and in this imitation, some changes also took place and the names of the modes were swapped.</p>\n<h2>Music in the Middle Ages</h2>\n<p>Music in the early centuries of Christianity, from the third century onward, gradually found a place in the Church. In fact, the Church had a dual role in the advancement of music: it both supported it and at the same time placed obstacles in the way of the advancement of art outside the Church. Nevertheless, folk music (and mostly instrumental) continued to live and progress despite its prohibition by the Church. The performers of this type of music were initially wandering minstrels who went from this city to that city and from this castle to that castle and were busy entertaining people.</p>\n<p>Later, when the system of aristocracy took root and stabilized in European lands, non-religious music found stronger supporters. The nobility themselves took to learning <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">music</a> and hired wandering minstrels to perform their works. On the other hand, the Church also made regular efforts toward the advancement of music.</p>\n<p>Over the centuries, it progressed cautiously in theory and practice (the practical field of music, that is, the techniques and methods of performance). Church music was mostly and principally vocal, and the use of instruments in the Church was initially considered forbidden, and it was after a long time that the organ entered the Church and playing it was permitted in official programs.</p>\n<h2>Greek Modes, the Basis of Church Music (Church Modes)</h2>\n<p>Church modes or religious modes formed the basis of the music theory of the Middle Ages. The number of these modes was 8, and every two modes started and ended with one of the notes “D,” “E,” “F,” or “G.”</p>\n<p>Each of the church modes had a range of about one octave. The aforementioned notes in the related modes were called the final note (the “finalis” note), and as was said, in every two modes the finalis note was common, and the distinction between the two was in their sound field and also in their names. In the main church modes (authentic), the sound field was from the finalis note to the upper octave, and in the secondary church modes (plagal), it was from the perfect fourth below the finalis to the perfect fifth above it. The sound range sometimes extended up to one (and even two) degrees higher than the octave, and one note lower than the lowest note of the mode.</p>\n<p>The figure below shows the range and sound field of the two main and secondary modes on the note “D” (in the finalis position).</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_018_church_mode/webp/sornaz_018_church_mode_06.webp\" alt=\"Comparison of the main mode and the secondary mode from the church modes\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 6- Comparison of the main mode and the secondary mode from the church modes[/caption]\n<p>* The notes “C” — in the main mode — and “G” — in the secondary mode — had such value that they were even given the name “subfinalis.”</p>\n<p>The figure below shows the eight modes.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_018_church_mode/webp/sornaz_018_church_mode_07.webp\" alt=\"The eight church modes\" width=\"1440\" height=\"1440\" /> 7- The eight church modes[/caption]\n<p>In each of the modes, in addition to the finalis (the note shown as a whole note), there was another important note that was called the “dominant,” “tenor,” or “recitative” (the note marked with a fermata sign in the figure above). The dominant note in the main modes was always the fifth degree of the mode, except when it fell on the note “B,” in which case it was placed on “C.” In the secondary modes, it was always counted two degrees lower than the dominant note of the related main mode; here too, whenever the calculated dominant fell on the note “B,” it went one degree higher to “C.”</p>\n<p>In the eighth century, the eight-mode system was expanded, and four more modes (two main modes and two secondary modes) were added to the previous modes on the notes “A” and “C,” bringing the number to 12. These two added modes are in fact the models of the common scales of today’s Western music: the mode that starts with the note “C” is the model of the major scale, and the mode that starts with the note “A” is the model of the minor scale.</p>\n<p>The four added modes are shown in the figure below:</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_018_church_mode/webp/sornaz_018_church_mode_08.webp\" alt=\"Church modes added in the eighth century\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 8- Church modes added in the eighth century[/caption]\n<p>The figure below shows a summary of the aforementioned twelve modes.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_018_church_mode/webp/sornaz_018_church_mode_09.webp\" alt=\"The twelve church modes\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 9- The twelve church modes[/caption]\n<p>Similar to the previous examples of church modes, the finalis note is shown as a whole note, the dominant note with a fermata sign, and the range and extent of each mode is also specified with a ladder-shaped sign.</p>\n<h2>Changing Signs in the Main Church Modes</h2>\n<p>As is evident in figures 8 and 9, the modes all have five whole steps and two diatonic half steps. The half-step intervals in some modes are placed between degrees I and II or VII and VIII. In these modes, the leading tone (whether ascending or descending) exists naturally.</p>\n<p>The leading tone can be descending, and this phenomenon comes from those periods when descending scales were also frequently used. For example, the “Phrygian mode,” which is shown in the figure below, is in fact the exact opposite of the pattern of today’s major scale, and the leading tone is placed on its second degree.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_018_church_mode/webp/sornaz_018_church_mode_10.webp\" alt=\"Comparison of today’s major scale and the Phrygian church mode\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 10- Comparison of today’s major scale and the Phrygian church mode[/caption]\n<p>For example, among the church modes, the Lydian mode has a half-step interval from the seventh degree note to the finalis note (in the upper octave), and therefore this mode has an ascending leading tone. In the Phrygian mode, the second degree note to the finalis note has a half-step interval, and therefore it has a descending leading tone.</p>\n<p>But the modes in which the intervals of I and II or VII and VIII are one whole step lack a leading tone (or more precisely, the leading tone characteristic of degrees II and VIII in them is very weak). In the latter modes, composers, in order to strengthen the leading tone, only at the end of the piece artificially raised the seventh degree by a half step. These modes were: “Dorian,” “Mixolydian,” and “Aeolian.”</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_018_church_mode/webp/sornaz_018_church_mode_11.webp\" alt=\"The church modes “Dorian,” “Mixolydian,” and “Aeolian”\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 11- The church modes “Dorian,” “Mixolydian,” and “Aeolian”[/caption]\n<p>From what we have learned in the article “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d8%a7%da%98%d9%88%d8%b1/\">The Major Scale, Different Types of Major Scale, Key Signatures, and Detecting Types of Scales</a>” about the characteristics of the major scale, and the article “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%db%8c%d9%86%d9%88%d8%b1/\">The Minor Scale, Key Signatures and Detecting Types of Scales, Difference Between 3 Different Types of Minor Scale</a>” about the characteristics of the minor scale, and also what we have learned during this article about the characteristics of church modes, we can reach the following points:</p>\n<p>1) In the past, instead of today’s major-minor system, the eight or twelve church modes mentioned existed.</p>\n<p>2) From the end of the Baroque period until today, the church modes have gradually disappeared, and only two of the church modes, “Ionian” and “Aeolian,” have remained with the names “major mode” and “minor mode,” respectively. Apart from these two modes, until the time of Johann Sebastian Bach, that is, until the 18th century, the Phrygian mode still remained and composers used it. The three modes above were like this until Bach’s time.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_018_church_mode/webp/sornaz_018_church_mode_12.webp\" alt=\"Comparison of the Phrygian, Dorian, and Aeolian church modes\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 12- Comparison of the Phrygian, Dorian, and Aeolian church modes[/caption]\n<p>3) With a little attention, it becomes clear that the Phrygian mode, in terms of the order of whole steps and half steps, is the exact opposite of today’s major scale.</p>\n<p>4) Artificially raising the seventh degree in the harmonic minor scale is not a new innovation either and was widely practiced in past times among other church modes as well.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding church modes and ancient Greek music, please share it in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this educational content with the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>.</p>', '2023-06-06 15:51:28', 0, '2024-08-30 19:20:30', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1476, 'posts', 19, 'en', NULL, '4 main characteristics of sound and musical tone', NULL, 'This article examines the characteristics of musical sound along with fundamental concepts of sound physics. It begins by معرفی four main properties of sound: pitch, duration, intensity (loudness), and timbre (tone color). Pitch is مرتبط with frequency, duration refers to how long a sound lasts, intensity indicates its قوة, and timbre distinguishes sounds from different instruments or voices.\n\nThe article then explains the physics of sound. Sound is a mechanical wave that propagates through elastic media such as air. Waves are classified into transverse and longitudinal, with sound being a longitudinal wave. Sound production in instruments involves three elements: a vibrating source, a mechanism that initiates vibration, and a resonator that amplifies the sound.\n\nKey concepts such as oscillatory and periodic motion, amplitude, period, and frequency are also discussed. The relationship between frequency and period (f = 1/T) is highlighted, showing that higher frequency results in higher pitch, while lower frequency produces lower pitch.\n\nFinally, the article معرفی the musical pitch range, which consists of repeating notes across octaves. It also discusses the range of musical instruments, noting that the piano has one of the widest usable ranges. In acoustics, the typical musical range spans from C0 to C8 (approximately 16 to 4096 Hz), covering eight octaves.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article from the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>, we introduce the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">characteristics of musical sound</span></strong>. We examine the origin of these characteristics and, from the perspective of a musician and as far as it is discussed in the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">science of music theory</a>, a musical sound has 4 characteristics: pitch (high and low), duration (time length), intensity (volume), and timbre (tone color or quality). In the continuation of the article, we examine these features:</p>\n<h3>Pitch</h3>\n<p>It is clear that musical sound can be produced at different levels of high and low. For example, we know that the main sound of men is “thicker” and lower than the sound of women or children, and the sound of children is “thinner” and higher than the sound of men. In the santur instrument, the strings become shorter from bottom to top, and relative to each other, they go from lower to higher. In general, shorter (and lighter) strings produce higher musical sound, and longer (and heavier) strings produce lower sound. Also, the longer the sound tube of an instrument like the flute or ney, the lower the sound produced by it.</p>\n<p>The words “high” and “low” in the technical language of music have become commonly used with the terms “upper” and “lower,” respectively.</p>\n<h3>Duration</h3>\n<p>Duration means the “time” that each musical sound continues. It is clear that a melody or music is composed of sounds that usually have different time values, meaning some are shorter and some are longer. For example, if we draw the <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A2%D8%B1%D8%B4%D9%87\">bow</a> for a long time on the string of a violin or kamancheh, the resulting musical sound is much longer than the sound produced by drawing the bow for a shorter time.</p>\n<p>In musical notation, different durations of sounds are shown with specific shapes, which we became familiar with in the article “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%85-%d8%ae%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%b1-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">Rhythm Reading in Music, Familiarity with the Main Rules and Concepts of Timing in Rhythm Reading</a>.”</p>\n<h3>Intensity</h3>\n<p>Musical sounds may be weaker or stronger relative to each other. For example, if we strike the string of a <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%86%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%b3%d9%86%d8%aa%d9%88%d8%b1/\">santur</a> once gently and once with force using the special plectrum, the second musical sound will be stronger than the first sound.</p>\n<p>In musical notation, signs (often in the form of letters) are used to indicate the amount of intensity of the notes, which we will become more familiar with in later articles.</p>\n<h3>Timbre and Tone Color</h3>\n<p>Sounds can also differ from each other in terms of timbre and tone color (sound personality). In fact, it is because of the tone color of the musical sound of each instrument that we distinguish and separate a sound in one instrument from its similar sound in another instrument. The tone color of sound also differs in human voices, and for this reason, we can distinguish the voice of a friend while we do not even see him from the voice of another person.</p>\n<p>In musical <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">notation</a>, there is no sign for determining the tone color of musical sound, and for this reason, most music theory sources have not discussed this. However, in order to assign each melodic part to an instrument in orchestration, we must have sufficient knowledge of the sound color and the difference in the musical sound of the instruments.</p>\n<h2>Physics of Sound</h2>\n<p>Now that we have understood the characteristics of sound and musical sound, in order to better understand these characteristics and how to distinguish them from each other, we need to become familiar with some basic concepts in the science of sound physics and acoustics:</p>\n<h3>Wave</h3>\n<p>A vibration or oscillation that transfers energy without transferring matter is called a “wave.” Surface waves on water, sound and musical and non-musical sound, X-rays, and visible light are examples of waves. Some of these waves, such as surface waves on water and sound, which are only propagated in a material (solid, liquid, and gas) and elastic medium, are called “mechanical waves.” Another category of waves, such as X-rays and light, which are propagated both in a material medium and in vacuum, are called “electromagnetic waves.”</p>\n<p>Electromagnetic waves are generally called “light,” of which visible light is a subset. Electromagnetic waves, in order of increasing frequency, are: radio waves, microwaves, infrared, visible light, ultraviolet, X-rays, and gamma rays.</p>\n<p>An “elastic medium” is a medium in which, if a deformation occurs in it, the restoring forces created between the particles tend to return the medium to equilibrium. Most solids, liquids, and gases are elastic media.</p>\n<p>“Mechanical waves” are waves that are created by the vibration of the parts of a material and elastic medium and require a material and elastic medium for propagation.</p>\n<p>Whenever a particle in an elastic medium leaves equilibrium and begins to oscillate, its oscillation is transferred to the surrounding particles. But the “elastic force” or restoring force that is created between these particles tends to return them to equilibrium. For example, if we change the length of a spring by compressing or stretching its coils, a force is created between the adjacent coils that tends to return the spring to equilibrium (the state of rest and calm). A spring or any other medium that behaves like it is called an elastic medium.</p>\n<p>According to Newton’s third law (Isaac Newton), the English mathematician, physicist, and astronomer of the 17th and 18th centuries, for every action there is an equal and opposite reaction. For this reason, when a deformation occurs in an elastic medium, a restoring force is created that tends to return the medium to its equilibrium state.</p>\n<p>If we submerge a small ball placed on the surface of still water and release it, it returns to the surface of the water. If we slightly bend a metal blade and then release it, it returns to its initial state, and if we block the tip of a syringe filled with air and push its piston inward and then release it, the piston is pushed outward and returns to its initial position. These simple examples show that water, metal, and air are examples of elastic media.</p>\n<p>“Sound” is the result of certain vibrational movements. Every vibrating object in the air is an oscillator or “vibrator” and can be a source of sound production (musical or non-musical sound), such as a string in “string instruments,” the column of air inside a tube in “wind instruments,” skin in “percussion instruments,” and the vibration of the body of “self-sounding instruments.”</p>\n<h4>The three factors in producing sound and musical sound in instruments are:</h4>\n<p>1) The vibrator or vibrating factor: what vibrates and produces sound.</p>\n<p>2) The mechanical factor: what creates the necessary force for vibration, such as striking, hitting, shaking, blowing, scraping, plucking, bowing, stirring, hitting, etc.</p>\n<p>3) The resonator factor: sound amplifier, such as the body of the instrument, and also the amplifier in electric instruments.</p>\n<p>The transmission of sounds and musical sounds from the source to the listener is done through the propagation of sound waves in the air, which is a material and elastic medium. Sound is also propagated in solids and liquids in addition to gases. For example, by placing the ear on a train rail, we can hear the sound of the train moving from several kilometers away.</p>\n<p>A “tuning fork” is a two-pronged metal object that, if struck on one of its prongs, both prongs oscillate with simple harmonic motion.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_3287\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"600\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3287 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/دیاپازون.jpg\" alt=\"Tuning fork\" width=\"600\" height=\"600\" /> Tuning fork is a tool for experiments related to frequency changes[/caption]\n<p>We usually hear musical and non-musical sound through the air. But these simple experiments show that sound is also propagated in solids and liquids.</p>\n<p>Sound is a mechanical wave because it transfers energy without transferring matter and requires a material and elastic medium for propagation.</p>\n<h4><strong>Transverse Wave</strong></h4>\n<p>As mentioned, when a particle in an elastic medium begins to oscillate, it also puts its neighboring particles into vibration. If the direction of the particle vibration is perpendicular to the direction of propagation, the wave is called “transverse.” For example, consider a spring, one end of which is attached to a wall and the other end is continuously moved up and down (in the direction of the perpendicular axis). As a result of this movement, a transverse wave is created that propagates along the spring (in the direction of the horizontal axis).</p>\n<p>Transverse waves are produced in solids and on the surface of liquids. But they are not formed inside liquids and gases.</p>\n<p>The vibration of the string in “string instruments” leads to the propagation of a special transverse wave along the string. This vibration causes the molecules of the surrounding air to oscillate and, as a result, produces sound. But sound or musical sound is not a transverse wave.</p>\n<h4><strong>Longitudinal Wave</strong></h4>\n<p>If the direction of the particle vibration is in the same direction as the direction of wave propagation, the wave is called “longitudinal.” For example, consider a spring, one side of which is attached to a wall and the other end is alternately moved forward and backward (in the direction of the x-axis). As a result of this movement, the coils of the spring are successively brought closer together and moved apart.</p>\n<p>As a result, a longitudinal wave is created that propagates along the spring (in the direction of the x-axis). The longitudinal wave leads to the formation of high-pressure (compressed) and low-pressure (expanded) layers. For this reason, the longitudinal wave is also called a “pressure wave.” Each of the compressed and expanded layers is a wavefront.</p>\n<p>Longitudinal waves are produced in gases, solids, and inside liquids. The vibration of the air column molecules inside the tube of “wind instruments” also leads to the propagation of a special longitudinal wave that results in the production of sound and musical sound.</p>\n<p>In the propagation of sound, air molecules are not transferred; rather, they oscillate around their equilibrium point and lead to the formation of high-pressure and low-pressure air layers that propagate in all directions from the sound source in the form of concentric circles, and their radius increases as they move away from the sound source.</p>\n<p><strong>“Sound or musical sound is a longitudinal wave”</strong>, because the vibration of air molecules is in the same direction as the propagation of sound.</p>\n<p>A wave that has more complete oscillations in one second (has a shorter period) is for a higher sound, and a wave that has fewer complete oscillations in one second (has a longer period) is for a lower sound.</p>\n<h3>Oscillatory and Periodic Motion</h3>\n<p>The rotation of the Earth around the Sun, the rotation of the Moon around the Earth, the heartbeat of a human, the sunrise and sunset, the movement of a simple pendulum, the vibration of a “string” in string instruments, the vibration of the “air column inside a tube” in wind instruments, the vibration of “skin” in percussion instruments, the vibration of the “body” of self-sounding instruments — all are examples of oscillatory or back-and-forth motion.</p>\n<p>A motion in which the oscillator returns to its initial state (the state it had in equilibrium and before vibration) after a certain period of time and begins its motion anew is called “periodic motion.” In other words, periodic motion is a repetitive or cyclical oscillatory motion.</p>\n<h3>Simple Harmonic or Sinusoidal Motion</h3>\n<p>An oscillatory (back-and-forth) motion that is performed on a straight line segment and around a point in its center is called “simple harmonic motion” or “sinusoidal.” Such an oscillator is a simple harmonic oscillator or sinusoidal oscillator, and the wave created as a result of sinusoidal motion is called a “sinusoidal wave.”</p>\n<p>Musical sounds (C, D, E, F, G, A, B) are sounds that have a fixed and constant frequency, and therefore their wave is sinusoidal.</p>\n<h3>Amplitude of Oscillation</h3>\n<p>The greatest distance of the oscillator from the origin of motion (the starting point of motion) or the greatest distance that the oscillating particle finds from the equilibrium point is called the “amplitude of oscillation.”</p>\n<p>The distance that a sinusoidal oscillator travels in one complete oscillation is four times the amplitude of oscillation.</p>\n<p>Regarding sound and musical sound, the greater the amplitude of oscillation, the greater the intensity of the sound, and as a result, that sound is “louder.” In contrast, the smaller the amplitude of oscillation, the lower the intensity of the sound, and as a result, that sound is “softer.”</p>\n<h3>Period</h3>\n<p>The time required for one complete oscillation is called the “period,” which is denoted by the letter “T.” The period of a particle moving on the circumference of a circle is equal to the time it takes for that particle to completely traverse the circumference of the circle once. The greater the speed of the particle’s motion, the shorter the period, and the lower the speed, the longer the period.</p>\n<h3>Frequency</h3>\n<p>The number of complete oscillations of a vibrating object (for example, the strings of an instrument) that occur in one second is called “frequency,” and it is denoted by the letter “f.” In general, the number of times an action is repeated in a unit of time is called frequency.</p>\n<p>Because one complete oscillation occurs in the time “T,” frequency is “the number of periods in one second.” Therefore, the relationship between frequency and period is f = 1/T. With regard to this relationship, it is clear that frequency and period have an inverse relationship, meaning that the longer the period, the lower the frequency, and the shorter the period, the higher the frequency.</p>\n<p>The unit of frequency measurement is 1/s, which is called <strong>Hertz</strong> (Hz) in honor of the German physicist “Heinrich Rudolf Hertz.”</p>\n<p>One hertz is the frequency of an oscillator that performs one complete oscillation in one second.</p>\n<p>One of the characteristics of sounds and musical sounds is “pitch,” which is directly related to frequency. The higher the frequency, the “higher” the sound, and the lower the frequency, the “lower” the sound. In musical terminology, a sound that is higher is called “upper,” and a musical sound that is lower is called “lower.”</p>\n<p>In Iranian musical literature, the terms high and low are respectively synonymous with the terms upper and lower, which are opposite to each other in terms of literal meaning. The reason for this contradiction is related to the structure of the tar and setar instruments, in which the vibration of the lower (bottom) string produces sounds that are written in the higher parts of the staff lines, and the vibration of the upper (bass) string produces sounds that are written in the lower parts of the staff lines.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_3265\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"900\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3265 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/شکل-موج-صوت-هایی-با-فرکانس-و-دوره-تناوب-متفاوت.jpg\" alt=\"Waveform of sounds with different frequency and period\" width=\"900\" height=\"489\" /> Waveform of sounds with different frequency and period[/caption]\n<h2>Musical Sound Range</h2>\n<p>In the science of acoustics, the seven-note row “C – D – E – F – G – A – B” is repeated at eight different levels (from zero to seven) and creates the musical sound range or the musical sound ladder. In such a way that the frequency ratio of each sound to its similar sound in the higher row is 1/2.</p>\n<p>In the science of acoustics, the frequency of the lowest and highest sound of this ladder is considered to be 16 and 4096 cycles per second, respectively.</p>\n<h3>Piano Sound Range</h3>\n<p>Only with large church “organs” can the entire musical sound range be performed. Other instruments provide the performer with part of the musical sound range. That part of the musical sound range that can be performed by the instrument is called the “instrument’s sound range.” Apart from the large church organ, the greatest sound range is performable by the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%86%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d9%be%db%8c%d8%a7%d9%86%d9%88/\">piano instrument</a>.</p>\n<p>The large church organ was titled “king of instruments” in the 17th century.</p>\n<p>In the American method, the seven-note rows in the musical sound range are marked from bottom to top with numbers zero to 7, which are written after the Latin name of the musical sounds and are called octaves zero to seven in terminology. In this way, precise naming of the notes becomes possible. For example, “C4” represents the note “C” in the fourth row or the fourth “C” of the piano from the left, and “F3” represents “F” in the third row or the third “F” of the piano from the left.</p>\n<p>Regarding the notes “A” and “B,” more care must be taken. Because the first and second white keys of the piano from the left (A0 and B0) belong to the zero row. So “A1” and “B1” respectively represent the note “A” in the first row or the second “A” of the piano from the left, and the note “B” in the first row or the second “B” of the piano from the left. The lowest sound of the piano is “A0” and its highest sound is “C8,” which is the only note belonging to the eighth row on the piano.</p>\n<h2>Frequency Range of Musical Sound in Acoustics</h2>\n<p>The lowest and highest musical sound in the science of acoustics (sound science) is called the note “C,” which oscillates at 16 and 4096 cycles per second, respectively. Therefore, the distance from the lowest (C0) to the highest (C8) musical sound is eight perfect eighth intervals or eight octaves. This sound range includes 9 “C” notes, and 8 of the other notes exist in this sound range.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding the article “Main Characteristics of Sound and Musical Sound,” please share it in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this content with the Sornaz Music School website.</p>', '2023-06-11 05:03:08', 0, '2024-08-30 19:20:43', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1477, 'posts', 20, 'en', NULL, 'What is a chord? How did triads and seventh chords come into existence?', NULL, 'This article explores the concept of chords in music, their origins, structure, and different types. It begins by explaining that chords are derived from musical scales (diatonic and chromatic) and are based on the relationships between notes and musical intervals. The role of overtones (harmonics) in shaping and reinforcing chords is also discussed, showing that a basic triad consists of three notes: root, third, and fifth.\n\nThe article then explains how chords are constructed on different درجات of major and minor scales and introduces four main types of triads:\n\nMajor chord (major third + perfect fifth)\nMinor chord (minor third + perfect fifth)\nDiminished chord (minor third + diminished fifth)\nAugmented chord (major third + augmented fifth)\n\nIt also discusses the historical concept of consonance and dissonance, including the theory proposed by Hermann von Helmholtz.\n\nLater sections focus on chord inversions, where the order of notes changes without altering the chord identity (first, second, and—in seventh chords—third inversion). The article also introduces four-note chords (seventh chords) and their structures.\n\nFinally, it highlights how chords can function مشترک across different scales, meaning a single chord can have multiple harmonic roles depending on the musical context.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article from the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>, we introduce <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">chords</span></strong>. We examine the origin of chords, review the types of three-note and four-note chords, and introduce their inversions. Continue with us in this article...</p>\n<h2>The Initial Basis for the Emergence of Chords in Music</h2>\n<p><strong>The Role of Each Degree in the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">Scale</a>:</strong> We know that chromatic scales consist of 13 degrees and diatonic scales of 8 degrees. The difference in the characteristics of these two scales (chromatic and diatonic) is not only due to the difference in the number of their degrees, but also because of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%a7%d8%b5%d9%84%d9%87-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%d8%a7%db%8c%db%8c/\">musical intervals</a> that each degree has with the starting note of the scale and with each of the other degrees.</p>\n<p>Since in the chromatic scale, the interval of each degree with the other notes of the scale follows regular and uniform sizes, none of the degrees of the scale has a distinct personality from the other degrees. But in diatonic scales, the interval of each degree with the sum of the other degrees differs to some extent, and this state gives a special quality to that degree.</p>\n<p>If a composer uses the sounds of a scale (for example, a <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d8%a7%da%98%d9%88%d8%b1/\">major scale</a> or a <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%db%8c%d9%86%d9%88%d8%b1/\">minor scale</a>) to create a <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%84%d9%88%d8%af%db%8c/\">melody</a>, his work is actually like the work of a <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%86%D9%85%D8%A7%DB%8C%D8%B4%D9%86%D8%A7%D9%85%D9%87%E2%80%8C%D9%86%D9%88%DB%8C%D8%B3\">playwright</a> who uses roles in his play. Each of these roles (father, mother, boss, servant, policeman, son, daughter, etc.) finds distinct relationships with other roles during the play (it is through creating these relationships that the playwright conveys the message he wants to say to the audience).</p>\n<p>In the same way, each degree of the scale has a distinct role during the melody, and it is the relationship of these roles that gives a particular meaning to the melody. Just as the role described in the play gains strength by coming to life on stage, with scenery, music, etc., each musical sound, as a degree of the intended scale, gains more power with the help of special factors. One of the most important factors in strengthening each sound in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">music</a> is the actual and practical presence of its “overtones.”</p>\n<p><strong>Overtones of a Sound:</strong> Every sound in nature has countless “overtones.” The overtones of a non-musical sound are much more varied and jumbled than these sounds in a musical sound. The overtones of a musical sound have such order that one can, by knowing their formula, determine them before hearing. For example, if we play the sound “C” on the piano, in addition to the sound we hear, other sounds (with very low intensity that must be heard with an equipped ear) are also produced; the strongest of them is the sound “C” in one higher octave, and then the sound “G” (at the interval of a twelfth).</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_020_chord/webp/sornaz_020_chord_01.webp\" alt=\"Harmonics of a musical note and their intervals with the main note\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 1- Harmonics of a musical note and their intervals with the main note[/caption]\n<p>Regarding the above figure, we need to know a few points:</p>\n<p>1) Overtones are unlimited; the higher they go, the closer they are to each other in terms of their intervals, and their intensity also decreases, so that a trained and sensitive ear may not be able to hear beyond the 5th overtone (and possibly the 6th or 10th).</p>\n<p>2) In the above figure, any other note can be placed instead of the note “C” (note number 1 in the above example). In this case, the overtones of that note have the same intervals with it and with each other as we see in the above example.</p>\n<p>3) The hollow notes in the above figure (“B natural,” “F suri,” and “A koron”) sound “false” (out of tune or mistuned) relative to the sounds of the Western scale (and even sometimes with the corresponding notes in Iranian music). Naturally, if we continue the process of overtones from number 16 onward, the number of “false” sounds increases.</p>\n<p>4) The frequency of the main sound, whatever it is, the 2nd overtone will be twice, the 3rd three times, the 4th four times, the 5th five times, the 6th six times... the main sound. From this rule, the following results can be reached:</p>\n<p>a) The frequency of each note is twice the frequency of its namesake note in one lower octave.</p>\n<p>b) The frequency of the sound of the twelfth interval of each note is three times the frequency of that note. With a simple calculation, one can understand that:</p>\n<p>c) The frequency of each sound is one and a half times the frequency of the sound that is placed at the perfect fifth interval below it. This point can be understood by examining the figure below:</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_020_chord/webp/sornaz_020_chord_02.webp\" alt=\"Intervals of the second and third harmonics of a note\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 2- Intervals of the second and third harmonics of a note[/caption]\n<p>In the above figure, the note “Do” is assumed with frequency N, and from point a, it can be understood that the higher octave note (do) has a frequency of 2N.</p>\n<p>From point b, it can be understood that the note “sol” will have a frequency of 3N, and the note “sol” has a frequency of 3N relative to the main note. In other words, every perfect fifth interval has a frequency ratio of one and a half.</p>\n<p>d) Similarly, by knowing the frequency ratio of the sounds in the previous example and having a little knowledge of mathematics, one can calculate the frequency ratio of any interval.</p>\n<p>e) It was said that the intensity of the higher overtones is very low, and they have such a negligible effect in creating the quality (color) of the main sound that one can, at least at the beginning of the discussion of strengthening musical sounds, ignore any overtone higher than number 5. The five sounds, as they are seen, consist of one main sound (and its octave, the 2nd overtone), the twelfth sound (number 3, which can be simplified by ignoring its compound interval and calling it the perfect fifth interval), and the 5th sound (after simplification, it will be the third interval).</p>\n<p>Therefore, the members of an initial “chord” (which is technically called a triad) consist of one main sound (called the “root of the chord”) and two other sounds at the intervals of a third (the third of the chord) and a fifth (the fifth of the chord).</p>\n<h2>Building a Chord</h2>\n<p>On each of the degrees of the diatonic scale (major and minor scales), one can form a chord by adding notes from the same scale at the interval of a third and fifth from that degree. The point that must be known and observed in chord construction is that the foundation of the third and fifth intervals of each chord must follow the diatonic notes of the scale (because the ear can get used to the pitch levels of the notes and their relationships with each other).</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_020_chord/webp/sornaz_020_chord_03.webp\" alt=\"Triads built on the degrees of the C major and C harmonic minor scales\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 3- Triads built on the degrees of the C major and C harmonic minor scales[/caption]\n<p><strong>Reason</strong></p>\n<p>As mentioned above, the 5th overtone, after simplification, turns into a major third interval, and the 3rd overtone, after simplification, turns into a perfect fifth interval. That is, the 5th and 3rd overtones should naturally have major and perfect foundations, respectively, but in the created chords (examples in figure 3), this is not always the case.</p>\n<p>Because preserving the state of the scale and the character of its degrees is more important than observing the principle of natural connection between sounds, since the lower overtones (possibly up to numbers 6 and 7) all together have pleasant intervals, and among them a minor third interval (overtones numbers 5 and 3) and also a diminished fifth (between overtones numbers 5 and 7) are seen.</p>\n<p>Therefore, considering the habit of the ear and attention to the characters of the sounds in the scale, one can benefit from minor third intervals, diminished fifths, and even augmented fifths (degree III in the harmonic minor scale) in forming chords.</p>\n<h2>Consonant and Dissonant Chords</h2>\n<p>The boundary between the consonance or dissonance of musical intervals has not only always changed throughout the history of Western music, but is also not completely the same among different nations and societies. At the time when Western music was gradually emerging from single-part sounds to become two (or multi-part), musical pieces were written according to the examples below. In the two examples below — as two-part (and religious) pieces — all simultaneous intervals were considered pleasant.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_020_chord/webp/sornaz_020_chord_04.webp\" alt=\"An organum from the 11th and 12th centuries AD\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 4- An organum from the 11th and 12th centuries AD[/caption]\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_020_chord/webp/sornaz_020_chord_05.webp\" alt=\"A French motet from the 11th and 12th centuries AD\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 5- A French motet from the 11th and 12th centuries AD[/caption]\n<p>From these examples, it appears that the pleasant intervals (between simultaneous notes) in the 11th and 12th centuries were unison, fourth, fifth, and octave (figure 4); and also the intervals of seventh, third (figure 5), passing intervals were considered, and to some extent dissonant. In the theory of “multi-part” music of that time (that is, the knowledge that is now learned under the name “theory of harmony”), basically the issue of interval — except in rare cases — and its division into two groups of “consonant” and “dissonant” was not discussed and examined (however, augmented and diminished intervals were considered “dissonant”).</p>\n<p>The composers of that time devoted most of their time and attention to creating “correct and beautiful” melodies, and it was in the way of achieving this goal (and of course observing some other principles) that very difficult and complex rules were established; these rules were so strict and rigid that observing them — according to the taste of that time — caused the pleasantness of multi-part pieces.</p>\n<p>This knowledge is now taught in composition under the name “Old Counterpoint.” In the history of Western music, changes gradually appeared in the grouping of consonant and dissonant intervals. <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%85%D9%88%D8%B3%DB%8C%D9%82%DB%8C_%D8%AF%D8%A7%D9%86%D8%A7%D9%86\">Musicians</a> and theorists until the beginning of the Baroque period had more or less the same theories about the grouping of double intervals, similar to physicists (acousticians), but from the second half of this period onward, severe differences of opinion about the consonance and dissonance of intervals arose between these two groups (musicians and acousticians), which still persist to this day.</p>\n<p>From the last decades of the 19th century, gradually some avant-garde composers each took a separate path in creating their works, and as a result, even among musicians, various theories appeared. Here, as an example, we present two theories, one from physicists and the other from musicians:</p>\n<p>The most important and newest physical theory about the consonance of musical intervals was expressed by <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%87%D8%B1%D9%85%D8%A7%D9%86_%D9%81%D9%88%D9%86_%D9%87%D9%84%D9%85%D9%87%D9%88%D9%84%D8%AA%D8%B2#:~:text=%D9%87%D9%90%D8%B1%D9%85%D8%A7%D9%86%20%D9%84%D9%88%D8%AF%D9%88%DB%8C%DA%AF%20%D9%81%D9%90%D8%B1%D8%AF%DB%8C%D9%86%D8%A7%D9%86%D8%AF%20%D9%81%D9%88%D9%86%20%D9%87%D9%90%D9%84%D9%85%D9%87%D9%88%D9%84%D8%AA%D8%B2,%D8%A7%D8%AB%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%AA%20%D9%82%D8%A7%D9%86%D9%88%D9%86%20%D9%BE%D8%A7%DB%8C%D8%B3%D8%AA%DA%AF%DB%8C%20%D8%A7%D9%86%D8%B1%DA%98%DB%8C%20%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA.\">Hermann Ludwig Ferdinand Helmholtz</a>. His theory is as follows:</p>\n<p><strong>“Those two musical notes have a more consonant interval whose overtones coincide with each other sooner.”</strong></p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_020_chord/webp/sornaz_020_chord_06.webp\" alt=\"Consonance of simultaneous notes in music\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 6- Consonance of simultaneous notes in music[/caption]\n<p>As seen in the above figure, the octave interval is more consonant than the tenth interval because in example “a,” the second harmonic of the first note is equal to the second note, but in example “b,” the fifth harmonic of the first note is equal to the second harmonic of the second note.</p>\n<p>In the middle decades of the 20th century, some avant-garde composers turned to breaking some of the boundaries of music from previous periods in creating their works, and a style emerged that became famous as “modern music.” With the spread of this style, the theoretical foundations of interval became shaky, and the discussion of consonance and dissonance and the boundary between the two lost its value to some extent and broke the walls of past laws.</p>\n<p>But as is clearly seen throughout all periods of transformation of musical style and expression, it was necessary for new boundaries to be created, new laws to emerge, and music theory to be organized in a new way. Regarding interval and its consonance or dissonance, among those who made great efforts was <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%86%D8%B3_%DB%8C%D9%84%DB%8C%D9%86%DA%A9\">Hans Blink</a>, professor of composition at the “Vienna High School of Music.”</p>\n<p>Blink believes that the psychological feeling of an acoustic phenomenon resulting from the simultaneous hearing of two (or more) <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%88%db%8c%da%98%da%af%db%8c-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%b5%d9%88%d8%aa/\">musical sounds</a> should not be considered a quantitative element, but rather “an artistic and purposeful expression.” He first divides the interval into two groups:</p>\n<p>1) The interval between notes of the same name (unison, octave, and multiple octaves)</p>\n<p>2) Other intervals</p>\n<p>Then, in a table, he places the double groups of intervals in boxes I and II, and gradually makes them smaller or larger.</p>\n<p>The letter E in the table is the abbreviation of the word Extreme (extreme, end), representing “the extreme of expectation and eagerness resulting from hearing the related intervals.”</p>\n<p>The letter M in the table means Middle, Mitte (medium, middle state), indicating intervals that are in the middle of “expectation” and “satisfaction.”</p>\n<p>The intervals are gradually made smaller or larger with half-step increases and decreases from the E part to the M part (boxes I and II) and then to the last box. Parallel to this path, two curves (solid and dotted lines) show the characteristics of “eagerness and expectation” and “strangeness,” respectively. Blink places the intervals that are at the same level in terms of these two characteristics under each other.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_020_chord/webp/sornaz_020_chord_07.webp\" alt=\"Blink\'s interval chart\" width=\"1440\" height=\"1440\" /> 7- Blink\'s interval chart[/caption]\n<h2>Types of Chords</h2>\n<p>The chords built in figure 3, from the point of view of the interval between the notes, are divided into two general groups:</p>\n<p><strong>1) Consonant Chords:</strong> To this group of chords, in which the fifth intervals are perfect, we say <strong>“complete chords”</strong>. This group itself is divided into two smaller sections:</p>\n<p>1 - Chords whose third interval is major. <strong>(Major complete chord)</strong></p>\n<p>2 - Chords whose third interval is minor. <strong>(Minor complete chord)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>2) Dissonant Chords:</strong> The fifth interval in these chords is not perfect. These chords are also divided into two sections:</p>\n<p>1 - Chords whose fifth interval is diminished. <strong>(Diminished chords)</strong></p>\n<p>2 - Chords whose fifth interval is augmented. <strong>(Augmented chords)</strong></p>\n<p>Therefore, all chords built on a major scale and a minor scale are of 4 types:</p>\n<p><strong>1) Diminished Chord:</strong></p>\n<p>It is created with minor third and diminished fifth intervals. It is built on degree VII in the major scale and degrees II and VII in the harmonic minor scale.</p>\n<p><strong>2) Minor Complete Chord:</strong></p>\n<p>It is created with minor third and perfect fifth intervals. It is built on degrees II, III, and VI in the major scale and degrees I and IV in the harmonic minor scale (and V in the natural minor scale).</p>\n<p><strong>3) Major Complete Chord:</strong></p>\n<p>It is created with major third and perfect fifth intervals. It is built on degrees I, IV, and V in the major scale and degrees V and VI in the harmonic minor scale.</p>\n<p><strong>4) Augmented Chord:</strong></p>\n<p>It is created with major third and augmented fifth intervals. It is built on degree III in the harmonic minor scale.</p>\n<p>The mentioned intervals are calculated from the root of the chord.</p>\n<h3>Intervals Within the Chord</h3>\n<p>With a little examination, we see that in each chord, there are a total of three intervals:</p>\n<p>1) The third interval (between the root note to the third of the chord)</p>\n<p>2) The third interval (between the third to the fifth of the chord)</p>\n<p>3) The fifth interval (between the root note to the fifth of the chord)</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_020_chord/webp/sornaz_020_chord_08.webp\" alt=\"Triad and its internal intervals\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 8- Triad and its internal intervals[/caption]\n<p>Now we can examine the four types of chords mentioned above from this angle as well:</p>\n<p>1) In diminished chords, both third intervals are minor. (Minor third + minor third = diminished fifth, 3 half steps + 3 half steps = 6 half steps)</p>\n<p>2) In minor complete chords, the first interval is minor and the next is major. (Minor third + major third = perfect fifth, 3 half steps + 4 half steps = 7 half steps)</p>\n<p>3) In major complete chords, the first interval is major and the next is minor. (Major third + minor third = perfect fifth, 4 half steps + 3 half steps = 7 half steps)</p>\n<p>4) In augmented chords, both third intervals are major. (Major third + major third = augmented fifth, 4 half steps + 4 half steps = 8 half steps)</p>\n<p>Now, for more practice, on the degrees of the “D” major scale and its parallel harmonic minor scale, that is, the “D” harmonic minor scale, we form triads.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_020_chord/webp/sornaz_020_chord_09.webp\" alt=\"Triads built on the degrees of the D major and D harmonic minor scales\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 9- Triads built on the degrees of the D major and D harmonic minor scales[/caption]\n<p>From examining figure 9, the same previous results are obtained, that is:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Diminished chords: Built on degree VII of the “D” major scale and degrees II and VII of the “D” harmonic minor scale.</li>\n  <li>Minor complete chords: Built on degrees II, III, and VI of the “D” major scale and degrees I and IV of the “D” harmonic minor scale.</li>\n  <li>Major complete chords: Built on degrees I, IV, and V of the “D” major scale and degrees V and VI of the “D” harmonic minor scale.</li>\n  <li>Augmented chords: Only built on degree III of the “D” harmonic minor scale.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>We repeat the experiment once more. This time on the “G” major scale and the “G” harmonic minor scale.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_020_chord/webp/sornaz_020_chord_10.webp\" alt=\"Triads built on the G major and G harmonic minor scales\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 10- Triads built on the G major and G harmonic minor scales[/caption]\n<p>In figure 10, we again see the same previous achievements. No matter how much we continue the experiment, the same results are always obtained.</p>\n<p>In figure 11, we have presented this achievement in a summarized form.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_020_chord/webp/sornaz_020_chord_11.webp\" alt=\"Comparison of triads on the degrees of the major scale and the harmonic minor scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 11- Comparison of triads on the degrees of the major scale and the harmonic minor scale[/caption]\n<h2>Common Chords</h2>\n<p>From comparing figures 9 and 10, this result is obtained that some chords are common in these four scales. In the “G” major scale and the “G” harmonic minor scale, degree V is the same, and this same chord is the same as degree I of the “D” major scale. The chord of degree I in the “G” major scale and degree IV in the “D” major scale and even degree V in the “C” major scale (figure 3) is the same.</p>\n<p>These similarities also lead us to other results. From examining the chords built in the “C” major, “C” harmonic minor, “G” major, “G” harmonic minor, “D” major, “D” harmonic minor scales and any other scale, one can understand that in every major scale, degrees I, IV, V and also in every minor scale, degrees V and VI are major complete chords. Therefore, if we build a major complete chord on any desired note, this chord can be degree I of a major scale, degree IV of another major scale, and degree V of a third major scale, and also degrees V and VI of two different harmonic minor scales.</p>\n<p>A minor complete chord can be degrees II, III, and VI of three different major scales and degrees I and IV of two different harmonic minor scales.</p>\n<p>A diminished chord can be degree VII of a major scale and degrees II and VII of two different harmonic minor scales.</p>\n<p>Finally, an augmented chord can only be built on degree III of the harmonic minor scale.</p>\n<p>Example: The major complete chord “B♭, D, F” can be degree I of the “B♭” major scale, degree IV of the “F” major scale, degree V of the “E♭” major scale, and also degree V of the “E♭” harmonic minor scale and degree VI of the “D” harmonic minor scale.</p>\n<p>The minor complete chord “F♯, A, C♯” can be degree II of the “E” major scale, degree III of the “D” major scale, degree VI of the “A” major scale, and also degree I of the “F♯” harmonic minor scale and degree IV of the “C♯” harmonic minor scale.</p>\n<p>The diminished chord “C♯, E, G” can be degree VII of the “D” major scale, degree II of the “B” harmonic minor scale, and degree VII of the “D” harmonic minor scale.</p>\n<p>Finally, the augmented chord “D, F♯, A♯” can only be degree III of the “B” harmonic minor scale.</p>\n<h2>Inversions of Chords</h2>\n<p>Just as intervals are inverted, chords are also capable of being inverted. A chord is inverted when the root or the lowest note is placed above one of the other two notes or above both other notes.</p>\n<p>The chords we have seen so far all had 3 notes: root, third, and fifth. Three-note chords, which are better to call triads or three-part, have two types of inversions:</p>\n<p><strong>1) First Inversion:</strong> When the third of the chord is placed lower than its root.</p>\n<p>If in this state we want the interval of the chord notes relative to each other to be in the smallest possible amount, we must lower the root note by only one octave.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_020_chord/webp/sornaz_020_chord_12.webp\" alt=\"Triads built in first inversion on the degrees of the C major and C harmonic minor scales\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 12- Triads built in first inversion on the degrees of the C major and C harmonic minor scales[/caption]\n<p>The internal intervals of the chords in the first inversion, from the lowest note to the other notes, are third and sixth, respectively.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_020_chord/webp/sornaz_020_chord_13.webp\" alt=\"Triad in first inversion and its internal intervals\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 13- Triad in first inversion and its internal intervals[/caption]\n<p><strong>2) Second Inversion:</strong> When the fifth of the chord is placed lower than the other notes. The internal intervals of the second inversion chord (from the lowest note) in this state are fourth and sixth.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_020_chord/webp/sornaz_020_chord_14.webp\" alt=\"Triad in second inversion and its internal intervals\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 14- Triad in second inversion and its internal intervals[/caption]\n<p>The result is that every triad can be written in three positions and with three signs:</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_020_chord/webp/sornaz_020_chord_15.webp\" alt=\"Triads in three positions and the numbers representing each\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 15- Triads in three positions and the numbers representing each[/caption]\n<p><strong>a) Root Position</strong></p>\n<p><strong>b) First Inversion</strong></p>\n<p><strong>c) Second Inversion</strong></p>\n<h2>Four-Note Chords</h2>\n<p>The chords we have seen so far were composed of three notes: root, third, and fifth, in which the intervals between the notes were two third intervals. Now, if we add another note at the interval of a third from the highest note of the chord, the interval of this note to the root will be a seventh. The mentioned chord, as seen in the figure below, is a four-note chord with the numerical sign 7 5 3 according to its intervals from the root note (or the numerical sign 7), and for this reason, it is called a seventh chord.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_020_chord/webp/sornaz_020_chord_16.webp\" alt=\"Four-note chord and its internal intervals\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 16- Four-note chord and its internal intervals[/caption]\n<p>Seventh chords should be counted from the group of dissonant chords.</p>\n<p>It is clear that four-note chords can be built on the degrees of the major or minor scale in the same way as triads.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_020_chord/webp/sornaz_020_chord_17.webp\" alt=\"Four-note chords built on the degrees of the C major and C harmonic minor scales\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 17- Four-note chords built on the degrees of the C major and C harmonic minor scales[/caption]\n<h2>Inversions of Four-Note Chords</h2>\n<p>Each of the sounds of this chord can be placed lower than the other sounds. Therefore, a seventh chord can be in four positions:</p>\n<p><strong>1) Root Position:</strong> When the order of the notes from root to seventh has been without change.</p>\n<p><strong>2) First Inversion:</strong> When the third of the chord is placed as the lowest note of the chord.</p>\n<p><strong>3) Second Inversion:</strong> When the fifth of the chord is placed as the lowest note of the chord.</p>\n<p><strong>4) Third Inversion:</strong> When the seventh of the chord is placed as the lowest note of the chord.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_020_chord/webp/sornaz_020_chord_18.webp\" alt=\"Four-note chords in four positions and the numbers representing each\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 18- Four-note chords in four positions and the numbers representing each[/caption]\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding chords and their types, please share it in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this educational content with the Sornaz Music School.</p>', '2023-06-17 16:15:09', 0, '2024-08-30 19:21:00', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1478, 'posts', 21, 'en', NULL, 'Dastgah-e Shur, the most extensive dastgah in Iranian music (1) | Ruhollah Khaleqi\'s theory | Mayeh-shenasi in Ali-Naqi Vaziri\'s 24-quarter-tone scale', NULL, 'Shur is one of the most fundamental and widely used modal systems in Persian classical music, often referred to as the “mother of modes” because several important vocal forms (such as Abu Ata, Bayat-e Tork, Afshari, and Dashti) are derived from it. This article examines the structure of Shur based on the theory of Ruhollah Khaleghi and analyzes it within the 24-quarter-tone system.\n\nStructurally, the Shur scale has unique interval patterns that distinguish it from Western scales and is considered descending in nature. The fourth degree plays a more significant role than the fifth, and characteristic cadences such as the “Bal-e Kabutar” are commonly used. In some cases, microtonal adjustments (quarter-tone alterations) are applied to create a stronger resolution to the tonic.\n\nThe article also explores modal transposition (maqam analysis), explaining how Shur can be constructed across different tonal centers in both the 12-tone chromatic and 24-quarter-tone systems. It provides rules for identifying key elements such as the tonic, second, and sixth degrees, as well as the formation of key signatures.\n\nShur’s derivatives (related modes) share the same scale but differ in their “important tones” (shahed) and stopping notes (ist), giving each a distinct identity. Modulation in Shur typically occurs to the fourth above.\n\nFrom an auditory and emotional perspective, Shur is expressive, poetic, and introspective. It conveys gentle melancholy, spirituality, and deep emotional nuance, reflecting the essence of Iranian cultural and artistic identity.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>The <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Shur Dastgah</span></strong></span> is known as the most extensive dastgah in Iranian music, from which 4 Avazs (Abu Ata, Bayat-e Tork, Afshari, and Dashti) or, in other words, 5 Avazs (including the Avaz of Kurd Bayat) are derived. For this reason, it is also known as “Umm al-Avaz” or “Mother of Avazs.” In this article from the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz website</a>, we introduce the Shur Dastgah and its structure, and examine the degrees, interval fretting, and tetrachords of the Shur scale.</p>\n<p>In Iranian music, because a group of great masters and experts have not yet gathered together and reached an agreement regarding the examination of its structure, one cannot comment completely accurately, and there have always been differences of opinion among the experts. For this reason, in this series of articles, we examine the different viewpoints presented in the field of understanding modal music and the radif.</p>\n<p>In the first article of the series on familiarity with the Shur Dastgah in Iranian music, we examine the theory of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a>, which is presented in the second volume of the book “A Theoretical View of Music.” It is worth noting that Ruhollah Khaleqi, following his teacher Ali-Naqi Vaziri, has placed the dastgahs and Avazs of Iranian music within the framework of a musical scale (8 consecutive notes with an octave interval).</p>\n<p>Also, considering the theory of the 24-quarter-tone scale or the 24-part scale of Ali-Naqi Vaziri, we find the key signatures of the Shur Dastgah in 24 different modes, introduce its related parts in the end, and present some points regarding modal change and its auditory quality.</p>\n<p>We have also examined and analyzed the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d9%88%d8%b4%d9%87-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b4%d9%88%d8%b1/\">gushehs of the Shur Dastgah</a> in a separate article. Stay with us ...</p>\n<h2>Structure of the Shur Dastgah</h2>\n<p>The Shur Dastgah is the most common and extensive dastgah in the radif of Iranian music, and its scale in the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">theory of Iranian music</a> is defined as follows.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_021_shur/webp/sornaz_021_shur_001.webp\" alt=\"Interval pattern of the Shur Dastgah scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 1- Interval pattern of the Shur Dastgah scale[/caption]\n<p>One of the common modes of ancient Greek music that Europeans mention in their history of music books is the Aeolian mode (Éolien), which was later used by <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%BE%D8%A7%D9%BE_%DA%AF%D8%B1%DB%8C%DA%AF%D9%88%D8%B1%DB%8C_%DB%8C%DA%A9%D9%85#:~:text=%D9%BE%D8%A7%D9%BE%20%DA%AF%D8%B1%DB%8C%DA%AF%D9%88%D8%B1%DB%8C%20%DB%8C%DA%A9%D9%85%20(%D8%A8%D9%87%20%D8%A7%D9%86%DA%AF%D9%84%DB%8C%D8%B3%DB%8C,%D9%85%D9%86%D8%B8%D9%88%D8%B1%20%D8%A8%D9%87%20%D8%A2%D9%86%D8%AC%D8%A7%20%D8%B1%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%86%D9%87%20%D8%B3%D8%A7%D8%AE%D8%AA.\">Pope Gregory the Great</a> from the sixth century AD in European churches as well. This scale, without any changing signs, started from the note A and ended on the note A in the higher octave, and it also exists among the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%af%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%da%a9%d9%84%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%a7%db%8c%db%8c/\">church modes</a>.</p>\n<p>Considering the intervals of the Shur Dastgah of A, we notice that only the note B has the koron changing sign, and the only difference between the Shur Dastgah and the Aeolian mode in ancient Greek music is the note B. From here, one can understand the relationship between ancient Iranian and Greek music, and it is even possible that the Aeolian mode was more similar to the Shur mode than to the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%db%8c%d9%86%d9%88%d8%b1/\">minor scale</a> of Western music.</p>\n<h2>Properties of the Shur Dastgah</h2>\n<p>The properties of the Shur scale are as follows:</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_021_shur/webp/sornaz_021_shur_02.webp\" alt=\"Degrees and interval fretting of the Shur Dastgah in “A”\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 2- Degrees and interval fretting of the Shur Dastgah in “A”[/caption]\n<p>1) The intervals of the Shur Dastgah scale in order are: half-augmented second, half-augmented second, major second, major second, minor second, major second, major second.</p>\n<p>2) The intervals of each degree of the Shur scale relative to the first degree are: half-augmented second, minor third, perfect fourth, perfect fifth, minor sixth, minor seventh, octave.</p>\n<p>3) The tetrachords of the Shur Dastgah scale are not equal; the first tetrachord is the Shur tetrachord and the second tetrachord is the Dashti tetrachord.</p>\n<p>4) The interval between the Shur tetrachords is a major second (one whole tone).</p>\n<p>5) The Shur scale is descending because in the ascending state it has no leading tone (the leading tone is the seventh degree note whose interval with the eighth degree note is a half step or 3/4 tone), whereas when descending, the second degree of the scale has a half-augmented second interval (3/4 tone) with the first degree, which is more similar to a half step. (For example, the note “B koron” in the Shur Dastgah of “A” is more inclined toward the note “A.”)</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_021_shur/webp/sornaz_021_shur_04.webp\" alt=\"Resolution of the second degree on the tonic in the Shur Dastgah of “A”\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 3- Resolution of the second degree on the tonic in the Shur Dastgah of “A”[/caption]\n<p>Of course, it should not be imagined that we have arbitrarily called the Shur Dastgah scale descending; rather, while playing the Avaz of Shur, this matter can be well understood because the importance of the fourth degree of this <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">scale</a> is greater than its fifth degree, and usually the fourth degree of the scale in the ascending state is the fifth degree of the same scale in the descending state. Therefore, in the Shur Dastgah scale, the true dominant is the fourth degree, and a special descending movement that is always used in this Avaz is the movement from the fourth degree (true dominant) to the first degree, which in the radif of Avazs is called the <strong>Bal-e Kabutar</strong> (Pigeon’s Wing).</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_021_shur/webp/sornaz_021_shur_03.webp\" alt=\"The cadence known as Bal-e Kabutar in the Shur Dastgah of “A”\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 4- The cadence known as Bal-e Kabutar in the Shur Dastgah of “A”[/caption]\n<p>6) Because the seventh degree of the Shur Dastgah scale has a major second interval with the octave and cannot become a leading tone and go to the tonic (or, in the terminology of the science of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d9%88%d9%86%db%8c/\">harmony</a>, resolve on the tonic), whenever they want to go from the seventh degree to the tonic, they raise the sixth degree a quarter step so that it becomes closer to the leading tone, and this action makes the resolution of the leading tone on the tonic better and more beautiful. Of course, nature has taught this to the players, and without being aware of this matter, they have performed this arrangement.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_021_shur/webp/sornaz_021_shur_05.webp\" alt=\"Resolution of the seventh degree on the tonic in the Shur Dastgah of “A”\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 5- Resolution of the seventh degree on the tonic in the Shur Dastgah of “A”[/caption]\n<p>So in fact, in the Shur scale, it is possible for both the second degree to resolve on the tonic and the seventh degree, but in the resolution of the seventh degree, the note before it, that is, the sixth degree of the scale, rises a quarter step.</p>\n<h2>Modal Analysis in the Shur Dastgah</h2>\n<p>Until the Qajar era, there were no rules for the modal analysis of Iranian Avazs because due to the lack of skill and need of the players, each Avaz was only played in two or three different modes, and the instrumentalists, through practice and performance, had become familiar with those two or three modes and their frets. For example, the Shur Dastgah was usually played on the tar in the modes of “G,” “D,” and “A,” and on the kamancheh and violin in the modes of “A” and “E.”</p>\n<p>But considering the introduction of the 24-part scale for Iranian music by Ali-Naqi Vaziri, any Avaz can be played in 24 different modes, although not all of these modes are yet common, and we do not have a fixed instrument that has all these quarter tones, and performing the Avazs in all 24 quarter tones is a very difficult task.</p>\n<p>By a fixed instrument, we mean an instrument like the piano that has fixed and unchangeable frets, and if in the future a piano is built that has 24 quarter tones in one octave instead of 12 half steps, it will be better to perform any Avaz in different modes.</p>\n<p>But from a scientific point of view, their rules must be known, and of course, after building suitable instruments or through repetition and practice on common instruments, perhaps any Avaz can be performed in all 24 quarter tones. For this reason, rules must be provided regarding the modal analysis of Iranian dastgahs and Avazs.</p>\n<p>Therefore, to find the different modes of each dastgah or Avaz, we take one note as the starting point of movement and go up from it once with perfect fifth intervals and once with perfect fourth intervals so that the other modes are found.</p>\n<p>In this way, only the modes that are common in the 12 half steps of the chromatic scale can be found, and with fourth and fifth intervals, one can never find the modes that are formed on quarter-tone notes. Now, to find the combined signs of the different modes of the Shur Dastgah, it is better to first find the 12 modes of the chromatic scale with fourth and fifth intervals as usual, and then find the remaining quarter-tone modes in another way.</p>\n<h3>Modal Analysis of the Shur Dastgah in the 12 Chromatic Half Steps</h3>\n<p>In order to find the combined signs of the 12 modes of the chromatic scale, we take the note “A,” which is the tonic of Shur of “A,” as the starting point of movement, and first go up from it with perfect fifth intervals so that the following modes are found:</p>\n<p>A – E – B – F♯ – C♯ – G♯ – D♯ – A♯.</p>\n<p>The combined signs of these modes are as follows:</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_021_shur/webp/sornaz_021_shur_06.webp\" alt=\"Key signatures of the Shur Dastgah in sharp/suri chromatic modes\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 6- Key signatures of the Shur Dastgah in sharp/suri chromatic modes[/caption]\n<p>After these modes are found, we go up from the note “A” with perfect fourth intervals so that the other modes are also found:</p>\n<p>A – D – G – C – F – B♭ – E♭</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_021_shur/webp/sornaz_021_shur_07.webp\" alt=\"Key signatures of the Shur Dastgah in flat/koron chromatic modes\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 7- Key signatures of the Shur Dastgah in flat/koron chromatic modes[/caption]\n<p>Among the above 14 modes, Shur of “E♭” with “D♯” and Shur of “B♭” with “A♯” are equivalent and consonant, and if we remove two of these four modes, we have formed the 12 modes of the Shur Dastgah on the 12 chromatic half steps, which if we write them chromatically half step by half step, it becomes as follows:</p>\n<p>A – B♭ – B – C – C♯ – D – E♭ – E – F – F♯ – G – G♯</p>\n<p>But the rules of modal analysis in these 12 chromatic half steps are as follows:</p>\n<p>First rule) The sequence of korons is like the sequence of flats, and the sequence of suris is like the sequence of sharps.</p>\n<p>Second rule) In each of the above 12 modes, the last sign is either koron (in this case, if there is a sign before the koron, it is flat) or suri (in this case, if there is a sign before the suri, it is sharp).</p>\n<p>Third rule) The last combined sign, which is always koron or suri, is the second degree of the Shur Dastgah, and because the interval between the tonic and the second degree in the Shur Dastgah is a half-augmented second, if we go down a half-augmented second interval from the last combined sign, we find the tonic of Shur.</p>\n<p>By observing the above modes and how their combined signs are, one can understand the correctness of the three mentioned rules.</p>\n<h3>Modal Analysis of the Shur Dastgah in the 24-Part Scale</h3>\n<p>As we said, to find the modes that are themselves formed on one of the quarter-tone notes (koron and suri) specific to <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian radif music</a>, a new way must be found, and for this work, several methods can be used:</p>\n<p>First method) Because with the intervals of the 12-part music (chromatic scale) one can never reach quarter-tone notes, one must move by means of one of the quarter-tone intervals. This interval is better to be half of the perfect fifth interval, that is, the half-augmented third, because since with the perfect fifth interval we could move in 12 modes and finally reach the main mode, with the half-augmented third interval, which is half of the perfect fifth, after 24 movements we can again reach the main mode.</p>\n<p>For example, if we start from one of the seven notes of music such as “C” and go up 12 times with the perfect fifth interval, we find its consonant or equivalent note.</p>\n<p>C – G – D – A – E – B – F♯ – C♯ – G♯ – D♯ – A♯ – E♯ – B♯.</p>\n<p>As can be seen, the thirteenth note, that is, “B♯” is equivalent to the note “C.”</p>\n<p>Now if we start from the note “C” and go up with half-augmented third intervals, after 24 movements and on the twenty-fifth note, we will reach the note equivalent to the first note:</p>\n<p>C – E koron – G – B koron – D – F suri – A – C suri – E – G suri – B – D suri – F♯ – A suri – C♯ – E suri – G♯ – B suri – D♯ – F♯ suri – A♯ – C♯ suri – E♯ – G♯ suri – B♯.</p>\n<p>The way of writing notes with repeated ascending signs (two sharp and suri signs) and the position of these notes is as follows. In this figure, the note “F♯ suri” is shown.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_021_shur/webp/sornaz_021_shur_08.webp\" alt=\"Simple and repeated ascending changing signs\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 8- Simple and repeated ascending changing signs[/caption]\n<p>As can be seen, the twenty-fifth note “B♯” is equivalent to the first note “C.”</p>\n<p>So with this arrangement, one can form one Shur mode on all 24 quarter tones of the 24-part scale, but if we start from the note “C,” we reach modes that have two repeated signs (such as “F♯ suri,” “C♯ suri,” and “G♯ suri,” whose equivalent modes are respectively “G koron,” “D koron,” and “A koron”), whereas their equivalents are not among these twenty-five notes, and as a result, the modes that are necessary to use are not found, and their equivalents, which are more difficult to use, are found.</p>\n<p>In order to solve this problem, we must find a note from which, after going up 24 times with the half-augmented third interval, all the necessary modes are found.</p>\n<p>After searching among the notes, we notice that the notes “D♭,” “A♭,” and “E♭” have this property that after going up 24 times from them with the half-augmented third interval, we do not reach notes that have repeated signs.</p>\n<p>Among the above notes, we choose the note “A♭,” which seems easier in practice, and go up from it with the half-augmented third interval, and after 24 times going up, we reach the note “G♯,” which is equivalent to “A♭.”</p>\n<p>1- A♭                    2- C koron</p>\n<p>3- E♭                   4- G koron</p>\n<p>5- B♭                   6- D koron</p>\n<p>7- F                     8- A koron</p>\n<p>9- C                     10- E koron</p>\n<p>11- G                    12- B koron</p>\n<p>13- D                    14- F suri</p>\n<p>15- A                    16- C suri</p>\n<p>17- E                    18- G suri</p>\n<p>19- B                    20- D suri</p>\n<p>21- F♯                   22- A suri</p>\n<p>23- C♯                   24- E suri</p>\n<p>25- G♯.</p>\n<p>Now we must find a way to obtain the combined signs of the above modes. Previously, we found the combined signs of the 12 chromatic modes. To find the combined signs of the modes that are on quarter-tone notes, we must act as follows.</p>\n<p>1) We write the Shur Dastgah of “A,” which only has one combined sign (B koron), then to find the signs of Shur of “A♭,” we lower all degrees of Shur of “A” by a half step. As a result, all notes become flat except for the note “B,” which because it was koron, turns into “B♭ koron,” and the Shur scale of “A♭” becomes as follows:</p>\n<p>A♭ – B♭ koron – C♭ – D♭ – E♭ – F♭ – G♭ – A♭.</p>\n<p>The way of writing notes with repeated descending signs (two flat and koron signs) and the position of these notes is as follows. In this figure, the note “B♭ koron” is shown.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_021_shur/webp/sornaz_021_shur_09.webp\" alt=\"Simple and repeated descending changing signs\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 9- Simple and repeated descending changing signs[/caption]\n<p>2) Because the signs of this scale are composed of flat and koron, both of which follow the sequence of flats, we can write the combined signs of the Shur scale of “A♭” as follows:</p>\n<p>B♭ koron – E♭ – A♭ – D♭ – G♭ – C♭ – F♭.</p>\n<p>Now if we examine the sequence of combined signs of the Shur modes of “A,” “D,” “G,” “C,” “F,” “B♭,” and “E♭,” we notice that in Shur of “A” there is only the combined sign “B koron,” and in the other modes, in each one a flat is added and the koron is placed at the end until in Shur of “E♭,” whose combined signs are six flats and one koron.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_021_shur/webp/sornaz_021_shur_10.webp\" alt=\"Key signature of the Shur Dastgah of “E♭”\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 10- Key signature of the Shur Dastgah of “E♭”[/caption]\n<p>So in this way, each time the Shur mode goes up a perfect fourth interval, and as a result, the combined sign that is placed at the end (that is, the koron-ed note) also ascends a perfect fourth interval. Now, because in Shur of “E♭” the note “F” is the last koron, in Shur of “A♭” the last koron must be “B,” but because “B” itself is flat, it becomes “B♭ koron,” and the combined signs of Shur of “A♭” consist of one “B♭ koron” (B) and six flats (E♭ – A♭ – D♭ – G♭ – C♭ – F♭).</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_021_shur/webp/sornaz_021_shur_11.webp\" alt=\"Key signature of the Shur Dastgah of “A♭”\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 11- Key signature of the Shur Dastgah of “A♭”[/caption]\n<p>3) From the Shur of “A♭,” whose combined signs we have in hand, we must find the combined signs of Shur of “C koron,” which is the second dastgah of the 24 mentioned dastgahs and is a half-augmented third interval higher than it, but this work will be difficult for people who do not yet have complete familiarity with Iranian modes, and it is better to write Shur of “C,” whose combined signs we know, and then lower each note of its scale by a quarter step so that the signs of Shur of “C koron” are found as follows:</p>\n<p>Because the last combined sign in the Shur modes that we have had so far is the second degree of the Shur scale, so if we have the tonic and ascend a half-augmented second interval from it, the last combined sign is found. Therefore, if the tonic of Shur is the note “C,” its second degree is “D koron” and it is the last combined sign, and before it is also the sequence of flats, so the combined signs of Shur of “C” will be as follows:</p>\n<p>B♭ – E♭ – A♭ – D koron.</p>\n<p>After we wrote Shur of “C” with the above combined signs and lowered each degree of the scale by a quarter step, Shur of “C koron” is found, which has the following combined signs:</p>\n<p>B♭ koron – E♭ koron – A♭ koron – D♭ – G koron – C koron – F koron.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_021_shur/webp/sornaz_021_shur_12.webp\" alt=\"Key signature of the Shur Dastgah of “C koron”\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 12- Key signature of the Shur Dastgah of “C koron”[/caption]\n<p>Now, considering that from Shur of “A♭” we went up with perfect fifth intervals and found the 12 modes, we do this work this time from Shur of “C koron” and go up with perfect fifth intervals so that we find the 12 quarter-tone modes of the Shur Dastgah. After this work, the combined signs of the quarter-tone modes of the Shur Dastgah are obtained as follows.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_021_shur/webp/sornaz_021_shur_13.webp\" alt=\"Key signatures of the Shur Dastgah in quarter-tone modes\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 13- Key signatures of the Shur Dastgah in quarter-tone modes[/caption]\n<p>As can be seen, in the above 13 modes, we started from the note “C koron” and reached its equivalent note “B suri,” so in this way, one Shur mode was formed on all quarter-tone notes. Now, by observing the 13 quarter-tone modes of the Shur Dastgah, the following points can be deduced:</p>\n<p>1- The above 13 modes in the list of 24 modes have even numbers.</p>\n<p>2- If we also place the odd numbers among them, the 24 modes of the Shur Dastgah will be found.</p>\n<p>3- Among the 13 quarter-tone modes, the modes of “C koron” and “B suri” are equivalent to each other, and if one of them (B suri) is removed, in fact, on the 12 quarter-tone notes, we have correctly made the 12 modes that we lacked.</p>\n<p>4- If we start from the first mode (C koron) and observe sequentially, we see that the second degree of each mode ascends a quarter step relative to the previous mode. For example, in the mode of “G koron,” the second degree, that is, “A♭,” has become a quarter step higher than “A♭ koron,” which belongs to Shur of “C koron.”</p>\n<p>5- The fifth degree of each mode has ascended a quarter step relative to the previous mode. For example, in the mode of “G koron,” the note “D,” which in the mode of “C koron” was “flat,” has become “koron.”</p>\n<p>Rules numbers 4 and 5 are also valid in the modes that are formed on the frets of the chromatic scale.</p>\n<p>6- The differences of each mode with the previous mode are in the same two notes that were mentioned in rules numbers 4 and 5. With this arrangement, it can be seen how the modes are created with a certain order and regularity and are placed after each other.</p>\n<p>7- The combined signs in the first 4 modes according to numbers 1, 2, 3, and 4 are composed of flat and koron. In these 4 modes, the sequence of combined signs follows the rule of the sequence of flats.</p>\n<p>8- The combined signs in the last modes (12 and 13) are composed of sharp and suri, and in these two modes, the sequence of combined signs follows the rule of the sequence of sharps.</p>\n<p>9- The combined signs of number 4 are only composed of koron, and number 11 only of suri.</p>\n<p>10- In the other modes (6, 7, 8, 9, and 10), there are two series of combined signs: suris and korons, and each has followed its own sequence, that is, the suris are placed like the sequence of sharps, and the korons like the sequence of flats.</p>\n<p>11- Among the 13 quarter-tone modes of the Shur Dastgah, six of them (1, 2, 3, 4, 12, and 13) have seven combined signs, and the other seven (5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, and 11) have six combined signs.</p>\n<p>12- In the above modes, the number of “B♭ korons” and “flats” gradually decreases, and the number of suris increases.</p>\n<p>After mentioning the above points, now we mention the rules of modal analysis of the above quarter-tone modes of the Shur Dastgah:</p>\n<p><strong>First rule</strong> – In any mode whose combined signs are from one series, either “flat and koron” or “suri and sharp,” the sign that is in the minority, that is, the least and most concise of all, is counted as the second degree (rotonic) of the Shur scale. These modes are numbers (1), (2), (3), (4), (12), and (13) and all the 12 chromatic half-step modes that were mentioned earlier.</p>\n<p><strong>Second rule</strong> – In any mode whose combined signs are formed from two different series, that is, from suri and koron or only from koron, the last combined sign is the sixth degree of the scale, and these modes are numbers (5), (6), (7), (8), (9), and (10).</p>\n<p><strong>Third rule</strong> – In any mode whose combined signs are only suri, the first combined sign is the sixth degree, according to number 11.</p>\n<p><strong>Fourth rule</strong> – When we know the second degree of the Shur scale according to the first rule, we go down a half-augmented second interval from it to find the tonic. When we find the sixth degree of the Shur scale according to the second and third rules, we go down a minor sixth interval from it or go up a major third interval from that degree to find the tonic.</p>\n<h2>Related Parts of the Shur Dastgah</h2>\n<p>As we said, the related parts of the Shur Dastgah are many, but our goal here is to mention those that are counted as a separate Avaz, such as the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%b9%d8%b7%d8%a7/\">Avaz of Abu Ata</a>, the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork, the Avaz of Afshari, and the Avaz of Dashti.</p>\n<p>Although Nava is usually considered an independent dastgah, it can still be counted as part of Shur because, considering the scale or mode of the dastgahs and Avazs, we observe that the scale of the Shur Dastgah and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%86%d9%88%d8%a7/\">Nava</a> is the same.</p>\n<p>In each of these Avazs, in addition to the tonic of the Shur scale, which does not lose its main importance, one or two other notes are also not unimportant. It is from stopping on those same notes that these Avazs are distinguished from each other, and we call these two notes the <strong>witness</strong> and the <strong>cadence</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Witness note</strong> – It is a note that shows itself more than other notes. In the Avaz of Abu Ata, the Avaz of Afshari, and the Nava Dastgah, it is the fourth degree of the Shur Dastgah; in the Avaz of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%aa%d8%b1%da%a9/\">Bayat-e Tork</a>, the third degree; and in the Avaz of Dashti, the fifth degree of the Shur scale has this state and is called the witness note.</p>\n<p><strong>Cadence note</strong> – It is a note on which, after the repeated appearance of the witness note, one can stop, but stopping on this note does not completely show the cadence and ending of the Avaz, and to finish the melodies and complete cadence, one must finally stop on the tonic of the Shur Dastgah. In the Avazs of Abu Ata and Afshari, the second degree, and in the Avaz of Dashti, the third degree of the Shur scale is considered the cadence note. In the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork, the second or seventh degree of the Shur Dastgah may be called the cadence note, but in fact, this Avaz has no cadence note, and it is better to consider the third degree as both witness and cadence because all cadences are on the third degree.</p>\n<p><strong>For familiarity with the santur and kamancheh instruments and knowing the different parts of these instruments and learning practical points for choosing the best santur and kamancheh, study the articles <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%86%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%b3%d9%86%d8%aa%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Comprehensive Guide to Buying Santur</a> and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%86%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%da%a9%d9%85%d8%a7%d9%86%da%86%d9%87/\">Comprehensive Guide to Buying Kamancheh</a>.</strong></p>\n<h2>Modal Change in the Shur Dastgah</h2>\n<p>One of the properties of the Shur Dastgah is that it always, after a short stop in the main mode, goes to the fourth higher mode, and the important part of the secondary melodies of this Avaz is in the new mode, and for returning to the main mode, the fifth degree of the scale, which had changed to go to the new mode, returns to its original state.</p>\n<p>For example, if we are in Shur of A, after a little stop, we go to Shur of D, and this is done by making the note E koron, and if we go too much in the new mode toward the lower and higher registers, the note B also becomes flat, that is, the combined signs of Shur of A turn into Shur of D, but whenever we want to return again to the main Shur, we make the note E natural and by making the note B koron, we enter Shur of A.</p>\n<p>Of course, other changes also occur in the Shur Dastgah, which we examine in the articles related to the related parts of the Shur Dastgah (the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork, the Avaz of Abu Ata, the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d9%81%d8%b4%d8%a7%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Avaz of Afshari</a>, the Avaz of Dashti, and the Nava Dastgah).</p>\n<h2>Auditory Quality of the Shur Dastgah</h2>\n<p>As we said, Shur has been the common dastgah among the people of Iran, and especially its related parts, which are more common among the general public, and even people who do not know <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">music</a>, if they sometimes hum, often sing one of the related parts of the Shur Dastgah.</p>\n<p>The Shur Dastgah has a special charm and attraction and is very poetic and captivating. It tells of inner sorrow and melancholy and sometimes with an effective lament and a dignity covered with modesty and shyness, it advises the listeners and consoles them.</p>\n<p>This Avaz is very suitable for expressing inner feelings such as love and affection, emotion and compassion, and feelings of this kind. The laments of the Avaz of Shur are completely natural, and its sorrow is not home-destroying and soul-burning like the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%af%d8%b4%d8%aa%db%8c/\">Avaz of Dashti</a> (of course, it should be noted that the Avaz of Dashti is one of the related parts of Shur), but one can liken Shur to an experienced old man who, instead of putting his head on the knee of sorrow and shedding tears of regret in the face of the incompatibilities of the times, with complete willingness practices patience and forbearance and gives the lesson of lack of attachment and non-attachment to the material and transient world.</p>\n<p>The Avazs of the Shur Dastgah are a complete example of our Iranian national feelings and morals. It is as if it completely embodies the mystical and Sufi spirit of the Iranian. A careful listener can understand from hearing the Avazs of Shur the beauty of the landscapes of Iran and the special characteristics of the people of this land. This Avaz has a strange effect in the moonlight and beside a clear and calm stream and in the silence and quiet of nature, which fascinates every Iranian.</p>\n<p>This Avaz is a glimpse of the mysteries of mystical worlds that the taste of the people of the East has been infinitely interested in exploring since long ago. In short, the melody of the Shur Dastgah is dignified and noble and is a manifestation of the detachment of the Iranian soul.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>The above materials are taken from the second volume of the book “A Theoretical View of Music” by Ruhollah Khaleqi, and not all the cases mentioned in it have necessarily been fully and completely accepted by the Iranian musicians’ community.</p>\n<p>In article number 2 of the series of articles on familiarity with the Shur Dastgah, we examine Farhad Fakhreddini’s theory regarding the structure of the Shur Dastgah in Iranian music.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding the Shur Dastgah, please share it in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this content with the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School website</a>.</p>', '2023-06-24 21:32:12', 0, '2024-08-30 19:14:44', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1479, 'posts', 22, 'en', NULL, 'Avaz-e Abu Ata or Dastan al-Arab | The avaz most similar to Dastgah Shur among the 4 secondary avaz of Shur', NULL, 'Abu Ata (also known as Dastan al-Arab) is one of the main derivatives of the Shur mode in Persian classical music. Although its scale is identical to Shur, it is distinguished by its emphasis on specific notes: the fourth degree serves as the “shahed” (dominant tone), and the second degree as the “ist” (resting tone), while the final resolution still returns to the tonic of Shur.\n\nStructurally, Abu Ata consists of the same two connected tetrachords as Shur, and its distinct identity arises from melodic emphasis rather than differences in scale. One of its most important melodic sections (gusheh) is Hijaz, which has strong roots in Arabic music and is widely used in Quranic recitation and religious chants. In Hijaz, the focal note changes, but the overall scale structure remains unchanged.\n\nThe name “Abu Ata,” meaning “father of Ata,” reflects the mode’s strong resemblance to Arabic musical styles, hence the alternative name “Dastan al-Arab.” This highlights the historical connections between Persian and Arabic musical traditions.\n\nFrom an auditory perspective, Abu Ata is a popular and widely recognized mode among the general public. It is emotionally expressive, gentle, and closely related in character to Shur, with a particularly strong impact in spiritual and religious contexts.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>The <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Avaz of Abu Ata</span></strong></span> or Dastan al-Arab is considered one of the related parts of the Shur Dastgah in the radif of Iranian music, which has more similarity to this dastgah than other related parts of Shur. In this educational post from the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz website</a>, we examine the structure and gushehs of the Avaz of Abu Ata, review the gushehs mentioned for the Avaz of Abu Ata in several different radifs. We bring an analysis of the gushehs of the Avaz of Dastan al-Arab or Abu Ata in the radif of Aqa Hossein Gholi, and at the end, we also state Ruhollah Khaleqi’s opinion regarding the auditory quality of this dastgah.</p>\n<p>In another article, we also examine and analyze the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d9%88%d8%b4%d9%87-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%b9%d8%b7%d8%a7/\">gushehs of the Avaz of Abu Ata</a> in various radifs of Iranian music. Stay with us...</p>\n<h2>Structure of the Avaz of Abu Ata (Dastan al-Arab)</h2>\n<p>In the radif of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a>, the scale of the Avaz of Abu Ata has no difference with the Shur Dastgah, and the only difference is in the repeated stops on the fourth degree (the witness note of Abu Ata) and the second degree (the cadence note of Abu Ata), and with this minor difference, the Avaz of Abu Ata or Dastan al-Arab takes on a special character that is different from Shur. But this difference is not from the point of view of the scale and mode of Abu Ata, but as we said, only from the point of view of the character and quality of that Avaz.</p>\n<p>The notes of the Avaz of Abu Ata scale from the first degree of the Shur scale of “A,” from which the Avaz of Abu Ata of “D” is derived, are as follows.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_022_abuata/webp/sornaz_022_abuata_02.webp\" alt=\"Notes of the Avaz of Abu Ata in “D” from the first degree of the Shur scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 1- Notes of the Avaz of Abu Ata in “D” from the first degree of the Shur scale[/caption]\n<p>Stopping on the second degree of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">Shur scale</a> with its special manner (which cannot be described and must either be known from the Avazs or referred to the radif of Avazs and understood in practice) creates the Avaz of Abu Ata, but from a practical point of view, one cannot imagine a difference between Abu Ata and Shur, especially since the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%af-%d8%af%d8%b1-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">ending and cadence</a> of both occur on the tonic of the Shur Dastgah.</p>\n<p>Abu Ata, like Shur, is composed of two connected Shur and Dashti tetrachords (for more familiarity with the tetrachords of Iranian music, read the article “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Theory of Iranian Music, Examination of the Theory of Tetrachords and the 4 Basic Tetrachords in Iranian Music</a>”). Therefore, the scale of the Avaz of Abu Ata is the same as the Shur scale, with the difference in the cadence and witness notes, which in Shur are the first degree and in Abu Ata the 2nd and 4th degrees.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_022_abuata/webp/sornaz_022_abuata_01.webp\" alt=\"Interval pattern of the Avaz of Abu Ata or Dastan al-Arab scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 2- Interval pattern of the Avaz of Abu Ata or Dastan al-Arab scale[/caption]\n<p>In the figure above, the cadence note is marked in green and the witness note of the Avaz of Abu Ata in red.</p>\n<p>One of the important related parts of Shur is Hijaz, which is a special melody of the Arabs and is also used in our country for reading the Quran and religious supplications, and gradually it has become part of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">Iranian music</a>, and every singer, while singing Abu Ata, also sings Hijaz.</p>\n<p>The witness note of Hijaz is the fifth degree of Shur. For returning from Hijaz to Abu Ata, no change is made in the degrees of the scale, and the cadence of Hijaz is also on the tonic of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b4%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Shur</a>. So Abu Ata and Hijaz have no difference from Shur from a scientific point of view except for the witness note.</p>\n<h2>Naming of the Avaz of Abu Ata</h2>\n<p>The name of the Avaz of Abu Ata is composed of two parts “Abu” and “Ata,” meaning “father of Ata,” which, considering the naming of this Avaz, makes it clear that the Avaz of Abu Ata has the most similarity to Arabic melodies among all dastgahs and Avazs of the radif of Iranian music. Of course, as was previously said in articles related to the history of Iranian music, Arabic and Turkish music have common roots with Iranian music, and in fact, the origin of Arabic and Turkish music is Iranian modal music.</p>\n<p>This can be well understood by examining the music of Arab countries. In the modal music of the countries of Egypt, Syria, and Lebanon, which consists of 52 modes, 30 modes have Iranian names, and similarly, in the music of the country of Iraq, out of 15 common and usual modes, 8 modes have Iranian names.</p>\n<p>So, considering the explanations given above, this result is obtained that when Iranian music reached Arab-speaking countries and the Arabs became familiar with Iranian music, it is likely that the modes of Iranian music that had more similarity to the current Avaz of Abu Ata in the radif were more in accordance with the taste of the Arabs, and by making more or less changes in these modes, they also created their own special melodies.</p>\n<p>Then, when the radif of Iranian music was compiled in its current form, because the gushehs and melodies of this Avaz had a great similarity to Arabic music, the name “Abu Ata” or “Dastan al-Arab” was given to this Avaz. However, this Avaz is mentioned in the “Solhi” compiled radif with the name “Avaz of Saranj.”</p>\n<p>As we know, after the Daramad gusheh, the most important gusheh of the Avaz of Abu Ata is the Hijaz gusheh, which is one of the special melodies and accents of the Arabs for reading the Holy Quran.</p>\n<p>The Avaz of Dastan al-Arab or Abu Ata, as it exists today in the radif, is not observed in the old modes of Iranian music, but from the point of view of intervals, it is similar to the old modes “Janfaza,” “Bustan,” and “Hosseini.”</p>\n<h2>Auditory Quality of the Avaz of Abu Ata (Dastan al-Arab)</h2>\n<p>Abu Ata is a street Avaz that is completely popular among the masses. Despite this, it has a special charm and beauty, and at the same time, its character is not indifferent to Shur. The Hijaz that the Arabs sing has a little difference in character from the Hijaz that is common among us, and on the whole, the Arabs perform this Avaz better, and Iranians sing it in a different style. The sound of the Qurra when reading the Quran, which is always in the Hijaz melody, is very effective and has a special effect on the spirits of <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%85%D8%B3%D9%84%D9%85%D8%A7%D9%86\">Muslims</a>.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding the Avaz of Abu Ata, please share it in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this content with the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School website</a>.</p>', '2023-06-25 16:32:13', 0, '2024-08-30 19:14:29', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1480, 'posts', 23, 'en', NULL, 'Avaz-e Bayat-e Tork or Bayat-e Zand | One of the 4 avaz belonging to Dastgah Shur', NULL, 'Bayat-e Tork (Bayat-e Zand) is one of the four vocal branches of the Shur mode in Persian classical music. Although its scale intervals are identical to Shur, its defining feature is the emphasis on the third degree of the scale as the finalis (shahed), which gives it a distinct melodic identity. Frequent repetition of this note can even make it function like a tonal center.\n\nBayat-e Tork shares strong similarities with the Mahur mode, including several common melodic pieces (gushehs) such as Shekasteh, Delkash, Khosravani, and Fili. Despite these similarities, the overall character and emotional expression differ significantly from Mahur.\n\nStructurally, it is built from two connected tetrachords similar to those in Shur and Dashti, and it typically resolves back to Shur, often through melodic forms like Masnavi. It also includes several rhythmic gushehs that are applicable in other modes.\n\nFrom an auditory perspective, Bayat-e Tork is considered somewhat repetitive and widely popular among the general public. Its heavy reliance on the central note can make it sound monotonous to some listeners, but when performed skillfully, it reveals a unique charm and subtle beauty.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>The <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Avaz of Bayat-e Tork</span></strong></span> or Bayat-e Zand is one of the 4 Avazs related to the Shur Dastgah in the radif of Iranian music, which also has a great similarity to the Mahur Dastgah, and for this reason there are many common gushehs between Mahur and the Avaz of Bayat-e Zand. In this article, we examine the structure of the scale of the Avaz of Abu Ata, talk a little about the common gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork and Mahur, and then review the gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork in several different radifs. At the end, we also talk about the auditory quality of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork from the words of the prominent musician Ruhollah Khaleqi.</p>\n<p>In another article, we also examine and analyze the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d9%88%d8%b4%d9%87-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%aa%d8%b1%da%a9/\">gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork</a> in various radifs of Iranian music. Stay with us...</p>\n<h2>Structure of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork (Bayat-e Zand)</h2>\n<p>If we consider the tonic of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork as the tonic of Shur, there is no difference in the intervals of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">scale</a> of the Shur Dastgah and the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork in the radif of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a>, but its witness note is the third degree of the Shur scale. In Bayat-e Tork, one cannot distinguish between the tonic and the witness because this Avaz always cadences on its own witness, that is, the third degree of the Shur scale, and only at the end it tends toward the tonic of Shur.</p>\n<p>So it is better to consider the witness note as the tonic and start the Bayat-e Tork scale from there, and with this arrangement, the intervals of the scale will change position. In this case, it can be said that Bayat-e Tork is a kind of tonic change of Shur (this difference cannot be considered a modal change or dastgah change because the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b4%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Shur scale</a> and Bayat-e Tork have no difference in terms of changing signs and intervals of degrees.) that at the end returns to the tonic of Shur. Therefore, in this case, the scale of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork will be as follows:</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_023_bayat_tork/webp/sornaz_023_bayat_tork_02.webp\" alt=\"Interval pattern of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork scale from the third degree of the Shur scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 1- Interval pattern of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork scale from the third degree of the Shur scale[/caption]\n<p>Another reason we have considered the witness note of Bayat-e Tork as the tonic is that it is possible that this Avaz may not even return to the tonic of Shur at the end, and because its witness note is repeated so much, it takes on the character of the tonic and will not have much desire to go to the tonic of Shur at the end. Whereas the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%b9%d8%b7%d8%a7/\">Avaz of Abu Ata</a> and Hijaz never pause much on their witness note and from beginning to end at least go to the tonic of Shur several times and return again to their witness, and at the end going to the tonic of Shur is necessary and obligatory, otherwise the accustomed ears will remain in anticipation.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_023_bayat_tork/webp/sornaz_023_bayat_tork_03.webp\" alt=\"Notes of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork in “C” derived from Shur in “A”\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 2- Notes of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork in “C” derived from Shur in “A”[/caption]\n<p>In the figure above, the cadence note is marked in green and the witness note of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork in red.</p>\n<p>With this arrangement, the intervals of the degrees of the Bayat-e Tork scale relative to the tonic are: major second, major third, perfect fourth, perfect fifth, major sixth, half-augmented seventh, and octave.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_023_bayat_tork/webp/sornaz_023_bayat_tork_04.webp\" alt=\"The Avaz of Bayat-e Tork in “C” has a great similarity to the Mahur Dastgah in “F”.\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 3- The Avaz of Bayat-e Tork in “C” has a great similarity to the Mahur Dastgah in “F”.[/caption]\n<p>From observing the intervals of the above scale, it may be imagined that Bayat-e Tork is a <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d8%a7%da%98%d9%88%d8%b1/\">major scale</a> whose seventh degree has fallen a quarter step, and perhaps a European musician, after observing this scale, would imagine it as a major scale whose seventh degree has been mistakenly written wrong.</p>\n<p>But this point is noteworthy that the major scale, which is the same as the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%85%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Mahur Dastgah</a> scale, and the Bayat-e Tork scale are both common in Iranian music, and Iranian singers, when singing Bayat-e Tork, do not mistakenly enter Mahur, and they give each its right at the right time without unknowingly entering from one to the other, and this itself is proof of the skill of our singers who, without paying attention to this issue, never make a mistake.</p>\n<p>This issue itself is one of the reasons for the naturalness of the quarter-tone intervals, and those who think that Iranian singers have become accustomed to singing wrongly are mistaken because if they listen to the Avaz of Mahur and Bayat-e Tork one after the other even without the support of an instrument, they will confess to the correctness of the quarter tones and their naturalness.</p>\n<p>Most importantly, most of the gushehs of Mahur, such as <strong>Shekasteh</strong> and <strong>Delkash</strong>, also exist in the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork, and the skilled singer and player, without mistaking any of them with the other, enters Mahur or Bayat-e Tork from Delkash, which is similar to the Avaz of Afshari, at the right time.</p>\n<p>Delkash is one of the related parts of the Mahur Dastgah, which is in fact a modal change from Mahur to Shur, and previously it was not played in Bayat-e Tork, but now it is sometimes also played in this mode. Shekasteh is also one of the related parts of Mahur and Bayat-e Tork, which is not dissimilar to the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d9%81%d8%b4%d8%a7%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Avaz of Afshari</a>.</p>\n<p>Bayat-e Tork, with the complete similarity it has to Mahur, has an independent scale and also differs greatly from Mahur in terms of character, which becomes completely clear when playing and referring to the radif of Avazs.</p>\n<p>At the end of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork, it usually enters Shur by means of the <strong>Masnavi</strong> melody, which is common in most Avazs but has a special character in each. For this reason and other similarities that were mentioned, the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork is considered one of the related parts of the Shur Dastgah.</p>\n<p>In <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">Iranian music</a>, Bayat-e Tork, like Shur, is composed of two connected Shur and Dashti tetrachords (for more familiarity with the tetrachords of Iranian music, read the article “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Theory of Iranian Music, Examination of the Theory of Tetrachords and the 4 Basic Tetrachords in Iranian Music</a>”). Therefore, the scale of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork or Bayat-e Zand is the same as the Shur scale, with the difference in the witness note, which in Shur is the first degree and in Bayat-e Tork the 3rd degree.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_023_bayat_tork/webp/sornaz_023_bayat_tork_01.webp\" alt=\"Interval pattern of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 4- Interval pattern of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork scale[/caption]\n<p>In the figure above, the cadence note is marked in green and the witness note of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork in red.</p>\n<p>As was said above, the Bayat-e Tork scale is sometimes written from the third degree, but in order to make it clearer that Bayat-e Tork is one of the related parts of the Shur Dastgah, we drew the interval pattern of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork scale in the same form as the Shur scale.</p>\n<p>The prominent Iranian tasnif-singer Aref Qazvini in his divan has called Bayat-e Tork Bayat-e Zand and has written about it as follows:</p>\n<p>Bayat-e Zand has unfortunately become known as Bayat-e Tork, whereas the spirit of the Turk has no knowledge of such an Avaz and melody. It is not far-fetched that some Iran-loving foreigners, at the time of the dominance of the Turks, in order to perhaps please one of the Mongol sultans with this melody, out of flattery called it Bayat-e Tork.</p>\n<p>The Avaz of Bayat-e Tork has a very small difference with the Mahur Dastgah, to the extent that if we consider the Shur scale of “G” and in this scale raise the note “A koron” a quarter step higher and use “A natural” instead of it, we can perform the Mahur scale of “F.” For this reason, there are many common gushehs between the Mahur Dastgah and the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork, such as the Shekasteh gusheh, which is used in Mahur and Bayat-e Tork for compound playing and compound singing and going to the Avaz of Afshari.</p>\n<p>Other common gushehs between the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork and the Mahur Dastgah include the Khosravani, Fili, and Delkash gushehs.</p>\n<p>Among the gushehs existing in the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork, many rhythmic gushehs can be seen that have the ability to be performed in other dastgahs and Avazs of the radif of Iranian music. These gushehs are: Khosravani, Haji Hassani, Baste Negar, Ghatar, Zanguleh, Naghmeh, Mehrabani, and Mehdi Zarabi.</p>\n<h2>Auditory Quality of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork (Bayat-e Zand)</h2>\n<p>This Avaz, like Abu Ata, is a street Avaz that is popular among the common people. Bayat-e Tork is an extremely monotonous Avaz, to the extent that ears accustomed to modulation quickly become tired of hearing it. When each of the Avazs and dastgahs is performed well by a good player and <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%AE%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%86%D9%86%D8%AF%D9%87\">singer</a>, even if they are tiring, the skill and mastery of the player and singer removes this flaw.</p>\n<p>However, if a modal change is made in it, it is not without character and has a special charm that is very different from the Mahur Dastgah.</p>\n<p>What causes a lot of fatigue in this Avaz is the repetition of the witness, which, being heard too much, makes the listener bored and tired of the witness note. In fact, it can be said that this Avaz is a complete example of monotonous Iranian music, which in this respect has no equal among other Avazs, and it is natural that the common people are more familiar with this <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">music</a> and for this reason we have called it the street Avaz.</p>\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork, please share it in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this content with the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School website</a>.</p>', '2023-06-26 20:44:14', 0, '2024-08-30 20:44:57', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1481, 'posts', 24, 'en', NULL, 'Avaz-e Afshari, one of the derivatives of Dastgah Shur in the radif of Iranian music | Similarities of Avaz Afshari to Dastgah Segah and Shur', NULL, 'Afshari is one of the subordinate modes (āvāz) of the Shur system in Persian classical music. Although it belongs to Shur, the presence of a variable note—especially the sixth degree—sometimes makes its interval structure resemble the Segah mode. However, due to its final resolution (cadence) to Shur and overall structural characteristics, it is still classified under Shur.\n\nThe scale of Afshari is often derived from the seventh degree of Shur. Its most important tones are the tonic (1st degree), the resting tone (3rd degree), and the شاهد (dominant-like tone, 5th degree). A defining feature of Afshari is its variable note, which contributes to melodic flexibility and allows connections to other modes like Dashti and Segah.\n\nAfshari has asymmetrical tetrachords, and unlike many systems, the third degree (resting tone) plays a more prominent role than the tonic. It includes important melodic pieces (gushehs) such as Jameh-Daran, Iraq, Qarai, and Rohab, and allows modulation to modes like Iraq and Rak.\n\nFrom an auditory perspective, Afshari is deeply emotional and melancholic, often used to express sorrow, separation, and inner pain. In contrast, the Rohab section at the end provides a calmer, more hopeful mood, balancing the intense emotional character of the mode.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>The <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Avaz of Afshari</span></strong></span> is considered one of the related parts of the Shur Dastgah in the radif of Iranian music, but the fifth degree of this Avaz, as will be said below, is variable, and in this variability, it causes the Dashti tetrachord of this Avaz to change to the Shur tetrachord, in which case the fretting intervals of the Avaz of Afshari become equal to the Segah Dastgah, and for this reason the Avaz of Afshari can also be considered one of the related parts of Segah.</p>\n<p>Therefore, in this article, which is placed in the category of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86/\">articles related to Iranian music</a> of the Sornaz Music School, we examine the structure of the Avaz of Afshari and explain with reasons why the Avaz of Afshari is one of the related parts of Shur and is not placed among the related parts of Segah. Then we mention the important gushehs of the Avaz of Afshari and at the end present Ruhollah Khaleqi’s opinion regarding the auditory quality of this Avaz, which is mentioned in the second volume of the book “A Theoretical View of Music.”</p>\n<p>In another article, we also examine and analyze the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d9%88%d8%b4%d9%87-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d9%81%d8%b4%d8%a7%d8%b1%db%8c/\">gushehs of the Avaz of Afshari</a> in various radifs of Iranian music. Stay with us ...</p>\n<h2>Structure of the Avaz of Afshari</h2>\n<p>In the radif of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a>, the difference of the Avaz of Afshari among the related parts of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b4%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Shur Dastgah</a> is greater than the others, and its <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">scale</a> is more independent, to the extent that if it did not have one or two minor reasons, we could have considered it independent from Shur because its scale, as will be clear later, is closer to the Segah Dastgah than to Shur.</p>\n<p>It is better to write the Afshari scale like the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%aa%d8%b1%da%a9/\">Avaz of Bayat-e Tork</a> in such a way that it does not start from the tonic of Shur because the tonic of Shur is neither the first degree of the Afshari scale nor the witness note or the cadence note of the Avaz of Afshari. Moreover, one of the degrees of the Avaz of Afshari scale is also variable and almost in the important part of this Avaz differs from the main note that is common in the Shur scale, but for reasons that will be mentioned below, the Afshari scale starts from the seventh degree of the Shur scale.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_024_afshari/webp/sornaz_024_afshari_01.webp\" alt=\"Interval pattern of the Avaz of Afshari scale from the seventh degree of the Shur scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 1- Interval pattern of the Avaz of Afshari scale from the seventh degree of the Shur scale[/caption]\n<p>In the figure above, the cadence note is marked in green, the witness note of the Avaz of Afshari in red, and the variable note in blue.</p>\n<p>In some Avazs like the Avaz of Afshari and the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%af%d8%b4%d8%aa%db%8c/\">Avaz of Dashti</a>, there is a note that is often changing and becomes a quarter step flat or sharp, like the sixth degree of the Afshari scale, which we call the variable note.</p>\n<p>As can be seen, the intervals of the Afshari scale relative to its tonic are in order: major second, half-augmented third, perfect fourth, perfect fifth, half-augmented sixth, minor seventh, and octave.</p>\n<p>Therefore, as can be seen, the tetrachords of the Avaz of Afshari are unequal because the first tetrachord consists of one major second and two half-augmented second intervals, but the second tetrachord consists of two half-augmented seconds and one major second. The interval between the two tetrachords is also a major second, so the second tetrachord of Afshari is exactly the inverse of its first tetrachord.</p>\n<p>In the Avaz of Afshari, the first (tonic), third (cadence note), and fifth (witness note) degrees are important, and in fact, the tonal or modal notes of this Avaz are the first, third, and fifth degrees instead of the first, fourth, and fifth degrees.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_024_afshari/webp/sornaz_024_afshari_05.webp\" alt=\"Notes of the Avaz of Afshari in “D” from the seventh degree of the Shur scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 2- Notes of the Avaz of Afshari in “D” from the seventh degree of the Shur scale[/caption]\n<p>The Avaz of Afshari usually starts from the fifth degree of its own scale (the witness note or the red note in figure 1), and this degree shows itself several times and then pauses on the third degree (the cadence note or the green note in figure 1) and sometimes does not stop long there and cadences on the first degree.</p>\n<p>If during playing the Avaz of Afshari we pay attention to the notes of the first, third, and fifth degrees, we see that most cadences are on the third degree (but in the Shekasteh Avaz, which is also not dissimilar to Afshari, most cadences are on the first degree of Afshari, where it becomes the tonic of Mahur, which is examined in the article “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%85%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%88%d8%b1/\">The Mahur Dastgah in Iranian Music</a>”). For this reason, we call it the cadence note, and the first degree of the scale can be considered the tonic of Afshari.</p>\n<p>The fifth degree almost has the character of the dominant, and the initial cadences, which are technically known in the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d9%88%d9%86%db%8c/\">science of harmony</a> as a half-cadence, occur on this degree, but in the complete cadence of the Afshari melody, instead of going to the tonic, it stops on the third degree, and when harmonizing the Afshari melody, it is possible to close the chord on the first degree but keep the melody on the third degree.</p>\n<p>Now we can say that in the scale of the Avaz of Afshari, unlike other scales, the importance of the third degree is more than the first degree (as is also the case in the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b3%d9%87-%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">Segah Dastgah</a>), and it may be said that in this case, why have we not started the Afshari scale from the third degree in the following form:</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_024_afshari/webp/sornaz_024_afshari_02.webp\" alt=\"Interval pattern of the Avaz of Afshari scale from the second degree of the Shur scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 3- Interval pattern of the Avaz of Afshari scale from the second degree of the Shur scale[/caption]\n<p>In the figure above, the cadence note is marked in green, the witness note of the Avaz of Afshari in red, and the variable note in blue.</p>\n<p>The reason for this issue cannot be simply stated here because it is necessary to be somewhat familiar with the general rules of the science of harmony and to have some information about the science of sound. But it can be said that in any scale, it is necessary that the interval between the tonic and the dominant be a perfect fifth, and this matter is naturally present in all scales. So if we write the scale of the Avaz of Afshari from the cadence note as in the above figure, the interval between the tonic and the fifth degree becomes a diminished fifth, and from the point of view of the science of harmony and when forming chords, we would face difficulty, and as a result, harmonizing the Avaz of Afshari would become disproportionate and unnatural.</p>\n<p>For this reason, we did not start the scale from the cadence note, just as we will do the same in the Segah Dastgah. Therefore, in the scale of the Avaz of Afshari, the third degree, that is, the cadence note, has more importance in the melodies of this Avaz, and the first degree is of credit from the point of view of the science of harmony of sounds.</p>\n<h3>Degrees and Interval Fretting of the Afshari Scale Based on the Shur Scale</h3>\n<p>The notes of the Avaz of Afshari scale from the first degree of the Shur scale of “A,” from which the Avaz of Afshari of “D” is derived, are as follows.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_024_afshari/webp/sornaz_024_afshari_04.webp\" alt=\"Notes of the Avaz of Afshari in “D” from the first degree of the Shur scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 4- Notes of the Avaz of Afshari in “D” from the first degree of the Shur scale[/caption]\n<p>One of the characteristics of the Avaz of Afshari is that its sixth degree is always changing, meaning sometimes it is in the same form as seen in the Shur scale (corresponding to the fifth degree of the Shur scale from which the Avaz of Afshari scale is derived), and sometimes it falls a quarter step, and such a note can be called the variable note.</p>\n<p>Although this note is used with the change it has made at the beginning of the Avaz of Afshari, but because we want the notes of the Afshari scale (since it is one of the related parts of Shur) and the Shur scale to be common, whenever this note changes, we use it with the accidental changing sign, and in the modal analysis of them, we are not forced to mention new rules.</p>\n<p>With this arrangement, the changing signs of the Avaz of Afshari are also the same as the changing signs of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%b9%d8%b7%d8%a7/\">Avaz of Abu Ata</a> and Bayat-e Tork, according to the changing signs of the Shur Dastgah from which it is derived.</p>\n<p>In <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">Iranian music</a>, Afshari, like Shur, is composed of two connected Shur and Dashti tetrachords (for more familiarity with the tetrachords of Iranian music, read the article “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Theory of Iranian Music, Examination of the Theory of Tetrachords and the 4 Basic Tetrachords in Iranian Music</a>”). Therefore, the scale of the Avaz of Afshari is the same as the Shur scale, with the difference in the witness note and the cadence note, which in Shur is the first degree and in Afshari the 4th and 2nd degrees.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_024_afshari/webp/sornaz_024_afshari_03.webp\" alt=\"Interval pattern of the Avaz of Afshari scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 5- Interval pattern of the Avaz of Afshari scale[/caption]\n<p>In the figure above, the cadence note is marked in green, the witness note of the Avaz of Afshari in red, and the variable note in blue.</p>\n<p>As was said above, the Avaz of Afshari scale is sometimes written from the seventh degree of Shur, but in order to make it clearer that Afshari is one of the related parts of the Shur Dastgah, we drew the interval pattern of the Avaz of Afshari scale in the same form as the Shur scale.</p>\n<h3>Why Is the Avaz of Afshari One of the Related Parts of the Shur Dastgah?</h3>\n<p>Now we must see, with this difference that exists between Shur and the Avaz of Afshari, why according to the usual rule we have counted Afshari as part of Shur. To answer this question, the following reasons can be mentioned:</p>\n<p>1) What causes the most difference in the Afshari scale is its sixth degree, that is, the dominant of Shur, which in Afshari sometimes falls a quarter step, but this note is not a permanent change because if it continued in all the gushehs of the Avaz of Afshari, without doubt, although it was against the usual rule, we should have considered Afshari as one of the related parts of Segah and not counted it as part of Shur. But because the change of the sixth degree of the Avaz of Afshari (the fifth degree of Shur) is temporary, the Avaz of Afshari in most parts becomes similar to Shur.</p>\n<p>2) Usually, the Avaz of Afshari cadences to Shur, and with the help of the “Rahab” gusheh, entry to the Avaz of Shur is performed, which has a complete similarity to Shur and is considered one of the gushehs of the Shur Dastgah. In this way, the sixth degree of the Avaz of Afshari is raised a quarter step and enters Shur. Among the pieces that have been composed in Iranian music according to the usual manner, at the end of the work, they stop less on the tonic of Afshari and always go to the tonic of Shur, and this itself is a strong reason for considering Afshari as one of the related parts of Shur.</p>\n<p>However, going from Afshari to Shur is not an unchangeable fixed rule, and the player and composer may, according to their wish, stop on the first degree of Afshari or the tonic of Shur.</p>\n<p>3) According to the scientific rule, the Avaz of Afshari should either be part of Shur or Segah. If Afshari was considered part of Segah, it would be possible sometimes to go from Afshari to Segah as well, whereas this is not usual, and the cadence of Afshari always happens in Shur.</p>\n<p>It is possible that those who are not familiar with our Avazs may imagine that Afshari, at least when its sixth degree has changed, is exactly Segah, and this imagination is completely correct from the point of view of intervals, but from the point of view of the character of the Avaz, it is completely different, and perhaps if a new player is suddenly told during playing the Avaz of Afshari to play Segah on the same intervals, it is likely that they cannot easily handle it.</p>\n<p>Another noteworthy point in the Avaz of Afshari is the change of the seventh degree of the Afshari scale (the sixth degree of Shur), which sometimes rises a quarter step, but this is not done to make the leading tone correct because whenever the Avaz of Afshari goes to the tonic, the seventh degree of Afshari is not shown, but this is done when the melody starts from the tonic and comes to the dominant, and the Avaz starts from there. But as soon as we enter the second tetrachord, if the seventh degree is shown, it returns to its original state.</p>\n<h3>Modal Change of the Avaz of Afshari</h3>\n<p>Among the modal changes that are used in the Avaz of Afshari, the most important is the modal change to the Avaz of <strong>Iraq</strong> and <strong>Rak</strong>. The description of this Avaz has been given in the article “The Mahur Dastgah in the Iranian Music Radif.” Now it is enough to know that Afshari goes to Iraq, whose tonic is the same as the tonic of the Afshari scale, and the seventh degree of Afshari also rises a half step to become equal to the seventh degree of Iraq and becomes the leading tone. There are also other changes that will be mentioned later, but when returning to Afshari, the seventh degree of Afshari returns to its original state.</p>\n<h2>Similarity and Difference Between the Avazs of Afshari and Dashti</h2>\n<p>In the scale of the Avaz of Afshari, the fifth degree has a variable state, and if we consider Shur of G, the note D, which is the fifth degree, is used in two states: koron and natural. This variable note is created due to the simultaneous use of two Shur and Dashti tetrachords in this region.</p>\n<p>Shur tetrachord: D – E koron – Eb – F</p>\n<p>Dashti tetrachord: D – E – Eb – F</p>\n<p>This variability of the fifth degree of the Avaz of Afshari, which in this example is the note D, causes its similarity to the Avaz of Dashti, and the difference between these two Avazs in this section becomes prominent in that in Dashti this note is the most important note of the scale, and in the Avaz of Afshari the note before the variable note is used as the witness note, and the melodic movements in the Afshari scale mostly revolve around the fourth degree (in this example, the note C).</p>\n<h2>Gushehs of the Avaz of Afshari in the Music Radif</h2>\n<p>Due to the fact that in the Avaz of Afshari, the mode of C, which is derived from the fourth degree of the Shur scale of G, the note F has a very great importance, the tar and setar instruments are tuned in the form of C – F – C.</p>\n<p>The Daramad of the Avaz of Afshari revolves around the note “C,” and this note is introduced as the witness of the Daramad gusheh. Temporary stops in Afshari occur on the note “A koron,” and complete stops occur on the note F, and in the Avaz of Afshari, there is very little tendency to settle and cadence on the note G as the witness of the Shur Dastgah.</p>\n<p>The Jame Daran gusheh in the Avaz of Afshari is formed between the notes Re and Eb, that is, the notes related to the half-step interval in the Dashti tetrachord of the Afshari scale.</p>\n<p>In the perfect fourth interval higher than the witness of the Daramad (the note C) of the Afshari scale, that is, on the note F, the Iraq gusheh is created. In the Iraq gusheh, two Shur and Dashti tetrachords are used together in such a way that we have one Dashti tetrachord from the witness note of the Avaz of Afshari, and from the second degree of the Dashti tetrachord we form one Shur tetrachord, which causes the third degree of the Dashti tetrachord to become variable.</p>\n<p>In ascending movements, using the Shur tetrachord and the note “E koron,” we go toward the note G, and in descending movements or cadences, using the Dashti tetrachord and the note “Eb,” we move toward C.</p>\n<p>On the same note F, we also have the Qaraei gusheh. The type of movements in the Qaraei gusheh is different from the Iraq gusheh, and in this gusheh, more importance is given to the note G, but nevertheless, these two gushehs have almost similar atmospheres.</p>\n<p>The overall texture of the Avaz of Afshari and its gushehs is as follows. Now we can also perform the Rahab gusheh in between and perform a fourth movement from the note F to Bb, which can be considered as a movement from Afshari toward the Shur and Nava dastgahs, and finally the final stop occurs on the note G.</p>\n<p><strong>For familiarity with the tar and setar instruments, knowing the different parts of them and the necessary points for choosing tar and setar, study the articles <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%86%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%aa%d8%a7%d8%b1/\">Comprehensive Guide to Buying Tar</a> and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%86%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%b3%d9%87-%d8%aa%d8%a7%d8%b1/\">Comprehensive Guide to Buying Setar</a>.</strong></p>\n<h2>Auditory Quality of the Avaz of Afshari</h2>\n<p>Afshari is a mournful and painful Avaz that speaks of the story of separation and parting. It is as if it is a tired and hopeless lover who laments from the intensity of pain and suffering and has nothing to do with anything but lamenting, and sometimes from the excess of pain and hardship, he draws a cry from his fiery chest that makes the <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%B4%D9%86%D9%88%D9%86%D8%AF%D9%87\">listener</a> deeply affected. In general, this Avaz is the best example for showing states such as soul-burning laments of separation and complaining of the unfaithfulness of friends and expressing inner pain and sorrow and hopelessness and failures and expressing sorrow and grief from the hardships and pains of life and mentioning regretful memories of sorrow and grief.</p>\n<p>The Rahab Avaz, which is played at the end of Afshari, is advisory, and its character is the opposite of the Avaz of Afshari. It does not have burning and lamenting and moaning, but it is like an experienced old man of the world (like the character of the Shur Dastgah) who wants to pour cool water on the burning hearts of those who have suffered misfortune and make them hopeful with his pleasant advice and, at the same time, say that human desires are not fulfillable, so in order to live comfortably, we must pull the skirt of desire so that the continuation of life, which is sometimes accompanied by hardship and sometimes with joy and happiness, becomes easy.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding the Avaz of Afshari, please share it in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this content with the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School website</a>.</p>', '2023-06-27 20:53:07', 0, '2024-09-18 22:46:37', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1482, 'posts', 25, 'en', NULL, 'Avaz-e Dashti | The most common avaz among the 4 avaz dependent on Dastgah Shur in the radif of Iranian music', NULL, 'Dashti is one of the most popular and expressive subordinate modes (āvāz) of the Shur system in Persian classical music, widely heard among rural and folk communities. Structurally, its scale is identical to Shur, but its شاهد (main tone) is the fifth degree of Shur, which is variable—sometimes remaining natural and sometimes lowered by a quarter tone. This feature gives Dashti its distinctive melancholic and emotional character.\n\nIn Dashti, the fifth degree (the شاهد) plays the most central role, followed by the third degree as the resting tone, while the final cadence usually resolves back to Shur. The presence of a variable note creates similarities with Afshari and Segah, though their melodic identities remain distinguishable.\n\nImportant melodic sections (gushehs) in Dashti include Daramad, Owj, Jameh-Daran, Gilaki, Chupani, and Ghamangiz. These differ more in expressive character than in structural framework, and some—like Gilaki and Masnavi—are also used in other modes.\n\nFrom an auditory perspective, Dashti is deeply emotional, simple, and intimate, often associated with rural and pastoral life. It conveys feelings of sorrow, purity, and natural simplicity, reflecting a close connection to nature and traditional lifestyles, and can evoke strong emotional responses in listeners.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>The <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Avaz of Dashti</span></strong></span> is considered one of the most pleasant Avazs and related parts of the Shur Dastgah in the radif of Iranian music, which is heard and hummed a lot among the people of various regions of Iran.</p>\n<p>The fifth degree of the Avaz of Dashti scale, like the Avaz of Afshari, is variable, and in this change of the fifth degree, the Dashti tetrachord of this Avaz changes to the Shur tetrachord, in which case the fretting intervals of the Avaz of Dashti become similar to the Segah Dastgah, and for this reason the Avaz of Dashti, in terms of fretting, in some cases becomes similar to the Avaz of Afshari and the Segah Dastgah. But over time and with continuous listening to the Avazs and melodies of Dashti, Afshari, and Segah, the difference between them will be distinguishable.</p>\n<p>Of course, it should be noted that even experienced radif singers and players sometimes become confused in understanding the difference between the Avazs of Dashti, Afshari, and Segah, and this boundary between the Avazs has a shadowy and relative state.</p>\n<p>Therefore, in this article, which is placed in the category of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86/\">articles related to Iranian music</a> of the Sornaz Music School, we examine the structure of the Avaz of Dashti. We review the gushehs of the Avaz of Dashti in several radifs and then examine the gushehs of the Avaz of Dashti in the radif of Mirza Hossein Gholi. We also state things from the words of the prominent Iranian musician Ruhollah Khaleqi regarding the auditory quality of the Avaz of Dashti, which is found in the second volume of the book “A Theoretical View of Music.”</p>\n<p>In another article, we also examine and analyze the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d9%88%d8%b4%d9%87-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%af%d8%b4%d8%aa%db%8c/\">gushehs of the Avaz of Dashti</a> in various radifs of Iranian music. Continue with us ...</p>\n<h2>Structure of the Avaz of Dashti</h2>\n<p>In the radif of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a>, the scale of the Avaz of Dashti has no difference with the scale of the Shur Dastgah, but its witness note is the fifth degree of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">Shur scale</a> (the witness note of Hijaz in the Avaz of Abu Ata). But one should not imagine that because Dashti and Hijaz share the witness note, the Avaz of Dashti and Hijaz are also like each other, because on one hand the character of these two Avazs is different from each other, and on the other hand the fifth degree, that is, the witness note in the Avaz of Dashti, is variable, meaning sometimes it is in its original state and sometimes it falls a quarter step, whereas the witness note of Hijaz is always fixed and does not change.</p>\n<p>Whenever in the Avaz of Dashti a stop is made on the witness note, it is in its original state in the Shur scale, and whenever we descend from this note and go toward the lower notes of the instrument, it changes and becomes a quarter step flatter, and this very change makes the Avaz of Dashti sad and sorrowful and gives it a special charm and beauty.</p>\n<p>As we previously said about the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d9%81%d8%b4%d8%a7%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Avaz of Afshari</a>, this Avaz also has a variable note, which is the dominant (the fifth degree) of Shur, but the difference between Dashti and Afshari is that in Dashti the witness, that is, the fifth degree of Shur, is variable, but in Afshari the sixth degree, which is also the fifth degree of the corresponding Shur, changes. Another difference is that in Dashti, because the variable note is the witness of the Avaz, a stop is made on it, but in Afshari we never pause on the variable note because the witness of the Avaz of Afshari is the fourth degree of Shur.</p>\n<p>The Dashti scale can be written like the Shur scale, and its only difference with Shur is in the following two points:</p>\n<p>1) Its fifth degree has two different states, meaning sometimes it is in its original state and sometimes it falls a quarter step.</p>\n<p>2) In Shur, the tonic and then the fourth degree are important. In Dashti, the most important note is the witness (the fifth degree of Shur), and after that the third degree, on which we stop, and in Dashti it is the cadence note. But because the cadence of Dashti is to Shur, the tonic is also not unimportant, and in this scale, like Afshari, the first, third, and fifth degrees, which are the tonal or modal notes of the Avaz of Dashti, are of importance and credit.</p>\n<p>The notes of the Avaz of Dashti scale in “E,” which is formed on the fifth degree of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b4%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Shur Dastgah</a> of “A,” are as follows.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_025_dashti/webp/sornaz_025_dashti_02.webp\" alt=\"Notes of the Avaz of Dashti in “E” from the first degree of the Shur scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 1- Notes of the Avaz of Dashti in “E” from the first degree of the Shur scale[/caption]\n<p>In Dashti, whenever the witness note changes, it takes an accidental sign. Therefore, the combined signs of Shur and Dashti are similar to each other.</p>\n<p>With this arrangement, the changing signs of the Avaz of Afshari are also the same as the changing signs of the Shur Dastgah from which it is derived.</p>\n<p>In <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">Iranian music</a>, the Avaz of Dashti, like Shur, is composed of two connected Shur and Dashti tetrachords (for more familiarity with the tetrachords of Iranian music, read the article “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Theory of Iranian Music, Examination of the Theory of Tetrachords and the 4 Basic Tetrachords in Iranian Music</a>”). Therefore, the scale of the Avaz of Dashti is the same as the Shur scale, with the difference in the witness note and the cadence note, which in Shur is the first degree and in Dashti the 5th and 3rd degrees.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_025_dashti/webp/sornaz_025_dashti_01.webp\" alt=\"Interval pattern of the Avaz of Dashti scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 2- Interval pattern of the Avaz of Dashti scale[/caption]\n<p>In the figure above, the cadence note is marked in green, the witness note of the Avaz of Dashti in red, and the variable note, which is the same as the witness, in blue.</p>\n<h2>Related Parts and Gushehs of the Avaz of Dashti</h2>\n<p>The related parts of the Avaz of Dashti, such as <strong>Ghamangiz</strong>, <strong>Chupani</strong>, and <strong>Gilaki</strong>, have no important difference with Dashti, and the only difference is in their characters. There is also one common aspect among them, and that is the state of sadness and sorrow.</p>\n<p>The variable note in the Avaz of Dashti is usually considered in the natural state (with the half-step sign), and in movements toward the tonic of Shur or the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%af-%d8%af%d8%b1-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">cadences</a>, it is used in the koron form (or suri – with the quarter-step sign). This quarter-step change of the fifth degree of the Avaz of Dashti gives a sad and lament-like character to this Avaz. In this Avaz, we constantly return to Shur.</p>\n<p>The Daramad of the Avaz of Dashti is placed on the fifth degree of the Shur scale. Therefore, if we consider Shur of G, the Daramad of Dashti occurs on the note D, and the movements are toward the note G.</p>\n<p>The Jame Daran gusheh is the next important gusheh in the Avaz of Dashti, which is formed between the notes of the half-step interval of the Dashti tetrachord, that is, the notes D and Eb. But this gusheh is not mentioned in most radifs.</p>\n<p>The Oj gusheh can also be considered as one of the other important gushehs of the Avaz of Dashti. In the Oj gusheh, two Shur and Dashti tetrachords are used together in such a way that we have one Dashti tetrachord from the fourth degree of the Dashti scale, and from the second degree of the Dashti tetrachord we form one Shur tetrachord, which causes the third degree of the Dashti tetrachord to become variable (Eb and E koron).</p>\n<p>In ascending movements, using the Shur tetrachord and the note “E koron,” we go toward the note G, and in descending movements or cadences, using the Dashti tetrachord and the note “Eb,” we move toward C.</p>\n<p>The overall structure of the Avaz of Dashti and its important gushehs, which are important from a modal and positional point of view, are as follows.</p>\n<p>The Bidgani and Hajiani gushehs are more different states and atmospheres.</p>\n<p>The Ghamangiz gusheh can be considered a trill or descending phrasing for the Avaz of Dashti and is similar to the Daramad. It cadences from the D region toward G; its melodies have more importance than its sonic range and do not have much importance from a modal or positional point of view.</p>\n<p>The Gilaki gusheh also moves in the Daramad and Oj region, and more the character of its trills is important. The famous Gilaki trill is an important characteristic of this gusheh and has a famous phrasing for cadence.</p>\n<p>The Masnavi gusheh is one of the gushehs in which its rhythmic weight and character is important and can be performed in most dastgahs and Avazs.</p>\n<p>The Gilaki gusheh, in addition to the Avaz of Dashti, is also performed in the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%b9%d8%b7%d8%a7/\">Avaz of Abu Ata</a>.</p>\n<h2>Auditory Quality of the Avaz of Dashti</h2>\n<p>Dashti is one of the most beautiful related parts of Shur and is a sad, sorrowful, and painful Avaz, but at the same time delicate and refined, and it can be called the shepherd’s Avaz of Iran.</p>\n<p>Among most nations, the inhabitants of pleasant plains and areas with good weather have their own special melodies that have come into existence according to their taste and needs. The Avaz of Dashti can also be called the shepherd’s Avaz of Iran, and perhaps its origin is from the Dashti and Dashtestan region (located in <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%A7%D9%86_%D9%81%D8%A7%D8%B1%D8%B3\">Fars Province</a>). Anyway, this Avaz is today common and customary among tribes whose livelihood is from herding, as well as in villages, and is perhaps the unique Avaz of them.</p>\n<p>In the north of Iran, especially on the southern coasts of the Caspian Sea (<a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%A7%D9%86_%D9%85%D8%A7%D8%B2%D9%86%D8%AF%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%86\">Mazandaran</a> and <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%A7%D9%86_%DA%AF%DB%8C%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%86\">Gilan</a>), this Avaz has undergone a slight change of form and has become the Gilaki melody, and the people of Gilan show strange skill in singing this melody and create a strong effect on the listener. Gilaki is in fact the native Avaz of the southern coasts of the Caspian Sea, especially Gilan County.</p>\n<p>In ancient Greece as well, the shepherd’s Avaz was common, and the French call it Chant pastoral. One of the famous poets of Greece, known as Theocritus, had great skill in composing shepherd’s poems.</p>\n<p>With the mention of the above points, one can understand how the Avaz of Dashti should be or what character it should have.</p>\n<p>The simple life of desert dwellers and the natural feelings of nomadic and shepherd people have caused the creation of the simple and natural melody of Dashti. Although this Avaz is sad and sorrowful, its simplicity, lack of adornment, and lack of formality have also given it a special charm, beauty, and elegance. It is as if the Avaz of Dashti comes out of a throat whose owner is unaware of trickery, deceit, and hypocrisy. His love is a pure and sincere love that has been created in him according to natural need for creating intimacy and affection and forming a family, and honesty and truthfulness have been his long-standing profession and the souvenir of his ancestors.</p>\n<p>This Avaz speaks of various and beautiful views of nature, that is, from the same pleasant plains and flowing streams and azure sky and shining stars and fragrant wild flowers and the calm, monotonous, and unceremonious life of simple villagers. It deals with pure hearts and calm and untroubled hearts. It is like a shepherd who talks to his sheep and guides them and keeps the fierce wolf away from them. It is a lover who looks at his beloved with pure and innocent eyes and does not dare to speak. It is an old man who is an advisor and with one sentence prevents people from committing ugly and unpleasant acts.</p>\n<p>In summary, Dashti is a sample Avaz of simple and unadorned life, that is, the same unceremonious desert and shepherd life, which, although science and industry had no way among those people, their pure morals and simple natural feelings governed them.</p>\n<p>The Avaz of Dashti, in its simplicity, is sometimes so effective and sorrowful that the listener sheds tears of regret and remorse on his cheeks, and these are the same droplets that our ancestors, due to thousands of revolutions and misfortunes and helplessness of the past, have poured into the lap of their descendants, and now those collected droplets have turned into the melody of Dashti and inform us of the revolutions and events that have happened in this land.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding the Avaz of Dashti, please share it in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this content with the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com\">Sornaz Music School website</a>.</p>', '2023-06-28 20:50:30', 0, '2024-09-18 22:46:18', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1483, 'posts', 26, 'en', NULL, 'Dastgah-e Nava or Avaz-e Nava? The dastgah that Iranian musicologists disagree about | Similarities and differences between Dastgah Nava and Shur | 2 different scale patterns for Nava', NULL, 'The Nava Dastgah is one of the principal modal systems in Persian classical music and is structurally very similar to the Shur Dastgah. There has long been debate among musicians regarding its classification. Traditional musicians consider Nava an independent dastgah, while theorists such as Ali-Naqi Vaziri and Ruhollah Khaleghi argue that, due to identical interval structures, it should be regarded as a derivative of Shur.\n\nIn terms of structure, Nava shares the same scale intervals as Shur, but differs in tonal emphasis. The most important درجات (degrees) in Nava are the first, fourth, and sixth. The sixth degree often serves as the main resting tone (shahed), while the first degree functions as the finalis (ist). Nava is generally considered a descending mode and lacks a leading tone, which gives its cadences a distinctive character.\n\nHistorically, Nava originates from the modal system of ancient Persian music and was one of the twelve primary maqams. In its modern form, it includes important gushehs (melodic pieces) such as “Gavesht,” “Nehft,” and “Iraq,” each contributing unique melodic variations.\n\nFrom an auditory perspective, Nava is calm, dignified, and reflective. It conveys emotions in a balanced way—neither overly joyful nor deeply sorrowful. Because of its contemplative and philosophical nature, it is often performed at the end of musical gatherings, leaving a profound and introspective impact on the listener.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>The <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Nava Dastgah</span></strong></span> or the Avaz of Nava in the radif of Iranian music has a very close structural similarity to the Shur Dastgah. There are differences of opinion among Iranian musicians regarding the Nava Dastgah.</p>\n<p>In this way, traditional Iranian musicians and those who have learned music orally and from mouth to mouth, following their masters, have considered the Nava and Shur dastgahs as independent from each other. But musicians who learned music theoretically and were familiar with Western music theory, such as Ali-Naqi Vaziri and his students like Ruhollah Khaleqi, believed that the intervals and degrees of Shur and Nava are the same, so Nava should be considered an Avaz dependent on Shur and one of its related parts.</p>\n<p>In this article from the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>, we first examine the fretting structure of the Nava Dastgah. Then we state the reasons that Ruhollah Khaleqi presented in the second volume of his book “A Theoretical View of Music” regarding the similarity of Nava to Shur and for this reason counts Nava as an Avaz belonging to Shur and not an independent dastgah. Next, we examine the position of Nava in old Iranian music and then review the gushehs of today’s Nava Dastgah. At the end, we also bring Ruhollah Khaleqi’s opinion regarding its auditory quality.</p>\n<p>In another article, we also examine and analyze the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d9%88%d8%b4%d9%87-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%86%d9%88%d8%a7/\">gushehs of the Nava Dastgah</a> in various radifs of Iranian music. Stay with us ...</p>\n<h2>Structure of the Nava Dastgah</h2>\n<p>The Nava Dastgah is one of the seven dastgahs in the radif of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a>, but many Iranian musicians, including Ali-Naqi Vaziri and Ruhollah Khaleqi, because the structure of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">scale</a> of Nava and Shur is the same, do not consider it independent from Shur and count Nava as one of the related parts of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b4%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Shur Dastgah</a> and for this reason call it the Avaz of Nava.</p>\n<p>The notes of the Avaz of Nava scale from the first degree of the Shur scale of “A,” from which the Avaz of Nava of “D” is derived, are as follows.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_026_nava/webp/sornaz_026_nava_03.webp\" alt=\"Notes of the Nava Dastgah of “D” from the first degree of the Shur scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 1- Notes of the Nava Dastgah of “D” from the first degree of the Shur scale[/caption]\n<p>The notes of the Avaz of Nava scale from the fourth degree of the Shur scale of “A,” from which the Avaz of Nava of “D” is derived, are as follows.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_026_nava/webp/sornaz_026_nava_04.webp\" alt=\"Notes of the Nava Dastgah of “D” from the fourth degree of the Shur scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 2- Notes of the Nava Dastgah of “D” from the fourth degree of the Shur scale[/caption]\n<p>Therefore, the changing signs of the Nava Dastgah are also the same as the changing signs of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%b9%d8%b7%d8%a7/\">Avaz of Abu Ata</a>, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%aa%d8%b1%da%a9/\">Avaz of Bayat-e Tork</a>, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d9%81%d8%b4%d8%a7%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Avaz of Afshari</a>, and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%af%d8%b4%d8%aa%db%8c/\">Avaz of Dashti</a>, according to the changing signs of the Shur Dastgah from which it is derived.</p>\n<p>The Nava scale, like Bayat-e Tork and Afshari, does not start from the tonic of Shur, and its first degree is the real dominant of Shur in this way:</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_026_nava/webp/sornaz_026_nava_02.webp\" alt=\"Interval pattern of the Nava Dastgah scale from the fourth degree of the Shur scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 3- Interval pattern of the Nava Dastgah scale from the fourth degree of the Shur scale[/caption]\n<p>In the figure above, the cadence note is marked in green and the witness note of the Nava Dastgah in red.</p>\n<p>The intervals of the degrees of the Nava scale relative to the tonic are as follows: major second, minor third, perfect fourth, perfect fifth, half-augmented sixth, minor seventh, and octave.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the tetrachords of the Nava scale in this state are unequal, and its first tetrachord is like the first tetrachord of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%db%8c%d9%86%d9%88%d8%b1/\">minor scale</a>. If the sixth degree was a quarter step flatter, the Nava scale would become like the minor scale.</p>\n<p>In the Nava scale, the first, fourth, and sixth degrees are important. One part of the cadences of Nava is on the sixth degree, and the final cadences are on the first degree, and the fourth degree also has the character of the dominant. So the Nava scale is descending because it has no leading tone, and the fourth degree is also considered its real dominant, and the same cadence that is common in Shur from the fourth degree to the first degree (Bal-e Kabutar) is also common in the Nava Dastgah.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_026_nava/webp/sornaz_026_nava_05.webp\" alt=\"The cadence known as Bal-e Kabutar in the Nava Dastgah of “D”\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 4- The cadence known as Bal-e Kabutar in the Nava Dastgah of “D”[/caption]\n<p>The melodies of Nava usually stop on the sixth degree, and this note is the second degree of the Shur scale from which the Avaz of Nava is derived. That is, the same degree that is the cadence note of Abu Ata and the cadence note of Afshari. For this reason, the melodies of Nava are somewhat similar to Abu Ata and Afshari as well. So this degree can also be called the witness of Nava.</p>\n<p>After short stops on the witness note, the cadences are on the first degree of the Nava scale (the fourth degree, that is, the real dominant of Shur), and this degree can be called the cadence note of Nava.</p>\n<p>The reason we do not start the Nava scale from the tonic is that the tonic of Shur in this scale has no importance and is never stopped on. Only in some gushehs of Nava that are also common in Shur is there a stop on the tonic of Shur. But the reasons we count the Nava Dastgah as one of the related parts of Shur are as follows:</p>\n<p>1) The degrees of Nava have no difference with Shur, and as was said earlier, the combined signs of Nava and Shur are the same.</p>\n<p>2) From the point of view of character and manner, there is a complete similarity between Shur and Nava, as can be seen from comparing the characters of the Avaz of Nava and Shur.</p>\n<p>3) If we are to not count the Nava scale as one of the related parts of Shur, there is no reason that the Afshari scale, despite the great difference it has with Shur, should be considered part of Shur. Because in the Avaz of Afshari, even in the daramads and introduction of the Avaz, there is a variable note that is different from Shur, whereas in Nava this is not the case. In fact, the daramads of Nava, as is common in the radif of Avazs, move exactly around the same notes of the Shur scale from the second degree to the sixth degree of the Shur from which the Avaz of Nava is derived.</p>\n<p>In <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">Iranian music</a>, the Nava Dastgah, like Shur, is composed of two connected Shur and Dashti tetrachords (for more familiarity with the tetrachords of Iranian music, read the article “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Theory of Iranian Music, Examination of the Theory of Tetrachords and the 4 Basic Tetrachords in Iranian Music</a>”). Therefore, the scale of the Nava Dastgah is the same as the Shur scale, with the difference in the witness note and the cadence note, which in Shur is the first degree and in Nava the 4th and 2nd degrees.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_026_nava/webp/sornaz_026_nava_01.webp\" alt=\"Interval pattern of the Nava Dastgah scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 5- Interval pattern of the Nava Dastgah scale[/caption]\n<p>In the figure above, the cadence note is marked in green and the witness note of the Nava Dastgah in red.</p>\n<p>One of the characteristics of the Nava scale is that it has no leading tone (neither in the ascending form nor in the descending form), and for this reason, the interval of the sixth degree (the note before the seventh degree that should be the leading tone) with the seventh degree is a half-augmented second, and it somewhat helps the seventh degree, which is not a real leading tone, due to this interval so that the cadences are more natural.</p>\n<p>This state, as we said, also exists in Shur, and the sixth degree of the Shur scale rose a quarter step. But the difference between Shur and Nava is that in Shur the sixth degree became a variable note during the Avaz and approached the seventh degree, whereas in the Avaz of Nava this note by itself has a half-augmented second interval with the seventh degree, so the note is fixed, not variable.</p>\n<p>The Avaz of Nava may end on its own tonic with the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%af-%d8%af%d8%b1-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">Bal-e Kabutar cadence</a> at the end of the gushehs, and this itself is another reason that we did not start the Nava scale from the tonic of Shur. It is also possible to go to Shur and end on the tonic of the mode from which it is derived.</p>\n<h2>The Position of Nava in Old Iranian Music</h2>\n<p>The Nava mode was one of the 12 modes of old Iranian music, which was considered the second circle of the 12 circles. (The second circle of the twelve circles)</p>\n<p>Also, Nava was considered the fourteenth circle of the 84 circles, circles that were created from the combination of 7 dhul-arba‘ with 12 dhul-khums and were used in old Iranian music.</p>\n<p>Dhul-arba‘ is the same as tetrachord or tetrachord, which consists of 4 notes with a perfect fourth interval or 2.5 steps.</p>\n<p>Dhul-khums was created from the combination of 5 notes that formed a perfect fifth interval or 3.5 steps. (One dhul-arba‘ plus one additional resonant interval or one whole step)</p>\n<p>From the point of view of similarity of melodies (equality of scale degrees and notes), the Nava mode completely matched the Ushaq and Buselik modes.</p>\n<p>The Nava mode had 2 branches named “Nowruz-e Khara” and “Mahur,” of which the Nowruz-e Khara branch itself has two other gushehs named “Molaviyat” and “Khosasteh,” and the Mahur branch also had two gushehs “Ikiyat” and “Tur.”</p>\n<p>The old intervals of the Nava mode were as follows:</p>\n<p>Resonant – remainder – resonant – resonant – remainder – resonant – resonant</p>\n<p>The modern intervals of the Nava Dastgah are as follows:</p>\n<p>Mujannab – mujannab – resonant – resonant – remainder – resonant – resonant</p>\n<p>As we see, in the old Nava mode and the modern Nava Dastgah in the radif of Iranian music, the Dashti tetrachord (whole step – half step – whole step) played a central role, and if we consider the old intervals of the Nava mode, we realize that it completely matches and corresponds with modern Nava.</p>\n<p>The intervals of the gushehs of Daramad, Kereshmeh, Gardanieh, and Ushaq in the modern Nava Dastgah can be considered almost matching with the old mode “Janfaza,” and the intervals of the gushehs of Bayat-e Raje, Naheft, and Neyshaburak can be considered similar to the old mode “Nava.”</p>\n<p>After the change of musical style from the old modal music to the modern system of 7 dastgahs and 5 Avazs in the Qajar era and around that time, two names from the old modes were used in the dastgah system as radif dastgahs: one the Nava Dastgah and the other the name of the Rast Panjgah Dastgah, which was taken from the “Rast” mode.</p>\n<p>Nava is also one of the 7 main modes and dastgahs in the modal music of the country of Azerbaijan.</p>\n<p><strong>For familiarity with the santur instrument, knowing the different parts of this instrument and the necessary points for choosing a good and high-quality santur, study the article <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%86%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%b3%d9%86%d8%aa%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Comprehensive Guide to Buying Santur</a>.</strong></p>\n<h2>Gushehs of the Nava Dastgah in the Music Radif</h2>\n<p>One of the gushehs of the Nava Dastgah that causes a change in the degrees of the scale is the <strong>Gosht</strong> (Gosht), in which the second and fifth degrees of Nava each fall a quarter step, and the witness note of this melody is the second degree of Nava. So in the Nava of D, which is derived from Shur of A, the notes that change for playing or singing the Gosht are E and A, both of which become koron.</p>\n<p>Gosht is not unrelated to the Segah Dastgah, and after becoming familiar with the Segah scale, it may be compared with it.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Naheft</strong> gusheh is another important gusheh of the Nava Dastgah, in which no change is made to the Nava scale, and its witness note is the fifth degree of Nava. The Naheft gusheh is not dissimilar to the Hijaz of Abu Ata, which, by referring to the character of these two Avazs and the radif of Avazs, one may realize this similarity.</p>\n<p>Another famous gusheh of the Nava Dastgah is the <strong>Iraq</strong> gusheh, which was also used in Afshari and which we will talk about in the articles related to the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%85%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Mahur Dastgah</a>. Here it is enough to know that the fourth degree of the Nava scale becomes the witness note of Iraq, and the third degree rises a half step (F# becomes), but in the cadence it ends on the first degree of the Nava scale.</p>\n<p>Other gushehs of Shur may also be used in the Nava Dastgah, but the important gushehs are the ones mentioned.</p>\n<p>The Nava Dastgah at the end of the gushehs has similar cadences, and because in the cadences it usually comes back, in the ascending form it does not reach a stop, and naturally in this form it does not need a leading tone and has a descending scale.</p>\n<h2>Auditory Quality of the Nava Dastgah</h2>\n<p>Nava is an Avaz with dignity and solemnity and advisory in nature, which expresses feelings with a gentle and moderate melody, neither too cheerful nor too painful and sorrowful. When tired and at leisure, listening to the Avaz of Nava is very pleasant, and it is usually called the “Avaz of Kharab” and was played at the end of sessions of intoxication and joy.</p>\n<p>In fact, Nava is a patient and experienced advisor who presents his advice to the listeners with a wonderful and skillful expression. Especially if suitable poems, such as the mystical poems of Hafez, are chosen for singing Nava, especially in the bass region with a mature and warm voice, it has a strange effect and involuntarily makes the listener obedient and receptive to advice.</p>\n<p>Nava, while giving advice of patience, also complains of the pains and sufferings of life, but the complaint of this Avaz is not full of lament and moaning like Dashti and Segah, and it expresses sorrow and grief in a hidden and concealed way. For this reason, Nava, like Shur, is a complete example of the lofty philosophical, Sufi, and mystical feelings of the people of the East and is a representative of the states and qualities of <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%B9%D8%B1%D9%81%D8%A7%D9%86\">mystics</a> and <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%81%DB%8C%D9%84%D8%B3%D9%88%D9%81\">philosophers</a> and those who have well tasted the flavor of the experiences of life.</p>\n<p>The Nava Dastgah has a thought-provoking dignity and presents very special and calming music, and it was usually played at the end of sessions, for which reason it has sometimes been referred to as the “Avaz of Kharab,” due to the good conclusion it gave to the sessions.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding the Nava Dastgah, please share it in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this content with the Sornaz Music School website.</p>', '2023-06-29 15:24:49', 0, '2024-09-18 22:46:05', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1484, 'posts', 27, 'en', NULL, 'Dastgah-e Mahur | Similarity of Dastgah Mahur in Iranian music to the major scale | A dastgah with about 50 gushehs | Structure and mayeh-shenasi', NULL, 'Mahur is one of the principal modes (dastgahs) in Persian classical music, closely resembling the major scale in Western music in terms of intervals and structure. It is built from two disjunct tetrachords and is generally associated with a bright, uplifting, and majestic character.\n\nIn Mahur, the tonic and the main focal note (shahed) are the same, playing a central role in establishing the tonal center. Unlike Western music, there is also notable emphasis on the second degree of the scale, which gives Mahur a distinctive melodic identity. The most important scale degrees are the first, fourth, and fifth.\n\nMahur allows for extensive modulation. Important melodic sections (gushehs) such as Shekasteh, Delkash, Iraq, and Rak introduce alterations in intervals, enabling transitions to other modes like Shur, Afshari, Homayun, and Esfahan.\n\nFrom a theoretical perspective, Mahur can be analyzed within a 24 quarter-tone system, with specific rules for identifying key signatures and tonics based on accidentals such as koron and sori.\n\nIn terms of musical expression, Mahur conveys dignity, grandeur, and joy. It is particularly suitable for expressing emotions like strength, courage, and vitality. Contrary to the common perception of Persian music as predominantly melancholic, Mahur represents its lively and optimistic dimension.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>The <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Mahur Dastgah</span></strong></span> in the radif of Iranian music has a very close similarity to the major scale in Western music. The Mahur Dastgah is composed of two separate and equal Mahur tetrachords, and in terms of intervals and fretting structure, Mahur and the major scale are considered the same. Just as the major scale has an ascending, cheerful, and energetic character, this quality is also seen in Mahur, and this dastgah is mostly used for cheerful states and expressing joyful feelings.</p>\n<p>In this article from the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>, we first examine the fretting structure of the Mahur Dastgah, name the gushehs of the Mahur Dastgah in the Iranian music radif according to several different narrations, and introduce 4 of its important gushehs and melodies. Then we pay attention to the modal analysis of the Mahur Dastgah in Ali-Naqi Vaziri’s 24-quarter-tone system and state the rules for modal analysis of this dastgah. Finally, we also express the opinion of the great Iranian musician Ruhollah Khaleqi regarding the auditory quality of the Mahur Dastgah, which is found in the second volume of his book “A Theoretical View of Music.”</p>\n<p>We have also examined and analyzed the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d9%88%d8%b4%d9%87-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%85%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%88%d8%b1/\">gushehs of the Mahur Dastgah</a> in a separate article. Stay with us ...</p>\n<h2>Structure of the Mahur Dastgah</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">scale</a> of the Mahur Dastgah, without difference in key signatures, is like the major scale, which is the most natural scale and the basis of Western music, but the Mahur mode also has other properties.</p>\n<p>The notes of the Mahur scale in “C” are as follows.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_027_mahur/webp/sornaz_027_mahoor_02.webp\" alt=\"Notes of the Mahur Dastgah in “C”\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 1- Notes of the Mahur Dastgah in “C”[/caption]\n<p>The tonic note and the witness note in Mahur are the same, and if we consider the scale of the Mahur Dastgah as C, the tonic and witness of Mahur will be the first degree of the scale, that is, the note C.</p>\n<p>After the Avaz of Mahur starts from the tonic and moves around the subtonic, dominant, subdominant, and leading tone notes, and the initial daramads of the Avaz are finished and it stops on the tonic, it stops on the subtonic with a special melody, and this degree finds the character of the second witness.</p>\n<p>Stopping the Mahur Dastgah on the second degree, which from a scientific point of view is considered one of the middle notes of the scale, has a special character because in Western melodies, they stop less on the second degree of the major scale to the extent that it stops in the Mahur Dastgah. Of course, in Mahur as well, the stop on this degree does not last long, and after playing melodies on it for some time, they return again to the tonic of Mahur.</p>\n<p>From the point of view of the science of harmony and the consonance of sounds, the best and most important notes of the scale are the tonal or tonic degrees of the scale, that is, the first, fourth, and fifth degrees. The second and sixth degrees are considered middle, and the third and seventh degrees are bad. The purpose of this classification for the notes of the scale is that stopping too much on the middle and bad degrees makes recognizing the mode and tonality of the dastgah difficult.</p>\n<p>Stopping on the third degree of the Mahur Dastgah is less common, and after short stops on the fourth degree, they go to Shur by modal change (which happens here with the Delkash gusheh) or by another modal change to the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d9%81%d8%b4%d8%a7%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Avaz of Afshari</a> (which is known here as the Shekasteh gusheh and is also performed in the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%aa%d8%b1%da%a9/\">Avaz of Bayat-e Tork</a>), and in this way, they stop on the fifth degree of the scale (the dominant).</p>\n<p>In <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a>, the Mahur Dastgah is composed of two separate Mahur tetrachords (for more familiarity with the tetrachords of Iranian music, read the article “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Theory of Iranian Music, Examination of the Theory of Tetrachords and the 4 Basic Tetrachords in Iranian Music</a>”).</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_027_mahur/webp/sornaz_027_mahoor_001.webp\" alt=\"Interval pattern of the Mahur Dastgah scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 2- Interval pattern of the Mahur Dastgah scale[/caption]\n<p>In the figure above, the witness note of the Mahur Dastgah is marked in red and the cadence note in green.</p>\n<h2>The Shekasteh Melody in Mahur</h2>\n<p>To go to Shekasteh, the third degree of the Mahur Dastgah falls a quarter step, and this Avaz moves around the sixth, fifth, fourth, and third degrees of Mahur (of course, the third degree is variable), and its first stop is on the dominant of Mahur and then on the third degree that has fallen a quarter step. The seventh degree of the Mahur Dastgah also falls a half step if necessary, and if in Shekasteh we also lower the sixth degree of Mahur by a quarter step, Shekasteh turns into Afshari.</p>\n<p>The witness note of the Shekasteh melody is the dominant of the Mahur Dastgah, on which it stops for some time. The cadence note is first the third degree of Mahur, which has fallen a quarter step, and then the tonic of Mahur. In fact, this melody has two cadence notes, and even before cadencing to Mahur, it stops on the tonic of Mahur. In the cadence to Mahur, the notes that have changed, especially first of all the third degree, return to their original state, and in this way, they enter the original mode, that is, Mahur.</p>\n<h2>The Delkash Melody in Mahur</h2>\n<p>To go to Delkash, which is itself a kind of Shur Avaz, the Mahur Dastgah changes mode to a Shur whose tonic is the dominant of Mahur. For example, from Mahur of C we go to Shur of G, with the difference that the third degree of Mahur, which in fact should become equal to the sixth degree of Shur, that is, become minor, remains in its own state.</p>\n<p>Example: The notes of the Shur scale of G, as we know, are: G, A koron, Bb, C, D, Eb, F, G.</p>\n<p>But the notes of the Delkash gusheh of G become: G, A koron, Bb, C, D, E natural, F, G.</p>\n<p>The witness note of Delkash, as was said, is the dominant of the Mahur Dastgah, which is the tonic of the related Shur, but its final cadence is on the fourth degree of Mahur (the seventh degree of Shur). So we can call the dominant of Mahur the witness of Delkash and the subdominant of the Mahur Dastgah the cadence note of Delkash, and stopping on the subdominant of Mahur gives it a special character and, at the same time, makes the return to Mahur easier. After stopping on the subdominant of Mahur, because the third degree of Mahur has also not changed in Delkash, it is very easy to enter the Mahur mode.</p>\n<p>Therefore, from Delkash one can go to Shur and all its related parts and return again to Delkash and enter Mahur. Entering the Mahur Dastgah is also done by means of special melodies that can be called the link between Delkash and Mahur, and in these melodies the notes that have changed return to their original state.</p>\n<h2>The Iraq and Rak Melodies in Mahur</h2>\n<p>Another modal change is Iraq and Rak, which were also mentioned in the articles about the Avaz of Afshari and the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b3%d9%87-%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">Segah Dastgah</a>. The witness note of Iraq and Rak is the same as the tonic of the Mahur Dastgah. The only difference is that in Iraq the third degree of Mahur falls a half step and the third of the Mahur scale becomes minor, but in Rak, in addition to this change, the sixth degree of the Mahur Dastgah also falls a quarter step. So if we consider the Mahur scale as “C,” the Iraq and Rak scale becomes as follows:</p>\n<p>Iraq scale: C, D, Eb, F, G, A, B, C.</p>\n<p>Rak scale: C, D, Eb, F, G, A koron, B, C.</p>\n<p>As can be seen, the Rak scale is similar to the minor scale with a slight difference because if only A was also flat in the above scale, the harmonic minor scale would be formed, and in fact, the modal change of Mahur to Rak is a change to a mode of the same name. But as we will see later, the Rak scale is exactly the Isfahan scale and similar to the Avaz of Homayun.</p>\n<p>Therefore, by means of Iraq and Rak, each of which moves a little away from Mahur, one can go to the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%a7%d8%b5%d9%81%d9%87%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Avaz of Isfahan</a> and the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%87%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c%d9%88%d9%86/\">Homayun Dastgah</a> or the minor scale.</p>\n<p>Now we can say that modal change in the Mahur Dastgah is more than in Shur because the changes of Shur cannot be called modal change because all of them are modal shifts, but the Mahur scale both goes to Delkash, which is the means of going to Shur, and goes to Rak, which is the means of going to the Isfahan and Homayun modes.</p>\n<p dir=\"RTL\" style=\"direction: rtl; unicode-bidi: embed; text-align: justify;\"><span lang=\"AR-SA\" style=\"font-family: IRANSansX;\">In addition, we only mention the modal shifts and modal changes that are used in the radif of today’s Avazs and music, but a player who is familiar with the rules of modulation and the basics of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d9%88%d9%86%db%8c/\">the science of harmony</a> can start from any Avaz and go to all Avazs and dastgahs and cadence wherever he wishes.</span></p>\n<p dir=\"RTL\"><strong>For familiarity with the oud and tar instruments and knowing the different parts of these instruments and learning practical points for choosing the best model of the instrument, study the articles <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%86%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%b9%d9%88%d8%af-%d9%88-%d8%a8%d8%b1%d8%a8%d8%b7/\">Comprehensive Guide to Buying Oud and Barbat</a> and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%86%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%aa%d8%a7%d8%b1/\">Comprehensive Guide to Buying Tar</a>.</strong></p>\n<h2>Modal Analysis of the Mahur Dastgah</h2>\n<p>The modal analysis of Mahur on the twelve half steps of the chromatic scale is according to the general rule of modal analysis of major scales, but because our purpose is the modal analysis of quarter tones, we must know the combined signs of the twenty-four modes that are formed on the quarter steps of the twenty-four-quarter-tone scale. Therefore, we say:</p>\n<p>1- We take the note “Ab” as the starting point of circular movement, as in the modal analysis in the Shur Dastgah, and go up from it with half-augmented third intervals until we find the 24 modes, and the twenty-fifth note, as we said before, will be “G#,” which is equivalent to “Ab.”</p>\n<p>2- The combined signs of the thirteen modes that are formed on the half steps of the chromatic scale are like the combined signs of the 13 chromatic modes of the major scale.</p>\n<p>3- To find the combined signs of the modes that are formed on the rest of the notes of the twenty-four-part scale, we act as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Whenever the tonic is a suri note, such as F suri, C suri, etc., we consider the degrees of the mode whose tonic is the same in name but has no sign and is in the natural state, and we raise all the degrees of that mode by a quarter step, and as a result, the combined signs of the mode whose tonic has suri are found:</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example: We want to know if the tonic of the Mahur Dastgah is F suri, what are the combined signs of that mode?</p>\n<p>We know that the F major mode has only one sign, and its scale is as follows:</p>\n<p>F – G – A – Bb – C – D – E – F.</p>\n<p>Then we raise all the degrees of the above scale by a quarter step in this way:</p>\n<p>F suri – G suri – A suri – B koron – C suri – D suri – E suri – F suri.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the combined signs of that mode will be as follows:</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_027_mahur/webp/sornaz_027_mahoor_03.webp\" alt=\"Key signatures of the Mahur Dastgah in “F suri”\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 3- Key signatures of the Mahur Dastgah in “F suri”[/caption]\n<p>The combined signs of this mode are composed of two groups: koron and suri, that is, it has one koron and six suri.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>If the tonic of the Mahur Dastgah is a koron note, such as B koron, E koron, etc., we consider the degrees of the mode whose tonic is the same in name but has no sign and is in the natural state, and we lower all the degrees of that mode by a quarter step, and as a result, the combined signs of the mode whose tonic has koron are found.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example: We want to know if the tonic of the Mahur Dastgah is C koron, what are the combined signs of that mode?</p>\n<p>We know that the C major mode has no sign. So we lower all its degrees by a quarter step, and as a result, the Mahur Dastgah of C koron will have seven koron in this way:</p>\n<p>B koron – E koron – A koron – D koron – G koron – C koron – F koron.</p>\n<p><strong>Result</strong></p>\n<p>After finding all the scales that are formed on the quarter steps in the mentioned way, we write them in order, and as we started from C koron in Shur, here too we take the same note as the basis and go up with perfect fifth intervals and find the twelve quarter-tone modes that, together with the previous thirteen half-tone modes, form the twenty-five degrees of the twenty-four-part scale.</p>\n<p>As we observe, the thirteen modes above start from C koron and end with B suri, which is equivalent to it, and in this way all the modes whose tonic is a quarter-tone note have been found.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_027_mahur/webp/sornaz_027_mahoor_04.webp\" alt=\"Key signatures of the Mahur Dastgah in quarter-tone modes\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 4- Key signatures of the Mahur Dastgah in quarter-tone modes[/caption]\n<p>The order of the sequence of the twenty-four modes is as follows:</p>\n<p>1- Ab</p>\n<p>2– C koron</p>\n<p>3– Eb</p>\n<p>4– G koron</p>\n<p>5– Bb</p>\n<p>6– D koron</p>\n<p>7– F</p>\n<p>8– A koron</p>\n<p>9– C</p>\n<p>10– E koron</p>\n<p>11– G</p>\n<p>12– B koron</p>\n<p>13– D</p>\n<p>14– F suri</p>\n<p>15– A</p>\n<p>16– C suri</p>\n<p>17– E</p>\n<p>18– G suri</p>\n<p>19– B</p>\n<p>20– D suri</p>\n<p>21– F#</p>\n<p>22– A suri</p>\n<p>23– C#</p>\n<p>24– E suri</p>\n<p>25– G#.</p>\n<p>From paying attention to the above thirteen quarter-tone modes, we realize the following:</p>\n<p>1- The thirteen modes above in the table of the twenty-four modes have even numbers.</p>\n<p>2- If we also place the odd numbers between them, the twenty-four modes of the Mahur Dastgah are found, and comparing their combined signs is not without benefit.</p>\n<p>3- Between the above thirteen modes, the modes C koron and B suri are equivalent, and if we remove the B suri mode, which was not in the table, we have corrected the twelve modes we were missing.</p>\n<p>4- If we pay attention to the above modes, we see that the seventh degree of each mode has ascended a half step relative to the previous mode.</p>\n<p>This order was also in the previous twelve half-tone modes, so that when we took the note “C” as the starting point of movement and regularly went to the modes “G,” “D,” “A,” “E,” etc., the fourth degree of the previous mode, which was the seventh degree of the next mode, ascended a half step.</p>\n<p>5- All the above modes have seven combined signs.</p>\n<p>6- The difference of each mode with the next mode is in one combined sign.</p>\n<p>7- The combined signs in mode number one are composed of koron. In modes 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, and 7, they are composed of koron and suri. Number eight has only suri, and numbers 9, 10, 11, 12, and 13 are composed of suri and sharp.</p>\n<p>8- In each mode where the combined signs are composed of koron, the sequence of signs is like the sequence of flats. If it is composed only of “suri” or of “sharp-suri,” the sequence of signs is according to the sequence of sharps. But if it is composed of suri and koron, in the series of combined signs, there is a series of suri that has become like the sequence of sharps, and a series of koron that has become like the sequence of flats.</p>\n<p>9- In the above modes, gradually and in order, in each mode one koron is reduced and suri takes its place until all the combined signs turn into suri. Then each of the suri turns into the repeated sign sharp-suri, and this order continues until the end.</p>\n<p>After mentioning the above points, the rules for modal analysis in the Mahur Dastgah are stated as follows:</p>\n<p><strong>First rule –</strong> If we only have “koron,” the koron before the last is the tonic.</p>\n<p><strong>Second rule –</strong> If we only have “suri,” the last suri is the leading tone, and a half step above it is the tonic.</p>\n<p><strong>Third rule –</strong> If we have “suri” and “sharp-suri,” the last repeated sign, that is, the last “sharp-suri,” is the leading tone, and a half step above it is the tonic.</p>\n<p><strong>Fourth rule –</strong> If we have “koron” and “suri,” the tonic can be found in two ways:</p>\n<p>1- The koron before the last is the tonic.</p>\n<p>2- The last suri is the leading tone, and a half step above it is the tonic.</p>\n<p><strong>Result</strong> – Now we mix the above four rules with the previous rules of modal analysis of the major scale (or the Mahur Dastgah) and obtain the following rule, which is a summary:</p>\n<p><strong>In the modal analysis of the major scale or the Mahur Dastgah, the flat or koron before the last is the tonic, and the last sharp or suri is the leading tone.</strong></p>\n<p>The above rule applies to all twenty-four modes of the major scale or the Mahur Dastgah for modal analysis.</p>\n<h2>Auditory Quality of the Mahur Dastgah</h2>\n<p>Mahur is an Avaz with dignity and solemnity. When singing this Avaz, if the singer reads a suitable poem, it gives a special grandeur and magnificence to this Avaz. The Shekasteh and Delkash Avaz, which are used in Mahur, are more similar to other Eastern music melodies than the daramads and introductions of the Mahur Dastgah. The Iraq Avaz, which is usually the lower and high part of the Mahur Dastgah, is often accompanied by a lot of trills and is specific to the vocal artistry of the singer.</p>\n<p>The Mahur Dastgah is less popular than the Avazs, and the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b4%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Shur Dastgah</a> and Segah are more common because people have become accustomed to sad and sorrowful music. But from the time when cheerful Western music has become popular, tastes have turned toward this Avaz.</p>\n<p>Because the Mahur Dastgah and the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d8%a7%da%98%d9%88%d8%b1/\">major scale</a> are similar, the things that are attributed to the major scale can also be attributed to the Mahur Dastgah. The major scale, unlike the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%db%8c%d9%86%d9%88%d8%b1/\">minor scale</a>, is cheerful and exciting. For this reason, exciting and passionate military <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%85%D8%A7%D8%B1%D8%B4_(%D9%85%D9%88%D8%B3%DB%8C%D9%82%DB%8C)\">marches</a> are often composed in the major scale. So wherever the composer and player want to show an example of greatness, bravery, courage, and dignity, if the major scale or the Mahur Dastgah is chosen, it is far better than the minor scale or other dastgahs of Iranian music.</p>\n<p>Contrary to those who think that <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a> is always sad, the Mahur mode is cheerful and lively. However, the intensity of people’s interest in sorrowful music has not made it popular, and today, because the Iranian nation, which was withered and depressed for a long time, has found the joy of youth and has prepared itself to enter social life, it feels more need for cheerful and happy music. For this reason, some people also express disinterest in their own music, which has been excessively sad, and this itself has caused more credit for the Avazs of the Mahur Dastgah.</p>\n<p>If composers create new cheerful melodies that are suitable for the condition and circumstances of our today’s life in this mode, they will both provide the grounds for the progress of the music of the homeland and create pleasant and soul-refreshing melodies that represent the feelings of a hopeful and strong nation like the Iranian nation, which will cause the honor and importance of the music of our nation.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding the Mahur Dastgah, please share it in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this content with the Sornaz Music School website.</p>', '2023-06-30 06:42:09', 0, '2024-09-18 22:45:55', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1485, 'posts', 28, 'en', NULL, 'Dastgah-e Segah | Similarities and differences between Dastgah Segah and Avaz Afshari in the radif of Iranian music | Mayeh-shenasi of Segah in Ali-Naqi Vaziri\'s 24-quarter-tone scale', NULL, 'The Segah mode (Dastgah-e Segah) is one of the most distinctive systems in Persian classical music, characterized by its unique interval structure. Unlike typical scales, it is neither fully ascending nor descending. It is formed from two connected tetrachords similar to the Shur mode, with a strong emphasis on the third degree (the “shahed” or focal note), where most melodic resolutions occur.\n\nSegah shares notable similarities with the Afshari mode, but due to its structural independence and rich repertoire of melodic pieces (gushehs), it is considered a separate dastgah. Important gushehs include Zabol, Muyeh, Mokhalef, and Maghloob, each introducing specific modal shifts and expressive variations.\n\nIn modal analysis (based on the 24 quarter-tone system), Segah follows defined rules for identifying tonal centers:\n\nThe first koron (quarter-flat) or the last sori (quarter-sharp) helps determine the main note (shahed)\nThe tonic is derived relative to this focal note\n\nIn terms of emotional character, Segah is known as one of the most melancholic and expressive modes in Persian music. It conveys deep sorrow, longing, and introspection, making it especially powerful for expressing intense emotional states.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>The <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Segah Dastgah</span></strong></span> in the radif of Iranian music has a very different scale because this scale, due to its special fretting structure, neither has a complete descending form nor takes on an ascending form. The Segah Dastgah is composed of two connected Shur tetrachords with one whole step at its beginning, and for reasons that we will mention below, the Segah scale cannot be written in a way that is considered descending, and the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%af-%d8%af%d8%b1-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">cadences</a> of Segah occur on the third degree of the scale.</p>\n<p>The Segah scale has a great similarity to the Avaz of Afshari, which, if it were not for one or two special reasons, we should have considered the Avaz of Afshari as one of the related parts of Segah rather than Shur. We will explain these reasons completely and in detail below.</p>\n<p>In this article from the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>, we examine the structure of the Segah Dastgah, review the relationship of Segah with the Avaz of Afshari scale, and then present the gushehs and related parts of Segah. Next, we also examine the modal analysis of the Segah Dastgah in Ali-Naqi Vaziri’s 24-quarter-tone system and, at the end, express Ruhollah Khaleqi’s theory regarding the auditory quality of the Segah Dastgah, which is found in the second volume of his book “A Theoretical View of Music.”</p>\n<p>In another article, we also examine and analyze the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d9%88%d8%b4%d9%87-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b3%d9%87-%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">gushehs of the Segah Dastgah</a> in various radifs of Iranian music. Stay with us ...</p>\n<h2>Structure of the Segah Dastgah</h2>\n<p>The Segah Dastgah, as its name suggests, has been one of the old melodies of Iran because the words “Yegah,” “Dogah,” “Segah,” “Chahargah,” and “Panjgah” are mentioned in old books of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">music</a> in Iran. The words “Sheshgah” and “Haftgah” have also been mentioned rarely, and it is very likely that the above seven terms were the names of the degrees of the scale. Therefore, instead of tonic, we can use “Yegah” and instead of subtonic, “Dogah.”</p>\n<p>Some people also imagine that this Avaz is specific to the Turks. On the other hand, the Avaz of Segah is common among the people of the country of <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%AC%D9%85%D9%87%D9%88%D8%B1%DB%8C_%D8%A2%D8%B0%D8%B1%D8%A8%D8%A7%DB%8C%D8%AC%D8%A7%D9%86\">Azerbaijan</a> and the land of <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%82%D9%81%D9%82%D8%A7%D8%B2\">Caucasus</a>, and the Turks show more skill in singing this Avaz and create intense sadness and sorrow in the listeners when singing this Avaz. However, Persian speakers sing the Avaz of Segah in a different way that is more sorrowful and mournful.</p>\n<p>The Segah Dastgah has an independent <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">scale</a> that is different from other dastgahs and Avazs of Iranian music, including the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b4%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Shur Dastgah</a> and the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%87%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c%d9%88%d9%86/\">Homayun Dastgah</a>, and the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d8%a7%da%98%d9%88%d8%b1/\">major</a> and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%db%8c%d9%86%d9%88%d8%b1/\">minor</a> scales in Western music.</p>\n<p>The notes of the Segah scale in “G” are as follows.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_028_segah/webp/sornaz_028_segah_02.webp\" alt=\"Notes of the Segah Dastgah in “G”\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 1- Notes of the Segah Dastgah in “G”[/caption]\n<p>In <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a>, the scale of the Segah Dastgah is composed of two connected Shur tetrachords (for more familiarity with the tetrachords of Iranian music, read the article “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Theory of Iranian Music, Examination of the Theory of Tetrachords and the 4 Basic Tetrachords in Iranian Music</a>”).</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_028_segah/webp/sornaz_028_segah_001.webp\" alt=\"Interval pattern of the Segah Dastgah scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 2- Interval pattern of the Segah Dastgah scale[/caption]\n<p>In the figure above, the witness note of the Segah Dastgah is marked in red.</p>\n<p>The intervals of the degrees of the Segah scale relative to the tonic are as follows: major second, half-augmented third, perfect fourth, perfect fifth, half-augmented sixth, minor seventh, and octave.</p>\n<p>So the tetrachords of the Segah Dastgah scale are unequal (by unequal tetrachords we mean the interval between the first to fourth degrees as the first tetrachord and the interval between the fifth to eighth degrees) because the first tetrachord is composed of one major second and two half-augmented second intervals, but the second tetrachord has two half-augmented seconds and one major second. The interval between the tetrachords of the Segah Dastgah scale is the inversion of each other, and the interval between the two tetrachords is also a major second.</p>\n<h2>The Witness Note of the Segah Dastgah</h2>\n<p>The witness note of the Segah Dastgah is the third degree of the scale, and the importance of this degree in the melodies is greater than the tonic because all the melodies of Segah cadence on this degree, and the tonic has no importance or credit in the melodies of Segah. With this arrangement, the question arises that why have we not started the scale of the Segah Dastgah from the witness note so that the third degree of the Segah scale is both the witness and considered the tonic?</p>\n<p>To answer this question, we must refer to the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d9%81%d8%b4%d8%a7%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Avaz of Afshari</a> scale and what we wrote about it, because in the Segah Dastgah as well, if we ignore the variable note of Afshari and even listen carefully to the Daramad of the Avaz of Afshari, we see that neither the intervals of the Afshari and Segah scales differ nor is there a clear difference in their character. But according to the reasons we mentioned before, we have considered Afshari as one of the related parts of Shur. Now we must see why, with this arrangement, we have not considered Segah like Afshari as one of the branches of Shur. For this purpose, the following reasons must be mentioned:</p>\n<p><strong>First reason</strong> – The Segah Dastgah has many gushehs, and it cannot be considered like Afshari as a secondary Avaz and counted as one of the branches of Shur.</p>\n<p><strong>Second reason</strong> – The gushehs and characters of the Segah Dastgah are more similar to the Chahargah Dastgah than to the Avaz of Afshari and Shur, and if we consider it as one of the parts of Chahargah, it is closer to its nature. Despite this aspect, we have not destroyed its independence and have not considered it as one of the related parts of Chahargah, so there is no reason that, despite the many differences it has with Shur, it should be considered one of its related parts!</p>\n<p><strong>Third reason</strong> – The Segah Dastgah has no direct relationship with Shur and is more similar to Afshari, so either Afshari should be considered part of Segah or vice versa. In the first case, the reasons mentioned in the article about the Avaz of Afshari will be contradicted, and in the second case, the first reason mentioned above will be eliminated.</p>\n<p>So it is better that the Segah Dastgah, because it has a special scale and a special character, should be a separate mode, as it is also the case in the custom of musicians. Only the above points and objections are from a scientific point of view, otherwise most musicians of today may be surprised how there is a relationship between Afshari and Segah.</p>\n<p>Anyway, in summary, it can be said that Afshari, from a scientific point of view, may be considered part of the Segah Dastgah, and from the point of view of character and way of cadence and having a variable note (which makes its degrees similar to Shur), it is one of the related parts of the Shur mode.</p>\n<h2>Related Parts and Gushehs of the Segah Dastgah</h2>\n<p>The Segah Dastgah, after stopping on the witness note, makes a complete stop on the fifth degree of its scale, and its witness becomes the fifth degree and takes on a special character. At this point, we enter the <strong>Zabol</strong> Avaz, which is one of the gushehs of Segah.</p>\n<p>Another gusheh of the Segah Dastgah is the <strong>Muye</strong> gusheh, whose witness note is the sixth degree of the Segah scale, and this Avaz is very sad and sorrowful.</p>\n<p>The two above Avazs, because they are in the degrees of the Segah Dastgah, are not modal changes. The important change that occurs in the Segah scale happens in the <strong>Mokhalef</strong> gusheh.</p>\n<p>In the Mokhalef Avaz, the first degree of the Segah Dastgah becomes a leading tone, but the interval between the leading tone and the tonic is a half-augmented second, and the witness note of Segah also falls a quarter step, and as a result, the Segah mode turns into a mode of the same name that is completely different from it and, as we will say below, is similar to the old Avaz of Isfahan.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_028_segah/webp/sornaz_028_segah_03.webp\" alt=\"Notes of the Mokhalef scale of Segah in “G”\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 4- Notes of the Mokhalef scale of Segah in “G”[/caption]\n<p>As can be seen, the above scale is similar to the Isfahani scale that was common in the past, and if we raise only its seventh degree another quarter step (so that the note “F” becomes sharp), the old Isfahan scale will be created.</p>\n<p>The stop of the Mokhalef Avaz is first on the tonic and then on the sixth degree, and these two degrees gain importance in the Mokhalef scale.</p>\n<p>After the Mokhalef Avaz, another gusheh is also common in the Segah Dastgah, and that is the <strong>Maghlub</strong> Avaz, which is played after Mokhalef in such a way that it starts from the tonic of Mokhalef and stops on the tonic of Segah and then returns again to Mokhalef.</p>\n<p>Therefore, to return from Mokhalef to the Segah Dastgah, it is only enough that the seventh degree of the Mokhalef scale falls a quarter step and turns into the form it was in the Segah scale. After that, entering Segah is easy, and it is usually played at the time of the cadence of the Muye Avaz that was mentioned earlier, and after a short stop on the sixth degree of the Segah scale, we cadence on the witness of the Segah Dastgah.</p>\n<p>Now we can say that the Segah Dastgah stops on the following degrees:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Third degree (witness): for the Daramad Avaz and the final cadences of phrases.</li>\n  <li>Fifth degree (dominant): for the Zabol gusheh.</li>\n  <li>Sixth degree (subdominant): for the Muye gusheh.</li>\n  <li>First degree (tonic): for the Mokhalef gusheh.</li>\n  <li>Third degree in one octave higher than the Daramad: for the Maghlub gusheh.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So from this it becomes clear that usually no stop is made on the second and seventh degrees of the Segah Dastgah.</p>\n<p><strong>For familiarity with Iranian and world instruments and knowing the different parts of each of these instruments and learning practical points for choosing the best possible instrument, study the articles related to the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%b3%d8%a7%d8%b2/\">instrument buying guides</a>.</strong></p>\n<h2>Modal Analysis of the Segah Dastgah</h2>\n<p>To find the twenty-four modes of the Segah Dastgah, as we did in Shur and Mahur, we take the note “Ab” as the starting point and go up from it with half-augmented third intervals, and the twenty-fifth note will be “G#,” which is equivalent to “Ab.” After paying attention to these twenty-five notes, we observe that the odd numbers have a perfect fifth interval with each other, and also the even numbers have a perfect fifth interval with each other. So if we start once from “Ab” and go up with perfect fifth intervals, the following thirteen modes (the modes of the chromatic scale) are obtained:</p>\n<p>Ab – Eb – Bb – F – C – G – D – A – E – B – F# – C# – G#.</p>\n<p>Now, if we take each of the above notes as the tonic and form a Segah scale on it, and then write the combined signs of the above modes, we realize the following points:</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_028_segah/webp/sornaz_028_segah_04.webp\" alt=\"Key signatures of the Segah Dastgah in chromatic modes\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 5- Key signatures of the Segah Dastgah in chromatic modes[/caption]\n<p>The properties of the above thirteen modes are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Six modes have two koron signs, and if there is a sign before them, it is flat.</li>\n  <li>Six modes have two suri signs, and if there is a sign before them, it is sharp.</li>\n  <li>Always the last two combined signs are quarter-tone (koron or suri).</li>\n  <li>If we start from the Segah of “G,” which has two koron signs (B and E), and go up from it with perfect fourth intervals, each new mode has one more combined sign, and the koron notes gradually move forward in the total of combined signs. For example, if in the mode “G” the notes “B” and “E” are koron, in the mode “C,” which is a fourth higher than it, the notes “E” and “A” become koron.</li>\n  <li>If we start from the Segah of “A” and go up from it with perfect fifth intervals, the suri modes are found, in which each new mode finds one more combined sign, and the suri notes gradually move forward in the total of combined signs. For example, if in the mode “A” the notes “F” and “C” are suri, in the mode “E,” which is a fifth higher than it, the notes “C” and “G” become suri.</li>\n  <li>Between the koron and suri modes, or in other words between the mode “G” and “A,” the Segah of “D” is located, which has both quarter-tone signs, that is, it has one koron and one suri.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>From observing the combined signs of the modes of the Segah Dastgah, we conclude that two modes of the same name, Shur and Segah, differ in three notes. For example, Shur of “D” has “Bb” and “E koron,” and Segah of “D” has “B koron” and “F suri,” and the difference of three notes is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>In Shur, B was flat, and in Segah it has become koron.</li>\n  <li>In Shur, E was koron, and in Segah it has become natural.</li>\n  <li>In Shur, F was natural, and in Segah it has become suri.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The above points can be summarized as follows:</p>\n<p>In the thirteen modes of the Segah Dastgah that are formed on the half steps of the chromatic scale, the last signs are always two koron or two suri. If it is koron, before it is flat, and if it is suri, before it is sharp. The only exception is in Segah of “D,” which has one koron and one suri, and altogether has two quarter-tone combined signs.</p>\n<p>The way of modal analysis of the above modes is as follows:</p>\n<p><strong>First rule</strong> – If the combined signs are composed of “koron” or “flat and koron,” the first koron is the witness note of the Segah Dastgah.</p>\n<p>For example, when we have three combined signs (that is, one flat and two koron in this order: Bb – E koron – A koron), the first koron, that is “E koron,” is the witness, and a half-augmented third lower than it, the tonic is found. So the tonic of this mode is the note “C.”</p>\n<p><strong>Second rule</strong> – If the combined signs are composed of “suri” or “suri and sharp,” the last suri is the witness.</p>\n<p>For example, if we have five combined signs mixed from sharp and suri, according to the rules we mentioned earlier, it is as follows: F# – C# – G# – D suri and A suri. So the last suri, that is “A,” is the witness note of the Segah Dastgah, and a half-augmented third lower than it, that is “F#,” will be the tonic.</p>\n<p><strong>Third rule</strong> – If the combined sign is only composed of one koron and one suri, the suri note, that is “F suri,” is the witness of the Segah Dastgah, and a half-augmented third lower than it, that is “D,” will be the tonic.</p>\n<p>Summary – Now the rules can be summarized as follows:</p>\n<p><strong>First – The last koron is the seventh degree, and one whole step above it is the tonic.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Second – The last suri is the second degree, and one whole step below it is the tonic.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> Do not be surprised why we do not write the combined signs of the twenty-four modes together and consecutively, and we do this work in two stages (once for the twelve half steps of the chromatic scale and once for the twelve quarter steps between them).</p>\n<p>Because if we write all twenty-four modes consecutively, since the interval of each mode to the next mode is a half-augmented third, the difference in combined signs is large, but with this arrangement, because the modes go up with perfect fifth intervals, there is always a difference in one or two signs, and the combined signs of the modes become more regular, and this method is easier for study and learning.</p>\n<p>If you write the twenty-four modes of the Segah Dastgah starting from “Ab” and following each other with half-augmented third intervals, you will notice this point.</p>\n<h3>Quarter-Tone Modes</h3>\n<p>Just as we started from the note “Ab” and went up with perfect fifth intervals and found the modes that were formed on the degrees of the twelve-part chromatic scale, now we start from the note “C koron” and ascend with perfect fifth intervals to find the modes that are formed on the twelve quarter steps, and then if we place each of the previous modes among the new modes, we will observe that we have formed one Segah mode on all the degrees of the twenty-four-part scale. In this way, the twelve modes that are formed on the quarter steps are as follows (of course, the thirteenth mode is equivalent to the first mode):</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_028_segah/webp/sornaz_028_segah_005.webp\" alt=\"Key signatures of the Segah Dastgah in quarter-tone modes\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 6- Key signatures of the Segah Dastgah in quarter-tone modes[/caption]\n<p>C koron – G koron – D koron – A koron – E koron – B koron – F suri – C suri – G suri – D suri – A suri – E suri – B suri</p>\n<p>By paying attention to the above quarter-tone modes of the Segah Dastgah, we realize the following points:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The koron signs gradually move forward until we reach the Segah of “E koron,” where we reach the first suri.</li>\n  <li>Then the number of suri signs gradually increases one by one, and the number of koron signs decreases until the Segah of “G suri,” where there is no more koron.</li>\n  <li>Then gradually in each mode, one sharp is added until the last two modes find the signs “sharp suri.”</li>\n  <li>The combined signs of the first three modes are composed of flat and koron.</li>\n  <li>The combined signs of the last four modes are composed of suri and sharp.</li>\n  <li>The fourth mode has only koron, and the ninth mode has only suri.</li>\n  <li>Modes 5, 6, 7, and 8 have both koron and suri. These modes in fact have two series of combined signs.</li>\n  <li>In the modes that are composed only of koron or of flat and koron, the sequence of combined signs is like the sequence of flats.</li>\n  <li>In the modes that are composed of suri or of sharp and suri, the sequence of combined signs is like the sequence of sharps.</li>\n  <li>In the modes that are composed of suri and koron, the sequence of suri signs is like the sequence of sharps, and the sequence of koron signs is like the sequence of flats.</li>\n  <li>The six modes 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, and 9 have 5 combined signs.</li>\n  <li>The two modes 3 and 10 have six combined signs.</li>\n  <li>The rest of the modes, that is, the five modes 1, 2, 11, 12, and 13, have seven combined signs.</li>\n  <li>In all the above thirteen modes, there are five quarter-tone signs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>After studying and observing the above points, we state the rules for modal analysis of the mentioned modes:</p>\n<p><strong>First rule</strong> – In modes that have koron, the last koron is the seventh degree of the Segah Dastgah.</p>\n<p><strong>Second rule</strong> – In modes that have suri, the last suri is the second degree of the Segah Dastgah.</p>\n<p><strong>Third rule</strong> – In modes that have both suri and koron, the mode can be recognized in two ways:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The last koron is the seventh degree of the Segah Dastgah.</li>\n  <li>The last suri is the second degree of the Segah Dastgah.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Fourth rule</strong> – In modes that introduce the seventh degree of the Segah Dastgah, we go up one whole step from the seventh degree to find the tonic, and in modes where the second degree is determined, we go down one whole step from the second degree to find the tonic.</p>\n<p>Summary – Now the rules can be summarized as follows:</p>\n<p><strong>First – The last koron is the seventh degree, and one whole step above it is the tonic.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Second – The last suri is the second degree, and one whole step below it is the tonic.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> Do not be surprised why we do not write the combined signs of the twenty-four modes together and consecutively, and we do this work in two stages (once for the twelve half steps of the chromatic scale and once for the twelve quarter steps between them).</p>\n<p>Because if we write all twenty-four modes consecutively, since the interval of each mode to the next mode is a half-augmented third, the difference in combined signs is large, but with this arrangement, because the modes go up with perfect fifth intervals, there is always a difference in one or two signs, and the combined signs of the modes become more regular, and this method is easier for study and learning.</p>\n<p>If you write the twenty-four modes of the Segah Dastgah starting from “Ab” and following each other with half-augmented third intervals, you will notice this point.</p>\n<h2>Auditory Quality of the Segah Dastgah</h2>\n<p>Segah is an infinitely sad and sorrowful Avaz, and its soul-burning laments uproot the foundation of a person and its burning sparks burn sensitive people in the fire of separation, and its flames scatter the harvest of human existence to the wind.</p>\n<p>Segah is a collection of prolonged burning sighs and sad and sorrowful laments. It speaks of the secrets and whispers of lovers suffering from separation and of the misfortune and helplessness of the poor and the weak. Its laments are effective and like a helpless person who, out of intense fear, terror, and horror, cries and laments from the bottom of his heart. One can well understand that the sad Avaz of the Segah Dastgah is a souvenir of the misfortunes and pains of our ancestors, and it can also be said to what extent our thoughts were in harmony and agreement with our fathers.</p>\n<p>For this reason, although we have forgotten those bad periods of misfortune and the days of captivity and slavery and live in a period when our national independence is established, we still enjoy hearing a melody that makes us sad and depressed. The reason is the same: life is a mixture of sadness and joy, and music, as a complete representative of human feelings, and since a person cannot always be happy and cheerful because of the events of time, at the right time, just as he needs cheerful music, he also enjoys sad music.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding the Segah Dastgah, please share it in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this content with the Sornaz Music School website.</p>', '2023-07-01 15:26:23', 0, '2025-02-07 09:05:22', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1486, 'posts', 29, 'en', NULL, 'Dastgah-e Chahargah | The most unique dastgah in the radif of Iranian music | Mayeh-shenasi in Ali-Naqi Vaziri\'s 24-division scale', NULL, 'The Chahargah mode is one of the most significant and complex systems in Persian music, known for its heroic and powerful character. It is widely used in epic storytelling and poetic traditions that emphasize bravery and valor. Structurally, it is built on two symmetrical tetrachords and is unique because it functions both ascending and descending, featuring leading tones in both directions.\n\nFrom a theoretical perspective, Chahargah combines characteristics of both the major scale and minor scale, making it a highly comprehensive and balanced musical system. It includes distinctive intervals such as augmented seconds, which are essential to the identity of Persian music.\n\nThe mode contains various melodic sections (gushehs) like Daramad, Zabol, Mokhalef, Muyeh, Hesar, and Mansouri, each contributing to expressive diversity. It also allows modulation to related tonal centers (fourth and fifth degrees), demonstrating notable flexibility.\n\nChahargah shares close similarities with Segah mode, often considered its relative counterpart. Sonically, it encompasses a wide emotional range—from grandeur and joy to sorrow and introspection—making it one of the richest and most expressive modes in Persian music.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>The <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Chahargah Dastgah</span></strong></span> in the radif of Iranian music has an epic-like character and is usually used, due to this quality, in Shahnameh recitation and poems like it that speak of the bravery and heroism of Iranian champions and heroes. The Chahargah Dastgah, due to having two identical Chahargah tetrachords and possessing a leading tone in both ascending and descending forms, is considered the most unique scale in Iranian and world music because it has an ascending character like major scales and the Mahur Dastgah, and also takes on a descending character like minor scales and the Shur Dastgah.</p>\n<p>In the Chahargah Dastgah, cadences on all its degrees are common, and among them, the fewest cadences occur on the second and seventh degrees because they are very close to the tonic, and the attraction and pull of the tonic of the Chahargah Dastgah is so strong that it does not allow a firm and solid cadence on these degrees. Usually, temporary stops occur on these degrees, and in the final cadence, we settle on the tonic.</p>\n<p>In this article from the Sornaz website, we examine the structure of the Chahargah Dastgah, review the relationship of Chahargah with major and minor scales, and then talk about the importance of the Chahargah scale. Next, we review the gushehs and related parts of Chahargah and pay attention to its similarity with Segah and the specific cadences in these two dastgahs.</p>\n<p>Finally, we examine the modal analysis of the Chahargah Dastgah in the 24-quarter-tone system of Ali-Naqi Vaziri and express the opinion of the prominent Iranian musician Ruhollah Khaleqi regarding the auditory quality of this dastgah.</p>\n<p>In another article, we also examine and analyze the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d9%88%d8%b4%d9%87-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%da%86%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">gushehs of the Chahargah Dastgah</a> in various radifs of Iranian music. Stay with us ...</p>\n<h2>Structure of the Chahargah Dastgah</h2>\n<p>From a scientific point of view, the Chahargah Dastgah is the most important dastgah in the radif of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a>. Its <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">scale</a> is descending like the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b4%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Shur Dastgah</a> and the Homayun Dastgah, and ascending like the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%85%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Mahur Dastgah</a> and the Avaz of Isfahan, because it has a leading tone in both ascending and descending forms.</p>\n<p>The notes of the Chahargah scale in “C” are as shown in the image below.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_029_chahargah/webp/sornaz_029_chahargah_02.webp\" alt=\"Notes of the Chahargah Dastgah in “C”\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 1- Notes of the Chahargah Dastgah in “C”[/caption]\n<p>Among the scales of Iranian music, the Mahur Dastgah is like the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d8%a7%da%98%d9%88%d8%b1/\">major scale</a>, and the Homayun Dastgah is somewhat similar to the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%db%8c%d9%86%d9%88%d8%b1/\">minor scale</a>. The specific scales of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">Iranian music</a> are the Shur, Segah, and Chahargah Dastgahs. Among these, the Chahargah Dastgah is the most important from a scientific point of view for the following reasons:</p>\n<p>1) Its tetrachords, unlike those of the Shur and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b3%d9%87-%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">Segah Dastgahs</a> (the Segah Dastgah is composed of two Shur tetrachords, but because its tetrachords are connected, it does not have a symmetrical form), are equal.</p>\n<p>2) The Chahargah scale is both ascending and descending and has a leading tone in both forms, whereas the Shur scale is descending and has no leading tone when ascending. The Segah scale also has a special form that is neither similar to an ascending scale nor a descending scale and has no leading tone in either form. The Mahur scale is ascending and has no leading tone in the descending form.</p>\n<p>3) If the Chahargah Dastgah scale is ascending, its fifth degree is the dominant, and when descending, it also has a natural dominant. The fourth degree of the scale in the ascending form and in any case, the interval between the dominant and the tonic forms a perfect fifth because its fourth and fifth degrees are perfect.</p>\n<p>In <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a>, the Chahargah Dastgah is composed of two separate Chahargah tetrachords (for more familiarity with the tetrachords of Iranian music, read the article “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Theory of Iranian Music, Examination of the Theory of Tetrachords and the 4 Basic Tetrachords in Iranian Music</a>”).</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_029_chahargah/webp/sornaz_029_chahargah_001.webp\" alt=\"Interval pattern of the Chahargah Dastgah scale\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 2- Interval pattern of the Chahargah Dastgah scale[/caption]\n<p>In the figure above, the tonic note of the Chahargah Dastgah is marked in red and the tonic note in green.</p>\n<h2>Relationship Between the Chahargah Dastgah and the Major Scale</h2>\n<p>The Chahargah Dastgah is similar to the major scale in several respects:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Like the major scale, it is ascending.</li>\n  <li>Its third interval, like the major scale, is major, and its tonic chord, like the tonic chord of the major scale, is composed of two third intervals, one major and the other minor (C – E – G).</li>\n  <li>Its tetrachords, like those of the major scale, are equal, and the last interval of the tetrachords is a half step.</li>\n  <li>Its seventh interval, like the major scale, is major, and the interval between the leading tone and the tonic is a half step.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Relationship Between the Chahargah Dastgah and the Minor Scale</h2>\n<p>The Chahargah Dastgah is similar to the minor scale in several respects:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>The minor scale is sometimes ascending, like the harmonic minor scale or the melodic minor scale in ascending form, and sometimes descending, like the melodic minor scale in descending form. The Chahargah Dastgah simultaneously has both ascending and descending forms.</li>\n  <li>The augmented second intervals that are located in the middle of each tetrachord are similar to the augmented second interval that is in the middle of the second tetrachord of the minor scale.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Importance of the Chahargah Dastgah</h2>\n<p>If we pay close attention, we see that the Chahargah Dastgah has some properties of the major scale and some properties of the minor scale, and as a result, it has formed a mode that is far better than the major and minor scales. Because it is superior to the major and minor scales in several respects:</p>\n<p>1) The Chahargah Dastgah is both ascending and descending, but the major scale is only ascending, and the melodic minor scale has differences in its degrees when ascending and descending, whereas the Chahargah scale, without any difference in its intervals and degrees, has both ascending and descending forms.</p>\n<p>2) The augmented second interval of the harmonic minor scale, which causes difference and inequality of the tetrachords, has become a half-augmented second in Chahargah and exists in both tetrachords, and therefore has not caused a difference between the tetrachords. For this reason, the scale of the Chahargah Dastgah is completely correct from a scientific and natural point of view.</p>\n<p>Now, if we say that the Chahargah Dastgah is a complete and comprehensive representative of all the states and qualities of Iranian music, it should not cause surprise because with a little attention, one can realize that this scale encompasses all the qualities of our music:</p>\n<p>1) It is an example of the Iranian descending scale that goes to the tonic with a half-augmented second interval.</p>\n<p>2) It has augmented second intervals in both tetrachords, and these intervals are very important in Iranian music. For example, one of these intervals also exists in the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%87%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c%d9%88%d9%86/\">Homayun Dastgah</a> and the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%a7%d8%b5%d9%81%d9%87%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Avaz of Isfahan</a>.</p>\n<p>These intervals should not be considered like the augmented second interval of the minor scale, which is used less in melodies and is said to be difficult for singers, and to avoid making mistakes, the minor scale is written in different forms.</p>\n<p>But the half-augmented second interval of the Chahargah scale is completely natural, and Iranians also like it. For this reason, because the augmented second interval exists in the minor scale and is similar to the half-augmented second, melodies composed in the minor scale are more pleasing to us.</p>\n<p>3) Because it has two leading tones, it resolves to the tonic from both sides, and this state especially gives special importance to the Chahargah Dastgah when harmonizing melodies (creating harmony).</p>\n<p>So, it can be said that the Chahargah Dastgah by itself is a comprehensive representative of the perfection, importance, charm, beauty, correctness, and proportion of our music.</p>\n<h2>Related Parts and Gushehs of the Chahargah Dastgah</h2>\n<p>As mentioned in the article about the Shur Dastgah, in Shur we also go up to the fourth degree, but unlike Chahargah, we do not go up to the fifth Shur, and this itself is a reason that Chahargah has an ascending and descending scale.</p>\n<p>Because the Shur Dastgah, which was descending, goes to the real dominant of the scale or the fourth upper Shur, but because Chahargah actually has two dominants (the fourth and fifth degrees), it can transfer to both. Therefore, as can be seen, the modal changes of Chahargah, although in accordance with scientific rules, nature has made the hands and fingers of Iranian players familiar with it, and this itself is a reason for the correctness of the famous English proverb whose translation is: “Let nature be your teacher.”</p>\n<p>7) The “Maghlub” Avaz, which we mentioned in Segah, is also common in Chahargah, and after a short stop on the “Hangam,” it returns again to the opposing witness note of Chahargah (the sixth degree) and from there goes toward the tonic. The recent Avaz is also not a modal change or dastgah change and is in the main degrees of the Chahargah Dastgah.</p>\n<p>So, in the Avaz of Chahargah, stops are made in order on the first degree (Daramad), third (Zabol), and fourth (Muye), fifth (Hesar), sixth (Mokhalef), and eighth (Mansuri) degrees, and only stops on the second and seventh degrees are not common.</p>\n<p><strong>For familiarity with Iranian and world instruments and knowing the different parts of each of these instruments and learning practical points for choosing the best possible instrument, study the articles related to the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%b3%d8%a7%d8%b2/\">instrument buying guides</a>.</strong></p>\n<h2>Relationship Between the Segah Dastgah and the Chahargah Dastgah</h2>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The Chahargah Dastgah has special stops, some of which are modal changes and some are in the main mode, as follows:</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">1) The first stop is on the tonic, which is known as the </span><b>Daramad</b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> of Chahargah.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">2) The second stop is on the middle note of the first tetrachord, i.e., the third degree, which is called </span><b>Zabol</b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">3) The third stop is on the subdominant, which is known as </span><b>Mokhalef</b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> and has the character of the Homayun Dastgah. From there, one can enter the Homayun Dastgah because, due to the equality of the second tetrachord of Chahargah and the first tetrachord of Homayun, when we stop on the sixth degree of Chahargah, it is as if we have stopped on the second degree of Homayun, which is its witness note. It should be noted that the Mokhalef of Chahargah differs from the Mokhalef of Segah because the Mokhalef of Chahargah is in the degrees of Homayun, and the Mokhalef of Segah is in the degrees of old Isfahan (whose leading tone has a half-augmented second interval with the tonic).</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">4) Another stop occurs on the Hangam, which is in fact the </span><b>Oj</b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> of the Avaz of Chahargah and is called </span><b>Mansuri</b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. The character of this Avaz is very sad and sorrowful. </span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Therefore, stopping on the first, third, sixth, and eighth degrees does not constitute a modal change or dastgah change and is in the degrees of the Chahargah Dastgah, but when stopping on the subdominant, it is possible to pass through a few usual notes and enter Homayun.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">5) Stopping on the dominant is known as </span><b>Hesar</b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, and Hesar is in fact a modal change of Chahargah to a Chahargah a perfect fifth higher than the main Chahargah, and here the Chahargah scale is transferred a perfect fifth higher. So if we are in Chahargah of C, in Hesar we will go to Chahargah of G, whose degrees compared to each other are as follows:</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Chahargah of C: C – D koron – E – F – G – A koron – B – C</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Chahargah of G: G – A koron – B – C – D – E koron – F# – G</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">When playing Hesar, usually not all degrees of the upper fifth mode are shown, and more stops are made on the first, second, sixth, and seventh degrees of the new mode (Hesar), i.e., on the fifth, sixth, third, and fourth degrees of the main mode, with the difference that the fourth degree of the main mode has risen a half step, and its third has also fallen a quarter step. But because by means of Hesar the way of modal change to the upper fifth is opened, it is possible to show all the degrees of the new mode and then lower the leading tone of the upper fifth Chahargah Dastgah by a half step and also raise its sixth degree by a quarter step and enter the main Chahargah.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">6) Another stop of Chahargah that is considered a modal change is stopping on the subdominant of the scale, which is called </span><b>Muye</b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, and by this means one goes to the upper fourth Chahargah or the lower fifth. For example, from Chahargah of C one can enter Chahargah of F, whose degrees compared to each other are as follows:</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Chahargah of C: C – D koron – E – F – G – A koron – B – C</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Chahargah of F: F – G koron – A – Bb – C – D koron – E – F</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In this Chahargah, more stops are made on the first, second, third, and seventh degrees, which are the fourth, fifth, sixth, and third degrees of the main mode, with the difference that the fifth degree of the main mode has fallen a quarter step and its sixth degree has also risen a quarter step. But because the second tetrachord of the new Chahargah (Muye) is equal to the first tetrachord of the main Chahargah, after showing the above notes, they enter the common tetrachord, which is the first tetrachord of the main Chahargah, and then enter the main Chahargah.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Anyway, because the Muye Avaz opens the way of modal change to the upper fourth scale or the lower fifth, it is possible to move in all the degrees of the new Chahargah and then, by showing the fifth and seventh degrees of the main Chahargah that have changed, enter the main Chahargah.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">So Chahargah is the only mode that transfers to its upper fourth and fifth modes, and these two modes, as we know, are among the neighboring modes of the first group that are considered the most natural modal changes.</span> If we listen carefully to the Avaz of Segah and Chahargah and study both modes and consider the similarity of the related parts of each in terms of name and character, we see that these two dastgahs are close to each other, and one can consider the Segah scale, provided that we start from the witness note, as the minor version of the Chahargah scale.</p>\n<p>Among the related parts of Chahargah, Mansuri and Hesar are not common in Segah, and the rest, namely Zabol, Mokhalef, Muye, and Maghlub, are played in both, and although these four Avazs in each of these two scales have their own specific degrees, their difference is minor and their character is almost the same. For example, the Mokhalef of Chahargah is in the degrees of Homayun, and the Mokhalef of Segah is in the degrees of old Isfahan (whose leading tone has a half-augmented second interval with the tonic). The Muye of Chahargah is the means of modal change to the upper fourth Chahargah, but the Muye of Segah is in the main degrees of Segah.</p>\n<p>Although Zabol of Segah and Chahargah are not means of modal change, they differ from each other in terms of intervals, which becomes clear in practice.</p>\n<p>The Maghlub of Segah and Chahargah also have little difference in terms of intervals, and their character is the same.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, the four common Avazs between Segah and Chahargah are formed on different degrees.</p>\n<p>For example, the witness note of Zabol in Segah is the fifth degree, and in Chahargah it is the third degree.</p>\n<p>The witness note of Mokhalef in Segah is the first degree, and in Chahargah it is the sixth degree.</p>\n<p>The witness note of Muye in Segah is the sixth degree, and in Chahargah it is the fourth degree.</p>\n<p>The witness note of Maghlub in Segah is the third degree, and in Chahargah it is the first degree.</p>\n<p>The reason for this difference is that we did not start the Segah scale from the witness note, and if we consider the witness note as the first degree, i.e., the tonic of the scale, we observe that Zabol of Segah, like Chahargah, and Maghlub are on the third degree, Mokhalef on the sixth degree, Muye on the fourth degree, and Maghlub on the first degree. But as we said, for reasons mentioned in the article about the Segah Dastgah, we did not start the Segah scale from the witness note, and for this reason, there appears to be a difference in the witness notes of Segah and Chahargah.</p>\n<p>To make this matter clearer, we must compare the Segah and Chahargah scales, and for this purpose, as an example, we write the Chahargah scale of “D” and the Segah scale of “B koron,” whose tonic of one is common with the witness note of the other.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_029_chahargah/webp/sornaz_029_chahargah_03.webp\" alt=\"Comparison of the notes of the Chahargah scale of “D” and the Segah scale of “B koron”\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 3- Comparison of the notes of the Chahargah scale of “D” and the Segah scale of “B koron”[/caption]\n<p>Chahargah of D: D – E koron – F# – G – A – B koron – C# – D</p>\n<p>Segah of B koron: D – E koron – F suri – G – A koron – B koron – C suri – D</p>\n<p>Of course, it should be noted that in the above example, we started the Segah scale from the witness note so that it can be more easily compared with the Chahargah scale. As can be seen, the two scales above have five notes in common, which we have shown with a dotted line, and differ in three notes. So whenever we want to convert the Chahargah scale to Segah, we must lower its third, fifth, and seventh degrees by a quarter step and consider the tonic of Chahargah as the witness of Segah.</p>\n<p>So it is not without reason that we consider Segah as the minor or small version of the Chahargah scale because whenever the third and seventh of Chahargah are major, they become minor, and its fifth, which is perfect, becomes less perfect, and if we start this scale a half-augmented third lower than the tonic of Chahargah, a Segah scale is created that is related to the Chahargah scale. So we can consider Chahargah as the major scale and Segah as its relative minor.</p>\n<h2>Specific Cadences of the Chahargah Dastgah</h2>\n<p>One of the characteristics of the Avaz of Chahargah is that at the beginning and cadence, the subdominant note takes on the character of the dominant and resolves to the tonic, which is also the case in Segah. But if in Chahargah we want to consider the subdominant as the tonic and the tonic as the witness, the Chahargah scale becomes incorrect because the interval from the subdominant to the tonic is a half-augmented third, and the interval from the tonic to the middle note is a major third, the sum of these two intervals making an augmented fifth. So if we had considered the subdominant as the tonic, Chahargah would have become augmented, and the scale would not have had a correct scientific dominant. (For this reason, we do not write the Segah Dastgah and the Avaz of Afshari from the witness note.)</p>\n<p>Whereas in Segah, the interval between the note that has the character of the subdominant of Chahargah at the time of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%af-%d8%af%d8%b1-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">cadence</a> (which is the tonic) and the witness, and the interval between the witness and the fifth degree of the scale, is a half-augmented third, and the sum of two half-augmented thirds makes a perfect fifth. For this reason, we have considered the note that has the character of the subdominant of Chahargah at the time of cadence as the tonic so that its interval with the fifth degree of the scale becomes perfect.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_029_chahargah/webp/sornaz_029_chahargah_04.webp\" alt=\"Comparison of the specific cadences of Segah in “G” and Chahargah in “C”\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 4- Comparison of the specific cadences of Segah in “G” and Chahargah in “C”[/caption]\n<h2>Modal Analysis of the Chahargah Dastgah</h2>\n<p>Like other Iranian modes, we first find the modes that are formed on the twelve half steps of the chromatic scale in such a way that, like Shur, Mahur, and Segah, we take the note “Ab” as the starting point and go up from it with fifth intervals until the thirteen modes of the Chahargah Dastgah are found.</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_029_chahargah/webp/sornaz_029_chahargah_05.webp\" alt=\"Key signatures of the Chahargah Dastgah in chromatic modes\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 5- Key signatures of the Chahargah Dastgah in chromatic modes[/caption]\n<p>Among the thirteen modes of the Chahargah Dastgah, the mode “G#” is equivalent to “Ab,” so in reality, on the twelve half steps of the chromatic scale, twelve modes are formed:</p>\n<p>Ab – Eb – Bb – F – C – G – D – A – E – B – F# – C# – G#.</p>\n<p>As can be seen, we also found these same modes in the Shur and Segah modes. From observing the combined signs of the above modes, we realize the following points:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>In each mode, there are two quarter-tone signs. In the first seven modes, these two signs are koron. In the eighth mode, there is one koron and one suri, and in the rest of the modes, both quarter-tone signs are suri.</li>\n  <li>The first mode has eight signs (we have counted the “flat koron” sign as two signs), and gradually the number of combined signs decreases until in the fifth mode, where the number of signs reaches four. Then in modes 5, 6, 7, 8, and 9, the same four signs remain (but in different forms). Then again from the tenth mode, five signs appear, and gradually the number of signs increases until in the thirteenth mode, where it reaches eight signs (we have considered the “double sharp” sign as two signs).</li>\n  <li>Each mode has three differences compared to the previous mode: its fifth has risen a quarter step, its sixth has fallen a quarter step, and its seventh has ascended a half step. And if attention is paid, this difference also exists between two Chahargah modes that have a perfect fifth difference with each other.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Important Note:</strong> Now we can say that two modes that have a perfect fifth interval relative to each other, if they are Mahur, differ in one note; if they are Shur and Segah, they differ in two notes; and if they are Chahargah, they differ in 3 notes.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>In all modes, two quarter-tone signs are placed consecutively, which together form a perfect fifth or fourth interval, and these two signs in fact belong to the second and sixth degrees of the Chahargah Dastgah scale, which together form a perfect fifth interval or, inversely, a perfect fourth interval. These two signs are separated from each other in the mode “A.”</li>\n  <li>The two quarter-tone signs, together with the signs before or after them, alternately form half-augmented second intervals, and these are the same intervals that are in the middle of the Chahargah tetrachords.</li>\n  <li>The mode “A,” which has one koron sign and one suri sign, is a common mode, meaning that if we go up from it with fifth intervals, we gradually find the suri modes, and if we ascend from it with fourth intervals, the koron modes come into existence:</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Suri modes: A – E – B – F# – C# – G#.</p>\n<p>Koron modes: A – D – G – C – F – Bb – Eb – Ab.</p>\n<p>The reason we have gone further in the koron modes is to find the mode “Ab,” which we have taken as the starting point everywhere.</p>\n<p>After studying the above points, the rules for modal analysis of the twelve chromatic modes of the Chahargah Dastgah are stated as follows:</p>\n<p>First rule – The last koron is the tonic of the Chahargah Dastgah. By descending a half-augmented second from it, we find the tonic.</p>\n<p>Second rule – If we have more than one suri, the first suri is the second degree, which is found by descending a half-augmented second from the tonic of the Chahargah Dastgah.</p>\n<p>Third rule – The last sharp is the leading tone. By ascending a minor second from it, we find the tonic of the Chahargah Dastgah.</p>\n<p>Fourth rule – In modes that have more than one flat, the first flat is the leading tone of the Chahargah scale.</p>\n<p>According to the above four rules, in each of the twelve chromatic modes, the tonic can be found in two ways.</p>\n<p>If we want to find a simpler rule that finds the tonic in each mode in one way, we must say:</p>\n<p><strong>The last koron and the first suri is the tonic of the Chahargah Dastgah.</strong> (But the second part of this rule does not apply to Chahargah of “A.”)</p>\n<p>After finding the twelve chromatic modes, to find the modes whose tonic has a quarter tone, as we said before, we start from the mode “C koron” and go up with perfect fifth intervals and write them in order.</p>\n<p>Because we know the combined sign of Chahargah of “C,” if we lower all its notes by a quarter step, the combined signs of Chahargah of “C koron” are found, and then from it we write the other modes as follows:</p>\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1440\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_029_chahargah/webp/sornaz_029_chahargah_06.webp\" alt=\"Key signatures of the Chahargah Dastgah in quarter-tone modes\" width=\"1440\" height=\"810\" /> 6- Key signatures of the Chahargah Dastgah in quarter-tone modes[/caption]\n<p>After paying attention to the above quarter-tone modes of the Chahargah Dastgah, we realize the following points:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Except for the mode “D suri,” which has five signs, and the mode “A suri,” which has six signs, the other modes have seven combined signs.</li>\n  <li>Each mode has five quarter-tone signs.</li>\n  <li>The first mode is composed only of koron and flat, and the sequence of combined signs in it is like the sequence of flats.</li>\n  <li>From mode 8 to 13, the sequence of combined signs is like the sequence of sharps.</li>\n  <li>Modes 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, and 7 are composed of two groups of combined signs, one group according to the sequence of flats and the other group like the sequence of sharps.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>After studying the above points, the rules for modal analysis of the quarter-tone modes of the Chahargah Dastgah should be referred to:</p>\n<p>First rule – The koron before the last is the tonic.</p>\n<p>Second rule – The last koron is the subdominant.</p>\n<p>Third rule – The last suri is the leading tone (wherever the notes are “sharp suri,” the last sharp suri is the leading tone, which is actually counted as the last added suri).</p>\n<h2>Auditory Quality of the Chahargah Dastgah</h2>\n<p>The Avaz of Chahargah is a complete example of all the states and qualities of our music. Because its Daramad, like Mahur, is dignified, solemn, cheerful, happy, and smiling. The Zabol Avaz has the inner sadness of most of our Avazs. The Mokhalef Avaz is complaining and like Homayun is an experienced and capable advisor. The Muye and Mansuri Avaz is sad and sorrowful. So Chahargah both rejoices and cries and laments; sometimes it is joyful and sometimes sorrowful.</p>\n<p>It embodies pride, nobility, dignity, and solemnity, and also speaks of a mystical and unceremonious life and the state of patience and forbearance. Then with painful laments, it expresses affectionate feelings and desires mixed with <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%B9%D8%B4%D9%82\">love</a>.</p>\n<p>On the whole, it can be said that the Chahargah Dastgah is like a complete human being who possesses all the characteristics and excellences of Eastern taste and lofty human feelings, and its taste never becomes dull. This means that in the face of the hardships of life, it shows patience and endurance and has the power to resist, but sometimes it also becomes weary and affected by the failures and disappointments of life and pours a rain of tears from its eyes. Therefore, the Chahargah Dastgah is suitable for showing all states, qualities, and conditions, and for this reason, we have introduced it as the most important mode of Iranian music.</p>\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding the Chahargah Dastgah, please share it in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this content with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>.</p>', '2023-07-02 16:47:51', 0, '2024-09-18 22:45:23', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1487, 'posts', 30, 'en', NULL, 'Dastgah-e Homayun | Similarities and differences between Dastgah Homayun and Shur and Bayat-e Esfahan in the radif of Iranian music and harmonic minor scale | Mayeh-shenasi in Vaziri\'s 24-quarter-tone scale', NULL, 'The Homayoun mode is one of the most majestic and expressive systems in Persian classical music. Structurally, it shares strong similarities with the Shur mode, Bayat-e Esfahan, and the harmonic minor scale. It is built from two unequal tetrachords and is typically considered a descending scale, where the fourth degree plays a prominent role as the main dominant.\n\nThe primary difference between Homayoun and Shur lies in the third degree, which is higher in Homayoun. Due to their close relationship, modulation between these two modes is relatively easy. Bayat-e Esfahan is considered a derivative of Homayoun, beginning from its fourth degree.\n\nHomayoun allows modulation to other modes such as Shur and Chahargah, and includes various melodic sections (gushehs) like Bidād, Shushtari, and Chakavak, which create diversity without altering the core structure.\n\nIn terms of musical character, Homayoun is dignified, profound, calm, and emotionally rich. It conveys spiritual depth, contemplation, and maturity. According to Ruhollah Khaleghi, it is one of the most noble and impactful modes in Persian music, capable of evoking deep reflection and inner emotional experiences.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">dastgah of Homayun</span></strong>, which is known as one of the most dignified and majestic avazes and melodies in the Iranian music radif. It has a very close resemblance to the dastgah of Shur, the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan in Iranian music, and the harmonic minor scale in world music.</p>\n\n<p>In this article from the Sornaz website, we examine the structure of the dastgah of Homayun and then pay attention to its similarities and differences with the dastgah of Shur, the avaz of Esfahan, and the harmonic minor scale. Next, we also describe the important modulations of the dastgah of Homayun to the dastgahs of Shur and Chahargah in the music radif and review the mayeh analysis of this dastgah. At the end, we also express Ruhollah Khaleqi’s opinion about the auditory quality of the dastgah of Homayun.</p>\n\n<p>In another article, we also examine and analyze the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d9%88%d8%b4%d9%87-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%87%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c%d9%88%d9%86/\">gushehs of the dastgah of Homayun</a> in different radifs of Iranian music. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>The Structure of the Dastgah of Homayun</h2>\n\n<p>The <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">scale</a> of the dastgah of Homayun in the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a> radif is similar to the harmonic minor scale in Western <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">music</a>, in such a way that if we write the harmonic minor scale from the leading tone note (fifth degree) and raise the sub-leading tone note (sixth degree) of the harmonic minor scale by a quarter-step, the scale of the dastgah of Homayun is obtained.</p>\n\n<p>The notes of the Homayun “La” scale are as follows.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_030_homayoon/webp/sornaz_030_homayoon_02.webp\" alt=\"نت های دستگاه همایون «لا»\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 1- Notes of the dastgah of Homayun “La”[/caption]\n\n<p>The Homayun scale, like the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b4%d9%88%d8%b1/\">dastgah of Shur</a>, has a descending state because when descending, the second degree with the first degree has a large over-second interval (¾ step), which is close to a minor second (0.5 step). On the other hand, the fourth degree, which is the tonic of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%db%8c%d9%86%d9%88%d8%b1/\">minor scale</a> from which the dastgah of Homayun is derived, has more importance than the fifth degree and is considered the true leading tone, but at the same time the fifth degree also, in its own turn, is sometimes used in foruds.</p>\n\n<p>The stopping note in Homayun is the seventh degree, which after repeated stops on the second degree lands on the seventh degree and finally stops on the tonic for the final forud.</p>\n\n<p>In Iranian music, the dastgah of Homayun is composed of two connected dangs of Chahargah and Dashti (for more familiarity with the dangs of Iranian music, read the article “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Theory of Iranian Music, Examination of the Theory of Dangs and the 4 Basic Dangs in Iranian Music</a>”).</p>\n\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_030_homayoon/webp/sornaz_030_homayoon_001.webp\" alt=\"الگوی فاصله های گام دستگاه همایون\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 2- Interval pattern of the scale of the dastgah of Homayun[/caption]\n\n<p>In the image above, the witness note of the dastgah of Homayun is marked in red.</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, the most important degrees of the scale of the dastgah of Homayun are the first degree (tonic), the second degree (witness), the fourth degree (leading tone), and the fifth and seventh degrees (stopping notes).</p>\n\n<p>The intervals of the Homayun scale relative to the tonic are, in order: large over-second, major third, perfect fourth, perfect fifth, minor sixth, minor seventh, and octave.</p>\n\n<p>So the dangs of the Homayun scale are unequal because the first dang has a large over-second interval, a large over-major second interval, and a minor second interval, while the second dang has major second, minor second, and major second intervals. (The interval between the two dangs is a major second interval.)</p>\n\n<p>One of the properties of Homayun is that when starting and forud, the sixth degree (in the above example, the note Fa) rises by a quarter-step and finds a large over-second interval with the seventh degree. Because the Homayun scale can also be imagined as ascending, and the reason the sixth degree rises is to get closer to the seventh degree, which, since it is not the true leading tone, helps it a little and makes the forud more appropriate.</p>\n\n<p>This arrangement is also common in the dastgah of Shur. In fact, when forud, two notes resolve on the tonic: one is the second degree, which is the leading tone of the descending scale, and the other is the seventh degree, which tends to be the leading tone of the ascending scale. Since the scale of the dastgah of Homayun has both aspects, for this reason its fourth degree is important, which is the leading tone of the scale in the descending state (the fifth note from top to bottom), and its fifth degree is also important and valid, which is considered the leading tone of the scale in the ascending state.</p>\n\n<h2>The Relationship Between the Dastgah of Shur and the Dastgah of Homayun</h2>\n\n<p>If we look carefully at the Homayun scale, we detect a relationship between it and Shur. For example, if we compare the Shur “La” scale with the Homayun “La” scale, we easily realize the closeness of these two scales:</p>\n\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_030_homayoon/webp/sornaz_030_homayoon_04.webp\" alt=\"مقایسه نت های گام همایون «لا» و گام شور «لا»\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 3- Comparison of the notes of the Homayun “La” scale and the Shur “La” scale[/caption]\n\n<p>Shur “La” scale: La, Si koron, Do, Re, Mi, Fa, Sol.</p>\n\n<p>Homayun “La” scale: La, Si koron, Do diez, Re, Mi, Fa, Sol.</p>\n\n<p>From comparing these two scales, we conclude as follows:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>The Shur scale and the dastgah of Homayun share the tonic note.</li>\n  <li>The Shur scale and the dastgah of Homayun also share the second, fourth, fifth, sixth, and seventh degrees.</li>\n  <li>The only difference between Shur and Homayun is in the third degree, and this degree in the dastgah of Homayun is a half-step lower than Shur.</li>\n  <li>The intervals of the Shur scale and Homayun scale relative to the tonic are the same except for their sixth interval.</li>\n  <li>The third interval in the Shur scale is minor and in the Homayun scale is major.</li>\n  <li>The dangs of the Shur scale and the dastgah of Homayun are unequal.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>If we pay attention to the points mentioned, we realize other similarities between Shur and Homayun:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>Both the Shur scale and Homayun are descending, and in this case, the interval of the second degree (the note that has the leading tone state in the descending scale) with the tonic is a large over-second interval.</li>\n  <li>Whenever in the Shur and Homayun scales the seventh degree wants to resolve on the tonic, the sixth degree approaches it by a quarter-step to help its resolution on the tonic.</li>\n  <li>Since both the Shur and Homayun scales are descending, their fourth degree is the true leading tone and is important.</li>\n  <li>The second degree of the dastgah of Homayun is the witness note, but in Shur the witness and tonic are the same. Of course, stopping on the second degree in Shur is also common, and if that degree is the witness, the avaz of Abu Ata, which is one of Shur’s dependents, appears.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><b>Conclusion</b></p>\n\n<p>Now you may say that the dastgah of Homayun and Shur are equal in every respect except that the third degree in the Shur scale is minor and in the dastgah of Homayun is major. So one can consider Shur and Homayun as relative maqams to each other, meaning that the Homayun scale is the relative of the Shur scale and vice versa. But the relationship that exists between the Shur scale and the dastgah of Homayun is different from the relationship that the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d8%a7%da%98%d9%88%d8%b1/\">major scale</a> and minor have, because the minor scale starts from the sixth degree of the major scale and two of its intervals (third and sixth) differ from the major scale, but the difference in the dastgah of Homayun and Shur is much less than this.</p>\n\n<p>You may say that the relationship between the dastgah of Homayun and Shur is closer to the relationship that exists between two same-named scales, because the only difference between two same-named scales (for example, Do major and Do minor) is in two intervals, the third and sixth. But if we pay attention, the connection between the dastgah of Homayun and Shur is less than this because:</p>\n\n<p>1. The difference between two relative scales is in three things: difference in tonic and in the third and sixth intervals.</p>\n\n<p>2. The difference between two same-named scales is in two things: difference in the third and sixth intervals.</p>\n\n<p>3. The difference between the dastgah of Homayun and Shur is only in the third degree.</p>\n\n<p>On the other hand, the similarity between the dastgah of Homayun and Shur (from a scientific point of view) and the relationship that exists between them causes their mayeh analysis and combined signs to also be close to each other.</p>\n\n<h2>Dependents of the Dastgah of Homayun</h2>\n\n<p>The dastgah of Homayun has a very close relationship with the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan, to the extent that it can be said that the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan starts from the fourth degree of the Homayun scale and has similar fret arrangements and intervals. For this reason, due to this similarity between the fret arrangements and also because in the Iranian music radif landing from Bayat-e Esfahan to Homayun is common, the avaz of Esfahan is counted among the dependents of the dastgah of Homayun.</p>\n\n<h2>Similarity Between the Harmonic Minor Scale and the Homayun Scale</h2>\n\n<p>As is clear in image number 4, the Homayun “La” scale and the harmonic minor “Re” scale differ in only one note by a quarter-step. Of course, it should be noted that the tonic of these two scales differs, and similar notes in these two scales have different mayehs, and in the same mayehs the differences are greater.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_030_homayoon/webp/sornaz_030_homayoon_03.webp\" alt=\"مقایسه نت های گام همایون «لا» و گام مینور هارمونیک «ر»\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 4- Comparison of the notes of the Homayun “La” scale and the harmonic minor “Re” scale[/caption]\n\n<p>The harmonic minor scale, like the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan, starts from the fourth degree of Homayun, and the similarity of the harmonic minor and Bayat-e Esfahan is more than the similarity of the harmonic minor to Homayun.</p>\n\n<p>The small gushehs that are played during the dastgah of Homayun, such as Chakavak, Bidad, Shushtari, Leyli and Majnun, etc., none of them can be considered maqam change or mayeh change, because in none of them do the degrees of the dastgah of Homayun change; only in Chakavak the fourth degree and in Bidad the fifth degree is the witness note.</p>\n\n<p>In <strong>Bidad</strong>, the fifth degree is the witness and the fourth degree is the stopping note. In <strong>Shushtari</strong>, the fourth degree is the witness and the second degree is the stopping note.</p>\n\n<p>In the first Bidad gusheh, we stop on the fifth degree of the dastgah of Homayun, then we go to the fourth degree, and except when entering the dastgah of Homayun, we do not land on the witness note and tonic of Homayun.</p>\n\n<p>In the <strong>Chakavak</strong> gusheh, first we pause on the fourth degree of the dastgah of Homayun, then we go to the witness of Homayun. In Shushtari it is the same, and although the frets and the manner of stopping and forud in these two avazes are the same, they are still different in terms of state and effect, and this difference should be learned after referring to the radif of avazes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>For familiarity with the tar and kamancheh instruments and knowing the various components of these instruments and learning practical tips and points for choosing the best possible instrument, study the articles <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%86%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%aa%d8%a7%d8%b1/\">Comprehensive Guide to Buying a Tar</a> and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%86%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%da%a9%d9%85%d8%a7%d9%86%da%86%d9%87/\">Comprehensive Guide to Buying a Kamancheh</a>.</strong></p>\n\n<h2>Modulation of the Dastgah of Homayun to the Dastgah of Shur</h2>\n\n<p>Because the dastgah of Homayun and Shur have a lot of similarity in terms of intervals, of course going from one to the other is also easy and simple, and only by making the third interval of the dastgah of Homayun smaller can one easily enter Shur. For this reason, this modulation is common among musicians, and after playing Bidad, by means of a small melody that some call Oshaq, they enter Shur (of course, the same Shur dastgah that is the relative of the dastgah of Homayun).</p>\n\n<p>Now it can be said that the Shur scale and the dastgah of Homayun are, on the one hand, relative to each other in a special way, and on the other hand, they are two same-named maqams, and for this reason, modulation from one to the other is easy.</p>\n\n<p>Usually, one goes from the dastgah of Homayun to Shur, and does not enter Homayun from Shur. As we explained in the section on modulations of the dastgah of Shur, we did not mention this manner of modulation, but as we said, this modulation is scientifically easy and simple and will of course become common later, and if players become familiar with the rules of maqam change and mayeh change and also know the principles of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d9%88%d9%86%db%8c/\">harmony</a>, in addition to these changes, other modulations will also become common.</p>\n\n<h2>Modulation of the Dastgah of Homayun to the Dastgah of Chahargah</h2>\n\n<p>A state of modulation has also recently become common in the dastgah of Homayun in this way: when the avaz of Shushtari is played, the sixth degree of Homayun is raised by a half-step and the fifth degree is also lowered by a quarter-step, and by means of the avaz of Mansouri, they enter the dastgah of Chahargah.</p>\n\n<p>In the dastgah of Chahargah, we have a stop on the sub-leading tone note, which is known as Mokhalef and has the state of the dastgah of Homayun, and from there one can enter Homayun, because the second dang of Chahargah and the first dang of Homayun are equal, and therefore when we stop on the sixth degree of Chahargah, it is as if we have paused on the second degree or the witness note of the dastgah of Homayun.</p>\n\n<p>Now it must be noted that usually modulation from Shur to another maqam is not common, but from Mahur one goes to Shur and Homayun, and from the dastgah of Homayun one enters Shur and Chahargah. But as we said, knowledge of the rules of theoretical music will open the way for maqam change and mayeh change, and from any avaz we want, it is possible to go to another avaz and return again to the main avaz.</p>\n\n<h2>Mayeh Analysis of the Dastgah of Homayun</h2>\n\n<p>The mayeh analysis of the dastgah of Homayun is very easy, because just as when we knew the combined signs of any major mayeh, we could also find the tonic and accidental sign of its relative minor scale, because we are aware of the mayeh analysis and combined signs of the Shur mayehs, the mayeh analysis of Homayun will not be difficult. So we say that whenever we see the combined signs of the Shur mayehs, we should not just imagine that these signs are specific to the Shur mayehs. We should keep in mind that the combined signs of the Homayun mayehs also become the same, and the only difference between Shur and Homayun is in the accidental sign, meaning the third degree of Homayun must always be shown with an accidental sign.</p>\n\n<p>So the tonic of the dastgah of Homayun is the same as the tonic of Shur, but if during the piece the third degree of Shur had risen by a half-step and become major, we are in the Homayun mayeh; otherwise, we are in Shur. So now we can mention the twenty-five Shur scales that we previously wrote only by the name of the tonic, and consider their tonic, with the difference of the third degree (one accidental sign), as the tonic of Homayun:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Tonic of the Shur and Homayun mayeh      Accidental sign of Homayun</strong></p>\n\n<p>La bemol                   Do natural</p>\n\n<p>Do koron                   Mi koron</p>\n\n<p>Mi bemol                   Sol natural</p>\n\n<p>Sol koron                   Si koron</p>\n\n<p>Si bemol                   Re natural</p>\n\n<p>Re koron                   Fa sori</p>\n\n<p>Fa                     La natural</p>\n\n<p>La koron                   Do sori</p>\n\n<p>Do                     Mi natural</p>\n\n<p>Mi koron                   Sol sori</p>\n\n<p>Sol                     Si natural</p>\n\n<p>Si koron                   Re sori</p>\n\n<p>Re                     Fa diez</p>\n\n<p>Fa sori                   La sori</p>\n\n<p>La                     Do diez</p>\n\n<p>Do sori                   Mi sori</p>\n\n<p>Mi                     Sol diez</p>\n\n<p>Sol sori                   Si sori</p>\n\n<p>Si                     Re diez</p>\n\n<p>Re sori                   Fa diez sori</p>\n\n<p>Fa diez                   La diez</p>\n\n<p>La sori                   Do diez sori</p>\n\n<p>Do diez                   Mi diez</p>\n\n<p>Mi sori                   Sol diez sori</p>\n\n<p>Sol diez                   Si diez</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, the combined signs of each of the above mayehs are according to the combined signs of the Shur mayehs, but the dastgah of Homayun also has one accidental sign during the musical pieces.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Note</strong></p>\n\n<p>Since the notation of the avazes and maqams of Iranian music with quarter-step signs has become common, because the rules of theoretical music and the manner of mayeh analysis of the avazes were not yet known, it has become common to write the third degree of the dastgah of Homayun as part of the combined signs as well. For example, for Homayun “La,” after the clef, two signs are placed: Si koron and Do diez. In this way, new rules must be found for the mayeh analysis of Homayun, and the work becomes more difficult. For the reasons mentioned, because Shur and Homayun are relative to each other, it is better that they also share the combined signs.</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, the method mentioned above is both simpler, easier, and more scientific, and Ruhollah Khaleqi has approved this style and used it.</p>\n\n<h2>The Auditory Quality of the Dastgah of Homayun</h2>\n\n<p>Homayun is a majestic, grand, calm, and at the same time effective, attractive, captivating, and beautiful avaz. This avaz, in the opinion of Ruhollah Khaleqi, who is one of the prominent Iranian musicians, is more dignified and effective than the other dastgahs and avazes. Because its speech is full of <a href=\"https://vajehyab.com/dehkhoda/%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%88-3#:~:text=%2D%20%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%88%20%D9%BE%DB%8C%D8%AF%D8%A7%20%DA%A9%D8%B1%D8%AF%D9%86%20%D8%9B%20%D8%A8%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%20%D8%B1%D9%81%D8%AA%D9%86,%D8%B3%DB%8C%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%20%D8%A2%D9%86%20%D8%AE%D8%B3%D8%B1%D9%88%20%D8%AF%DB%8C%D9%86%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1...\">elevation</a> and greatness and glory, and its advice is with complete skill and the utmost experience and maturity. Its expression is magical and masterful; the listener involuntarily becomes silent in front of this avaz, and the power of speech is taken from him.</p>\n\n<p>Homayun is an affectionate and kind advisor who converses and shares his heart with his listeners with complete modesty and shyness, and with an eloquent expression, he advises and counsels in such a way that no speaker has this skill and mastery.</p>\n\n<p>Homayun is a collection of delicate and lofty spiritual and mystical feelings. Sometimes it makes a person attentive, and sometimes it speaks with complete pride and arrogance. Sometimes it draws a loud cry from a burning heart and sheds tears from the eyes with every lament, but it quickly changes its manner of speech and gives advice of patience, wipes the tears from the cheeks of its listeners, and gives solace and peace to depressed and disturbed hearts.</p>\n\n<p>The sadness, melancholy, and depression of Homayun is not superficial and passionate like the crying and lamentation of inexperienced children. The dastgah of Homayun, like the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%af%d8%b4%d8%aa%db%8c/\">avaz of Dashti</a>, does not lament, does not shed tears, and does not make moaning and wailing its profession, but like the experienced and hardship-enduring, it has the patience and endurance, and its sadness, regret, and longing is internal and hidden. Its lament deals with the hearts of the tested and experienced people.</p>\n\n<p>As a result of hearing this avaz, a person becomes immersed in the world of thought. If the player is skillful, he leads the ready listener to a world beyond the material world so that he thinks and contemplates matters beyond what is related to the body and corporeality.</p>\n\n<p>In summary, it can be said that Homayun is a mixture made and formed from all lofty spiritual feelings and qualities, and in truth its name is appropriate to it, and it is truly Homayun, but in performance there must be a skillful player who delivers its right in the way it deserves.</p>\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the dastgah of Homayun, please share them in the comments section of the article in order to improve the quality of this content with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>.</p>', '2023-07-03 15:35:59', 0, '2024-09-18 22:45:05', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1488, 'posts', 31, 'en', NULL, 'Avaz-e Bayat-e Esfahan in the radif of Iranian music | Relation of Avaz Esfahan to Dastgah Homayun and Shur | Its similarity to the harmonic minor scale', NULL, 'Bayat-e Esfahan is one of the most beautiful and significant modes in Persian classical music. It is closely related to the Homayoun mode and, to a lesser extent, to Shur, while also showing strong similarities to the harmonic minor scale.\n\nStructurally, Bayat-e Esfahan can be considered as starting from the fourth degree of Homayoun. Its main difference from the harmonic minor scale lies in the sixth degree, which is slightly raised (a neutral or “half-large” interval). The scale is ascending, includes a leading tone (7th degree), and has an important dominant on the fifth degree. Its tetrachords are unequal.\n\nCompared to Shur, Bayat-e Esfahan differs in several intervals (especially the 2nd, 6th, and 7th), although some similarities remain. A common modulation in this mode is shifting to Shur, typically through the “Oshaq” melodic passage.\n\nDespite its connections to Shur, Bayat-e Esfahan is classified as a branch of Homayoun due to its closer structural and functional relationship. In terms of tonal analysis, it shares key signatures with Shur and Homayoun but features a distinct accidental on the seventh degree.\n\nEmotionally, Bayat-e Esfahan conveys a balanced mood between sadness and joy. It is expressive and captivating, yet neither fully melancholic nor cheerful, offering a refined and nuanced musical character.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan</span></strong>, which is one of the most beautiful avazes in Iranian music. It has a very close resemblance to the dastgahs of Homayun and Shur in the Iranian music radif and is also very close to the harmonic minor scale.</p>\n\n<p>In this article from the Sornaz Music School website, we examine the structure of the scale of the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan, discuss its relationships and similarities with the dastgahs of Shur and Homayun and the harmonic minor scale, and also study the differences between the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan and these dastgahs. Then we speak about modulation and the gushehs and dependents of the avaz of Esfahan. Next, we discuss the mayeh analysis and the change of the seventh degree of the scale of the avaz of Esfahan, and at the end, we mention Ruhollah Khaleqi’s opinion about the auditory quality of the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan.</p>\n\n<p>In another article, we also examine and analyze the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d9%88%d8%b4%d9%87-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%a7%d8%b5%d9%81%d9%87%d8%a7%d9%86/\">gushehs of the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan</a> in different radifs of Iranian music. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>The Structure of the Avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan</h2>\n\n<p>The <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">scale</a> of the avaz of Esfahan in the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a> radif has more similarity to the harmonic minor scale in Western <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">music</a> than to the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%87%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c%d9%88%d9%86/\">dastgah of Homayun</a>, because its tonic is the same as the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%db%8c%d9%86%d9%88%d8%b1/\">minor scale</a> harmonic, and the only difference between them is in the sixth degree of the scale. In the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan, the interval of the sixth degree with the tonic is a large over-sixth (4.75 steps), while in the harmonic minor scale it is a small sixth (4.5 steps), and the sixth degree of Esfahan is a quarter-step higher than the sixth degree of the harmonic minor scale.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_031_bayat_esfahan/webp/sornaz_031_bayat_esfahan_03.webp\" alt=\"مقایسه نت های گام اصفهان «ر» و گام مینور هارمونیک «ر»\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 1- Comparison of the notes of the Esfahan “Re” scale and the harmonic minor “Re” scale[/caption]\n\n<p>In fact, it can be said that the scale of the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan starts from the fourth degree of Homayun and has no other difference with Homayun.</p>\n\n<p>Bayat-e Esfahan has all the properties of the harmonic minor scale because:</p>\n\n<p>1) Bayat-e Esfahan has an ascending scale.</p>\n\n<p>2) In the Esfahan scale, there is a leading tone note (the leading tone is the seventh-degree note whose interval with the eighth-degree note is a half-step or ¾ step).</p>\n\n<p>3) Its true leading tone, like all ascending scales, is the fifth degree of the scale (which is the same as the tonic of Homayun).</p>\n\n<p>4) Its dangs are unequal.</p>\n\n<p>5) Its intervals, except for the interval of the sixth degree, are equal to the intervals of the harmonic minor scale.</p>\n\n<p>But as mentioned, the difference is in the sixth interval of Bayat-e Esfahan, which, instead of being small, is a large over-sixth. The interval between the sub-leading tone (sixth degree of the scale) and the leading tone (seventh degree of the scale) is more similar to an augmented second than to a major second, because it is a quarter-step more than a major second. This interval in Homayun is placed between the second and third degrees of the scale, that is, in the first dang, and in the avaz of Esfahan it is placed in the second dang. So it can be said that the first dang of Homayun is the same as the second dang of Esfahan.</p>\n\n<p>In Iranian music, the avaz of Esfahan is composed of two separated dangs of Dashti and Chahargah (for more familiarity with the dangs of Iranian music, read the article “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Theory of Iranian Music, Examination of the Theory of Dangs and the 4 Basic Dangs in Iranian Music</a>”).</p>\n\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_031_bayat_esfahan/webp/sornaz_031_bayat_esfahan_001.webp\" alt=\"الگوی فاصله های گام آواز بیات اصفهان\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 2- Interval pattern of the scale of the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan[/caption]\n\n<p>In the image above, the witness note of the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan is marked in red.</p>\n\n<p>The important degrees of the scale of the avaz of Esfahan are as follows:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>The tonic, from which the avaz of Esfahan starts and to which it ends.</li>\n  <li>The fifth degree (leading tone).</li>\n  <li>The sixth degree, which is the stopping note, and sometimes the forud of Esfahan occurs on it. This degree is the same degree that in the dastgah of Homayun is the witness note, and in Homayun it was considered the second degree of the scale.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>The notes of the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan “Re” are as follows.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_031_bayat_esfahan/webp/sornaz_031_bayat_esfahan_02.webp\" alt=\"نت های آواز اصفهان «ر»\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 3- Notes of the avaz of Esfahan “Re”[/caption]\n\n<p>Just as Homayun had a relationship with Shur, the avaz of Esfahan is also close to Shur (from a scientific point of view), but the difference between Homayun and Shur was only in the third degree, which in Homayun had a major third interval with the tonic and in Shur it was a minor third. But between Esfahan and Shur the difference is greater, as follows:</p>\n\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_031_bayat_esfahan/webp/sornaz_031_bayat_esfahan_04.webp\" alt=\"مقایسه نت های گام اصفهان «ر» و گام شور «لا»\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 4- Comparison of the notes of the Esfahan “Re” scale and the Shur “La” scale[/caption]\n\n<p>1 — The tonic of Shur and Bayat-e Esfahan is different, meaning the fourth degree of Shur is the tonic of Esfahan.</p>\n\n<p>2 — Because the fourth degree of Shur has become the tonic of the avaz of Esfahan, as a result, the intervals of the Esfahan scale also differ from Shur, so that the Esfahan scale and Shur share the following intervals:</p>\n\n<p>1 – The third interval, which is minor in both.</p>\n\n<p>2 – The fourth and fifth intervals, which are perfect in both.</p>\n\n<p>But they differ in the following intervals:</p>\n\n<p>1 – The second interval of Shur is a large over-second and the second of Esfahan is major.</p>\n\n<p>2 — The sixth interval of Shur is minor and the sixth of Bayat-e Esfahan is a large over-sixth.</p>\n\n<p>3 – The seventh interval of Shur is minor and the seventh of the avaz of Esfahan is major.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>Modulation in the Avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan</h2>\n\n<p>In the avaz of Esfahan, the important modulation that has become common is going to the dastgah of Shur, that is, to the same Shur from which Homayun and Esfahan are derived. In this way, the modulation from Homayun to Shur was going to the relative and same-named maqam, but in the avaz of Esfahan, entering a Shur whose tonic is the leading tone of the Bayat-e Esfahan scale occurs. So, just as we went from Homayun “La” to Shur “La,” from Esfahan “Re” we will also go to Shur “La.” This modulation is done by means of the gusheh of <strong>Oshaq</strong>, and the note that changes in this modulation is the leading tone of the Esfahan scale, which is lowered by a half-step to become equal to the third degree of Shur.</p>\n\n<p>The secondary melodies of the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan do not have any important difference with Esfahan itself and do not create any change in the degrees of the Esfahan scale; for this reason, they are neither mayeh changes nor maqam changes. One of the most beautiful parts of the avaz of Esfahan is stopping on the second degree of the scale, which is known as <strong>Bayat-e Rajeh</strong> and has a happy state, and it has a little of the state of <strong>Bidad of Homayun</strong> and is formed in the same frets.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n\n<p><strong>For familiarity with the santur and tombak instruments and knowing the various components of these instruments and learning practical tips and points for choosing the best possible instrument, study the articles <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%86%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%b3%d9%86%d8%aa%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Comprehensive Guide to Buying a Santur</a> and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%86%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%aa%d9%86%d8%a8%da%a9/\">Comprehensive Guide to Buying a Tombak</a>.</strong></p>\n\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>Is the Avaz of Esfahan Part of Shur or Homayun?</h2>\n\n<p>From the expression of the above points, one can understand why we have placed the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan as part of the dastgah of Homayun, because, as observed, its relationship with Shur is less than with Homayun. Moreover, the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%af-%d8%af%d8%b1-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">forud</a> of the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan sometimes occurs on the tonic of Homayun, whereas it never lands on Shur, and only Shur is used as a maqam change in Esfahan.</p>\n\n<p>Now it must be seen that although the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan is closer to the minor scale than the dastgah of Homayun, why have we not considered Esfahan as the main maqam and Homayun as one of its branches? The reason for this issue is almost clear because:</p>\n\n<p>1 — The dastgah of Homayun is larger than the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan and has more dependents than it.</p>\n\n<p>2 — Bayat-e Esfahan is a small avaz and has fewer gushehs compared to Homayun.</p>\n\n<p>3 — The avaz of Esfahan sometimes lands on Homayun at the end, but the dastgah of Homayun never lands on Esfahan.</p>\n\n<p>4 — Because the purpose is to classify Iranian dastgahs, it is better to consider their relationship with each other. So, since Homayun is close to Shur and in fact is its relative and same-named maqam, it is better that the avaz of Esfahan, since it is farther from Shur, be considered as one of the dependents and branches of Homayun.</p>\n\n<p>5 — If we say that the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan has separated from the minor scale, perhaps they will say that its sixth interval should also be small, and this minor difference will also disappear. But because it belongs to Homayun, it has this difference with the minor scale, and it is natural as well.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>The Seventh Degree of the Avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan</h2>\n\n<p>In the past, in the Bayat-e Esfahan scale, there was a large over-half-step interval, and as a result, the sixth degree had a major second interval with the seventh, and the tar fret arrangement style shows this arrangement well. But since the violin became common in Iran and the ears of violinists did not correctly detect this difference, in addition to their lack of skill, it caused the seventh degree of Esfahan to gradually become larger, because finger placement on the violin in that state was a bit difficult. In any case, the avaz of Esfahan is now performed on almost all instruments in the same way as we wrote.</p>\n\n<p>One of the important reasons for this difference is also because violinists gradually and earlier became familiar with European music, and as a result of playing pieces that are in minor scales, they gradually got used to considering the interval between the leading tone and the tonic as a small second interval, and the tar players also, out of necessity, followed them and currently have added one fret to the tar so that they can play the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan.</p>\n\n<p>This fret is “Fa diez,” which is necessary for playing Bayat-e Esfahan “Sol.” In the past, instead of “Fa diez,” they took the note “Fa sori.” Ruhollah Khaleqi says that although the kamancheh is an old instrument and was used before the violin became common, on this instrument as well, the avaz of Esfahan had a natural leading tone (the half-step interval between the seventh and eighth degrees of the scale, that is, “Fa diez” and “Sol” in Bayat-e Esfahan “Sol”).</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>Mayeh Analysis of the Avaz of Esfahan</h2>\n\n<p>The mayeh analysis of the avaz of Esfahan is simple in this way: in the combined signs, it is common with the dastgahs of Shur and Homayun, and the only difference between them is that it always has one accidental sign like the Homayun scale, but the note that has taken the accidental sign in the avaz of Esfahan, as we know, is the seventh degree of the scale, that is, the leading tone, whereas in Homayun it was the third degree.</p>\n\n<p>However, whenever we see the combined signs of the dastgah of Shur, we must pay attention to the melodies (notes) and the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%84%d9%88%d8%af%db%8c/\">melody</a> of the musical piece. If the third degree of Shur had risen by a half-step (had become sharp), this degree or mediant note is either the mediant of Homayun or the leading tone of the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan, and distinguishing which one we are in (Homayun or Esfahan) becomes clear by paying attention to the manner of foruds.</p>\n\n<p>If the third of Shur is major and the foruds are on the tonic or, in other words, on the tonic agreement (<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%da%a9%d9%88%d8%b1%d8%af/\">chord</a> whose base note is the tonic of the scale) of Homayun, we are in the dastgah of Homayun, and if the foruds are on the tonic of Esfahan, we will be in the avaz of Esfahan.</p>\n\n<p>For example, suppose after the clef there is one combined sign, Si koron. In this case, we say we are in Shur “La.” Then we pay attention to the melodies of the musical piece. If the third of Shur (the note Do) had risen by a half-step (had become sharp), we are in Homayun “La” or Esfahan “Re.” Now, if the foruds are on the tonic “La” or the tonic agreement “La,” we are in Homayun, and if they have landed on the tonic of Bayat-e Esfahan, that is, “Re,” or on its tonic agreement, we are in Esfahan “Re.”</p>\n\n<p>The purpose of the tonic agreement is an agreement whose base is the tonic. Now, if the scale is ascending, the tonic agreement will be composed of the first, third, and fifth degrees; for example, the tonic agreement of Esfahan “Re” becomes: Re – Fa – La. And if the scale is descending, its tonic agreement consists of the tonic, sixth, and fourth degrees; for example, the tonic agreement of the Shur “La” scale becomes: La – Fa – Re, which is also the tonic agreement of Homayun “La.”</p>\n\n<p>Of course, distinguishing whether we are in the dastgah of Homayun or the avaz of Esfahan is done in two ways: either through habit and ear, or according to scientific rules. Those who are familiar with the avazes recognize it by themselves, but if they are not familiar with the radif of avazes, they should not be content with this brief explanation and should know the elementary rules of the science of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d9%88%d9%86%db%8c/\">harmony</a> for complete mayeh analysis.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>The Auditory Quality of the Avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan</h2>\n\n<p>Bayat-e Esfahan is one of the old Iranian avazes whose name has been mentioned in the old books of Iranian music, and it is also called Bayat-e Esfahan. Esfahan is an avaz that is sometimes cheerful and happy and sometimes melancholic and sad, but overall it is attractive and captivating. Stopping on the sub-leading tone (sixth degree of the scale) creates a special state in it that is different from the state of the minor scale.</p>\n\n<p>From hearing the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan, a person does not become very <a href=\"https://vajehyab.com/moein/%D9%85%D9%84%D9%88%D9%84\">melancholic</a> and affected, whereas he does not become happy and cheerful either. So its state is between sadness and joy. In summary, Esfahan is a beautiful avaz but is devoid of the specific qualities that we mentioned in the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b4%d9%88%d8%b1/\">dastgah of Shur</a> and Homayun.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan, please share them in the comments section of the article in order to improve the quality of this content with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>.</p>', '2023-07-04 16:23:25', 0, '2026-02-20 15:24:16', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1489, 'posts', 32, 'en', NULL, 'Dastgah-e Rast-Panjgah | The final technique in the radif of Iranian music | Similarity of the scale of Dastgah Rast-Panjgah and Mahur | A dastgah for compound playing and compound singing', NULL, 'Rast-Panjgah is one of the most advanced and complex systems (dastgahs) in Persian classical music. Traditionally, it was taught only after mastering all other dastgahs, as it requires a deep understanding of modal transitions.\n\nStructurally, its scale is identical to the Mahur dastgah and closely resembles the Western major scale, but its uniqueness lies in its extensive use of modulation (modulation between modes). Because of this, some musicians consider it less as an independent mode and more as a framework for teaching complex modal transitions.\n\nRast-Panjgah is typically performed in F (on tar) or G (on violin) and involves specific tuning techniques depending on the instrument.\n\nA key feature of this dastgah is its ability to transition into other modes such as Shur, Nava, Homayun, and Segah, using various melodic pieces (gushehs) like Oshaq, Zabol, and Qaracheh. It also includes unique gushehs such as Mobarqa, Sepahr, and Mavara al-Nahr.\n\nOverall, Rast-Panjgah can be seen as a composite modal system, encompassing characteristics of multiple dastgahs. Rather than having a single emotional identity, it represents a wide spectrum of musical expressions in Persian music.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">dastgah of Rast Panjgah</span></strong>, which was known in the past as the final skill among Iranian musicians. The music masters taught this dastgah to their students only after completely teaching the other dastgahs and avazes of the Iranian music radif. In the dastgah of Rast Panjgah, modulation to other dastgahs occurs very frequently, and some radif experts believe that this dastgah does not have an independent character and was created in the past for teaching compound reading and compound playing.</p>\n\n<p>In this article from Sornaz Music School, we examine the structure of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah and, by introducing the melodies and gushehs existing in Rast Panjgah, we also describe the various modulations of this dastgah to other dastgahs and avazes of the music radif. At the end, we also mention Ruhollah Khaleqi’s theory regarding the auditory quality of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah.</p>\n\n<p>In another article, we also examine and analyze the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d9%88%d8%b4%d9%87-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b1%d8%a7%d8%b3%d8%aa-%d9%be%d9%86%d8%ac%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">gushehs of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah</a> in different radifs of Iranian music. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>The Structure of the Dastgah of Rast Panjgah</h2>\n\n<p>The <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">scale</a> of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah is the same as the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%85%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%88%d8%b1/\">dastgah of Mahur</a> in Iranian music and the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d8%a7%da%98%d9%88%d8%b1/\">major scale</a> in Western <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">music</a>, and the only minor difference between Rast Panjgah and Mahur is in the difference of states and modulations of these two dastgahs.</p>\n\n<p>Usually, Rast Panjgah is played on the tar in the key or tonality of Fa and on the violin in the key of Sol. This avaz is less common today and only those who are completely familiar with the radif of avazes know it.</p>\n\n<p>Rast Panjgah has a special tuning on the tar, in this way that the yellow strings (the third and fourth strings on the tar), which are normally tuned to Sol, are lowered by one whole step and tuned to Fa so that the sound has more resonance. On the violin, since it is played in the Sol scale, the <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%81%D8%B1%D9%86%DA%AF\">Western</a> tuning (Mi – La – Re – Sol) is more suitable for performing it, although usually the dastgah of Rast Panjgah is played in the tuning “Re – La – Re – Sol.”</p>\n\n<p>In the image below, you can see the notes of the Rast Panjgah “Fa” scale, which is common on the tar.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_032_rast_panggah/webp/sornaz_032_rast_panggah_02.webp\" alt=\"نت های دستگاه راست پنجگاه «فا»\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 1- Notes of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah “Fa”[/caption]\n\n<p>In <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a>, the dastgah of Rast Panjgah is composed of two separated Mahur dangs (for more familiarity with the dangs of Iranian music, read the article “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Theory of Iranian Music, Examination of the Theory of Dangs and the 4 Basic Dangs in Iranian Music</a>”).</p>\n\n[caption id=\"\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"960\"]<img class=\"size-medium\" src=\"https://dl.sornaz.com/pictures/article/sornaz_032_rast_panggah/webp/sornaz_032_rast_panggah_001.webp\" alt=\"الگوی فاصله های گام دستگاه راست پنجگاه\" width=\"960\" height=\"540\" /> 2- Interval pattern of the scale of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah[/caption]\n\n<p>In the image above, the witness note of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah is marked in red and the tonic note is marked in green.</p>\n\n<p><strong>For familiarity with the tar and setar instruments and knowing the various components of these instruments and learning practical tips and points for choosing the best instrument model, study the articles <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%86%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%aa%d8%a7%d8%b1/\">Comprehensive Guide to Buying a Tar</a> and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%86%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%b3%d9%87-%d8%aa%d8%a7%d8%b1/\">Comprehensive Guide to Buying a Setar</a>.</strong></p>\n\n<h2>Secondary Melodies of the Dastgah of Rast Panjgah</h2>\n\n<p>After the daramad of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah, whose <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%af-%d8%af%d8%b1-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">forud</a> is on the tonic, sometimes they briefly stop on the second degree as well, and small melodies are played that often have a slight difference from the main frets of Rast Panjgah.</p>\n\n<p>For example, in the gushehs of <strong>Zang-e Shotor</strong>, <strong>Parvaneh</strong>, and <strong>Naghmeh</strong>, the seventh degree of the scale, which is shown as a reference, is lowered by a half-step.</p>\n\n<p>In the gushehs of <strong>Ruh Afza</strong> and <strong>Neyriz</strong>, the third degree is also lowered by a quarter-step and then returns to the main mayeh.</p>\n\n<p>The gushehs of <strong>Panjgah</strong> and <strong>Sepehr</strong> have the state of the daramad of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%86%d9%88%d8%a7/\">dastgah of Nava</a>, and it is as if we have gone to the fifth upper note (the note Do). In this moment, the gusheh of <strong>Oshaq</strong>, which is also played in the dastgah of Nava, comes after Panjgah and Sepehr, and Rast Panjgah turns into Nava (Oshaq is used for compound playing to go from the dastgah of Rast Panjgah to Nava).</p>\n\n<p>Then, by means of the gusheh of <strong>Zabol</strong>, they enter the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b3%d9%87-%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">dastgah of Segah</a>. That is, they go to a Zabol whose witness is the fifth degree of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah. So if we are in Rast Panjgah “Fa,” its Zabol will be in Segah “Fa,” whose scale will be as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Segah “Fa” scale: Fa – Sol – La koron – Si bemol – Do – Re koron – Mi bemol – Fa.</p>\n\n<p>After that, by means of one of the secondary melodies of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b4%d9%88%d8%b1/\">dastgah of Shur</a> known as <strong>Qorcheh</strong>, we enter the fifth upper Shur of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah. That is, for example, from Rast Panjgah “Fa” we go to Shur “Do,” whose scale becomes as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Shur “Do” scale: Do – Re koron – Mi bemol – Fa – Sol – La bemol – Si bemol – Do.</p>\n\n<p>Then, with the help of two specific melodies of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah known as <strong>Mobraq’e</strong> and <strong>Sepehr</strong>, we return to the main maqam again.</p>\n\n<p>After that, the way to enter the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%87%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c%d9%88%d9%86/\">dastgah of Homayun</a> is opened by means of some secondary melodies of the dastgah of Mahur. For example, by means of the gushehs of <strong>Nahib</strong> and <strong>Iraq</strong>, whose witness is the tonic of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah, but the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%a7%d8%b5%d9%84%d9%87-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%d8%a7%db%8c%db%8c/\">interval</a> from the third degree to the tonic of Rast Panjgah becomes smaller, then by means of the gushehs of <strong>Nowruz Arab</strong>, <strong>Nowruz Khara</strong>, and <strong>Nowruz Saba</strong>, all of which have the state of <strong>Rak</strong>, we go to Homayun, that is, to the Homayun whose tonic is the leading tone of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah, in this way:</p>\n\n<p>Homayun “Do” scale: Do – Re koron – Mi natural – Fa – Sol – La bemol – Si bemol – Do.</p>\n\n<p>After that, some other gushehs of Homayun such as <strong>Nafir</strong>, <strong>Abolchap</strong>, <strong>Leyli and Majnun</strong>, <strong>Ravandi</strong>, and <strong>Tarz</strong> are played here, and then we enter the dastgah of Rast Panjgah. After that, Rak, whose witness is the tonic of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah, is played again, and we return to Rast Panjgah.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ma’vara al-Nahr</strong> is one of the specific gushehs of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah, which is similar to the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d9%81%d8%b4%d8%a7%d8%b1%db%8c/\">avaz of Afshari</a>, and its tonic is the same as the tonic of Rast Panjgah, and its scale is as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Afshari “Do” scale: Fa – Sol – La koron – Si bemol – Do – Re koron or Re natural – Mi bemol – Fa.</p>\n\n<p>Ma’vara al-Nahr is almost similar to the gusheh of <strong>Shekasteh</strong> as well. Just as the Shekasteh of Mahur has the same tonic as the tonic of Mahur, the same similarity exists in Ma’vara al-Nahr and the Shekasteh of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah.</p>\n\n<p>After Ma’vara al-Nahr, we return to the main maqam again.</p>\n\n<p>From the expression of the above points, it becomes clear that in the dastgah of Rast Panjgah, modulation to Shur, Nava, Homayun, and Segah occurs, and only to the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%da%86%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">dastgah of Chahargah</a> it does not go. But because in Rak and Homayun we are mostly in the first dang, and the first dang of Homayun has the same intervals as the first dang of Chahargah (large over-second – major third – perfect fourth), one can also go to Chahargah. As a result, it is observed that in the dastgah of Rast Panjgah, one can enter all Iranian maqams (Shur – Nava – Homayun – Mahur – Segah – Chahargah), and in fact, it is an avaz composed of the other maqams of Iranian music.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>The Auditory Quality of the Dastgah of Rast Panjgah</h2>\n\n<p>Rast Panjgah does not have a specific state of its own; only some of its gushehs are specific to this dastgah itself, which are not played anywhere else, such as Mobraq’e, Sepehr, Ma’vara al-Nahr, Rast, and so on.</p>\n\n<p>Because modulation to other avazes occurs a lot in Rast Panjgah, every part of it shows the state of one of the dastgahs or avazes. For this reason, Rast Panjgah is more complete than the other dastgahs, because it also has all the states and qualities of the other avazes. So Rast Panjgah is not actually a specific maqam and is a way to practice modulation to other avazes, and as its name also implies, in this avaz, modulation occurs to five maqams.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the dastgah of Rast Panjgah, please share them in the comments section of the article in order to improve the quality of this content with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>.</p>', '2023-07-05 18:59:04', 0, '2024-09-18 22:44:47', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1490, 'posts', 33, 'en', NULL, 'Theory of the 24-quarter-tone scale in Iranian music by Ali-Naqi Vaziri', NULL, 'This article explores the theory of the 24-quarter-tone scale proposed by the prominent Iranian musician Ali-Naqi Vaziri. This theory aims to systematize the pitch structure of Persian music and distinguish it from Western musical systems.\n\nIt begins by explaining that musical traditions may differ either in their structural foundations (interval systems) or in their expressive qualities. Western music, especially in countries like France, Germany, and Italy, is based on diatonic scales consisting of whole and half steps. In contrast, Eastern music—particularly Persian music—uses smaller intervals such as quarter tones.\n\nThe article highlights that many Eastern musical traditions (Iranian, Arabic, Turkish, and Indian) share both structural and expressive similarities. Influential theorists such as Al-Farabi, Avicenna, and Safi al-Din al-Urmawi played key roles in shaping these traditions.\n\nThrough a comparison of fretting systems on instruments like the oud and tar, the article shows that traditional Persian music historically used 17 intervals, later reduced to 15, and then expanded again through Vaziri’s reforms. Theoretically, these intervals can be reorganized into 24 equal quarter tones.\n\nAlthough in practice these intervals are not always perfectly equal, the 24-quarter-tone system provides a standardized theoretical model, similar to equal temperament in Western music. This scale includes 25 notes per octave and serves as the foundation for constructing Persian modes (dastgahs).\n\nIn conclusion, the 24-quarter-tone scale offers a scientific framework for understanding the unique intervallic structure of Persian music and highlights one of its key differences from Western music.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the theory of the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">24-quarter-tone scale</span></strong> presented by the prominent Iranian musician, Ali Naqi Vaziri.</p>\n\n<h2>The Difference Between the Music of Nations</h2>\n\n<p>The music of two nations may differ from each other in two respects: one in terms of origin and foundation, or in terms of the state and quality of the music. In some countries, only the second difference is seen, and there is no difference in the first respect. For example, the music of the countries of <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%81%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%86%D8%B3%D9%87\">France</a>, <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A2%D9%84%D9%85%D8%A7%D9%86\">Germany</a>, <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A7%DB%8C%D8%AA%D8%A7%D9%84%DB%8C%D8%A7\">Italy</a>, and <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%B1%D9%88%D8%B3%DB%8C%D9%87\">Russia</a>, although in reality built on the same foundation, differ from each other in terms of quality and state.</p>\n\n<p>The foundation of the music of these countries is formed from <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d8%a7%da%98%d9%88%d8%b1/\">major scales</a> and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%a7%d8%b5%d9%84%d9%87-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%d8%a7%db%8c%db%8c/\">intervals</a> of one step and half-step. But they are distinguished from each other in terms of state, so that anyone familiar with the science of music and its rules and laws, who examines and compares the different styles of the music of these countries, will notice this difference. It has been written in the history of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">music</a> that each of these countries has a specific style for its music, which differs only in terms of state and quality.</p>\n\n<p>For example, it can be said that their music is like several buildings that have different external colors but the materials used in them are the same, and the building plan and architectural methods are the same.</p>\n\n<p>These countries, due to their proximity to each other and having political, historical, and literary relations, and the similarity of the environment and geographical conditions and the quality of social life of the nations, have acquired one type of music.</p>\n\n<p>The foundation of their music, like their other sciences and industries, has been borrowed from the two ancient countries of <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%DB%8C%D9%88%D9%86%D8%A7%D9%86\">Greece</a> and <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%B1%D9%88%D9%85\">Rome</a>. Since they share the same religious beliefs and have had historical, political, and social connections for a long time, their music has also been built on the same foundation.</p>\n\n<p>Although the national music of the Russians, due to that country’s proximity to the East, has acquired a distinguished and different style compared to Western countries, and the melodies and tunes of Russian music are the boundary between Eastern and Western music, because the foundation of the knowledge of Russian musicians has also been in other European countries, the official music of Russia has also come under the influence of the music of France, Germany, and Italy. After the new Russian musicians wanted to establish their music on a scientific basis, they fully adapted it to the rules and laws of the music of those three countries.</p>\n\n<p>The only advantage it had was that they preserved their national melodies and tunes with some changes. Since these tunes were created by the taste of musicians who had relations with Eastern music, even now the music of this country seems more attractive and pleasant to us than the music of the more distant European countries.</p>\n\n<p>In any case, the European countries are closer to each other and are historically newer than the Eastern countries. Their social, literary, moral, and religious relations have also made their industries similar to each other, and the manifestation of the science and industry of art has given their music a special form that differs from our music both in terms of foundation and origin and in terms of state and quality.</p>\n\n<p>On the other hand, the same relationship and proximity among the Eastern countries has also existed since ancient times. For example, the countries of Iran, India, Iraq, Turkey, Afghanistan, and even some neighboring regions of Iran such as the Caucasus and Turkestan, which are close to our country and have historical connections, have acquired another type of music that, in addition to having a common foundation and origin, also has a natural commonality in state and quality.</p>\n\n<p>For this reason, understanding and comprehending the national music of the Caucasus, Arabs, Turks, and Indians is very simple for us, and not only music experts and Iranian composers and players recognize the music of these countries as extremely close to each other, but also the general public of Iran derives complete pleasure from hearing the music of these countries, which they call Eastern music. The difference that, for example, exists between the voice of an Iranian singer and an Arab, Turkish, or Caucasian singer is so small that it is not comparable to the voice of a German, French, or Italian singer.</p>\n\n<p>In the music encyclopedia that was compiled in France, and the music of each country was written by its own experts, in a discussion about Turkish, Arab, and Iranian music, the author who has spoken about Arab music and the music of Arab-speaking countries such as Algeria, Morocco, and southern Spain has mentioned <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%81%D8%A7%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A8%DB%8C\">Farabi</a>, <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A7%D8%A8%D9%86_%D8%B3%DB%8C%D9%86%D8%A7\">Ibn Sina</a>, and <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%B5%D9%81%DB%8C%E2%80%8C%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AF%DB%8C%D9%86_%D8%A7%D8%B1%D9%85%D9%88%DB%8C\">Safi al-Din Urmawi</a>, who are famous Iranian musicians, as Arab musicians and has spoken about the rules of Arab music.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_2933\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"300\"]<img class=\"wp-image-2933 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/صفحه-ای-رساله-شرقیه-صفی-الدین-اُرموی.jpg\" alt=\"صفحه ای رساله شرقیه صفی الدین اُرموی\" width=\"300\" height=\"208\" /> A page from Safi al-Din Urmawi’s Sharqiyya Treatise[/caption]\n\n<p>Similarly, the author who has studied Turkish music has considered Ibn Sina, Farabi, Safi al-Din Urmawi, <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%B9%D8%A8%D8%AF%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%A7%D8%AF%D8%B1_%D9%85%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%BA%D9%87%E2%80%8C%D8%A7%DB%8C\">Abd al-Qadir Maraghi</a>, and even <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A7%D8%A8%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D8%B1%D8%AC_%D8%A7%D8%B5%D9%81%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%86%DB%8C\">Abu al-Faraj Isfahani</a> (with reference to his relation to the city of Isfahan) as Turkish musicians and has spoken in detail about their books, writing style, and principles of music. But what is surprising here is that the person who has written a few brief pages about Iranian music has been content with references and has spoken about books that have no value compared to the books of Farabi and Safi al-Din Urmawi.</p>\n\n<p>Because most of the books that have been written on music are in Arabic, i.e., the scientific language of the author’s period, although the authors were Iranian, their writing in Arabic has caused some to consider the composers and authors as Arabs and of Arab origin. That is why Arab music has been explained with such expansion and detail in the aforementioned encyclopedia, while Iranian music has been passed over with a brief reference. On the other hand, the author of the article on Turkish music has considered famous Iranian musicians and philosophers as Turks and, although their books are in Arabic, has considered their statements as those of Turkish musicians.</p>\n\n<p>Of course, the reason for this inclination to such attribution is understandable, because the music of Iran, Turkey, and the Arabs is the same in foundation, and if we want to find a scientific document for the music of these three countries, there is no solution but to first consider Farabi as the writer of music and build the foundation of the music of these countries on his statements. For this reason, Farabi has been considered Arab in some pages of the book and Turkish in other pages of the same book.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_2935\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"569\"]<img class=\"wp-image-2935 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/ابونصر-فارابی.jpg\" alt=\"ابونصر فارابی فیلسوف ایرانی\" width=\"569\" height=\"700\" /> Abu Nasr Farabi, Iranian philosopher[/caption]\n\n<p>On the other hand, the person who has spoken about Iranian music, since he himself was European, has not paid attention to the origin and lineage of this famous philosopher, and since his book was written in Arabic, he has looked for books that were composed in Persian, and since he did not have access to the Durrat al-Taj or the books of Abd al-Qadir Maraghi either, he has collected brief information from Jami’s music treatise and one or two incomplete books.</p>\n\n<p>So, just as Farabi, the Iranian philosopher, has been considered Arab and Turkish in the French music encyclopedia, it is well evident and clear that the music of Iran, Turkey, and the Arabs has been built on one foundation and origin, and their principle is the same, and Farabi, while being universal — so that other nations also consider him as their own for the sake of pride — is Iranian.</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, we know that the music of Eastern countries has many aspects in common. Now it must be said that the music of the East and the West differs in two respects: one in terms of foundation, origin, and basis, and the other in terms of state and quality. The foundation and basis of Eastern music, as mentioned, is the scale. Here, by Eastern countries, we mean countries that are close to Iran, such as India, Afghanistan, Turkestan, the Caucasus, Turkey, Iraq, Syria, and Palestine, not the Far Eastern countries such as China, Japan, and Korea.</p>\n\n<p>As we mentioned earlier, in world music, there are two types of scales called <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d8%a7%da%98%d9%88%d8%b1/\">major scale</a> and minor scale, and the minor scale exists in three states: theoretical minor, harmonic minor, and melodic minor. Therefore, the foundation of European music is formed by the major scale and various types of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%db%8c%d9%86%d9%88%d8%b1/\">minor scale</a>. The intervals used in these scales are the same as those we have mentioned earlier in the relevant articles.</p>\n\n<p>In other words, the melodies of Europeans are formed from half-steps, whole steps, and the intervals that are composed of them. But as we mentioned earlier, in the old music of Iran, there were also other intervals that are not found in the music of European countries. For example, the scale of the old music of Iran had 17 intervals or dimensions, whereas the chromatic scale has no more than 12 intervals.</p>\n\n<p>These five additional intervals are intervals that are smaller than a half-step, and as a result of the emergence of intervals smaller than a half-step, other dimensions or intervals have been created that are larger than a half-step and smaller than a whole step. It is not known for sure that these intervals are necessarily quarter-steps or three-quarter-steps, but for reasons, in order to bring our music under the well-known rule of temperament, it is better to consider the Iranian scale as composed of 24 equal quarter-step intervals, and it is these intervals that have created the maqams of our music and distinguished them from Western scales.</p>\n\n<p>So, from a scientific point of view, the fundamental difference between Iranian and Western music is that Western scales are formed from half-steps and the intervals that are composed of half-steps, while Eastern scales are formed from quarter-step intervals and their combinations.</p>\n\n<p>In addition to this, the state of Iranian music is different from Western music, and this topic becomes clearer and better after explaining and describing the maqams of Iranian music.</p>\n\n<p>Now, for greater clarity of the matter and especially to prove how much Iranian music is still similar to its old music, we compare the frets that are tied at one time on the famous old instrument, namely the oud, and the usual instrument in contemporary Iranian music, namely the tar. This point should be noted before examining the fret arrangements: we have started this one time from the note “Do,” which is the open string of the bass of the oud and the open strings of the white strings of the tar (the pair of the first string).</p>\n\n<p>Frets of the oud in one time   Frets of the tar in one time</p>\n\n<p>Do               Do</p>\n\n<p>Re bemol            This fret is used in 28-fret tars and does not exist in 25-fret instruments.</p>\n\n<p>Re koron            Re koron</p>\n\n<p>Re               Re</p>\n\n<p>Mi bemol            Mi bemol</p>\n\n<p>Mi koron            Mi koron</p>\n\n<p>Mi               Mi</p>\n\n<p>Fa               Fa</p>\n\n<p>Sol bemol            Fa sori (instead of Sol bemol)</p>\n\n<p>Sol koron            Sol koron</p>\n\n<p>Sol               Sol</p>\n\n<p>La bemol            La bemol</p>\n\n<p>La koron            La koron</p>\n\n<p>La               La</p>\n\n<p>Si bemol            Si bemol</p>\n\n<p>-               Si koron</p>\n\n<p>Si               Si</p>\n\n<p>Si sori             (This fret is not tied on the tar)</p>\n\n<p>Do               Do</p>\n\n<h2>The 24-Quarter-Tone Scale</h2>\n\n<p>From comparing the frets of these two instruments, the following points are clearly evident:</p>\n\n<p>1- In the past, the scale of Iranian music had seventeen intervals, and later two intervals were removed, and the Iranian scale in the Qajar period had fifteen intervals. In this period, the prominent theorist of Iranian music, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d9%86%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Ali Naqi Vaziri</a>, added several new frets to the tar, and the number of intervals of the Iranian music scale reached 18.</p>\n\n<p>2- Instead of the “Sol bemol” fret, the “Fa sori” fret was tied, because, as we will say in the future, we will need it to play the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan. Overall, this fret was not completely fixed on the tar, so that when necessary it went a little higher and became Sol bemol. Of course, today the tar is also tied in the 28-fret style, and in this case we will have both the “Fa sori” and “Sol bemol” frets.</p>\n\n<p>3- Of the seventeen intervals that existed in the old scale, ten of them were quarter-steps and seven of them were half-steps, which altogether, after dividing each half-step into two parts, become twenty-four quarter-steps, and the 24-quarter-tone scale is formed in Iranian music in this way.</p>\n\n<p>4- Of the fifteen intervals that were later found in one time (from the fret arrangement of the tar until the Qajar period), seven intervals were half-step, seven intervals were quarter-step, and one interval was also three-quarter-step, which altogether (after dividing each half-step into two parts and converting the three-quarter-step interval into three parts) becomes twenty-four quarter-steps and creates the 24-quarter-tone scale.</p>\n\n<p>This point should also be noted that some of the frets of the tar are still not tied completely equally, meaning the rule of temperament has not been observed in them. For example, in tars that are fretted in the usual style, they have used four frets from “Fa” to “Sol” (Fa – Fa sori – Sol koron – Sol natural), whose intervals are quarter-step – half-step – quarter-step.</p>\n\n<p>With this arrangement, if we place one more correct fret exactly in the middle of Fa sori and Sol koron (which is done in 28-fret instruments), so that all intervals become quarter-steps, we see that the frets will not be completely equal, and “Fa sori” will be closer to “Fa diez” than to “Fa natural.”</p>\n\n<p>The inequality of the frets is because the rule of temperament has not yet been properly observed, and if we want to have a scale with twenty-four quarter-steps, we must consider all quarter-steps as equal and take them out of their natural state so that if one day an instrument is made that has twenty-four equal quarter-steps, its sound will not differ from the sound of other instruments. Of course, whenever such an instrument is made and becomes common, the duty of other instruments will also become clear, and these minor points, which are not even noticeable to trained and precise ears, will not be so perceptible.</p>\n\n<p>Now, if we add one more fret between the half-step intervals and tie two frets between the interval that is formed from three-quarter-steps, we will find a scale composed of twenty-five notes and twenty-four quarter-steps (the 24-quarter-tone scale).</p>\n\n<p>This scale is the foundation of Iranian music, from which, by selecting eight of its notes, one can form a maqam, and the scales and maqams of Iranian music are all formed in the same way. As we will explain in detail later, our music has maqams that the current Western music lacks, and for this reason, we consider the foundation of our music to be more complete than Western music. In this regard, we have spoken in the articles related to the introduction of dastgahs and avazes.</p>\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the article on the theory of the 24-quarter-tone scale, please share them in the comments section of the article with us to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-07-21 07:46:51', 0, '2024-09-18 22:44:22', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1491, 'posts', 34, 'en', NULL, 'What is a motif? Beethoven\'s Symphony No. 5 as a prime example of the development and expansion of a single motif', NULL, 'This article explores the concept of the “motif” as the smallest independent unit in music. Motifs are short musical ideas consisting of a few notes that combine to form musical phrases and ultimately complete melodies. They are categorized into three types: melodic, rhythmic, and melodic-rhythmic, depending on whether pitch intervals, rhythm, or both are emphasized.\n\nThe most important way to identify a motif in a piece is through its repetition throughout the music. Motifs are often followed by a pause or longer note values and are typically succeeded by new material. The article also discusses various techniques for developing motifs, such as transposition, partial repetition, reordering, rhythmic alteration, and interval changes. As a notable example, Beethoven’s Symphony No. 5 is highlighted, where an entire composition is developed from a short initial motif that appears in different forms throughout the piece.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the concept of <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">motif</span></strong> in music and the ways to identify and develop and expand it.</p>\n\n<h2>What is a Motif?</h2>\n\n<p>A <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%84%d9%88%d8%af%db%8c/\">melody</a> manifests itself in the minds of music listeners as a complete statement that expresses a specific concept, but <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A2%D9%87%D9%86%DA%AF%D8%B3%D8%A7%D8%B2#:~:text=%D8%A2%D9%87%D9%86%DA%AF%D8%B3%D8%A7%D8%B2%20%DB%8C%D8%A7%20%DA%A9%D9%8F%D9%85%D9%BE%D9%88%D8%B2%DB%8C%D8%AA%D9%88%D8%B2%20(%D8%A8%D9%87%20%D9%81%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%86%D8%B3%D9%88%DB%8C,%D9%85%D9%88%D8%B3%DB%8C%D9%82%DB%8C%20%D8%A8%D9%87%20%D8%B1%D8%B4%D8%AA%D9%87%D9%94%20%D9%86%DA%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1%D8%B4%20%D8%AF%D8%B1%D9%85%DB%8C%E2%80%8C%D8%A2%D9%88%D8%B1%D8%AF.\">composers</a> create the melodies of their works by placing small components next to each other in various forms. These smaller components, which cause the creation of musical phrases and the formation of melodies, are called motifs. In this way, a motif is recognized as the smallest independent unit of music, which is composed of a limited number of notes.</p>\n\n<p>Motifs are usually divided into the following three structural categories:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Melodic motif: They gain importance due to the existence of specific intervals between their constituent notes.</li>\n  <li>Rhythmic motif: They gain importance due to the existence of a specific rhythm that is repeated continuously, and the melodic intervals between the motif’s notes are not of special importance compared to its rhythm.</li>\n  <li>Melodic-rhythmic motif: These motifs are a combination of both previous states, because they have a specific and repeatable rhythm, and in addition, the intervals between the notes also gain importance.</li>\n</ul>\n\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>Methods of Identification</h2>\n\n<p>An important question that always occupies the minds of music students and is always asked is: how can one detect and identify a motif in a musical work? And what are the most important methods for detecting and identifying it? The most important and prominent sign for detecting a motif in a musical piece is its repetition throughout the melody.</p>\n\n<p>In summary, three signs can be mentioned for identifying a motif:</p>\n\n<p>1) They are usually repeated immediately after completion.</p>\n\n<p>2) Generally, a silence sign or long durations are placed at their end.</p>\n\n<p>3) After the end, new material is presented.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>Methods of Expansion</h2>\n\n<p>As we said earlier, in a musical piece, the composer usually creates phrases by placing motifs next to each other, and melodies are formed by placing musical phrases next to each other. Therefore, one of the most important characteristics of a good and professional composer is the ability to develop and expand motifs and create musical phrases. There are various ways to expand motifs, the most important of which are:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Repetition from another interval (repetition from a higher or lower note)</li>\n  <li>Repetition of part of the notes (if we consider the rhythm or melody of the motif in several sections, sometimes one or two sections of it are repeated and then the next section comes)</li>\n  <li>Displacement of its sections (meaning that if we divide the rhythm and melody of the motif into two or more sections, we change the original and initial order of these sections)</li>\n  <li>Creating change in the duration of some of its notes (this happens by shortening or lengthening part of the motif’s notes)</li>\n  <li>Change in the intervals between the motif’s notes (which is sometimes accompanied by a change in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">scale</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n\nAn excellent example for the development and expansion of a motif is Beethoven’s Symphony No. 5, which is completely formed by the development and expansion of a short initial motif. This motif is developed and performed with various states, diverse and different orchestration, different dynamics and tempos, and in different sonic registers, and in various places of the piece and at every moment of it, we can feel and hear this short initial motif.\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the concept of motif, please share them in the comments section of the article with us to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-07-23 03:33:46', 0, '2024-09-18 22:43:45', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1492, 'posts', 35, 'en', NULL, 'Biography and works of Master Mohammad Reza Lotfi; the Qalandar of Iranian music', NULL, 'This article explores the life and artistic career of Mohammad Reza Lotfi, one of the most influential Iranian musicians and masters of the tar and setar.\n\nBorn in 1946 in Gorgan, he grew up in a culturally rich and music-friendly family. He later moved to Tehran to pursue formal music education and studied under prominent masters. Lotfi quickly rose to prominence, earning awards and eventually teaching at the University of Tehran.\n\nHis major contributions include his work with the Center for Preservation and Propagation of Music, collaboration with national radio, formation of the Sheida Ensemble, and a leading role in the Chavosh Cultural Center. These efforts played a key role in reviving traditional Persian music and attracting younger audiences.\n\nHe is also known for innovative achievements such as the famous Rast-Panjgah performance and introducing the daf into formal Iranian classical music.\n\nAfter the 1979 revolution, due to increasing restrictions, he left Iran and spent many years in the United States, where he founded the Ava-ye Sheida institute. Upon returning to Iran, he continued teaching, composing, and mentoring students.\n\nLotfi passed away in 2014, leaving behind a lasting legacy through his works, influence on musical taste, and significant role in the revival and evolution of Iranian traditional music.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the biography and artistic life of Master <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Mohammad Reza</span> <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Lotfi</span></strong></span>, the prominent tar and setar player, and become familiar with his works, collaborations, and legacy. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>Family and Birth</h2>\n\n<p>Mohammad Reza Lotfi was born on 17 Dey 1325 solar year (7 January 1947 AD) in the city of Gorgan in a cultured family where both his father and mother were teachers. His mother was from Gorgan and his father was born in Khalkhal. Because of the outbreak of cholera, which took his entire family, he was sent to Rudsar to live with one of his relatives. These two, during their youth and when they were working as teachers, married each other following a decree from Reza Shah that required all single teachers to marry within three months, and they taught for 15 years in the Turkmen Sahara. His father left the teaching profession and engaged in trade and agriculture.</p>\n\n<p>Master Mohammad Reza Lotfi describes his father and mother as lovers and enthusiasts of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">music</a> and adds that his father was also a singer and tar player. He believes that his father’s voice was similar to that of Reza Qoli Mirza Zelli, but with more resonance. In addition to his father, his brother was also a tar player, both of whom had become familiar with this instrument through one of their father’s friends. Lotfi says he grew up with the sound of his brother’s tar and was more influenced by his brother’s tar playing than by his father’s cultural life.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3002\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"512\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3002 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/محمدرضا-لطفی-1.jpg\" alt=\"محمدرضا لطفی متولد 17 دی ماه 1325\" width=\"512\" height=\"341\" /> Mohammad Reza Lotfi, born 17 Dey 1325[/caption]\n\n<h2>Education of Master Mohammad Reza Lotfi</h2>\n\n<p>Mohammad Reza Lotfi completed his general education in the same city of Gorgan and, in early youth, moved to Tehran to learn the tar. He initially enrolled in the evening classes of the music conservatory and then also gained admission to the daytime classes. Lotfi’s acquaintance with Hossein Alizadeh and Dariush Talaei also dates back to this year (his first year at the music conservatory). He studied music for five years in the free classes of the music conservatory under masters such as <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b2%d9%86%d8%af%da%af%db%8c%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%85%d9%87-%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d8%a7%da%a9%d8%a8%d8%b1-%d8%b4%d9%87%d9%86%d8%a7%d8%b2%db%8c/\">Ali Akbar Shahnazi</a> and Habibollah Salehi.</p>\n\n<p>Mohammad Reza Lotfi, during his conservatory period, was invited by Hossein Dehlavi to Saba Orchestra and worked as a tar player there, and in 1343 solar year (1964 AD) he also won the first prize of the Young Musicians. After entering university, in the 1352–1353 academic year, under the supervision of Mohammad Taghi Massoudieh and with a thesis on Vocal Music of Iran – Isfahan and Tabriz Schools, he successfully received a bachelor’s degree from the Faculty of Fine Arts of Tehran and began teaching there. In 1353 solar year (1974 AD), he became a member of the scientific group of the music faculty and for one and a half years was the head of the music group of the Faculty of Music of the <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%AF%D8%A7%D9%86%D8%B4%DA%AF%D8%A7%D9%87_%D8%AA%D9%87%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%86\">University of Tehran</a> until he finally resigned from this position.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3003\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"600\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3003 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/محمد-رضا-لطفی.jpg\" alt=\"محمد رضا لطفی نوازنده برجسته تار و سه تار\" width=\"600\" height=\"460\" /> Mohammad Reza Lotfi, prominent tar and setar player[/caption]\n\n<h2>Center for the Preservation and Propagation of Music</h2>\n\n<p>After entering university, Mohammad Reza Lotfi joined the Center for the Preservation and Propagation of Music, and at this time, in learning vocal and instrumental radifs, he also benefited from masters such as Nour Ali Khan Boroumand, Abdollah Khan Davami, and Saeed Hormozi; but he very soon left the Center for the Preservation and Propagation, which is said to be due to problems between him and Dariush Safvat, the director of the center at that time, because of Lotfi’s leftist beliefs. Arshad Tahmasbi believes that Master Mohammad Reza Lotfi left this center because it had deviated from its main path. However, Lotfi maintained his relationship with Nour Ali Boroumand and remained loyal to him.</p>\n\n<p>Master Mohammad Reza Lotfi, simultaneously with the tar, was also engaged in playing classical violin, and according to himself, at the age of 23, after completing all the training of the radif, rengs, zarbis, etc., he abandoned Iranian music to go to Western classical music and continue his studies in the <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A7%DB%8C%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AA_%D9%85%D8%AA%D8%AD%D8%AF%D9%87_%D8%A2%D9%85%D8%B1%DB%8C%DA%A9%D8%A7\">United States of America</a>; therefore, for a long time, he only engaged in activities in the field of Western classical music and violin playing.</p>\n\n<p>He himself recounts that after some time, one day his eyes fell on his setar hanging on the wall of the room, and out of longing, he picked up the setar and started playing. He adds:</p>\n\n<p>“As soon as I struck two nails on it, tears flowed from my eyes. I saw that Western music never gives me such a state.”</p>\n\n<p>Master Lotfi says that after this incident, since Iranian music needed him, he decided to devote himself only to Iranian music and to keep Western classical music only as a support in his mind.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3012\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"720\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3012 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/استاد-لطفی.jpg\" alt=\"استاد لطفی و نوازندگی سه تار\" width=\"720\" height=\"557\" /> Master Lotfi and setar playing[/caption]\n\n<h2>Entry into Radio and Formation of the Sheyda Group</h2>\n\n<p>Mohammad Reza Lotfi’s official activity began in 1350 solar year (1971 AD), although before that he had also performed on stage several times in Sanandaj and Gorgan.</p>\n\n<p>After leaving the Center for the Preservation and Propagation of Music, Mohammad Reza Lotfi went to the radio and, with the support of Houshang Ebtehaj (Saya), was attracted to this organization. For presence on the radio, he had to pass the entrance exam, but on the suggestion of his friends, he decided not to participate in the exam, but to compose a piece and take it to the radio. Lotfi took a tasnif he had composed years earlier on one of Molana’s poems to the radio to Houshang Ebtehaj, the music director of the radio at the time.</p>\n\n<p>This tasnif was ultimately approved by the radio, and with arrangement by Javad Ma’rufi for the large radio orchestra and singing by Marzieh, it was recorded. This was Mohammad Reza Lotfi’s first work and was broadcast on the radio under the name “Golha-ye Tazeh No. 126” in 1354 and 1355 solar years (1975–1976 AD). In this program, Master Lotfi himself took on the tar solo, and Nasser Farhangfar performed the tombak accompaniment. Before this program, artists who had memorized even one complete radif of Iranian music were very rare, and performing the program based on radif sentences was unusual.</p>\n\n<p>However, Mohammad Reza Lotfi had based his solos in this program on radif sentences, which at that time was considered an innovative move. From that time, a deep division arose among music lovers regarding Lotfi’s work style; some were strongly fascinated by his work, and some denied the beauties of his work.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3013\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"533\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3013 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/محمدرضا-لطفی-قلندر-موسیقی-ایران.jpg\" alt=\"محمدرضا لطفی قلندر موسیقی ایران\" width=\"533\" height=\"800\" /> Mohammad Reza Lotfi, the Qalandar of Iranian Music[/caption]\n\n<p>After Mohammad Reza Lotfi’s entry into the radio, Houshang Ebtehaj, who had obtained a separate budget from the radio director at the time to promote and propagate Iranian music, asked Lotfi to form a group and produce works in music. The initial members of Mohammad Reza Lotfi’s group were the Kamkar family, Zeydollah Tolu’i, Hadi Montazeri, and Darvish Reza Montazemi. This group, at Ebtehaj’s request, engaged in re-creating the works of the past, and because the first work produced by this group was one of Ali Akbar Sheyda’s compositions, at Ebtehaj’s suggestion, the group was named Sheyda.</p>\n\n<p>Of course, Mohammad Reza Lotfi, in collaboration with Ali Akbar Shekarchi, Hossein Omoumi, and Nasser Farhangfar, had laid the foundations for the establishment of the Sheyda Group in 1351 solar year (1972 AD) at the University of Tehran, but the official activity of this group began in 1353 solar year (1974 AD) with the collaboration of Houshang Ebtehaj on Iran Radio.</p>\n\n<p>Ebtehaj, to better introduce the works of this group, had established a program on the radio called “Golchin-e Hafteh,” which every Friday morning broadcast the works of the Sheyda Group.</p>\n\n<p>This program had a very great impact on introducing Iranian music based on the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%af%db%8c%d9%81/\">radif</a> to young people and attracting them to Iranian music. The enthusiasm that was created among people at that time for learning and playing the tar caused the price of the tar to increase from two tomans at that time to fifty tomans; in other words, as Ebtehaj says, Lotfi made the tar expensive.</p>\n\n<h2>Rast Panjgah Concert</h2>\n\n<p>Mohammad Reza Lotfi, in 1354 solar year (1975 AD), at the Shiraz Arts Festival, along with Mohammad Reza Shajarian and Nasser Farhangfar, performed in the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b1%d8%a7%d8%b3%d8%aa-%d9%be%d9%86%d8%ac%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">dastgah of Rast Panjgah</a>, which received a great deal of attention. The dastgah of Rast Panjgah was known among the ancients as the final skill, and in fact, this dastgah was the last dastgah that the masters taught to a player or singer.</p>\n\n<p>Until before the performance of this work at the Shiraz Arts Festival by Master Lotfi, for many years no piece had been performed in this dastgah, and in fact, this dastgah had been neglected. The reason for not performing in this dastgah was its difficult learning; because in Rast Panjgah, modulation is much more than in other dastgahs, and for this reason, great precision is necessary in learning and performing it. This performance, according to Arshad Tahmasbi, is still the best criterion for measuring the works of other artists in performing instrumental and vocal music.</p>\n\n<p>Tahmasbi says this was the first time that listeners, without feeling tired, listened to a long instrumental and vocal performance to the end. This performance, in addition to attracting a special audience of traditional music, also drew the attention of a wide range of general audiences. Arshad Tahmasbi considers the reason for this attractiveness to be Lotfi’s rule-based performance and believes that Mohammad Reza Lotfi, by removing the usual superficial embellishments of those days, manifested the depth and essential essence of Iranian music.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3029\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"270\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3029 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/محمدرضا-لطفی،-محمدرضا-شجریان-و-ناصر-فرهنگ‌فر-اجرای-زنده-در-حافظیه-نهمین-جشن-هنر-شیراز،-سال-۱۳٥٤.jpg\" alt=\"محمدرضا لطفی، محمدرضا شجریان و ناصر فرهنگ‌فر اجرای زنده در حافظیه نهمین جشن هنر شیراز، سال ۱۳٥٤\" width=\"270\" height=\"187\" /> Mohammad Reza Lotfi, Mohammad Reza Shajarian, and Nasser Farhangfar live performance at Hafezieh, Ninth Shiraz Arts Festival, 1354 solar year[/caption]\n\n<h2>Chehre be Chehre Concert and the First Use of Daf</h2>\n\n<p>In addition to the Golchin-e Hafteh program, which broadcast the works of the Sheyda Group, this group also performed concerts in Gorgan, Tus, and Shiraz. At the Shiraz Arts Festival, where the Sheyda Group performed pieces in the dastgah of Nava with the singing of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b6%d8%a7-%d8%b4%d8%ac%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Mohammad Reza Shajarian</a>, Lotfi for the first time used the daf instrument in traditional Iranian music, with Bijan Kamkar taking on the playing of it. This instrument, until before this, was only played in Kurdistan and khanqahs and was unknown to most Iranians; because they believed that this instrument should not be played for non-mahrams.</p>\n\n<p>The daf now holds a special position in Iranian music, but before Lotfi introduced it into Iranian music, it was practically not used in Iranian music. Lotfi himself had become familiar with this instrument during his stay in Kurdistan. He says that since he could not bring a daf player with him to Tehran, he personally learned to play this instrument and decided to introduce the heroism present in the sound and rhythm of the daf into various forms of traditional music, including pishdaramad, tasnif, and even some parts of the radif.</p>\n\n<h2>Resignation and Exit from Radio</h2>\n\n<p>The members of the Sheyda Group were preparing themselves in 1357 solar year (1978 AD) to perform the “In Memory of Aref” concert on 21 Shahrivar 1357 solar year (12 September 1978 AD) in Moscow when the events of 17 Shahrivar 1357 solar year (8 September 1978 AD) occurred. Lotfi says that he himself witnessed these events. He says that the day after this incident, when he entered the radio building for rehearsal, he encountered guard forces who had been stationed in the radio building’s grounds to hide from the public.</p>\n\n<p>Lotfi, according to his own words, became agitated upon seeing this scene and, after informing Ebtehaj of this matter and his discomfort, announced that as a musician he could not work in a radio that had become a base for armed forces and intended to resign from the radio as a sign of protest against the events of 17 Shahrivar.</p>\n\n<p>Mohammad Reza Lotfi informed the group members of the decision he had made but asked them to continue their work. But ultimately, at Ebtehaj’s suggestion, who believed that if Lotfi resigned alone, it could be dangerous for him and he might be arrested, all members of the Sheyda Group resigned together. The resignation letter of the Sheyda Group was written in Mohammad Reza Shajarian’s handwriting and was sent to the director-general of radio and television the next day. Houshang Ebtehaj also resigned independently from radio and television one day later.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3024\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"600\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3024 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/محمدرضا-لطفی-و-هوشنگ-ابتهاج.jpg\" alt=\"محمدرضا لطفی و هوشنگ ابتهاج\" width=\"600\" height=\"425\" /> Mohammad Reza Lotfi and Houshang Ebtehaj[/caption]\n\n<h2>Formation of the Chavosh Cultural and Artistic Center</h2>\n\n<p>After the resignation of the members of the Sheyda Group, the Aref Group — which had been formed on the radio a short time after the formation of the Sheyda Group with the same goal of re-reading the works of the past with Ebtehaj’s support and composition by Hossein Alizadeh and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%be%d8%b1%d9%88%db%8c%d8%b2-%d9%85%d8%b4%da%a9%d8%a7%d8%aa%db%8c%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Parviz Meshkatian</a> — also resigned from radio and television in protest against the 17 Shahrivar incident. These musicians were called the Revival Movement and were under the support of Reza Ghotbi, the director of radio and television at the time.</p>\n\n<p>From then on, the Sheyda Group, along with the Aref Group, which had joined them after the resignation, continued their activities independently. The basement of Mohammad Reza Lotfi’s house was the place where these two groups gathered and performed and recorded revolutionary songs. The group members themselves duplicated and sold these performances in the same basement after recording them.</p>\n\n<p>These songs were performed and recorded in three stages. In the first stage, these songs were recorded without instruments and in the form of a chorus so that the group members would not be identified; then the same performances were recorded again by Hossein Alizadeh with a mandolin and chorus; and finally, the same performances were recorded and duplicated in the form of group playing in the studio, the first product of which was the songs “Shab-e Navard” and “Azadi,” which later took the name Chavosh 2.</p>\n\n<p>Some time later, with the addition of other players to this group, the basement of Master Lotfi’s house no longer had enough capacity. Houshang Ebtehaj, to solve this problem, placed a large three-story building at the disposal of this group, and at his own suggestion, named this center the Chavosh Center. According to Zeydollah Tolu’i, since Hossein Alizadeh had just joined them, Lotfi had asked him to obtain the license for this center in Hossein Alizadeh’s name so that he could keep him in the center; ultimately, the license was registered in Hossein Alizadeh’s name.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3026\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"600\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3026 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/استاد-محمدرضا-لطفی،-شهرام-ناظری-و-پشنگ-کامکار-در-جشن-هنر-شیراز.jpg\" alt=\"استاد محمدرضا لطفی، شهرام ناظری و پشنگ کامکار در جشن هنر شیراز\" width=\"600\" height=\"416\" /> Master Mohammad Reza Lotfi, Shahram Nazeri, and Peshang Kamkar at the Shiraz Arts Festival[/caption]\n\n<p>Houshang Ebtehaj took on the management of the center, Mohammad Reza Lotfi became the chairman of the board of trustees, and the management of the school was delegated to Hossein Alizadeh. In addition to the playing classes, the school section of the center had three avaz classes, each of which was managed by Mohammad Reza Shajarian, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b4%d9%87%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%86%d8%a7%d8%b8%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Shahram Nazeri</a>, and Nasrollah Naseh Pour.</p>\n\n<p>The works of the Chavosh Center are both of great social-political importance and, from a musical point of view, created an important transformation and progress in the field of traditional Iranian music, which had a great impact both on the style and performance method (both playing and singing), on the approach to a musical piece as a whole, on the method of coloring and sound production of the orchestra, and especially on the effort to use traditional Iranian multi-voice writing techniques.</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%af%d9%87/\">Hossein Alizadeh</a> says these works were a new experience in Iranian music that had no precedent before, and we had not learned anywhere that Iranian music could, in addition to being salon music, also be music in the service of society; and this is something that society itself taught us.</p>\n\n<p>Mohammad Reza Lotfi in Chavosh 2, which was the first work of the Chavosh Center influenced by the political atmosphere of Iranian society at that time, used the same dastgah music and instrumental and vocal music with some changes. For example, in addition to choosing revolutionary poems for this work, he used declamation tools at the beginning of the work, and also, by making changes in Shajarian’s avaz singing, contrary to the usual custom in traditional avaz, instead of starting the performance from the daramad, the avaz started from the ouj.</p>\n\n<p>Mohammad Reza Lotfi also, in the song “Azadi,” used the tombak rize, and in repeating the poetry, replaced group singing with Shahram Nazeri’s voice.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3027\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"600\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3027 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/محمدرضا-لطفی-در-کنار-شهرام-ناظری-و-حسین-علیزاده.jpg\" alt=\"محمدرضا لطفی در کنار ارشد طهماسبی و حسین علیزاده\" width=\"600\" height=\"424\" /> Mohammad Reza Lotfi with Arshad Tahmasbi and Hossein Alizadeh[/caption]\n\n<p>In addition to these, during Nazeri’s avaz singing, instead of answering the avaz according to custom, he performed group playing of a zarbi melody in the background of the avaz, which after that became his own technique and has been repeated and imitated many times in the works of others to this day.</p>\n\n<p>Then 12 Chavosh albums (officially 10 albums) including Chavosh 1, Chavosh 2, Chavosh 11, and Chavosh 12 were released; among these albums, collections 2 to 8 were made under the influence of the political atmosphere of Iran at that time, with clear references and allusions to the events, people, and political groups of that period.</p>\n\n<p>Alongside these activities, every Thursday, “educational concerts” of solo or ensemble playing with tombak were also held and recorded in front of a limited audience at the Chavosh Center. The purpose of these concerts was to create a space for experiencing solo playing, and Mohammad Reza Lotfi himself also played in these sessions; one of his solos in these sessions in the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan was released in Chavosh 8 album.</p>\n\n<p>Arshad Tahmasbi considers this solo of Mohammad Reza Lotfi to be the most beautiful solo in the history of the tar, and Keyvan Sakt also introduces it as a unique piece. Houshang Ebtehaj says about this solo: “… no player, neither before him, nor in his time, nor probably after him, has done or can do this work.”</p>\n\n<h2>Restrictions After the 1357 Revolution</h2>\n\n<p>The authorities of that time ultimately prevented the release of music cassettes at the Chavosh Center and closed the duplication room of this center. But Lotfi, with a special capacity, circumvented the law and released the cassette “In Memory of Darvish Khan.” Lotfi wrote on the spine of this cassette: “Private cassette for music lovers (not for duplication) price 150 tomans.”</p>\n\n<p>He also released the album “In Memory of Taherzadeh” in the same way; and in this way, others such as Ata’ollah Jangouk and Zeydollah Tolu’i also sent their playing albums to the market with the same method.</p>\n\n<h2>Release of Mohammad Reza Lotfi’s First Setar Playing</h2>\n\n<p>Mohammad Reza Lotfi, while playing the tar, also played the setar; but the first time Lotfi’s setar playing was officially heard was in 1362 solar year (1983 AD), simultaneously with the release of the album “In Memory of Darvish Khan” — even though Ebtehaj had asked him eight years earlier to release a work with setar playing.</p>\n\n<p>The release of this album created great enthusiasm among young people for learning the setar; to the extent that the setar teachers of the Chavosh Center, who were only Lotfi and Mohammad Firouzi, could not cope with teaching this number of applicants, and the tar teachers of the center also ultimately engaged in teaching the setar. The setar instrument, until before the revolution, was a neglected instrument, which for years only one prominent player was engaged in playing this instrument; Ahmad Ebadi.</p>\n\n<p>Mohammad Reza Lotfi, in the first performance he released with the setar, followed the same method he had previously followed with the tar. He based his playing on the radif and combined it with salon playing in a proper way and presented it. In setar playing, he was more influenced than anyone by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%84%d8%ad%d8%b3%d9%86-%d8%b5%d8%a8%d8%a7/\">Abolhasan Saba</a> and also had a slight inclination toward Ahmad Ebadi’s playing.</p>\n\n<p>The album “In Memory of Darvish Khan” was the result of eight years of Lotfi’s effort and hard work on the setar instrument, which had an extraordinary result. Lotfi later, in the performances he gave with the setar outside Iran, mostly used this same setar playing method. Also, another noteworthy point of the album “In Memory of Darvish Khan” was that Lotfi, for the first time in this album, engaged in singing.</p>\n\n<h2>Exit from Iran and Dissolution of the Chavosh Center</h2>\n\n<p>In 1363 solar year (1984 AD), most music schools and centers were closed and sealed one after another by the “Places Administration.” At that time, Lotfi could neither perform a concert, nor release an album, nor even hold teaching classes. In those years, according to Arshad Tahmasbi, no one could even carry an instrument with them. In this way, Mohammad Reza Lotfi could no longer tolerate staying in Iran; he, who had once strongly opposed Majid Derakhshani’s exit from Iran, himself had to leave Iran.</p>\n\n<p>So Lotfi first traveled to Italy for a concert, which some believe led to his residence in Europe after this trip. But Arshad Tahmasbi, considering the activities he had with Mohammad Reza Lotfi at that time and the dates Lotfi had registered in his notebook, believes that Lotfi returned to Iran after this trip, spent a few months in Iran, and ultimately traveled to Italy again, and this second trip led to his residence in Europe.</p>\n\n<p>He himself mentions the date of exit from Iran as 1364 solar year (1985 AD) and explains that he was supposed to return to Iran after two weeks, but with his placement in European and American concert tours, after two years, his conditions changed in such a way that it prevented his return to Iran.</p>\n\n<p>The Chavosh Center continued its activities for some time after the trip of Mohammad Reza Lotfi and Hossein Alizadeh — who had gone to Europe before him — but ultimately, during the visit of government officials to this center, due to the absence of the manager and license holder (Hossein Alizadeh), this place was sealed and the Chavosh Center was practically closed in 1363 solar year (1984 AD).</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3045\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"720\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3045 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/121800_171.jpg\" alt=\"محمدرضا لطفی در کنار همکاران خود\" width=\"720\" height=\"421\" /> Mohammad Reza Lotfi with his colleagues[/caption]\n\n<h2>Mohammad Reza Lotfi’s Residence in America</h2>\n\n<p>After leaving Iran, Mohammad Reza Lotfi was in very close contact with Hossein Alizadeh and played duets with him. Hossein Alizadeh considers these joint duets with Mohammad Reza Lotfi, due to the common feeling they had with different expressions in their performances, to be revolutionary performances.</p>\n\n<p>These two then joined Hossein Omoumi and Mohammad Qavi Helm. In 1986, their four-member group held a large concert in Paris. This group then went to America for a concert tour, which, according to Hossein Alizadeh, Mohammad Reza Lotfi very reluctantly accompanied them on the trip to America, but as soon as he arrived in America, he looked for ways to stay in America.</p>\n\n<p>He ultimately resided in America in 1365 solar year (1986 AD) and lived in this country until 1385 solar year (2006 AD), when he returned to Iran. During these years, in addition to numerous concerts throughout America, he founded the Cultural and Artistic Institute of Avay-e Sheyda in Washington, D.C., and opened other branches of it in various cities of the world, including Paris, Zurich, and Geneva.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3044\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"600\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3044 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/محمد-رضا-لطفی-2.jpg\" alt=\"محمد رضا لطفی در آمریکا\" width=\"600\" height=\"370\" /> Mohammad Reza Lotfi in America[/caption]\n\n<h2>Return to Iran</h2>\n\n<p>Mohammad Reza Lotfi returned to Iran in 1385 solar year (2006 AD) and continued his artistic activity by training students in the Mirza Abdollah School and recording works at the Avay-e Sheyda Institute. In these years, by preparing programs on music recognition and music history and broadcasting them on Radio Farhang (1391–1392 solar years), he took another step in the path of familiarizing people with Iranian music.</p>\n\n<h2>Collaboration with Others in Iran</h2>\n\n<p>Among the singers who collaborated with him throughout his artistic activity years, one can mention <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%af%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%85%db%8c/\">Abdollah Khan Davami</a>, Marzieh, Nasrollah Naseh Pour, Mohammad Reza Shajarian, Shahram Nazeri, Hangameh Akhavan, Sadiq Define, Zoya Sabet, Alireza Shahmohammadi, and Mohammad Motamedi.</p>\n\n<p>Lotfi, in a joint work with Ehsan Tabari in an album named “Az Miyan-e Rig-ha va Almas-ha,” after the 1357 revolution, engaged in improvisation alongside Tabari’s poetry reading.</p>\n\n<p>The music for the film “Haji Washington,” directed by Ali Hatami, is composed by Mohammad Reza Lotfi. This film was made in 1361 solar year (1982 AD).</p>\n\n<p>Mohammad Reza Lotfi with improvisation on the tar and Mahmoud Dowlatabadi with improvised reading of the great novel “Kelidar” created a joint work. According to Mahmoud Dowlatabadi, the revised text of this novel had been sent to the Ministry of Culture and Islamic Guidance by Thaleth Publications from the beginning of 1383 solar year (2004 AD) to obtain permission, but ultimately, according to Mahmoud Dowlatabadi, this work did not receive publication permission and was not published.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3054\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"477\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3054 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/آلبوم-از-میان-ریگ‌ها-و-الماس‌ها.jpg\" alt=\"محمدرضا لطفی در زمان ساخت آلبوم «از میان ریگ‌ها و الماس‌ها»\" width=\"477\" height=\"335\" /> Mohammad Reza Lotfi at the time of making the album “From Among the Sands and Diamonds”[/caption]\n\n<h2>Ketab-e Sal-e Sheyda</h2>\n\n<p>From the early 1370s solar decade (1990s), a book titled Ketab-e Sal-e Sheyda was published under the effort of Mohammad Reza Lotfi. Until the 1390s solar decade (2010s), this book entered the book market, and the subject and content of the book contained compiled articles by Iranian musicians as well as translated articles on music. Mohammad Reza Lotfi presented a research article on music in each issue of the book. This book was printed in each edition sometimes by different publishers. The cover design of the book was by Morteza Momayez.</p>\n\n<h2>Avay-e Sheyda Cultural and Artistic Institute</h2>\n\n<p>With Mohammad Reza Lotfi’s return to Iran in 1385 solar year (2006 AD), the Avay-e Sheyda Cultural and Artistic Institute began its new period of activity. The cultural policy of this institute was designed by Mohammad Reza Lotfi, the artistic and executive director, and its goal was to produce artistic music of any kind. In addition to Lotfi’s works, Avay-e Sheyda also had the production of artistic works by other artists on its agenda.</p>\n\n<h2>Sheyda Women’s Group</h2>\n\n<p>After returning to Iran, Mohammad Reza Lotfi established the Sheyda Women’s Group and performed concerts with this group. Some members of this group were: Nazanin Bazargani, Pouya Loghaei, Katayoun Malek Mote’i, Tina Pirhadi, Elmira Nasiri, Azadeh Shams, Farima Mofaghi, Hangameh Mashhadi al-Asl, Sanaz Setarzadeh, Sepideh Meshki.</p>\n\n<h2>Mirza Abdollah School</h2>\n\n<p>The Free Music Conservatory (Mirza Abdollah School) is an educational institute that was established in 1372 solar year (1993 AD) under the supervision and direction of Mohammad Reza Lotfi. This school teaches Iranian instruments and avaz with its historical method, i.e., mouth-to-ear (oral). Its content is based on Mirza Abdollah’s radif, which is known as “Matn va Hashiyeh-ye Radif.” In addition to teaching the radif, attention is also paid to the progress of the students’ technique with the method derived from the radif.</p>\n\n<h2>Controversies</h2>\n\n<p>In the final years of Mohammad Reza Lotfi’s life, some of his concerts were not allowed to be held. In Shahrivar 1391 solar year (September 2012 AD), Mohammad Reza Lotfi’s concert tour, which started on 25 Shahrivar 1391 solar year (15 September 2012 AD) in the city of Sari and was supposed to be performed in four cities of Mazandaran Province, was canceled in Babolsar due to not receiving permission. In Ordibehesht 1392 solar year (May 2013 AD), Mohammad Reza Lotfi’s concert in Andimeshk and also the second night’s concert of this artist in Behbahan were also canceled. The cancellation of this concert was in conditions where this concert had permission and the audience had also bought tickets for the second night’s performance.</p>\n\n<p>Mohammad Reza Lotfi, in a press conference in Aban 1390 solar year (November 2011 AD), criticizing the problems that the Ministry of Culture and Islamic Guidance created for his concert poster, said: The poster of our recent concert, which has a picture of me, has run into problems because of my hair and beard! While they are being strict with this picture of me on this poster, the same picture of me is printed in newspapers, and even the time I first went to radio and television, I went with such hair and beard.</p>\n\n<h2>Passing</h2>\n\n<p>Finally, Master Mohammad Reza Lotfi, on the morning of Friday, 12 Ordibehesht 1393 solar year (2 May 2014 AD), passed away at the age of 68 due to prostate cancer at Pars Hospital in Tehran.</p>\n\n<h2>Legacy of Master Mohammad Reza Lotfi</h2>\n\n<p>Mohammad Reza Lotfi, with his artist colleagues and companions, was seeking a renaissance (rebirth) in traditional Iranian music and was very influential and successful in this path. In addition to this important matter, he has left valuable works in Iranian national music that will remain eternal.</p>\n\n<p>Among other legacies of Mohammad Reza Lotfi is changing the musical taste of the people and deepening the culture of traditional and classical Iranian music. The use of the term “fakhre music” for authentic Iranian music is one of Mohammad Reza Lotfi’s achievements.</p>\n\n<p>In addition to these, Mohammad Reza Lotfi had a special playing style and transferred it to his students and left it as a legacy for Iranians.</p>\n\n<p>Being popular and creating a wave in Iranian artistic society was another valuable legacy of this liberated artist.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3055\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"720\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3055 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/محمدرضا-لطفی-در-حال-سه-تار-نوازی.jpg\" alt=\"محمدرضا لطفی در حال سه تار نوازی\" width=\"720\" height=\"487\" /> Mohammad Reza Lotfi playing the setar[/caption]\n\n<p></p>', '2023-08-01 12:55:27', 0, '2024-09-18 22:43:16', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1493, 'posts', 36, 'en', NULL, 'Jalil Shahnaz, the Shah-Navaz of Iranian music, unparalleled tar player', NULL, 'Jalil Shahnaz was one of the most influential tar players in Persian classical music, born in 1921 in Isfahan. Raised in a musical family, he began learning the tar at a young age and quickly developed exceptional skills. His career flourished after joining Iranian radio, especially through programs like “Golha,” where he collaborated with renowned musicians such as Abolhassan Saba, Hassan Kassai, and Mohammad Reza Shajarian.\n\nShahnaz was known for his expressive improvisation, vocal-like phrasing, and unique tonal quality. While deeply rooted in the traditional radif system, he expanded its possibilities through creativity and innovation. His music often drew inspiration from Iranian folk traditions and showcased a masterful blend of technique and emotion.\n\nHe was recognized as a lasting figure in Iranian music in 2004 and passed away in 2013. His artistic legacy continues to influence generations of musicians and remains a cornerstone of Persian classical music.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article from Sornaz Music School, we examine the biography and artistic life of Master <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Jalil</span> <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Shahnaz</span></strong></span>, the great tar player, and become familiar with his works and the opinions of music masters about him. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>Birth and Family of Jalil Shahnaz</h2>\n\n<p>Jalil Shahnaz was born in 1300 solar year (1921 AD) in the city of <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A7%D8%B5%D9%81%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%86\">Isfahan</a>. Almost all members of his family were familiar with music and reached mastery in various fields of the art of music, including tar, setar, santur, and kamancheh. His father, “Shaban Khan,” had a strong interest in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%d8%b5%db%8c%d9%84-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">authentic Iranian music</a> and, in addition to the tar, which was his main instrument, also played setar and santur. His uncle, Gholamreza Sarang (Saranj), was also a kamancheh player.</p>\n\n<p>Jalil Shahnaz became interested in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">music</a> from childhood and began learning to play the tar with his older brother, Hossein Shahnaz, who played the instrument well. Jalil’s great perseverance and remarkable talent were such that in his youth he became one of the good players of Isfahan.</p>\n\n<p>In the book “Iranian Musicians” written by Pejman Akbarzadeh, it is stated: “Shahnaz began playing on Isfahan Radio from 1328 solar year (1949 AD) and in 1336 solar year (1957 AD), at the invitation of the Radio Organization, he came to Tehran and participated in various programs such as the Golha program, Hossein Yahaghi’s orchestra, and… as a soloist and accompanist. He also performed many concerts inside and outside Iran with the ‘Yaran-e Salaseh’ group (along with Taj Isfahani and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ad%d8%b3%d9%86-%da%a9%d8%b3%d8%a7%db%8c%db%8c/\">Hassan Kasaei</a>) and the Masters of Iranian Music group. Shahnaz also had an active presence in the Shiraz Arts Festival programs.”</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3061\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"500\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3061 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/جلیل-شهناز-در-کنار-ابوالحسن-صبا-و-حسن-کسایی.jpg\" alt=\"جلیل شهناز در کنار ابوالحسن صبا و حسن کسایی\" width=\"500\" height=\"733\" /> Jalil Shahnaz with Abolhasan Saba and Hassan Kasaei[/caption]\n\n<p>This great tar player, throughout his artistic life, collaborated with other artists of the country, including <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%84%d8%ad%d8%b3%d9%86-%d8%b5%d8%a8%d8%a7/\">Abolhasan Saba</a>, Faramarz Payvar, Jahangir Malek, Asadollah Malek, Hassan Kasaei, Seyed Mohammad Mousavi, Hossein Tehrani, Taj Isfahani, Adib Khansari, Habibollah Badi’i, Parviz Yahaghi, Homayoun Khorram, Ali Tajvidi, Mansour Saremi, Reza Varzandeh, Amir Naser Eftetah, Mahmudi Khansari, Abdolvaheb Shahidi, Akbar Golpayegani, Hossein Khajeh Amiri, Mohammad Reza Shajarian, Shahram Nazeri, Hassan Zirak, Ali Asghar Shahzidi, Tabatabai, and Mohammad Isfahani and Ali Raste mian.</p>\n\nJalil Shahnaz, in the 1360s solar decade (1980s), together with Faramarz Payvar (santur), Mohammad Esmaeili (tombak), and Seyed Mohammad Mousavi (ney), Ali Asghar Bahari (kamancheh), formed the “Masters of Iranian Music Group” and with this group traveled extensively to European, Asian countries, and America. The album “Concert of the Masters of Iranian Music” with the voice of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b4%d9%87%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%86%d8%a7%d8%b8%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Shahram Nazeri</a> is one of the important outputs of this collaboration.\n\n<p>Most masters and experts of Iranian music consider 1336 solar year (1957 AD) as the beginning and start of Jalil Shahnaz’s rise and brilliance, those days when the “Golha” program was performed on national radio by Davoud Pirnia. Jalil Shahnaz, in various parts of this program, accompanied the greatest and most famous Iranian singers of his era, including “Taj Isfahani” and “Adib Khansari,” with his instrument.</p>\n\n<p>Jalil Shahnaz is known to everyone as a tar player, but in addition to playing the tar, which was his main instrument, he was also familiar with playing instruments such as violin, santur, and tombak. It is very famous that Master Shahnaz, in his playing style, could sing with his instrument in addition to playing. Shahnaz’s improvisations are counted among the most prominent musical features of him.</p>\n\n<p>Master Jalil Shahnaz had special respect and importance for the Iranian music radif and considered the radif as the entire capital of Iranian music. He says about this: “The radif is the entire capital of Iranian music and teaching it is very important. The manner and quality of transmitting the radif entirely depends on the teacher and instructor. The teacher must first understand the radif and have lived with it. Teaching the radif is not the work of every teacher.”</p>\n\n<p>Master Jalil Shahnaz was selected as a lasting figure in art and music in 1383 solar year (2004 AD). Also, on 27 Tir 1383 solar year (18 July 2004), the first-class artistic degree (a degree equivalent to a doctorate) was awarded to Jalil Shahnaz in appreciation of a lifetime of artistic activity.</p>\n\n<h2>Distinguished Features of Shahnaz’s Tar</h2>\n\n<p>Although Master Jalil Shahnaz had great respect for the old music of Iran and the playing styles of the great masters, he never limited himself to these styles and, in the shadow of his own genius, every day created new and sweet pieces and works, developing and perfecting the methods and styles of the past masters. He believed in gradual change, progress, and evolution in music and did not consider radical and revolutionary methods correct in destroying the playing styles of the past.</p>\n\n<p>Unlike many modernists and critics who believed that Iranian music is in a closed space and has no capacity for any kind of change and evolution and strongly resists change and evolution, Jalil Shahnaz believed that Iranian music has a very wide space for innovation and evolution, especially through combining dastgahs and avazes and compound playing in the radif, one can create the widest spaces for creating new works.</p>\n\n<p>Master Jalil Shahnaz’s instrument in expressing avazes, gushehs, and trills was like a miracle and in fact reaches the limit of wonder; when he played a dastgah from the daramad to the ouj and finally forud to the daramad, it seemed that he was narrating the story of human life from the moment of birth and childhood to the moment of the end of life.</p>\n\n<p>The pleasant and melodic pishdaramads of Master Jalil Shahnaz represented the purity and simplicity of childhood and adolescence, his chaharmezrabs were a manifestation of the passions, concerns, and excitements of youth, and finally the avazes, whispers, and non-metric sentence phrasings of Shahnaz spoke of the conditions of middle age and maturity and the way of thinking and states of man in this period of life.</p>\n\n<h3>Improvisational Sentence Phrasing of Master Jalil Shahnaz</h3>\n\n<p>In examining Master Shahnaz’s playing, we come to the conclusion that all the sentences used are placed in their most appropriate position, and the left and right plectrum strikes are performed in a calculated and ultimately delicate, masterful, and artistic manner, and not even one note less or more is seen in these sentences. Sometimes Shahnaz’s plectrum strikes were counted and calm like the bells of a church, resonant and thought-provoking, and sometimes these right and left plectrum strikes became so fast, fine, and consecutive that they caused the listener to be surprised and amazed.</p>\n\n<h3>Technique in the Service of Thought and Idea</h3>\n\n<p>Many tar players, especially those who have learned their music in conservatories and universities, have very good and excellent techniques, but because their works are usually devoid of warmth, passion, and feeling, they have less lasting effect on the hearts and souls of listeners.</p>\n\n<p>The playing techniques in Jalil Shahnaz’s instrument were so intertwined with mystery and meaning that one cannot imagine anything better. In simpler terms, Jalil Shahnaz is a pleasant and appropriate combination of technique, passion, and feeling. The beginning and end of each of his sentences, the shortness and length of the notes, the place and amount of silences, and the use of different strings are in such a way that they can have the best effect on the listeners.</p>\n\n<h3>Drawing Inspiration from Local Melodies</h3>\n\n<p>Another prominent feature of Master Shahnaz’s tar playing art is his great familiarity with the melodies and songs of local and folkloric works of Iran, and in his wonderful memory, there is a vast treasure of local songs and old and ancient Iranian tasnifs, and a large part of his playing was shaped by drawing inspiration from these tasnifs, songs, and old melodies.</p>\n\n<p>Among the works that have left a great impact on Jalil Shahnaz’s tar are the authentic Bakhtiari melodies and songs, which originate from the ancient musics of Iran and represent this great cultural and artistic heritage.</p>\n\n<h3>Innovations in Shahnaz’s Works</h3>\n\n<p>Master Jalil Shahnaz is one of the greatest innovators who has existed in contemporary Iranian music and has created very unique and fresh methods and styles in tar playing that serve as a lamp and guide for other tar players.</p>\n\n<p>He, while having great mastery of the radif and complete knowledge of Iranian music and familiarity with most Iranian instruments, as well as knowledge of classical Iranian poetry and literature, had gathered unique features in his instrument: answering avaz, clear and brilliant sonic tone, mastery of rhythm, changing sound production and sonority by changing the place of the plectrum on the index finger, and various and repeated carvings.</p>\n\n<h3>Unique Sonority, the Distinguishing Feature of Jalil Shahnaz</h3>\n\n<p>The points that distinguished this prominent tar player from his contemporaries were his different outlook on the radif and the creation of sentence phrasings specific to himself. Master Jalil Shahnaz, with a strong foundation he had from dastgah music, created sentences in the moment that were unique in their kind.</p>\n\n<p>It can be said that his understanding and perception of rhythm in the radif and traditional Iranian music and his improvisations in different rhythmic periods were another characteristic of this famous Iranian musician’s playing that differed from all his contemporary players.</p>\n\n<p>Jalil Shahnaz’s mastery in playing brought him a unique sonority or sound production in such a way that music lovers, upon hearing his sentence phrasings and plectrum strikes, quickly recognized it and said this is the sound of Shahnaz’s tar.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_4077\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"660\"]<img class=\"wp-image-4077 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/sornaz_jalil_shahnaz_003.jpg\" alt=\"جلیل شهناز نوازنده ای بود که با ساز خود آواز می خواند\" width=\"660\" height=\"720\" /> Jalil Shahnaz was a player who sang with his instrument[/caption]\n\n<h2>Music Masters’ Opinions About Master Jalil Shahnaz</h2>\n\n<p>In 1385 solar year (2006 AD), in a commemoration ceremony held for Master Jalil Shahnaz and Darvish Khan at the Honar Cultural Center, several great figures of Iranian music spoke about this great master, some of which we mention below.</p>\n\n<p>Master Hassan Kasaei, the prominent ney player: Understanding and knowing music is not everyone’s work. All people listen to music and are interested in it, but those who can distinguish the pure from the impure and can understand the maqams that Master Jalil Shahnaz has played are very rare and scarce. Everyone plays an instrument and everyone plays well, but Mr. Jalil Shahnaz’s playing power, his memorized repertoire, and the musical moments he knows and has performed are not something that is in everyone’s hands, meaning it is not the Iranian music radif.</p>\n\n<p>Mr. Shahnaz’s ability in duet playing and answering avaz is extraordinary. I have played with him for 60 years and my instrument is intertwined with Jalil Shahnaz’s instrument.</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b6%d8%a7-%d8%b4%d8%ac%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Master Mohammad Reza Shajarian</a> said in praise of Jalil Shahnaz: Shahnaz’s instrument lives in me and I also live with Shahnaz’s instrument. I am the only singer who considers myself a student of Master Jalil Shahnaz. This unparalleled player’s instrument is an instrument with an avaz-like state; unlike other players who play 80 percent in an instrumental state and only 20 percent in an avaz-like manner. Master Jalil Shahnaz has reached a truly expressive state in tar playing; because he did not remain in the turmoil of the radif and imitation of others’ methods and is flowing like a roaring waterfall.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_4075\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"548\"]<img class=\"wp-image-4075 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/sornaz_jalil_shahnaz_001.jpg\" alt=\"ساز استاد جلیل شهنازی سازی است که با حالت آوازی آن شناخته می شود\" width=\"548\" height=\"720\" /> Master Jalil Shahnaz’s instrument is an instrument known for its avaz-like state[/caption]\n\n<p>Dariush Pirniakan: In Jalil Shahnaz’s tar playing, considering all his innovations, one can see the effects and traces of the music radif and his mastery of the radif. Shahnaz’s sentence phrasing is avaz-like in the full sense; it is as if a singer is singing avaz and you can follow it… In other ways, Shahnaz’s answers to avaz are among the masterpieces of avaz answering in Iranian music. He uses all the possibilities that the instrument places at his disposal and, with beautiful mirroring and question-and-answer exchanges, brings power and technique into the service of beauty. I became interested in tar playing by hearing his instrument and continued my work.</p>\n\n<p>Parviz Yahaghi also said in a short sentence about Jalil Shahnaz’s instrument: Shahnaz closed the tar notebook.</p>\n\n<p>There is also a quatrain attributed to Parviz Yahaghi that was composed in praise of Jalil Shahnaz:</p>\n\n<p>One lifetime to become harmonious with the instrument  And from playing the instrument, to become proud</p>\n\n<p>One can strike the tar for a hundred years   But one cannot become Jalil Shahnaz</p>\n\n<p>Farhad Fakhreddini: Master Jalil Shahnaz was one of the prominent tar players who played very sweetly and his instrument was very listenable. We lost a great player and a Shahnaz like him will not easily come into existence again.</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%af%d9%87/\">Hossein Alizadeh</a>: Not Shahnaz, but Shah-e Saz (King of the Instrument). No words or speech can express the name and eternal melodies of his.</p>\n\n<p>Kayhan Kalhor: Master Jalil Shahnaz, the Lord of the Tar, unknowingly and unintentionally was the music teacher of all of us. Although he apparently had no students, he was the standard from intervals and sentence phrasings to artistic taste and feeling and a hundred other things. He was our reference.... The sound of his tar was the music of musicians’ solitude.</p>\n\n<p>Keyvan Sakt: For more than six decades, his instrument was the soothing of the hearts of the people of this era.</p>\n\n<p>Alireza Ghorbani: We all know Jalil Shahnaz with the tar, and perhaps it is right to say that we knew the tar with Jalil Shahnaz. So much of the melody and avaz of this Jalil of Iranian music has remained in our hearts that we have truly become accustomed to his sounds.</p>\n\n<p>Salar Aghili: Master Jalil Shahnaz was a man whose hands smelled of love and music dripped from his fingertips.</p>\n\n<p>Ali Raste mian, regarding Master Shahnaz’s improvisations, says: The improvisations that Master Jalil Shahnaz performed were performed entirely from the heart and it seemed that they were inspired to him and he played according to the feeling of that time, so that if we asked him again to repeat the same improvisation in the same mayeh, something else would come out.</p>\n\n<p>When Master Shahnaz entered the feeling and state of playing, it was as if he entered another sweet world in which the passage of time was not an issue. Truly, reaching this level in playing and improvisation is very valuable and is the wish of every player.</p>\n\n<h3>Works of Jalil Shahnaz</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Album “Atrafshan” (with tombak by Mohammad Esmaeili)</li>\n  <li>“Zaban-e Tar” (with tombak by Jahangir Malek)</li>\n  <li>Book “Golha-ye Javidan” (fifteen pieces for tar and setar) with notation by Houshang Zarif. Sarood Publications, Tehran, 1379 solar year (2000 AD).</li>\n  <li>Presence in some numbers of the album series “Familiarity with the Avaz Style of Master Gholamhossein Banan”</li>\n  <li>Ghamzadeh (Homayoun Khorram, Jalil Shahnaz, Amir Naser Eftetah)</li>\n  <li>Fifteen selected pieces for tar</li>\n  <li>Tar and Termeh</li>\n  <li>Shur and Zendegi</li>\n  <li>Eshgh and Zendegi</li>\n  <li>Entezar-e Del, Khalvat Gozideh (with the voice of Master Mohammad Reza Shajarian)</li>\n  <li>Narges-e Mast (with the voice of Hessam al-Din Saraj)</li>\n  <li>Sokut (with the voice of Iraj Bastami)</li>\n  <li>Gol-e Man (with the voice of Iraj (Hossein Khajeh Amiri))</li>\n  <li>Tamanna-ye Vesal (with the voice of Abdolhossein Mokhtabad)</li>\n  <li>Concert of the Masters of Iranian Music (with the voice of Shahram Nazeri and performance by the Masters of Iranian Music Group)</li>\n  <li>Kereshmeh-ye Narges (with the voice of Siamak Shajarian, brother of Master Mohammad Reza Shajarian)</li>\n  <li>Sham-e Larzan (Asadollah Malek, Jalil Shahnaz, Mansour Saremi, Jahangir Malek)</li>\n  <li>Del-e Shida (with the voice of Shahram Nazeri and performance by the Masters of Iranian Music Group)</li>\n  <li>Daftar-e Tar</li>\n  <li>Atr Afshan</li>\n  <li>Eftekhar-e Afaq</li>\n  <li>Bagh-e Nava</li>\n  <li>Rahavard</li>\n  <li>Zaban-e Tar</li>\n  <li>Avay-e Jan</li>\n  <li>Avay-e Shahnaz</li>\n  <li>Ghatreh-ye Shabnam (Asadollah Malek, Mansour Nariman, Majid Najahi, Jalil Shahnaz, Farhang Sharif, Fazlollah Tavakkol, Jahangir Malek)</li>\n  <li>Hamaseh-ye Iran (Adib Boroumand, Jalil Shahnaz, Parviz Yahaghi, Jahangir Malek)</li>\n  <li>Chaharbagh (Masters of Iranian Music Group)</li>\n  <li>Nowbahar (with the voice of Ali Raste mian)</li>\n  <li>Del-e Man (with the voice of Ali Raste mian)</li>\n  <li>Yaran-e Zendeh Rud (with the voice of Jalal al-Din Taj Isfahani)</li>\n  <li>Be Isfahan Ro (with the voice of Jalal al-Din Taj Isfahani)</li>\n  <li>Mowj-e Atash (Homayoun Khorram, Jalil Shahnaz, Amir Naser Eftetah)</li>\n  <li>Sad Sal Tar (various artists including Jalil Shahnaz, Mohammad Reza Lotfi, etc.)</li>\n  <li>Chaharmezrab</li>\n  <li>Shab-e Mahtab (with the voice of Ali Raste mian)</li>\n  <li>Naghmeh-ye Nowruzi (with the voice of Ali Raste mian)</li>\n  <li>Raz (Javad Ma’rufi, Parviz Yahaghi, Jalil Shahnaz, Jahangir Malek, Mohammad Esmaeili)</li>\n  <li>Mehr (Parviz Yahaghi, Jalil Shahnaz, Amir Naser Eftetah)</li>\n  <li>Navid-e Bahari</li>\n  <li>Shahnaz-e Shahnavaz</li>\n  <li>Panzdah Ghat’eh baraye Tar</li>\n  <li>Chaharbagh</li>\n  <li>Dar Sokut-e Bagh (duet with Hassan Kasaei)</li>\n</ul>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_4077\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"660\"]<img class=\"wp-image-4077 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/sornaz_jalil_shahnaz_003.jpg\" alt=\"جلیل شهناز نوازنده ای بود که با ساز خود آواز می خواند\" width=\"660\" height=\"720\" /> Jalil Shahnaz was a player who sang with his instrument[/caption]\n\n<h2>Passing of Jalil Shahnaz</h2>\n\n<p>Finally, Jalil Shahnaz, the prominent master of tar and setar, after enduring a long period of illness and due to old age, passed away on Monday, 27 Khordad 1392 solar year (17 June 2013 AD) at Arad Hospital in Tehran at the age of 92.</p>\n\n<p>After the passing of Jalil Shahnaz, Ramiz Quliyev, the prominent tar player of the Republic of Azerbaijan, sent a message of condolence on this occasion through the Embassy of the Islamic Republic of Iran.</p>\n\n<h3>Jalil Shahnaz’s Grave in the Artists’ Section of Behesht-e Zahra</h3>\n\n<p>His body was also buried in the artists’ section of Behesht-e Zahra. Mohammad Reza Shajarian, after the passing of Jalil Shahnaz, said: “Master Jalil Shahnaz created passion and love in me and I have reached here with the sound of Shahnaz’s tar, and the word ‘master’ is only worthy of Shahnaz, and Master Jalil Shahnaz is unrepeatable like Hafez, and with respect to all tar players after Master Shahnaz, the notebook of Iranian tar should be closed.”</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the article “Jalil Shahnaz, the Shahnavaz of Iranian Music,” please share them in the comments section of the article to improve the quality of this content with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com\">Sornaz Music School</a>.</p>', '2023-08-06 19:36:31', 0, '2024-09-18 22:41:22', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1494, 'posts', 37, 'en', NULL, 'Dastgah-shenasi and avaz-shenasi of Iranian music, recognizing the key signatures of the 12 maqams in Iranian music', NULL, 'This article explains the principles of modal analysis in Iranian music, focusing on identifying musical modes (dastgahs) using tonic notes and key signatures (accidentals).\n\nFirst, it describes how to determine the tonic when the accidentals are known. In modes such as Mahur and Rast Panjgah, the tonic can be identified through the position of sharps/flats (in 12-tone systems) or koron/sori (in microtonal systems).\n\nThe article then discusses major modes like Shur and Homayoun, explaining how they are distinguished by the interval between the tonic and the third degree. It also explains how related modes (avaz) such as Bayat Tork, Afshari, Dashti, and Abuata are identified based on their finalis (shahed note).\n\nNext, the article covers Segah and Chahargah, which are distinguished by the number and type of microtonal or semitonal accidentals and specific interval structures.\n\nIn the second part, the reverse process is explained: given a tonic, how to construct the correct set of accidentals. This depends on identifying important scale degrees such as the leading tone and subdominant, and applying the correct sequence of accidentals.\n\nFinally, the article concludes that:\n\nMost modes use seven accidentals in total.\nThe type and number of accidentals are key to identifying each mode.\nWith practice, these seemingly complex rules become intuitive and easier to apply.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article from Sornaz Music School, we examine the term <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">maqam analysis (mayeh-shenasi)</span></strong> of Iranian music maqams. Although the rules of dastgah recognition and avaz recognition of Iranian music maqams have been explained with great precision, detail, and expansion, it is still possible to make mistakes in identifying the dastgahs and avazes of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a>, because after memorizing all these rules, if someone knows the tonic of a maqam and wants to write its combined signs, or sees the combined signs of a mayeh and wants to find its tonic and maqam, they may face difficulty. Therefore, the following two series of rules, which are a summary of the rules of mayeh analysis of Iranian maqams, are presented so that if there is any ambiguity, it may be resolved.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3114\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"600\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3114 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/دستگاه-شناسی-و-آوازشناسی.jpg\" alt=\"دستگاه شناسی و آوازشناسی\" width=\"600\" height=\"358\" /> Dastgah Recognition and Avaz Recognition[/caption]\n\n<h2>If We Know the Combined Signs of a Maqam, How Should We Find Its Tonic?</h2>\n\n<h3>Dastgah Recognition of Mahur and Rast Panjgah Maqams</h3>\n\n<p>In the dastgah recognition of Mahur and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b1%d8%a7%d8%b3%d8%aa-%d9%be%d9%86%d8%ac%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">Rast Panjgah</a> maqams, we have two states:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>In scales formed on 12 half-step chromatic scales, all signs are half-step. In this case, the last sharp is the leading tone, and the flat before the last is the tonic (the last flat is below the leading tone).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>By half-step signs in dastgah recognition, we mean flat, sharp, and natural signs.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>In <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">scales</a> formed on 12 quarter-step notes of the 24-part Iranian music scale, all signs are quarter-step. In this case, the last sori is the leading tone, and the koron before the last is the tonic (the last koron is below the leading tone).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>By quarter-step signs in Iranian dastgah recognition, we mean koron and sori signs (“sharp sori” and “flat koron” are also part of this category).</p>\n\n<h3>Dastgah Recognition of Shur, Homayun, and Their Dependents Maqams</h3>\n\n<p>In the dastgah recognition of Shur and Homayun maqams, we have two states:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>In scales formed on 12 half-step chromatic scales, if there is one quarter-step sign, that quarter-step sign is the root tonic.</li>\n  <li>In scales formed on 12 quarter-step notes of the 24-part Iranian music scale, if there is one half-step sign and the other signs are quarter-step, in this case the half-step sign is the root tonic.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>If there were only six quarter-step signs in the key signature, the note without a sign originally has the natural half-step sign and for this reason it is without a sign in the key signature and its sign has not been written; it is the root tonic. For example, if we have six korons: Si – Mi – La – Re – Sol and Do, then the note Fa, which is not part of the combined signs and is natural, is the root tonic of the scale).</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Another rule in the dastgah recognition of Shur and Homayun maqams is that the last koron is the sixth degree and the last sori is the fifth degree.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>After we find the tonic, during the musical piece we observe: if the interval between the tonic and the “mediant” (the third note of the scale) is a major third, we are in Homayun, and if it is minor, we are in Shur. The tonic of Shur and Homayun is the same.</p>\n\n<p>In the avaz recognition of Iranian music, the combined signs of Shur’s dependents, i.e., the avazes of Abu Ata, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%aa%d8%b1%da%a9/\">Bayat-e Tork</a>, Afshari, Dashti, and also the dastgah of Nava, are similar to Shur. Only the name of each of them is determined based on the witness note, which will be as follows:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>The fourth degree of the Shur scale is the witness note of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%b9%d8%b7%d8%a7/\">Avaz of Abu Ata</a>, and Avaz of Abu Ata is known by this name.</li>\n  <li>The fourth degree of the Shur scale is the witness note of Avaz of Afshari, and Avaz of Afshari is known by this name.</li>\n  <li>The fourth degree of the Shur scale is the witness note of the dastgah of Nava, and the dastgah of Nava is known by this name.</li>\n  <li>The third degree of the Shur scale is the witness note of Avaz of Bayat-e Tork, and Avaz of Bayat-e Tork is known by this name.</li>\n  <li>The fourth degree of the Shur scale is the witness note of Avaz of Dashti, and Avaz of Dashti is known by this name.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>In avaz recognition, the combined signs of Avaz of Esfahan, which is counted among the dependents of the dastgah of Homayun, are similar to Homayun, and the fourth degree of the dastgah of Homayun is the witness note of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%a7%d8%b5%d9%81%d9%87%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan</a>, and Avaz of Esfahan is known by this name.</p>\n\n<h3>Dastgah Recognition of Segah Maqam</h3>\n\n<p>In the dastgah recognition of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b3%d9%87-%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">Segah maqam</a>, we have two states:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>In scales formed on 12 half-step chromatic scales, there are two quarter-step signs and the other signs are half-step. In this case, the first koron or the last sori is the witness note of the Segah scale (the third degree of the scale).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>In Segah on “Re,” which has one koron sign and one sori sign, according to the “Fa sori” rule, it is the witness, and Si koron should not be imagined as the witness because when we have two korons, Si koron is the witness (koron before the last). So when we have one koron (Si koron), the note before the last is in fact the same “Fa sori.”</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>In scales formed on 12 quarter-step notes of the 24-part Iranian music scale, there are two half-step signs and the rest of the signs are quarter-step. In this case as well, the last koron is the seventh degree and the last sori is the second degree of the Segah scale.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Dastgah Recognition of Chahargah Maqam</h3>\n\n<p>Like Segah, in the dastgah recognition of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%da%86%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">Chahargah</a>, we have two states:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>In scales formed on 12 half-step chromatic scales, it has two quarter-step signs and the other signs are half-step. In this case, the last koron is the root tonic and the last sharp is the leading tone.</li>\n  <li>In scales formed on 12 quarter-step notes of the 24-part Iranian music scale, it has two half-step signs and the rest of the signs are quarter-step. In this case, the last koron is below the leading tone (koron before the last tonic) and the last sori is the leading tone.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>The first part of the rules we mentioned for the dastgah recognition of Segah and Chahargah maqams is similar because Chahargah and Segah either have two half-step notes or two quarter-step notes. Now, to distinguish these two maqams from each other, we say:</p>\n\n<p>If those two half-step or quarter-step signs that are in the minority, along with the next note in the scale, have over-large second intervals (which are the specific intervals of the dang and dastgah of Chahargah), we are in the maqam of Chahargah; otherwise, we are in the dastgah of Segah.</p>\n\n<p>Of course, in mayehs that have fewer than seven combined signs, such as the mayeh of “Re sori” (having 5 combined signs) and “La sori” (having 6 combined signs), the half-step signs in the first are the same two natural signs (Mi natural and Si natural) that have not been written, and in the second there is one natural sign (Si natural) that is not part of the combined signs, and the over-large second intervals must be measured relative to them.</p>\n\n<h2>If We Know the Tonic of a Maqam, How Do We Write Its Combined Signs?</h2>\n\n<h3>Dastgah Recognition of Mahur and Rast Panjgah Maqams</h3>\n\n<p>In the dastgah recognition of Mahur and Rast Panjgah maqams, if the tonic of the given scale is a half-step note, we must find its leading tone, in which case 2 states arise:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>If the leading tone was a sharpened note, that same leading tone is the last sharp according to the sequence of sharps, and whatever sign is before it is also sharp.</li>\n  <li>If the leading tone was a natural or flattened note, that mayeh has flats, and the tonic is the combined sign before the last according to the sequence of flats, and whatever is before it is also flat.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>And if the tonic of the given scale is a quarter-step note, in this case 2 states arise:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>If the tonic of the scale is a koron note, its sub-leading tone (fourth degree) is the last koron, and before it there must also be six other signs (because if the tonic is a quarter-step note, the number of combined signs is always seven). Now, if there are fewer than six signs before it, we add whatever is missing from the sequence of soris (which matches the sequence of sharps) until the number of signs reaches seven.</li>\n  <li>If the tonic is a sori note, the leading tone is the last sori. After we have written the soris according to the sequence of soris, if their number is less than seven, the other notes take koron (and this is only in one case, which is Mahur “Fa sori,” which has six soris and one koron). Of course, if the leading tone was a “sharp sori” note, whatever sign is before it is “sharp sori,” and after it, the rest of the signs until reaching seven must be “sori.”</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Because in dastgah recognition, when one of the combined signs is repeated, the number of combined signs is definitely seven, and the notes before it take the repeated sign, and the notes after it take the non-repeated sign of the same type.</p>\n\n<h3>Dastgah Recognition of Shur, Homayun, and Their Dependents Maqams</h3>\n\n<p>In the dastgah recognition of Shur and Homayun maqams, if the tonic of the given maqam is a half-step note, the root tonic of the scale is the last combined sign. Now, with this description, 2 states arise:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>If the root tonic of the desired scale was koron, the other signs before it will be flat.</li>\n  <li>If the root tonic of the desired scale was sori, the other signs before it will be sharp.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>And if the tonic of the desired dastgah is a quarter-step note, we find the root tonic of the scale, which is definitely a half-step note, in which case 3 states arise:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>If the root tonic of the desired scale was flat, we write it, and for the notes before it, if necessary, we place the repeated sign “flat koron,” and for the notes after it, we place the “koron” sign until seven combined signs are obtained.</li>\n  <li>If the root tonic of the desired scale was sharp, we write it, and for the notes before it, if necessary, we place the repeated sign “sharp sori,” and for the notes after it, we place the “sori” sign until seven combined signs are obtained.</li>\n  <li>If the root tonic of the desired scale was natural, it is the last combined sign, which does not need to be written (because we do not write natural signs in the key signature), and the six signs before it, if needed, must be koron. If the korons are finished, we add soris in their place until the korons are eliminated and soris take their place, and this is for when the root tonic of the scale is the note “Si natural.”</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Because the note “Si” is the first koron in the sequence of korons and flats, and there is no koron or flat sign before it, all signs at this point are sori).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>After writing the combined signs, if the <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%82%D8%B7%D8%B9%D9%87_%D9%85%D9%88%D8%B3%DB%8C%D9%82%DB%8C#:~:text=%D9%82%D8%B7%D8%B9%D9%87%20%D9%85%D9%88%D8%B3%DB%8C%D9%82%DB%8C%20%D8%AA%DA%A9%D9%87%E2%80%8C%D8%A7%DB%8C%20%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%20%DA%A9%D9%87,%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%AA%D9%82%D9%84%20%D8%A8%D8%A7%20%D9%86%D8%A7%D9%85%DB%8C%20%D9%88%DB%8C%DA%98%D9%87%20%D9%85%DB%8C%E2%80%8C%D8%B4%D9%88%D8%AF.\">piece</a> is in the dastgah of Homayun, the accidental sign must be observed as previously mentioned, meaning the third degree of Homayun is a half-step larger than the third degree of the dastgah of Shur and must be indicated with an accidental sign.</p>\n\n<p>In the avaz recognition of Iranian music, the combined signs of Shur’s dependents, i.e., the avazes of Abu Ata, Bayat-e Tork, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d9%81%d8%b4%d8%a7%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Afshari</a>, Dashti, and also the dastgah of Nava, are similar to Shur. Only the name of each of them is determined based on the witness note, which will be as follows:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>The third degree of the Shur scale is the witness note of Avaz of Bayat-e Tork, and Avaz of Bayat-e Tork is known by this name.</li>\n  <li>The fourth degree of the Shur scale is the witness note of Avaz of Abu Ata, and Avaz of Abu Ata is known by this name.</li>\n  <li>The fourth degree of the Shur scale is the witness note of Avaz of Afshari, and Avaz of Afshari is known by this name.</li>\n  <li>The fourth degree of the Shur scale is the witness note of the dastgah of Nava, and the dastgah of Nava is known by this name.</li>\n  <li>The fourth degree of the Shur scale is the witness note of Avaz of Dashti, and Avaz of Dashti is known by this name.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Therefore, considering that we know what degree of the Shur dastgah the witness note of each of Shur’s dependents is, we first obtain the tonic of the Shur dastgah. To obtain the tonic of the Shur dastgah, we act as follows:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>From the witness note of Avaz of Bayat-e Tork, we descend a major third interval (1.5 steps) to reach the Shur tonic.</li>\n  <li>From the witness note of Avaz of Abu Ata, we descend a perfect fourth interval (2.5 steps) to reach the Shur tonic.</li>\n  <li>From the witness note of Avaz of Afshari, we descend a perfect fourth interval (2.5 steps) to reach the Shur tonic.</li>\n  <li>From the witness note of the dastgah of Nava, we descend a perfect fourth interval (2.5 steps) to reach the Shur tonic.</li>\n  <li>From the witness note of Avaz of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%af%d8%b4%d8%aa%db%8c/\">Dashti</a>, we descend a perfect fifth interval (3.5 steps) to reach the Shur tonic.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>After we obtain the Shur tonic, we obtain the combined signs of the desired Shur scale according to the explanations given, and these combined signs will be the signs of the desired avaz.</p>\n\n<p>In avaz recognition, the combined signs of Avaz of Esfahan, which is counted among the dependents of the dastgah of Homayun, are similar to Homayun, and the fourth degree of the dastgah of Homayun is the witness note of Avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan, and Avaz of Esfahan is known by this name.</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, considering that we know what degree of the Homayun dastgah the witness note of Avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan is, we first obtain the tonic of Homayun, which is a perfect fourth interval (2.5 steps) below the witness note of Esfahan.</p>\n\n<p>After we obtain the Homayun tonic, we obtain the combined signs of the desired <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%87%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c%d9%88%d9%86/\">Homayun scale</a> according to the explanations given, and these combined signs will be the signs of the desired Avaz of Esfahan.</p>\n\n<h3>Dastgah Recognition of Segah Maqam</h3>\n\n<p>In the dastgah recognition of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b3%d9%87-%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">Segah maqam</a>, if the tonic is a half-step note, we find the witness.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>If it is koron, it is the first koron, and after it, one more koron is necessary (because the Segah scale at this point has two quarter-step signs), and before them, if any sign is necessary, it is flat according to the sequence.</li>\n  <li>But if the witness is sori, it is the last sori, and there is also one sori before it, and before them, if any sign is necessary, it is sharp according to the sequence.</li>\n  <li>Now, if the last sori is the note “Fa,” because there is no other sori before it, instead of one other sori sign, we place “Si koron.” Because Segah on “Re” (whose witness is “Fa sori”) has two combined signs of two different states (“Fa sori” and “Si koron”).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>In the dastgah recognition of Segah, if the tonic is a quarter-step note, we look at the second and seventh degrees of the scale.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>If the second degree is sori, we place it as the last sori and place four other soris before it (because at this point Segah must have 5 quarter-step signs). But if we placed the soris and the sequence of combined signs was not complete, before the soris we place as many sharps as needed until the sequence is complete.</li>\n  <li>But if after we wrote the soris, five quarter-step signs were not obtained, instead of sori we add from the sequence of korons until five quarter-step signs are obtained. So that if the second degree was “sharp sori,” if a sign is necessary to the left of it, of course it will be “sharp sori,” and if it is not necessary, to the right of it we add as many soris as needed until five quarter-step signs are obtained, and if a sign is necessary before them, of course it is sharp.</li>\n  <li>If the second degree was not sori, we place the seventh degree, which is definitely koron, as the last koron, and of course four other korons are also necessary before it (because at this point five quarter-step signs are necessary). But if we placed the korons and the sequence of combined signs was not complete, before the korons we place as many flats as needed until the sequence is complete.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Of course, it is clear that if the seventh degree is flat koron, whatever is before it up to four koron signs and the rest are flat.</p>\n\n<p>In the dastgah recognition of the Segah scale, it must be noted that if, for example, the combined signs of Segah are as follows: “Si flat koron – Mi flat – Re koron – Sol koron – Do koron – Fa koron,” of course “flat koron” is the last combined sign (even though it apparently appears first), and the sign before it, in order, is: Fa koron – Do koron – Sol koron – Re koron – La flat and Mi flat.</p>\n\n<h3>Dastgah Recognition of Chahargah Maqam</h3>\n\n<p>In the dastgah recognition of Chahargah maqam, if the tonic is a half-step note.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>If the root tonic is koron, we place it as the last koron, and before it, one more koron is also necessary (because at this point Chahargah has two quarter-step signs, like Segah). So if the root tonic itself is the first koron, in this case we take the other quarter-step sign from the soris.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Before these two korons, two half-step signs must be placed, one of which, in the middle, is a large over-second higher than the korons (the quality of these two signs is such that if before those two korons the sequence of korons is incomplete, those two signs will be natural, and again if the sequence of korons is incomplete, we add flats, and if the sequence is complete, each of those two signs or both, if necessary, are selected from the sharps.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>In the case where the root tonic is not koron, we place the leading tone, which is definitely sharp, as the last sharp, and before it, one more sharp is also necessary, and in front of these two signs we must write two soris (because at this point Chahargah also has two half-step notes, like Segah), and if the sequence of sharps is not complete, in front of those soris we place as many sharps as needed until the sequence of combined signs is complete.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>In the dastgah recognition of Chahargah, if the tonic is a quarter-step note, it is either koron or sori.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>If the tonic is a koron note, we have seven combined signs, two of which are half-step notes and five of which are quarter-step notes. We write the sub-leading tone, which is the last combined sign and koron, and before it we place two korons (but if the sequence of korons is finished, in place of the number of necessary korons at the beginning of the combined signs we place soris). In this way, from the seven combined signs, three signs have been placed, and two other quarter-step signs and two other half-step signs remain.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Now, from the sequence of flats and korons, if four signs remain, we start from the beginning, place two koron signs and then two flats, and if three signs are missing, from the beginning we place one koron and two flats, and in the case where only two signs are missing from the sequence of flats, we place those same two flats (in the last two cases, we compensate for the deficiency of korons from the beginning of the sequence of soris).</p>\n\n<p>But if after placing one flat the sequence of korons is finished, we start from the beginning of the soris, place one natural and two soris, and in the case where there is no place even for placing a flat, we place two naturals and two soris.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>If the tonic is a sori note, we write the leading tone, which is the last sori or the last “sharp sori.” If it is sori, the signs before it will be three soris and two naturals and one sori, which are written from the beginning according to the sequence of soris.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>But if the leading tone is “sharp sori,” in the case where a sign is necessary before it, of course one “sharp sori” and two sharps will be there, and the subsequent signs, if only three signs or fewer are necessary, sori is written, and if more than three signs are necessary, after three soris, two naturals and one sori are necessary, which will be written wherever there is space until the end of the sequence of soris.</p>\n\n<h2>The Number of Combined Signs in the Dastgah Recognition of Iranian Maqams</h2>\n\n<p>At the end of this article, we present the number of combined signs used in the dastgah recognition and avaz recognition of Iranian maqams as follows:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>The maqams of Mahur and Rast Panjgah either have all their signs as half-step or quarter-step. If all their signs are quarter-step, they have seven combined signs.</li>\n  <li>The maqams of Shur and Homayun and their dependents (assuming the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%86%d9%88%d8%a7/\">dastgah of Nava</a> is one of Shur’s dependents) either have one quarter-step sign and the rest of their signs are half-step, or they have one half-step sign and the rest of their signs are quarter-step. If they have one half-step sign, they have seven combined signs, with the note that some of their mayehs may apparently have only six quarter-step combined signs. In this case, the half-step note is natural and for this reason it is not written.</li>\n  <li>The maqams of Segah and Chahargah either have two quarter-step signs or two half-step signs. If they have two half-step signs, the number of combined signs is seven, and it is possible that some of them, since they are in the natural state (natural), are not written, and apparently the number of combined signs is less.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>So, in short, it can be said that in the dastgah recognition of Iranian music, if the tonic is a quarter-step note, the number of combined signs is in fact seven; however, if apparently there are five quarter-step signs, the other two signs are natural, and such a maqam is Segah or Chahargah, and if apparently there are six quarter-step signs, one other sign is natural, and such a maqam will be Shur or Homayun.</p>\n\n<p>With the presentation of the above points, the dastgah recognition of maqams whose tonic is a quarter-step note becomes easy because:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>If all seven signs are quarter-step, it is the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%85%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%88%d8%b1/\">maqam of Mahur</a> or Rast Panjgah.</li>\n  <li>If one half-step sign is seen, it is the maqam of Shur, Homayun, or their dependents.</li>\n  <li>In the case where two half-step signs exist, it will be the maqam of Segah or Chahargah.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>The difference between the maqams of Shur and Homayun with the maqams of Segah and Chahargah in dastgah recognition is also evident from here: that the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b4%d9%88%d8%b1/\">maqam of Shur</a> and Homayun whose tonic is a quarter-step note have six quarter-step notes, and if we see five quarter-step signs, it is Segah or Chahargah.</p>\n\n<p>Recognizing mayehs whose tonic is a half-step note, because they have simple and easy rules, will not be difficult with learning those rules. So if the rules of mayeh analysis of Iranian maqams seemed difficult at first glance, with a little attention and practice they will become simple.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the article “Dastgah Recognition and Avaz Recognition of Iranian Music,” please share them in the comments section of the article in order to improve the quality of this content with the Sornaz Music School website.</p>', '2023-08-11 16:39:18', 0, '2024-08-30 19:13:59', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1495, 'posts', 38, 'en', NULL, 'What does musical texture mean?', NULL, 'This article explores the concept of musical texture in music and highlights its importance in shaping the overall sound and perception of a musical piece. Musical texture refers to the combination of elements such as tempo, rhythm, melody, and harmonic structure that together define the sonic character of music.\n\nThe article begins by comparing texture in music with similar concepts in other arts like carpet weaving, poetry, and painting, showing that texture represents how elements are arranged and perceived as a whole. In music, texture is understood through listening and can convey qualities like softness, intensity, or complexity.\n\nIt then outlines the main factors influencing musical texture, including:\n\nLegato (smooth) vs. staccato (detached) sounds\nInteraction between melodies and accompaniment\nRhythm, meter, and syncopation\nInstrumentation and timbre\nThe relationship between melody and chord\n\nFinally, the article introduces three primary types of musical texture:\n\nMonophonic: a single melodic line without accompaniment\nHomophonic: a main melody supported by chords\nPolyphonic: multiple independent melodies performed simultaneously\n\nIn conclusion, understanding musical texture helps listeners and musicians better analyze, interpret, and appreciate the expressive qualities of music.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article from Sornaz Music School, we examine the term <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">musical texture</span></strong> and introduce the most important factors affecting musical texture. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n\n<h2>Musical Texture (Musical Texture)</h2>\n\n<p>In all arts, the term “texture” or another term with the same meaning and concept is used. In carpet weaving and textiles, the two terms “texture” and “pattern” have many applications. The first term usually means the fineness or coarseness of the knots of the warp and weft of the carpet, and the term “pattern” is more or less used in the sense of the intertwining of the lines of shapes, the repetition of “subjects” such as flowers, birds, etc., in a symmetrical or row form, or the position of colors and subjects against each other, etc.</p>\n\n<p>The dual use of the terms “texture” and “pattern” in <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%82%D8%A7%D9%84%DB%8C\">carpet weaving</a>, despite one affecting the other, stems from the fact that judging the value or low value of a carpet is possible, in addition to touching it, by looking at it.</p>\n\n<p>Whereas in music, this judgment is only possible through the ear. For this reason, the term “texture” in music more or less conveys a concept equal to the meanings of both terms “texture” and “pattern” in carpet weaving.</p>\n\n<p>In the art of poetry, what distinguishes one poem from another is, first and foremost, its meter and then its successive words, as well as the effect that the sequence of the concepts of the words has on the listener’s mind, and even the roughness or softness of the letters from which the words are composed — all of these form the “texture” of the poem.</p>\n\n<p>In the art of painting, one can see that the artist, only by applying line, design, color, light, etc., even on a flat and polished surface, creates a rough and coarse texture, or conversely, a soft and delicate one.</p>\n\n<p>In the art of music, these feelings are also attainable. The listener of a musical piece may describe all or part of it with adjectives such as soft, rough, grand, delicate, colorful, and disturbing. In orchestral music, one of the important factors manifested in musical texture is its orchestration, that is, the choice of this or that instrument, or a combination of this or that group of instruments at a certain moment in the path of the music’s progress.</p>\n\n<p>Musical texture is not only worthy of examination in multi-part and instrumental music, but can also be discussed to a large extent in single-part music or group singing. For example, a melody that has staccato <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%88%db%8c%da%98%da%af%db%8c-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%b5%d9%88%d8%aa/\">musical sounds</a> and it seems that an indefinite but limited silence appears between every two musical sounds has a different texture compared to a melody whose sounds are connected in a lyrical manner. A song-like – epic, martial – melody and a romantic song, as well as a prayer-like song,</p>\n\n<p>of course have various musical textures. In short, the factors that affect musical texture can be mentioned here:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>The staccato or legato nature of the sounds</li>\n  <li>The combination of both forms, possibly one in the main <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%84%d9%88%d8%af%db%8c/\">melody</a> and the other in the accompanying layer</li>\n  <li>Meter and what can be invented in connection with meter, such as time signature, syncopation, upbeat, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>These few factors are, of course, the simplest factors affecting musical texture. Other factors, such as:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>The combination of melodies with each other</li>\n  <li>The combination of melody with chords and also the connection of chords in such a way that as a result of this connection, the melody appears stronger</li>\n  <li>And also the coloring of music through various orchestrations</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>and so on, all play a role in musical texture.</p>\n\n<h2>What Does Musical Texture Mean?</h2>\n\n<p><b><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Musical texture</span></b> or <span lang=\"en\"><bdi>Texture</bdi></span> Musical means a combination of tempo, rhythm, melody, and harmonic structure of a work or piece that determines the overall quality and auditory space of a musical piece.</p>\n\n<h2>Types of Texture in Music</h2>\n\n<p>Musical works are divided into several different forms in terms of their internal structure or the same musical texture, and these forms are as follows:</p>\n\n<h3>Monophonic Texture:</h3>\n\n<p>Etymologically, mono means one and phonic means sound. Therefore, monophonic music means one-voice music, which may be performed with one instrument or one singer or even with several instruments. In this type of musical texture, only one melody is heard and there are no accompanying sounds.</p>\n\n<h3>Homophonic Texture:</h3>\n\n<p>In this type of musical texture, there is one main melody and at the same time <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%da%a9%d9%88%d8%b1%d8%af/\">chords</a> appropriate to that melody accompany it. A large part of musical works have homophonic texture.</p>\n\n<h3>Polyphonic Texture:</h3>\n\n<p>Etymologically, polyphonic means multi-voice. In this type of texture, two or more melodies are heard simultaneously and all have equal value, meaning their melodic importance is the same. Polyphonic texture has various forms, including canon, invention, and fugue.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding this article and the concept of musical texture, please share them in the comments section of the article with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a> to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-08-15 19:30:53', 0, '2024-09-18 22:40:55', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1496, 'posts', 39, 'en', NULL, 'What is a cadence? Application of different types of cadences in music', NULL, 'This article explains the concept of cadence in music, which refers to a sense of resolution, pause, or ending in a musical piece. Cadence appears in two main forms:\n\nAs an independent section:\nIn concertos, especially near the end of movements, a cadence is often performed as a solo passage (traditionally improvised) that showcases the technical skill of the soloist.\nAs a harmonic function:\nCadence is created through chord progressions and can produce feelings of closure, pause, or suspense.\n\nThe main types of cadences include:\n\nPerfect (Authentic) Cadence: V → I, the strongest and most conclusive ending\nPlagal Cadence (Amen): IV → I, softer and less final\nDeceptive Cadence: expected resolution is avoided, creating surprise\nHalf Cadence: ends on V, producing tension and expectation\nEvaded Cadence: interrupted resolution with sudden musical changes\nCovered Cadence: structurally complete but used to continue musical flow\nMasculine & Feminine Cadences: based on strong or weak beat endings\n\nOverall, cadences are essential in shaping musical structure, expression, and emotional direction, helping composers control resolution, tension, and continuity within a piece.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the concepts of <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">cadence, ending, or forud</span></strong> in music. Cadence in music exists in two different forms, which are:</p>\n\n<p>1) As an independent piece:</p>\n\n<p>As an independent piece, it appears at the end of the movements of concertos, especially before the end of the first movement, and was usually performed in the form of improvisation. In fact, the cadence at the end of a movement is a piece derived from the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%84%d9%88%d8%af%db%8c/\">melodies</a> and performance techniques of that same movement, the performance of which was usually free and left to the soloist or concerto soloist.</p>\n\n<p>In early concertos, the cadence was performed freely, and the performer, according to his technical ability, performed it without the accompaniment of the orchestra and in an improvisational manner. But gradually, composers took on the task of composing them in their concertos. Although many composed cadences for various concertos have a specific meter, since they are performed without orchestral accompaniment, performers have a great deal of freedom in performing them.</p>\n\n<p>In fact, these cadences are written to demonstrate the technical abilities of the soloists. In early concertos, they were written only for the first movement, but gradually cadences were also used in other movements. Cadences are performed at the end of movements without orchestral accompaniment, and after its end, the orchestra enters, and after a few moments, that part of the concerto ends with full power by the orchestra and the soloist.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Perhaps because these pieces were performed at the end of movements in concertos and were a sign of the ending or conclusion of a section of the concertos, they are called by this name.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>2) The other with a harmonic concept for creating a state of complete ending or temporary pause in the melody. In this way, by placing <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%da%a9%d9%88%d8%b1%d8%af/\">chords</a> one after another in different ways, states such as definite ending, temporary pause, and expectation can be created.</p>\n\n<h2>Types of Cadence</h2>\n\n<p>The types of cadence in its harmonic sense are as follows:</p>\n\n<h3>Perfect (Authentic)</h3>\n\n<p>The most important and strongest cadence that is performed to create a definite pause at the end of musical <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">works</a> is the perfect cadence. This cadence is obtained from the resolution of the fifth degree (V) to the first (I), which is usually preceded by the fourth degree (IV) or the second degree (II) or other degrees that can expand the cadence to create a better ending effect. This cadence is definitely the strongest cadence in music and creates the feeling of complete ending, which may be written in two ways:</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3197\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"500\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3197 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/Perfect-Cadence-in-C-minor-sheet-music.jpg\" alt=\"کادانس کامل کامل\" width=\"500\" height=\"300\" /> An example of a perfect authentic cadence in C minor[/caption]\n\n<h4>First State:</h4>\n\n<p>The final chord has the tonic note of the scale in the bass and soprano sections. This state is the strongest perfect cadence and is called a perfect authentic cadence or perfect proper cadence.</p>\n\n<h4>Second State:</h4>\n\n<p>The tonic note of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">scale</a> in the final chord is heard only in the bass section, and in the soprano section, the third note and in some cases the fifth note is placed. Although this type of perfect cadence creates a completely strong ending, it is weaker compared to the perfect cadence in the first state (perfect authentic cadence) and is simply called a perfect cadence or imperfect authentic cadence.</p>\n\n<h3>Plagal or Amen</h3>\n\n<p>The plagal cadence is obtained from the resolution of the fourth degree (IV) of the scale to its first degree (I), which does not create the feeling of definite ending but creates a kind of temporary pause and therefore prepares the ground for repeating the melody or repeating the musical phrase. This type of cadence is used in cases where the poetry changes with the repetition of a musical phrase. Because this type of cadence has been widely used in vocal church <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%DA%A9%D9%84%DB%8C%D8%B3%D8%A7\">works</a> and the word “Amen” was usually placed on this cadence, this cadence is also called the Amen cadence.</p>\n\n<p>The plagal cadence can have a desirable effect after performing the perfect cadence (resolution of degree V to I).</p>\n\n<h3>Deceptive (Interrupted)</h3>\n\n<p>In this type of cadence, all the conditions for performing a perfect cadence are prepared, but at the end, instead of the first degree (I), other degrees of the scale are placed, which changes depending on the degree of strangeness of the final chord, the amount of shock and ambiguity created. This cadence, which is famous as the deceptive cadence, appears in various forms. In this type of cadence, even completely strange chords relative to the tonality of the work may be used, which are referred to as altered chords.</p>\n\n<p>One of the most common types of deceptive cadence is the broken cadence. In this cadence, after the fifth degree (V) of the scale, the fourth degree (IV) is placed and creates a kind of temporary pause.</p>\n\n<p>This cadence is used in cases where the composer’s goal is to create more excitement and also to expand the perfect cadence, and for this reason, it is seen in many musical works, especially the works of Johann Sebastian Bach. After performing the broken cadence, at the point where it is expected that the musical piece will end, the composer, by using this cadence, creates a kind of shock in the work and immediately ends the music by performing the perfect cadence.</p>\n\n<h3>Imperfect or Half Cadence</h3>\n\n<p>The imperfect cadence is often used in the middle of musical works and in cases where a kind of pause in the music is desired. This cadence stands on the fifth degree (V) of the scale and for this reason creates a strong feeling of expectation, but nevertheless does not create any ending effect.</p>\n\n<p>This cadence is used in cases where the composer intends to create a relatively long silence in his musical piece without the listeners feeling that the piece has ended. Also, its use in concertos before the start of the soloist’s performance, when the orchestra comes to a complete stop on one chord, finds a completely logical application.</p>\n\n<p>The second inversion of the first-degree chord of the scale, which has a very effective application in cadences, is also placed before the fifth degree here, and in this way, the most important and most common resolutions in the half cadence are made.</p>\n\n<h3>Evaded (Interrupted)</h3>\n\n<p>The evaded cadence is created by the sudden interruption and abrupt cutting of factors such as melody, sonic range, tempo, sound intensity and weakness (nuance), and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a8%d8%a7%d9%81%d8%aa-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">musical texture</a>. In fact, this cadence occurs when all the conditions for performing a perfect cadence are prepared, but at the moment of resolution on the first degree (I), one of the musical factors or several of the mentioned factors change, and as a result, the uniformity and integrity of the perfect cadence is destroyed, and the music continues with changes.</p>\n\n<p>In this way, composers, by using these kinds of cadences, while creating variety in their musical piece, expand the cadence and present a melody with different colorings.</p>\n\n<h3>Covered (Concealed)</h3>\n\n<p>The covered cadence is a full-fledged cadence but is used to continue the movement and texture of the music and to expand the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d9%88%d9%86%db%8c/\">harmony</a>. Compared to deceptive and evaded cadences, this cadence is closer to the main meaning of cadence, and perhaps it would be better to use other titles for deceptive and evaded cadences. The covered cadence is exactly a cadence but does not create a definite ending; rather, it is used for the continuation of texture, movement, and the structure of musical pieces and its expansion.</p>\n\n<h3>Masculine and Feminine</h3>\n\n<p>In the topic of cadence, the issue of creating stronger or weaker cadences has always been raised. For this reason, expressions such as masculine and feminine cadences have been created. The most important distinguishing factor in these expressions is the accents; if the cadence ends on a strong beat of the measure (the first beat), it is called a masculine cadence, and if it ends on a weak beat (the second beat) or a semi-strong beat (the third beat of four-beat measures), it is called a feminine cadence.</p>', '2023-08-15 20:00:17', 0, '2024-09-18 22:40:11', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1497, 'posts', 40, 'en', NULL, 'The concept of radif in Iranian music, which are the 7 dastgahs and 5 avaz in the radif of Iranian music?', NULL, 'This article explores the concept of Radif in Persian classical (Dastgahi) music. Radif refers to an organized sequence of musical phrases (melodies) arranged systematically within a cultural and aesthetic framework. It includes a collection of dastgahs (modal systems), avaz (sub-modes), and gusheh (melodic units).\n\nRadif is used in two main senses:\n\nAs the complete body of melodies in Iranian music.\nAs the ordered sequence of melodies within a specific dastgah.\n\nAlthough different Radifs share a common structural foundation, they vary in the number, naming, and ordering of gushehs due to oral transmission and different interpretations by masters such as Mirza Abdollah.\n\nRadif is generally divided into:\n\nVocal Radif: Based on poetry and vocal expression.\nInstrumental Radif: Designed according to the technical capabilities of instruments.\n\nThe article highlights Radif’s educational role, including teaching musical intervals, modal structures, phrasing, rhythm, and expressive nuances. It is considered the official classical music system of Iran and a result of centuries of cultural evolution.\n\nFurther, the article explains key concepts such as modes (mayeh), structural pathways, and gushehs, describing how they shape the emotional and structural flow of music.\n\nIt also discusses differences between Iranian and Western music, particularly in interval systems, octave function, and tetrachord (dang) structures.\n\nFinally, the concept of “Hal” (inner emotional/spiritual state) is emphasized as the ultimate goal of Persian music—representing a deep, transcendental experience achieved through performance and listening.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">radif</span></strong> in Iranian music, become familiar with its different types, and analyze its structure from several perspectives. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>What is the Radif?</h2>\n\n<p>In Persian, “radif” means a regular, orderly arrangement one after another. Whenever we place a large number of related components in an orderly, logical, and specially appropriate sequence, we say we have arranged them in a “radif.”</p>\n\n<p>In dastgah music, this term (“radif”) is used with the same meaning. In dastgah music, radif means arranging melodies in an orderly, logical, aesthetic, and culturally specific manner. The word radif is used in two senses in dastgah music:</p>\n\n<p>1) In an absolute sense, meaning all the existing melodies in the seven dastgahs.</p>\n\n<p>2) As a prefix to each dastgah, meaning the orderly arrangement of the melodies specific to that dastgah. For example: the radif of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%85%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%88%d8%b1/\">dastgah of Mahur</a> means arranging the existing melodies in the dastgah of Mahur one after another.</p>\n\n<p>The concept of radif in music, based on its general and literal meaning, is the ordering and systematization of dastgahs, maqams, and gushehs. Therefore, making a radif means how to place melodies one after another.</p>\n\n<p>“One by one I will count everything that exists  Until the radif of the dastgah comes to hand” (Mehdi Qoli Hedayat).</p>\n\n<p>The vocal and instrumental radifs of music are numerous, but the main framework of all of them is the same, and their foundation is introducing gushehs through presenting a specific melodic movement in the framework of those maqams.</p>\n\n<p>Most radifs, regardless of their specific melodic movements, are the same in presenting content and naming the head maqams and famous maqams such as Bidad and Iraq, etc., but they differ in the number of gushehs and how they are arranged: “It should be known that in the order written for each dastgah, differences have occurred. Some have proceeded in a different order, meaning they have mentioned the names of the avazes (meaning gushehs) with advancement and delay. Others have not named several avazes, although they too were masters” (Bahur al-Alhan: 38).</p>\n\n<p>The greatest differences relate to how gushehs are named in the radifs of the two main schools, namely the schools related to the cities of Isfahan and Tehran. Also, the number of dastgahs in various radifs differs. For example, the structure of some radifs is seven dastgahs, some are twelve dastgahs, and some are thirteen dastgahs. The number of gushehs is also not equal in different radifs, and in some of them more gushehs have been narrated, some of which also have various types.</p>\n\n<p>On the other hand, it is worth mentioning that the nature of the Iranian dastgah music system has the capacity to accept new gushehs and dastgahs — and not new maqams — because gushehs only flow in the framework of ten or fifteen maqams, and only their specific melodic movement distinguishes them from their other frameworks. Therefore, one can never claim or announce how many gushehs exist in the Iranian music radif. On this basis, the owners and narrators of the radifs have counted and arranged the gushehs according to their taste, memorized repertoire, and level of knowledge. For example, we see that:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>The dastgah of Nava in Davami’s radif (one of the vocal radifs of the Tehran school) has 17 gushehs,</li>\n  <li>The dastgah of Nava in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ad%d8%b3%d9%86-%da%a9%d8%b3%d8%a7%db%8c%db%8c/\">Kasaei’s</a> radif (one of the vocal radifs of the Isfahan school) has 26 gushehs, and</li>\n  <li>The dastgah of Nava in Kazemi’s radif (one of the vocal radifs of the Isfahan school) has 13 gushehs, and the position of their shared gushehs is also not the same.</li>\n  <li>“The total number of gushehs or maqams that exist in Iranian music is 228” (Scientific Thoughts of Farabi on Music: 54 and 55);</li>\n  <li>“That is, in 297 parts, I played all of Iranian music and the dastgahs and gushehs...” (Concert Brochure of Iranian Music, Commemoration of Master Ney Davud);</li>\n  <li>“Iranian music has 178 gushehs; I also sing them without needing notation” (With the Whisper of Hezar Dastan: 115);</li>\n  <li>“Now I have collected a set of 412 gushehs and melodies along with their specific poetry” (Maqam, No. 3: 92, interview with Abbas Kazemi).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Inventing a new maqam is not impossible, but its establishment and practical use, in a way that penetrates all layers of society, requires decades or even centuries of refinement. In other words, a new dastgah and gusheh can be created immediately, but not a new maqam.</p>\n\n<h3>Different Narrations of the Radif</h3>\n\n<p>We want to know, for example, what Mirza Abdollah’s radif and the “narration” of Mirza Abdollah’s radif mean, and this topic should be examined and explained in the right direction. The origin of all today’s instrumental radifs is the radif of a player named <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d8%a7%da%a9%d8%a8%d8%b1-%d9%81%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Aqa Ali Akbar Khan Farahani</a>, who of course must have learned the entire radif or parts of it from his previous masters, but here we are not concerned with his predecessors and we assume that he himself, Aqa Ali Akbar Khan, was the originator of his radif. Therefore:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Aqa Ali Akbar has a radif and teaches it to his nephew, Aqa Gholamhossein;</li>\n  <li>Aqa Gholamhossein, as the first narrator of Aqa Ali Akbar’s radif, teaches it to Aqa Ali Akbar’s son, Mirza Abdollah;</li>\n  <li>Mirza Abdollah, as the second narrator of Aqa Ali Akbar’s radif (and the first narrator of Aqa Gholamhossein’s radif), teaches it to his students;</li>\n  <li>Each of Mirza Abdollah’s students, as the third narrator of Aqa Ali Akbar’s radif (and the first narrator of Mirza Abdollah’s radif), reports it to later generations;</li>\n  <li>In these hand-to-hand transmissions and cycles of oral narrations, each narrator deliberately or accidentally, influenced by personal desire and taste or due to forgetfulness and memory deficiency, makes changes in the original and initial radif; therefore, in our time, different narrations of Mirza Abdollah’s radif (originally Aqa Ali Akbar Khan’s radif) and other radifs are seen.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>In this way, the music radif has as many types as there are owners of the radif and also the number of different narrations made from a specific radif. But regardless of this topic and in general, it is divided into two branches: vocal and instrumental.</p>\n\n<h4>Instrumental Radif</h4>\n\n<p>Instrumental radifs are a type of dastgah radif whose melodic movements, depending on which instrument they are intended for, have been designed and compiled based on the performance capabilities of the instruments. The compilers of instrumental radifs in the Qajar period were generally tar and setar players, and therefore the structure of the trills and sentence movements in these types of radifs are more compatible with the performance capabilities of the tar and setar.</p>\n\n<p>In some instrumental radifs, especially those related to the santur, specific techniques and tricks have been used that are impossible to perform with the voice. The simplest example that can be given in this regard is the existence of “double” notes in these radifs, which can be performed with instruments such as santur, tar, setar, and oud, and naturally the singer will not be able to perform them.</p>\n\n<p>Instrumental radifs have no poetry or lyrics, and for this reason, compared to vocal radifs, they can have a faster meter and tempo. Usually, the number of gushehs in instrumental radifs is more than the gushehs in radifs related to avaz, because in addition to the maqams and gushehs used in vocal radifs, they also consist of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%86%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d8%b6%d8%b1%d8%a7%d8%a8/\">chaharmezrabs</a>, rengs, and zarbis, and in addition, the number of their wandering gushehs is also more than in vocal radifs.</p>\n\n<p>The most famous and widespread instrumental radifs are Mirza Abdollah’s and Aqa Hossein-Qoli’s radifs for tar and setar, and also Saba’s radif for santur. The origin of the instrumental radif is the vocal radif, because the principle of Iranian music is based on avaz.</p>\n\n<h4>Vocal Radif</h4>\n\n<p>Vocal radifs are a type of dastgah radifs that are accompanied by poetry, and the design of melodic movement in their maqams and gushehs follows the emphases and meters of their poems, which are generally in the form of “ghazal,” and also parts of the melodic movement in the maqams and gushehs are presented in the form of “tahrir.” The design of the melody in vocal radifs is appropriate to the ability of Iranian singers’ voices, and therefore, compared to instrumental radifs, they have a slower meter and are more in harmony with the nature of Iranian avaz. Although in principle the vocal radif is the origin of the instrumental radif, it lacks gushehs such as chaharmezrab, reng, and zarbi — which some believe are purely instrumental.</p>\n\n<p>The two famous vocal radifs of Tehran, namely Abdollah Davami’s and Mahmoud Karimi’s radifs, are very concise compared to the vocal radifs of the Isfahan school. In the Qajar period, most singers were either ta’zieh singers or learned music from ta’zieh singers. Ta’zieh and ta’zieh singing have had a great share in preserving vocal music throughout past eras.</p>\n\n<h2>Familiarity with Iranian Music</h2>\n\n<h3>Auditory Culture</h3>\n\n<p>The transmission of concepts, emotions, and auditory beauties, and in general, receiving all auditory experiences, takes place through the ear, and among them, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%88%db%8c%da%98%da%af%db%8c-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%b5%d9%88%d8%aa/\">musical sounds and tones</a>, due to having pleasant frequencies for the ear, have auditory pleasantness and auditory pleasure.</p>\n\n<p>Iranian music, as one of the strongest cultural sections of the Iranian nation, has been refined generation after generation throughout history and has reached the present generation, which is us, for use.</p>\n\n<p>This art, in the course of its historical evolution, has included laws that were in fact its own creators. Or, in other words, the human ear, with the help of the sense of aesthetics, has naturally caused the creation of specific laws in this art.</p>\n\n<p>Cultural and ritual values such as: religion and mysticism, literature, music, and other artistic sections, climatic factors (such as: the angle of sunlight, the degree of cloudiness of the sky, the moon and stars, etc.), and other geographical characteristics throughout history have determined the nature of the Iranian human being, his taste, and his aesthetic approach, and the flow of this aesthetics in auditory culture has led to the explanation of these laws.</p>\n\n<p>And in this way, with the creation of this auditory culture over thousands of years, our duty regarding the preservation of dastgah music becomes even clearer.</p>\n\n<h3>Official Music of Iran</h3>\n\n<p>The connection between our mind or emotion or inner self with auditory culture is formed from childhood with mothers’ lullabies, and this type of connection in the land of Iran (due to the great diversity in language, dialect, climate, and type of life) has a very wide variety.</p>\n\n<p>One of the most prominent, most important, and most authentic types of these musics is “ordered dastgah music,” which is considered the “official music of Iran” and is used with a general scope for the entire Iranian nation.</p>\n\n<p>For example, one can point to the use and role of the Persian language as the official language of the country (despite the great diversity of languages and dialects) for understanding, communication, and creating commonality among different ethnic groups.</p>\n\n<h3>Tradition in Music</h3>\n\n<p>Tradition includes all the cultural essences of a country that have been obtained in different forms such as: written, auditory, and visual sections, etc., in a historical course, and this very historical course itself indicates the importance of tradition.</p>\n\n<p>Tradition, throughout successive eras, has been influenced by the generations in which it has flowed; and these generations have added or subtracted things from it throughout history, and this means that tradition (in the East) does not have a fixed and unchanging concept, and Iranian music, as one of the components of this tradition, has not been exempt from this rule and includes deletions and additions among generations.</p>\n\n<h3>Maqam Music and Dastgah Music</h3>\n\n<p>For example, one of the noteworthy points in the field of dastgah and maqam music in the present era is the impact of the revolution and the presence and settlement of people from different cities in cities such as Tehran, which with the presence of different ethnic groups with tendencies toward different maqam musics, this topic gained more intensity and severity in that period.</p>\n\n<p>Almost during one decade, with the attention and value given by organizations such as the “Islamic Art and Thought Domain” to this topic, this matter ultimately led to the preparation of numerous articles on this subject and more attention to it in festivals such as the Fajr Music Festival, which ultimately resulted in a great impact of maqam music on Iranian dastgah music.</p>\n\n<p>Maqam music is originally considered a prerequisite and, in a way, the shaper of dastgah music. In its historical course (after the rise of Shia governments in Iran), with more attention paid to the cultures specific to the land of Iran and changing concepts that were mostly imposed on Iranian music from Arab countries (and had become standard), it tended toward Iranian names and concepts such as the concept of “dastgah” (or, for example, replacing the word “sho’beh” with “gusheh” and using names such as “daramad” and “gosha’esh,” etc.).</p>\n\n<p>This tendency toward Iranian names and titles became the foundation for the creation of dastgah music that still had traces of ancient pasts and took on a Persian color. So that when looking at the radif as a whole, the Iranians’ effort to use Iranian words is clearly recognizable.</p>\n\n<p>As Dr. Barkeshli says: Seventy percent of the names of the radif are authentic Persian words, and thirty percent include Turkish and Arabic words.</p>\n\n<p>Professor Bruno Nettl, the musicologist orientalist, has said about Iranian dastgah music: “Iranian dastgah music is one of the most important, greatest, and most beautiful phenomena of music in the region that has evolved and has only come into existence in Iran and has gone from Iran to various places, and it is not clear what happened in Iran that only these Iranians created dastgah music.”</p>\n\n<p>Although it is clear that dastgah music is the evolved form of maqam music and traces of maqams still exist in dastgah music, the concept of dastgah is completely different from the concept of maqam: dastgah music has a compressed and systematized system called the radif, which has a specific course (like an article that has an introduction, content, poems, proverbs, and conclusion), and therefore one should not use maqam instead of gusheh or avaz.</p>\n\n<p>For example, there is an Arabic maqam called “Rast,” which is a kind of equivalent to the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b1%d8%a7%d8%b3%d8%aa-%d9%be%d9%86%d8%ac%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">dastgah of Rast Panjgah</a> in radif music (in which, in addition to the gusheh of Rast, we also have five other important melodies in it).</p>\n\n<p>In the maqam space, the range, space, and notes are completely defined, and changing them leads to exiting the maqam, but for example, in the dastgah of Rast Panjgah, in addition to the space of the daramad (which can be likened to the ground floor of a building), floors (or degrees or intervals or steps) with a different space have been formed that are built on the first floor and have a specific line of movement toward the space of the ouj.</p>\n\n<h3>The Concept and Objectives of the Dastgah Radif</h3>\n\n<p>Radif means orderly arranging all the melodies of Iranian music in a very long cycle that includes seven dastgahs (named: Shur, Nava, Mahur, Segah, Chahargah, Homayun, and Rast Panjgah) and five avazes (including: Abu Ata, Bayat-e Tork, Dashti, Afshari, and Esfahan) and the gushehs inside them.</p>\n\n<p>So far, various opinions have been presented by researchers and experts regarding the word “dastgah,” and overall it can be said that dastgah was formed in the continuation of the word “dastan” (meaning the place of placing the hand and the size of one dang or tetrachord), and with the combination of dastans throughout the evolution of Iranian music, the word dastan no longer sufficed for it and changed to the word dastgah.</p>\n\n<p>Dastgah here means a collection that includes components as its builders (similar to the use of this word in the phrase “one dastgah of apartments”), and literally includes two words: hand and gah (indicator of place and time). This word, next to the word radif, creates a correct meaning for the dastgah music radif as a large organized collection.</p>\n\n<p>Since those who founded this order for the radif and systematized it were all mathematicians, geometricians, architects, and possessors of logical sciences, a specific logic has also governed the Iranian dastgah music radif. For this reason, the design of the dastgahs is very similar to the design in Iranian architecture.</p>\n\n<p>The largest components forming the dastgahs are the gushehs, which mean the various angles of a dastgah. In other words, it can be said that the gushehs are like the different rooms of a house. To go to each room, there are specific regulations, and each of these rooms has a specific application, and one cannot go from any room to any other room.</p>\n\n<p>In the music of Saudi Arabia, these dastgahs are also named “house.”</p>\n\n<p>So, in a way, one can imagine that in a garden called the radif, there are seven houses, and around one of the houses there are also five small houses (which are considered the children of that house).</p>\n\n<p>The radif in practice means the logic by which these rooms (gushehs) are connected to each other, and it is this reasoning and logic that creates the dastgah. In the past, instead of the word gusheh, the word sho’beh was used, but after the rise of the Safavid government and with the promotion of Shia culture and outlook, Arabization was removed from Persian speech and Arabic names (in musical titles) were replaced with Persian words.</p>\n\n<p>Considering the sanctity of the numbers 7 and 14 from ancient times, the classification of the dastgahs was also formed in this way.</p>\n\n<p>The logical line of movement of the radif in the music dastgahs can be divided into two categories from the perspective of the type of movement:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>The main axis of the radif</li>\n  <li>The secondary axes</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4><strong>The Main Axis of the Radif</strong></h4>\n\n<p>It is like the main road between two cities (to go from daramad to ouj and vice versa), on which there are also stopping places at specific intervals, each of which has a specific name and space.</p>\n\n<p>The unit of these intervals (which are measured in kilometers in the outside world) is called frequency in music.</p>\n\n<p>Of course, as an executive note, it should be mentioned that in Iranian dastgah music, the origin and end of the line of movement in the radif is very important. Because movement in dastgah music is always purposeful, and by constantly showing the beginning and direction of movement, we should be able to specify our goal (ouj) and line of movement. Therefore, even when we enter the main gushehs on the main axis, at the end of that gusheh, we must again return to the origin and space of the daramad and have a reference and forud to it so that we have specified our origin.</p>\n\n<h4><strong>The Secondary Axes</strong></h4>\n\n<p>On the other hand, along the way, there are also side roads on which one can use their space, atmosphere, and mood during the journey, or take a tour in them and return to the main path again, or move to another main axis, or reach the same initial axis from another path.</p>\n\n<p>To go from the main axis to the secondary axis, an event must occur, and that event is usually a change in frequency or the same accidental signs for the notes (sori, koron, diez, bemol, and bekar), which by going to the secondary axis, our mode or, in other words, our mood and atmosphere also changes.</p>\n\n<h3>A Brief Description of the Gushehs in Dastgah Music</h3>\n\n<p>Unfortunately, in naming the gushehs in dastgah music, the name gusheh has been given to all melodies, structures, states, qualities, and sometimes plectrum strikes, which has caused a major problem in our radif.</p>\n\n<p>Whereas the structural gushehs (as the main gushehs) in each dastgah should be separated and distinguished from the non-structural gushehs. For example, a small gusheh like “Hazin” is only a decorative and state gusheh... and a gusheh like “Mokhalef-e Chahargah” is a detailed and structural gusheh.</p>\n\n<p>On this basis, the gushehs can be categorized as follows:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Structural gushehs:</strong> In these gushehs, one can perform different melodic compositions and proceed to create chaharmezrabs, pishdaramads, tasnifs, and various musical forms.</li>\n  <li><strong>Qualitative-state gushehs dependent on structural gushehs:</strong> Such as the gusheh of Hazin on the structure of the gusheh of Mokhalef-e Segah.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rhythmic and metric gushehs dependent on structural gushehs:</strong> Such as kereshmeh in the structure of the daramad of Shur or in the structure of Zabol in the dastgah of Chahargah.</li>\n  <li><strong>Purely melodic gushehs:</strong> Such as the gusheh of Masnavi, which follow a specific melody but are built in different structures and their melody and meter never change.</li>\n  <li><strong>Connecting and linking gushehs:</strong> Which start from one place to reach a specific degree and end at the destination.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>The Relationship Between Gushehs and Mayehs</h3>\n\n<p>Among the types of gushehs, only the structural gushehs indicate the different mayehs in a dastgah. Among them, there are some gushehs that point to the mayehs (and so to speak, create an illusion) but do not take us into that mayeh. For example, when we play the gusheh of Neyriz in the dastgah of Mahur, we do not reach an independent and powerful mayeh on which we can stand.</p>\n\n<p>Or, as another example, in the second degree of Shur (the gusheh of Rahab), although the mayeh changes, our stand is temporary, and we do not find stability in it to evoke a new mayeh and approach Segah, and we only temporarily pass through it in the form of a reference and passage.</p>\n\n<p>Now, considering the spaces created by these structural gushehs, the mayehs can also be categorized in terms of state. In this way, some are happy, some sad, some thoughtful, some epic, etc.</p>\n\n<p>For example, in the daramad of the dastgah of Chahargah, we face a martial and heroic mayeh. For this reason, in delivering the words and poetry in it, a firmer tone is used, but in the same dastgah of Chahargah in the Zabol mayeh, we face a more lyrical and amorous state, and in its continuation in the side circuit of Hesar, we again face different plectrum strikes, states, qualities, and tones. Then in the forud, we face a weak mayeh called Muye (only in the form of a reference), and then in the Mokhalef, we again face changes. In the continuation, in the Maghlub, the plectrum strikes and the foundation of the mayeh become strong, and in Mansouri, it becomes even stronger.</p>\n\n<h2>Iranian Music and Western Music</h2>\n\n<h3>A Structural Difference in Iranian and Western Music</h3>\n\n<p>Unlike Western music, in which the eighth note is the octave of the same first note (and is similar to it in terms of position and only one octave lower or higher), in the Iranian music radif, the eighth note no longer has its previous position and itself acts as a different position in the structure of the radif’s course and plays a different role. In Iranian music, due to the dominant philosophy of avaz over music and the existence of a different feeling and atmosphere in the ouj, in the second octave, we have a different course compared to the first seven notes.</p>\n\n<p>Sometimes, in line with that different feeling and atmosphere, changes also occur in the accidental signs of the notes (diez, sori, bekar, koron, and bemol) in the next octave. For example, in the dastgah of Shur (for example, Shur on Re), in the course of Salmak to Qorcheh and Razavi (in the second octave), the note La becomes koron. For this reason, to have more possibilities in radif music, in addition to Shur (or for example Homayun) in the lower register, we also have a Shur (or Homayun) in the upper register, or for example, in the lower register of Shur, we do not have the important gusheh of Shahnaz.</p>\n\n<p>In the old educational methods (in which there was no notation), letters of the abjad were used (like in Europe and America where letters were used). In this way, by stating the first degree of each dastgah, the structure of the subsequent degrees was explained and taught separately. Using the degree structure was one of the most important advantages of education in the old method.</p>\n\n<p>For these reasons, the type of outlook and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">theory of Western music</a> cannot be exactly matched with Iranian radif and dastgah music.</p>\n\n<h3>Dang and Its Combinations in Iranian Music</h3>\n\n<p>The combinations of dangs (or tetrachords, which include four notes or two and a half steps) in the Iranian music radif take on different categorizations compared to Western music. This categorization has a great impact on some theoretical concepts of Iranian music.</p>\n\n<p>These combinations include three different types:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Type one: In which the first dang and the second dang are one after another and there is no separation between them; this causes softness in the melodies, and as a result of this softness, it becomes difficult to distinguish between changes in melodies and gushehs. In this type of dang combinations, the fourth and fifth notes have special importance (Fa and Sol).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>For example, in the dastgah of Mahur, we face this type of dang combination. In this way, the first two dangs are formed from the consecutive notes: Do, Re, Mi, and Fa, and the second dang is formed from Fa, Sol, La, and Si bemol, each of which is two and a half steps or a complete dang, and there is no interval between them.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Type two: Between the first and second dang, there is an interval of one step (a whole tone interval). Like the dastgah of Chahargah, in which Do, Re koron, Mi, and Fa form the first dang, then between Fa and Sol we have a whole tone, and then Sol, La koron, Si, and Do form the next dang.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>In classical Western music, only this type of dang combination is used. In some types of their music, such as serial music or modern music, new combinations have also been added to it.</p>\n\n<p>In this type of dang combinations, the fifth note has more importance and a special position, and more emphasis is placed on it.</p>\n\n<p>Like the gusheh of Hesar in Chahargah, which emphasizes Sol.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Type three: In which, before the first and second dang, a whole tone or whole tone interval is placed right at the beginning. (Like Greek music) which is separated from the first and second dang. Like the gusheh of Dad in the dastgah of Mahur.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Composers such as <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d9%86%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Colonel Ali Naqi Vaziri</a>, due to the similarity between the second type of dang combinations with Western music, used this type more for their compositions, and due to the existing theoretical weakness in our music, the first and third types have been used less and rarely.</p>\n\n<p>Although composers such as Ruhollah Khaleqi paid attention to this issue, only the late Morteza Hannaneh was the one who built the principles of his <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d9%88%d9%86%db%8c/\">harmony</a> in Iranian music on the basis of fourths and presented many theories and pieces in this type of combination.</p>\n\n<p>In the Iranian music radif, usually for each main dang, we will have an order like this in front of us:</p>\n\n<p>Pre-dang – Main dang – Post-dang</p>\n\n<p>As an example, one can mention the following gushehs in the dastgah of Chahargah:</p>\n\n<p>Pre-Hesar – Hesar – Post-Hesar</p>\n\n<p>Sometimes in the radif, the pre-dang is used as a single resonant note (like Do before Fa or Do before Sol) or several consecutive notes (in the form of a complete dang or even more than one dang).</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the concept of radif in Iranian music, please share them in the comments section of this article with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a> to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-08-15 20:16:23', 0, '2024-09-18 22:39:18', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1498, 'posts', 41, 'en', NULL, 'What is the concept of dastgah and avaz in Iranian music? Fundamental differences between these structures', NULL, 'This article explores the concepts of Dastgah (modal system) and Avaz in traditional Iranian music, along with the historical development of the Dastgah system. The term “Dastgah” is used in two senses: first, as a collection of melodies and musical pieces (gushehs) within a structured framework, and second, as finger positioning on musical instruments. Iranian classical music repertoire (Radif) consists of seven main Dastgahs—Shur, Segah, Chahargah, Homayun, Mahur, Nava, and Rast-Panjgah—along with several subsidiary Avaz forms derived from them.\n\nThe article explains how the older modal system (Maqami music) gradually declined between the late Safavid and early Qajar periods, being replaced by the Dastgah system. Although the exact causes and timeline of this transformation remain unclear, some sources attribute it to a musician named Aghababa Makhmoor. Over time, the number of Dastgahs evolved—from twelve to seven main systems—and later expanded again with the inclusion of Avaz branches.\n\nIt also highlights the lack of a comprehensive and universally accepted theoretical framework for Iranian Dastgah music, attributing this gap to the absence of collaborative and institutional research. The article further examines the internal structure of Dastgahs, including elements such as gusheh (melodic units), maqam (principal modes), and musical forms like pishdaramad, chaharmezrab, reng, and tasnif. Ultimately, it emphasizes that each Dastgah is formed through a combination of multiple maqams and gushehs, and that a more systematic and scientific classification is still needed for deeper understanding.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the concepts of <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">dastgah and avaz</span></strong> in Iranian music and provide a brief history of the formation of dastgah music and the obsolescence of the old maqam music of Iran. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>What is Dastgah?</h2>\n\n<p>The prefix “dastgah” before any noun means a totality composed of various details. For example, the ruling dastgah is a totality composed of various different dastgahs, or the judicial dastgah is composed of various sections in judicial affairs. The word “dastgah” also applies to objects composed of various details, such as a fax machine or a computer system.</p>\n\n<p>The suffix “gah” in Persian, after any verb, means the place where that action is performed. For example: university (place of acquiring knowledge), sports stadium (place of sport).</p>\n\n<p>In dastgah music, the word “dastgah” is used in two senses.</p>\n\n<p>1) Dastgah, in the specific sense of the totality of melodies in a dastgah. For example, the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b4%d9%88%d8%b1/\">dastgah of Shur</a> has various details. For example: the dastgah of Shur consists of Shur, Abu Ata, Bayat-e Tork, Afshari, Dashti, and their related gushehs.</p>\n\n<p>2) Dastgah in the sense of the place of the hand (or the same as fingering on various instruments).</p>\n\n<p>The Iranian music radif consists of 7 main dastgahs and 5 secondary sections, which in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">dastgah music</a> are technically called “avaz” for the branches separated from the main dastgah. (“Avaz” here does not mean singing.) As a result, the radif consists of seven dastgahs and five avazes, which are:</p>\n\n<p>1- Dastgah of Shur</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">The avazes of the dastgah of Shur are: Avaz of Abu Ata, Avaz of Bayat-e Tork, Avaz of Afshari, and Avaz of Dashti.</p>\n\n<p>2- Dastgah of Segah</p>\n\n<p>3- Dastgah of Chahargah</p>\n\n<p>4- Dastgah of Homayun</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\"><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%a7%d8%b5%d9%81%d9%87%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan</a> is one of the dependents of the dastgah of Homayun.</p>\n\n<p>5- Dastgah of Mahur</p>\n\n<p>6- Dastgah of Nava</p>\n\n<p>7- Dastgah of Rast Panjgah</p>\n\n<h2>Dastgah Music</h2>\n\n<p>In the time span from the end of the Safavid period to the beginning of the Qajar period, the old maqam system became obsolete and the modern dastgah system replaced it. No one knows what the causes, main factors, and exact time of creating this transformation were, because no report has been obtained about this, and no treatise has been written about it either. Apparently, the term “dastgah” was first used in the treatise Kolliyat-e Yusofi, and its author attributed the creation of the dastgah to a musician named <strong>Aqa Baba Makhmur</strong>:</p>\n\n<p>“And in the time of the government of the heavenly Khaghan Fath Ali Shah Qajar [reign 1212–1250 AH / 1176–1212 SH], Master Aqa Baba Makhmur, who was the foremost master of his time in the art of music, established the foundation of the dastgah on twelve:”</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li style=\"list-style-type: none;\">\n<ul>\n  <li>First: Rast Panjgah,</li>\n  <li>Second: Navaneshabur,</li>\n  <li>Third: Homayun,</li>\n  <li>Fourth: Mahur,</li>\n  <li>Fifth: Rahab,</li>\n  <li>Sixth: Shul and Shahnaz,</li>\n  <li>Seventh: Chahargah Mokhalef,</li>\n  <li>Eighth: Segah,</li>\n  <li>Ninth: Dogah,</li>\n  <li>Tenth: Zabol,</li>\n  <li>Eleventh: Ashiran,</li>\n  <li>Twelfth: Neyriz” (Kolliyat-e Yusofi: 37).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Here it must be said that the belief held by some musicians that the reason for naming the dastgah of Rast Panjgah is that it is composed of “five maqams” is not correct. Moreover, if we look at the content of this dastgah, we will see that “Panjgah” is the first important maqam of this dastgah after the maqam of Rast. Therefore, the main title of the dastgah has nothing to do with the number “5” or “five maqams.” In addition, for example, are the dastgahs of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%85%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Mahur</a> and Nava and ... composed of only one maqam? In the dastgah of Mahur, just like in Rast Panjgah, many maqams have been used. So why don’t we call it “Mahur and Panjgah,” or for example, why don’t we call the dastgah of Nava “Nava and Sheshgah”? Unfortunately, this perception has been made by one of the most prominent musicians of this land, namely <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a> (A View on Music: 192), but what is strange is that his mistake is still repeated and quoted: “… and the dastgah of Rast Panjgah is in fact ‘Rast’ and ‘Panjgah,’ meaning a combination of Rast (which has the intervals of the Mahur scale) and five other maqams” (Analysis and Explanation of the Iranian Music Radif: 118).</p>\n\n<p>If we accept the opinion of the author of the treatise and consider Aqa Baba Makhmur as the founder of this transformation in music, and assume its time to be the year 1200 SH, attention to several points is important:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>The dastgah system of Iranian music has a history of approximately two hundred years;</li>\n  <li>The belief that some musicians consider Aqa Ali Akbar Khan Farahani (father of Mirza Abdollah) as the founder of the dastgah is not correct;</li>\n  <li>The twelve new dastgahs and avazes were created in parallel with the twelve old maqams and based on the number of celestial towers;</li>\n  <li>At the beginning of the formation of the new system, at least the names of four dastgahs were composed of the names of two maqams and compound titles: Rast Panjgah, Navaneshabur, Shul and Shahnaz, Chahargah Mokhalef;</li>\n  <li>Shur, which had no precedent in maqam music and suddenly gained a prominent position in this period (this may be one of the special innovations of Aqa Baba Makhmur, who was from Isfahan and his birthplace was near the Shulestan region), was originally “Shul” and attributed to a region of the same name in southern Iran whose center is now the city of Mamassani. Therefore, today’s Shur does not mean “joy and excitement” but originally means “Shuli” or “Shuri,” which, like many new names of dastgahs, is attributed to one of the regions;</li>\n  <li>The names of the seven main dastgahs of today, with slight changes, are the same as the dastgah names of Aqa Baba Makhmur: Rast Panjgah, Navaneshabur, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%87%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c%d9%88%d9%86/\">Homayun</a>, Mahur, Shul and Shahnaz, Chahargah Mokhalef, Segah;</li>\n  <li>About sixty or seventy years after Aqa Baba Makhmur, the number of dastgahs was reduced to seven because until before the years 1300 SH, the avazes or secondary dastgahs of today had not yet gained independence, and the totality of Iranian music was classified into seven dastgahs. Forsat Shirazi also, in Bahur al-Alhan in the year 1292 SH and during the lifetime of Mirza Abdollah, placed the dastgah of Rast Panjgah in the same position (Bahur al-Alhan: 34) and introduced the dastgah of Shur as the seventh dastgah (Bahur al-Alhan: 37);</li>\n  <li>About sixty or seventy years after Aqa Baba Makhmur, the titles Rahab, Dogah, Zabol, Ashiran, and Neyriz, which were the names of five main dastgahs in the time of Fath Ali Shah, become gushehs within the text of the seven dastgahs of Mirza Abdollah’s radif;</li>\n  <li>About one hundred years after Aqa Baba Makhmur, new dastgahs (avazes or secondary dastgahs) named Abu Ata, Bayat-e Tork, Afshari, Dashti, and Bayat-e Esfahan replaced his dastgahs of Rahab, Dogah, Zabol, Ashiran, and Neyriz, and Iranian music again became twelve dastgahs:</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>“The melodies of Shur consist of Abu Ata, Tork, Afshari, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%af%d8%b4%d8%aa%db%8c/\">Dashti</a>, each of which has gushehs and therefore are to some extent independent, and for this reason, gradually in the new music of Iran, a specific pishdaramad, tasnif, and reng has become customary for each of them” (Avaz Shenasi: 35).</p>\n\n<p>Another treatise titled “Dar Bayan-e Chahar Dastgah-e A’zam” by an unknown author from the Qajar period has remained, which was probably written after Kolliyat-e Yusofi because each of the four great dastgahs described by the author is, in some form, also seen in Aqa Baba Makhmur’s list of twelve dastgahs:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Chahargah, Nava, Rast, and Rahab-e Davudi.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>We have no concern with this part of the treatise, but pay attention to the titles that perhaps for the first time appear in this treatise and are among the names of today’s radif, including:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Bayat-e Qajar [Qajar], Ma’vara al-Nahr, Mansouri, Rak, and Sarang [Saranj].</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>The editor of the treatise, Amir Hossein Pourjavadi, has guessed it may be from the eleventh or twelfth century treatises (Mahur, No. 12: 84), and Arshad Tahmasbi, considering the existence of titles such as “Bayat-e Qajar” in this treatise, considers it related to the Qajar period, which of course does not contradict Mr. Pourjavadi’s opinion.</p>\n\n<p>With the addition of these names to the names of music in that period, we realize that changes were taking place, and therefore we are witnessing a refinement within the newly created dastgahs. These changes should be considered the result of the nature of the dastgah itself, which is an arrangement of multiple elements, because the dastgah system has a nature that can accept any modification, deletion, or addition, just as this matter has occurred many times in the process of the years after that period, and people such as Abolhasan Saba, Musa Ma’rufi, and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ad%d8%b3%d9%86-%da%a9%d8%b3%d8%a7%db%8c%db%8c/\">Hassan Kasaei</a>, and ... have added gushehs to the music radifs.</p>\n\n<p>Up to this point, we have been able to somewhat follow the process of changing the maqam music system and also the obsolescence of some titles and the displacement of some names. We will continue this process and in this stage we reach a turning point, which is the writing of a book called Bahur al-Alhan by Forsat al-Dowleh Shirazi. The names and classifications mentioned in this book, as an old and of course reliable text, are the closest to our music today, and the author has explained and interpreted some terms of the new dastgah system and their application in the radif music, which are very important and enlightening.</p>\n\n<p>It is very regrettable that players such as Aqa Ali Akbar Farahani and his sons Mirza Abdollah and Aqa Hossein-Qoli, who could have been, from this particular perspective, the link between the almost unknown world of early Qajar music and contemporary music, have left no book, treatise, or even a brief handwritten note that, if something had remained, today many of the concerns of music connoisseurs would not have been relevant. Perhaps they, like many today, were immersed in pragmatism and carefree about this matter. Perhaps they did not imagine that a day would come when many would seek to know at least the order and process of the gushehs of the dastgah from the point of view of people like them.</p>\n\n<p>Forsat Shirazi (Forsat al-Dowleh), who was not a musician either, in that period of the musicians’ peace of mind, had this insight and foresight to preserve a large part of the musical heritage of his time. About thirty or forty pages of the introduction to his Bahur al-Alhan, whose main subject is the selection and suggestion of suitable poems for singing in various maqams, contains important points that clarify many aspects of the music of that time for us.</p>\n\n<p>Interestingly, another wise man of that time, namely Mehdi Qoli Hedayat (Mokhber al-Saltaneh), although he was not a musician, undertook an unexpected and exhausting task and, despite his many preoccupations as an active and prominent politician, with the help of Mehdi Solhi (Montazem al-Hokama), who was one of Mirza Abdollah’s distinguished students, wrote down Mirza Abdollah’s radif. He spent seven years of his life on this work. In addition to this, Mehdi Qoli Hedayat also wrote a book called Majma‘ al-Advār, which contains important points and notes about theoretical music.</p>\n\n<p>But among the musicians of that period, only Ali Naqi Vaziri was the one who, in addition to his extraordinary ability in tar playing, also valued knowledge, and before traveling to Europe, he wrote down Aqa Hossein-Qoli’s radif and also wrote a book titled Theoretical Music in three volumes analyzing the music of his time. Therefore, in truth, these three people were the ones who, in explaining the new dastgah system, were able, to some extent, to compensate for the inattention of the founders of the dastgah system to this matter.</p>\n\n<h3>The Theory of Iranian Dastgah Music</h3>\n\n<p>Before we get to the main topic, it is good to talk a little about the “theory of Iranian dastgah music.” The structure of Iranian dastgah music lacks a comprehensive and all-encompassing theory that has gained general acceptance. The reason for this is related to the background and overall performance of Iranian society in a broad totality. Creating a comprehensive and all-encompassing theory for Iranian music is something that should have been addressed many years ago, and unfortunately, this has not happened so far.</p>\n\n<p>Even if any action has been taken in this regard, it has been nothing more than the individual efforts of people such as Ali Naqi Vaziri and ..., and perhaps because of this self-reliance and individualism, we have not been able to achieve this important goal so far.</p>\n\n<p>It is natural and obvious that achieving this goal will not be possible through individual work but through a collective performance and through the gathering, discussion, and exchange of views of the great musicians in assemblies such as academies. The same thing that the Arabs did and we were neglectful of doing. For this reason, they are aware of the ins and outs of their today’s music, while we still do not know how many maqams and dastgahs our music has, and there is no consensus on this matter, and we do not know what should be called a maqam and what should not, and ... .</p>\n\n<p>Arshad Tahmasbi wrote an article years ago titled “A Defense of Music,” which was published in issues 62 and 63 of the magazine Honar-e Musiqi in 1384, and was also printed in the book Pahlevan-e Tar in 1393. Some excerpts from it, related to the topic under discussion, have been brought here:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">“In 1932 AD, corresponding to 1310 SH, a music congress was held in Egypt under the chairmanship of Muhammad Hilmi Pasha, the Minister of Culture of that country, and with the membership of Egyptian and Western scholars, including ‘Farmer’ and ‘Erlanger.’ Several specialized committees were formed regarding various issues, including maqams and iqa‘, scale, musical instruments, notation, education, and history, and they examined the raised issues. The result of the Maqams Council’s discussion was a statement in which 52 maqams common in Egypt were examined and announced ... .</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">At the same time, or more precisely in 1306 SH in Iran, when the 1306 budget was approved in the parliament, one of the opponents of the budget, Mr. Mo’tazed al-Islam Astrabadi, suggested in the session that the 600 toman grant for the music school and also the music budget from the provincial schools’ budget, which was 23 tomans per month, be deleted, and stated: ‘I consider it my religious duty to say for what reason this money is spent.’</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Meanwhile, when some opponents leave the session, he shouts: ‘The supporters of music have left.’ Then he continues and says: ‘I don’t know what this pen expense is for. If we make the music school a little more Persian, we should say that a group goes there and plays the tar.’ ... .</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">About 55 years after Mr. Mo’tazed al-Islam mockingly called Vaziri’s music school a tar-playing school and called tar playing something outside of religion, the words of a person who appeared on the Islamic Republic of Iran Broadcasting in the guise of an interpreter of Imam Khomeini’s (ra) eight-point message caused Mr. Mo’tazed al-Islam to be justified in the eyes of history.</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Imam Khomeini (ra), in his eight-point message, which was a worthy and important decree in those years, considered the private sphere and homes of citizens as a secure sanctuary and considered intrusion into it contrary to Sharia and law. The aforementioned interpreter, in guiding the agents and implementers of the law, interpreted the Imam’s message as follows: ‘If you enter someone’s house by law and for a specific matter and see something outside the scope of your mission in that house, do not intrude. If there is a tar or violin there, do not break it, but woe to the one who plays the tar!’” (Pahlevan-e Tar: 99–101).</p>\n\n<p>Based on what we said, the discussions that writers such as Ali Naqi Vaziri (in the book Avaz Shenasi), Ruhollah Khaleqi (in the book A View on Music), Morteza Hannaneh (in the book The Lost Scales), Dariush Talaei (in the book A New Look at the Theory of Iranian Music), Neriman Hojjati (in the book Theoretical Foundations of Iranian Music), Farhad Fakhreddini (in the book Analysis and Explanation of the Iranian Music Radif), Khosrow Jafarzadeh (in the book Iranian Music Studies), and ... have brought in their theoretical books should not be considered a “theory” but a “hypothesis.”</p>\n\n<p>It is natural and obvious that if, for example, Ali Naqi Vaziri’s opinions had turned into a “theory” in its scientific and real sense, there would no longer be any need to write any book or treatise on this matter except for writing articles and raising issues to complete, explain, and interpret the same theory of Vaziri. Entering into a meticulous discussion of the opinions raised in the hypotheses of these writers is not our agenda here, but we recommend studying these books to students of music in order to become familiar with the viewpoints of their authors.</p>\n\n<h3>Dastgah and Gusheh</h3>\n\n<p>Dastgah in Iranian music is a totality encompassing different and multiple components. In modern texts and in the view of musicians, the components of the dastgah, even though they have significant differences in terms of importance, position, and quantity of melody, are called “gusheh”:</p>\n\n<p>“These gushehs have a specific meter and among them, the following gushehs also have the title of reng: zarb-e osul, shahr ashub, the last two gushehs of the dastgah of Shur” (The Vocal Radif of Traditional Iranian Music according to Mahmoud Karimi, First Book, Transcription and Analysis by Mohammad Taghi Massoudieh: 41).</p>\n\n<p>Dr. Mohammad Taghi Massoudieh, based on the above phrases, considers the reng-e Shahr Ashub of the dastgah of Shur, which is a metrical piece and circulates in most frameworks of the dastgah of Shur and whose performance takes about sixteen minutes, as a gusheh.</p>\n\n<p>On this basis, if we refer to the radif books, we will see that each of the components of the dastgah (the correct performance is to write everywhere “dastgahs and avazes,” but here for the sake of brevity, and with tolerance and leniency, we usually omit “avazes”), with any quality and importance, has taken a place in the list of names. For example, in Payvar’s radif, the list of gushehs of the dastgah of Mahur is as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Mahur 174–156</p>\n\n<p>Name of gusheh   Page number</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad    156</p>\n\n<p>Second Daramad   156</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab    157</p>\n\n<p>Gosha’esh     160</p>\n\n<p>Dad Afarid     161</p>\n\n<p>Sarbang      162</p>\n\n<p>[Panjeh] Muye    162</p>\n\n<p>Chaharbagh ...    162</p>\n\n<p>Nasir Khani     164</p>\n\n<p>Tahrir-e Haj Hosseini  164</p>\n\n<p>Neyshaburak     165</p>\n\n<p>Neyriz       166</p>\n\n<p>Tak-e Moghaddam   167</p>\n\n<p>Bal-e Kabutar     167</p>\n\n<p>and ...       and ...</p>\n\n<p>In this way, from Faramarz Payvar’s point of view, “Bal-e Kabutar,” which is a short, two-note figure with a duration of about two seconds, is also considered a gusheh. Compare this Bal-e Kabutar with the same reng-e Shahr Ashub of the dastgah of Shur, or compare it with the starting frameworks of the dastgahs (daramads or head maqams), in which one can perform various types of avazes, zarbis, etc., for a long time.</p>\n\n<p>The latter components, i.e., the head maqams, cannot even be considered the same as examples like Shahr Ashub because they have such importance that the names of the dastgahs are derived from the names of these very ones, and the foundation of the dastgah rests on the shoulders of these very maqams.</p>\n\n<p>In Ma’rufi’s radif, the components forming the dastgah of Homayun have been counted as follows: “1. Chaharmezrab, 2. Daramad; 3. Kereshmeh; 4. Zang-e Shotor; 5. Gusheh of Chahargah; 6. Mowalian; 7. Chakavak; 8. Naghmeh-e Chakavak; 9. Forud; 10. Bidad; 11. Forud; 12. Bidad, another part; 13. Bidad, another part; 14. Ney Davud; 15. Bavi; 16. Chaharmezrab; 17. Abolchap; 18. Ravandi; 19. Moreh; 20. ...”.</p>\n\n<p>We see that in counting the sub-components of this dastgah, equal treatment has been given to important maqams such as Homayun (daramad) and Bidad, and small components like “Moreh,” which has a “one-note” content.</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, not all elements forming the dastgah can be considered equal, and we inevitably must separate them from each other and classify them in a principled way. For example, like this:</p>\n\n<p>1. Maqams or main gushehs, which have a special and unique sonic framework, and the performance of any content in their framework is possible:</p>\n\n<p>a. Avaz (non-metrical and with any type of special timing of this “genre,” with any type of lyrics, with wordless instrument, and with any amount of duration),</p>\n\n<p>Whenever a player, without the accompaniment of a singer, performs this genre, he is engaged in “avaz” playing. Avaz, besides being one of the “genres” or “types” of music, also has other concepts in Iranian music.</p>\n\n<p>b. Pishdaramad (with any meter and tempo and with a duration appropriate to this “genre”),</p>\n\n<p>Pishdaramads have a duration of approximately 2 to 5 minutes. The longest of them are the works of Rokn al-Din Mokhtari.</p>\n\n<p>c. Zarbi (with any meter and tempo, whether wordless or with lyrics, and with any amount of duration),</p>\n\n<p>d. Chaharmezrab (with any tempo and with a duration appropriate to this “genre”),</p>\n\n<p>The radif chaharmezrabs are usually short, and the chaharmezrabs of today’s composers have a duration of approximately 2 to 4 minutes and are shorter than pishdaramads.</p>\n\n<p>e. Reng (with any tempo and with a duration appropriate to this “genre”),</p>\n\n<p>The duration of non-radif rengs is less than all other types (approximately between 50 to 120 seconds), but the radif rengs, especially the reng-e Shahr Ashub of different dastgahs, are the longest pieces of Iranian music. Performing the reng-e Shahr Ashub of the dastgah of Shur takes about sixteen minutes.</p>\n\n<p>f. Tasnif (with any meter and tempo, with any type of lyrics, and with a duration appropriate to this “genre”),</p>\n\n<p>The duration of a tasnif, without wordless answers in the middle of its poems, is something around one to four minutes. Kār o amal is a type of long tasnif, which we will explain in another place.</p>\n\n<p>g. Kār o amal (with any meter and tempo, with any type of lyrics, and with any amount of duration),</p>\n\n<p>h. Any traditional, modern, new, ... piece.</p>\n\n<p>In fact, every maqam is like a potentially ready land for construction and, of course, with a “specified area” that is ready to accept any type of application and can accept any type of “construction” in its framework to become active and applicable. In other words, every maqam is comparable to land on which one can build a school, restaurant, house, swimming pool, tower and skyscraper, or fruit market or anything else.</p>\n\n<p>2. Metrical instrumental gushehs such as pishdaramad, chaharmezrab, reng, and zarbi, which can appear in the framework of any maqam and, in terms of sonic framework, be clothed in the garment of that same framework.</p>\n\n<p>These forms can also be with lyrics, and in this case, they are considered “tasnif.” Like the famous pishdaramad of Bayat-e Esfahan by Morteza Ney Davud, for which they wrote poetry and performed it in the form of a tasnif (Mah-e Man, Shah-e Man, rahmat kon be hal-e zar-e man...). Or Ali Akbar Shahnazi’s reng-e Dashti, which just a few years ago Sadiq Define sang it with poetry. In the old tradition of tasnif composing, including in the tasnifs that Abolqasem Aref Qazvini composed, they usually added a part with a faster rhythm and in the form of “tasnif-reng” at the end of the tasnifs.</p>\n\n<p>If one day that academy and cultural center we mentioned earlier is established, one of the main duties of its members can be to classify and name the various types of tasnif and, of course, the various types of pishdaramads, chaharmezrabs, and new pieces in specific “genres.” Today, any type of metrical melody with lyrics, without considering what concepts are used in its poetry, what type of poetry it has, what its meter is, how long it is, etc., is called a tasnif.</p>\n\n<p>As Abdollah Davami has named 186 of them, without any uniformity or consistency being seen in them, in a general classification “tasnif” (Vocal Radif and Old Tasnifs: 165–453). Now pay attention to this definition of taraneh, one of the genres of maqam music, that Abd al-Qadir Maraghi described its features with what precision about six centuries ago:</p>\n\n<p>“But taraneh, and taraneh is made on a poem that is in the ruba’i meter, whether Arabic or Persian, and on the same rhythmic cycle that qawl and ghazal are composed on. And if it is in a cycle that has sixteen naqras, like thaqil al-awwal or thaqil al-thani, the entry of the taraneh should be from the seventh, and if it is in a cycle that has twenty-four naqras, like thaqil al-ramal, the entry of the taraneh should be chosen from the ninth.</p>\n\n<p>The ratio of the numbers of naqras of that cycle with the entry of the taraneh should be double and seventh, and in the entry of other tasnifs, no condition has been made; they can enter from any number they want, and in the middle of the house and the return of the taraneh, the condition is that the entry should be in accordance with the entry method” (Jame‘ al-Alhan: 242 and 243).</p>\n\n<p>3. Metrical and potentially metrical instrumental-vocal gushehs such as zanguleh, basteh negār, naghmeh, and ....</p>\n\n<p>All of these, except for tasnif and kār o amal, can also be performed with an instrument and without lyrics and still retain their title. Performing tasnif and kār o amal instrumentally can be confused with types such as pishdaramad and .... Unless in the performance of famous tasnifs that are completely familiar to the listener. Like the tasnif “Morgh-e Sahar” and .... In the old tradition of tasnif composing, including in the tasnifs composed by Abolqasem Aref Qazvini, they usually added a part with a faster rhythm and in the form of “tasnif-reng” at the end of the tasnifs.</p>\n\n<p>4. Instrumental-vocal metrical and potentially metrical gushehs such as zanguleh, basteh negār, naghmeh, and ....</p>\n\n<p>5. Movements and figures that have a very short duration and there is doubt about their being a “melody.” Such as moreh and bal-e kabutar.</p>\n\n<h4>Maqam (Main Gusheh)</h4>\n\n<p>Earlier we said that every maqam, potentially, requires its own special pleasant framework. Up to this point, with the existence of this sonic framework, we have a barren land in our hands whose position, boundaries, and area are known. If we want such a framework to, so to speak, “sound” and become developed, observing two other conditions is necessary. So we collect the necessary cases for the “sounding” of a maqam as follows:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Creating a sonic framework,</li>\n  <li>Choosing the axial sound,</li>\n  <li>Creating an appropriate and pleasant melodic movement.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Ali Naqi Vaziri called the “axial” sound “witness” in Avaz Shenasi and of course mentioned the term axial once or twice there as well. However, here we preferred to use the word axial because, in comparison with witness, it has a stronger weight, and if we want to mention its neighboring notes as well, we can use the terms “pre-axial” and “post-axial,” which are naturally more pleasant-sounding than “pre-witness” and “post-witness.”</p>\n\n<p>The axial sound is the same sound that is used the most in any melody and is more important than the other notes of the melody and, in most cases, is also the stopping and resting point of the melody. If we count the number of notes of any melody and divide the result by the number of notes, i.e., the number “seven,” the share of the axial sound will be much more than one-seventh of the total notes of that melody.</p>\n\n<p>Here we must remind and emphasize this point that the starting point in performing maqams and melodies is not necessarily the axial note.</p>\n\n<p>Melodic movement means how to move in the sonic framework, how the musical rise and fall is, or how the movement of the notes is. It is obvious that the adjectives “appropriate” and “pleasant” are completely relative concepts, and a concept like pleasant can be related to taste and based on the auditory habits of every nation and people. Therefore, here it is necessary to complete the third factor and express it more precisely:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Creating a melodic movement appropriate to the nature of Iranian music and the auditory taste of Iranians.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>But perhaps this question is raised: what kind of movements are these and of what nature? The answer is very easy and can be something like this:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Movements of the nature of mothers’ lullabies and ...,</li>\n  <li>Movements of the nature of monājāt and ...,</li>\n  <li>Movements of the nature of the avazes of night-wandering street singers and ...,</li>\n  <li>Movements of the nature of the avazes of Banan and Shajarian and ...,</li>\n  <li>Movements of the nature of the playing of Shahnaz and Lotfi and ....</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Now let us review some of the maqams of today’s music, citing that the ancients composed tasnifs and pieces in these fields, without any order or prioritization:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Maqam of Shur (and Rahab),</li>\n  <li>Maqam of Abu Ata,</li>\n  <li>Maqam of Bayat-e Tork,</li>\n  <li>Maqam of Segah,</li>\n  <li>Maqam of Chahargah,</li>\n  <li>Maqam of Homayun,</li>\n  <li>Maqam of Bidad,</li>\n  <li>Maqam of Shushtari,</li>\n  <li>Maqam of Iraq (old and new),</li>\n  <li>Maqam of Zabol-e Chahargahi (open Zabol),</li>\n  <li>Maqams of Afshari and Nava,</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>The maqams that we have named next to each other have the same sonic framework and axial note, and their difference is in some movements and some other points. For example, the greatest difference between the maqams of Nava and Afshari is related to the existence of a variable fret in Afshari.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Maqams of Mahur and Rast,</li>\n  <li>Maqams of Dashti, Hejaz, and Bayat-e Kord,</li>\n  <li>Maqams of old Bayat-e Esfahan<sup>5</sup> and Mokhalef-e Segahi (closed Mokhalef),</li>\n  <li>Maqams of new Bayat-e Esfahan<sup>6</sup> and Rak,</li>\n  <li>Maqams of Zabol-e Segahi (closed Zabol), Ouj, and Shahnaz,</li>\n  <li>Maqams of Naheft and Khavaran.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>From the gathering of several maqams — and a number of gushehs — a dastgah is formed. The number of maqams used in each dastgah depends on the extent that the narrators have considered for that dastgah.</p>\n\n<p>Usually, most musicians, based on their attachment to symbolic numbers and considering the number of old maqams, consider the number of dastgahs of music to be twelve and classify them into two groups of seven and five:</p>\n\n<p>a. Main dastgahs:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>Dastgah of Shur,</li>\n  <li>Dastgah of Segah,</li>\n  <li>Dastgah of Chahargah,</li>\n  <li>Dastgah of Mahur,</li>\n  <li>Dastgah of Homayun,</li>\n  <li>Dastgah of Nava,</li>\n  <li>Dastgah of Rast Panjgah.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>b. Secondary dastgahs or “avazes”:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>Abu Ata (dependent on the dastgah of Shur),</li>\n  <li>Bayat-e Tork (dependent on the dastgah of Shur),</li>\n  <li>Afshari (dependent on the dastgah of Shur),</li>\n  <li>Dashti (dependent on the dastgah of Shur),</li>\n  <li>Bayat-e Esfahan (dependent on the dastgah of Homayun).</li>\n</ol>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the concept of dastgah in Iranian music, please share them in the comments section of the article with us to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-08-15 20:33:41', 0, '2024-09-18 22:37:33', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1499, 'posts', 42, 'en', NULL, 'What does a phrase mean in music? 4 important methods for constructing musical phrases', NULL, 'This article explains the concept of a musical phrase as a complete musical idea that ends with a cadence, either final or temporary. A phrase continues until it reaches a point of rest or resolution, often referred to as a cadence. Cadences play a key role in shaping musical structure, with different types serving different purposes—some create a final ending, while others produce a temporary pause or a sense of expectation.\n\nThe most important type is the perfect cadence, formed by the progression from the dominant (V) to the tonic (I), which typically provides a strong and conclusive ending. This cadence can appear in two forms depending on the placement of the tonic note in the voices.\n\nThe article also discusses how musical phrases are constructed. They are generally formed through motifs and their variations, combining motifs with freer musical material, using multiple distinct motifs, or through free movement of notes without repetition. These methods have been widely used by composers throughout history to create both simple and complex musical phrases.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the concept of <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">phrase</span></strong> in music. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>The Concept of Phrase in Music</h2>\n\n<p>A musical phrase, or Phrase, refers to a completed idea that ends with a cadence, which may be perfect or imperfect. A musical phrase continues until it reaches a melodic stopping point and the music acquires a state of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%af-%d8%af%d8%b1-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">forud</a> or conclusion (perfect or imperfect). This definition may be somewhat incomplete. The correct answer can be reached when this question is answered:</p>\n\n<p>To end musical sentences or create a temporary stop, and in any case to achieve a definite or temporary conclusion of musical pieces or sentences, cadences are used. <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%a9%d8%a7%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%86%d8%b3/\">Cadence</a> is used in various types and for different purposes. For example, some cadences create a definite ending, while others only create a temporary ending, and some, while stopping on a specific <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%da%a9%d9%88%d8%b1%d8%af/\">chord</a>, create a state of expectation.</p>\n\n<p>The strongest and most important cadence, which is used to create a definite ending at the end of musical pieces or musical sentences and almost all musical works end with it, is the perfect authentic cadence, which arises from the progression of degree V to I (both in root position). To achieve a stronger ending and create a background for making the perfect authentic cadence more effective, other degrees such as degree II or degree IV are placed before degree V.</p>\n\n<p>The perfect authentic cadence can be used in two forms:</p>\n\n<p>If in the final chord, i.e., the chord of degree I, the root of the chord (the tonic note of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">scale</a>) is placed in the bass and soprano parts, it is called a perfect authentic cadence (perfect authentic). If the root of the degree I chord is in the bass and one of the third or fifth notes is heard in the soprano part, it is simply called a perfect cadence (imperfect authentic).</p>\n\n<h2>What Factors Create a Musical Phrase?</h2>\n\n<p>A musical phrase, whether simple or complex, short or long, is constructed and expanded in 4 ways, which are:</p>\n\n<p>1) Through a <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%aa%db%8c%d9%81-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">motif</a> and its variations (motif changes)</p>\n\n<p>2) Through a motif and its variations plus free material</p>\n\n<p>3) Through the use of two motifs: In this method, in order for the motifs to be completely separated and identifiable from each other and not confused with one another, composers usually repeat the first motif before the entry of the second motif and take this method to stabilize it. They also repeat the second motif to stabilize it.</p>\n\n<p>4) Through the free movement of notes without repeating a motif: These kinds of sentences, in which the motif is not repeated and are formed based on the free movement of notes and motifs, have been of interest to <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d8%b2%d9%86%d8%af%da%af%db%8c%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%85%d9%87-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%86/\">composers</a> from ancient times until today.</p>', '2023-08-16 03:35:56', 0, '2024-09-18 22:34:49', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1500, 'posts', 43, 'en', NULL, 'What is a period in music? 3 commonly used periods in music', NULL, 'This article explains the concept of a musical period, defined as a group of two or more musical phrases that create a question-and-answer relationship. The most common type is the two-phrase period, where the first phrase functions as a question and typically ends with an incomplete cadence, leaving a sense of suspension. The second phrase acts as an answer and concludes with a perfect cadence, providing a sense of resolution and completeness.\n\nTwo-phrase periods generally appear in three main forms:\n\nParallel period: The second phrase begins similarly to the first but diverges and leads to a conclusive ending.\nSequential period: The second phrase repeats the melodic idea of the first at a different pitch level, sometimes resulting in modulation.\nContrasting period: The second phrase introduces new melodic material, though it may still incorporate motifs from the first phrase.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the concept of <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">period</span></strong> in music. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>The Concept of Period in Music</h2>\n\n<p>A group of two or more <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ac%d9%85%d9%84%d9%87/\">musical sentences</a> in which the sentences have a question-and-answer character is called a period.</p>\n\n<p>Among musical periods, two-sentence periods are more common and prevalent, in which the first sentence has a question character and the second sentence has an answer character. The first sentence often ends with a cadence other than a <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%a9%d8%a7%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%86%d8%b3/\">perfect authentic cadence</a>, and this very fact causes an unfinished state to remain in the first sentence. When the second sentence ends with a perfect authentic cadence, the effect of the period’s ending and completion is felt.</p>\n\n<p>Periods composed of two sentences can have three standard forms:</p>\n\n<p>1) Periods made of two parallel sentences: In this type of period, the beginning of the second sentence is similar to the first sentence, but later it changes and moves toward <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%af-%d8%af%d8%b1-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">forud</a> and the performance of a perfect authentic cadence so that the period is completed and the feeling of ending is conveyed.</p>\n\n<p>2) Periods made from sequential repetition of the second sentence: In this type of period, the second sentence usually begins with the same melodic elements as the first sentence but in a different degree of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">tonality</a>. Sometimes the repetition of the melody from another degree may cause modulation.</p>\n\n<p>3) A period made of two sentences that are different in terms of melodic movement: In these kinds of periods, the second sentence is not a repetition of the first sentence and new material is used, although some <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%aa%db%8c%d9%81-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">motifs</a> from the first sentence may also be used in the second sentence.</p>', '2023-08-17 21:03:57', 0, '2025-02-14 00:58:47', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1501, 'posts', 44, 'en', NULL, 'What is the concept of forud in Iranian music? What are the similarities and differences between forud and cadence?', NULL, 'This article explores the concept of “Forud” in Persian music, which refers to the return from various melodic areas back to the main tonal center or mode. It is a fundamental structural element in the Iranian modal system, helping to unify and stabilize the identity of a musical piece through repeated returns to the main mode.\n\nForud is comparable to the concept of cadence in Western music and includes different types such as complete, incomplete, and complementary cadences. Related ideas like “half-cadence” (temporary resting on non-tonic notes) and “full cadence” (return to the tonic) are also discussed. Beyond providing closure, forud plays roles in creating anticipation, connecting sections, and preparing the listener for transitions.\n\nIn the radif (the traditional repertoire of Persian music), most melodic sections (gushehs) include some form of forud, even if not always explicitly labeled. In some cases, instead of returning directly to the main mode, the music transitions to another melodic area. Various terms such as complementary forud, inverse forud, and “forud-avar” are used to describe different types of melodic returns and phrase endings.\n\nOverall, forud is a key expressive and structural device that strengthens the connection between performer and listener, and mastering it is especially important for vocalists in Persian music.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the term <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">forud</span></strong> in Iranian music and become familiar with its application and the way this concept is used in the radif of dastgah music. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>Application of Forud in Iranian Music</h2>\n\n<p>In the course of a dastgah, after performing the mother maqam and entering the subsequent maqams, in most sections a return to the framework of the mother maqam occurs, which is called <strong>forud</strong>. It is because of these repeated returns to the first maqam that the foundation of the dastgah can be justified. Forud means descending, and therefore by applying forud from a sonic region, at any height, we return to a lower sonic region. The lower region can be the origin of the dastgah, or a region in the middle of the dastgah, in which case, according to radif tradition, the framework to which the return is made must be announced.</p>\n\n<p>For example, if a return is made from the ouj region of the dastgah of Segah to a lower region, but not to the mother maqam, the phrase “forud to Mokhalef” or “forud to …” will be announced, but when the goal is to return to the starting point and the head maqam of the dastgah, the announcement of forud will be made alone and without announcing the framework of the head maqam, in which case the point of return will be the maqam of Segah itself. At the end of each dastgah, of course only in instrumental radifs, its rengs are played. Therefore, vocal radifs lack <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%90%d9%86%da%af/\">reng</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>Forud or Cadence</h2>\n\n<p>It is the ending or returning element to the tonic or origin of the scale or maqam in which we are located.</p>\n\n<p>Forud exists in all musics.</p>\n\n<p>In Western music, it is called <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%a9%d8%a7%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%86%d8%b3/\">cadence</a>, for which we have different categories such as perfect cadence, imperfect cadence, etc.</p>\n\n<p>An imperfect cadence creates a state of expectation, and then we complete the piece with a perfect cadence.</p>\n\n<p>Forud in Iranian music is divided into three types: imperfect, complementary, and perfect.</p>\n\n<p>Most gushehs of the Iranian music radif have these foruds.</p>\n\n<p>However, in some gushehs, because they are parts of other gushehs of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%af%db%8c%d9%81/\">radif</a>, we may not see these foruds and instead have a reference or transition to a new gusheh or category in the radif.</p>\n\n<p>For example, in the gusheh of Golriz in Mirza Abdollah’s radif, instead of forud to the daramad (the open G string, second pair), we go to the gusheh or maqam of Hosseini (the G note, eleventh fret, first pair on 28-fret tars). Therefore, there is no forud state in it.</p>\n\n<p>After Golriz, we have the gusheh of Majles Afrouz, which is located in the same Hosseini region, and its forud is again on Hosseini (the G note, eleventh fret, first pair on 28-fret tars).</p>\n\n<p>In Ozzal, which is in the middle frets of the second string (the Khara section), we stop on the G note in the middle of the neck, first pair of the tar, which is in fact the same G note, open second pair, one octave higher.</p>\n\n<p>In the gusheh of Sayakhi from the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%b9%d8%b7%d8%a7/\">avaz of Abu Ata in Mirza Abdollah’s radif</a>, instead of forud on Shur or stopping in Abu Ata, by a passing glissando we are placed in the Hejaz region.</p>\n\n<p>In the gusheh of Ozzal Hosseini in Abdollah Davami’s radif, instead of returning to Shur on D, we stop on the higher octave D, and after that the gusheh of Dobeyti, which is located in this region, is performed.</p>\n\n<p>Many foruds are for preparing the listener for the beginning of a new region, gusheh, or a new start, and most of the time this happens in the radif.</p>\n\n<p>Dr. Massoudieh says in the book of Mahmoud Karimi’s radif:</p>\n\n<p>“Forud is like a refrain that lacks a complete text or complete gusheh, but completes the text or melody of the relevant gusheh.”</p>\n\n<p>Forud is one of the links between the performer and the listener (the harmonizer of the listener’s and the artist’s feeling).</p>\n\n<p>In maqam music, the term “mohet” was used to express forud.</p>\n\n<p>For example, in the description of Maraghi’s Advār it is stated:</p>\n\n<p>“Chahargah that descends to Dogah is called rakb.” (A chahargah that descends to dogah is called rakb.)</p>\n\n<p>Ending the singing in tasnifs and zarbi pieces that had an avazi character was called forudasht.</p>\n\n<p>One of the basic issues in the radif was teaching forud to singers, because naturally when a singer went to a higher region, he had to be able to return us with great mastery over his voice to the daramad of the dastgah or the main base of the avaz or dastgah collection, or bring us down.</p>\n\n<p>In singers who have an amateur or non-professional character, we see that when they change a gusheh — for example, they go to Hesar of Chahargah — they encounter many problems in the returns and may not return to the main chahargah or many singers may mistake the dastgah or maqam and descend somewhere else.</p>\n\n<p>Although in most gushehs we do not have this title of forud, we see that part of every gusheh is usually dedicated to this forud in the last few phrases.</p>\n\n<p>In the radif, sometimes other words are seen along with forud. For example, in Musa Khan Ma’rufi’s radif, we have “forud to Shushtari.” Although Shushtari is part of the dastgah of Homayun, when we go to the ouj of Shushtari or, for example, we develop Shushtari and move to the lower dangs, in the return we have forud to Shushtari.</p>\n\n<p>In Ma’rufi’s radif, we also have other foruds, which are:</p>\n\n<p>- Forud to Parvaneh - Forud to Naghmeh - Forud-e Tarz</p>\n\n<p>- Forud-e Hesar - Forud-e Muye - Forud-e Mansouri</p>\n\n<p>- Forud-e Buselik - Forud-e Motammam - Forud-e Gerili</p>\n\n<p>- Forud to Shur - Forud to Kord</p>\n\n<p>Although forud is not an independent gusheh, sometimes gushehs are seen in the radif as forud, which are usually in parts where we had development in a section of the radif, and we consider a complete forud or a forud that can be similar to a gusheh and has a coherent structure as forud.</p>\n\n<p>Every gusheh has an opening daramad and a forud at the end.</p>\n\n<p>In harmony and the science of consonance, we also have specific foruds to separate the members of musical sentences from each other, which are formed by these foruds or cadences.</p>\n\n<p>The types of forud in the science of consonance are:</p>\n\n<p>1) Completing the sentence: finishing the sentence.</p>\n\n<p>2) Foruds that are used as separators between two musical sentences.</p>\n\n<p>3) Foruds that create a state of expectation to guide us to the next section of the music.</p>\n\n<p>Good combinations in Western music are the tonic and fifth of the scale, especially in the second inversion, which make the creation of forud more evident to the listener.</p>\n\n<h2>Half Forud and Complete Forud</h2>\n\n<p>Ali Naqi Vaziri, in his book Avaz Shenasi, mentions half forud and complete forud.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Half Forud</strong>: Foruds that land on notes other than the main scale or the main note of the dastgah are called half forud. For example, in some gushehs of the avaz of Abu Ata, we may land on the “E koron” note, or in a dastgah or avaz whose witness or stopping note is “D,” we stop on the “G” note.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Complete Forud</strong>: Foruds that land on the tonic of the dastgah.</p>\n\n<p>One of the notable examples for half forud is the gusheh of Qaracheh in Abdollah Davami’s radif, in which in Shur on D we perform complete stops on the D note, and at the end, Qaracheh stops on the F note.</p>\n\n<p>In the performance of some singers, we see these states that are a special artistry that can place us on a note other than the main note of the dastgah and create the feeling of temporary forud or half forud, in which sometimes a state of expectation occurs.</p>\n\n<p>In the radif music of every dastgah or avaz, there are gushehs that have an ascending role (changing the sonic range from the low part to the high part). For example, if the daramad of the dastgah is on the “G” note, in one gusheh we are on “A,” and in one gusheh on “B,” and so on until the end, but finally we try to return to the daramad of the main maqam or base to remind.</p>\n\n<p>We can see this state in most avazes and gushehs. For example, when we go to the Naheft of Nava (the D note), at the end we may return as the ending section of Nava to the daramad (the G note).</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, forud in a dastgah that has many gushehs may be repeated several times and appear, and take on the role of the refrain of the dastgah collection.</p>\n\n<p>Forsat Shirazi writes in the book Bahur al-Alhan: “Wherever in a dastgah the name forud is mentioned, the intention is that forud and return to the main maqam should occur there.”</p>\n\n<p>Master Abdollah Davami, who used the word “tikeh” instead of gusheh, considered having daramad and forud as the necessary condition for a collection or part of the radif to be a tikeh, and considered two main sections for a tikeh or gusheh.</p>\n\n<p>Sometimes when we have half forud according to Ali Naqi Vaziri or our forud does not occur on the main note of the initial maqam, usually the name of the gusheh on which the forud occurs is mentioned. For example, forud-e Parvaneh in Hedayat’s radif.</p>\n\n<p>But in most gushehs, although forud is used, the name of forud is not mentioned. Usually, the name of forud appears in the radif where we intend to stop more or have more development in the forud section.</p>\n\n<p>Sometimes we also do not place the forud in the main part of the dastgah. For example, in the dastgah of Shur or Nava in Mirza Abdollah’s radif or Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand’s radif, we see that the foruds at the end of the dastgah occur on a note other than the main note of the dastgah, or one octave higher.</p>\n\n<p>Considering that in the dastgah of Shur we also have Shur-e Pa’in Dasteh, in the gusheh of “Shahnaz Kot” our forud is on the G note (middle of the neck, first pair of the tar and setar), which is in fact one octave higher than the daramad.</p>\n\n<p>In the dastgah of Nava, although the last gushehs have the role of moving toward Shur, instead of stopping on the G note in the G of Nava, in a gusheh such as Neyriz we stop on the D note, and the complete forud of the dastgah occurs on the D note.</p>\n\n<p>In the reng-e Nastari as well, we see that we again forud on the D note instead of stopping like Nava itself on the G note.</p>\n\n<p>In some radifs such as Hedayat’s radif, some foruds are named with the gusheh on which we forud. For example, forud-e Tarz, or in the abbreviation that existed for notation so that the previous forud is not repeated, it is written “Buselik – forud – Neyriz – forud-e Buselik” and it is no longer written in the notation, and the same forud that occurred after Buselik is also performed after Neyriz.</p>\n\n<p>The word forud is probably derived from the same word “forudasht” in old Iranian music, which referred to the fourth section of tasnifs or melodies and songs that were sung with poetry and were called “nowbat-e morattab” and were four-part.</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d9%86%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Ali Naqi Vaziri</a>, in his book “Avaz Shenasi-e Qadim,” used the term “foruz.” (In the old days, the final “dals” of words were pronounced as “zal,” and Mr. Cyrus Shamisa writes that every “dal” that had a vowel before it was pronounced as “zal” in the old days, and for this reason, in the old days, rhymes that we see today with “dal” at the end were rhymed with “zal.”)</p>\n\n<p>For example, in Sa’di’s quatrains it is said:</p>\n\n<p>“When he saw his own face in the mirror  \nAnd that mouth and lips and teeth so sweet  \nHe said as much as he could be heard  \nMany souls came to the lips that did not reach this lip.”</p>\n\n<p>In the old days, all “dals” that came at the end of hemistichs were pronounced as “zal.”</p>\n\n<p>Vaziri also used the term “foruz,” which means the same as “forud.” Also, in the radif of “Danesh,” in the dastgah of Mahur, forud is written with “z” as “foruz.”</p>\n\n<p>In some radifs, such as Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s radif, the term “forud-avar” is used, and sometimes the term “forud” is also seen.</p>\n\n<p>Forud-avar: A half-step change of the note downward that causes the state of forud to the main dastgah.</p>\n\n<p>Lotfollah Mofakham Payan, in the radif he has written, has used the term “forud-avar.”</p>\n\n<p>In the radif of Alam al-Soltan and Shahnazi’s list, considering the old pronunciation of “forud,” the term “foruz-avar” has been used.</p>\n\n<p>In some radifs, we also have reverse forud. For example, in Bayat-e Esfahan in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s radif or in Dashti in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s and Mirza Abdollah’s radifs, we see this state, meaning that if we were on the D note (lower part of the neck, first pair of the tar and setar) in Dashti, instead of forud on the G note (middle of the neck, first pair), we forud on the open G of the second pair (one octave lower).</p>\n\n<p>The term “forud-e motammam” or a forud that is finishing or completing is used in Hedayat’s radif or in Abdollah Davami’s radif that Mr. Payvar presented. This term has come, meaning for example in Shur, where we have Shur-e Pa’in Dasteh, at the end in the Shahnaz section we move and finally stop in the main Shur.</p>\n\n<p>In Payvar’s radif, in the dastgah of Shur, a term called “forud-e Aqa Hossein-Qoli” has come, which probably Aqa Hossein-Qoli invented this forud and the phrasing of this forud is from him.</p>\n\n<p>In Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi’s radif of the dastgah of Shur, the term “tahrir-e forud” or “tahrir-e forud-e Shahnaz” has been used, which means the same phrasing that is used for forud.</p>\n\n<p>In Hang Afarin’s radif, the terms complete forud and imperfect forud have been used, which meant the extent and quantity of the melody that was used for forud.</p>\n\n<p>Sometimes we use numbering for foruds. For example, in the dastgah of Shur in Hedayat’s radif, “first forud” and “second forud” have been titled, which means that we had an initial forud that for example occurred to Abu Ata, and the final forud or second forud was to Shur.</p>\n\n<p>In the same radif, in the avaz of Bayat-e Kord section, the term “second forud” is seen, which comes after a forud for which the numbering adjective “first” has been titled. (Kord – Rah-e Rud – forud-e Kord – second forud)</p>\n\n<p>In Forsat al-Dowleh’s radif, the phrase “forud-e se qesm” has come, which is for three different movements.</p>\n\n<p>We see the term “forud-e now’ digar” in Musa Khan Ma’rufi’s radif or in Hedayat’s radif.</p>\n\n<p>The term “forud-e maqabl” is for abbreviation in notation so that the previous forud is not repeated.</p>\n\n<p>In Saba’s radif, a term “forud-e zarbi” has been used, which means that we want to perform the forud with a zarbi and no avazi or non-rhythmic state is seen in it.</p>\n\n<p>Naturally, the names of foruds are seen with different gushehs such as “forud-e pardeh shekasteh” and “forud-e muye” in the radif, or the dastgah is also mentioned, for example “forud to Esfahan” or “forud to Shur.”</p>\n\n<p>The term “forugozasht” has also been used. For example, in Abd al-Qadir Maraghi’s treatise “Kanz al-Sahf,” in the section on the etiquette of gatherings and assemblies, in a recommendation he had for players, he said: “And when a resentment appears in an assembly or a dispute occurs between two people, immediately ‘forugozasht’ should be done.”</p>\n\n<p>Although “forugozasht” can also mean putting the instrument on the ground, it may have been intended that they should immediately forud and finish the music.</p>\n\n<p>The term “forud-avar,” which has been used more in Aqa Hossein-Qoli’s radif and means a half-step and one-quarter-tone (quarter-tone) lower change, is also seen in the avaz of Abu Ata in Mirza Abdollah’s radif. For example, when we play Mahur on “Do,” in a series of foruds we come to the G note and make “Fa” sharp, then perform the forud, or in the “forud-avar” of Shur in Aqa Hossein-Qoli’s radif, after Panjeh-e Muye we see that the “Mi” note has become “bMi,” half a step lower, and at the end it returns to “bMi” again and then foruds to Shur.</p>\n\n<p>In Mirza Abdollah’s radif, in the avaz of Abu Ata, in the gusheh of Chaharpareh, we see that it makes the “bSi” note a quarter-tone lower (“Si koron”), and then returns to “bSi” again, which can play the role of “forud-avar.”</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding this article and the concept of forud in Iranian music, please share them in the comments section of the article with us to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-08-18 18:25:27', 0, '2025-02-14 00:59:08', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1502, 'posts', 45, 'en', NULL, 'What is the form of reng in Iranian music? Which are the important Iranian rengs?', NULL, 'This article explores the musical form of “Reng” in Iranian classical (Dastgāh) music, examining its evolution from older to modern forms.\n\nIn its traditional (old) form, Reng was a rhythmic instrumental piece typically performed at the end of a musical suite (radif). These pieces had various metric structures (such as 2/4, 6/8, and 6/4), and their composers were generally unknown. The term “Reng” is believed to have originated from Indian music and entered Iranian music during the Safavid period.\n\nIn the modern era (late Qajar period onward), with the emergence of formal composition, Reng became more standardized. It is now almost exclusively composed in 6/8 meter, shorter in duration, pre-composed (non-improvisational), and characterized by a lively, dance-like quality. It is typically performed as the closing piece in musical programs.\n\nOverall, Reng has evolved from a diverse and historical form within the radif into a more structured, rhythmic, and concise musical genre, though its use has declined in contemporary performances.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the form of <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">reng</span></strong> in Iranian dastgah music from the perspective of the prominent Iranian musician and radif master, “Arshad Tahmasbi.” Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>Old Reng</h2>\n\n<p>Among the general urban population of Iranians, reng is the most famous instrumental genre because the movements and styles of Persian dance (Tehrani dance) have been shaped in accordance with the six-eight meter of reng. However, the rengs of the Qajar radif have a different discussion, and for this reason, we will examine them under the title “Old Rengs.” The term “reng” is derived from the vocabulary of the Hindi language:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Arshad Tahmasbi: Reng is taken from the Hindi language, which in the form of “Ranga Bhumi” means the place of dance, just as rak is also from the musical terms of India in the form of raga.</p>\n\n<p>This term entered Iranian music approximately from the beginning of the Safavid period along with other words such as dinaseri, rak-e Kashmir, rak-e Hendi, ramkali, and sayakhi, which is the result of musical exchange between two cultures and the travel of musicians of that time to India. Many artists and free-thinkers of the Safavid period migrated from Iran due to lack of social security and pressure from fanatical clerics, and most of them went to India:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Hassan Mashhun: Although the Safavid religious policy was useful and effective in advancing their political goals and caused national unity, it dealt a severe blow to the literature and art of Iranian music.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Mohammad Ebrahim Bastani Parizi: Regarding the situation of musicians in the time of Shah Abbas, Eskandar Beyg Monshi has an interesting discussion. He says: “Because His Majesty the Shah of the Heavenly Abode completely avoided prohibitions, the musicians had no value or credit in the eyes of Sharia, and a group that had the honor of serving the royal court were dismissed [...]</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">In the last days of his life, on the suspicion that perhaps the princes might be inclined to their company, some individuals who were tutors and nannies, by nature accustomed to it, with their presence in the camp might cause inclination and desire for prohibitions, the notables of this class who were in the camp, such as Hafez Ahmad Qazvini — who was unique in singing and famous in the world for twisting the voice and salty singing — and Hafez Laleh Tabrizi and others were expelled from the camp, and Master Hossein Sornayi was imprisoned for several months because he had gone to gatherings, and finally they made him swear that except for the sornay — which he plays in the royal naqqareh khaneh — he should not play anywhere else.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Mohammad Taghi Bahar: Therefore, from the poet to the preacher, writer, horseman, and every artist who could escape from Iran — especially in the early days — headed to India, because the kings of the Timurid dynasty of India considered Iran their homeland and Iranians as fellow citizens and speakers of the same language [...]</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">So in Delhi a larger court was established, which should be called the second court of Iran, or rather the main court of Iran, because the prevalence of the Persian language, literature, and sciences in the Delhi court was more than in the Isfahan court, and apparently in the Isfahan court the king and the special ones of the court spoke in Turkish, but in the Delhi court, the king, the court, and the harem spoke in Persian.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">So many poets went from Iran to India  \nDelhi and Agra became Ray and Isfahan of India (Gholamreza Rashid Yasemi)</p>\n\n<p>Until the tenth century, the term “reng” was not common in Iranian music. For example, in none of Abd al-Qadir Maraghi’s books is there any mention of this genre, and therefore one must guess that this term became common in Iranian music from the beginning of the tenth century. Qazi Samarqandi, in the early Safavid period and during the reign of Shah Tahmasb I (930–984 AH), writes in Javahir al-Ulum Homayuni:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">In explaining the different rengs, know that there are many rengs and they are also called “khalit,” and what is common is this: Dogah, Rast, Sepehri, Negar, Negarinek, Esfahank, Mokhalef, Mokhalefak, Aloudeh Iraq, Chakavak, Nahavand, Bostankar, Asiran-e Ashra, Ziraftand-e Bozorg, Ziraftand-e Kochak, Barbari, Havayi-e Rast, Khori [Khozi].</p>\n\n<p>There is no information about the melodic character and rhythmic framework of any of the rengs of the Safavid period, and therefore two basic questions can be raised about this:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">- Did the characteristics of the “reng” genre in this period have any similarity or connection with the characteristics of the genres common in Maraghi’s period, which were mentioned earlier?</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">- Do the characteristics of the “reng” genre in the radif music of the Qajar period have any similarity or connection with the characteristics of the “reng” genre in the Safavid period?</p>\n\n<p>When the maqam system gave way to the dastgah system, fundamental changes occurred in all areas of music. Among other things, many maqams and shobehs changed position, and at the same time, common genres of music became obsolete and many terms disappeared:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Arshad Tahmasbi: In the time span from the end of the Safavid period to the beginning of the Qajar period, the old maqam system became obsolete and the modern dastgah system replaced it. No one knows what the causes, main factors, and exact time of creating this transformation were, because no report has been obtained about this, and no treatise has been written about it either.</p>\n\n<p>In contrast, new names and new terms entered the dastgah system and gained great importance. For example, the title “Shur,” which had no precedent in maqam music, found a special place as the name of one of the dastgahs. If we consider the time of the establishment of the dastgah system as the end of the Safavid period, from about the years 1110 to 1292 SH, that is, in a time span of one hundred and eighty years, no valid and reliable treatise about the nature of the new music was written; until in the last years of the Qajar period, Forsat Shirazi (Forsat al-Dowleh) compiled the book “Bahur al-Alhan”:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Arshad Tahmasbi: Forsat Shirazi, taking advantage of Mehdi Solhi’s information, drew seven house-by-house tables in Bahur al-Alhan and registered in them the names of the components of the seven dastgahs of Mirza Abdollah’s time, and according to him. It is here that for the first time, in addition to the names of old and new avazes, we encounter terms that are new and are repeated in almost all dastgahs. Among them: daramad, naghmeh, forud, chaharmezrab, reng, and ....</p>\n\n<p>Until the end of the Qajar period, composing in today’s sense was not common, and the earlier musicians taught what they had learned from their masters in a collection called the “music radif” to their students. On this basis, we see that at the end of the instrumental radifs, metrical pieces are placed whose time and creators are unknown. It should be noted that in the Qajar musical tradition, all components of a dastgah, whatever genre they were in, were called gusheh:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Arshad Tahmasbi: Dastgah in Iranian music is a totality encompassing different and multiple components. In modern texts and in the view of musicians, the components of the dastgah, even though they have significant differences in terms of importance, position, and quantity of melody, are called “gusheh.”</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Mohammad Taghi Massoudieh considers the rengs of the dastgah of Shur as part of the “gushehs” of this dastgah:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">These gushehs have a specific meter [and] among them, the following gushehs also have the title of reng: zarb-e osul, shahr ashub, the last two gushehs of the dastgah of Shur.</p>\n\n<p>Such gushehs are always metrical and appear at the end of the dastgahs after the completion of the instrumental avaz gushehs:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Forsat Shirazi: And after mentioning [the avaz gushehs] of each dastgah, we will place the melodies of that dastgah, which are called reng, at the bottom of the tables.</p>\n\n<p>The short and eloquent phrase below expresses the position of chaharmezrab and reng in the dastgahs of the radif:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Mehdi Qoli Hedayat: Chaharmezrab is a reng-like piece during the avaz for amusement.</p>\n\n<p>The rengs of the radif have four common features:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>They are only used in instrumental radifs;</li>\n  <li>All of them are played after the completion of the avaz gushehs of the dastgahs;</li>\n  <li>All of them are metrical;</li>\n  <li>Their creators are unknown.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>The most important point about these rengs, however, is their difference, because every type of metrical melody, with any type of structure, that has come at the end of the radif has taken the general title of reng. All these radif rengs have been made in three types of meter:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>In the framework of “two-four” meter, such as the reng-e Harbi of the dastgah of Mahur, the reng-e Delgosha of the dastgah of Segah, and ...</li>\n  <li>In the framework of “six-eight” meter, such as the reng-e Shahr Ashub of the dastgahs of Shur and Chahargah, and ...</li>\n  <li>In the framework of “six-four” meter, such as the reng-e Farah of the dastgah of Homayun, the reng-e Nastari of the dastgah of Nava, and ...</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>We said that the radif rengs are performed at the end of the avaz gushehs, but there is a reng titled “Kuraoghli” in the dastgah of Mahur of Mirza Abdollah’s radif, according to Mehdi Solhi and written by Mehdi Qoli Hedayat, which exceptionally is also played at the beginning of the dastgah, and of course no justification has been given for the reason for its dual position in the radif. Hedayat has narrated this reng, like the other rengs, at the end of the dastgah, but at the top of it he has written:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Kuraoghli [or Kuraoghli], which is also played instead of pishdaramad.</p>\n\n<p>On this basis, Musa Ma’rufi has placed Kuraoghli at the beginning of the dastgah:</p>\n\n<p>At the beginning of this same discussion, we said that several names of the radif, including the term “reng” itself, have been taken from Indian music terms. Several names are also seen in the radif music related to the music of the Caucasus, which was once part of the territory of Iran, one of which is also Kuraoghli:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Arshad Tahmasbi: Its rhythm and movement are reminiscent of the fast rengs of the Caucasus region, and its title is also Turkish. Kuraoghli means the son of a blind man (a son whose father is blind) and is the name of a legendary hero in Caucasian literature. He is a fighter who fights against injustices, and the story of his life and the description of his struggles has caused the creation of many works in the literature and music of that region. In Azerbaijan, the ashughs, as popular and itinerant musicians, have always been the narrators of the story of Kuraoghli’s life [...]</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Its entry into our music was probably in the Safavid period, and from the poem of Kokabi Georgian, it is clear that Kuraoghli, like “Versaqi,” was a musical form and framework, not a specific melody:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">They call Kuraoghli Turkish  \nMeaning that they have the instrument of war</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">But Versaqi is from the words of the Turks  \nIt is the definition of feast and the pleasure of the beautiful (Kokabi Georgian)</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">In our music, in addition to Kuraoghli, the following words and combinations are related and attributed to the “Turks” and the Caucasus region: Otayli, Afshari-e Turki, Afshari-e Qarabaghi, Bayat-e Turk, Bayat-e Shiraz, Daghestani, Segah-e Qafqaz, Shekasteh Qarabaghi, Shalakh, Qaradaghli, Qarabaghi, Gerili, Yetim Segah.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Jahangir Nasri Ashrafi: Some consider Kuraoghli a character arising from the beliefs and culture of the Turkmens of Iran and have mentioned the original word as “Guraoghli,” meaning a son who has risen and been born from the grave.</p>\n\n<h3>Tempo and Duration of Radif Rengs</h3>\n\n<p>The amount of tempo of rengs in musical custom depends on their rhythmic framework, so much so that the performers of the radif have also played the “six-eight” rengs at a moderate speed and the “six-four” rengs at a slow speed. However, this rule has not been followed everywhere. For example, Nour-Ali Boroumand played the reng-e Harbi, which is in “two-four” meter, at a tempo of 112 beats per minute, and the reng-e Delgosha, which is in the same meter, at a tempo of 72 beats per minute. It is possible that other masters have performed these same rengs at slower or faster speeds. Therefore, one of the determining factors in this regard is the taste and “personal interpretation” of the performers.</p>\n\n<p>The duration of the radif rengs is also very different. For example, in Mirza Abdollah’s radif, the shortest reng is the reng of the text of the dastgah of Chahargah with a duration of “00:16,” and the longest reng is the reng-e Shahr Ashub of the dastgah of Shur with a duration of “13:52.”</p>\n\n<p>At the end of the Qajar period, the scrolls of the instrumental radifs that ended with reng were rolled up. After this period, new radifs were compiled by musicians such as Faramarz Payvar, which lack the final rengs, and the only exception in this regard is the Advanced Course Radif of Ali Akbar Shahnazi, which we will discuss elsewhere. Therefore, the features of the radif rengs can be summarized in these cases:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">- They are old, metrical, instrumental melodies with specific names.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">- The identity of their creators is unknown.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">- They have been made in different meter frameworks and do not have the same tempo and duration.</p>\n\n<h2>New Reng</h2>\n\n<p>From the end of the Qajar period, gradually, composing instrumental pieces became common, and some composers, by working in this field, also gave importance to this type of music. In fact, until before this time, apart from the radif rengs whose creators are unknown and are very old, we only know of the existence of a few instrumental pieces that are the works of Aqa Hossein-Qoli and Hossein Esmaeilzadeh.</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, in the new period, Gholam Hossein Darvish Khan, Rokn al-Din Mokhtari, and Ali Naqi Vaziri should be considered the founders of composing in the dastgah system. All three of these composers, who were also masterful players, almost simultaneously started composing instrumental pieces and left many works.</p>\n\n<p>All the rengs of these masters have been made in a uniform framework and only in “six-eight” meter. Therefore, it should be said that from the beginning of the present century, only one branch of the radif rengs has been able to survive and grow under the same name.</p>\n\n<p>The second branch, i.e., the “six-four” radif rengs — such as Nastari and Farah and .... — became the origin of the development of pishdaramads because undoubtedly Darvish Khan and Mokhtari took inspiration from this same framework in making their new-style pishdaramads; on the other hand, we know that, for example, the “six-four” reng-e Nastari of the radif had a lot of similarity with the new pishdaramads.</p>\n\n<p>The last branch, i.e., the “two-four” melodies, such as Harbi and Delgosha, also lost the title of reng and later continued their life under the title “dozarbi.”</p>\n\n<p>In this way, reng in post-Qajar music became a more regulated genre, and the composers made their rengs only in the “six-eight” meter framework and with a rhythm appropriate to dance (Shateri dance, Tehrani dance). Dance in regions such as Kurdistan and Khorasan and ... has various types, each of which is performed with melodies in different meters, but Tehrani dance is limited only to performing rhythmic movements appropriate to the rhythm of the “six-eight” reng. Ruhollah Khaleqi has said about the types of old dances:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">One of the scholars said that fifty years ago there was a man in Shiraz named Hakim Abolqasem who knew various dances well and taught them to talented students, and he narrated that in the Safavid period a book had been written for Iranian dances, and his teachings are remnants of those same principles.</p>\n\n<p>The pioneering composers in making new rengs are Gholam Hossein Darvish Khan and Rokn al-Din Mokhtari, whom we said earlier were also the innovators of new-style pishdaramads. Of course, there is also one reng left from Aqa Hossein-Qoli, Darvish Khan’s teacher, which he composed inspired by a theme from the reng-e Shahr Ashub of the radif of the dastgah of Shur, and therefore his reng should be considered a work within the framework of radif rengs.</p>\n\n<p>Twelve rengs from the works of Gholam Hossein Darvish Khan and six rengs from the works of Rokn al-Din Mokhtari have been recorded in the books Collection of Darvish Khan’s Works and A Collection of Rokn al-Din Khan’s Works, all of which are in the “six-eight” meter framework. Also, all the rengs of composers such as Ali Naqi Vaziri, Musa Ma’rufi, Ali Akbar Shahnazi, Reza Mahjoubi, and ... that are included in the book “One Hundred Rengs” are in the same meter framework. Also, later composers such as Faramarz Payvar and Mohammad Reza Lotfi and ... made their rengs in this same framework. Therefore, in examining the reng genre, unlike other genres, we face a historical duality:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>Radif reng (reng until the Pahlavi period),</li>\n  <li>Non-radif reng (reng after the Pahlavi period).</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>In the radif rengs, especially in the shahr ashubs, there are melodic phrases in the form of “tarji’ band” that have been repeated in different sections of the reng. For example, the “Parvaneh” part of the reng-e Shahr Ashub has been repeated many times in this reng, but in Darvish Khan’s rengs and those of his later composers, these melodic themes (tarji’ bands), as well as the rhythmic bases of the middle of the piece, have been deleted, and in the works of these composers, in every section of the reng, we face new melodies that have not been used in the previous sections.</p>\n\n<p>A prominent feature in the new rengs, and of course in most of the “six-eight” radif rengs, is the frequent use of a “beat” consisting of the durations “dotted crotchet, semiquaver, crotchet.” Such a pattern has been called “Sicilian rhythm” in Western musicology, and of course this same pattern, combined with a beat consisting of the durations “crotchet, minim,” was known in our old musical culture as “Shir-e Madar.”</p>\n\n<p>The frequent use of “Sicilian rhythm” in reng — as a genre appropriate and harmonious with Tehrani dance — creates a so-called “drunk and ecstatic” and “quirky” state that is one of the features of “Iranian reng,” and therefore it is possible that it does not exist in all existing “six-eight” melodies. For example, if we examine Darvish Khan’s second reng of Mahur, we will see that out of the total of 306 “beats” in this melody, 81 of them have a Sicilian texture and consist of “dotted crotchet, semiquaver, crotchet” or “silence of dotted crotchet, semiquaver, crotchet.”</p>\n\n<p>It should be noted that the reng genre, because of its cheerfulness and quirkiness, has a special place in the repertoire of motrebi and ruhowzi music:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Sasan Fatemi: The pure motrebi repertoire of Tehran, from my point of view, is limited to only two genres: reng and zarbi [...] Rengs are dance music that in the majority of cases, if we want to speak with Western terms, are compatible with the “six-eight” meter and have a limited melodic range and a very simple structure. Reng is also found in the radif and for concluding a chain of gushehs, it almost comes at the end of all dastgahs. However, the radif rengs are more complex and less suitable for dance than motrebi rengs.</p>\n\n<p>In making new rengs, a relative limit has been considered for the duration of these works, and therefore their length has become more balanced compared to the radif rengs. Previously, in the chart of Mirza Abdollah’s radif rengs, we saw that performing the shortest “six-eight” reng took 31 seconds, and the longest “six-eight” reng took 13 minutes and 52 seconds, while the average duration of eleven of Darvish Khan’s rengs recorded in the album Darvish Khan’s Works is about 2 minutes and 7 seconds. The shortest of them is the reng of Mahur (Qahr o Ashti) with a duration of “01:36,” and the longest of them is the reng of Afshari with a duration of “04:07.”</p>\n\n<p>Of course, the length of Darvish Khan’s Afshari reng is because it is made in a multi-rhythmic form and its beginning has a slow rhythm. Therefore, the longest single-rhythm reng of Darvish Khan should basically be considered his second Mahur reng, which took “02:59.” As a result, we see that the duration of the reng genre, in its new position, is balanced and something around two to three minutes.</p>\n\n<p>The place of performing reng in program music has always been at the end of the work and after performing the tasnif, which from this perspective has a position similar to the radif rengs. In fact, the new rengs, because of their cheerfulness and dynamism, have been considered the “good conclusion” of music programs.</p>\n\n<h3>The General Structure of the New Reng</h3>\n\n<p>The “reng” genre in the new period has these characteristics:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>It is a metrical, traditionally wordless genre;</li>\n  <li>It is a non-improvised and pre-composed genre;</li>\n  <li>It is always in the compound “six-eight” meter framework;</li>\n  <li>Because of its fast rhythm, it is one of the metrical genres that, unlike for example pishdaramad, has a short duration (“avaz” as a non-metrical genre has no time limit):</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Duration of Darvish Khan’s Mahur reng (Qahr o Ashti): “01:36.”</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Duration of Darvish Khan’s Mahur pishdaramad: “04:25.”</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Duration of Rokn al-Din Mokhtari’s Segah reng: “01:35.”</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Duration of Rokn al-Din Mokhtari’s Segah pishdaramad: “05:19.”</p>\n\n<ol start=\"5\">\n  <li>Usually, compared to a genre such as pishdaramad, it has less rise and fall and circulates in limited maqams of the dastgah;</li>\n  <li>Unlike a genre such as pishdaramad, it has a cheerful nature.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>In this way, a comprehensive and exclusive definition for the new rengs can be something like this: “A metrical and six-eight, instrumental and non-improvised genre that is performed at the end of Iranian program music.”</p>\n\n<p>The new reng in Iranian performance music, during its evolution, unlike tasnif and chaharmezrab, suffered the fate of pishdaramad and practically disappeared from music programs after about six decades. Today’s musicians have little inclination to compose reng, and in this way, this genre too, like pishdaramad, has lost its place in today’s performances. However, it should be said that still some traditionalist musicians include the performance of this genre in their programs, and in this case, they usually perform the rengs of old composers.</p>\n\n<p>Perhaps the last reng that was composed before 1978 and was performed in the “Golha-ye Taze” program number 201 was Mohammad Reza Lotfi’s reng-e Bayat-e Kord, which is also the only reng composed in this field.</p>\n\n<p>One of the features of Mohammad Reza Lotfi’s work was focusing on performing maqams and spaces that the musicians before him had not seriously dealt with. He is the first musician who prepared and performed a complete program in the spaces of Rast Panjgah, Rak, and Bayat-e Kord. The connoisseurs know that Bayat-e Kord and Dashti have the same framework and axis and are in fact considered one maqam. The slight difference between these two, apart from the variable sound of Dashti, is in the specific melodic models of Bayat-e Kord, which Lotfi has been able to incorporate well in this reng, especially at its beginning.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>The article you read under the title “The Form of Reng in Iranian Music” is taken from the book “The Nature of Iranian Music – An Investigation into Musical Genres” written by Arshad Tahmasbi.</p>\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the form of reng in Iranian music, please share them in the comments section of the article with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a> to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-08-19 18:46:17', 0, '2025-02-14 00:59:23', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1503, 'posts', 46, 'en', NULL, 'What is the chahormezrab form in Iranian music? Did old forms similar to chahormezrab exist?', NULL, 'This article examines the form of Chaharmozrab (a traditional Iranian musical form) within Persian classical (Dastgahi) music, focusing on both its historical (radif-based) and modern developments. Originally derived from older rhythmic concepts such as “four ضرب” (four-beat patterns), Chaharmozrab in the Qajar-era radif consists of short, fast, and rhythmically repetitive instrumental pieces used to create contrast within largely non-metric vocal sections.\n\nIn its modern form, Chaharmozrab has evolved into a more technically demanding and structured composition, often showcasing a performer’s virtuosity. It is typically built upon two key elements: a repeating rhythmic motif called the “base” (pāyeh) and a recurring melodic theme known as the “refrain” (tarji‘band). These features distinguish it from other forms like Reng.\n\nChaharmozrab is primarily suited for plucked instruments—especially the santur—and its tempo varies depending on the instrument and performer. Today, it is recognized as a composed, metric piece (often in 6/16 time) with a clear structure, usually performed in the middle of a musical program rather than at the end.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the form of <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">chaharmezrab</span></strong> in Iranian dastgah music from the perspective of the prominent Iranian musician and radif master, “Arshad Tahmasbi.” Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>Old Chaharmezrab (Radif Chaharmezrab)</h2>\n\n<p>The chaharmezrab form is one of the components and genres of the radif of dastgah music. In the old maqam music, the “tarji’” had a structure similar to today’s radif chaharmezrabs:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Abd al-Qadir Maraghi: Know that tarji’ in the terminology of those who play stringed instruments means that they perform a melodic line on one string, and for each naqra (strike) or more that they play on that string, they necessarily play one naqra or more on another open string.</p>\n\n<p>From Maraghi’s statement, we understand that in that period the tarji’ form had various types.</p>\n\n<p>The term chaharmezrab, like <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%90%d9%86%da%af/\">reng</a>, entered musical terminology from the time of the emergence of the dastgah system and is derived from “chahar zarb,” one of the metrical genres of old maqam music. Mohammad Hossein Gharib (Shams al-Olama) described four different meters of this genre in his book:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">“They have divided chahar zarb into these four branches: 1. fa’ilatun fa’ilun; 2. fa’ilatun fa’ilun; 3. fa’ilatun fa’ilun; 4. fa’ilatun fa’ilun.”</p>\n\n<p>Arshad Tahmasbi: Chahar zarb or chahar zarb in old music had the same fame that chaharmezrab has in today’s music and, as one of the seventeen principles, held a special place among the people, so much so that proverbs and allusions inspired by its name were common, having a meaning synonymous with “complete and perfect,” such as:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>La’n-e chahar zarb: “A type of curse that fanatical Shiites use” (Dehkhoda, under the entry chahar zarb) or</li>\n  <li>Chahar zarb zadan: “Metaphor for shaving the beard, mustache, and two eyebrows” (Dehkhoda, under the entry chahar zarb zadan).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab in the instrumental radifs of the Qajar period, most of which were compiled by tar players such as Mirza Abdollah and Aqa Hossein-Qoli, is sometimes narrated at the beginning of the dastgahs’ radifs and sometimes in their middle, and has a completely metrical structure. It is one of the genres that, according to musical tradition, is considered purely instrumental and therefore has no place in any of the vocal radifs:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Mohammad Baharloo: Until 32 years ago, the chaharmezrab form was brief and existed only in the old radifs, which one can study to understand what it was like in the past. This form was mostly dedicated to the tar because the complete radif of Iranian music that has remained for us belongs to the old tar masters. Of course, santur players were not deprived of performing these kinds of chaharmezrabs, but kamancheh players were much less familiar with this form and [rarely] used it.</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrabs in the radifs related to tar and setar are pieces in compound duple meter and usually in “sixteenth-note” time with a relatively fast tempo. The melodic movement in these pieces is very uniform and generally consists of a simple theme that is repeated in different frets and maqams. Most parts of the radif are dedicated to non-metrical avazes, and therefore chaharmezrab, like metrical gushehs such as zanguleh, kereshmeh, and naghmeh, creates variety in the structure of the radif; so much so that Mehdi Qoli Hedayat, considering this usage, gave a brief and interesting definition of it:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">“Chaharmezrab is a reng-like piece during the avaz for amusement.”</p>\n\n<p>Unlike the rengs of the radif, the chaharmezrabs of the radif, because they are all in “sixteenth-note” time, do not have specific names and are only identified by the maqam framework in which they are narrated, such as chaharmezrab of Segah, chaharmezrab of Delkash, chaharmezrab of Hesar, etc. Most chaharmezrabs of the radif have a rhythmic pattern in the meter “fa’ilu fa’ilu” or “fa’lu fa’ilu.” For example, the chaharmezrab of Hesar in the dastgah of Chahargah in Mirza Abdollah’s radif according to Boroumand has a meter equal to “fa’ilu fa’ilu” and is performed at a very fast tempo. These kinds of chaharmezrabs, on the tar, are in fact only repetitions of the “base” of the melody, and performing a complex melody in them, because of their high speed, is impossible or very difficult.</p>\n\n<p>Among them, one of the chaharmezrabs of the radif, which comes at the beginning of the dastgahs of Homayun, Nava, and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b1%d8%a7%d8%b3%d8%aa-%d9%be%d9%86%d8%ac%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">Rast Panjgah</a>, was known as “Pishdaramad-e Chakavaki.” This chaharmezrab has the same melodic movement in all three of these dastgahs, but depending on which dastgah it is in, its melodic movement has been adapted to the sonic framework of one of the head maqams, namely the maqams of Homayun, Nava, and Rast. Mehdi Qoli Hedayat is the only one who has named these chaharmezrabs with this title and, in addition, has pointed out the uniformity of the radif chaharmezrabs:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Mehdi Qoli Hedayat: It is regrettable that in the three dastgahs of Nava, Homayun, and Rast Panjgah, they have been content with the pishdaramad-e chakavaki, and it cannot be attributed to anything but the taste and talent of the masters and performers. What the ancients composed, such as the avaz daramad of Mahur, Shur, and Homayun, has a core that is not present in the later pishdaramads; this is also the fault of the listeners who pay attention to the meters of rengs and zarbi pieces.</p>\n\n<p>The ancients gave the title pishdaramad to these chaharmezrabs because they are placed at the beginning of the dastgah and are played before the “daramads.” Therefore, they have no connection with the “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d9%be%db%8c%d8%b4-%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%a2%d9%85%d8%af/\">pishdaramad</a>” genre that we explained earlier and are constructed in a completely different framework from them. The main place for performing these chaharmezrabs in the periods before Mirza Abdollah was in the dastgah of Homayun, and because the first melodic movement of it, after presenting the initial bases, begins from the pivot fret of the “Chakavak” maqam, it was called “Chakavaki,” and thus whoever first transferred it to the maqams of Nava and Rast also brought it in those parts with the same name (pishdaramad-e chakavaki):</p>\n\n<p>Faramarz Payvar wrote about the naming of chaharmezrab:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">The term chaharmezrab comes from a type of rhythmic melody that the old masters of Iranian music played on the tar. These types of zarbi pieces initially started with four strikes of the plectrum: the first and second strikes hit the white strings of the tar, the third strike hit the yellow strings, and the fourth strike hit the fourth strings of the tar [the third strings of the tar: bam and hangam], and were considered the base of the melody. The best example of it is the chaharmezrab of Esfahani, which is written in the first volume of the violin radif of Master Abolhasan Saba.</p>\n\n<p>As we said earlier, each beat of the chaharmezrabs of Hesar, Delkash, Homayun, and Nava, in the smallest divisions, is divided into “three” sixteenth notes and is in fact “three plectrum strikes” that do not match their name, but apparently the original chaharmezrab at the beginning of its formation started with a four-plectrum base (two crotchet durations and two sixteenth-note durations in each “sixteenth-note” measure), which naturally this rhythmic motif could also be transformed into a six-plectrum texture (three sixteenth notes in each beat); as in the chaharmezrab of Bidad in Mirza Abdollah’s radif it is the same, and even the chaharmezrab of Segah in this radif also has a four-plectrum texture.</p>\n\n<p>After examining the existing radifs, we realize that the tempo in the radif chaharmezrabs is very different (from a tempo of 76 to a tempo of 192), and in this way some of them, from the point of view of tempo, are like six-eight rengs, so much so that Boroumand performed the reng of Shahr Ashub of the dastgah of Shur almost with the same speed as the chaharmezrab of the dastgah of Segah (72 beats per minute).</p>\n\n<p>The chaharmezrabs of the radif do not have a fixed performance position. Some dastgahs basically start with chaharmezrab, and in addition, other chaharmezrabs may also be placed in the middle of the radif, even in several places. What can be said with certainty about this is that they have never come at the end of the dastgah.</p>\n\n<h2>New Chaharmezrab (Non-Radif Chaharmezrab)</h2>\n\n<p>In non-radif music, chaharmezrab is usually a very fast piece and for this reason it can be one of the indicators of the speed and technique of the player. Also, as its name suggests, it is originally a piece specific to plectrum instruments, and playing it with bowed instruments is not as attractive as with plectrum instruments. Among plectrum instruments, the santur has more possibilities for performing diverse and dynamic chaharmezrabs because the santur is a fast instrument with many sympathetic strings (yellow strings) that can easily be used in performing the bases and sympathetic notes of chaharmezrab.</p>\n\n<p>For this reason, we see that the first diverse chaharmezrabs in post-Qajar music belong to santur players, including Habib Somai, Faramarz Payvar, and Parviz Meshkatian:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Music of Iran: From the beginning of its establishment, the Radio Administration was proud to have such an artist among its players because he was unique and unparalleled in playing the santur, and there was no one who heard his instrument and was not captivated by his skill.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Mohammad Reza Shajarian: I did not know Parviz at all. In the 56 Art Festival, when I presented Nava with Lotfi’s group, he also participated with Alizadeh’s group [...] But it was the first time I saw him and heard his instrument. I saw what good chaharmezrabs he plays and said to myself: “How well this young man works.” Meshkatian played the instrument very well in that program. Even Mr. Shahnaz, who had seen that program, later said: “The chaharmezrab he played was very good.” Well, Parviz’s chaharmezrabs were very pleasant and beautiful.</p>\n\n<p>Also, among tar players, Ali Akbar Shahnazi included some chaharmezrabs of his own compositions in the Advanced Course Radif that he compiled, which, compared to the usual chaharmezrabs in Mirza Abdollah’s and Aqa Hossein-Qoli’s radifs, are considered diverse and attractive pieces.</p>\n\n<p>The most famous chaharmezrab in Iranian music is a piece whose main theme was composed by Darvish Khan, and his student, Abolhasan Saba, expanded and developed it. As we said, Darvish Khan is the first influential composer in the Qajar period who left many works, and his chaharmezrab of Mahur has been performed many times by various players, both as solo and in ensemble playing. This chaharmezrab is built based on the two main features of this genre, namely the use of a rhythmic motif called the “base” and a melodic theme called the “tarji’ band.” In fact, it is these features that distinguish chaharmezrab from fast “six-eight” rengs.</p>\n\n<p>In chaharmezrab, the focus is on a repeating rhythmic motif that does not have a complex melody and is called the “base,” and it is emphasized in various places in the piece. The “tarji’ band” is also one of the main themes of the melody that the composer refers to and repeats in different sections of the piece. In contrast, the new rengs that were composed after Darvish Khan’s period lack base and tarji’ band. We can show the structural difference between reng and chaharmezrab (if we consider both as “six-eight” and “sixteenth-note”) for example in a poem.</p>\n\n<p>If we read all the couplets of this poem by “Owhadi Maragheh” one after another as they are, it has a structure like a “reng”:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Muslims, greetings as much as you can, I said  \nDo not grieve the hearts of the helpless, I said</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">You should not be so proud of wealth and status  \nShake the dust from your skirt from this and that, I said</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">In this garden that you have attached your heart to, if you have the means  \nPlant a good tree for yourself, I said</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">The situation will change from this order and this custom that you see  \nYou too, now turn it upon yourself, I said...</p>\n\n<p>Now if we consider one or two of the words as the “base” and choose one of the couplets or hemistichs as the “tarji’ band,” Owhadi’s poem takes on a structure similar to chaharmezrab:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">“Muslims”<sup>base</sup> “Muslims”<sup>base</sup> “Muslims”<sup>base</sup> “Muslims”<sup>base</sup></p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">“Muslims”<sup>base</sup> greetings as much as you can “I said”<sup>base</sup></p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">{Do not grieve the hearts of the helpless “I said”}<sup>tarji’ band, base</sup></p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">You should not be so proud of wealth and status</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Shake the dust from your skirt from this and that “I said”<sup>base</sup></p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">“Muslims”<sup>base</sup> “Muslims”<sup>base</sup> “Muslims”<sup>base</sup> “Muslims”<sup>base</sup></p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">{Do not grieve the hearts of the helpless “I said”}<sup>tarji’ band, base</sup></p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">In this garden that you have attached your heart to, if you have the means</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Plant a good tree for yourself “I said”<sup>base</sup></p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">The situation will change from this order and this custom that you see</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">You too, now turn it upon yourself “I said”<sup>base</sup></p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">“Muslims”<sup>base</sup> “Muslims”<sup>base</sup> “Muslims”<sup>base</sup> “Muslims”<sup>base</sup></p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">{Do not grieve the hearts of the helpless “I said”}<sup>tarji’ band, base</sup></p>\n\n<p>The performance of chaharmezrab in program music does not have a specific place, but according to tradition, it is never played at the end of the work and, unlike pishdaramad, reng, and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%aa%d8%b5%d9%86%db%8c%d9%81/\">tasnif</a>, it can be performed not once but more times.</p>\n\n<p>After the joint work of Darvish Khan – Saba, the most famous work in this field is Abolhasan Saba’s famous chaharmezrab “Zang-e Shotor” in Segah, which has also been performed many times by various groups and soloists and has a “base” and “tarji’ band.”</p>\n\n<p>Farhad Fakhreddini wrote:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">[Farhad Fakhreddini: Yehudi] Menuhin said: “Performing Iranian music is not difficult for me, and I can play anything you can play on the violin.” Some friends played a trick and said: “We have pieces that you cannot play! [...] Yehudi Menuhin said: “You can test me.” It should be noted that he had joint programs with “Ravi Shankar,” the masterful sitar player, and was familiar with Indian music. Therefore, he thought he could also handle performing Iranian music and was unaware of the various tunings of the violin in Iranian music.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">The first piece chosen was “Zang-e Shotor” composed by Saba, in which the international tuning of the violin changed from “E, A, D, G” to “D, A, B koron, G,” and as a result, for someone unfamiliar with this tuning, fingering on the violin strings would be very difficult.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Mr. Rahmatollah Badi’i, after playing a few measures of this piece, handed the violin to Menuhin, and Menuhin confidently took the violin and started playing and immediately realized that he was unable to play with such a tuning and said: “If you had not changed the tuning of the violin, I could have played it.”</p>\n\n<p>Abolhasan Saba also has another famous chaharmezrab called “Samani” in the dastgah of Segah, which, like his chaharmezrab “Zang-e Shotor,” is composed at a tempo of 120 beats per minute and therefore, from the point of view of tempo, both of them are more similar to a reng than to fast chaharmezrabs, and therefore traditional Tehran-style dancing with them will be possible, while for example Faramarz Payvar has many chaharmezrabs whose tempo is completely different from “six-eight” rengs.</p>\n\n<p>These chaharmezrabs are truly “sixteenth-note,” fast, and technical pieces that, of course, performing them exactly with instruments such as tar and ney and ... is impossible and they are arranged according to the capabilities of the santur; like Faramarz Payvar’s chaharmezrab of Shekasteh in the avaz of Bayat-e Tork, which is recorded at a tempo of 176 beats per minute and therefore has a much faster tempo than the usual speed of rengs, and for this reason, dancing with such chaharmezrabs in the traditional way is not possible.</p>\n\n<p>Here it is necessary to mention that usually the tempo of santur players’ chaharmezrabs is higher than the tempo of Abolhasan Saba’s chaharmezrabs and also the works of players of other instruments, so much so that one of Parviz Meshkatian’s chaharmezrabs, which incidentally is also composed in the shekasteh of the avaz of Bayat-e Tork, is recorded at a tempo of 184 beats per minute and even faster than Faramarz Payvar’s shekasteh chaharmezrab.</p>\n\n<p>While the speed of none of the chaharmezrabs of Hossein Alizadeh, the tar player who was introduced as the most technical player of this instrument in the 1980s, is more than 168 beats per minute. Among them are his chaharmezrabs of Mahur and Rast Panjgah, which are included in the book “Ten Pieces for Tar, Volume 1.”</p>\n\n<p>In the same vein, we see that the tempo of chaharmezrabs performed by ney and kamancheh players has always been less than 168 beats per minute, and therefore we come to the conclusion that the way the tempo of this genre is in music, unlike all other genres, has a direct relationship with which instruments’ players composed and performed it. On this basis, we can classify the tempo of performing non-radif chaharmezrabs in Iranian music at least in a dual way as follows:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Fast santur chaharmezrabs</li>\n  <li>Semi-fast non-santur chaharmezrabs</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Essentially, the possibilities and capabilities of the santur for performing this genre have caused the players of this instrument to be more active in this field than the players of other instruments, so much so that Faramarz Payvar has published at least thirty pieces of his chaharmezrabs in a book titled Thirty Chaharmezrab Pieces for Santur, and most of Parviz Meshkatian’s works in the book Twenty Pieces for Santur are dedicated to his chaharmezrabs.</p>\n\n<p>Usually, chaharmezrabs in music come from the fast zarbi improvisations of players. In fact, the player selects the best and most original themes that form in his mind while playing alone and in solitude from among the themes that form in his mind during improvisation and uses them as the basis of his work, and with a compositional approach, proceeds to arrange a coherent piece with a balanced and conventional duration. Therefore, his work, depending on each person’s perception, ability, and performance method, may reach the result after hours, days, etc.</p>\n\n<p>On this basis, we can find hundreds and thousands of unrefined chaharmezrab-like works in the radio performances of various players, which are usually performed as question and answer between two or more instruments and have durations sometimes up to 10 to 12 minutes. These kinds of performances are considered “zarbi playing” in musical custom.</p>\n\n<p>We have heard and know that in each of the fast zarbi playing performances that various players performed in radio programs before 1978, there are original and beautiful phrases that can be the basis for making a coherent and refined chaharmezrab of two or three minutes. Every skilled improvising player such as Hassan Kasaei, Jalil Shahnaz, Parviz Yahaghi, Lotfollah Majd, Farhang Sharif, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b6%d8%a7-%d9%84%d8%b7%d9%81%db%8c/\">Mohammad Reza Lotfi</a>, and others could improvise for hours and without pause in any genre with slow or fast tempos, but certainly not all parts of his long work have the same musical quality. This is exactly comparable to the impromptu speeches of eloquent orators who can speak for hours on a specific topic, but naturally the impact of such long and detailed speeches is completely different from a written speech arranged for presentation in a formal and specific situation, such as assemblies like the United Nations, etc.</p>\n\n<p>In such situations, the speaker or speech arranger shows great sensitivity to the use of each word, the way of phrasing, the duration of the speech, and what topics should be covered in his speech so that he can present coherent content to his audience in a certain period that is, in a word, “comprehensive and exclusive.” In fact, this is where the creator of the work signs his work with confidence, and as a result, we can derive this expression from it: “This is the utmost effort and ability of mine.”</p>\n\n<p>The foundation of the work of the solo players of the 1960s and 1970s who had continuous activity in the radio and some of whom we mentioned was based on improvisation, and most of these artists were usually not bound by arranging coherent pieces for a composed work with a “signature” in today’s sense.</p>\n\n<p>For example, among the earlier and later tar players, one can mention Jalil Shahnaz and Mohammad Reza Lotfi, who had unparalleled mastery in playing original and beautiful zarbi chaharmezrab-like pieces, but they never attempted to refine and organize these works and register and publish them as a completely designed coherent chaharmezrab. In contrast to them, for example, Faramarz Payvar, as mentioned, documented, registered, and published at least thirty pieces of his chaharmezrabs.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Arshad Tahmasbi: If I want to choose from among several musicians whom I like very much and, by highlighting one of his features, prefer him over others, what should I say? What is the most prominent and distinctive feature of him in the history of Iranian music and tar playing that I can rely on? A feature that is exclusively or more applicable to him and specific to him, and at the same time, my personal feeling and compliment, etc., are not involved in it, a feature that the conscience of history also testifies to. I remembered his zarbi playing and improvised chaharmezrab playing, which is unparalleled and unique.</p>\n\n<h2>Chaharmezrab in Unconventional Frameworks</h2>\n\n<p>Some musicians, without paying attention to the “sixteenth-note” framework of chaharmezrab as it appears in all radifs, have called pieces chaharmezrab that are not in compound meters such as “sixteenth-note” and “six-eight” and their similars. For example, Ali Naqi Vaziri, in his New Instruction for Tar and Setar, has registered a two-four melody as chaharmezrab simply because it has a “base,” and also in the same book has brought a two-four Bayat-e Esfahan melody with the title chaharmezrab. Also, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%af%d9%87/\">Hossein Alizadeh</a> has called one of his lang pieces chaharmezrab.</p>\n\n<p>Of course, applying the term chaharmezrab to such pieces in the works of composers has very little precedent, and therefore, based on what was said, we can define today’s chaharmezrab in this way:</p>\n\n<p><strong>“A metrical, instrumental, and non-improvised genre with a generally ‘sixteenth-note’ rhythm and having a ‘base’ and ‘tarji’ band’ that is usually performed in the middle of the work in program music.”</strong></p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>The article you read under the title “The Form of Chaharmezrab in Iranian Music” is taken from the book “The Nature of Iranian Music – An Investigation into Musical Genres” written by Arshad Tahmasbi.</p>', '2023-08-20 17:35:28', 0, '2025-02-14 00:59:44', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1504, 'posts', 47, 'en', NULL, 'Comprehensive guide to buying a guitar for beginners | Most important points and specifications | Introduction to 5 affordable and suitable models for starting', NULL, 'This article provides a comprehensive guide for buying a guitar, especially for beginners. It highlights the importance of choosing the right guitar based on your preferred music style, emphasizing that a wrong choice may reduce motivation to learn.\n\nThe article introduces different types of guitars, including classical, acoustic, electro-acoustic, and electric guitars, explaining their features, advantages, and suitable use cases. It also outlines key factors to consider when purchasing a guitar, such as budget, physical condition, neck straightness, string height, and testing the instrument before buying.\n\nAdditionally, several affordable beginner-friendly guitar models and reputable brands are recommended. The article concludes with tips on essential accessories and proper guitar maintenance, such as cleaning, storing in suitable conditions, and using protective covers, to ensure long-term quality and performance.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Buying a guitar</span></strong> is the first step to starting to play and learn the guitar. The guitar, as one of the most popular instruments in the world, has always been one of the most favored instruments for entering the world of music. Therefore, in this article from the series of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%b3%d8%a7%d8%b2/\">buying musical instruments</a> articles, we introduce this instrument and provide a comprehensive guide for buying different guitars.</p>\n\n<h2>Introduction to the Guitar</h2>\n\n<p>Today, the guitar is considered one of the most popular instruments in different parts of the world, and this instrument has many fans among the general public. Therefore, if you are also interested in playing this popular instrument, you first need to become familiar with different styles of guitar playing and the various types of guitars. After gaining comprehensive and complete information about the different playing styles and choosing the right type of guitar for your style, you can proceed to acquire and buy a guitar.</p>\n\n<p>You should keep in mind that there are various types of guitars, each designed for a specific style. If you make a mistake in choosing your guitar — because the sound of your guitar is not the sound that matches your favorite style — learning the guitar and playing it will become difficult for you.</p>\n\n<p>On the other hand, if the sound of your playing does not match the sound of your favorite style, it will cause confusion for you and may even lead to giving up playing the guitar.</p>\n\n<p>So in this article from <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>, please continue with us to become familiar with playing styles and types of guitars. After acquiring the necessary and appropriate information, you can obtain the guitar of your choice.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3300\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1200\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3300 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/learning-guitar-online.webp\" alt=\"The guitar is one of the most popular and best-selling instruments in the world\" width=\"1200\" height=\"800\" /> The guitar is one of the most popular and best-selling instruments in the world.[/caption]\n\n<h2>Necessary Steps for Buying a Guitar for the First Time:</h2>\n\n<p>Before and during buying a guitar, you must carefully examine several important points, which we mention below:</p>\n\n<p>1. The first step is choosing the playing style and type of guitar.</p>\n\n<p>2. Determine the amount of budget you have in mind for buying a guitar.</p>\n\n<p>3. Examine the guitar visually.</p>\n\n<p>4. If possible, before buying the guitar, see the instrument in person and preferably play it for a few moments or ask the seller to test it for you.</p>\n\n<p>5. Prepare the necessary accessories such as extra strings, a cover and guitar maintenance equipment, guitar picks, and so on.</p>\n\n<p>Now, to help you choose and buy a suitable guitar for starting to learn and train, in this article we answer the following questions:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>How should we obtain the best guitar for starting to play?</li>\n  <li>What are the differences between different types of guitars, and what points should we pay attention to when buying a guitar?</li>\n  <li>Which accessories should we prepare for starting to learn the guitar?</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>If you intend to buy a guitar for someone else, you must pay attention to a few points:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Will that person have a role in choosing and buying the guitar, or do you intend to surprise them?</li>\n  <li>Are you buying the desired guitar for one of your friends or family members or for a child?</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Depending on who the person in question is, the shape and size of the guitar will be different. Before proceeding to buy a guitar, gain as much information as possible.</p>\n\n<h2>The Most Important Point in Buying a Guitar Is Choosing the Playing Style and Type of Guitar</h2>\n\n<p>The first and most important point for buying a guitar is choosing your desired playing style, and for this purpose, the best way is to consult with a professional guitar player.</p>\n\n<p>On the other hand, even with this consultation, you still need to listen to different styles of guitar playing for hours before acquiring and buying a guitar so that you can conclude which of these styles is suitable for you and which one you are more interested in. Because the most important issue in progressing in playing is the continuity of learning and making the path of professional guitar playing enjoyable — it is that the sound of your instrument is close to your favorite style so that while practicing, you enjoy hearing the sound of your instrument, feel good, and find motivation to continue playing.</p>\n\n<p>Many professional players and guitar teachers make this mistake: they suggest to people interested in playing the guitar that at the beginning of work, without paying attention to their favorite style and knowing the different playing styles, they should use a classical guitar because they consider playing this type of guitar simpler than others.</p>\n\n<p>So at the beginning, determine whether you are interested in an acoustic guitar or an electric guitar. Acoustic guitars are excellent options for starting to learn and train the guitar; because their structure is simpler than electric guitars and they do not have some of the troubles of electric guitars.</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, with an acoustic guitar, beginners and novice students can put their full concentration on playing the instrument during training. Also, among acoustic guitars with nylon strings (the same as classical guitars) and acoustic guitars with steel strings (the same as acoustic guitars), one can be chosen. Classical guitars have less intensity and volume and are physically simpler and easier to play, but acoustic guitars have a louder sound and playing them puts more pressure on the player’s fingers.</p>\n\n<p>On the other hand, many greats and the best and most famous guitarists in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">the history of music</a> started their work with electric guitars; simply because their favorite type of instrument was the electric guitar. The electric guitar has more variety in terms of sound character, appearance, and playing styles, but electric guitars, like acoustic guitars, put relatively high pressure on the player’s fingers.</p>\n\n<p>Despite all these talks, for starting to play the guitar, there is no right or wrong choice. So the best thing is to pay attention to your favorite players and styles so that you realize what type of guitar they mostly play with.</p>\n\n<p>You should know that <strong>the best type of guitar for you is the guitar with which you can play in your favorite playing style.</strong></p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3298\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1200\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3298 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/guitar-finger-pain.webp\" alt=\"The most important issue in buying a guitar is choosing the playing style.\" width=\"1200\" height=\"800\" /> The most important issue in buying a guitar is choosing the playing style and the instrument appropriate for that style.[/caption]\n\n<h2>Practical and Important Points When Buying a Guitar</h2>\n\n<p>There are important points that must be paid special attention to when buying a guitar, and examining these points is very important when buying a suitable and practical guitar for students and beginners. Paying attention to these points and observing them can help you choose a suitable guitar with good quality. In the following, we introduce these important points:</p>\n\n<h3>1. Tuning the Guitar</h3>\n\n<p>After choosing and buying the guitar, the first thing you should do is tune your instrument. If you are a beginner and do not have the ability to tune the guitar, ask the seller to tune it for you, because tuning the guitar requires an experienced and professional person. Usually, guitar sellers are professional players themselves or, even if not, they are familiar with tuning the instrument and their knowledge is sufficient for tuning the guitar.</p>\n\n<h3>2. The Straightness of the Guitar Neck</h3>\n\n<p>The second important point that you must pay special attention to when choosing and buying a guitar is the straightness of the guitar neck. The guitar neck is usually designed straight for beginners to prevent the strings and frets from touching each other while playing the instrument.</p>\n\n<h3>3. The Distance of the Strings from the Fretboard</h3>\n\n<p>You must pay attention to the fact that the performance of a guitar has a direct relationship with the distance of the strings from its fretboard. Therefore, you must examine this point completely, or if you do not have the necessary expertise in this field, ask a specialist in this area to take a look at the guitar you want, because if there is a problem in this regard, it may cause trouble for you when playing the instrument.</p>\n\n<h3>4. The Fretboard of the Guitar</h3>\n\n<p>When buying a guitar, if the sound of the desired guitar is good and it has no action, you must also test whether you can easily reach the entire fretboard of the guitar or not. For this purpose, run all your fingers along each side of the guitar neck and check whether you can feel the protrusion of any of the metal fret ends or not. If you feel that some frets are not level with the wood, it means that the desired guitar is probably fake and does not have good quality or the manufacturing brand is not a reputable brand.</p>\n\n<h2>Types of Guitars</h2>\n\n<p>In the following, we examine the different types of guitars and become familiar with the advantages and disadvantages of each:</p>\n\n<h3>Classical Guitar (Acoustic Guitars with Nylon Strings)</h3>\n\n<p>Generally, acoustic guitars whose strings are made of nylon are called classical or flamenco guitars. This type of guitar is suitable for classical, flamenco, pop, and jazz styles, and with them, you can play in these styles and enjoy the sound of your instrument.</p>\n\n<p>Classical guitars, because they do not require side equipment such as sound cards or amplifiers, allow you to easily play with them and not get involved with side equipment of the instrument. Also, with these guitars, you can play with more power and get a stronger and brighter sound from your guitar.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3804\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1920\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3804 size-large\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/sornaz_guitar_02-1920x768.webp\" alt=\"Acoustic Guitar with Nylon Strings (Classical Guitar)\" width=\"1920\" height=\"768\" /> Acoustic Guitar with Nylon Strings (Classical Guitar)[/caption]\n\n<h3>Acoustic Guitar (Acoustic Guitars with Steel Strings)</h3>\n\n<p>Acoustic guitars are divided into two different types. In the first type, as mentioned earlier, nylon strings are used and they are known as classical guitars. In the other type of acoustic guitars, steel strings are used, which we generally know as acoustic guitars.</p>\n\n<p>The interesting point about buying acoustic guitars is that the sound you hear from them is a very flexible sound, meaning that you will be able to easily use this type in different guitar playing styles and get the sound you like.</p>\n\n<p>Acoustic guitars are designed in different models in which the body material of the instrument is different, and therefore the output sound of the instrument will also be different. Also, because of the different body design of acoustic guitars and the use of steel strings instead of nylon, they have a much brighter sound than classical guitars and therefore have their own special fans.</p>\n\n<h3>Electro-Acoustic Guitars (Electric Acoustic Guitars)</h3>\n\n<p>Electro-acoustic guitars are essentially the same as acoustic guitars, with the difference that they have the possibility of connecting to amplifiers and sound cards so that you can amplify the sound of your instrument with them. They also use an equalizer and a volume button to adjust the volume level.</p>\n\n<h3>Electric Guitars</h3>\n\n<p>The electric guitar can be considered the most flexible and special type of guitar in the world. Because with this type of guitar, you will have the ability to practice in all guitar playing styles.</p>\n\n<p>On the other hand, the variety of sounds you can get with this instrument is very high.</p>\n\n<p>The interesting point regarding buying electric guitars is that not only is the difference in appearance in these guitars very high, but the electric guitars themselves are also produced in various types.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3807\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1920\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3807 size-large\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/sornaz_guitar_05-1920x1252.webp\" alt=\"View of an Electric Guitar\" width=\"1920\" height=\"1252\" /> View of an Electric Guitar[/caption]\n\n<h2>Introduction to 5 Cheap and Suitable Guitar Models for Starting</h2>\n\n<p>Buying a guitar for starting work is difficult, and for this reason, people who are not yet familiar with brands and types of guitars become confused in buying a guitar due to the high variety. Therefore, here we introduce 5 cheap and suitable guitar models for starting so that by limiting the choices for starting work and examining these options together, you can have a simpler choice:</p>\n\n<p><strong>1. Yamaha C40 Guitar:</strong> Certainly, the history that <a href=\"https://de.yamaha.com/\">Yamaha company</a> has in producing musical instruments alone shows the very high credibility of the instruments made by this company. Yamaha company always considers cases such as quality and various prices for different levels of playing in making its instruments. Yamaha C40 guitars are a model with acceptable quality and affordability that is a very suitable option for buying a guitar and starting to learn and train. This model guitar is suitable for people over 11 years old.</p>\n\n<p><strong>2. Yamaha C70 Guitar:</strong> The Yamaha C70 model guitar is very similar to the C40 model, and because of this similarity and the little difference between these two models, the choice of buying a guitar between these two models is usually more related to the amount of your budget. The C70 model, due to the better quality of wood used in its construction, has a better sound than the C40 model, and therefore, if you have enough budget, it will be a better choice than the C40 model.</p>\n\n<p><strong>3. Alhambra Z Nature Guitar:</strong> The Spanish brand Alhambra is one of the most popular and famous guitar manufacturers in the world. The Alhambra Z Nature model has high quality due to the use of the CNC production method, and because the amount of wasted wood in this method is low, its final price is more affordable, and for this reason, it is a suitable option for buying a guitar for beginner students.</p>\n\n<p><strong>4. Vafaei MV05 Classical Guitar:</strong> The Vafaei brand is also one of the reputable Iranian brands in making guitars. Its side and back are made of mahogany wood and its top is made of spruce wood, and for this reason, this model makes it very ideal for performing initial exercises at the beginning of the playing path. This guitar is very suitable for classical and pop styles and can be one of the good options in your list of choices for buying a guitar.</p>\n\n<p><strong>5. Diamond TS600 Guitar:</strong> Diamond is one of the reputable and high-quality Iranian brands in producing guitars that has gained high popularity among beginner students in recent years. The size of this instrument is 4/4 (standard) and it is suitable for students 11 years and older.</p>\n\n<h2>Determine Your Budget for Buying a Guitar!</h2>\n\n<p>The cost and budget you have in mind for buying a guitar must be determined. Determine the buying budget before proceeding to buy a guitar; because in the future you may be tempted to spend a little more to get a guitar with a more beautiful appearance. Try to start with a cheaper guitar first and gradually, with the progress of playing and after you are sure of continuing the path of guitar playing, upgrade your guitar.</p>\n\n<p>When you have limited your search range to a few specific guitars, go to a specialized guitar store and get help from the sellers for buying your guitar. In the store, you can understand which guitar attracts you more up close and whether holding the guitar gives you a good feeling or not. When you hold the neck of the instrument in your hand, pay attention to whether the neck is comfortable in your hand.</p>\n\n<h2>Prices of Buying Guitars in Different Levels and Qualities</h2>\n\n<p>Before buying a guitar, you must also be familiar with the approximate prices of the instruments so that you can make your guitar purchase with better and more information. Therefore, you should know that the price range of guitars is from about 3 million tomans to prices above 75 million tomans.</p>\n\n<p>Beginner guitars that are suitable for students and beginners are priced between 3 to 10 million tomans. Intermediate-level guitars that are used for semi-professional players and students with more than 2-3 years of experience are priced between 10 million to about 30 million tomans, and professional guitars that are suitable for performing in concerts and recording music works are bought and sold from 30 million tomans to prices above 75 million tomans.</p>\n\n<p>If you intend to buy your first guitar and start learning the guitar, it is suggested that you purchase beginner or intermediate-level guitars. If the amount of budget considered for buying is low, it is better to use beginner guitars.</p>\n\n<p>This suggestion is given because buying a standard and higher-quality guitar, considering the more beautiful sound, gives you more motivation and interest for practicing and learning music, and there is no need to buy a professional guitar at the beginning of work because, in addition to the issue of price and the level of playing quality, there is also the possibility that you will become interested in another instrument in the continuation of the path and give up playing the guitar. But try as much as possible to buy standard guitars at the intermediate level.</p>\n\n<h3>Types of Guitars and Reputable Companies and Brands for Buying Each</h3>\n\n<p>As mentioned earlier, the guitar instrument is generally divided into three categories: acoustic guitar, electric guitar, and classical guitar. Classical guitars are actually a type of acoustic guitars whose string material differs from each other. Flamenco guitars are also produced with different wood and string material for their specific styles.</p>\n\n<p>Each of these types of guitars is suitable for a specific style of music and is used for a specific style. For example, acoustic guitars for jazz and blues styles, classical guitars for pop and classical styles, and finally electric guitars are mostly used for rock and metal styles. Which type of instrument you use at the beginning of guitar training depends on your favorite music style. In the following, we examine reputable and popular companies and brands that produce guitars and also have suitable prices.</p>\n\n<h3>Popular and Reputable Companies and Brands for Buying a Classical Guitar</h3>\n\n<p>Reputable companies and brands that produce classical guitars with suitable prices at the beginner and intermediate level are the brands Yamaha, Epiphone, and Ibanez, whose guitars are suitable for buying and are available from prices of 3 million tomans to 30 million tomans. Another popular and reputable company and brand for guitars is the “Cort” brand, which produces guitars from the beginner to professional level. Many commercial brands of acoustic and classical guitars are produced, and there is a lot of variety in this type of guitars. Other commercial brands of acoustic and classical guitars include Martin, Taylor, and Fender.</p>\n\n<h3>Popular and Reputable Companies and Brands for Buying an Electric Guitar</h3>\n\n<p>Beginner-level electric guitars are a bit more expensive compared to beginner-level acoustic guitars and are sold from prices of about 10 million tomans and above. Suitable companies and brands for electric guitars that also have suitable prices for beginner and novice students are Squier, Jackson, and Epiphone guitars, among which the Squier brand can be a good option for buying an electric guitar because of its style and suitable price. As other reputable and popular brands of electric guitars that are at higher levels, one can mention the brands Fender, Gibson, PRS, and Ibanez.</p>\n\n<h2>Make Sure of the Shape and Appearance of the Guitar You Buy and Examine It Well</h2>\n\n<p>The guitar you choose for buying must attract your attention visually. If you have a specific color or appearance in mind for your guitar, be sure to look for a guitar with that shape and color and ignore other matters. On the other hand, you can also examine more expensive guitars to better understand the features you want.</p>\n\n<p>It should be noted that acoustic guitars have less variety in terms of appearance, but on the other hand, acoustic guitars are divided into several groups in terms of body size, which is why each will have a different sound. In addition, guitars may have a “cutaway” or not.</p>\n\n<p>The indentation of the guitar body near the place where the neck is attached to the body is called a cutaway. This indentation makes it easier to access the higher frets. The bodies of old-model classical and acoustic guitars are completely symmetrical, but in some acoustic guitars, part of the body is cut at the place where the neck is attached. For better recognition, if you look at electric guitars, the Fender Stratocaster has two cutaways, one at the bottom and the other at the top of the place where the neck is attached. The Fender Telecaster has only one cutaway at the bottom of the place where the neck is attached.</p>\n\n<p>Dreadnought-size acoustic guitars have a louder and fuller sound, but because of their large body size, they may not be suitable for people with a small build. On the other hand, Parlour-size guitars have narrower bodies and are suitable for practicing while sitting.</p>\n\n<p>If you intend to buy a guitar for a child, take a look at beginner small guitars. These guitars have a shorter neck, and there is no need to stretch the fingers excessively to reach different notes. Some manufacturing companies also produce 3/4-size and even smaller guitars specifically for children. For example, Jackson’s Minion series are very suitable instruments for children.</p>\n\n<h2>Before Buying a Guitar, If Possible, See It Up Close</h2>\n\n<p>If you have a small budget for buying a guitar, you should know that guitars in the low price range generally have similar specifications and features, and there is no need for too much sensitivity on the quality of the materials used in making the guitar. The most important points in the low price range are probably a more beautiful appearance, and listening to the sound of the guitar and establishing a better connection with the appearance and sound of the instrument.</p>\n\n<h2>Before Buying a Guitar, Be Sure to Test It</h2>\n\n<p>Before you proceed to buy a guitar, be sure to test the instrument. Buy the instrument from a place that gives you the possibility to test it completely. Hold the instrument in your hand and check whether the guitar is comfortable in your hands or whether its size is suitable for your build. If you intend to buy a guitar for your child, the size of the instrument and its appearance are important so that the instrument is not heavy for the child and its volume is not too large relative to the build and body of your child. It is suggested to buy smaller 3/4 or 1/2-size guitars for children.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>The guitar instrument must be compatible with your hand and body, and you should not feel discomfort in the neck and hand area when playing the instrument. On the other hand, the guitar neck should be such that it does not put pressure on your fingers and your hand does not feel tired when holding the neck of the instrument.</li>\n  <li>Before buying a guitar, be sure to test the tuners of the instrument so that they are soft so that no problem arises when tuning the instrument.</li>\n  <li>You should keep in mind that the sound of different guitars differs from each other, and each has its own special sound output when playing and combining the sound of the strings. So when buying a guitar, pay attention to the sound character of your instrument and test several instruments and choose the one whose sound is more pleasant for you.</li>\n  <li>When buying a guitar, pay attention to the volume of its sound. Guitars are made with different body sizes, and the larger the guitar body, the greater the volume of its output sound will be.</li>\n  <li>After examining the above points, try to play some chords with the guitar. If you are a beginner and cannot test the chords, ask the instrument seller to play it a little for you so that you can hear the sound of the instrument.</li>\n  <li>Finally, you must also examine the visual shape of the desired instrument because guitars are produced with different colors and shapes. Choose a guitar whose color and appearance you also like and that sits well with you. But keep in mind that the color and appearance of the instrument should be the last factor in buying a guitar for you.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Examine All Parts of the Guitar Completely Before Buying</h2>\n\n<p>Just as you carefully examine any other item before buying it, you must also examine the guitar with great care to make sure it is healthy and suitable. Examine all parts of the guitar body and neck with great care and precision so that there is no crack or scratch anywhere. Examine all the frets of the guitar and listen to the sound of the instrument on all strings on all frets so that it does not produce a buzzing sound or a bad sound. The sound of the instrument must be completely clear and distinct on all frets.</p>\n\n<p>For buying acoustic and classical guitars, you must pay attention to the fact that the guitar has not been kept in humid environments or very dry or very hot ones. There is a possibility that the shape of the body of a guitar kept in a humid environment will be distorted or part of its wood will be raised. If the guitar is kept in very dry or hot environments, it may also suffer from cracking or breaking. The best place to keep a guitar or any other instrument is a normal-temperature environment — neither too hot nor too cold — where it is not exposed to moisture and direct sunlight.</p>\n\n<h2>Prepare the Necessary Accessories When Buying a Guitar</h2>\n\n<p>It is better to also consider buying beginner guitar packages when buying your guitar. These packages include a guitar, an amplifier, and accessories such as a guitar strap, cable, and pick. Such packages also exist for acoustic guitars (such as the Fender CC-60S package). Of course, you can buy the guitar separately and then purchase the necessary accessories for it separately as well.</p>\n\n<p>Except for items such as a pick, amplifier, and cable for electric guitars, buying other accessories is optional, but it is better to at least buy an extra set of strings so that you can quickly replace them if the strings become old. You can also purchase a fretboard and neck cleaner solution because it is very useful for maintaining and caring for the instrument. If you intend to purchase other accessories to increase creativity, a capo and a slide are good options and will increase the capabilities of the guitar.</p>\n\n<h2>Important Points for Maintaining a Guitar</h2>\n\n<p>As you know, after buying a guitar, you must take great care of your guitar so that over time the sound quality of your instrument does not decrease and the guitar is not damaged. By doing the following points, you can always keep your guitar healthy and get better and better sound quality from your instrument day by day.</p>\n\n<h3>Keeping the Guitar Clean and Tidy</h3>\n\n<p>After buying the guitar, you must always keep all parts of your instrument — including the body, neck, and strings — tidy and clean, and every once in a while, dust and clean your guitar with a soft cloth. This work, in addition to having a great effect on keeping the guitar beautiful, also prevents it from becoming old. On the other hand, over time, the frets and strings of the guitar take some of the dirt and finger grease from playing a lot, and this matter may have an undesirable effect on the sound quality of your instrument.</p>\n\n<h3>Maintenance in Suitable Temperature</h3>\n\n<p>You must always keep in mind that you should not expose the guitar to heat and high temperature because in the long run it causes the wood of the guitar to become old and damaged. Try to always keep your guitar or any other instrument you have in normal temperature and in a dry place.</p>\n\n<h3>Placing in a Suitable Cover</h3>\n\n<p>Always, after practicing or when you are not using the instrument, place it in strong and moisture-proof covers. Too much moisture can cause serious and irreparable damage to the instrument.</p>\n\n<h3>Guitar Strings</h3>\n\n<p>If for any reason you do not intend to use the instrument for a while, loosen the guitar strings and empty the tuning of the guitar, because the tightness of the strings in the long run can lead to changing the shape of the guitar neck.</p>\n\n<p>The points mentioned above for maintaining the instrument after buying a guitar are for guiding you, students, and lovers of the guitar instrument so that when buying, you can obtain a suitable guitar and after buying your suitable guitar, take good care of it so that the quality of the instrument gets better day by day.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding buying a guitar, please share them in the comments section of the article with us to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-09-03 21:49:24', 0, '2025-02-14 01:00:04', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1505, 'posts', 48, 'en', NULL, 'Comprehensive guide to buying a piano for beginners and professionals | Most important specifications along with introduction to 3 popular and affordable models', NULL, 'This article provides a comprehensive guide to buying a piano, covering different types of pianos, key purchasing considerations, and well-known brands. Pianos are mainly divided into acoustic and digital types. Acoustic pianos offer a more natural sound and come in grand and upright models, while digital pianos are more affordable, lightweight, and feature-rich, making them ideal for beginners.\n\nImportant factors when buying a piano include budget, purpose (hobby or professional use), sound quality, number of keys, key sensitivity, and brand reputation. Consulting professionals and choosing reliable brands is highly recommended. Popular brands such as Yamaha, Casio, and Roland are highlighted for their quality and innovation.\n\nThe article also discusses piano prices in the Iranian market, compares different types of pianos, and provides guidance on buying second-hand pianos. Ultimately, it emphasizes that choosing the right piano depends on individual needs, skill level, and budget, and that mastering the instrument requires patience and consistent practice.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>The piano is known as one of the most attractive and popular instruments in the world. On the other hand, <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">buying a piano</span></strong></span> is a difficult and complicated process for beginners and newcomers because they usually do not have knowledge of the different types of pianos available, their prices, or other important specifications.</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, in this article on the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz website</a>, we aim to help you purchase the piano of your choice by providing a comprehensive piano buying guide so that you can make a better purchase with sufficient information and knowledge of the types of pianos available in the Iranian market.</p>\n\n<h2>Choosing the Type of Piano – The First Step in Buying</h2>\n\n<p>To determine the type of piano you want, you must first answer the following questions about your goal for acquiring and buying a piano:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>What budget do you have in mind for buying a piano?</li>\n  <li>What is your purpose for buying a piano? For hobby or professional playing?</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>After answering the above questions, it is time to get to know the types of pianos. The two most commonly used types of pianos available in the Iranian market are:</p>\n\n<h2>Types of Pianos</h2>\n\n<h3>1. Acoustic Piano</h3>\n\n<p>The acoustic piano is the oldest and best type of piano, with all its parts being mechanical, which creates a very natural and high-quality sound.</p>\n\n<p>Acoustic pianos come in two types: grand (pianos used in concerts that have much larger dimensions than other pianos) and upright (vertical or Upright).</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3320\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"900\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3320 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/gx-2_1_617.jpg\" alt=\"Grand Model Acoustic Piano\" width=\"900\" height=\"600\" /> Grand Model Acoustic Piano[/caption]\n\n<p>The fundamental differences between grand and upright pianos lie in their dimensions, placement method, and string length. Because grand pianos are designed in larger sizes, it is much easier to achieve compatibility between the strings and keys, and the dimensional design limitations of upright pianos are not seen in grand pianos.</p>\n\n<p>The strings in a grand piano are placed horizontally, and these pianos have a wing-like shape. In upright pianos, the strings are placed vertically next to each other, which is why their sound quality is lower than that of grand pianos.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3322\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"720\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3322 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Klavier_K_Black_03.jpg\" alt=\"Upright or Vertical Model Acoustic Piano\" width=\"720\" height=\"720\" /> Upright or Vertical Model Acoustic Piano[/caption]\n\n<h3>2. Digital or Electronic Piano</h3>\n\n<p>Digital or electronic pianos are another type of piano that are much more affordable than acoustic pianos and have a much lower price. They are also the most diverse type of pianos.</p>\n\n<p>In these pianos, strings and hammers are not used, and the sounds heard are produced by digital sampling and storing these sounds in the piano’s electronic boards. When each key is pressed, the corresponding sound is heard.</p>\n\n<p>The quality of the sampled sounds taken from real pianos and used in the design of digital pianos greatly affects the price of these instruments. It is also better to consult with your teacher or a professional pianist before buying a digital piano and try to purchase from brands that have proven themselves.</p>\n\n<p>Another important point when buying digital pianos is the number of keys. Digital pianos are generally designed with either 61 keys or 88 keys. If you intend to play professionally or are a professional pianist, you should use complete 88-key pianos.</p>\n\n<p>Another important factor in buying digital pianos is the sensitivity of the keys and pedals. High-quality digital pianos are designed to measure the intensity of your finger strikes on the keys and produce louder or softer sounds accordingly.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_3323\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"810\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3323\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/YDP-145B_a_0001_a91446934e2022e1d4b89d1b76fe8697-300x300.jpg\" alt=\"Digital or Electronic Piano\" width=\"810\" height=\"810\" /> Digital or Electronic Piano[/caption]\n\n<p>Now that we are familiar with the two most commonly used types of pianos, the next step in buying a piano is to examine the prices of different pianos and compare them with your intended budget.</p>\n\n<h4>Advantages of Digital Pianos</h4>\n\n<p>Digital pianos, due to their much lower price compared to the acoustic type, are an excellent choice for starting to learn and for beginners and newcomers. Even higher-quality models of digital pianos provide very good sounds that sometimes meet the needs of semi-professional and professional players as well.</p>\n\n<p>Digital pianos are produced in smaller dimensions than acoustic pianos and therefore have less weight, and you will have much fewer problems when moving and transporting them.</p>\n\n<h2>Buying an Acoustic Piano</h2>\n\n<p>Currently, <strong>buying an acoustic piano</strong> — whether for a beginner or a professional player — requires sufficient and specialized knowledge of the piano. In such a way that a player or buyer needs complete knowledge of the instrument and a piano buying guide for an optimal choice. As you know, the level of playing improves over time and with more practice, and playing an instrument, in addition to being a hobby and art, becomes an enjoyable job for every person. Given that the piano is one of the most popular and beloved instruments in the world, playing it will be even more enjoyable.</p>\n\n<p>But unfortunately, there are many people who, without having the necessary and sufficient information for buying a suitable piano, proceed to purchase this relatively expensive instrument and only realize their mistake when it is too late and they have paid a high cost.</p>\n\n<h3>Types of Acoustic Pianos</h3>\n\n<p>Acoustic pianos are generally divided into two types: grand and upright. Grand-type acoustic pianos are mostly used for concerts and artistic music. Acoustic grand pianos are the most standard instruments for learning and are more expensive than upright acoustic pianos. Upright acoustic pianos have a much lower price; moreover, this type of piano is more suitable for use in apartment homes that have more limited space compared to concert halls and villa houses.</p>\n\n<p>Upright pianos are a good option for starting your learning, but their sound is not as beautiful as the grand type. However, this point should not be forgotten that at the beginning of work and starting to play and for use in limited spaces, they are a very suitable option. In general, the upright model of acoustic piano is better than digital piano models and weaker than the grand model of acoustic piano.</p>\n\n<p>The lifespan of upright acoustic pianos is between 20 to 30 years, and grand acoustic pianos is between 30 to 50 years.</p>\n\n<h3>Acoustic Piano Buying Guide</h3>\n\n<p>If your purpose for buying a piano is professional playing and the price of the piano does not affect your choice, it is strongly recommended that you definitely go for buying an <strong>acoustic piano</strong>. In addition to having a strong and excellent sound, these models of pianos are also incomparable in terms of beauty with digital pianos and are much more beautiful than digital ones. In such a way that the presence of an acoustic piano in your home multiplies the beauty of your house.</p>\n\n<p>It should be noted that acoustic pianos also have more durability and strength compared to digital-type pianos. That is why they are much more expensive than digital pianos. Finally, if you decide to buy an acoustic piano, study the following points very carefully so that when buying an acoustic piano, you are familiar with the important and vital points in buying a piano:</p>\n\n<h4>Buying an Acoustic Piano from Reputable Brands</h4>\n\n<p>The first step in buying acoustic pianos is to become familiar with reputable brands in the field of acoustic piano production and manufacturing. Two of the famous and reliable brands in making acoustic pianos are the “Korg” and “Dynatone” brands.</p>\n\n<h4>Consulting with a Specialist or Professional Piano Players</h4>\n\n<p>You should keep in mind that if you do not have sufficient information about buying a piano, be sure to consult with a specialist throughout the buying process, and at the time of the final purchase of the piano, ask that specialist or piano player to accompany you. Because that person will thoroughly examine the acoustic piano you want and point out the strengths and weaknesses of the piano for you. In this way, you can be sure of the health of your purchase.</p>\n\n<h4>Sound Quality</h4>\n\n<p>When buying acoustic pianos — whether grand or upright — the first thing that is examined is the sound quality of the instrument. You must listen to the sound of the piano very carefully when buying, because each acoustic piano has its own special sound, which is why the final choice depends on your taste and ear.</p>\n\n<h4>Standard Keys</h4>\n\n<p>The piano keys must have the necessary resistance against pressure. Also, examine all the keys visually to ensure they have no cracks, protrusions, or indentations.</p>\n\n<h4>The Bottom of the Piano</h4>\n\n<p>When <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%b3%d8%a7%d8%b2/\">buying an instrument</a>, be sure to examine all parts of it, especially check the bottom of the piano, because it is often overlooked. Make sure that the wood used in the construction of the lower parts of the piano is of good quality and that the woods have no cracks or splits and look completely healthy. Be sure to look under the piano; there are wooden pieces under it to which strings are attached. Make sure there is no large crack under the piano, but do not worry if there are small cracks.</p>\n\n<h4>Pedals</h4>\n\n<p>Be sure to examine the pedals of the piano; because when pressing the pedal, you should feel a little resistance. You must also check the movement of the dampers when moving the pedals.</p>\n\n<h4>Strings, Hammers, and Dampers</h4>\n\n<p>When buying a piano, with the help of the seller, carefully examine the internal parts of the piano, which include the strings, dampers, and hammers. Make sure that when you move the strings, the hammers also move correctly. Likewise, when you move the pedals, the dampers should move. Finally, look closely at the hammers to make sure the woods used in them have not become worn. Regarding the strings, because they play the role of sound production in the piano and bear a lot of pressure, be sure to ensure their health.</p>\n\n<h4>Valid Warranty</h4>\n\n<p>One of the most important points you should pay attention to when buying a piano is that acoustic pianos must have at least a 5-year warranty. Therefore, it is better to make your purchase from reputable agencies and centers in this field.</p>\n\n<h4>Final Word Regarding Buying an Acoustic Piano</h4>\n\n<p>If you are a professional piano player, depending on your needs and budget, you can choose a piano from among the grand and upright models. But if you are a beginner piano student, get advice about buying a piano and, after consulting with your teachers and according to the budget you have, proceed to buy an acoustic piano.</p>\n\n<h2>Prices of Different Types of Pianos in the Iranian Market</h2>\n\n<p>The piano is considered one of the most expensive instruments in the world. Upright acoustic pianos currently have a price of over 200 million tomans in the lowest state in the Iranian market. The acoustic pianos available in the country are generally priced at a minimum of over 500 million tomans, but as we said earlier, the price of digital or electronic pianos is more reasonable compared to acoustic pianos, and good pianos can be found for amounts around 30–40 million tomans or even less.</p>\n\n<p>Of course, in recent years, portable digital pianos have also entered the Iranian market, and these pianos can be purchased for prices even less than 20 million tomans.</p>\n\n<p>The most famous brand of digital pianos available in Iran is the Yamaha brand, which produces digital pianos in different qualities and prices. This gives piano buyers a relatively wide range of choices for selecting a piano according to their budget.</p>\n\n<h2>Introduction to Reputable and Well-Known Brands for Buying a Piano</h2>\n\n<p>For each type of piano, there are various brands and manufacturers that produce pianos in different prices and qualities. Before buying a piano, you should become familiar with reputable and well-known brands so that you can make your purchase with more precision and awareness. In the following, we introduce some of the most famous and best brands for buying a piano.</p>\n\n<h3>Yamaha Piano</h3>\n\n<p>Yamaha is a very authentic and old Japanese brand for buying a piano that has a high reputation not only in the piano manufacturing industry but also in other musical instruments in general. This reputation is due to providing high-quality and affordable products in the field of musical instruments. One of the most important features of the Yamaha brand is a standard for pianos called Graded Hammer Standard (GHS).</p>\n\n<p>GHS is one of the most important features of Yamaha digital pianos. Examples of these pianos include: Yamaha P115, DGX-660, and P45.</p>\n\n<p>GHS is a feature that aims to bring the sound output of a digital piano closer to the sound of an acoustic piano. Digital pianos do not have hammers and strings, but nevertheless, this new technology and innovation by Yamaha tries to operate a system completely similar to the hammer and string system and produce sound.</p>\n\n<p>Besides the points mentioned, the Yamaha brand uses Pure CF Sound Engine in its pianos. This feature can be considered the most important feature of Yamaha digital pianos, which creates a good sound when played. Yamaha pianos such as P115 and P45 also have this feature. In addition to digital pianos, the Yamaha company also produces grand and upright acoustic pianos, and one of the best models of Yamaha acoustic pianos is the CFIIIS grand piano. This piano sets the standard for Yamaha pianos and is suitable for concert playing.</p>\n\n<h3>Casio Piano</h3>\n\n<p>The Casio brand is another famous and reputable manufacturer for buying a piano in the world. This brand is not specialized in acoustic pianos, but nevertheless, the Casio company produces very high-quality digital and electronic pianos. The system of Casio digital pianos is known as AIR (Acoustic Intelligence Resonator), which is designed to recreate the sounds of acoustic pianos as well and accurately as possible.</p>\n\n<p>Before buying Casio brand pianos, it is worth knowing that the pianos of this brand have Tri-Sensor Hammer Action keyboards. The existence of this feature in Casio pianos allows you to perform all piano styles as beautifully as possible and measure your speed when striking the piano keys. This adds more depth and dimension to the sound of the instrument. This is a special advantage when buying a piano, and therefore Casio brand pianos have their own special fans.</p>\n\n<h3>Roland Piano</h3>\n\n<p>Roland is another leading company in piano production. Roland brand pianos are the best piano models. The specialty of this company is the production of acoustic pianos, especially grand-type pianos. For example, the GP607 model is a grand piano that is produced in a smaller size to solve the problems of transporting and placing large pianos.</p>\n\n<p>The keyboard of this model is PHA-50, which is one of the main features that can attract your attention when buying an acoustic piano. Its keys are made of ivory and ebony. With the appropriate weight of the keys, it provides the best playing quality for professional players.</p>\n\n<p>Roland pianos use highly advanced SuperNatural Piano Modeling technology to produce a pure sound.</p>\n\n<p>The Roland company also produces digital pianos. These digital pianos are designed to imitate luxurious acoustic pianos and provide a sound very close to them. In addition, they produce upright or vertical pianos to save on the space occupied by the instrument and money. Roland is considered one of the luxury piano brands, and therefore has much higher prices compared to Yamaha and Casio pianos.</p>\n\n<p>You should know that buying a piano is the first step and the beginning of your work, and after buying the piano, your determination and patience for learning and training on the piano is what will turn you into a successful pianist and player. Learning to play the piano well and becoming a professional player may take many years, but if you are interested in this instrument, the difficulties of training will certainly be bearable for you, and while learning this unique instrument, you can make this path easier and more enjoyable for yourself with great enthusiasm.</p>\n\n<h2>Keyboards and Their Different Types</h2>\n\n<p>Before buying a piano, be sure to examine different keyboards. As the name suggests, we know that a keyboard means a set of keys, but here we intend to talk about keyboards as a musical instrument. Like digital pianos, there are various types of digital keyboards as well. For example, one can mention portable keyboards, arranger keyboards, synthesizers, combiners, MIDI controllers, and so on; but it is not easy to distinguish these keyboards from each other.</p>\n\n<p>In general, different models and types of keyboards, except for the portable type, are professional musical instruments that usually cost more than 1000 dollars and are designed for composers, singers, music arrangers, and other musicians.</p>\n\n<h3>Portable Keyboards</h3>\n\n<p>Often, beginners and newcomers mistake keyboards for digital pianos. Usually, a portable or transportable keyboard is the first musical instrument that beginners consider for starting to learn the piano. The most important reason for this is their much lower and cheaper price compared to digital pianos.</p>\n\n<p>For a beginner who does not necessarily need to use and play an instrument for several years, these keyboards are a very attractive option; because beginners and newcomers do not face the problem of spending a lot of time on this tool to realize whether they want to continue working with it or not.</p>\n\n<p>You should also keep in mind that this approach is not without flaws, because you cannot fully realize and experience how playing these keyboards is similar to playing the piano; since portable keyboards rarely provide a relatively realistic level similar to acoustic and digital pianos in terms of sound output and especially key or keyboard performance. Ordinary portable keyboards are offered with 76, 73, or 60 semi-weighted or unweighted keys.</p>\n\n<p>Unlike the fully weighted keys found on digital and acoustic pianos, semi-weighted keys do not use hammers to recreate the feel of an acoustic piano. Instead, these keyboards use a spring-loaded structure that, although it adds some firmness and resistance to the keys, is much lighter compared to pianos.</p>\n\n<h4>Benefits and Advantages of Buying Portable Keyboards Compared to Buying a Piano</h4>\n\n<p>In addition to the advantage of a lower price compared to a piano, portable keyboards have other advantages as well. First of all, as their name suggests, they are easily portable. Most of these keyboards weigh about 15 to 20 kilograms, so you can easily move them and simply place them on a table and, when not in use, remove them from the table and put them in a cupboard.</p>\n\n<p>Another advantage of portable and transportable keyboards is the additional features and functions that come with them. Often, keyboards are loaded with hundreds of sounds, rhythms, songs, and other bells and whistles. While most people prefer quality over quantity, and although many of the built-in and inherent sounds of keyboards are unrealistic, this is exactly an advantage for those who want to explore different musical tools and styles and be entertained by interactive features, characteristics, and modes.</p>\n\n<h4>Portable Keyboards – A Popular Choice Among Children</h4>\n\n<p>For the same reason mentioned above, the keyboard is a popular choice among children. But as we said before, all these points relate to your personal needs and your intention for buying a keyboard or piano and the goal you are trying to achieve. But we emphasize again that if playing the piano is your main goal, the keyboard is not a suitable option.</p>\n\n<h3>Synthesizer Keyboards</h3>\n\n<p>In fact, a synthesizer is a type of electronic keyboard that can produce and copy a very wide range of musical sounds and is generally used in music production. Synthesizers allow you to virtually create any sound you can imagine, such as the sound of other musical instruments, siren sound, wind sound, explosion sound, car sound, and hundreds of other sounds that were not mentioned. But how does this happen?</p>\n\n<p>To understand this, you should know that synthesizers come with a set of sounds with different waveforms that are pre-recorded, and therefore you can combine them and also change the attack, sustain, and release process of the sound and add filters to get the sound you need.</p>\n\n<h3>Arranger Keyboards</h3>\n\n<p>First and foremost, arranger keyboards are designed for professional musicians and provide a wide range of chord and rhythm patterns that match the rhythm, tempo, and style of any instrument you play. This feature allows arrangers, composers, and songwriters to quickly and easily produce an accompanying and supporting sound for playing along with a song (adding chords appropriate to the main melody of the musical piece) without needing the presence of musicians to play other live musical instruments.</p>\n\n<p>In some cases, cheaper arranger keyboards are called portable arranger keyboards, which are actually the same as the portable keyboards we talked about earlier.</p>\n\n<h3>Workstation Keyboards</h3>\n\n<p>Workstation keyboards are like computers embedded inside a keyboard. These keyboards combine a very wide range of tools and allow users to perform a large range of tasks including sound synthesis and integration, sound recording, sequencing, working with sound effects and filters, and more. Workstation keyboards are usually offered with hundreds or thousands of first-class sound patterns that can be customized using buttons and sliders that allow control of sound components while working.</p>\n\n<h2>Second-Hand Piano</h2>\n\n<p>Because the piano is considered an expensive item, buying it is in a way also an investment. There are many reasons why a person might want to buy a used and second-hand piano instead of a new one. Now, considering this point, in the continuation of this article, we intend to provide a second-hand piano buying guide as well and talk about everything you need. Continue with us in the following as well.</p>\n\n<h2>Reason for Buying a Second-Hand Piano?</h2>\n\n<p>Perhaps the most important question in this regard is why one should buy a second-hand piano instead of a new one, and can buying a used and second-hand piano meet our needs and be as useful as buying a first-hand piano?</p>\n\n<p>It should be said that if all the points mentioned are observed, yes. Pianos that were produced in the past and have been played with the same excellent quality for many years are still functional. That is why buying a second-hand piano can be a smart decision. In the continuation of this section, we will discuss the most important points that you need when buying a second-hand piano. Points that show that buying a second-hand piano is a good choice.</p>\n\n<h3>1. Cost Management and Savings</h3>\n\n<p>Perhaps the first and most important benefit of buying a second-hand piano is the cost management and savings you achieve. In this way, you can have a second-hand piano for a smaller amount, and if you pay attention to all the points mentioned in the continuation of this piano buying guide, you will buy a piano that will also have high quality compared to new samples.</p>\n\n<h3>2. High Sound Quality of the Piano</h3>\n\n<p>The sound quality of the piano is one of the important points regarding buying a piano. The more time passes and the instrument is used, the better the sound quality becomes. This happens because of the instrument being regulated.</p>\n\n<p>Regulation of the instrument means that the performance and function of the instrument’s actions and the materials used in the construction and production of the piano improve over time. Therefore, by buying second-hand pianos, you buy an instrument that has a better sound than its new sample, and for this reason, buying a second-hand piano can be considered a smart choice.</p>\n\n<h3>3. Suitable for Practice by Students and Beginners</h3>\n\n<p>If you intend to start playing the piano, buying a new and expensive piano for a person who is a beginner may not be the right thing. Because it will have a high cost, and there is also the possibility that you will give up playing the piano and turn to playing another instrument or abandon music and playing altogether. For this reason, it is better to buy a second-hand piano for your work and, after becoming professional, purchase a new piano for continuing your work.</p>\n\n<h3>4. Buying Special Models of Piano</h3>\n\n<p>Perhaps you intend to buy a specific model of piano from your desired brand, but the new sample of it has a high price that your budget does not reach. In these conditions, you can buy a second-hand sample of the same model at a much lower and cheaper price with less cost, and the interesting point is that in some cases it has better quality than the new sample.</p>\n\n<h2>Important Points for Buying a Second-Hand Piano</h2>\n\n<p>The piano is an expensive instrument, and therefore when buying it, you must pay attention to many points and issues so that you can prepare and buy an ideal option for yourself. In this section, we will discuss the most important points that should be paid attention to.</p>\n\n<h3>Paying Attention to the Sound Quality of the Piano</h3>\n\n<p>It is good to know that even two pianos that are from the same model and the same brand and are made on the same day also have different sound qualities from each other. So the first thing you should do before buying a second-hand piano is to examine the sound quality of the desired piano. Considering that the sound of the piano also depends on the place and method of maintenance and other matters, it is necessary to examine the sound quality well.</p>\n\n<h3>Correct Functioning of the Pedals</h3>\n\n<p>Before buying a piano, you should know that acoustic pianos are produced and offered with 2 or 3 pedals. It is better to make sure of the number of pedals on the piano and their correct functioning before buying. The pedals of the piano are used to make sound changes in the piano sound and ultimately improve the output sound of the instrument.</p>\n\n<h3>Examining the Piano Keys</h3>\n\n<p>The piano keys are the same as the piano keys that by pressing them you can hear the sound of the instrument. The main points regarding the quality of the keys are that there should be no gap between them and that the keys should be level with each other.</p>\n\n<p>Another point that is important in examining the piano keys is that you should place your finger on the keys and, without pressing it downward, see if they move well to the right and left or not? This movement to the right and left should be limited.</p>\n\n<h3>The Manufacturing Company and Brand of the Piano</h3>\n\n<p>As you know, many brands and companies produce pianos, each of which has different sound qualities. If you are interested in a particular brand or are accustomed to its sound, it is better that the second-hand piano you buy is also from the same brand.</p>\n\n<h3>Examining the Authenticity of the Piano Instrument</h3>\n\n<p>You should know that not all pianos are authentic, and before buying, you must carefully examine its authenticity. Most pianos that have been produced in recent years have a serial number that is printed on their body, and you can enter the website of the piano manufacturing company and enter the serial number in the required place to obtain complete information about the manufacturing brand, model, and other specifications of your piano. We recommend that you seek help from a specialist in this field so that you can buy an authentic and high-quality piano.</p>\n\n<h3>Tuning and Regulation of a Second-Hand Piano</h3>\n\n<p>In normal condition, an acoustic piano should be tuned twice a year, and if this is not done, it will naturally have a negative effect on the sound quality and performance of the instrument and, in addition to reducing its useful lifespan, may not have a desirable sound. For this reason, you must make sure of this before buying.</p>\n\n<h3>The Visual Beauty of the Piano Placement Location</h3>\n\n<p>One of the other important points that sometimes people even buy a piano for this reason is the beautiful appearance it adds to the decoration of the placement location. Before buying, check the piano well and examine its appearance.</p>\n\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding buying a piano, please share it with us to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-09-04 22:20:45', 0, '2025-02-14 01:00:19', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
