(1508, 'posts', 51, 'en', NULL, 'Comprehensive guide to buying a tar | Necessary tips for purchase | 10 different parts of the tar and features of a good instrument', NULL, 'This article provides a comprehensive guide to the tar, one of the most important traditional Iranian musical instruments. The tar is a plucked string instrument known for its rich and expressive sound and plays a key role in Persian classical music.\n\nThe article explains the structure of the instrument, including its main parts such as the bowl (kaseh), neck, skin membrane, bridge, strings, and frets, and how each component affects sound quality. It also offers practical tips for buying a good tar, such as checking sound clarity, ensuring the neck is straight, evaluating wood quality (especially mulberry and walnut), and examining the bridge, skin, and frets.\n\nAdditionally, it emphasizes that craftsmanship and material quality are more important than brand names when choosing a tar. Finally, the article provides maintenance tips, including protecting the instrument from heat, humidity, and physical damage, and storing it properly in a case to preserve its quality and longevity.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In the article <strong>Comprehensive Guide to Buying a Tar</strong> from the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com\">Sornaz website</a>, we intend to familiarize you with the different parts of the tar instrument’s structure and provide you with the necessary points you need when preparing and buying a tar so that you can buy a tar and start learning, studying, and playing this very attractive and lovable Iranian instrument with better knowledge. Stay with us in the continuation of this article...</p>\n<h2>Introduction to the Tar Instrument</h2>\n<p>In traditional Iranian music, from the past until now, there have been a wide range of musical instruments. Each of these musical instruments has its own special features. One of the plucked string instruments of Iranian music that has a very pleasant and ear-catching sound and, because of this beautiful sound, has found a place in the hearts of many people in Iran is the tar instrument. <strong>Types of tar</strong> are not only used in Iran but also in other countries such as <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%AC%DB%8C%DA%A9%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%A7%D9%86\">Tajikistan</a>, Azerbaijan, and Armenia for playing traditional music.</p>\n<p>The tar instrument is a symbol of the national musical identity of Iran, and its history dates back to about the fourth century AH. Of course, at that time, it was slightly different in appearance from today’s tars, and over time and according to the needs of the players and in order to produce better sound, the instrument makers made changes to its appearance until it reached its current form.</p>\n<p>The tar is one of the completely Iranian instruments, and with the help of this instrument, all the dastgahs and gushehs of Iranian music can be played. The great figures of Iranian music who created the radif of Iranian modal music were generally tar players. Among them, we can mention <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d8%a7%da%a9%d8%a8%d8%b1-%d9%81%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Mirza Ali Akbar Khan Farahani</a>, the great figure of the Farahani family, who was the founder of the Iranian music radif. After him, his sons Mirza Hossein Gholi and Mirza Abdollah continued their father’s path and gradually perfected the modal music radif. By teaching many students, they took a very big step in the elevation of national Iranian music.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the tar has been named the national instrument of Iran, and because of this and the very pleasant sound of the tar, it is considered one of the most popular Iranian instruments that has many fans.</p>\n<h2>Parts of the Tar</h2>\n<p>One of the most important issues in buying a tar is familiarity with the structure of this instrument and the different parts of the tar, as knowing the materials used in tar making leads to a better choice of instrument. Therefore, in the following, we examine the structure of the tar and its different parts:</p>\n<p><strong>1- Bowl:</strong> Technically also called the large bowl. As seen in the picture, it is the bowl-shaped part at the beginning of the instrument on which the skin and bridge are placed.</p>\n<p><strong>2- Naqareh:</strong> The crescent-shaped part that is located between the large bowl and the neck of the tar is called the naqareh or small bowl.</p>\n<p>The bowl and naqareh of the tar (or the large and small bowls of the tar) are generally made of mulberry wood. Each bowl is made as two symmetrical crescents and then connected to each other with special glue.</p>\n<p><strong>3- Neck:</strong> This is the neck of the instrument that connects the naqareh to the headstock, nut, and tuning pegs. As seen in the picture, the neck of the tar is made of two different materials. The brown parts are made of walnut wood, and the white part is made of camel bone.</p>\n<p><strong>4- Skin (Pusteh):</strong> The skin of the tar is made from the belly skin of a lamb. When placed on the bowl and naqareh, it makes the sound of the instrument more beautiful and gives resonance to the tar’s sound.</p>\n<p><strong>5- Tailpiece:</strong> The part that is installed on the large bowl to hold the beginning of the tar strings is called the tailpiece.</p>\n<p><strong>6- Bridge:</strong> It is made of wild goat horn and is placed on the skin of the tar. It is the place for adjusting the distance between the tar strings from each other, in such a way that usually 6 grooves are carved on it, each groove for the passage of one string, and in this way the distance between the strings is determined. Of course, in some cases there are more than 6 grooves, but this is not very common.</p>\n<p><strong>7- Tuning Pegs:</strong> The cylindrical pegs that are made of wood and are placed at the top of the tar neck. The tar strings are wound around them, and by turning these pegs, the tuning of the instrument can be changed.</p>\n<p>In recent years, due to the difficulty of tuning the instrument with wooden pegs and the problem of the tuning slipping with this type of pegs, metal pegs with gears have been used, which make the tuning of the tar much easier. Another type of pegs is the peacock headstock model, which has appeared in recent years. With these pegs, the tuning of the tar has become somewhat similar to guitar tuning, which both has a special beauty and makes tuning the instrument easier.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_3508\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"280\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3508 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/216x216.jpg\" alt=\"Metal tuning pegs of the tar\" width=\"280\" height=\"280\" /> Metal tuning pegs of the tar[/caption]\n<p><strong>8- Nut:</strong> The part that is located at the end of the tar neck and has 6 grooves for the passage of the instrument’s strings. Together with the bridge, it adjusts the distance of the strings from each other.</p>\n<p><strong>9- Strings:</strong> In the past, the tar had 5 strings. Darvish Khan, one of the great tar players of the Qajar era, added the drone (vakhun) string to the tar, and since then, the tar has been made with 6 strings. The strings of the tar are arranged in three pairs. One end is tied to the tailpiece, passes over the special places on the bridge, and finally each string is wound around its specific tuning peg.</p>\n<p><strong>10- Frets:</strong> The strands that are placed on the neck of the tar and show the location of the notes on the neck of the instrument are called frets. They are usually made from sheep gut, although in recent years plastic has also been used for the frets of the tar.</p>\n<p>Usually, 28 frets are tied on the tar, which show the location of the notes. Because the fret intervals in Iranian modal music are not equal (for example, the half-tone or ¾-tone intervals in different dastgahs are slightly different), these frets are movable on the neck of the instrument and are not fixed like the frets of a guitar.</p>\n<p>Sometimes 25 frets are also tied on the tar. In this case, the first fret (C# or Db), the ninth fret (F# or Gb), and the nineteenth fret (C# or Db) are not tied.</p>\n<p>Sometimes the neck of the instrument is made a little longer so that it extends over the naqareh, and in this way a few more frets are added to the instrument. These instruments are called “Vaziri neck tar” or “three-octave tar.”</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_3507\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"600\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3507 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/تار-سیامک-میرزاپور-2-600x686-1.jpeg\" alt=\"Three-octave tar or Vaziri neck tar\" width=\"600\" height=\"686\" /> Three-octave tar or Vaziri neck tar[/caption]\n<h2>Important Features for Buying a Good and Good-Sounding Tar</h2>\n<p>The tar instrument has been one of the popular instruments among the people and lovers of traditional Iranian music since ancient times. In the present era as well, every year it attracts the attention of many music students and people who start playing. Therefore, many people need to buy a tar and should become familiar with the features of this instrument and the factors affecting the quality of the tar so that when buying a tar, they choose their instrument with more knowledge and precision.</p>\n<p>The tar instrument is made by hand. Therefore, when buying a tar, you may see one model of the tar instrument with the name of one maker and one seal, but with several different materials in the market. So buying a tar instrument based on the seal and the name of the maker is not very accurate, and for this reason, you must have the necessary skills to buy the best type of tar. If you intend to buy a tar, stay with us in the continuation of this article so that we can provide you with the necessary information for buying a good and good-sounding tar.</p>\n<p>The quality and type of construction of the instrument has a great impact on the good sound of the tar. Therefore, for buying a tar, you must have sufficient information about all parts of the instrument, including the most important ones, namely the bowl and naqareh of the instrument, the neck, and the bridge.</p>\n<p>Currently, in most cities of the country, there are individuals and workshops for making tars that can prepare the required instrument for beginners and students and meet their needs.</p>\n<p>In the following, we have brought the important points you need for recognizing and buying a good and good-sounding tar:</p>\n<h3>1- Sound Production of the Tar Instrument</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>One of the very important features for buying a good tar is the existence of a clear sound in the lower part of the neck or the base of the neck (frets close to the naqareh) of the instrument.</li>\n  <li>When buying a tar, examine the neck of the instrument well so that it has no curvature or warping. To better understand this issue, none of the instrument’s strings should produce a “gez” sound. If you hear a “gez” sound and it is not resolved by changing the string and its fret, you will realize that the neck of the instrument is crooked or twisted.</li>\n  <li>One of the interesting features of good tar instruments is that over time and due to frequent playing, they become better-sounding. In such a way that old and good-sounding instruments have a high price, and these instruments sound better than new ones.</li>\n  <li>The type of material and quality of construction of the plectrum has a great effect on the sound of the tar, and when buying a tar, you must pay attention to its material and type.</li>\n  <li>Before buying a tar, be sure to pay attention to the volume and amount of the instrument’s sound so that its sound is not short or nasal. In other words, no “gez” sound should be heard when playing, and the sound quality of the instrument should be the same on all strings, and the sounds of the different strings should be in harmony with each other.</li>\n  <li>When <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%b3%d8%a7%d8%b2/\">buying an instrument</a>, try with the help of a tuner to check the sound of the C strings (the lower pair and the fifth or drone string) and the G strings (the middle pair) up to the end of the neck so that they do not go out of tune with each other. It is possible that at the beginning of the neck they are in tune, but at the end the G string falls behind or ahead.</li>\n  <li>In the tar instrument, when playing, there should be no extra sounds, and in better words, a clear and transparent sound should be heard from it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2- Bowl and Naqareh of the Tar</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>When buying a tar, check the size of the bowl and the opening of the naqareh so that they are proportionate to each other. This issue determines the sound quality of the instrument.</li>\n  <li>The bowl of the instrument should not be heavy so that the instrument hangs toward the bowl, because if the bowl is heavy, it will tire the player a lot during playing.</li>\n  <li>The weight of the neck should be balanced with the bowl of the tar. To understand this issue, hold the base of the neck. Balance should be established in the instrument.</li>\n  <li>The sound quality of the instrument depends on the leveling of the bowl.</li>\n  <li>Again, we must emphasize and point out the lightness of the instrument because it is very important. Good instruments are usually made from mulberry and walnut woods, and mulberry wood is usually better-sounding.</li>\n  <li>When buying a tar, make sure that the wood used in the construction of the instrument does not have clay because it may crack over time.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>3- Quality and Material of the Skin Used in the Instrument</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>The way the tar skin is installed and the type of skin play a very important role in the bass and treble of the instrument’s sound. Unfortunately, nowadays artificial skin is used in the construction of the tar, and for the neck, plastic has replaced camel bone.</li>\n  <li>In the construction and production of the tar, the best type of skin is several-year-old skin, provided that it is not dry and prevents its vibration.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>4- Type of the Instrument’s Bridge</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>One of the most important criteria for buying a good and good-sounding tar is the material and shape of the instrument’s bridge.</li>\n  <li>One of the most important parts of the tar, and perhaps the most important part of the instrument, is its bridge, which is referred to as the heart of the instrument and must be made of wild ram horn.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>5- Type of the Instrument’s Neck</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>When buying a tar, pay attention to the fact that the distance of the strings from the skin should not be too much, in other words, the “mezrab-khor” of the instrument should be low. Because in this case, a good sound is not produced from the instrument. The material of the strings, the distance of the strings from the neck, and the material of the frets are also very important in choosing the instrument.</li>\n  <li>The neck of the tar should be made in such a way that the player’s hand can easily move up to the note “Ab” or the last fret.</li>\n  <li>Pay attention that if the neck of the instrument is flat, the sound will be “gez-dar.” Therefore, pay attention to this issue when buying a tar.</li>\n  <li>Nowadays, various tars are made, including the traditional headstock and the peacock headstock. The traditional headstock has more authenticity, and the peacock headstock is for easier tuning.</li>\n  <li>The neck of the tar instrument should have a fish-back shape and be well carved.</li>\n  <li>The thickness of the tar neck from bottom to top should be the same and should be made of black walnut wood or ebony.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>6- Type of the Instrument’s Frets</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>The frets of the tar instrument must be well knotted because if they are thick, they will bother the hand.</li>\n  <li>One of the main reasons for the “gez” sound in the tar instrument is the frets, which are usually ninety percent on the yellow string.</li>\n  <li>When buying a tar, also pay attention to the material and size of the frets; they should neither be too thick nor too thin.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Method of Maintaining the Tar Instrument</h2>\n<p>In maintaining the tar instrument, weather conditions have a great impact, in such a way that the best weather conditions for maintaining the tar are in moderate and spring-like weather, because usually scattered rains and wind blowing help preserve moisture. If you intend to travel to dry and desert areas of the country, there is no danger threatening your instrument, and you can take it with you with peace of mind.</p>\n<p>If you also intend to travel to the north of the country, due to the humid weather of northern Iran and the rainy nature of these areas in most seasons of the year, it is better to use moisture-absorbing bags or silica gel inside the instrument’s cover. To preserve the quality of the silica gel, every once in a while take it out of the case and place it in front of sunlight to regenerate it.</p>\n<p>As much as possible, do not hang your instrument on the wall because it may hit the ground and break. Never place the instrument in direct sunlight or heat. The safest place for maintaining the tar is its special case.</p>\n<p>When you intend to move house, avoid placing heavy and sharp objects next to the tar instrument as much as possible.</p>', '2023-09-10 22:13:44', 0, '2024-09-18 22:33:07', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1509, 'posts', 52, 'en', NULL, 'Comprehensive guide to buying a santur for beginners | 9 important tips for buying the best santur', NULL, 'This article provides a comprehensive guide to the santoor, a traditional Persian musical instrument, and explains how to choose the right one when buying. The santoor is a trapezoid-shaped, hammered string instrument played with two wooden mallets and has a wide tonal range of over three octaves. It typically features 18 bridges and 72 strings, producing a rich and resonant sound.\n\nThe article introduces the main parts of the instrument, such as bridges, strings, tuning pegs, and the soundboard, explaining how each component contributes to sound quality. It also highlights the importance of understanding different types of santoors and the materials used in their construction.\n\nKey factors affecting the price include the quality of materials (especially the type of wood), craftsmanship, and sound quality. The article խորհուրդs testing the instrument before purchase whenever possible.\n\nImportant buying tips include checking the finish and wood quality, avoiding poor coatings, ensuring the instrument holds tuning well, evaluating string quality, and confirming that the body is straight and produces a consistent sound. Ultimately, choosing a good santoor depends on sound preference, build quality, and careful inspection.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In buying a santur, one of the important points is having sufficient knowledge about the different types of santur instruments. In this article from <a href=\"https://sornaz.com\">Sornaz Music School</a>, we intend to familiarize you with the types of santur instruments, the different parts of the instrument, and the approximate price of santurs in various qualities so that you can purchase and buy a santur with a better perspective.</p>\n<h2>Introduction to the Santur</h2>\n<p>As you know, the santur is one of the authentic and ancient Iranian instruments that has a very pleasant sound, and anyone who is interested in Iranian music and the radif develops a special fondness for this good-sounding instrument.</p>\n<p>The santur is classified as a plucked string instrument that is played using two wooden plectrums (mallets), and in appearance, it has a symmetrical trapezoidal shape.</p>\n<p>Usually, felt is placed on the front part of these mallets to prevent the sharpness of the instrument’s sound and create a softer sound. Of course, in the past, to produce a softer sound before adding felt to the tips of the mallets, santur players would place a cloth on the instrument and then play it, which would make the sound softer and reduce the sharpness of the santur’s sound.</p>\n<p>The tonal range of the santur is more than three octaves, and in this respect, it is considered one of the instruments with the widest range in Iranian music.</p>\n<p>Santurs that are currently most used and popular among players of this instrument usually have 18 bridges, with 4 strings passing over each bridge that are tuned the same way. Therefore, each note is produced by sounding 4 strings, which is why the volume of the santur instrument is higher than other instruments.</p>\n<p>Of course, it should be noted that the high number of strings per note on the santur does not mean that this instrument is not suitable for playing songs with low and gentle sounds. Playing gentle melodies is easily achieved by striking the strings more softly with the mallets, which is gradually obtained with progress in playing techniques.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_3521\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"900\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3521 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/santoor_gohar_vije_1.jpg\" alt=\"Santur, a popular Iranian instrument\" width=\"900\" height=\"600\" /> The santur is one of the popular instruments of traditional Iranian music.[/caption]\n<p>As mentioned, usual santurs are made with 18 bridges, with 4 strings passing over each bridge. Therefore, in total, the santur has 18 × 4 or 72 strings, and there are also the same number of tuning pegs on this instrument.</p>\n<p>To tune the santur, we must use these pegs. These pegs are usually made of steel. For tuning the instrument, we use the special tool for this purpose, namely the tuning wrench or tuning hammer, which by placing it on the pegs and turning the wrench, we can tune the santur strings. On the left side of the santur, the tailpiece is installed on the side. The tailpiece is used to hold the strings and is usually made of steel.</p>\n<p>The bridges of the santur are divided into two groups of 9, with 9 of them close to the tuning pegs on the right side of the instrument, and the other 9 bridges can be said to be in the center of the instrument and slightly inclined to the left. This design causes the bass notes to be on the right side of the instrument and the treble notes to be on the left side of the instrument.</p>\n<p>The trapezoidal box of the santur, which is also called the resonant box, is made of wood and is generally made from cypress and walnut woods. The longer base of the trapezoid is about 90 cm, and the shorter base is about 35 cm. The height of the santur’s resonant box is designed between 8 to 10 cm.</p>\n<h2>Why Do We Need a Santur Buying Guide?</h2>\n<p>One of the most important factors for starting to play any instrument is <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%b3%d8%a7%d8%b2/\">buying a suitable instrument</a>, which has a great impact on your playing and increasing your motivation for more practice. Therefore, for playing the santur, we first need to study information about the types of santurs, its parts, and the quality of the materials used in the instrument so that we can buy a santur with more knowledge and a better perspective.</p>\n<p>Of course, it goes without saying that consulting with a professional player or a santur teacher is not without benefit because they have high experience in playing this instrument and will certainly be very helpful to students at the beginning of their journey in buying a santur.</p>\n<h3>Factors Affecting the Price of a Santur</h3>\n<p>The three important factors in buying a santur are the raw materials used in the construction of the instrument, the construction quality of the santur, and the sound quality produced by the instrument.</p>\n<p>When buying a santur, first pay attention to the maker of the instrument and then consider the price of the santur. The price of a santur, like any other instrument, varies according to the quality of the materials used in its construction, and the higher quality and better raw materials used in its production, the higher the price of the santur. In all instruments, the type of wood has a great impact on its price.</p>\n<p>After checking the quality of the materials and raw materials used in the instrument, when buying a santur, go to its construction method, which affects the price of the instrument. An instrument made with good raw materials but with low construction quality still has no value. A cheap santur may not be able to hold its tuning, and also weak and low-quality woods will warp and bend after a short time.</p>\n<p>When buying a santur, be sure to listen to the sound of the instrument. If you have the ability to play the santur, play with it for a few minutes, and if you do not have the ability to play the santur, ask the seller to play a little with the instrument for you so that you can hear the sound of the instrument, because ultimately the most important issue in buying a santur is that the sound of the instrument pleases you.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_3839\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"925\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3839 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/sornaz_santoor_01.jpg\" alt=\"Santur with light color\" width=\"925\" height=\"602\" /> Santur with light color[/caption]\n&nbsp;\n<h3>Important Points to Pay Attention to Before Buying a Santur</h3>\n<p>In order to make a successful purchase and choose a good santur, when <strong>buying a santur</strong>, pay attention to the following points:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Experience has proven that usually darker-colored santurs are better. Of course, you must be careful that a light-wood instrument has not been darkened with shellac and sold to you instead of dark wood. But also keep in mind that a good santur may have a light color and at the same time have acceptable and good quality.</li>\n  <li>Pay attention that the santur has not been colored with <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%B3%DB%8C%D9%84%D8%B1%D9%87%D8%A7\">shellac</a> because shellac, due to its covering property, covers the pores of the wood and reduces the quality of the sound produced by the instrument.</li>\n  <li>Another important point in buying a santur is the aesthetic appearance of the instrument, meaning that there should be no saw marks or heat burns on the wood.</li>\n  <li>Before buying a santur instrument, to make sure of the santur’s ability to hold tuning, experimentally retune a few strings and get help from experienced people for this task. One of the most important features of a good and high-quality santur, in addition to its good sound, is its ability to hold tuning and ease of tuning the instrument, because the santur has 72 strings, and if the instrument does not hold tuning well and technically goes out of tune quickly, or the quality of the tuning pegs is low, it will cause problems for us in tuning the instrument’s strings and we will have to spend a lot of time tuning the instrument each time, which causes us to have less time for practice and get tired before starting practice.</li>\n  <li>The volume of the santur’s sound depends on personal taste, but in instruments that have a high volume of sound, the number of bridges and their thickness are reduced, and also light wood is used in their construction, which causes a reduction in the sound quality of the santur.</li>\n  <li>When buying a santur, distinguishing the beautiful and uniform sound of the instrument is a task that requires years of experience and skill in this instrument. Hearing extra sound in the instrument is related to the lack of proportion in the thickness of the soundboard, its material, and the placement of the bridges.</li>\n  <li>The quality of the strings used in the instrument is very important and should be paid special attention to when buying a santur.</li>\n  <li>In buying a santur, the suitable dimensions of the santur’s resonant box and the quality of the wood used in its construction are very important, and generally, walnut and cypress woods are used in making good santurs.</li>\n  <li>The bottom board of the santur should not have any curvature. To detect curvature or warping of the instrument on the bottom board, it is enough to place the instrument on a flat surface like a table and then check the different parts to see that they have no gap from the table and are completely placed on the table.</li>\n</ol>\n[caption id=\"attachment_3841\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"600\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3841 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/sornaz_santoor_03.jpg\" alt=\"The bridges of the santur must be well placed so that the sound quality of the santur does not decrease.\" width=\"600\" height=\"471\" /> The bridges of the santur must be well placed so that the sound quality of the santur does not decrease.[/caption]\n&nbsp;', '2023-09-12 22:30:12', 0, '2024-09-18 22:32:14', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1510, 'posts', 53, 'en', NULL, 'Comprehensive guide to buying a setar | Examination of setar structure and components | Prices of different setars | 8 important points regarding setar sound quality', NULL, 'This article provides a comprehensive guide to buying a Setar, a traditional Persian string instrument. It begins by معرفی the Setar as a soft, melodic instrument typically played in intimate settings, known for its simplicity and expressive sound. The main parts of the instrument—such as the body, neck, strings, frets, and tuning pegs—are explained, along with suitable materials (e.g., mulberry wood for the body and walnut for the neck).\n\nThe article also categorizes Setars into beginner, intermediate, and professional types. It highlights key factors affecting sound quality, including clarity, balance between low and high tones, absence of unwanted noise, and overall harmony between strings.\n\nAdditionally, practical buying tips are provided, such as checking the straightness of the neck, build quality, string height, instrument weight, smooth tuning pegs, and testing all frets.\n\nFinally, the article discusses the importance of the maker’s label (brand), buying from reputable stores, and proper maintenance—such as avoiding humidity, heat, and direct sunlight, and storing the instrument in a protective case.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>Buying a setar is the first step to starting to learn this instrument, which is somewhat difficult for beginners and those who are not very familiar with the structure and parts of the setar. Therefore, in this article from the series of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%b3%d8%a7%d8%b2/\">instrument buying articles</a>, we intend to provide a comprehensive guide for <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">buying a setar</span></strong> to reduce this complexity and difficulty for you a little.</p>\n<h2>Introduction to the Setar</h2>\n<p>The setar is one of the plucked string instruments in Iranian music that is played with the nail of the right index finger or a plectrum (which is also technically called a nail) placed on the right hand, and it has a very pleasant and gentle sound. Because of this pleasant and gentle sound, it has many fans.</p>\n<p>Master Abolhasan Saba, one of the prominent Iranian musicians, says about the setar: “The setar is an instrument that has the most closeness and affinity to the mystical atmosphere of Iranian literature and culture.”</p>\n<p>Also, Master Abolhasan Saba says elsewhere: “The setar is an instrument that is too little for one person and too much for two people.”</p>\n<p>Jean During, the French musician who wrote down and notated Mirza Abdollah’s radif, says about the setar: “The setar is the most inward and concentrated instrument. It is played more for oneself than for others.”</p>\n<p>In the same regard, Master Ahmad Ebadi, the great setar player, says: “The setar is an instrument of state, not form.” He continues to complete his statement by saying that the setar is an instrument suitable for playing in solitude, and all the charm of the setar lies in playing it calmly and with its gentle sound, while some players create more sound from the setar by striking powerful plectrums and use the setar for showing off in crowded gatherings and holding concerts, which is contrary to the nature of this instrument.</p>\n<p>From the collection of these statements about the setar instrument, it can be concluded that the setar is the best instrument for human solitude and is an instrument that speaks more than any other instrument with human inner feelings.</p>\n<p>The setar has a very close resemblance to the tanbur instrument and is considered from the family of tanbur and rebab instruments. Due to its simpler playing method compared to instruments such as tar, kamancheh, and santur, it has great popularity, and many players of other instruments choose the setar as their second instrument.</p>\n<h2>Parts of the Setar</h2>\n<p>In buying a setar and any other instrument, familiarity with the structure and parts of the instrument and the quality of its materials is of special importance. Therefore, in the following, we examine the main parts that make up the structure of the setar instrument:</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_3534\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"509\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3534 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/اجزاء-سه-تار.jpg\" alt=\"Parts of the setar instrument\" width=\"509\" height=\"409\" /> Parts of the setar instrument[/caption]\n<ul>\n  <li>Bowl: The setar has a pear-shaped bowl made of wood, which is usually made from walnut, mulberry, and maple woods for making the setar bowl. Among these, experience over time has shown that mulberry wood is the best option for making a setar bowl, and the result obtained from mulberry wood bowls has been better.</li>\n  <li>Soundboard on the bowl: On the setar bowl, a thin wooden board is placed, on which the setar bridge is located, which has 4 grooves, and together with the nut, it is responsible for adjusting the distance of the setar strings.</li>\n  <li>Neck: The setar neck is usually made of walnut wood, which causes better durability and greater resistance of the instrument’s neck for long-term use.</li>\n  <li>Headstock: The section at the top of the neck where the tuning pegs are installed is called the headstock or pegbox, and at the bottom of the setar headstock is the nut, which has 4 grooves and, together with the bridge, is responsible for adjusting the distance of the strings.</li>\n  <li>Tuning pegs: The tuning pegs of the setar are located in the headstock and are responsible for tuning the setar strings. The pegs are generally made of wood, but in recent years, the setar headstock is also made in another form known as the peacock headstock, in which case the tuning pegs are similar to guitar tuning pegs, and thus tuning is done much more easily.</li>\n  <li>Strings: The setar has 4 strings, one end of which is tied at the back of the bowl where the tailpiece is located, and after passing over the relevant grooves on the bridge and nut, the other end of the string is wound around its specific tuning peg.</li>\n  <li>Frets: On the neck of the setar, frets or, as the ancients called them, the “dastans” of the instrument are placed, which determine the locations of the setar notes. Usually, the setar is fretted with 25 or 28 frets. The frets of the setar are usually made from the gut of animals such as cows and sheep, but in recent years, nylon frets have also been used for the setar’s dastans.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In an instrument like the guitar, the frets, which are also technically called “frets,” are fixed because the intervals in Western music are fixed and without change. But in the setar instrument, due to the nature of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian modal music</a>, which does not have equal and tempered intervals, these frets are movable and not fixed.</p>\n<p>You can play it both sitting on the ground and on a chair. Hold the setar so that it forms a 45-degree angle with your thigh, and the hand can easily move along the neck of the instrument, and strike the strings with the index finger or a plectrum.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_3537\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"800\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3537 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/7edf62c5ea49fe6f8cf7a01335477fffb7d0729a_1640760122.webp\" alt=\"An example of a setar with a peacock headstock\" width=\"800\" height=\"800\" /> An example of a setar with a peacock headstock[/caption]\n<h2>Types of Setar</h2>\n<p>When buying a setar, they can be classified into three categories in terms of quality, which are:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Practice setar: Practice setars are suitable for starting for new players.</li>\n  <li>Ordinary setar: These types of setars can be used in the early years of playing and when you are still learning, and they meet your needs as a beginner player in the early years.</li>\n  <li>Professional setar: The construction quality of professional setars is very high and they are made for professional players who have been playing the setar for many years. They are used for recording works in studios and live performances in concerts and are the best type of setar in terms of sound production quality.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Sound Quality of the Setar Instrument</h2>\n<p>When buying a setar, you should know this point that the setar is an instrument with a very gentle and low sound. Usually, people who play the setar have a solitary spirit and are away from noise. In the following, we mention 8 important points about the sound quality of the setar that you should pay attention to when buying a setar:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>The most important factor affecting the sound of the setar is the type and material of the instrument’s soundboard, and there should be no gap between its joints.</li>\n  <li>The high-neck sound and low-neck sound of the setar should be in harmony with each other.</li>\n  <li>When buying a setar, make sure that you do not have any problem performing the “nala” (vibrato) technique.</li>\n  <li>The sound of the setar should not be so low that you need a sound amplification device, nor so high that it becomes annoying.</li>\n  <li>The sound of the instrument should not be harsh, and when playing the setar, there should be no extra or tinny sounds.</li>\n  <li>The second string should not have a dull state, and its sound should be completely clear.</li>\n  <li>When playing the bass string of the setar, the sound of this string should be completely soft and natural.</li>\n  <li>When listening to the setar, the separation of the sounds of the different strings from each other should be difficult, and they should be in harmony with each other and follow one another. The more difficult it is to separate the sounds of the setar strings from each other, the better the sound quality of the instrument.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Important and Practical Points When Buying a Setar</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li>When buying a setar, the more regular and dense (more parallel and closer) the lines on the soundboard are, the better its quality.</li>\n  <li>If the setar string is rusty, you must definitely replace them. Even the best setars, depending on the type of strings and the player’s conditions and the duration of use of the instrument, need string replacement.</li>\n  <li>When buying a setar, pay attention to the material and quality of the instrument’s parts, and if necessary, get help from an experienced person to test the sound quality of the instrument.</li>\n  <li>After tuning the instrument, test all the frets of the instrument one by one so that they do not technically “bite” the sound.</li>\n  <li>When buying a setar, make sure that the instrument has no damage or scratches, and also check the connection between the string and the tailpieces so that it is firm. This ensures that when playing the setar, it does not go out of tune.</li>\n  <li>Pay attention to the fact that there should be a small distance between the string and the neck of the instrument so that the hardness of the strings is not felt during performance.</li>\n  <li>When buying a setar, check that there is balance between the bowl and the neck of the setar in terms of weight so that when playing, the instrument can be easily held.</li>\n  <li>When buying a setar, pay attention to the fact that the instrument should definitely have little weight.</li>\n  <li>If the wood of the setar neck is not old and does not have good quality, it may warp after some time. In fact, due to the pressure of the strings, it bends. Pay attention that the transverse distance of the first and last strings with the neck should not be different; if it is, it means the neck has warped.</li>\n  <li>The neck of the instrument should neither be too wide nor too narrow; it should have a suitable width and thickness.</li>\n  <li>The setar should not be too colored because it has a negative effect on its sound. Lacquer and alcohol have been used for polishing the instrument.</li>\n  <li>When buying a setar, the lighter the color of the instrument, the better it is from a psychological point of view, and also if there is any defect, it is clearly visible.</li>\n  <li>When buying a setar, make sure that the pegs are soft and smooth so that the instrument can be tuned easily.</li>\n  <li>Use a tuner for tuning the instrument so that the strings do not become too tight and do not put pressure on the neck.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The Seal of the Setar</h2>\n<p>In Iran, there are various setars with different names and seals, where the seal is actually the name of the maker or his pseudonym. When buying a setar, the mark or seal of the instrument affects its price, and famous marks have higher prices, but a good mark may be cheaper than its sound quality in the market due to not being famous. Therefore, for buying a setar, refer to reputable stores and pay special attention to the points mentioned in this article.</p>\n<h2>Important Points for Maintaining the Setar</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li>You should not place the setar in humid environments, and it should also not be exposed to direct sunlight and heat because it causes the instrument to change shape.</li>\n  <li>When you are not using the instrument, place it in its case and cover.</li>\n  <li>If you intend to perform a specific dastgah from the radif for a concert or in a group, it is better to tune the setar to that dastgah from one month before the performance day so that the instrument gets used to that tuning and does not go out of tune during performance and has a better tone and sound.</li>\n  <li>Use a dehumidifier in the instrument’s case.</li>\n  <li>Never play the instrument with dirty and greasy hands, and wash your hands before playing the instrument.</li>\n  <li>Use the instrument every once in a while and play it because if you do not use it for some time, the sound of the instrument changes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding the setar instrument, please share it in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this content with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com\">the Sornaz Music School website</a>.</p>', '2023-09-13 21:22:26', 0, '2024-08-04 20:26:16', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
