(1511, 'posts', 54, 'en', NULL, 'Comprehensive guide to buying a kamancheh | Introduction to kamancheh components | Essential tips and specifications for buying a kamancheh | Introduction to 5 reputable kamancheh manufacturing brands', NULL, 'This article provides a comprehensive guide to buying a Kamancheh, a traditional Persian bowed string instrument. It begins by introducing the instrument, its structure, and its historical background, including references in the works of Al-Farabi and its recognition by UNESCO.\n\nThe main components of the Kamancheh—such as the resonating body, skin, bridge, neck, tuning pegs, strings, and bow—are explained, along with their roles in sound production. The article also highlights key characteristics of a high-quality instrument, including proper construction, symmetry, and the absence of cracks or defects.\n\nImportant buying tips include evaluating the sound quality, choosing appropriate wood (such as mulberry or walnut), checking wood density, ensuring proper string height (action), and selecting a size suitable for the player’s hands. Testing the instrument across different tonal ranges is also recommended.\n\nFinally, the article discusses choosing a suitable beginner instrument, consulting experts, different types of Kamancheh (by size and design), and the impact of materials like wood and skin on the overall sound quality.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>The kamancheh, as one of the traditional instruments of Iranian music, has many fans. Therefore, in this article, we decided to provide a comprehensive guide for <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">buying a kamancheh</span></strong> in the series of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%b3%d8%a7%d8%b2/\">instrument buying articles</a> so that you become familiar with the main parts of the kamancheh instrument and we explain the necessary and essential points and specifications for preparing and buying a kamancheh so that you can purchase a kamancheh with better knowledge.</p>\n<h2>Introduction to the Kamancheh Instrument</h2>\n<p>Before buying a kamancheh, you should become somewhat familiar with this instrument, its history, and its appearance. The kamancheh is a bowed string instrument in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a>. Its bowl, neck, head, and base that is placed on the ground or the player’s knee form its components. There is a type of open-back kamancheh that is played among the <strong>Lur</strong> people and is known as “<strong>Tal</strong>.”</p>\n<h3>A Review of the Historical Background of the Kamancheh</h3>\n<p>In the book “Al-Musiqi al-Kabir” by <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%81%D8%A7%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A8%DB%8C\">Abu Nasr Farabi</a>, the kamancheh instrument is mentioned. He used the Arabic equivalent of kamancheh, namely “<strong>rebab</strong>,” in his book. The kamancheh was among the main instruments of Iranian music during the Safavid and Qajar dynasties. The kamancheh was registered as a world heritage by UNESCO in Azar 1396 solar year.</p>\n<h3>The Appearance of the Kamancheh Instrument</h3>\n<p>The spherical and hollow bowl of the kamancheh is made of mulberry wood in the form of ribs consisting of pieces of wood placed next to each other. A small part of it in the front is dedicated to the opening, on which skin is stretched. On the skin, a bridge similar to the tar bridge, but not completely, is placed perpendicular to the strings, on which there are grooves for the passage of the instrument’s strings.</p>\n<p>The neck of the kamancheh is a solid tube that is turned in the shape of an inverted cone, and the upper end of the tube is hollow and has a slit in the front that plays the role of the pegbox.</p>\n<p>The neck of the kamancheh has no frets. The pegbox is located at the top of the instrument. On both sides of it, two pegs are installed, and a dome that stands out at the top of the box. The length of the instrument up to the dome is 80 cm.</p>\n<p>In the past, this instrument did not have a tailpiece, and the strings were directly attached to the instrument. After the violin entered Iran, small tuning screws known as fine tuners were added to the kamancheh, making tuning the instrument easier and more precise for the player. Initially, the strings of this instrument were made of silk or twisted animal gut, which sometimes did not provide acceptable and required tuning. In the continuation of the path of change and transformation, violin strings replaced kamancheh strings.</p>\n<p>The kamancheh is a bowed string instrument and has 4 strings. Of course, it initially had 3 strings, and following the violin, a fourth string was added to it. The strings of the kamancheh extend along the instrument and produce a pleasant and captivating sound. However, it is somewhat nasal.</p>\n<h3>The Playing Style of the Kamancheh</h3>\n<p>The base of the kamancheh is placed on the ground, a chair, or the player’s knee who is sitting, and it is played with a bow or kaman. During performance, the instrument rotates a little around its axis, and this capability makes it easier for the bow to contact the strings.</p>\n<p>The Iranian bowed string instrument kamancheh is held vertically in the left hand, and the player’s fingers slide on the strings. The bow is also drawn on the strings with the right hand.</p>\n<h2>Familiarity with the Different Parts of the Kamancheh Instrument</h2>\n<p>One of the important points in buying a kamancheh that should be mentioned is knowing the different parts of the kamancheh instrument, which we introduce below and state the material used in the construction of each:</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_3670\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"500\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3670 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/sornaz_kamancheh_002.jpg\" alt=\"Different parts of the kamancheh\" width=\"500\" height=\"500\" /> Different parts of the kamancheh[/caption]\n<h3><strong>1- Resonant Bowl:</strong></h3>\n<p>As seen in the figure above, the spherical section at the bottom of the kamancheh, on which the skin is mounted and made of wood, is called the resonant bowl, which is for amplifying the sound of the instrument.</p>\n<h3><strong>2- Skin:</strong></h3>\n<p>The skin that is placed on the resonant bowl of the kamancheh, and usually the skin of a lamb’s belly, goat, or deer is used to make it.</p>\n<h3><strong>3- Bridge:</strong></h3>\n<p>The part that is placed on the skin of the instrument and is the place for the passage of the instrument’s strings is called the bridge. Usually, there are 4 grooves on the bridge, each for the passage of one of the kamancheh’s strings. The bridge is usually made of bone or wood.</p>\n<h3><strong>4- Neck:</strong></h3>\n<p>The wooden neck that connects the bowl to the headstock of the instrument and is the place for fingering the kamancheh is called the neck. The neck of the kamancheh is usually about 25 cm long, and like many bowed string instruments (such as violin and cello), there is no fretting on the neck of the kamancheh, and technically it is “fretless.” The front part of the neck, which is the place for fingering the instrument, is also called the fingerboard, which is generally made of bone or wood.</p>\n<h3><strong>5- Headstock:</strong></h3>\n<p>The place where the tuning pegs and the nut of the kamancheh are located is called the headstock.</p>\n<h3><strong>6- Tuning Pegs:</strong></h3>\n<p>The cylindrical pegs that are located in the headstock and are installed for connecting the ends of the strings and tuning the kamancheh strings are called tuning pegs. The number of tuning pegs of the kamancheh is equal to the number of strings of the instrument (4).</p>\n<h3><strong>7- Nut:</strong></h3>\n<p>The part that is located at the end of the instrument’s neck and in the headstock, and on which there are 4 grooves for the passage of the kamancheh strings, is called the nut.</p>\n<h3><strong>8- Tailpiece:</strong></h3>\n<p>The metal part that is located under the resonant bowl and to which the beginning ends of the strings are attached is called the tailpiece. Also, there are small screws on the tailpiece for tuning the kamancheh, which are used for more precise tuning of the strings.</p>\n<h3><strong>9- Strings:</strong></h3>\n<p>The strings of the instrument are attached to the tailpiece at the beginning and then, after passing over the grooves of the kamancheh bridge, are stretched along the neck of the instrument and pass through special grooves on the nut and are wound around the tuning pegs of the kamancheh at the end.</p>\n<h3><strong>10- Bow:</strong></h3>\n<p>The bow or kaman, which is the tool for playing the kamancheh, is made of a piece of wood 60 cm long, to which thin strands, usually made of horse tail hair, are attached. By drawing them on the strings, the kamancheh is sounded.</p>\n<h2>Important Features for a Good Kamancheh</h2>\n<p>The kamancheh instrument is made of various parts that are placed together and produce the special sound of the kamancheh instrument. Therefore, the construction quality of each of these parts plays a very important role in the desired sound production of the instrument. The three main parts in the construction of the kamancheh instrument are the “bowl,” “neck,” and “headstock.” Each of these parts must be made precisely and according to standards and be in harmony and proportion with the other parts.</p>\n<p>Therefore, when buying a kamancheh, pay attention to this point that the appearance of the instrument has a direct connection with its sound production and determines the sound quality of the instrument. The structure of the kamancheh instrument must have a completely symmetrical form, and all its parts must be connected to each other in the best possible way. The existence of breakage or cracks in the instrument indicates the poor construction quality of the kamancheh.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>Choosing and Buying a Suitable Kamancheh</h2>\n<p>When buying a kamancheh, listening to the sound of the instrument is the most important issue for choosing a good kamancheh. The quality of the sound produced by the instrument depends on many parameters, the most important of which are the material of the kamancheh’s parts and the delicacy in their construction. Most parts of the kamancheh instrument are made of wood; therefore, paying attention to the density of the wood texture used is very important. The denser the texture of the wood used for making the different parts of the kamancheh, the clearer and more resonant the sound of the instrument will be.</p>\n<p>For buying a kamancheh, you should know that the woods of walnut, mulberry, maple, ash, and malaj trees are the most suitable options for making a kamancheh, among which mulberry and walnut are the best. The distance between the strings and the surface of the instrument’s neck is also of special importance in the sound quality produced by the kamancheh. This distance is called “action,” and if it is more than the standard amount, the sound of the instrument will have little resonance and will become tinny.</p>\n<p>One of the most important points when choosing and buying a kamancheh is the suitable dimensions of it relative to the player’s hands. Therefore, when choosing and buying a kamancheh, pay special attention to the dimensions of the instrument’s neck. It is also recommended to evaluate the sound of the instrument in various sonic regions to ensure its integrated and precise construction.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>Buying the Best Kamancheh for Beginners</h2>\n<p>When buying a kamancheh, do not forget this very important point that the student’s first instrument will play a very prominent role in their musical ear training. Choosing and buying a suitable kamancheh can cause the student to become more interested in the instrument and provide the groundwork for the student’s faster progress in kamancheh playing.</p>\n<p>For buying a kamancheh, it is recommended to act by considering the opinions of professional players and people who are specialized in this field and choose the appropriate instrument for yourself. For beginner and new students, buying a cheap or second-hand kamancheh may be a suitable option for starting kamancheh playing.</p>\n<p>It is better to consider the price of an educational kamancheh a little higher when determining priorities for buying a kamancheh so that you can prepare a well-made and good-sounding instrument.</p>\n<h2>An Important and Notable Point When Buying a Kamancheh</h2>\n<p>When buying a kamancheh, pay attention to this point that all the components of an instrument affect the quality of its sound production, and the only part that has no effect on the sound quality of the kamancheh is the decoration that is located at the top of the kamancheh’s headstock, and the only duty of this part is to beautify the kamancheh instrument. The kamancheh instrument is made in two forms: one-piece and ribbed.</p>\n<p>Kamanchehs are made in two types: open-back and spherical, and the wood material used in its construction is mulberry wood. The open-back kamancheh is made from one piece of wood, but the spherical kamancheh is made from both types, that is, both from one piece of wood and from ribbed wood.</p>\n<p>The way the kamancheh instrument is placed on the player’s leg is done by means of a base connected to the body by the tailpiece, and it is necessary to mention this point that you should know that the material of the strings of this instrument is the same as the material of violin strings.</p>\n<h2>Familiarity with Types of Kamancheh</h2>\n<p>Before buying a kamancheh, it is very necessary that you know its types. Kamancheh instruments are divided into four categories in terms of size, which are called (normal, alto, bass, contrabass), and in terms of brand, they also have five reputable brands, which are the names of these kamancheh-making brands (Nariman kamancheh, Ataei kamancheh, Khaleghi kamancheh, Ramin kamancheh, and Maleki kamancheh). Note that if you are also interested in bowed string instruments like the violin, you can now study the comprehensive guide to <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%86%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d9%88%db%8c%d9%88%d9%84%d9%86/\">buying a violin</a>.</p>\n<h2>What Material Is the Skin of the Kamancheh Made Of?</h2>\n<p>The skin of the kamancheh is made from the belly skin of a lamb or gut, and this is very vital because all the pressure of the bridge is on the skin.</p>\n<p>Another important point when buying a kamancheh is that if the sound quality produced by the kamancheh instrument is not good, you should know that a one-piece skin has not been used in the construction of the instrument.</p>\n<p>The third point in the construction of the kamancheh instrument that should be paid attention to before buying a kamancheh is that old skin must be used for the instrument so that the skin of the instrument has good quality and produces good sound.</p>\n<h2>What Is the Wood of the Kamancheh Made Of?</h2>\n<p>Usually, the wood used for the bowl, neck, and other parts of the kamancheh is from the mulberry tree, but sometimes walnut wood is also used for making the neck of the instrument, in which case the neck of the instrument will be heavy. When buying, you should know that the wood used in the instrument must be old so that the sound coming out of the instrument has better resonance.</p>\n<p>Some makers use fresh wood for making the kamancheh and boil the wood in water to remove its sap. After this, they bury the wood under the ground so that it dries and becomes rotten. With this work, distinguishing whether the wood used in the instrument is new or old when buying a kamancheh becomes very difficult and almost impossible.</p>\n<p>When buying a kamancheh, also pay attention to this point that the neck of the desired instrument should not be heavy and thick.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding buying a kamancheh, please share it with us to help improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-09-18 22:08:15', 0, '2024-08-04 20:29:55', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1512, 'posts', 55, 'en', NULL, 'Violin buying guide | What matters in choosing a violin? | 11 important and practical tips for buying a violin', NULL, 'This article provides a comprehensive guide to buying a violin, especially for beginners. It starts by introducing the main parts of the violin—such as the body, strings, bridge, fingerboard, bow, and tuning pegs—and explains how the quality of each component affects the instrument’s sound.\n\nIt emphasizes the importance of checking the violin’s physical condition before purchase, ensuring there are no cracks, warping, or defects. The smoothness of tuning pegs, the straightness of the fingerboard, and the overall build quality are also key factors. Listening to the sound of the violin before buying is essential, as appearance alone does not guarantee good sound quality.\n\nFor children, selecting the correct violin size based on age and hand size is crucial. Beginners are advised to consult a teacher or expert and preferably purchase the instrument in person.\n\nThe article also includes maintenance tips, such as keeping the violin away from humidity, storing it in a proper case, cleaning it regularly, replacing strings periodically, and loosening the bow after use.\n\nOverall, choosing a high-quality and well-built violin can significantly enhance motivation and learning progress for students.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>The violin is one of the most popular instruments in the world and also in Iran. In this article from the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>, we provide a comprehensive guide for <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">buying</span> <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">a violin</span></strong></span> to help you make the best possible choice for starting to play this very pleasant and listenable instrument. Stay with us...</p>\n<p>Before starting violin lessons, you need to buy a suitable violin. Buying a violin for beginner students is relatively difficult because they have little knowledge of the characteristics of a good violin. Therefore, to make a better and more informed choice when buying a violin, we first introduce the different parts of the violin and then provide the necessary points and tips for buying a violin, such as the price of the violin, the features of a good violin, and introducing reputable violin-making companies and brands.</p>\n<h2>Introduction to the Parts of the Violin</h2>\n<p>Knowing the structure and becoming familiar with the different parts of the violin greatly helps you in recognizing and buying a suitable violin. In the construction of a good and standard violin, specific standards must be observed, and all its parts must be completely healthy and of acceptable quality. Below, we introduce the different parts of the violin:</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_3789\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"700\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3789 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/sornaz_violin_01.png\" alt=\"Before buying a violin, you must fully know the different parts of the instrument.\" width=\"700\" height=\"951\" /> Before buying a violin, you must fully know the different parts of the instrument.[/caption]\n<h3>1- The Scroll of the Violin</h3>\n<p>The upper part of the violin is called the scroll, which is made in spiral shapes for the beauty and decoration of the violin. In most instruments, this part is hand-designed, made of wood, and has a decorative aspect that gives a special beauty to the violin.</p>\n<h3>2- Pegbox and Tuning Pegs</h3>\n<p>Below the scroll of the violin and attached to it is the pegbox, where the tuning pegs of the instrument are located. Each of the violin’s strings is tied to its own peg, and the pegs are used to tune the violin. Observing the standards related to the distance and size of this part in the violin is of very high importance.</p>\n<h3>3- Violin Strings</h3>\n<p>The violin has four strings that start from the tailpiece and continue to the tuning pegs and are tied at both ends. The strings of the violin are generally made of steel, aluminum, or animal gut with metal coatings.</p>\n<p>The strings of the violin are produced in different qualities, which will affect the sound production of the instrument. Using low-quality strings in the construction of a violin, even on professional violins, lowers the sound quality of the instrument.</p>\n<h3>4- Fingerboard or Fingerboard of the Violin</h3>\n<p>The fingerboard or fingerboard is the black section that is installed on the neck of the instrument and under the strings, which is usually made of black <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A2%D8%A8%D9%86%D9%88%D8%B3_(%DA%86%D9%88%D8%A8)\">ebony wood</a>. This part is for the player’s fingering and is completely flat. There is no fretting on the violin’s fingerboard, and technically it is “fretless” (frets in Western music are called frets). Therefore, the quality of this part is highly important in producing appropriate notes.</p>\n<h3>5- The Body of the Violin</h3>\n<p>The main part of the violin is its body, through which the sound of the instrument is amplified. When sound is produced through the vibration of the strings, it enters the body of the violin, and its reflection creates the beautiful and pleasant sound of the violin. The body of the violin is usually made of spruce or maple wood.</p>\n<p>The f-holes on the body of the violin are created for sound to enter the bowl of the instrument, which are called “f-holes” or “F holes.” Changing the location and size of the f-holes can affect the sound of the violin.</p>\n<h3>6- The Bridge of the Violin</h3>\n<p>The bridge is a wooden piece that is placed on the violin’s soundboard between the two f-holes. The bridge is used to adjust the distance between the strings and to separate the strings from each other. The strings pass over the bridge and are tied to the tuning pegs. The position of the violin’s bridge is very important because it is directly related to the quality of the sound produced by the violin. The height and curvature of the bridge are also very important and determine the distance between the strings.</p>\n<h3>7- The Chin Rest of the Violin</h3>\n<p>This part of the violin is made of wood or plastic and is installed on the body so that the player can easily place the violin under their chin. This piece was invented by Louis Spohr in the early nineteenth century so that the weight of the instrument rests on the shoulder and the player’s left hand is free.</p>\n<h3>8- The Bow of the Violin</h3>\n<p>The tool for playing the violin is called the bow, which is made of wood, and strands of horsehair are tied to the wood for playing.</p>\n<h2>Features to Pay Attention to When Buying a Standard Violin</h2>\n<p>For buying a violin and starting lessons and playing, in addition to the price of the violin, other details should also be considered, because the instrument you buy is one that you will practice with, and its standard and suitable sound will cause more motivation, better learning, and also higher enjoyment for you when performing violin training exercises.</p>\n<p>If the violin you choose for starting learning does not have at least the necessary standards, it may cause many problems for you during practice and fingering, and the unsuitable sound of the violin may cause demotivation and lack of good feeling when performing exercises and playing your favorite songs with the violin.</p>\n<p>To ensure that the violin you are buying is standard, you must examine all parts of the violin in terms of construction quality and sound production, and most importantly, the sound of the violin should be suitable and pleasant to you. In the following, we state the necessary points that you need to consider for a complete examination before buying your violin.</p>\n<h3>Complete Examination of the Instrument Before Buying a Violin</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Before buying a violin, examine all its parts with great care and make sure the instrument is healthy.</li>\n  <li>Check that none of the parts of the violin have any cracks in the wood, repairs, or breaks.</li>\n  <li>The fingerboard or fingerboard of the violin must be completely flat and without any scratches so that no extra sound is produced when playing the violin.</li>\n  <li>Examine the tuning pegs of the violin in terms of comfort and smoothness for tuning the instrument, and if the pegs of the instrument are too tight for tuning, repair and adjust the pegs before buying the violin.</li>\n  <li>The bridge of the violin must be completely resistant to prevent the pressure of the strings on the soundboard. If there is a crack or the bridge is old before buying the violin, replace it.</li>\n  <li>Examine the neck of the desired violin and make sure that the neck of the instrument has no curvature or warping.</li>\n  <li>Pay attention to the material of the violin bow and its quality, and examine the bow completely and precisely so that it has no warping or curvature and its strands are not rotten.</li>\n  <li>If the strings of the instrument are old or of poor quality, replace the strings before buying the violin. The strings of the instrument should not have any discoloration or rust.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Before Buying a Violin, Be Sure to Listen to Its Sound</h2>\n<p>Before buying a violin, you must definitely listen to the sound of the instrument, and if you have the ability to play the violin, be sure to play a little with it. If you are a beginner and intend to buy your first instrument, ask the seller or someone who is a violin player to play with the violin for you. The sound of the violin should be clear and uniform. Test the sound of all four strings of the violin, and also test all the notes on the entire fingerboard or fingerboard.</p>\n<p>Some sellers remove the bridge of new instruments and install the bridge after the sale. If you encounter this issue, be sure to ask the seller to install the bridge. Never buy a violin without listening to its sound because the beautiful and good appearance of the instrument has no connection with high sound quality.</p>\n<h2>Guide to Buying a Violin for Children</h2>\n<p>If you intend to buy a violin for your child, you must choose an instrument suitable for their age according to the child’s age. It is recommended to use smaller-sized violins for children under ten years old.</p>\n<p>The violin is designed and produced in 9 different sizes. For children, there are sizes 3/4, 1/2, 1/4, 1/8, 1/10, 1/16, and 1/32. The standard size for professional and adult players is 4/4.</p>\n<p>Pay attention to the size of the child’s hand when buying a violin and place the instrument in the child’s hand before buying to check its size for your child. For children under ten years old, sizes 1/2 and 3/4 are mostly used.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_3795\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1800\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3795 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/sornaz_violin_08.jpeg\" alt=\"Before buying a violin for children, be sure to test it in the child’s hand.\" width=\"1800\" height=\"1200\" /> Before buying a violin for children, be sure to test it in the child’s hand.[/caption]\n<h2>Buying a Violin Online</h2>\n<p>For buying a violin online for beginners and new students, first consult with your teacher or talk online with the consultants of the desired store so that you can make the best decision for purchasing and buying a violin. For buying your first violin, if you have the possibility of in-person purchase, visit the stores in person so that after testing the sound of the instrument and making sure of its suitable quality, you can make your purchase.</p>\n<p>Online violin purchases are mostly recommended to people who have worked a lot in this field, are professional players, have a lot of experience, and are familiar with all violin-making brands.</p>\n<p>If you have no choice but to buy online, you can ask the desired store to send you the sound test of the instrument so that if after the online purchase you are satisfied with the quality and sound of the violin, you can take it to a specialized store or to a professional person so that they can adjust the instrument for you in the best possible way.</p>\n<h2>Important Points for Maintaining and Caring for the Violin</h2>\n<p>After buying your violin, there are points that you must observe for the instrument to remain healthy and for proper care and maintenance, which we mention below:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Exposure of the violin to moisture will cause damage to the wooden body of the instrument; therefore, keep your instrument in a dry environment and prevent moisture from reaching the instrument.</li>\n  <li>In the summer season, avoid placing the violin in the path of the air conditioner wind.</li>\n  <li>You should always clean your violin so that dust does not settle on it and cause aging and reduced quality of the instrument.</li>\n  <li>When buying a violin, be sure to buy a very sturdy and moisture-resistant cover for it and place it in the cover when you are not practicing with the instrument.</li>\n  <li>You should replace the strings of your violin every few months depending on how much you play it. On average, you should change the violin strings about every 6 to 12 months.</li>\n  <li>When you are not practicing with the violin, loosen the bow through the screw located at its end, because keeping the bow tight for a long time causes it to warp.</li>\n</ul>\n[caption id=\"attachment_3790\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"640\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3790 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/sornaz_violin_02.webp\" alt=\"When you are not practicing with the instrument, be sure to place the violin in its cover.\" width=\"640\" height=\"480\" /> When you are not practicing with the instrument, be sure to place the violin in its cover.[/caption]\n<h2>11 Practical and Important Tips for Preparing and Buying a Suitable Violin</h2>\n<p>Undoubtedly, after choosing a good music teacher for private violin lessons, choosing and buying a suitable violin from a reputable company is of great importance, because a good-sounding violin can encourage the student to learn more music and playing and practice, and conversely, an unsuitable instrument can completely discourage the student from the instrument and music.</p>\n<p>Due to the relatively simple structure of the violin, many manufacturers can easily produce violins with a very attractive and deceptive appearance and offer them to the market. Therefore, it is recommended that you buy the violin as much as possible with the help of a teacher or a violin player or someone who is at least familiar with the structure of the violin.</p>\n<p>The teacher can make your work easier for buying a violin by testing different sounds and examining the structure of the instrument itself, because there is so much variety and different brands of violin manufacturers in the market that it makes the choice very difficult for a beginner, and also sellers usually praise their own violins, and naturally, in the end, they want to sell their own brand and model to the student and new people!</p>\n<p>Now it is time to provide you with the important points that you should consider for preparing and buying a suitable violin:</p>\n<p>1- Before anything else when buying a violin, pay attention to the body of the instrument. The body and surface of the violin should not have the slightest crack because it unintentionally has a negative effect on the sound of the instrument. Also, the darker the instrument is, the more precise and sensitive this examination should be because there may be small cracks that have been filled with materials.</p>\n<p>2- There should be no curvature or warping on the wood of the violin bow. This can be easily examined with the eye; the wood of the bow must be completely parallel to the bow hair. Otherwise, buy another bow so that you do not face problems during violin lessons and learning.</p>\n<p>3- Try to have a violin teacher with you so that with their help, you can better examine the body and sound quality of the instruments and choose a suitable and good-sounding violin from among several instruments, or if you have gone to the store alone to buy a violin, make a condition with the seller that if the violin is approved by the teacher, you will buy the instrument, and otherwise, you will return or exchange the instrument.</p>\n<p>4- Pay attention to this point that not every more expensive instrument can necessarily be a good instrument, and when <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%b3%d8%a7%d8%b2/\">buying an instrument</a>, do not look for its cheapness or expensiveness, because manufacturers can easily make the appearance of the instrument beautiful by painting and mounting pieces of wood on each other, or conversely, the cheapness of an instrument is not a reason for its low sound quality.</p>\n<p>5- The instrument you have in mind must have technical health; for example, the action should be low, and it is better if it is a German brand. The bridge should be exactly in its place and technically not fallen, and also the pegs should be healthy and without problems in appearance.</p>\n<p>6- The best types of wood for making a violin and its parts are maple, ebony, spruce, willow, and purple balsam woods, and other woods are usually cheaper and have lower quality.</p>\n<p>7- Examine the tuning pegs of the violin with great care, because the most important feature of the violin is that it can maintain the tuning well. Test them by turning the pegs a little in both directions so that they are firm enough to remain stable after tuning, and also they should not become loose and technically slip out of tune. This will make the sound coming out of your instrument better in violin class.</p>\n<p>8- Most violin buyers are not aware of the existence of the instrument’s information label. This label contains complete information about the manufacturing company, the person or country that made the violin, and also the year of production of that instrument, which you can see in the right f-hole of the violin’s soundboard. Of course, this f-hole has been removed in most new violins, and unfortunately, when buying a violin, you have to ask the seller for the instrument’s information.</p>\n<p>9- When buying a violin, pay attention to its size. Generally, for private lessons up to the age of 6, violins of size 1/4 are used, from 6 to 8 years old violins of size 1/2, from 8 to 10 years old violins of size 3/4, and above 10 years old the large or standard violin size, which is 4/4, is used.</p>\n<p>10- The fingerboard of the violin should not have any warping and must be completely flat. You can examine this by looking from the sides of the instrument and from the front. Also, the side edges of the violin from top or bottom should not protrude and should be well carved. Otherwise, you will face problems during violin lessons by the teacher.</p>\n<p>11- The final point in buying a violin is that if you intend to get a good violin, try to make sure it is from Italian or German brands. Usually, the price of Italian violins is above 5 million tomans and German violins above 3 million tomans. Of course, some Chinese brands also exist in the market with the TF mark, which relatively have suitable quality and price. This Chinese brand is one of the best-selling violin brands, and suitable price, good quality, and good sound production are among its features. The soundboard of the instrument is made of spruce, and the wood of the back, sides, and neck is maple, and they are designed for starting music education and playing.</p>\n<p></p>', '2023-09-29 22:34:29', 0, '2024-08-01 23:28:55', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1513, 'posts', 56, 'en', NULL, 'Comprehensive guide to buying a tombak | 6 practical tips for buying a tombak | 4 important questions when buying a tombak', NULL, 'This article provides a comprehensive guide to buying a tombak (tonbak), a traditional Iranian percussion instrument, and explains its importance in rhythm training and music education.\n\nIt introduces the main parts of the tombak—such as the skin, large opening, body, neck, and small opening—and highlights that the quality of each component, especially the drumhead (skin), directly affects the sound. The skin should be made from suitable natural materials (like calf or lamb) and properly stretched.\n\nThe body of the instrument is typically made from wood such as walnut or mulberry, and it is recommended to choose a one-piece, solid structure without cracks or defects. The guide also outlines key factors to consider when buying, including wood quality, skin condition, weight, price, appearance, and ergonomics.\n\nAdditionally, it answers common questions, noting that the color of the skin does not affect sound quality, excessive heating can damage the instrument, and decorative carvings are purely aesthetic and increase the price without improving sound.\n\nOverall, choosing a good tombak depends on build quality, sound, and the player’s skill level rather than just price or appearance.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article from the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz website</a>, a comprehensive guide for buying a tombak is provided, and the types of tombaks available in the market will be introduced.</p>\n<p>The tombak or tonbak is one of the ancient percussion instruments in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a>. Because rhythmic aspects are important in this instrument, it is very useful and practical for rhythm reading and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%85-%d8%ae%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%b1-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">rhythm recognition</a>, and it is presented as one of the general courses in music conservatories.</p>\n<h2>Buying a Good Tombak at a Cheap Price</h2>\n<p>You should always pay attention to the fact that buying a good tombak or any other quality instrument does not mean buying an expensive and professional instrument. Before anything else, you should choose the appropriate instrument according to your level, and first determine how much of a beginner or professional player you are, and then proceed to buy a tombak or any other instrument.</p>\n<p>In general, the tombak is divided into 5 parts: the skin, the large opening, the body, the neck, and the small opening. The shape and structure, size, and material of each of these sections will affect the final sound quality produced by the instrument.</p>\n<p>Among these, the most importance is given to the quality of the skin on the instrument’s opening, which is usually made from calfskin, sheepskin, camel skin, or lambskin. The older and more worn the skin is, the higher the final quality of the instrument will be. The skin should be well stretched on the tombak, and when pressing the skin, it should not go down more than a few millimeters.</p>\n<p>Usually, the wood of the tombak is from walnut, mulberry, ash, maple, and other forest trees. The wood of the tombak should be solid, dense, without knots or cracks. Avoid buying tombaks whose body wood is made of several pieces glued together.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_3816\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"446\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3816 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/sornaz_tonbak-02.jpg\" alt=\"Different parts of the tombak\" width=\"446\" height=\"504\" /> Different parts of the tombak[/caption]\n<h2>Important Points for Buying a Tombak</h2>\n<p>When buying a tombak, you should pay attention to 6 important points to make a better choice. These points are as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Wood type of the tombak</strong>: When buying a tombak, the first thing that should be considered is the wood type of the instrument. The wood of the tombak should be without any cracks, knots, or damages. In fact, it can be said that a good tombak is solid and heavy. Walnut and mulberry woods are the best options for making a tombak. Also, you should pay attention to the fact that the wood used in making the tombak should be one piece, and the tombak should not be made by gluing several pieces of wood together.</li>\n  <li><strong>Skin quality</strong>: The skin used in producing the tombak should have a suitable quality, and there should be no signs of damage, holes, or tears in it. If you press your hand on the tombak skin, the skin should not move or go down more than a few millimeters. In other words, it should not be too tight and firm or too loose and soft.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tombak price</strong>: In buying any item, price is one of the very important and determining issues. The price of the tombak is no exception to this rule and plays an important role in choosing this instrument. Therefore, tombaks made from walnut wood are more expensive than tombaks made from other woods. Pay attention to this point that if you go for buying cheap tombaks, you will have to use tombaks made from other trees instead of walnut wood tombaks, which have much lower quality.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tombak weight</strong>: The weight of the tombak should not be excessively heavy because it causes fatigue for the player and puts a lot of pressure on the player’s hand during practice. The low quality of the tombak body material leads to its heaviness. Finally, it should be said that paying attention to the weight of the instrument is very important, and it should not be heavy so that using it for long hours does not cause a feeling of fatigue.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tombak color</strong>: Coloring the tombaks has no role in their sound and is only for aesthetic purposes and helps in cleaning the instrument. Choosing different colors depends only on your taste.</li>\n  <li><strong>Appearance and shape of the tombak</strong>: The shape and appearance of the tombak should be beautiful, and it can be said in technical terms that the tombak should be well-styled and comfortable to hold. Although there is no specific rule for it, it is better that the length of the tombak’s neck is equal to the size of its small opening. The type and quality of construction and carving of the tombak plays a very important and special role in the aesthetic appearance of the instrument.</li>\n</ol>\n[caption id=\"attachment_3815\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"700\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3815 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/sornaz_tonbak-03.webp\" alt=\"Important points in buying a tombak\" width=\"700\" height=\"469\" /> In buying a tombak, 6 important points should be considered[/caption]\n<h2>Important Questions When Choosing and Buying a Tombak</h2>\n<p>Below, we answer several of the most important questions that arise when buying a tombak:</p>\n<h3>1- Can the color of the tombak skin affect the sound quality of the instrument?</h3>\n<p>The color of the tombak skin is only for aesthetic purposes and is intended to create more beauty in the instrument and has no effect on the sound quality of the tombak. On the other hand, it should be noted that natural skin must definitely be used on the instrument’s opening and stretched sufficiently so that it is neither too tight nor too loose. When pressing the tombak skin downward, it should not go down more than a few millimeters. In other words, if you are somewhat familiar with tombak playing, you will notice from its sound whether the skin used on the instrument is tight or loose.</p>\n<h3>2- Is it necessary to heat the tombak skin?</h3>\n<p>Applying excessive heat to the tombak skin will cause it to stretch, and this action will cause the tombak to sound bad in the long run. Another point that should be mentioned in this regard is that water should not be applied to heated skin. Although this action may temporarily cause the instrument to sound better, it will gradually cause the skin of the instrument to deteriorate.</p>\n<h3>3- What is the best type of wood for making a tombak?</h3>\n<p>Tombaks are made from walnut, maple, mulberry, ash, and some other forest trees. The wood used in making and producing the tombak should be solid, dense, without knots or damages. Therefore, you should try to avoid buying multi-piece tombaks made from glued pieces of wood, because when changing the skin, they crack and break.</p>\n<h3>4- Does inlay work affect the quality of the tombak?</h3>\n<p>Try to avoid buying tombaks that have khatam-kari and inlay work and have miniature designs and patterns, as much as possible, because they mostly have an aesthetic aspect and are made in this way for more beauty, which makes the price of the instrument several times higher. Therefore, buying inlaid tombaks depends on your taste and the budget you have considered for <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%b3%d8%a7%d8%b2/\">buying an instrument</a>.</p>\n<p></p>', '2023-10-01 12:52:22', 0, '2024-08-04 20:31:46', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
