(1514, 'posts', 57, 'en', NULL, 'Comprehensive guide to buying oud and barbat | Introduction to 8 different parts of the oud | Important tips for buying barbat', NULL, 'This article introduces the oud (barbat), one of the oldest musical instruments in Iranian music, and explores its history, structure, and characteristics. The oud is a plucked string instrument with a large pear-shaped body and a short neck, typically featuring 10 to 13 strings and no frets. Originally an Iranian instrument, it later spread to the Arab world, evolved in design, and eventually inspired the development of the guitar in Europe.\n\nThe article then explains the main components of the oud, including the soundbox, soundboard, internal braces, bridge, tailpiece, neck, pegbox, nut, strings, and plectrum, describing their roles in sound production. It also highlights the materials used in construction, such as different types of wood and string materials.\n\nIn the buying guide section, the importance of understanding the instrument’s structure and build quality is emphasized. Key factors such as wood quality, craftsmanship, structural integrity, and sound quality are discussed as essential criteria for choosing a good oud. Overall, the article helps beginners and learners make informed decisions when purchasing an oud.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article from the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com\">Sornaz Music School</a>, we first introduce the oud or barbat instrument and get familiar with its different parts. Then we provide a comprehensive guide for <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">buying</span> <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">an oud</span></strong> or barbat.</p>\n<h2>Introduction to the Oud or Barbat</h2>\n<p>The “oud,” “barbat,” or “rud” is one of the oldest instruments in the history of Iranian music, which in recent years, after a long period of being neglected and rarely used, has once again gained many fans among Iranian musicians. The oud or barbat also has many fans among Arabs. This instrument, with an appearance similar to a pear, is made of mulberry or ebony wood with a large resonant bowl and a short neck.</p>\n<p>In fact, the barbat is an Iranian instrument that, after the Arab invasion of Iran, penetrated among them and gradually, with the rise of non-Iranian governments that did not have a good opinion of Iranian instruments and music, was largely set aside and forgotten.</p>\n<p>But at the same time, due to the superiority of Iranian culture over Muslim countries, it gained great popularity among Arabs and Muslims, and after making some changes to its structure, they named their new instrument the oud. In the same historical period, due to frequent travels to <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A7%D9%86%D8%AF%D9%84%D8%B3_(%D8%A7%D8%B3%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%85%DB%8C)\">the land of Al-Andalus</a> (present-day Spain and Portugal) and the association of Iranian scholars with the Arabs who had colonized it, this instrument also reached Spain, and later the Spaniards, inspired by the oud, created the guitar.</p>\n<p>From the above, it is clear that the oud is a modified form of the barbat, but today the terms oud and barbat actually refer to the same instrument. This instrument is classified as a plucked string instrument. Its length is about 85 cm, and when playing, it is placed horizontally on the thigh. The oud or barbat is, in a way, a historical symbol for Iranian instruments and is used in traditional music of the Middle East and parts of East Africa.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_3823\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"397\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3823 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/sornaz_oud_barbat_03.jpg\" alt=\"An example of an oud or barbat with 11 strings, which is the most common form of this instrument.\" width=\"397\" height=\"274\" /> An example of an oud or barbat with 11 strings, which is the most common form of this instrument.[/caption]\n<p>The oud has a short neck and a large pear-shaped bowl and, unlike other plucked string instruments in Iranian music, lacks frets and has between 10 and 13 strings.</p>\n<p>In the past, the strings of the barbat were called “vatar,” and because this instrument had been neglected for a long time, the old names are still used for it, and it is common to refer to the strings of the barbat with the old name “vatar.”</p>\n<p>Because the barbat was made of wood, after making changes to it, Muslims named the instrument “oud” (oud in Arabic means wood). The word “oud” is actually the Arabic form of the Persian word “rud,” which is both another name for the barbat and means string in string instruments.</p>\n<h2>The different parts of the oud are as follows:</h2>\n<p>One of the most important points when <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%b3%d8%a7%d8%b2/\">buying an instrument</a> is knowing the structure and components of this instrument. In the following, we introduce the main parts that make up this instrument:</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_3826\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"510\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3826 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/sornaz_oud_barbat_06.jpg\" alt=\"Different parts of the barbat or oud instrument.\" width=\"510\" height=\"597\" /> Different parts of the barbat or oud instrument.[/caption]\n<h3><strong>1- Resonant Bowl and Soundboard:</strong></h3>\n<p>The resonant bowl of the oud is made in a pear shape and is divided lengthwise into two equal parts, made of multiple glued wooden ribs. The soundboard of the barbat is made of wood, which, for better sound production, is made of pine wood. To allow sound to exit the bowl, two small circles and one larger circle made of bone are created on the soundboard, and the bridge of the instrument is located at the bottom of this board.</p>\n<h3><strong>2- Braces:</strong></h3>\n<p>In five places on the soundboard of the barbat, horizontal braces connected to the soundboard are placed toward the inside of the bowl, which prevent the soundboard from changing shape.</p>\n<h3><strong>3- String Holder and Bridge:</strong></h3>\n<p>The bridge of the oud is a piece of wood about 10 cm long, located at the bottom of the bowl, with shallow grooves on it for the passage of the strings (oud strings). In the construction of some barbats, the strings are attached to a string holder at the end of the bowl body, and in others, the bridge of the instrument plays this role, in such a way that the strings are tied in holes on the bridge and go toward the tuning pegs. Usually, the bridge is not glued directly onto the soundboard, and a piece of wood or shell is placed between the bridge and the soundboard.</p>\n<h3><strong>4- Neck:</strong></h3>\n<p>The barbat has a short neck made of wood, about one-third the length of the bowl, with a diameter of about 10 cm. In the past, frets were placed on the neck of the barbat, but today the neck of this instrument lacks frets.</p>\n<h3><strong>5- Pegbox or Headstock:</strong></h3>\n<p>The headstock or pegbox refers to the part of the oud that is a hollow chamber located at the beginning of the neck length and is made slightly tilted backward. The headstock is the place where the tuning pegs of the instrument are located, and there are 5 pegs on each side, each for one of the instrument’s strings.</p>\n<h3><strong>6- Nut:</strong></h3>\n<p>It is a thin, low-height wooden piece that has a width equal to the width of the instrument’s neck and a height of about one millimeter, located between the neck and the headstock of the oud, through whose shallow grooves the strings pass.</p>\n<h3><strong>7- Number, Material, and Strings:</strong></h3>\n<p>The barbat usually has 10 strings or “vatar” (sometimes up to 13 strings are also made), which are tuned in pairs of two in unison. In some barbats, the lowest string is single and is mostly used for a drone sound. The strings of the oud are made of gut (twisted sheep intestines), silk twisted with a metal coating, or nylon strings of various thicknesses.</p>\n<p>In general, most ouds have 11 strings, which are divided into five pairs of double strings and one single string. Also, 11 tuning pegs are used to tune and tie the strings of the instrument. The way to tune the oud is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The first pair of strings is tuned to the note “C”.</li>\n  <li>The second pair of strings is tuned to the note “G”.</li>\n  <li>The third pair of strings is tuned to the note “D”.</li>\n  <li>The fourth pair of strings is tuned to the note “A”.</li>\n  <li>The fifth pair of strings is tuned to the note “G”.</li>\n  <li>The sixth string is tuned to the note “D”.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3><strong>8- Plectrum:</strong></h3>\n<p>The plectrum of the barbat is made of feathers from large birds or soft plastic, which is held in the right hand when playing, and with the left hand, similar to violin and kamancheh instruments that have no frets, pressure is applied on the neck of the instrument at the location of the notes.</p>\n<p>The usual range of the barbat’s sound is about 2 octaves, and due to the shortness of the instrument’s neck, about one and a half octaves from the middle sonic region of the instrument have better sound production.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_3824\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"800\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3824 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/sornaz_oud_barbat_04.jpg\" alt=\"The tonal range of the barbat is approximately 2 octaves.\" width=\"800\" height=\"451\" /> The tonal range of the barbat is approximately 2 octaves.[/caption]\n<p></p>', '2023-10-02 12:49:03', 0, '2024-08-01 23:14:09', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1515, 'posts', 58, 'en', NULL, 'Comprehensive guide to buying a recorder (flute)', NULL, 'This article provides a comprehensive introduction to the flute (especially the recorder), covering its history, structure, materials, and key characteristics. The flute dates back to the 14th century and has roots in Italian culture. Over time, particularly during the Renaissance and Baroque periods, the instrument evolved significantly in design, improving its tonal range and tuning capabilities.\n\nThe article also explains the flute’s sound range, typically around two and a half octaves, and discusses the materials used in its construction. Various types of wood—such as pear, plum, maple, tulipwood, and boxwood—are examined, along with their impact on sound quality.\n\nIn the buying guide section, important considerations are outlined, including choosing an instrument suitable for the player’s level, paying attention to wood density and weight, selecting reputable brands (especially German and Dutch makers), and using a protective case. It also emphasizes that a higher price does not always guarantee better quality.\n\nFinally, the article highlights essential maintenance and care tips, such as drying the instrument after use, avoiding extreme temperature and humidity, warming up the flute before playing, and addressing issues like clogging or sound adjustment. Overall, it helps learners make informed decisions when purchasing and maintaining a flute.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the history, construction materials, tonal range, and other features of the recorder, and provide a comprehensive guide for <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">buying</span> <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">a recorder</span></strong></span>.</p>\n<h2>History of the Recorder</h2>\n<p>The history of the recorder dates back to the fourteenth century and has its roots in the customs and culture of Italy. At that time, this instrument differed slightly in appearance from the modern one because its finger holes were larger, its middle section was wide and flat, and its end was conical. However, this instrument has been perfected in every era, and changes have been made to its structure until it reached its current form. For example, between 1600 and 1700 AD, the design of this instrument changed again; its head became narrower, and its holes became smaller.</p>\n<p>From approximately the Renaissance period until 1700 AD, which was called the Baroque period, boxwood was used to make recorders. However, today this instrument has a design with specific principles, and woods other than boxwood are used in its construction.</p>\n<p>When buying a recorder, keep in mind that old recorders do not have very high quality, and you cannot judge them based on string instruments because the older string instruments become, the more their quality does not decrease, and their sound becomes even more desirable. However, this is not the case with wind instruments, especially the recorder. The interesting point is that all recorders are made based on old versions and are designed according to models available in museums.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h3>Changes and Completion of the Recorder in the Renaissance Period</h3>\n<p>During the Renaissance, the recorder was played in concerts and ensemble performances. In the time span between the Renaissance and Baroque periods, changes occurred in the style of music, and this instrument was also played in solo performances. In the early years of the Baroque period (around 1610 AD), ensemble playing in the form of arranging repertoire, solo performances with an accompanying instrument, had many applications and could be played on various instruments, such as the violin, cornetto (an early example of the trumpet), and the recorder.</p>\n<p>The instrument most often used for playing this witty solo music was the Ganassi or G treble, which produced a higher tone than the standard Baroque treble and in design was more similar to the Renaissance recorder.</p>\n<p>In the continuation of the Baroque period, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%D8%B2%D9%86%D8%AF%DA%AF%DB%8C%D9%86%D8%A7%D9%85%D9%87-%D9%85%D9%88%D8%B3%DB%8C%D9%82%DB%8C-%D8%AF%D8%A7%D9%86%D8%A7%D9%86/\">musicians and composers</a> began writing music for specific selected instruments. Although the performance of violin repertoire on the recorder had decreased, adaptation of music between recorder and flute music was still common because the two were very compatible with each other.</p>\n<p>Advances in the design and construction of the Baroque recorder increased its tonal range and made it easier for the instrument to stay in tune while playing. With the narrower middle section, its higher octaves are more acceptable. Although its overall dynamics are quieter, using an alternative fingering method can achieve an increased dynamic range that produces different musical characteristics. This model of the instrument was also a model of the recorder that was sometimes featured in Bach’s chamber opera music or Baroque operas by Purcell.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>Familiarity with the Tonal Range of Recorder Notes</h2>\n<p>As the most important point when buying a recorder, familiarity with the range of notes and the tonal range of this instrument can be mentioned. With this in mind, it is better to know that the recorder has a complete range of two and a half octaves, and if you intend to hear or produce higher notes from this instrument, it is better to be professionally skilled in playing and also purchase a suitable instrument. It is worth noting that the lowest note usually played by the recorder is C or F.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>Materials Used in Recorder Construction</h2>\n<p>If we want to examine the recorder in terms of its construction material, it is better to consider different woods. Pear wood is one of the practical woods that mostly grows in central Europe and has a special softness that is mostly used for making instruments with a base note. The second practical type of wood is from the plum tree, which also grows in central Europe, and the instrument made with this material has a high sound power.</p>\n<p>The third wood used is from the linden tree, which grows in central America, and the instrument made with this type of wood has a very clear sound. The fourth material is maple, which grows in Canada, and the interesting point is that the instrument produced with this material is very light. But the last material is boxwood, which grows in southern Europe, and the instrument produced with this type of wood has a clear and high sound.</p>\n&nbsp;\n[caption id=\"attachment_3921\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"720\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3921 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/sornaz_recorder_flute_02.jpg\" alt=\"The material used in the construction of the instrument greatly affects the price of buying a recorder.\" width=\"720\" height=\"600\" /> The material used in the construction of the instrument greatly affects the price of buying a recorder.[/caption]\n&nbsp;\n<h3>Buying a Recorder from Which Country or Company?</h3>\n<p>The German company Moeck, with almost 40 years of experience in recorder making, is probably the most powerful company active in the field of recorder production. The Rottenburgh model of this company is very famous. Relatively inexperienced buyers should avoid buying instruments that have reached the end of their life or are made by unknown manufacturers, and in general, buy their instrument from reputable companies so that if a problem arises, they can easily contact the manufacturer. The Netherlands and Germany, with many private makers in this field, are very famous, although buying a recorder from these makers requires more cost.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>Instruments Specific to Repertoire</h2>\n<p>When buying a recorder, you should know that at higher levels, repertoire-specific recorders come into play, which have special designs and belong to specific periods. The wide middle section and large finger holes are specific to Renaissance recorders, which produce an open and relatively loud sound. These instruments were used in consorts and to some extent resembled pipe organs, and their sound was completely compatible with the vocal group that played the music.</p>\n<p>This simple design means that the range of Renaissance recorders is slightly smaller than their Baroque counterparts, and the fingering method differs from one instrument to another. Because the finger holes of this model are larger, the smallest movements cause large changes in the instrument’s tuning, and therefore special skill is required to play well and stay in tune in a Renaissance consort.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>Stages of Choosing and Buying a Suitable Recorder</h2>\n<p>When buying a recorder, you should choose an instrument that is suitable for you both in terms of maintenance and the place of playing. For example, instruments with a more delicate structure (lower density) are more suitable for playing at home or in ensemble playing, while heavier recorders are better options for solo performances and public performances.</p>\n<p>Recorders made from darker (denser) woods will also be heavier, and when buying a recorder, you should keep this point in mind. Be sure to get a protective case for your recorder. These cases come in different types, including: soft sewn leather bag, padded roll bag, or sturdy box.</p>\n<p>Now we should point out that remember that spending a lot of money on <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d8%ae%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%b3%d8%a7%d8%b2/\">buying an instrument</a> does not always mean getting a better instrument. Of course, it must be accepted that when choosing and buying a recorder or any other instrument, price is a main factor, and to a large extent, the quality of the instrument depends on the amount you spend.</p>\n<p>Denser woods naturally cost more. Also, smaller and private manufacturers usually charge you more because they spend more time making the instrument, there is high demand for their instruments, and instead of factory work, they offer handmade work. Remember that although the American company Von Huene produces many of the most expensive recorders, this company is also responsible for designing the more affordable Moeck Rottenburgh model.</p>\n&nbsp;\n[caption id=\"attachment_3922\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"580\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3922 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/sornaz_recorder_flute_04.jpg\" alt=\"Before buying a recorder, be sure to study the points mentioned in this article.\" width=\"580\" height=\"232\" /> Before buying a recorder, be sure to study the points mentioned in this article.[/caption]\n&nbsp;\n<h2>Recorder Compatibility and Practice</h2>\n<p>The first step to getting your recorder compatible is to do your best to take care of your new instrument. Wood is a living material and must be properly cared for. To start, daily practice and playing of the recorder is very important. Until your instrument gains the ability to be played regularly, try to practice in shorter periods and gradually increase the length of your practice. Gradual and continuous start is the key point and reduces possible future problems.</p>\n<p>It is very important that in the initial stages, practicing and playing the recorder happens regularly so that the wood gets used to moisture and heat. Doing this also helps develop the sound of the instrument because the sound coming out of the instrument gradually changes over time and becomes “more open.”</p>\n<h3>Important Points to Observe</h3>\n<p>Before practicing and playing, always warm up your instrument properly. This action is beneficial for both the instrument and the player. This is essential in colder temperatures and for denser woods. During practice and performance, and at the beginning to warm up the instrument, start from lower notes, and when your instrument is warm, play the higher notes.</p>\n<p>Before buying a recorder, remember to always disassemble your recorder after playing pieces and completely clean the moisture inside it with a special cloth or with a cleaning rod and cloth (a cloth made of linen or linen is the best option because its fibers do not remain inside the instrument). Leave the recorder out of its protective case from night until morning so that it dries completely; of course, if you leave it in an open case, it is better.</p>\n<p>Never place the recorder in places that do not have good and moderate temperature and humidity conditions, such as extreme cold, on a radiator, or in direct sunlight; always try to keep your instrument away from chemicals.</p>\n&nbsp;\n[caption id=\"attachment_3920\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"300\"]<img class=\"wp-image-3920 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/sornaz_recorder_flute_01.jpg\" alt=\"Warming up the recorder before starting practice and performance is essential.\" width=\"300\" height=\"220\" /> Warming up the recorder before starting practice and performance is essential.[/caption]\n&nbsp;\n<h2>Important Recommendations for Care, Repair, and Maintenance Before Buying a Recorder</h2>\n<p>Before buying a recorder, you should know recommendations for maintaining and caring for the instrument. If you take proper care of your instrument, you will not need professional repair and maintenance, although sometimes these repairs and maintenances are inevitable. The most common problem with the recorder is that after playing for a while, the recorder gradually becomes blocked, and its sound becomes muffled. This problem occurs due to the constant contact of the recorder with moisture. This contact causes the wood to alternately expand and contract in small amounts, and as a result, the windway in the mouthpiece swells.</p>\n<h3>Clearing a Blocked Recorder</h3>\n<p>Blockage of the recorder sometimes reduces the efficiency of the instrument. Blockage occurs more in denser (darker) woods. Usually, blockage and obstruction of the recorder is reduced by dropping a few drops of descaling liquid or a special cleaner. For this, hold the recorder upside down, then pour the liquid into the windway through the window or labium and blow into it to open the windway and remove the blockage.</p>\n<p>In some cases, repair, maintenance, and continuous care are the responsibility of the instrument owner. Keeping the cork joints (or thread) lubricated ensures that the recorder pieces fit well together and are also easily separated (small cork oil containers are usually sold with new instruments, but Vaseline can also be used for this purpose). Repairing broken corks or opened threads is not difficult and should not be left alone until they lead to the wear of the instrument.</p>\n<p>In larger recorders, the threads and key and pad mechanisms constantly require your attention. Re-tuning the recorder’s sound should be done by a professional, and sometimes it is necessary.</p>\n<h3>Tuning the Recorder</h3>\n<p>To tune the sound of your recorder, you must separate its head from the recorder and shave or correct its shape, and before reconnecting it to the recorder, clean it carefully so that the particles stuck to it are cleaned.</p>\n<p>Some manufacturers perform sound tuning in the first year at a lower cost or for free, especially for more expensive models, because when the instrument is played for a certain period, changes occur in the quality of its sound. The instrument going out of tune can also be corrected by enlarging the finger holes with precise drilling or by pouring <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%85%D9%88%D9%85\">wax</a> to make them smaller.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding buying a recorder, please share it with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a> in the comments section to help improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-10-14 21:08:38', 0, '2024-08-04 20:34:01', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1516, 'posts', 59, 'en', NULL, 'What is the pish-daramad form in Iranian music?', NULL, 'The Pishdaramad is an important and relatively modern form in Persian classical (Dastgahi) music, typically performed at the beginning of a concert. Its purpose is to introduce the melodic atmosphere and key motifs (gushehs) of a musical mode (dastgah or avaz), preparing the listener for the main performance.\n\nPishdaramads are usually slow, metered, and pre-composed pieces, often written in time signatures like 6/8 or 6/4. Unlike improvisational sections, they have a structured form and often move through several tonal areas within a mode. Compared to forms like Rang, they are generally more serious and less lively.\n\nThis form emerged during the late Qajar period, with early examples attributed to musicians such as Darvish Khan and Rokneddin Mokhtari. While rooted in older musical traditions, the Pishdaramad evolved through innovative melodic development into a distinct compositional form.\n\nAlthough it once held a significant role in Persian music performances, its prominence declined after the 1979 Iranian Revolution, and today it is less commonly featured in concerts.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article from the series of articles on <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">formology</a> of Sornaz Music School, we examine the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Pishdaramad form</span></strong> in Iranian modal music. Stay with us in the following...</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>What is Pishdaramad?</h2>\n<p>Pishdaramad is one of the new forms and frameworks in Iranian music, and its name became common from the middle of the Qajar period. As far as we have examined, it seems that Foroughat Shirazi first mentioned this term in “Buhur al-Alhan.” The reason for its naming is that, according to tradition, it is performed before the “Daramad” of the dastgahs and avazes. Therefore, it is necessary to first explain the term “Daramad”:</p>\n<p>Arshad Tahmasebi, Daramad: In the dictionary, it means entering, and in musical terminology, it is a borrowed title for any maqam that is placed at the beginning of a dastgah and avaz [...] Therefore, Daramad by itself does not indicate a specific maqam or context. In other words, if a musician is asked to perform a Daramad, he will not know what he should play or sing. Daramad only gains meaning when a context is also mentioned for it: Daramad of Shur, Daramad of Chahargah, Daramad of ... . Thus, Daramads in the radif have meaning when the name of the dastgah or avaz has come before them: “Dastgah of Shur: Daramad (maqam of Shur), Salmak (maqam of Salmak), etc.”</p>\n<p>In dastgahi music, the name of the dastgah is the same as the name of the first maqam in which it is performed. Daramad, in its main and general application in the radif, refers to maqams that, together with several other maqams, have formed a dastgah and avaz (in most radifs, twelve dastgahs and avazes): Daramad of Shur, Daramad of Segah, Daramad of Chahargah, Daramad of Homayun, Daramad of Mahur, Daramad of Nava, Daramad of Rast (Rast Panjgah), Daramad of Abu Ata, Daramad of Bayat-e Tork, Daramad of Afshari, Daramad of Dashti, and Daramad of Bayat-e Isfahan, and in some thirteen-dastgah radifs like Boroumand’s radif: Daramad of Bayat-e Kurd [...].</p>\n<p>The term Daramad has not appeared in any of Abd al-Qadir Maraghi’s books, but it is rarely seen in other texts of the same period. In Homayun Nameh, written in the early ninth century, there is a letter from Mir Sadr al-Din Nami addressed to one of the lords of melody. In his letter, in addition to the common musical terms of that period, Daramad is also seen:</p>\n<p>“May it not remain hidden from the enlightened opinion of the high servants that this deprived and resourceless one, among the lovers, has become a wanderer from the overwhelming separation of Iraq, intending to go with a group of Ajam via the Hejaz road to the Kaaba of union. And since the closed veil of the beloved was an obstacle, he thought from the language of the great and small in opposition. It is hoped that when the note reaches the presence, he will act in a Husseini manner from the straight path and bring this humble one out of the distant circle of wandering. And peace.”</p>\n<p>Thus, usually, the Daramads of the dastgahs and avazes of music are placed in the “Avaz” type and are performed in a non-metric form and are the introducers of the first maqam of the dastgahs and avazes of the radif. In instrumental radifs, it is possible that before the beginning of the Daramad of one of the metric types, a type called “Chaharmezrab” is placed. For example, in Mirza Abdollah’s radif according to Boroumand:</p>\n<p>Dastgah of Segah: Chaharmezrab, Daramad, Naghmeh, etc.;</p>\n<p>Dastgah of Homayun: Chaharmezrab, First Daramad, Second Daramad, Mowalian, etc.</p>\n<p>But because vocal radifs, according to tradition, lack types such as Chaharmezrab, Rang, etc., the first part of their dastgahs is always Daramad or titles similar and synonymous with this term. Therefore, Daramads in instrumental radifs are in the “Instrumental Avaz” type and in vocal radifs in the “Vocal Avaz” type.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>The Story of the Creation of the First Pishdaramad</h2>\n<p>Pishdaramad is considered a new form and framework in Iranian music. Regarding the history of the creation of the first Pishdaramad, it has been said that in one of the concerts of the “Ikhvat Association,” when Rokn al-Din Khan Mokhtari and Gholamhossein Darvish (Darvish Khan) intended to perform, Rokn al-Din Khan Mokhtari suggested that before starting the Daramad of the dastgah, they perform a rhythmic piece together, and <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%85%D8%B4%DB%8C%D8%B1_%D9%87%D9%85%D8%A7%DB%8C%D9%88%D9%86_%D8%B4%D9%87%D8%B1%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1\">Moshir Homayoun Shahrdar</a> named this form Pishdaramad because it was played before the execution of the Daramad of the dastgah.</p>\n<p>Therefore, Darvish Khan created the first Pishdaramad and performed it with Rokn al-Din Mokhtari, and from these two prominent Iranian musicians, many Pishdaramads have remained as a legacy.</p>\n<p>Pishdaramads are slow and calm pieces that are composed in simple rhythms and meters, referring to various gushehs of a dastgah or avaz that the player intends to play in the main pieces, so that the listener’s ear is prepared to hear the Daramad of the dastgah.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>General Structure of Pishdaramad</h2>\n<ol>\n  <li>A metric type that, according to tradition, is without lyrics;</li>\n  <li>A non-improvised type composed in advance;</li>\n  <li>Usually in the slow compound meter of six-four or six-eight;</li>\n  <li>Because of its slow tempo, it is one of the metric types that, unlike for example <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%90%d9%86%da%af/\">Rang</a>, has a long duration (“Avaz,” as a non-metric type, has no time limitation):</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Duration of Darvish Khan’s Mahur Pishdaramad (04:25).</p>\n<p>Duration of Darvish Khan’s Mahur Rang (Qahr o Ashti) (01:36).</p>\n<p>Duration of Rokn al-Din Mokhtari’s Segah Pishdaramad (05:19).</p>\n<p>Duration of Rokn al-Din Mokhtari’s Segah Rang (01:35).</p>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n  <li>Usually, compared to a type like Rang, it is accompanied by more ups and downs and moves in several maqams of the dastgah;</li>\n  <li>Unlike types such as Rang and Chaharmezrab, it does not have a cheerful nature;</li>\n</ol>\n<p>We said that Pishdaramad is a type without lyrics, but sometimes poems have also been placed on some Pishdaramads that have become famous and well-known. Rouhollah Khaleqi has written about one of Rokn al-Din Mokhtari’s Pishdaramads:</p>\n<p>“His Homayun Pishdaramad has such beautiful melodies that at one time poems were composed for it, and part of it was sung like a Tasnif, and part of it was also recorded by Qamar al-Moluk [Vaziri] on a record, and they had named it ‘Asheqam Man’ because the first hemistich started with these two words.”</p>\n<p>Another case is Morteza Ney-Davoud’s Bayat-e Isfahan Pishdaramad, for which lyrics were composed and it became a Tasnif and gained widespread fame for this reason:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Mahe man shah-e man rahmati be hal-e zaram<br>\nBefkan az rou-ye khod roshani be sham-e taram</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Akhar ey negaram bin faghan o zaram cheshm-e zhaleh baram ra</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Ey gol-e zendegi bish az in makon to kharam ... (Hossein Pezhman Bakhtiari)</p>\n<p>We saw that most of the initial Pishdaramads, that is, the creations of Gholam Hossein Darvish and Rokn al-Din Mokhtari, were in the six-four framework. At the end of the old instrumental radifs, there are metric pieces that, according to tradition, are called Rang in a general classification. The Rangs at the end of the radif have various structures and differ completely in terms of duration, performance speed, and meter framework. In truth, Rang in its Qajari radif concept had a meaning equal to metric instrumental melody. For example, “Harbi,” which is a relatively fast melody in the two-four framework, and “Nastari,” which is a slow melody in the six-four framework, were both classified under the general title of Rang.</p>\n<p>If we pay attention to the melody of the Nastari Rang of the Homayun dastgah radif, we will see that it has a structure similar to Darvish Khan’s Pishdaramads and other composers’ melodies.</p>\n<p>Even the duration of Darvish Khan’s and Rokn al-Din Mokhtari’s Pishdaramads is close to the duration of the six-four pieces of the radif:</p>\n<p>Duration of Nastari Rang performance “05:17,” and duration of Darvish Khan’s Mahur Pishdaramad “04:24.”</p>\n<p>At the beginning of a number of Pishdaramads, there is a short rhythmic motif as a “base” that has a familiar rhythmic pulse and is famous among musicians; tombak players also usually base their work on this base when performing six-four rhythms. This motif was known among musicians until a few decades ago as the “Darvish base.”</p>\n<p>Darvish Khan’s Abu Ata and Afshari Pishdaramads, Rokn al-Din Mokhtari’s Afshari, Dashti, old Isfahan, and new Isfahan Pishdaramads, and some Pishdaramads by other composers have started with this motif. However, it is certain that Darvish Khan borrowed the theme of this base from old music, and as we see, the beginning theme of the Farrah Rang of the Homayun dastgah radif is formed based on this same motif. Earlier we said that the composers of the metric pieces at the end of the dastgahs of the radif, including Farrah Rang, are unknown.</p>\n<p>Based on this, we see that Darvish Khan and Rokn al-Din Mokhtari borrowed both the six-four instrumental “framework,” the rhythmic “base,” and the name of the type from unknown old composers, and therefore, their main performance as composers is creating innovation in melodic methods and creating new and fresh themes in the works they have left behind. In truth, they caused the old Pishdaramad to be “transformed” and its performance to become “common.” These two artists, at the crossroads of Iranian history, are the first high-ranking composers of “instrumental” music whose name, numerous works, and the content of their works we are aware of.</p>\n<p>Mohammad Reza Shafiei Kadkani has said: “The first step in the path of transformation in the field of content is always taken.”</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>The Fate of Pishdaramad</h2>\n<p>After the events of 1357 solar year, which effectively led to the closure of all music programs in concert halls, radio, television, etc., the performance of Pishdaramad, Tasnif, Rang, etc., was not possible, and for many years only single lyrical songs, most of which were low-quality music and poems to the taste of the government men, were broadcast from the radio and television of the Islamic Republic under the title of “Sorud”:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Arshad Tahmasebi: After the victory of the revolution, contrary to the expectations of musicians and the promises of the statesmen of that time, new policies were imposed on music. All Iranian music programs were removed from radio and television, and the music officials of these organizations were dismissed; the golden period of broadcasting “Western classical music” from radio and television as “Sorud”!</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Arshad Tahmasebi: Sorud, in a sense equal to melody, has no application in today’s music, but it is the title of a rhythmic form in which patriotic, epic, martial, and advisory poems are sung, like the Sorud “Ey Iran” composed by Rouhollah Khaleqi. During the years after the Islamic Revolution of Iran, Sorud, due to avoiding the use of the word “music,” replaced this word: “Center for Revolutionary Soruds and Melodies.” In this period, any Tasnif and Taraneh, with any meter, content, and poem, was called Sorud.</p>\n<p>In this period, all veteran and famous music masters, including all famous violinists of the 1340s and 1350s solar decades, and masters such as Hassan Kasaei, Jalil Shahnaz, Ali Asghar Bahari, Faramarz Payvar, etc., had no opportunity to perform music and were forced to stay at home because they were not involved in composing Soruds; but younger people proficient in Iranian music, either out of belief or to preserve music, etc., engaged in activity in this field for some time:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Arshad Tahmasebi: Lotfi, who in previous years had appeared in the role of the leader of the “Return Period” music, how could he use the same type of music and its tools and instruments and also play the role of leading “revolutionary music”? [In this era] dastgahi music cannot and should not be responsive to all needs. Avaz and Tasnif and... all of these can only be responsive to the inner states of humans, and that too not at every age.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Lotfi knew this well and had also realized that if he wanted to give a revolutionary form to this music, he had to act delicately; a very difficult task! Before him, Aref Qazvini — who had the same problem — had used the tool of poetry because the concepts and meanings of the words could clearly and openly convey his slogans and thoughts to the listener: “From the blood of the youth of the homeland, tulips have bloomed,” but this was not enough for Lotfi.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">The easiest thing for him could have been to compose a number of Soruds and include his creations as “singles” in a collection and publish them, but this easiest work, as his first action in this regard, did not satisfy him. Like the past, he wanted a complete programmatic music that would both be in line with musical tradition and responsive to the needs of the day. The result of his work became Chavosh 2; the nature of the work was the same <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a8%d8%b1%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%85%d9%87-%d8%a7%db%8c/\">programmatic dastgahi music</a> and included Tasnif (here Sorud) and Avaz with the difference that in this work he used seemingly minor musical tricks that could turn this collection into revolutionary music.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">For example, apart from choosing excellent poems with revolutionary concepts, his use of the tool of “declamation” at the beginning of the work; or applying taste in the way of Mohammad Reza Shajarian’s singing, who started his Avaz from the high register instead of the Daramad, contrary to the current and previous method of presenting traditional Avaz, and this made the work extraordinarily effective; or the creative use of “drum roll” in the Sorud “Azadi” and also replacing collective singing instead of the singer’s voice in repeating the same Sorud at the end of the work; or performing a passionate piece with tar and kamancheh ensemble playing; or using collective rhythmic work as a background and in accompaniment with part of Shahram Nazeri’s Avaz.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">This same method, that is, using a rhythmic melody in accompaniment with Avaz instead of answering Avaz in the form of solo playing, became one of Lotfi’s special tricks, and later others imitated it repeatedly.</p>\n<p>In such circumstances, there was no place for performing Pishdaramad and especially Rang and Chaharmezrab. For example, Mohammad Reza Lotfi released the collection “In Memory of Aref,” which he had recorded before the revolution, without its ending Rang. From the late 1360s solar onward, music gradually found its way out of the previous constraints, and openings for its performance opened in society, but at this time, the viewpoint of the musicians themselves had completely changed, and it reached a point where types such as Pishdaramad and Rang practically lost their previous position, and today we rarely encounter these two terms in concert brochures and album booklets.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>The article you read under the title “The Pishdaramad Form in Iranian Music” is taken from the book “How Iranian Music Is – An Investigation into Musical Types” written by Arshad Tahmasebi.</p>\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding the Pishdaramad form in Iranian music, please share it with us in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-10-16 19:21:55', 0, '2024-03-31 00:32:01', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1517, 'posts', 60, 'en', NULL, 'What is the tasnif form in Iranian music?', NULL, 'This article explores the tasnif form in Iranian classical (dastgahi) music. A tasnif is a metrical, lyrical, and pre-composed musical form that, unlike improvisational vocal music (avaz), is highly accessible and widely محبوب among the general public due to its simplicity and collective performability.\n\nHistorically, tasnif originated from early human vocal music, which was initially non-metrical but gradually evolved into rhythmic and structured forms. Over time, tasnifs became one of the most popular musical genres in Iran, covering themes such as love, nature, social commentary, and humor. They have evolved through different historical periods—from ancient times to the Qajar era—sometimes as folk creations and sometimes as more refined artistic works.\n\nReligious tasnifs (such as noheh) also form part of this tradition, sharing musical structures with secular tasnifs but differing in lyrical content. Structurally, tasnifs are characterized by melodic repetition, stepwise motion, simple vocal ornamentation, and avoidance of complex modulations, making them easy to learn and perform.\n\nThe lyrics may be drawn from classical Persian poetry (e.g., Hafez, Saadi) or newly composed texts adapted to the music. Overall, the tasnif serves as a vital bridge between artistic expression and popular culture in Iranian music and continues to maintain its relevance even in modern musical forms.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article from the series of articles on <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">formology</a> of Sornaz Music School, we examine the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Tasnif form</span></strong> in Iranian modal music from the perspective of the prominent Iranian musician and radif expert, “Arshad Tahmasebi.” Stay with us in the following...</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>Tasnif</h2>\n<p>As one might guess, the first musical actions of humanity and all nations and peoples were undoubtedly engaging in the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">art of music</a>, which they began with vocal music performance. Certainly, at the beginning, the vocal music of all nations was a type of “non-metric” music used in prayers and invocations.</p>\n<p>When humans elevated their aesthetic sense and reached a stage where they could understand the effect and beauty of symmetry, meter, and rhythm, and simultaneously recognize the difference between ordinary speech and “rhythmic” and “versified” speech, and began to arrange words, “metric” vocal music took shape. The contemporary Tasnif of our culture’s land, in a general sense, is the offspring of this initial action.</p>\n<p>Tasnif should be considered the most widespread and “popular” type of music in today’s Iran because ordinary people can easily connect with this metric type and its lyrics, unlike “Avaz” and instrumental types. Whenever we remember that the possibility of collective performance of this type by everyone and in any situation, without the need for an instrument, is available, its “popular” characteristic becomes more prominent and evident. This unique feature is not found in “Avaz,” nor in “Rang,” nor in “Pishdaramad,” nor in any other musical types. Yahya Aryanpour has written about the history of this term:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">The term Tasnif, which became common from the tenth century AH and perhaps emerged in the eighth or ninth century, replaced the terms “Qawl” and “Ghazal” of the first post-Islamic era. In the terminology of poets and composers of the past, it referred to a type of melodic poetry that had both prosodic and rhythmic meter — that is, it did not differ in appearance from other ordinary poems, but in terms of the choice of meter and the arrangement of words, it had the quality and property of being compatible and harmonious with musical melodies, modes, and the low and high tones of instruments and singing.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Tasnifs that were common and prevalent during the Safavid era, like today’s Tasnifs, were not syllabic and were in harmony with prosodic poetry. Unfortunately, this type of Tasnifs or poems accompanied by Iranian melody and music has not been considered part of literature by anyone and has not been recorded in divans and tazkirehs, and the identity of their composers is mostly unknown to us [...]</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">The subject of Tasnif is sometimes love and affection, description of the beloved’s unfaithfulness, the wretched state of the lover, and praise of flowers, wine, and beauty. These characteristics bring Tasnif close to mystical ghazals and give it a kind of grace and authenticity. Sometimes it is humor, jest, or praise and criticism of people and situations. These kinds of Tasnifs always have a specific purpose and mission and are almost always composed in the common colloquial language. Nevertheless, they possess a special poetic art and occasionally contain lofty thoughts expressed in a simple language without poetic embellishments [...]</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Tasnif in its current form is a relatively new phenomenon in Iranian literature, and it can be said that when Persian poetry left the courts of kings and fell into the hands of the common people of the streets and bazaars, it chose the Tasnif form for itself. After the Constitutional period, poets and composers paid more attention to it and gave it a literary and artistic aspect.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>There is no trace left of poems related to ancient musical melodies, but some narratives told about Barbad the Sassanid make us certain that at least some of his melodies, like “Sabz dar Sabz” or “Yazdan Afrid,” as mentioned earlier, had lyrics, and it can be assumed that some of them, especially the melodies related to festive and joyful gatherings, were performed in a metric form:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Arthur Christensen: Tha’alibi says that in the first meeting, Barbad sang the Yazdan Afrid melody for Khosrow, then presented the Parto-ye Farkhar melody to his ear, which gave the same joy that wealth gives after poverty.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>Naturally, metric and lyrical types such as Tasnif, Taraneh, and Sorud have always been popular, popular, and favored types in all eras. Shams Qays Razi, in the seventh century, in the book “Al-Mu’jam fi Ma’ayir Ash’ar al-Ajam,” while explaining melodies named Uramanan (in the Hazaj meter), Bahr-e Mushakil, and the Dobeyti meter, wrote:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">This meter is the most pleasant of the Pahlaviats, whose melodic versions are called Uramanan [...] I found all the people of Iraq, from scholar to commoner, from lowly to noble, delighted in composing and reciting it, and nothing affects them like:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">The melody of Uraman and the Pahlavi verse, the stroke of the rud and the royal sama’</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">[...] Special and common people have become enamored with this type. Scholars and commoners have become delighted with this poetry; the ascetic and the sinner have a share in it, the righteous and the wicked have a desire for it. Those with crooked taste who cannot distinguish poetry from prose and have no knowledge of meter and rhythm come to dance on the pretext of a Taraneh.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>The reason for the author’s agitation and his criticism of the “crooked-tasted” inclined toward Taraneh was that he considered the newly emerged poems of this type of melody to be non-literary and lowly, and contrary to the rules and forms of prosodic poetry. Thus, it must be said that composing such poems as Tasnif, contrary to Yahya Aryanpour’s opinion, became common from the seventh century:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Tasnif, unlike literary poetry that belonged to the elite, belongs to the common classes, and therefore the grammatical rules that cannot be overlooked in literary poetry are not so strictly observed in Tasnifs. Ultimately, because music is an inseparable and integral part of it, the form of Tasnif is more dependent on the melody.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>Abd al-Qadir Maraghi, in the ninth century, while listing musical types and explaining the “kinds of Tasnifs,” which we discussed earlier, mentioned in his book “Sharh al-Adwar” Tasnifs titled “Havayi,” which can be said to have been formed in continuation of the same Taranehs of Shams Qays Razi’s era:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">As for Havayi: it is such that a melody or sound that appears among people every once in a while, which people call “Zad,” and anyone can improvise it as they wish, sometimes it is constrained and sometimes not, and it is the lightest of the kinds of Tasnifs.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>Of course, from the context of Abd al-Qadir Maraghi’s words, it is clear that he did not lump all Tasnifs of his time together and only considered Havayi light and the “lightest” of Tasnifs, and therefore he certainly had a different judgment about the so-called “heavy and colorful” Tasnifs of that era. In any case, given the attribute “people-born” that Maraghi gave to this type of Tasnif, it is clear that the lyrics of the Havayis had a street-market structure and the prosodic and formal rules were not observed in them.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Yahya Aryanpour: Tasnifs, unlike literary poems that are the product of great artists and masters, are not composed for specific classes. Their composers are mostly unknown, and because they do not leave a pen name or any trace of themselves, their identity remains unknown forever. Tasnifs speak from the language of the majority of the people and for the people themselves, and in them feeling prevails over thought.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Hassan Mashhoon: Edward Browne writes: “Iranians not only read the poems of Saadi, Hafez, and Khaghani with the setar and under the shade of trees [they read], but when they are engaged in recreation in gardens, they also read other poems.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Besides the great, lofty, eloquent, and solid poems, Iranians have light poems whose meter is different from the usual meters seen in books, and these poems are precisely Tasnif, which they read in streets and gardens, and sometimes with the melody of the setar. As far as I have investigated, their composers are unknown, and those who compose Tasnif prefer to remain anonymous.”</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>Apparently, until Malek al-Sho’ara Bahar composed poems for the Tasnifs of Darvish Khan and ..., the view of all prominent and famous poets of that time about composing Tasnif was the same. Iraj Mirza, in a satirical poem he composed for Aref Qazvini, considered the status and rank of a “Tasnif composer” lower than that of a poet:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">You show off your cleverness with bad poetry<br>\nYou compose ghazals, and that too in politics</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Don’t act like a deer, my dear, you are a boar<br>\nYou are not a poet, you are a Tasnif composer</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>Until the early Pahlavi period, such a belief existed even among musicians themselves about Tasnif and Tasnif singing, and most “Avaz-khans” mockingly and contemptuously called those who were not expert in Avaz singing and engaged in Tasnif singing “Tasnif-khan”:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Rouhollah Khaleqi: In Iranian music, it was customary for those who had a loud voice and had tahrir to sing Avaz, and famous singers considered singing Tasnif and performing rhythmic melodies a simple matter and beneath their dignity.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h3>Popular Tasnif</h3>\n<p>Today, we have no information about the “melody” of Tasnifs that were composed in the past with everyday themes and “humor, jest, or praise and criticism of people and situations” because the formation of these melodies was influenced by the specific social events of that time and naturally lost their application over time. Nevertheless, some tazkireh writers and researchers have recorded the “lyrics” of some of these Tasnifs, the most famous of whom is Valentin Zhukovsky (1858–1918), the Russian orientalist. He recorded a number of popular poems from the Qajar period in the book “Popular Poems of Iran.”</p>\n<p>Many of these Tasnifs were read in the style of rhythmic children’s poems, like “Atal Matal Tutuleh,” and lacked a specific melody. Also, the melody of most “melodic” Tasnifs that were created with contemporary themes has been forgotten over time. In the meantime, the melody of some “educational” songs that have a long-term application still remains in the memory of some parents and is taught to children, like the melody of this educational poem that even some singers have performed:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Gandom gol-e gandom ey khoda<br>\nDokhtar mal-e mardom ey khoda<br>\nGandomo chi mikhore<br>\nGandomo moosh mikhore ...</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>Many examples of such Qajar-era poems can be cited, but here we will suffice with this amount. However, it is worth reminding that composing street-market Tasnifs continued to flourish until one or two decades after the establishment of the Pahlavi dynasty.</p>\n<p>These Tasnifs gradually moved away from their “people-born” form and were mostly placed in the domain of the productions of motrebs, that is, second-rate and semi-professional musicians and players. Most of these motrebs who performed at private gatherings were stationed until twenty or thirty years ago in the entertainment houses on Sirous Street in Tehran and, for a usually small fee, would warm up people’s gatherings and parties by singing these kinds of Tasnifs.</p>\n<p>The identity of most composers of these new Tasnifs, like the past, has remained unknown, but considering the terms and vocabulary used in the lyrics of some of these Tasnifs, such as “matik,” “mademoiselle,” “sandwich,” “machine,” etc., one can roughly estimate which period they belong to.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h3>Religious Tasnif (Nowheh)</h3>\n<p>Here, before we get to the main subject of Tasnif, that is, lyrical Tasnifs, it is necessary to make a reference to religious Tasnifs as well.</p>\n<p>The music used in religious gatherings and ceremonies has always been vocal music without instrumental accompaniment, which is performed either in the “Avaz” type or in the “Tasnif” type. The only factor that distinguishes religious music from non-religious music is the type of lyrics and the theme of the poems.</p>\n<p>Religious Tasnif is the same as “Nowheh,” which is performed along with chest-beating in religious times and especially on the occasions of the month of Muharram. Naturally, because of its “group” nature and the fact that the rhythm of its melody is followed and reinforced by the strikes of the chest-beaters, it cannot be in the form of “Avaz” or non-metric music. The performers of religious Tasnifs are Nowheh-khans, whom the chest-beaters and chain-beaters join at certain points of the Nowheh and repeat and sing along with the Nowheh-khans the main verses of the Nowheh poem as a refrain:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Hassan Mashhoon: In an era when musicians were rejected and the act of music was considered contrary to Sharia and among the forbidden acts, and had lost its popularity and credibility and was declining, maintaining mourning ceremonies such as processions and Nowheh-singing, which itself was a “Tasnif” with religious words and themes with different rhythms in chest-beating and Rawzeh-khani, itself became the showcase of national musical melodies and tones [...]</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Chest-beating, whose Nowheh has rhythm and melody; considering the technical characteristics of music composed by knowledgeable masters, and only differing in theme and words from rhythmic and melodic [non-religious melodic] Tasnifs, was the best means of preserving and propagating the rhythmic melodies of national music.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>Therefore, if there is to be any comparison, the melody of all religious Tasnifs, the melody of all lyrical Tasnifs, and the melody of all motrebi, popular, and street-market Tasnifs have the same origin and source and are formed according to the main framework of Iranian music and within the framework of the modes of the dastgahs; but when it comes to lyrics, each of them plays a different tune and separates its path from the other.</p>\n<p>Between the composers of Nowhehs and lyrical Tasnifs, there has always been a musical exchange, and each of them borrowed melodies from the opposite group and, after changing their lyrics, performed them in their own gatherings. Even today, we see that some Nowheh-khans arrange their Nowhehs by borrowing from today’s pop music songs:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Bahman Kazemi: But it should not be forgotten that some Nowheh-composing poets were not the creators of the melodies and tunes of the Nowhehs and only limited themselves to poetry, while another person composed the melodies. Among them was Mirza Baba-ye Tafreshi — the master of Nekisa-ye Tafreshi — who prepared the Avazes and Nowhehs for the Ta’zieh in Tekyeh Dowlat, and as Mashhoon narrates, he composed Nowhehs based on old Tasnifs whose melodies he was familiar with, while preserving the same meter and melody. Therefore, the Tasnifs composed in the past were sometimes a source of inspiration for Nowheh-khans in mourning processions.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>We said that the melody trend in all types of Tasnif, whether religious, formal, motrebi, popular, or street-market, has been based on the framework of official and dastgahi music. It is worth reminding a point here; today’s dastgahi music is based on foundations that are hundreds and even thousands of years old and, from ancient times until about a hundred years ago, apart from its gradual and inevitable historical changes, has revolved around roughly one axis.</p>\n<p>But after the changes that occurred in Iran following the industrialization of the Western world, we have witnessed the emergence and prevalence of other musics such as jazz, pop, rap, underground, fusion, etc., all of whose products by their creators have been produced and presented as “singles” accompanied by Persian lyrics, and therefore, in a general phrase, all of them can be considered “Tasnif.” This new type of music is usually not within the framework of the modes of dastgahi music, and even if it is, there have been turns and leaps in their melodies that have no history of use in our official music.</p>\n<p>As far as we know, in recent times, no street-market Tasnif that has a “people-born” nature has become widespread and popular among the people in this new type of music, and what has been created in non-dastgahi musics has always been by “professionals” who have delivered their productions to the ears of the people through mass media or through the publication of “albums.” However, in the field of religious music, as mentioned, we see that in the last one or two decades, melodies of the “pop” music genre have also been used by today’s maddahs and Nowheh-khans.</p>\n<p>Therefore, when we said that the melody trend in all types of Tasnif, whether religious, formal, motrebi, popular, or street-market, has been based on the framework of official and dastgahi music, we mean that all of them have been formed in frameworks such as Dashti, Afshari, Segah, Abu Ata, etc., with melodic turns and movements compatible and in harmony with the framework of dastgahi music.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h3>Lyrical Tasnif</h3>\n<p>The main themes of the Tasnifs of official and classical Iranian music have always been the description of love and affection, the description of the beloved’s beauty, complaint of separation and the beloved’s unfaithfulness, and praise of the beauty of flowers, nightingales, spring, greenery, and nature, like the Tasnif that Abd al-Qadir Maraghi composed at the end of the eighth century based on these verses:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">When the flower came out of its private chamber<br>\nIt saw a world longing for its face</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">On the throne of beauty, it became the sultan of the beauties of the garden<br>\nDrunk with its own beauty and longing for its own face</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Until you become annihilated in love like the moth<br>\nHow can you rule over your own existence</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>We have no information about the melody of this Tasnif and Maraghi’s other Tasnifs. He himself wrote that he recorded the melodies of his works in a book called “Kanz al-Alhan,” but no trace of this book has been found so far:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">And we mentioned the description and explanation of those pieces in the book Kanz al-Alhan so that if someone has acquired scientific and practical knowledge of this [art], it would be possible for him to extract them.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>Years ago, Farhad Fakhreddini managed to extract the melody of one of his Tasnifs, which had been recorded according to Maraghi’s own melodic notation method, and used it in the music of the television series “Imam Ali,” directed by Davoud Mirbaqeri.</p>\n<p>In any case, the melody trend and how it moves in the Tasnifs of Maraghi’s era and the periods before him is unknown, but perhaps the melody of some Tasnifs by unknown composers that have reached this era orally may be remnants of the periods from the ninth century onward, especially the Safavid era. Our probability in this regard particularly concerns certain metric vocal gushehs of the radif, like Saghinameh and Chaharpareh, etc., which are in fact considered “Tasnif”:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Arshad Tahmasebi: Experience has shown that people forget verse later than prose, and on this basis, in the memory of musicians as well, metric melodies such as Tasnif and Rang, compared to Avaz (non-metric pieces), have more durability and are forgotten later. For this reason, it can be said that certainly many classical Tasnifs of today — which are referred to as old Tasnifs and whose composers are unknown — belong to periods before the Qajar era.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">It is not bad to give an example in this regard. Today, we do not know anyone who has not heard the melody of Chahargah “Emshab Che Shabi St Shab-e Morad Ast Emshab ...” which is sung in wedding ceremonies. Perhaps this is the only old Tasnif whose antiquity can be estimated by referring to a treatise. This melody dates back at least to the middle of the Safavid period, that is, about 350 years ago, because the mentioned treatise was written during the reign of Shah Soleiman the Safavid.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">The name of the treatise is “Aqa’id al-Nisa’ wa Mir’at al-Bulaha’,” written by Aqa Jamal Khansari, one of the great religious scholars and famous jurists of that time, and in it he mocked the superstitious beliefs of the people of that era, and for this reason, it has long been known as the “Treatise of Kolthum Naneh.” The author, in the section on the rites related to the wedding night, wrote: “And on the wedding night, if there is a little rain, Baji Yasaman believes that the bride has eaten tahdig [...] and Bibi Shah Zaynab said that scattering candy is obligatory [...] and playing the daf is obligatory and women singing loudly that:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Bada bada khanom mobarak bada<br>\nEmshab che shab ast shab-e vasal ast emshab</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">In khaneh por az sham’ o cheragh ast emshab”.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">In the Qajar period, besides a few Tasnifs that remain from Aqa Ali Akbar Farahani and contemporaries of Ali Akbar Shida, like Shurideh Shirazi, Zahir al-Dowleh, Sama’ Hozur, and Salar Mo’azzaz, the composers of Tasnifs related to the period before Shida are unknown. Out of a total of 186 Tasnifs narrated in the book “Vocal Radif and Old Tasnifs according to Abdollah Davami,” 100 Tasnifs belong to unknown composers, most of which may be Tasnifs related to the Safavid period or before.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h3>Lyrics of Tasnif</h3>\n<p>The lyrics of most Tasnifs whose composers are unknown are prosodic poetry and composed by famous old poets, especially Hafez and Saadi, like these Tasnifs:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Tasnif of Bayat-e Isfahan:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Ey padshah-e khoban dad az gham-e tanha’i<br>\nDel bi to be jan amad vaght ast ke baz ayi (Hafez)</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Tasnif of Bayat-e Isfahan:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Eysham madam ast az la’l-e delkhah<br>\nKaram be kam ast alhamdolellah ... (Hafez)</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Tasnif of Bayat-e Isfahan:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Biya ta gol barafshanim o mey dar saghar andazim<br>\nFalak ra sagf beshkafim o tarhi no darandazim ... (Hafez)</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Tasnif of Bayat-e Isfahan:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Man nadanestam az avval ke to bi mehr o vafayi<br>\nAhd nabastan az an beh ke bebandi o napayi ... (Saadi)</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Tasnif of Homayun:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Ankeh halak-e man hami khahad o man salamatash<br>\nHar che konad be shahdi kas nakonad malamatash ... (Saadi)</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>The matla’ or some verses of these Tasnifs are also composed by old poets, and the rest of the lyrics and phrases are additions by the Tasnif composer himself, which probably should be considered newer Tasnifs:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Yahya Aryanpour: Tasnifs that were composed after the Safavid period are to a large extent free from prosodic meter, and although in some of them verses or hemistichs can be seen that match prosodic meters, they are mostly and in general not bound by prosodic meter, and their foundation is on the number of syllables.</p>\n<p>Like these Tasnifs:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Tasnif of Bidad:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Khosh bovad yari o yari dar kenar-e sabze zari<br>\nMehrabanan rou bar ham vaz hosoudan bar kenari ... (Saadi)</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Ey habibam ey tabibam eshq-e rouyat shod nasibam<br>\nEy negara to dar dasht o kohsar man be zanjir-e zolfat gereftar (additions by the Tasnif composer)</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Tasnif of Shushtari:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Ma ra nabovad deli ke kar ayad az ou<br>\nJoz naleh ke har dami hezar ayad az ou (Abu Saeed Abolkheir)</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Ey yar-e aziz barkhiz o bariz janam hamin hala bebem mey-e a’la<br>\nBe sorkhi-ye laleh hamra be saghar kon to az mina<br>\nMosaffa chon rokh-e azra karam kon badeh ma ra (additions by the Tasnif composer)</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Tasnif of Bidad:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Be jahan khorram az anam ke jahan khorram az oust<br>\nAsheqam bar hame alam ke hame alam az oust ... (Saadi)</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Yar-e khoshgelam sham’-e mahfelam khoun makon delam negaram<br>\nYar-e khosh eda shoukh o delruba az ghamat zar o nazaram ... (additions by the Tasnif composer)</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>Hassan Mashhoon has called the additions by Tasnif composers to the lyrics of old poets, which are usually not bound by prosodic meter, “bargardan” (refrain):</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Hassan Mashhoon: It is not far-fetched that using one verse or the matla’ of ghazals by others and making a bargardan poem for it and turning it into a Tasnif, which was common among professionals in the Qajar era, has an older history.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>The lyrics of some Tasnifs are also adaptations of poems by old poets, plus compositions by the Tasnif composer himself, most of which can probably be considered Tasnifs after the Safavid period, like this one:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Tasnif of Shushtari:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Ze dast-e mahboub che ha keshidam<br>\nBe joz jafayash vafa nadidam (additions by the Tasnif composer)</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Na ham zabani ke yek zamani be man begouyad gham-e ke dari<br>\nNa ahl-e dardi na gham gosari ze man beporsad gham-e ke dari (adaptation of a poem by Mohtasham Kashani)</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>The poems of some Tasnifs are from the compositions of Foroughi Bastami (1176–1236 solar) and Qa’ani (1186–1232 solar), poets of the Qajar period, and therefore the period of their creation is known, like these two Tasnifs:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Tasnif of Bayat-e Isfahan:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Chin-e zolf-e moshkin ra bar rokh-e negaram bin<br>\nHalqeh-ha-ye ou beshmar oqdeh-ha-ye karam bin ... (Foroughi Bastami)</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Tasnif of Afshari:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Doush ke in gerd gerd-e gonbad-e mina<br>\nAbaleh goun shod do cheshm-e [cho chehr-e] man ze sorraya ... (Qa’ani)</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>In some Tasnifs, poems from two poets have been used, or for example, in Tasnif number 181 from the book “Vocal Radif and Old Tasnifs according to Abdollah Davami,” two different poems by Saadi have been used:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li style=\"list-style-type: none;\">\n<ol>\n  <li>Ma ra hame shab nemibarad khab ...</li>\n  <li>Ey zolf-e to har khami kamandi ...</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ol>\n&nbsp;\n<h4>Weaknesses in the Lyrics of Old Tasnifs up to the Qajar Era</h4>\n<p>One of the common aspects in the lyrics of Tasnifs, until the end of Aref Qazvini’s activity and the beginning of Mohammad Ali Amirjahad’s activity, is that one can rarely find a poem in these Tasnifs that has been set to the melody without “hashwiyat” and “zavayed.” In truth, it must be said that until the period of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d8%a7%d9%85%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%ac%d8%a7%d9%87%d8%af/\">Mohammad Ali Amirjahad</a>, the main emphasis of most Tasnif composers was not on the quality and content of the poem but on the beauty of the melodic movement. The flaws in the lyrics of such Tasnifs are as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>The existence of “redundant vav,” repeatedly, on certain extended syllables of the poem to resolve musical syncopation, such as “Afaqo ra gardideh am” instead of “Afaq ra gardideh am”;</li>\n  <li>The existence of redundant and superfluous words, such as janam, khoda, aziz, vay, etc., to compensate for the mismatch between the phrases of the poem and the sentences of the melody;</li>\n  <li>The existence of “cliché” phrases such as yar-e khoshgelam, mahboub-e man, etc., as well as the juxtaposition of superficial phrases and themes alongside the poems of famous poets:</li>\n</ol>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Cheshm-e Reza o marhamat bar hame baz mikoni<br>\nChon ke be bakht-e ma beresad in hame naz mikoni ... (Saadi)</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Vallahi ballahi akh che boudeh taghsiram ke kardi zanjiram<br>\nAz hejr-e rouyat mimiram abrou t zadeh be shamshiram (additions by the Tasnif composer)</p>\n<ol start=\"4\">\n  <li>The existence of breakage and gaps between the syllables of a word:</li>\n</ol>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Ta key be taman</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Ta key be tamannay-e vesal-e to yeganeh (Sheikh Bahai)</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Jana saf-e mozh</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Jana saf-e mozhgan ra yek martebeh bar ham zan (unknown)</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h3>Structural Features of Tasnifs</h3>\n<p>In the following, we examine several structural features of the Tasnif form:</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h4>Melodic Movement in Tasnif</h4>\n<p>The way the melody moves at the beginning of old Tasnifs, like most metric types of music, is based on the element of “repetition and association”; following this principle, that is, repeating the first melodic theme, especially in Tasnif as a popular type, gives the audience the opportunity to digest the melodic theme and establish a better connection with it. In 77 cases out of 100 old Tasnifs, the theme at the beginning of the melody has been repeated, and only in 23 cases of Tasnifs has such an action not taken place. Obviously, the purpose of repeating the melodic movement in Tasnif is repeating the melody with different lyrics, because the melody accompanied by fixed lyrics can be repeated many times.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h4>Tahrir in Tasnif</h4>\n<p>Tahrir in the text of old Tasnifs has appeared in simple forms because composers, considering the “popular” nature of this type, have avoided using various and complex tahrirs that are used in the “Avaz” type. Tahrir is considered one of the “technical” elements of singing and is one of the tricks that shows the practical skill of a “professional” singer.</p>\n<p>In addition, the metric nature of Tasnif to some extent deprives it of the possibility of using diverse and long tahrirs. Also, the use of these same simple and uniform tahrirs is usually in their shortest form, which consists of four or six notes, and rarely have eight-note tahrirs been used in Tasnifs.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h4>Absence of Disjointed Intervals in Tasnif</h4>\n<p>The nature of melodic movement in the themes of Iranian music is based on a “step-by-step” movement and passing through continuous intervals, and basically, the use of disjointed intervals and leaping to distant notes does not exist in the nature of this music. Following this, the melodic movement of musical phrases in old Tasnifs has also been carried out with a step-by-step movement, sometimes passing to the next note and sometimes passing to the previous note.</p>\n<p>In the course of the melody, it is sometimes possible that one of the notes is omitted and the melodic movement continues by passing over the next or previous note of the missing sound, in which case a “third interval” will be created.</p>\n<p>In Iranian music and consequently in old Tasnifs, leaping to non-adjacent notes in farther intervals occurs when there is an intention to enter another maqam of the dastgah. Entering the new path usually takes place after the end of the previous path.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h4>Absence of Interval-Breaking Maqams in Tasnif</h4>\n<p>Considering the popular nature of Tasnif, usually in old Tasnifs, entry into “interval-breaking” maqams, whose sonic bed is different from the main maqam (the first maqam, the mother maqam, or the head dastgah maqam), has been avoided. In truth, the melodic movement in old Tasnifs has only taken place in one path and without entering unfamiliar side paths. Observing this point in composing Tasnif makes its learning easier for people who do not have the skill to perform broken notes and variable intervals. Also, not entering multiple and diverse maqams reduces the duration of Tasnifs, and therefore these types of Tasnifs can be registered in the listeners’ memory faster:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Abolhasan Saba: In private gatherings [...] the master would start playing the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d9%be%db%8c%d8%b4-%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%a2%d9%85%d8%af/\">Pishdaramad</a> (which had become common recently) in complete silence, and a light rhythm would accompany him. Then he would start playing the Avaz [daramad] related to the same dastgah in which the Pishdaramad was played [...]</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Until, by means of the Tasnif, which was rhythmic, the silence and calm would be broken and a state of ecstasy and joy would be given to the gathering by singing the Tasnif, so that sometimes individuals would harmonize with the Tasnif singer and a commotion and excitement would arise, and at the end, the music would conclude with the specific Rang of the dastgah; of course, second-degree masters in smaller gatherings would behave in the same manner.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>If we add these points to the absence of complex tahrirs in Tasnif, and also the existence of a clear message in its lyrics, we will see that every trick has been used in this type of music to turn it into a completely “popular” and widespread type.</p>\n<p>We said that in composing old Tasnifs, entry into interval-breaking maqams has been avoided. For example, in none of the Tasnifs of the Dastgah of Chahargah have the maqams of “Hesar” and “Mouyeh Kabir,” whose frets differ from the frets of the main maqam (the first maqam, the mother maqam, or the head dastgah maqam: Chahargah), been used.</p>\n<p>The composers of all these Tasnifs, after presenting the first maqam of the dastgah, that is, the maqam of Chahargah, have entered either “Zabol,” or “Mokhalef,” or “Maghloub,” whose frets are in continuation of the frets of Chahargah. In simpler terms, if the audience of such Tasnifs can understand and perform the seven sounds, for example, “Do, Re koron, Mi, Fa, Sol, La koron, and Si,” they can perform the Tasnif.</p>\n<p>It is also worth reminding that what we said about interval-breaking does not include Tasnifs dedicated to the maqams of “Dashti,” “Afshari,” and “Iraq” because these three maqams inherently and by themselves have “variable frets”; therefore, it is not at all unlikely that, for example, in every Tasnif related to the Afshari context, there are multiple “arazi” signs.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>Structure and Characteristics of Tasnifs in Iranian Music</h2>\n<p>The non-use of multiple maqams in old Tasnifs indicates the trace of the old maqam music system in them, and thus it can be assumed that some of these Tasnifs were composed before the emergence of the dastgah system.</p>\n<p>As far as possible, in this concise discussion, we expressed a summary of the how and structure of old Tasnifs, and ultimately, we can summarize the characteristics of an old Tasnif as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Tasnif is a metric type with lyrics;</li>\n  <li>It is a non-improvised type composed in advance;</li>\n  <li>It has more fame and a “popular” characteristic than any other musical type;</li>\n  <li>It usually has a heavy meter and in most cases is composed in meters such as “six-four”;</li>\n  <li>In some cases, it is composed in a polymetric form, and its ending rhythm is relatively fast and cheerful;</li>\n  <li>It does not have a fixed duration and may last from half to three minutes;</li>\n  <li>It moves in one or two maqams, and its movement range, unlike other musical types, is limited;</li>\n  <li>It can be formed with old and pre-composed poems, as well as with poems composed for the melody;</li>\n  <li>The theme of its poems is lyrical and romantic;</li>\n  <li>Its place of performance is before the Rang:</li>\n</ol>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Abolhasan Saba: After the Tasnif, the music would conclude with the specific Rang of the dastgah.</p>\n<p>Thus, a comprehensive and exclusive definition for Tasnif can be something like this:</p>\n<p><strong>“A metric and lyrical type, vocal and non-improvised, which is performed before the last part of programmatic Iranian music (Rang).”</strong></p>\n&nbsp;\n<h3>The Difference Between the Types of Taraneh and Tasnif</h3>\n<p>In Iranian music, the difference between the two terms or types “Taraneh” and “Tasnif” is still not clear. The common oral understanding among musicians, especially in the years before the revolution, was that supporters of official and dastgahi music considered Taraneh a light and popular type with lower artistic value than Tasnif, while for supporters of pop and cafe music, Tasnif was considered a type of Taraneh of the old music genre and, of course, tedious.</p>\n<p>Nevertheless, neither of these two groups had provided a comprehensive and complete definition to express the characteristics and structural differences of these two terms. In general, the prevalence of these two terms from the Qajar period to today has been as follows:</p>\n<p>- All such works of dastgahi music until the period of the formation of the “Golha” programs on the radio, which coincided with the peak activity of new Taraneh composers, were called Tasnif.</p>\n<p>- All such works of dastgahi music from the period of the formation of the “Golha” programs on the radio until 1357 solar were sometimes called Taraneh and sometimes Tasnif:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Rouhollah Khaleqi: As far as I have investigated [Morteza Mahjoubi’s works], there are twenty-two “Taranehs” [...] I say with complete boldness that Mahjoubi’s “Tasnifs,” especially a few of them, are among those eternal melodies that will remain immortal in our music.</p>\n<p>- All such works of dastgahi music from 1357 solar onward have been called Tasnif.</p>\n<p>- All such works of non-dastgahi musics from the beginning until now have been called Taraneh.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>From the 1350s solar decade onward, a group of fundamentalist musicians inclined toward the Qajar style, most of whom were students of Nour Ali Boroumand, began their activities with the reconstruction of old and Qajari works, including the Tasnifs of Shida, Aref, etc., on the radio. Musicians following this style, the most active of whom was “Mohammad Reza Lotfi,” as revivers of old traditions, always called their old lyrical works or their own compositions “Tasnif,” and following this, radio program announcers also, for example in the “Golchin-e Hafteh” program, introduced these works to listeners under the title of Tasnif.</p>\n<p>Therefore, from this period, simultaneously with the revival of old music, the term Tasnif, which had been obsolete for a long time, was revived again:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Sasan Fatemi: Later, the musicians of this new wave began to create Tasnifs and revived this title (Tasnif) to distinguish themselves from Taraneh composers.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>In general, it can be said that in those years, the “dual reading” of these types of works was not due to their inherent difference but with regard to the style and viewpoint of “the owners of those works.” In other words, usually the works of musicians committed to radifi traditions were called “Tasnif,” and the works of musicians who had a freer outlook were called “Taraneh.” For example, in those days, no one would call a lyrical work by “Faramarz Payvar” or “Mohammad Reza Lotfi” a Taraneh, while the works of, for example, “Homayoun Khorram” and “Parviz Yahaghi” were more often called Taraneh, and sometimes Tasnif.</p>\n<p>Nevertheless, due to the lack of a clear boundary between these types of melodies, musicians and writers, when referring to these works and even works related to pop music, have used the terms Tasnif and Taraneh interchangeably:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Milad Azimi: Do you pay attention to the lyrics of these works?</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Houshang Ebtehaj: Very much. I have paid attention from the old days. For example, I paid a lot of attention to the works of Shahriar Qanbari, Ardalan Sarfaraz, and Iraj Genti Attai. I still do. In my opinion, they were more pioneering than others. Even more pioneering than the poets of modern poetry. There were very fresh and novel things in their Taranehs that were not even in modern poetry. They have many beautiful works with good poetic moments. For example, the poet’s look in that Tasnif “Do Mahi” that Googoosh sang and placed herself in the position of a fish is extraordinary.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>In the meantime, the only point that should be mentioned and perhaps can be a good criterion for showing the distinction between Tasnif and Taraneh is that in the lyrics of none of the works of famous musicians who were involved in official and dastgahi music, such as Darvish Khan, Vaziri, Khaleqi, Payvar, etc., colloquial words are seen, while the lyrics of some works by other musicians have been said in a colloquial and informal form, like a song that Akbar Golpayegani has sung:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Mou-ye sepid“o” touy “Aineh” didam<br>\nAhi boland az tah-e del keshidam</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Ta zir-e lab shokouh “ro” kardam aghaz<br>\nAql heyam zad ke “khodet” “ro” nabaz</p>\n<p>Or this famous song by Ata’ollah Khorram sung by Mohammad Reza Shajarian:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Man o to ghesseh-ye yek kohneh ketabim “mageh” na “mageh” na<br>\n“Ye” so’alim “ye” so’al-e bi javabim “mageh” na “mageh” na<br>\n“Ye” rouzi ghesseh-ye por ghosseh-ye ma “tamoum” “misheh”<br>\n“Akhresh” noghteh-ye payan-e ketabim “mageh” na “mageh” na (Bijan Samandar)</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>Sometimes we see that although the words of the lyrics of some songs have been composed in a formal and non-colloquial way, the Taraneh composer, by changing the “vocalization” of the words, has made his poem colloquial and informal, like the poem of the song “Tavallod,” composed by Anoushiravan Rouhani, in which the poet has said “labet shad o delet khosh” instead of “labat shad o delat khosh.”</p>\n<p>After the events of 1357 solar, which effectively led to the closure of music programs on radio, television, etc., all violinists and Taraneh composers, as well as most singers and soloists of other instruments, became housebound and had no opportunity to work. Obviously, pop music was also completely banned in this period, and the music scene for a while turned into a place for the absolute dominance of anthem composers. Therefore, for a while, not only Tasnifi and Taraneh songs but even the terms themselves were never heard from state media.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>Prominent Tasnif Composers of Iran</h2>\n<p>In Iran, Ali Akbar Shida and Aref Qazvini are, after the revival period, in a way the pioneers of the Tasnif composition industry in Iran, and later Amirjahad also engaged in this work.</p>\n<p>The oldest Tasnif in the contemporary era dates back to 60 years before the birth of “Shida,” which was very common in southern Iran and in the Fars region, and its theme is about the betrayal that happened to Lotf Ali Khan Zand, and he was forced to abdicate, and Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar came to power.</p>\n<p>Simultaneously with Shida or a little before him, a Tasnif had been composed on the poem “Ey Tir-e Ghamat Ra Del-e Oshagh Neshaneh” by Khayali Bukhara’i, and it was very common in that period.</p>\n<p>Also, at the age of 33 of Shida, a Tasnif was common in the Fars region (the birthplace of Ali Akbar Khan Shida) that describes the summoning of Farhad Mirza, the strict governor of Fars, to Tehran.</p>\n<p>Ali Akbar Khan Shida is one of the most prominent Iranian Tasnif composers who has composed many Tasnifs. Some of Shida’s Tasnifs were very popular and had a more popular structure, and some others, which mostly date back to his period in the khanqah, have a more serious structure. Nevertheless, the beauty of the structure and more serious compositions of Shida caused these Tasnifs to also become very popular among the people.</p>\n<p>Before the period of Aref and even a little before Shida, the Tasnif had become very popular and had declined; even Aref says that before he composed national and patriotic poems, the Tasnif composition system had become so childish and popular that they had even composed a Tasnif for the king’s cat and sang it, or Tasnifs had been composed in which insulting words were used.</p>\n<p>Aref’s Tasnif composition system and the integration of poetry and music is very interesting. There are two general views in examining Aref’s works: some believe that Aref sacrificed poetry for music, and another view says that because both poetry and music were by Aref, surely Aref had many Tasnifs, and they recognize this model of integrating poetry and music as a style, and the places where it seems that the poems have been sacrificed for music are considered Aref’s contradictions.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>The article you read under the title “The Tasnif Form in Iranian Music” is taken from the book “How Iranian Music Is – An Investigation into Musical Types” written by Arshad Tahmasebi.</p>\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding the Tasnif form in Iranian music, please share it with us in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-10-20 18:59:44', 0, '2024-08-28 23:28:59', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1518, 'posts', 61, 'en', NULL, 'What is the meaning of the two terms rast-kuk and chap-kuk in Iranian music?', NULL, 'This article explains the concepts of “rast-kook” (right tuning) and “chap-kook” (left tuning) in Iranian music. These traditional terms refer to the use of different pitch ranges in instruments and vocal performance. In fretted instruments like the tar and setar, lower notes are located on the right side and higher notes on the left; therefore, performing in lower ranges is called rast-kook, while higher ranges are referred to as chap-kook.\n\nTraditionally, male voices (with lower pitch ranges) are associated with rast-kook, and female voices (with higher ranges) with chap-kook. However, this classification is not as precise as Western vocal range systems (such as soprano, alto, tenor, etc.), and some male singers are also capable of singing in higher (chap-kook) ranges.\n\nThe article also reviews how different tonal centers (modes) in Iranian classical music are categorized as rast-kook or chap-kook. For instance, in the Shur mode, certain tonalities are considered rast-kook while others are chap-kook. Similar classifications are provided for other systems such as Segah, Homayoun, Chahargah, Mahur, and Rast-Panjgah.\n\nOverall, rast-kook and chap-kook serve as practical, traditional references for identifying vocal ranges and adapting musical performance to singers’ capabilities within the Iranian classical music system.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article from the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com\">Sornaz website</a>, we examine the terms <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Rast Kook</span></strong> and <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Chap Kook</span></strong> in Iranian music. Afterward, we will review the commonly used and frequently performed modes in the dastgahs and avazes of the Iranian music radif. Stay with us...</p>\n<h2>Concepts of Rast Kook and Chap Kook</h2>\n<p>The terms Rast Kook and Chap Kook in Iranian music have very ancient roots, but they became widely used and popular after the <a href=\"https://www.kojaro.com/history-art-culture/192556-qajar-confrontation-culture-western-world/\">Qajar period</a>.</p>\n<p>These terms probably came into existence because the necks of fretted instruments (such as tar and setar) and older instruments like the oud and barbat — which in ancient times, unlike today, had frets — were usually positioned diagonally and upward. Typically, the bass frets were on the right side of the instrument and the higher (treble) frets were on the left side. When a singer used the bass frets located on the right side of the instrument, it was said that the performance was in Rast Kook mode. If the player used the higher frets on the left side, it was said that the performance was in Chap Kook mode.</p>\n<p>Based on the above definition, the term Rast Kook was usually used for male singers and Chap Kook for female singers. Of course, these terms are not very precise. In Western music, much more accurate vocal classifications have been made for different voices and ranges, such as soprano, mezzo-soprano, alto, tenor, baritone, bass, contrabass, etc., in which European musicians have precisely defined the vocal range of each from beginning to end.</p>\n<p>In any case, in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a>, generally because women usually sing in higher registers, they are called Chap Kook, and men, because they sing lower, are called Rast Kook.</p>\n<p>Of course, we have also had male singers who sing in Chap Kook. Usually these singers gained attention because in certain periods in Iran there was a ban on women’s voices, and some male singers try to sing in the range of women — that is, the same vocal ranges known as Chap Kook. They use special techniques for voice production and sing very high, which is quite remarkable. Examples include many songs that Master <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b6%d8%a7-%d8%b4%d8%ac%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Mohammad Reza Shajarian</a> or Master Iraj Bastami have sung in the Chap Kook range.</p>\n<h2>Examining Which Commonly Used Modes in Iranian Music Dastgahs and Avazes Are Rast Kook or Chap Kook</h2>\n<p>Now we will examine which commonly performed modes in Iranian music dastgahs are Rast Kook or Chap Kook.</p>\n<p>In general, when examining the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b4%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Dastgah of Shur</a>, the Shur modes that are placed on the yellow string in the first hand position (the frets that are in the first hand placement on the string) can be considered somewhat Rast Kook, such as Shur in “G,” “A,” and “B.” The Shur modes in “C,” “D,” and “E” are called Chap Kook. Shur in “F” is also sometimes performed and is usually considered in the Rast Kook range.</p>\n<p>Accordingly, the affiliates of Shur are also classified. For example, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%b9%d8%b7%d8%a7/\">Abu Ata</a> in “A koron” is Rast Kook and Abu Ata in “D koron” is Chap Kook. Afshari in “C” and “D” are Rast Kook, while Afshari in “G,” “A,” and even “F” are considered Chap Kook. Also, Dashti in “D” is Rast Kook and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%af%d8%b4%d8%aa%db%8c/\">Dashti</a> in “A” is Chap Kook. Finally, Bayat-e Tork in “C” and “B flat” are Rast Kook, while Bayat-e Tork in “E flat” and “F” are Chap Kook.</p>\n<p>The note “C” is considered Rast Kook and the note “F” is considered Chap Kook.</p>\n<p>Segah in “A koron” and “B koron” are Rast Kook, while <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b3%d9%87-%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">Segah</a> in “D koron,” “E koron,” and “F sori” are counted as Chap Kook.</p>\n<p>Homayun in “A koron,” “B koron,” and “C sori” are Rast Kook, while Homayun in “D koron,” “E koron,” and “F sori” are Chap Kook.</p>\n<p>Chahargah in “C” and “D” are Chap Kook, while Chahargah in “G” and “A” are considered Rast Kook.</p>\n<p>Mahur in “B flat,” “G,” and “F” are Rast Kook, while Mahur in “E flat” and “C” are Chap Kook.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b1%d8%a7%d8%b3%d8%aa-%d9%be%d9%86%d8%ac%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">Rast Panjgah</a> in “B flat,” “G,” and “F” are Rast Kook, while Rast Panjgah in “E flat” and “C” are Chap Kook.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding the concepts of Rast Kook and Chap Kook in Iranian music, please share it with us in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-10-21 19:11:45', 0, '2025-02-14 01:00:45', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1519, 'posts', 62, 'en', NULL, 'Biography and works of Hossein Alizadeh, prominent Iranian musician | Member of 2 groups: Aref and Chavosh Center', NULL, 'This article introduces and reviews the life and artistic career of Hossein Alizadeh, one of the most prominent Iranian composers and instrumentalists. Born in 1951 in Tehran, he began his musical journey by entering a music conservatory and quickly rose to prominence under the guidance of renowned masters of Persian classical music.\n\nAlizadeh has created many influential works, including the famous album Ney Nava, and collaborated with major ensembles such as Chavosh. He also pursued further studies and teaching in Germany before returning to Iran, where he continued his extensive work in performance, composition, education, and research.\n\nIn addition to numerous concerts worldwide, he has composed successful film scores and received several prestigious honors, including multiple Grammy nominations. He has also authored important educational books for tar and setar, contributing significantly to music education in Iran. Overall, Hossein Alizadeh is considered one of the most influential figures in contemporary Persian music.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article from the series of articles on the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d8%b2%d9%86%d8%af%da%af%db%8c%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%85%d9%87-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%86/\">biographies of prominent musicians</a>, we introduce the distinguished and talented Iranian musician, <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Hossein</span> <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Alizadeh</span></strong></span>. We talk about his entry into the music conservatory and then mention the honors, works, and books authored by Master Alizadeh. Stay with us in the following...</p>\n<h2>Biography of Hossein Alizadeh</h2>\n<p>Hossein Mohammad Alizadeh, better known as Hossein Alizadeh, was born on 1 Shahrivar 1330 solar year (23 August 1951) in the Seyed Nasrollah Bazaar area of Tehran.</p>\n<p>Hossein Alizadeh was the second child in an eight-member family; he had two brothers and three sisters. His father was a skilled and honorable tailor from Urmia who was a soldier during World War II. After escaping from Russian soldiers, he came to Tehran and met his wife in this city. Hossein Alizadeh’s mother was from Arak but was born in Tehran and occasionally taught the Quran to others. Music had no history in Hossein Alizadeh’s family before him; however, imagination, creativity, and perhaps destiny guided him toward music.</p>\n<p>Master Alizadeh successfully completed his elementary education at Hafez School in Tehran. Since he also achieved good results in his studies, his family was thinking of enrolling him in a good high school. On the other hand, Hossein did not show much enthusiasm for choosing a field or going to high school until he was influenced by his cousin, who was a violin player, and became interested in music.</p>\n<p>Earlier in elementary school, he had become acquainted with Houshang Zarif, who worked as the school singing teacher, and Zarif had recognized Hossein Alizadeh’s talent in music from the very beginning. Although Alizadeh’s entry into the music conservatory was quite accidental, after entering he climbed the ladder of progress with full speed and excellence, and his playing was always different and distinctive from others.</p>\n<p>In that period, due to the prevailing cultural conditions in society, most families were not in favor of studying at the conservatory and in the field of music. However, Hossein Alizadeh’s family left the decision to him and agreed with his choice of the music conservatory. Unfortunately, in those days, addiction was very common in the artistic and musical community, and this was the only concern of Hossein Alizadeh’s father. But with the promise Hossein gave his father, this worry was resolved, and upon seeing his talent and progress in music, the family’s support for him increased even more.</p>\n<p>Hossein Alizadeh’s mother, despite having religious beliefs, did not prevent him from entering the music conservatory and was very happy about this event. From the beginning of his activities, she greatly encouraged and supported Hossein. She could also distinguish the sound of her son’s instrument from others and always attended Hossein Alizadeh’s concerts.</p>\n<p>They had very good and intimate relationships within the family and always tried to help each other. The calmness and warm, noble personality of Hossein Alizadeh are the result of this good educational environment and the love and affection present in the family.</p>\n<p>In that period, the costs of the music conservatory were higher than other high schools, and managing this issue was somewhat difficult for the Alizadeh family. However, through cost management and his mother’s savings, he was able to pass this period. On the other hand, because Hossein Alizadeh was considered a good student, from the third year and at the age of 17, he was employed in the Rudaki Orchestra under the direction of Dehlavi and was able to cover the expenses of the conservatory and his studies.</p>\n<p>After completing his studies at the music conservatory, Hossein Alizadeh was able to enter the Faculty of Fine Arts at the University of Tehran and graduated in two fields: performance and composition. During this student period, he engaged in further learning of authentic Iranian music at the Center for Preservation and Propagation of Music. He also performed concerts in the field of radifi music with great figures such as <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b6%d8%a7-%d9%84%d8%b7%d9%81%db%8c/\">Mohammad Reza Lotfi</a>, Arshad Tahmasebi, Mohammad Reza Shajarian, and other prominent musicians.</p>\n<p>Hossein Alizadeh completed his apprenticeship under many masters in the field of the Iranian music radif, including these great figures: Houshang Zarif, Habibollah Salehi, Mahmoud Karimi, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b2%d9%86%d8%af%da%af%db%8c%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%85%d9%87-%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d8%a7%da%a9%d8%a8%d8%b1-%d8%b4%d9%87%d9%86%d8%a7%d8%b2%db%8c/\">Ali Akbar Shahnazi</a>, Dariush Safvat, Nour Ali Boroumand, Saeed Hormozi, Yousef Foroutan, and Abdollah Davami.</p>\n<p>As mentioned earlier, Hossein Alizadeh became a member of the Rudaki Orchestra in 1347 solar year (1968) when he was only 17 years old. He also joined the Chavosh Institute along with other prominent Iranian music masters such as Mohammad Reza Lotfi and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%be%d8%b1%d9%88%db%8c%d8%b2-%d9%85%d8%b4%da%a9%d8%a7%d8%aa%db%8c%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Parviz Meshkatian</a> and performed and released immortal works in the field of Iranian modal music in the form of the Chavosh album collection. He also had a great impact on training musicians and players after the revolution by teaching many eager music students. A significant part of the revolutionary tasnifs and songs of that time, including the tasnif “Shahid” performed with the voice of Shahram Nazeri, are among Hossein Alizadeh’s many compositions from that period.</p>\n<h3>Creation of the Album “Neynava”</h3>\n<p>Some time after the revolution and in the midst of the imposed war, Master Alizadeh, influenced by the atmosphere of that period in the country and using his own ideas and creativity, produced the unique album “Neynava.” This album was released in 1362 solar year (1983) in collaboration with the “Mahour Cultural and Artistic Institute.” Neynava is one of Hossein Alizadeh’s most famous albums, which brought him great fame both inside and outside Iran.</p>\n<p>Many interpretations have been made of the name of this album, and many people consider it a religious piece. However, Master Alizadeh himself has rejected this theory and, although he believes that ideas from religious rituals were used in composing this piece, he does not consider the work to be entirely religious.</p>\n<p>Hossein Alizadeh explains the chosen name of this album as follows: because of the use of the ney in this song and his great interest in the Dastgah of Nava, he chose the name “Neynava” for his album. In this piece composed by Hossein Alizadeh, the ney is the narrator and storyteller, and Jamshid Andalibi performed the ney playing. Alizadeh stated that his motivation for releasing this album was to express the memories of that period of society through the language of music.</p>\n<p>After creating this work and before its release, Hossein Alizadeh left Iran for France. However, due to problems in obtaining a visa, he spent some time in Rome, Italy, instead of France, until he immigrated to Germany for study and teaching.</p>\n<h3>Immigration to Germany and Continuing Education</h3>\n<p>In the early 1360s solar decade (1980s), Hossein Alizadeh immigrated to <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A2%D9%84%D9%85%D8%A7%D9%86\">Germany</a> and continued his studies in world music at the Free University of Berlin.</p>\n<p>For his composition thesis, he created the piece “Osyan.” Alongside continuing his music studies, he also pursued his compositional activities with great perseverance and seriousness.</p>\n<p>After completing his studies and returning to Iran in 1367 solar year (1988), he rebuilt the Sheyda and Aref groups. The result was the passionate concert with the voice of Shahram Nazeri, Sediq Define, and Bijan Kamkar at Vahdat Hall. This concert was later publicly released as an album with the same name featuring the voice of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b4%d9%87%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%86%d8%a7%d8%b8%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Shahram Nazeri</a>. The years of Hossein Alizadeh’s study and effort in Germany brought very good results for him.</p>\n<h3>Powerful Restart After Returning to the Homeland</h3>\n<p>After this concert, Alizadeh gained experience in creating and producing various types of music. In the field of traditional and authentic Iranian music in the form of solo performance, one can mention works such as “Turkmen,” “Paykubi,” “Homayoun” (with setar), “Mahur,” “Hejrani,” “Hamnavayi,” and “Nava” (tar). In the field of ensemble playing, albums such as “Raz o Niyaz” (with the singing of Alireza Eftekhari), “Sobhgahi” (with the singing of Mohsen Karamati), and ensemble works (“Avay-e Mehr,” “Raz-e No,” “Be Tamashay-e Abha-ye Sepid,” “Neynava,” “Osyan,” and “Variation-e Kurdi”) can be mentioned. With the performance of the pieces “Shourangiz” and “Turkmen,” Hossein Alizadeh succeeded in opening a new window onto radifi Iranian music.</p>\n<p>Hossein Alizadeh’s solo and ensemble performances with Arshad Tahmasebi, as well as lectures and numerous concerts inside and outside Iran, recording of works and pieces, performance of the radif of Mirza Abdollah Farahani, compilation of the books “Ten Pieces for Tar” in which he presented his works for the tar, and other educational books and cassettes, teaching of tar and setar, work on an innovative maqam, and performance of the concerts “Dad o Bidad” (Dad is a gusheh of the Dastgah of Mahur and Bidad is a gusheh in the Dastgah of Homayoun), film music composition, etc., are among Master Hossein Alizadeh’s activities in the years after the revolution.</p>\n<p>Master Alizadeh was also a founding member and the first managing director of the House of Music and is currently the spokesperson of the Supreme Council of the House of Music.</p>\n<p>During these years, by creating and performing works and pieces such as “Savaran-e Dasht-e Omid” and “Hesar,” Hossein Alizadeh added a new and fresh form to Iranian modal music, which demonstrates his genius in this field.</p>\n<p>The creation of the “Ney” concerto and the orchestral piece titled “Neynava” in the early 1360s solar years brought even more fame to Hossein Alizadeh’s name. This piece, performed with the accompaniment of Jamshid Andalibi as the ney soloist, is considered one of the most prominent pieces in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a7%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%ae-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86/\">the history of Iranian music</a>. Master Hossein Alizadeh composed it inspired by the transformations of the early 1360s.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, in the early 1370s solar decade, Hossein Alizadeh took over the presidency of the National Music Conservatory. Another rich and prominent part of his career can be pointed to from the late 1370s to 1384 solar, when he, along with Mohammad Reza Shajarian in a four-member group with Kayhan Kalhor and Homayoun Shajarian, was busy holding concerts and performing programs in various countries.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_4089\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"550\"]<img class=\"wp-image-4089 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/sornaz_alizadeh_03.jpg\" alt=\"Hossein Alizadeh and the four-silver group with Mohammad Reza Shajarian, Kayhan Kalhor, and Homayoun Shajarian\" width=\"550\" height=\"403\" /> Hossein Alizadeh and the four-silver group with Mohammad Reza Shajarian, Kayhan Kalhor, and Homayoun Shajarian[/caption]\n<p>Hossein Alizadeh has two sons named Saba, who is a kamancheh player, and Nima, who is a robab and tar player. They are currently active in the “Hamavayan” group under his supervision.</p>\n<h3>Experience in Film Music Composition</h3>\n<p>Master Hossein Alizadeh also had very successful experiences in film music production, including the music for the following films:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>“Chopanan-e Kavir” by Hossein Mahjoub,</li>\n  <li>“Delshodegan” by Ali Hatami,</li>\n  <li>“Gabbeh” by Mohsen Makhmalbaf,</li>\n  <li>“Eshgh-e Taher” by Mohammad Ali Najafi,</li>\n  <li>“Iran Saray-e Man Ast” by Parviz Kimiavi,</li>\n  <li>“Zamani bara-ye Masti-ye Asbha” by Bahman Ghobadi,</li>\n  <li>“Abr o Aftab” by Mahmoud Kalari,</li>\n  <li>“Mirath-e Kohan” by Seyed Mohammad Mirsoltani,</li>\n  <li>“Zesht o Ziba” by Ahmad Reza Motamedi,</li>\n  <li>“Lakposhtha Ham Parvaz Mikonand” by Bahman Ghobadi,</li>\n  <li>“Niwemang” by Bahman Ghobadi,</li>\n  <li>Music for the television series “Zir-e Tigh.”</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Honors of Hossein Alizadeh</h2>\n<p>Throughout the years, Hossein Alizadeh has received many honors in various fields of performance and composition, some of which we mention below:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Hossein Alizadeh has been nominated three times for the Grammy Award in the category of Best Traditional World Music Album for the albums “Faryad,” “Bi To Be Sar Nemishavad,” and “Be Tamashay-e Abha-ye Sepid.” It should be noted that the Grammy Awards are considered the most prestigious award in the American recording and music broadcasting industry.</li>\n  <li>He has won the award for Best Film Music four times at the Fajr Film Festival for the composition of the films “Gabbeh,” “Zesht o Ziba,” “Avaz-e Gonjeshkha,” and “Malakeh.”</li>\n  <li>He received the award for Best Film Score for the film “Aseman-e Zard-e Kam Omgh” from the Association of Film Critics in 1392 solar year.</li>\n  <li>On 4 Khordad 1396 solar, he received the Asia World Music Biennial Award from the World Music Center Foundation in South Korea. This award is given to artists who have connected their country with other parts of the world.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Refusal to Receive the French Legion of Honour</h3>\n<p>On 7 Azar 1393 solar (28 November 2014), in a letter without stating a clear and obvious reason, Hossein Alizadeh announced his refusal to receive the French “Knight of Arts and Letters” medal. In this letter, he wrote that he considers himself in no need of receiving any medal. At the same time, in the text of the letter, Master Alizadeh stated that we value the gift of the cultured French nation.</p>\n<p>He also wrote in his letter with a critical tone that perhaps if in our own land there was attention and understanding of the sublime art of music among the officials, receiving a non-native gift would not have had so much reflection. When there is not enough light in the artistic space, a small lamp turns into the sun.</p>\n<p>He ended his letter as follows: While thanking the officials of the country and the French embassy, out of respect for the art-loving and artist-loving people of Iran, I will be content with the name Hossein Alizadeh and will not add any prefix or suffix to it until the end of my life.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h3>Books Authored by Hossein Alizadeh</h3>\n<p>Hossein Alizadeh should be considered one of the most influential players and composers in contemporary Iranian music. He has gone through the traditional and oral method of learning the radif in the presence of great masters such as Houshang Zarif, Ali Akbar Shahnazi, Nour Ali Boroumand, Mahmoud Karimi, Abdollah Davami, Yousef Foroutan, and Saeed Hormozi. On the other hand, Hossein Alizadeh studied composition and musicology at the University of Berlin. Therefore, learning music, especially the tar and setar, from him can be a bright and pleasant window for the musical spirit of enthusiasts.</p>\n<p>Master Alizadeh has authored numerous works in the field of teaching tar, setar, and the fundamentals of Iranian music. There are also tasnifs in his works for developing the finger power of players in this field. Now, to become more familiar with this area, we will review Master Hossein Alizadeh’s books.</p>\n<h4>Books “Ten Pieces for Tar” in 6 Volumes</h4>\n<p>Hossein Alizadeh has authored the books “Ten Pieces for Tar” specifically in 6 volumes for teaching the tar. In these books, Hossein Alizadeh has systematically divided everything that enthusiasts and tar students need to learn about the dastgahs and avazes of Iranian music into different sections. Therefore, each volume of the book has a separate topic.</p>\n<p>For this reason, this series of books includes a wide range of exercises such as Dashti exercises, Shourangiz, Chaharmezrab Rast Panjgah, left-hand strokes, Dastgah Mahur, Avaz Isfahan, Dastgah Shur, Chaharmezrab Bayat-e Tork, Avaz Afshari, Zarbi Mokhalef in Dastgah Segah, Avaz Abu Ata, etc.</p>\n<p>In these exercises, by choosing pleasant works and rhythms, Hossein Alizadeh causes students to connect more with the tar and traditional music. They also become familiar with fingering, time changes, advanced stroke techniques, and various and new rhythms.</p>\n<p>Master Alizadeh has addressed the major challenges of students in the field of tar in these 6 volumes and, in each volume, by providing various exercises, gives them a correct and precise idea of a specific piece. As a result, after doing these exercises, in addition to being able to play the notes correctly, the student’s playing will also progress significantly over time.</p>\n<h4>Book “Setar Instruction – Elementary Level”</h4>\n<p>The setar is one of the Iranian plucked string instruments that has many fans in traditional Iranian music. Therefore, Hossein Alizadeh’s book “Setar Instruction – Elementary Level,” which addresses important topics of setar teaching in a very fluent and professional manner, can be considered one of the best works in recent years for enthusiasts and students of this pleasant instrument.</p>\n<p>In this book, in four sections, Hossein Alizadeh teaches students the basic and important principles of setar. First, in the first section, he familiarizes the student with rhythm and how to perform it. Then, in sections two and three, the student completely learns the techniques of fret recognition, fingering, stroking, and intervals. The fourth and final section of the book also contains various exercises from the Dastgah of Shur to avazes such as Bayat-e Tork, Afshari, Dashti, etc., to meet the needs of the student and help them understand what they have learned from this work.</p>\n<h4>Book “Fundamentals of Iranian Music Theory”</h4>\n<p>Writing about the principles and fundamentals of Iranian music by one of the prominent masters of this art will certainly be attractive and readable. Hossein Alizadeh, who has high education in the field of music, has researched the basis of Iranian music in this work. In this work, Master Alizadeh has examined the fundamental and basic concepts of Iranian music from the past to the present in a very fluent and specialized manner. As a result, after studying it, the reader can gain very comprehensive and complete information in various fields such as the principles and rules of Iranian music, the Iranian music radif, classical Iranian music, etc.</p>\n<h4>Book “Tar and Setar Instruction – Intermediate Level” by Hossein Alizadeh</h4>\n<p>In this book, Master Hossein Alizadeh has gone to the teaching of tar and setar and has provided students with all seven dastgahs and five avazes derived from them in very fluent, simple, and professional topics. This book is available in the market along with two separate CDs.</p>\n<p>After studying this work, students and enthusiasts of tar and setar education, in addition to becoming familiar with the melodies and different forms of the dastgahs and avazes of the Iranian music radif, will learn the methods of performing common forms such as pishdaramad, chaharmezrab, zarbi, and rang, as well as the main gushehs of each dastgah and avaz.</p>\n<p>One of the topics that Master Hossein Alizadeh has fully focused on in this book is the correct use of strokes and proper fingering. For this reason, stroking and fingering are taught in various and diverse sections so that the student learns rhythmic and melodic preservation, creating variety in tahrirs, creating accents and different intonations, playing technique, reaching speed, etc.</p>\n<h4>Book “Bouseh-ha-ye Baran” (Kisses of Rain)</h4>\n<p>To experience the pleasure of playing Master Hossein Alizadeh’s compositions, you should go to the book “Bouseh-ha-ye Baran.” This book contains Hossein Alizadeh’s tasnifs from 1357 solar until the revolution. This book is divided into three sections dealing with various anthems and tasnifs, in each of which you will experience the atmosphere of the revolutionary period, traditional tasnifs of the Qajar era, and modern tasnifs.</p>\n<p>Among the exquisite and delightful works of this collection, one can mention the following pieces:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Zhaleh Khoun Shod,</li>\n  <li>Biya Saaghi,</li>\n  <li>Ahou-ye Vahshi,</li>\n  <li>Biya Ta Gol Barafshanim,</li>\n  <li>Bi To Be Sar Nemishavad,</li>\n  <li>Delshodegan,</li>\n  <li>Golchehreh,</li>\n  <li>Parvaneh Shou,</li>\n  <li>Raz-e No,</li>\n  <li>Bouseh-ha-ye Baran.</li>\n</ul>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding this article and the artistic life of the prominent Iranian musician, Hossein Alizadeh, please share it with us in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this content.</p>', '2023-11-02 20:48:59', 0, '2025-02-14 00:51:57', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
