(1520, 'posts', 63, 'en', NULL, 'Parviz Meshkatian, the free cypress of Iranian music | Member of 2 groups: Aref and Sheyda and Chavosh Center', NULL, 'Parviz Meshkatian was one of the most influential Iranian santur players and composers, born in 1955 in Neyshabur. He began learning music at a young age under his father’s guidance and quickly demonstrated exceptional talent. He later studied at the Faculty of Fine Arts in Tehran, where he trained with prominent masters such as Nur-Ali Boroumand and Dariush Safvat.\n\nMeshkatian started his professional career at the Center for Preservation and Propagation of Iranian Music and later founded the Aref Ensemble. His long collaboration with Mohammad-Reza Shajarian resulted in some of the most iconic works of Persian classical music, including Bidad, Dastan, and Astan-e Janan.\n\nHis musical style combined deep respect for tradition with innovative rhythmic structures and a strong connection to Persian poetry. In addition to performing and composing, he was also dedicated to teaching and nurturing new generations of musicians.\n\nDespite a period of withdrawal from the music scene, he eventually returned to performing. Meshkatian passed away suddenly in 2009, leaving behind a rich legacy. Today, he is remembered as a master of rhythm and a poetic voice in Iranian classical music.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we intend to introduce the Cypress of Iranian music, the unparalleled santur player and renowned composer, Master <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Parviz Meshkatian</span></strong>. We will talk about his birth and education, then discuss the beginning of his artistic activities, his unique style and method in playing, tasnif composition, and arranging. We will cover his marriage, his eight-year withdrawal from stage activities, and his bitter and painful end. We will also bring the opinions of great figures in Iranian art, music, and literature about Parviz Meshkatian. Stay with us throughout this article...</p>\n<h2>Birth and Childhood of Parviz Meshkatian</h2>\n<p>Parviz Meshkatian was born on 24 Ordibehesht 1334 solar year (14 May 1955) in the city of Nishapur. Meshkatian began his music education at the age of 6 under the instruction of his father. When he was only 8 years old, he successfully performed his first concert at Amir Mo’ezzi School in Nishapur. After this performance, he repeatedly participated in the music festivals of the Ramsar camp and achieved excellent ranks in all of them. He continued his training and music practice under his father until the end of high school.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_4098\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"304\"]<img class=\"wp-image-4098 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/sornaz_parviz_meshkatian_04.jpg\" alt=\"Parviz Meshkatian learned setar playing from his father during his teenage years.\" width=\"304\" height=\"451\" /> Parviz Meshkatian learned setar playing from his father during his teenage years.[/caption]\n<h2>Parviz Meshkatian’s Education</h2>\n<p>In 1353 solar year (1974), Parviz Meshkatian successfully entered the Faculty of Fine Arts at the University of Tehran. He learned the radif of Mirza Abdollah under Nour Ali Khan Boroumand and completed the traditional music radif course under Dr. Dariush Safvat. On the other hand, he studied the fundamentals and theory of world music and Iranian music in the classes of masters such as Mohammad Taghi Massoudieh, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%af%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%85%db%8c/\">Abdollah Khan Davami</a>, Saeed Hormozi, and Yousef Foroutan.</p>\n<h2>Beginning of Official Activities</h2>\n<p>Parviz Meshkatian very quickly, just one year after entering the university, in 1354 solar year (1975), started working as a santur player and instructor at the Center for Preservation and Propagation of Music. Dariush Safvat, who had great trust in Meshkatian, appointed him as the head of the group. Through his work at the Center, Meshkatian was able to significantly increase his skill and focus in santur playing and create many brilliant and unique works in the fields of composition and performance, especially in creating ensemble (orchestral) pieces, tasnifs, and solo performances.</p>\n<p>Master Meshkatian performed many concerts during this golden period with singers such as Parisa (Fatemeh Vaezi) and Hengameh Akhavan. As a result of these activities, the Aref group was formed in 1356 solar year (1977) and quickly became a prominent group in the field of authentic Iranian music. In the same year, 1356, Parviz Meshkatian participated in the Barbad competition, which was held at the initiative of Master Nour Ali Boroumand, and won first place in the santur playing category together with Peyman Kamkar. He also achieved an excellent rank in the radif playing section alongside Dariush Talaei.</p>\n<h2>Beginning of Professional Activities</h2>\n<p>In 1356 solar year (1977), at the suggestion of Houshang Ebtehaj, who at that time carefully monitored talented and gifted individuals, Parviz Meshkatian began his collaboration with National Radio and performed pieces as a santur player and soloist in several radio programs. However, less than a year later, in protest against the events of 17 Shahrivar 1357 (8 September 1978), he stopped his collaboration with National Radio as a sign of protest.</p>\n<p>In the same year, after ending his collaboration with National Radio, Meshkatian, along with other artists from the Aref and Sheyda groups, established the Chavosh Institute. Afterward, with the singing of Shahram Nazeri, he performed the tasnif “Mara Ashegh,” which he had composed based on ghazals by Molavi.</p>\n<p>One of Meshkatian’s other activities at the Chavosh Institute, along with other artists, was teaching music to students and enthusiasts. For santur instruction, Parviz Meshkatian focused more on the radif of Mirza Abdollah and the santur instruction book, while for stronger and more talented students, he seriously concentrated on teaching the radif of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%84%d8%ad%d8%b3%d9%86-%d8%b5%d8%a8%d8%a7/\">Abolhasan Saba</a>. Ali Vazifeh Doost, one of Master Meshkatian’s students at the Chavosh Institute, says about his teaching: “Parviz Meshkatian’s classes were extremely good and attractive, and he was very serious and strict in teaching. He had two goals in teaching:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Serving the state of music in the country by training and nurturing new students</li>\n  <li>Familiarizing people with real music and distancing them from superstitions through this means.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>One of the most important professional activities of Master Parviz Meshkatian, and perhaps the most significant part of it, was his collaboration with the Khosrow of Iranian singing, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b6%d8%a7-%d8%b4%d8%ac%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Mohammad Reza Shajarian</a>, which lasted continuously for 9 years from 1358 to 1367 solar (1979–1988) and also resumed after a few years’ break in the early 1370s solar (1990s). During this long-term collaboration, many brilliant and lasting works such as “Bidad,” “Astan-e Janan,” “Serr-e Eshgh,” “Nava,” “Dastan,” “Gonbad-e Mina,” “Dood-e Oud,” “Jan-e Oshagh,” and “Ghasedak” were created, each of which holds a very special place in Iranian modal music.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_4096\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1300\"]<img class=\"wp-image-4096 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/sornaz_parviz_meshkatian_02.webp\" alt=\"Parviz Meshkatian with Mohammad Reza Shajarian\" width=\"1300\" height=\"731\" /> Parviz Meshkatian with Mohammad Reza Shajarian[/caption]\n<p>In these immortal works of Iranian music, Meshkatian played the role of santur player and composer (he played setar in “Serr-e Eshgh” and only composed in “Dood-e Oud” and “Jan-e Oshagh”). Most experts of Iranian music consider “Bidad” to be the best collaboration between these two, citing Master Meshkatian’s unparalleled santur playing in this album as the reason. However, all collaborations between these two great masters are among the most prominent works of Iranian music that captivate the heart of anyone who loves music and art.</p>\n<h3>Style and Method of Playing and Composition</h3>\n<p>The works of Master Parviz Meshkatian can be divided into several structural patterns, which include: pishdaramad, chaharmezrab, zarbi, chandomozrab, mezrab-parani, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%90%d9%86%da%af/\">rang</a>, tasnif, taraneh, and arranged fusion works for large ensembles of Iranian and world instruments.</p>\n<p>A very important point seen in most of Parviz Meshkatian’s tasnifs is the selection of poetry appropriate to the chosen dastgah. In this regard, because Meshkatian had a high knowledge of Persian literature and language, he chose the dastgah that best matched the poems with better insight and understanding. He also greatly valued compound playing or modulation, which is an important technical principle in music. Meshkatian’s composed <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%86%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d8%b6%d8%b1%d8%a7%d8%a8/\">chaharmezrabs</a> are full of diverse melodies. Speed in playing while maintaining high precision so that all notes are performed clearly and transparently was also one of the characteristics of his performance.</p>\n<p>Hessamoddin Saraj says about him: “Parviz Meshkatian combined the experiences of his predecessors and what he learned from his masters with the innovation and creativity that existed within him. He created works that are more complex than previous styles while possessing a certain state — that is, he blended the sense of state and contemplation that exists in Iranian music with santur playing technique.”</p>\n<p>But the factor that distinguishes Parviz Meshkatian from his contemporaries or, better to say, establishes him as a prominent player and composer, is the connection of art with social issues. He thought deeply about his works, whether with or without lyrics, and worked well on them. In fact, he prepared and presented his work in a very strong and flawless manner so that both the listener was affected by his work and the experts followed it with interest.</p>\n<p>Keyvan Saket has said in an interview: “Meshkatian always emphasized strong playing and rhythmic power. He fully knew the historical and cultural background of Iran, and it can be said that rhythmic games after Saba belong to the late Meshkatian.”</p>\n<p>Saket also believes that Master Parviz Meshkatian was the unrivaled leader in santur playing and contemporary tasnif composition for more than two decades. He considers the albums “Bidad,” “Nava,” “Dastan,” and “Astan-e Janan,” which were created in collaboration with Mohammad Reza Shajarian, to be among Meshkatian’s most lasting and immortal works.</p>\n<h2>Marriage and Children</h2>\n<p>Parviz Meshkatian married Afsaneh Shajarian, the daughter of Mohammad Reza Shajarian, and this marriage resulted in two children named Ava and Ayin. However, Parviz Meshkatian and Afsaneh Shajarian’s marriage eventually ended in divorce and separation. As a result of these family issues and problems, the collaboration between Parviz Meshkatian and Master Mohammad Reza Shajarian stopped after 9 years in 1367 solar year (1988).</p>\n<h2>Collaboration with Other Singers</h2>\n<p>After this event and the cessation of collaboration with Mohammad Reza Shajarian, Master Meshkatian also collaborated with other great singers such as Ali Jahandar (in the album “Sobh-e Moshtaqan”), Iraj Bastami (in the albums “Mousem-e Gol,” “Afshari-ye Morakab,” “Mozhdeh-ye Bahar,” and “Vatan-e Man”), Alireza Eftekhari (in the album “Magham-e Sabr”), Hamidreza Nourbakhsh and Ali Rastegar (Aref group concert), and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b4%d9%87%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%86%d8%a7%d8%b8%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Shahram Nazeri</a> (in the album “Laleh Bahar”). Meshkatian also participated in the “Rouh-e Man” festival held in <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A7%D9%86%DA%AF%D9%84%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%A7%D9%86\">England</a> and succeeded in winning first place in this festival.</p>\n<h2>Withdrawal from Artistic and Stage Activities</h2>\n<p>Due to personal problems and a reclusive spirit, Parviz Meshkatian entered a self-imposed isolation for 8 years (from 1376 to 1384 solar / 1997–2005). During this period, he had almost no artistic activities or stage performances and did not release any albums. However, during these years, he worked on his books and works in solitude but clearly distanced himself from the noisy and bustling life of the past. Finally, after 8 years of seclusion and rest, in the summer of 1384 solar (2005), Master Parviz Meshkatian decided to revive the structure of the Aref and Sheyda groups again. The result of this decision was a concert at the Vahdat Hall with the voice of Shahram Nazeri.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_4097\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"1280\"]<img class=\"wp-image-4097 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/sornaz_parviz_meshkatian_03.jpg\" alt=\"Master Meshkatian withdrew from musical activities, stage performances, and concerts for 8 years.\" width=\"1280\" height=\"720\" /> Master Meshkatian withdrew from musical activities, stage performances, and concerts for 8 years.[/caption]\n<h2>Master Parviz Meshkatian in the Eyes of Others</h2>\n<p>Bijhan Kamkar, at the closing ceremony of the second Saba Music Festival in Azar 1398 solar (December 2019), in tribute to Parviz Meshkatian, said: “Parviz was very well-dressed. He lived elegantly and composed elegant pieces. I became more acquainted with Parviz Meshkatian at the Shiraz Arts Festival. I collaborated with him on the song ‘Amadha-ye Por Atash’ with the voice of Shahram Nazeri.</p>\n<p>For this piece, Parviz told me: ‘The daf in this song sounds good, and while composing it, I always had the sound of your daf in my ears.’ I asked Parviz, ‘How do you create these works?’ He replied, ‘These songs have been inside me since childhood. I didn’t know how to create them, but now I have found this knowledge.’ One of Parviz’s strengths was his unparalleled mastery of Iranian literature.”</p>\n<p>Hossein Alizadeh said in the “Nistan” program on Radio Farhang: “Parviz Meshkatian composed music fluently because he grew up in a cultured and musical family. Music had taken shape inside him since childhood. Parviz’s works are bound to tradition, yet they have a special freshness and novelty. Everyone creates a work according to their own nature. Meshkatian’s works are connected to the roots, but his tradition is not repetitive. Parviz Meshkatian did not change the foundation of music, but he displayed his own feeling in his works.</p>\n<p>Meshkatian had become reclusive in the later years of his life, but he was still witty. Anyone who listens to Parviz’s music does not become sad. This great player had a humorous view of life. Even if his works had sadness, it was a constructive sadness. In the last days of his life, he came to my concert and backstage told me that his mental state was good and he intended to work with me again. I had been waiting for a long time to hear these hopeful words from him, but this hope remained only in words, and he left us very soon.”</p>\n<p>Pejman Taheri, composer and santur player, believes that even just one of Parviz Meshkatian’s albums, for example “Astan-e Janan,” is enough for him to be remembered. However, despite this, Meshkatian strangely remained somewhat unknown and was treated unjustly.</p>\n<p>Alireza Javahiri considers one of the prominent and outstanding points in Parviz Meshkatian’s works and thoughts to be his mastery over ancient and contemporary Iranian literature. Also, his extensive study of various literary and artistic styles, especially the philosophy of art, caused us to witness a different and prominent perspective in Master Parviz Meshkatian’s thoughts and works. Master Meshkatian’s special attention to the inner content of poems and matching them with the mood of each gusheh of the dastgahs or avazes resulted in very organized and coherent collections, both in the instrumental and vocal sections, as well as in the structural design of the works.</p>\n<p>Majid Derakhshani, the great tar player and prominent composer, says: “Meshkatian’s playing influenced not only santur players but all his contemporaries, and it will still take time for someone to discover and recognize these traces in Meshkatian’s works. But in the meantime, I am very happy that Master Parviz Meshkatian was favored by everyone, especially the people of Iranian society, during his lifetime, and his works were well listened to.”</p>\n<p>Keyvan Saket, tar player and composer, has stated: “Master Parviz Meshkatian was a rhythm genius, and rhythm erupted from under his fingers. None of his peers could match him, and I told him many times that I was jealous of him. Meshkatian guided melody and rhythm in a very beautiful and extraordinary way, and he should be called the ‘Poet of the Santur’ because he was a master of Iran’s history and culture.”</p>\n<h2>The Untimely and Bitter Passing of Parviz Meshkatian</h2>\n<p>Master Parviz Meshkatian passed away on 30 Shahrivar 1388 solar year (21 September 2009) at his home in Tehran at the age of 54 due to heart failure. The body of this great Iranian musician was carried in a funeral procession on the morning of Thursday, 2 Mehr 1388 solar (24 September 2009) from in front of Vahdat Hall. At the funeral ceremony of Master Meshkatian, the representative of the Minister of Culture also gave a speech, which was met with opposition from the attendees. During his speech, the audience whistled and clapped to prevent the minister’s representative from speaking. Homayoun Shajarian performed the tasnif “Ghasedak,” one of Parviz Meshkatian’s compositions, at this ceremony.</p>\n<p>Dariush Pirniakan, spokesperson of the House of Music and prominent tar and setar player, said: “Today we repeat Meshkatian’s own words about himself: His death in the distortion of art was not a sad event, but a tragedy.”</p>\n<p>Mohammad Reza Darvishi, composer, author of the Encyclopedia of Iranian Instruments, and music researcher, also said about Master Meshkatian: “There is no doubt about the greatness of Parviz Meshkatian, and today instead of praising and describing him, we should raise the questions of the music community about the lack of attention to great figures such as Master Meshkatian and the culture and music of Iran.”</p>\n<p>Aidin Meshkatian, Parviz Meshkatian’s son, in his speech only asked the attendees to accompany his father’s body in peace and silence.</p>\n<p>Two days later, on 4 Mehr 1388 solar (26 September 2009), the funeral procession of Parviz Meshkatian was held in his birthplace, Nishapur, starting from in front of the Culture and Islamic Guidance Department, with a crowd of nearly 15,000 people.</p>\n<p>At this ceremony, which was attended by artists such as Hossein Alizadeh, Mohammad Reza Darvishi, Hamid Reza Nourbakhsh, Ali Rastegar, Mohammad Reza Rastegar, Homayoun Shajarian, Keyvan Saket, Shervin Mohajer, and Pejman Taheri, a group of students from the Faculty of Arts in Nishapur had wrapped some of their instruments in shrouds as a sign of mourning. The Nishapur city music band also performed a funeral march during the procession of the body of the master santur player Parviz Meshkatian. After the funeral procession and the prayer at the Jame Mosque of Nishapur, the body of Master Meshkatian was buried in the outer grounds of Attar Garden, next to the tomb of Attar of Nishapur.</p>\n<h3>Messages and Opinions Regarding Meshkatian’s Mourning</h3>\n<p>Bahram Beyzaie wrote in mourning for Parviz Meshkatian:</p>\n<p>Khoroshi khast vah vah vay dar vay<br>\nKe ghanun-e falak bogosast nagah!</p>\n<p>Magar saz-e jahan az kouk oftad<br>\nKe soug-e tar o daf barkhast ta mah!</p>\n<p>Faghan bar shod ze har sou shour dar shour<br>\nKe sazi ra shekasteh gahr-e taghdir!</p>\n<p>Navayi rokh nehan kardeh st dar khak<br>\nChe dani joz ke gahr az in be ta’bir</p>\n<p>Gharyv amad ze giti marg dar marg<br>\nKe khonyagar rah-e khoun-e ravan bast!</p>\n<p>Cho khandeh did rafteh az lab-e saz<br>\nNa saz, ari, ke jan-e khish beshkast!</p>\n<p>Bar amad moye az chantar-e changi<br>\nKe zakhm-e del be zakhmeh baz kardam!</p>\n<p>Cho yadet ra be mezrab-e dotayi<br>\nBe goush-e in jahan avaz kardam!</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>Mohammad Reza Shafiei Kadkani also composed the following poem about this bitter event:</p>\n<p>Ey doust vaght-e khoftan o khamoushi at naboud<br>\nVaz in diyar-e dour-e faramoushi at naboud</p>\n<p>To roshana soroud-e vatan boudi o cho ab<br>\nBa khak-e tireh rouz hamagoushi at naboud</p>\n<p>Meykhaneha ze na’reh to mast mishodand<br>\nRandi harif-e masti o mey noushi at naboud</p>\n<p>Doud-e cheragh-e moushi dozdan tora chenin<br>\nMadhoush kard o mousem-e khamoushi at naboud</p>\n<p>Sohrab-e ezterab-e vatan boudi o kasi<br>\nZinan be fekr-e darou-ye bihoushi at naboud</p>\n<p>Dar pardeh mand naghmeh-ye azadi-ye vatan<br>\nKandish-e joz be raftan o chavoushi at naboud</p>\n<p>Dar chang-e to soroud-e rahayi nehofteh mand<br>\nZin naghmeh hich gah faramoushi at naboud</p>\n<p>Ey sougvar-e sobh-e neshabour-e sormeghoun<br>\nAsri chenin sazay-e siyehpoushi at naboud</p>\n&nbsp;\n<p>Mohammad Reza Shajarian also published a writing in mourning for Meshkatian:</p>\n<p>“He left and no longer looked at his back<br>\nHe took a heart from us but left it ruined.”</p>\n<p>I heard the bitter news of Parviz Meshkatian’s passing in shock and deep sorrow. I still cannot believe that Parviz has left us alone. Parviz Meshkatian created many melodies for our time and recorded the memory of those years in our minds with his art. His music was always with us in the ups and downs of time; it cried with our sorrows, rejoiced with our joys, and empathized with our passion and hope. In Meshkatian’s works, one can hear the sound of a heart that beats for the homeland and freedom. Few artists have the ability to reflect the pains and aspirations of their people in their art and be the honest and true translation of their era.</p>\n<p>Parviz is one of these few. A musician whose talent for tasnif composition, skill in ensemble playing, poetic delicacy, and commitment to the ideals of the people will make his memory immortal in Iranian art. I do not believe in the death of such an artist. Parviz Meshkatian is alive in our hearts and minds.”</p>\n<p>Seyed Mohammad Khatami, the former president, sent a message of condolence on the passing of Parviz Meshkatian. In part of this message, it is stated: “The capable hands of this composer have now joined history, but his memory and name and his valuable works are a great asset for all culture lovers, art connoisseurs, and God-worshipping humanists.”</p>\n<p>Houshang Ebtehaj, the great Iranian poet, said at the commemoration gathering for Master Parviz Meshkatian: “Parviz Meshkatian, just as he climbed the high ranks in music, culture, and the development of real traditional art in the country in the shortest possible time and with the speed of lightning and wind, and was advancing rapidly in this field, he also had speed and haste in his journey to the hereafter, and he reached his destination very quickly.”</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding this article and the life of Parviz Meshkatian, the Cypress of Iranian music, please share it with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a> in the comments section to help improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-11-03 20:18:34', 0, '2025-02-14 00:54:37', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1521, 'posts', 64, 'en', NULL, 'Mohammad Reza Shajarian, the Khosrow of Iranian singing', NULL, 'This article explores the life and artistic journey of Mohammad-Reza Shajarian, one of the most influential figures in Persian traditional music. Born in 1940 in Mashhad, he grew up in a religious family and began reciting the Quran under his father’s guidance—an experience that deeply shaped his vocal style and technique.\n\nShajarian started his career in radio at a young age, initially performing under a pseudonym. He later moved to Tehran, where he trained with renowned masters such as Ahmad Ebadi, Nur Ali Boroumand, and Abdollah Davami, refining his mastery of Persian classical music.\n\nThroughout his career, he collaborated with leading musicians like Mohammad Reza Lotfi, Hossein Alizadeh, and Parviz Meshkatian, producing some of the most iconic works in Iranian music history.\n\nKnown for reviving complex vocal styles and integrating poetry with music, Shajarian became a central figure in preserving and advancing Persian classical music. His extensive discography, radio performances, and international concerts have left a lasting legacy, earning him the title “The King of Persian Vocal Music.”', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article from the series of articles on the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d8%b2%d9%86%d8%af%da%af%db%8c%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%85%d9%87-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%86/\">biographies of great musicians</a>, we examine the biography of <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Mohammad Reza</span> <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Shajarian</span></strong></span>, the Khosrow of Iranian singing. We will get to know step by step the different stages of Shajarian’s life and his rise to the pinnacle of singing in Iranian music. Stay with us...</p>\n<h2>Birth and Early Years of Mohammad Reza Shajarian</h2>\n<p>Mohammad Reza Shajarian was born on 1 Mehr 1319 solar year (23 September 1940) in Mashhad. He spent the early years of his childhood in a religious and artistic family. His father, Haj Mehdi Shajarian, was one of the great landowners of Mashhad and had a clear and beautiful voice; he sang in his youth. His mother’s family was also active in music and calligraphy. His mother, Afser Shahverdi, was a housewife. He had four brothers named Alireza, Siamak, Amir, and Ahmad Ali, and one sister named Nasrin.</p>\n<p>Because he lived in a religious and conservative environment, Shajarian’s father was forced to abandon singing at a young age and turned to Quranic recitation. For this reason, Mehdi Shajarian never returned to singing for the rest of his life and only engaged in Quranic recitation. Due to his Quranic recitation, Mehdi Shajarian achieved a high and special status in his city and trained many students in this field, one of whom was his son Mohammad Reza.</p>\n<p>From the age of 4, he attended his father’s Quranic recitation sessions. As one might expect, Mohammad Reza Shajarian started reading very early, almost at the age of 5. One year after entering school (1326 solar) and beginning primary education, that is, in 1327 solar year, he turned to Quranic recitation. His training under his father began, and he started cultivating his voice. Afterward, at the age of 9, he began reciting the Quran in various ceremonies.</p>\n<p>In the memories of Master Mohammad Reza Shajarian, it is mentioned that from a very young age he attended Quranic recitation sessions held in Mashhad and became attached to the beautiful voices of some great reciters. He recounts that one night during the month of Ramadan, while he had not yet reached school age, he was so moved by his father’s supplication and intimate prayer that tears flowed from his eyes.</p>\n<p>It took several years for the young Mohammad Reza Shajarian to become a professional Quran reciter in Mashhad and for his name to become well-known. When “Radio Mashhad” resumed its activities in 1331 solar year (1952), the 12-year-old Shajarian, with his father’s permission, appeared on the radio as a Quran reciter, and his voice was broadcast for the first time on Radio Mashhad.</p>\n<p>After entering the Preliminary Teacher Training College in Mashhad in 1338 solar year (1959), he became acquainted with a music teacher there. His simultaneous interest in Quranic recitation and singing led him, in 1338 solar year, to perform avaz pieces without accompaniment on Radio Khorasan alongside Quranic recitation. After receiving his diploma from the Higher Teacher Training College, he was employed by the Mashhad Education Department and began teaching. During this time, he became interested in the santur and began learning this instrument under Jalal Akhbari.</p>\n<p>In 1339 solar year (1960), Master Mohammad Reza Shajarian was employed by the Ministry of Culture. Seven years later, in 1346 solar year (1967), he was transferred to Tehran as an employee of the Ministry of Culture. At the same time, he began his collaboration with Radio Iran.</p>\n<p>Until 1350 solar year (1971), because his father wanted the family (Shajarian) to be more recognized for Quranic recitation and a religious image, Mohammad Reza Shajarian performed singing on the radio under the pseudonym “Siavash Bidgani.” After that year, his father allowed him to use his real name.</p>\n<h2>The Beginning of Mohammad Reza Shajarian’s Artistic Activities in Tehran</h2>\n<p>Unpleasant events during the transfer to Tehran and the problems that arose had caused Shajarian to lose hope of coming to Tehran. However, his friend Mahmoud Farrokh, a poet and literary figure from Mashhad, provided great help in this path so that he could move to Tehran and continue his musical activities in the capital. Fortunately, these efforts bore fruit, and Mohammad Reza Shajarian moved to Tehran.</p>\n<p>After arriving in Tehran, he became acquainted with Master Ahmad Ebadi, the skillful setar player and son of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87/\">Mirza Abdollah Farahani</a>, and a good relationship developed between the two. The young and ambitious Mohammad Reza Shajarian, thanks to this acquaintance, came under the instruction of one of the greatest players of that period and managed to identify many flaws in his own work and correct them with the help of Ahmad Ebadi. During this period, he also attended the classes of Esmail Mehrtash.</p>\n<p>Shajarian worked in the Education Department until 1347 solar year (1968), after which he was transferred to the Ministry of Natural Resources. During his service in the Education Department, he taught in Radkan, a district of Chenaran, and lived in that city for some time. Master Shajarian began his official activities in National Iranian Television in 1349 solar year (1970). Shajarian, who did not have a prominent master before coming to Tehran and becoming acquainted with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a7%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af-%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%a7%d8%af%db%8c/\">Ahmad Ebadi</a>, was extremely thirsty for learning and used every opportunity to learn more. Therefore, in 1350 solar year (1971), he became acquainted with Faramarz Payvar and studied vocal radifs and santur under him.</p>\n<p>In 1351 solar year (1972), during the Golha program on the radio, Shajarian became acquainted with Nour Ali Khan Boroumand and learned the vocal style of Hossein Taherzadeh under this master. In the same year, Amir Houshang Ebtehaj (Saya) was appointed by Reza Ghotbi as the director and supervisor of the Golha music section on the radio and changed the direction of this section. Mohammad Reza Shajarian was one of the artists who had an effective presence in the new format of the Golha program and participated as a singer in 97 out of 210 programs of this section.</p>\n<p>From 1352 solar year (1973), he studied the vocal radif and tasnif singing styles under <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%af%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%85%db%8c/\">Abdollah Davami</a>. In the same year, together with a group of artists including Mohammad Reza Lotfi, Nasser Farhangfar, Hossein Alizadeh, Jalal Zolfonoun, and Davoud Ganjei, he became a member of the Center for Preservation and Propagation of Music, headed by Dariush Safvat.</p>\n<p>During this period, he performed the tasnif “Daroug,” which was based on a melody by Master Mohammad Reza Lotfi on a poem by Nima Yushij. “Daroug” is one of the first tasnifs in which modern poetry was used. Among the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%aa%d8%b5%d9%86%db%8c%d9%81/\">tasnifs</a> by Mohammad Reza Shajarian that are in colloquial language, one can mention the tasnif “Mageh Na” (poetry by Bijan Samandar, melody by Ataollah Khorram), which was performed during the same period.</p>\n<p>His live performance in the long-forgotten Dastgah of Rast Panjgah in 1354 solar year (1975) at the Hafeziyeh in Shiraz during the Shiraz Arts Festival, accompanied by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b6%d8%a7-%d9%84%d8%b7%d9%81%db%8c/\">Mohammad Reza Lotfi</a> and Nasser Farhangfar, was well received. Later, this performance was released as an album with the same name. At that time, Rast Panjgah was rarely performed and the dastgah was on the verge of being forgotten.</p>\n<p>In the early years after moving to Tehran, in addition to his artistic activities in music, Master Shajarian also studied calligraphy at the Calligraphers Association under the instruction of Master Ebrahim Bouzari. From 1347 solar year (1968), Shajarian continued calligraphy under Hassan Mirkhani and, after passing the Calligraphers Association exam, reached the excellent level in 1349 solar year (1970).</p>\n<h2>List of Works by Master Mohammad Reza Shajarian</h2>\n<p>Mohammad Reza Shajarian left behind a very large number of works for Iranian music, which we list below. Some of the following albums are joint works between Mohammad Reza Shajarian and singers such as Shahram Nazeri or Homayoun Shajarian. Among these great works of Master Mohammad Reza Shajarian, collaborations with composers such as Mohammad Reza Lotfi, Parviz Meshkatian, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%af%d9%87/\">Hossein Alizadeh</a>, and Faramarz Payvar are notable.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Robaiyat-e Khayyam (1351 solar)</li>\n  <li>Chavosh 1 (In Memory of Aref) (1356 solar)</li>\n  <li>Golbang-e Shajarian (1357 solar)</li>\n  <li>Chavosh 2 (1357 solar)</li>\n  <li>Sepideh (Chavosh 6) (1358 solar)</li>\n  <li>Chavosh 7 (1358 solar)</li>\n  <li>Jan Jan (Chavosh 9) (1358 solar)</li>\n  <li>Raz-e Del (1358 solar)</li>\n  <li>Entezar-e Del (1358 solar)</li>\n  <li>Payvand-e Mehr (1363 solar)</li>\n  <li>Astan-e Janan (1364 solar)</li>\n  <li>Bidad (1364 solar)</li>\n  <li>Serr-e Eshgh (Mahur) (1365 solar)</li>\n  <li>Nava, Morakab-khani (1365 solar)</li>\n  <li>Dastan (1367 solar)</li>\n  <li>Saz-e Ghesse-gu and the Grand Concert in Honor of Hafez (1367 solar)</li>\n  <li>Dood-e Oud (1368 solar)</li>\n  <li>Del-e Majnoun (1369–1370 solar)</li>\n  <li>Khalvat Gozideh (1370 solar)</li>\n  <li>Payam-e Nasim (1370 solar)</li>\n  <li>Sarv-e Chaman (1370 solar)</li>\n  <li>Aseman-e Eshgh (1370 solar)</li>\n  <li>Delshodegan (1371 solar)</li>\n  <li>Yad-e Ayam (1371 solar)</li>\n  <li>Cheshmeh-ye Noosh (1372 solar)</li>\n  <li>Bahariyeh (1373 solar)</li>\n  <li>Gonbad-e Mina (1373 solar)</li>\n  <li>Jan-e Oshagh (1374 solar)</li>\n  <li>Payam-e Ahl-e Del (Homayun Masnavi) (1374 solar)</li>\n  <li>Dar Khial (1375 solar)</li>\n  <li>Rasvay-e Del (1375 solar)</li>\n  <li>Eshgh Danad (1376 solar)</li>\n  <li>Shab-e Vasl (1376 solar)</li>\n  <li>Moamma-ye Hasti (1376 solar)</li>\n  <li>Rast Panjgah (1377 solar)</li>\n  <li>Chehre be Chehre (1377 solar)</li>\n  <li>Shab, Sokut, Kavir (1377 solar)</li>\n  <li>Aram-e Jan (1377 solar)</li>\n  <li>Ahang-e Vafa (1378 solar)</li>\n  <li>Boy-e Baran (1378 solar)</li>\n  <li>Zemestan Ast (1380 solar)</li>\n  <li>Bi To Be Sar Nemishavad (1381 solar)</li>\n  <li>Faryad (1381 solar)</li>\n  <li>Ham Nava ba Bam (1382 solar)</li>\n  <li>Jam-e Tahi (1383 solar)</li>\n  <li>Saz-e Khamush (1386 solar)</li>\n  <li>Sorud-e Mehr (1386 solar)</li>\n  <li>Ghogha-ye Eshgh Bazan (1386 solar)</li>\n  <li>Concert of Mohammad Reza Shajarian and the Ava Group (1387 solar)</li>\n  <li>Concert of Mohammad Reza Shajarian and the Shehnaz Group (Rendan-e Mast va Morgh-e Khoshkhan) (1387 solar)</li>\n  <li>Ah-e Baran (1388 solar)</li>\n  <li>Zaban-e Atash (1388 solar)</li>\n  <li>Rendan-e Mast (1388 solar)</li>\n  <li>Concert of Mohammad Reza Shajarian and the Shehnaz Group in Dubai (1389 solar)</li>\n  <li>Morgh-e Khoshkhan (1390 solar)</li>\n  <li>Rangha-ye Ta’ali (1392 solar)</li>\n  <li>Tariq-e Eshgh (1395 solar)</li>\n  <li>Khorasaniyat (1398 solar)</li>\n  <li>Deylaman (undated)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Singer of film title tracks:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Delshodegan (1370 solar)</li>\n  <li>Abjad (1381 solar)</li>\n  <li>Zemestan Ast (1384 solar)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Works That Were Not Officially Released and Were Privately Distributed</h3>\n<p>Many private pieces and works by Master Mohammad Reza Shajarian with various artists, composers, and players were not officially released, and we do not have complete or accurate information about many of them. However, some of them include:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Chehre be Chehre (Shiraz Arts Festival, 1356 solar)</li>\n  <li>Payam-e Nasim</li>\n  <li>Yad-e Ayam</li>\n  <li>Ghasedak</li>\n  <li>Concert with the National Orchestra of Iran conducted by Farhad Fakhreddini (Bagh-e Muzeh-ye Chehel Sotun, Tir 1377 solar)</li>\n  <li>Zemestan Ast</li>\n  <li>Faryad</li>\n  <li>Autumn 1384 Concert (Saz-e Khamush and Sorud-e Mehr)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Of course, the film of the Rast Panjgah concert (Shiraz Arts Festival, 1354 solar) and the concert with the Shehnaz Group in London were broadcast by television but were never released to the market by any publisher.</p>\n<h3>Shajarian’s Radio Programs</h3>\n<p>In the early days of his presence on the radio, due to his father’s dissatisfaction, Mohammad Reza Shajarian used the pseudonym “Siavash Bidgani” in most of his radio performances. Mohammad Reza Shajarian performed in numerous Golha programs such as Golha-ye Rangarang, Golha-ye Tazeh, Golchin-e Hafteh, Barg-e Sabz, Yek Shakheh Gol, and the Iranian Music program. One of his first very successful programs was the performance of the dastgahs of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b1%d8%a7%d8%b3%d8%aa-%d9%be%d9%86%d8%ac%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">Rast Panjgah</a> and Nava. These two dastgahs, due to their great complexity and difficulty, were rarely performed or used.</p>\n<h2>Concerts Held by Master Mohammad Reza Shajarian</h2>\n<p>Below is a list of some of the special and prominent concerts by Mohammad Reza Shajarian during his musical career:</p>\n<p>1354 solar – Live performance of the album “Rast Panjgah” at the 1354 Shiraz Arts Festival in Hafeziyeh, Shiraz, accompanied by Mohammad Reza Lotfi and Nasser Farhangfar. This is one of the first and most important concerts of Mohammad Reza Shajarian and is actually a turning point in the artistic record of Shajarian and Lotfi. This work was also released as the audio album “Rast Panjgah,” and television broadcast its film at the time.</p>\n<p>1356 solar – Live performance of the album “Chehre be Chehre” at the 1356 Shiraz Arts Festival in Hafeziyeh, Shiraz, accompanied by the Sheyda group led and composed by Mohammad Reza Lotfi. This work was also released as the audio album “Chehre be Chehre.”</p>\n<p>1370 solar – Live performance of the album “Payam-e Nasim” in Switzerland accompanied by Daryoush Pirniakan, Jamshid Andalibi, and Morteza Ayan. An interesting point about this album is that Mohammad Reza Shajarian himself composed all the pieces. This work was released as the audio album “Payam” and its film has been published on the internet.</p>\n<p>1371 solar – Live performance of the album “Yad-e Ayam” in the United States accompanied by Daryoush Pirniakan, Jamshid Andalibi, and Homayoun Shajarian. This performance is also the first work featuring Homayoun Shajarian. This work was released as the audio album “Yad-e Ayam” and its film has been published on the internet.</p>\n<p>1373 solar – The album “Ghasedak” with Parviz Meshkatian and Homayoun Shajarian, which was also the last collaboration between Shajarian and Meshkatian. This album was never officially released with proper quality.</p>\n<p>1377 solar – Performance with the National Orchestra of Iran with 93 musicians conducted by Farhad Fakhreddini and singing by Mohammad Reza Shajarian at the <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%DA%86%D9%87%D9%84%E2%80%8C%D8%B3%D8%AA%D9%88%D9%86\">Chehel Sotun Garden Museum</a> in Tir 1377 solar, with Hassan Kasaei, Shahzidi, Fereydoun Moshiri, and Ali Tajvidi as honorary guests.</p>\n<p>1379 solar – Live performances of the album “Zemestan Ast” based on the poem “Zemestan” by Mehdi Akhavan Sales, accompanied by Homayoun Shajarian, Hossein Alizadeh, and Kayhan Kalhor in Europe, the United States, and Canada (winter 1379 solar). The California performance was released as the audio album “Zemestan Ast.”</p>\n<p>1381 solar – Live performance of the album “Faryad” in Los Angeles (autumn 1381 solar) accompanied by Homayoun Shajarian, Hossein Alizadeh, and Kayhan Kalhor.</p>\n<p>1382 solar – The “Ham Nava ba Bam” concert, performed in Tehran to help the earthquake victims of Bam and to show solidarity with the people of that city, accompanied by Homayoun Shajarian, Hossein Alizadeh, and Kayhan Kalhor. Delawaz released this concert as a video album.</p>\n<p>1384 solar – Live performance of the albums “Saz-e Khamush” and “Sorud-e Mehr” in Tehran (autumn 1384 solar) accompanied by Homayoun Shajarian, Hossein Alizadeh, and Kayhan Kalhor.</p>\n<p>1386 solar – Mohammad Reza Shajarian and the Ava Group concert, consisting of two parts named “Sokhan-e Eshgh” and “Ghogha-ye Eshgh Bazan,” performed with the Ava Group and released by Delawaz in 1387 solar.</p>\n<p>1387 solar – Mohammad Reza Shajarian and the Shehnaz Group concert: live performance of the albums “Rendan-e Mast” and “Morgh-e Khoshkhan” in Tehran (autumn 1387 solar) with the Shehnaz Group. Delawaz released this concert as a video album some time after the studio albums were released.</p>\n<p>1389 solar – Large concert tour with the Shehnaz Group in the United States, Australia, Turkey, England, etc. Among them, the concert performed in Dubai at Sheikh Rashid Hall (February 2011) was released by Delawaz, and the BBC officially broadcast the London concert.</p>\n<p>1392 solar – Performance of “Rangha-ye Ta’ali” with the Pournazeri brothers.</p>\n<p>1393 solar – Large concert tour with the Shehnaz Group in various countries titled “Sargardegan-e Eshgh.”</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding this article and the artistic life of Mohammad Reza Shajarian, the master of Iranian singing, please share it with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a> in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-11-06 21:10:17', 0, '2025-02-14 00:54:23', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1522, 'posts', 65, 'en', NULL, 'Shahram Nazeri, the knight of music', NULL, 'This article reviews the life and artistic career of Shahram Nazeri, one of the most prominent figures in Persian traditional and mystical music. Born in 1949 in Kermanshah, he grew up in a culturally rich family and was introduced to poetry and music from an early age. He trained under renowned masters such as Nur Ali Boroumand, Abdollah Davami, and Ahmad Ebadi, while also developing his skills in playing the setar.\n\nNazeri began performing at a young age and later expanded his professional career in Tehran. His collaborations with major ensembles like Sheida and Aref, alongside artists such as Mohammad Reza Lotfi, Hossein Alizadeh, and Parviz Meshkatian, led to the creation of many influential works in Persian music.\n\nHe is especially known for integrating mystical poetry—particularly the works of Rumi—into music, creating a distinctive spiritual style. Albums like Gol-e Sadbarg and Atash Dar Neyestan are among his most notable works.\n\nThroughout his career, Nazeri has performed internationally and received prestigious awards, including France’s Legion of Honour and the title of Knight of Arts and Letters. His efforts have played a significant role in introducing Persian mystical music to a global audience.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the biography of <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Shahram</span> <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Nazeri</span></strong></span>, the renowned Iranian singer and musician. We will discuss his professional activities, published albums, and the awards received by this prominent figure in Iranian music. Stay with us throughout this article...</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>A Summary of Shahram Nazeri’s Biography</h2>\n<p>Shahram Nazeri was born on 29 Bahman 1328 solar year (18 February 1950) in Kermanshah, in the Barzeh Demagh neighborhood, on Yakhchali Alley. He was born into a Kurdish-speaking family familiar with Persian poetry and literature as well as authentic Iranian music. This great Iranian singer and musician inherited his pleasant voice from his father and mother and became acquainted with poetry and singing from childhood through his mother’s efforts.</p>\n<p>Shahram Nazeri’s father also had a very delicate and pleasing voice. In addition to being familiar with the dastgahs and avazes of various radifs of Iranian music and singing, he also played the setar. Besides benefiting from the style of the old masters and the singers of his own region (his most important role model in singing can be considered Sheikh Davoudi, the great singer of that time), he passed on his knowledge, understanding, and perceptions of Iranian music and singing to his son.</p>\n<p>Shahram Nazeri first performed at the age of only 7 in an artistic program on Kermanshah Radio, accompanied by the tar of Darvishi, one of the famous players of that time in Kermanshah. A few years later, at the age of 11, he also performed several other Iranian avaz programs on Iran Radio and Television.</p>\n<p>Afterward, Shahram Nazeri studied under Nour Ali Khan Boroumand and became familiar with the works of past Iranian musicians. At the same time, to complete his training in the vocal radif of Iranian music, he benefited from the teachings of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%af%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%85%db%8c/\">Master Abdollah Khan Davami</a>. He also learned more vocal subtleties and nuances from masters such as Mahmoud Karimi, Abdolali Vaziri, and Hossein Ghavami.</p>\n<p>The Iranian singing master, Shahram Nazeri, while studying singing under various masters, continued learning the setar and was one of the people who had the most contact and interaction with the famous setar master, Master Ahmad Ebadi, from whom he received extensive training. In addition to benefiting from the teachings of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a7%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af-%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%a7%d8%af%db%8c/\">Master Ahmad Ebadi</a>, he also drew upon the knowledge and skills of masters such as Mahmoud Tajbakhsh, Jalal Zolfonoun, and Mahmoud Hashemi (the famous setar maker) and learned points from these great figures as well. During the period when Reza Ghasemi collaborated with Nazeri on the Gol-e Sadbarg album, he also learned notable points from him.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>Professional Activities of Master Shahram Nazeri</h2>\n<p>At the age of 17–18 in 1345 solar year (1966), Shahram Nazeri traveled to Tehran to study and benefit from the teachings of masters such as Abdollah Davami, Nour Ali Khan Boroumand, Abdolali Vaziri, Mahmoud Karimi, and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b6%d8%a7-%d8%b4%d8%ac%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Mohammad Reza Shajarian</a>. While benefiting from these masters, he also learned the setar under masters Ahmad Ebadi, Jalal Zolfonoun, and Mahmoud Hashemi.</p>\n<p>In 1354 solar year (1975), at the suggestion of Nour Ali Khan Boroumand, Nazeri was employed by Iran Radio and Television. He performed his first program with the Sheyda group led by Mohammad Reza Lotfi, singing a masnavi by Molavi and a tasnif by Sheikh Bahai. After collaborating with the Sheyda group, he continued his work with the other famous group of that period, the Aref group, led by Hossein Alizadeh and Parviz Meshkatian.</p>\n<p>In 1355 solar year (1976), Shahram Nazeri participated in the first traditional music competition in Iran, held under the name “Barbad,” and succeeded in winning first place in the singing category. In 1356 solar year (1977), he was selected along with the Soma’i group led by Asghar Bahari and Hassan Nahid to perform at the Tus Festival.</p>\n<p>Between 1357 and 1360 solar years (1978–1981), Nazeri sang in several albums of the Chavosh Institute (Nos. 2, 3, 4, 7, and 8) in collaboration with members of the Sheyda and Aref groups from the Chavosh Cultural and Artistic Institute, led by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b6%d8%a7-%d9%84%d8%b7%d9%81%db%8c/\">Mohammad Reza Lotfi</a>, Hossein Alizadeh, and Parviz Meshkatian.</p>\n<p>Shahram Nazeri produced the album “Masnavi-ye Musa va Shaban” in collaboration with Jalal Zolfonoun and Behzad Foruhari, “Benmay Rokh” with the players of the Center for Preservation and Propagation of Traditional Music and the Molana group led by Mohammad Jalil Andalibi, the album “Sedaye Sokhan-e Eshgh” with the Tanbur-e Shams group, and “Laleh Bahar” with the Aref group led by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%be%d8%b1%d9%88%db%8c%d8%b2-%d9%85%d8%b4%da%a9%d8%a7%d8%aa%db%8c%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Parviz Meshkatian</a>.</p>\n<p>During the years 1360 to 1364 solar (1981–1985), he was engaged in teaching music and vocal radifs to students and enthusiasts in various ways. During this period, Nazeri also released the works “Zemestan,” “Beshno az Ney,” and “Najva.” From 1364 solar year (1985) onward, in collaboration with several <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a> groups, he presented many beautiful works to the market of mystical and authentic Iranian music. Among the most brilliant of these works are “Shourangiz” with Hossein Alizadeh, “Gol-e Sadbarg” and “Atashi dar Neyestan” with Jalal Zolfonoun, “Yadegar-e Doost,” and “Saghinameh 1” and “Saghinameh 2” with Kambiz Roshanravan.</p>\n<p>During these years, Master Shahram Nazeri also performed and released the album “Bigharar” in collaboration with Jalil Andalibi’s group, as well as the concert “Ostadan-e Musighi-ye Iran” with the Masters of Iranian Music group (led by Faramarz Payvar). It should be noted that “Gol-e Sadbarg” is Shahram Nazeri’s most famous work, and many lovers of Iranian music remember him for the tasnif “Andak Andak Jam’e Mastan Mirasand,” which he performed with a poem by Molavi in this album.</p>\n<p>During the 1370s solar decade (1990s), Shahram Nazeri performed many works, including the albums “Nowruz” with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%af%d9%87/\">Hossein Alizadeh</a>, “Mahtab Ro” with the Tanbur-e Shams group (with composition by Kaykhosro Pournazeri), and “Del-e Shida” with composition by Faramarz Payvar. His collaboration with the Kamkar family also resulted in two lasting works: the 1376 Kamkar Concert and the 1377 Kamkar Concert. With the release of the two albums “Saz-e No, Avaz-e No” and “Safar be Digar Su,” he showed a new and innovative path in his music.</p>\n<p>Shahram Nazeri has used poems by Molavi in most of his works and, during more than 30 years of artistic activity, has succeeded in creating a new style of music blended with the epic and mystical literature of Iran.</p>\n[caption id=\"attachment_4116\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"780\"]<img class=\"wp-image-4116 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/sornaz_shahram_nazeri_007.jpg\" alt=\"In addition to singing, Shahram Nazeri is also a setar player.\" width=\"780\" height=\"439\" /> In addition to singing, Shahram Nazeri is also a setar player.[/caption]\n&nbsp;\n<h3>Shahram Nazeri’s Shahnameh Reading Concert in Morocco</h3>\n<p>In 1390 solar year (2011), Shahram Nazeri released an album titled “Amir Kabir” with interesting composition by Pejman Taheri, in which most of the poems used were modern poetry. Some time later, after a 6-year delay, the concert of Shahram Nazeri and Loris Tjeknavorian was also released.</p>\n<p>This work by Master Nazeri relates to a performance with the large Komitas Orchestra of Armenia conducted by Maestro Loris Tjeknavorian in 1384 solar year (2005), in which most of the pieces were performed in Kurdish (Nazeri’s mother tongue). However, it should be noted that four pieces in this work were performed in Persian: “Zin Do Hezaran Man o Ma,” “Shida Shodam,” “Avaz ba Hamrah-e Chang,” and “Ab-e Hayat-e Eshgh.” On the other hand, most of the pieces in this album had previously been performed in other Nazeri works with Iranian instruments, but in this work he presented them in an orchestral format with Western instruments.</p>\n<p>Among other prominent works by Master Nazeri performed on the poems of the great Persian poet Molavi is the album “Molavieh,” known outside Iran as “The Passion of Rumi.” This beautiful album was nominated for the American Grammy Award in 2007 (1386 solar). Indian-American philosopher Deepak Chopra also appears in this album.</p>\n<p>Shahram Nazeri released this album in the year of Molavi’s commemoration, and it was introduced as one of the best compact discs of the year worldwide. The American newspapers Boston Globe and Los Angeles Times, in a report titled “Molavi’s Poetry in Motion,” examined the efforts of Shahram and Hafez Nazeri to keep Molavi’s poems alive.</p>\n<h2>Albums by Master Shahram Nazeri</h2>\n<p>The albums that Shahram Nazeri has released during his long years of activity are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Chavosh 2 in 1357 solar</li>\n  <li>Chavosh 3 in 1357 solar</li>\n  <li>Chavosh 4 in 1357 solar</li>\n  <li>Chavosh 7 in 1358 solar</li>\n  <li>Chavosh 8 in 1357–59 solar</li>\n  <li>Masnavi-ye Musa va Shaban in 1358 solar</li>\n  <li>Sedaye Sokhan-e Eshgh in 1358 solar</li>\n  <li>Bad-e Saba Miyayad in 1360 solar</li>\n  <li>Beshno az Ney (performed in 1360 solar)</li>\n  <li>Gol-e Sadbarg in 1360 solar</li>\n  <li>Laleh Bahar in 1360 solar</li>\n  <li>Nowruz in 1362 solar</li>\n  <li>Benmay Rokh in 1363 solar</li>\n  <li>Yadegar-e Doost in 1363 solar</li>\n  <li>Baharan-e Abidar in 1364 solar</li>\n  <li>Kish-e Mehr in 1365 solar</li>\n  <li>Bigharar in 1366 solar</li>\n  <li>Zemestan in 1366 solar</li>\n  <li>Dar Golestaneh in 1366 solar</li>\n  <li>Shourangiz in 1367 solar</li>\n  <li>Saghinameh 1 in 1367 solar</li>\n  <li>Saghinameh 2 in 1367 solar</li>\n  <li>Atashi dar Neyestan in 1367 solar</li>\n  <li>Sokhan-e Tazeh in 1367 solar</li>\n  <li>Konserte Ostadan-e Musighi-ye Iran in 1368 solar</li>\n  <li>Konserte Digar in 1368 solar</li>\n  <li>Leyli va Majnun in 1368 solar</li>\n  <li>Cheshm be Rah-e Ordibehesht in 1370 solar</li>\n  <li>Heyrani in 1375 solar</li>\n  <li>Saz-e No, Avaz-e No in 1377 solar</li>\n  <li>Konserte 77 in 1377 solar</li>\n  <li>Safar be Digar Su in 1377 solar</li>\n  <li>Avaz-e Asatir in 1379 solar</li>\n  <li>Gham-e Ziba in 1382 solar</li>\n  <li>Lulian in 1384 solar</li>\n  <li>Bist Sal ba Asar-e Parviz Meshkatian 2 – Zemestan 1385 solar</li>\n  <li>Molavieh (Shour-e Roumi) in 1386 solar</li>\n  <li>Safar-e Osrat in 1388 solar</li>\n  <li>Amir Kabir in 1390 solar</li>\n  <li>Konserte Shahram Nazeri va Loris Tjeknavorian in 1390 solar</li>\n  <li>Bad-e Yazdahom in 1392 solar</li>\n  <li>Derafsh-e Kaviani, Kaveh-ye Ahangar in 1393 solar</li>\n  <li>Raghsaneh in 1394 solar</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Honors and Awards of Shahram Nazeri</h2>\n<p>Among the many honors and awards Shahram Nazeri has received during his years of activity, the following can be mentioned as the most important ones:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Receiving the “Concours” Award for Folk Music in 1975</li>\n  <li>Receiving the “Best World Mystical Music” Award at the Fes Festival in Morocco in 1997 (After receiving this award, Shahram Nazeri was also called “the Pavarotti of Iran” by the American press.)</li>\n  <li>Receiving a special award from the city of Irvine, California, in appreciation of Shahram Nazeri’s efforts in promoting and spreading Molavi’s spiritual messages and peace through the language of music in 2006</li>\n  <li>Receiving the <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%84%DA%98%DB%8C%D9%88%D9%86_%D8%AF%D9%88%D9%86%D9%88%D8%B1\">Legion of Honour</a> from the French government in 2007</li>\n  <li>Receiving the “Knight of Arts and Letters” medal from the French government in 2007 (This is the highest cultural honor of France and is awarded by the French government to artists who have made special efforts to elevate culture and art.)</li>\n  <li>Receiving the title of “Best Artist of Asia” from the Asia Society in 2007 (In this ceremony, then-UN Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon paid special tribute to Shahram Nazeri.)</li>\n  <li>Receiving the “Living Legend” Award from UCLA</li>\n  <li>Receiving the Golden Sama Award from the Molavi Shrine from the hands of Esin Çelebi, Molavi’s granddaughter, in 1386 solar in Konya, Turkey, and being selected as the honorary president of the Molavi Studies Center at Selçuk University.</li>\n  <li>Naming February 25, 2006, as “Shahram Nazeri Day” by the president of the city council and mayor of San Diego in the geographical area of San Diego, California. Shortly after this naming, the U.S. Congress also presented him with a letter of appreciation.</li>\n  <li>Receiving a commendation from Harvard University for Nazeri’s effective role in introducing Molavi and his mystical poems to the Western world, as well as his innovation in Iranian music and attracting Western audiences.</li>\n</ul>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding this article, please share it with us in the comments section of the article to help improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-11-07 20:51:39', 0, '2025-02-14 00:54:11', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1523, 'posts', 66, 'en', NULL, 'Biography of Kayhan Kalhor, prominent Iranian kamancheh player and musician', NULL, 'This article explores the life and artistic career of Kayhan Kalhor, a renowned Iranian kamancheh player, composer, and musician. He was born in 1964 in Tehran into a Kurdish family and developed an interest in music from an early age. He began learning music at the age of five and became a professional musician during his teenage years.\n\nKalhor’s passion for the kamancheh was inspired by the legendary musician Ali-Asghar Bahari. He later pursued higher education in music composition abroad and earned a master’s degree in Canada.\n\nThroughout his career, Kalhor has collaborated with prominent Iranian artists such as Mohammad Reza Shajarian, Shahram Nazeri, and Hossein Alizadeh. He has also worked extensively with international musicians, playing a significant role in introducing Persian traditional music to a global audience.\n\nKalhor has received international recognition, including Grammy nominations and winning the prestigious WOMEX Awards. In addition to performing and composing, he is actively involved in teaching and promoting Iranian music worldwide.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the life of the great Iranian music master and kamancheh player, <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Kayhan</span> <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Kalhor</span></strong></span>. We first discuss his paternal family and the reasons for his interest in music and kamancheh playing. Then we talk about his education, artistic activities, and domestic and international collaborations. Finally, we present the story of Master Kalhor’s marriage and his published albums. Stay with us...</p>\n<h2>Family, Childhood, and Interest in Music</h2>\n<p>Kayhan Kalhor, an Iranian musician, player, and composer, was born on 3 Azar 1343 solar year (24 November 1964) in Tehran. His family was Kurdish-speaking and originally from Kermanshah.</p>\n<p>His father, Abbas Heydari Kalhor, was the grandson of Mehr Ali Khan. Mehr Ali Khan was one of the khans of the Kalhor tribe, which is the second largest tribe in Iran and the largest tribe in Kermanshah province. Abbas Heydari Kalhor was an employee of the Ministry of Agriculture and was considered one of its high-ranking officials.</p>\n<p>His father had a great interest in different musical cultures and civilizations and listened to Kurdish, Turkish, and Arabic music at home. In fact, it was he who encouraged the young Kayhan Kalhor to become familiar with various musical styles and strive to learn them. Perhaps the creativity and genius that Kalhor shows in creating diverse works is the result of his early acquaintance with the classical elements of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a> and the music of neighboring countries.</p>\n<p>The Kalhor family had a great interest in art and music, and for this reason, Kayhan Kalhor became familiar with instruments and various types of music from a very young age. Although there was no musician in Kayhan Kalhor’s family, their strong interest in music meant that authentic and high-quality music pieces were always played in their home. Kayhan Kalhor’s mother, whose name was Aghdas, sometimes sang Kurdish <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%aa%d8%b5%d9%86%db%8c%d9%81/\">tasnifs</a> for her own pleasure at home. She had a very good voice, and Kayhan learned these Kurdish tasnifs completely from his mother.</p>\n&nbsp;\n[caption id=\"attachment_4122\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"749\"]<img class=\"wp-image-4122 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/sornaz_keyvan_kalhor_003.jpg\" alt=\"Kayhan Kalhor began his music education at the age of 5.\" width=\"749\" height=\"894\" /> 1- Kayhan Kalhor began his music education at the age of 5.[/caption]\n&nbsp;\n<p>The Kalhor family provided tremendous support for their son in music education and learning. It was this support that paved the way for Kayhan Kalhor’s professional activities and enabled him to discover his passion in childhood. Kayhan Kalhor turned to <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">music</a> from early childhood and began his music education at the age of only 5. After several years of training and practice, he started his professional activities at the age of 12 and became a professional musician during his teenage years.</p>\n<p>During his school years in the first year of high school, the Kalhor family returned to Kermanshah. In these circumstances, Kayhan, due to the high skill he had acquired in playing, along with his brother Kamran, managed to join the orchestra of Kermanshah Radio and Television. The broadcasting of the artistic activities of these two brothers on radio and television caused them to be quickly recognized by musicians in Kermanshah and receive special attention.</p>\n<p>With the beginning of the imposed war, Kayhan Kalhor’s family returned to Tehran again, and Kayhan continued his musical activities in Tehran.</p>\n<h2>Kayhan Kalhor’s Interest in the Kamancheh</h2>\n<p>Kayhan Kalhor became interested in the kamancheh after hearing the sound of “Ali Asghar Bahari’s” kamancheh playing. Master Kalhor has mentioned this several times in various interviews, stating that “Ali Asghar Bahari” is his role model in playing the kamancheh.</p>\n<p>Ali Asghar Bahari is one of the greatest kamancheh players in Iran who gave new life to this sweet and pleasant instrument after a period of stagnation when it had been almost forgotten. At a time when Master Bahari took up the kamancheh, most people looked at this instrument with contempt. In those days, the kamancheh was considered lowly and was thought to belong only to wandering entertainers.</p>\n<p>With his unique performances and beautiful kamancheh playing on the radio, Bahari was invited by the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">University of Tehran</a> to teach and instruct this instrument. After that, the kamancheh became known not only in Iran but also in other countries around the world.</p>\n<p>Bahari’s great mastery over rhythmic pieces and the radif of Iranian music had a profound influence on Kayhan Kalhor. However, because finding a teacher in this field was difficult for him, Kayhan Kalhor initially turned to learning the santur, violin, and several other instruments. It was only after becoming familiar with the radifs of Iranian music and after the age of 12 that he turned to his lifelong passion, the kamancheh, and from then on, this instrument became Master Kayhan Kalhor’s entire life.</p>\n<p>In this way, through the efforts and activities of masters such as Ali Asghar Bahari, Hossein Yahaghi, Ardavan Kamkar, and later Kayhan Kalhor, new life was breathed into the lifeless body of the kamancheh, and this instrument emerged from isolation.</p>\n<h2>Studying in the Field of Composition</h2>\n<p>Kayhan Kalhor collaborated for a while with the “Sheyda” group led by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b6%d8%a7-%d9%84%d8%b7%d9%81%db%8c/\">Mohammad Reza Lotfi</a>, but shortly afterward he decided to go abroad to continue his studies in music, become more familiar with world music, and study composition. He first went to Italy, then to <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Carleton University in Ottawa</a>, Canada, and finally graduated with a master’s degree in composition from this university.</p>\n<p>While Kayhan Kalhor was abroad, his family home in Tehran was hit by a missile from the Iraqi Ba’ath army, resulting in the death of his father, mother, and brother.</p>\n<p>Kayhan Kalhor’s first stay in Canada lasted about seven years, and finally in 1373 solar year (1994) he returned to Iran and Tehran for a short period. Upon his return to Iran, Kalhor became acquainted with the “Dastan” music group, and this acquaintance led to collaboration with the group. During this collaboration, in the early 1370s solar (1990s), with the accompaniment of the Dastan group and the singing of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Iraj Bastami</a>, Kayhan Kalhor was able to perform his first official concert.</p>\n<p>Master Kayhan Kalhor went to Canada again in 1374 solar year (1995) to continue his doctoral studies. After returning to Canada, he continued his collaboration with great Iranian musicians, including Master <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Shahram Nazeri</a>. However, Kalhor gained special fame with the album “Shab, Sokut, Kavir” (Night, Silence, Desert) in 1377 solar year (1998) with the singing of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b6%d8%a7-%d8%b4%d8%ac%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Master Mohammad Reza Shajarian</a>.</p>\n&nbsp;\n[caption id=\"attachment_4123\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"700\"]<img class=\"wp-image-4123 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/sornaz_keyvan_kalhor_004.jpg\" alt=\"Kayhan Kalhor with the great Iranian singing master, Mohammad Reza Shajarian.\" width=\"700\" height=\"437\" /> 2- Kayhan Kalhor with the great Iranian singing master, Mohammad Reza Shajarian.[/caption]\n&nbsp;\n<h2>Kayhan Kalhor’s Artistic Activities and Introducing Iranian Music to the World</h2>\n<p>Kayhan Kalhor has remarkable and extraordinary skill in playing the specialized instruments of kamancheh, tanbur, setar, and shah kaman. For this reason, he has collaborated with prominent artists such as Mohammad Reza Shajarian, Shahram Nazeri, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%af%d9%87/\">Hossein Alizadeh</a>, Ali Akbar Moradi, Alireza Eftekhari, Iraj Bastami, Homayoun Shajarian, Majid Kiani, and the members of the Dastan group, where he has worked as both composer and performer.</p>\n<p>Master Kayhan Kalhor has a very creative, persistent, and humble personality, qualities that have turned him into a professional and precise musician with high technical knowledge and skill.</p>\n<p>With his strokes on the body of the kamancheh, Kayhan Kalhor creates an authentic and passionate sound that delights listeners. He has made proper and authentic innovations in his style that have distinguished his work from previous players.</p>\n<p>Kayhan Kalhor was a member of the Dastan group for a very long time and played the kamancheh in the group’s concerts alongside the singing master <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b4%d9%87%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%86%d8%a7%d8%b8%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Shahram Nazeri</a>.</p>\n<p>Kayhan Kalhor has also been active in film music composition and has composed the music for the films “Lantouri” and “Majburim” (We Have to). His works have also been used in the soundtrack of Ridley Scott’s film “Kingdom of Heaven”. Kayhan Kalhor also collaborated on the music for Francis Ford Coppola’s film “Youth Without Youth” and the animation “Soul”.</p>\n<p>His first experience composing music for an Iranian TV series was for the series “Khatun”.</p>\n<p>In addition to his professional activities, Master Kayhan Kalhor is also active in teaching performance and holds educational classes around the world to promote and preserve the rich traditions of Iranian music. No one can deny the contributions Kayhan Kalhor has made to introducing and promoting Iranian music in other countries of the world.</p>\n<p>In addition to extensive collaborations with great figures of Iranian music, Kayhan Kalhor has made continuous and tireless efforts to introduce Iranian music to the world and has collaborated with many great and prominent musicians from around the globe. These artistic collaborations with artists from different musical cultures, including Erdal Erzincan from Turkey, Shujaat Hussain Khan from India, Yo-Yo Ma from China, Zhao Jiping, Alim Qasimov from Azerbaijan, Brooklyn Rider from the United States, the New York Philharmonic Orchestra, the Kronos Quartet, and the Dutch Wind Ensemble, have made him a well-known artist on the international stage.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, these joint works with foreign musicians have made him one of the most honored Iranian musicians in the world, and through this method, he has managed to bring Iranian music to global fame. Master Kalhor was nominated for the prestigious Grammy Award for the albums “Bi To Be Sar Nemishavad” and “Faryad”, which were composed by Hossein Alizadeh with the singing of Mohammad Reza Shajarian.</p>\n<p>Therefore, Kayhan Kalhor’s fame and popularity are not limited to Iran; his reputation has crossed cultural and geographical borders and is recognized and famous internationally. His collaborations with great Iranian musicians and non-Iranian singers and players have made him a beloved figure in the world.</p>\n<p>Kalhor has stepped into a world beyond music and, by connecting the cultures of different countries, has created very beautiful fusion pieces. One of the reasons for Kayhan Kalhor’s great fame is his tireless efforts on the international stage and introducing traditional Iranian music throughout the world.</p>\n<h3>Kayhan Kalhor’s International Collaborations with Musicians from Other Parts of the World</h3>\n<p>One of Master Kalhor’s greatest international collaborations is the album “Baran” with the “Ghazal” group, in which Kayhan Kalhor plays kamancheh, Shujaat Hussain Khan plays sitar, and Sandeep Das plays tabla. This performance and collaboration was nominated for a Grammy Award in 2004.</p>\n<p>In January 2008, Kayhan Kalhor performed a concert with the Brooklyn Rider string quartet in New York City, in which pieces from the album “City of Silence” were also played.</p>\n<p>Kayhan Kalhor has performed concerts in various parts of the world with the Turkish baglama player Erdal Erzincan and has also released an album titled “The Wind” in collaboration with this Kurdish player on the ECM label.</p>\n<p>Furthermore, at the 59th Grammy Awards, Kayhan Kalhor, along with the Silk Road Ensemble led by Yo-Yo Ma from China, won the Grammy Award for Best World Music Album with the album “Sing Me Home”. In 2019, at the WOMEX World Music Festival, Kayhan Kalhor was selected as the Best Artist of the Year in the world and thus became the first Iranian musician to win the prestigious global “<a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%88%D9%88%D9%85%DA%A9%D8%B3\">WOMEX</a>” award.</p>\n<h2>Kayhan Kalhor’s Marriage</h2>\n<p>Kayhan Kalhor married Zohreh Soltanabadi, a professional and active breeder of Arabian horses. In an interview, they announced that they met when Kalhor went to the Soltanabadi complex for horseback riding through a mutual friend, and this meeting led to their acquaintance and marriage.</p>\n&nbsp;\n[caption id=\"attachment_4124\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"745\"]<img class=\"wp-image-4124 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/sornaz_keyvan_kalhor_005.jpg\" alt=\"Kayhan Kalhor with his wife Zohreh Soltanabadi.\" width=\"745\" height=\"745\" /> 3- Kayhan Kalhor with his wife Zohreh Soltanabadi.[/caption]\n&nbsp;\n<h2>Albums by Master Kayhan Kalhor</h2>\n<p>The list of albums that Master Kayhan Kalhor has released from 1371 solar year (1992) until today, with the help of famous Iranian composers, players, and singers, is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Album “Buy-e Nowruz” in the winter of 1371 solar (1992) with composition by Hamid Motebassem and singing by Iraj Bastami, accompanied by the Dastan group.</li>\n  <li>Album “Shab, Sokut, Kavir” (Night, Silence, Desert) in 1377 solar (1998) with composition by Kayhan Kalhor and singing by Mohammad Reza Shajarian, with the presence of Bijan Kamkar (published by Delawaz).</li>\n  <li>Album “Safar be Digar Su” (Journey to the Other Side) in 1377 solar (1998) with composition by Shahram Nazeri and arrangement by the Dastan group, with Master Kalhor as setar player (published by Soroush).</li>\n  <li>Album “Sarv-e Simin” in 1378 solar (1999) with composition by Mohammad Ali Kiani Nejad and singing by Alireza Eftekhari, with Master Kayhan Kalhor as kamancheh player (published by Fars Nova).</li>\n  <li>Album “Ghazal 1” in 1380 solar (2001), the first album of four albums from the Ghazal music group collaboration, in which Master Kalhor played the kamancheh (published by Mahriz).</li>\n  <li>Album “Zemestan Ast” (It Is Winter) in 1380 solar (2001) with singing by Mohammad Reza Shajarian, in which Master Kalhor played the kamancheh and Homayoun Shajarian played the tombak (published by Delawaz).</li>\n  <li>Album “Bi To Be Sar Nemishavad” in 1381 solar (2002) with singing by Hossein Alizadeh, in which Master Kalhor performs alongside Hossein Alizadeh in the Dastgah of Nava and Avaz-e Bayat-e Kurd (published by Delawaz).</li>\n  <li>Album “Faryad” (Cry) in 1382 solar (2003), arranged with singing by Mohammad Reza Shajarian and tar by Hossein Alizadeh in the Dastgah of Rast Panjgah (published by Delawaz).</li>\n  <li>Album “Baran” (Rain) in 1382 solar (2003) with Master Kayhan Kalhor playing the kamancheh, a fusion of Iranian and Indian music, which was nominated for a Grammy Award.</li>\n  <li>Album “Dar Aineh-ye Aseman” (In the Mirror of the Sky) in 1382 solar (2003), accompanied by the singing of Ali Akbar Moradi, with Pejman Khodadadi also accompanying Master Kalhor.</li>\n  <li>Album “Nokhostin Didar-e Bamdad” (First Morning Meeting) in 1385 solar (2006) with kamancheh playing by Kayhan Kalhor and Pejman Hadadi (published by Avay-e Doost).</li>\n  <li>Album “Bad” (Wind) published in 1385 solar (2006) in collaboration with Kayhan Kalhor and Erdal Erzincan (published by Avay-e Doost).</li>\n  <li>Album “Sorud-e Mehr” (Hymn of Love) produced in 1386 solar (2007) in collaboration with Hossein Alizadeh and singing by Mohammad Reza Shajarian (published by Delawaz).</li>\n  <li>Album “Saz-e Khamush” (Silent Instrument) published in 1386 solar (2007) with singing by Mohammad Reza Shajarian and kamancheh playing by Kayhan Kalhor in Avaz-e Dashti (published by Delawaz).</li>\n  <li>Album “Shahr-e Khamush” (City of Silence) produced in 1387 solar (2008) with a number of foreign musicians (published by Avay-e Khorshid).</li>\n  <li>Album “Saman-e Sayeha” (The Arrangement of Shadows) produced in 1390 solar (2011) as a duet of kamancheh and tombak, with Majid Khelaj playing tombak alongside Kayhan Kalhor.</li>\n  <li>Album “Havniyaz” in 1395 solar (2016), with composition and performance by Kayhan Kalhor, accompanied by Azerbaijani and Turkish musicians.</li>\n</ul>\n&nbsp;\n[caption id=\"attachment_4121\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"750\"]<img class=\"wp-image-4121 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/sornaz_keyvan_kalhor_002.jpg\" alt=\"Kayhan Kalhor performing and playing the kamancheh.\" width=\"750\" height=\"500\" /> 4- Kayhan Kalhor performing and playing the kamancheh.[/caption]\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding this article, please share it with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com\">Sornaz Music School</a> in the comments to help improve the quality of this content.</p>', '2023-11-09 16:39:20', 0, '2025-02-14 00:53:59', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1524, 'posts', 67, 'en', NULL, 'Biography of Abolhasan Saba, prominent Iranian musician and composer | The player who played all instruments', NULL, 'Abolhassan Saba was a prominent Iranian musician, composer, and teacher born in 1902 (1281 SH) into an artistic family. He began learning music with his father and later studied under renowned masters such as Mirza Abdollah, Darvish Khan, and Ali Naqi Vaziri.\n\nSaba played a crucial role in modernizing Iranian music by integrating Western violin techniques with traditional Persian music, creating a distinctive style. He was also a pioneer in collecting and incorporating Iranian folk melodies into the classical repertoire, with notable works like “Deylaman.”\n\nThroughout his life, he trained many influential students and contributed significantly to music education. Beyond music, he was skilled in painting and instrument making. He passed away in 1957, and his house was later turned into a museum. His legacy includes educational books, compositions, and a lasting impact on Iranian violin performance.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we talk about the biography of Master <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Abolhasan</span> <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Saba</span></strong>, the violinist, composer, and genius of Iranian music. We discuss the birth and upbringing environment of Abolhasan Khan Saba, then move on to his teachers, his presence at Ali Naghi Vaziri’s music school, managing the music school in Rasht, and adding gushehs from different regions to the radif of Iranian modal music. We talk about Master Saba’s marriage and children and get to know some of his famous students. Stay with us in the following...</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>Birth and Upbringing Environment of Abolhasan Saba</h2>\n<p>Abolhasan Saba was born in 1281 solar year (1902) into an aristocratic and relatively affluent family familiar with music and literature. His father, Kamal al-Saltaneh, was a physician, man of letters, and music lover who played the setar for entertainment and recreation, not professionally, and occasionally composed poetry. He was the first teacher of his son Abolhasan.</p>\n<p>Kamal al-Saltaneh was one of the prominent physicians and sages of the court and, due to his artistic taste, was always highly regarded. Because of this artistic taste, the Saba house became a gathering place for poets, musicians, and music lovers of that era. Abolhasan Saba was very fortunate to grow up in an environment full of love for art and music.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>Music Education and Teachers of Abolhasan Khan Saba</h2>\n<p>From childhood, Abolhasan was encouraged to learn and play various instruments. Abolhasan Saba received his initial music training from his father, who was a skilled setar player. He also learned the subtleties and complexities of Iranian music rhythms while playing the tombak from Robabeh Ravanbakhsh, his aunt’s companion and his mother’s close friend. A few years later, he professionally learned tombak techniques from Haji Khan Zarbi.</p>\n<p>After learning the basics of setar from his father, at the age of 10, Abolhasan Khan began more professional setar training under the prominent setar player of the time, Mirza Abdollah Farahani, who was also a close friend of his father. After Mirza Abdollah’s death in 1294 solar year (1915), Saba continued his musical education with the great musician Gholamhossein Khan Darvish and learned to play the tar under Darvish Khan’s instruction. At the same time as learning tar and setar, he studied santur under Ali Akbar Khan Shahi.</p>\n<p>In his early teens, Abolhasan Saba became fascinated with the violin. This instrument had recently shown, due to its remarkable adaptability to Iranian melodies and tunes, that it was more flexible than native Iranian instruments. Abolhasan Saba’s first violin teacher was Hossein Esmailzadeh, the famous kamancheh player who also played the violin. Hossein Khan was also Saba’s kamancheh teacher and had his own unique style of playing kamancheh, moving the bow in short forward and backward motions.</p>\n<p>Later, Abolhasan Khan Saba began more serious violin training under Hossein Hangafarin, who was himself a graduate of the music school and had been trained by French teachers at Dar al-Fonun. On the other hand, Abolhasan Saba learned the basics of Western music, music notation, and music writing from Hangafarin. In classical education, Saba was sent to one of the best schools in Tehran at the time, the Elmieh Primary School, where they also used American college methods for secondary education. During this period, Abolhasan Saba also learned ney playing under Akbar Khan.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h3>Presence at Ali Naghi Vaziri’s Music School</h3>\n<p>In 1302 solar year (1923), after the announcement of the Higher Music School by Colonel Ali Naghi Vaziri, Abolhasan Saba and his friend Rouhollah Khaleghi were the first to register for the school. Thanks to his great seriousness and perseverance, Abolhasan Khan Saba became an outstanding student and soloist of the Higher Music School’s programs at the age of 22 and was always respected and encouraged by his teachers and friends.</p>\n<p>The first recorded work of Master Abolhasan Saba was the piece “Zard Malijeh” performed on violin, which was released in 1306 solar year (1927) on a record between two parts of the anthem “Ey Vatan” with the enchanting voice of Rouh Angiz and received great attention from art lovers.</p>\n<p>Along with other students, he was fully committed to the views of his teacher, Ali Naghi Khan Vaziri, and believed in the necessity of reforming and modernizing Iranian music. When the first orchestra of Vaziri’s music school was formed, seat number one – the leader’s position in the violin section – was given to Abolhasan Khan Saba. Ali Naghi Vaziri created this orchestra for a series of concerts in the city of Rasht.</p>\n<p>In 1306 solar year (1927), Vaziri’s Higher Music School held a concert on the occasion of the inauguration of the Rasht Municipality Hall, which was met with great enthusiasm by the people of Rasht. After the concert, the people asked Ali Naghi Vaziri to open a music school in the city. Vaziri, in response to this request, wrote the necessary letters to obtain permission to establish a school of music and fine industries in Rasht under the management of his best student, Abolhasan Saba.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_4133\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"800\"]<img class=\"wp-image-4133 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/sornaz_abolhasan_saba_004.jpg\" alt=\"Master Saba collected local melodies of northern Iran during his management of the Gilan music school, among which the gusheh of Deylaman stands out as the most prominent.\" width=\"800\" height=\"500\" /> Master Saba, during his management of the Gilan music school, collected local melodies of northern Iran, among which the gusheh of Deylaman is the jewel in the crown.[/caption]\n&nbsp;\n<h4>Exploration of Folk and Regional Music and the Gusheh of Deylaman</h4>\n<p>After the establishment of this school, Abolhasan Saba managed it for nearly two years in Rasht. During this time, in addition to teaching music, he traveled to the cities, villages, and foothills of Gilan and Mazandaran, collecting existing local melodies and bringing back many treasures from these trips.</p>\n<p>Therefore, Master Abolhasan Saba can be considered the first Iranian musician who studied Iranian folk music. Some of the local songs he collected and gave a fresh color and flavor to were later used as the basis for his violin compositions. Among these collected melodies are the gushehs of Deylaman, Zard Malijeh, Dar Qafas, Chupi Dance of Qasem Abad, Kuhestani, and Amiri Mazandarani, which are souvenirs of this period of his life. The gusheh of Deylaman, as the jewel in the crown of these pieces, found its place among the gushehs of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%af%d8%b4%d8%aa%db%8c/\">Avaz-e Dashti</a>.</p>\n<p>There are many performances of this gusheh by great masters of Iranian music, including Gholamhossein Banan and Mohammad Reza Shajarian. However, despite the beautiful performances of these great masters, it can be said that the most memorable performance of the gusheh of Deylaman is heard in the closing credits of the series “Pas az Baran” (After the Rain), which was broadcast on national television in the early 2000s, with the voice of Master Fereydoun Pourreza. Because Master Pourreza is the only Gilaki person who performed this gusheh with a poem in the local Gilaki dialect, and thanks to his auditory memory of this melody from childhood in this land, he left behind a unique masterpiece.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h3>Separation from Ali Naghi Vaziri and the Beginning of a New Period</h3>\n<p>From 1310 solar year (1931) onward, while maintaining his friendly and intimate relationship with Colonel Ali Naghi Khan Vaziri, Abolhasan Khan Saba completely stepped out from under his teacher’s supervision and way of thinking and began working independently as a private teacher.</p>\n<p>Master Abolhasan Saba, in friendly and informal gatherings when he wanted to play for his own pleasure, would play the setar. This shows Abolhasan Saba’s deep attachment to the setar. Most art lovers and musicians who associated with him believed that Master Saba was a skilled and inspiring setar player who had a delicate and captivating style perfectly in harmony with the calm and gentle nature of this instrument.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_4132\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"800\"]<img class=\"wp-image-4132 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/sornaz_abolhasan_saba_003.jpg\" alt=\"Master Abolhasan Khan Saba had a special affection for the setar and always played it in his private moments.\" width=\"800\" height=\"500\" /> Master Abolhasan Khan Saba had a special affection for the setar and always played it in his private moments.[/caption]\n\n<p>Regarding the violin, it is difficult to name any skilled and pleasant-sounding violinist up to the 1960s and 1970s solar calendar who did not learn to play this instrument in some way under Saba. Among these great violinists are Homayoun Khorram, Habibollah Badi’i, Ali Tajvidi, Mehdi Khalaji, Mahmoud Zolfonoun, and Abbas Shapouri, all of whom were admirers and devotees of Master Abolhasan Saba.</p>\n<p>The unique method and style founded thanks to Master Abolhasan Saba was based on the Western style that he had become familiar with during his apprenticeship under Ali Naghi Khan Vaziri. However, Abolhasan Saba had given it a different color and flavor and had adapted his method to a great extent to Iranian music. His bowing technique was remarkably more advanced than those who had come to the violin from the path of kamancheh playing. Abolhasan Khan Saba used up-and-down bow movements as well as full bow strokes for soft and legato sounds.</p>\n<p>Master Abolhasan Saba also had a very interesting approach to dynamics and understood their value in creating deep dramatic and emotional effects. He introduced other Western technical methods into Iranian music that had not been used before, such as portamento (continuous movement from one note to another), spiccato (short bow strokes where the bow leaves the string after each note), staccato (detached and short notes with half their time value), and pizzicato (playing notes with the finger on the string).</p>\n<p>At the same time, Master Abolhasan Saba created methods for phrasing specific decorative musical figures in Iranian music. In this way, in his violin playing style, he benefited from Western violin techniques that were enhanced by the decorative characteristics of native music. Although Abolhasan Saba was a student of Vaziri, unlike Vaziri’s educational approach, he did not recommend pure Western technical etudes to his students. Abolhasan Saba believed that the necessary skill would be acquired through the process of learning practical musical pieces.</p>\n<p>In Saba’s view, emphasis on technical exercises in a dry and soulless manner leads to undesirable results. Abolhasan Saba was not very active as a composer and primarily saw himself as a performer and teacher. However, his creative role, especially as the inventor of a distinct style of chaharmezrab playing for the violin, deserves examination and reflection.</p>\n<p>Technically, the traditional <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%86%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d8%b6%d8%b1%d8%a7%d8%a8/\">chaharmezrab form</a> was viewed by people as part of the 19th-century classical radif, considered as separate pieces based on fixed rhythmic patterns that lacked melodic character. These chaharmezrabs were mostly used as short melodic interludes to familiarize the listener’s ear with the dastgah and its gushehs, usually performed improvisationally between avaz sections. Due to their usually fast rhythms, chaharmezrabs were also considered indicators of the players’ skill.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, Abolhasan Saba’s chaharmezrabs had combinations that also contained melodic content. Moreover, unlike the old chaharmezrabs that did not use compound playing or modulation, Saba’s chaharmezrabs incorporated both modulation and compound playing.</p>\n<p>In addition to chaharmezrabs, Saba composed other pieces in different forms and frameworks. For example, his three-volume book on piano playing methods includes composed pieces that meet the needs of students from beginner to advanced levels. Abolhasan Saba also composed a <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d9%be%db%8c%d8%b4-%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%a2%d9%85%d8%af/\">pishdaramad</a> in Avaz-e Bayat-e Tork, which was published in the book “Eighteen Pishdaramad Pieces” (published by Lotfollah Mofakham Payan, Tehran Publishing Company, 1329 solar year).</p>\n<p>Furthermore, Master Abolhasan Khan Saba composed a very beautiful <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%90%d9%86%da%af/\">rang</a> in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%aa%d8%b1%da%a9/\">Avaz-e Bayat-e Tork</a> consisting of 25 rhythmic pieces, which was edited by Lotfollah Mofakham Payan and published by the same Tehran Publishing Company in 1327 solar year. Most traditional Iranian musicians who engaged in composing tasnifs were drawn to music accompanied by singing. <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%aa%d8%b5%d9%86%db%8c%d9%81/\">Tasnifs</a> or songs seem to have been the type favored by the general public. On the other hand, Master Abolhasan Saba also composed non-vocal pieces that clearly demonstrate his creative motivations in violin playing and were a response to the needs of his students.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>Interest in Non-Musical Fields</h2>\n<p>Master Abolhasan Khan Saba also showed a deep attachment to painting throughout his life. For this reason, in his youth, he studied for a while under Kamal al-Molk Mohammad Ghaffari, the famous and prominent Iranian painter, and learned painting and charcoal drawing. Some of Abolhasan Saba’s oil paintings have also survived; these paintings clearly show that he had the innate potential to become a skilled and capable painter.</p>\n<p>He also acquired skills in fields such as file work, carpentry, casting, marquetry, and khatam-kari, and later applied these trainings in the repair and construction of musical instruments. Abolhasan Saba read the works of Nima Yushij and Sadegh Hedayat and had very close relationships with Nima and Shahriar.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h3>Presence at the Qor-khaneh and Learning Carpentry and Metalworking</h3>\n<p>During the 1300s solar decade (1920s), while still under Vaziri’s instruction, Abolhasan Saba worked as his teacher’s assistant and helper in teaching for 7 years. In this decade, Saba was employed at the state weapons manufacturing workshop (Qor-khaneh) in Tehran, where he learned to work with metal and wood. This experience at the Qor-khaneh later helped him as a hobby in making musical instruments, especially setar and santur, in the following years of his life.</p>\n<p>Abolhasan Saba’s reputation as a musician and a compassionate person whose precision in teaching music became legendary. In the mid-1940s solar decade, the name of Master Abolhasan Saba was not only known as an outstanding violinist but also as an inspiring teacher. Although his fame was primarily due to violin playing, he also trained students who learned tombak, santur, and setar in his presence.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>Marriage and Children</h2>\n<p>In 1311 solar year (1932), Abolhasan Saba married one of his students, Montakhab Esfandiari Kuh Nour, who was Nima Yushij’s cousin. The marriage resulted in three daughters named Ghazaleh, Zhaleh, and Roxana.</p>\n<p>Saba Khoi is the daughter of Roxana, the youngest daughter of Master Abolhasan Khan Saba and Mr. Esmail Khoi. She also plays the tar and continues her grandmother’s artistic activities in producing Iranian local clothing. Saba Khoi is currently the newsroom presenter and one of the program producers of the “Man o To” television network. Another grandchild of Master Saba is Abolhasan Mir Vali, son of Ghazaleh, the eldest daughter of the Master, who, like his grandfather, is engaged in music education.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>Death and Will of Abolhasan Khan Saba</h2>\n<p>After many years of effort and training numerous students, Abolhasan Saba finally passed away on the night of Friday, 29 Azar 1336 solar year (December 20, 1957) due to heart problems at the age of 55. He was buried in Zahir al-Dowleh Cemetery. After Master Abolhasan Saba’s death, according to his will, his house was turned into a museum by the Faculty of Fine Arts on 29 Aban 1353 solar year (November 20, 1974).</p>\n<p>Saba Khoi, the granddaughter of Master Saba, seriously pursued the matter of restoring the house and museum for many years, and in 1383 solar year (2004), these efforts finally bore fruit. The museum was restored and reached its current condition so that it could welcome Iranian art lovers.</p>\n<p>Shahriar composed the following verse in mourning for the untimely death of Abolhasan Saba:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Ey Saba ba to che goftand ke khamush shodi?     Che sharabi be to dadand ke madhush shodi?</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h3>Saba’s House</h3>\n<p>The residential house of Saba, which his father Kamal al-Saltaneh had purchased in 1276 solar year (1897), was located on Zahir al-Islam Street in Tehran. After Saba’s passing, the Ministry of Culture and Art of the time purchased this house, placed the Master’s works and memorabilia (provided by Mrs. Montakhab Saba, the Master’s wife) in it, and with the follow-up of Faramarz Payvar, the santur master and loyal student of Saba, converted the house – which had undergone changes in its building plan due to street widening – into the Saba Museum.</p>\n<p>The works and belongings of Saba kept in this museum include: a Swiss-made Zenith pocket watch with a silver hinged case, a Parker (Vicomatic) fountain pen, an amber rosary, letters, photographs, a pipe, a lighter, a notebook, several books, an oil painting by himself, santur, kamancheh, tombak, ney, a carved setar made by himself, the handmade violin he played on which the famous miniaturist Behzad had painted the portrait of a girl with disheveled hair on the back, with the inscription: “Souvenir to Master Saba, 25 Khordad 1330 solar, Behzad”.</p>\n<p>In addition: an adjustable tuning fork for different notes, a wind-up metronome with a bell, an old gramophone, several gramophone records (including Saba’s violin record recorded in Syria “In Memory of Ghazaleh”), a wooden desk, photographs from the Higher Music School period with Master Ali Naghi Vaziri, several official appointments such as the appointment letter for managing the School of Fine Industries in Rasht (15/11/1308), photo albums, school report cards, handwritten music notations, and Saba’s special violin that was repaired and adjusted by one of the violin makers in 1319 solar year.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h3>Abolhasan Saba’s Style</h3>\n<p>Abolhasan Saba’s main instrument was the violin, which he had learned with proper technique at the Higher Music School. Saba was an authentic, honest, and sincere artist who, apart from his performing aspect, was unparalleled in creativity in composing chaharmezrabs and other pieces, and in his excellent taste in summarizing and performing the radif.</p>\n<p>He meticulously notated authentic melodies and pieces ranging from Darvish’s masnavi singing in the bazaar to the avaz of Deylaman villages in the north and the Chupi dance of Qasem Abad, thereby greatly enriching the diversity and richness of authentic Iranian music works. He was very patient in teaching music and, despite his physical weakness and heart condition, even in the last weeks of his life, he did not withhold patience and precision in teaching his students.</p>\n<p>The tonality of Abolhasan Saba’s violin, unlike most of his contemporary violinists, was clear, clean, and pleasant, and he observed correct violin technique with special care. He set regular fingerings for the violin and invented specific bowings (in terms of up and down strokes, jumps, and their sequence) for performing Iranian rangs that had no precedent before him. When playing the violin, he did not sound the adjacent strings and, unlike those who mostly used the first position, he correctly used other positions.</p>\n<p>In playing, he did not produce unwanted vibration in the left hand, and the tonality of his instrument was close to that of the flute in terms of clarity, which he himself preferred. Saba’s violin did not sound like a kamancheh, and the bow and fingers played their desired roles. Today, the desired school of violin playing originates from Abolhasan Saba. He summarized the old radif, which was sometimes lengthy and tedious, and in fact compiled a type of chamber music radif.</p>\n<p>He composed pieces based on local melodies, especially those from the Gilan region, which can be mentioned as: Zard Malijeh, Kuhestani, Deylaman, Tabari, and several other pieces such as “Dar Qafas”, some of which he composed and notated during his stay in the north under the influence of the famous local kamancheh player Feyzollah in the Amarlo region. Saba continued his continuous violin practice until 1310 solar year and after that devoted himself more to music work and training his students.</p>\n<p>Due to his familiarity with the secrets of violin playing, as well as his talent, good taste, and deep knowledge of Iranian music, Abolhasan Saba well realized that the violin, with its brilliant tone and wide range, is completely suitable for performing Iranian music and can well present the states of national music. (Yehudi Menuhin, the prominent violinist, said: Any instrument that can present the characteristics of a country’s national music can be counted among the instruments of that land. The violin is such in Iranian music.) For this reason, he transferred what he knew to the violin and created a special school for this sweet-sounding instrument.</p>\n<p>Before Saba, Iranian violinists imitated the kamancheh playing method when performing this instrument, and from this perspective, their fingering range rarely exceeded the first position, and naturally achieving the desired tone and correct use of the bow was not possible for them.</p>\n<p>Saba said: Before the establishment of Vaziri’s music school, the violin was played in a confused manner (i.e., based on kamancheh), and the players of this instrument had not realized the greatness of violin technique, especially bow technique. Since I had played and studied Iranian music with other instruments and also used European methods for positions and violin playing, I transferred my knowledge to the violin.</p>\n<p>Abolhasan Saba used specific tunings for each dastgah or its avazes (affiliates of the dastgahs) using the desired pedal note in chaharmezrabs and considered executive skills and technique presentation to the extent that the artistic value and beauty of the piece would not be sacrificed for technical display. In combining short and long syllables of poetry and accents of speech with music, he used his own innovative fingering and bowing method and had found poems with suitable meters for each avaz in different gushehs of the dastgahs.</p>\n<p>Dr. Sasan Sepanta says:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">When learning the violin from him, I asked him to introduce one of his outstanding recorded works to me. He introduced a record that had been recorded in 1312 solar year (1933) of his violin solo performance. After some searching, I found and purchased the desired record with great effort and used it for violin practice.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">About fifteen years after the passing of Master Abolhasan Saba, while researching his artistic characteristics, I transferred the sound of the mentioned record to magnetic reel tape at the same speed that the Columbia factory engineer (the original recorder of the record) had used in 1933 AD when recording Saba’s violin performance. Then, in the acoustics laboratory, after preparing a spectrogram of it and interpreting and analyzing the patterns on it, I deduced characteristics from that artistic work that were unparalleled in performing Iranian music with the violin.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">The tuning of Saba’s violin in this work followed the standard note of 439 Hz at that time, and for this reason, the brilliant tone of the violin is manifested throughout the work. Saba performed the daramad of Abu Ata with “vibrato”, heavy and deliberate, starting from the witness note of Abu Ata, and also performed other periods in the daramad of Abu Ata in other octaves.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Then he began “Hejaz” from its witness note, which is the fifth degree of the Shur scale, with various figures in three octaves starting from note B<sup>4</sup> (B) on the second string of the violin, playing its second octave on the first string with note B<sup>5</sup> and its third octave on the same string from note B<sup>6</sup> (frequency 1975 Hz) in the ninth position on the E string.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">In all these stages, Saba performed each note so correctly and without increase or decrease (while observing the states and interpretation of musical figures and phrases) that the precise electroacoustic devices of the laboratory show each of his notes exactly within its own frequency range. The proportion of the scale notes in a difficult range such as the ninth position has been performed completely correctly, clearly, and distinctly with a negligible tolerance not exceeding five cents. The average ratio of Master Saba’s vibratos on sustained and extended notes, according to laboratory measurements, is about one and a half per second.</p>\n<p>Master Saba, despite his deep and extensive knowledge of the gushehs of Iranian music dastgahs, did not perform the old radif in detail with some gushehs that sometimes lacked attractive musical personality and character. In a writing by Master Ali Tajvidi we read:</p>\n<p>“I remember when the commission of masters [regarding the traditional music radif] was dissolved without result, the discussion around this difference of opinion was circulated in artistic gatherings and music classes.</p>\n<p>One day, Master Ebrahim Qanbari Mehr, who at that time was studying violin under Master Abolhasan Saba and had just started making violins and later became an unparalleled master in making violin, kamancheh, tar, setar, qanun, santur, and even tombak, asked Master Saba:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Sir, what is the difference between Salmak and Salamak? (Note that Salmak is a gusheh in the Shur dastgah and Salamak, as far as I have heard, is a gusheh with a slight difference similar to Salmak in Hossein Khan Esmailzadeh’s radif.)</p>\n<p>Master Saba put on a meaningful smile and said humorously and mockingly:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Ostad jun, besides Salmak and Salamak, we also have the gusheh of Malmak, and the difference between them is several di ri lim, di ri lim. Dear reader!</p>\n<p>We do not have Malmak in the traditional music radif, and this word is a distorted form of “Salmak”, and Master Abolhasan Saba, who did not have a pleasant memory of the disrupted masters’ commission, uttered such a word in jest. May his soul rest in peace. Anyway, the difference of opinion and taste in naming gushehs and the way of performing them has not yet been resolved, and sometimes it has even intensified. Again, dear reader, I should not tell you all the unspeakable things. You should also do some research yourself and see what has been said recently about the radif and the number of gusheh names, and what made-up names are written as traditional music gushehs in magazines and books.” [Tajvidi, 1377 solar]</p>\n<p>In playing the setar, Abolhasan Saba’s right and left strokes were regular and orderly. He used the thumb for playing bass notes in chaharmezrabs, which was borrowed from Vaziri’s innovation on the tar. He played the beginning syllables of poetry words with the right stroke and did not like making the setar strings crowded or tangled. He performed the accents regularly, orderly, and neatly. He did not believe in excessive vibration and was specialized in playing chaharmezrabs. He always said: “Do not wrestle with the instrument.”</p>\n<p>He particularly emphasized to his students the importance of mental concentration on the piece and the relaxation of the hand and body muscles while playing. The tone of Saba’s setar was delicate and clear. He wisely paid attention to limping rhythms in Iranian music (five or seven-beat combinations, “five-eighth” or “seven-eighth” meters) which most notators mistakenly correct to “six-eighth” meter, and performed examples of these meters.</p>\n<p>Esmail Navab Safa, the famous poet and lyricist, writes:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">“In Mordad 1327 solar (August 1948), the sound of Ebadi’s setar was broadcast on the radio. The late Saba said: I will perform setar one week and violin the next week on the radio. Ebadi called Saba and said: You are a peerless master. Is it right that now that the son of your teacher (Mirza Abdollah) is playing setar on the radio, after so many years you think of playing setar? Out of respect for Ebadi, Saba refrained from playing setar on the radio as long as he lived!” [Navab Safa, 1377 solar]</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h3>Abolhasan Saba’s Teaching Method</h3>\n<p>As we mentioned, Abolhasan Saba was very patient in teaching and could well identify the weaknesses of the student. Unlike his teacher Vaziri, he did not believe much in teaching etudes and trained the student with pishdaramads and beautiful melodies, saying: Etude exercises make the student’s hand dry. Vaziri did not approve of this method and believed that the student should also play exercises and scales. In teaching violin, after simple pishdaramads of Avaz-e Dashti, Saba would start radif one and say: The avazes themselves are the best exercise.</p>\n<p>Sometimes he would recite poems to better explain the melody. He paid attention to the correct performance of each note while teaching so that the student would play correctly, and he taught everything he knew without withholding. After finishing the avazes, he gradually taught more difficult rhythmic pieces and taught bow throws in more difficult rangs and chaharmezrabs as etudes. The development of violin and santur playing among players in the last thirty years has been largely due to Abolhasan Saba. He was very generous and had a noble character in receiving fees, and he was passionately devoted to teaching and the survival of Iranian music.</p>\n<p>What we have presented here about Abolhasan Saba’s teaching method are the points that the Master emphasized and taught to Dr. Sasan Sepanta while teaching violin and Iranian music. Saba had classified the accents of Iranian music and taught them to the student according to a specific order in a certain period of the student’s progress. During teaching, Saba emphasized that Iranian music vibratos differ from European ones and that imitating European vibratos harms the expression of Iranian music; especially fast vibratos whose often mechanical application reduces clarity and eloquence in performing pieces of Iranian music dastgahs.</p>\n<p>In performing musical phrases, he recommended observing clarity of notes and said that during performance one should avoid “half-chewed” performance of accents and unnecessary portamento. In general, he did not consider portamento permissible on every note and recommended specific jumping bowings with defined right and left strokes according to his own rules for performing Iranian rangs (such as Shahrashub). He placed special emphasis on observing eloquence in expression during performance and would repeat a phrase many times with the instrument or voice so that the student would become aware of its state and interpretation.</p>\n<p>Dr. Sasan Sepanta, around 1341 solar year (1962) in America, came across the teaching notes of the famous violin teacher Leopold Auer (1845–1930) and with great surprise realized that many of Master Abolhasan Saba’s recommendations in terms of teaching method were similar to Auer’s recommendations. Leopold Auer was a renowned 20th-century violin teacher and a student of the great violinist Joseph Joachim, and in his educational school, he trained students such as Mischa Elman and Jascha Heifetz.</p>\n<p>After studying under Joachim from 1862 to 1865 in Hanover, Germany, and performing concerts in European countries, Auer went to Russia at the invitation of the Imperial Conservatory of St. Petersburg to teach violin and succeed Henrik Wieniawski, the former violin teacher at that conservatory.</p>\n<p>Abolhasan Saba had probably learned valuable violin performance and teaching techniques during his studies under Serge Khotsief and Madame Mantafel, both of whom were educated in Russia and had been influenced directly or indirectly by the “Auer” school, and he used them. He emphasized mental work during practice by making good use of the teachings of the above schools. He determined the way of holding the violin according to the student’s hand condition based on precise principles. He believed that rhythm should be felt mentally and the piece should be played with the help of mental sense, and if necessary, controlled externally with the help of a metronome.</p>\n<p>His explanations during teaching were brief, and with a smile he would say: This phrase should be performed this way, and he would repeat it for the student. A teacher who only relies on words and does not provide a performance example to the student has engaged in “dumb” teaching. Saba considered the A string in the violin as the king string and in teaching bowing method said: Do not press the bow with the arm. Vibratos must be under the player’s control. Detached bow strokes should be played as much as possible with wrist movement. The thumb should not firmly grip the neck of the instrument.</p>\n<p>In teaching his third book, he gave the student the necessary exercises for playing “pizzicato” and while teaching pieces from his first, second, and third radif books, he emphasized the subtle differences in states and said that one should pay attention to the expression and spirit of the music of the piece. In general, Abolhasan Saba, by using the experiences of great violin masters at the global level, established a school for this instrument for the eloquent expression of Iranian music states, which remains unparalleled and unrivaled to this day.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>Prominent Students of Abolhasan Saba</h2>\n<p>During his lifetime, Abolhasan Saba trained many students, among whom we can mention great names such as Gholamhossein Banan, Lotfollah Mofakham Payan, Ali Tajvidi, Jahangir Kamian, Homayoun Khorram, Asadollah Malek, Rahmatollah Badi’i, Habibollah Badi’i, Mohammad Toghaniyan Dehkordi, Shahriar, Babak Radmanesh, Hossein Tehrani, Hassan Kasaei, Dariush Safvat, Faramarz Payvar, Manouchehr Sadeghi, Sasan Sepanta, Ebrahim Qanbari Mehr, Esmail Cheshm Azar, Mehdi Khalaji, Mehdi Moftah, Hossein Khajeh Amiri (Iraj), Parviz Yahaghi, and Farhad Fakhreddini. Each of these names has held a very high and special position in Iranian music, which is obvious to everyone.</p>\n<p>Faramarz Payvar once said, “Whatever we have, we have from Saba.” This single sentence from a great musician and player like Payvar alone reveals the lofty position of Master Abolhasan Saba in Iranian music.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_4131\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"850\"]<img class=\"wp-image-4131 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/sornaz_abolhasan_saba_002.jpg\" alt=\"Abolhasan Saba, a genius whose many prominent Iranian musicians were his students.\" width=\"850\" height=\"500\" /> Abolhasan Saba, a genius whose many prominent Iranian musicians were his students.[/caption]\n&nbsp;\n<h2>Works of Abolhasan Khan Saba</h2>\n<p>Abolhasan Saba achieved skill and proficiency in playing all instruments of the Iranian radif music and could play all Iranian instruments such as santur, tar, setar, tombak, ney, and kamancheh, as well as Western instruments such as violin and piano at a masterful level. However, he chose the violin and setar as his specialized instruments.</p>\n<p>In 1318 solar year (1939), when Radio Tehran was established, Abolhasan Saba, along with other great figures of that period, worked as a performer on the radio. Nevertheless, he continued teaching and researching music at the music conservatory, and in the later years of his life, he held music classes at his home and taught the principles of music and performance to enthusiasts.</p>\n<p>Master Abolhasan Saba played instruments, taught, participated in orchestras, wrote books, and left a direct and positive impact on all currents of Iranian music for forty years. He was skilled in all branches of Iranian music and even other arts such as instrument making, painting, and literature. He knew English and was a comprehensive encyclopedia of the science and practice of Iranian music.</p>\n<p>The modern school of Iranian music, which had begun with Darvish Khan, reached its peak with Master Abolhasan Saba, and Saba’s students also followed their teacher’s path.</p>\n<p>Many records containing his solo and ensemble performances have been released from Master Abolhasan Saba, which demonstrate his extraordinary skill in violin playing. He also recorded many private tapes that serve as references for students and testify to his extraordinary mastery in setar playing.</p>\n<p>From Master Saba, three violin teaching courses, four santur teaching courses, and one tar and setar teaching course have been published, and other works of Abolhasan Saba have not yet been released.</p>\n<p>Abolhasan Saba is the first Iranian musician for whom a special museum (his personal house) has been established in his own name on Zahir al-Islam Street in Tehran.</p>\n<p>Overall, the works left by Master Abolhasan Saba can be categorized as follows:</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h3>Independent Works:</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Violin First, Second, and Third Course Books</li>\n  <li>Santur First, Second, Third, and Fourth Course Books</li>\n  <li>Setar First Course Book</li>\n</ul>\n&nbsp;\n<h3>Non-Independent Works</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Solo “Ey Vatan” in Vaziri’s Violin Instruction Book</li>\n  <li>Chaharmezrab Segah in Vaziri’s Violin Instruction Book</li>\n  <li>Rang Bayat-e Tork, Book of 23 Rhythmic Pieces</li>\n  <li>Pishdaramad-e Tork, 18 Pishdaramad Pieces</li>\n  <li>Navid-e Bahar, First Violin Book</li>\n  <li>Simple Chaharmezrab in Mahur, First Violin Book</li>\n  <li>Tombak Instruction</li>\n  <li>Complete Radif of Iranian Avazes</li>\n  <li>Chaharmezrab Nava</li>\n</ul>\n&nbsp;\n<h3>Works Inspired by Folk Music</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Deylaman (Dashti)</li>\n  <li>Amiri or Mazandarani (Dashti)</li>\n  <li>Zard Malijeh (Dashti)</li>\n  <li>Be Zendān (Shushtari)</li>\n  <li>Dar Qafas (Dashti)</li>\n  <li>Chupi Dance (Afshari)</li>\n  <li>Kuhestani (Dashti)</li>\n</ul>\n&nbsp;\n<h4>Published Works of Saba</h4>\n<p>Abolhasan Saba notated some pieces by Habib Soma’i, such as Chaharmezrab Abu Ata and Hejaz with some modifications in the second santur radif, and also Chahargah, Homayun, and Avaz-e Isfahan by Soma’i with modifications in his third santur radif, and left them as a legacy. His two volumes of violin radif include gushehs, chaharmezrabs, and local melodies that have been arranged and compiled with excellent taste.</p>\n<p>Three violin course books, several santur course books, Zard Malijeh as a solo in Vaziri’s violin instruction “Ey Vatan”, Chaharmezrab Segah in the same book, Rang Bayat-e Tork in twenty-five rhythmic pieces, Pishdaramad-e Tork in eighteen pishdaramad pieces, Navid-e Bahar in the first violin radif, simple Mahur chaharmezrab, and a piece in G minor in the second violin radif of the National Music Conservatory, the introduction of Zard Malijeh in the elementary tar and setar instruction, as well as santur, tar, and setar books are works that were published after his passing.</p>\n<p>He also authored other works such as old tasnifs according to Abdollah Davami, tombak instruction, setar, etc. Mehdi Khalaji said that Abolhasan Saba needed seven hundred tomans to print his books, which he did not have. Mofakham Payan, his student, prepared a pre-sale receipt and with the help of one of Saba’s friends, the cost of printing his first violin radif was provided, and finally his three-volume violin books were printed. They wanted to increase the price of the book to five rials, but Abolhasan Saba did not agree and said: Why should the children pay money?</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h4>Recorded Works of Saba</h4>\n<p>The first piece recorded from Saba was Zard Malijeh, which was performed on his violin on the Dashti “Ey Vatan” record in late 1306 solar year (1927).</p>\n<p>Abolhasan Saba played this piece in Dashti mode with diapason tuning. His other works include: Afshari in the introduction of the Afshari record; Tasnif Bahar with Vaziri’s orchestra and the enchanting voice; Segah (violin with the singing of Reza Qoli Mirza Zali around 1312 solar); Abu Ata and Hejaz (solo violin in Shur scale with diapason tuning – 439 Hz – from Columbia records, recorded around 1312 solar, which Saba considered one of his distinguished performances); Kuhestani and Be Yad-e Gilan (solo violin in Dashti mode); and Bayat-e Tork (with G witness note in diapason tuning from Columbia records).</p>\n<p>On the background of several works by Ali Naghi Vaziri, such as Musical Gymnastics, Rang Segah, and Zaban-e Eshq, Saba had composed humorous poems that he himself performed and sang, and they were recorded on records. One of these is the comic tasnif “Golbandak” (GP11) WO-91 from Columbia records, recorded around 1312 solar in Tehran, on whose label is written: “Golbandak, comic tasnif, music, lyrics, and violin by Abolhasan Saba, piano by Mokhtari”, and it begins with this poem:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Mi gozashtam shabi zir-e bazarche-ye Golbandak,     Cheshm-am oftad va didam zani ra be zir-e einak.</p>\n<p>In 1317 solar year (1938), some records of him were made at the “Sudva” factory in Syria¹, among which Segah and Chaharmezrab Zang-e Shotor are famous. Some of these records include: Bayat-e Isfahan with a green label on which is written “In Memory of Ghazaleh” [Saba’s daughter] number 577-W-1206, and Solo Hosseini with a golden yellow label number 2495.</p>\n<p>The trip that Abolhasan Saba made with several artists to record records in Aleppo and Damascus was accompanied by dangers. In a letter from Aleppo (Syria) he writes:</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">“It has been two days since I entered Aleppo. I am not feeling bad. We came by tram between Beirut and Aleppo. I entered Aleppo early in the morning with insomnia. From Baghdad to Sham, which is Damascus, there is a strange desert with a distance of almost one hundred and fifty farsangs. There is nothing in the middle of the way. The car reaches in twenty-four hours, but because of the heavy rain that had fallen in the desert, we were in the desert for almost four days and four nights. For two days we had no food and quenched our thirst with rainwater.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">They say this desert has never had such rain before. The car got stuck in mud every hour, and immediately the drivers would harshly instruct the passengers in Arabic to get out of the car, collect stones and bushes, pour them under the car, and push the car from behind to get it out of the mud. Anyway, my dear, this operation was repeated perhaps more than fifty times. My friends have taken funny pictures of the desert nature that are worth seeing. Finally, after much hardship, we entered the beautiful city of Damascus and after four days of resting, we went to Beirut.”</p>\n<p>Master Abolhasan Saba made two trips to Beirut. The first trip in 1315 solar year (1936) with Mr. Aristozadeh to Aleppo and Beirut, in which he recorded Homayun, Bayat-e Isfahan (In Memory of Ghazaleh), Mahur, and Segah (Chaharmezrab Zang-e Shotor) records. The second trip in 1317 solar year (1938) with Esmail Mehrtash, Taj Isfahani, Morteza Mahjubi, Molouk Borumand, Badi’zadeh, and Amir Nasser Farhad Mo’tamed (piano), in which they recorded several records.</p>\n<p>From 1332 solar year (1953) onward, when magnetic tape recording and playback devices became common, some private performances of Saba’s setar and violin were recorded in friendly gatherings. Saba performed in some of the early “Golha” programs, which can be mentioned as: Segah with santur by Faramarz Payvar and the singing of Azar Azima; Abu Ata with the singing of Qamar al-Moluk Vaziri (which I later did not hear the tape of); pieces such as Deylaman and Shushtari named “Be Zendān” with Banan’s singing; a piece according to Sadr al-Mohaddesin named Sadri in Afshari with Hossein Ghavami’s singing; and “Karavan” in Dashti.</p>\n<p>Chaharmezrabs of Mahur and Segah (Zang-e Shotor) and rhythmic pieces from the third violin radif of Abolhasan Saba such as “Dar Qafas” and “Be Yad-e Gozashteh” have been performed many times with various orchestras. Saba composed several pieces on poems by Vahshi Bafqi (Dashti), Sheikh Bahai (Segah), Molavi “Kasi nist chonin asheq o bichareh ke mayim” (Homayun), and Iraqi (Chahargah) for the “Golha” program. A film named “Master Abolhasan Saba” was also produced by Ahmad Faroughi (one of the former colleagues of the country’s fine arts).</p>\n<p>One of the works remaining from Abolhasan Saba in private recordings (on reel tape) is Bayat-e Isfahan and Homayun with the singing of Seyyed Ali Mohammad Qazi Asgar, in which Saba played violin along with his singing. It was recorded in 1329 solar year (1950) at Reza Kasaei’s house.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>In Memory of Saba</h2>\n<p>After Saba’s passing, programs in his memory were organized by his loyal students such as Ali Tajvidi, Mehdi Khalaji, and Faramarz Payvar (mostly in Rudaki Hall), and works such as Segah Golha-ye Rangarang 174 with music by Mehdi Khalaji, poetry by Navab Safa, and singing by Banan were performed in his memory.</p>\n<p>A 33 rpm gramophone record named “Life and Works of Abolhasan Saba” with narration by Firoozeh Amir Mo’ez, including a brief biography and samples of Saba’s performances, was published by the Institute for the Intellectual Development of Children and Young Adults in 1352 solar year (1973). One issue of the Music Magazine (No. 18, Third Period, Bahman 1336 solar) was dedicated as a memorial and special issue to Abolhasan Saba.</p>\n<p>The council for holding the commemoration of the fortieth anniversary of the passing of Master Abolhasan Saba, which was held on 30 Dey 1376 solar (January 20, 1998) at Vahdat Hall (Tehran), assigned the examination of the specialized aspects of Abolhasan Saba’s music to Dr. Sasan Sepanta. On this occasion, a lecture was prepared and delivered at the mentioned ceremony, the text of which was published in the Art Quarterly, No. 34, Winter 1376 solar.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding this article and the biography, activities, and artistic works of Abolhasan Saba, please share it with us in the comments section of the article to improve the quality of this content.</p>', '2023-11-10 19:01:05', 0, '2024-03-22 20:37:25', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1525, 'posts', 68, 'en', NULL, 'The avaz form as the most important genre in Iranian music', NULL, 'This article explores the concept of “Avaz” (vocal form) in Persian traditional music and compares it with the “Tasnim” (composed song form). Avaz is the most important genre in Iranian classical music, characterized by being non-metric (free rhythm) and غالباً performed improvisationally. It typically involves a dynamic interaction between the singer and instrumentalist, resembling a musical dialogue. شعر انتخابی معمولاً از شاعران کلاسیک فارسی مانند Hafez و Saadi است.\n\nThe term “Avaz” has multiple meanings in Iranian music, including: musical voice, a genre, a sub-mode (dastgah), or a melodic section (gusheh). Its key feature is rhythmic freedom while maintaining an internal sense of balance and proportion.\n\nAvaz exists in two forms:\n\nVocal Avaz (with lyrics)\nInstrumental Avaz (without lyrics)\n\nPerforming Avaz requires deep knowledge of the radif (traditional repertoire), extensive experience, and strong improvisational skills. Historically, master singers preferred Avaz over Tasnim, as Tasnim relies more on pre-composed structures and gives less creative freedom to the singer.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article from the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>, we intend to get familiar with the <span style=\"color: #0000ff;\"><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Avaz form</span></strong></span> in Iranian music and examine the differences between the Avaz form and the Tasnif form. Stay with us in the following.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>What is the Avaz Form in Iranian Music?</h2>\n<p>In <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">traditional Iranian music</a>, there was a specific structure for concerts and musical performances. In this format and specific structure, the main or more professional singer and the prominent player of the group or various musicians take turns improvising (badahenavazi) and improvisational singing (badahekhani) in free meter (without rhythm), and at the end of these gatherings or between different vocal sections, usually another novice singer, known as the secondary singer, along with the player or group of musicians, would perform a Tasnif.</p>\n<p>In the Avaz form, usually one singer along with the main player improvises, and sometimes with a little practice before the performance, they choose a poem and start singing the Avaz and playing the instrument. The poems chosen in improvisational singing and Avaz performances are mostly from great poets such as Hafez, Saadi, Molavi (Rumi), and other prominent figures of Persian literature. Due to the very high richness of Persian literature and its unparalleled poets, there are many poems available for these improvisational Avaz performances, which gives the singer a wide choice in selecting the poem.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h2>The Usage of the Term \"Avaz\" in Iranian Music</h2>\n<p>The term “Avaz” in Iranian music has various and diverse meanings and applications:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>It is equivalent to the main concept of the word and means voice, and more specifically, a musical voice: “<strong>Hearing the sound of the drum from afar is pleasant</strong>”.</li>\n  <li>It is the name of the main “type” or genre in Iranian music. Unlike other types of music such as <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d9%be%db%8c%d8%b4-%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%a2%d9%85%d8%af/\">Pishdaramad</a>, Rang, Chaharmezrab, etc., Avaz is the only non-metric (non-rhythmic) type. The major part of modal (Dastgahi) and radifi music consists of Avaz, and that is why the singer, who traditionally performs this type more than others, is called an Avaz-khan or Avazeh-khan. Therefore, a player can also be an “Avaz” player because when responding (“Javab”) to the singer or when performing this type without a singer, they have done “Avaz-navazi”. In the Qajar period, distinguished singers only performed “Avaz” and had no desire to sing types such as “Tasnif”, “Zarbi”, or “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%da%a9%d8%a7%d8%b1-%d9%88-%d8%b9%d9%85%d9%84/\">Kar-e Amal</a>”, considering it beneath their dignity. For this reason and based on such a belief, to humiliate those who claimed to be singers, they would say, for example, “So-and-so is not an Avaz-khan, he is a Tasnif-khan!”</li>\n  <li>A usage synonymous with Dastgah. In this sense, Avaz means a small or secondary Dastgah, and this usage has a 40-50 year history; before that, the term “Naghmah” was used to refer to a “secondary Dastgah”.</li>\n  <li>The name of a specific gusheh in different parts of the radif, and in this form, it can be considered a synonym for “Daramad”.</li>\n  <li>A general term applied to all melodies that are non-metric, and in this older sense, it is synonymous with gusheh: such as Avaz-e Bakhtiari, Avaz-e Hajiani, Avaz-e Rak, etc.</li>\n</ol>\n<div></div>\n<h2>Characteristics of Avaz as the Most Important Genre in Iranian Music</h2>\n<div>\nThe majority of parts in the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%af%db%8c%d9%81/\">Iranian music radif</a> are dedicated to what is technically called “Avaz”. In instrumental radifs, elements such as <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%86%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d8%b6%d8%b1%d8%a7%d8%a8/\">Chaharmezrabs</a>, Rangs, etc., and in vocal radifs, parts such as Saghinameh, etc., are not considered “Avaz” in the sense of the genre. Avaz in this concept should be understood as the genre to which the “Avaz-khan” or traditional Iranian singer is most attached and committed, and spends most of their singing time on it.\nThe most important and primary feature of this genre is its non-metric nature. Therefore, to better introduce Avaz, especially for those who have no knowledge of Iranian music, the best example is to say that Quranic recitation (Qari) (Quran reading with melody) is in the “Avaz” genre because Quranic recitation has always been a non-metric vocal performance, and of course without instrumental accompaniment.\nIt is very important to note that the non-metric nature of Avaz does not necessarily mean it is without rhythm, because a non-metric Avaz can be performed with proper time ratios, silences, and well-placed tahrirs, in which case it is also rhythmic. Just like a speaker correctly pronouncing the durations of syllables when reading a prose text and having pauses and rises and falls appropriate to the nature of that prose. For example, the proper reading of such a text:\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px;\">What man has acquired during his evolution is a large brain and delicate, grasping hands; the very brain that has given him the power of thinking and speaking, and the very hands that have put his thoughts into practice; a large brain that, in terms of complexity and perfection, is a masterpiece that has enabled man to free himself from the burden of nature\'s compulsion to rule over his destiny and rise from the animal world to the human world.</p>\n&nbsp;\nAnd a non-metric Avaz can be performed without observing the necessary principles, in which case it is inappropriate and unrhythmic, like such a reading of the following prose:\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px;\">What maaaaan during his evoooooolution has acquired,,,,,,, a laaaarge brain and delicaaaate and grasping hands ... .</p>\n&nbsp;\nMohammad Reza Shafiei Kadkani has defined rhythm in this way:\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px;\">Perhaps the best definition is to say that rhythm is a kind of proportion. Proportion is a quality resulting from the perception of unity among multiple parts. If proportion occurs in space, it is called symmetry, and if it occurs in time, it is called rhythm.</p>\n</div>\n<div></div>\n<div>In this way, if we consider the “Avaz” genre as something like “prose”, the rest of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">musical genres</a> have the nature of “poetry” and are considered metric. Certainly, the antiquity of the “Avaz” genre in all nations and civilizations is as old as the first human action in producing music.</div>\n<div style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">“The first artistic action of man was certainly a musical action; even before he engaged in painting and sculpture; and even before he spoke. Man could, even before reaching the final stages of his evolution, produce the first musical sounds from his throat, however unpleasant and without principles, and strive to imitate the melodies and sounds of the nature around him, such as the song of birds, the howl of animals, the sound of dripping drops, etc.”</div>\n<div></div>\n<div>\nOf course, we are certain that the singing of primitive humans was a non-metric, wordless performance, probably something like the howl of a wolf, and we must add that the product of his work was most likely unrhythmic as well; quite the opposite of the nightingale’s warbling.\nThe “Avaz” genre in Iranian music has a dual application:\n</div>\n<div style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">۱. Vocal Avaz: a non-metric and word-based type whose performer is a singer, and its lyrics are usually a poem in the form of a ghazal.</div>\n<div>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px;\">۲. Instrumental Avaz: a non-metric and wordless type whose performer is the player.</p>\n</div>\n<div>\n&nbsp;\n<h3> Vocal and Instrumental Avaz</h3>\nIranian Avaz, one of whose main elements is “tahrir”, has been considered an imitation of the nightingale’s melodic singing and the bird’s tahrirs, which is famous as “Hazār Dastān”:\n</div>\n<div style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\"><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b6%d8%a7-%d9%84%d8%b7%d9%81%db%8c/\">Mohammad Reza Lotfi</a>: The old singers tried to give more possibility to tahrir, and for this reason, they took the nightingale as their model and learned the variety of tahrir from this melodious bird.</div>\n<div></div>\n<div>To perform a complete Avaz, the singer first needs to choose a Dastgah or Avaz, and also choose a suitable ghazal to sing in that <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%88-%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2/\">Dastgah and Avaz</a>. Then, it is necessary to separate the lines after the matla’ of the ghazal, and in appropriate cases, rearrange them, and according to the concepts of the poems and the way the lines sit in the maqams of the Dastgah, decide which line or lines to sing in which maqam and gusheh of the Dastgah.</div>\n<div>It is also possible that in between the lines of the ghazal and in line with the course of the Dastgah, forms such as rubai, dobayti, chaharpareh, or any other type of poem are used to sing in one of the gushehs related to the maqam, and then resume singing the rest of the ghazal lines in the subsequent maqams of the Dastgah. In the middle of the lines, or wherever appropriate, the player responds with a suitable “Javab-e Avaz”, and in fact performs an “Avaz-navazi” based on themes heard from the singer.</div>\n<div></div>\n<div></div>\n<div>\nIranian music has always been based on vocal singing and singer-centricity, and the player, especially in the Qajar period, until instrumental music and group playing seriously took shape and became common, always and absolutely followed the singer. The players of this period, even those who played plucked instruments, considered no duty for themselves other than absolute adherence to the singer’s intonation. One of the signs of this in the music of that period is the highly detailed (por-riz) Tar playing style of the old players.\nThese rizzes, which were performed continuously in the pauses between sentences, and also on the last notes of the sentence, were an exact imitation of the singers’ pauses on sounds and extended durations in Avaz singing. Alec Robertson, about Indian music which has many similarities with Iranian music, has written:\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px;\">In India, in the past and even today, music relies entirely and absolutely on singing and the human voice. This is so true that even when an instrument is played without the [human] voice, it is still the human voice that the instrument must express.</p>\n&nbsp;\nWhile, according to Robertson, Iranian musicians from the tenth century AD onwards, that is, about a thousand years ago, tried to make instrumental music common as well:\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px;\">The prominent and main feature of the music of this world in this period was solo singing accompanied by the oud. In fact, until the tenth century AD, a higher and more important position was given to singing and vocal performance than to instrumental music [...]</p>\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px;\">As we have seen before, vocal music has been of primary importance from the earliest periods of the life of the peoples of the East, but from the early tenth century AD, the taste and nature of Iranians, Turkmens, Mongols, and Turks made instrumental music common. The main form of music in this period was a kind of vocal suite called Nowbat or Towali, which consisted of several consecutive sections, and before the beginning of each section, an instrumental introduction called Tariq came, thus giving the players the opportunity to play in turn.</p>\n&nbsp;\nTherefore, it can be said that the path that had been opened in the distant past for the evolution and independence of instrumental music was blocked again in recent centuries. In any case, today’s instrumental music has regained much of its independence, but our discussion for now is about the “Avaz-navazi” of the players. A player, during Avaz-navazi, uses two vocal elements: madd (extended and sustained sounds) and tahrir.\nHe chooses a poem in his mind and, following its rhythm, arranges movements that, if not absolutely, at least to a large extent evoke the Avaz singing of a singer. If we pay attention to the performances of, for example, Tar players of the Qajar period and the early Pahlavi period, we will understand this point well. From the 1300s solar calendar onwards, with the transformation that occurred in the growth of instrumental music, gradually the players, especially radio players, created and followed a moderate style that had distanced itself to a large extent from absolute and detailed adherence to Avaz.</p>\n</div>\n<div></div>\n<div>\n<h3>Improvised and Non-Improvised Avaz</h3>\nAvaz, in both its vocal and instrumental types, can also be performed in a non-improvised manner based on what has been composed in advance. In fact, when an Avaz-khan sings the non-metric parts of the radif of the old masters, he has performed a “non-improvised vocal Avaz”, and similarly, when a player performs the non-metric parts of the radif of the old masters, he has presented a “non-improvised instrumental Avaz”. Based on what has been said, we can define the Avaz genre as follows:\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px;\">“A non-metric genre, vocal and instrumental, improvised or non-improvised, which in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a8%d8%b1%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%85%d9%87-%d8%a7%db%8c/\">programmatic Iranian music</a> is performed after metric genres (except Tasnif and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%90%d9%86%da%af/\">Rang</a>).”</p>\n&nbsp;\n</div>\n<h3>What is Needed for Singing Avaz and Improvisational Singing?</h3>\n<p>As mentioned earlier, in the Avaz form of classical Iranian music, there are two very important sections: the singer’s improvisational singing and the player’s improvisational playing.</p>\n<p>For a singer to have good improvisational singing, he must have worked hard from several aspects. Below, we point out a few important points:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The singer must first of all be familiar with the vocal radif of Iranian music and the specific tahrirs of each gusheh of the radif, and in short, it can be said that he must be a radif-dan.</li>\n  <li>For improvisational singing, he must have many years of experience in singing different gushehs of the radif.</li>\n  <li>He must be familiar with choosing a suitable poem for the atmosphere of the musical gathering and the correct and proper way of expressing the poem, which is technically also called “monaseb-khani”.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The player also needs several important skills for improvisational playing, which we will mention below:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Complete understanding of the Iranian music radif and the different gushehs of each Dastgah and Avaz from the radif, so that he can give a suitable response to the singer according to the gusheh in which the singer is singing the poem.</li>\n  <li>For improvisational playing, he must also have many years of experience in performing different gushehs of the radif.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>As you have realized so far, the Avaz form is like a question and answer between a skilled singer and a skilled player, and for this purpose, both must have gone through many years of effort and experience.</p>\n&nbsp;\n<h3>Why Did Great Singers in the Old Days Sing Avaz and Avoid Tasnif Singing?</h3>\n<p>According to the explanations given about the Avaz form, it can be understood that Avaz singing and responding to Avaz required special experience and expertise, which indicated the technical quality of the singer and player. However, in performing a <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%aa%d8%b5%d9%86%db%8c%d9%81/\">Tasnif</a>, usually the singer follows the composer and the player’s choice of poem and must perform according to the melody that the player or composer has created. This means that in Tasnif singing, the role of the player and composer is much more prominent than that of the singer. For this reason, great singers such as Taj Isfahani, Ali Akbar Golpayegani, Iraj Khajeh Amiri, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b6%d8%a7-%d8%b4%d8%ac%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Mohammad Reza Shajarian</a>, and other great Avaz masters, especially in earlier periods, avoided Tasnif singing and considered it beneath their dignity.</p>\n<p>In fact, the Avaz form is similar to a contest of strength between a powerful and dominant singer and a very strong player. In this, the player does not have the prominent role, and if we do not say that the singer’s role is more prominent than the player’s, we must say that in this form, the singer and the player have equal roles. One of the important reasons for great singers not singing Tasnif is these equal roles with the player in performance, or even a more prominent role than the player.</p>\n<p>Regarding the way of thinking that existed towards Avaz and Tasnif forms, we can refer to this interesting story from Master Mohammad Reza Shajarian’s presence in the radio and his singing test in front of the greats of radio music. When Mohammad Reza Shajarian, as a novice, performs Avaz in the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%aa%d8%b1%da%a9/\">Bayat-e Tork</a> mode, he faces the encouragement of the jury panel, including Rokn al-Din Khan Mokhtari, Homayoun Khorram, Moshir Homayoun Shahrdar, and Ali Tajvidi.</p>\n<p>Here, after Shajarian’s Avaz singing, Ali Tajvidi asks him: “Do you also sing Tasnif?”</p>\n<p>Shajarian replies: “No, I only sing Avaz.”</p>\n<p>Later, Shajarian says in an interview: “At that time, it was customary for good singers to only sing Avaz and not sing Tasnif, and I also believed that a singer should only be a good Avazeh-khan, and I considered singing Tasnif beneath my dignity.”</p>\n<p>In performing Tasnif, because the poem and music were pre-prepared and some rehearsals were done before the performance for coordination, and they did not have an improvisational state, it was enough for the singer to have mastery only over the gushehs that should be sung in that Tasnif, and there was no need to necessarily know the entire Iranian music radif or even know one complete Dastgah. For this reason, usually novice singers who are at the beginning of their training period and do not have enough experience would sing Tasnif.</p>\n<p>In the old days, female singers generally did not have mastery over the radif, and for this reason, they only sang Tasnif. The first female singer who sang Avaz was the eternal lady of Iranian music, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%82%d9%85%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%85%d9%84%d9%88%da%a9-%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Qamar al-Moluk Vaziri</a>, who was trained under Morteza Khan Ney-Davud and became familiar with the Iranian music radif and monaseb-khani.</p>\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n<p>If you have any opinion or question regarding the Avaz form in Iranian music, please share it with us in the comments section of this educational article to improve the quality of the content.</p>', '2023-11-13 21:41:37', 0, '2025-02-14 00:53:45', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1526, 'posts', 69, 'en', NULL, 'The concept of subject or theme in music', NULL, 'This article introduces the concept of “Theme” or “Subject” in music. A theme refers to the main idea or central concept of an artistic work, which can also be found in other arts such as poetry and painting.\n\nIn music, a theme is a musical idea that recurs throughout a composition, playing a key role in creating unity and coherence. It is especially important in forms such as sonata, fugue, and theme and variations.\n\nAlthough the terms “theme” and “idea” are often used interchangeably, an “idea” may sometimes imply a more complex structure. In sonata form, there are typically two themes, while a fugue generally features one main subject.\n\nOverall, the theme serves as the core element that shapes and guides the musical structure of a piece.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article from <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>, we become familiar with the concept of subject or theme in music. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>Theme in Music (Theme) or Subject (Subject)</h2>\n\n<p>Theme or subject refers to the topic or topics discussed in an artistic work. The subject discussed in a poem and sometimes in painting, and possibly in carpet design or even in the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">art of music</a>, is sometimes called an “idea.”</p>\n\n<p>It seems that the difference between the terms “idea” and “theme” lies in the fact that in an “idea,” the elements are combined with greater mathematical complexity. However, this definition is by no means complete or precise, and in the analysis of musical pieces, these two terms are frequently used interchangeably.</p>\n\n<p>A theme is an idea that serves as a point of return in the musical expression of a piece, especially a piece in sonata form (such as a symphony, quartet, concerto, sonata piece, etc.), or in a piece in fugue form, or in a piece in “theme and variations” form.</p>\n\n<p>In pieces in sonata form and in fugue form, the theme is also called the “subject.” Sonatas from the eighteenth century to the present have usually had two subjects (or two groups of thematic material), while a fugue (except for special and double fugues) usually has one subject. Since the “theme” is the subject discussed in the artistic expression of a work, it can be understood that this element also plays a role in giving unity to the artistic work.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the concept of subject or theme in music, please share them in the comments section of this article with Sornaz Music School to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-11-23 18:40:07', 0, '2025-02-14 00:53:21', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1527, 'posts', 70, 'en', NULL, 'Tempo or speed of performing musical pieces', NULL, 'This article explains the concept of “Tempo” in music. Tempo refers to the speed at which a musical piece is performed, ranging from very slow to very fast. Common tempo markings include Largo, Adagio, Andante, Moderato, Allegro, Presto, and Prestissimo.\n\nIn addition to these terms, composers often use metronome markings to indicate precise tempo (e.g., quarter note = 100). Speeds between 60 and 80 BPM are generally considered natural and moderate, similar to walking pace or a human heartbeat.\n\nTempo plays a crucial role in the expression and interpretation of music. Choosing the correct tempo significantly affects how a piece is perceived. As a result, musicians, conductors, and critics often debate the appropriate tempo for a composition. Factors such as concert hall acoustics, instrumental tone colors, and performance conditions also influence tempo decisions.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Tempo</span></strong> means the speed of performing a musical piece. Continue with us to learn more about the concept of tempo in music...</p>\n\n<h2>Tempo</h2>\n\n<p>The simple meaning of the word tempo is the speed at which a musical piece or a section of it is performed. This speed ranges from extremely slow to extremely fast. The tempo markings in this speed spectrum are:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Largo</li>\n  <li>Adagio</li>\n  <li>Andante</li>\n  <li>Moderato</li>\n  <li>Allegro</li>\n  <li>Presto</li>\n  <li>Prestissimo</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>If desired, composers can determine the speed of their work more precisely using markings known as “metronome markings.” For example, the marking “♩ = 100” means that each quarter note (crotchet) should last 1/100 of a minute. The common metronome markings actually used in the performance of most musical pieces range from 50 to 120.</p>\n\n<p>Speeds between 60 and 80 represent normal speeds — neither fast nor slow — that correspond to many natural human pulses, such as walking pace or heartbeat.</p>\n\n<p>Performance speed is one of the factors that plays a special and important role in the “expression” of music. Therefore, research into the correct and proper tempo of a particular piece has almost always been one of the notable topics of discussion among <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d8%b2%d9%86%d8%af%da%af%db%8c%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%85%d9%87-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%86/\">musicians</a> (performers, conductors, and composers) and listeners (especially critics). In these discussions, attention is paid to details of external factors such as the size and acoustics (reverberation) of the concert hall, the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%88%db%8c%da%98%da%af%db%8c-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d8%b5%d9%88%d8%aa/\">tone colors</a> of the instruments, the size of the orchestra, and the time and context in which the piece was composed.</p>\n\n<p>These detailed interpretations are, of course, in addition to the differences of opinion that usually arise among performers regarding different interpretations of a single work. They accuse each other of not understanding the meaning of the piece, taking the tempo too fast or too slow, and so on — accusations that are by no means less common than those concerning “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ac%d9%85%d9%84%d9%87/\">phrasing</a>” or “orchestral balance.”</p>\n\n<p>In any case, in a general view, it can be said that today’s conductors, when performing works of the great classical composers such as <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%88%D9%84%D9%81%DA%AF%D8%A7%D9%86%DA%AF_%D8%A2%D9%85%D8%A7%D8%AF%D8%A6%D9%88%D8%B3_%D9%85%D9%88%D8%AA%D8%B3%D8%A7%D8%B1%D8%AA#:~:text=%D9%88%D9%84%D9%81%DA%AF%D8%A7%D9%86%DA%AF%20%D8%A2%D9%85%D8%A7%D8%AF%D8%A6%D9%88%D8%B3%20%D9%85%D9%88%D8%AA%D8%B3%D8%A7%D8%B1%D8%AA%20(%D8%B2%D8%A7%D8%AF%D9%87%D9%94%20%DB%B2%DB%B7,%D9%85%D9%88%D8%B3%DB%8C%D9%82%DB%8C%20%D8%A8%D8%B1%D8%A7%DB%8C%20%DA%AF%D8%B1%D9%88%D9%87%20%DA%A9%D9%8F%D8%B1%20%D8%B3%D8%A7%D8%AE%D8%AA.\">Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart</a>, Beethoven, and especially Johann Sebastian Bach, often perform slow movements more slowly and fast movements more quickly.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding tempo in music, please share them in the comments section of the article with us to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-11-23 18:49:43', 0, '2025-02-14 00:53:07', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1528, 'posts', 71, 'en', NULL, 'The concept of form in music', NULL, 'This article discusses the concept of “Form” in music, which is a fundamental element in distinguishing and understanding musical works. Form refers to the structure or overall shape of an artistic piece, within which its content is organized.\n\nIn any artwork, elements are arranged in a purposeful and structured way, and three key components shape the form:\n\nRepetition (creates cohesion and familiarity)\nVariety (adds contrast and interest)\nUnity (maintains overall harmony)\n\nIn music, form is not just an external framework; it is deeply integrated with the music itself. Elements such as rhythm, pitch relationships, and musical texture all contribute to shaping the form.\n\nAdditionally, “musical forms” refer to the structural patterns of compositions, similar to architectural blueprints that define how a piece is constructed.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Form in music</span></strong> is a very important concept that distinguishes between different musical pieces and works. To become familiar with this concept, please continue with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>...</p>\n\n<h2>Form in Music (Form)</h2>\n\n<p>Every artistic work has a design, framework, or pattern in which the artist places the “content” of their work. In fact, form refers to the shape and external appearance of a work, as opposed to content, which is the material arranged inside that shape with a special order and sequence.</p>\n\n<p>Every artistic work is a collection of small pieces, each of which has a special relationship with the others and with the work as a whole.</p>\n\n<p>In examining the external shape of an artistic work, three elements can be identified (to simplify the subject, let us consider a decorative vase):</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Element of repetition: The contour lines of a vase are symmetrical, similar, homogeneous, or complementary to one another. Even in an asymmetrical or semi-symmetrical vase, these lines have a homogeneous or complementary aspect relative to each other.</li>\n  <li>Element of variety: The contour lines traverse more or less diverse paths across their length; although in most cases they are symmetrical relative to the central axis, the curve of the rim or base of the vase forms a vertical or non-vertical angle with the contour lines.</li>\n  <li>Element of unity: The varieties are not so scattered that they create disharmony in the overall appearance of the vase. Moreover, every part of the vase’s shape — for example, its handle — is proportionate to the overall form, such that if we were to replace the handle of this vase with that of another, we would most likely damage the form of both vases.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>When we speak of form in music, depending on whether we mean the “discussion of form in music” or “musical forms,” slightly different meanings emerge. In discussing “form,” it should be said that <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">music</a> is not merely a pile of sounds stacked on top of one another, but rather consists of organized and purposeful elements that together have precise, delicate, and sometimes hidden relationships and pursue a special goal with one another’s help. In other words, each element plays a part in bringing the music closer to the composer’s intention.</p>\n\n<p>In this sense, form is so intertwined and inseparable from music itself that one truly cannot recognize one without the presence of the other. Even in a simple <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ac%d9%85%d9%84%d9%87/\">musical sentence</a>, the elements of rhythm, intervals, relationships of pitch, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a8%d8%a7%d9%81%d8%aa-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">musical texture</a>, and, in a word, form in music, are all perceptible together.</p>\n\n<p>Musical forms, like poetic forms (such as dobayti, ghazal, chaharpareh, qasideh, masnavi, and others), are in fact the “structural shapes” of an entire musical piece. More precisely, every musical form is the structural blueprint of a musical composition, not unlike the plan of a building.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the concept of form in music, please share them in the comments section of this article with us to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-11-23 18:59:56', 0, '2025-02-14 00:52:53', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1529, 'posts', 72, 'en', NULL, 'Schools of Iranian music | Examination of the Isfahan school and Tehran school as the two main schools of the radif of Iranian music', NULL, 'This article examines the schools of Iranian music, focusing on the two major traditions of Isfahan and Tehran. Although both schools share the same modal systems (dastgahs), they differ in naming conventions, performance styles, and musical interpretation.\n\nThe Isfahan school, influenced by the historical period of the Safavid dynasty, places a stronger emphasis on vocal music and singing, preserving a richer and more authentic vocal tradition. In contrast, the Tehran school developed under the influence of the Qajar court and the migration of musicians to the capital.\n\nHistorical events and the movement of influential musicians, such as Aqa Ali Akbar Farahani, played a key role in shaping these differences.\n\nKey distinctions between the two schools include:\n\nDifferences in the naming and function of melodic units (gushehs)\nGreater emphasis on vocal elements in the Isfahan school\nVariations in the inclusion of composed songs (tasnif) within the repertoire\nDifferences in structural elements like masnavi and saqi-nameh sections\n\nOverall, these schools reflect the diversity and richness of Iranian classical music, shaped by historical, cultural, and regional influences.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">schools of Iranian music</span></strong> and study the schools of Isfahan and Tehran as two of the most important schools of radif music. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>Schools of Iranian Music</h2>\n\n<p>The main and most important schools of today’s Iranian music were formed primarily in the cities of Isfahan and Tehran. The names of the dastgahs are the same in these two schools, but the names of many gushehs and some maqams differ in the Isfahan school compared to the Tehran school. Moreover, in the radifs of the Isfahan school, which are generally vocal, the authenticity and richness of “avaz” and “avaz-khani” (vocal performance) are greater than in the radifs of the Tehran school.</p>\n\n<p>The main center of the Safavid government — the last dynasty that revived power, wealth, vastness, and unity in Iran — was the city of Isfahan. Naturally, the existence of security, prosperity, and wealth in any society leads to the flourishing of the art of music and other fields.</p>\n\n<p>On this basis, and considering the historical centrality of this part of Iran, as well as the rule of the <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%AF%D9%88%D8%AF%D9%85%D8%A7%D9%86_%D8%B5%D9%81%D9%88%DB%8C\">Safavids</a> and the short years after it — from 907 AH (880 SH) to 1193 AH (1157 SH), a total of about 280 years — it can be said that the main traces of the music of this period of Iran should be sought in the cities of Isfahan and Shiraz and their surrounding regions. A quick look at the names of maqams and gushehs attributed to the central and southern regions of Iran confirms this point:</p>\n\n<p>Shur, Dashti, Bayat-e Isfahan, Esfahank, Shushtari, Dezfuli, Bakhtiari, Bayat-e Shiraz, Neyriz, Gerili, Dashtestani, Esfahank, Ahvazi, Barazjani, Boyerahmadi, Behbahani, Bidgani, Bidgoli, Tangestani, Joushaqani, Jonaqani, Jahromi, Khorramabadi, Dareh Gozi, Deylami, Zabol, Zarqani, Samali, Kohgiluyeh, Loristani, Marvdashti, Meygoli, and others.</p>\n\n<p>Furthermore, many renowned religious singers and instrumentalists after whom some gushehs of the radif are named were from Isfahan and Shiraz. Among them one can mention the gushehs of Shahabi, Sadri, Haj Hosseini, Nayeb Asadollahi, Mohammad Sadeq Khani, Sorour al-Molki, the Daramad of Banafsheh (attributed to Mirza Lotfollah Esfahani), and others.</p>\n\n<p>Political and historical events, in addition to their general impact on social life, have been highly influential in the way our music has continued to exist. On this basis, for example, if Aqa Mohammad Khan Qajar had not established his government in Tehran, the radif music of today would naturally have had a different character and would have been the same as what is currently established and practiced in the Isfahan school.</p>\n\n<p>Also, relying on this point, let us not forget the role of figures such as Amir Kabir and Qa’em Maqam, who supported instrumentalists like <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d8%a7%da%a9%d8%a8%d8%b1-%d9%81%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Aqa Ali Akbar Farahani</a> and brought them from their common homeland to the court in Tehran. Certainly, the radif and musical interpretation of such instrumentalists differed from those of the musicians of Isfahan and Shiraz. Differences in the way musicians from various regions perceive musical materials and melodies (two-fold and multi-fold readings of melodies) have existed in all periods of music and are a natural matter:</p>\n\n<p>“And these combinations and some of the cycles may, according to differences in dialects, be called by another name in other cities… just as Dogah is called Sepehri in Khorasan” (Three Persian Treatises on Music: 106).</p>\n\n<p>“Know that there are no more than twenty-four shobehs, but some are sung in two forms… and Zabol is also called Dam Aray, Del Fereb, and Sepehri” (Resaleh-e Keramiyyeh).</p>\n\n<p>It is worth mentioning here some of the differences between the schools of Iranian music in the radifs of Isfahan and Tehran:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Fili in the Isfahan school radif (Kasai’s radif) is also called Panjgah, which is a fitting name because the emphasis of Fili in the dastgah of Mahur is on the “fifth” degree of the Mahur maqam.</li>\n  <li>Hesar of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%85%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%88%d8%b1/\">dastgah of Mahur</a> in the Isfahan school radif (Kasai’s radif) is also called “Pas Mahur,” which is a meaningful name because the hesar of this dastgah creates the Mahur maqam in another mode, and the title “Pas Mahur” can imply “the next Mahur” or “another Mahur after Mahur.”</li>\n  <li>Mollanazi in the Isfahan school (Mansouri’s radif) refers to a tasnif with lyrics in a specific meter that is a wandering gusheh performed in most frameworks. In contrast, Mollanazi in the Tehran school is a gusheh in the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b4%d9%88%d8%b1/\">dastgah of Shur</a>.</li>\n  <li>Tusi in the Isfahan school (Mansouri’s radif) refers to a tasnif with lyrics in a specific meter that is a wandering gusheh performed in most frameworks. In contrast, Tusi in the Tehran school is a gusheh in the dastgah of Mahur.</li>\n  <li>Tarz in the Isfahan school (Mansouri’s radif) refers to a wandering gusheh accompanied by poetry. In contrast, the same Tarz in the instrumental radifs of the Tehran school is called Pish-e Zanguleh.</li>\n  <li>Most dastgahs and avazes in the Isfahan school radif (Mansouri’s radif), following what was customary in the past, include masnavi and saqi-nameh. In contrast, saqi-nameh in the Tehran school (Davami’s radif) is only narrated in the dastgah of Mahur. Forsat Shirazi wrote in Bahur al-Alhan: “It should be known that according to the ancients, every avaz has one masnavi and one saqi-nameh” (Bahur al-Alhan: 32).</li>\n  <li>In the Isfahan school radif (Mansouri’s radif), tasnifs are also considered part of the gushehs of the dastgah. In contrast, in the radifs of the Tehran school (Davami’s radif), except for gushehs such as Gerili of the dastgah of Shur and Mehrabani of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%aa%d8%b1%da%a9/\">avaz of Bayat-e Tork</a>, usually no tasnif is seen within the body of the dastgahs. Mehdi Qoli Hedayat wrote in Majma‘ al-Advār: “In complete tasnifs, one deviates from the main framework and then returns to it, and such tasnifs are called kār o amal, and some of them that have remained from the ancients have become an inseparable part of the dastgah” (Majma‘ al-Advār, Third Turn: 119).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the schools of Iranian music, please share them with us in the comments section of the article to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-12-10 20:45:37', 0, '2025-02-14 00:52:39', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1530, 'posts', 73, 'en', NULL, 'The form of kar-o amal or kar-e amal in Iranian music', NULL, 'The “Kar-o-Amal” form in Iranian music is a developed and more complete type of tasnif (composed song). Unlike regular tasnifs that usually remain within one or two modes, Kar-o-Amal moves through multiple modes and melodic sections within a musical system (dastgah), making it structurally richer and more complex.\n\nHistorically, especially during the Qajar period, it was considered a “complete composition” because it could encompass various melodic parts and return to the original theme. Some of these compositions eventually became integral parts of the musical repertoire.\n\nAlthough today it is sometimes interpreted as a kind of improvised tasnif, its original meaning refers to a carefully structured and comprehensive composition. Overall, Kar-o-Amal can be seen as a more elaborate and complete form compared to the standard tasnif in Iranian classical music.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the form of <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">kār o amal</span></strong> (work and practice) in Iranian music and explore its differences from the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%aa%d8%b5%d9%86%db%8c%d9%81/\">form of tasnif</a>. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>The Form of Kār o Amal in Iranian Music</h2>\n\n<p>In this section, we become familiar with the form of kār o amal in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a> and examine what characteristics this form or genre has within the dastgah system of Iranian music, and we come to understand its difference from tasnif.</p>\n\n<p>“A type of tasnif — a complete tasnif. In the Qajar period, tasnif was a metered form with lyrics that moved within the framework of one or two maqams, whereas kār o amal was a tasnif that circulated through more maqams and gushehs. Today, kār o amal is regarded as a kind of improvised tasnif singing, but its original meaning has been expressed identically by Abdollah Mostowfi and Mehdi Qoli Hedayat, both of whom were distinguished scholars of their time.</p>\n\n<p>Mostowfi, in response to a musician, said: ‘I said that since you are a musician, of course you know that every tasnif belongs to one avaz, but some tasnifs that are the work of master musicians are arranged in such a way that they not only fit one avaz but also contain and include all or most of the gushehs of that avaz. The ancients called this ‘complete and perfect’ tasnif kār o amal. They also had and used the same kār o amal and non-kār o amal in rengs’ (Mostowfi, Vol. 1: 297).</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%85%D9%87%D8%AF%DB%8C%E2%80%8C%D9%82%D9%84%DB%8C_%D9%87%D8%AF%D8%A7%DB%8C%D8%AA\">Mehdi Qoli Hedayat</a> (Mokhber al-Saltaneh) also expressed the same meaning, and the noteworthy point in his statement is the use of the word ‘complete,’ which appears in Mostowfi’s text as ‘kāmel al-‘eyār’ and can indicate the difference between kār o amal and ordinary tasnif. A complete and detailed tasnif: ‘In complete tasnifs, one deviates from the main framework and then returns to it, and such tasnifs are called kār o amal, and some of them that have remained from the ancients have become an inseparable part of the dastgah’ (Majma‘ al-Advār, Third Turn: 119).</p>\n\n<p>According to Mostowfi’s definition, the gusheh of Gerili in the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b4%d9%88%d8%b1/\">dastgah of Shur</a>, which is originally a kind of tasnif and circulates through many frameworks, can be called a kār o amal tasnif, or the reng of Shahr Ashub in the same dastgah should be considered a kār o amal reng” (Book of Gusheh, entry on kār o amal).</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the form of kār o amal in Iranian music, please share them with us in the comments section of the article to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-12-10 21:05:55', 0, '2025-02-14 00:52:19', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1531, 'posts', 74, 'en', NULL, 'Gushehs of Dastgah Shur | Examination of Shur gushehs in different radifs', NULL, 'This article examines the melodic sections (gushehs) of the Dastgah Shur across different radif traditions of Iranian classical music. The number, order, and naming of these gushehs vary depending on the master and version of the radif (such as those by Mirza Abdollah, Mirza Hossein-Qoli, Ali Akbar Shahnazi, and Abolhassan Saba), but their core melodic identity remains consistent.\n\nCommon gushehs include Daramad, Keresmeh, Salmak, Golriz, Shahnaz, Qara-cheh, Razavi, and Hosseini. Among them, “Keresmeh” is especially important as a rhythmic gusheh used across multiple dastgahs.\n\nThe article also analyzes the melodic structure of these gushehs, highlighting their pitch ranges, central tones, and characteristic patterns. For example, Daramad introduces the mode, Owj expands the range, and gushehs like Razavi and Qara-cheh contribute to melodic development and variation.\n\nOverall, Dastgah Shur is one of the most important and extensive systems in Iranian music, playing a fundamental role in both education and performance due to its rich variety of gushehs.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">gushehs of the dastgah of Shur</span></strong> and review the gushehs of Shur in various narrations of the radifs of Iranian music. Please continue with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>...</p>\n\n<h2>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Shur in the Radif</h2>\n\n<p>In the various narrations of the radif according to different masters, the number of gushehs of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b4%d9%88%d8%b1/\">dastgah of Shur</a> differs slightly. In the following, we examine these gushehs in several radifs:</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Shur in Mirza Abdollah’s Radif according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Shur in the different notations of Mirza Abdollah’s radif vary slightly. Below we present two versions:</p>\n\n<h4>Jean During’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>In Mirza Abdollah’s radif notated by <a href=\"https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%DA%98%D8%A7%D9%86_%D8%AF%D9%88%D8%B1%DB%8C%D9%86%DA%AF\">Jean During</a>, the gushehs of the dastgah of Shur are mentioned in the following order:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Second Daramad – Third Daramad (Kereshmeh) – Fourth Daramad (Gusheh of Rahab) – Fifth Daramad (Ouj) – Sixth Daramad (Mollanazi) – First Naghmeh – Second Naghmeh – Zirkesh Salmak – Golriz – Majles Afrouz – Ozzal – Safa – Bozorg – Kochak – Dobeyti – Khara – Qajar, Forud – Hazineh – Shur-e Pa’in Dasteh – Gusheh of Rahab – Chahar Gusheh – Moghaddameh Gerili – Razavi, Forud – Hazineh – Shahnaz – Qaracheh – Shahnaz-e Kot (Ashegh Kosh) – Reng-e Osul – Gerili – Reng-e Shahr Ashub</p>\n\n<h4>Dariush Talaei’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Shur in Dariush Talaei’s book “Tahlil-e Radif,” based on Mirza Abdollah’s radif, include the following:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Panjeh She’ri – Kereshmeh – Rahab – Ouj – Mollanazi – First Naghmeh – Second Naghmeh – Zirkesh Salmak – Salmak – Golriz – Majles Afrouz – Ozzal – Safa – Bozorg – Kochak – Dobeyti – Khara – Qajar – Hazineh – Shur-e Pa’in Dasteh – Rahab – Chahargusheh – Moghaddameh Gerili – Razavi – Kereshmeh Razavi – Third Part of Razavi – Qajar – Qaracheh – Hosseini – Forud – Qajar – Shahnaz</p>\n\n<h4>Gushehs Mentioned in the Book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi</h4>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Shur mentioned in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi, from the radif attributed to “Mirza Abdollah” according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Second Daramad – Kereshmeh – Gusheh of Rahab – Ouj – Mollanazi – First Naghmeh – Second Naghmeh – Zirkesh Salmak – Mollanazi – Salmak – Golriz – Majles Afrouz – Ozzal – Safa – Bozorg – Kochak – Dobeyti – Khara – Qajar – Forud – Hazineh – Shur-e Pa’in Dasteh – Gusheh of Rahab – Chahar Gusheh – Moghaddameh Gerili – Razavi – Forud – Hazineh – Shahnaz – Qaracheh – Shahnaz-e Kot or Ashegh Kosh – Reng-e Osul – Gerili – Reng-e Shahr Ashub</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Shur in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Shur mentioned in the book “Radif of Mirza Hossein-Qoli according to Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Pirniakan are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Second Daramad – Third Daramad or Panjeh She’ri – Kereshmeh – Ouj – First Naghmeh – First Zirkesh Salmak – Second Zirkesh Salmak – Golriz – Neshīb o Faraz – Ouj o Haziz – Moghaddameh Safa – Safa – Bozorg – Kochak – Dobeyti – Qajar – Second Naghmeh – Ozzal (Shur-e Pa’in Dasteh) – Chaharmezrab – Rest of Ozzal – Panjeh Kordi or Avaz – Panjeh She’ri – Kereshmeh-e Pa’in Dasteh – Ouj in Pa’in Dasteh – Chahar Gusheh – Moghaddameh Gerili – Second Naghmeh in Pa’in Dasteh – Razavi – Kereshmeh Razavi – Third Part of Razavi – Qajar – Qaracheh – Hosseini – Forud – Qajar – Shahnaz</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Shur in Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi’s Advanced Course Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Shur mentioned in the book “Advanced Course Radif of Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Talaei are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad (Kereshmeh) – Chaharmezrab – First Daramad – Second Daramad – Avaz (Naghmeh) – Kereshmeh – Gusheh-e Tork – Chaharmezrab of Gusheh-e Tork – Tahrir Forud – Hazineh and Forud – Ozzal and Kereshmeh (Shur-e Pa’in Dasteh) – Avaz – Forud-Avar (Kereshmeh) – Heshtari and Forud to Shur – Ozzal – Naghmeh Do Zarbi – Shahnaz and Kereshmeh – Kereshmeh Razavi – Qaracheh – Kereshmeh Ouj, Chaharmezrab and Forud – Gerili – Forud to Shur</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Shur in Musa Khan Ma’rufi’s Radif of Seven Dastgahs</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Shur mentioned in the book “Radif of Seven Dastgahs of Iranian Music” by Musa Khan Ma’rufi, edited by Mehdi Barkeshli, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Moghaddameh – First Daramad – Second Daramad – Third Daramad – Fourth Daramad – Kereshmeh – Gusheh of Rahab – Kereshmeh – Fifth Daramad – Kereshmeh – Avaz – Chaharmezrab – Naghmeh – Hazineh – Zirkesh Salmak – Zirkesh Salmak, Another Part – Zirkesh Salmak, Another Type – Salmak – Salmak, Another Part – Mollanazi – Mollanazi, Another Part – Moghaddameh Golriz – Mohammad Sadeq Khani – Golriz – Safa – Chaharmezrab – Gusheh of Abu Ata – Moghaddameh Bozorg – Majles Afrouz – Moghaddameh Bozorg – Bozorg – Dobeyti – Khara – Qajar – Hazineh – Mollanazi – Panjeh Kordi – Ozzal – Chaharmezrab</p>\n\n<p>Beginning of the Upper Shur Section – First Daramad – Second Daramad – Third Daramad – Avaz – Gusheh Gerili – Razavi – Qajar – Shahnaz – Qaracheh – Qaracheh, Another Type – Qaracheh, Another Part – Ashegh Kosh or Oghdeh Gosha – Hosseini – Forud – Masnavi – Bayat Kord, First Part – Bayat Kord, Second Part – Bayat Kord, Third Part – Bayat Kord, Fourth Part – Rah-e Ruh – Majles Afrouz – Bayat Kord, Fifth Part – Chaharmezrab – Forud to Shur – Gerili – Gerili Shasti – Gerili Shasti, Another Part – Heshtari – Zarb-e Osul – Shahr Ashub</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Shur in Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Shur mentioned in the book “Javab-e Avaz based on Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif” by Arshad Tahmasbi are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Kereshmeh – Daramad Khara – Rahavi – Ouj – Shahnaz – Qaracheh – Razavi – Razavi with Javad Khani Tahrir – Bozorg – Dobeyti – Hosseini – Zirkesh Salmak, Hazineh, Forud – Gerili – Masnavi</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Shur in Abdollah Davami’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Shur in the book “Vocal Radif and Old Tasnifs according to Master Abdollah Davami” by Master Faramarz Payvar are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad Khara – Daramad Shur – Kereshmeh – Gusheh Rahavi – Salmak – Qaracheh – Forud Motammam – Razavi – Tahrir Neshīb o Faraz – Tahrir Javad Khani – Forud – Zirkesh Salmak – Ozzal – Hosseini – Malek Hossein – Dobeyti – Khara – Forud Motammam – Shahnaz – Qajar – Forud to Shur – Gerili</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Shur in Abbas Kazemi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Shur mentioned in Abbas Kazemi’s vocal radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Rahab – Sayakh – Tarz – Qaracheh – Rahab-e Masih – Kereshmeh – Daghestani 1 – Daghestani 2 – Takht-e Khosrow – Orangi – Mollanazi – Sama’-e Sufiyan – Naghmeh-e Qomri – Shabdezi – Khosrow o Shirin – Ma’vara al-Nahri – Ravandi – Barazjani – Nokhjirgah – Ziraftand – Shur – Razavi – Ashuravand – Shahr Ashub – Tusi – Haj Hosseini – Safa – Suz o Godaz – Malek Hosseini – Raz o Niyaz – Sarvestani – Jahromi – Dashtestani – Loristani – Shahnaz – Salmak – Gerili – Gerili Shasti – Hossein-e Azarbaijani – Masnavi</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Shur in A’lam al-Soltan’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Shur mentioned in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi from the radif attributed to “A’lam al-Soltan” are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Four Pishdaramads – Chaharmezrab-e Daneshvar – Daramad – Salmak – Gerili – Mollanazi – Golriz – Neshīb o Faraz – Ouj o Haziz – Bozorg – Kochak – Qajar – Razavi – Qaracheh – Hasht Dari – Foruz-Avar</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Shur in Abolhasan Saba’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Shur mentioned in Abolhasan Saba’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab Shur – Daramad Khara – First Daramad Shur – Second Daramad – Third Daramad – Daramad Rahavi (Gusheh Rahab) – Gerili (Daramad Gerili) – Daramad Shahnaz (Daramad Ouj) – Zarbi Shahnaz (Do Mezrab) – Shahnaz – Qaracheh – Naghmeh (Panjeh Kordi) – Razavi – Hosseini (Daramad) – Zarbi Hosseini – Majles Afrouz – Moghaddameh Bozorg (Abu Eytam) – Dobeyti – Khara – Qajar – Forud to Shur</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Shur in Faramarz Payvar’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Shur mentioned in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi from Faramarz Payvar’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Second Daramad – Panjeh She’ri – Panjeh Muye – Daramad Razavi – Naghmeh – Muye – Zirkesh Salmak – Avaz (Zirkesh) – Salmak – Mollanazi (Mollaniyazi) – Golriz – Moghaddameh Golriz (Neshīb o Faraz) – Ozzal – Mohammad Sadeq Khani – Chaharmezrab – Bozorg – Kochak – Hazineh – Forud Mirza Hossein-Qoli – Shahnaz (Ashegh Kosh or Oghdeh Gosha or Shahnaz Kot) – Masnavi – Khatameh</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Shur in the First Volume of “The History of Iranian Music”</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Shur mentioned in the first volume of “Sar-gozasht-e Musiqi-ye Irani” quoted from <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a> are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramads – Gusheh Rahab (Rahevi or Rahavi) – Naghmeh – Zirkesh Salmak – Salmak – Golriz – Safa – Bozorg – Dobeyti – Khara – Qajar – Mollanazi – Hazineh – Ozzal – Majles Afrouz – Shahnaz – Qaracheh – Razavi – Ashegh Kosh – Bayat Kord – Haj Hosseini – Basteh Negar – Ghatar – Qaza’i – Bayat Tork – Dogah – Fili – Shekasteh – Jameh Daran – Mehdi Zarabi – Ruh al-Arvah – Abu Ata (Sarang) – Sayakhi – Hejaz – Chaharpareh (Chaharbagh) – Gabri (Gori) – Dashti – Bidgani [Gilaki – Ghamangiz – Dashtestani – Hajiyani – Chupani] – Gerili – Afshari – Gusheh Iraq – Masnavi</p>\n\n<p>Among the gushehs of the dastgah of Shur, the gusheh of Kereshmeh is seen as one of the most important rhythmic gushehs in the radif of Iranian music, which has the ability to be performed in other dastgahs and avazes of the Iranian music radif. This gusheh is based on the prosodic meter “mafa’ilun fa’alatun mafa’ilun fa’al” and many poems in this meter are sung with the kereshmeh gusheh, one of the most frequently used of which is the following verse:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">“Hezar o martabeh be beh, az an lab-e shekarinat<br>Khoda konad ke nabashad ajal be ghasd o kaminat”</p>\n\n<h2>Analysis of the Gushehs of the Dastgah of Shur in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h2>\n\n<p>Now we examine the gushehs of the dastgah of Shur in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s radif mentioned earlier:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Daramad gusheh</strong>: The main range of melodic movement in the daramad of the dastgah of Shur is the upper dang above the witness note (the note heard most frequently in a melody, around which melodic movements revolve) and the concluding note (“A – D”). The note before the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol), is also considered the starting note. The fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re), is the lowest note used in the daramad range. The note before the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol), is not used during the gusheh. This note is heard only in the two stages of beginning and forud.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Panjeh She’ri and Kereshmeh gushehs</strong>: After the daramads of the dastgah of Shur, in the form of the daramad mode and with gradual expansion of the sonic range, the gushehs of Panjeh She’ri and Kereshmeh, which have rhythmic patterns (motifs), are presented.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ouj gusheh</strong>: The sonic range expands. In this gusheh too, the main range of melodic movement is in the form of the upper dang above the concluding note. The opening phrases revolve around the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re), and the final phrase, with emphasis on the concluding note, i.e., “A” (la), concludes on the same note.</p>\n\n<p><strong>First Naghmeh gusheh</strong>: It begins on the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do). In this gusheh too, there is a rhythmic pattern that expands in a sequential manner (melodies with successive progression) and finally ends on the concluding note of Shur. In some phrases the second, fourth, and fifth degrees above the concluding note change. These changes are temporary and mostly due to melodic embellishments.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Zirkesh Salmak gusheh</strong>: This gusheh has two parts. Its first part begins on the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “E” (mi). This note is variable in the mentioned gusheh and is used in two forms: “E koron” and “E bekar.” The existence of the variable fifth degree above the concluding note is one of the structural features of Zirkesh Salmak. Sequential descending movements are also part of the structural design of this gusheh.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Second Part of Zirkesh Salmak gusheh</strong>: It begins on the seventh degree above the concluding note.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Golriz gusheh</strong>: It begins on the seventh degree above the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol), and ends on the concluding note of Shur, i.e., “A” (la).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Neshīb o Faraz gusheh</strong>: It begins on the second degree below the concluding note and, with a stop on the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re), evokes the transition to Shur on “D” (re).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ouj o Haziz gusheh</strong>: It begins on the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do), and stops on the concluding note of Shur, i.e., “A” (la). This gusheh, after Neshīb o Faraz, creates the state of return to Shur on “A” (la).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Bozorg gusheh</strong>: It begins on the octave above the concluding note and witness of Shur, i.e., “A” (la). This gusheh has a rhythmic pattern that mainly emphasizes the two poles “D” (re) and “A” (la). The first part of this gusheh stops on “A” (la) and its second part stops on the note before the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol), which prepares the ground for the beginning of the Kochak gusheh.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Kochak gusheh</strong>: It begins on the note before the concluding note in the upper octave, i.e., “G” (sol), and stops on the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re). This movement once again evokes the transition to Shur on “D” (re).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Dobeyti gusheh</strong>: It begins on the note before the concluding note in the upper octave, i.e., “G” (sol). Melodic movements in this gusheh mainly take place within the structural interval of “D” (re) and “A” (la). The conclusion of some phrases on “D” (re) evokes the transition to Shur on “D” (re). The temporary stop of this gusheh is on the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Qajar gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “E” (mi). This note is used in variable form as “E koron” and “E bekar.” The descending figure used in this gusheh stops on the concluding note of Shur, i.e., “A” (la).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Second Naghmeh gusheh</strong>: It has a rhythmic pattern that begins on the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do), and concludes on “A” (la).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ozzal or Shur-e Pa’in Dasteh gusheh</strong>: In this part, the melodic content presented in the lower sonic range in the daramad area is performed in the lower octave. In the dastgah of Shur, the starting, concluding, and witness note is the same note “A” (la), but in the lower octave the chaharmezrab also has a base that emphasizes the witness note “A” (la).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Panjeh Kordi gusheh</strong>: It begins on the note before the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol), and concludes on “A” (la). The main range of melodic movement is within the dang “A – D.”</p>\n\n<p><strong>Panjeh She’ri, Kereshmeh, and Ouj gushehs</strong>: They are presented within the same modal space.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Chahar Gusheh gusheh</strong>: It begins on the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do), and concludes on “A” (la). The third and fourth degrees above the concluding note are emphasized in this gusheh.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Moghaddameh Gerili gusheh</strong>: It begins on the third degree above the concluding note of Shur, i.e., “C” (do), and emphasizes this note. The conclusion of this gusheh is also on the main concluding note of Shur, i.e., “A” (la).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Second Naghmeh gusheh</strong>: It has a rhythmic pattern that is structurally similar to the previous gusheh.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Razavi gusheh</strong>: It begins on the third degree above the concluding note and the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re), is considered the witness of this gusheh, which is the main axis of melodic movement. This gusheh stops on “D” (re) and, after presenting the kereshmeh (which has a rhythmic pattern “mafa’ilun fa’alatun mafa’ilun fa’aln”), with the passage through Qajar, descends to the concluding note of Shur, i.e., “A” (la).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Qaracheh gusheh</strong>: It begins on the sixth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “F” (fa), and concludes on “A” (la).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hosseini gusheh</strong>: It begins on the third degree above the concluding note of Shur, i.e., “C” (do), and descends to the concluding note of Shur, i.e., “A” (la).</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the gushehs of the dastgah of Shur, please share them in the comments section of the article with us to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-12-12 18:12:33', 0, '2024-12-19 19:13:17', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1532, 'posts', 75, 'en', NULL, 'Gushehs of Dastgah Mahur | Examination of Mahur gushehs in different radifs', NULL, 'This article examines the gushehs (melodic pieces) of the Mahur mode in the Persian classical music repertoire (Radif), comparing their presentation across different narrations by prominent masters such as Mirza Abdollah, Mirza Hossein-Qoli, Ali-Akbar Shahnazi, Mousa Maroufi, and others. It highlights that while the number and sequence of gushehs vary between different versions of the Radif, the core structure and principal gushehs—such as Daramad, Dad, Delkash, Khavaran, Shekasteh, Rak, and Iraq—remain largely consistent.\n\nThe analytical section focuses on the melodic characteristics of key gushehs in the Mirza Hossein-Qoli Radif, including their starting notes, tonal centers (shahed), pitch range, and cadential patterns. It also discusses modal modulations (for example, temporary shifts to Shur or Bayat-e Esfahan in gushehs like Delkash and Zirafkan) and the role of rhythmic motifs in shaping certain sections. Overall, the article demonstrates that despite variations in transmission, the Mahur mode maintains a coherent and structured melodic and modal system.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">gushehs of the dastgah of Mahur</span></strong> and review the gushehs of Mahur in various narrations of the radifs of Iranian music. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Mahur in the Radif</h2>\n\n<p>In the various narrations of the radif according to different masters, the number of gushehs of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%85%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%88%d8%b1/\">dastgah of Mahur</a> differs slightly. In the following, we examine these gushehs in several radifs:</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Mahur in Mirza Abdollah’s Radif according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Mahur in the different notations of Mirza Abdollah’s radif vary slightly. Below we present two versions:</p>\n\n<h4>Jean During’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>In the radif of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87/\">Mirza Abdollah</a> notated by Jean During, the following gushehs are mentioned for the dastgah of Mahur:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Kereshmeh – Avaz – Moghaddameh Dad – Dad – Majles Afrouz – Khosravani – Delkash – Chaharmezrab – Forud – Khavaran – Tarabangiz – Neyshaburak – Nasir Khani (Tusi) – Chaharpareh (Morad Khani) – Fili – Mahur Saghir – Azarbaijani – Hesār Mahur (Abol) – Zanguleh – Naghmeh – Ziraftand – Neyriz – Shekasteh – Second Part – Third Part – Iraq – Nahib – Mohayyer – Ashuravand – Esfahank – Hazineh – Kereshmeh – Zanguleh – Rak-e Hendi – Rak-e Kashmir – Rak-e Abdollah – Kereshmeh Rak and Safir Rak – Reng-e Harbi – Reng-e Yek Choobeh – Reng-e Shalakh – Saqi Nameh – Koshteh Mordeh – Sufi Nameh</p>\n\n<h4>Dariush Talaei’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>The dastgah of Mahur in Dariush Talaei’s book “Tahlil-e Radif,” based on Mirza Abdollah’s radif, includes the following gushehs:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Kereshmeh – Avaz – Moghaddameh Dad – Dad – Majles Afrouz – Khosravani – Delkash – Chaharmezrab and Forud – Khavaran – Tarabangiz – Neyshaburak – Nasir Khani or Tusi – Chaharpareh or Morad Khani – Fili – Mahur Saghir – Azarbaijani – Hesār Mahur or Abol – Ziraftand – Neyriz – Shekasteh – Iraq – Nahib – Mohayyer – Ashuravand – Esfahank – Hazineh – Kereshmeh – Zanguleh – Rak-e Hendi – Rak-e Kashmir – Rak-e Abdollah – Kereshmeh Rak – Safir Rak – Reng-e Harbi – Reng-e Yek Choobeh – Reng-e Shalakh – Saqi Nameh, Koshteh and Sufi Nameh</p>\n\n<h4>Gushehs Mentioned in the Book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi</h4>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Mahur mentioned in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi, from the radif attributed to “Mirza Abdollah” according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Kereshmeh – Avaz – Moghaddameh Dad – Dad – Majles Afrouz – Khosravani – Delkash [with Chaharmezrab and Forud] – Khavaran – Tarabangiz – Neyshaburak – Nasir Khani or Tusi – Chaharpareh or Morad Khani – Fili – Mahur Saghir – Azarbaijani – Hesār Mahur or Abol – Ziraftand – Neyriz – Shekasteh – Iraq – Nahib – Mohayyer – Ashuravand – Esfahank – Hazineh – Kereshmeh – Zanguleh – Rak-e Hendi – Rak-e Kashmir – Rak-e Abdollah – Kereshmeh Rak – Safir Rak – Reng-e Harbi – Reng-e Yek Choobeh – Reng-e Shalakh – Saqi Nameh – Koshteh Mordeh – Sufi Nameh</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Mahur in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Mahur in the book “Radif of Mirza Hossein-Qoli according to Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Pirniakan are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Second Daramad – Third Daramad – Avaz – Second Part of Avaz – Third Part of Avaz – Gosha’esh – Khosravani – Dad – Khwarazmi – Delkash – Chaharmezrab – Khavaran – Tarabangiz – Hazineh – Nahib – Soroush – Azarbaijani – Fili – Mahur Saghir – Nasir Khani – Morad Khani or Chaharpareh – Second Part of Morad Khani – Abol or Hesār Mahur – Basteh Negar – Ziraftand – Neyriz Saghir – Neyriz Kabir – Shekasteh – Rak – Safir Rak – Chaharmezrab Rak – Naghmeh Rak – Rak-e Abdollah – Rak-e Hendi – Iraq – Mohayyer – Ashuravand – Esfahank – Hazineh – Zanguleh – Forud</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Mahur in Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi’s Advanced Course Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Mahur in the book “Advanced Course Radif of Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Talaei are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Kereshmeh – Daramad – Dad – Chaharmezrab Dad – Fili – Delkash – Chaharmezrab Delkash – Gusheh of Abu Ata, Chaharmezrab and Forud – Neyriz, Chaharmezrab and Forud – Rak – Safir Rak with Chaharmezrab – Kereshmeh Rak – Rak-e Kashmir – Do Zarbi Rak – Kereshmeh Iraq – Avaz Iraq – Forud to Mahur</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Mahur in Musa Khan Ma’rufi’s Radif of Seven Dastgahs</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Mahur mentioned in the book “Radif of Seven Dastgahs of Iranian Music” by Musa Khan Ma’rufi, edited by Mehdi Barkeshli, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Moghaddameh – Kuraoghli, Chaharmezrab, Bardasht – Kereshmeh – First Daramad – Second Daramad – Avaz – Gosha’esh – Dad – Khosravani – Delkash – Chaharmezrab – Hajiyani – Khwarazmi – Khavaran – Do-ta Yeki – Tarabangiz – Neyshaburak – Tusi – Nasir Khani – Chaharpareh – Azarbaijani – Fili – Ziraftand – Mahur Saghir – Abol – Hesār Mahur – Zanguleh – Naghmeh – Avaz Abol – Ziraftand – Forud – Neyriz – Shekasteh – Nahib – Soroush – Iraq – Mohayyer – Ashur – Basteh Negar – Esfahank – Hazineh – Kereshmeh – Zanguleh – Rak – Safir Rak – Rak-e Hendi – Rak-e Hendi, Another Part – Chaharmezrab – Naghmeh Rak – Rak-e Kashmir – Rak-e Kashmir, Another Part – Rak-e Abdollah – Masnavi – Saqi Nameh – Sufi Nameh – Koshteh – Harbi – Shahr Ashub</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Mahur in Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Mahur in the book “Javab-e Avaz based on Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif” by Arshad Tahmasbi are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Gosha’esh – Dad – Hesār, Ziraftand, Azarbaijani, Mahur Saghir, Forud – Fili – Shekasteh – Delkash – Neyshaburak – Neyriz – Morad Khani or Azarbaijani – Tusi – Nasir Khani – Khavaran – Iraq, Mohayyer, Nahib, Ashur, Hazineh, Forud – Rak-e Hendi – Safir Rak – Rak-e Abdollah – Rak-e Kashmir – Saqi Nameh, Sufi Nameh, Koshteh – Masnavi</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Mahur in Abdollah Davami’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Mahur in the book “Vocal Radif and Old Tasnifs according to Master Abdollah Davami” by Master Faramarz Payvar are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Panjeh She’ri – Dad – Sarbang – Hesār – Abol – Azarbaijani – Nasir Khani (Tusi) – Morad Khani – Chaharpareh (Nasir Khani) – Fili – Shekasteh, Forud – Khavaran – Delkash – Iraq – Mohayyer – Hazineh – Rak – Safir – Forud – Rak-e Abdollah – Rak-e Kashmir – Neyshaburak – Neyriz – Masnavi – Saqi Nameh – Sufi Nameh</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Mahur in Abbas Kazemi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Mahur mentioned in Abbas Kazemi’s vocal radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Mahur – Dad – Majles Afrouz – Kereshmeh – Shekasteh Mahur – Fili – Tarabangiz – Foruz – Delkash – Hesār – Gardaniyeh – Atesh Daneh – Abol – Shahnaz – Salmak – Pas Mahur – Malanazi – Basteh Negar – Suz o Godaz – Masnavi</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Mahur in A’lam al-Soltan’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Mahur mentioned in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi from the radif attributed to “A’lam al-Soltan” are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Pishdaramad – Daramad – Gosha’esh – Khosravani – Dad – Khwarazmi – Delkash – Khavaran – Tarabangiz – Nahib – Soroush – Azarbaijani – Fili – Hesār Mahur – Nasir Khani – Morad Khani – Abolchap – Basteh Negar – Ziraftand – Neyriz – Shekasteh – Rak – Iraq – Mokhber – Hazineh – Zanguleh</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Mahur in Abolhasan Saba’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Mahur mentioned in Abolhasan Saba’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Second Daramad – Gosha’esh – Zarbi Gosha’esh – Khwarazmi or Majles Afrouz – Azarbaijani (Moghaddameh) – Tusi (Dad) – Fili – Ziraftand – Mirzai – Hesār – Abol – Mahur Saghir – Ziraftand – Mirzai – Shekasteh – Delkash – Khavaran – Zarbi Khavaran (Kordi) – Iraq (Nahib) – Kereshmeh – Rak – Safir Rak – Masnavi – Saqi Nameh</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Mahur in Faramarz Payvar’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Mahur mentioned in Faramarz Payvar’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad (Banafsheh) – Second Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Gosha’esh – Dad Afarid – Sarbang – Muye – Chaharbagh or Chaharpareh (Tusi) – Nasir Khani – Takhir Hajiyani – Neyshaburak – Neyriz – Tak-e Moghaddam – Bal-e Kabutar – Soroush – Mahur Saghir – Mirzai – Rak – Safir Rak – Rak-e Abdollah – Rak-e Hendi – Rak-e Kashmir – Ma’vara al-Nahr – Morad Khani</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Mahur in the First Volume of “The History of Iranian Music”</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Mahur mentioned in the first volume of “Sar-gozasht-e Musiqi-ye Irani” quoted from <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a> are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Pishdaramad – Gosha’esh – Khosravani – Dad – Foruz – Delkash – Khavaran – Tarabangiz – Nahib – Iraq – Rak – Ashur Ravand – Esfahank – Mohayyeran – Hazineh – Zanguleh – Zanguleh Bargardan – Neyshaburak – Azarbaijani – Fili – Mahur Saghir – Hesār Mahur – Mehdi Khani – Nasir Khani (Tusi) – Abol – Shekasteh – Neyriz – Saqi Nameh – Reng-e Harbi</p>\n\n<h2>Analysis of the Gushehs of the Dastgah of Mahur in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h2>\n\n<p>Now we examine the gushehs of the dastgah of Mahur in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s radif mentioned earlier:</p>\n\n<p><strong>First Daramad gusheh</strong>: It begins on the note “C” (do), which also serves as the witness and concluding note. The main range of melodic movement is the lower dang below the concluding note “G – C.” Melodic movement also extends to the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol). In fact, this range can be considered a combination of one dang “G – C” and one chahārgāh “C – G” (a perfect fifth in a five-note sequence). The concluding note is taken with an ascending cadential figure from the third degree below the concluding note.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Second and Third Daramad gushehs</strong>: They are similar to the first daramad in modality, with the difference that, in addition to expanding the sonic range, they use rhythmic patterns derived from the kereshmeh figure. The starting note in the second and third daramads is also the fourth degree below the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Avaz gusheh</strong>: It has three parts. The starting note in the first part is the concluding note itself. However, in the second and third parts, the starting note is the second degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re). The emphasis on the second degree above the concluding note in the avaz can be seen as preparing the ground for the subsequent gushehs, in which this note serves as the witness.</p>\n\n<p>Although in the avaz this note can also be considered to have the function of witness, in the subsequent gushehs (especially Dad) this role appears more prominent. Some phrases temporarily stop on the second degree above the concluding note, evoking a state of suspension. This suspension is resolved by the stopping of subsequent phrases on the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Gosha’esh gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “F” (fa), and concludes on the concluding note of the dastgah of Mahur, i.e., “C” (do). In this gusheh the main range of melodic movement is the upper dang above the concluding note. Some phrases temporarily stop on the second degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re), but finally conclude on “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Khosravani gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol). The temporary stop of this gusheh is also on the second degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re), and its forud is on the concluding note of Mahur, i.e., “C” (do). The main melodic pattern in Khosravani consists of sequential descending movements performed in a question-and-answer form.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Dad gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “F” (fa). The witness and temporary stop in this gusheh is also the second degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re). The concluding note in this gusheh is also “C” (do). In fact, the emphasis on “D” (re) makes this gusheh evoke a secondary mode with a change in the function of the degrees, without changing the intervals relative to the daramad mode.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Khwarazmi gusheh</strong>: It has a rhythmic pattern and its starting note is the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “E” (mi). In this gusheh too, some phrases temporarily stop on the second degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re), but finally conclude on “C” (do). The main range of melodic movement is within the upper fifth above the concluding note.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Delkash gusheh</strong>: It is one of the main gushehs of the dastgah of Mahur, in which a temporary modal shift to Shur occurs. This gusheh can be considered to have an initial mode with changed interval ratios and also changed functions of the degrees. Delkash begins on the fifth degree above the concluding note of Mahur, i.e., “G” (sol). This note also serves as the witness in Delkash, around which melodic movement revolves.</p>\n\n<p>The temporary stop on “F” (fa) and finally on “G” (sol) prepares the ground for performing the chaharmezrab, whose base revolves around the witness of Delkash, i.e., “G” (sol), and begins on the same note. After the chaharmezrab ends, the return to the main Mahur mode (daramad mode) occurs, concluding on “C” (do), and the changed degrees return to their original state.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Khavaran gusheh</strong>: It begins on the sixth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “A” (la). The main range of melodic movement in this gusheh is within the two continuous upper dangs above the concluding note. In the final part, the lower dang below the concluding note is used for the forud and taking the conclusion. Some phrases in this gusheh temporarily stop on the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “F” (fa), and also on the second degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re), but the definite conclusion is on “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Tarabangiz gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol), and the temporary stop in this gusheh is on the second degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hazineh gusheh</strong>: It begins from the temporary stop of the previous gusheh, i.e., “D” (re). This note, in addition to serving as the starting note in this gusheh, also has the function of temporary stop. The definite conclusion of this gusheh is also on “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nahib gusheh</strong>: In this gusheh the sonic range expands. The starting note in this gusheh is the octave above the concluding note of Mahur, i.e., “C” (do). Temporary stops of some phrases are on the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol), while the definite forud is on the concluding note of Mahur, i.e., “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Soroush gusheh</strong>: It begins on the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “E” (mi). The sixth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “A” (la), is microtonally lowered in this gusheh (A koron), but in the final phrases this note returns to its previous state and finally the gusheh concludes on the concluding note of Mahur, i.e., “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Azarbaijani and Fili gushehs</strong>: These gushehs have almost similar opening patterns. The starting note in both gushehs is the concluding note itself, i.e., “C” (do), and some phrases temporarily stop on the second degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re). Emphasis on the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “F” (fa), is also one of the features of these two gushehs.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mahur Saghir gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol), and finally ends on the concluding note of Mahur, i.e., “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nasir Khani gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fourth degree below the concluding note. This gusheh revolves around the axis of the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do), and finally concludes on the same note.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Morad Khani or Chaharpareh gusheh</strong>: It has a rhythmic pattern based on the prosodic meter “mutafa’ilun mutafa’ilun” that begins on the fourth degree below the concluding note and finally ends on “C” (do). In the second part of this gusheh, a temporary modal shift to the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan occurs, after which it descends back to Mahur.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Abol or Hesār Mahur gusheh</strong>: It begins on the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do), but its stop is on the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “F” (fa), which also serves as the witness in this gusheh. After that, Basteh Negar, which has a rhythmic pattern, is presented with emphasis on this note and stopping on it.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ziraftand gusheh</strong>: It begins on the second degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re). In this gusheh a brief modal shift to the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan occurs, after which it descends to Mahur with the conclusion on “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Neyriz Saghir gusheh</strong>: It begins on the second degree below the concluding note, i.e., “B” (si), and stops on the second degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re). Melodic movement in this gusheh revolves around the axis “E koron” (mi koron). The microtonal change of the third degree above the concluding note to lowered (E koron) and emphasis on this note are features of this gusheh.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Neyriz Kabir gusheh</strong>: It begins on the third degree above the concluding note, which is microtonally lowered, i.e., “E koron” (mi koron), which in the forud returns to its original state, i.e., “E bekar” (mi bekar), and finally ends on “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Shekasteh gusheh</strong>: In this gusheh the interval ratios also change and it evokes the Afshari mode. This gusheh begins on the fifth degree above the concluding note, “G” (sol). The third degree above the concluding note is microtonally lowered (E koron) and the seventh degree above the concluding note also changes to “B bemol” (si bemol). At the end of this gusheh, with the return of these degrees to their original state, the forud to Mahur, i.e., “C” (do), occurs.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rak gusheh</strong>: It is performed with the sixth degree above the concluding note microtonally lowered (A koron) and its witness is also “C” (do). The first part of Rak begins on the sixth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “A koron” (la koron), and melodic movement in it revolves around the concluding note of Mahur in the upper octave, i.e., “C” (do), and finally stops on the same note.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Safir Rak gusheh</strong>: It begins with a leap in glissando form from the octave of the concluding note to the fifth degree in the lower octave, i.e., “G” (sol), and finally stops on “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Chaharmezrab Rak gusheh</strong>: It is performed after Rak and its base emphasizes the fifth degree above the concluding note in the octave, i.e., “G” (sol), and stops on “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Naghmeh Rak gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fifth degree above the concluding note in the lower octave, i.e., “G” (sol), and ends on “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rak-e Abdollah gusheh</strong>: The starting note is the same as the concluding note and witness of Mahur in the lower octave, i.e., “C” (do), which finally stops on the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rak-e Hendi gusheh</strong>: It is the last gusheh in this section, which begins on the lower octave of the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do), and finally returns to the daramad mode of Mahur and concludes on “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Iraq gusheh</strong>: This gusheh and the gushehs attached to it are in fact considered the climax of Mahur. In this gusheh the witness note of the Mahur daramad, i.e., “C” (do) in the lower octave, is presented as the witness. The starting note in this gusheh is the sixth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “A” (la), and it stops on “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ashuravand gusheh</strong>: The starting note in this gusheh is the fourth degree above the witness, i.e., “F” (fa), and the stop is “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Esfahank gusheh</strong>: It begins on the second degree below the witness of Iraq and stops on the witness of Iraq, i.e., “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hazineh gusheh</strong>: It begins on the witness note of Iraq and stops on the fifth degree above the witness of the daramad or, in other words, the fourth degree below the witness of Iraq, i.e., “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Zanguleh gusheh</strong>: It has a rhythmic pattern that begins on the second degree below the witness of Iraq and finally descends to the lower sonic range and the area of the Mahur daramad. The concluding note in this gusheh is the same as the concluding note of the Mahur daramad, i.e., “C” (do).</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the gushehs of the dastgah of Mahur, please share them in the comments section of the article with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a> to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-12-14 18:47:47', 0, '2024-12-19 19:13:04', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1533, 'posts', 76, 'en', NULL, 'Gushehs of Dastgah Segah | Examination of Segah gushehs in different radifs', NULL, 'This article explores the gushehs of the Segah mode in the Persian classical music repertoire (Radif), comparing their presentation across different narrations by masters such as Mirza Abdollah, Mirza Hossein-Qoli, Ali-Akbar Shahnazi, Mousa Maroufi, and others. Although the number and sequence of gushehs vary among these versions, core elements such as Daramad, Zabol, Muyeh, Mokhalef, Maghlub, and Hesar consistently appear and form the structural foundation of Segah.\n\nThe analytical section focuses on the melodic structure of key gushehs in the Mirza Hossein-Qoli Radif. It highlights the role of the tonal center (E koron), the starting and ending tones of each gusheh, pitch range, and microtonal variations. The progression from the Daramad to sections like Zabol and Hesar, followed by transitions into Mokhalef and Maghlub, and finally returning to the cadence (Forud), is also discussed. Overall, the article demonstrates that Segah is characterized by its distinctive intervals, expressive qualities, and a coherent yet nuanced modal structure.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">gushehs of the dastgah of Segah</span></strong> and review the gushehs of Segah in various narrations of the radifs of Iranian music. Please continue with the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>...</p>\n\n<h2>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Segah in the Radif</h2>\n\n<p>In the various narrations of the radif according to different masters, the number of gushehs of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b3%d9%87-%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">dastgah of Segah</a> differs slightly. In the following, we examine these gushehs in several radifs:</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Segah in Mirza Abdollah’s Radif according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Segah in the different notations of Mirza Abdollah’s radif vary slightly. Below we present two versions:</p>\n\n<h4>Jean During’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>In the radif of Mirza Abdollah notated by Jean During, the following gushehs are mentioned for the dastgah of Segah:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab – Daramad – Naghmeh – Kereshmeh with Muye – Zang-e Shotor – Zabol – Basteh Negar – Muye – Mokhalef – Hajiyani – Basteh Negar – Maghlub – Naghmeh Maghlub – Hazineh – Muye – Rahab – Masihi – Shah Khatayi – Takht-e Taqdis (Takht-e Kavus) – Reng-e Delgosha</p>\n\n<h4>Dariush Talaei’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>The dastgah of Segah in Dariush Talaei’s book “Tahlil-e Radif,” based on Mirza Abdollah’s radif, includes the following gushehs:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab – Daramad – Naghmeh – Kereshmeh – Kereshmeh with Muye – Zang-e Shotor – Zabol – Basteh Negar – Muye – Mokhalef – Hajiyani – Basteh Negar – Maghlub – Naghmeh – Hazineh – Muye – Rahab – Masihi – Shah Khatayi – Takht-e Taqdis – Reng-e Delgosha</p>\n\n<h4>Gushehs Mentioned in the Book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi</h4>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Segah mentioned in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi, from the radif attributed to “Mirza Abdollah” according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab – Daramad – Naghmeh – Kereshmeh – Kereshmeh with Muye – Zang-e Shotor – Zabol – Basteh Negar – Muye – Mokhalef – Hajiyani – Basteh Negar – Maghlub – Naghmeh Maghlub – Hazineh – Muye – Rahab – Masihi – Shah Khatayi – Takht-e Taqdis or Takht-e Kavus – Reng-e Delgosha</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Segah in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Segah in the book “Radif of Mirza Hossein-Qoli according to Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Pirniakan are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Second Daramad – Third Daramad or Two Daramads – Naghmeh – First Zabol – Second Zabol with Basteh Negar – Mo’arebed – Pish Hesār – Pas Hesār – Chaharmezrab – Haft Hesār – Zanguleh – Muye Hesār – Maghlub – Mokhalef – Basteh Negar – Naghmeh Mokhalef – Hazineh – Muye Mokhalef – Hodi – Pahlavi – Rajaz or Arkhoreh – Forud</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Segah in Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi’s Advanced Course Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Segah in the book “Advanced Course Radif of Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Talaei are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab – First Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Second Daramad – Third Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Kereshmeh and Forud – Chaharmezrab and Forud – Hesār – Muye – Zabol with Chaharmezrab – Bardasht of Mokhalef – First Daramad of Mokhalef – Kereshmeh Mokhalef – Second Daramad of Mokhalef – First Chaharmezrab of Mokhalef – Maghlub – Third Daramad of Mokhalef – Second Chaharmezrab of Mokhalef – Kereshmeh (Continuation of Mokhalef) – Chaharmezrab – Forud to Muye State – Masnavi – Zarbi with Kereshmeh – Forud</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Segah in Musa Khan Ma’rufi’s Radif of Seven Dastgahs</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Segah mentioned in the book “Radif of Seven Dastgahs of Iranian Music” by Musa Khan Ma’rufi, edited by Mehdi Barkeshli, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Moghaddameh – First Daramad – Second Daramad – Third Daramad – Kereshmeh – Pish Zanguleh – Zanguleh – Zang-e Shotor – First Part of Zabol – Second Part of Zabol – Basteh Negar – Zanguleh – Panjeh Muye – Avaz Muye – Forud Muye – Rest of Zabol – First Part of Hesār – Second Part of Hesār – Zanguleh – Naghmeh – Third Part of Hesār – Kereshmeh – Forud Hesār – Hozzan – Pas Hesār – Mo’arebed – First Part of Mokhalef – Second Part of Mokhalef – Third Part of Mokhalef – Hajiyani – Basteh Negar – Maghlub – Naghmeh Maghlub – Dobeyti – Par Parastu – Kereshmeh – Hazineh – Hozzan – Muye – Reng-e Delgosha</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Segah in Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Segah in the book “Javab-e Avaz based on Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif” by Arshad Tahmasbi are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Second Daramad – Zabol – Muye – Shekasteh Muye – Hesār – Mokhalef – Mokhalef to Maghlub – Hodi and Pahlavi – Masnavi Mokhalef</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Segah in Abdollah Davami’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Segah in the book “Vocal Radif and Old Tasnifs according to Master Abdollah Davami” by Master Faramarz Payvar are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Zabol – Muye – Shekasteh Muye – Mokhalef – Zang-e Shotor (Neshib o Faraz) – Maghlub – Masnavi Mokhalef</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Segah in Abbas Kazemi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Segah mentioned in Abbas Kazemi’s vocal radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Segah – Dogah – Mahzun – Nahib – Kereshmeh – Zabol – Gori Zabol – Shahin – Shadvard – Palizban – Muye 1 – Hesār 1 – Hesār 2 – Muye 2 – Azadvar – Mokhalef – Hesār 3 – Muye 3 – Mo’arebed – Segah Qafqazi – Zanguleh – Shah Khatayi Zi Khatayi – Segah Yatīm – Najva – Mokhalefak – Ghataravand – Jur – Maghlub – Masnavi</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Segah in A’lam al-Soltan’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Segah mentioned in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi from the radif attributed to “A’lam al-Soltan” are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daneshvar’s Chaharmezrab – Segah [Daramad of Segah] – Segah Qafqazi – Zabol – Hesār – Mokhalef – Maghlub</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Segah in Abolhasan Saba’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Segah mentioned in Abolhasan Saba’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab of Segah – Avaz of Segah (First Daramad) – Pish Zanguleh – Second Daramad – Naghmeh (Moghaddameh Zabol) – Zabol – Se Mezrab Zabol – Moghaddameh Muye – Muye (Avaz Muye) – Chaharmezrab Mokhalef – Mokhalef – Maghlub – Zang-e Shotor (Naghmeh Maghlub) – Masnavi Mokhalef – Joghatayi – Shah Khatayi (Hodi) – Pahlavi (Rajaz) – Zarbi Segah</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Segah in Faramarz Payvar’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Segah mentioned in Faramarz Payvar’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Second Daramad (Kereshmeh) – Zanguleh – Mohammad Sadeq Khani – Zang-e Shotor (Oshtori) – Panjeh Muye – Zabol – Do Mezrab – Basteh Negar – Zabol Gabri – Mo’arebed – Daramad Mokhalef – Chaharmezrab – Dobeyti – Maghlub (Maghlub) – Naghmeh Maghlub or Zang-e Shotor – Hazineh – Hozzan – Shekasteh Muye – Khatameh</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Segah in the First Volume of “The History of Iranian Music”</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Segah mentioned in the first volume of “Sar-gozasht-e Musiqi-ye Irani” quoted from <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a> are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramads – Zabol – Muye – Zang-e Shotor – Rahab – Masihi – Shah Khatayi – Takht-e Taqdis</p>\n\n<h2>Analysis of the Gushehs of the Dastgah of Segah in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h2>\n\n<p>Now we examine the gushehs of the dastgah of Segah in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s radif mentioned earlier:</p>\n\n<p><strong>First Daramad gusheh</strong>: The first daramad of Segah begins on the note “G” (sol) and concludes on “E koron” (mi koron). The note “E koron” (mi koron) can be considered the witness of the dastgah of Segah. The concluding note is taken in the form of an ascending cadential figure with a leap from “C” (do) to “E koron” (mi koron), which is the structural interval of Segah.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Second Daramad gusheh</strong>: It is similar to the first daramad, with the difference that it begins on “C” (do) in the lower octave with a leap to the witness (E koron).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Third Daramad gusheh</strong>: It begins on the note before the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re). Melodic movement in this gusheh also revolves around “E koron” (mi koron) and concludes on the same note.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Naghmeh gusheh</strong>: It has a rhythmic pattern that begins on the witness note, i.e., “E koron” (mi koron). The witness and concluding note in this gusheh are also like the daramads on “E koron” (mi koron).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Zabol gusheh</strong>: It begins on the note “E koron” (mi koron) and concludes on the same note. Melodic movement mainly revolves around the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Second Part of Zabol gusheh</strong>: It has the same characteristics as the first part.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Basteh Negar gusheh</strong>: It is presented with a rhythmic pattern revolving around “G” (sol) and finally stops on “E koron” (mi koron).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mo’arebed gusheh</strong>: It begins on the note before the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re). The note “A koron” (la koron), which is microtonally lowered, is also emphasized in this gusheh. The conclusion of this gusheh is also on “E koron” (mi koron).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Pish Hesār gusheh</strong>: It begins on “C” (do) and emphasizes the same note, finally stopping on “G” (sol), which is the note of the next gusheh, Pas Hesār.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Pas Hesār gusheh</strong>: This gusheh stops on “B koron” (si koron) and emphasizes this note.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Chaharmezrab gusheh</strong>: Its base is built on “B koron” (si koron) and finally stops on the same note.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Haft Hesār gusheh</strong>: This gusheh begins on “G” (sol). Temporary stops in some phrases are also on “B koron” (si koron), but it finally stops on the sixth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Zanguleh gusheh</strong>: It is a gusheh with a rhythmic pattern that begins on “B koron” (si koron) and stops on the second degree above the concluding note, i.e., “F” (fa). The microtonal change of the fourth degree above the concluding note to “A koron” (la koron) also occurs in this gusheh.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Muye Hesār gusheh</strong>: It begins on the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol), and emphasizes this note. The fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “A koron” (la koron), alternately appears as variable “A bekar” and “A koron” throughout this gusheh. This gusheh finally stops on the concluding note of the dastgah of Segah, i.e., “E koron” (mi koron).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Maghlub gusheh</strong>: It begins on the sixth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do). This note also serves as the witness. The conclusion of this gusheh is on “E koron” (mi koron), but the way this note is taken is not like the daramad area with a leap of a neutral third.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mokhalef gusheh</strong>: It begins on the sixth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do), which also serves as its witness. The stop of this gusheh is on the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “A koron” (la koron), which is microtonally lowered.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Basteh Negar gusheh</strong>: It has a rhythmic pattern that begins on the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol). In this gusheh too, “C” (do) serves as the witness and its stop is on “A koron” (la koron).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Naghmeh Mokhalef gusheh</strong>: It has a rhythmic pattern that begins on the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol). In this gusheh too, “C” (do) serves as the witness and “A koron” (la koron) serves as the stop.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hazineh gusheh</strong>: After emphasis on “A koron” (la koron), it finally concludes on “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Muye Mokhalef gusheh</strong>: It begins on the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “E” (mi). The fifth degree above the concluding note is presented as “B bemol” (si bemol). The starting note in this gusheh is the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol), and its conclusion is on “E koron” (mi koron). Melodic movement revolves around “A koron” (la koron). In the forud section, “B koron” (si koron) is used, which finally, like the daramad gusheh, the cadential figure “C – E koron” concludes this gusheh.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hodi gusheh</strong>: It begins on the concluding note and witness of the dastgah of Segah, i.e., “E koron” (mi koron). The sonic range of this gusheh is within the sixth interval “C – A koron,” and the way this gusheh concludes is similar to the daramad on “E koron” (mi koron).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Pahlavi gusheh</strong>: It begins on the sixth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do). Melodic movement in it mainly revolves around the dang “G – C.” In the final part, the concluding note “E koron” (mi koron) is taken from “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rajaz gusheh</strong>: It begins on the witness and concluding note of the dastgah of Segah, i.e., “E koron” (mi koron). The sonic range in this gusheh expands and the note “C” (do) is also emphasized. The conclusion of this gusheh is also on “E koron” (mi koron).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Forud gusheh</strong>: It is in the final section. Melodic movements return to the lower sonic range in the daramad area and finally the concluding note “E koron” (mi koron) is taken with a leap from “C” (do) and the complete conclusion is made.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the gushehs of the dastgah of Segah, please share them in the comments section of the article with us to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-12-15 05:51:28', 0, '2024-12-19 19:12:49', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1534, 'posts', 77, 'en', NULL, 'Gushehs of Dastgah Nava | Examination of Nava gushehs in different radifs', NULL, 'This article explores the gushehs (melodic pieces) of the Nava Dastgah in Persian classical music. It begins by presenting the gushehs of Nava across different radif traditions, including those of Mirza Abdollah, Mirza Hossein Gholi, Mousa Maroufi, Mahmoud Karimi, Abdollah Davami, Abolhassan Saba, and others. The article highlights that while the number and order of gushehs vary across these narrations, their overall structure remains largely consistent.\n\nIt also notes that the Nava Dastgah is absent from some sources, such as the advanced radif of Ali-Akbar Shahnazi, despite being taught by him.\n\nA significant part of the article focuses on the analysis of Nava gushehs in the radif of Mirza Hossein Gholi. In this section, each gusheh is examined in terms of its starting note, tonal center (shahed), cadence (ist), and melodic range. For instance:\n\nThe daramad sections are centered around the note “Sol” as the main tonal axis\nGushehs like Gardaniyeh, Nahft, Oshaq, and Iraq expand the melodic space\nSome gushehs such as Mohayyer, Hazin, and Zanguleh function as continuations or developments of earlier ones\nFinally, gushehs like Neyriz return to the initial tonal space and conclude the dastgah\n\nOverall, the article provides a comprehensive overview of the structure, diversity, and melodic characteristics of the Nava dastgah across different radif traditions.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">gushehs of the dastgah of Nava</span></strong> and review the gushehs of Nava in various narrations of the radifs of Iranian music. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Nava in the Radif</h2>\n\n<p>In the various narrations of the radif according to different masters, the number of gushehs of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%86%d9%88%d8%a7/\">dastgah of Nava</a> differs slightly. In the following, we examine these gushehs in several radifs:</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Nava in Mirza Abdollah’s Radif according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Nava in the different notations of Mirza Abdollah’s radif vary slightly. Below we present two versions:</p>\n\n<h4>Jean During’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>In the radif of Mirza Abdollah notated by Jean During, the following gushehs are mentioned for the dastgah of Nava:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab – First Daramad – Second Daramad – Kereshmeh – Gardaniyeh – Naghmeh – Bayat-e Raje – Hazineh – Oshshaq – Nahoft – Gosht – Ashiran – Neyshaburak – Majusli – Khajasteh – Malek Hossein – Hosseini – Buselik – Neyriz – Reng-e Nastari (Nastali) – Reng-e Nava</p>\n\n<h4>Dariush Talaei’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>The dastgah of Nava in Dariush Talaei’s book “Tahlil-e Radif,” based on Mirza Abdollah’s radif, includes the following gushehs:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab – First Daramad – Second Daramad – Kereshmeh – Garduniyeh – Naghmeh – Bayat-e Raje – Hazineh – Oshshaq – Nahoft – Gosht – Ashiran – Neyshaburak – Majusli – Khajasteh – Malek Hossein – Hosseini – Buselik – Neyriz – Reng-e Nastari – Reng-e Nava</p>\n\n<h4>Gushehs Mentioned in the Book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi</h4>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Nava mentioned in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi, from the radif attributed to “Mirza Abdollah” according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab – First Daramad – Second Daramad – Kereshmeh – Garduniyeh – Naghmeh – Bayat-e Raje – Hazineh – Oshshaq – Nahoft – Gosht – Ashiran – Neyshaburak – Majusli – Khajasteh – Malek Hossein – Hosseini – Buselik – Neyriz – Reng-e Nastari or Nastali – Reng-e Nava</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Nava in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Nava in the book “Radif of Mirza Hossein-Qoli according to Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Pirniakan are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Second Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Naghmeh – Gardaniyeh – Gosht – Nahoft – Bayat-e Raje – Hazineh in Bayat-e Raje – Oshshaq – Iraq – Mohayyer – Hazineh – Zanguleh – Neyshaburak – Khajasteh – Majusli – Zamineh Hosseini – Hosseini – Buselik – Nastari – Rahab – Ashiran – Neyriz</p>\n\n<h3>Note on Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi’s Advanced Course Radif</h3>\n\n<p><strong>The dastgah of Nava is not included in the book “Advanced Course Radif of Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Talaei! Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi taught the dastgah of Nava in his radif, but unfortunately the gushehs of the dastgah of Nava were not recorded by any of his students or at least have not been presented yet.</strong></p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Nava in Musa Khan Ma’rufi’s Radif of Seven Dastgahs</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Nava mentioned in the book “Radif of Seven Dastgahs of Iranian Music” by Musa Khan Ma’rufi, edited by Mehdi Barkeshli, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab – First Daramad – Second Daramad – Third Daramad – Kereshmeh – Gardaniyeh – Naghmeh – Bayat-e Raje – Hazineh – Oshshaq – Iraq – Mohayyer – Ashur – Basteh Negar – Esfahank – Hazineh – Zanguleh – Nahoft – Nahoft, Another Part – Gosht – Ashiran – Neyshaburak – Majusli – Khajasteh – Hosseini – Malek Hosseini – Buselik – Neyriz Saghir and Kabir – Rahavi – Masihi – Naqus – Naqus, Another Part – Kereshmeh – Shah Khatayi – Takht-e Taqdis – Nastari</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Nava in Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Nava in the book “Javab-e Avaz based on Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif” by Arshad Tahmasbi are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Gardaniyeh – Naghmeh – Bayat-e Raje – Oshshaq – Nahoft – Khajasteh – Neyshaburak – Hosseini – Iraq – Neyriz – Rahab and Masihi – Takht-e Taqdis – Shah Khatayi</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Nava in Abdollah Davami’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Nava in the book “Vocal Radif and Old Tasnifs according to Master Abdollah Davami” by Master Faramarz Payvar are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Gardaniyeh – Naghmeh – Neyshaburak – Oshshaq – Raje – Iraq – Nahoft – Khajasteh – Malek Hosseini – Hosseini – Neyriz Kabir – Rahab – Masihi – Naqus – Takht-e Taqdis – Shah Khatayi</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Nava in Abbas Kazemi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Nava mentioned in Abbas Kazemi’s vocal radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Nava – Rast – Kaykavusi – Takht-e Taqdis – Rast Nava – Palizban – Shah Khatayi Zi Khatayi – Gosht – Qeysari – Nakisa – Hossein Azarbaijani – Buselik – Masnavi Nava</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Nava in A’lam al-Soltan’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Nava mentioned in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi from the radif attributed to “A’lam al-Soltan” are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Naghmeh – Gardaniyeh or Garduniyeh – Gosht – Nahoft – Bayat-e Raje – Oshshaq – Iraq – Mohayyer – Hazineh – Zanguleh – Neyshaburak – Malek Hosseini – Khajasteh – Majusli – Zamineh Hossein – Hossein – Buselik – [Reng] Nastari – Rahab – Ashiran – Neyriz Saghir</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Nava in Abolhasan Saba’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Nava mentioned in Abolhasan Saba’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab – First Daramad – Second Daramad (Kereshmeh) – Bayat-e Raje – Neyshaburak – Nahoft – Masihi (Gusheh of Masihi) – Hosseini – Buselik – Neyriz – Gardaniyeh – Naghmeh – Oshshaq – Hazineh – Gosht – Ashiran – Majusli – Khajasteh</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Nava in Faramarz Payvar’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Nava mentioned in Faramarz Payvar’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Second Daramad – Panjeh Kordi – Third Daramad – Masihi – Do-ta Yeki Mezrab – Naqus – Delaviz – Nahoft – Chaharmezrab – Hosseini or Malek Hosseini – Buselik – Shah Khatayi – Ashiran (Ashirun) – Bal-e Kabutar</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Nava in the First Volume of “The History of Iranian Music”</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Nava mentioned in the first volume of “Sar-gozasht-e Musiqi-ye Irani” quoted from <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a> are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramads – Gardaniyeh – Naghmeh – Bayat-e Raje – Oshshaq – Hazineh – Nahoft – Gosht – Ashiran – Neyshaburak – Majusli – Khajasteh – Hossein – Malek Hosseini – Buselik – Neyriz [Nahib, Iraq, Mohayyer, Ashuravand, Esfahank, Hazineh, Naghmeh, Zanguleh]</p>\n\n<h2>Analysis of the Gushehs of the Dastgah of Nava in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h2>\n\n<p>Now we examine the gushehs of the dastgah of Nava in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s radif mentioned earlier:</p>\n\n<p><strong>First Daramad gusheh</strong>: It begins on the note “G” (sol). This note also serves as the witness and concluding note. Temporary stops also occur on the third degree below the concluding note, i.e., “E” (mi).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Second Daramad gusheh</strong>: This daramad is similar to the first one, with the difference that it begins on the note before the concluding note, i.e., “F” (fa).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Chaharmezrab gusheh</strong>: It begins on the same degree and its base emphasizes the witness note, i.e., “G” (sol). The sonic range in this gusheh expands.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Naghmeh gusheh</strong>: It has a rhythmic pattern that begins on the note before the concluding note, i.e., “F” (fa). The witness and concluding note in this gusheh are also similar to the previous gushehs within the daramad range.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Gardaniyeh gusheh</strong>: It begins on “E koron” (mi koron). Temporary stop is on the same degree and the conclusion is on “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Gosht gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do), and concludes on “G” (sol). The second degree above the concluding note, i.e., “A” (la), appears in some phrases as lowered, i.e., “A koron” (la koron), but finally returns to its original state and concludes on the concluding note of the dastgah of Nava.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nahoft gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re). The sonic range in this gusheh also expands and finally concludes on the concluding note of Nava.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Bayat-e Raje gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do), and emphasizes the second degree above the concluding note, i.e., “A” (la). This gusheh also concludes on the concluding note of Nava, i.e., “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hazineh gusheh</strong>: Hazineh can be considered a continuation of Bayat-e Raje. It begins on the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do), and finally concludes on “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Oshshaq gusheh</strong>: It begins on the second degree below the concluding note. The third degree above the concluding note changes to “B” (si) in some phrases, which can be seen as preparing the ground for presenting the Iraq gusheh. The conclusion of this gusheh is also on “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Iraq gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fourth degree above the concluding note. The change of the third degree above the concluding note to “B bekar” (si bekar) is a feature of this gusheh. In this gusheh the note “C” (do) is strongly emphasized, so it can be considered to have the role of witness. The conclusion of this gusheh is on the second degree above the concluding note, i.e., “A” (la), with reference to the third degree “B bemol” (si bemol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mohayyer, Hazineh, and Zanguleh gushehs</strong>: These are actually continuations of the Iraq gusheh, and melodic movement in them is mostly similar to the Iraq gusheh. The Mohayyer gusheh begins on the concluding note of the dastgah of Nava, i.e., “G” (sol), and stops on the witness of Iraq. Hazineh also begins on the same note, i.e., “C” (do), and finally ends on “G” (sol). Zanguleh also has a rhythmic pattern that begins on the third degree above the concluding note of Nava, i.e., “A bekar” (la bekar), and ends on “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Neyshaburak gusheh</strong>: It begins on the note before the concluding note of Nava, i.e., “F” (fa), and stops on the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Khajasteh gusheh</strong>: It begins on the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “B bemol” (si bemol). Melodic movement mainly revolves around the fifth degree above the concluding note of Nava, i.e., “D” (re). The stop of this gusheh is also on the mentioned note, i.e., “D” (re).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Majusli gusheh</strong>: It begins on the sixth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “E” (mi). In this gusheh, like Khajasteh, the temporary stop and also the axis of melodic movement revolve around the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re). Majusli also stops on “D” (re).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Zamineh Hosseini gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fourth degree above the concluding note of Nava. This note is also considered the note before the witness. The stop of this gusheh is also on “D” (re).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hosseini gusheh</strong>: It begins on the concluding note and witness of the dastgah of Nava, i.e., “G” (sol). Temporary stops of its phrases are on “E koron” (mi koron), and its final stop is on the fourth degree below the concluding note of Nava, i.e., “D” (re).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Buselik gusheh</strong>: Like Hosseini, it begins from the conclusion of Nava and stops on the fourth degree below the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nastari gusheh</strong>: It actually has the form of a calm reng. The starting note in this gusheh is the fourth degree below the concluding note of Nava, i.e., “D” (re), and its base is also formed on that note. This note also serves as the stop in this gusheh.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rahab gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fifth degree below or, in other words, the fourth degree above the concluding note of Nava and stops on “D” (re).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ashiran gusheh</strong>: Like the Rahab gusheh, it begins on “C” (do) and stops on “D” (re).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Neyriz gusheh</strong>: It begins on the concluding note of Nava, i.e., “G” (sol). Melodic movement revolves around this note. At the conclusion of this gusheh, it returns to the lower sonic range in the area of the Nava daramad and finally ends on the concluding note of the dastgah of Nava, i.e., “G” (sol).</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the gushehs of the dastgah of Nava, please share them in the comments section of the article with us to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-12-15 05:51:33', 0, '2024-12-19 19:12:36', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1535, 'posts', 78, 'en', NULL, 'Gushehs of Dastgah Chahargah | Examination of Chahargah gushehs in different radifs', NULL, 'This article examines the gushehs (melodic pieces) of the Chahargah mode in Persian classical music and compares their variations across different radifs (traditional repertoires). Various narrations and transcriptions—from masters such as Mirza Abdollah, Mirza Hossein Gholi, Ali-Akbar Shahnazi, Mousa Maroufi, Mahmoud Karimi, and Abdollah Davami—demonstrate that while the number and order of gushehs differ, many core elements like Daramad, Zabol, Hesar, Mokhalef, Maghlub, and Mansouri are commonly shared.\n\nThe analytical section focuses on the modal structure of Chahargah. The note “C” (Do) serves as both the tonic and central pitch, and the interval “A koron – C” is a defining characteristic. Gushehs such as Zabol and Naghme emphasize the tonic, while the Hesar group revolves around “G” (Sol). Mokhalef and Maghlub introduce modal variations centered on “A koron,” and Mansouri represents the climax of the mode. Finally, gushehs like Hodi, Pahlavi, and Rajaz guide the music back toward the main modal space (Daramad).', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine and analyze the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">gushehs of the dastgah of Chahargah</span></strong> and review the gushehs of Chahargah in various narrations of the radifs of Iranian music. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Chahargah in the Radif</h2>\n\n<p>In the various narrations of the radif according to different masters, the number of gushehs of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%da%86%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">dastgah of Chahargah</a> differs slightly. In the following, we examine these gushehs in several radifs:</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Chahargah in Mirza Abdollah’s Radif according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Chahargah in the different notations of Mirza Abdollah’s radif vary slightly. Below we present two versions:</p>\n\n<h4>Jean During’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>In the radif of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87/\">Mirza Abdollah</a> notated by Jean During, the following gushehs are mentioned for the dastgah of Chahargah:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Second Daramad – Third Daramad (Pish Zanguleh and Zanguleh) – Fourth Daramad – Naghmeh – Kereshmeh and Kereshmeh with Muye – Zang-e Shotor – Zabol – Basteh Negar – Muye – Hesār – Second Part – Third Part with Chaharmezrab – Pas Hesār – Muye – Mokhalef – Second Part – Hajiyani – Basteh Negar – Maghlub – Naghmeh Maghlub – Hazineh – Hodi – Pahlavi – Rajaz – Mansuri – Second Part – Third Part – Lezgi – Matn and Hashiyeh – Reng-e Shahr Ashub</p>\n\n<h4>Dariush Talaei’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>The dastgah of Chahargah in Dariush Talaei’s book “Tahlil-e Radif,” based on Mirza Abdollah’s radif, includes the following gushehs:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Second Daramad – Third Daramad: Pish Zanguleh and Zanguleh – Fourth Daramad – Naghmeh – Kereshmeh – Kereshmeh with Muye – Zang-e Shotor – Zabol – Basteh Negar – Muye – Hesār – Hesār, Second Part – Hesār, Third Part – Hesār, Fourth Part – Chaharmezrab of Hesār – Pas Hesār – Muye – Mokhalef – Hajiyani – Basteh Negar – Maghlub – Naghmeh – Hazineh – Hodi – Pahlavi – Rajaz – Mansuri – Mansuri, Second Part – Mansuri, Third Part – Lezgi – Matn and Hashiyeh – Reng-e Shahr Ashub</p>\n\n<h4>Gushehs Mentioned in the Book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi</h4>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Chahargah mentioned in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi, from the radif attributed to “Mirza Abdollah” according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Second Daramad – Third Daramad [Pish Zanguleh and Zanguleh] – Fourth Daramad – Naghmeh – Kereshmeh – Kereshmeh with Muye – Zang-e Shotor – Zabol – Basteh Negar – Muye – Hesār – Second Part of Hesār – Third Part of Hesār [with Chaharmezrab] – Pas Hesār – Muye – Mokhalef – Hajiyani – Basteh Negar – Maghlub – Naghmeh Maghlub – Hazineh – Hodi – Pahlavi – Rajaz – Mansuri – Second Part of Mansuri – Third Part of Mansuri – Reng-e Lezgi – Reng-e Matn – Reng-e Hashiyeh – Reng-e Shahr Ashub</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Chahargah in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Chahargah in the book “Radif of Mirza Hossein-Qoli according to Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Pirniakan are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Second Daramad – Third Daramad – Fourth Daramad with Zanguleh – Fifth Daramad – Sixth Daramad with Muye – Naghmeh – First Zabol – Second Zabol, Basteh Negar and Muye – Third Zabol – Mo’arebed – Pish Hesār – Pas Hesār – Chaharmezrab – Haft Hesār – Zanguleh – Muye Hesār and Forud – Maghlub – Mokhalef – Basteh Negar – Naghmeh Mokhalef – Hazineh – Muye Mokhalef – Mansuri – Hodi – Pahlavi – Rajaz or Arjuzeh</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Chahargah in Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi’s Advanced Course Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Chahargah in the book “Advanced Course Radif of Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Talaei are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab – Kereshmeh – Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Zabol – First Chaharmezrab of Zabol – Continuation of Avaz of Zabol with Kereshmeh – Second Chaharmezrab of Zabol – Continuation of Avaz of Zabol – Hesār – Forud – Mokhalef – Chaharmezrab of Mokhalef – Continuation of Mokhalef – Kereshmeh and Maghlub – Forud – Mansuri – Mansuri (Hodi)</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Chahargah in Musa Khan Ma’rufi’s Radif of Seven Dastgahs</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Chahargah mentioned in the book “Radif of Seven Dastgahs of Iranian Music” by Musa Khan Ma’rufi, edited by Mehdi Barkeshli, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab – First Daramad – Second Daramad – Third Daramad – Fourth Daramad – Muye – Kereshmeh – Pish Zanguleh – Zanguleh – Muye Saghir – Naghmeh – Kereshmeh – Zang-e Shotor – First Part of Zabol – Panjeh Muye – Second Part of Zabol – Basteh Negar – Third Part of Zabol – Zanguleh – Avaz Muye – Fourth Part of Zabol – First Part of Hesār – Second Part of Hesār – Zanguleh – Naghmeh – Third Part of Hesār – Do-ta Yeki – Chaharmezrab – Kereshmeh – Hozzan – Pas Hesār – Mo’arebed – First Part of Mokhalef – Second Part of Mokhalef – Hajiyani – Basteh Negar – Maghlub – Maghlub, Another Part – Naghmeh – Dobeyti – Kereshmeh – Hazineh – Hozzan – Muye – Hodi – Pahlavi – Rajaz or Arjuzeh – Mansuri – Panjeh Muye – Zanguleh – Naghmeh – Kohkeluyeh – Kereshmeh – Forud – Lezgi – Hashiyeh – Matn – Shahr Ashub – Hesār – Hozzan – Maghlub – Shalakh</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Chahargah in Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Chahargah in the book “Javab-e Avaz based on Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif” by Arshad Tahmasbi are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Zabol – Hesār – Forud – Mokhalef – Mokhalef to Maghlub – Mansuri – Hodi and Pahlavi – Rajaz – Masnavi</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Chahargah in Abdollah Davami’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Chahargah in the book “Vocal Radif and Old Tasnifs according to Master Abdollah Davami” by Master Faramarz Payvar are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Zabol and Basteh Negar – Panjeh Muye – Rajaz – Hodi – Pahlavi – Hesār – Mokhalef – Kereshmeh – Mirzai – Maghlub – Naghmeh Maghlub – Hozzan – Panjeh Muye – Mansuri</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Chahargah in Abbas Kazemi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Chahargah mentioned in Abbas Kazemi’s vocal radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Chahargah – Nushin Laban – Golriz – Shab-e Farrokh – Zabol – Shahin – Muye – Mokhalef – Maghlub – Hesār – Hodi – Rajaz – Arjuzeh 1 – Arjuzeh 2 – Mansuri – Dogah – Masnavi Mokhalef – Shadbaz</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Chahargah in A’lam al-Soltan’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Chahargah mentioned in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi from the radif attributed to “A’lam al-Soltan” are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Pishdaramad – Three Daramads – Daneshvar’s Chaharmezrab – Naghmeh – Three Zabol – Muye – Pish Hesār – Haft Hesār – Pas Hesār – Maghlub – Mokhalef – Hozzan – Muye Mokhalef – Mansuri – Hodi – Pahlavi – Arjuzeh</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Chahargah in Abolhasan Saba’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Chahargah mentioned in Abolhasan Saba’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab – Avaz of Chahargah (First Daramad) – Pish Zanguleh – Zabol – Hesār – Zanguleh (in Hesār) – Chaharmezrab of Hesār – Mokhalef – Maghlub – Zang-e Shotor (Naghmeh Maghlub) – Mansuri – Zarbi Khatameh</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Chahargah in Faramarz Payvar’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Chahargah mentioned in Faramarz Payvar’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Sar Navazi – Second Daramad (Kereshmeh) – Badr (Naghmeh) – Pish Zanguleh (Naghmeh) – Zanguleh – Zabol – Basteh Negar – Kereshmeh (Zabol) – Chaharmezrab – Par-e Parastu – Muye – Daramad of Mokhalef – Zarbi Mokhalef – Hodi – Pahlavi – Rajaz (Arjuzeh)</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Chahargah in the First Volume of “The History of Iranian Music”</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Chahargah mentioned in the first volume of “Sar-gozasht-e Musiqi-ye Irani” quoted from <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a> are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramads – Pish Zanguleh – Zanguleh – Naghmeh – Zabol – Basteh Negar – Muye – Hesār – Mokhalef – Maghlub – Hodi – Pahlavi – Rajaz (Arjuzeh) – Mansuri</p>\n\n<h2>Analysis of the Gushehs of the Dastgah of Chahargah in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h2>\n\n<p>Now we examine the gushehs of the dastgah of Chahargah in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s radif mentioned earlier:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Daramad gushehs</strong>: The daramads of the dastgah of Chahargah revolve around the note “C” (do), which serves as both the concluding note and the witness of this dastgah. The starting note in them is the fourth degree below the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol). The main structural interval in the dastgah of Chahargah is a neutral third (A koron – C), which is especially used and emphasized in cadential figures.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Muye gusheh</strong>: It begins on the note before the concluding note, i.e., “B” (si). The witness and concluding note in this gusheh are also similar to the daramad. The fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol), is sometimes presented slightly lowered (G koron).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Naghmeh gusheh</strong>: It has a rhythmic pattern that begins on “C” (do) and concludes on the same note.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Zabol gusheh</strong>: It begins on “C” (do) and concludes on the same note. The third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “E” (mi), is emphasized in this gusheh, so it can be considered to have the role of witness.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mo’arebed gusheh</strong>: It begins on the second degree below the concluding note, i.e., “B” (si), and concludes on “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Pish Hesār, Pas Hesār, Chaharmezrab, and Haft Hesār gushehs</strong>: These gushehs form a set in which the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol), serves as the witness. The change in interval ratios in this set is such that it forms two dangs of Chahargah around “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p>The starting note in Pish Hesār is “A” (la), in Pas Hesār it is “E koron” (mi koron), and in Chaharmezrab it is “G” (sol). These gushehs stop on “G” (sol). The last gusheh in this set is Haft Hesār, which begins on “E koron” (mi koron) and finally stops on “A koron” (la koron).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Zanguleh gusheh</strong>: It has a rhythmic pattern that begins on “G” (sol) and stops on “D” (re).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Muye Hesār gusheh</strong>: The next gusheh is Muye Hesār, which is accompanied by a forud to the daramad mode.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Maghlub gusheh</strong>: It begins on “A koron” (la koron), the sixth degree above the concluding note of the dastgah of Chahargah. This note can be considered the witness of this gusheh. The conclusion of this gusheh is on “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mokhalef gusheh</strong>: It begins on the sixth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “A koron” (la koron), and is also considered its witness. The temporary stop of this gusheh is on the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “F” (fa).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Basteh Negar and Naghmeh Mokhalef gushehs</strong>: They have rhythmic patterns and stop on “F” (fa), with melodic movement revolving around the witness “A koron” (la koron).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hazineh gusheh</strong>: After emphasis on “A koron” (la koron), it finally concludes on “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Muye Mokhalef gusheh</strong>: It begins on the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “E” (mi). One of the features of this gusheh is the microtonal change of the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol) to “G koron” (sol koron). The conclusion of this gusheh and also the axis of melodic movement is on “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mansuri gusheh</strong>: It is considered the ouj (climax) of the dastgah of Chahargah, whose witness is the same as the witness of the daramad in the lower octave, i.e., “C” (do). This gusheh begins on the second degree above the witness and finally descends to the witness of the daramad, i.e., “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hodi gusheh</strong>: This gusheh begins on “C” (do) and concludes on the same note. Emphasis on the structural neutral third interval (A koron – C) is repeated in this gusheh.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Pahlavi gusheh</strong>: It begins on the sixth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “A” (la), and temporarily stops on “E” (mi), but finally, with emphasis on the structural interval (A koron – C), it concludes on “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rajaz gusheh</strong>: It begins with an expansion of the sonic range and at the end, with the use of the lower range, descends to the daramad area.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the gushehs of the dastgah of Chahargah, please share them in the comments section of the article with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a> to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-12-21 11:49:10', 0, '2024-12-19 19:12:12', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1536, 'posts', 79, 'en', NULL, 'Gushehs of Dastgah Homayun | Examination of Homayun gushehs in different radifs', NULL, 'This article explores the gushehs (melodic pieces) of the Homayoun mode in Persian classical music across different radif traditions. It highlights that the number and sequence of gushehs vary depending on the روایت (interpretation) of masters such as Mirza Abdollah, Mirza Hossein Gholi, Saba, and others. Key gushehs include Daramad, Chaharkavok, Bidād, Ney Davood, Shushtari, Bakhtiari, and Mansouri.\n\nIn the analytical section (based on Mirza Hossein Gholi’s radif), the modal structure of Homayoun is examined. The note “Sol” functions as the final (tonic), while “La koron” serves as the central tone (shahed). Each gusheh has distinct melodic characteristics:\n\nChakavak emphasizes the note “Do,”\nBidād and Ney Davood focus on “Re,”\nSuz-o-Godaz revolves around “La koron,”\nand gushehs like Zabol, Bayat Ajam, and Bahr-e Noor shift toward modal spaces similar to Segah.\n\nAdditionally, some gushehs such as Ozzal and Danaseri evoke the atmosphere of the Shur mode, while others like Shushtari and Bakhtiari guide the music back to the main Homayoun mode through cadential movement (forud).', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">gushehs of the dastgah of Homayun</span></strong> and review the gushehs of Homayun in various narrations of the radifs of Iranian music. Please continue with the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>...</p>\n\n<h2>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Homayun in the Radif</h2>\n\n<p>In the various narrations of the radif according to different masters, the number of gushehs of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%87%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c%d9%88%d9%86/\">dastgah of Homayun</a> differs slightly. In the following, we examine these gushehs in several radifs:</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Homayun in Mirza Abdollah’s Radif according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Homayun in the different notations of Mirza Abdollah’s radif vary slightly. Below we present two versions:</p>\n\n<h4>Jean During’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>In the radif of Mirza Abdollah notated by Jean During, the following gushehs are mentioned for the dastgah of Homayun:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab – First Daramad – Second Daramad (Zang-e Shotor) – Mawaliyan – Chekavak – Tarz – Bidad – Bidad Kot – Ney-Davud – Bavi – Suz o Godaz – Abolchap – Leyli o Majnun – Ravandi – Nowruz Arab – Nowruz Saba – Nowruz Khara – Nafir – Farang and Shushtari Gardan – Shushtari – Jameh Daran – Raz o Niyaz – Meygoli – Mo’alef – Bakhtiari with Mo’alef – Ozal – Denaseri – Reng-e Farah</p>\n\n<h4>Dariush Talaei’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>The dastgah of Homayun in Dariush Talaei’s book “Tahlil-e Radif,” based on Mirza Abdollah’s radif, includes the following gushehs:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab – First Daramad – Second Daramad – Mawaliyan – Chekavak – Tarz – Bidad – Bidad Kot – Ney-Davud – Bavi with Chaharmezrab – Suz o Godaz – Abolchap – Leyli o Majnun – Ravandi – Nowruz Arab – Nowruz Saba – Nowruz Khara – Nafir – Farang with Shushtari Gardan – Shushtari – Jameh Daran – Raz o Niyaz – Meygoli – Mo’alef – Bakhtiari with Mo’alef – Ozal – Denaseri – Reng-e Farah</p>\n\n<h4>Gushehs Mentioned in the Book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi</h4>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Homayun mentioned in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi, from the radif attributed to “Mirza Abdollah” according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab – First Daramad – Second Daramad or Daramad of Zang-e Shotor – Mawaliyan – Chekavak – Tarz – Bidad – Bidad Kot – Ney-Davud – Bavi – Suz o Godaz – Abolchap – Leyli o Majnun – Ravandi – Nowruz Arab – Nowruz Saba – Nowruz Khara – Nafir – Farang with Shushtari Gardan – Shushtari – Jameh Daran – Raz o Niyaz – Meygoli – Bakhtiari with Mo’alef – Ozal – Denaseri – Reng-e Farah</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Homayun in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Homayun in the book “Radif of Mirza Hossein-Qoli according to Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Pirniakan are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Zang-e Shotor or Naqus – Chekavak – Gusheh of Chahargah – Mawaliyan – Bidad – Ney-Davud – Bavi – Suz o Godaz – Abolchap – Ravandi – Leyli o Majnun – Tarz – Nowruz Arab – Nowruz Saba – Nowruz Khara – Nafir – Jameh Daran – Mo’alef – Zabol – Bayat-e Ajam – Bahr-e Nour – Gusheh of Mansuri – Mobarqa’ – Sepehr – Ozal – Denaseri – Zanguleh – Shushtari Gardan – Shushtari and Mansuri – Bakhtiari – Muye Bakhtiari</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Homayun in Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi’s Advanced Course Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Homayun in the book “Advanced Course Radif of Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Talaei are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Kereshmeh – Chaharmezrab – Kereshmeh – Chaharmezrab – Chekavak – Chaharmezrab of Chekavak – Bidad – Chaharmezrab of Bidad – Bayat-e Raje (Continuation of Bidad) – Chaharmezrab of Bayat-e Raje – Shushtari and Mansuri – Zarbi Mansuri – Bakhtiari – Forud</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Homayun in Musa Khan Ma’rufi’s Radif of Seven Dastgahs</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Homayun mentioned in the book “Radif of Seven Dastgahs of Iranian Music” by Musa Khan Ma’rufi, edited by Mehdi Barkeshli, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab – Daramad – Kereshmeh – Zang-e Shotor – Gusheh of Chahargah – Mawaliyan – Chekavak – Naghmeh of Chekavak – Forud – Bidad – Forud – Bidad, Another Part – Bidad, Another Part – Ney-Davud – Bavi – Chaharmezrab – Abolchap – Ravandi – Moreh – Leyli o Majnun – Gusheh of Tarz – Nowruz Arab – Ravandi – Nowruz Saba – Nowruz Khara – Nafir – Farang – Zabol – Bayat-e Ajam – Bahr-e Nour – Ozal – Denaseri – Zanguleh – Oshshaq – Oshshaq Mohammad Sadeq Khani – Shushtari – Second Daramad – Meygoli – Marvdashti – Behbahani – Gusheh of Mansuri – Forud to Shushtari – Bakhtiari – Bakhtiari, Another Part – Mo’alef – Delnavaz – Forud – Gusheh of Neyriz – Forud to Homayun – Jameh Daran – Reng-e Farah – Reng-e Shahr Ashub</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Homayun in Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Homayun in the book “Javab-e Avaz based on Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif” by Arshad Tahmasbi are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Chekavak – Leyli o Majnun – Shushtari – Tarz – Ney-Davud – Bidad – Bayat-e Raje – Raz o Niyaz – Raz o Niyaz Another Type – Shushtari Another Type – Bakhtiari and Mo’alef</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Homayun in Abdollah Davami’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Homayun in the book “Vocal Radif and Old Tasnifs according to Master Abdollah Davami” by Master Faramarz Payvar are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Daramad of Chekavak – Chekavak – Leyli o Majnun – Shushtari – Dezfuli – Bakhtiari – Mo’alef – Gusheh of Tarz – Raz o Niyaz – Ney-Davud – Bidad – Dad – Ouj – Mawaliyan</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Homayun in Abbas Kazemi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Homayun mentioned in Abbas Kazemi’s vocal radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Homayun – Bavi – Mokhalefak – Mawaliyan 1 – Bakhtiari – Chekavak – Basteh Negar – Mawaliyan 2 – Rak-e Kashmir – Bidad – Shapsandi – Boyerahmadi – Zarbi Boyerahmadi – Nahoft – Nafir – Zarbi Nafir – Shushtari – Ebrahimi – Bagh-e Ardeshir – Dezfuli – Shahin – Nasir Khani – Kin-e Siavash – Mah-e Razayi – Tangestani – Ganj-e Badavard – Suz o Godaz – Sham’uni – Leyli o Majnun – Jameh Daran – Mansuri – Hodi – Kohkeluyeh – Changi – Masnavi Shushtari – Dashti Arjan</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Homayun in A’lam al-Soltan’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Homayun mentioned in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi from the radif attributed to “A’lam al-Soltan” are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Pishdaramad – Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Naghmeh – Chekavak – Mutawaliyan – Bidad – Ney-Davud – Bavi – Suz o Godaz – Abolchap – Ravandi – Leyli o Majnun – Tarz – Nowruz Arab – Nowruz Saba – Nowruz Khara – Naghmeh Farang – Jameh Daran – Mo’alef – Zabol – Bayat-e Ajam – Mansuri – Bahreh-e Nour – Ozal – Denaseri – Zanguleh – Shushtari Gardan – Shushtari – Bakhtiari – Muye Bakhtiari</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Homayun in Abolhasan Saba’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Homayun mentioned in Abolhasan Saba’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab – Avaz of Homayun (First Daramad) – Second Daramad – Chekavak (Third Daramad) – Bidad – Zarbi Oshshaq – Forud to Homayun – Tarz (Gusheh of Tarz) – Bakhtiari – Shushtari – Zarbi Shushtari – Dezfuli – Khatameh – Saqi Nameh (Sufi Nameh)</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Homayun in Faramarz Payvar’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Homayun mentioned in Faramarz Payvar’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Second Daramad – Zang-e Shotor – Tarz – Parvaneh – Naghmeh – Ney-Davud – Mo’alef – Chaharmezrab – Bavi (Babavi) – Raz o Niyaz – Ravandi (Rahavandi) – Zanguleh – Meygoli – Delnavaz – Khatameh</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Homayun in the First Volume of “The History of Iranian Music”</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Homayun mentioned in the first volume of “Sar-gozasht-e Musiqi-ye Irani” quoted from <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a> are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramads – Mawaliyan – Chekavak – Tarz – Bidad – Ney-Davud – Bavi – Abolchap – Leyli o Majnun – Ravandi – Nowruz Arab – Nowruz Saba – Nowruz Khara – Nafir – Farang – Shushtari – Jameh Daran – Bakhtiari – Mo’alef – Ozal – Denaseri – Bayat-e Esfahan – Bayat-e Raje (Rajeh) – Suz o Godaz – Raz o Niyaz – Masnavi</p>\n\n<h2>Analysis of the Gushehs of the Dastgah of Homayun in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h2>\n\n<p>Now we examine the gushehs of the dastgah of Homayun in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s radif mentioned earlier:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Daramad gusheh</strong>: The daramad of Homayun begins on the note “G” (sol). This note also serves as the concluding note, and melodic movement revolves around the witness note, i.e., “A koron” (la koron).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Chaharmezrab and Zang-e Shotor gushehs</strong>: They are presented within the same mode. The starting note in the chaharmezrab is the second degree below the concluding note, i.e., “F” (fa), and in the Zang-e Shotor gusheh it is the third degree below the concluding note, i.e., “E koron” (mi koron).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Chekavak gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do). This note also serves as the witness of Chekavak. The concluding note in this gusheh is also “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Chahargah gusheh</strong>: It begins on the sixth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “E koron” (mi koron). The witness and stopping note in this gusheh is the same note “G” (sol), which is presented in the lower octave. The interval ratios change in this gusheh and, as its name suggests, it shifts to Chahargah on “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mawaliyan gusheh</strong>: It begins on “E koron” (mi koron) and ends on the concluding note of Homayun, i.e., “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Bidad gusheh</strong>: It begins on the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “B” (si). The fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re), serves as the witness, and the forud occurs on the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ney-Davud gusheh</strong>: It begins on the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “B” (si). The witness note in this gusheh is also the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re), and the definite conclusion of this gusheh is on “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Bavi gusheh</strong>: It has a rhythmic pattern in the form of chaharmezrab whose base is on the witness note of Bidad and Ney-Davud, i.e., “D” (re). The temporary stop of this gusheh is on the witness of the daramad, i.e., “A koron” (la koron).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Suz o Godaz gusheh</strong>: It begins on the witness note of the daramad, i.e., “A koron” (la koron). Melodic movement in this gusheh revolves around this note, and its conclusion is on “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Abolchap gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do). This note serves as the stop, and the main range of melodic movement is mainly within the axis “A koron – C.”</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ravandi gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re). The fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do), also serves as the witness. The conclusion of this gusheh is on “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Leyli o Majnun gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do). Some phrases temporarily stop on the sixth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “E bemol” (mi bemol). The witness note of the daramad, i.e., “A koron” (la koron), is also emphasized in this gusheh. The conclusion of this gusheh is also on “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Tarz gusheh</strong>: It also begins on the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do). The main axis of melodic movement is within the dang “C – G.” During the forud on “G” (sol), the lower dang below the concluding note is also used.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nowruz Arab gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do). Some phrases temporarily stop on the same note. The sixth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “E bemol” (mi bemol), appears in some phrases as “E koron” (mi koron). The stopping note at the end of this gusheh is also the fourth degree above the concluding note of Homayun, i.e., “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nowruz Saba gusheh</strong>: It begins on the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “B” (si). The melodic movement and stop of this gusheh are also like the previous gusheh on the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nowruz Khara gusheh</strong>: Like Nowruz Saba, it begins on the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “B” (si). In this gusheh too, the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do), is the main axis of melodic movement and also the stopping note.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nafir gusheh</strong>: It begins on the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “B” (si). The temporary stop in this gusheh is on the witness of Homayun, i.e., “A koron” (la koron). The sixth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “E bemol” (mi bemol), also appears as “E koron” (mi koron).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Jameh Daran gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re), and its temporary stop is on the witness of Homayun, i.e., “A koron” (la koron).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mo’alef gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do). Melodic movement revolves around the witness note of Homayun, i.e., “A koron” (la koron). In this gusheh the third and fourth degrees above the concluding note temporarily change, i.e., they appear as “B koron” and “C bemol,” but finally return to their original state and conclude on “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Zabol gusheh</strong>: It begins on the sixth degree above the concluding note, which is used as “E koron,” and stops on “G” (sol). Melodic movement revolves around the notes “G – E koron.” The final figure of this gusheh, with emphasis on the neutral third interval (C – E koron), evokes the dastgah of Segah.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Bayat-e Ajam gusheh</strong>: Like Zabol, it begins on the sixth degree above the concluding note of Homayun, i.e., “E koron,” and the same interval ratios are used. Melodic movement revolves around “G” (sol) (the octave of the concluding note of Homayun) and stops on the fourth degree above the concluding note of Homayun, i.e., “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Bahr-e Nour gusheh</strong>: It begins on the third degree below the concluding note, which is changed to “B koron,” and stops on “E koron.” The interval ratios and melodic movement in this gusheh are also like the previous gushehs in the mode of Segah.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mansuri gusheh</strong>: It begins on the third degree above the main concluding note of Homayun in the lower octave, i.e., “B bemol,” and stops on “E koron.” Melodic movement revolves around “G” (sol). This gusheh is also still in the mode of Segah.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mobarqa’ gusheh</strong>: It manifests in the mode of Segah. The starting note and temporary stop in this gusheh is “E koron.”</p>\n\n<p><strong>Sepehr gusheh</strong>: It actually represents the return to Homayun. The interval ratios in this gusheh return to the Homayun mode. The starting note is the seventh degree above the concluding note and it ends on “G” (sol), the concluding note of Homayun.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ozal gusheh</strong>: It begins on “G” (sol) in the lower octave and stops on the same note. The interval ratios and manner of melodic movement evoke the dastgah of Shur.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Denaseri gusheh</strong>: It begins on “E koron” and ends on “D” (re). This gusheh also evokes the dastgah of Shur with base “D.”</p>\n\n<p><strong>Zanguleh gusheh</strong>: It has a rhythmic pattern that begins on the octave of the concluding note of Homayun, i.e., “G” (sol), and finally ends on the same note “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Shushtari Gardan gusheh</strong>: It evokes the definite return to the dastgah of Homayun. The starting note is the second degree below the concluding note, i.e., “F” (fa), and the concluding note is the same as the ending note of Homayun, i.e., “G” (sol). The range of melodic movement, with emphasis on the witness of Homayun and also the concluding note of Homayun, causes the return to the daramad mode.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Shushtari and Mansuri gushehs</strong>: They begin from the conclusion of the dastgah of Homayun. Melodic movement in this gusheh mainly takes place in the upper dang above the concluding note “G – C” and finally ends on “G” (sol). One of the features of this gusheh is the emphasis on the fourth degree above the concluding note and the change in interval ratios to “D koron” and “E bekar” in some phrases, which return to their original state during the forud.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Bakhtiari gusheh</strong>: It begins on the concluding note of Homayun, i.e., “G” (sol), and stops on “E koron” in the lower octave.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Muye Bakhtiari gusheh</strong>: It is actually the continuation of the previous gusheh, in which the forud to the daramad mode of Homayun is performed. The temporary change in interval ratios, especially “B bemol” and “A koron,” is a feature of this gusheh, which in the final part returns to the original state and ends on the concluding note of Homayun.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the gushehs of the dastgah of Homayun, please share them in the comments section of the article with us to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-12-21 12:46:41', 0, '2024-12-19 19:11:50', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1537, 'posts', 80, 'en', NULL, 'Gushehs of Dastgah Rast-Panjgah | Examination of Rast-Panjgah gushehs in different radifs', NULL, 'This article examines the gushehs (melodic pieces) of the Rast-Panjgah mode in different radifs of Persian classical music. It highlights that while the number and order of gushehs vary across narrations by masters such as Mirza Abdollah, Mirza Hossein-Gholi, Ali-Akbar Shahnazi, Mousa Maroufi, and others, their overall structure remains comparable.\n\nIn the analytical section (based on Mirza Hossein-Gholi’s radif), the modal and melodic characteristics of each gusheh are discussed. Rast-Panjgah begins with a daramad centered on the note F (Fa) and gradually modulates through various gushehs such as Panjgah, Oshaq, Zabol, Bayat Ajam, Bahr-e Noor, and Mansouri, moving into other modal spaces like Shur, Segah, Homayun, and Esfahan.\n\nAdditional gushehs like Iraq, Mohayyer, Ashuravand, and Esfahanak enrich the modal diversity, followed by further developments in sections such as Nowruz variants, Nafir, and Rak. Finally, through Forud and gushehs like Parvaneh and Neyriz, the music returns to the main modal framework. Overall, Rast-Panjgah is considered one of the most complex Persian modes, characterized by its rich modulations and structural diversity.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">gushehs of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah</span></strong> and review the gushehs of Rast Panjgah in various narrations of the radifs of Iranian music. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Rast Panjgah in the Radif</h2>\n\n<p>In the various narrations of the radif according to different masters, the number of gushehs of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b1%d8%a7%d8%b3%d8%aa-%d9%be%d9%86%d8%ac%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">dastgah of Rast Panjgah</a> differs slightly. In the following, we examine these gushehs in several radifs:</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Rast Panjgah in Mirza Abdollah’s Radif according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah in the different notations of Mirza Abdollah’s radif vary slightly. Below we present two versions:</p>\n\n<h4>Jean During’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>In the radif of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87/\">Mirza Abdollah</a> notated by Jean During, the following gushehs are mentioned for the dastgah of Rast Panjgah:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad of Rast – Second Daramad (Zang-e Shotor) – Zanguleh Saghir and Kabir – Naghmeh – Khosravani – Ruh Afza – Panjgah – Sepehr – Oshshaq – Neyriz – Bal-e Kabutaran – Bayat-e Ajam – Bahr-e Nour – Qarcheh – Mobarqa’ – Tarz – Abolchap – Leyli o Majnun – Ravandi – Nowruz Arab – Nowruz Saba – Nowruz Khara – Ma’vara al-Nahr (from Aqa Hossein-Qoli’s radif) – Nafir – Farang.</p>\n\n<h4>Dariush Talaei’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>The dastgah of Rast Panjgah in Dariush Talaei’s book “Tahlil-e Radif,” based on Mirza Abdollah’s radif, includes the following gushehs:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad (Rast) – Second Daramad (Daramad of Zang-e Shotor) – Zanguleh Saghir and Kabir – Naghmeh – Khosravani – Ruh Afza – Panjgah – Sepehr – Oshshaq – Neyriz – Bal-e Kabutaran – Bayat-e Ajam – Bahr-e Nour – Qarcheh – Mobarqa’ – Tarz – Abolchap – Leyli o Majnun – Ravandi – Nowruz Arab – Nowruz Saba – Nowruz Khara – Ma’vara al-Nahr – Nafir – Farang.</p>\n\n<h4>Gushehs Mentioned in the Book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi</h4>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah mentioned in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi, from the radif attributed to “Mirza Abdollah” according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Second Daramad, Zang-e Shotor – Zanguleh – Naghmeh – Khosravani – Ruh Afza – Panjgah – Sepehr – Oshshaq – Neyriz – Bayat-e Ajam – Bahr-e Nour – Qarcheh – Mobarqa’ – Tarz – Abolchap – Leyli o Majnun – Ravandi – Nowruz Arab – Nowruz Saba – Nowruz Khara – Ma’vara al-Nahr – Nafir – Farang.</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Rast Panjgah in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Rast Panjgah in the book “Radif of Mirza Hossein-Qoli according to Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Pirniakan are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Zang-e Shotor or Naqus – Naghmeh – Ruh Afza – Parvaneh – Panjgah – Oshshaq – Zabol – Bayat-e Ajam – Bahr-e Nour – Mansuri – Mobarqa’ – Sepehr – Iraq – Mohayyer – Ashuravand – Esfahank – Hazineh – Zanguleh – Shushtari Gardan – Nowruz Arab – Nowruz Saba – Nowruz Khara – Nafir – Abolchap – Ravandi – Leyli o Majnun – Tarz – Parvaneh – Ma’vara al-Nahr – Rak-e Abdollah – Rak-e Hendi – Forud – Parvaneh – Neyriz Saghir – Neyriz Kabir – Parvaneh.</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Rast and Panjgah in Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi’s Advanced Course Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Rast and Panjgah in the book “Dastgah Rast va Panjgah from the Advanced Course Radif of Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Houshang Zarif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab – Daramad – Zang-e Shotor – Naghmeh – Ruh Afza – Panjgah and Sepehr – Oshshaq – Zabol – Bayat-e Ajam – Mansuri – Bahr-e Nour – Mobarqa’ – Sepehr – Nahib – Iraq – Mokhber (Master Shahnazi called the gusheh Mohayyer as Mokhber) – Ashuravand – Esfahank – Hazineh – Zanguleh – Shushtari Gardan – Nowruz Arab – Nowruz Saba – Nowruz Khara – Nafir – Abolchap – Ravandi – Leyli o Majnun – Tarz – Parvaneh – Ma’vara al-Nahr – Rak-e Hendi – Rak-e Abdollah – Neyriz – Neyriz Saghir – Parvaneh</p>\n\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> In the book “Advanced Course Radif of Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Talaei, the dastgah of Rast Panjgah is not included!</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Rast Panjgah in Musa Khan Ma’rufi’s Radif of Seven Dastgahs</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah mentioned in the book “Radif of Seven Dastgahs of Iranian Music” by Musa Khan Ma’rufi, edited by Mehdi Barkeshli, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Second Daramad – Zang-e Shotor – Zanguleh Saghir and Kabir – Parvaneh – Naghmeh – Khosravani – Ruh Afza – Neyriz – Panjgah – Sepehr – Oshshaq – Zabol – Bayat-e Ajam – Bahr-e Nour – Qarcheh – Mobarqa’ – Sepehr – Nahib – Iraq – Mohayyer – Ashur – Esfahank – Hazineh – Kereshmeh – Zanguleh – Parvaneh – Tarz – Naghmeh of Tarz – Avaz of Tarz – Abolchap – Leyli o Majnun – Nowruz Arab – Nowruz Saba – Nowruz Khara – Nafir and Farang – Ma’vara al-Nahr – Rak – Naghmeh of Rak – Safir of Rak – Rak-e Hendi – Chaharmezrab – Rak-e Kashmir – Rak-e Kashmir, Another Part – Rak-e Abdollah – Harbi – Reng-e Shahr Ashub</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Rast Panjgah in Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Rast Panjgah in the book “Javab-e Avaz based on Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif” by Arshad Tahmasbi are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Parvaneh – Naghmeh – Ruh Afza – Neyriz – Panjgah and Sepehr – Oshshaq – Bayat-e Ajam, Bahr-e Nour, Qarcheh, Mobarqa’, Sepehr, Forud – Iraq, Mohayyer, Nahib, Hazineh, Ashur, Forud – Tarz – Leyli o Majnun – Rak-e Hendi and Safir-e Rak – Rak-e Abdollah – Rak-e Kashmir.</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Rast Panjgah in Abdollah Davami’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the dastgah of Rast Panjgah in the book “Vocal Radif and Old Tasnifs according to Master Abdollah Davami” by Master Faramarz Payvar are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Second Daramad – Panjeh She’ri – Ruh Afza – Panjgah – Rast – Parvaneh – Naghmeh – Takhir Zir o Ru – Oshshaq – Sepehr – Neyriz – Repeated Sepehr – Bayat-e Ajam – Mohayyer – Qarcheh – Mobarqa’ – Tarz – Leyli o Majnun – Ma’vara al-Nahr – Another Type of Ma’vara al-Nahr – Rak – Safir – Rak-e Abdollah – Rak-e Kashmir</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Rast Panjgah in Abbas Kazemi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah mentioned in Abbas Kazemi’s vocal radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Nowruz Bozorg – Taqdis – Nowruz Khara – Basteh Negar – Suz o Godaz – Mah Bari – Parvaneh – Kuraoghli – Abol Malih – Ziraftand – Ashur – Bokharayi – Hadavandi – Koshteh o Mordeh – Mahur Saghir – Harbi – Panjgah – Jameh Daran – Nafir-e Farang – Rast – Nowruz Saba – Khavaran – Rahavandi – Malanazi – Hozni – Rak-e Hendi – Lezgi – Zanganeh – Bahr-e Nour – Sufi Nameh – Masnavi</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Rast Panjgah in A’lam al-Soltan’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah mentioned in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi from the radif attributed to “A’lam al-Soltan” are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Zang-e Shotor – Naghmeh – Ruh Afza – Panjgah – Parvaneh – Oshshaq – Zabol – Bayat-e Ajam – Mansuri – Bahreh-e Nour – Mobarqa’ – Sepehr – Mokhber – Ashuravand – Esfahank – Hazineh – Zanguleh – Shushtari Gardan – Nowruz Arab – Nowruz Saba – Nowruz Khara – Nafir – Abolchap – Ravandi – Leyli o Majnun – Tarz – Rak – Rak-e Hendi – Rak-e Abdollah – Neyriz – Neyriz Saghir</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Rast Panjgah in Abolhasan Saba’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah mentioned in Abolhasan Saba’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Chaharmezrab – Second Chaharmezrab – First Daramad – Second Daramad (Panjeh She’ri) – Zanguleh – Parvaneh (Bal-e Kabutaran) – Naghmeh – Khosravani – Ruh Afza – Neyriz – Panjgah – Oshshaq – Bayat-e Ajam – Mobarqa’ – Nahib – Kereshmeh – Tarz – Abolchap – Leyli o Majnun – Nowruz Arab – Nowruz Saba – Nowruz Khara – Rak – Safir-e Rak</p>\n\n<h3>Note on Faramarz Payvar’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p><strong>The dastgah of Rast Panjgah is not included in Faramarz Payvar’s santur radif!</strong></p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Dastgah of Rast Panjgah in the First Volume of “The History of Iranian Music”</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah mentioned in the first volume of “Sar-gozasht-e Musiqi-ye Irani” quoted from <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a> are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramads – Zanguleh – Parvaneh – Naghmeh – Ruh Afza – Khosravani – Panjgah – Sepehr – Oshshaq – Neyriz – Bayat-e Ajam – Mobarqa’ – Qarcheh – Bahr-e Nour – Abolchap – Leyli o Majnun – Ravandi – Nowruzs [Nowruz Arab, Nowruz Saba, Nowruz Khara] – Nafir – Farang – Iraq with all its attachments [Nahib, Iraq, Mohayyer, Ashuravand, Esfahank, Hazineh, Naghmeh, Zanguleh]</p>\n\n<h2>Analysis of the Gushehs of the Dastgah of Rast Panjgah in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h2>\n\n<p>Now we examine the gushehs of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s radif mentioned earlier:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Daramad gusheh</strong>: The daramad of Rast Panjgah is formed within the fifth interval “D – A” with melodic movement revolving around the note “F” (fa). The note “F” serves as the starting note, concluding note, and witness in the daramad. The second degree above the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol), is also strongly emphasized in the daramad of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Chaharmezrab gusheh</strong>: It is a rhythmic gusheh presented after the daramad and is also within the mode of the Rast Panjgah daramad. The starting note in the chaharmezrab is the second degree below the concluding note, i.e., “E” (mi). In this gusheh, the melodic range extends up to “D” (re). The witness and concluding note in this gusheh, like the daramad, is “F” (fa).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Naqus or Zang-e Shotor gusheh</strong>: It has a rhythmic pattern presented within the daramad range. The starting note in this gusheh is the third degree below the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Naghmeh gusheh</strong>: This gusheh also has a rhythmic pattern presented within the daramad mode. The main range of melodic movement consists of the two upper dangs above the concluding note.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ruh Afza gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do), and melodic movement takes place within the two upper dangs above the concluding note. The third degree in Ruh Afza is temporarily lowered microtonally (A koron), but at the end and in the forud section it returns to its original state (A bekar). The concluding note in this gusheh is also “F” (fa).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Parvaneh gusheh</strong>: It begins on the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “A” (la bekar), and concludes on “F” (fa). In the opening phrases of this gusheh, the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do), is also emphasized. The main range of melodic movement is formed within the fifth interval “F – C.”</p>\n\n<p><strong>Panjgah gusheh</strong>: Due to the microtonal lowering of the third degree above the concluding note (A koron) and the manner of melodic movement, it evokes a modal shift toward Shur. The opening pattern of this gusheh also evokes the gusheh of Zabol in the dastgah of Segah. First the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do), and then the sixth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re), are emphasized, but this gusheh finally returns the third degree above the concluding note to its original state (A bekar) and presents cadential figures of Rast Panjgah on “F” (fa).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Oshshaq gusheh</strong>: It begins similarly to the Panjgah gusheh. In this gusheh too, the third degree above the concluding note is lowered microtonally (A koron). After the opening phrases, a chaharmezrab is presented in this gusheh whose melodic movement revolves around “F” (fa). The stop of this gusheh is on the fifth degree above the concluding note of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah, i.e., “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Zabol gusheh</strong>: This gusheh begins on “F” (fa). The third and sixth degrees above the concluding note of Rast Panjgah are presented microtonally lower (A koron and D koron). In fact, in this gusheh a modal shift to Segah occurs, in which melodic movement revolves around “C” (do) and stops on “A koron.” The final figure of this gusheh also represents the structural third interval of Segah, i.e., “A koron – F.”</p>\n\n<p><strong>Bayat-e Ajam gusheh</strong>: It is in the mode of Segah. The starting note in this gusheh is “A koron,” and melodic movement revolves around “C” (do). The stopping note in the Bayat-e Ajam gusheh is “F” (fa).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Bahr-e Nour gusheh</strong>: It is in the mode of Segah and its starting note is “E bemol,” which in the lower register is presented a semitone higher (bekar). The stop in the Bahr-e Nour gusheh is on “A koron.”</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mansuri gusheh</strong>: It begins on “E bemol” in the lower register and is still in the mode of Segah. The stop in this gusheh is also on the previous note “A koron.”</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mobarqa’ gusheh</strong>: This gusheh is also in the mode of Segah. Its starting note is the same as the stopping note of the previous gusheh (A koron) and it stops on the same note.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Sepehr gusheh</strong>: It actually presents the forud from Segah to Rast Panjgah. In this gusheh the sixth degree above the concluding note returns to its original state (D bekar). The seventh degree above the concluding note is also presented as “E bekar.” The starting note in this gusheh is the second degree above the concluding note of Rast Panjgah, i.e., “G” (sol), and the final figure of this gusheh also represents the return to the dastgah of Rast Panjgah, stopping on “F” (fa).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Iraq gusheh</strong>: It begins on the concluding note of Rast Panjgah, “F” (fa). Melodic movement is formed within the upper dang “F – B flat” and the lower dang “C – F,” with emphasis on the common note between these two dangs. The Iraq gusheh stops on the third degree below the concluding note, “D” (re).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mohayyer gusheh</strong>: This gusheh can also be considered a continuation of the Iraq gusheh. It begins on the fourth degree below the concluding note and the beginning of Iraq, and stops on the concluding note of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ashuravand gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fourth degree above the concluding note, “B flat” (si bemol). Melodic movement in this gusheh is formed within the fifth interval “F – C” and finally stops on the concluding note of Rast Panjgah.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Esfahank gusheh</strong>: It begins on the second degree below the concluding note, “E” (mi). The main range of melodic movement in this gusheh is formed by the fourth interval “F – B flat.” Esfahank also stops on the concluding note of Rast Panjgah.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hazineh gusheh</strong>: It begins on the concluding note of the previous gusheh, “F” (fa). The melodic movement in this gusheh is actually formed by the sequential repetition of a main pattern that is presented in many dastgahs and avazes. The final stop of this gusheh is on “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Zanguleh gusheh</strong>: It has a rhythmic pattern that is presented in many dastgahs and avazes. This gusheh begins on the second degree below the concluding note of Rast Panjgah, i.e., “E” (mi). Melodic movement in the Zanguleh gusheh is based on the sequential repetition of the main pattern characteristic of this gusheh, which at the end, with the return to the lower range and stopping on the concluding note of Rast Panjgah, evokes the return from the Iraq space to the main space of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Shushtari Gardan gusheh</strong>: It begins on the note before the concluding note, i.e., “E” (mi). Melodic movement in this gusheh is presented in the lower sonic range and finally stops on the concluding note of Rast Panjgah.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nowruz Arab gusheh</strong>: In this gusheh a modal shift to Homayun occurs. The third degree above the concluding note is lowered (A koron – A bemol) and the sixth degree above the concluding note is also microtonally lowered (D koron). The two main poles of melodic movement in this gusheh are the notes “F” and “C.”</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nowruz Saba and Nowruz Khara gushehs</strong>: Like Nowruz Arab, they begin on the note before the concluding note, “E” (mi), and stop on “F” (fa).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nafir gusheh</strong>: It begins on the note before the concluding note of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah, i.e., “E” (mi). In this gusheh emphasis is placed on the fifth degree above the concluding note, “C” (do), and it finally stops on “F” (fa).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Abolchap, Ravandi, Leyli o Majnun, and Tarz gushehs</strong>: These gushehs are also presented in the mode of the dastgah of Homayun. The starting and stopping note in Abolchap is the main concluding note of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah, “F” (fa). The main range of melodic movement in this gusheh is formed within the dang “C – F.” The starting note in the Ravandi gusheh is the second degree above the concluding note, “G” (sol), and the stopping note in this gusheh is the fourth degree below the concluding note, “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p>The Leyli o Majnun gusheh begins on the concluding note of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah (“F”) and after emphasis on the third and fourth degrees below the concluding note, it finally stops on the fourth degree below, “C” (do). The Tarz gusheh is the concluding part of presenting the Homayun mode in the dastgah of Rast Panjgah. This gusheh begins on “F” (fa) and concludes on the same note in the lower register.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Parvaneh and Forud gushehs</strong>: They are presented to return to the main mode in the dastgah of Rast Panjgah. In this gusheh the altered notes from the previous gusheh (to create the Homayun space) return to their original state.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Parvaneh gusheh</strong>: It begins on the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “A bekar,” and stops on the concluding note of Rast Panjgah, “F” (fa).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ma’vara al-Nahr gusheh</strong>: In this gusheh another modal shift occurs and the third and sixth degrees above the concluding note change as in the previous gushehs. Melodic movement in this gusheh is presented in the lower sonic ranges and begins on “C” (do). The Ma’vara al-Nahr gusheh begins on “C” (do) and finally stops on the concluding note of Rast Panjgah, “F” (fa). The interval ratios and the temporary stop of some phrases on “A koron” evoke the space of the avaz of Esfahan and prepare the ground for performing the Rak gushehs.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rak-e Abdollah and Rak-e Hendi gushehs</strong>: They begin on “F” (fa) and stop on the fourth degree below, “C” (do). Temporary stops in some phrases of the two mentioned gushehs occur on the note before the concluding note, i.e., “E” (mi).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Forud gusheh</strong>: After performing the Rak-e Hendi gusheh, the forud to the dastgah of Rast Panjgah occurs. In this gusheh the altered degrees return to their original state and finally stop on “F” (fa).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Parvaneh gusheh</strong>: With the performance of this gusheh, the main space of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah is re-established.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Neyriz Saghir and Neyriz Kabir gushehs</strong>: With the microtonal change of the third degree above the concluding note (A koron) and emphasis on this note, another modal shift occurs, which finally, with the presentation of the Parvaneh gusheh, re-establishes the main space of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah and the final forud is performed.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the gushehs of the dastgah of Rast Panjgah, please share them in the comments section of the article with us to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-12-21 14:02:51', 0, '2024-08-30 19:49:51', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1538, 'posts', 81, 'en', NULL, 'Gushehs of Avaz Abu Ata | Examination of Abu Ata gushehs in different radifs', NULL, 'This article explores the gushehs of the Abu Ata vocal mode (Dastan al-Arab) in different radifs of Persian classical music. It shows that while the number and sequence of gushehs vary among masters such as Mirza Abdollah, Mirza Hossein-Gholi, Ali-Akbar Shahnazi, Mousa Maroufi, and others, their overall structure remains largely consistent.\n\nIn the analytical section (based on Mirza Hossein-Gholi’s radif), Abu Ata begins with daramads centered around G (as the tonal center) and resolving on D, followed by the development of melodic material through gushehs such as Sayakhi, Naghmeh, and Hijaz. The Hijaz section is particularly important, emphasizing the note A and including related gushehs like Basteh Negar, Bozorg, Koochak, and Chaharmezrab.\n\nFurther sections such as Tahrir Baghdadi, Samali, and Chaharpāreh (Chaharbāgh) add rhythmic and melodic variety. Finally, gushehs like Gabri, Shomali, and Gilaki lead to a conclusive descent back to D, reinforcing Abu Ata’s connection to the Shur mode. Overall, Abu Ata is a prominent and expressive vocal mode in Persian music, known for its emotional depth and coherent modal structure.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">gushehs of the avaz of Abu Ata</span></strong> (also known as <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Dastan al-Arab</span></strong>) and review the gushehs of Abu Ata in various narrations of the radifs of Iranian music. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>Gushehs of the Avaz of Abu Ata in the Radif</h2>\n\n<p>In the various narrations of the radif according to different masters, the number of gushehs of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%b9%d8%b7%d8%a7/\">avaz of Abu Ata</a> differs slightly. In the following, we examine these gushehs in several radifs:</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Abu Ata in Mirza Abdollah’s Radif according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Abu Ata in the different notations of Mirza Abdollah’s radif vary slightly. Below we present two versions:</p>\n\n<h4>Jean During’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>In the radif of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87/\">Mirza Abdollah</a> notated by Jean During, the following gushehs are mentioned for the avaz of Abu Ata:</p>\n\n<p>Ramkeli – Daramad – Sayakhi – Hejaz – Basteh Negar – Yaqulun – Chaharpareh – Gabri – Ghamangiz – Gilaki</p>\n\n<h4>Dariush Talaei’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>The avaz of Abu Ata in Dariush Talaei’s book “Tahlil-e Radif,” based on Mirza Abdollah’s radif, includes the following gushehs:</p>\n\n<p>Ramkeli – Daramad – Sayakhi – Hejaz – Basteh Negar – Chaharpareh – Gabri</p>\n\n<h4>Gushehs Mentioned in the Book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi</h4>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Abu Ata mentioned in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi, from the radif attributed to “Mirza Abdollah” according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Ramkeli – Daramad – Sayakhi – Hejaz – Basteh Negar – Yaqulun – Chaharpareh – Gabri – Ghamangiz – Gilaki</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Abu Ata in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the avaz of Abu Ata in the book “Radif of Mirza Hossein-Qoli according to Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Pirniakan are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Second Daramad – Third Daramad or Sayakhi – First Naghmeh – Second Naghmeh – First Hejaz – Second Hejaz – Basteh Negar – Third Hejaz – Bozorg – Kochak – Fourth Hejaz – Chaharmezrab of Hejaz – Takhir-e Baghdadi – Samali – Chaharpareh – Gabri – Shomali – Gilaki</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Abu Ata in Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi’s Advanced Course Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the avaz of Abu Ata in the book “Advanced Course Radif of Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Talaei are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Avaz – Kereshmeh – Second Daramad – Third Daramad – Fourth Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Fifth Daramad – Nahib – Rahavi (Rohab) – Masnavi – Greili – Continuation of Rohab and Forud</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Abu Ata in Musa Khan Ma’rufi’s Radif of Seven Dastgahs</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Abu Ata mentioned in the book “Radif of Seven Dastgahs of Iranian Music” by Musa Khan Ma’rufi, edited by Mehdi Barkeshli, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Part – Second Part – Third Part – Fourth Part – Fifth Part – Sixth Part – Gusheh of Mohammad Sadeq Khani – Kereshmeh – Sayakhi – First Hejaz – Second Hejaz – Basteh Negar – Baghdadi – Third Hejaz – Dobeyti – Shomali (Samali?) – Chaharbagh – Gabri – Forud – Ramkeli – Masnavi</p>\n\n<p>In this list, the gusheh “Tak-e Moghaddam” is actually a type of tahrir, and the gusheh “Baghdadi” is very similar to the gusheh “Hejaz.”</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Abu Ata in Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the avaz of Abu Ata in the book “Javab-e Avaz based on Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif” by Arshad Tahmasbi are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Sayakhi – Ramkeli – Another Type of Ramkeli – Khosrow o Shirin – Another Type of Khosrow o Shirin – Gori – Hejaz – Chaharpareh or Chaharbagh</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Abu Ata or Dastan Arab in Abdollah Davami’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the avaz of Abu Ata or Dastan Arab in the book “Vocal Radif and Old Tasnifs according to Master Abdollah Davami” by Master Faramarz Payvar are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Ramkeli – Takhir of Nayeb Asadollah – Takhir of Abu Ata – Hejaz – Takhir of Abu Ata – Chaharpareh – Tork (in Chaharpareh) – Ouj (in Chaharpareh) – Khosrow o Shirin – Gabri – Masnavi</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Abu Ata in Abbas Kazemi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Abu Ata mentioned in Abbas Kazemi’s vocal radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Abu Ata – Amiri – Ganj-e Sukhteh – Deh Naseri – Meygoli – Behjat Afza – Majusli – Leyli o Majnun – Hezar Dastan – Zirkesh – Hejaz – Hayati – Joghatayi – Rahavandi – Shirin Bayan – Kurd Bayat – Gol-e Malih – Kucheh Baghi – Mohayyer – Amirkhani – Hejaz-e Masri – Koshteh o Mordeh – Baghdadi – Yatimek – Ramkeli 1 – Ramkeli 2 – Gabri 1 – Gabri 2 – Hassan Musa – Pahlavi – Oshshaq – Shahr Ashub – Mehrgani – Naghmeh-e Barbad – Shahnaz – Salmak – Masnavi</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Abu Ata in A’lam al-Soltan’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>A’lam al-Soltan’s radif includes seven dastgahs, and the avazes are not independent; some gushehs of these avazes are narrated after the dastgah of Shur under the title “The Form of Bayats that are in Shur.” Notable points in this list include titles such as Abu Ata or Abu Aytam, Bahreh-e Nour, Kucheh Esfahan, Hasht Dari, and Haft Hesār. Daneshvar also has sections titled “Daneshvar’s Chaharmezrab” in several dastgahs, which are his own compositions.</p>\n\n<p>The gushehs listed under the title “The Form of Bayats that are in Shur” from A’lam al-Soltan’s radif in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Bayat-e Kurd – Bayat-e Tork – Daneshvar’s Chaharmezrab – Mehdi Zarabi – Basteh Negar – Afshari – Hajiyuni – Bidgoli – Bayat-e Esfahan – Ghatar – Qarai – Hejaz – Abu Ata or Abu Aytam – Gabri – Shomali – Gilaki – Ghamangiz – Kucheh Esfahan</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Abu Ata in Abolhasan Saba’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Abu Ata mentioned in Abolhasan Saba’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab of Abu Ata – Avaz of Abu Ata (First Daramad) – Sayakhi – Se Mezrab – Zarbi Forud – Hejaz – Chaharmezrab – Gabri (Gori) – Masnavi of Gabri – Khosrow o Shirin – Chaharbagh</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Abu Ata in Faramarz Payvar’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Abu Ata mentioned in Faramarz Payvar’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Second Daramad (Mohammad Sadeq Khani) – Sayakhi – Tak-e Moghaddam – Hazineh – Ramkeli – Shomali – Hejaz – Mokhber – Chaharmezrab – Zarbi Chaharbagh – Masnavi – Khatameh</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Abu Ata in the First Volume of “The History of Iranian Music”</h3>\n\n<p>In the first volume of the book “Sar-gozasht-e Musiqi-ye Irani” quoted from <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a>, the avazes are not presented separately and are placed within the gushehs of the mother dastgah. The gushehs of the dastgah of Shur, as the mother dastgah of the avaz of Abu Ata, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramads – Gusheh of Rohab (Rahavi or Rahavi) – Naghmeh – Zirkesh Salmak – Salmak – Golriz – Safa – Bozorg – Dobeyti – Khara – Qajar – Malanazi – Hazineh – Ozal – Majles Afrouz – Shahnaz – Qazcheh – Razavi – Ashegh Kosh – Bayat Kurd – Hajiyani – Basteh Negar – Ghatar – Qazai – Bayat Tork – Dogah – Fili – Shekasteh – Jameh Daran – Mehdi Zarabi – Ruh al-Arvah – Abu Ata (Sarang) – Sayakhi – Hejaz – Chaharpareh (Chaharbagh) – Gabri (Gori) – Dashti – Bidgani [Gilaki – Ghamangiz – Dashtestani – Hajiyani – Chupani] – Greili – Afshari – Gusheh of Iraq – Masnavi</p>\n\n<h2>Analysis of the Gushehs of the Avaz of Abu Ata in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h2>\n\n<p>Now we examine the gushehs of the avaz of Abu Ata in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s radif mentioned earlier:</p>\n\n<p><strong>First and Second Daramad gushehs</strong>: The first and second daramads of the avaz of Abu Ata begin on the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol), which also serves as the witness (shahed). Melodic movement takes shape within the structural interval “D – G.” Some phrases stop on the second degree above the concluding note, “E koron,” and finally conclude on “D” (re). Melodic movement mainly occurs with emphasis on the second degree “E koron” and the fourth degree “G” above the concluding note.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Third Daramad or Sayakhi gusheh</strong>: It begins on the second degree above the concluding note, i.e., “E koron” (mi koron). The melodic movement and conclusion in this gusheh are similar to the previous gushehs.</p>\n\n<p><strong>First and Second Naghmeh gushehs</strong>: Then two gushehs with rhythmic patterns titled First Naghmeh and Second Naghmeh are presented, in which the melodic movement is also similar to the daramad.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hejaz gusheh</strong>: After the daramad range, the gusheh of Hejaz is presented in four parts. The witness and starting note in the avaz of Hejaz is the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “A” (la).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Basteh Negar, Bozorg and Kochak (Majles Afrouz), and Chaharmezrab gushehs</strong>: Within the Hejaz range, several other gushehs with rhythmic patterns are presented with emphasis on the witness of Hejaz (“A”), including: Basteh Negar, Bozorg and Kochak (Majles Afrouz), and Chaharmezrab with a base on the witness of Hejaz.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Takhir-e Baghdadi</strong>: Then a special tahrir titled Takhir-e Baghdadi is presented, in which the melodic movement also revolves around the witness of Hejaz. The concluding note is also the same as the conclusion of Abu Ata, i.e., “D” (re).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Samali gusheh</strong>: After Takhir-e Baghdadi, a gusheh titled Samali is presented, which begins on the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “A” (la), and ends on “D” (re).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Chaharpareh or Chaharbagh gusheh</strong>: Then a gusheh with a rhythmic pattern based on the prosodic meter “mutafa’ilun mutafa’ilun” (equivalent to the rhythm “ta-ta-tan ta-nan”) titled Chaharpareh or Chaharbagh is presented, which begins on the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “A” (la), and descends to “D” (re).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Gabri gusheh</strong>: It begins on the note before the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do). Melodic movement in this gusheh occurs within one octave “C – C” and concludes on the concluding note of Abu Ata, “D” (re).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Shomali gusheh</strong>: Then a rhythmic gusheh titled Shomali is presented, which begins on the sixth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “B flat” (si bemol), and finally descends to “D” (re), the conclusion of the avaz of Abu Ata.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Gilaki gusheh</strong>: The conclusion of the avaz of Abu Ata and the final forud is achieved through the gusheh of Gilaki. The starting note in this gusheh is the third degree above the conclusion. The ending of Abu Ata on “D” (re) indicates that this avaz belongs to the dastgah of Shur (on the base “D”).</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the gushehs of the avaz of Abu Ata, please share them in the comments section of the article with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a> to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-12-21 16:12:36', 0, '2024-07-05 21:47:06', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1539, 'posts', 82, 'en', NULL, 'Gushehs of Avaz Bayat-e Tork | Examination of Bayat-e Tork gushehs in different radifs', NULL, 'This article examines the gushehs (melodic pieces) of Bayat-e Tork (also known as Bayat-e Zand) within the Iranian classical music radif, comparing different narrations by prominent masters. Various sources—including the radifs of Mirza Abdollah, Mirza Hossein-Qoli, Ali-Akbar Shahnazi, Musa Maroufi, Mahmoud Karimi, and Abdollah Davami—present differences in the number and sequence of gushehs. However, several key gushehs such as Daramad, Dogah, Jameh-Daran, Naghmeh, Shekasteh, Mahdi Zarabi, and Ruh al-Arvah appear consistently across most versions.\n\nThe article also provides a melodic analysis based on the Mirza Hossein-Qoli radif. Bayat-e Tork typically begins on the note F and resolves on G, highlighting its connection to the Shur mode. The note B-flat serves as the central tone (shahed), and melodic movements are mainly structured within the interval between B-flat and F.\n\nDifferent gushehs—including Daramad sections, Chaharmezrab, Jameh-Daran, Naghmeh, and Mahdi Zarabi—are discussed in terms of their melodic characteristics. The avaz ultimately concludes with the Mahdi Zarabi gusheh, which facilitates the final descent back to the Shur mode.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">gushehs of the avaz of Bayat-e Tork</span></strong> (also known as <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Bayat-e Zand</span></strong>) and review the gushehs of Bayat-e Tork in various narrations of the radifs of Iranian music. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>Gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork in the Radif</h2>\n\n<p>In the various narrations of the radif according to different masters, the number of gushehs of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%aa%d8%b1%da%a9/\">avaz of Bayat-e Tork</a> differs slightly. In the following, we examine these gushehs in several radifs:</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Bayat-e Tork in Mirza Abdollah’s Radif according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Bayat-e Tork in the different notations of Mirza Abdollah’s radif vary slightly. Below we present two versions:</p>\n\n<h4>Jean During’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>In the radif of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87/\">Mirza Abdollah</a> notated by Jean During, the following gushehs are mentioned for the avaz of Bayat-e Tork:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Dogah, Forud – Second Daramad – Third Daramad – Hajiyani – Basteh Negar – Zanguleh – Khosravani – Naghmeh – Fili – Shekasteh – Mehrabani – Jameh Daran – Mehdi Zarabi – Ruh al-Arvah</p>\n\n<h4>Dariush Talaei’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>The avaz of Bayat-e Tork in Dariush Talaei’s book “Tahlil-e Radif,” based on Mirza Abdollah’s radif, includes the following gushehs:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Dogah – Second Daramad – Third Daramad – Hajiyani – Basteh Negar – Zanguleh – Khosravani – Naghmeh – Fili – Shekasteh – Mehrabani – Jameh Daran – Mehdi Zarabi – Ruh al-Arvah – Ghatar</p>\n\n<h4>Gushehs Mentioned in the Book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi</h4>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Bayat-e Tork mentioned in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi, from the radif attributed to “Mirza Abdollah” according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Dogah – Forud – Second Daramad – Kereshmeh – Forud – Third Daramad – Hajiyani – Forud – Basteh Negar – Zanguleh – Khosravani – Naghmeh – Fili – Gusheh of Shekasteh – Mehrabani – Jameh Daran – Mehdi Zarabi – Ruh al-Arvah</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the avaz of Bayat-e Tork in the book “Radif of Mirza Hossein-Qoli according to Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Pirniakan are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Second Daramad – Third Daramad – Fourth Daramad – Fifth Daramad with Zanguleh – Chaharmezrab – Jameh Daran – Naghmeh – Mehdi Zarabi</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Bayat-e Tork in Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi’s Advanced Course Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the avaz of Bayat-e Tork in the book “Advanced Course Radif of Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Talaei are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab – Bardasht – First Daramad – Second Daramad (Kereshmeh) – Third Daramad – Fourth and Fifth Daramad – Sixth Daramad – Seventh Daramad – Fili – Delkash – Kereshmeh of Delkash – Chaharmezrab and Continuation of Delkash – Chaharmezrab of Gusheh of Abu Ata – Gusheh of Afshari – Chaharmezrab – Rahavi (Rohab) – Forud to Bayat-e Tork – Masnavi – Zarbi – Greili – Forud to Delkash – Forud to Bayat-e Tork</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Bayat-e Tork in Musa Khan Ma’rufi’s Radif of Seven Dastgahs</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Bayat-e Tork mentioned in the book “Radif of Seven Dastgahs of Iranian Music” by Musa Khan Ma’rufi, edited by Mehdi Barkeshli, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Kereshmeh – Third Part – Khosravani – Jameh Daran – Dogah – Ruh al-Arvah – Mohammad Sadeq Khani – Mehrabani – Mehdi Zarabi – Masnavi – Ghatar – Naghmeh – Forud – Qarai – Qarai, Another Type</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork in Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the avaz of Bayat-e Tork in the book “Javab-e Avaz based on Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif” by Arshad Tahmasbi are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Second Daramad – Dogah – Ruh al-Arvah – Jameh Daran – Fili – Shekasteh – Ghatar – Qarai – Shahabi – Mehrabani – Masnavi</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork in Abdollah Davami’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the avaz of Bayat-e Tork in the book “Vocal Radif and Old Tasnifs according to Master Abdollah Davami” by Master Faramarz Payvar are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad of Sama’ Hozur – First Daramad – Second Daramad – Hajiyani – Third Daramad – Jameh Daran – Mohammad Sadeq Khani – Dogah – Mehdi Zarabi – Ghatar – Amiri, Shahabi – Ruh al-Arvah – Shekasteh – Abol – Masnavi</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork in Abbas Kazemi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Bayat-e Tork mentioned in Abbas Kazemi’s vocal radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Tork – Mehdi Zarabi – Dogah – Amiri – Nahvi 1 – Ganj-e Sukhteh – Mobadi – Rak-e Khorasani – Rak-e Hendi – Naghmeh of Rak – Rak-e Kashmir – Rak-e Abdollah – Khajasteh – Oghdeh Gosha – Nahvi 2 – Majzubi – Ruh al-Arvah – Shekasteh – Mehrabani – Behbahani – Shahabi – Ghatar – Dobeyti – Shekasteh Qarabaq – Masnavi of Tork – Hazineh – Gosha’esh</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork in A’lam al-Soltan’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>A’lam al-Soltan’s radif includes seven dastgahs, and the avazes are not independent; some gushehs of these avazes are narrated after the dastgah of Shur under the title “The Form of Bayats that are in Shur.” Notable points in this list include titles such as Abu Ata or Abu Aytam, Bahreh-e Nour, Kucheh Esfahan, Hasht Dari, and Haft Hesār. Daneshvar also has sections titled “Daneshvar’s Chaharmezrab” in several dastgahs, which are his own compositions.</p>\n\n<p>The gushehs listed under the title “The Form of Bayats that are in Shur” from A’lam al-Soltan’s radif in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Bayat-e Kurd – Bayat-e Tork – Daneshvar’s Chaharmezrab – Mehdi Zarabi – Basteh Negar – Afshari – Hajiyuni – Bidgoli – Bayat-e Esfahan – Ghatar – Qarai – Hejaz – Abu Ata or Abu Aytam – Gabri – Shomali – Gilaki – Ghamangiz – Kucheh Esfahan</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork in Abolhasan Saba’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Bayat-e Tork mentioned in Abolhasan Saba’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab – Avaz (First Daramad) – Chaharmezrab of Dad – Jameh Daran – Shekasteh – Chaharmezrab of Shekasteh – Ruh al-Arvah (Daramad) – Dogah – Shahabi (Amiri) – Ghatar – Masnavi</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork in Faramarz Payvar’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Bayat-e Tork mentioned under the title “Avaz of Tork” in Faramarz Payvar’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Second Daramad (Sarbang) – Sorur al-Molki – Gusheh of Dashti – Mohammad Sadeq Khani – Par-e Parastu (Par-e Parastuk) – Basteh Negar – Naghmeh (Zang-e Shotor) – Zanguleh – Muye – Shekasteh – Chaharmezrab – Mehdi Zarabi – Do-ta Yeki – Abol – Mehrabani</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork in the First Volume of “The History of Iranian Music”</h3>\n\n<p>In the first volume of the book “Sar-gozasht-e Musiqi-ye Irani” quoted from <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a>, the avazes are not presented separately and are placed within the gushehs of the mother dastgah. The gushehs of the dastgah of Shur, as the mother dastgah of the avaz of Bayat-e Tork, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramads – Gusheh of Rohab (Rahavi or Rahavi) – Naghmeh – Zirkesh Salmak – Salmak – Golriz – Safa – Bozorg – Dobeyti – Khara – Qajar – Malanazi – Hazineh – Ozal – Majles Afrouz – Shahnaz – Qazcheh – Razavi – Ashegh Kosh – Bayat Kurd – Hajiyani – Basteh Negar – Ghatar – Qazai – Bayat Tork – Dogah – Fili – Shekasteh – Jameh Daran – Mehdi Zarabi – Ruh al-Arvah – Abu Ata (Sarang) – Sayakhi – Hejaz – Chaharpareh (Chaharbagh) – Gabri (Gori) – Dashti – Bidgani [Gilaki – Ghamangiz – Dashtestani – Hajiyani – Chupani] – Greili – Afshari – Gusheh of Iraq – Masnavi</p>\n\n<h2>Analysis of the Gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Tork in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h2>\n\n<p>Now we examine the gushehs of the avaz of Bayat-e Tork in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s radif mentioned earlier:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Daramad gushehs</strong>: The daramads of the avaz of Bayat-e Tork begin on the note “F” (fa). This note also serves as a temporary stop for this avaz. The final conclusion of the avaz of Bayat-e Tork occurs on the note “G” (sol), which indicates that the avaz of Bayat-e Tork belongs to the dastgah of Shur. The third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “B flat” (si bemol), is also considered the witness of the avaz of Bayat-e Tork. The main range of melodic movement in the daramads is formed based on the structural interval “B flat – F” with emphasis on these notes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Second to Fourth Daramad gushehs</strong>: The sonic range of the second to fourth daramads extends up to the note “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Fifth Daramad gusheh</strong>: It has a structure similar to the previous daramads, which is formed by expanding and developing a rhythmic pattern – Zanguleh.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Chaharmezrab gusheh</strong>: After the fifth daramad, a rhythmic gusheh titled Chaharmezrab is presented, whose base emphasizes the note “F” (fa). After moving around the witness note, i.e., “B flat” (si bemol), it stops on the same note.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Jameh Daran gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fifth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re), and after repeated emphasis on the witness, it stops on that note. The main melodic movements in this gusheh take place within the fifth interval “B flat – F,” which is actually the inversion of the structural interval “F – B flat” in the lower register.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Naghmeh gusheh</strong>: It is formed based on a rhythmic pattern that begins on the second degree above the concluding note, i.e., “A koron” (la koron), and stops on the temporary stop of Bayat-e Tork, i.e., “F” (fa). The fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do), is also emphasized in this gusheh.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mehdi Zarabi gusheh</strong>: The avaz of Bayat-e Tork concludes with the gusheh of Mehdi Zarabi, which in fact represents the forud to the dastgah of Shur. This gusheh begins on the concluding note “G” (sol) and ends on the same note, which evokes the return to Shur on “G” (sol).</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the gushehs of the avaz of Bayat-e Tork, please share them in the comments section of the article with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a> to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-12-21 16:18:03', 0, '2024-08-30 19:14:59', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1540, 'posts', 83, 'en', NULL, 'Gushehs of Avaz Afshari | Examination of Afshari gushehs in different radifs', NULL, 'This article explores the gushehs of Avaz-e Afshari within the Iranian classical music radif, comparing different interpretations by various masters. Across sources such as the radifs of Mirza Abdollah, Mirza Hossein-Qoli, Ali-Akbar Shahnazi, Musa Maroufi, Mahmoud Karimi, and Abdollah Davami, the number and sequence of gushehs vary, but several key elements—such as Daramad, Jameh-Daran, Qaraei, Nahib, Naghmeh, Iraq, and Masnavi—are commonly shared.\n\nThe melodic structure of Afshari is analyzed based on the Mirza Hossein-Qoli radif. This avaz typically begins on the note G (as the central tone or shahed) and resolves on C. The descent is characterized by a neutral third interval (E half-flat to C), which is a defining feature of the mode.\n\nDifferent gushehs—including the Daramad sections, Jameh-Daran, Nahib, Naghmeh, and Suz-o-Godaz—are discussed in terms of their melodic behavior. The avaz ultimately concludes with the Suz-o-Godaz gusheh in the lower register, providing the final resolution.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">gushehs of the avaz of Afshari</span></strong> and review the gushehs of Afshari in various narrations of the radifs of Iranian music. Please continue with the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>...</p>\n\n<h2>Gushehs of the Avaz of Afshari in the Radif</h2>\n\n<p>In the various narrations of the radif according to different masters, the number of gushehs of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d9%81%d8%b4%d8%a7%d8%b1%db%8c/\">avaz of Afshari</a> differs slightly. In the following, we examine these gushehs in several radifs:</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Afshari in Mirza Abdollah’s Radif according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Afshari in the different notations of Mirza Abdollah’s radif vary slightly. Below we present two versions:</p>\n\n<h4>Jean During’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>In Mirza Abdollah’s radif notated by Jean During, the following gushehs are mentioned for the avaz of Afshari:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Basteh Negar – Iraq</p>\n\n<h4>Dariush Talaei’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>The avaz of Afshari in Dariush Talaei’s book “Tahlil-e Radif,” based on Mirza Abdollah’s radif, includes the following gushehs:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Basteh Negar – Iraq – Qarai</p>\n\n<h4>Gushehs Mentioned in the Book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi</h4>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Afshari mentioned in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi, from the radif attributed to “Mirza Abdollah” according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Basteh Negar – Iraq</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Afshari in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the avaz of Afshari in the book “Radif of Mirza Hossein-Qoli according to Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Pirniakan are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Second Daramad – Third Daramad – Fourth Daramad – Fifth Daramad – Sixth Daramad – Seventh Daramad with Basteh Negar – Jameh Daran – Qarai – Nahib – Naghmeh – Forud – Suz o Godaz</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Afshari in Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi’s Advanced Course Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the avaz of Afshari in the book “Advanced Course Radif of Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Talaei are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Avaz – Kereshmeh – Second Daramad – Third Daramad – Fourth Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Fifth Daramad – Nahib – Rahavi (Rohab) – Masnavi – Greili – Continuation of Rohab and Forud</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Afshari in Musa Khan Ma’rufi’s Radif of Seven Dastgahs</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Afshari mentioned in the book “Radif of Seven Dastgahs of Iranian Music” by Musa Khan Ma’rufi, edited by Mehdi Barkeshli, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Moghaddameh – First Part – Second Part – Third Part – Fourth Part – Fifth Part – Kereshmeh – Avaz – Sixth Part – Seventh Part – Eighth Part – Ninth Part – Jameh Daran – Basteh Negar – Nahib – Nahib, Another Type – Nahib, Another Type – Gusheh of Rohab – Masihi – Masnavi Pich</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Afshari in Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the avaz of Afshari in the book “Javab-e Avaz based on Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif” by Arshad Tahmasbi are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Jameh Daran – Hesār – Qarai – Nahib – Hazineh – Rohab – Sadri – Masnavi</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Afshari in Abdollah Davami’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the avaz of Afshari in the book “Vocal Radif and Old Tasnifs according to Master Abdollah Davami” by Master Faramarz Payvar are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Jameh Daran – Mirzai – Qarai – Iraq – Masnavi – Shah Khatayi</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Afshari in Abbas Kazemi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Afshari mentioned in Abbas Kazemi’s vocal radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Afshari – Rast – Nahib – Muye – Sadri – Sabri – Sarhadi – Mobarqa’ – Darreh Gazi – Ilkhani – Ashegh Kosh – Sallali – Iraq – Zanganeh – Razghi (Delangizan) – Qarai – Deyr-e Raheb – Masihi – Zanguleh – Khojandi – Masnavi</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Afshari in A’lam al-Soltan’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>A’lam al-Soltan’s radif includes seven dastgahs, and the avazes are not independent; some gushehs of these avazes are narrated after the dastgah of Shur under the title “The Form of Bayats that are in Shur.” Notable points in this list include titles such as Abu Ata or Abu Aytam, Bahreh-e Nour, Kucheh Esfahan, Hasht Dari, and Haft Hesār. Daneshvar also has sections titled “Daneshvar’s Chaharmezrab” in several dastgahs, which are his own compositions.</p>\n\n<p>The gushehs listed under the title “The Form of Bayats that are in Shur” from A’lam al-Soltan’s radif in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Bayat-e Kurd – Bayat-e Tork – Daneshvar’s Chaharmezrab – Mehdi Zarabi – Basteh Negar – Afshari – Hajiyuni – Bidgoli – Bayat-e Esfahan – Ghatar – Qarai – Hejaz – Abu Ata or Abu Aytam – Gabri – Shomali – Gilaki – Ghamangiz – Kucheh Esfahan</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Afshari in Abolhasan Saba’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Afshari mentioned in Abolhasan Saba’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Avaz of Afshari (First Daramad) – Chaharmezrab – Avaz of Afshari (Second Daramad) – Qarai (Daramad of Qarai) – Rahavi – Iraq – Takht-e Taqdis – Sadri – Masnavi of Afshari</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Afshari in Faramarz Payvar’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Afshari mentioned in Faramarz Payvar’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Second Daramad (Kereshmeh) – Jameh Daran – Qarai – Chaharmezrab – Hazineh</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Afshari in the First Volume of “The History of Iranian Music”</h3>\n\n<p>In the first volume of the book “Sar-gozasht-e Musiqi-ye Irani” quoted from <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a>, the avazes are not presented separately and are placed within the gushehs of the mother dastgah. The gushehs of the dastgah of Shur, as the mother dastgah of the avaz of Afshari, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramads – Gusheh of Rohab (Rahavi or Rahavi) – Naghmeh – Zirkesh Salmak – Salmak – Golriz – Safa – Bozorg – Dobeyti – Khara – Qajar – Malanazi – Hazineh – Ozal – Majles Afrouz – Shahnaz – Qazcheh – Razavi – Ashegh Kosh – Bayat Kurd – Hajiyani – Basteh Negar – Ghatar – Qazai – Bayat Tork – Dogah – Fili – Shekasteh – Jameh Daran – Mehdi Zarabi – Ruh al-Arvah – Abu Ata (Sarang) – Sayakhi – Hejaz – Chaharpareh (Chaharbagh) – Gabri (Gori) – Dashti – Bidgani [Gilaki – Ghamangiz – Dashtestani – Hajiyani – Chupani] – Greili – Afshari – Gusheh of Iraq – Masnavi</p>\n\n<h2>Analysis of the Gushehs of the Avaz of Afshari in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h2>\n\n<p>Now we examine the gushehs of the avaz of Afshari in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s radif mentioned earlier:</p>\n\n<p><strong>First to Sixth Daramad gushehs</strong>: The first to sixth daramads of the avaz of Afshari begin on the note “G” (sol), which also serves as the witness (shahed). The way the concluding note is taken in the avaz of Afshari is by using a neutral third interval (intervals of a quarter tone or three-quarter tone) in a descending manner (E koron – C).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Seventh Daramad gusheh</strong>: It has a structure almost similar to the previous daramads, with the difference that it begins on the octave above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do), and ends on the same note in the lower register. Temporary stops of some phrases on “E koron” create a state of anticipation for the forud on the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Jameh Daran gusheh</strong>: It begins on the sixth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “A” (la), and concludes on “C” (do). Melodic movement in this gusheh also occurs within the octave “C – C,” with temporary stops of some phrases on the third degree above the concluding note and the final conclusion on “C” (do) revolving around the witness note.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nahib gusheh</strong>: It begins on the octave above the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do), and after stopping on “G” (sol), it begins with the rhythmic pattern of Basteh Negar from “C” (do) and temporarily stops on the second degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re), preparing the ground for the next gusheh.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Naghmeh gusheh</strong>: It has a rhythmic pattern that begins on the third degree above the concluding note, i.e., “E koron” (mi koron), and after emphasis on the witness, i.e., “G” (sol), it finally concludes on “C” (do). Then a forud is presented in the lower register, which begins on the concluding note, i.e., the same note “C” (do), and ends on the same note.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Suz o Godaz gusheh</strong>: The conclusion of the avaz of Afshari is with the gusheh of Suz o Godaz, which is presented in the lower sonic range. Melodic movements in this gusheh take shape within the range of two consecutive lower dangs below the concluding note. The starting note in the Suz o Godaz gusheh is the fourth degree below the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol), and it finally ends on “C” (do).</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the gushehs of the avaz of Afshari, please share them in the comments section of the article with us to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-12-21 16:54:03', 0, '2024-08-30 19:11:14', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1541, 'posts', 84, 'en', NULL, 'Gushehs of Avaz Dashti | Examination of Dashti gushehs in different radifs', NULL, 'This article examines the gushehs (melodic pieces) of the Dashti vocal mode in Persian classical music and compares their presentation across different radif traditions. It highlights that the number and order of gushehs vary among masters such as Mirza Abdollah, Mirza Hossein-Gholi, Saba, and Payvar. Key gushehs include Daramad, Owj, Bidgani, Hajiani, Oshaq, Gilaki, and Ghamangiz.\n\nIn the analytical section (based on Mirza Hossein-Gholi’s radif), the melodic structure of Dashti is explored:\n\nIt typically begins from the third degree above the final note.\nThe note “E” (with microtonal variations) serves as the central tone (shahed).\nThe melodic movement mainly revolves around the interval between A and E.\nGushehs like Oshaq, Hajiani, and Bidgani play important roles in development and resolution.\nThe final cadence resolves on “A,” emphasizing Dashti’s connection to the Shur mode.\n\nOverall, the article provides both a comparative overview of gushehs across different radifs and a detailed analysis of the melodic characteristics of the Dashti mode.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">gushehs of the avaz of Dashti</span></strong> and review the gushehs of Dashti in various narrations of the radifs of Iranian music. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>Gushehs of the Avaz of Dashti in the Radif</h2>\n\n<p>In the various narrations of the radif according to different masters, the number of gushehs of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%af%d8%b4%d8%aa%db%8c/\">avaz of Dashti</a> differs slightly. In the following, we examine these gushehs in several radifs:</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Dashti in Mirza Abdollah’s Radif according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Dashti in the different notations of Mirza Abdollah’s radif vary slightly. Below we present two versions:</p>\n\n<h4>Jean During’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>In Mirza Abdollah’s radif notated by Jean During, the following gushehs are mentioned for the avaz of Dashti:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Ouj – Bidgani – Hajiyani – Ouj</p>\n\n<h4>Dariush Talaei’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>The avaz of Dashti in Dariush Talaei’s book “Tahlil-e Radif,” based on Mirza Abdollah’s radif, includes the following gushehs:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Ouj – Bidgani – Hajiyani – Ghamangiz – Gilaki</p>\n\n<h4>Gushehs Mentioned in the Book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi</h4>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Dashti mentioned in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi, from the radif attributed to “Mirza Abdollah” according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Ouj – Bidgani – Hajiyani [and Ouj]</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Dashti in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the avaz of Dashti in the book “Radif of Aqa Hossein-Qoli according to Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Pirniakan are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Second Daramad – Third Daramad – Fourth Daramad – Oshshaq – Chaharmezrab – Hajiyani – Bidgoli with Oshshaq – Bidgani</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Dashti in Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi’s Advanced Course Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the avaz of Dashti in the book “Advanced Course Radif of Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Talaei are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Second Daramad – Avaz – Chaharmezrab of Oshshaq – Avaz of Oshshaq – Gusheh of Tork with Chaharmezrab – Avaz of Tork – Masnavi – Ghamangiz</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Dashti in Musa Khan Ma’rufi’s Radif of Seven Dastgahs</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Dashti mentioned in the book “Radif of Seven Dastgahs of Iranian Music” by Musa Khan Ma’rufi, edited by Mehdi Barkeshli, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Hajiyani, First Part – Hajiyani, Second Part – Hajiyani, Third Part – Hajiyani, Fourth Part – Hajiyani, Fifth Part – Hajiyani, Sixth Part – Hajiyani, Seventh Part – Hajiyani, Eighth Part – Dashti, First Part – Dashti, Second Part – Dashti, Third Part – Dashti, Fourth Part – Bidgani – Chaharmezrab – Bidgani, Another Part – Chupani – Dashtestani – Ghamangiz – Gilaki – Kucheh Baghi – Samali</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Dashti in Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the avaz of Dashti in the book “Javab-e Avaz based on Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif” by Arshad Tahmasbi are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Ouj – Gilaki – Bidgani – Chupani – Dashtestani and Hajiyani – Ghamangiz – Deylaman – Masnavi</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Dashti in Abdollah Davami’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the avaz of Dashti in the book “Vocal Radif and Old Tasnifs according to Master Abdollah Davami” by Master Faramarz Payvar are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Dashtestani – Hajiyani – Bidgoli – Ouj – Gilaki – Kucheh Baghi, Ghamangiz – Samali</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Dashti in Abbas Kazemi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Dashti mentioned in Abbas Kazemi’s vocal radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Dashti – Bayat-e Raje – Kurd Bayat – Sarvi – Deylami – Azar – Samali – Hajiyani – Junaghani – Ghamangiz – Deylaman – Gilaki – Kuhestani – Khalil Khani – Damavandi – Jan Godaz – Zarghani – Bayat-e Shiraz – Erami – Qasem Abadi – Bidgani – Morad Khani – Eskandari – Darreh Shuri – Oshshaq – Ozal – Buselik – Tarabangiz – Katayuni – Tangsiri – Shahr Ashub – Shahnaz – Salmak – Masnavi – Chahar Takhir Samali, Tork, Amiri, Hazineh and Forud of Dashti</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Dashti in A’lam al-Soltan’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>A’lam al-Soltan’s radif includes seven dastgahs, and the avazes are not independent; some gushehs of these avazes are narrated after the dastgah of Shur under the title “The Form of Bayats that are in Shur.” Notable points in this list include titles such as Abu Ata or Abu Aytam, Bahreh-e Nour, Kucheh Esfahan, Hasht Dari, and Haft Hesār. Daneshvar also has sections titled “Daneshvar’s Chaharmezrab” in several dastgahs, which are his own compositions.</p>\n\n<p>The gushehs listed under the title “The Form of Bayats that are in Shur” from A’lam al-Soltan’s radif in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Bayat-e Kurd – Bayat-e Tork – Daneshvar’s Chaharmezrab – Mehdi Zarabi – Basteh Negar – Afshari – Hajiyuni – Bidgoli – Bayat-e Esfahan – Ghatar – Qarai – Hejaz – Abu Ata or Abu Aytam – Gabri – Shomali – Gilaki – Ghamangiz – Kucheh Esfahan</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Dashti in Abolhasan Saba’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Dashti mentioned in Abolhasan Saba’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab of Dashti – Avaz of Dashti (First Daramad) – Hajiyani – Oshshaq (Ouj Daramad) – Chaharmezrab of Oshshaq – Oshshaq (Ouj) – Chupani (Dashtestani) – Gilaki – Ghamangiz – Dashtestani (Chupani) – Masnavi – Deylaman – Be Yad-e Gozashteh [one of Saba’s compositions]</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Dashti in Faramarz Payvar’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Dashti mentioned in Faramarz Payvar’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Second Daramad – Oshshaq – Chaharmezrab – Zar o Nizar – Bidgoli or Bidgani – Samali or Samali – Kucheh Baghi – Masnavi – Saranj (Masnavi)</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Dashti in the First Volume of “The History of Iranian Music”</h3>\n\n<p>In the first volume of the book “Sar-gozasht-e Musiqi-ye Irani” quoted from <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a>, the avazes are not presented separately and are placed within the gushehs of the mother dastgah. The gushehs of the dastgah of Shur, as the mother dastgah of the avaz of Dashti, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramads – Gusheh of Rohab (Rahavi or Rahavi) – Naghmeh – Zirkesh Salmak – Salmak – Golriz – Safa – Bozorg – Dobeyti – Khara – Qajar – Malanazi – Hazineh – Ozal – Majles Afrouz – Shahnaz – Qazcheh – Razavi – Ashegh Kosh – Bayat Kurd – Hajiyani – Basteh Negar – Ghatar – Qazai – Bayat Tork – Dogah – Fili – Shekasteh – Jameh Daran – Mehdi Zarabi – Ruh al-Arvah – Abu Ata (Sarang) – Sayakhi – Hejaz – Chaharpareh (Chaharbagh) – Gabri (Gori) – Dashti – Bidgani [Gilaki – Ghamangiz – Dashtestani – Hajiyani – Chupani] – Greili – Afshari – Gusheh of Iraq – Masnavi</p>\n\n<h2>Analysis of the Gushehs of the Avaz of Dashti in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h2>\n\n<p>Now we examine the gushehs of the avaz of Dashti in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s radif mentioned earlier:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Daramad gushehs</strong>: The daramads of the avaz of Dashti begin on the note “C” (do), which is the third degree above the conclusion. Melodic movement revolves around the witness note, i.e., “E natural” (mi bekar). This note, the fifth degree above the concluding note, is sometimes used in a slightly lowered form (E koron). The alternating change of this note between “E koron” and “E natural” is one of the most important structural features of the avaz of Dashti. In fact, the main range of melodic movement is formed within the structural interval “A – E.” The conclusion of the avaz of Dashti on the note “A” emphasizes that this avaz belongs to the dastgah of Shur.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Oshshaq gusheh</strong>: After the four daramads of Dashti, the gusheh of Oshshaq is performed. This gusheh also begins on “C” (do), the third degree above the conclusion, and after emphasizing the octave above the concluding note, i.e., “A” (la), it stops on the witness of Dashti and prepares the ground for performing the chaharmezrab, whose base is the witness note, i.e., “E” (mi).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hajiyani gusheh</strong>: It begins on the seventh degree above the concluding note, i.e., “G” (sol). The sonic range gradually becomes lower until it finally stops on the lower octave of the witness note.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Bidgoli with Oshshaq gusheh</strong>: It begins on the sixth degree above the concluding note, which is microtonally lowered (intervals less than a semitone), i.e., “F suri” (fa suri), and after emphasizing the octave above the concluding note, i.e., “A” (la), it finally stops on the witness of Dashti, the note “E” (mi).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Bidgani gusheh</strong>: The conclusion of the avaz of Dashti is with the gusheh of Bidgani, which begins on the fourth degree above the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re), and after repeated emphasis on the witness note, it finally concludes on the conclusion of Dashti, i.e., “A” (la), which evokes the return to the dastgah of Shur.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the gushehs of the avaz of Dashti, please share them in the comments section of the article with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a> to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-12-21 17:00:53', 0, '2024-08-30 19:10:59', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1542, 'posts', 85, 'en', NULL, 'Gushehs of Avaz Bayat-e Esfahan | Examination of Bayat-e Esfahan gushehs in different radifs', NULL, 'This article examines the gushehs (melodic pieces) of Bayat-e Esfahan within different radifs of Persian classical music and highlights their variations across various master traditions. Sources such as the radifs of Mirza Abdollah, Mirza Hossein-Qoli, Ali-Akbar Shahnazi, Musa Maroufi, Mahmoud Karimi, and Abdollah Davami present slightly different sets of gushehs. However, key elements like Daramad, Jameh-Daran, Bayat-e Raje, Sooz o Godaz, Keresmeh, and Masnavi are commonly shared among most versions.\n\nThe analytical section focuses on the melodic structure of Bayat-e Esfahan. It typically begins on the note “G” (as the central tone) and resolves on “C.” Various gushehs such as Bayat-e Raje, Hazin, Dotayeki, Chaharmezrab, Sooz o Godaz, Shekasteh, Shur, Keresmeh, and Masnavi each contribute distinct melodic characteristics and help develop the modal atmosphere of this avaz. Overall, Bayat-e Esfahan is considered a rich and complex form in Persian music due to its diverse gushehs and subtle interval variations.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">gushehs of the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan</span></strong> and review the gushehs of Bayat-e Esfahan in various narrations of the radifs of Iranian music. Please continue with the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">Sornaz Music School</a>...</p>\n\n<h2>Gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan in the Radif</h2>\n\n<p>In the various narrations of the radif according to different masters, the number of gushehs of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%a7%d8%b5%d9%81%d9%87%d8%a7%d9%86/\">avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan</a> differs slightly. In the following, we examine these gushehs in several radifs:</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Bayat-e Esfahan in Mirza Abdollah’s Radif according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan in the different notations of Mirza Abdollah’s radif vary slightly. Below we present two versions:</p>\n\n<h4>Jean During’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>In the radif of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87/\">Mirza Abdollah Farahani</a> notated by Jean During, the following gushehs are mentioned for the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Jameh Daran – Bayat-e Raje – Second Part – Forud – Kereshmeh – Suz o Godaz</p>\n\n<h4>Dariush Talaei’s Notation</h4>\n\n<p>The avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan in Dariush Talaei’s book “Tahlil-e Radif,” based on Mirza Abdollah’s radif, includes the following gushehs:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Jameh Daran – Bayat-e Raje and Forud – Naghmeh – Suz o Godaz</p>\n\n<h4>Gushehs Mentioned in the Book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi</h4>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan mentioned in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi, from the radif attributed to “Mirza Abdollah” according to Nour-Ali Khan Boroumand, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Jameh Daran – Bayat-e Raje [with Forud] – Naghmeh – Suz o Godaz</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan in the book “Radif of Mirza Hossein-Qoli according to Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Pirniakan are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Second Daramad – Third Daramad – Bayat-e Raje – Hazineh in Bayat-e Raje – Do-ta Yeki – Basteh Negar – Forud – Chaharmezrab – Suz o Godaz – Forud – Shekasteh Esfahan – Gusheh of Shur – Kereshmeh with Forud – Masnavi – Jameh Daran</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Bayat-e Esfahan in Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi’s Advanced Course Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan in the book “Advanced Course Radif of Ali Akbar Khan Shahnazi” by Dariush Talaei are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad (Kereshmeh) – Chaharmezrab – Second Daramad – Bayat-e Raje – Chaharmezrab of Bayat-e Raje – Avaz of Bayat-e Raje – Oshshaq (Ouj) – Shah Khatayi – Rohab (Rahavi) – Gusheh of Afshari – Chaharmezrab of Afshari – Avaz of Afshari – Shah Khatayi – Oshshaq – Forud to Esfahan</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of Bayat-e Esfahan in Musa Khan Ma’rufi’s Radif of Seven Dastgahs</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan mentioned in the book “Radif of Seven Dastgahs of Iranian Music” by Musa Khan Ma’rufi, edited by Mehdi Barkeshli, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Moghaddameh – First Daramad – Second Daramad – Third Daramad – Fourth Daramad – Kereshmeh – Avaz – Jameh Daran – Forud – Dobeyti – Bayat-e Raje – Second Phrase – Third Phrase – Fourth Phrase – Bayat-e Raje, Another Part – Hazineh – Kereshmeh – Forud – Kereshmeh – Oshshaq – Gusheh of Rohab – Shah Khatayi – Suz o Godaz – Kereshmeh – Gusheh of Raz o Niyaz – Masnavi – Reng-e Farahangiz</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan in Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan in the book “Javab-e Avaz based on Mahmoud Karimi’s Vocal Radif” by Arshad Tahmasbi are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Second Daramad – Jameh Daran – Bayat-e Raje – Suz o Godaz – Ouj – Naghmeh – A Gusheh in Esfahan – Bayat-e Shiraz – Masnavi – Sufi Nameh</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan in Abdollah Davami’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs mentioned for the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan in the book “Vocal Radif and Old Tasnifs according to Master Abdollah Davami” by Master Faramarz Payvar are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramad – Jameh Daran – Bayat-e Shiraz – Takhir-e Chekashi – Bayat-e Raje – Takhir-e Do-ta Yeki – Gusheh of Iraq – Takhir-e Zang-e Shotor – Ouj – Do Tikeh – Suz o Godaz – Masnavi – Sufi Nameh</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan in Abbas Kazemi’s Vocal Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan mentioned in Abbas Kazemi’s vocal radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Esfahan – Takhir-e Sepehr – Sepehr – Neyriz – Raje – Bayat-e Darvish Hassan – Bayat-e Ajam – Qajari – Sabali – Savaji – Mawaliyan 3 (two of them appear in the dastgah of Homayun) – Vajdi – Joghatayi – Musa Eshaq – Kanise’i – Oshshaq – Shahnaz – Salmak – Baharmast – Saqi Nameh – Masnavi – Esfahank – Ashiran – Ebri</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan in A’lam al-Soltan’s Radif</h3>\n\n<p>A’lam al-Soltan’s radif includes seven dastgahs, and the avazes are not independent; some gushehs of these avazes are narrated after the dastgah of Shur under the title “The Form of Bayats that are in Shur.” Notable points in this list include titles such as Abu Ata or Abu Aytam, Bahreh-e Nour, Kucheh Esfahan, Hasht Dari, and Haft Hesār. Daneshvar also has sections titled “Daneshvar’s Chaharmezrab” in several dastgahs, which are his own compositions.</p>\n\n<p>The gushehs listed under the title “The Form of Bayats that are in Shur” from A’lam al-Soltan’s radif in the book “Gusheh” by Arshad Tahmasbi are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Bayat-e Kurd – Bayat-e Tork – Daneshvar’s Chaharmezrab – Mehdi Zarabi – Basteh Negar – Afshari – Hajiyuni – Bidgoli – Bayat-e Esfahan – Ghatar – Qarai – Hejaz – Abu Ata or Abu Aytam – Gabri – Shomali – Gilaki – Ghamangiz – Kucheh Esfahan</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan in Abolhasan Saba’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan mentioned in Abolhasan Saba’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Chaharmezrab of Esfahan – Avaz of Esfahan – Jameh Daran – Bayat-e Raje – Oshshaq (Ouj) – Ouj and Haziz – Zarbi Oshshaq – Suz o Godaz – Masnavi</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan in Faramarz Payvar’s Santur Radif</h3>\n\n<p>The gushehs of the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan mentioned under the title “Avaz of Esfahan” in Faramarz Payvar’s santur radif are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>First Daramad – Second Daramad – Chaharmezrab – Bayat-e Shiraz – Zarbi Suz o Godaz – Oshshaq – Chaharmezrab – Gusheh of Shur – Hazineh – Denaseri – Masnavi (Darvish) – Khatameh</p>\n\n<h3>Gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan in the First Volume of “The History of Iranian Music”</h3>\n\n<p>In the first volume of the book “Sar-gozasht-e Musiqi-ye Irani” quoted from <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a>, the avazes are not presented separately and are placed within the gushehs of the mother dastgah. The gushehs of the dastgah of Homayun, as the mother dastgah of the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan, are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>Daramads – Mawaliyan – Chekavak – Tarz – Bidad – Ney-Davud – Bavi – Abolchap – Leyli Majnun – Ravandi – Nowruz Arab – Nowruz Saba – Nowruz Khara – Nafir – Farang – Shushtari – Jameh Daran – Bakhtiari – Mo’alef – Ozal – Denaseri – Bayat-e Esfahan – Bayat-e Raje (Rajeh) – Suz o Godaz – Raz o Niyaz – Masnavi</p>\n\n<h2>Analysis of the Gushehs of the Avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s Radif</h2>\n\n<p>Now we examine the gushehs of the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan in Mirza Hossein-Qoli’s radif mentioned earlier:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Daramad gusheh</strong>: Bayat-e Esfahan begins on the note “G” (sol), which also serves as the witness (shahed). The third degree below the witness, i.e., “E koron” (mi koron), is considered a temporary stop for Esfahan, and the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan concludes on the fifth degree below the witness, i.e., “C” (do). Some phrases also stop on the fourth degree below the witness, i.e., “D” (re).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Bayat-e Raje gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fourth degree above the witness, i.e., “C” (do), and stops on the witness note of Esfahan. The second degree above the witness of the Esfahan daramad, i.e., “A” (la), can be considered the witness of Bayat-e Raje.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hazineh and Do-ta Yeki gushehs</strong>: They begin from the witness note of the Bayat-e Raje gusheh, i.e., “A” (la). The main axis of melodic movement revolves around the witness of Esfahan. Hazineh stops on the concluding note, i.e., “C” (do), while Do-ta Yeki stops on the witness of Esfahan, i.e., “G” (sol). Then a forud occurs, returning the melodic movement to the domain of the daramad, finally stopping on the concluding note of Esfahan, i.e., “C” (do).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Chaharmezrab gusheh</strong>: It has a base in which the fifth degree above the witness and the witness note itself form the two main poles. This gusheh also finally stops on the witness of Bayat-e Esfahan, i.e., “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Suz o Godaz gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fourth degree above the witness of Esfahan, i.e., “C” (do). Temporary stops of its phrases are on the third and fourth degrees below the witness, but it finally stops on “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Shekasteh Esfahan gusheh</strong>: It begins on the fourth degree below the witness of the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan, i.e., “D” (re). The microtonal lowering of the sixth degree below the witness, i.e., “B koron” (si koron), is also a feature of this gusheh. Melodic movement in this gusheh mainly revolves around the fourth degree below the witness, i.e., “D” (re), and finally concludes on “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Shur gusheh</strong>: It begins on “D” (re). Melodic movements around this note create the space of Shur. The sixth and third degrees below the witness are also microtonally lower (B koron and E koron), and this change in interval ratios (B koron) plays an important role in presenting the modal space of the dastgah of Shur. The concluding note in this gusheh is “G” (sol).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Kereshmeh gusheh</strong>: It has a rhythmic pattern that begins from the witness of the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan, and its melodic movements around the main axis prepare the return to the daramad space and forud on the fifth degree below the witness, i.e., “C” (do), which is the conclusion of the avaz of Esfahan.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Masnavi gusheh</strong>: It has a rhythmic pattern based on the prosodic meter of masnavi. This gusheh also begins on the witness note of Esfahan, i.e., “G” (sol), and its melodic movements mainly revolve around this note. Temporary stops of some phrases occur on the fourth degree below the concluding note, i.e., “D” (re), and its final stop is on the third degree below the witness, i.e., “E koron” (mi koron).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Jameh Daran gusheh</strong>: It begins on the second degree above the witness of Esfahan, i.e., “A” (la). Temporary stops of its phrases are on the third degree below the witness of Esfahan, i.e., “E koron” (mi koron), and finally, with a forud pattern similar to the daramad, it concludes on the concluding note of Esfahan, which is the fifth degree below the witness, i.e., “C” (do).</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the gushehs of the avaz of Bayat-e Esfahan, please share them in the comments section of the article with us to improve the quality of this educational content.</p>', '2023-12-21 17:19:57', 0, '2024-08-30 19:10:45', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1543, 'posts', 86, 'en', NULL, 'Biography of Ali Akbar Shahnazi', NULL, 'This article explores the life and artistic career of Ali Akbar Shahnazi, one of the most prominent tar players in Persian classical music. Born in 1897 in Tehran into a highly musical family, he began learning the tar at a young age under the guidance of his father, Aqa Hossein-Qoli, and quickly achieved a high level of mastery during his teenage years.\n\nAfter his father’s death, Shahnazi assumed his teaching position and played a crucial role in preserving and transmitting the radif tradition through teaching and establishing music institutions. In addition to his performance skills, he was also an accomplished composer, creating works such as pishdaramad, reng, and tasnif, and leaving behind his notable “Advanced Radif.”\n\nHe collaborated with renowned poets and singers and trained many influential students who later became key figures in Persian music. Ali Akbar Shahnazi passed away in 1985, leaving a lasting legacy in Iranian classical music.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>Master <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Ali Akbar Shahnazi</span></strong> was born in 1276 solar in Tehran, on Ein al-Dowleh Street, Nasir Hozur Alley. Because he was born on the day of Eid al-Adha (Qurban), he was called Haji Ali.</p>\n\n<p>His grandfather <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d8%a7%da%a9%d8%a8%d8%b1-%d9%81%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Ali Akbar Khan Farahani</a> was a renowned tar player in the Naseri period and at the court of Naser al-Din Shah. His father was Aqa Hossein-Qoli, known as Jenab Mirza. His father’s uncle, Aqa Gholam Hossein, was a masterful tar player and the teacher of his father. His uncle Mirza Abdollah was a master of tar and setar. His cousin <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a7%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af-%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%a7%d8%af%db%8c/\">Ahmad Ebadi</a> was a setar player. His two younger brothers, Mohammad Hassan and Abdolhossein Shahnazi, were tar players, and his sisters’ husbands — Baqer Khan (kamancheh player) and Reza Khan (tar player) — were also musicians.</p>\n\n<p>Ali Akbar Khan completed his studies at the “Ashraf-e Sa’adat” and “Saint Louis” schools, but as his classmates said, his innate musical talent was greater than his enthusiasm for school lessons and formal education. Master Ali Akbar Shahnazi, within his artistic family, began learning the tar at the age of eight under his father’s supervision and learned the Iranian radif from his father in six years. Thanks to the abundant musical talent that was hereditary in this family, he was able to reach such a high level of proficiency in a short time that at the age of fourteen his first recordings were made in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%aa%d8%b1%da%a9/\">Bayat-e Tork</a> and Afshari with the voice of “Jenab Damavandi.”</p>\n\n<p>After the death of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86%d9%82%d9%84%db%8c/\">Aqa Hossein-Qoli</a> in 1294 solar, at the age of eighteen, he took his father’s place as a master teacher. In 1308 solar he founded the Shahnazi Music School and from 1334 solar, alongside running the Shahnazi School, he also taught at the Free Music School. From 1345 solar, for five years, he worked on compiling his own radif and that of his late father.</p>\n\n<p>After the Free Music School was merged into the Music School in 1346 solar, Master Ali Akbar Shahnazi closed his own school and began teaching at the National Music School. From 1350 solar until the end of his life, he devoted himself to teaching and transmitting his artistic heritage in the advanced courses of the National Music School.</p>\n\n<p>Ali Akbar Shahnazi was a highly skilled, creative, and technically brilliant player. In addition to composing many beautiful <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d9%be%db%8c%d8%b4-%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%a2%d9%85%d8%af/\">pishdaramads</a>, rengs, and tasnifs based on the dastgahs of Iranian music, he left behind a radif of his own called the “Advanced Course Radif.”</p>\n\n<p>Ali Akbar Shahnazi collaborated with poets such as Malek al-Sho’ara Bahar, Vahid Dastgerdi, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d8%a7%d9%85%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%ac%d8%a7%d9%87%d8%af/\">Amir Jahed</a>, and Hossein Masrur. Singers such as Eqbal al-Soltan, Hossein Ali Khan, Nakisa, Reza-Qoli Zoli, Taherzadeh, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%82%d9%85%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%85%d9%84%d9%88%da%a9-%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Qamar al-Moluk Vaziri</a>, and Adib Khansari performed his works.</p>\n\n<p>During his seventy years of teaching, Master Ali Akbar Shahnazi trained many students from several generations. Mirza Hassan Khan Moshar, Mirza Isa Khan Khatam, Baser al-Dowleh, Montazem al-Hokama, Haj Mohammad Aqa Mojarad Irani, Habibollah Salehi, Houshang Zarif, Reza Vahdani, Asadollah Hejazi, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b6%d8%a7-%d9%84%d8%b7%d9%81%db%8c/\">Mohammad Reza Lotfi</a>, Hossein Alizadeh, Dariush Talaei, Dariush Pirniakan, and many others were among his students.</p>\n\n<p>Finally, Aqa Ali Akbar Shahnazi passed away on 28 Esfand 1363 solar at the age of eighty-seven in the Oil Company Hospital in Tehran. His resting place is in the Imamzadeh Abdollah mausoleum in Shahr-e Rey.</p>', '2023-12-23 20:47:22', 0, '2026-01-23 10:10:22', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1544, 'posts', 87, 'en', NULL, 'Music of the Hakhamaneshian | The status of music in Iran during the Hakhamaneshian period', NULL, 'This article examines music during the Achaemenid Empire and its role within the social and cultural structure of that era. During this period, the two main institutions—politics and religion—served as centers of power and wealth, providing the foundation for artistic development, including music. Royal courts and temples not only hosted architects and visual artists but also poets and musicians who contributed to the entertainment and prestige of the elite.\n\nHistorical descriptions indicate that the Achaemenid aristocracy enjoyed music, singing, and dancing, using instruments such as the flute, drum, and daf in their gatherings. With the emergence of non-religious music, performers were divided into two groups: skilled musicians who entertained the elite and others who performed among the general public.\n\nOverall, the music of this period can be categorized into three main types: religious, courtly, and folk, each with its own distinct function and context.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Music of the Achaemenid Period</h2>\n\n<p>Now let us take a brief look at the art of the Achaemenid period. The two institutions of politics and religion have, from ancient times to the present, been arenas for the gathering of ambitious and power-seeking people, and the main centers of these two institutions — namely courts and temples — have been the centers of wealth accumulation and, consequently, the provision of all kinds of facilities for rulers and clergy.</p>\n\n<p>The most prominent and exquisite buildings created by humans are those built at the command of the leaders of politics and religion to display the power and wealth of these institutions to ordinary people and force them to bow before such grandeur; these are the very buildings that are the product of the artistry of the distinguished architects, sculptors, and painters of each era.</p>\n\n<p>In this context, the production and creation of two other groups of artists — namely poets and musicians — was postponed until the lamps of these palaces were lit and the wealthy took up residence in them and wished to spend their nights in pleasure:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Morteza Ravandi: Will Durant describes the life of the nobility in the Achaemenid period as follows:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">“... They had beautiful houses and splendid gardens that sometimes became hunting grounds and places for keeping various collections of animals. In their homes they collected valuable furnishings, such as tables with gold and silver covers or inlaid with these two precious metals, and beds whose splendid covers were brought from other countries, and soft carpets that ... they used to cover the floors of their rooms.</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">They drank wine from golden cups, and the shelves of the rooms were decorated with vases made by foreigners. They loved singing and dancing and enjoyed playing the lute [or musical instruments?], the flute, the drum, and the daf.”</p>\n\n<p>With the emergence of non-religious music in the Assyrian period, willingly or unwillingly, the performers of this new music were divided into two groups. The most skilled among them became the entertainers of the gatherings of the nobility and upper classes, while the group with lesser skill served the common people and performed their art in streets, alleys, and the homes of the lower classes.</p>\n\n<p>Up to this point, without having any knowledge of the characteristics and details of such <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">music</a>, we can classify the music of this period into three groups: “religious,” “court,” and “popular,” and naturally each of these types must have been presented in ceremonies such as celebrations and mourning or weddings and funerals in different manners and expressions specific to themselves.</p>\n\n<p></p>', '2024-02-09 22:07:32', 0, '2024-08-30 19:03:56', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1545, 'posts', 88, 'en', NULL, 'Music of the Ashkanian(Parthian) | The status of music in Iran during the Ashkanian(Parthian) period', NULL, 'This article examines music during the Parthian Empire. Very limited information is available about music in this period, and it appears that Parthian rulers showed relatively little interest in the arts compared to later dynasties. As a result, few significant or advanced artistic achievements from this era have survived.\n\nHistorical sources suggest that the range of musical instruments was limited, mainly including the flute, ney, pipe, drum, and dhol, with less emphasis on string instruments. However, some scholars also mention the use of instruments such as the tanbur. The music of this period was likely simpler and somewhat rough in character, with a strong rhythmic and percussive nature.\n\nThe article also highlights that much of the cultural and historical record of the Parthian period was lost, partly due to the destruction or neglect of their legacy by subsequent dynasties, particularly the Sassanids. Overall, Parthian music can be considered less developed compared to other periods and primarily associated with celebrations and social gatherings.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the music of the Parthian (Ashkanian) period. The Parthian kings, unlike the accounts mentioned about the Arsacids, were not particularly fond of art and did not strongly support music. From the Parthian period, which lasted about five centuries, we know very little about the state of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">music</a>.</p>\n\n<p>Morteza Ravandi: Because the Parthian kings and their courtiers did not pay much attention to artistic matters, during this long period, no remarkable works were left in the artistic fields [...] From the buildings and artifacts that remain from this period and from the collection of stone reliefs, one can perceive the technical weakness of the artists.</p>\n\n<p>Also, the instruments used in the music of this period were limited, and apparently stringed instruments were used less:</p>\n\n<p>Morteza Ravandi: Their musical instruments consisted of: flute, reed pipe, drum, and large drum. In banquets and festivals, the gathering usually ended with dancing.</p>\n\n<p>Of course, other researchers, while emphasizing that the music of the Parthian period was not very advanced, have also considered the tanbur among their instruments:</p>\n\n<p>Hassan Pirnia: From these Parthian arts, we can only say that they had a type of music, but from the musical instruments it is clear that their music was harsh because in banquets they mostly played the reed pipe and tanbur [...] Therefore, their music was mostly rhythmic (Herodian, Book 4, section 20) ... No information about Parthian literature has reached us, but it is known that they had songs that were not in plain prose (prose without rhetorical and semantic devices) and they sang those songs.</p>\n\n<p>However, regarding the culture and works of the Parthian period, there is a point that should not be overlooked. Unfortunately, in all historical periods, the first action of Iranians after triumphing over previous dynasties and establishing new governments has been to deny all the positive and effective achievements and performances of the previous ones, and in most cases, they have made a strong effort to destroy the works and achievements of their predecessors:</p>\n\n<p>Morteza Ravandi: The Parthians ruled Iran for about five centuries (247 BCE to 224 CE). Their successors, the Sassanids, tried harder than the Abbasids did with the Umayyads to erase their memory and history.</p>\n\n<p></p>', '2024-02-09 22:09:17', 0, '2024-08-30 23:18:38', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1546, 'posts', 89, 'en', NULL, 'Music of the Sassanian | The status of music in Iran during the Sassanian period', NULL, 'This article examines the music of the Sasanian era, highlighting its significant development compared to earlier periods. Sasanian kings—especially Bahram Gur and Khosrow Parviz—were strong patrons of music, leading to its widespread popularity in both royal courts and among the general public.\n\nMusicians were generally divided into two groups: court musicians (elite professionals) and folk performers. One of the most important figures of this era was Barbad, considered the greatest musician of his time. He organized music into structured categories: Khosrovani, Lahns, and Dastans. His famous “Thirty Lahns” are among the most notable musical legacies, although their exact musical structure remains unknown.\n\nMusic during this period existed in both vocal (with lyrics) and instrumental (without lyrics) forms. Instruments such as the oud, ney, tanbur, and harp were commonly used. Historical accounts also suggest that music had the power to convey meaning even without words, as illustrated in stories about Barbad.\n\nOverall, the Sasanian period is considered one of the most flourishing eras in the history of Persian music, marked by artistic growth, diversity, and early forms of musical systematization.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article from the Sornaz Music School, we examine the biography of <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Gholamhossein Darvish</span></strong>, the prominent Iranian tar player known as <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Darvish Khan</span></strong>. We will also discuss his surviving works and pieces, as well as the innovations of this prominent figure in Iranian music. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>Biography of Gholamhossein Darvish, known as Darvish Khan</h2>\n\n<p>Gholam Hossein Darvish, son of Haji Bashir Taleqani, was born in 1251 solar. His father had some familiarity with playing the setar. Around the age of eleven, Gholam Hossein entered the music department of Dar al-Fonun, where he learned musical notation (to some extent) and playing the small drum and trumpet. Because his father addressed his friends as “darvish,” this title was applied to Gholam Hossein, and later his family name was also chosen as “Darvish.”</p>\n\n<p>Darvish Khan also played the small drum for some time in the music band of Malek-e Jahan (Aziz al-Soltan) and Kamran Mirza Nayeb al-Saltaneh. After a while, he attended the music class of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86%d9%82%d9%84%db%8c/\">Aqa Hossein-Qoli</a> and learned tar and setar from him. He joined the ensemble of Sho’a al-Saltaneh (son of Mozaffar al-Din Shah), the governor of Fars, and went with him to Shiraz, where he got married. To compensate for the insufficient stipend he received from Sho’a al-Saltaneh and to make a living, he was forced to accept invitations from other dignitaries such as “Qavam Shirazi,” which caused him to incur the wrath of Sho’a al-Saltaneh. The latter ordered that Darvish Khan’s fingers be cut off.</p>\n\n<p>Kamal al-Saltaneh, the father of Abolhasan Saba and a friend of Darvish Khan, interceded with Sho’a al-Saltaneh and saved him from that terrible punishment. Some time later, Darvish opened a music class in his own house in Tehran.</p>\n\n<p>When Sho’a al-Saltaneh learned of this, he sent a servant to his class to force him to return to Shiraz. Darvish escaped the servant of Sho’a al-Saltaneh through some arrangements. Knowing that intercession would no longer be effective, he sought refuge with another friend who was the caretaker of the British Embassy. The caretaker introduced him to the British ambassador. Darvish succeeded in winning their favor by playing several European melodies on the tar accompanied by the ambassador’s wife on piano, and obtained a letter from the embassy to Sho’a al-Saltaneh requesting Darvish’s freedom. With that letter, he was freed from Sho’a al-Saltaneh’s harassment.</p>\n\n<p>From then on, Darvish Khan devoted his time to his music class, training students, and composing musical pieces. He became the conductor of the Akhavan Safa’i orchestra. The common metric pieces during Darvish’s time were mostly in 6/8 meter, along with some <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%90%d9%86%da%af/\">rengs</a>, metric pieces from the radif, and some <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%86%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d8%b6%d8%b1%d8%a7%d8%a8/\">chaharmezrabs</a>. Darvish Khan invented the pishdaramad for ensemble playing by orchestra members. Before him, short pishdaramads by Mohammad Sadeq Khan and Aqa Hossein-Qoli were played, but Darvish developed the pishdaramad in a new style.</p>\n\n<p>Darvish’s works are partly traditional and partly innovative, and his pishdaramads have characteristics that had no precedent at that time. We will discuss them in the examination of his style.</p>\n\n<p>Darvish Khan would call his friends “Yāpir Jān,” and they would also address him by the same title.</p>\n\n<p>He traveled abroad twice to record records. In the first trip, together with Moshir Homayun Shahrdar, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b3%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%b7%d8%a7%d9%87%d8%b1%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%af%d9%87/\">Seyed Hossein Taherzadeh</a>, Reza-Qoli Khan Nowruzi, Hossein Hang-Afarin, Baqer Khan Rameshgar, Asadollah Khan, and Akbar Khan Flutist (brother of Hossein Hang-Afarin), he went to London via Russia and recorded records at the His Master’s Voice company celebration. This trip took place around 1910 CE, and they stayed in London for about three months.</p>\n\n<p>Darvish Khan’s second trip was about a year later, when he went to Tbilisi with Baqer Khan, Taherzadeh, Eqbal al-Soltan, and Abdollah Davami and recorded records there. Because it coincided with World War I in 1914 CE, only a few of those records reached Tehran.</p>\n\n<p>In addition to the concerts of the Okhovat Association, Darvish held several other concerts, including:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>A concert at the Grand Hotel hall on Lalehzar Street in Tehran to help fire victims in Tehran’s bazaar;</li>\n  <li>And other concerts, mostly in the same location, to help victims of the looting in Urmia, fire victims in Amol, and famine victims in Russia (at the Russian Embassy).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Darvish had a great love for flowers and growing them, and he cultivated various beautiful and fragrant flowers in his small house. He was very close to nature and occupied himself with the scent of flowers and the song of the nightingale. He had a precise ear and could even play the fretless tar with delicacy.</p>\n\n<p>Before Darvish Khan, the tar had five strings. He expanded the range of the tar and added another string (the white string between the yellow and the bass string), which is considered an interesting innovation in the playing of this instrument.</p>\n\n<p>Darvish Khan was an unceremonious, friendly, perceptive, and polite man. Another of his innovations was preparing a gold medal in the shape of a tabarzin (dervish symbol), which he gave to the graduates of his music class. Receiving this medal was a source of pride for them. Because of his humanitarian concerts and artistic services, Darvish Khan received the scientific medal from the Ministry of Culture.</p>\n\n<p>In a concert he held with the assistance of Mirza Abbas Qoli Khan Motarjem al-Mamalek Qarib at the Grand Hotel, where the French minister plenipotentiary was also present, a medal was awarded to him at the request of the minister. In the later years of his life, Darvish Khan became impoverished. It is said that he sold his residential house and used the money for his trip abroad to record records and to preserve examples of Iranian music.</p>\n\n<p>On the night of Wednesday, 2 Azar 1305 solar, while riding in a carriage from Amirieh Street toward the north, his carriage collided with an automobile driven by the driver’s apprentice instead of the driver. He died in the Nazmieh (police) hospital from a head injury and was buried in the Zahir al-Dowleh mausoleum (Darband Street, Shemiran). On 7 Azar 1305 solar, a memorial ceremony was held by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d9%86%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Ali-Naqi Vaziri</a>, president of the Higher School of Music, at the same school. Vaziri delivered a speech in praise of Darvish, and the Afshari tasnif “Bad-e Khazan Vazān Shod” by Darvish Khan was performed by the orchestra. The session ended with Vaziri’s solo tar playing.</p>\n\n<p>Morteza Ney-Davud said that one day in a class session, Darvish Khan was unable to teach everyone for some reason. One of the students named “Khan Baba” said: “Yāpir Jān, if you cannot teach everyone, please play a little for us.” Darvish accepted and played the setar in such a way that it seemed as if he had hypnotized everyone. Both he played beautifully and the listeners were receptive. When he put the instrument down, no one realized that the playing had ended, and for a few seconds everyone was stunned.</p>\n\n<p>Some students became discouraged from continuing the lessons and said that if this is what playing the instrument is, we will never reach such a level. One of them was myself (Morteza Ney-Davud), who said to myself that I would never become Darvish Khan and I stopped going to his class.</p>\n\n<p>No matter how much my father insisted, I said I would not go anymore. Finally, my father went to Darvish, who called me and said: “My son Morteza, come and finish your work.” Of course, I was also young at that time.</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%b1%d8%aa%d8%b6%db%8c-%d9%86%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%88%d8%af/\">Morteza Ney-Davud</a> himself later became one of the famous figures of Iranian music, about whom we will speak regarding him and his masterful playing. He himself said that Darvish Khan correctly assessed the talent and capability of his students. During teaching, he would select and simplify the detailed radif of the old masters. When performing, after the avaz, he would play a suitable metric piece or tasnif. He lived contentedly, and although he lived frugally, he was still generous and open-handed.</p>\n\n<h2>Students of Darvish Khan</h2>\n\n<p>Shokrollah Qahremani (1261–1327 solar), known as Shokri, was a student of the military music department and played the “Abu A.” He benefited from the musical teachings of his elder brother Esma’il Qahremani and later attended Darvish Khan’s class. Shokri had a close friendship with Aref Qazvini and participated in his concerts. His plectrum was delicate and sweet, and he also collaborated with the famous Isfahani singer Habib Shater Haji. In his later years, Shokri became disabled, and Qamar al-Moluk Vaziri sang in a concert held for his benefit.</p>\n\n<p>Shokrollah Qahremani also composed the Bayat-e Tork tasnif in honor of Qamar al-Moluk Vaziri, which begins with these lyrics:</p>\n\n<p>Be del joz gham-e an qamar nadaram — Khosham keh gham-e degar nadaram</p>\n\n<p>The lyrics of this tasnif were written by Malek al-Sho’ara Bahar and were recorded on Columbia records with the voice of Nahid Nikbakht, accompanied by the violin of Nowzad and the tar of Yahya Zarpanjeh, catalog number 15029.</p>\n\n<p>Akbar Khan Nowruzi, the Isfahani player (deceased 1332 solar), was one of Shokri’s students. He had a delicate and emotional playing style and played the tar with excellent taste and sweetness.</p>\n\n<p>Ali Mohammad Safa’i (1276–1318 solar), in addition to playing, also made magnificent setars with mother-of-pearl inlay and multi-part bowls.</p>\n\n<p>Hossein-Qoli Ghaffari (deceased 1299 solar), Abdollah Dadvar (Qavam al-Saltaneh), Musa Ma’rufi, Morteza Ney-Davud, Abolhasan Saba, Hossein Sanjari, and Arslan Dargahi were also students of Darvish Khan. Morteza Ney-Davud said that because Darvish had no son, he opened a music class in Tehran and named it “Darvish Music School” to commemorate his master’s name.</p>\n\n<h2>Works of Gholamhossein Darvish Khan</h2>\n\n<h3>Pishdaramads of Darvish Khan</h3>\n\n<p>Pishdaramads of Mahur in 3/4 meter, Abu Ata in 3/4 meter (two pishdaramads), Segah in 2/4 meter, Shushtari in 3/4 meter, Afshari in 3/4 meter, and Rak in 3/4 and 6/8 meters.</p>\n\n<h3>Rengs of Darvish Khan</h3>\n\n<p>Qahr o Āshti (Mahur), Abu Ata, Segah Shushtari, Homayun, Afshari, Parichehr o Parizad (Esfahan), Ghani o Faqir (Esfahan), and Darvish’s Polka melody.</p>\n\n<h2>Published Works of Darvish</h2>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d9%be%db%8c%d8%b4-%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%a2%d9%85%d8%af/\">Pishdaramad</a> of Dashti in 3/4 meter (Ali-Naqi Vaziri’s Tar Instruction, 1315 solar), whose notation is attributed to Darvish, but Ruhollah Khaleqi and some others consider it to belong to Rokn al-Din Mokhtari; pishdaramad of Abu Ata (Ali-Naqi Vaziri’s Violin Instruction, 1316 solar); reng of Shushtari and reng of Esfahan (Twenty-five Metric Pieces, 1327 solar); reng of Qahr o Āshti in Mahur; pishdaramad of Afshari; reng of Esfahan; march (Second Violin Book of the National Music School); reng of Afshari; tasnif of Afshari “Bad-e Khazan” with lyrics by Malek al-Sho’ara Bahar (Third Violin Book of the National Music School); pishdaramad of Segah; the Hesār section of the pishdaramad of Chahargah;</p>\n\n<p>reng of Mahur (Fourth Violin Book of the National Music School); tasnif “Arous-e Gol az Bad-e Saba” in Segah with lyrics by Malek al-Sho’ara Bahar; tasnif of Abu Ata “Bahār-e Delkash” with lyrics by Bahar; reng of Esfahan and march (Second Tar and Setar Book of the National Music School); tasnif of Mahur “Ze Man Negāram” with lyrics by Malek al-Sho’ara Bahar, whose notation was written by Salar Mo’azzaz and printed in Tehran in 1300 solar; reng of Shushtari; and Parichehr o Parizad (Chang Magazine, first and fourth issues, 1325 solar).</p>\n\n<p>Darvish’s polka is in 2/4 meter and cheerful in the key of F major, similar to local Russian dance melodies. The modulation from Mahur to the fifth above in Chahargah in this melody is one of Darvish’s interesting innovations. Darvish adopted the name “polka” from foreigners, and the beginning of the piece is in the European music style, but the main theme is completely Iranian. [Khaleqi, 1340 solar]</p>\n\n<p>In Darvish Khan’s <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%aa%d8%b5%d9%86%db%8c%d9%81/\">tasnifs</a>, the melodic progression is appropriate to the subject of the poem in terms of mood. For example, in the Afshari tasnif “Bad-e Khazan” with lyrics by Malek al-Sho’ara Bahar, which contains the theme of winter and the expression of the hardship of the poor, the melody has a heavy rhythm and the melody with specific sustains on the witness note of the Afshari naghmah and an ascending reference to the variable note conveys a feeling of tenderness and compassion that matches Bahar’s poem (“Bad-e khazan vazān shod, chehreh-ye gol nehan shod”). The choice of the Afshari naghmah with the mentioned rhythm reminds me (Dr. Sasan Sepanta) of a sentence by Ali-Naqi Vaziri, who said about the Afshari naghmah: “In the Afshari naghmah, delicate feelings can be expressed.”</p>\n\n<p>Another characteristic of Darvish’s melodies is rhythmic variety, and he avoided monotonous repetition. This property of variety and developing different rhythms for Iranian rengs demonstrates Darvish Khan’s innate genius.</p>\n\n<p>In the contemporary period, national instrument orchestras, the Higher National Music School, and other orchestras under the direction of Ruhollah Khaleqi, Faramarz Payvar, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b6%d8%a7-%d9%84%d8%b7%d9%81%db%8c/\">Mohammad Reza Lotfi</a>, and a few others have performed some of Darvish’s works with a new appearance in terms of orchestral instruments. In summary, Darvish’s characteristics are as follows:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>Interest in learning music from available sources, such as the music department of Dar al-Fonun and the music masters of that period;</li>\n  <li>Love of freedom and desire for liberation from the monopoly of the Qajar nobility;</li>\n  <li>Interest in group work, ensemble playing, forming an orchestra, and holding concerts, in contrast to the prevalent tendency toward individualism and solo playing in that period;</li>\n  <li>Using art for humanitarian purposes and allocating concert proceeds to the needy;</li>\n  <li>Interest in training students and teaching and transmitting knowledge without jealousy or stinginess;</li>\n  <li>Independence of spirit and avoidance of greed and avarice, and not using art as a means to accumulate wealth;</li>\n  <li>Having artistic creative power and innovation in creating new rhythmic variety and avoiding certain monotonous methods;</li>\n  <li>Expanding the range of the tar by adding a sixth string to this instrument; excellent taste in playing and controlled, clean strokes; elegance in fingering and plectrum; physical and mental comfort during performance; and variety and innovation in responding to the singer;</li>\n  <li>Moderation, contentment, and simplicity in playing, in contrast to the common habit of showing off and ostentation in performances of that period;</li>\n  <li>Excellent use of what he had learned in composition and the invention of pishdaramad, various rengs, tasnifs, etc., with pleasant melodies. Dr. Hormoz Farhat says about him: “If he had been born in a more favorable period and environment for the development of a musician, such as the environment of advanced European countries, there is no doubt that the cultivation of his natural talent would have led to the creation of works that would have placed him among the great composers of the world.” [Farhat, 1337 solar];</li>\n  <li>He played the tar and setar with utmost mastery and had a fondness for performing Iranian music with metric rhythm. He was also somewhat familiar with musical notation, and some of his notations that he wrote while teaching Mohammad Hassan Mirza (the crown prince of Ahmad Shah) were with him.</li>\n</ol>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the biography and works of Darvish Khan, please share them in the comments section of the article with us to improve the quality of this content.</p>', '2024-02-09 22:13:03', 0, '2024-08-30 21:30:37', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1547, 'posts', 90, 'en', NULL, 'Biography of Gholam-Hossein Darvish, known as Darvish Khan, one of the innovators of Iranian music', NULL, 'This article explores the life and works of Gholamhossein Darvish (Darvish Khan), one of the most prominent tar players of the Qajar era. He began his musical journey at a young age and studied under masters such as Aqa Hossein-Qoli. Beyond his virtuosity, he played a key role in developing Persian music by introducing innovations like the modern pishdaramad (prelude), expanding rhythmic compositions, and adding a sixth string to the tar.\n\nDespite courtly pressures, Darvish Khan pursued an independent artistic path, trained many influential students including Morteza Neydavood and Abolhasan Saba, and contributed to public and charitable concerts. His works include preludes, rhythmic pieces (reng), and songs, some set to poetry by notable poets like Bahar.\n\nHis musical style is characterized by creativity, rhythmic diversity, simplicity, and expressive clarity. Darvish Khan significantly influenced later generations of Iranian musicians. Although his life ended in a tragic accident, his artistic legacy remains deeply embedded in Persian music.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article from the Sornaz Music School, we examine the biography of <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Gholamhossein Darvish</span></strong>, the prominent Iranian tar player known as <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Darvish Khan</span></strong>. We will also discuss his surviving works and pieces, as well as the innovations of this prominent figure in Iranian music. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>Biography of Gholamhossein Darvish, known as Darvish Khan</h2>\n\n<p>Gholam Hossein Darvish, son of Haji Bashir Taleqani, was born in 1251 solar. His father had some familiarity with playing the setar. Around the age of eleven, Gholam Hossein entered the music department of Dar al-Fonun, where he learned musical notation (to some extent) and playing the small drum and trumpet. Because his father addressed his friends as “darvish,” this title was applied to Gholam Hossein, and later his family name was also chosen as “Darvish.”</p>\n\n<p>Darvish Khan also played the small drum for some time in the music band of Malek-e Jahan (Aziz al-Soltan) and Kamran Mirza Nayeb al-Saltaneh. After a while, he attended the music class of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86%d9%82%d9%84%db%8c/\">Aqa Hossein-Qoli</a> and learned tar and setar from him. He joined the ensemble of Sho’a al-Saltaneh (son of Mozaffar al-Din Shah), the governor of Fars, and went with him to Shiraz, where he got married. To compensate for the insufficient stipend he received from Sho’a al-Saltaneh and to make a living, he was forced to accept invitations from other dignitaries such as “Qavam Shirazi,” which caused him to incur the wrath of Sho’a al-Saltaneh. The latter ordered that Darvish Khan’s fingers be cut off.</p>\n\n<p>Kamal al-Saltaneh, the father of Abolhasan Saba and a friend of Darvish Khan, interceded with Sho’a al-Saltaneh and saved him from that terrible punishment. Some time later, Darvish opened a music class in his own house in Tehran.</p>\n\n<p>When Sho’a al-Saltaneh learned of this, he sent a servant to his class to force him to return to Shiraz. Darvish escaped the servant of Sho’a al-Saltaneh through some arrangements. Knowing that intercession would no longer be effective, he sought refuge with another friend who was the caretaker of the British Embassy. The caretaker introduced him to the British ambassador. Darvish succeeded in winning their favor by playing several European melodies on the tar accompanied by the ambassador’s wife on piano, and obtained a letter from the embassy to Sho’a al-Saltaneh requesting Darvish’s freedom. With that letter, he was freed from Sho’a al-Saltaneh’s harassment.</p>\n\n<p>From then on, Darvish Khan devoted his time to his music class, training students, and composing musical pieces. He became the conductor of the Akhavan Safa’i orchestra. The common metric pieces during Darvish’s time were mostly in 6/8 meter, along with some <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%90%d9%86%da%af/\">rengs</a>, metric pieces from the radif, and some <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%86%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d8%b6%d8%b1%d8%a7%d8%a8/\">chaharmezrabs</a>. Darvish Khan invented the pishdaramad for ensemble playing by orchestra members. Before him, short pishdaramads by Mohammad Sadeq Khan and Aqa Hossein-Qoli were played, but Darvish developed the pishdaramad in a new style.</p>\n\n<p>Darvish’s works are partly traditional and partly innovative, and his pishdaramads have characteristics that had no precedent at that time. We will discuss them in the examination of his style.</p>\n\n<p>Darvish Khan would call his friends “Yāpir Jān,” and they would also address him by the same title.</p>\n\n<p>He traveled abroad twice to record records. In the first trip, together with Moshir Homayun Shahrdar, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b3%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%b7%d8%a7%d9%87%d8%b1%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%af%d9%87/\">Seyed Hossein Taherzadeh</a>, Reza-Qoli Khan Nowruzi, Hossein Hang-Afarin, Baqer Khan Rameshgar, Asadollah Khan, and Akbar Khan Flutist (brother of Hossein Hang-Afarin), he went to London via Russia and recorded records at the His Master’s Voice company celebration. This trip took place around 1910 CE, and they stayed in London for about three months.</p>\n\n<p>Darvish Khan’s second trip was about a year later, when he went to Tbilisi with Baqer Khan, Taherzadeh, Eqbal al-Soltan, and Abdollah Davami and recorded records there. Because it coincided with World War I in 1914 CE, only a few of those records reached Tehran.</p>\n\n<p>In addition to the concerts of the Okhovat Association, Darvish held several other concerts, including:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>A concert at the Grand Hotel hall on Lalehzar Street in Tehran to help fire victims in Tehran’s bazaar;</li>\n  <li>And other concerts, mostly in the same location, to help victims of the looting in Urmia, fire victims in Amol, and famine victims in Russia (at the Russian Embassy).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Darvish had a great love for flowers and growing them, and he cultivated various beautiful and fragrant flowers in his small house. He was very close to nature and occupied himself with the scent of flowers and the song of the nightingale. He had a precise ear and could even play the fretless tar with delicacy.</p>\n\n<p>Before Darvish Khan, the tar had five strings. He expanded the range of the tar and added another string (the white string between the yellow and the bass string), which is considered an interesting innovation in the playing of this instrument.</p>\n\n<p>Darvish Khan was an unceremonious, friendly, perceptive, and polite man. Another of his innovations was preparing a gold medal in the shape of a tabarzin (dervish symbol), which he gave to the graduates of his music class. Receiving this medal was a source of pride for them. Because of his humanitarian concerts and artistic services, Darvish Khan received the scientific medal from the Ministry of Culture.</p>\n\n<p>In a concert he held with the assistance of Mirza Abbas Qoli Khan Motarjem al-Mamalek Qarib at the Grand Hotel, where the French minister plenipotentiary was also present, a medal was awarded to him at the request of the minister. In the later years of his life, Darvish Khan became impoverished. It is said that he sold his residential house and used the money for his trip abroad to record records and to preserve examples of Iranian music.</p>\n\n<p>On the night of Wednesday, 2 Azar 1305 solar, while riding in a carriage from Amirieh Street toward the north, his carriage collided with an automobile driven by the driver’s apprentice instead of the driver. He died in the Nazmieh (police) hospital from a head injury and was buried in the Zahir al-Dowleh mausoleum (Darband Street, Shemiran). On 7 Azar 1305 solar, a memorial ceremony was held by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d9%86%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Ali-Naqi Vaziri</a>, president of the Higher School of Music, at the same school. Vaziri delivered a speech in praise of Darvish, and the Afshari tasnif “Bad-e Khazan Vazān Shod” by Darvish Khan was performed by the orchestra. The session ended with Vaziri’s solo tar playing.</p>\n\n<p>Morteza Ney-Davud said that one day in a class session, Darvish Khan was unable to teach everyone for some reason. One of the students named “Khan Baba” said: “Yāpir Jān, if you cannot teach everyone, please play a little for us.” Darvish accepted and played the setar in such a way that it seemed as if he had hypnotized everyone. Both he played beautifully and the listeners were receptive. When he put the instrument down, no one realized that the playing had ended, and for a few seconds everyone was stunned.</p>\n\n<p>Some students became discouraged from continuing the lessons and said that if this is what playing the instrument is, we will never reach such a level. One of them was myself (Morteza Ney-Davud), who said to myself that I would never become Darvish Khan and I stopped going to his class.</p>\n\n<p>No matter how much my father insisted, I said I would not go anymore. Finally, my father went to Darvish, who called me and said: “My son Morteza, come and finish your work.” Of course, I was also young at that time.</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%b1%d8%aa%d8%b6%db%8c-%d9%86%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%88%d8%af/\">Morteza Ney-Davud</a> himself later became one of the famous figures of Iranian music, about whom we will speak regarding him and his masterful playing. He himself said that Darvish Khan correctly assessed the talent and capability of his students. During teaching, he would select and simplify the detailed radif of the old masters. When performing, after the avaz, he would play a suitable metric piece or tasnif. He lived contentedly, and although he lived frugally, he was still generous and open-handed.</p>\n\n<h2>Students of Darvish Khan</h2>\n\n<p>Shokrollah Qahremani (1261–1327 solar), known as Shokri, was a student of the military music department and played the “Abu A.” He benefited from the musical teachings of his elder brother Esma’il Qahremani and later attended Darvish Khan’s class. Shokri had a close friendship with Aref Qazvini and participated in his concerts. His plectrum was delicate and sweet, and he also collaborated with the famous Isfahani singer Habib Shater Haji. In his later years, Shokri became disabled, and Qamar al-Moluk Vaziri sang in a concert held for his benefit.</p>\n\n<p>Shokrollah Qahremani also composed the Bayat-e Tork tasnif in honor of Qamar al-Moluk Vaziri, which begins with these lyrics:</p>\n\n<p>Be del joz gham-e an qamar nadaram — Khosham keh gham-e degar nadaram</p>\n\n<p>The lyrics of this tasnif were written by Malek al-Sho’ara Bahar and were recorded on Columbia records with the voice of Nahid Nikbakht, accompanied by the violin of Nowzad and the tar of Yahya Zarpanjeh, catalog number 15029.</p>\n\n<p>Akbar Khan Nowruzi, the Isfahani player (deceased 1332 solar), was one of Shokri’s students. He had a delicate and emotional playing style and played the tar with excellent taste and sweetness.</p>\n\n<p>Ali Mohammad Safa’i (1276–1318 solar), in addition to playing, also made magnificent setars with mother-of-pearl inlay and multi-part bowls.</p>\n\n<p>Hossein-Qoli Ghaffari (deceased 1299 solar), Abdollah Dadvar (Qavam al-Saltaneh), Musa Ma’rufi, Morteza Ney-Davud, Abolhasan Saba, Hossein Sanjari, and Arslan Dargahi were also students of Darvish Khan. Morteza Ney-Davud said that because Darvish had no son, he opened a music class in Tehran and named it “Darvish Music School” to commemorate his master’s name.</p>\n\n<h2>Works of Gholamhossein Darvish Khan</h2>\n\n<h3>Pishdaramads of Darvish Khan</h3>\n\n<p>Pishdaramads of Mahur in 3/4 meter, Abu Ata in 3/4 meter (two pishdaramads), Segah in 2/4 meter, Shushtari in 3/4 meter, Afshari in 3/4 meter, and Rak in 3/4 and 6/8 meters.</p>\n\n<h3>Rengs of Darvish Khan</h3>\n\n<p>Qahr o Āshti (Mahur), Abu Ata, Segah Shushtari, Homayun, Afshari, Parichehr o Parizad (Esfahan), Ghani o Faqir (Esfahan), and Darvish’s Polka melody.</p>\n\n<h2>Published Works of Darvish</h2>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d9%be%db%8c%d8%b4-%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%a2%d9%85%d8%af/\">Pishdaramad</a> of Dashti in 3/4 meter (Ali-Naqi Vaziri’s Tar Instruction, 1315 solar), whose notation is attributed to Darvish, but Ruhollah Khaleqi and some others consider it to belong to Rokn al-Din Mokhtari; pishdaramad of Abu Ata (Ali-Naqi Vaziri’s Violin Instruction, 1316 solar); reng of Shushtari and reng of Esfahan (Twenty-five Metric Pieces, 1327 solar); reng of Qahr o Āshti in Mahur; pishdaramad of Afshari; reng of Esfahan; march (Second Violin Book of the National Music School); reng of Afshari; tasnif of Afshari “Bad-e Khazan” with lyrics by Malek al-Sho’ara Bahar (Third Violin Book of the National Music School); pishdaramad of Segah; the Hesār section of the pishdaramad of Chahargah;</p>\n\n<p>reng of Mahur (Fourth Violin Book of the National Music School); tasnif “Arous-e Gol az Bad-e Saba” in Segah with lyrics by Malek al-Sho’ara Bahar; tasnif of Abu Ata “Bahār-e Delkash” with lyrics by Bahar; reng of Esfahan and march (Second Tar and Setar Book of the National Music School); tasnif of Mahur “Ze Man Negāram” with lyrics by Malek al-Sho’ara Bahar, whose notation was written by Salar Mo’azzaz and printed in Tehran in 1300 solar; reng of Shushtari; and Parichehr o Parizad (Chang Magazine, first and fourth issues, 1325 solar).</p>\n\n<p>Darvish’s polka is in 2/4 meter and cheerful in the key of F major, similar to local Russian dance melodies. The modulation from Mahur to the fifth above in Chahargah in this melody is one of Darvish’s interesting innovations. Darvish adopted the name “polka” from foreigners, and the beginning of the piece is in the European music style, but the main theme is completely Iranian. [Khaleqi, 1340 solar]</p>\n\n<p>In Darvish Khan’s <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%aa%d8%b5%d9%86%db%8c%d9%81/\">tasnifs</a>, the melodic progression is appropriate to the subject of the poem in terms of mood. For example, in the Afshari tasnif “Bad-e Khazan” with lyrics by Malek al-Sho’ara Bahar, which contains the theme of winter and the expression of the hardship of the poor, the melody has a heavy rhythm and the melody with specific sustains on the witness note of the Afshari naghmah and an ascending reference to the variable note conveys a feeling of tenderness and compassion that matches Bahar’s poem (“Bad-e khazan vazān shod, chehreh-ye gol nehan shod”). The choice of the Afshari naghmah with the mentioned rhythm reminds me (Dr. Sasan Sepanta) of a sentence by Ali-Naqi Vaziri, who said about the Afshari naghmah: “In the Afshari naghmah, delicate feelings can be expressed.”</p>\n\n<p>Another characteristic of Darvish’s melodies is rhythmic variety, and he avoided monotonous repetition. This property of variety and developing different rhythms for Iranian rengs demonstrates Darvish Khan’s innate genius.</p>\n\n<p>In the contemporary period, national instrument orchestras, the Higher National Music School, and other orchestras under the direction of Ruhollah Khaleqi, Faramarz Payvar, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b6%d8%a7-%d9%84%d8%b7%d9%81%db%8c/\">Mohammad Reza Lotfi</a>, and a few others have performed some of Darvish’s works with a new appearance in terms of orchestral instruments. In summary, Darvish’s characteristics are as follows:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>Interest in learning music from available sources, such as the music department of Dar al-Fonun and the music masters of that period;</li>\n  <li>Love of freedom and desire for liberation from the monopoly of the Qajar nobility;</li>\n  <li>Interest in group work, ensemble playing, forming an orchestra, and holding concerts, in contrast to the prevalent tendency toward individualism and solo playing in that period;</li>\n  <li>Using art for humanitarian purposes and allocating concert proceeds to the needy;</li>\n  <li>Interest in training students and teaching and transmitting knowledge without jealousy or stinginess;</li>\n  <li>Independence of spirit and avoidance of greed and avarice, and not using art as a means to accumulate wealth;</li>\n  <li>Having artistic creative power and innovation in creating new rhythmic variety and avoiding certain monotonous methods;</li>\n  <li>Expanding the range of the tar by adding a sixth string to this instrument; excellent taste in playing and controlled, clean strokes; elegance in fingering and plectrum; physical and mental comfort during performance; and variety and innovation in responding to the singer;</li>\n  <li>Moderation, contentment, and simplicity in playing, in contrast to the common habit of showing off and ostentation in performances of that period;</li>\n  <li>Excellent use of what he had learned in composition and the invention of pishdaramad, various rengs, tasnifs, etc., with pleasant melodies. Dr. Hormoz Farhat says about him: “If he had been born in a more favorable period and environment for the development of a musician, such as the environment of advanced European countries, there is no doubt that the cultivation of his natural talent would have led to the creation of works that would have placed him among the great composers of the world.” [Farhat, 1337 solar];</li>\n  <li>He played the tar and setar with utmost mastery and had a fondness for performing Iranian music with metric rhythm. He was also somewhat familiar with musical notation, and some of his notations that he wrote while teaching Mohammad Hassan Mirza (the crown prince of Ahmad Shah) were with him.</li>\n</ol>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the biography and works of Darvish Khan, please share them in the comments section of the article with us to improve the quality of this content.</p>', '2024-02-12 22:22:20', 0, '2024-12-19 19:08:37', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1548, 'posts', 91, 'en', NULL, 'Biography of Aref Qazvini | Master tasnif composer', NULL, 'Abolqasem Aref Ghazvini (c. 1882–1934) was an Iranian poet, composer, and songwriter born in Qazvin. From a young age, he showed a strong interest in poetry and traditional Persian vocal music. After moving to Tehran, he was briefly introduced to court circles, but his critical and independent personality soon distanced him from royal patronage.\n\nAref became one of the most influential cultural figures of the Iranian Constitutional Revolution. He used poetry and especially “tasnif” (lyrical songs) as a powerful tool for political expression and social awareness. His patriotic and protest songs quickly spread among the public and became widely sung across society.\n\nHis works combined simple but emotional language with Persian musical modes, making them highly accessible and impactful. Many of his compositions were later performed and rearranged by prominent singers and orchestras in Iran.\n\nAref Ghazvini died in 1934 in Hamedan and was buried in the Avicenna Mausoleum.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Biography of Aref Qazvini</h2>\n\n<p>Abolqasem Aref was born around 1261 solar in Qazvin. At the age of thirteen, he studied musical avaz and appropriate poetry for each avaz with Haji Sadeq Kharrazi. Because he had a good voice, at his father Mulla Hadi Vakil’s wish, he spent several years chanting elegies at the pulpit of Mirza Hossein Vaez Qazvini. During the same period, he became familiar with some Persian literary texts such as Sa’di’s Golestan and Tayyebat, attended school, and learned calligraphy from three calligraphers of that time.</p>\n\n<p>As he himself writes, in the summer of 1277 solar he came to Tehran with Sadr al-Islam from Qazvin. Through a friend he became acquainted with Sadr al-Mamalek, who introduced Aref to courtiers who had come to Tehran from Tabriz with Mozaffar al-Din Shah. One of them was Amir Bahador Soltan Ali Khan, the Minister of Court, and the other was Prince Movassaq al-Dowleh, the disabled Mirza. Through them, Aref became acquainted with Ali Asghar Khan Atabak.</p>\n\n<p>He himself writes: “Once or twice when the royal entourage went to Jajrud for hunting, I went to Atabak’s table. He graciously handed me a chicken leg with his own blessed hand, and among the table guests and parasites, I was considered the most honored and proudest.” [Aref Qazvini, 1337 solar]</p>\n\n<p>After some time, his reputation reached Mozaffar al-Din Shah. After being summoned and reciting several ghazals in the Shah’s presence, the Shah ordered Movassaq al-Dowleh to give Aref five hundred tomans and to register his name among the private attendants. Aref writes: “Hearing this sentence had less effect on me than a heavenly thunderbolt.” Aref did not sleep that night until morning and finally went to Sadr al-Mamalek’s house and said: “This calamity is coming upon me because of you.” Eventually, through some arrangements, he was freed from the gatherings of the Shah and Qajar princes.</p>\n\n<p>At the dawn of the freedom-seeking movement and the Constitutional Revolution, Aref, who had closely witnessed the hypocrisy and deception of despotism, cooperated with the constitutionalists. He participated in their struggles with patriotic poems and by composing <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%aa%d8%b5%d9%86%db%8c%d9%81/\">tasnifs</a>, and during these activities he became acquainted with Iraj Mirza, Malek al-Sho’ara Bahar, Mirzadeh Eshqi, and other poets, writers, and artists of his time.</p>\n\n<p>Aref considered poetry, and especially the tasnif, the best means of defending freedom and arousing the people, and he created his works in this direction. In that period, wherever there was music and singing, most of Aref’s tasnifs were on everyone’s lips, and all people would sing them together and join the main singer in chorus.</p>\n\n<p>Throughout the period of despotism, Aref criticized the incompetent rulers, and this group was never safe from his poems. During World War I and the subsequent events and the invasion of foreigners into Iran, Aref went to Istanbul with Iranian mujahideen. After returning to the country in 1337 AH and observing Iran’s chaotic situation, with an even more sorrowful heart he delivered messages of freedom to the nation through his poems.</p>\n\n<p>He had great devotion to Colonel Mohammad Taqi Khan Pessian, the patriotic and honorable officer, and their acquaintance began during the same period of migration. After the Colonel’s uprising in Khorasan, Aref went to Mashhad and hurried to meet the Colonel in Bagh-e Khuni and called him the savior of the homeland.</p>\n\n<p>The martyrdom of the Colonel on the order of Qavam al-Saltaneh saddened Aref even more. With a voice full of emotion and heartfelt sorrow, he sang the tasnifs he had composed for the Colonel in several concerts. In the last years of his life, Aref was forced to live in seclusion in Hamedan and spent the rest of his life in silence, isolation, and poverty. Except for a few close friends and eager admirers, no one visited him, and he also accepted no help from anyone.</p>\n\n<p>Dr. Rezazadeh Shafaq, in a letter on the occasion of the English translation of Aref’s poems, addressed to the translator and publisher, Dinshah Irani, the learned president of the Zoroastrian Association of Bombay, wrote:</p>\n\n<p>“Allow me to seize the opportunity and make an important request from that respected association. It is that Aref, now, due to his freedom-loving nature, honesty, and self-respect, has become very distressed, needy, and desperate. If it is possible for that honorable association to provide him with some assistance, it would be most timely and appropriate. And since Aref does not accept anything as a gift from friends, it would be better to present it under the title of the translation of his poems and offer him some help.” (From the letter dated 25 Shahrivar 1308 solar from Dr. Rezazadeh Shafaq to Dinshah Irani, the original of which is with the writer.)</p>\n\n<p>Aref passed away on 2 Bahman 1312 solar in Hamedan and was buried in the tomb of Avicenna.</p>\n\n<h2>Aref’s Tasnifs</h2>\n\n<p>Aref’s tasnifs recount the struggles of the freedom-seekers during the fifteen-year period of the constitutionalists’ fights. Because he both had a poetic talent and was familiar with the avaz of Iranian music, with his creative talent and spirit of freedom, he was able to create tasnifs that were inspiring and served as the backbone of the social and political events of his time.</p>\n\n<p>Aref’s concerts had a great impact on spreading the ideas of freedom. As soon as he sang a tasnif with his pleasant voice in a concert, it would not be long before that tasnif spread throughout the country, and everyone, high and low, would hum it. Aref composed about twenty-five patriotic tasnifs, all of whose lyrics and melodies are his own. In this regard, he wrote: “If I have rendered no other service to <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">Iranian music</a> and literature, when I composed a patriotic tasnif, not one in ten thousand Iranians knew what ‘vatan’ (homeland) meant. They only imagined that vatan was the city or village where a person was born.” [Aref Qazvini, 1337 solar: 388]</p>\n\n<h3>Tasnif of Shur</h3>\n\n<p>It is Aref’s first patriotic tasnif, which he composed for the entry of the nation’s conquerors into Tehran (1287 solar).</p>\n\n<h3>Tasnif of Dashti</h3>\n\n<p>Composed around 1289 solar. Because of Heydar Khan Amu Oghli’s great fondness for it, Aref printed it in his Divan in his memory. It begins with these lyrics:</p>\n\n<p>Hengam-e mey o fasl-e gol o gasht-e chaman shod ...</p>\n\n<p>Az khun-e javanan-e vatan laleh damideh — Az matam-e sarv-e qadshan sarv-e khamideh...</p>\n\n<p>This is one of Aref’s earliest tasnifs. In the Qajar period, it was recorded on a gramophone record with the voice of “Zari” and the tar of “Arshak Khan” around 1292 solar in an acoustic manner (without electricity) with the specifications E.M.I, D.C.14-12580-1. This tasnif, which is one of Aref’s most passionate works and has special appeal in both melody and lyrics, was later arranged for orchestra by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a> in Golha-ye Rangarang program 246 and performed with the voice of Elaheh.</p>\n\n<h3>Tasnif of Abu Ata</h3>\n\n<p>Related to the year 1290 solar, at a time when Mohammad Ali Mirza, encouraged by the Russians, rose up in Gomesh Tappeh (Gomishan) to reclaim the lost throne. This tasnif, which called true freedom-seekers to sacrifice, begins with this couplet:</p>\n\n<p>Del havas-e sabzeh o sahra nadarad — Meyl be gol gasht o tamasha nadarad</p>\n\n<p>The <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%b9%d8%b7%d8%a7/\">Abu Ata</a> tasnif was first recorded in the late Qajar period with the voice of “Zari” and the tar of “Arshak Khan” in an acoustic manner with the specifications G.C.13177. Years later, it was performed in Golha-ye Rangarang program 321 with arrangement and direction by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ac%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%af-%d9%85%d8%b9%d8%b1%d9%88%d9%81%db%8c/\">Javad Ma’rufi</a> and the voice of Sima Bina.</p>\n\n<h3>Tasnif of Dashti</h3>\n\n<p>Composed in 1289 solar. Because of its publication, Naser al-Molk, based on some false reports given to him, ordered Yeprem Khan to arrest Aref, and Aref was forced to take refuge in the shrine of Hazrat Abdolazim. This tasnif, which was later performed on radio with the voice of Abdolwahab Shahidi and another performance with the voice of Marziyeh, begins with this couplet:</p>\n\n<p>Geryeh ra be masti bahaneh kardam — Shekveh ha az dast-e zamaneh kardam</p>\n\n<h3>Tasnif of Afshari</h3>\n\n<p>Composed in 1290 solar and recorded around 1294 solar with the voice of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%af%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%85%db%8c/\">Abdollah Davami</a> and the tar of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ba%d9%84%d8%a7%d9%85%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%af%d8%b1%d9%88%db%8c%d8%b4/\">Darvish Khan</a> in an acoustic manner. This is the same tasnif whose lyrics concerned the expulsion of Shuster. Aref had asked Davami to sing and record it during the Tbilisi trip, but Darvish refused because the lyrics were political. Finally, it was recorded with different lyrics and a new opening line:</p>\n\n<p>Az kafam raha shod qarar-e del — Nist dast-e man ekhtiyar-e del</p>\n\n<p>The American Morgan Shuster was invited in 1289 solar to reform Iran’s financial affairs, and there was hope that the situation of the Ministry of Finance would improve. However, the Russian Tsarist government demanded his immediate expulsion. Aref composed the tasnif “Nang-e An Khaneh Keh Mehman ze Sar-e Khan Baravad” on this occasion.</p>\n\n<h3>Tasnif of Shur</h3>\n\n<p>Composed around 1297 solar on the occasion of the Turks’ greed for Azerbaijani soil. It begins with this couplet:</p>\n\n<p>Cheh shurha keh man be pa ze Shahnaz mikonam — Dar shekayat az jahan be shah baz mikonam</p>\n\n<p>This tasnif was recorded around 1306 solar with the voice of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%82%d9%85%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%85%d9%84%d9%88%da%a9-%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Qamar al-Moluk Vaziri</a> on a Polydor record (V-1334). Another performance with the voice of Iran Khanom was recorded in 1309 solar (H.M.V-AX.697). Around 1336 solar, Ruhollah Khaleqi arranged it for orchestra and performed it with the voice of Banan. There is also a performance with the voice of Eqbal al-Soltan, who was fond of Aref.</p>\n\n<h3>Tasnif of Dashti</h3>\n\n<p>Aref composed this tasnif in memory of his honorable friend “Colonel Mohammad Taqi Khan Pessian” in 1301 solar. In the winter concert of the same year at the Grand Hotel hall (the previous location of Tehran’s theater on Lalehzar Street), while several baskets of flowers were around Aref and the musicians were seated in a semicircle around him, he sang it with a voice full of emotion and sorrow. At the beginning of the program, Shokrollah Qahremani started the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%af%d8%b4%d8%aa%db%8c/\">Dashti melody</a> with his pleasant plectrum and strokes, and after the avaz, Aref sang the tasnif with the orchestra:</p>\n\n<p>Geryeh kon keh gar seyl-e khun gari samar nadarad — Naleh’i keh nayad ze nay-e del asar nadarad</p>\n\n<p>This tasnif was first recorded with the voice of Qamar al-Moluk Vaziri and the tar of Morteza Ney-Davud around 1306 solar on a record (H.M.V-AX.355). In later years, Ruhollah Khaleqi arranged it and performed it with the voice of Banan in Golha-ye Rangarang program No. 250. The Golha Orchestra also performed this tasnif with the voice of Abdolwahab Shahidi.</p>\n\n<h3>Tasnif of Esfahan</h3>\n\n<p>It begins with this couplet:</p>\n\n<p>Ta rokhat moqayyad-e neqab ast — Del cho picheh-at be pich o tab ast</p>\n\n<p>This tasnif was recorded in 1306 solar with the voice of Qamar al-Moluk Vaziri and the tar of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%b1%d8%aa%d8%b6%db%8c-%d9%86%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%88%d8%af/\">Morteza Ney-Davud</a> on a record (H.M.V-AX.342).</p>\n\n<h3>Tasnif of Shur</h3>\n\n<p>It concerns the black cabinet of Seyed Zia’ al-Din Tabatabai and begins with this couplet:</p>\n\n<p>Ey dast-e haqq posht o panahat baz a — Cheshm-e arzumand-e negahat baz a</p>\n\n<p>This tasnif was also recorded with the voice of Qamar al-Moluk Vaziri and the tar of Morteza Ney-Davud on a record (H.M.V-AX.385).</p>\n\n<h3>Republic March</h3>\n\n<p>Aref sang this tasnif in the concert of 22 Esfand 1303 solar. It begins with this couplet:</p>\n\n<p>Ruy-e delkash muy-e dijur — Ruy andar muy-e mastur</p>\n\n<p>Qamar al-Moluk Vaziri said she had recorded this tasnif on a record, but on the order of Sardar Sepah (Reza Shah), its broadcast was prevented, and wherever the record was found, it was broken, because the policy had changed and Reza Shah at that time preferred monarchy to republic.</p>\n\n<p>In addition to the above tasnifs, Aref has several other tasnifs that were printed in his Divan, and some of them were also performed and recorded. As examples, we mention a few works:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Tasnif of Afshari “Bad-e Khazani” with the voice of Iran Khanom on a record (H.M.V-AX.676).</li>\n  <li>Tasnif of Dashti “Shaneh bar zolf-e parishan zadeh’i” on a record (H.M.V-AX.670).</li>\n  <li>Tasnif of Segah “Eftkhar-e Afaq,” in memory of Eftkhar al-Saltaneh, performed by an orchestra under the direction of Faramarz Payvar with the voice of Mohammad Reza Shajarian on cassette;</li>\n  <li>Tasnif of Bayat-e Tork “Rahm ey Khoda-ye Dadgar kardi nakardi,” with an orchestra under the direction of Faramarz Payvar and the voice of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b6%d8%a7-%d8%b4%d8%ac%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Mohammad Reza Shajarian</a> on cassette;</li>\n  <li>Tasnif of Dashti “Shaneh bar zolf-e parishan zadeh’i,” with the voice of Nader Golchin;</li>\n  <li>Tasnif “Bolbol-e Shurideh Faghan Mikonad” and the tasnif of Afshari “Az kafam raha shod qarar-e del” with the voice of Marziyeh and radio orchestra performance.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>The Style of Aref</h2>\n\n<p>Aref was the first tasnif composer who introduced social, political, and critical themes into the tasnif. Because he knew music, he had excellent taste in combining beautiful melodies. His tasnif-composing style was such that in a gusheh of a dastgah or avaz of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a>, he would develop a short theme and combine suitable poems with it in such a way that usually at the end of each poetic phrase or hemistich there was also a refrain. The melodic progression, considering the rhythm of the piece, is generally appropriate to the subject and content of the tasnif.</p>\n\n<p>For example, the rhythm of the Dashti tasnif “Geryeh Kon” and the sustains of the final notes of its hemistichs convey the sorrow and grief of the subject of the tasnif, which is the martyrdom of “Colonel Mohammad Taqi Khan Pessian.” The beginning of the tasnif is a phrase that starts on the witness note of Dashti and includes an ascending fourth interval on the tonic note of Shur. The opening words of the tasnif, in the form of the imperative verb “Geryeh Kon,” are delivered with that same ascending fourth interval, and then the melody descends in a falling manner with sustain on the witness note. The descending melody when delivering “seyl-e khun gari samar nadarad” expresses the futility of crying.</p>\n\n<p>Aref’s tasnifs have an introduction that is played by the instrument and is usually the same main melody as the first verse of the tasnif. Most tasnifs have a metric rhythm. In the Shur tasnif (“Cheh Shurha”), Aref developed a three-beat figure.</p>\n\n<p>Regarding the range of Aref’s voice, different opinions have been expressed. Some have written that he had a two-dang voice, which is not correct. One cannot judge from occasional humming in a friendly gathering. Aref’s concerts were held both in the Grand Hotel hall and in open spaces without the use of sound amplification equipment and with natural voice.</p>\n\n<p>Eyewitnesses have told the writer that Aref’s voice was resonant and, according to his natural taste, God-given talent, and practice, it was penetrating and had appropriate power even in the high register. Seyed Mohammad Ali Jamalzadeh writes that when he saw Aref in Baghdad around 1916 CE, Heydar Ali Amu Oghli, Aref’s beloved friend who had freedom-seeking ideas and extraordinary courage, was present there. They were sitting in a room overlooking the rooftop. The audience was eager for Aref to sing an avaz, but because of Aref’s temperament, no one dared to make such a request.</p>\n\n<p>Amu Oghli, who was beloved by Aref, asked him to sing a piece, and Aref accepted his invitation. While singing, Aref’s voice was so loud and reached such a high register that the window panes of the room overlooking the alley began to vibrate. It did not take long before a young Arab outside sang one of Aref’s tasnifs. Jamalzadeh writes: “I realized that good art, whether poetry or music, finds its place everywhere in the world.” [Jamalzadeh, 1356 solar]</p>\n\n<p>In the last two decades, it has become customary to use the good reputation of some musicians and, as admirers, collect materials about them. For example, in a book published about Banan, in addition to the fact that the names of the composers of several Golha programs are incorrectly mentioned, three different vocal ranges (different sound ranges) — bass, baritone, and tenor — which cannot be combined, have been assigned to his voice without any criterion and based on imagination. In that book and some other writings, Banan’s voice has been described as two-dang!</p>\n\n<p>The field of music should be taken seriously from the point of view of specialized research, and any writing for this field out of mere amusement is not appropriate. In recent years, some poets, using the fame they have gained in the press, allow themselves to write about music as well. For this reason, Mr. Fereydoun Moshiri, in the preface he wrote for the book of tasnifs, writes Yahya Zarpanjeh, the tar player of the 1305 solar years, as Zarrin Panjeh and confuses him with Nasrollah Zarrin Panjeh, who is about thirty years apart from the previous player. And this mistake has also been copied verbatim, without mentioning the source, from the writing of another young person by Mr. Fereydoun Moshiri.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;', '2024-02-14 22:09:47', 0, '2024-12-19 19:08:53', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1549, 'posts', 92, 'en', NULL, 'Mohammad Ali Amir-Jahed, prominent poet and tasnif composer', NULL, 'Mohammad-Ali Amir Jahed (b. 1895) was a prominent Iranian poet and songwriter known for his significant contribution to Persian “tasnif” (lyrical song). He completed his education in Tehran and worked in journalism and cultural administration. In 1926, he launched the “Pars Yearbook,” which continued for nearly three decades.\n\nAmir Jاهد is best known for his patriotic, social, and romantic songs, many of which were performed and recorded by the famous singer Qamar-ol-Molouk Vaziri, accompanied by musicians such as Morteza Neydavoud. His works played an important role in the development and popularization of Persian classical song.\n\nHe was also active in official music institutions, serving as secretary of a music association and head of the National Music Conservatory. He passed away in 1977, leaving behind a valuable legacy of poetry and musical compositions.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Mohammad Ali Amir-Jahed</span></strong> is a prominent Iranian poet and tasnif composer who had close collaboration with the famous female singer Qamar al-Moluk Vaziri and the distinguished tar player Morteza Ney-Davud. In this article, we examine the biography and works of Mohammad Ali Amir-Jahed.</p>\n\n<h2>Mohammad Ali Amir-Jahed</h2>\n\n<p>He is one of the poets and tasnif composers whose fame began around 1305 solar. He was born in 1274 solar, completed his education in Tehran, and for some time worked in the press. While migrating westward to join the emigrants, he was captured by the Russian Tsarist Cossacks in Qazvin but eventually escaped. In 1299 solar, he entered the service of the National Consultative Assembly, and in 1305 solar he published the Pars Yearbook, which continued for about 28 years. His tasnifs were patriotic, social, and romantic, and were published with their musical notation in his Divan (1333 solar, second edition 1349 solar).</p>\n\n<p>Most of these tasnifs were sung by Qamar al-Moluk Vaziri with the tar of Arslan Dargahi and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%b1%d8%aa%d8%b6%db%8c-%d9%86%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%88%d8%af/\">Morteza Ney-Davud</a> and were recorded on Polydor records at the same time (1307 solar). Amir-Jahed was the secretary of the Association for the Promotion and Elevation of Music and for a time was also the director of the evening National Music School. He passed away in 1356 solar.</p>\n\n<h2>Works of Mohammad Ali Amir-Jahed</h2>\n\n<p>The works of Mohammad Ali Amir-Jahed are as follows:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Divan of Amir-Jahed, first edition 1333 solar, second edition 1349 solar;</li>\n  <li>Pars Yearbook from 1305 solar for 28 years;</li>\n  <li>Tasnif of Dashti “Dar Molk-e Iran”;</li>\n  <li>Tasnif of Mahur “Keshvar-e Del”;</li>\n  <li>Tasnif of Shur “Jan-e Man”;</li>\n  <li>Tasnif of Segah “Aman az In Del”;</li>\n  <li>Tasnif of Mahur “Be Gardesh-e Farvardin” in memory of Darvish Khan;</li>\n  <li>Tasnif of Afshari “Dar Bahar-e Omid”;</li>\n  <li>Tasnif of Chahargah “Hezar Dastan be Chaman”;</li>\n  <li>Tasnif of Abu Ata “Aman ze Hejr-e Rokh-e Yar”;</li>\n  <li>Tasnif of Bayat-e Tork “Baharest va Hengam-e Gasht”;</li>\n  <li>Tasnif of Shur “Narges-e Mast”</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>which were sung by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%82%d9%85%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%85%d9%84%d9%88%da%a9-%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Qamar al-Moluk Vaziri</a> on records around 1357 solar.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>The song “Pariyush” in Bayat-e Esfahan</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>was also sung by Badi’ Zadeh on Iran Javan records (recorded and manufactured in Bombay) in 1327 solar.</p>\n\n<p>Some of these tasnifs were later performed by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b6%d8%a7-%d8%b4%d8%ac%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Mohammad Reza Shajarian</a> and Soroush Izadi.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding Mohammad Ali Amir-Jahed and the works of this prominent figure in Iranian music, please share them in the comments section of this article with us to improve the quality of this content.</p>', '2024-02-16 12:09:10', 0, '2024-12-19 19:09:10', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1550, 'posts', 93, 'en', NULL, 'Agha Ali Akbar Farahani, tar player during Naser al-Din Shah and the oldest known radif master', NULL, 'Agha Ali-Akbar Farahani is regarded as one of the earliest and most influential masters of the Persian tar and a foundational figure in the development of the Iranian classical music repertoire (radif). He was born around 1236 AH during the Qajar era.\n\nHe was known as a highly skilled and expressive musician whose playing deeply impressed both musicians and listeners. Many stories describe his improvisational talent and emotional depth in performance. His musical legacy was passed on through his sons and disciples, most notably Mirza Abdollah and Agha Hossein-Qoli, who played a crucial role in preserving and transmitting the Persian radif.\n\nHis style is associated with a strong connection between Persian poetry and musical phrasing, as well as a highly intuitive approach to improvisation. Although no recorded works of him exist, his influence survives entirely through oral tradition and the teachings of his musical descendants.\n\nHe likely passed away around 1274 AH, leaving behind a profound legacy that shaped the foundation of Persian classical music.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the biography of the oldest known radif master of Iranian music, <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Aqa Ali Akbar Farahani</span></strong>. Please continue with us...</p>\n\n<h2>Aqa Ali Akbar Farahani</h2>\n\n<p>The term “Farahani,” which follows the name Aqa Ali Akbar, most likely does not indicate his affiliation with Farahān (near Arak). Rather, it is because he was the special musician of Mirza Amir Kabir Farahani and had an artistic relationship with him.</p>\n\n<p>He was the son of Shah Vali and also had a poetic talent.</p>\n\n<p>Mirza Abdollah attributed these two couplets to him, which are played and sung in the avaz of Tusi (Dastgah-e Mahur):</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Agar az to joda basham nabasham — Be ghayri asha basham nabasham</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Man bi dast o pa dar zir-e tighat — Be fekr-e dast o pa basham nabasham</p>\n\n<p>By order of Naser al-Din Shah, a watercolor painting was created by Mirza Abolhasan Khan Ghaffari (Sani’ al-Molk) in 1273 AH depicting him and several of his students. His birth date can be estimated around 1236 AH. The French Count de Gobineau heard his instrument in Tehran and wrote about him: “The most famous tar player is Ali Akbar. Europeans who had no interest in Eastern music were moved when they heard his playing, because he plays the tar with great soul and sensitivity. However, according to some artists, he has a quick temper and one must insist greatly for him to play.” [Gobineau]</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b5%d8%a7%d8%af%d9%82-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%86-%d8%b3%d9%8f%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%85%d9%84%da%a9/\">Mohammad Sadeq Khan Sorour al-Molk</a>, the famous santur player of the Naser al-Din Shah era who had heard Aqa Ali Akbar’s playing, recounted: “When Aqa Ali Akbar struck the strings with his plectrum, I would find myself in another world and forget my own playing.”</p>\n\n<p>Aqa Ali Akbar would place his tar beside his bed at night and sleep. Sometimes he would wake up in the middle of the night and play a new gusheh. One night, someone told him in a dream: “I will teach you the reng of Osul, which is one of the old pieces and has been completely forgotten,” and did so. Aqa Ali Akbar woke up in the middle of the night, took his tar from beside the bed, and played it. The next day he taught the reng to his nephew Aqa Gholam Hossein, who in turn taught it to his cousins Aqa Hossein-Qoli and Mirza Abdollah.</p>\n\n<p>Sa’di says in one of his ghazals:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Be dusti keh ze dast-e to zarbat-e shamshir — Chonan movafeq-e tab’ oftadam keh zarbat-e osul</p>\n\n<p>Moa’yyer al-Mamalek remembered this story from Aqa Hossein-Qoli and had heard the mentioned reng. Mehdi Qoli Hedayat narrated the same story differently and wrote that, according to Mirza Abdollah, the reng that Aqa Ali Akbar learned in his dream is the inverted part of Shahr Ashub of Chahargah, which is produced without a plectrum, only through finger movements (fingering). [Hedayat, 1317 solar]</p>\n\n<p>Aqa Ali Akbar, who had a dervish-like character, did not live a long life. It is said that before his death he had an argument with one of his neighbors about the sound of the instrument. One night he took his tar, named “Qalandar,” to the rooftop of the house and engaged in supplication and intimate conversation with God through his instrument. He fell asleep there, and in the morning they found him dead. [Khaleqi, 1333 solar]</p>\n\n<p>The portrait painting of him playing the tar, as mentioned, is the work of Mirza Abolhasan Khan Sani’ al-Molk, which shows the teaching session of Aqa Ali Akbar.</p>\n\n<p>Naser al-Din Shah sent Mirza Abolhasan Khan to Europe to perfect his painting skills. He studied this art in Paris and Rome, visited museums, and upon his return drew portraits of dignitaries for the illustrated newspapers of the Naser al-Din Shah period (such as Sharaf and Sharafat).</p>\n\n<p>The other individuals depicted in this painting are: Mirza Abdollah Khan Ala’ al-Molk, Mas’ud Mirza, Hassan Khan, Mirza Heydar Ali Sarhang, Ali Akbar Bazigar, Soltan Khanom, and Koukab Khanom, all of whom were students of Aqa Ali Akbar.</p>\n\n<p>According to the account of Khan Malek Sasani, Soltan Khanom was the dancer Jahān Khanom, whom Mahd-e Olya, the mother of Naser al-Din Shah, had entrusted to Aqa Ali Akbar to teach her the instrument. Naser al-Din Shah also had an eye for her. The tar in Aqa Ali Akbar’s hand, named “Qalandar,” as seen in Sani’ al-Molk’s painting, had twenty-four frets. The mentioned painting was for some time with Mirza Abdollah, son of Aqa Ali Akbar, then passed to his son Ahmad Ebadi, and finally was seen with Ali Akbar Shahnazi, the grandson of Aqa Ali Akbar.</p>\n\n<p>Aqa Ali Akbar had three sons: Mirza Hassan (a tar and setar player who died young), Mirza Abdollah, and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86%d9%82%d9%84%db%8c/\">Aqa Hossein-Qoli</a> (the famous tar players). His death can be estimated around 1274 AH.</p>\n\n<p>A tasnif in the gusheh of Iraq (Mahur) with lyrics by Sa’di (“Be jahan khorram az anam keh jahan khorram az ust”) is attributed to Aqa Ali Akbar. It was notated by Ebrahim Mansouri according to Haji Khan and was attributed to Aqa Ali Akbar by Abdollah Davami. This tasnif was played by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87/\">Mirza Abdollah</a>, son of Aqa Ali Akbar.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding Aqa Ali Akbar Farahani and the biography of this prominent radif master, please share them with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">us</a> to improve the quality of this content.</p>', '2024-02-16 22:21:11', 0, '2024-12-19 19:09:26', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1551, 'posts', 94, 'en', NULL, 'Agha Gholam Hossein, student and nephew of Agha Ali Akbar Khan', NULL, 'Agha Gholam Hossein was a prominent tar performer of the Qajar-era court and the nephew of Agha Ali Akbar Farahani. He was known for his fast and intricate right-hand technique and played a key role in preserving and transmitting early Persian classical repertoire. He taught several important musicians, including Mirza Abdullah and Agha Hossein Qoli, and later trained other students from outside the family. Despite initial reluctance, he accepted responsibility for teaching his cousins after his uncle’s death, helping to sustain and pass on the musical heritage of the Farahani family.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Aqa Gholam Hossein</h2>\n\n<p>Aqa Gholam Hossein, son of Mohammad Reza, was the nephew of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d8%a7%da%a9%d8%a8%d8%b1-%d9%81%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Aqa Ali Akbar</a>. He was a student of his uncle and one of the musicians of Naser al-Din Shah’s court. His fast and rapid tremolos (riz) were famous. In addition to his cousins <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87/\">Mirza Abdollah</a> and Aqa Hossein-Qoli, he trained other students such as Ne’matollah Khan Atabaki Kermani (in the service of Firooz Mirza Nosrat al-Dowleh, governor of Kerman) and Yusef Khan Safa’i (related to Zahir al-Dowleh) and Mohammad Ali Khan Mostowfi.</p>\n\n<p>Because of Aqa Ali Akbar’s short life, his young children Mirza Abdollah and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86%d9%82%d9%84%db%8c/\">Aqa Hossein-Qoli</a> could not benefit from their father’s teachings. Initially, Aqa Gholam Hossein was not inclined to teach them, but due to the insistence of their mother — who after Aqa Ali Akbar’s death married Aqa Gholam Hossein — he devoted himself to teaching the two boys and preserved the old melodies. Aqa Gholam Hossein’s tar had twenty-two frets.</p>', '2024-02-17 11:42:15', 0, '2024-12-19 19:09:37', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1552, 'posts', 95, 'en', NULL, 'Mirza Abdollah, owner of the most common version of the radif of Iranian music', NULL, 'Mirza Abdollah was one of the most influential figures in Iranian traditional music and the primary codifier of the seven-dastgah system. He learned music from his elder brother Mirza Hasan and later from his cousin Agha Gholam Hossein, eventually passing his knowledge to many prominent students such as Abolhasan Saba and others. Known for his generosity, spiritual outlook, and teaching style, he regarded Persian music as a deeply معنوی (spiritual) art requiring entry into the “world of meaning.”\n\nHe was primarily known for his mastery of the setar, and his playing style emphasized structured melodic phrasing, responsiveness to vocal lines, and the use of rhythmic pieces for contrast. His radif became the foundation for later documentation and transmission of Persian classical music, securing his legacy as a central figure in Iranian musical heritage. He passed away in 1918 (1297 SH) in Tehran.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Mirza Abdollah</h2>\n\n<p>Mirza Abdollah, son of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d8%a7%da%a9%d8%a8%d8%b1-%d9%81%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Aqa Ali Akbar Farahani</a>, first learned music from his elder brother Mirza Hassan and later from his cousin <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%ba%d9%84%d8%a7%d9%85-%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86/\">Aqa Gholam Hossein</a>. He also taught his younger brother Aqa Hossein-Qoli. Mirza Abdollah was born around 1260 AH. He taught the radif of the seven dastgahs of Iranian music to his students and was generous and open-handed in teaching.</p>\n\n<p>Abolhasan Saba, Haji Aqa Mohammad Irani Mojarrad (in the radif section), Seyed Hossein Khalifeh, Dr. Mehdi Solhi (Montazem al-Hokama) — from whom Mehdi Qoli Hedayat benefited and notated his radif — Esma’il Qahremani, the rifleman of Naser al-Din Shah (whose student was Nour Ali Boroumand), Seyed Ali Mohammad Khan Mostowfi and his sons Seyed Baqer Dabiri (Bayan al-Saltaneh) and Seyed Mehdi Dabiri were among Mirza Abdollah’s students. Among Mirza Abdollah’s children, Ahmad Ebadi, who had studied with his sister, continued the musical tradition and became one of the famous setar players of the contemporary period.</p>\n\n<p>It appears that Mirza Abdollah also had a dervish-like disposition. Dr. Daryush Safvat writes: “Mr. Davami would tell an interesting story. He quoted from Mirza Abdollah (the greatest setar player in Iran, who was also a master of the tar and was considered like a book of Iranian music) that Iranian music is a spiritual music, and unless a person enters the spiritual realm, he cannot learn anything from it. Davami even said that Mirza Abdollah had recommended that whoever wants to study with you, first send him to enter the spiritual realm and then learn Iranian music.” [Safvat, 1354 solar]</p>\n\n<p>“The spiritual realm” and “people of the spiritual realm” are Sufi terms that refer to those who pay attention to the inner dimension and contrast it with “people of form and appearance.” Molavi says:</p>\n\n<p>Ankeh por maghz ast chon meshk ast pak — Van keh pusideh st nabud gheir khak</p>\n\n<p>Ankeh ba ma’ni st khod payda shavad — Van keh pusideh st an rasva shavad</p>\n\n<p>Ru be ma’ni kush ey surat parast — Zanke ma’ni bar tan-e surat par ast</p>\n\n<p>Hamneshin-e ahl-e ma’ni bash ta — Ham ‘ata yabi va ham bashi fana</p>\n\n<p>Mirza Abdollah arranged a music class in Tehran, and enthusiasts would come to his presence and benefit from him. However, establishing a music class also brought him some risks. Baha’ al-Din Bamshad writes about this: “Some time later, together with his brother Aqa Hossein-Qoli, he arranged a teaching class in the Imamzadeh Yahya neighborhood, where young people and modernists gathered around him. Before long, due to incitements, opponents raided his house, broke the teaching equipment, and closed down his teaching setup.</p>\n\n<p>Some time later he moved his class to Ein al-Dowleh Street (Iran) and decided to continue teaching more diligently despite the opponents. Although he was repeatedly mocked and attacked by ruffians in the streets and bazaar, nevertheless, he did not stop teaching until his last breath. Even on his sickbed, he taught pieces as a memento to his prominent students with the setar.</p>\n\n<p>He would give free lessons to poor students and also provide them with financial help. At that time, it was customary for students to place a two-rial coin under the master’s carpet at the end of each lesson. It is said that sometimes at the end of the lesson sessions, he would distribute these amounts among the financially needy students under the name of a loan, saying: ‘What should have reached me has already reached me yesterday, and now I have no need for these amounts.’” [Bamshad, 1337 solar]</p>\n\n<p>Baha’ al-Din Bamshad was one of the first graduates of the State Music School (under the presidency of Ali-Naqi Vaziri) in 1312 solar. Later, from 1331 to 1335 solar, he served as president of the Higher School of Music.</p>\n\n<p>According to various accounts and narratives, Mirza Abdollah was a noble, generous, and kind man who devoted himself mostly to his work and teaching and was somewhat inclined toward seclusion. As mentioned, his class was located in the Imamzadeh Yahya neighborhood in the eastern part of Tehran during Naser al-Din Shah’s reign, north of the Chaleh Meydan neighborhood and north of the Zargaran tekkiyeh, and west of the former Dowlat Gate. In comparison with Mirza Abdollah, his younger brother <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86%d9%82%d9%84%db%8c/\">Aqa Hossein-Qoli</a> participated more in musical gatherings.</p>\n\n<p>Ali-Naqi Vaziri notated the radif of the seven dastgahs of Mirza Abdollah directly from his playing during the year and a half he attended his presence. Of these seven dastgahs, the notation of Dastgah-e Chahargah was donated to the library of the National Music School, while the notations of the remaining dastgahs were lost during Vaziri’s trip to Europe around 1918 CE (1297 solar). These notations were later found, and Musa Ma’rufi used them in compiling the radif of Iranian music (published in 1342 solar). Mirza Abdollah passed away in 1297 solar in Tehran.</p>\n\n[caption id=\"attachment_4767\" align=\"aligncenter\" width=\"799\"]<img class=\"wp-image-4767 size-full\" src=\"https://sornaz.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/sornaz_mirza_abdollah_002.jpg\" alt=\"Mirza Abdollah trained many students to preserve the radif of Iranian music\" width=\"799\" height=\"1298\" /> Mirza Abdollah trained many students to preserve the radif of Iranian music[/caption]\n\n<h2>The Style of Mirza Abdollah</h2>\n\n<p>The creation of some metric pieces is attributed to him. Mirza Abdollah’s fame was mainly in playing the setar. Unlike the tanbur, which is played with four fingers of the right hand, the setar is played with the nail of the index finger of the right hand. As the name of this instrument suggests, it originally had three strings. It is said that Moshtaq Ali Shah added another string to it, and now it is played with four strings. This added string was known as the “zang” string or “Moshtaq” string and is the same white string tied between the yellow “Sol” string and the low yellow (fourth) string.</p>\n\n<p>Considering Mirza Abdollah’s playing as heard from the early records (Dr. Sasan Sepanta), in chamber pieces accompanied by avaz, after performing several gushehs of the dastgah, he would play a <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b6%d8%b1%d8%a8%db%8c-%d8%b3%d8%a7%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9/\">metric piece</a> for variety. In the recorded works, the avaz phrases are mostly answered by him in the old style, but sometimes he answers the avaz with a different melody, which adds variety to the instrument.</p>\n\n<p>His tremolos (riz) and single strokes are balanced, but compared to Aqa Hossein-Qoli, he used more single strokes. His musical phrases are orderly and measured, and when accompanying avaz, after the singer finishes a couplet, Mirza Abdollah himself would start a new gusheh and thus guide the singer. He was precise and orderly in maintaining the rhythm of the piece and usually ended his playing with a fast reng-like piece. His <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%db%8c%d9%86%da%af/\">rengs</a> are varied and share common features in phrasing with Aqa Hossein-Qoli.</p>\n\n<p>The recorded works from him that I (Dr. Sasan Sepanta) have heard are on the tar. Because the early recordings, as explained in the book “History of the Transformation of Music Recording in Iran,” were made without electricity and acoustically, probably due to the weak sound projection of the setar, Mirza Abdollah did not use this instrument for recording and chose the tar instead. As can be seen from the existing photographs from Naser al-Din Shah’s period, he also used the tar in ensemble performances.</p>\n\n<p>Although the recorded works from Mirza Abdollah belong to around the age of sixty-five, his plectrum is fluent and he performs the pieces comfortably. Of course, the speed and agility of executing accents and melodic ornaments are not as nimble as those of his younger brother Aqa Hossein-Qoli. The musical phrases in his tar playing are also of the vocal music type, and the vocal phrasing style — which itself is somewhat dependent on the syllabic structure of poetry — prevails over instrumental phrasing. These characteristics are not exclusive to Mirza Abdollah’s style and can be observed in the performances of most old players.</p>\n\n<p>Mirza Abdollah’s tar had twenty-two frets. In metric pieces, his left and right strokes in each measure are measured and controlled. As an example of the tuning range of the tar, in the piece Bayat-e Shiraz in the mode of Esfahan “Sol,” Mirza Abdollah played the tar accompanying the avaz of Mirza Abbas, and the vocal range of the male singer while singing is in the lower part of the “alto” range!</p>\n\n<h2>Works of Mirza Abdollah</h2>\n\n<h3>Published Works</h3>\n\n<p>A short excerpt from avaz of Chahargah [Vaziri, 1301: 116]</p>\n\n<p>Names of the gushehs of the seven dastgahs attributed to Mirza Abdollah [Khaleqi, 1333: 491]</p>\n\n<p>Names of the gushehs of the seven dastgahs according to Mehdi Solhi (Montazem al-Hokama) [Hedayat, 1317, Vol. 3: 183]</p>\n\n<h3>Recorded Works of Mirza Abdollah’s Tar</h3>\n\n<p>E.M.I 17.5 cm records</p>\n\n<p>19285 — Mashq-e Rumi, Tork</p>\n\n<p>3-12670 — Avaz of Nowruz Arab with (avaz of) Qorban Khan Shahi</p>\n\n<p>25 cm records</p>\n\n<p>4-12032 — Daramad of Mahur, Abolchap with reng, avaz of Qorban Khan, tar by Mirza Abdollah</p>\n\n<p>4-12033 — Shekasteh-ye Mahur, tasnif “Jan az To Ast Hala Bestan,” tar by Mirza Abdollah</p>\n\n<p>4-12034 — Bayat-e Shiraz, Shurangiz, tasnif “Khud Bedeh Ensaf,” avaz of Mirza Abbas, tar by Mirza Abdollah</p>\n\n<p>4-12035 — Tasnif “Seh Panj Ruz Ast Keh Yar-e Man Nayamad,” avaz of Mirza Abbas, tar by Mirza Abdollah</p>\n\n<p>30 cm records</p>\n\n<p>012091 — Shur-e Ghazali, tasnif “Eshq-e Panjeh,” avaz of Qorban Khan</p>\n\n<p>012092 — Homayun, tasnif “Ma Sheikh o Zahed,” avaz of Qorban Khan</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding Mirza Abdollah and the biography of the person who provided the most widely used version of the current radif of Iranian music, please share them with us to improve the quality of this content.</p>', '2024-02-17 11:56:56', 0, '2024-12-19 19:09:52', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1553, 'posts', 96, 'en', NULL, 'Agha Hossein-Qoli, the most prominent tar player of the Qajar era and one of the reliable narrators of the radif of Iranian music', NULL, 'Agha Hossein Qoli was one of the most influential tar players of the Qajar period and a key figure in the development of the Persian classical music tradition. He was the son of Aghā Ali-Akbar Farahani and the brother of Mirza Abdollah. After early training within his family, he became a prominent teacher and established a structured school of tar performance. His style is characterized by precise rhythm, fast and clean picking techniques, and a strong connection to vocal phrasing in Persian music. His recordings and influential students, including Ali-Naqi Vaziri and Mortaza Ney-Davood, demonstrate his lasting impact on later generations of Iranian music.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Aqa Hossein-Qoli</h2>\n\n<p>Aqa Hossein-Qoli was born around 1270 AH. He was a child when his father <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d8%a7%da%a9%d8%a8%d8%b1-%d9%81%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%87%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Aqa Ali Akbar Khan Farahani</a> passed away. He learned to play the tar first from his elder brother Mirza Abdollah and later from his cousin <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%ba%d9%84%d8%a7%d9%85-%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86/\">Aqa Gholam Hossein</a>. He was a hardworking man with sufficient skill in his field. Initially, he opened a music class together with his elder brother Mirza Abdollah, and later established his own separate music class.</p>\n\n<p>His house was located on Ein al-Dowleh Street, in the Nasir Hozur alley. Aqa Hossein-Qoli was very strict with his students during teaching and considered regular daily practice essential for both performer and student. His class was held three days a week. Aqa Hossein-Qoli and Mirza Abdollah were among the special musicians favored and encouraged during Naser al-Din Shah’s reign, but in later periods, because Mozaffar al-Din Shah lacked good taste in music, they gradually withdrew into seclusion.</p>\n\n<p>Aref, the famous freedom-loving poet and songwriter, held great respect for Aqa Hossein-Qoli and his art. Regarding his passing, Aref said: “The greatest tar master, whom nature will need centuries to produce another plectrum of such power, has departed.” [<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%81-%d9%82%d8%b2%d9%88%db%8c%d9%86%db%8c/\">Aref Qazvini</a>, 1337 solar]</p>\n\n<p>Aqa Hossein-Qoli showed excellent taste in arranging the radif of Iranian music. In the final years of the Qajar period, he had close relations with prominent families in Tehran. In his memoirs, Doust Ali Khan Moa’yyer al-Mamalek writes about Monday, the third of Ramadan 1329 AH: “Several of his friends wanted to hold an entertainment gathering in one of the gardens of Shemiran and sent for Aqa Hossein-Qoli, and he came. The weather was half-rainy. The skin of Mirza’s tar had absorbed moisture. Nevertheless, he played the plectrum in such a way that one was left astonished. At the end of the gathering, due to an untimely and irrelevant remark by Aqa Hossein-Qoli, the attendees laughed so hard that they continued laughing for half an hour.” [Moa’yyer al-Mamalek, 1361 solar]</p>\n\n<p>Aqa Hossein-Qoli had a garden in Ja’farabad, Shemiran, where he would go on holidays, especially in summer. According to what Doust Ali Khan Moa’yyer al-Mamalek told Dr. Sasan Sepanta, Aqa Hossein-Qoli was a serious, hardworking, and determined man with a sense of order and discipline. After Naser al-Din Shah was assassinated and untalented musicians gained access to the Qajar court, he spent most of his time with his old friends. Sometimes on Monday nights, friendly gatherings were arranged at the house of Doust Ali Khan’s father (Doust Mohammad Khan).</p>\n\n<p>In summer, in Mehrabad, which was the summer residence of the Moa’yyer family, they would benefit from his artistry and that of several other masters of the Naser al-Din Shah period. Works were recorded on the phonograph cylinders (sound recording) of solo tar and sometimes tar with avaz by Aqa Hossein-Qoli by Doust Mohammad Khan Moa’yyer al-Mamalek. These are among the early works of this artist and were heard before his gramophone records, which I will mention in the section on the artist’s works.</p>\n\n<p>As it appears, in the later years of his life, like most artists, Aqa Hossein-Qoli lived in hardship. Seyed Hassan Taqizadeh writes:</p>\n\n<p>“Caruso, the Italian singer, passed away recently and left behind a great fortune (thirty million Italian lire). In America, the former Secretary of State, Mr. Bryan, improved his livelihood by giving scientific lectures, and the former president, Roosevelt, earned his living by writing articles. But in Iran, the life of Aqa Hossein-Qoli, the famous tar player, ended in poverty. Yet an ignorant minister and prime minister who prided himself that when he came before the National Consultative Assembly, the representatives of the nation could find no fault with him except comparing him in intelligence and understanding to one of the less intelligent animals, had become the owner of several crores of tomans.” [Taqizadeh, 1340 AH]</p>\n\n<p>As <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a> has mentioned, Aqa Hossein-Qoli once traveled with Baqer Khan Rameshgar, Asadollah Khan (tar and santur player), Seyed Ahmad Khan (singer), and Mohammad Baqer (percussionist) to Paris to record gramophone records, and some of his works were recorded there. They also gave a concert in Paris, but because Eastern singing did not appeal to foreign ears, they limited themselves to instrumental performance. On the way back, after a one-month stay in Istanbul, they arranged a concert at the request of Arfa’ al-Dowleh, the Iranian minister plenipotentiary there, and half of the proceeds were donated to the Iranian school in Istanbul. [Khaleqi, 1333 solar]</p>\n\n<p>Among the famous students of Aqa Hossein-Qoli, the following can be mentioned: Arfa’ al-Molk, Gholam Hossein Darvish, Mirza Gholam Reza Shirazi, Baser al-Dowleh, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d9%86%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Ali-Naqi Vaziri</a> (who notated part of his radif in his presence), Ali Mohammad Fakham Behzadi, Mohsen Mirza Zelli, and Morteza Ney-Davud.</p>\n\n<p>Among Aqa Hossein-Qoli’s children, Ali Akbar Shahnazi (deceased 27 Esfand 1363 solar) and his younger brother Abdolhossein Shahnazi (deceased 1327 solar) gained fame in tar playing and left behind notable works. Aqa Hossein-Qoli passed away around 1334 AH in Tehran and was buried in the Chahardah Ma’sum cemetery (on the right side of the Tehran to Shahr-e Rey road).</p>\n\n<h2>The Style of Aqa Hossein-Qoli</h2>\n\n<p>Aqa Hossein-Qoli’s playing style was a continuation of the traditional method of the old masters. His tar had twenty-two frets. His left and right strokes were orderly, measured, agile, and resonant, and his tremolos (riz) were clean and fast. From the early works of his playing that I (Dr. Sasan Sepanta) extracted and listened to from phonograph cylinders (sound recordings), as well as from his early gramophone records, it appears that in metric pieces he exactly imitates the short and long syllables of the singer’s poetry with the tar. Sometimes he answers the avaz in a lower octave (lower on the neck of the tar), which shows some innovation compared to the old masters’ playing style.</p>\n\n<p>He is precise in maintaining the rhythm of the piece. Sometimes, instead of directly responding to the singer, he answers with a different melody, which can be considered a kind of innovation compared to Mirza Abdollah’s style. His tremolos are faster than those of Mirza Abdollah. The surviving works of Aqa Hossein-Qoli’s playing on phonograph cylinders belong to his performance at the age of forty-five, while the recording of his gramophone records began at the age of fifty-six. During this decade of his life, there was no noticeable difference in the speed of his plectrum or his playing technique.</p>\n\n<p>Compared to Mirza Abdollah, he was more inclined to play the gushehs of the radif consecutively and one after another rather than inserting metric pieces in between the gushehs. For this reason, there was generally less rhythmic variety. His musical phrases, like the traditional school, are influenced by vocal music, and for this reason, detached intervals are rarely heard in his playing.</p>\n\n<p>The sound of his instrument is prominent and clear. He plays the musical phrases of the radif in a measured and orderly manner, and this measured quality is also evident in his strokes. In the works recorded on phonograph cylinders, because they were played in private and friendly gatherings, his performance is freer and more natural compared to the gramophone records, which were made about twelve years later. In most cases, he also sang two-dang avaz along with his own tar.</p>\n\n<p>Aqa Hossein-Qoli’s musical phrases are in harmony with the melodic and syllabic patterns of the poetry that was evoked in his mind. In transferring these two aspects — the pronounced poetry and the tar — he skillfully used fingering techniques. He even tried to deliver the pronounced “tone” and the short and long syllabic patterns of speech exactly as they are uttered, through the tar.</p>\n\n<p>For example, before playing a piece, he would introduce himself with his own voice in the mode of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%a7%d8%b5%d9%81%d9%87%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Bayat-e Esfahan</a> by singing: “Avaz-e Bayat ast, Bayat-e Esfahan.” Or before starting Dastgah-e Nava with the tar, he would sing in a two-dang voice in the daramad of Nava: “Hossein-Qoli mikhanad va mizanad avaz-e Nava ra,” and with left and right strokes and fast tremolos and appropriate fingering, he would match the melody of Esfahan and Nava to the short and long vowel patterns of the above phrases, trying to deliver the same pronounced phrase through the melodic imitation on the tar.</p>\n\n<p>From this, one can somewhat justify some accounts about the playing style of his father, Aqa Ali Akbar Farahani, according to which he would play certain prayers and supplications on his instrument as a form of worship, and the listener would perceive a kind of speech from the instrument.</p>\n\n<p>In his playing style, compared to today, a great deal of tremolo (riz) is heard. In his singing, vocal ornaments and techniques such as trills, decorative notes, and successive accents — which are common in traditional Iranian avaz — are hardly heard, except slightly and in appropriate places. His singing style differs from most traditional avaz singers, and perhaps this is because he was not primarily a singer.</p>\n\n<p>The tone of his voice in singing is somewhat nasal (ghunnei). Instead of focusing on excessive trills, he paid attention to the delivery of the poetry and, unlike most old singers who paid no attention to the clear pronunciation and delivery of the words of the poem, Aqa Hossein-Qoli paid attention to clear pronunciation and conveying the meaning.</p>\n\n<p>In other works that I have heard from him on phonograph cylinders, again in Bayat-e Esfahan, before starting the tar, he introduced himself with singing in this manner and melodically recited: “Hossein-Qoli tar mizanad va ham mikhanad Bayat-e Esfahan ra,” and immediately with the tar, using short and long syllabic patterns of the words and morphemes of the avaz in the mode of Bayat-e Esfahan, he played the melody and performed his own name with emphasis on the witness note of the Esfahan scale, both with singing and with melodic imitation of the singing on the instrument.</p>\n\n<p>Aqa Hossein-Qoli performed detached intervals at the beginning of a suitable gusheh (the interval from the witness note to the fourth degree of the scale) comfortably with both singing and the tar. He sometimes participated in ensemble playing with several musicians of his period; his chamber ensemble performances with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b5%d8%a7%d8%af%d9%82-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%86-%d8%b3%d9%8f%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%85%d9%84%da%a9/\">Mirza Mohammad Sadeq Khan</a> and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%86%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%a8-%d8%a7%d8%b3%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Nayeb Asadollah</a> are examples of this. They would play the metric piece — which was usually accompanied by avaz — in the common monophonic manner.</p>\n\n<h2>Works of Aqa Hossein-Qoli</h2>\n\n<h3>Published Works</h3>\n\n<p>A short excerpt from the daramad of Mahur [Vaziri, 1301: 96]</p>\n\n<p>A short excerpt from avaz of Shur [Vaziri, 1301: 121]</p>\n\n<p>Khwarezmi [Third Violin Book of the National Music School, 1332: 20]. Mehdi Qoli Hedayat wrote the name of this piece as Khwarezmshahi, which was performed in the framework of Shur following Delkash.</p>\n\n<p>Khwarezmi from Dastgah-e Mahur [Ma’rufi, 1342: 16]</p>\n\n<p>Introduction to Chahargah [Twenty-five Metric Pieces, 19:1327]</p>\n\n<h3>Recorded Works of Aqa Hossein-Qoli’s Tar</h3>\n\n<p>A: Phonograph cylinders from friendly gatherings, recorded privately as solo tar and tar with avaz by Aqa Hossein-Qoli (second decade of the fourteenth AH century, corresponding to the final years of the nineteenth century CE).</p>\n\n<p>B: Gramophone records of Aqa Hossein-Qoli’s tar playing</p>\n\n<p>E.M.I 25 cm records</p>\n\n<p>19382 — Pishdaramad of Mahur</p>\n\n<p>19383 — Avaz of Iraq and Mahur</p>\n\n<p>4-12063 — Azerbaijani, with tasnif and reng, with the voice of Mirza Seyed Ahmad Khan</p>\n\n<p>4-12056 — No. 1 — Dastgah-e Chahargah, daramad, with the voice of Seyed Ahmad Khan</p>\n\n<p>4-12057 — No. 2 — Zabol and Moyeh, daramad, with the voice of Seyed Ahmad Khan</p>\n\n<p>4-12058 — No. 3 — Hesār, daramad, with the voice of Seyed Ahmad Khan</p>\n\n<p>4-12059 — No. 4 — Mokhalef, daramad, with the voice of Seyed Ahmad Khan</p>\n\n<p>4-12060 — No. 5 — Maghlub, daramad, with the voice of Seyed Ahmad Khan</p>\n\n<p>4-12061 — No. 6 — Tasnif “Shah Raft Farangestan” (on the occasion of Mozaffar al-Din Shah’s trip to Europe), with the voice of Seyed Ahmad Khan</p>\n\n<p>4-12062 — No. 7 — Mokhalef with reng, with the voice of Seyed Ahmad Khan</p>\n\n<p>C-4-12052 — Avaz Azerbaijani with tasnif and reng, with the voice of Mirza Seyed Ahmad Khan</p>\n\n<p>30 cm records</p>\n\n<p>o 12094 — Avaz Nowruz Arab with tasnif and reng, with the voice of Mirza Seyed Ahmad Khan</p>\n\n<p>o 12095 — Mansuri with tasnif and reng, with the voice of Seyed Ahmad Khan</p>\n\n<p>o 12096 — Abolchap with tasnif and reng, with the voice of Mirza Seyed Ahmad Khan</p>\n\n<p>o 12097 — Avaz Shahanshahi (?) with tasnif and reng, with the voice of Seyed Ahmad Khan</p>\n\n<p>C-14-12594 — Afshari Tar Chalur, tar by Mirza Hossein-Qoli Khan, voice of Janab Damavandi, Mokhalef with Hossein’s kamancheh</p>\n\n<p>C-14-12683 — Bayat-e Esfahan, with the voice of Janab Damavandi</p>\n\n<p>C-14-12684 — Dashti, with the voice of Janab Damavandi</p>\n\n<p>C-14-12688 — Dashti, with the voice of Janab Damavandi</p>\n\n<p>18343 — Pishdaramad of Mahur</p>\n\n<p>18346 — Mokhalef</p>\n\n<p>18347 — Shur</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the biography of Aqa Hossein-Qoli, the most prominent tar player of the Qajar era, please share them in the comments section of the article with us to improve the quality of this content.</p>', '2024-02-17 12:40:45', 0, '2024-12-19 19:10:07', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1554, 'posts', 97, 'en', NULL, 'Ali-Naqi Vaziri, innovative composer and player of Iranian music and creator of the 24-division scale theory', NULL, 'This article presents the life and works of Ali Naqi Vaziri, one of the most influential modernizers of Iranian music. Born in Tehran, Vaziri was introduced early to both military discipline and music. He later studied music theory, harmony, and Western instruments, particularly the tar, and gradually developed a modern approach to Iranian music.\n\nAfter studying in Europe, he returned to Iran and played a key role in establishing formal music education, founding the Music School and later the Higher Conservatory of Music. His most important innovations include the introduction of scientific notation, the use of quarter-tone accidentals (sori and koron), the expansion of Iranian instruments for orchestral use, and the development of modern orchestration techniques.\n\nVaziri also composed numerous works, including songs, marches, and orchestral pieces, blending Iranian musical traditions with Western classical principles. As a performer, he was known for his technical precision, strong finger technique, and systematic approach to the tar.\n\nDespite political and cultural opposition that led to periods of isolation, his impact on the transformation of Iranian music education and performance remains profound and lasting.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the biography of <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Ali-Naqi Vaziri</span></strong>, the innovative and modernist performer and composer of Iranian music. We will also discuss his performing and composing style and present some of his innovations and views on Iranian music. Please continue with us ...</p>\n\n<h2>Ali-Naqi Vaziri</h2>\n\n<p>Ali-Naqi Vaziri was born in 1266 solar in Tehran. His father, Musa Khan Mir Panj, was a military officer, and his mother was among the first women to establish a girls’ school and had some knowledge of music. From his teenage years, Ali-Naqi learned military skills from his father in the Cossack barracks. At the age of fifteen, he took his first music lessons and French language classes with Dr. Hossein-Qoli Qezel Ayagh.</p>\n\n<p>Some time later, together with Mohammad Hejazi — who was a student at Saint Louis School and also played the violin — they went to a priest named Père Joffroy and formed a small home orchestra. In this group, the priest played piano, Hejazi played violin, and Ali-Naqi Vaziri played mandolin. Ali-Naqi learned music theory and harmony from the priest and, on his recommendation, studied the book “Compendium,” a kind of music encyclopedia, and mastered its topics. During this period, Ali-Naqi Vaziri joined the Okhovat Association, where he became acquainted with Darvish Khan. For a year and a half, he notated the radif of the seven dastgahs from <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87/\">Mirza Abdollah</a> and practiced the tar diligently.</p>\n\n<p>During the period in question, the conflict between the constitutionalists and Mohammad Ali Shah reached its peak, and he bombarded the National Consultative Assembly. Ali-Naqi Khan was returning from a mission in Shiraz when he heard the news of the suppression of the constitutionalists in Isfahan. Distressed, he returned to Tehran and secretly cooperated with the constitutionalist associations until his connection was discovered during a mission in Karaj. He was forced to resign from military service. Ali-Naqi Vaziri rented a house for three tomans a month and spent three years practicing his instrument and music. Since he had no income, he lived an ascetic life by selling household items.</p>\n\n<p>In 1290 solar, after perfecting the notation system, he notated the radif of Mirza Abdollah and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86%d9%82%d9%84%db%8c/\">Aqa Hossein-Qoli</a> and preserved it for posterity. In 1292 solar, on the recommendation of one of his friends, Soleyman Khan Mikdeh, deputy minister of the interior, Ali-Naqi Vaziri went to Birjand to train the cavalry of Amir Showkat al-Molk. The Amir was satisfied with Vaziri’s work, and together they traveled to Russia. Ali-Naqi Vaziri was appointed commander of the Qa’enat cavalry regiment with the rank of colonel. From this time, the title “Colonel” was added to his name, and he served in Birjand for about two years.</p>\n\n<p>During World War I, when the British brought their troops from India into Iran and intervened, the commander of the British forces wanted to enter the Birjand barracks for inspection but was met with resistance from Ali-Naqi Vaziri. Eventually, the resistance was unsuccessful, and the situation reached a point where not only the Colonel but even Amir Showkat al-Molk himself could no longer remain in Birjand. As a result, the Colonel returned to Tehran and removed his military uniform for the last time.</p>\n\n<p>After the flames of World War I subsided, with the assistance and companionship of Mostafa Qoli Khan Bayat (Samsam al-Molk), Ali-Naqi Vaziri traveled to Europe via Russia and Turkey. He spent three years at the Higher School of Music in Paris studying piano, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d9%88%d9%86%db%8c/\">harmony</a>, and singing. Afterward, he went to Berlin with the intention of establishing a music school in Iran and attended various classes at the “Hochschule für Musik.” As “Ella Zonis,” former professor of humanities at the University of Chicago, wrote, Ali-Naqi Vaziri studied counterpoint and composition at the Hochschule in Berlin. Ali-Naqi Vaziri returned from Germany in 1352 solar, and it was in the same year that he founded the Higher School of Music.</p>\n\n<p>Ali-Naqi Vaziri would not allow anyone to request him to perform in a gathering. For example, Arbab Keykhosrow, the Zoroastrian representative in the National Consultative Assembly, made such a request, and Ali-Naqi Vaziri left the session. Another case was the request of the Minister of Court at the time, Teymurtash, for him to perform at Reza Shah’s coronation ceremony (4 Ordibehesht 1305 solar). The Colonel declined, citing pain in his hand. Once, the orchestra of the Higher School of Music was invited to perform at a party in Sa’dabad Palace, but Vaziri refused, which led to his dismissal.</p>\n\n<h3>The State Music School</h3>\n\n<p>In 1293 solar, Brigadier General Gholam Reza Salar Mo’azzaz (Minbashian) proposed to the Ministry of Education the establishment of a music class at Dar al-Fonun. Two years later, this class became the “Music School” and was separated from Dar al-Fonun. The course lasted six years, and its curriculum was mostly suitable for military music. Because employment regulations prohibited receiving two salaries from the government, and Salar Mo’azzaz held both the position of the mentioned school and the head of military music, the presidency of the State Music School was assigned to Ali-Naqi Vaziri. Vaziri took over the school in the summer of 1307 solar and reorganized its program for a five-year secondary school. The school continued its work under the directorship of Esma’il Zarrin Far.</p>\n\n<p>Esma’il Zarrin Far first learned kamancheh from his maternal uncle Alireza Changi and later continued with Esma’il Zadeh. At the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b3%d9%87-%d8%b9%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">Higher School of Music</a>, he learned violin with Ali-Naqi Vaziri. Through his acquaintance with Mir Mehdi Varzandeh, he took up sports. After his appointment as director of the State Music School in 1307 solar, he began teaching there and studied harmony himself. Later, he opened violin classes in Mashhad and Tehran and trained students such as Mahmoud Tajbakhsh, Mohammad Mirnaqibi, and Abbas Shapuri. Zarrin Far passed away in 1373 solar at his residence in Karaj.</p>\n\n<p>The first graduates of the school — Ali Mohammad Khadem Misagh, Ahmad Forutan Rad, and Baha’ al-Din Bamshad — continued their musical work. In 1308 solar, the school orchestra was formed under the direction of Ali-Naqi Vaziri himself, and he also took charge of teaching orchestration, composition, and piano.</p>\n\n<p>The students initially used European methods and, in parallel, played the Iranian music instructions written by Ali-Naqi Vaziri. The school orchestra gradually became organized and performed some European pieces and compositions by Vaziri. Some time later, on the proposal of Mokhber al-Saltaneh Hedayat, the prime minister at the time, the name of the school was changed to “Musiqar School,” and a sound laboratory, books, and collections of sheet music were ordered from Europe. Later, its name was changed to “Higher School of Music,” and an advanced course was added.</p>\n\n<p>At the end-of-year celebration in 1312 solar, the school orchestra performed the piece “Zaban-e Eshgh” by Ali-Naqi Vaziri, with lyrics by Gol-Golab and sung by Abdolali Vaziri. At this ceremony, on the proposal of the Minister of Culture, the first-class scientific medal was awarded to Vaziri.</p>\n\n<p>During his tenure as president, Ali-Naqi Vaziri did not allow the school orchestra to perform anywhere. This issue upset some government officials, and they were looking for an opportunity to strike. In the winter of 1313 solar, Crown Prince Gustaf Adolf of Sweden and his two sons were guests of the Iranian government. The schemers seized the opportunity and told influential figures that it would be better for Vaziri to appear with the school orchestra at one of the royal palaces and play in the adjacent room to the dining hall while the guests were eating dinner.</p>\n\n<p>The matter was conveyed to Vaziri, and he said that this type of performance would be an insult to the orchestra and the students of the school. If the guests wished to hear music, the orchestra would perform after dinner in the concert hall. The schemers and ill-wishers conveyed Vaziri’s response differently to the authorities and gave it the appearance of disobedience.</p>\n\n<p>After hearing Ali-Naqi Vaziri’s response, Reza Shah ordered: “Remove him and put a young, obedient man in his place!” With a one-line notice, Vaziri’s service in the Ministry of Culture was terminated, and he became a homebody. The notice read: “Mr. Ali-Naqi Vaziri, from this date you will have no position in the Ministry of Culture.” The acting Minister of Education in Mohammad Ali Foroughi’s cabinet was Ali Asghar Hekmat, who had awarded the first-class scientific medal to Ali-Naqi Vaziri one year earlier.</p>\n\n<p>Two years later, in 1315 solar, Ali-Naqi Vaziri was selected as a professor at the University of Tehran and began teaching there. The courses he mostly taught at the university were: history of art, integration of poetry and music, history of music, principles of writing, history of visual arts, and aesthetics. In 1319 solar, he became president of the Fine Arts Association of the university. After the events of Shahrivar 1320 solar, Reza Shah’s abdication, and his departure from Iran, Vaziri was reappointed, while retaining his university duties, as president of the Higher School of Music and director of the country’s music administration.</p>\n\n<p>In Mehr 1325 solar, due to the influence of Parviz Mahmoud and the support of the Tudeh Party of Iran for him, Dr. Keshavarz, the Minister of Culture and a member of the same party, issued the dismissal order for Vaziri from the presidency of the Higher School of Music and the country’s music administration, and Parviz Mahmoud was appointed in his place. From that date, Ali-Naqi Vaziri practically became disillusioned with musical activities, withdrew, and secluded himself in his house, which was far from the city of Tehran. He would come to the University of Tehran one day a week to teach, and he spent the rest of his time studying. His social interactions were limited to a few old friends.</p>\n\n<p>Due to the above conditions and years of experience in group work, Ali-Naqi Vaziri gradually lost hope and was not very optimistic about the interest of responsible government officials in artistic matters. He would say: “The work of knowledge and education in Iran is the plaything of the ignorant, and charlatans have the field.” In a letter to his friend Dr. Qasem Ghani, he writes:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">“My current life in many respects resembles that of ascetics. I go to the city once a week, and that is only for my class. I see no one else anymore … I have withdrawn from the affairs of the city, music, and all those activities that are called art but, in this environment, brought me nothing but wasted time, jealousy, and resentment.” [Ghani, 1367 solar]</p>\n\n<p>In another letter dated 27 Shahrivar 1327 solar to Dr. Ghani, who was in Washington, he writes:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">“You probably would not mind if I also tell you something about my own life. Since I left the city six years ago, my health has become much better. I am constantly moving farther away from the noise of the city and its crowds. My salary [from the University of Tehran] is sufficient for living here, so there is no worry in that regard. My spiritual state is becoming clearer day by day, to the point that little by little I can say I am engaged in silence, fasting, dawn vigils, seclusion, and constant remembrance. There is no news of playing the instrument. I do not know why it has become like this.” [Ghani, 1367 solar]</p>\n\n<p>With these examples from Ali-Naqi Vaziri’s correspondence, the reason for his withdrawal from <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">music</a> and his seclusion becomes somewhat clear to the respected reader.</p>\n\n<p>In later years, when the National Music Association was established and the National Music School was founded by Khaleqi, Ali-Naqi Vaziri, at his invitation, participated in several concerts of the association. He even played solo tar and attended the graduation ceremony of the first graduates of the National Music School. When, during a visit to the National Music School, he learned of the young students’ enthusiasm for learning instruments, especially the setar, he gradually wrote exercises and etudes for the setar. He himself played them on reel tape and, under the name “Setar School,” recorded them regularly in 1333 and 1334 solar.</p>\n\n<p>In these tapes, Ali-Naqi Vaziri performed etudes for practicing various left- and right-hand strokes with different fingerings and provided the necessary explanations at the beginning of each etude.</p>\n\n<p>In the years around 1326 solar, when he had withdrawn, if he touched the instrument at all, it was mostly improvisation, and he rarely recalled his own compositions. In a letter dated 28 Dey 1326 solar to Dr. Ghani, he writes:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">“The reason I have not left any examples of poems matching my feelings is that I have a very bad memory, and I have never used it in my art either. That is why I always improvise. I do not even remember one in a hundred of my own compositions, and if I had your extraordinary memory, perhaps I could give some examples.” [Ghani, 1367 solar]</p>\n\n<p>Dr. Sasan Sepanta says:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">I remember that when I was studying under Master Ali-Naqi Vaziri on the introduction of Ruhollah Khaleqi and was studying Persian language and literature at the University of Tehran, I obtained permission from the master to also attend his classes on “Aesthetics in Art and Nature,” which he taught in the archaeology department of the University of Tehran.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">My attendance at those classes was from Mehr 1337 to the end of Khordad 1338 solar. When the lesson discussion reached music and he spoke about types of music, types of instruments, melody, harmony, rhythm, pieces such as menuet, and types of trio, quartet, sonata, and concerto, and then Iranian dastgahi music, pishdaramad, avaz, tasnif, and reng, etc.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">I informed him that since about thirty years had passed since his musical activities and the students were not as familiar as they should be with his recorded musical works — especially since those works were neither broadcast on mass media such as radio nor existed in an archive for use — if he would allow, I would select some of them that I had collected over the past few years according to his own opinion and play the tapes in class, and the master would provide the necessary explanations.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">He agreed to a few records, and it was arranged that the following week I would bring a reel tape player to the university and play the master’s works for the students. He selected several pieces such as Bandbaz, Dokhtarak-e Julideh, Dust, Ey Vatan, Del-e Zar, and Kenar-e Golzar, whose lyrics were written respectively by Dr. Hossein Gol-Golab, Abdolazim Qarib, and Mohit Tabatabai, and with utmost generosity left the explanations to me.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">The following week I prepared the pieces, and after about thirty years, for the first time, the performed works of the “Higher School of Music” by Master Ali-Naqi Vaziri resonated in his class at the University of Tehran. Dr. Simin Daneshvar, who was then an associate professor under Vaziri, was present in the session. Among the students of that period who had the most knowledge about Master Vaziri was Dr. Parviz Varjavand, who sincerely loved Iranian art and culture.</p>\n\n<p>Ali-Naqi Vaziri’s subsequent activities included forming a modern orchestra for radio, cooperating with the National Music Association, composing several new melodies, and writing instructions for the setar. He retired from the university in 1344 solar but continued teaching at the university’s invitation and was selected as a distinguished professor of the university in 1350 solar. Finally, Ali-Naqi Vaziri passed away on 18 Shahrivar 1358 solar in Tehran.</p>\n\n<h3>Works of Ali-Naqi Vaziri: Books and Articles</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Dastur-e Tar (Music Instruction). Berlin, 1301 solar.</li>\n  <li>Dar Alam-e Musiqi va San’at (In the World of Music and Industry). Four lectures, edited by Sa’id Nafisi, 1304 solar.</li>\n  <li>Dastur-e Violin, Vol. 1, lithograph print, Tehran, 1312 solar.</li>\n  <li>Musiqi-ye Nazari (Music Theory). 3 volumes, Tehran, 1313 solar.</li>\n  <li>Vol. 1: Rules of Notation.</li>\n  <li>Vol. 2: Vocalization in Iranian Music.</li>\n  <li>Vol. 3: Detailed Western Music Theory.</li>\n  <li>Dastur-e Violin, Vols. 1 and 2, Tehran, 1316 solar.</li>\n  <li>Dastur-e Jadid-e Tar. Lithograph print, Tehran, 1315 solar.</li>\n  <li>Sorud-e Madāres (School Anthems). Tehran, 1312 solar.</li>\n  <li>Pandha-ye Sa’di (Sa’di’s Advices). Tehran, 1316 solar.</li>\n  <li>Zibayi Shenasi (Aesthetics). Tehran, 1329 solar.</li>\n  <li>Zibayi Shenasi-ye Tahlili (Analytical Aesthetics). 1336 solar.</li>\n  <li>Tarikh-e Honarha-ye Mosavvar (History of Visual Arts). 2 volumes, Tehran, 1340 solar.</li>\n  <li>“Sanaye’-e Mostazrafeh” (Fine Arts). Armaghan, 4th period, No. 2-3, Khordad-Tir 1302 solar.</li>\n  <li>“San’at, Masnu’, Saane’” (Industry, Artifact, Artisan). Iranshahr, 2nd year, No. 3, 1 Hamal 1302 solar and Nos. 5-6, Azar-Esfand 1302 solar.</li>\n  <li>“Musiqi-ye Iran” (Iranian Music). Musiqi, 2nd period, Nos. 6-7, Tehran, Shahrivar-Mehr 1320 solar.</li>\n  <li>“Armoni dar Musiqi-ye Iran” (Harmony in Iranian Music). Musiqi, 3rd period, Nos. 8-9, Aban-Azar 1320 solar.</li>\n  <li>“Fosul-e Armoni” (Intervals of Harmony). Musiqi, 3rd period, Nos. 10-11, Dey-Bahman 1320 solar.</li>\n  <li>“Honar-e Namayesh” (The Art of Theater). Musiqi, 3rd year, Nos. 8-9, Aban and Azar 1320 solar and Nos. 10-11.</li>\n  <li>“Eslahat-e Adabi (Eslahat-e Arouzi)” (Literary Reforms – Prosodic Reforms). Mehr, 5th year.</li>\n  <li>“Eslahati dar Bab-e Qafieh” (Reforms Regarding Rhyme). Mehr, 6th year, No. 1, Khordad 1317 solar.</li>\n  <li>“Zowq” (Taste). Musiqi, 3rd period, No. 15, Aban 1336 solar.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Unpublished Works of Ali-Naqi Vaziri</h3>\n\n<p>Exercises and etudes for the setar (Setar School, 1333 solar), which Vaziri himself performed on the setar on tape. The pieces “Marsh-e Harkat” and “Bandbaz” were also recorded on tape in Mehr 1334 solar, some with tombak by Hossein Tehrani and some with Vaziri’s own voice.</p>\n\n<p>The books “Saz Shenasi” (Organology), “Harmony in Iranian Music,” “Advanced Violin Etudes,” “Advanced Tar Course,” and several other treatises.</p>\n\n<h3>Recorded Works of Ali-Naqi Vaziri on Disc</h3>\n\n<p>As Ali-Naqi Vaziri himself stated in his speech at the opening ceremony of the Higher School of Music (1312 solar), thirty-seven gramophone records (double-sided) were recorded of his works during the years the Higher School of Music was active. Among them, the following can be mentioned:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Vesal-e Dust (Chahargah),</li>\n  <li>Jur-e Falak (Bayat-e Esfahan),</li>\n  <li>Ey Zahedan (Shur) with the voice of Abdolali Vaziri,</li>\n  <li>Basteh-ye Dam (Dashti),</li>\n  <li>Dust (Dashti),</li>\n  <li>Asrar-e Eshgh (Mahur),</li>\n  <li>Sohbat-e Del (Bayat-e Tork), and</li>\n  <li>Marsh-e Harkat.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>These <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%aa%d8%b5%d9%86%db%8c%d9%81/\">tasnifs</a> have lyrics by Hossein Gol-Golab and were mostly performed with the voice of Rouhangiz and some with the choral singing of Ali-Naqi Vaziri himself.</p>\n\n<p>Marsh-e Varzeshkaran (Athletes’ March), Sorud-e Mehr-e Iran, Sorud-e Sobh, Gonjeshgak, Madar, Kudak-e Yatim, Tati Tati, Bahar (Afshari) with lyrics by Shahzadeh Afser, and two tasnifs Del-e Zar (Homayun) and Kenar-e Golzar (Nava) with lyrics by Mohit Tabatabai.</p>\n\n<p>Sorud-e Ey Vatan with lyrics by Abdolazim Qarib, Suz-e Man (Segah) with lyrics by Sa’di, and a piece from the operetta Golrokh titled Manqal va Afur with the voice of Rouhangiz and Vaziri himself and lyrics by Gol-Golab, Marsh-e Madāres with lyrics by Heydar Ali Kamali, Bandeh-ye Eshgh (Mahur) with lyrics by Hafez, and Hosn-e Ahd (Afshari), both with the voice of Rouhangiz and the tar of Hossein Sanjari, and Bayat-e Esfahan with lyrics by Hafez, the voice of Rouhangiz, and the tar of Ali-Naqi Vaziri.</p>\n\n<p>Dokhtarak-e Julideh (Chahargah), Bandbaz (Mahur), Jimnastik-e Musiqal (Dashti), Radif-e Ali-ye Dashti, Radif-e Ali-ye Bayat-e Esfahan, Badah-e Sarayi (Chahargah and Mahur), and Hazer Bash (Mahur), all of which are solo tar performances by Vaziri.</p>\n\n<p>Rang-e Chahargah, Rang-e Dashti, orchestra in the usual style, and several other <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%82%d8%b7%d8%b9%d9%87/\">pieces</a> performed with the orchestra.</p>\n\n<p>Tapes were also recorded in private sessions, such as tar and setar with the voice of Ali-Naqi Vaziri himself (etudes for the setar), Marsh-e Harkat, Bandbaz, and the piece Khodpasand in Mahur.</p>\n\n<h3>Other Works of Ali-Naqi Vaziri</h3>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Shekayat-e Ney (Esfahan),</li>\n  <li>Bolbol-e Mast (Dashti) with lyrics by Gol-Golab,</li>\n  <li>Kharidar-e To (Mahur) with lyrics by Sa’di,</li>\n  <li>Deltang (Homayun) with lyrics by Hafez,</li>\n  <li>Nimshab with lyrics by Hafez,</li>\n  <li>Moshtaq va Parishan (Abu Ata) with lyrics by Sa’di,</li>\n  <li>Asheq-e Saz (Segah) with lyrics by Gol-Golab,</li>\n  <li>Karavan (Dashti) with lyrics by Sa’di,</li>\n  <li>Gilak (based on a theme by Saba),</li>\n  <li>Gerili (Shur) with lyrics by Hafez,</li>\n  <li>Do Asheq (Shushtari) with lyrics by Hafez,</li>\n  <li>Zaban-e Eshgh (Segah) with lyrics by Gol-Golab,</li>\n  <li>Kar-e Khish (Abu Ata) with lyrics by Gol-Golab,</li>\n  <li>Na Omid (Segah),</li>\n  <li>Ghamgin with lyrics by Rudaki,</li>\n  <li>Morghak with lyrics by Parvin E’tesami,</li>\n  <li>Do Bolbol with lyrics by Kamali,</li>\n  <li>Saqi Nameh with lyrics by Sa’di,</li>\n  <li>Marsh-e Payam be Koroush with lyrics by Adib al-Mamalek Farahani,</li>\n  <li>Dozd-e Buseh,</li>\n  <li>Raqs-e Dokhtar-e Man,</li>\n  <li>Vals,</li>\n  <li>Do Namzad,</li>\n  <li>Chasht-e Man (Chahargah for tar and violin),</li>\n  <li>Nahar-e Man (Bayat-e Tork for two violins),</li>\n  <li>Sham-e Man (Homayun and Bayat-e Esfahan for two tars),</li>\n  <li>Dokhtar-e Nakam,</li>\n  <li>Jodayi,</li>\n  <li>Plays “Khanom-e Khab And” and “Tashak-e Par-e Qu,”</li>\n  <li>Symphony-ye Shum (on the occasion of his father’s death),</li>\n  <li>Symphony-ye Shaban va Musi (based on a poem by Molavi in the Masnavi), and</li>\n  <li>Symphony-ye Naft (composed around 1328 solar during the Iranian people’s struggle against foreign oppression), whose score was with his friend and student Ali-Naqi Vaziri, Soleyman Sepanlou.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Soleyman Sepanlou, a good friend and student of Vaziri who played the tar in his style, passed away on 25 Farvardin 1340 solar at the age of sixty-three. Sepanlou bequeathed that his house be dedicated to a music club or music school.</p>\n\n<p>From 1336 solar onward, some of Ali-Naqi Vaziri’s works, such as Kharidar-e To, Deltang, Karavan, Shekayat-e Ney, and Gusheneshin, were performed by the Golha Orchestra or Radio Orchestra No. 1 under the direction of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a> or Javad Ma’rufi in the Golha programs. The singers of these melodies were mostly Banan and sometimes Abdolali Vaziri.</p>\n\n<h2>The Performing Style of Ali-Naqi Vaziri</h2>\n\n<p>Ali-Naqi Vaziri’s main instrument was the tar, but he also played the setar, violin, piano, and some other instruments, and he was also knowledgeable in singing. His mature and deep voice, which was around the baritone range and followed proper principles, naturally did not appeal to some who were only accustomed to hearing high, loud six-dang singing with continuous trills. In his youth, he would practice the tar for hours on end in the storeroom, and his strokes had a clear sound, clean tonality, speed, and skill. His single strokes were strong and resonant, and his left-right alternations were regular.</p>\n\n<p>His long, bony fingers presented melodies smoothly on the tar, and at appropriate moments he used all the fingers of his left hand, whereas most players rarely even used the little finger, let alone the thumb. Ali-Naqi Vaziri’s playing was full of beautiful melodies, strong technique, speed, and skill. He made excellent use of all the tar’s strings, different positions, and suitable arpeggios.</p>\n\n<p>Ali-Naqi Vaziri’s technical innovations on the tar enabled later composers and performers to broaden their expressive horizons.</p>\n\n<h2>Innovations of Ali-Naqi Vaziri</h2>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Creation of the two signs “sori” and “koron” for the intervals known as quarter tones, which correspond numerically to 270 and, in abjad calculation, to the two words “Ali-Naqi”;</li>\n  <li>Creation of new instruments for the orchestra, such as the soprano tar tuned a fifth higher than the ordinary tar, the alto tar tuned a fifth lower than the ordinary tar, and the bass tar, and the formation of a tar quartet;</li>\n  <li>Collection of local melodies and their use in pieces composed for orchestra and solo instruments;</li>\n  <li>Adding six frets to the neck of the tar, making twenty-two frets in total instead of the traditional ones;</li>\n  <li>Establishment of the Higher School of Music;</li>\n  <li>Holding concerts in Tehran and other cities;</li>\n  <li>Creation of delicate sounds similar to the bow of the violin with appropriate soft, weak, and other dynamics, or gradual crescendo and diminuendo, and the use of various strokes on all tar strings and practice on the fifth string so that its sound does not mix with the bass string;</li>\n  <li>Jumping of the left hand in detached intervals and use of all strings of the instrument;</li>\n  <li>Use of chords and writing pieces and etudes for violin in the correct style.</li>\n  <li>In his book “Dastur-e Tar” printed in Berlin, Vaziri for the first time presented the twenty-four-part quarter-tone scale and wrote on the same page (p. 99) that he had no insistence on publishing it and that the matter was theoretical. This theory was later criticized by some.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>The Composing Style of Ali-Naqi Vaziri</h2>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Presentation of the view that Iranian music has been limited to the usual <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d9%be%db%8c%d8%b4-%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%a2%d9%85%d8%af/\">pishdaramad</a>, reng, and tasnifs within the monotonous confines of the radif;</li>\n  <li>Composition of innovative pieces (for example, beginning a melody not from the daramad of the avaz but from another fret, such as Iraq, and after development returning to the main fret, Mahur);</li>\n  <li>Avoidance of repetition in responding to the avaz and answering the singer with creative and innovative melodies;</li>\n  <li>Avoidance of the rhythmic monotony that was very common in traditional pieces;</li>\n  <li>Use of the wide possibilities of instruments in composed pieces, such as the use of “pizzicato” on the violin and the like;</li>\n  <li>Division of orchestral instruments and determination of their positions, such as designating first and second violins, etc.;</li>\n  <li>Composition of pieces that describe and evoke poetry (such as the melody “Deltang” in Homayun on this hemistich by Hafez “Dani keh chist dowlat-e didar-e yar didan,” in terms of maqam, change of rhythm, and evoking states such as the blowing of the breeze, etc.);</li>\n  <li>Writing introductions for naghmahs and composing cheerful pieces, operettas, musical tableaux, pantomimes, dramas, and anthems for children.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Master Ali-Naqi Vaziri was the only Iranian musician who, from the early years of teaching (the 1300s to 1313 solar and afterward), raised the issues of manner of expression (which he called “balaghat”) and interpretation and understanding in performance (which he called “tarjoman”) and provided the necessary explanations regarding character and style in playing, singing, or conducting an orchestra. For example, in the piece “Deltang,” when the musicians reached the melodic introduction to the poem “Gah chon nasim …,” Ali-Naqi Vaziri would explain that the orchestra should evoke the sound of the breeze with the same lightness and wing-like delicacy. In orchestral conducting, he also emphasized that the conductor should be familiar with the character of the composer and the mood of the piece.</p>\n\n<p>After Ali-Naqi Vaziri, the only master who emphasized evoking the moods of melodies was <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%84%d8%ad%d8%b3%d9%86-%d8%b5%d8%a8%d8%a7/\">Abolhasan Saba</a>, who repeatedly recommended, during the teaching of local pieces such as “Deylaman,” “Amiri,” and others, focusing attention on the beauty and attractions of nature and the necessity of mental evocation in musical performance.</p>\n\n<p>Regarding the harmonization of Iranian melodies, when Ali-Naqi Vaziri decided to write harmony for a piece in the mode of Segah, he encountered difficulties. Finally, after theoretical and practical study, he realized that the witness note of Segah should not be taken as the root of the chord, because the fifth of the chord would be incomplete. As a result, he wrote the Segah scale in such a way that its witness became the third degree of the scale. This was an innovation of Ali-Naqi Vaziri, and it was he who recognized that the Shur scale is a descending scale.</p>\n\n<p>In composing duets for various instruments, Vaziri used the counterpoint method. Because he believed that counterpoint is more suitable for Iranian music than harmony, and with the use of counterpoint, the main melody — which plays an important role in Iranian music — would not be sacrificed to harmony, and its delicacy and attractiveness would be preserved. In cases where he used harmony, he believed that the harmony of Iranian music should be simple and not complex or heavy.</p>\n\n<p>Ali-Naqi Vaziri used harmony, especially the rules of counterpoint, in the following works of his:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Techniques such as “canon” in the Bayat-e Tork duet (Nahar-e Man) for violin, especially at the beginning of the piece;</li>\n  <li>A technique called inversion in the Sistani piece, or</li>\n  <li>Another technique called “round” in two-voice pieces such as “Sorud-e Khak-e Iran” in F major (tonality Fa major) with the voice of Rouhangiz (performing the leading part) and the voice of Ali-Naqi Vaziri himself (performing the following part). The same rules were used in the Mahur piece “Kharidar-e To” and also</li>\n  <li>the technique of “augmentation” in the Dashti reng.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>In Ali-Naqi Vaziri’s compositions, vocal music and instrumental music are in balance. In the orchestra and piano, the bass part appears desirably both melodically as the baritone section (i.e., the lower soprano) and in the orchestra as notes written in the F clef. This part, which in traditional music does not manifest itself due to the limited number of instruments and avaz, stands out in Ali-Naqi Vaziri’s music.</p>\n\n<p>Ali-Naqi Vaziri’s melodies have organized rhythm, clear phrasing, rhythmic movement, as well as dignity and beauty. In his melodies, the melody is distributed among the instruments, whereas in the traditional style, all instruments play the instrument with maximum power in ensemble. The appropriate use of vocal ornaments and emphasis on technique in solo instrumental pieces, especially the tar pieces he himself performed, are among the characteristics of his works. Other innovations of Ali-Naqi Vaziri were unusual yet desirable modulations, examples of which can be mentioned as follows:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>In the melody “Shekayat-e Ney,” which is based on the opening hemistichs of the Masnavi, after several phrases following the avaz, the melody descends on “Mi koron” (forud of Esfahan) and enters Segah, then returns to Esfahan through the gusheh of Oshshaq.</li>\n  <li>In the piece “Rah-e Eshgh” in Mahur “Sol,” contrary to the usual practice of going to Delkash or Shur “Re,” with the lyrics “Soltan guyad keh naqd-e ganjineh-ye man,” it goes to Delkash or Shur “La” and, by using the note “Si” which had been koron in Delkash, returns again to the main maqam. [Khaleqi, 1340 solar]</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>In playing the tar, with jumps, position changes, and adding frets to it, Ali-Naqi Vaziri showed that this instrument and most Iranian instruments are capable of playing more interesting and varied pieces, whereas most Iranian players content themselves with their own taste and feelings. In polyphonizing Iranian music, he did not polyphonize the dastgahs themselves but wrote his own composed pieces with a kind of polyphony (harmony) that was based on the characteristics of national music. Of course, his heavier pieces were not accepted by the general public, and we know that the acceptance of this group of people in a certain period and the artistic value of a work are two separate categories.</p>\n\n<h2>The Teaching Style of Ali-Naqi Vaziri</h2>\n\n<p>In a very brief summary, here are examples of the points that Ali-Naqi Vaziri emphasized in teaching music:</p>\n\n<p>Each instrument should be learned according to its own principles and method. Before starting to play and holding the instrument, notation reading, recognition of signs, rhythm reading (naming the notes with hand movement while considering the rhythm), and solfège (correctly singing the notes with the proper pitch) should be taught so that the student becomes familiar with reading notes and a little music theory at least a few weeks before holding the instrument, and his ear is somewhat trained.</p>\n\n<p>The correct and proper way of holding each instrument and placing the fingers on the neck of the instrument must be taught precisely by the master and practiced. From the very beginning of the work, one must strictly avoid contracting the fingers, hand, and body muscles. During practice, especially on tar and setar, do not crowd the strings and work only with the string that should be played. “Clarity of expression is necessary, not clamor.” (Vaziri especially emphasized sensitivity in santur playing and avoiding the overlapping of sounds, and for this reason he did not use this instrument in his orchestras.)</p>\n\n<p>Exercises and scales gradually become more difficult. First, they should be played heavily and slowly; then, once they are fluent, they should be played at the proper speed.</p>\n\n<p>Continue daily exercises regularly so that taste does not overtake skill and you do not become disgusted with the slackness of your work. In the first lessons, pay attention to the position of holding the instrument so that it does not deviate. Memorize the exercises and, especially on the tar, develop them on the other strings. In violin playing, try from the beginning to have a correct ear and do not get used to playing out of tune. In the first years, metric pieces such as anthems and marches are suitable. Learn certain moods by ear from the teacher.</p>\n\n<p>During practice, if you get tired, stop for a few moments so that the fatigue is relieved and then continue. It is better for the light to fall on the music from behind rather than from the front. In violin practice, stand straight and place the weight of the body on the left foot; during rhythm reading, one can indicate the value of the notes with the tip of the right foot.</p>\n\n<p>Ali-Naqi Vaziri approved of indicating rhythm with the tip of the foot on the ground but did not approve of exaggeration to the point of foot-stamping. Master Abolhasan Saba generally did not recommend foot-tapping and emphasized that we should keep the rhythm in the mind and even count it to preserve it. One day he jokingly said that the players in the military band who blew the surna would stamp their feet with pressure, and then he would imitate their movement in an interesting and humorous way (from Master Saba’s conversations with Dr. Sasan Sepanta in his violin class during teaching).</p>\n\n<p>The head of the violin should be somewhat raised so that the elbow does not touch the body. First, recognize the octave interval well and test the sounds with the open string of the hand, and if the finger is out of place, correct it. The fingers should fall firmly on the string. In rengs, to create joy and rhythmic expression, a separate bow is necessary. Playing fast and rushing forward is harmful, and the new lesson should not be introduced until the previous lesson is well played.</p>\n\n<p>When playing a duet, the teacher should first play in unison with the student; after gaining confidence, he should perform his own part. But in teaching, the teacher should not play along with the student at first but correct his mistakes with words and singing, and then play along with him so that the student also learns the moods from his strokes and becomes enthusiastic. First play certain positions in a lower octave, and when they settle in the ear, work on the main position. On average, if working with the violin is four hours a day, two hours should be practice, one hour singing and memorized pieces, and one hour dedicated to new topics.</p>\n\n<p>During fingering, the fingers should settle in their place without hesitation or searching. New pieces should first be rhythm-read. The wax of the tar plectrum should be mixed with a little wool or fluff so that it does not change shape. The frets of the tar should be tied correctly. The right stroke should be played from above and diagonally on two strings, applying equal pressure to both strings so that a uniform sound is obtained. The plectrum should not hook the string like a claw. The tar should not be placed in a damp place because both the skin and the frets will be damaged. To strengthen the fourth finger, pay attention and emphasize its exercises. When playing the tar, do not look at the neck.</p>\n\n<p>In violin practice, in double stops, try to hear both sounds well so that they settle in the ear. Become familiar with some European pieces. The etudes of Kreutzer and Ševčík are necessary for achieving skill. Start the exercises from the scale of “Do” major (C major or Dastgah-e Mahur) and gradually proceed to practicing the minor scale. Quarter-tone intervals should be taught after the exercises so that the ear becomes accustomed. The two great faults of a student are haste and fickleness. The cultivation of personal taste and composition should be done after school education and studying the field of composition.</p>\n\n<p>When learning singing, while observing the necessary points and becoming familiar with the basics of music theory, solfège (monophonic and polyphonic), and strengthening the power of improvisation, from the beginning the body should be standing and straight, the hands relaxed, and the shoulders dropped, and the mouth should not be too open when singing. For example, when singing the note “Fa,” the mouth should open to the width of one finger. When singing high notes, do not raise the head; the throat should not be constricted and the voice should not turn into a shriek. Shouting is not part of singing. The voice should come out of the mouth comfortably and pleasantly. Do not sing in the throat or nasally.</p>\n\n<p>When singing, from the beginning one must pay attention so that unwanted movements in the body and face do not occur and the body remains in a state of calm. Regarding breathing, during the silences of the piece, or before connected notes and long sustains, and at points of pause, the lungs must be full and ready. One should not breathe in the middle of a connected phrase or half-phrase or between connected notes and in high registers (climax).</p>\n\n<h2>Examples of Ali-Naqi Vaziri’s Views</h2>\n\n<p>These views, which concern various topics of Iranian music, have been collected by Dr. Sasan Sepanta from various sources and are presented in the following order:</p>\n\n<p>Some imagine that the melancholic nature of Eastern music may be related to the quarter tone, and this is a mistake. Its melancholy is related to the manner of intervals and the special style that is applied in the combination of the music. In Iranian music, the scale (in the European style) is often used for one dang or one fifth of it, and that is usually the first dang.</p>\n\n<p>The term “dastgah” should be applied to an avaz whose scale degrees and intervals do not resemble those of another scale. The condition for being a dastgah is that it has a correct fourth and fifth in terms of scale. The classification of Iranian avaz is as follows: dastgah, naghmah, and gusheh.</p>\n\n<p>As soon as a gusheh can acquire a different rhythmic or melodic character, it earns a name. Fortunately, the school and the teaching of notation have refined tastes, and musicians will no longer be satisfied and happy with the composition of trivial melodies with prominent names. The arrangement of gushehs is such that the avaz are gradually raised from the low register, fret by fret, and end in the high register, and are practiced in this way.</p>\n\n<p>The old masters incorporated any type of melody they obtained from various tribes into the dastgah according to these same principles. Most of the original naghmahs of tribes and clans are in the framework of Dastgah-e Shur.</p>\n\n<p>As mentioned, dastgah singing in the old style started from the low register and, by singing the gushehs, gradually moved toward the high register and ended in the climax, which is a monotonous and tedious method. Of course, the gushehs should be performed with rises and falls. Improvisation should only be done by those who are completely advanced, possess talent, and have sufficient skill in their work.</p>\n\n<p>Some gushehs, such as Nowruz Arab, Nowruz-e Saba, and Khara (in Homayun), have no personality and are in fact a kind of exercise between Bidad and Tarz for left and right mallet strokes on the tar, to which names have been given. Every gusheh and melody from Mahur is an example of the old music of Iran and Greece and various scales whose names and methods are mentioned in the history of old music.</p>\n\n<p>In Iranian music scales, some notes have a specific characteristic, such as the witness note, which is heard more than any other in naghmahs and gushehs and is in fact the axis of the melody. The variable note, which is temporarily changed from its natural form to express touching or joyful feelings, is usually estimated to change by about a quarter tone. The standing note, which is other than the main tonic of the dastgah, is used in naghmahs and gushehs for half-cadence and temporary pause.</p>\n\n<p>It is strange that after fifty years [the comparison refers to 1313 solar] since military polyphonic music was introduced in Iran and ten years since the establishment of the Higher School of Music and the promotion of polyphonic music, still in records — especially on piano — monophonic music is heard, which is due to the lack of interest of the people of the East in harmony and their enthusiasm for melody and rhythm.</p>\n\n<p>Our music must represent our morals and tastes. Music is not imitable like hats and clothes. Music is the representative of feelings and emotions. Hope that after a long period of forgetting their ancient civilization, Iranians will turn toward civilization and take the diligent Japanese as an example, who in the past forty years [the comparison refers to 1301 solar] have reached the highest degrees of excellence. Perhaps we too can bring the withered spirit of our nation to honor and glory.</p>\n\n<p>In Iranian scales, the falling tone is very much attended to, because in some avaz such as Homayun, in addition to the fact that the leading note has no personality and is often variable (variable note), on the contrary, its lower leading note is fixed and constantly used, and this can be expressed with the logic of the falling tone, and its harmony should also be taken in the same falling tone.</p>\n\n<p>Regarding the moods of the avaz: Chahargah is a majestic avaz in which most martial and boasting poems, etc., are sung. Segah is in fact a miniature of Chahargah, and a melancholic and exiled mood is evident in it. Nava is a noble and calm avaz that narrates its heartache in the form of advice, and listening to it in complete silence is pleasurable for relieving fatigue and leisure. In Nava, more importance is given to the low part. The scale of Rast Panjgah is the same as the Mahur scale; only in the modulations that occur in Rast Panjgah there are differences with Mahur.</p>\n\n<p>Bayat-e Tork is a very popular avaz in which the witness note is heard repeatedly. In Afshari, delicate feelings can be expressed. Esfahan is an avaz that is neither very melancholic nor very nervous and bold. A mood of healthy spirit and storytelling matches it, as the piece “Shekayat-e Ney” was composed in the mode of Esfahan.</p>\n\n<p>In playing, without regular practice, reaching the degree of musicianship is not possible. To avoid monotony, suitable modulations are necessary, and the artist’s skill in improvisation is precisely this.</p>\n\n<p>The limit of the koron quarter tone in music theory is the midpoint between natural and flat, and sori is between natural and sharp. I have no concern with how the so-called quarter-tone rule is in terms of the mathematical height of the sound, but all players know how to play it.</p>\n\n<p>The setar is an intimate instrument, low in volume, and with delicate form, soft sound, and captivating melody, beloved by philosophers, poets, exiled lovers, and thoughtful recluses.</p>\n\n<p>In composition and playing, to achieve healthy taste, precise and correct artistic training is necessary. The ear and eye must be protected from hearing ugliness and seeing crookedness. Of course, merely seeking beauty is not enough; it must be accompanied by moral elevation. The light of goodness and morality that shines in the body and soul of the creator of the artistic work will also be reflected in his work. A genius is ahead of his time and therefore is not necessarily the product of his time.</p>\n\n<h2>The Character and Personality of Ali-Naqi Vaziri</h2>\n\n<p>Ali-Naqi Vaziri was a hardworking, serious, orderly, patient, determined, straightforward man with a spirit of freedom and not submitting to force. Even in the years when Dr. Sasan Sepanta had the honor of studying under him and was learning music and aesthetics in art from him — that is, around the age of seventy — his morning exercise was never abandoned. He emphasized that one must exercise one hour daily. Vaziri had a serious appearance, penetrating eyes, and a deep and mature voice, and he was steadfast, determined, and persistent in his work and was not discouraged by obstacles.</p>\n\n<p>He emphasized respect for musicians with character and forbade them from playing in entertainment gatherings and expedient assemblies. He would always say: “Work makes difficulties easy.” He was interested in group work, especially orchestral work, and was not afraid of excuses and unfair criticism; he would continue his work and recommended positive work accompanied by joy and hope. The influence of a healthy spirit and body, hope, and movement is evident in his works, and perhaps for this reason, listening to his works does not suit lethargic spirits.</p>\n\n<p>Ali-Naqi Vaziri’s professional activities and his initial training under his father in the military and his studies in Europe were effective in his spiritual and physical condition in terms of management and leadership power. Most of his works were composed in the first decade of the fourteenth solar century, that is, at a time when social transformations toward modernity and renewal were observable in other arts such as poetry, storytelling, theater, cinema, and painting. Naturally, despite many obstacles and static traditionalists, that social transformation was suitable for presenting his works.</p>\n\n<p>Vaziri’s lifestyle was simple and unadorned, but in artistic matters, just as he disapproved of jealousy, he considered gentlemanly competition and the pursuit of perfection permissible. Most of his friends and companions were prominent writers and literary and artistic figures. His share in the transformation of Iranian music — both in terms of composition and performance and execution, and also in terms of serving as a model as a distinguished and characterful master — is unparalleled.</p>\n\n<p>The famous musician Musa Ma’rufi writes: “During the time that Master Ali-Naqi Vaziri was engaged in musical work, his behavior was such that musicians gradually respected him, and no one could look upon them with contempt.” [Ma’rufi, 1339 solar]</p>\n\n<p>It seems that Ali-Naqi Vaziri’s avoidance of playing in gatherings was a calculated reaction against the misuse by some people of artists whom they only wanted for their own pleasure assemblies, and also to convey the point that there is a great difference between a true artist and a professional instrumentalist — a difference that, unfortunately, some ordinary people are unable to understand.</p>\n\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n\n<p>If you have any comments or questions regarding the biography of Ali-Naqi Vaziri, please share them in the comments section of the article with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/\">us</a> to improve the quality of this content.</p>', '2024-02-18 16:56:35', 0, '2024-09-19 22:45:13', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1555, 'posts', 98, 'en', NULL, 'Ahmad Ebadi, prominent setar player and son of Mirza Abdollah', NULL, 'Ahmad Ebadi (born 1906 in Tehran) was one of the most influential Iranian setar players who helped popularize the instrument in modern Persian classical music. Coming from a prominent musical family and briefly trained by his father Mirza Abdullah, he developed his own distinctive style and later performed on Iranian radio and in concerts, significantly contributing to the visibility of the setar. He collaborated and interacted with major artists such as Qamar-ol-Moluk Vaziri and respected musicians like Abolhasan Saba. Ebadi was known for his refined technique, emphasis on improvisation, and commitment to the traditional Persian musical repertoire (radif), leaving a lasting legacy in Iranian classical music.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Ahmad Ebadi</h2>\n\n<p>Ahmad Ebadi was born in 1285 solar in Tehran. He was not yet eight years old when his father <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87/\">Mirza Abdollah</a> passed away, and he was only able to benefit from his father’s teachings for one or two sessions. Because he had a great love for music, he benefited from the teachings of his older sisters Moloud and Moluk, who had learned setar under their father’s supervision, and began playing the setar. Ebadi’s setar playing was already pleasant to listen to by the age of eighteen. In 1303 solar, he performed in a concert held at the Grand Hotel hall together with Moluk Zarabi, who had just stepped onto the theater stage.</p>\n\n<p>In 1300 solar, he was employed in the police force, but since this job did not suit his temperament, he was transferred to the Ministry of National Economy around 1323 solar. One gramophone record from his early period of playing exists; it was recorded around 1312 solar by the Columbia company in Tehran, sent to England for duplication, and distributed in Iran by the Akhavan Aristozadeh trading house. On this record, Ebadi accompanied the avaz of Reza-Qoli Mirza Zelli in Dastgah-e Shur with the setar.</p>\n\n<p>From 1327 solar, Ebadi performed setar for a fifteen-minute solo program on radio once a week. These programs, like the rest of the radio programs (except for the playing of gramophone records), were broadcast live. At that time, magnetic tape recording devices had not yet become common. A few years later, Ebadi also participated in the radio Golha programs and left behind some examples of his setar playing. During this period, he became interested in the music of neighboring countries such as Turkey and India and wished to travel to these countries to study their music, but this did not materialize.</p>\n\n<p>Ebadi avoided crowded and noisy gatherings and would often ask his friends to leave him free after ten o’clock at night so he could rest. He was fond of the voices of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%82%d9%85%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%85%d9%84%d9%88%da%a9-%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Qamar al-Moluk Vaziri</a> and Moluk Zarabi and held great respect for Banan and Adib Khansari. Among poets and writers, he was fond of Motia’ al-Dowleh Hejazi, Navab Safa, Rahe Mo’ayyeri, and Shahriar. Ebadi’s setar playing on radio was one of the effective factors in popularizing this instrument.</p>\n\n<p>Ebadi had not played the setar for more than a few sessions on radio when <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%84%d8%ad%d8%b3%d9%86-%d8%b5%d8%a8%d8%a7/\">Abolhasan Saba</a> also began his own setar program on radio every two weeks. Ebadi spoke to Saba by telephone and told him: “You are an unparalleled master and you learned the setar from my father. Now that the son of your master is playing the setar on radio, is it right that after so many years you have suddenly decided to play the setar?” Navab Safa writes:</p>\n\n<p>“Saba, this great and noble-born master, respected Ebadi’s feelings and did not want to hurt the sensitive heart of his late master’s son. For this reason, as long as he lived, he refrained from playing the setar on radio.” [Navab Safa, 1377 solar]</p>\n\n<p>In 1341 and 1342 solar, Ebadi expressed interest in hearing the sound of his father Mirza Abdollah’s instrument, which Dr. Sasan Sepanta had restored. Dr. Daryush Safvat mentioned the matter to Dr. Sasan Sepanta, and one day, according to a previous appointment, they went to Ebadi’s house. They took with them the restored magnetic reel tape of Mirza Abdollah’s playing and also that of Ebadi’s uncle <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86%d9%82%d9%84%db%8c/\">Aqa Hossein-Qoli</a>. Ebadi immediately recognized the sound of his father’s instrument and the style of his mallets. During the moments when Mirza Abdollah’s playing was broadcast in the room, he listened to his father’s sound with emotion and great attention.</p>\n\n<p>Among the precious objects Ebadi kept at home was a magnificent setar that had belonged to Naser al-Din Shah, which had reached him after ninety years. This setar was dark in color and had a painting on its bowl. After hearing his father’s playing, Ebadi became excited and played for Dr. Sepanta and Dr. Safvat on that historical setar that Mirza Abdollah had once played. Dr. Safvat took a photograph of the narrator and Ebadi as a memento of those moments. In this photo, Ebadi is holding that historical setar.</p>\n\n<p>The story of how Naser al-Din Shah’s setar reached Ebadi after ninety years in a strange way is as follows: During Naser al-Din Shah’s second trip to Europe, one of the Iranians who had gone to Europe and settled there came into the presence of Naser al-Din Shah and presented him with a beautiful setar that he had brought from Iran and used to play. After returning to Tehran, Naser al-Din Shah gave the setar to one of his favorite ladies in the harem named Haj Zohra Khanom, who in turn gave it to the young daughter of one of the women of the harem whom she had chosen as her adopted daughter.</p>\n\n<p>After Naser al-Din Shah was shot, the adopted daughter of Haj Zohra Khanom thought of learning the setar. At that time, many children of dignitaries and court adolescents were studying setar with Mirza Abdollah. Because most of Mirza Abdollah’s students were male, to resolve the issue of teaching this young lady, it was arranged for a temporary marriage contract (sigheh) to be performed, and so it was done, and she began learning the setar. Some time later the young lady got married, and household duties and her husband’s disapproval prevented her from continuing to attend Mirza Abdollah’s class.</p>\n\n<p>Mirza Abdollah also passed away after some time, and Naser al-Din Shah’s beautiful setar was put away in a corner of a trunk. Years passed, and the young lady became a mother and grandmother, with sons, daughters, sons-in-law, daughters-in-law, grandchildren, and great-grandchildren, and the snow of old age settled on her head.</p>\n\n<p>One day when her son-in-law and one of her daughters had come to her house, the sound of the setar was playing on the radio. She asked her son-in-law whose setar it was, and he replied that it was Master Ebadi’s setar and that he and Ebadi worked in the same office. The woman said that Ebadi’s setar reminded her of her teacher Mirza Abdollah. The son-in-law replied that Master Ebadi was the son of that same Mirza Abdollah. The lady said: “I have a setar at the bottom of one of my trunks in Abadan, and I want to give it to Mirza Abdollah’s son (who is also my own son!) in appreciation of the lessons I took from him. When you go to the office tomorrow, tell him I have a setar for him.” A few months later, Naser al-Din Shah’s souvenir setar arrived from Abadan and was presented to Ebadi.</p>\n\n<p>Ebadi would usually wake up early in the morning when it was still dark and light, and for about fifteen minutes he would play the setar along with the singing of his canaries. He said that when he first started on radio, the programs were broadcast live and he could not hear the sound of his own instrument. But after tape recorders arrived at the radio and he heard the sound of his own playing, he was able to realize that his playing style was somewhat crowded, and it took him a long time to reach a style in which there were no unwanted strokes on the strings. He was invited to the Paris Music Congress and also to Italy, Germany, and Austria, where he gave performances.</p>\n\n<p>Ebadi was precise in tuning the setar and emphasized improvisation. He played the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%af%db%8c%d9%81/\">radif</a> in his own pleasant and appealing manner. In addition to the gramophone records and tapes from the solo instrumentalists program and Golha-ye Rangarang that exist of his playing, two CDs (and one double-cassette album) of his solo setar performances in various dastgahs were also published by the Mahoor Institute in 1379 solar.</p>', '2024-02-19 19:13:29', 0, '2024-12-19 19:10:20', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1556, 'posts', 99, 'en', NULL, 'Qamar-ol-Moluk Vaziri, the eternal and lasting voice of Iranian singing', NULL, 'Qamar-ol-Molouk Vaziri Qamar-ol-Molouk Vaziri was one of the most influential female singers in early 20th-century Iranian music. Orphaned at a young age, she was raised by her grandmother and gradually entered the world of traditional Persian music, later studying under masters such as Morteza Neydavoud. With a powerful, clear, and wide-ranging voice, she gained fame through live concerts, radio performances, and early gramophone recordings, playing a key role in popularizing Persian classical singing. Her collaborations with musicians like Abdolhossein Saba further strengthened her legacy. Beyond her artistic achievements, she was also known for her generosity and charitable actions. Despite facing financial and social difficulties later in life, she remained a landmark figure in Iranian vocal music until her death in 1958.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Qamar al-Moluk Vaziri</h2>\n\n<p>Qamar al-Moluk Vaziri was born around 1282 solar, although her birth certificate recorded 1283. She lost her mother in childhood, and since her father had also passed away, her grandmother Molla Khayr al-Nesa, titled Eftekhar al-Zakerin, took charge of her upbringing. As a child, she would accompany her grandmother to women’s rawzeh-khani gatherings. Molla Khayr al-Nesa had a good voice and recited rawzeh and moludi. Little Qamar would also walk among the crowd at suitable moments and chant elegies. Years later she herself said: “This experience gave me the courage to be able to sing comfortably in front of a crowd.”</p>\n\n<p>Their home was in the Sanglaj neighborhood of Tehran. Because of leg pain and paralysis, Khayr al-Nesa had made a vow to go to Karbala, and as a result she entrusted Qamar to her aunt, who was the wife of Prince Abdolazim Mirza Kashfi. Qamar’s cousin later became the wife of Mojadd al-Sana’ye, and in her home musicians such as Darvish Khan, Rokn al-Din Khan Mokhtari, Haji Khan Ein al-Dowleh (percussionist), and Prince Hessam al-Saltaneh would come and go. From her teenage years, Qamar’s ear became familiar with music in that house.</p>\n\n<p>Around 1300 solar, at a wedding gathering where Qamar was among the guests, they asked her to sing a piece. She sang a tasnif with lyrics by Pezhman Bakhtiari that began with the hemistich: “Biya Morgh-e Hagh Emshab Faghan Namayim.” Her voice attracted the attention of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%b1%d8%aa%d8%b6%db%8c-%d9%86%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%88%d8%af/\">Morteza Ney-Davud</a>, who was also a guest. Ney-Davud took the tar from the player and accompanied Qamar. After the song ended, he suggested that she come to his class to learn the dastgahs and avaz. Qamar attended Ney-Davud’s music class and studied for more than two years. A summary of the main topics Qamar learned in Ney-Davud’s class is as follows:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>The dastgahs of Iranian music, avaz (naghmahs), and most of the common vocal gushehs</li>\n  <li>The manner of singing, how to perform the daramad, the course through the gushehs, and forud</li>\n  <li>Appropriate poems for singing each naghmah or gusheh from classical poets such as Sa’di, Hafez, and Molavi, and some contemporaries such as Adib Pishavari, Malek al-Sho’ara Bahar, Iraj, Aref Qazvini, Eshqi, Nezam Vafa, Heydar Ali Kamali, Pezhman Bakhtiari, Mohammad Ali Amir-Jahed, and several others</li>\n  <li>The manner of delivering poetry in avaz and suitable vocal trills and ornaments</li>\n  <li>Appropriate vocal modulations, especially skill in foruds and transitions from one gusheh or naghmah to suitable ones</li>\n  <li>Skill in reaching high notes in appropriate gushehs, such as Oshshaq, Hejaz, Bayat-e Raj’, Mokhalef Segah, the gusheh of Hossein in Shur, and suitable trills in the high register</li>\n  <li>Suitable metric pieces and common tasnifs for each avaz, such as compositions by Darvish, Aref, Ney-Davud, and others, along with maintaining meter, observing timely silences, and observing off-beats</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>After learning the techniques of avaz in Ney-Davud’s class, Qamar gave her first concert around 1303 solar at the Grand Hotel hall with Morteza Ney-Davud’s accompaniment. Shortly afterward, she held her second concert at the Palace Cinema hall (Islambol Street, Tehran), and these concerts increased her fame.</p>\n\n<p>Gradually she also became acquainted with the famous poets and writers of her time. Among the poets who were fond of Qamar’s voice was Mirzadeh Eshqi. Qamar spoke about her last meeting with Eshqi:</p>\n\n<p>“From time to time I would go to see Eshqi. The last time he received me with a cheerful face and open expression. A worn and faded kilim was spread in his room. At his insistence I sat on a chair. He apologized for the state of his life, saying he had no means to entertain me, and that he had pawned his cigarette case with the corner grocer for one pack of cigarettes and matches.</p>\n\n<p>Eshqi told me: ‘I will soon be gone, because the government is not on good terms with me.’ Some time later (Tir 1303 solar) one of my acquaintances came to see me in a hurry and without preamble said: ‘Qamar, they shot Eshqi!’ I quickly got dressed and went to Eshqi’s house in Dowlat Gate. Two police officers were guarding the place. I wanted to enter Eshqi’s house, but they prevented me. Later I learned that he had been taken to the police hospital and his condition was critical. Near noon the news came that Eshqi had died in the hospital.”</p>\n\n<p>A few years later Qamar recorded a gramophone record in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%b9%d8%b7%d8%a7/\">Abu Ata</a> and Hejaz in memory of Eshqi. With Hossein Yahaghi’s violin accompaniment, she sang this poem by Eshqi with a moving tone:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Hezar Bar Mara Marg Be Az In Sakhti St — Baraye Mardom-e Badbakht Marg Khoshbakhti St</p>\n\n<p>At the beginning of this record, a voice — probably Hossein Yahaghi’s — says: “In memory of the unfortunate poet Mirzadeh Eshqi.” The mentioned record was recorded in Iran and duplicated in Berlin in 1306 solar and put on the market. Buying, selling, and listening to this record was not very free during Reza Shah’s reign and it was usually confiscated.</p>\n\n<p>Qamar was among the first artists to give a concert for the benefit of a disabled artist. Around 1306 solar she organized a concert in honor of Shokrollah Qahremani, the old tar player, and sang.</p>\n\n<p>Around 1309 solar, during her trip to Khorasan, General Amanollah Jahangiri asked her to give a concert to help build Ferdowsi’s mausoleum, and she accepted. Qamar says:</p>\n\n<p>“The Amir [Jahangiri] thanked me for the mentioned concert, which I had sung as a volunteer. Although he worked hard to build Ferdowsi’s mausoleum, many of the funds were embezzled by opportunists. During that trip I visited the tomb of Colonel Mohammad Taqi Pessian, to whom I had been devoted during his lifetime. From the Khorasan trip, what I received was a small souvenir carpet that one of the local merchants gave me and a set of Afghan clothes that Soulat al-Saltaneh presented to me as a memento of the trip.”</p>\n\n<p>The famous writer Abdolrahman Faramarzi, confirming the above (in the editorial of Kayhan newspaper, 18 Mordad 1338 solar), writes:</p>\n\n<p>“One night she gave a concert in Mashhad. She earned four thousand tomans from that concert and donated all of it to charity. Four thousand tomans at that time was at least four hundred thousand tomans. Which singer today would give four hundred thousand tomans to charity?!”</p>\n\n<p>Among Qamar’s other concerts was one she held in Hamedan around 1310 solar together with her teacher and colleague Morteza Ney-Davud. Regarding this, Qamar says:</p>\n\n<p>“As soon as I arrived in Hamedan, I wanted to visit Aref, the great poet of Iran, but those who knew him prevented me and said that Aref had chosen seclusion and hardly accepted anyone. I stayed in Hamedan for two days, and finally on the third day I went to Aref’s house and managed to meet him. I invited him to attend my concert that same night.” (Qamar had previously recorded several <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%aa%d8%b5%d9%86%db%8c%d9%81/\">tasnifs</a> by Aref on gramophone records and was familiar with Aref’s name and works.)</p>\n\n<p>Early in the evening Aref went to the guesthouse where Qamar was staying and together they went to the concert venue. After the concert ended, several silver vases were given to Qamar, the largest of which was a gift from Prince Nayyer al-Dowleh, and Qamar presented it to Aref. The next day Nayyer al-Dowleh complained to Qamar why she had given his gifted vase to Aref. Because the government of the time was not on good terms with Aref, they forced Qamar to explain in the next night’s concert that she had given the vase to him out of the devotion she had long felt for Aref. Qamar says:</p>\n\n<p>“After the concert ended, I decided to meet a young man who had been writing letters to me from Hamedan and expressing interest in my singing. It turned out that because they had found one of my gramophone records (the Republic March) with him, they had imprisoned him on charges of republicanism. At that time Reza Shah had ordered that republican voices be silenced. They also confiscated my record on which I had sung the Republic March, and anyone who kept that record was prosecuted.”</p>\n\n<p>After learning of the young man’s imprisonment, Qamar insistently asked the chief of police of Hamedan to release him (his job was that of a coffeehouse owner), and according to one account, she made holding the concert conditional on this matter. Eventually the young man was freed from prison.</p>\n\n<p>The distribution of Qamar’s records and her concerts caused her fame to grow day by day, and some poets such as Bahar and Iraj composed poems in her praise. Malek al-Sho’ara Bahar wrote a poem in praise of Qamar in 1307 solar that begins:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Ey Nogol-e Bagh-e Zendegani — Ey Bartar va Behtar az Javani</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Ey Shabnam-e Sobh dar Lotafat — Ey Sabzeh-ye Taze dar Nazafat</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Mam-e To Cho Aftab Zadeh — Namat Zeh Cheh Ru Qamar Nehadeh</p>\n\n<p>On Mondays and Wednesdays, a group of poets and admirers would gather at Qamar’s house to listen to her voice.</p>\n\n<p>Ruhollah Khaleqi writes:</p>\n\n<p>“In 1357 solar one day Qamar came to the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b3%d9%87-%d8%b9%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">Higher School of Music</a>. Master Ali-Naqi Vaziri played the tar and Qamar sang. It was a friendly gathering, and her singing affected our hearts so deeply that even now that delightful memory has not been forgotten.”</p>\n\n<p>It is recounted that she gave a concert in Bandar Anzali. After the concert and dinner, she went for a walk by the sea. She talked with some workers who were working on a sand dredger and felt inclined to sing for them. When she began to sing, the others stopped working and gathered around her. When they recognized her, they entertained her. She herself said:</p>\n\n<p>“They brought me tea from their own two-burner kettle, and although I had eaten dinner, I ate a few bites of the bread and cheese they had so they would not be upset. It was a beautiful night. The sea was singing, and I was singing too.”</p>\n\n<p>Qamar’s cousin said:</p>\n\n<p>“Qamar had taken responsibility for several orphaned and parentless children who had been gathered from different parts of the city, and whatever she earned she spent on the poor and destitute. She would say: ‘I would not exchange the smile of an orphaned child for all the pleasures of the world.’”</p>\n\n<p>Eyewitnesses have repeatedly recounted that in concerts and more private gatherings, after the program ended, some attendees, moved by Qamar’s voice, would shower gold coins over her head or scatter them at her feet, and she would divide them among the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d8%b2%d9%86%d8%af%da%af%db%8c%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%85%d9%87-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%86/\">musicians</a> or donate them to charity.</p>\n\n<p>Morteza Ney-Davud recounted that Qamar had bought a house on Jami Street in Tehran, which she later sold and divided the money among the needy she had in mind. Therefore, Qamar’s importance lies not only in the excellence of her artistic activity and the value of her works, but also in her moral, spiritual, and human character.</p>\n\n<p>From 1319 solar, when the radio station was established in Tehran, Qamar collaborated with radio and performed programs accompanied by Habib Samaei on santur, Abolhasan Saba and Hossein Yahaghi on violin, and the Ma’rufi brothers on tar and violin (until around 1332 solar). Around 1334 solar, which were the last years of Qamar’s collaboration with radio, when her performances were being recorded, due to the unawareness of the officials or, more likely, because of jealousy and narrow-mindedness of some inexperienced and talentless new singers and their influence, her tapes were erased to be used for other purposes.</p>\n\n<p>In the article “Qamar al-Moluk Vaziri” in the magazine Musiq-e Iran, No. 14, Year 6, Shahrivar 1338 solar, Dr. Sasan Sepanta explicitly criticized and condemned the erasure of Qamar’s avaz tapes by radio. Mehdi Khaladi says in an interview: “Moshir Homayun (may God have mercy on him; may his hand be short from the world) did the greatest disservice to me and to everyone. He destroyed tapes of mine.”</p>\n\n<p>And Fereydoun Naser, composer and conductor of the Tehran Symphony Orchestra, addressing Khaladi in the same place, says: “Mr. Khaladi, please note that they erased all of Adib Khansari’s tapes so that not a single one remains!”</p>\n\n<p>And Khaladi replies: “That was also Adib thirty years ago, meaning Adib at his peak. I had played violin with him on all those tapes.” [Monthly Kelk, No. 31, Year 3, Mehr 1371 solar]</p>\n\n<p>Qamar rarely complained about the erasure of her tapes. One day she indirectly referred to the matter. In response, Dr. Sasan Sepanta recited this poem from Molavi’s Masnavi for her in Afshari avaz (the gusheh of Masnavi Pich, in her own style and trills):</p>\n\n<p>Gar Do Se Ableh To Ra Monkar Shavand — Talkh Ki Gardi Cho Hasti Kan-e Qand</p>\n\n<p>A calm appeared in her eyes and her answer was a smile.</p>\n\n<p>If the engraving needles of the German and English record companies had not existed, and if Qamar’s old records had not been collected from street vendors’ stalls and second-hand shops by admirers, today perhaps no trace would remain of the voice of Iran’s most famous avaz singer. The only recording that remained in the radio archive was her last program, performed in a state of weakness and disability after a mild stroke, accompanied by the tar of Esma’il Kamali (a student of Morteza Ney-Davud who played in his teacher’s style) in Afshari avaz.</p>\n\n<p>This recording, which existed in the radio archive under number 52, cannot be considered representative of her voice because it was made after her mild stroke, and her trills are not like those from a year earlier. Ruhollah Khaleqi wrote that <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b3%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%b7%d8%a7%d9%87%d8%b1%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%af%d9%87/\">Taherzadeh</a> told him: “When I heard Qamar’s record, I thought I myself was singing.” “This praise from the master is enough to understand the importance of Qamar’s artistic status… A woman who, among all the women of her time and even today’s singers, stands higher and more exalted than all.” [Khaleqi, 1333 solar]</p>\n\n<p>During her period of fame, Qamar never withheld kindness from people and considered her art to belong to them. She lived with a sense of independence and did not accumulate wealth. It is said that she would attend wedding parties of some orphaned and parentless girls and boys that were held with her assistance, and she would warm the occasion with her singing. Often on the same night she would refuse invitations from statesmen and rulers of the time to perform. After marriage, Qamar went with her husband to Qazvin for some time. Her husband’s opposition and environmental problems could not prevent her artistic activities, and eventually her married life ended in separation.</p>\n\n<p>From the time when she was at the height of her fame and could have accumulated wealth, she gave her income to the needy and never allowed herself to fear that one day she would be met with ingratitude and forgetfulness. In her later years Qamar faced financial hardship. A few of her friends in Tehran bought her a small multi-room house. To make a living, around 1330 solar she was forced to sing in one or two restaurants in Tehran.</p>\n\n<p>Dr. Sasan Sepanta says:</p>\n\n<p>“In her later years Qamar would sing at night in the Métropole Café at the beginning of Lalehzar Street. Her voice was calm in those days and she would usually give a suitable trill in the middle of the avaz, for which people often applauded. In my opinion, the reason Qamar sang in the café was not only financial need. She needed to see the audience in front of her and sing for them. In her youth she had given concerts in the same Lalehzar (of course in the splendid Grand Hotel hall). After her second stroke she repeatedly told me: ‘My only wish is to be able to sing once more.’”</p>\n\n<p>Samin Baghchehban, composer and professor at the Higher School of Music, recounts the day he saw Qamar in the accounting office of the radio administration in Tehran (Kayhan supplement, 14 Mordad 1355 solar). He says Qamar was sitting on a chair wearing a white headscarf and a thin long manteau, her foot hurting and the area under her eyes swollen. He asked her: “If there is any service I can do for you, please tell me.” Qamar replied: “No sir, thank you.” The accounting employee said: “They have set a monthly allowance of two hundred tomans for her, but now for some time she has been coming and going, but the administrative formalities have not yet been completed.”</p>\n\n<p>After a while Qamar got up to leave. Baghchehban took her arm and they silently and calmly went down the stairs. She made no complaint and did not say that for that two hundred tomans she had gone up and down the stairs of the administration so many times that her foot had broken. Baghchehban escorted her to the street, hailed a taxi for her, and wanted to pay the fare, but Qamar said: “No sir, thank you.” Baghchehban writes: “I did not insist, and I should not have insisted. When the taxi started moving, we exchanged a glance. Her eyes had become tearful. She did not recognize me and did not ask my name.”</p>\n\n<p>Dr. Sasan Sepanta says:</p>\n\n<p>“I had the honor of being in Qamar’s presence and in the years when she was singing her last avaz, I benefited from her voice. Her biography and a glimpse of her works and part of my in-person conversations with her were published in the Shahrivar and Mehr 1338 solar issues of Musiq-e Iran. Other materials about Qamar by me were also published in issues 7 and 10 of 1337, issue 3 of 1338, and also the articles ‘Avaz-e Iran’ in the same magazine. Several radio programs about introducing Qamar’s works with my narration titled ‘Tahavvol-e Musiqi-ye Sonnati-ye Iran’ were produced and broadcast by the Isfahan center in 1355 solar.</p>\n\n<p>In her later years, although the snow of old age had settled on her head, Qamar preserved her high spirit and did not withhold help from the needy.</p>\n\n<p>Qamar passed away on 14 Mordad 1338 solar. A few days before her death, while she had lost her strength and her speech had become impaired, she went to her cousin’s house and finally passed away in her arms.</p>\n\n<p>Qamar’s cousin says:</p>\n\n<p>“I ran in panic to call the doctor, but it was already too late. That night until morning her lifeless body remained in the room and no one was by her side. Only a small oil lamp flickered above her head.”</p>\n\n<p>The next morning Qamar’s family contacted the Publications and Radio Administration, and the director general at the time (Mo’iniyan) ordered the body to be taken to the forensic medicine morgue so that it could be announced on radio and admirers could be informed.</p>\n\n<p>On the day of the funeral, apart from a few relatives and a handful of artists such as Morteza Ney-Davud and a few others, no one else went to Qamar’s house. From there, with only about thirty people participating in the funeral procession, she was taken to the cemetery in Zahir al-Dowleh that had been purchased with the help of the radio administration. The municipal police at Darband detained the body for hours beside the street pending permission from the municipality, until finally in the afternoon of that day the lifeless body of the greatest singer of the recent century in Iran was buried with the presence of thirty mourners and two or three bouquets of flowers.</p>\n\n<p>Abdolrahman Faramarzi wrote in the editorial of Kayhan newspaper on 18 Mordad 1338 solar:</p>\n\n<p>“You read in the newspapers the details of her death and the transfer of her body and the police preventing the body from reaching Zahir al-Dowleh cemetery. Qamar died. No one was by her side. The radio administration tried very hard to create an artificial tribute for her but failed. From all those countless singers, musicians, and art lovers, not a single one bothered to take the trouble to come and stand for half an hour beside Qamar’s body or grave. So our claim of loving art is a lie, and these words we say about encouraging art and artists, we ourselves do not believe them.</p>\n\n<p>After Qamar, where will Mother Nature give birth to a more exalted artist so that someone can prove their love of art by encouraging her? You newborns of the garden of existence do not know who Qamar was. Ask those who witnessed her glory and majesty in her time… But that Qamar, with all her glory and majesty, died in the utmost hardship and the final degree of obscurity and helplessness, and showed that in this country nothing is stored for a person except money or a powerful clan and group that creates artificial glory and majesty for him.”</p>\n\n<p>The grief caused by the ingratitude and forgetfulness of some people toward Qamar led to the writing of articles in some newspapers. For example, the newspaper Estakhr published in Shiraz (30 Mordad 1338 solar) writes:</p>\n\n<p>“Some people wept at Qamar’s grave, but these were people who had family ties with her. From that vast crowd of people who had heard Qamar’s voice many times and benefited from her art, those who praised her, those who fed on her singing, and those who flattered her, paid no attention to Qamar’s death and refrained from escorting her body to the grave… Shame on those sycophants who are like flies around the honey as long as there is sweetness, who are humble and submissive as long as there is glitter and pomp, and when the table is cleared they turn to another table and open their babbling mouths to praise someone else.”</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a> considered Qamar’s death a great loss to the world of art and said:</p>\n\n<p>“The loss of Qamar, who in addition to having an artistic position spent her life in good reputation and service to her fellow human beings and helping the poor and those beneath her, is a cause of utmost regret for the artistic community.”</p>\n\n<p>Ali-Naqi Vaziri also said:</p>\n\n<p>“There is no doubt that Qamar was an unparalleled singer.”</p>\n\n<p>On 31 Shahrivar 1338 solar, the Publications and Radio Administration held a memorial ceremony on the occasion of Qamar’s passing. In this ceremony, after playing a few of her records, the director general of Publications and Radio, Khaleqi, and Mo’iniyan Kermanshahi gave speeches. In his speech Khaleqi said:</p>\n\n<p>“The absence of artists at Qamar’s funeral did not diminish her value… People like Qamar, in a society that does not think much about them, should not expect encouragement from people and should think about their own future.”</p>\n\n<p>Then Morteza Ney-Davud, Qamar’s teacher, performed some pieces on tar accompanied by the voice of Rouhangiz, and the National Instruments Orchestra performed some pieces with Farah’s voice.</p>\n\n<h2>The Style of Qamar al-Moluk Vaziri</h2>\n\n<p>Qamar’s voice was clear, attractive, and brilliant. Morteza Ney-Davud described her voice as powerful, resonant, and warm, and always said:</p>\n\n<p>“It is rarely possible to combine these two qualities — that the voice be both warm and attractive and also wide-ranging (powerful).”</p>\n\n<p>In Ney-Davud’s opinion, the voice of Habib Shater Haji (the famous singer from Isfahan) was extremely powerful, but Qamar’s voice was even stronger than his, and at the same time it had warmth and attractiveness. Qamar’s vocal range can roughly be compared to that of a mezzo-soprano in European music. The extent of her voice in performing trills in the low and high registers was a sign of her capability in singing. Her comfortable range for performing melodic ornaments of avaz, such as various trills and successive accents, etc., was around the notes Sol₃ to Re₄, that is, frequencies of approximately 392 to 587 Hz.</p>\n\n<p>For this reason she usually did not sing Oshshaq, Dashti, or Esfahan in the low register and could perform their trills in the high register, that is, from the note Si₃ to Fa₄. In gushehs such as Hejaz or Iraq, the high register of her voice would manifest itself well and display its brilliance. In this respect, among the old female singers, only Rouhangiz is comparable to her.</p>\n\n<p>The timbre of Qamar’s voice was unique to her. Because she had practiced from the beginning with a fretted instrument (tar), she pronounced the scale notes very accurately, and due to the pleasant reflection of the sound in the vocal cavities of her throat, her voice had a special ring and brilliance.</p>\n\n<p>The power of her voice depended on its timbre. Many radio singers of later periods, because of the microphone and amplification devices, compensated for the lack of range in their voice by singing close to the microphone, whereas Qamar’s wide-ranging voice could sometimes be heard at great distances in open air and the silence of the night. In such high registers her voice never broke and never went out of the main notes. She delivered trills without strain, comfortably, and pleasantly.</p>\n\n<p>Dr. Sasan Sepanta says:</p>\n\n<p>“In the last years of her singing and the period of her artistic maturity, I saw and heard Qamar’s avaz up close. When singing, her mouth was slightly open and she sang calmly and with dignity, with a serene expression, and her melodious, brilliant, and pleasant voice affected the listener. Upon hearing her avaz, this poem by Molavi involuntarily comes to mind:</p>\n\n<p>In ‘ajab bolbol ke bogshayad dahan — Ta khorad u khar ra ba golestan</p>\n\n<p>In na bolbol in nahang atashi st — Jomleh nakhosh ha ze eshq u ra khoshi st</p>\n\n<p>Overall, it can be said that Qamar’s voice was wide-ranging, emotional, powerful, and had varied trills, and while skillful in execution, it possessed spirit of expression and eloquence. She performed trills easily in the high or low parts of her vocal range (mezzo-soprano). Her voice had a kind of coquettishness, good-naturedness, broad-mindedness, and freedom. Her avaz came from her heart.</p>\n\n<p>Umm Kulthum, Qamar’s counterpart in Arab music, said about the characteristics of her own work:</p>\n\n<p>“In my art I have committed myself to sincerity, because people love sincerity. If I do not fully understand the meaning of a word or phrase, I cannot deliver it, because I cannot put it in its special sonic form.”</p>\n\n<p>The sonic space of her throat, mouth, and nasal resonance was such that it presented the pleasant harmonics fused with the fundamental tone in an appealing way. Qamar had naturally grasped and applied the rules of correct breathing while singing.</p>\n\n<p>Some singers of the period after her, those who imitated the voices of other singers by listening to them on radio, could not create a brilliant tone in their voice, because the sound coming from radio loudspeakers, especially ordinary AM radios, lacks sensitivity in the upper frequency range and does not deliver the ring of a brilliant voice. But singers who had worked with masters from the beginning, like Qamar and Rouhangiz, cultivated the natural brilliance of the voice and created the desired style. Qamar was a master and famous in both avaz and tasnif singing.</p>\n\n<h2>Works of Qamar</h2>\n\n<p>The beauty of her voice and her growing fame caused record companies to send representatives to Tehran to record her voice. The recording of her first works, including avaz, tasnif, and metric pieces, began around 1305 solar. Mohammad Ali Amir-Jahed, most of whose tasnifs were performed by Qamar, says:</p>\n\n<p>“Qamar was a very courageous and talented woman. I have not seen the power of her vocal cords in anyone else. The Polydor company had heard that Qamar had a delightful voice, so they wanted to print some records of her. The company wanted one avaz and one tasnif for their record. Qamar was full of avaz, but the tasnifs had to be composed by someone. Therefore I composed tasnifs for her. During the approximately six months that the company’s representative was in Iran, I would compose two tasnifs for her every week, and Qamar would understand the melody of the tasnif that same night and, without any hesitation, perform it the next morning. I have not seen such steadfastness in anyone.” [Mohammad Ali Amir-Jahed, 1352 solar]</p>\n\n<p>The records that Amir-Jahed refers to were recorded in Tehran and duplicated in Germany in 1306 solar and put on the market. Morteza Ney-Davud said that because of Qamar’s beautiful voice, the His Master’s Voice company brought a record-cutting machine to Tehran to record her voice on disc.</p>\n\n<p>That time marked the beginning of the second period of recording Iranian records. The recorded works of Qamar’s voice number about two hundred records, some of which are no longer available. At a time when mass media such as radio and television did not exist in Iran, these records enabled admirers to benefit from Qamar’s voice and, on the other hand, contributed to her fame. In the radio program “Yadi az Honarmandan-e Iran” broadcast on the occasion of Qamar’s passing, it was said about her records: “Unfortunately, the factory that produced these records was destroyed in the world war, and today new records of her voice are rarely found.”</p>\n\n<p>Selected Works of Qamar</p>\n\n<p>Tasnif of Esfahan “Ta Rokhat Moqayyad-e Neqab Ast”;</p>\n\n<p>Tasnif of Dashti “Geryeh Kon” and tasnifs “Ey Dast-e Haqq” and “Cheh Shurha,” all composed by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%81-%d9%82%d8%b2%d9%88%db%8c%d9%86%db%8c/\">Aref Qazvini</a>;</p>\n\n<p>Tasnifs “Dar Bahar-e Omid,” “Cheh Konam az Feraqat,” “Sanama Be Khoda,” and “Ze Man Negaram” with lyrics by Malek al-Sho’ara Bahar and melodies by Darvish Khan; tasnifs “Ghalb-e Madar” and “Asheghi Mehnet Besyar Kesheed” with lyrics by Iraj Mirza;</p>\n\n<p>Tasnifs “Negar-e Man,” “Ruzegar-e Gozashteh,” “Mah-e Man,” “Pardeh-ye Ghaflat,” “Zan dar Jame’eh,” “Atash-e Del,” and “Del-e Khasteh,” mostly with lyrics by Pezhman Bakhtiari and melodies by Morteza Ney-Davud;</p>\n\n<p>and many of Mohammad Ali Amir-Jahed’s tasnifs, mostly with the tar of Arslan Dargahi, such as the tasnif of Shur “Jan-e Man,” Bayat-e Tork “Baharest va Hengam-e Gasht,” Mahur “Keshvar-e Del,” Abu Ata “Aman ze Hejr-e Rokh-e Yar,” Chahargah “Hezar Dastan,” Mahur “Be Gardesh-e Farvardin” (in memory of Gholamhossein Darvish, whom Qamar mentioned in a sentence at the beginning of the record), Shur “Narges-e Mast,” Dashti “Dar Molk-e Iran,” and several other tasnifs.</p>\n\n<p>In addition to these, metric pieces, various masnavis, and avaz in different dastgahs and gushehs of the radif were recorded with Qamar’s voice, mostly accompanied by the tar of Morteza Ney-Davud and Arslan Dargahi, the violin of Musi Ney-Davud and Hossein Yahaghi, and the flute of Ya’qub Khan Rashti. Several records, such as the Mahur record with the tar of Mirza Hassan Khan E’temad Zadeh, were also recorded. In 1331 solar Qamar appeared in several scenes of the film “Madar,” produced by Pars Film, and sang a piece in Afshari avaz with lyrics by Sa’di that begins:</p>\n\n<p>Har Kasi Ra Natavan Goft Ke Saheb-e Nazar Ast — Eshq Bazi Degar va Nafs Parasti Degar Ast</p>\n\n<p>After Qamar’s first stroke, a tape was recorded including her avaz with the tar of Esma’il Kamali in Afshari, which still exists today. Around the early 1350s solar, several of Qamar’s old records were transferred to cassette by private companies and released; because of incorrect speed transfer from disc to tape and failure to observe technical precautions, her voice is heard outside its original state.</p>', '2024-02-20 18:32:20', 0, '2024-12-19 19:10:36', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1557, 'posts', 100, 'en', NULL, 'Ruhollah Khaleqi, prominent player, composer, and researcher of Iranian music', NULL, 'Ruhollah Khaleghi Ruhollah Khaleghi was one of the most influential Iranian composers, conductors, and music theorists of the 20th century. A student of Ali-Naqi Vaziri Ali-Naqi Vaziri, he played a central role in developing Iranian orchestral music by combining traditional Persian modal music with Western harmony and orchestration. He founded the National Music Association and the National Music Conservatory, significantly shaping modern Iranian music education. Among his best-known works are the patriotic anthem “Ey Iran,” performed by Gholamhossein Banan Gholamhossein Banan, as well as compositions like “Mey-e Nab,” “Jame Jam,” and numerous pieces from the Golha radio program. Khaleghi was also an important music historian and author, and he remained active in promoting Iranian national music until his death in 1964.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Ruhollah Khaleqi</h2>\n\n<p>Ruhollah Khaleqi was born in 1285 solar. He completed his secondary education in Tehran and, at the same time in 1302 solar, entered the Vaziri Higher School of Music. Under the supervision of Ali-Naqi Vaziri, he studied music and violin playing and made progress in music theory. After some time, he began teaching at the same school. In 1313 solar, he received a bachelor’s degree in literature from the Higher Teacher Training College and one year later entered the service of the Ministry of Culture. In 1320 solar, he became the deputy director of the Music Department and the Higher School of Music. In 1323 solar, he founded the National Music Association, and in 1328 solar, he established the National Music School. In 1338 solar, due to disillusionment with cooperating with the Fine Arts Administration at the time, he retired at his own request. After that, he took charge of conducting the Golha-ye Rangarang orchestra and, until the end of his life, never abandoned his artistic activities. Khaleqi passed away on 21 Aban 1344 solar.</p>\n\n<p>Khaleqi’s main work in music was composition. Following the style of his teacher Ali-Naqi Vaziri, he arranged his melodies with harmony for orchestra. Through experience and study of Vaziri’s methods, he had found and applied approaches to <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d9%88%d9%86%db%8c/\">harmony</a> for Iranian music. In the method he chose, he was sincere, determined, and thoughtful, and he made great efforts to advance and promote national music.</p>\n\n<h2>Works of Khaleqi</h2>\n\n<p>Khaleqi composed numerous works, the most famous of which are: Rangarang, Naghmaha-ye Mahur, Mey-e Nab, Ah-e Sahar, Buy-e Juy-e Mulian (Golha-ye Rangarang 254), Jam-e Jam, Shab-e Hejran, Hala Chera; anthems such as Pand-e Sa’di, Danesh, Shadi, Honar, Omid, Azerbaijan, Esfahan, Ey Iran, and the Oil Anthem with lyrics by Rahe Mo’ayyeri (on the occasion of the nationalization of oil in 1331 solar); several local melodies; and many tasnifs including: Yar-e Ramideh, Hasti-ye Asheghan, Peyman Shekan, Va’deh-ye Vasal, Laleh-ye Khunin, Naghmeh-ye Nowruzi, Bahar-e Asheq, and Atashin Laleh. Khaleqi’s last melody is titled “Khamush,” which was performed with lyrics by Rahe Mo’ayyeri, the voice of Banan, and the Golha Orchestra under his own direction in Golha-ye Rangarang program No. 237.</p>\n\n<p>Dr. Hossein Gol-Golab said: During the occupation of Iran by the Allied forces (1323 solar), one day while walking along Hedayat Street in Tehran, I saw an American soldier beating an Iranian grocer. I became very upset and went to the National Music Association on the same street to see Khaleqi. I told him with distress what I had witnessed. He also became very upset, and we decided that he would compose an anthem and I would write the lyrics. The result was the anthem “Ey Iran,” which was subsequently broadcast many times on radio to boost the morale of our compatriots. The Golha Orchestra performed it with the voice of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ba%d9%84%d8%a7%d9%85%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%a8%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Banan</a> and choral singing.</p>\n\n<p>He also composed three pieces titled Omid-e Zendegi, Shab-e Javani, and Shab-e Hejran for the film “Tufan-e Zendegi.”</p>\n\n<p>These tasnifs were performed by the orchestra of the National Music Association.</p>\n\n<p>The lyrics of most of Khaleqi’s <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%aa%d8%b5%d9%86%db%8c%d9%81/\">tasnifs</a> were written by Rahe Mo’ayyeri. Khaleqi composed several orchestral pieces, including Rangarang No. 1 in Esfahan and No. 2 in Mahur. Vaziri greatly enjoyed these two pieces and praised them. Khaleqi also wrote a number of pishdaramads, rengs, etudes, various exercises, and several duets and trios for violin and tar. He arranged some old tasnifs by Sheyda and Aref for orchestra and performed them. When he was the conductor of the Golha Orchestra, he also arranged and conducted several of Vaziri’s melodies, such as Shekayat-e Ney, Kharidar-e To, Deltang-e Karavan, and others, with the Golha Orchestra.</p>\n\n<p>Khaleqi wrote numerous articles and books on Iranian music, including: “Nazari beh Musiqi” in two volumes (1316–1317 solar), “Hamahangi-ye Musiqi” (1320 solar), and “Sargozasht-e Musiqi-ye Iran” in three volumes (the first volume on the Qajar period, the second on the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b3%d9%87-%d8%b9%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">Higher School of Music</a> and the activities of Ali-Naqi Vaziri, and the third on the Pahlavi period, edited and completed by Dr. Sasan Sepanta).</p>\n\n<p>All three volumes of “Sargozasht-e Musiqi-ye Iran” were published in one volume with a new edition by the Mahoor Institute in 1381 solar.</p>\n\n<p>Most of his articles were published in the magazines “Musiq-e Iran,” “Musiqi,” and “Payam-e Novin.” Most of Khaleqi’s melodies were performed by the orchestra of the National Music Association, Radio Orchestra No. 1, the Golha Orchestra, and the National Music School Orchestra with the voices of Abdolali Vaziri, Banan, Marziyeh, and others.</p>\n\n<p>Several of Khaleqi’s melodies were re-performed in 1370 solar with the voice of Kaveh Deylami (a student of Banan) and released on cassette and CD.</p>\n\n<p>He wrote and presented radio programs such as “Saz va Sokhan” (introducing the dastgahs of Iranian music) and “Yadi az Honarmandan.”</p>\n\n<h2>Khaleqi’s Views</h2>\n\n<p>Khaleqi believed that rhythm in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a> creates movement, and when a musical piece is written in polyphony, its sadness, melancholy, and monotony are reduced. The first book on the principles of Western harmony was translated and presented by him (1320 solar). At the end of this book he wrote:</p>\n\n<p>“Harmonizing melodies that belong to Iranian maqams and also contain quarter-tone intervals has another path after studying what has been written in this book. I hope that the publication of the book that my esteemed teacher Mr. Ali-Naqi Vaziri has written on this subject — and which he is currently preparing for print — will guide and assist those seeking this path, so that with its help they may succeed in presenting Iranian music in a proper and worthy manner to people of taste and art.”</p>\n\n<p>Regarding Rangarang No. 1 (<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%a7%d8%b5%d9%81%d9%87%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Bayat-e Esfahan</a>), which he had composed, he said:</p>\n\n<p>“My intention was to compose such a piece on each of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b4%d9%86%d8%a7%d8%b3%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2%d8%b4%d9%86%d8%a7%d8%b3%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Iranian music dastgahs</a> that could serve as a model for work, just as the sonata is the basis for students in European music. This too could be considered a model for work.”</p>\n\n<p>Regarding the harmonization of Iranian melodies, he believed that counterpoint is more suitable for Iranian music than harmony, provided that the main melody is not sacrificed to the secondary voices (harmony or counterpoint). He called the violin the king of the orchestra and said:</p>\n\n<p>“It is an instrument that, while brightening the sound of the orchestra, reflects the qualities of our national music very well. However, to preserve the brilliance of its sound, one should avoid lowering its tuning, because otherwise its sound becomes weak. Players of violin, tar, and other Iranian instruments should get used to playing pieces, as much as possible, without changing the tuning (lowering it) during ensemble performance. The moods of the pieces must be properly observed. In ensemble playing, passages should be performed without accents or trills; if the execution of a passage with accents is required, it should be assigned to one player.”</p>\n\n<p>Dr. Sasan Sepanta says:</p>\n\n<p>“As far as I remember, in the large orchestra he would assign certain passages to <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ad%d8%b3%d9%8a%d9%86-%d8%b9%d9%84%d9%89-%d9%88%d8%b2%d9%8a%d8%b1%d9%89-%d8%aa%d8%a8%d8%a7%d8%b1/\">Hossein Ali Vaziri Tabar</a>, the unparalleled qarnei player. He did not approve of long chaharmezrabs, especially for bowed instruments such as the violin, and recommended short chaharmezrabs. Khaleqi believed that the tombak should not be used to fill silences in ensemble playing, and one should avoid playing unnecessary strokes beyond the notes of the melody.</p>\n\n<p>He emphasized that the tombak should not seek to show off and should only maintain the rhythm, and that the tombak player should sit farther back. He considered excessive ornaments on the tombak to be disruptive.</p>\n\n<p>I was Khaleqi’s student in music for about ten years, and this relationship turned into a close friendship between us that continued until the end of his life.”</p>', '2024-02-21 03:32:54', 0, '2024-12-19 19:10:52', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1558, 'posts', 101, 'en', NULL, 'Gholam-Hossein Banan, influential singer of Iranian music', NULL, 'Gholamhossein Banan Gholamhossein Banan was one of the most prominent Iranian classical vocalists of the 20th century. Born into a cultured family in Tehran, he was introduced to Persian music early and trained under several masters before beginning his professional career. With the support of Ruhollah Khaleghi Ruhollah Khaleghi and approval from Ali-Naqi Vaziri Ali-Naqi Vaziri, he joined Iranian Radio and became a key performer in orchestras and the influential “Golha” (Flowers) radio program. Banan was known for his warm, refined, and controlled voice, as well as his mastery of Persian modal music (radif) and sensitive interpretation of poetry. His performances of works such as “Deylaman,” “Hala Chera,” and compositions by Vaziri and Khaleghi remain among the most iconic recordings in Iranian music history.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Gholamhossein Banan</h2>\n\n<p>Gholamhossein Banan, son of Karim Banan al-Dowleh, was born in Ordibehesht 1290 solar in Tehran. His father was a skilled calligrapher among the mostowfis and had some familiarity with music and also sang. His mother played the piano, and his sisters learned tar with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%b1%d8%aa%d8%b6%db%8c-%d9%86%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%88%d8%af/\">Morteza Ney-Davud</a>. Through his acquaintance with the Banan family, Ney-Davud recognized Banan’s pleasant voice and talent and encouraged him to learn and sing avaz.</p>\n\n<p>Because Banan al-Dowleh disliked singing in public, he would sometimes record his own voice on the phonograph he kept at home. From childhood and adolescence, in his father’s absence, Banan would listen to and imitate his father’s voice from the phonograph. After some time, he studied with Zia’ al-Zakerin Rasa’i and became familiar with the dastgahs of Iranian music. He then continued learning avaz with Naser Seif. In 1315 solar, he was employed as an archivist in the General Department of Agriculture. Some time later, he was transferred to the Iran Bar Company in Ahvaz and subsequently to the General Department of Grain and Bread.</p>\n\n<p>In 1321 solar, Abdolali Vaziri introduced Banan to Ruhollah Khaleqi, who was then the deputy director of the General Music Department of the country. <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d9%86%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Ali-Naqi Vaziri</a>, who was related to Banan by family, approved of his pleasant voice. Banan went to the radio administration to take the vocal examination.</p>\n\n<p>In the examination session, in addition to Khaleqi, Abolhasan Saba was also present. They asked Banan to sing in Segah. He sang the daramad of Segah while Saba accompanied him on violin. Khaleqi signaled Saba to stop playing and asked Banan to sing the gusheh of Hesār. Banan sang it and returned to Segah. He passed the test and began his collaboration with the modern radio orchestra, which had been formed under the direction of Ali-Naqi Vaziri.</p>\n\n<p>After founding the National Music Association, Khaleqi invited Banan to cooperate with its orchestra. Banan participated in the association’s concerts under Khaleqi’s direction and performed as a vocalist.</p>\n\n<p>Banan’s avaz, accompanied by artists such as Hossein Sanjari (tar), was heard on radio from 1321 solar. In 1327 solar, the orchestra of the National Music Association under Khaleqi’s direction performed the music for the film “Tufan-e Zendegi,” produced by Mitra Film and directed by Ali Daryabeigi. Banan sang several pieces, including “Deylaman” in Dashti according to <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%84%d8%ad%d8%b3%d9%86-%d8%b5%d8%a8%d8%a7/\">Saba</a>’s version, with the orchestra. For many years he collaborated with Radio Orchestra No. 1 under the direction of Javad Ma’rufi, and many of his performances were recorded in the early Golha-ye Rangarang and Golha-ye Javidan and Barg-e Sabz programs.</p>\n\n<p>Banan was a singer with strong character who placed great emphasis on preserving the dignity of this art and its worthy artists. In practice, he himself was an excellent example of a deserving artist. He passed away on 8 Esfand 1364 solar in Tehran.</p>\n\n<h2>The Style of Banan</h2>\n\n<p>Banan had a thorough knowledge of the radif of Iranian music. He showed excellent taste and skill in combining poetry with vocal music, in choosing suitable poems, and in matching them to the appropriate dastgah or gusheh. He correctly observed the meter of the piece, had a precise ear, and performed compound singing (morkab-khani) programs. His voice had a warm and mature timbre that deeply moved the listener. Banan was well aware of the moods of the measured pieces composed by master Iranian composers and, through his excellent performance of them, left behind enduring works.</p>\n\n<p>Some of the melodies Banan sang present unusual modulations that are difficult and require great talent and skill from the singer. For example, the unusual change of mode from <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%a7%d8%b5%d9%81%d9%87%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Bayat-e Esfahan</a> to Segah in the piece “Shekayat-e Ney” by Ali-Naqi Vaziri, or the innovative modulation from Mahur Sol in the piece “Rah-e Eshgh” by the same composer to Delkash of Shur on “La” (instead of Delkash of Shur on “Re”), demonstrate Banan’s precision, sensitive ear, and skill.</p>\n\n<p>His voice shows no sign of constriction or strain on the vocal cords, shouting, or forcing the vocal apparatus. Unlike some singers, he did not abuse his throat, and his voice was naturally and comfortably developed. This style and characteristic was appreciated by Ali-Naqi Vaziri and proved suitable for performing his works and those of Khaleqi.</p>\n\n<p>Banan’s avaz in his final performances retained the same warmth and charm as in his youthful recordings. During the period of Banan’s fame, when there were only a few qualified singers and most radio programs were entertainment-oriented and aimed at popular taste, he continued to follow the proper and worthy path in that environment and never lowered the level of his work for the sake of fleeting and false fame.</p>\n\n<p>A warm and flexible voice, pleasant and melodious tone, control of the voice and vocal apparatus in performing <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%84%d9%88%d8%af%db%8c/\">melody</a>, understanding of the poetic content and the ability to convey it to the listener, appropriate delivery, clear and measured trills, and artistic creativity are among the characteristics of Banan’s singing.</p>\n\n<p>No uncontrolled rise or fall (climax or cadence) in the vocal figures or varied trills (oral, nasal, etc.) is observed in his avaz. Based on the style of the master singers (such as Seyed Rahim), Banan would use trills after finishing a hemistich or couplet. If, rarely, short trills appear on the vowels of words between hemistichs in his tasnifs or avaz, they are appropriately employed to fit the poetry to the meter.</p>\n\n<p>The tone applied to the syllables of the words is done with delicacy and excellent taste. Overall, the melody of speech (intonation), prosodic features, and the use of pauses — both in tasnif and in non-metric dastgah singing — are used in Banan’s avaz while following well-formed patterns of the Persian language and paying attention to the integration of the poetic message’s meaning.</p>\n\n<p>Due to his mastery of the dastgahs of Iranian music and their gushehs, Banan skillfully sang certain rarely used gushehs that were less common in contemporary avaz. He was also somewhat familiar with playing instruments such as the organ and piano. Thanks to his control over his voice, he could sustain a desired note with sufficient duration without distortion and match the pitch of his avaz to the instrument or orchestra.</p>\n\n<p>According to Dr. Sasan Sepanta’s laboratory measurements, based on audio data from Banan’s earliest youthful works on gramophone records (recorded around 1325 solar) up to the last works he collected over approximately twenty years, his vocal range falls within the tenor register, covering from 131 to 494 Hz. The usual and comfortable range for his trills was generally around 260 Hz.</p>\n\n<h2>Works of Gholamhossein Banan</h2>\n\n<p>Among the numerous works of Gholamhossein Banan that were recorded on disc, the following can be mentioned as examples:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>“Cheh Shurha” by Aref on a His Master’s Voice record with the Mansouri Orchestra, catalog number OHB-1136-37.RR28;</li>\n  <li>“Deylaman” according to Abolhasan Saba’s version with Javad Ma’rufi’s orchestra, catalog number OHB-1149-2.RR50;</li>\n  <li>and “Gham-e Eshgh” by Hossein Sanjari with Javad Ma’rufi’s orchestra, catalog number OHB-1149-1.RR50.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>All of these are among Banan’s early works and were recorded during his youth (around 1325–1326 solar).</p>\n\n<p>Banan’s later works belong to the years after 1336 solar. Most were recorded with Radio Orchestra No. 1 and the Golha Orchestra, and some were released on cassette tapes by private companies around 1350 solar.</p>\n\n<p>Among these works are melodies by Vaziri such as “Shekayat-e Ney” with lyrics by Molavi, “Rah-e Eshgh” in Mahur with lyrics by Hossein Gol-Golab, “Bolbol-e Mast” in Dashti with lyrics by the same poet, “Nimshab” with lyrics by Hafez, “Moshtaq va Parishan” with lyrics by Sa’di in Abu Ata, and melodies by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a> such as “Ah-e Sahar,” “Mey-e Nab,” “Naghmaha-ye Mahur,” “Buy-e Juy-e Mulian” (with the voice of Marziyeh), “Shab-e Hejran,” “Khamush,” “Peyman Shekan,” “Laleh-ye Khunin,” as well as melodies by Morteza Mahjubi, Musa Ma’rufi, Nasrollah Zarrinpanjeh, Akbar Mohseni, and Javad Ma’rufi.</p>\n\n<p>Several Golha-ye Javidan radio programs featuring Banan’s voice, accompanied by Morteza Mahjubi and Javad Ma’rufi on piano, Reza Varzandeh on santur, Mehdi Khaladi, Ali Tajvidi, and Mahmoud Tajbakhsh on violin, Lotfollah Majd and Jalil Shahnaz on tar, and Hassan Kasa’i on ney, were recorded.</p>\n\n<p>Banan also participated in some Golha-ye Rangarang radio programs, including:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Golha-ye Rangarang 126 (Dashti, “Navay-e Ney” by Mahjubi),</li>\n  <li>Golha-ye Rangarang 174 (Segah, “Be Yad-e Saba”),</li>\n  <li>Golha-ye Rangarang 176 (“Didi Ey Mah”),</li>\n  <li>Golha-ye Rangarang 210 (Busalik, “Hala Chera” by Khaleqi),</li>\n  <li>Golha-ye Rangarang 212 (Mahur, “Del Miravad ze Dastam” by Khaleqi),</li>\n  <li>Golha-ye Rangarang 215 (Mahur, “Kharidar-e To” by Vaziri),</li>\n  <li>Golha-ye Rangarang 217 (Deylaman, “Chenan dar Qeid-e Mehrat Pay Bandam”),</li>\n  <li>Golha-ye Rangarang 224 (Esfahan, “Ta Bahar-e Delneshin” by Khaleqi),</li>\n  <li>Golha-ye Rangarang 230 (Dashti, “Morgh-e Hagh”),</li>\n  <li>Golha-ye Rangarang 232 (Dashti, “Navay-e Ney” by Mahjubi),</li>\n  <li>Golha-ye Rangarang 234 (“Cheh Shabha”),</li>\n  <li>Golha-ye Rangarang 249 (“Cheh Shurha” by Aref),</li>\n  <li>Golha-ye Rangarang 250 (Dashti, “Geryeh Kon” by Aref),</li>\n  <li>Golha-ye Rangarang 252 (“Deltang” by Vaziri),</li>\n  <li>Golha-ye Rangarang 254 (Esfahan, “Buy-e Juy-e Mulian” by Khaleqi), and</li>\n  <li>Golha-ye Rangarang 267 (Esfahan, “Dishab be Sil-e Ashk” by Khaleqi).</li>\n</ul>', '2024-02-21 17:56:47', 0, '2024-12-19 19:11:07', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1559, 'posts', 102, 'en', NULL, 'Hassan Kasaie | Prominent ney player, radif master nicknamed the complete master', NULL, 'Hasan Kassai was one of the most influential masters of the ney in Persian classical music, born in Isfahan. From an early age he was exposed to music and later studied under masters such as Mehdi Navaei and Abolhassan Saba Abolhassan Saba. He gained national recognition through performances on Iranian radio’s “Golha” program and collaborations with major artists including Gholamhossein Banan Gholamhossein Banan and Jalil Shahnaz Jalil Shahnaz. Kassai was known for his highly refined ney technique, producing a clear, warm, and expressive tone across different registers. His mastery of Persian radif, improvisation, and teaching played a key role in preserving and advancing traditional ney performance.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Hassan Kasa’i</h2>\n\n<p>Hassan Kasa’i was born in 1307 solar into a merchant family in Isfahan. His father had connections with some artists, and from childhood Kasa’i became familiar with their music and singing at home. From a young age he had a pleasant voice, and upon hearing the sound of a passerby’s ney, he became interested in this instrument. He studied it with Mehdi Navai (a student of Nayeb Asadollah). With his natural talent and persistent effort, Hassan Kasa’i elevated the art of ney playing to a masterful and delightful level.</p>\n\n<p>He greatly benefited from the teachings of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%84%d8%ad%d8%b3%d9%86-%d8%b5%d8%a8%d8%a7/\">Abolhasan Saba</a> and participated in the early Golha radio programs. The delightful sound of his ney, accompanying singers such as Adib Khansari, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ba%d9%84%d8%a7%d9%85%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%a8%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Gholamhossein Banan</a>, Hossein Qavami, and others, reached music lovers. Diverse recordings of his ney duets with the pleasant tar of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ac%d9%84%db%8c%d9%84-%d8%b4%d9%87%d9%86%d8%a7%d8%b2/\">Jalil Shahnaz</a> and the resonant voice of Taj Esfahani exist, as well as educational programs on the radif of Iranian music that were recorded for radio. In addition to the ney, Hassan Kasa’i also played the setar. His performances are counted among the outstanding works of Iranian music.</p>\n\n<h2>The Style of Hassan Kasa’i</h2>\n\n<p>The ney is one of the indigenous wind instruments of Iran. It is rich in terms of sound production and the number of acoustic harmonics, producing a pleasant and delicate tone. Generating desirable sounds with proper tone and executing the correct musical intervals on this instrument is very challenging. The ney has six holes, and with these holes all the intervals of Iranian music must be performed. Sometimes, to play a period or a figure from the gushehs of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music dastgahs</a>, two or three fingers must partially or approximately one-quarter cover the holes to produce the correct pitch.</p>\n\n<p>One of the characteristics of Kasa’i’s playing is his excellent use of the instrument to create a transparent and pleasing tone when performing different notes in both the low and high registers. The tone of his instrument in the low register is warm and rich in desirable harmonics. Although playing the ney in the low register requires more breath than in the high register (higher frequencies), Master Hassan Kasa’i’s instrument remains steady and clear in this range. In the high register (higher frequencies), he performed musical phrases with lower sound intensity (fewer decibels), resulting in greater delicacy and refinement in the instrument’s tone production.</p>\n\n<p>Clarity of execution and precision are also evident in his fast staccato notes. In performing the notes (musical intervals), Kasa’i’s instrument is accurate, and like Abolhasan Saba, he did not believe in fast vibratos in playing. According to Dr. Sasan Sepanta’s laboratory measurements, Kasa’i’s vibratos, like those of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%86%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%a8-%d8%a7%d8%b3%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Nayeb Asadollah</a> and Saba, range between one and a half to two oscillations (depending on the musical texture).</p>\n\n<p>Another characteristic of his playing is the appropriate use of silence between figures or <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%be%d8%b1%db%8c%d9%88%d8%af/\">periods</a> of performance. The gushehs of the Iranian music radif that he performs are a selection of beautiful and authentic melodies. Hassan Kasa’i shows variety and creativity in responding to the singer and, to avoid monotony that harms the music, he uses nuance and shading effectively.</p>', '2024-02-22 15:43:49', 0, '2024-12-19 19:11:21', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1560, 'posts', 103, 'en', NULL, 'Nayeb Asadollah Khan, ney player of the Qajar period', NULL, 'Nayeb Asadollah Khan was a prominent ney player of the Qajar era, born around 1845. He developed a distinctive and influential style of ney performance and collaborated with many court musicians of his time. His playing was characterized by strong breath control, advanced technique, and deep emotional expression. He performed in both court and spiritual gatherings and had a lasting influence on later generations of ney players. He spent his final years in Isfahan, where he passed away around 1925.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Nayeb Asadollah Khan</h2>\n\n<p>Nayeb Asadollah Khan is one of the famous musicians of the Naser al-Din Shah era and was born around 1224 solar. His sensitive ear, agile fingers, talent, and natural aptitude enabled him to produce musical sounds easily on the ney. As Khaleqi writes, he even played some European melodies on the ney. [Khaleqi, 1333 solar]</p>\n\n<p>Nayeb Asadollah participated in ensemble playing with prominent masters of the Naser al-Din Shah period, such as <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b5%d8%a7%d8%af%d9%82-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%86-%d8%b3%d9%8f%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%85%d9%84%da%a9/\">Sorour al-Molk</a> and Aqa Hossein-Qoli. His ney playing, with its loud and strong sound, is clearly heard in ensemble performances. Nayeb played with sustained breath and agile fingers and had complete mastery over the radif and the gushehs of avaz. He performed melodic lines, ornamental phrases, and ascending and descending accents with skill and ease on the ney. As it is recounted, he was a modest and good-natured man who usually carried a short ney in the left inner pocket of his sardari coat and played without ceremony.</p>\n\n<p>In addition to performing in Naser al-Din Shah’s court ensemble, he performed in the ensembles of Ali Asghar Khan Atabak, Doust Mohammad Khan, Doust Ali Khan Moa’yyer al-Mamalek, and other notables of the time such as Zell al-Soltan and his sons. In the final years of the Qajar period, Nayeb returned to Isfahan. Doust Ali Moa’yyer al-Mamalek, who traveled to Isfahan around 1303 solar, met him there. Like most famous musicians of the Naser al-Din Shah era, Nayeb had withdrawn due to the ingratitude and poor taste of Mozaffar al-Din Shah and the unrest and disturbances during Mohammad Ali Shah’s reign. In his old age, life became difficult for him, and he finally passed away around 1304 solar in Isfahan.</p>\n\n<p>Among Nayeb Asadollah’s students, one can mention Abdolkhaleq Esfahani and Mehdi Navai, of whom a few records remain. Master Jalal Homayi noted that Nayeb Asadollah was a student of Hossein-Qoli, the ney player from Isfahan. He would not participate in entertainment gatherings (even when offered large sums of money) and would not play there, but he would perform for free in ta’zieh performances and would even play the ney at the bedside of the sick to improve their condition. In reality, his ney playing depended on his mood and inner desire.</p>\n\n<p>Master Hossein-Qoli, the ney player from Isfahan, was a hospitable, dervish-like man who kept an open table. He lived until around 1274 solar. Master Homayi’s maternal uncle, Aqa Mohammad Mehdi Tajer, suffered from a severe, persistent fever and typhoid. According to the master, the doctors had given up on him. As a last resort, they brought Nayeb Asadollah, who sat at the patient’s bedside and began playing the ney. After some time, the patient regained consciousness, began to cry, gradually sat up, and his illness started to improve.</p>\n\n<p>Master Homayi participated in a gathering with friends of Nayeb Asadollah. Nayeb played the gusheh of Masnavi on the ney, and the famous singer Seyed Rahim recited the masnavi “Beshno az Ney Chun Hekayat Mikonad” with a moving voice. Master Homayi said that in those moments he felt the meaning of spiritual immersion and annihilation of the self from the warm breath of those masters of the age.</p>\n\n<p>According to one of the elderly contemporaries of Nayeb Asadollah, his music and instrument had an effect even on animals and sheep. Nayeb would wear a fur hat or sometimes a nightcap and mostly appeared in gatherings of his friends. The sound of his ney was a soothing balm for the hearts of the afflicted. In a few of these spiritual gatherings, he allowed his performance to be privately recorded on wax cylinders of the phonograph. Precious samples of Nayeb’s ney with the voice of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b3%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%b1%d8%ad%db%8c%d9%85-%d8%a7%d8%b5%d9%81%d9%87%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Seyed Abdolrahim Esfahani</a> (the master of avaz) exist, which Dr. Sasan Sepanta restored about forty years ago.</p>\n\n<p>The spiritual aspects of these gatherings are reflected in the music of some of the cylinders. For example, at the beginning of one wax cylinder recorded by Doust Mohammad Khan Moa’yyer al-Mamalek, the following can be heard: “The night of the ninth of the month of Ramadan, one thousand three hundred and sixteen AH [Friday night, 21 January 1899 CE]. ‘You are the son of the noble and I am the son of the insightful; we are brothers of the fortunate.’ Nayeb Asadollah, the soul of souls, performs the Indian-style melody on the surface of the earth!”</p>\n\n<h2>The Style of Nayeb Asadollah</h2>\n\n<p>Nayeb Asadollah possessed musical innovation and creativity. Due to his precision, diligence, and natural talent, he developed a style of ney playing that deeply moved the listener. Playing the ney with the teeth — placing it between the two front upper teeth and resting the tongue on the edge of the ney to produce sound — was the customary and characteristic method of the Isfahan school of ney playing.</p>\n\n<p>Nayeb Asadollah’s style follows this method, and it is probably one of his innovations. In comparison with other methods of playing the ney in countries such as the Soviet Union, Turkey, China, India, Arabia, and Pakistan — where the ney is played with the lips — in the Isfahan style the mouth is half open and the lips vibrate so that, when necessary, they can give character to the sound of the note. This style was and remains specific to Isfahan.</p>\n\n<p>Considering the surviving recordings of Nayeb Asadollah’s playing that Dr. Sasan Sepanta extracted from wax cylinders or heard from early gramophone records, Nayeb had a precise musical ear and sufficient breath control. He employed techniques that expanded the expressive range of the ney. He was skilled and tasteful in responding to the singer and in performing the varied trills and ornaments of the avaz. He was precise in maintaining the meter of the melody and followed the style of Mirza Abdollah and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%8a%d8%b1%d8%b2%d8%a7-%d8%ad%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a8-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%b3%d9%85%d8%a7%d8%b9-%d8%ad%d8%b6%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Sama’ Hozur</a> in form. That is, after performing several gushehs of the radif — especially when playing with a singer — at the appropriate moment he would conclude the musical phrase with a suitable metric piece.</p>\n\n<p>Considering the time in which he lived, his playing contains innovative points. For example, when responding to the singer, instead of merely repeating the avaz with the instrument, he would take a figure and develop it to add variety to the piece. He would play musical phrases in upper and lower octaves and make them varied and appealing. Sometimes he would divide a <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ac%d9%85%d9%84%d9%87/\">musical phrase</a> into two parts and perform the beginning in one octave and the ending in a higher or lower octave.</p>\n\n<p>The execution of accents and ornamental notes in his playing is balanced, and these decorations do not overshadow the overall movement and melodic design. In some of his works, the singer (Hossein Ta’zieh-khan) would suddenly begin singing in the middle of a beautiful musical phrase that Nayeb was playing, or during the performance of a metric piece, the percussionist would create interference with untimely strikes. The accents he performed, as heard in the Bidad record, are orderly and refined, and he showed artistic creativity in accompanying the singer.</p>\n\n<p>His playing style is calm, focused, and controlled, with mastery over the fingers. He played without tension, with ease, tranquility, and agility. In Nayeb Asadollah’s style, technique served emotional expression. In accordance with the performance of a passage or musical phrase, the breath (exhalation) was managed proportionally to its duration, with gradual exhalation that never faltered until the end of the phrase, ensuring the appropriate sustain of the notes.</p>\n\n<h2>Recorded Works of Nayeb Asadollah’s Ney</h2>\n\n<p>A: Private recordings on wax cylinders of the phonograph in the second decade of the fourteenth AH century, corresponding to the final years of the nineteenth century CE.</p>\n\n<p>B: EM.I 25 cm records</p>\n\n<p>4-12004 – Bayat-e Tork, “Bar-e Feraq-e Dustan,” with the voice of Aqa Hossein Ta’zieh-khan</p>\n\n<p>4-12005 – Bayat-e Esfahan, tasnif “Kordi,” with the voice of Aqa Hossein Ta’zieh-khan</p>\n\n<p>4-12006 – Dashti, tasnif “Gilaki,” with reng, with the voice of Aqa Hossein Ta’zieh-khan</p>\n\n<p>4-12007 – Rak, tasnif “Nemidanam ba Eshq,” with the voice of Aqa Hossein Ta’zieh-khan</p>\n\n<p>4-12008 – Avaz Abu Eytam (Abu Ata), tasnif “Aqim Mandeh Ast,” with the voice of Aqa Hossein Ta’zieh-khan</p>\n\n<p>4-12009 – Bidad, tasnif “Mozhdeh be Bolbol Dehid Fasle Bahar Amadeh,” Aqa Hossein Ta’zieh-khan</p>\n\n<p>4-12010 – Segah Mokhalef, tasnif “Ala Ey Saqi-ye Mahvash,” Aqa Hossein Ta’zieh-khan</p>', '2024-02-22 19:09:20', 0, '2024-12-19 19:03:36', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1561, 'posts', 104, 'en', NULL, 'Mohammad-Sadegh Khan Sorour-ol-Molk, santur player of the Naseri period', NULL, 'Mohammad Sadegh Khan Soroor-ol-Molk was a prominent musician of the Qajar era during the reign of Naser al-Din Shah. He served as the head of court musicians and the royal ceremonial orchestra. He was skilled in playing the santur, setar, and piano, and was known for his precise ear, creativity, and improvisational mastery. He was also noted for his innovative experiments in tuning instruments and producing sound, such as tuning piano systems and glass objects. His santur playing was characterized by fast, controlled plectrum techniques, strong sound projection, and well-structured musical phrases. He taught many musicians of his time, and several musical pieces and radif sections are attributed to him, securing his important place in Iranian classical music history.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Mohammad Sadeq Khan Sorour al-Molk</h2>\n\n<p>Mohammad Sadeq Khan Sorour al-Molk was the son of Mirza Abdolmotalleb. In the late years of Naser al-Din Shah’s reign, he received the title Sorour al-Molk. He was in charge of the court musicians and oversaw the naqqareh-khaneh (kettledrum ensemble). He first learned kamancheh from his father and then studied music with Santur Khan. He also played the setar. Sorour al-Molk was familiar with the tuning and playing method of the first pianos that arrived in Iran. He would create a <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">musical scale</a> by adding or removing water from crystal bowls and play on them with santur mallets to produce sound. This method was also known in ancient times as “ta’āt” and “kasāt.”</p>\n\n<p>Among Mohammad Sadeq Khan’s students, one can name Esmat al-Dowleh (daughter of Naser al-Din Shah, through the mediation of Tabsom, the court maid) and Habibollah Shahrdar (Moshir Homayun) in piano; <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%8a%d8%b1%d8%b2%d8%a7-%d8%ad%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a8-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%b3%d9%85%d8%a7%d8%b9-%d8%ad%d8%b6%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Mirza Habib Sama’ Hozur</a> in santur and percussion; and Mehdi Solhi in setar. Ekram al-Dowleh, daughter of Saheb Divan Shirazi and wife of one of Azod al-Molk’s sons (Nayeb al-Saltaneh), was among his vocal students. The nursemaid’s daughter of this Ekram al-Dowleh was the famous and sensitive singer Parvaneh, who later recorded several records with her sorrowful voice accompanied by Habib Samaei’s santur. She learned singing from Ekram al-Dowleh (also referred to as Ekram al-Saltaneh).</p>\n\n<p>In his later years, Mohammad Sadeq Khan lost the ability to walk due to rheumatism. He remained alive until the final years of Mozaffar al-Din Shah’s reign. Due to a grievance with the court, he also spent some time in Yazd.</p>\n\n<h2>The Style of Mohammad Sadeq Khan</h2>\n\n<p>Mohammad Sadeq Khan was the leader of the ensemble in Naser al-Din Shah’s court, and other famous musicians of the time, such as <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87/\">Mirza Abdollah</a>, Aqa Hossein-Qoli, and Nayeb Asadollah, acknowledged his mastery. The accounts of him tuning the piano for the Shur scale and adjusting the volume of water in crystal bowls demonstrate his precision and sensitive ear. In improvisation, he was a master and showed creativity and innovation.</p>\n\n<p>The pieces known as “Mohammad Sadeq Khani” and “Sorour al-Molki,” which are played in Shur, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%b9%d8%b7%d8%a7/\">Abu Ata</a>, and Bayat-e Tork, are attributed to him. From the samples of Mohammad Sadeq Khan’s santur playing that Dr. Sasan Sepanta extracted and listened to from wax cylinders, it is evident that his santur was precisely tuned and had a pleasant, resonant tone.</p>\n\n<p>His rapid tremolos (riz) were continuous and fast, and with single left-hand strokes on the yellow (bass) strings of the santur, he would play the lower-octave single notes in a rhythmic manner that complemented the right-hand tremolos. This indicates excellent control and coordination between his left and right hands while maintaining meter. His musical phrases were measured, orderly, and varied, and his santur had a strong, resonant sound. He would play phrases with rapid tremolos and answer them with single left-hand strokes. When playing in ensemble, the volume of his instrument would dominate the other instruments in the group.</p>\n\n<p>The bass register of the santur (yellow strings), due to the presence of higher-pitched instruments such as the ney (especially the short ney of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%86%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%a8-%d8%a7%d8%b3%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Nayeb Asadollah</a>) and to some extent the tar, enriched the sonic foundation of the ensemble. It also served as the rhythmic foundation for metric pieces, acting as the reference pulse for the group.</p>\n\n<p>Among his students, the playing style of Hassan Khan (Hassan Santuri), the santur player of Naser al-Din Shah’s era, is closest to Mohammad Sadeq Khan’s mallet technique. Another aspect of Mohammad Sadeq Khan’s santur style was placing a handkerchief over the strings, which was probably influenced by playing near Naser al-Din Shah’s sleeping quarters, where a softer and quieter sound was required.</p>\n\n<h2>Works of Mohammad Sadeq Khan</h2>\n\n<h3>Published Works</h3>\n\n<p>The gusheh “Mohammad Sadeq Khani” — gusheh 23 of Dastgah-e Shur, gusheh 7 of the Naghmeh of Dastan al-Arab (Abu Ata), gusheh 8 of the Naghmeh of Bayat-e Tork, and gusheh 35 in Oshshaq of Dastgah-e Homayun. [The Radif of the Seven Dastgahs by Musa Ma’rufi, 1342 solar]</p>\n\n<p>Mehdi Qoli Hedayat wrote that Mohammad Sadeq Khan had a divertimento consisting of Hajiani and Bidkani avaz. [Hedayat, 1317 solar]</p>\n\n<p>Coincidentally, one of the recorded works from Mohammad Sadeq Khan’s santur on wax cylinders, accompanied by Nayeb Asadollah’s ney and Aqa Hossein-Qoli’s tar with the singing of Sadeq Khan Navab, was performed in this very Hajiani gusheh.</p>\n\n<h3>Recorded Works</h3>\n\n<p>The recorded works of Mohammad Sadeq Khan’s santur were made on personal wax cylinders of the phonograph during friendly gatherings in the second decade of the fourteenth AH century, corresponding to the final years of the nineteenth century CE.</p>', '2024-02-23 20:08:30', 0, '2024-12-19 19:03:55', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1562, 'posts', 105, 'en', NULL, 'Mirza Habibollah Sama\' Hozur, santur player of the Naseri period', NULL, 'Mirza Habibollah Sama‘-Hozour was a prominent santur player of the late Qajar era, born into a musical family and trained under Mohammad Sadegh Khan Soroor-ol-Molk. Beyond his technical mastery, he was known for his spiritual character and deep respect for music as a sacred art, performing mainly in intimate and meaningful gatherings. His playing style emphasized precision, calmness, and clarity, avoiding excessive ornamentation and focusing on the pure expression of melody and emotional depth. His recorded legacy reflects a mystical approach to music, where performance is closely tied to spiritual discipline and inner devotion.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Mirza Habibollah Sama’ Hozur</h2>\n\n<p>Mirza Habibollah Sama’ Hozur was born around 1268 AH. His father, Aqa Mirza Gholam Hossein, known as Aqa Jan Santuri, was a student of Mirza Abdolmotalleb, the father of Sorour al-Molk. Aqa Jan had built an instrument similar to the kamancheh called “Majles Ara,” which had additional strings and a long neck. His mother was known as Koukab Sabilo. Both of Sama’ Hozur’s parents were among the singers and musicians in the ensemble of Haji Khan Moqaddam Hajeb al-Dowleh, the chief attendant at Naser al-Din Shah’s court. Hajeb al-Dowleh was a robust, handsome, and witty man whose residence was in Baharestan (the former location of the National Consultative Assembly). Sama’ Hozur was born in this very house.</p>\n\n<p>After the death of Mirza Gholam Hossein, his son Habib, titled Sama’ Hozur, studied percussion and then santur with Mirza <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b5%d8%a7%d8%af%d9%82-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%86-%d8%b3%d9%8f%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%85%d9%84%da%a9/\">Mohammad Sadeq Khan Sorour al-Molk</a>. Habib Santuri (Sama’ Hozur) was a tall, fair-skinned man with strong arms. He was considered one of the leading wrestlers of his time. [Moa’yyer al-Mamalek, 1361 solar]</p>\n\n<p>As Moa’yyer al-Mamalek wrote, after coming to power, he himself was also a master of the santur, and he had taught his two young sons to play percussion so they could accompany his instrument. One of them was Habib II, known as “Samaei.” Sama’ Hozur had a sister named Zivar Soltan, who later received the title Andalib al-Saltaneh. When she sang at the peak of her six-dang voice, her mouth barely opened. Andalib al-Saltaneh also played the hand organ and was in the ensemble of Sho’a al-Saltaneh, son of Mozaffar al-Din Shah.</p>\n\n<p>Doust Ali Khan Moa’yyer al-Mamalek, son of Esmat al-Dowleh and grandson of Naser al-Din Shah, who had close relationships with many luminaries of that era and published some of his memoirs, told Dr. Sasan Sepanta about Mirza Habib Sama’ Hozur: “Sama’ Hozur was a musician with soundness of body and soul. Unlike some musicians, he had a strong sense of dignity and would not easily play for just anyone. He attached importance and respect to the gathering in which he performed.”</p>\n\n<p>Dr. Sasan Sepanta, having heard samples of Sama’ Hozur’s santur playing extracted from wax cylinders, observed these same qualities in his melodies, playing style, and singing.</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c-%d9%85%d8%b9%d8%b1%d9%88%d9%81%db%8c/\">Musa Ma’rufi</a> recounted:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">“A group of his friends once asked the late Habibollah Sama’ Hozur to play the santur. He said: ‘Allow me to perform ablution and play in a state of ritual purity.’ One of them replied that playing the santur does not require ablution. He answered: ‘Because when I play the santur, I am engaged in intimate conversation and supplication with the Divine, it is necessary for me to be in a state of purity and not unclean.</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Indeed, the servants of God and those who have caught a whiff of the qalandar station and are not captive to carnal desires and misplaced passions consider music to be a heavenly sound. They say that since it has come to us from the pure realm, we too should not defile it. A true and genuine musician never allows himself to become involved with intoxicants or narcotics, etc.’”</p>\n\n<p>Ma’rufi continues:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">“Of course, such a musician is very rare — indeed, one of the rarities — and ordinary people cannot benefit from his existence, because there is a great distance between ordinary people and him in terms of ethics, spirituality, and inner qualities. [Ma’rufi, 1339 solar]</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a> recounted that around 1332 AH he saw Sama’ Hozur at a private gathering with his father and heard him play. In response to the repeated requests of the host to perform, Sama’ Hozur said:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">“I no longer have any desire or enthusiasm left. For some time now I have not been playing. I only play for myself occasionally when the urge arises, because true connoisseurs of art are very rare, and the art of music has fallen into the hands of those who are mere entertainers. I do not like to play for a crowd.”</p>\n\n<p>Khaleqi says that despite this, the mention of his teacher Sorour al-Molk stirred his enthusiasm. He spread a towel over the santur, and while his son <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ad%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a8-%d8%b3%d9%85%d8%a7%d8%b9%db%8c/\">Habib Samaei</a> accompanied him on tombak, he played: “Breaths were held in chests and all eyes were fixed on the master’s hands. The mallets struck the strings with such speed that nothing was visible in the air except a line perpendicular to the santur strings. The master sometimes played rhythmic pieces and sometimes melodic avaz passages. How can I describe the playing I heard from Sama’ Hozur?” [Khaleqi, 1333 solar]</p>\n\n<p>Sama’ Hozur was a Ni’matollahi dervish. His death can be estimated around 1336 AH.</p>\n\n<p>Dr. Sasan Sepanta says:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">“In the autumn of 1366 solar, Hossein Dehlavi and Faramarz Payvar expressed interest in hearing the surviving recordings of the music of the masters from Naser al-Din Shah’s era, which I had restored from wax cylinders about three decades earlier. Eventually an opportunity arose, and in the last days of Mehr and early Aban 1366 solar they came to Isfahan. During suitable moments, after listening to pieces by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%86%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%a8-%d8%a7%d8%b3%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Nayeb Asadollah</a>, Mirza Abdollah, Aqa Hossein-Qoli, Hassan Khan, Mohammad Sadeq Khan Sorour al-Molk and his duet with Nayeb, Aqa Hossein-Qoli, and the singing of Sadeq Khan Navab, they asked to hear Sama’ Hozur’s santur.”</p>\n\n<p>During the restoration of these works (1338–1339 solar), Doust Ali Khan Moa’yyer al-Mamalek told me about the friendly gatherings and the recording of those pieces. He said that Sama’ Hozur was a friend of his father, and at the time of the recordings Doust Ali Khan was a twenty-four-year-old young man. He explained that arranging a friendly gathering for Sama’ Hozur to play had its own special etiquette and conditions. In that gathering, strangers who were not people of purity were not allowed to attend.</p>\n\n<p>In short, Sama’ Hozur believed that music in his style possessed a latent power and should be performed in the service of sanctity. Therefore, its content, performance (playing style), and the atmosphere of the gathering were completely different from entertainment and recreational music. Sama’ Hozur’s performances took place at night by the light of candles and dim lamps, in the presence of pure-hearted and spiritual friends.</p>\n\n<p>The musician, in a state of contemplation, would play the pieces with skill, firm spiritual faith, and awakened awareness, and sometimes, calmly and comfortably, would sing along with his own instrument. His trills consisted of three or four notes. That music, in that special space and conditions, was performed with complete devotion to the Divine.</p>\n\n<p>Dr. Sasan Sepanta says:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">“After restoring Sama’ Hozur’s performance, Moa’yyer al-Mamalek asked me to always keep in mind, both in his lifetime and after his death, that Sama’ Hozur refrained from playing for listeners who sought mere entertainment and lacked spiritual understanding of music. For this reason, we agreed with Messrs. Dehlavi and Payvar that only they and I would be present when listening to those works.</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Several nights were spent listening to and discussing the pieces. From Darvish Khan’s tar to Ali-Naqi Vaziri’s setar, everything was heard. During suitable moments, we also listened to pieces from Mohammad Sadeq Khan Sorour al-Molk’s santur, Nayeb Asadollah’s ney, and Sama’ Hozur’s santur and singing, which I had restored from the wax cylinders.</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">On the last night, Mr. Payvar expressed a desire to hear another performance of Sama’ Hozur’s singing and santur. It was nearly midnight, and the atmosphere was completely calm and silent. I prepared the equipment for playback. The voice of the late Doust Mohammad Khan Moa’yyer al-Mamalek (the original recorder) introduced the date and the name of the performer, and then the sound of Sama’ Hozur’s santur resonated from the ‘Hi-Fi’ speakers in our living room.</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Mr. Payvar was sitting on the floor and gradually moved closer to the speakers. With his head lowered, he listened with deep concentration. Mr. Dehlavi and I were sitting on chairs beside him, listening to the music. Sama’ Hozur was playing the gusheh ‘Naghmah.’</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">In the middle of the piece, right before my eyes on the floor, a moving amber-colored mass of light appeared and moved from the center of the room toward the speakers, passing behind Mr. Payvar who was sitting on the floor. I watched this unexpected scene in great astonishment and wonder, feeling the presence of Sama’ Hozur, while at the same time reassuring myself that perhaps this was an illusion passing through my mind and not real.</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">At that moment, Mr. Dehlavi, who was sitting next to me, suddenly said to me: ‘Look, look — there is a light moving in the middle of the room and flowing toward the speakers.’ Mr. Payvar, with his back to the light, had almost pressed his head against the speakers and was absorbed in listening to the music with his whole being.”</p>\n\n<h2>The Style of Sama’ Hozur</h2>\n\n<p>In Sama’ Hozur’s playing style, skill and emotional expression are in perfect balance. He performs the melodies with ease, serenity, and joy of heart, without physical or mental tension. Discipline, order, and concentration give him mastery over the vocal and musical imagery in his mind. Undoubtedly, his regular athletic training and spiritual disposition enabled him to have control over his mind, senses, and body, leading him toward self-purification and the realm of knowledge and meaning. The singing he performed along with his santur melodies is pleasant and steady.</p>\n\n<p>In Sama’ Hozur’s music, there is no weakness of body or soul, no helplessness, lamentation, or weariness in his instrument or voice. The entire texture of the melody, the tone of performance, and his own voice are healthy, balanced, pleasant, and accompanied by a kind of joy. Unlike many traditional music works, his playing and singing are not overloaded with ornaments, so excessive decorative notes and trills do not overshadow the main melodic design. The melodic structure governs the ornamental details.</p>\n\n<p>Like his teacher Sorour al-Molk, Sama’ Hozur would sometimes place a handkerchief over the santur strings to make the sound softer and sweeter. His instrument had delicacy, dignity, elegance, and refinement. In his singing and appropriate trills, he imitates the patterns of his santur mallet accents, and he does not use fast trills.</p>\n\n<p>His melodies are measured, and unlike some musicians, the avaz is not dominated by the instrument. Instead of giving a merely imitative response with the instrument between vocal phrases, he maintains the underlying metric pulse with the santur and adds variety to the overall melody. The comfortable range of Sama’ Hozur’s singing was roughly in the tenor register, and the tuning of his santur for performing the piece in Rak was in the same range.</p>\n\n<h2>Recorded Works of Sama’ Hozur’s Santur</h2>\n\n<p>Private recordings on wax cylinders of the phonograph in the second decade of the fourteenth AH century, corresponding to the last years of the nineteenth century CE. (Restored by Dr. Sasan Sepanta in 1338–1339 solar)</p>\n\n<h2>Narrated Works</h2>\n\n<p>Tasnif in Homayun, performed by the orchestra of the Institute for the Intellectual Development of Children and Young Adults, recorded on the Homayun disc from the ten-disc set of Iranian music dastgahs, 1354 solar.</p>', '2024-02-23 20:27:16', 0, '2024-12-19 19:04:13', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1563, 'posts', 106, 'en', NULL, 'Alfred Jean Baptiste Lemaire, known as Monsieur Lemaire', NULL, 'Alfred Jean-Baptiste Lemaire was a French musician and educator invited to Iran during the reign of Naser al-Din Shah to establish and train military music ensembles at Dar al-Fonun. Despite language barriers and students with limited literacy, he taught music theory, wind instruments, piano, solfège, and harmony with the help of an interpreter, laying the foundation for Iran’s modern military bands. He organized concerts featuring European classical works and also transcribed Iranian musical modes into Western notation, publishing some of them in Europe. After nearly 41 years of service in Iran, Lemaire passed away in Tehran in 1909, leaving a lasting impact on the modernization of Iranian music education.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Alfred Jean Baptiste Lemaire (Monsieur Lemaire)</h2>\n\n<p>After the inauguration of Dar al-Fonun on 5 Rabi’ al-Awwal 1268 AH (which had been established at the suggestion of Mirza Taqi Khan Amir Kabir), because the need for military music bands drew the attention of Naser al-Din Shah, a music department was created at Dar al-Fonun. Two Frenchmen named Bousquet and his assistant Rouillon, who had come to Iran around 1856 CE and previously organized a music band, were unable to achieve significant results.</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, the Iranian government hired an expert music instructor and bandmaster named Alfred Jean Baptiste Lemaire from France. He was sent to Iran in 1285 AH (1868 CE). Lemaire took on the training of students, most of whom could not even read and write properly. Since Lemaire himself did not know Persian, Mirza Ali Akbar Khan Naqqashbashi (Mozzayyen al-Dowleh), the French and painting teacher at Dar al-Fonun, was appointed as his translator and rendered Lemaire’s theoretical lessons into Persian. [Mallāh, 1354 solar]</p>\n\n<p>The first <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d8%a6%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">music theory</a> book, translated by Mozzayyen al-Dowleh, was printed in 1301 AH (1883 CE) at the Dar al-Fonun printing house in Tehran and was taught at the school. This book was in octavo format with lithographic printing and contained theoretical music topics in two columns: French and its Persian translation. All lessons, including the teaching of wind instruments, piano, solfège, and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d9%88%d9%86%db%8c/\">harmony</a>, were handled by Monsieur Lemaire himself. Eventually, military music bands were formed and gave concerts in which they gradually performed works by some European composers, such as parts of Gounod’s Faust, pieces by Verdi, and others.</p>\n\n<p>The military music band performed in the following situations:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>On official holidays, they played the first piece of the “Salam” (greeting), composed by Monsieur Lemaire (this piece remained in use until the time of Mohammad Ali Shah and was changed during Ahmad Shah’s reign);</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>This piece, performed by the military music band during Naser al-Din Shah’s era under Lemaire’s direction, was broadcast with the original recording and an introduction and commentary by Dr. Sasan Sepanta on the “Jang-e Shab” radio program (11 Khordad 1351 solar).</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>During royal summer trips;</li>\n  <li>In official ceremonies for receiving foreign ambassadors;</li>\n  <li>During the ta’zieh performances at Tekyeh Dowlat;</li>\n  <li>In the one-hour-before-sunset ceremonies;</li>\n  <li>At large banquets, horse-racing events, and similar occasions.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Lemaire was present at the Iranian pavilion during Naser al-Din Shah’s third trip to Europe in 1306 AH (1889 CE) at the Paris Exhibition. He received the rank of general in Iran and became the head of the royal music bands. Several booklets containing notations of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a> were prepared by Lemaire and printed in Paris during the final decade of Naser al-Din Shah’s reign.</p>\n\n<p>In the mentioned booklets and those printed during Mozaffar al-Din Shah’s reign, avaz in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%da%86%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">Chahargah</a>, Homayun, Mahur, and several <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%aa%d8%b5%d9%86%db%8c%d9%81/\">tasnifs</a> and rengs were notated by Lemaire in international musical notation with a French introduction. Lemaire lived in Iran for about forty-one years and passed away in Tehran in 1327 AH (1909 CE) at the age of sixty-something.</p>', '2024-02-24 21:40:20', 0, '2024-12-19 19:04:34', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1564, 'posts', 107, 'en', NULL, 'Seyed Hossein Taherzadeh', NULL, 'Seyyed Hossein Taherzadeh was one of the most prominent Iranian classical vocalists of the late Qajar and early Pahlavi periods, born in Isfahan in 1882. From a young age, he developed a strong interest in singing and gradually entered the artistic circles of Tehran, where he worked with major masters such as Darvish Khan, Mirza Abdollah, and Mirza Habib Sama\' Hozur, which significantly shaped his artistic development. His early phonograph and later gramophone recordings in London and Tbilisi played a key role in establishing his reputation.\n\nTaherzadeh also collaborated with musicians such as Abdolhossein Saba and others, becoming one of the most important masters of the Persian radif vocal tradition. His style was characterized by precise ornamentation, clear poetic expression, and full mastery of traditional modal systems. In his later years, he performed mainly in private gatherings and passed away in 1955.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Seyed Hossein Taherzadeh</h2>\n\n<p>Seyed Hossein Taherzadeh was the son of Seyed Taher Tuiserkani. His father had come from Tuyserkan and Hamadan to study at the theological seminary (howzeh) in Isfahan and attended the Sadr School there. Seyed Hossein was born in 1261 solar in Isfahan. From his teenage years, during suitable opportunities — especially on the banks of the Zayandeh Rud — he began experimenting with <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2/\">avaz</a>, and those who gathered around him would encourage him.</p>\n\n<p>He came to Tehran around 1278 solar. At that time, one of the popular pastimes — especially on holidays — was to go to Rey and visit the shrine of Hazrat Abdolazim. People would spend the day strolling in the gardens and meadows, riding the steam train (the railway built during Naser al-Din Shah’s reign between Tehran and that area), and eating kebab and yogurt. During one of these outings, Seyed Hossein, as usual, was singing avaz when a small group gathered around him. Among them was a young man from a respected family in Isfahan who became acquainted with Taherzadeh and formed a friendship with him.</p>\n\n<p>Through this young man, Taherzadeh met Jahangir Mirza Hessam al-Saltaneh at his house. Jahangir Mirza, the grandson of the famous Hessam al-Saltaneh (the conqueror of Herat), was a talented young man who played the violin and composed several <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%aa%d8%b5%d9%86%db%8c%d9%81/\">tasnifs</a> with lyrics by Malek al-Sho’ara Bahar, some of which still survive. Through his friendship with Hessam al-Saltaneh, Taherzadeh gained access to his home. Hessam al-Saltaneh owned a phonograph (hâfez al-asvât) at his residence. This device, invented by Edison, was the first to record sound on wax cylinders and play it back.</p>\n\n<p>Each cylinder could record and play about 2.5 to 3 minutes of sound using purely acoustic and mechanical means without electricity. Taherzadeh recorded his earliest vocal works on this device accompanied by Hessam al-Saltaneh’s violin. By listening to the playback, he would critique his own performances and, with Hessam al-Saltaneh’s musical guidance, work to correct any shortcomings.</p>\n\n<p>Hessam al-Saltaneh’s house was a gathering place for several prominent artists of the time. There, Taherzadeh became acquainted with famous musicians such as <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%8a%d8%b1%d8%b2%d8%a7-%d8%ad%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a8-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%b3%d9%85%d8%a7%d8%b9-%d8%ad%d8%b6%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Mirza Habib Sama’ Hozur</a>, Mirza Abdollah, and other talented individuals such as Mojallal al-Dowleh (who played piano and percussion) and Naser Homayun, a student of the French musician Lemaire and head of Mozaffar al-Din Shah’s royal music who also played piano. He also formed friendships with them. At Mojallal al-Dowleh’s house, he met other artists of his era. As Ruhollah Khaleqi put it, “he gathered a sheaf from every harvest” until he became proficient in his art.</p>\n\n<p>For learning the art of singing, Taherzadeh’s friends recommended that he study with Seyed Rahim Esfahani, a capable master. Khatib al-Mamalek, a mutual friend, decided to introduce Taherzadeh to Seyed Rahim and invited both of them to his garden one evening. That night, Seyed Rahim sang accompanied by the tar of Mirza Gholam Reza Shirazi (a student of Aqa Hossein-Qoli, of whom a few records remain). Taherzadeh realized that Seyed Rahim was a capable master and the only one who could properly teach him.</p>\n\n<p>At that gathering, at the request of his friends, Taherzadeh also sang. Seyed Rahim remained silent after hearing him, leading people to assume that because he recognized Taherzadeh’s talent, he did not want him to imitate his own singing. After that session, Taherzadeh asked his friends to find any possible way for him to benefit from Seyed Rahim’s voice. Eventually, his friends arranged several occasions where Seyed Rahim was invited, and Taherzadeh, hidden behind a curtain, was able to listen and learn from his singing.</p>\n\n<p>Through his connection with Hessam al-Saltaneh, Taherzadeh met the famous tar player <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ba%d9%84%d8%a7%d9%85%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%af%d8%b1%d9%88%db%8c%d8%b4/\">Darvish Khan</a> at the home of Safa Ali Zahir al-Dowleh (the Qotb of the Safa’i dervishes and founder of the Akhavat Association). This acquaintance led to artistic collaboration and a close friendship between the two. Taherzadeh participated in the concerts of the Akhavat Association, which were organized at Zahir al-Dowleh’s encouragement, with proceeds dedicated to charitable causes.</p>\n\n<p>Around 1289 solar (1910 CE), at the invitation of the His Master’s Voice (H.M.V.) recording company, Taherzadeh traveled with Darvish Khan (tar), Habibollah Shahrdar (piano), Reza Qoli Khan (vocals), Hossein Hang-Afarin (violin), Baqer Khan Rameshgar (kamancheh), and Asadollah Khan (tar and santur) via Russia to London. On the way, in Rasht, they gave three concerts at the request of the constitutionalist mujahideen. Akbar Khan, the flute player (brother of Hossein Hang-Afarin), who was in that city, joined them on the journey. The group attended the His Master’s Voice company celebration in London, where several gramophone records of their works were recorded.</p>\n\n<p>Some time later, Taherzadeh traveled again to Tbilisi with Darvish Khan (tar), Baqer Khan Rameshgar (kamancheh), Eqbal al-Soltan (vocals), and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%af%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%85%db%8c/\">Abdollah Davami</a> (tasnif and percussion) to record more gramophone discs. In addition to the recordings, they gave two concerts at the Georgian theater. When these records reached Iran and were sold, Taherzadeh’s fame increased further.</p>\n\n<p>He continued to participate in several concerts until the early years of the fourteenth solar century. For example, in 1303 solar, he sang at a concert in the Grand Hotel (on Lalehzar Street in Tehran) accompanied by Darvish Khan on tar and performed the tasnif in Mahur “Ze Man Negaram” with music by Darvish Khan and lyrics by Malek al-Sho’ara Bahar. As mentioned, the proceeds from these concerts were dedicated to charitable causes and helping orphaned children.</p>\n\n<p>Taherzadeh never made singing his profession. In his middle and later years, he only sang in small private gatherings with a few close artist friends. In the final years of his life, he opened a printing house called Sepehr in Tehran and served for a few months as a member of the Supreme Music Council of Radio.</p>\n\n<p>This council was formed with full authority, based on a cabinet resolution, to reform the chaotic state of radio music. It began work on 15 Farvardin 1334 solar and operated for nearly four months to bring order to radio music. However, due to interference by certain individuals and the bureaucracy of the General Department of Propaganda (which oversaw radio), as well as obstruction by those whose powers had been limited, it gradually became disillusioned, and its sessions were discontinued.</p>\n\n<p>The members of this council were: Habibollah Shahrdar (Moshir Homayun), Seyed Hossein Taherzadeh, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%84%d8%ad%d8%b3%d9%86-%d8%b5%d8%a8%d8%a7/\">Abolhasan Saba</a>, Ruhollah Khaleqi, Musa Ma’rufi, Ali Akbar Shahnazi, and Javad Ma’rufi.</p>\n\n<p>Taherzadeh’s earliest vocal recordings were made by his friends — including Hessam al-Saltaneh — around 1279 solar on personal phonographs (wax cylinders). In Isfahan as well, a few such devices were available in some families, and in one or two cases, Taherzadeh’s voice was recorded on them.</p>\n\n<p>After this period of private, one-of-a-kind recordings came the era of gramophone disc recording. Around 1289 solar, records of Taherzadeh were made in London and later in Tbilisi. After being duplicated, they reached Iran and further increased his fame. The musicians who accompanied his singing on the surviving records include: Darvish Khan (tar), Asadollah Khan (tar and santur), Akbar Khan (flute), Hossein Hang-Afarin (violin), Habibollah Shahrdar (piano), and Baqer Khan (kamancheh).</p>\n\n<p>Each side of these shellac discs lasted about three minutes and was recorded using purely acoustic and mechanical methods without electricity. The recording process and catalog of Taherzadeh’s records, along with details of other early Iranian recordings, are thoroughly documented in Dr. Sasan Sepanta’s book “The History of Music Recording in Iran” (published by Mahoor Institute, 1377 solar).</p>\n\n<p>After magnetic tape recorders entered Iran (around 1332 solar), some private recordings of Taherzadeh’s avaz from the last two or three years of his life exist. Among them is a private tape with setar accompaniment by Nour Ali Boroumand.</p>\n\n<p>From around 1340 solar, based on old gramophone records he had collected for research on <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a>, Dr. Sasan Sepanta published a series of articles titled “Avaz-e Iran” and “New Research on Iranian Music Schools.” These began in the tenth issue of the seventh year of the monthly “Music of Iran” (Esfand 1337 solar) and continued for about ten years. Dr. Sasan Sepanta also played some of Taherzadeh’s old records — which he had transferred to magnetic tape with technical improvements — with explanatory commentary in several of his radio programs.</p>\n\n<h2>The Style of Seyed Hossein Taherzadeh</h2>\n\n<p>Regarding Taherzadeh’s vocal technique and style, it can be said that his trills, as evident from his surviving works, are coherent and measured. In comparison with most of his contemporary singers, a few recordings of Seyed Rahim’s avaz on wax cylinders that were rediscovered and restored about sixty years ago show that Seyed Rahim also had regular, appropriate, and measured trills.</p>\n\n<p>Eyewitnesses told Dr. Sasan Sepanta that Taherzadeh’s face remained natural while singing and that his mouth opened to the proper degree. From analyzing his vocal performances, one can see that he possessed the necessary focus and mastery over the flow of trills in different gushehs, and had sufficient control over entering and exiting gushehs — even with fast trills — as well as timely and sometimes rapid returns to the tonal center of the dastgah or desired mode. In delivering poetry (a term used by traditional singers), because he had classical education and understood the meaning of the verses, his expression had the necessary appeal.</p>\n\n<p>In Taherzadeh’s avaz, the vowels are sustained longer than in ordinary speech, and he used this opportunity to create variety in the melodic flow through trills, fast passages, or references to different desirable modulations, with timely and sometimes rapid cadences (forud) to the appropriate tonal area. Taherzadeh himself stated that his recordings were made at a time when, after long association with musicians such as Mirza Habib Sama’ Hozur, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87/\">Mirza Abdollah</a>, Darvish Khan, and Hessam al-Saltaneh, listening to Seyed Rahim’s singing (from behind a curtain), and hearing his own voice on the phonograph and correcting its flaws, he had developed a solid style in singing.</p>\n\n<p>To reach this level, he did not limit himself to a single singer or style. As he himself said, he “gathered a sheaf from every harvest” and made use of it.</p>\n\n<p>When listening to music, Taherzadeh was not a passive listener. His association with the artists mentioned above, as well as listening to live music in their gatherings and singing in their presence, provided him with every opportunity for critique and examination, which greatly contributed to the development of his style.</p>\n\n<p>Taherzadeh was skilled in both non-metric avaz and metric pieces/tasnifs, and examples of his performances in both categories survive. During performance, he paid attention to power, rhythm, and correct pitch. When delivering poetry in avaz, he respected the melodic patterns of Persian speech.</p>\n\n<p>In some of his trills, Taherzadeh used a varied melodic line. In certain cases, after delivering the first words of the opening hemistich and establishing the tonal space that evokes the desired gusheh or dastgah, he would, with a quick flourish or “nīsh” (as singers call it), move through ascending and descending melodic figures to other desirable gushehs, thereby adding variety and beauty to the avaz.</p>\n\n<p>In singing, emotional expression requires a close connection between the melody of speech and its semi-standard patterns. The more a singer follows well-formed standard and semi-standard speech intonation contours, the more effective the performance becomes in this regard. According to his natural talent, classical education, and capability, Taherzadeh, when delivering poetry, took into account the usual and desirable melodic patterns (intonation contours) of the Persian language and, by choosing suitable poems from great poets such as Sa’di, maintained harmony between the meter, meaning, and chosen melody.</p>\n\n<p>According to Dr. Sasan Sepanta’s laboratory measurements, Taherzadeh’s vocal range falls within the tenor register. In addition, he covered frequencies from approximately 130.8 to 523 Hz, that is, the range of notes C³ to C⁵. The natural and comfortable range of his voice, based on Dr. Sasan Sepanta’s estimation, was from 196 to 349 Hz, covering the notes G³ to F⁴.</p>\n\n<p>In the few pieces he sang without instrumental accompaniment in his youth — such as prayers and supplications (in Shur and Kurd Bayat) — he delivered melodic Arabic words within his natural and comfortable vocal range mentioned above. The melodic contour of his voice mostly centers around 220 to 329 Hz.</p>\n\n<p>In some of his works, when singing with instruments such as tar, flute, clarinet, or kamancheh, he reached up to 523 Hz and, in his trills, touched 587 Hz with ornamental (accented) notes. When performing avaz in this upper range, it appears that a certain contraction occurred in the vocal cords and the resonating cavities of the supralaryngeal vocal tract. It seems that approaching the upper limit of the singer’s vocal range, caused by following the melodic line of the instrument, naturally affected the pleasantness of his voice in those few upper notes. Taherzadeh passed away in Shahrivar 1334 solar.</p>\n\n<p>A collection of Taherzadeh’s gramophone records, gathered by Dr. Sasan Sepanta, was digitally restored and released by the Mahoor Institute in the autumn of 1378 solar on cassette and CD for enthusiasts. Taherzadeh’s records are listed in the catalog of works by Darvish, Akbar Khan, and others.</p>', '2024-02-25 22:08:03', 0, '2024-12-19 19:04:49', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1565, 'posts', 108, 'en', NULL, 'Morteza Ney-Davud, prominent tar player, student of Darvish Khan and teacher of Qamar-ol-Moluk Vaziri', NULL, 'Morteza Neydavoud was one of the most influential Iranian tar players and composers of the late Qajar and Pahlavi eras, born around 1900 in Tehran. He studied under masters such as Agha Hosseinqoli and Darvish Khan and became a leading figure in the Persian tar tradition. He collaborated closely with prominent singers such as Qamar-ol-Molouk Vaziri, producing numerous recordings and influential works in forms such as tasnif, pishdaramad, and reng.\n\nHis style is characterized by precise right-hand technique, agile left-hand control, and expressive responsiveness to vocal performance. Among his most famous compositions are “Morgh-e Sahar” and “Atash-e Del,” which remain widely performed today. Neydavoud was also among the first musicians to work with Iranian radio and recorded extensively on gramophone records. He passed away in 1990 and is regarded as a pillar of Persian classical music.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Morteza Ney-Davud</h2>\n\n<p>Morteza Ney-Davud was born around 1279 solar into a family familiar with music in Tehran. He completed his elementary education at the “Alliance” School. For seven months he studied tar with Ramazan Zolfaqari, a student of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86%d9%82%d9%84%db%8c/\">Aqa Hossein-Qoli</a>. He then studied tar and the radif for two years with Aqa Hossein-Qoli and for three years with Darvish Khan, eventually receiving Darvish’s gold axe medal.</p>\n\n<p>He composed several chaharmezrabs, tasnifs, and pishdaramads. Numerous gramophone records of his playing exist, both as solo tar and accompanying the voices of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%82%d9%85%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%85%d9%84%d9%88%da%a9-%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Qamar-ol-Moluk Vaziri</a> and Moluk Zarabi. The first melody he performed in a concert with Aref was the famous tasnif “Geryeh Kon” in the Dashti mode. Ney-Davud was among the first musicians to cooperate with radio. In 1359 solar, in his old age, he moved to the United States.</p>\n\n<h2>The Style of Morteza Ney-Davud</h2>\n\n<p>His primary instrument was the tar. In most of the surviving records, he performs pieces as solo tar or with violin accompaniment by his brother Musa Khan. Ney-Davud is considered one of the first-rank traditional tar players. His instrument has a clear and pleasant tone. His left hand is agile, fast, and controlled on the frets, and his plectrum strokes are resonant while his tremolos (riz) are famous. He conveys the moods of traditional music very well and responds to the singer with presence of mind and skill, matching trills and ornaments of the avaz.</p>\n\n<p>His compositions, including his pishdaramads and rengs, are fluent and attractive. Even after more than half a century, their performance remains pleasing. Some of his <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%aa%d8%b5%d9%86%db%8c%d9%81/\">tasnifs</a>, such as “Morgh-e Sahar” (Mahur) with lyrics by Malek al-Sho’ara Bahar and “Atash-e Del” in Dashti with lyrics by Pezhman Bakhtiari, have been performed for many years and are appreciated by connoisseurs.</p>\n\n<p>Some of his <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d9%be%db%8c%d8%b4-%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%a2%d9%85%d8%af/\">pishdaramads</a>, such as the one in Esfahan, are still arranged with a type of harmony for large orchestras. He also composed a tasnif called “Mah-e Man Shah-e Man” with lyrics by Pezhman Bakhtiari in Bayat-e Esfahan, which was recorded around 1308 solar on a gramophone record with the voice of Qamar-ol-Moluk Vaziri, Ney-Davud’s own tar, and violin by his brother Musa.</p>\n\n<h2>Works of Morteza Ney-Davud</h2>\n\n<p>H.M.V. records (second series of Iranian records):</p>\n\n<p>Bayat-e Esfahan, Afshari, tasnif of Afshari “Dar Bahar-e Omid”, Segah and tasnif “Cheh Konam az Feraqat”, Bayat-e Tork and tasnif of Tork, Abu Ata and Hejaz and tasnif “Sanama be Khoda”, tasnif of Mahur “Ze Man Negaram” by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ba%d9%84%d8%a7%d9%85%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%af%d8%b1%d9%88%db%8c%d8%b4/\">Darvish Khan</a>, Dashti and tasnif “Geryeh Kon” by Aref, tasnif of Mahur “Aknun ke Mara Vaz’-e Vatan dar Nazar Amad” with lyrics by Mirzadeh Eshqi, Chahargah and Mansuri and tasnif of Shur, tasnif of Segah “Ghalb-e Madar” with lyrics by Iraj Mirza, tasnif “Elahé”, tasnif of Shur “Ey Dast-e Haqq” by Aref, tasnif of Segah and Chahargah “Zad Lashkar-e Gol” — all recorded with the voice of Qamar-ol-Moluk Vaziri — and tasnif of Homayun “Asheghi Mohnat Besyar Kesheed”, tasnif of Mahur “Ba Vafa, Ey Khak-e Iran”, tasnif of Abu Ata “Bahar-e Now Resid”, and avaz in Chahargah, Bayat-e Esfahan and Masnavi, Mahur, Delkash and Bidad with the voice of Moluk Zarabi.</p>\n\n<p>Solo tar records: H.M.V. records from AX.388 to AX.390</p>\n\n<p>Mahur – Delkash, Bayat-e Esfahan – Oshshaq, Segah – Mokhalef,</p>\n\n<p>and on Polydor records: Concert of Darvish School by Ney-Davud, Mahur and tasnif “Morgh-e Sahar” with the voice of Moluk Zarabi, tasnif of Shur “Narges-e Mast”, tasnif of Dashti “Dar Molk-e Iran” by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d8%a7%d9%85%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%ac%d8%a7%d9%87%d8%af/\">Amir Jahed</a> with the voice of Qamar-ol-Moluk Vaziri, avaz of Bidad, tasnif of Bayat-e Tork “Roozegar-e Gozashteh” with lyrics by Pezhman Bakhtiari, tasnif of Homayun “Pardeh-ye Ghaflat”, tasnif of Dashti “Naleh-ye Ashegh” (Mowsem-e Gol) with lyrics by Vahid Dastgerdi, tasnif of Bayat-e Esfahan “Zan dar Jame’eh”, tasnif of Nava and Shur “Akhar Ey Saqi-ye Sangin Del”, tasnif of Bayat-e Esfahan “Sokhan-e Eshgh-e To Ange”,</p>\n\n<p>and on Parlophone records: tasnif of Dashti “Atash-e Del”, tasnif of Bayat-e Tork “Negar-e Man”, tasnif of Afshari “Delbakhteh”,</p>\n\n<p>and on Odeon records: Bayat-e Esfahan and Raj’ (Rajeh) — all recorded with the voice of Qamar-ol-Moluk Vaziri.</p>\n\n<p>In addition to the records mentioned above, which were mostly recorded between 1305 and 1318 solar, during one and a half years in 1348 solar, approximately 297 gushehs from the dastgahs of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a> were recorded on magnetic tape with solo tar by Morteza Ney-Davud at the Tehran radio studio. It was planned to release them as cassette tapes for enthusiasts, but this was never done. Morteza Ney-Davud expressed regret and disappointment about this in several interviews. In 1349 solar, Iraj Gol-Sorkhi was the Director General of Radio Iran.</p>\n\n<p>Dr. Sasan Sepanta says:</p>\n\n<p>“The pishdaramad in Esfahan (tasnif ‘Mah-e Man’) composed by Morteza Ney-Davud was arranged by Morteza Hannaneh for the television series ‘Hezar Dastan,’ but as far as I remember, the name of the original composer was not mentioned in the film’s credits!”</p>\n\n<p>The notation of some of Ney-Davud’s melodies, such as the pishdaramad in Esfahan, was published in the second volume of the violin method of the National Music School. He passed away in 1369 solar.</p>', '2024-02-28 21:48:24', 0, '2024-12-19 19:05:02', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1566, 'posts', 109, 'en', NULL, 'Musa Ma\'rufi', NULL, 'Mousa Maroufi was a prominent figure in traditional Persian music in the late Qajar and early Pahlavi eras. After studying tar with Darvish Khan and deeply engaging with the Persian classical repertoire (radif), he dedicated much of his life to systematically transcribing and preserving the radif using Western notation. His most important achievement is the “Radif of Seven Dastgahs of Iranian Music,” compiled from oral traditions and earlier written materials. Maroufi also composed several tasnifs and instrumental pieces, some of which were performed and recorded by famous singers such as Qamar-ol-Moluk Vaziri and Gholamhossein Banan. In addition to composition and performance, he was active in music education and radio, and consistently advocated both preservation and thoughtful modernization of Iranian classical music.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Musa Ma’rufi</h2>\n\n<p>Musa Ma’rufi was born in 1268 solar in Tehran. He studied Arabic grammar, syntax, logic, and parts of jurisprudence and Quranic exegesis under Haj Mirza Ebrahim (Molabashi). For some time he also learned photography from Yusef Suratgar (Naser al-Din Shah’s photographer) and gradually became interested in music. Finally, his uncle Mohammad Hassan Atabaki took him to <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ba%d9%84%d8%a7%d9%85%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%af%d8%b1%d9%88%db%8c%d8%b4/\">Darvish Khan</a>, and from 1297 solar he began studying tar with Darvish. After three years, he received the gold axe medal from Darvish.</p>\n\n<p>Ma’rufi was interested in notating the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%af%db%8c%d9%81/\">radif of Iranian music</a>. For this reason, he studied musical notation with Hossein Hang-Afarin. After Ali-Naqi Vaziri returned from Germany and established the Higher School of Music, although Musa Khan already had his own music class and was training tar students, he attended Vaziri’s school like an ordinary student to complete his musical education and decided to compile the radif of Iranian music.</p>\n\n<p>Using the notebooks in which Vaziri had notated the radif from the live performances of Mirza Abdollah and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86%d9%82%d9%84%db%8c/\">Aqa Hossein-Qoli</a> before going to Europe, Ma’rufi compiled and edited the radif. It was finally published in 1342 solar under the title “The Radif of the Seven Dastgahs of Iranian Music” by the Fine Arts Administration on high-quality paper with a gold-embossed cover. This beautiful and valuable edition of the radif of the seven dastgahs remains a lasting legacy.</p>\n\n<h2>Works of Musa Ma’rufi</h2>\n\n<p>Ma’rufi composed several melodies, some of which, such as the tasnif in Dashti “Mowsem-e Gol” with lyrics by Vahid Dastgerdi and the voice of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%82%d9%85%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%85%d9%84%d9%88%da%a9-%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Qamar-ol-Moluk Vaziri</a>, were recorded on gramophone records about sixty years ago.</p>\n\n<p>Dr. Sasan Sepanta says about the tasnif “Mowsem-e Gol”:</p>\n\n<p>“This melody was also arranged by Morteza Hannaneh and performed with orchestra in the television series ‘Hezar Dastan,’ but in the film’s credits, as far as I remember, Musa Ma’rufi’s name was not mentioned as the composer.”</p>\n\n<p>Some of his other melodies, such as “Morgh-e Hagh” in Dashti with lyrics by Rahe Mo’ayyeri and the voice of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ba%d9%84%d8%a7%d9%85%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%a8%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Gholamhossein Banan</a>, were performed in Golha-ye Rangarang radio program No. 230. Ma’rufi also composed other tasnifs such as “Delpasand” in Mahur and “Khandeh-ye Gol,” as well as several pishdaramads and rengs that have been published in some violin and tar books of the National Music School.</p>\n\n<p>From the very beginning of radio broadcasting and the establishment of the National Music Association, Ma’rufi cooperated with them. After the founding of the National Music School, he taught tar there. His most important work, as mentioned, is the radif of the seven dastgahs of Iranian music, which was published based on the notations of Ali-Naqi Vaziri and the handwritten notes of Mehdi Qoli Hedayat.</p>\n\n<p>Ma’rufi’s compositions are mostly in the style of traditional composers and contain varied and beautiful melodies. His playing style also originates from the old masters, but he believed in diversity and evolution in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a>. He was dissatisfied with the decline of common music caused by constant repetition and dry, monotonous phrases. He believed that uniform performances lacking development, along with long chaharmezrabs and mere imitation of old styles, could make Iranian music seem tiresome and push young people toward Western music.</p>\n\n<p>He also criticized mechanical, fast, and forceful playing of instruments and did not consider good playing to mean speed, strength, or wrestling with the instrument. He had a special devotion to the spiritual and moral qualities of Sama’ Hozur’s performance and often recalled his emphasis on purity of body and soul during musical performance and the angelic quality of his sound. He considered jealousy and stinginess to be two destructive factors in art and said:</p>\n\n<p>“A group of musicians, inevitably due to their scientific knowledge and artistic skill, become the target of jealousy and stinginess from other musicians who lag behind them in scientific status. Therefore, they create strife, noise, and uproar. As is unfortunately common in our country, if someone is superior to others in scientific rank, the ignorant try to bring him down from that position and take his place. In short, these worthy musicians and artists are always attacked by the ignorant. In the end, they either perish or, if they survive, are forced to withdraw and become unknown, leaving the field open for others. Why? Because society has not yet reached the level of growth and knowledge and does not want anything else.” [Ma’rufi, 1339 solar]</p>\n\n<p>Musa Ma’rufi finally passed away in Shahrivar 1344 solar in Tehran.</p>', '2024-03-01 22:00:44', 0, '2024-12-19 19:05:19', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1567, 'posts', 110, 'en', NULL, 'Javad Ma\'rufi', NULL, 'Javad Maroufi was one of the most influential Iranian pianists and composers of the 20th century, born in 1915 in Tehran. Son of Mousa Maroufi, he first studied music under his father and later at the Ali-Naqi Vaziri Conservatory, where he developed a strong foundation in Western classical piano technique alongside Persian music traditions under teachers such as alireza_vesiri and Tania Kharratian. He became a key figure in Iranian radio, the Golha orchestra, and the National Music Conservatory.\n\nMaroufi’s style blended Western harmonic language with Persian modal music, resulting in lyrical and refined orchestral and piano works. His notable compositions include “Deylaman,” “Rhapsody in Esfahan,” and various piano pieces and orchestrations. He also played an important pedagogical role, training prominent musicians such as Anoushirvan Rohani and Ofelia Parto, and remained a central figure in modern Iranian classical music until his death in 1993.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Javad Ma’rufi</h2>\n\n<p>The son of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c-%d9%85%d8%b9%d8%b1%d9%88%d9%81%db%8c/\">Musa Ma’rufi</a>, Javad Ma’rufi was born in 1294 solar in Tehran. After completing elementary school, he entered the Vaziri Higher School of Music and continued the musical education he had begun with his father. He first studied the tar, but after some time he became interested in playing the piano and began learning the instrument under Ali-Naqi Vaziri. A short while later, Vaziri entrusted him to Madame Tanya Kharatian. Kharatian had studied at the State Conservatory in Tbilisi and had spent three years in Paris perfecting her piano playing. She came to Iran in 1310 solar with her husband and held private classes. From 1315 solar, she taught piano at the Higher School of Music.</p>\n\n<p>Ma’rufi learned piano technique from her and received his diploma from the State Music School in 1311 solar. In 1312 solar, he was employed by the Ministry of Culture and began teaching music. While working at the Higher School of Music, he continued his studies in the fundamentals of international music and received his diploma there. He also taught solfège and musical dictation at the same school.</p>\n\n<p>Javad Ma’rufi turned to composition around 1315 solar and created several pieces based on the rubaiyat of Khayyam, which are considered among the memorable works of that period in his career. From the very beginning of radio broadcasting, he cooperated with the organization, initially as a piano player. After more than a decade, he became the conductor of Radio Orchestra No. 1 and performed works by Khaleqi, Vaziri, and others. He also collaborated with the Golha Orchestra under the direction of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Khaleqi</a> and arranged and accompanied several melodies for the Golha programs on piano. These pieces were mostly by Sheyda, Aref, Mokhtari, and Darvish Khan and were usually performed with the voices of Banan and Shahidi.</p>\n\n<p>After the establishment of the National Music Association by Khaleqi, Ma’rufi taught piano in the association’s classes and performed with its orchestra. Following the founding of the National Music School, he also taught piano there. Among his more famous students were Anoushiravan Rohani, Afliya Partow, and Mahin Zarrinpanjeh. Ma’rufi was a member of the Supreme Music Council of Radio for some time. He finally passed away on 16 Azar 1372 solar in Tehran.</p>\n\n<h2>The Style and Works of Javad Ma’rufi</h2>\n\n<p>In piano playing, Javad Ma’rufi believed that a student must first thoroughly master the technique of the instrument. This view was influenced by the opinions of his teacher Ali-Naqi Vaziri. He applied the same approach when teaching instruments such as tar, violin, and piano at the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b3%d9%87-%d8%b9%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">Higher School of Music</a>. Ma’rufi believed that the left hand, which has a role separate from the right hand, should be used appropriately and that both hands should not simply play the same line in two octaves. In the pieces he arranged or composed for piano, he followed this principle. When arranging melodies for orchestra, he also created delicate harmonies for Iranian melodies.</p>\n\n<p>One of the earliest works arranged and conducted by him is the famous piece “Deylaman,” which had been notated by Abolhasan Saba a few years earlier. Ma’rufi arranged this piece for orchestra around 1324 solar and performed it at a concert of the National Music Association. A short time later, it was recorded on a “His Master’s Voice” gramophone record with the voice of Banan (catalog number OHB-1149.RR50). For the phrases of this work, Ma’rufi wrote a type of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d9%88%d9%86%db%8c/\">harmony</a> and, in the vocal sections where Banan sang Sa’di’s poetry, he placed arpeggios on several characteristic notes of the Dashti tetrachord as a background for the avaz and performed them.</p>\n\n<p>Other recorded works by Ma’rufi include:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>The Bakhtiari local piece “Sholil” with the voice of Adib Khansari, accompanied by Ma’rufi’s orchestra and piano, recorded by the “His Master’s Voice” company around 1325 solar (catalog number OHB-1010-1019.RR12);</li>\n  <li>“Khun-e Del,” a local melody from western Isfahan and Bakhtiari regions, with the voice of Adib Khansari (catalog number 1032-1031-OHB);</li>\n  <li>And the Kermanshahi local song “Gowhari,” which Ma’rufi’s orchestra, under the name “Dasteh-ye Honar Dustan,” recorded on gramophone with pleasant harmonization.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>The Rhapsody in Bayat-e Esfahan and a piece in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%aa%d8%b1%da%a9/\">Bayat-e Tork</a> are among the first solo piano works composed by Javad Ma’rufi in his youth, around the age of thirty. They were recorded with catalog number OHB-1127-1128-RR66.</p>\n\n<p>Among the pieces by other composers that Ma’rufi arranged is “Gham-e Eshgh” by Hossein Sanjari, performed with the voice of Banan and lyrics by Sa’di. Its opening lines are:</p>\n\n<p>Khabar az eshq nadarad ke nadarad yari  \ndel nakhanand ke sayadash nakonad deldari</p>\n\n<p>This melody was arranged with pleasant harmony for orchestra and recorded with catalog number OHB-1149-1.RR50. The above records were recorded in Tehran by English engineers of the “His Master’s Voice” company, duplicated in England, and are of good quality in terms of standard speed, recording fidelity, and disc material.</p>\n\n<p>Among the pieces Ma’rufi composed for piano, besides the Rhapsody in Bayat-e Esfahan and the piece in Bayat-e Tork already mentioned, the following can be named:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Rhapsody in Homayun,</li>\n  <li>Rhapsody in Mahur,</li>\n  <li>Fantasy “Zhila,”</li>\n  <li>Prelude for Piano in Chahargah,</li>\n  <li>“Khazan-e Roozegar-e Man,”</li>\n  <li>“Khabha-ye Talayi,”</li>\n  <li>“Entezar,”</li>\n  <li>“Kuku,”</li>\n  <li>Various chaharmezrabs, and</li>\n  <li>Suites in Dashti, Segah, and Mahur.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>In some Golha programs, such as Golha-ye Tazeh No. 64 with the voices of Abdolvahab Shahidi and Sima Bina, and Golha-ye Tazeh No. 78, his works were performed. Javad Ma’rufi’s compositions are melodically fluent, coherent, and pleasant, and are counted among the enduring works of Iranian music.</p>\n\n<p>In the spring of 1372 solar, the radif of Iranian music for piano was released on tape featuring performances by Ma’rufi. Javad Ma’rufi passed away in Tehran in 1372 solar.</p>', '2024-03-01 22:09:31', 0, '2024-12-19 19:05:30', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1568, 'posts', 111, 'en', NULL, 'Reza Mahjubi', NULL, 'Reza Mahjoubi was a prominent Iranian violinist and composer of the early 20th century, born in Tehran into a musically inclined family. He began learning music at a young age, first informally by ear and later under several teachers. Despite his exceptional talent and the creation of expressive and memorable works such as prelude pieces and tasnifs in various Persian modes, his life was marked by emotional struggles and substance abuse, which eventually led to mental illness.\n\nBefore his decline, he produced influential compositions, many of which were recorded on gramophone records featuring renowned singers such as Qamar-ol-Moluk Vaziri. His playing style was highly emotional, improvisational, and innovative in tuning and expression, leaving a lasting impact on Iranian music. Reza Mahjoubi died in 1954 in Tehran and was buried in the Zahir-od-Dowleh cemetery.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Reza Mahjubi</h2>\n\n<p>Reza Mahjubi was born around 1287 solar in Tehran. His father, Abbas Ali, was a music lover who played the ney, and his mother, Fakhr al-Sadat, had some familiarity with playing the piano. Like his younger brother Morteza, Reza was interested in music from childhood. He studied for some time at Saint Louis School but left it to pursue music. He first began learning the violin with Ebrahim Azhang, but because Azhang taught from notation and Reza showed no interest in learning to read music, Azhang was not enthusiastic about teaching him.</p>\n\n<p>Reza was more interested in learning the violin by ear, so he went to the class of Hossein Khan Esma’ilzadeh and studied the violin by ear for several years. By the age of sixteen, he played the violin sweetly. Reza’s father opened the first café on Lalehzar Street in Tehran, and his sons Reza and Morteza played violin and piano there. <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a> quotes Morteza Mahjubi as saying:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">“Reza had an extraordinary perseverance in practicing. He would wake up at dawn and practice for at least several hours. During the day he practiced a great deal because he had a competitive spirit and did not want to fall behind me (Morteza). Until the age of twenty-five, when the writer [Ruhollah Khaleqi] also became his student, Reza was a well-organized young man and his violin playing was pleasant to hear. At that time he was also composing pieces and even gave one or two concerts. However, he gradually underwent a change and was admitted to the hospital. Although he received treatment, he slowly became mentally ill due to the use of intoxicants and excess in them.” [Khaleqi, 1333 solar]</p>\n\n<p>Before his illness, Reza had opened a violin teaching class in Atabak Alley on Lalehzar Street in Tehran. One of his students who later became famous in music was Majid Vafadar, who had a sweet plectrum technique. Reza’s friends recounted that he was a neat, stylish, and handsome young man, but due to associating with unworthy or hypocritical friends, he fell into the trap of alcohol and narcotics.</p>\n\n<p>Several pishdaramads, tasnifs, and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%86%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d8%b6%d8%b1%d8%a7%d8%a8/\">chaharmezrabs</a> composed by Reza have survived; they are very emotional and full of feeling. Among his works, the tasnif in Dashti (“Yek Zaman Chupani”), the pishdaramad in Bayat-e Esfahan and Shur, the pishdaramad in Segah, and the chaharmezrab in Segah are the most famous. Reza had innovative tunings for the violin and used suitable pedal notes in his pieces. One of his compositions is the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d9%be%db%8c%d8%b4-%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%a2%d9%85%d8%af/\">pishdaramad</a> in Dashti, for which Reza Shahrzad wrote lyrics. He matched the syllables of the poem to the rhythm and melody of the pishdaramad, turning it into the tasnif in Dashti that begins with these lines:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Yek zaman chupani  \nkhast az nadani</p>\n\n<p>This tasnif is one of Reza Mahjubi’s most passionate works. It was recorded on a Polydor gramophone record in Germany in 1927 with the voice of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%82%d9%85%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%85%d9%84%d9%88%da%a9-%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Qamar-ol-Moluk Vaziri</a> and the tar of Arslan Dargahi. The record still exists today.</p>\n\n<p>Another tasnif by Reza in Abu Ata exists; it is in three-beat meter and changes to 6/8 at the end. Its opening lines are:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Bahar-e now resid gol az bustan damid  \ney gol’azar na vaqt-e khab ast</p>\n\n<p>The eloquent poet Esma’il Navab Safa, who was acquainted with both Reza Mahjubi and his brother Morteza, wrote: “In the years after Shahrivar 1320, people walking along Lalehzar or Istanbul streets after ten in the morning would see a disheveled man wearing shabby, dirty-looking clothes, a red or sometimes pink tie with a loose knot that spoke of its owner’s indifference. He would occasionally stop briefly to speak with a passerby and then continue on his way. This passerby’s eyes and gaze were not normal; his face was gaunt and bony. He paid attention to no one. Some children who bothered him, he would gently push away with incoherent words. Those who saw him but did not know him would quickly pass by. Some would stop and speak to him respectfully, but he, like someone hurrying to an important appointment, would not engage in lengthy conversation even with acquaintances and would continue on his way.</p>\n\n<p>Every day at around the same time, his comings and goings repeated in the same manner. Naturally, those who did not know him would curiously ask each other: ‘Who is this?’ and would receive a short and painful answer: ‘Reza the madman…’ In the days of his health, he was the candle around which people gathered only to enjoy themselves for a few hours. They had an attractive appearance but an empty interior. Reza knew them well, and that is why even in the dawn of his madness — when he still possessed a magnificent intellect — he would flee from such people.</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">He associated with those who shared the same pains and were familiar with the sorrows of life, and who did not consider poverty a disgrace.” [Navab Safa, 1377 solar]</p>\n\n<p>Reza was closest to Reza Shahrzad and Amiri Firoozkuhi (the famous poet). For a while he also lived in his brother Morteza’s house. He did not want his violin playing to be recorded on gramophone discs. Navab Safa recounted that some of his friends secretly recorded some of Reza’s violin playing and Morteza’s piano using a wire recorder (which used a thin wire instead of tape).</p>\n\n<p>Sometimes in moments of delirium, Reza would talk to himself. When asked by curious onlookers, he would say: “I am speaking with the spirit of my mother or with my imaginary beloved, Mah Khanom.”</p>\n\n<p>He greatly cherished a red handkerchief that he kept in his jacket’s breast pocket and a red plastic ring, saying: “Mah Khanom sent them to me as a gift.”</p>\n\n<p>One day he was playing the violin in a gathering attended by prominent people. The guests, especially the ladies, were talking and paying little attention to his playing. Reza turned toward the wall and said: “Wall, you listen — these people have no taste.”</p>\n\n<p>Sometimes he would sing critical, social, and political tasnifs of that period, such as “These English have eaten Tehran.” When he was not in the mood, he would refuse to play. If someone handed him a violin in such a state, he would throw it toward them and leave the gathering.</p>\n\n<p>Reza Mahjubi finally passed away in 1333 solar in Tehran and was buried in Zahir al-Dowleh Cemetery.</p>\n\n<p>Reza Kamal Shahrzad, son of Mirza Hossein Monshi Bashi (Kamal al-Dowleh), was born in 1277 solar in Tehran. He completed his secondary education at Saint Louis School. From childhood he was sensitive, melancholic, and oppressed. He was interested in literature and storytelling and knew most of the tales from *One Thousand and One Nights* by heart. He also translated some French poetry and prose into Persian.</p>\n\n<p>In 1298 solar he wrote the musical drama “Parichehr and Parizad,” which was staged in 1300 solar at the Grand Hotel. Madame Pari Aqa Babian, who had returned to Iran after completing her theater studies in Europe, played the leading role. The year 1309 solar marked the peak of Shahrzad’s activity in theater, but when he realized that theater in Iran was not progressing due to cunning rivalries among profiteers, lack of encouragement, and poor judgment by some people, he gradually became disheartened and disappointed.</p>\n\n<p>One night, in a state of utter despair, he put on a beautiful new silk pajama he had just bought, drank a poisonous liquid, and committed suicide. At dawn on 20 Shahrivar 1316 solar, his lifeless body was found in his room.</p>\n\n<p>Another pioneer of Iranian theater, Mir Seyf al-Din Kermanshahi, who had returned to Iran after completing his theater studies abroad, also committed suicide on 7 Tir 1317 solar due to the same kind of cunning rivalries and plots by talentless and malicious competitors!</p>', '2024-03-02 22:00:15', 0, '2024-12-19 19:05:46', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1569, 'posts', 112, 'en', NULL, 'Habib Samaie', NULL, 'Habib Samaei was one of the most prominent Iranian santur players of the early 20th century, born in Tehran into a musical family. From childhood, he was influenced by his father, Sama-ol-Hozour, and quickly developed a highly creative and improvisational santur style. Although familiar with musical notation, he primarily relied on oral tradition and ear training, developing a distinctive personal approach to the Persian musical repertoire.\n\nHe recorded gramophone records with singers such as Parvaneh and participated in radio performances and the Iranian National Music Association. Despite his artistic success, his personal life was marked by tragedy and addiction, which affected his health and career. Nevertheless, his recordings remain among the most important works in Iranian santur performance. Habib Samaei passed away in 1946 due to illness.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Habib Samaei</h2>\n\n<p>The son of Sama’ Hozur, Habib Samaei was born in 1280 solar in Tehran. From the age of four, he would keep rhythm on the santur alongside his father, and by the age of ten, his santur playing was already pleasant to hear. Sometimes he would also sing while playing his instrument. His playing style was varied and highly creative in performing <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">Iranian music</a>. He recorded several records in Tehran with the sensitive singer Parvaneh, who had a sorrowful voice. The most famous of these are Shur and Gerili. These records were recorded by the “His Master’s Voice” company in Tehran, duplicated in London, and later distributed in Iran by the company’s representative, Rostam Rashid Rameshni.</p>\n\n<p>Habib studied music for a short time with Salar Mo’azzaz and became familiar with the basics of musical notation, but he never used notation in his own musical work. Abolhasan Saba notated some of his pieces and preserved them. Toward the end of his father’s life, Habib moved with his family to Mashhad. There he became interested in military uniform and remained in the service of the army until the end of his life. Occasionally he was sent on missions that involved travel; during one of these trips to Baluchistan, he became addicted to opium. This addiction had an effect on his temperament and, to some extent, contributed to his pessimism.</p>\n\n<p>In addition to the santur, which he played masterfully, he would sometimes also play the setar and a small hand organ. At the recommendation of Abolhasan Saba, Habib taught santur for a while in music classes, but he showed little interest in teaching. He was among the first musicians to perform on radio, but he left this activity unfinished as well. After the National Music Association was established, he cooperated with its classes for a short period and participated in its concerts. He also performed on several radio programs, both as a solo instrumentalist and accompanying the voice of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%82%d9%85%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%85%d9%84%d9%88%da%a9-%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Qamar-ol-Moluk Vaziri</a>.</p>\n\n<p>Samaei had a daughter named Nina from his second wife, to whom he was deeply attached. Nina passed away at the age of eighteen months, and this event deeply wounded Samaei’s soul and sensitive heart. He would often say: “I will soon follow her.”</p>\n\n<p>At the recommendation of his friends, in order to relieve some of his sorrow, he traveled to Iraq with his wife and visited the holy shrines.</p>\n\n<p>Among musicians, he was particularly fond of Abolhasan Saba. In private parties, he would rarely play more than once. During the “Millennium of Ferdowsi” celebration, he played in the play “Rostam and Sohrab” and received the attention of both domestic and foreign scholars who were guests at the event.</p>\n\n<p>Samaei used to say: “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">Music</a> is food for the soul and should not be swallowed without being properly chewed. The sound and avaz must be delicate and attractive enough not to cause indigestion for the listener.” For this reason, he avoided “long-winded playing” and recommended the same approach to his favorite singer, Parvaneh.</p>\n\n<p>Samaei’s body had been weak since his youth due to addiction. In the final years of his life, he suffered from pneumonia and, because of this illness and extreme weakness, passed away in Tir 1325 solar.</p>\n\n<p>Mrs. Arfa Atrai, in her book “The Life and Works of Habib Samaei” (Tehran, 1371 solar), and Mr. Majid Kiani, in his book “Habib Samaei” (Tehran, 1375 solar), have published interesting analytical points and their own research about this unique artist and master.</p>\n\n<h2>Works of Habib Samaei</h2>\n\n<p>25 cm (78 rpm) H.M.V. records:</p>\n\n<p>AX.674 – Gerili, Zarbi-ye Shahnaz with the voice of Parvaneh</p>\n\n<p>AX.675 – Tasnif in Bayat-e Esfahan with the voice of Parvaneh</p>\n\n<p>AX.687 – Avaz in Shur and Shahnaz with the voice of Parvaneh</p>\n\n<p>AX.703 – Avaz in Abu Ata and Hejaz with the voice of Parvaneh</p>\n\n<p>AX.705 – Avaz in Mahur and Abolchap with the voice of Parvaneh</p>\n\n<p>Some of Habib Samaei’s works have been notated and published in the twenty-five zarbi pieces and the santur books of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%84%d8%ad%d8%b3%d9%86-%d8%b5%d8%a8%d8%a7/\">Abolhasan Saba</a>. Some of his pieces and chaharmezrabs have also been performed and recorded by Faramarz Payvar and Majid Kiani on disc and tape.</p>\n\n<h2>The Style of Habib Samaei</h2>\n\n<p>Samaei used a santur with nine bridges and bare wooden mallets without felt. He played the mallets smoothly and executed the accents with delicacy and sweetness. He was creative and innovative and did not limit himself to the old radif. The same merit can be observed in his father’s <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%8a%d8%b1%d8%b2%d8%a7-%d8%ad%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a8-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%b3%d9%85%d8%a7%d8%b9-%d8%ad%d8%b6%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Sama’ Hozur</a> playing. He never rushed when expressing musical phrases and fully delivered the intended meaning. When playing metric pieces, it seems as though he was mentally reciting the poem of the piece, and the accents of his mallets clearly indicate that he had the prosodic syllables of the poetry in mind.</p>\n\n<p>Considering the limited recording time on each side of a record (three minutes), he avoided compressing musical phrases or presenting too much material in a short time. For this reason, the singer on each side of the record (for example in Avaz-e Shur) would sing only one couplet of poetry and fully deliver its meaning. Samaei’s santur mallets were both agile and controlled, and he used successive accents in the appropriate places.</p>\n\n<p>Most traditional musicians, when improvising, used the mental support of imagining the vocal syllables of poetry to maintain the melody. Sometimes this potential mental method would manifest as a faint, unconscious humming during playing and would flow into their plectrum or mallets.</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%b1%d8%aa%d8%b6%db%8c-%d9%86%db%8c-%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%88%d8%af/\">Morteza Ney-Davud</a> would also occasionally hum faintly while playing tar to accompany Qamar’s voice. Despite the limited sensitivity of recording equipment from about half a century ago, faint examples of this practice can occasionally be heard in some moments on his records. If more complete recordings of Habib Samaei’s santur performances — either solo or accompanying Qamar-ol-Moluk Vaziri — from the period of his artistic maturity had survived (which are about ten years later than his early records), we would have a more comprehensive legacy of his playing.</p>\n\n<p>However, as Khaleqi has recounted [Khaleqi, 1336 solar], the only recorded santur performance of his on radio was erased for other use. Had the engraving needle of the foreign companies not captured these few works in the delicate grooves of the records, and had his records not been rescued from the corners of household chests or the stalls of street vendors, even these few surviving works would not have remained.</p>', '2024-03-02 22:04:18', 0, '2024-12-19 19:06:01', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1570, 'posts', 113, 'en', NULL, 'Abdolhossein Shahnazi', NULL, 'Abdolhossein Shahnazi was a prominent Iranian tar player of the late Qajar and early Pahlavi periods, and the son of the legendary musician Agha Hossein-Qoli and younger brother of Ali-Akbar Shahnazi. He studied Persian classical music under leading masters and developed a delicate, expressive, and innovative style of tar performance, including unique tunings and personal interpretations of the radif.\n\nHe participated in early Iranian radio programs and collaborated with well-known singers and musicians of his time. A limited number of gramophone recordings of his solo performances and accompaniments have survived, showcasing his refined musical sensitivity. Despite his talent, he was emotionally affected by a lack of recognition from parts of society. Abdolhossein Shahnazi passed away in 1948, leaving behind a small but valuable musical legacy.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Abdolhossein Shahnazi</h2>\n\n<p>Abdolhossein Shahnazi is the son of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86%d9%82%d9%84%db%8c/\">Aqa Hossein-Qoli</a> and the younger brother of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b2%d9%86%d8%af%da%af%db%8c%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%85%d9%87-%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d8%a7%da%a9%d8%a8%d8%b1-%d8%b4%d9%87%d9%86%d8%a7%d8%b2%db%8c/\">Ali Akbar Shahnazi</a>. He studied tar for some time with his brother and learned the radifs of Iranian music from Baqer Khan Rameshgar, the kamancheh player and husband of his sister. He then continued his work, invented special tunings for the tar, and performed with the famous musicians and singers of his time. He was a friend of Vahid Dastgerdi and participated in his literary gatherings.</p>\n\n<p>Several records of his playing have survived. In the early years after the establishment of radio, he participated in its music programs, which were administered by Ebrahim Azhang. He was a sensitive man with a noble and independent spirit, and possessed a pleasant and graceful plectrum technique in his playing. Abdolhossein Shahnazi passed away in 1327 solar.</p>\n\n<h2>Works of Abdolhossein Shahnazi</h2>\n\n<p>His solo tar records include Homayun and Bidad, Rast-Panjgah and Mavarannahr, and the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%aa%d8%b5%d9%86%db%8c%d9%81/\">tasnif</a> of Chahargah and Shahr Ashub with the voice of S.M. Khan (?). Other works include Daramad and Naheft and Hoseini, Avaz-e Afshari and Nahib-e Afshari, Shekasteh and Qarabagh with the voice of Eqbal al-Soltan, Mahur and Homayun (“Shur-e Eshgh”) and Qarai with the voice of Badie Zadeh, and the tasnif of Shushtari “Bad-e Nowbahari” with the voice of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ba%d9%84%d8%a7%d9%85%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%a8%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Banan</a> and lyrics by Rahe Mo’ayyeri. This beautiful tasnif begins with these lines:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Mi agarash shur-afkan asari-st  \ncheshm-e to rā ta’sir-e degari-st</p>\n\n<p>Abdolhossein Shahnazi was a musician with great artistic talent and creativity. For this reason, he would sometimes feel hurt by the ignorance of some common people who could not recognize true art. As Master Homayi said, they could not distinguish a turquoise from a cheap bead. This lack of appreciation gradually affected his sensitive soul. Apart from the gramophone records mentioned above, no other recordings of his playing have survived.</p>', '2024-03-02 22:10:58', 0, '2024-12-19 19:06:12', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1571, 'posts', 114, 'en', NULL, 'Haj Taj Nishaburi', NULL, 'Haj Taj Nishaburi (Taj al-Wa\'ezin) was a renowned preacher and orator of the late Qajar era, known for his powerful voice, eloquent speech, and ability to blend poetry with religious sermons. He gained great popularity in various cities, especially Shiraz, where his sermons—often combining Qur’anic verses, poetry by Hafez, and rhythmic delivery—had a strong emotional impact on audiences.\n\nDespite his popularity, he also faced opposition and conspiracies driven by local rivalries and influential figures. His vocal style was later studied in acoustic research, revealing musical characteristics closely related to Iranian classical music (radif). In addition to his preaching, he was also a poet and passed away around 1907–1908 (1305 AH).', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Haj Taj Nishaburi</h2>\n\n<p>Haj Taj Nishaburi, also known as Haj Mirza Mohsen and famous as Taj al-Va’ezin, whose powerful preaching sessions became the subject of many stories, was born around 1227 solar. At the age of fifteen, he traveled to Najaf with his father. His father passed away there, and he returned to Nishabur. At twenty, he went to Mecca with his mother. Upon returning, he stayed in Nishabur for six years and got married there. At twenty-six, he went to Tabriz, where he faced jealousy and enmity. He then traveled to Karbala, and on his way back visited Shiraz and Isfahan, where he remarried.</p>\n\n<p>In 1313 AH (around 1275 solar), Haj Taj was in Baku when he heard the news of Naser al-Din Shah’s assassination. From there he went to Turkey. He would stay in different cities for periods of time. <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%84%d8%ad%d8%b3%d9%86-%d8%b5%d8%a8%d8%a7/\">Master Abolhasan Saba</a>, while teaching the Masnavi-e Gabri, would say: “Haj Taj was very clever and quick-witted. On the pulpit, he would first speak with a deep voice, then at the right moment suddenly raise his voice and captivate the entire gathering with his beautiful and attractive tone.”</p>\n\n<p>He was an eloquent and well-timed speaker with an attractive appearance and a captivating voice. In the later years of his life, he turned toward Sufism. He was born in Nishabur and would deliver sermons in mourning gatherings (rowzeh) in various cities. Every year during the holy month of Ramadan, he would preach at Aqa Baba Khan Mosque in Shiraz, where he enjoyed great popularity. Sometimes this popularity caused jealousy among some local influential figures, leading them to plot against him. Haj Taj Nishaburi passed away around 1305 solar.</p>\n\n<p>During a trip to Shiraz, as usual, Haj Taj went to preach at Aqa Baba Khan Mosque, and a large crowd of admirers gathered there. Ali Akbar Fal-e Asiri, who wanted to become the unrivaled mujtahid of Shiraz, was displeased that any of the preachers gained the attention of the people of Shiraz. To achieve his goal, he was willing to do anything. On the seventh day of Ramadan, Haj Taj Nishaburi ascended the pulpit as usual. After the customary opening, he recited this ghazal by Hafez with a clear and moving voice:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Del miravad ze dastam saheb delan khodara  \ndardā ke rāz-e penhān khāhad shod āshkārā</p>\n\n<p>When he reached this couplet:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Ān talkh-vash ke sufi umm al-khabā’ethash khānad  \nashbā lanā va ahlā min qeblat al-’adhārā</p>\n\n<p>A group of Fal-e Asiri’s supporters who were at the foot of the pulpit suddenly shouted “Wā shari’ata!” One of them stood up and said: “People, this poem is blasphemy because it has called the Prophet ‘Sufi.’ Therefore both the poet and the preacher who recited it are infidels.” A group of troublemakers, who had been pre-arranged and incited by Fal-e Asiri, pulled Haj Taj down from the pulpit, put his turban around his neck, and dragged him through the bazaar to the Vakil Mosque to Fal-e Asiri. Each of those present suggested a punishment for Haj Taj — shaving his beard, flogging, or exile from Shiraz were among the proposed penalties.</p>\n\n<p>A man named Molla Gholam, who had a rough face and tall stature, made a strange suggestion and took charge of Haj Taj. The surrounding crowd became agitated and headed toward Hafeziyeh to destroy Hafez’s tomb. The troublemakers broke the enclosure of the tomb with pickaxes and mattocks. The first person who reached the grave struck the corner of the tombstone with his pickaxe, causing a small piece of stone to fly into his eye, drawing blood. The attackers were terrified by this apparent miracle of the Master and, in a state of shock, returned to the Vakil Mosque.</p>\n\n<p>Dr. Sasan Sepanta extracted and analyzed the sound spectrum of a recorded sermon by Haj Taj Nishaburi. He transferred it to a spectrograph disc and subjected it to laboratory analysis. The description of this analysis was presented in his article “The Melody of Speech and Music” in the proceedings of the Third Linguistics Conference at Allameh Tabatabai University (5–6 Esfand 1374 solar). Interested readers can refer to that collection.</p>\n\n<p>Master Ali Tajvidi included the notation of the Masnavi in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%a7%d8%b5%d9%81%d9%87%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Bayat-e Esfahan</a> by Haj Taj Nishaburi, as narrated by the late Master Abolhasan Saba, in the first volume of his music book (published 1375 solar). Haj Taj Nishaburi also had a poetic talent and composed verses of mourning and moral advice. As an example, the following poem is from him:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Yeki mast dar kucheh o rahgozar  \nbazad mosht bar Ahmad-e Dinvar</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Beshod sheikh dar khāneh o dar jazāsh  \nferestād halvā-ye shirin barāsh</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Yeki az sahābeh begoft ey ajab  \nkhori mosht o halvā feresti be shab</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Javābash chonin goft ān sheikh-e rād  \nke bāyad bar-e mast halvā nehād</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Rasul-e mokarram jazā-ye jafā  \nhami kard dar haqq-e ommat do’ā</p>\n\n<p>From the samples of his preaching sessions that Dr. Sasan Sepanta extracted and listened to from wax cylinders, it appears that Haj Taj would begin his sermon with a verse from the Holy Quran. Afterward, he would warm up the gathering by reciting a poem in the style of a masnavi — recorded in Bayat-e Esfahan during that session. He would use sustained notes, appropriate delicate trills on the vowels of the poetry, and pleasant pauses between certain sections of speech, sometimes rendering even non-poetic prose in a melodic manner.</p>\n\n<p>The melodic contour of his speech was usually based on one of the gushehs of the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%af%db%8c%d9%81/\">traditional radif</a>. After delivering these rhythmic phrases with a bold tone and firm delivery — whose fundamental frequency was lower than in the previous melodic section — he would continue with other phrases. According to Dr. Sasan Sepanta’s laboratory measurements, the average fundamental pitch of both his speech and singing was around the note Mi (approximately 329 Hz). For variety, he would sometimes sing in the upper octave and sometimes in the lower octave.</p>\n\n<p>Haj Taj Nishaburi’s manner of speech was firm, eloquent, measured, and when speaking, somewhat similar to the style of traditional Shahnameh reciters (morsheds). His period of fame was mainly in the later years of Naser al-Din Shah’s reign and the early years of Mozaffar al-Din Shah. It is said that Sheikh Ismail Taj Esfahani, the father of the famous singer Jalal Taj Esfahani, was known as “Nim Taj” (Half Taj) during Haj Taj’s lifetime. After Haj Taj Nishaburi’s death, he chose the title Taj al-Va’ezin for himself.</p>', '2024-03-03 21:39:57', 0, '2024-12-19 19:06:33', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1572, 'posts', 115, 'en', NULL, 'Seyed Rahim Esfahani', NULL, 'Seyed Rahim Esfahani was one of the most influential singers of the Isfahan vocal tradition in Iranian classical music. He was known for his powerful voice, precise ornamentation, and balanced, disciplined singing style. He performed in the court of Zill al-Sultan and for prominent families in Isfahan, and trained important disciples such as Jalal Taj Esfahani and Adib Khansari. His teaching emphasized delivering the poetry clearly before adding vocal ornamentation, a principle that strongly influenced later generations of singers. Although only a few recordings of his voice survive, they demonstrate his mastery of classical Persian vocal music. He passed away around 1918 in Isfahan.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Seyed Rahim Esfahani</h2>\n\n<p>Seyed Rahim Esfahani was one of the most famous singers, especially in Isfahan. He performed in the court of Zell al-Soltan and in the homes of prominent Isfahan families. Seyed Rahim had a wide and powerful voice, and his trills were clear and well-articulated. He did not overuse filler words (such as “aman” or “deli deli”), which were common among singers of the ta’zieh style. His trills were measured, and he sang avaz with ease and calm confidence.</p>\n\n<p>Seyed Rahim’s fame spread from Isfahan throughout Iran. Taj Esfahani and Adib Khansari were his direct students, and Habib Shater Haji as well as instrumentalists such as Akbar Khan Nowruzi and Hossein Shahnaz (son of Shaban Khan) were among his close musical companions. Seyed Rahim Esfahani was a noble and dervish-like man. He had excellent taste in choosing appropriate poetry for the occasion and would consult scholars to correct the pronunciation of verses. He always selected poems that suited the mood of the gathering.</p>\n\n<p>Taj Esfahani said: “Seyed was extremely precise in teaching. He especially recommended trills in the right places and in moderation, and he warned against placing inappropriate trills in the middle of words or hemistichs.”</p>\n\n<p>Taj’s famous remark is: “Seyed used to say: first deliver the poem, then add the trills…”</p>\n\n<p>The late Reza Abu Ata, a famous painter from Isfahan and instructor at the Boys’ School of Fine Arts (formerly Honarha-ye Ziba), told Dr. Sasan Sepanta in response to his inquiry about Seyed Rahim Esfahani that Seyed’s house was located in the Sar-e Lat-e Mohammad Hasan Beyk neighborhood (near Dar-e Kushk) in Isfahan. In his youth, Reza Abu Ata had gone to Seyed to learn singing. After a few sessions, Seyed told him: “I observe my students carefully in every respect. Your voice may be trainable, but singing requires self-confidence and boldness. You have a modest and shy nature, and this quality becomes an obstacle when singing in front of an audience. Since you have talent in painting, pursue that art — you will succeed more quickly.”</p>\n\n<p>The late Abu Ata said: “I heeded Seyed’s advice and, when the School of Fine Arts opened in 1315 solar, I studied painting under the late Isa Bahadori.”</p>\n\n<p>Seyed Rahim did not travel to Tehran to record gramophone records during the reign of Mozaffar al-Din Shah. However, a few wax cylinders were recorded of his voice in prominent Isfahan families. One was accompanied by the ney of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%86%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%a8-%d8%a7%d8%b3%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Nayeb Asadollah</a>, and another with the tar of Mirza Baqer. Dr. Sasan Sepanta rediscovered and restored one of them in the 1340s solar. The representative of the Gramophone Company, who had come to Iran during the Mozaffar al-Din Shah period to record discs, negotiated with Monsieur Lemaire, the head of military music, to introduce his own orchestra and a group of musicians and singers for recording.</p>\n\n<p>After recording some orchestral pieces, it became clear that <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%84%d9%81%d8%b1%d8%af-%da%98%d8%a7%d9%86-%d8%a8%d8%a7%d9%be%d8%aa%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa-%d9%84%d9%88%d9%85%d8%b1/\">Lemaire</a> did not want to pay the singers. For this reason, average singers were used for the first series of recordings, and the voices of singers such as Seyed Rahim Esfahani or <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ad%d8%a7%d8%ac-%d8%aa%d8%a7%d8%ac-%d9%86%db%8c%d8%b4%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Haj Taj Nishaburi</a> were not recorded on gramophone discs.</p>\n\n<p>Seyed Rahim’s son, Aqa Vahab, studied with his father but turned to playing the tar. Master Homayi wrote: “In short, Seyed Rahim Esfahani, along with all other singers and musicians, was the last singer who had complete mastery of the secrets of old music and possessed his own innovations. All singers after him — such as Taj, Adib, Habib Shater Haji, Seyed Hossein Taherzadeh, and others — followed Seyed Rahim’s school, whether they formally studied with him or simply heard his voice and imitated it.” [Homayi, 1375 solar]</p>\n\n<p>Among his students, in addition to those already mentioned, were: <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b3%db%8c%d8%af-%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%b7%d8%a7%d9%87%d8%b1%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%af%d9%87/\">Seyed Hossein Taherzadeh</a> (indirectly), Qazi Asgar, and Hossein Adib (of Takht-e Fulad). Also, Molla Hossein Yazdi, known as “Hossein-e Musiqi,” can be named.</p>\n\n<p>Dr. Sasan Sepanta obtained some information about Seyed Rahim Esfahani from his direct student, Mr. Hossein Adib, and recorded it.</p>\n\n<p>Seyed Ali Mohammad Qazi Asgar was a renowned radif master from Isfahan who worked as a herbalist. His singing style was influenced by Seyed Rahim Esfahani and Habib Sarcheshmeh. A tape of his avaz accompanied by the violin of Master <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%84%d8%ad%d8%b3%d9%86-%d8%b5%d8%a8%d8%a7/\">Abolhasan Saba</a> in Bayat-e Esfahan and Homayun has survived. In it, he sings this poem by Sa’di:</p>\n\n<p>Asudeh khateram ke to dar khater-e mani  \ngar taj mi feresti va gar tigh mizani</p>\n\n<p>This tape was privately recorded in 1329 solar at the home of Reza Kasa’i during Master Saba’s trip to Isfahan. At the age of forty-eight, Master Saba presents the avaz with strong technique, while Qazi Asgar demonstrates excellent delivery of the poetry and a fine example of appropriate and measured trills in the Isfahan singing style. Seyed Rahim Esfahani passed away around 1318 solar in Isfahan.</p>', '2024-03-04 21:50:50', 0, '2024-08-30 21:30:03', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1573, 'posts', 116, 'en', NULL, 'Abdollah Davami', NULL, 'Abdollah Davami (1891–1979) was one of the most important preservers of Persian classical vocal music and traditional تصنیف repertoire. He studied under masters such as Agha Hosseinqoli, Darvish Khan, and Mirza Abdollah, gaining deep knowledge of both vocal performance and classical music traditions. Davami played a crucial role in transmitting old songs and trained notable students including Faegheh Saba, Mahmoud Karimi, Marzieh, and others. One of his most famous recordings is the Afshari تصنیف by Aref Qazvini, which was preserved through early gramophone recordings and is accompanied by well-known anecdotes involving Darvish Khan. He is regarded as a master of rhythmic precision and a key figure in preserving Persian vocal heritage.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Abdollah Davami</h2>\n\n<p>He was born in 1270 solar. He learned singing from Ali Khan Nayeb al-Saltaneh (known as “the one with the torn throat”) and acquired fine points from <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86%d9%82%d9%84%db%8c/\">Aqa Hossein-Qoli</a>, Hossein Khan Kamancheh-kesh, Darvish Khan, Majd al-Zakerin, and Mirza Abdollah. He had an excellent command of the old tasnifs by heart. In the evening classes at the Music School, he trained students including Fakhr-e Saba, Mahmoud Karimi, Marziyeh, Khatereh Parvaneh, Homa, and Elaheh.</p>\n\n<p>Mohammad Reza Shajarian also benefited from his teachings. There are several records featuring Abdollah Davami, the most famous of which is the tasnif in Afshari by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%81-%d9%82%d8%b2%d9%88%db%8c%d9%86%db%8c/\">Aref Qazvini</a>, which begins with these lines:</p>\n\n<p>Az kafam raha shod qarar-e del  \nnist dast-e man ekhtiyar-e del</p>\n\n<p>Abdollah Davami recounted: “During Darvish Khan’s second trip to Tbilisi, Aref, who had come to see them off, asked him to record the tenth tasnif related to Shushtar, composed by Aref. Darvish Khan refused and said this tasnif is political and we are not involved in politics.</p>\n\n<p>Because of the promise he had given to Aref, during the recording session Abdollah Davami suddenly began singing that tasnif. <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ba%d9%84%d8%a7%d9%85%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%af%d8%b1%d9%88%db%8c%d8%b4/\">Darvish Khan</a>, in a fit of anger, struck the tar skin with his fist and tore it. Eventually, at Davami’s insistence, he agreed to record the Afshari tasnif with the above opening lines — which had no political content — with Abdollah Davami’s voice and his own tar accompaniment. That record still exists today.”</p>\n\n<p>Abdollah Davami passed away in 1359 solar.</p>\n\n<p>Ruhollah Khaleqi writes: “It was customary for those who were complete masters of the dastgahs and expert avaz singers to consider singing <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%aa%d8%b5%d9%86%db%8c%d9%81/\">tasnif</a> beneath their dignity. Those with weaker voices showed greater interest in singing tasnifs and metric melodies and became skilled in this art. To become well acquainted with rhythm, they would also play the tombak while singing tasnifs.”</p>\n\n<p>For example, it was said that Taherzadeh was a dastgah master and avaz singer, while Mr. Abdollah Davami was a rhythm expert and tasnif singer. [Khaleqi, 1342 solar]</p>', '2024-03-04 22:11:28', 0, '2024-08-30 21:29:50', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1574, 'posts', 117, 'en', NULL, 'Hossein Ali Vaziri Tabar', NULL, 'Hossein Ali Vaziri-Tabar (1906–1958) was a distinguished Iranian clarinet (qarey-ni) player and an important figure in modern Persian classical music. He began his musical training in childhood and studied under masters such as Salar Mo’azzaz, Naser-ol-Molk Minbashian, and Ali-Naqi Vaziri. Vaziri-Tabar gained fame for his exceptional ability to perform Iranian music on the clarinet, participating in major ensembles including Iranian National Music Association and Radio orchestras, as well as the famous “Golha” programs. He also worked as a teacher and composer in cities like Shiraz, creating pieces such as “In Memory of Shiraz.” His precision, deep understanding of Persian musical modes, and unique interpretation of traditional melodies made him one of the most distinctive instrumentalists in Iranian music history.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Hossein Ali Vaziri Tabar</h2>\n\n<p>Hossein Ali Vaziri Tabar was born in 1285 solar. When he was twelve years old, his father encouraged him to learn music. From childhood, he loved the qarnei (clarinet). His first teacher, Salar Mo’azzaz, noticing the shape of Hossein Ali’s lips, mouth, and fingers, encouraged him to play the qarnei. He studied his favorite instrument under Colonel Nasrollah Minbashian and later joined the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b3%d9%87-%d8%b9%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">Vaziri School of Music</a>, where he participated in its concerts.</p>\n\n<p>Hossein Ali Vaziri Tabar held special respect for his teacher Ali-Naqi Vaziri and would say: “The night when the musical tableau ‘Midnight’ or ‘Hafez’s Dream,’ composed by Vaziri, was performed at the school’s concert, it created a poetic scene.”</p>\n\n<p>From the top of an old willow tree whose branches spread over the stage of “Hafez’s Dream,” the enchanting sound of the nightingale reached the ears of the audience. This sound was so delightful that everyone thought a real nightingale was singing in that charming and emotional “midnight” atmosphere. Later, when Vaziri introduced the performer of the nightingale’s voice, everyone realized that the sound had come from the qarnei of Hossein Ali Vaziri Tabar.</p>\n\n<p>In 1310 solar, Hossein Ali Vaziri Tabar went to Baghdad to teach music and singing in Iranian schools and taught for one year at the “Okhovat” School for Iranians. After returning to Tehran, he continued his studies at the Higher School of Music and received his diploma in 1315 solar. He then went to Shiraz to teach music and singing. The happiest memories of his life were his nine-year stay in Shiraz. There, he composed a melody based on a ghazal by Hafez called “In Memory of Shiraz,” which begins with this couplet:</p>\n\n<p>I have said it many times and say it once again  \nthat I have become a wanderer on this path not by my own will.</p>\n\n<p>This melody, composed in Dastgah-e Shur, was performed by the orchestra of the National Music Association under the direction of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a> with the voice of Banan. Another of his works is the tasnif in Bayat-e Esfahan with lyrics by Pakbin, whose first couplet is:</p>\n\n<p>O flower of the world’s garden, source of my hope  \nI have seen no fruit in life except sorrow for you.</p>\n\n<p>After returning from Shiraz, Hossein Ali Vaziri Tabar played the qarnei in the modern radio orchestra under the direction of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d9%86%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Ali-Naqi Vaziri</a>. Later, when the orchestra of the National Music Association was formed, he joined it.</p>\n\n<p>He praised Colonel Vaziri and Khaleqi for their composition and considered <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%84%d8%ad%d8%b3%d9%86-%d8%b5%d8%a8%d8%a7/\">Abolhasan Saba</a> unparalleled in performance. He enjoyed <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d9%81%d8%b4%d8%a7%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Avaz-e Afshari</a> and would become deeply upset when faced with lies and pretension. He was fond of the voice of Qamar-ol-Moluk Vaziri, praised the zarbi pieces sung by Moluk Zarabi and the enchanting and soul-nourishing voice of Ruhangiz, and had a special affection for Banan’s voice.</p>\n\n<p>He was dissatisfied with the radio and from the 1330s onward would say: “Our music, in the way it is currently performed on radio, will never progress.”</p>\n\n<p>In 1331 solar, his monthly salary at the radio was only 240 tomans, and he would say: “Recently they have deducted another 20 tomans, and I am waiting for them to cut the rest as well!”</p>\n\n<p>He received one letter of commendation, two medals, and one order from the Ministry of Culture at the time.</p>\n\n<p>In 1328 solar, he became a teacher at the National Music School. A few years later, he played the qarnei in Orchestra No. 1 under the direction of Javad Ma’rufi and also in the Golha Orchestra. Hossein Ali Vaziri Tabar performed on the qarnei in the early Golha-ye Rangarang radio programs, such as Golha No. 176 (led by Abolhasan Saba in Dashti with Banan’s voice, melody by Hossein Yahaghi, and lyrics by Rahe Mo’ayyeri — “Did you see, O moon, how you suddenly fled and left?”) and Golha-ye Rangarang No. 126 titled “Nava-ye Ney” in Dashti with the voice of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ba%d9%84%d8%a7%d9%85%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%a8%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Gholamhossein Banan</a>, melody by Morteza Mahjubi, and lyrics by Rahe Mo’ayyeri (“Such was the sound of the ney that it set my soul on fire”).</p>\n\n<p>One of the earliest recorded works of Hossein Ali Vaziri Tabar is his solo qarnei performance in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%87%d9%85%d8%a7%db%8c%d9%88%d9%86/\">Dastgah-e Homayun</a>, which has survived among the gramophone records of the Vaziri Higher School of Music. This record was produced by the Odeon factory in Germany and was recorded in 1308 solar with catalog number A-2435600.</p>\n\n<p>Hossein Ali Vaziri Tabar was the only musician who played Iranian music with a beautiful style and the necessary skill on the European instrument clarinet (qarnei), and to this day no other performer has been able to equal him on this instrument. He performed the notes correctly and completely on the qarnei and had a precise and sensitive ear in this regard.</p>\n\n<p>He possessed the necessary precision and skill in responding to avaz, in solo performance, and in working with the orchestra and reading notation. In developing Iranian musical phrases and in his excellent taste in expressing and conveying moods and interpretations on the qarnei, he was unrivaled.</p>\n\n<p>Hossein Ali Vaziri Tabar finally passed away on 23 Mordad 1337 solar at the age of fifty-two.</p>', '2024-03-05 22:08:43', 0, '2024-04-15 16:07:58', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1575, 'posts', 118, 'en', NULL, 'The zarbi-avazi form in Iranian music', NULL, 'This article examines the concept of “Zarbi Avazik” in Iranian classical (Dastgahi) music from the perspective of Arshad Tahmasebi, tracing its historical roots and performance practice, particularly in the Qajar era and earlier. In this style, singers perform improvised poetry within a fixed rhythmic framework—most commonly in 6/8 or 6/4 meter—while relying on strong rhythmic skills and familiarity with percussion instruments such as the tombak. The study shows that zarbi singing was not only linked to composed tasnifs but also represented a broader improvisational ability among skilled vocalists, with connections to traditional practices such as Zurkhaneh chanting. Drawing on accounts from figures like Ruhollah Khaleghi, the article concludes that “Zarbi Avazik” is a metrically structured yet improvisational vocal form that has significantly declined in modern Iranian music practice.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the form of <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Vocal Zarbi</span></strong> (Zarbi Avazik) in Iranian dastgahi music from the perspective of the prominent Iranian musician and radif master, “Arshad Tahmasbi.” Stay with us for the rest of the article...</p>\n\n<h2>Vocal Zarbi (Zarbi Avazik)</h2>\n\n<p>It appears that the term “zarbi” was common among musicians during the Qajar period. As Ruhollah Khaleqi explained when describing the nature of Iranian music of that era:</p>\n\n<p>The common music of that period, in terms of primary importance, was the radif of avaz. A musician would play it, and the singer would sing the same melody while hearing the response from the instrument. This was a section performed solo because it did not always have a regular meter that would allow several musicians to play together. Some singers — that is, those who played percussion and were familiar with metric melodies — would sing a type of avaz that had meter, and they called it “zarbi avaz.” In this way, they would choose a specific meter and sing a ghazal in one dastgah, while highlighting the important gushehs of the radif.</p>\n\n<p>The meter of zarbi avaz, as is still common today, was <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%88%d8%b2%d9%86-%d8%ae%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">6/8</a>. During trills, glissandi, and sustained notes, although the singer would free himself from the strict meter, because he was simultaneously playing the tombak, he would not lose the 6/8 meter while singing, and would eventually return the avaz to that rhythmic framework.</p>\n\n<p>In the book *Buhur al-Alhan* by Forsat Shirazi, written around 1282 solar, there is an introduction in which, for the first time after the decline of the old maqam system, topics about the characteristics of dastgahi music and its new terminology are discussed. Among these, one can mention <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d9%be%db%8c%d8%b4-%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%a2%d9%85%d8%af/\">pishdaramad</a>, reng, chaharmezrab, and so on. However, the term “zarbi” is not mentioned anywhere in this book:</p>\n\n<p>Now we shall begin to mention the new terminology. Know that in this recent century, the sages and scholars of this art have changed the old dastgahs [maqams] of the ancients and have organized them into seven dastgahs.</p>\n\n<p>From this, one can assume that for the author of *Buhur al-Alhan*, the term “zarbi” was relatively obscure compared to terms such as reng and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%86%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d8%b6%d8%b1%d8%a7%d8%a8/\">chaharmezrab</a>. Nevertheless, we know that at least this type of zarbi singing has been practiced since ancient times by the morsheds (masters) of zurkhanehs:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Hassan Mashhoun: Some zurkhaneh percussionists, who were called morshed, were skilled in singing <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%aa%d8%b5%d9%86%db%8c%d9%81/\">tasnifs</a> and metric melodies. With their pleasant or suitable voices, they would sing epic poems in specific maqams, including <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Dastgah Chahargah</a>, and for each athletic movement they would perform appropriate epic and moral poems in various meters.</p>\n\n<p>The foundation of the work of skilled traditional singers in performing zarbi was based on their ability to improvise. Apart from being proficient in singing pre-composed old tasnifs, such singers would sometimes also improvise tasnifs. In fact, an expert singer of music, just as he could improvise non-metric avaz, also had the ability to perform poetry in metric forms improvisationally — that is, to do “zarbi singing.” Apparently, as <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Khaleqi</a> mentioned, this type of zarbi singing was mostly performed in 6/8 meter in the past:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Javad Badie Zadeh: The above ghazal kept us entertained for an hour or two because it suited our mood. I sang part of the ghazal as avaz and part of it as zarbi.</p>\n\n<p>Of course, for a singer, spontaneously matching poetry to a “zarbi” piece is usually more difficult than performing it as “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">avaz</a>.” We also know that most singers of the Qajar period generally focused on avaz singing, and only some of them were skilled and successful in singing tasnifs and zarbis — for example, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%af%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%85%db%8c/\">Abdollah Davami</a>, who was more famous as a tasnif and zarbi singer than as an avaz performer.</p>\n\n<p>In the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%af%db%8c%d9%81/\">radif of Iranian music</a>, there are gushehs named after individuals whose melodic invention is attributed to specific musicians who mostly lived in the late Qajar period. Such melodies, most of which are in the form of tahrir, gained attention from other musicians of their time, became popular, and found a place in various radifs. The following gushehs are among them:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">- Mohammad Sadeq Khani and Sorour al-Molki, innovations of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b5%d8%a7%d8%af%d9%82-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%86-%d8%b3%d9%8f%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%85%d9%84%da%a9/\">Mohammad Sadeq Khan</a> (santur player);</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">- Haji Hassani, innovation of Haji Hassan (singer);</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">- Javad Khani, innovation of Javad Khan (kamancheh player);</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">- Mirza’i, innovation of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87/\">Mirza Abdollah</a> (tar player);</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">- Nayeb Asadollahi, innovation of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%86%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%a8-%d8%a7%d8%b3%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Nayeb Asadollah Khan</a> (ney player);</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">- Shahabi, innovation of Shahab al-Sadat Esfahani (singer), and so on.</p>\n\n<p>Among these, there is a famous gusheh called “Mehdi Zarabi” in Avaz-e Bayat-e Tork whose inventor remains unknown, and no person by this name is mentioned in any contemporary historical texts. This indicates that he lived before Aqa Ali Akbar Farahani and others, probably in the early Qajar period or even earlier.</p>\n\n<p>His title (Zarabi) indicates that he was a singer who was skilled in both playing and singing zarbi. Therefore, it can be said that zarbi singing in dastgahi music has a history that dates back at least to the pre-Qajar era. This is evident in the fact that most singers in the Republic of Azerbaijan — which was once part of Iran — still follow this tradition and, in addition to singing, are highly skilled in playing the daf.</p>\n\n<p>A singer of music, apart from innate creativity, reaches mastery in zarbi singing only when he is proficient and expert in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%85-%d8%af%d8%b1-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">rhythmology</a> and has a strong command of meter. It is obvious that this mastery is acquired more quickly and effectively by playing instruments such as the daf and tombak. During the Pahlavi era, artists such as Moluk Zarabi and tombak player Hossein Tehrani were famous and highly skilled in zarbi singing:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Ruhollah Khaleqi: For this reason, singers who had the talent for this work first became familiar with playing the tombak — which is the foundation of meter in our music — and practiced metric melodies with the help of this musical instrument until they gradually became familiar with different meters and were able to sing metric melodies while playing the tombak. Some of them became so skilled in this art that they became masters of percussion and tasnif.</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Hassan Mashhoun: Ali Khan Nayeb al-Saltaneh was one of the first-rank and famous singers of the Mozaffari era […] He had a resonant, powerful, and full-toned six-dang voice, and his vocal cords and throat were so strong that he was also called “Ali Khan with the torn throat” […] He was also skilled in playing percussion […] and was proficient in singing metric melodies.</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Music of Iran: Most of the neighbors of Haji Hossein, who still live in the Marvi Bazaar area, still believe that Eqbal al-Soltan had a “relationship” with Haji Hossein Aqa’s young daughter, and they have not forgotten that whenever Eqbal’s voice rang out in his house, a delicate and delightful voice from the neighbor’s daughter would respond to him […] But we can confidently claim that Moluk’s relationship with Eqbal was the purest relationship between a man and a woman […] Meanwhile, she began studying and learning percussion with the late Haji Khan Ein al-Dowleh, and one year later she composed the melody “Ashegham Man” [Ashegham man, manam nakonid, dardam berasid] and recorded it on a record, which was her first record.</p>\n\n<p>As you know, most old tasnifs were in 6/4 and 6/8 meters (compound two-beat), and therefore “zarbi” pieces were also mostly sung in compound meters according to the same rule. In contemporary Iranian music, vocal zarbi singing has almost become obsolete because, overall, the level of today’s singers — especially in poetic memorization, improvisation, and appropriate delivery — has greatly declined compared to the singers of the past.</p>\n\n<p>Based on the definition we provided for tasnif, we can define “Vocal Zarbi” as follows:</p>\n\n<p><strong>“A metric, vocal, and improvisational genre that is usually performed in 6/8 meter.”</strong></p>\n\n<p>The article you just read under the title “Vocal Zarbi” is taken from the book “The Nature of Iranian Music – An Exploration of Musical Genres” by Arshad Tahmasbi.</p>', '2024-03-08 14:14:04', 0, '2024-08-30 21:29:35', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1576, 'posts', 119, 'en', NULL, 'The zarbi-sazi form in Iranian music', NULL, 'The article “Zarbi-ye Sazik” examines the role of metric and improvised instrumental forms in Iranian classical music, particularly before and after the Constitutional era, from the perspective of Arshad Tahmasbi. In this period, due to the absence of musical notation and recording technology, music relied heavily on memory and improvisation, while composed works were rare. Metric pieces mainly existed in forms such as chahar-mezrab and reng and were often used to accompany vocal performance or group interaction. The article argues that “zarbi-ye sazik” originates from improvisational playing and can serve as a foundation for later composed works. With the rise of radio and programs like Golha, such performances evolved into interactive question-and-answer improvisations. Ultimately, zarbi-ye sazik is defined as a metric, instrumental, and improvisational form, typically performed in compound meters such as 6/8.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the form of <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Instrumental Zarbi</span></strong> (Zarbi Sazik) in Iranian dastgahi music from the perspective of the prominent Iranian musician and radif master, “Arshad Tahmasbi.” Stay with us for the rest of the article...</p>\n\n<h2>Instrumental Zarbi (Zarbi Sazik)</h2>\n\n<p>Essentially, in Iranian music before the era of Darvish Khan, Vaziri, and Mokhtari — or more accurately, before the Constitutional Revolution — composition in the modern sense of the word had no meaning. Most performers, whose performances took place only in private gatherings, relied solely on the memorized radif repertoire passed down from previous generations. In that period, instrumental music had no independence, group playing was not common, and therefore the main duty of solo instrumentalists was limited to responding to singers and following vocal music.</p>\n\n<p>The lack of familiarity with musical notation and the absence of recording tools meant there was no possibility of preserving works, whether written or recorded. As a result, musicians could only rely on their memory. Considering these factors, along with the mindset of society at that time toward <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9-%db%8c%d8%a7-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%da%86%db%8c%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%9f/\">music</a> and its practitioners, performers did not feel the need to create specific metric composed works. When there was no group playing or concerts, no books, scores, or buyers, what was the necessity of producing well-organized, documented, and coherent works?</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Ruhollah Khaleqi: The metric sections that several musicians could play together were <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%aa%d8%b5%d9%86%db%8c%d9%81/\">tasnifs</a> and radif rengs […] But even the rengs were limited […] Therefore, it appears that composing new melodies was not very common, and musicians spent their time practicing and repeating the old melodies that had survived […]</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">After the above introduction, we reach the conclusion that when an orchestra consisting of several musicians wanted to begin a group performance with a more substantial introduction, they had very few pieces available. For this reason, the music of a small ensemble would also begin with avaz, in which one player would perform a chaharmezrab or daramad and the singer would start the ghazal […]</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Therefore, for the start of a concert, other, more extended pieces were needed that would last several minutes and prepare the audience for listening to solo instrumental and vocal music. This need led to the creation of the pishdaramad […] It is said that when a concert was to be given, during rehearsal Rokn al-Din Khan suggested that it would be good to play a piece together first and then proceed to the avaz.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">When asked which piece, the proposer said he had a melody in this style. The audience wanted to hear it, so the composer played the piece and it was well received.</p>\n\n<p>Thus, before musicians after the Constitutional Revolution turned to creating new works, the metric instrumental pieces of that period were limited to radif chaharmezrabs and rengs, and at most one or two rengs composed by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86%d9%82%d9%84%db%8c/\">Aqa Hossein-Qoli</a> and Hossein Esma’ilzadeh. Nevertheless, it is hard to believe that performers such as Sama’ Hozur and Aqa Hossein-Qoli only contented themselves with playing these ten or twenty metric radif pieces. Therefore, it is certain that performing improvised “zarbi” pieces, especially for the virtuoso performers of that time, could compensate for this shortage and gap.</p>\n\n<p>Fundamentally, “improvised zarbi playing” is the origin of the emergence of every other “composed” form, because every existing “composed” work, by any composer, initially took shape through a “zarbi-playing” action by that composer. If a pleasant and appealing theme emerges from the mind of a musician who is practicing, warming up, and improvising in solitude, he naturally notices it and tries to create a work based on that theme — of course, this action will involve trial and error.</p>\n\n<p>Usually, no composed work is created all at once without revision and refinement. For example, it seems highly unlikely that Ali-Naqi Vaziri, when composing a piece like “Disheveled Girl,” played the first stroke on the tar and then composed the entire piece from beginning to end without any editing or polishing.</p>\n\n<p>Even if that were the case (which it is not), it must still be said that Vaziri performed an improvised zarbi that resulted in a coherent melody with a structured form that can be considered a “composed work” on the scale of a finished piece. However, we know that creating a “signed” work — not only in Iranian music but in the music of any country in the world — does not happen suddenly and instantaneously. Every composer repeatedly revises and refines his work until he finally reaches the point where he can call it a “work” and feel satisfied with the result:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Ruhollah Khaleqi: For several consecutive days we did not see the master; he was so busy working in his room that he had no time to come to us. We became worried. His servant said that sometimes he was busy with the piano and sometimes with the tar, but mostly he was writing, and his lamp stayed on until morning. One day he brought a pile of large sheets of paper covered with fine handwriting and gave them to us to copy. Without saying a word, he went back to his room and continued his work. At the top of the first page was written: “Do Asheq” (Two Lovers), composed by Vaziri, with lyrics by Hafez.</p>\n\n<p>In this way, improvised instrumental zarbis performed in gatherings, concerts, or live media broadcasts differ from pre-composed works only in that there is no opportunity for revision and refinement. Based on this, we can conclude that, except in exceptional cases, the zarbis played by a performer-composer never possess the same solidity and coherence as the pre-composed works of that same performer-composer.</p>\n\n<p>In general, the name of this genre indicates its main characteristic: being “metric” — something that a percussionist (tombak player) can accompany. For this reason, “zarbi” in instrumental music, in addition to being a specific title for metric improvisation, can also serve as a general title for all pre-composed metric genres:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">- Broken Zarbi,</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">- Segah Zarbi,</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">- Zarbi Piece.</p>\n\n<p>Following this general approach to the term “zarbi,” we see that in instructional books, notated radifs, and composers’ works, some pieces are introduced with titles such as two-beat, three-beat, four-beat, five-beat, and seven-beat:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">- Two-beat Piece,</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">- Seven-beat Chahargah,</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">- Five-beat Rast-Panjgah,</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">and so on.</p>\n\n<p>Sometimes in certain instructional books, when presenting specific genres such as “pishdaramad,” emphasis is placed on the number of beats in the measure of that piece:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">- Two-beat (Pishdaramad in Dashti),</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">- Pishdaramad (Three-beat),</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">and so on.</p>\n\n<p>Performing zarbi in its specific form as an improvisational genre requires sufficient time to present a complete programmatic music performance. Therefore, one cannot make a fair judgment about the nature of this genre based on the few zarbi works recorded on old gramophone records, because we know that the capacity of those records was only about 3 to 5 minutes.</p>\n\n<p>With the establishment of radio in 1319 solar, solo instrumentalists began performing on this medium. However, since the radio of that time was not equipped with a recording system, all programs were broadcast live. Therefore, here too we cannot rely on evidence about the style and manner of zarbi playing by the musicians of that period. Also, in the early days of radio, the maximum time allocated to <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Iranian music</a> was about 15 minutes, so musicians did not have the opportunity to present all musical genres. In the first Iranian music broadcast on radio, we see that not even a chaharmezrab was performed:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Music program for the inauguration day of Radio Tehran […]</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li style=\"list-style-type: none;\">\n<ol>\n  <li>Homayun (pishdaramad, avaz, tasnif, reng) – 15 minutes</li>\n  <li>Mahur (pishdaramad, avaz, tasnif, reng) – 15 minutes</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Performed by Iranian musicians […].</p>\n\n<p>After some time, radio was equipped with a recording system, and gradually complete music programs were produced. Musicians then had the opportunity to present comprehensive performances that included all genres. Such programs usually lasted between 30 and 40 minutes, and the most famous of them were the programs titled “Golha-ye Rangarang,” prepared by Davud Pirnia. The first Golha-ye Rangarang program (approximately number 100) roughly included the following sections:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li style=\"list-style-type: none;\">\n<ol>\n  <li>Instrumental avaz in question-and-answer form (violin and tar by Ali Tajvidi and Shapur Hatami), about 2 minutes</li>\n  <li>Improvised zarbi in compound meter in question-and-answer form (violin and tar with tombak by Majidi), about 3 minutes</li>\n  <li>Vocal avaz (sung by Marziyeh with violin and tar responses), about 7 minutes</li>\n  <li>Old tasnif “Suratgar-e Naghash-e Chin,” about 3 minutes</li>\n  <li>Old tasnif “Ze Dast-e Mahbub,” about 2 minutes</li>\n  <li>Instrumental avaz (violin), about 1 minute</li>\n  <li>Fast improvised zarbi in compound meter (violin and tombak), about 2 minutes</li>\n  <li>Slow improvised zarbi in compound meter (violin and tombak), about 5 minutes</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>Thus, we see that more than one third of the time of this programmatic music was dedicated to performing improvised zarbis. A noteworthy point here is that in the mentioned program, no <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d9%be%db%8c%d8%b4-%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%a2%d9%85%d8%af/\">pishdaramad</a> or reng was performed, and except for two tasnifs lasting a total of 5 minutes, the rest of the program was based on improvised music. Therefore, the performers — both instrumentalists and the singer — only had to rehearse the tasnifs together. When explaining how he prepared for a concert later released in the album “Astan-e Janan,” Mohammad Reza Shajarian mentioned only rehearsing the tasnifs:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Parviz Meshkatian had a student named Ahmadian who worked in the cultural section of the Italian embassy. He told Parviz that he wanted to arrange a program there for you and Mr. Shajarian to perform a concert. Parviz told me about the offer and I agreed. For this program, we rehearsed and prepared Parviz’s melodies (tasnifs). One summer afternoon in 1362 solar, Parviz, Farhangfar, and I went to the embassy garden in Farmaniyeh and performed that concert for a large audience.</p>\n\n<p>The easiest and fastest way to prepare a <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a8%d8%b1%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%85%d9%87-%d8%a7%db%8c/\">music program</a>, at least for creative and skilled improvisers, is to structure a program based on saz-o avaz and improvised zarbis. In such programs, there is no need for the artists to gather and rehearse pre-composed works. One or two decades after this period, especially in the radio programs “Golha-ye Tazeh,” we see that most of these programs were dedicated to performing such music. For example, in the first ten “Golha-ye Tazeh” programs, only programs number 1 and 5 used orchestral music, while the other eight programs were dedicated to saz-o avaz and improvised zarbis:</p>\n\n<p>Golha-ye Tazeh No. 2 in Avaz-e Abu Ata with the collaboration of:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Abdolvahab Shahidi (vocals and oud), Javad Ma’rufi (piano), Homayoun Khorram (violin), and Jahangir Malek (tombak).</p>\n\n<p>Golha-ye Tazeh No. 3 in Avaz-e Dashti with the collaboration of:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Mahmoudi Khansari (vocals), Habibollah Badi’i (violin), Farhang Sharif (tar), Mansour Saremi (santur), and Jahangir Malek (tombak).</p>\n\n<p>(And similarly for the other programs listed in the original text…)</p>\n\n<p>Improvising artists preparing such programs only needed to choose the dastgah and tonal base according to the singer’s vocal range; after that, nothing else — except tuning the instruments and proceeding with the performance.</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, in a music based on improvisation like Iranian music, the first and last word belongs to “saz-o avaz” and “zarbi pieces.” This characteristic — the reliance of official Iranian music on improvisation — is one of its most prominent virtues. In this type of music, skilled masters can sit together and create music without even having met or known each other before, as some of the enduring published works of Iranian music were recorded in private gatherings without any prior rehearsal. For example, a private performance by Mohammad Reza Shajarian, Mohammad Mousavi, and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%be%d8%b1%d9%88%db%8c%d8%b2-%d9%85%d8%b4%da%a9%d8%a7%d8%aa%db%8c%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Parviz Meshkatian</a> that was first released in the album “Mahur” and later as “Serr-e Eshgh”:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Interviewer: Then you went to Dastan and Nava, compound singing; it seems the vocal part is a recording of a private performance. Where was this work recorded?</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Mohammad Reza Shajarian: Nava was a vocal compound performance that I sang with Mohammad Mousavi at Mr. Mir Abolfathi’s house. The late Sadegh Tabatabai was also there. We had sessions on Thursdays; four or five of us would gather and it was only saz-o avaz. Mohammad Mousavi was the main pillar of these sessions, and each time several other artists would also participate. In these sessions we had, perhaps more than a hundred tapes were recorded, one of which was the Nava compound performance.</p>\n\n<p>Most zarbi performances in radio programs are in question-and-answer form. This type of zarbi has a function and nature similar to “avaz and response to avaz.” In both, the musicians, influenced by each other, become inspired and try to present more novel and beautiful phrases, gradually elevating themselves and the audience to higher levels of ecstasy and pleasure. In the field of “avaz and response to avaz,” the works of Mohammad Reza Shajarian and Mohammad Reza Lotfi are exemplary; one can confidently say they are the best saz-o avaz works in Iranian music:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Mohammad Reza Shajarian: Mohammad Reza Lotfi was undoubtedly a master and a genius in music, and he had his own taste in music. The best situation for him was to be by my side, and for me too, it was a very good situation to work with him. Because the saz-o avaz we created together never happened between me and anyone else, and what happened between the two of us was almost unique. Both of us knew this and cherished those good moments.</p>\n\n<p>Although we said that proficient masters can perform improvised music together without prior acquaintance or rehearsal and create delightful works, there is no doubt that a masterpiece is created when improvising masters who sit together are familiar with one another and deeply know each other’s temperament, style, and musical approach.</p>\n\n<p>We said that question-and-answer music creates mutual influence among improvisers and generates excitement in them to present more innovative themes, which naturally leads to the elevation of the performance. Question-and-answer music is an attractive and pleasant dialogue in which, instead of criticizing or challenging the other, each side praises and supports the other and gives them more space to shine. In this process, some musical improvisers reach such a level of mastery that they can ascend to the peak of musical “ecstasy” relying solely on their inner dialogue.</p>\n\n<p>In my opinion (Arshad Tahmasbi), the foremost among them in the art of solo performance — especially in developing and creating vocal-like phrases — is <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b6%d8%a7-%d9%84%d8%b7%d9%81%db%8c/\">Mohammad Reza Lotfi</a>. In his solo performances, for every question he poses to himself, he gives a worthy answer, and upon hearing his own response, he becomes so delighted that he raises an even more exciting question, continuing this process until he reaches the peak of ecstasy. In his solo playing, like a wise madman, he carries on a “loud” dialogue with himself that leaves even the wise bewildered:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Houshang Ebtehaj: It is astonishing; it is completely characteristic of Lotfi. No performer before him, during his time, or probably after him has done or can do this […] At times I think even Sa’di’s poetry cannot have this much creativity. Look, one after another, all freshness and innovation. From one state to another … Truly, poetry cannot reach this level. Words cannot do this.</p>\n\n<p>In question-and-answer zarbi playing — for example between two players and without considering the tombak player — after the first player performs, the responding player plays themes appropriate to what was just played, and this process continues until the end of the zarbi. However, sometimes the best themes are repeated as a duet, and both players, so to speak, “catch the wave” with it. Therefore, when the number of players exceeds two, these “highlight” phrases are performed as group playing.</p>\n\n<p>At this point, having a strong “ear” and “memory” plays a prominent role in such performances, because each player must remember these phrases after hearing them only once and repeat them immediately. Thus, when players are familiar with each other’s playing style and know the phrasing and technical tricks of the other artists well, they can more quickly and accurately anticipate what their partner will do, and naturally the music of such ensemble playing becomes more unified and beautiful.</p>\n\n<p>The best examples of question-and-answer zarbi playing in Iranian music must be considered the product of the work of a famous “artistic duo”: <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ad%d8%b3%d9%86-%da%a9%d8%b3%d8%a7%db%8c%db%8c/\">Hassan Kasa’i</a> and Jalil Shahnaz. Both were products of the Isfahan music school and natives of that city. Through frequent gatherings in the private circles of music lovers in Isfahan — which have always existed — they became so familiar with each other’s nuances and subtleties that the result of their collaboration can be described in one phrase as “one soul in two bodies.”</p>\n\n<p>Many of the zarbi performances by Kasa’i and Shahnaz, as well as the solo works of other prominent performers, were recorded in radio and private performances without the accompaniment of a tombak player — who plays an essential role in zarbi playing. Nevertheless, there is such spirit and ecstasy in these performances that the absence of the tombak is not felt at all:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Music of Iran: Two years ago in the beautiful city of Isfahan, a twenty-five-year-old young man [Hassan Kasa’i] sat before me. With attractive eyes full of modesty, family dignity, politeness, and innate refinement, he took up the ney, adjusted his fingers, and instead of playing avaz-e Dashti — I don’t know why it sounds better on the ney than on other instruments — he began playing chaharmezrab in Mahur. He created such joy and excitement among the discerning and knowledgeable audience in the gathering that it truly brought pleasure and wonder.</p>\n\n<p>The radio programs “Golha-ye Rangarang,” “Golha-ye Tazeh,” “Soloists,” and other radio programs before 1357 solar are filled with question-and-answer zarbi performances and solo playing performed by improvisers such as:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Hassan Kasa’i and Mohammad Mousavi, etc. (ney players),</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Jalil Shahnaz, Lotfollah Majd, Farhang Sharif, Houshang Zarif, Mohammad Reza Lotfi, etc. (tar players),</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Ahmad Ebadi (setar player),</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Reza Varzandeh, Faramarz Payvar, Mansour Saremi, etc. (santur players),</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Ali Tajvidi, Parviz Yahaghi, Habibollah Badi’i, Asadollah Malek, etc. (violin players),</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Morteza Mahjubi and Javad Ma’rufi (piano players),</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Amir Naser Eftetah, Jahangir Malek, Mohammad Esma’ili, Naser Farhangfar, etc. (tombak players),</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">and players of certain other instruments such as Mansour Nariman (oud), Emad Ram (flute), and others.</p>\n\n<p>These rich surviving recordings clearly show the style and approach of zarbi playing during that period and the foundation upon which it was performed.</p>\n\n<p>In this context, there is no doubt that the most prominent figure in zarbi playing, as acknowledged by most artists, is <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ac%d9%84%db%8c%d9%84-%d8%b4%d9%87%d9%86%d8%a7%d8%b2/\">Jalil Shahnaz</a>:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Ali Tajvidi: It should be noted that although improvisation is one of the characteristics of Iranian music, not every performer who has some skill in playing and even possesses the necessary technique is capable of improvisation. Only those performers who have the power of creativity and the ability to invent beautiful melodies can improvise […] It has often been observed that there are musicians with advanced degrees in composition who are nevertheless unable to create even one beautiful and valuable musical phrase.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">An improviser, apart from having good taste, must possess the necessary technique for playing, must know the radif and vocal style of traditional music well, and must have all the maqams, avaz, and gushehs of the radif in his memory […] A performer who improvises must know the meters and instinctively feel the rhythm he has chosen so that the meter he follows does not suffer from rhythmic arrhythmia […] Master Jalil Shahnaz is one of the few performers who possesses all the necessary conditions for improvisation. Jalil Shahnaz’s playing has a special majesty and grandeur that compels the listener to respect and admire it.</p>\n\n<p>About twenty years ago, I reached an interesting conclusion in a field study. Over several years, whenever the opportunity arose, I played the best metric (zarbi) and non-metric performances from various instruments — according to my own taste — for people from different social backgrounds and asked them to choose one that they liked best and rank it above the others.</p>\n\n<p>The participants in these surveys were educated and professional women and men, students, shopkeepers, repairmen, workers, and others whose musical preferences I did not know in advance. Therefore, they might have been interested in traditional dastgahi music, pop music, Western music, or any other type.</p>\n\n<p>The result was that approximately 70% of the listeners preferred Jalil Shahnaz’s zarbi performances over other zarbis and avaz. From this statistical survey, one can conclude to some extent that most people in Iranian instrumental music prefer the sound of the tar over other instruments and also prefer listening to metric melodies over non-metric avaz.</p>\n\n<p>In general, Iranians, due to widespread poverty and the many hardships they have endured throughout history, have had little opportunity for joy and celebration. For these mostly rural and agricultural people, the highest level of comfort and happiness was simply that their children would not starve and that they could survive the winter until spring. For people who, under the influence of others, considered their sufferings to be divinely ordained and had no motivation or hope even to strive for a better and more comfortable life, and who were deprived of all material pleasures and blessings of life, what concept could be more useful and comforting than “seize the moment”?</p>\n\n<p>Access to music, because of its “cost,” was not possible for most people. If an opportunity arose to enjoy it, they did not seek it for concentration, contemplation, or deep thought — because engaging in such matters had no meaning for them. So it was enough that, relying on a lively, happy, and cheerful music, they could seize the moment, clap, and move their hands and bodies a little.</p>\n\n<p>The sound of the tar also has great compatibility with the musical spirit of Iranians, and the melody of this instrument has always been more appealing to most people than the sound of other instruments. The same can be said about the Caucasian tar and the people of that region. Interestingly, until a few decades ago, most people used the word “saz” — which is a general term for all musical instruments — as the specific name for the tar. Therefore, in those years, phrases such as the following were commonly heard:</p>\n\n<p>“Last night they invited two musicians; one played saz and the other played kamancheh.”</p>\n\n<p>Thus, in the subconscious mind of Iranians, if there is any opportunity to engage with music, what could be more entertaining and better than listening to a merry and lively melody coming from the “tar”?</p>\n\n<p>Among poets, few can be found who have composed as many poems in “circular meters,” full of rhythm, as Rumi. Of course, in the poem quoted by Hedayat from Baba Afzal Kashani, a 6th-century poet, “sama’” (the music of spiritual audition) is not contrasted with metric music. Rather, the poet criticizes those shortsighted people who cannot distinguish authentic and profound sama’ from the superficial and showy hand-waving of pretenders.</p>\n\n<p>At the end of this discussion, and based on what has been said, we can define instrumental zarbi as follows:</p>\n\n<p><strong>“Instrumental zarbi is a metric, instrumental, and improvisational genre that is usually performed in 6/8 meter.”</strong></p>\n\n<p>The article you just read under the title “Instrumental Zarbi” is taken from the book “The Nature of Iranian Music – An Exploration of Musical Genres” by Arshad Tahmasbi.</p>', '2024-03-10 21:09:07', 0, '2024-08-30 21:29:21', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1577, 'posts', 120, 'en', NULL, 'Higher School of Music', NULL, 'The Higher School of Music was founded in 1924 (1302 SH) by Colonel Ali-Naghi Vaziri and became one of the most important institutions for modern music education in Iran. The school aimed to systematically teach music, starting with note reading, solfège, rhythm, and later instrumental training and Iranian classical repertoire, while also incorporating European methods. Vaziri’s disciplined and reformist approach helped establish a structured academic framework for Iranian music education and produced prominent students such as Ruhollah Khaleghi, Abolhasan Saba, and Javad Maroufi. The school later evolved into the State Music School and played a key role in the modernization of music education and the development of contemporary Iranian music.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<h2>Higher School of Music</h2>\n\n<p><strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">The Higher School of Music</span></strong>, which was a private music school, was founded in Esfand 1302 solar (February–March 1924) by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d9%86%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Ali-Naqi Vaziri</a> (known as Colonel Vaziri). Colonel Vaziri himself took charge of managing the school and teaching, while Soleyman Sepanlou served as its administrator.</p>\n\n<p>Colonel Vaziri obtained permission to establish the school from Kamal al-Molk, who was legally the deputy minister of Fine Arts and Industries. The Minister of Education at the time was displeased that the work had been carried out without his approval.</p>\n\n<p>The school began operating with about one hundred students. They attended music classes two days a week, and later the number of class hours was increased. For the first two weeks, classes were dedicated to note reading and solfège. Then students were taught how to hold instruments such as the violin and tar, along with basic exercises and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%af%d8%a7%d9%85-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88-%d8%aa%d9%88%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%84%db%8c%d8%aa%d9%87/\">scales</a>. In this manner, both theoretical and practical music lessons progressed. Vaziri placed great importance on solfège and believed that every musician should learn note reading and singing, even if their goal was not to become a vocalist. For this reason, he attached special significance to daily vocal exercises with piano accompaniment.</p>\n\n<p>Ali-Naqi Vaziri was a serious teacher. Despite his relatively indifferent appearance, he was precise, meticulous, energetic, and firm in his work. He avoided flattery and superficiality, and expressed his satisfaction with his students’ progress with a slight smile. He had a sporting spirit and believed in the importance of physical and mental health. He was not discouraged by criticism, nor was he troubled by difficulties; as he himself said, he sometimes even welcomed challenges.</p>\n\n<p>He had little interest in the Bayat-e Tork melodies that he called “market-style” and avoided singers who performed at entertainment gatherings. He considered learning the principles of European music — both in performance and theory — essential for Iranian students.</p>\n\n<p>As mentioned, the school first taught recognition of notes and time signatures, followed by solfège, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%88%d8%b2%d9%86-%d8%ae%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%86%db%8c/\">meter reading</a>, familiarity with scales, the sequence of sharps and flats, metronome movements, and musical terms. After three weeks, students moved on to note reading, fingering, and various exercises for each instrument, which gradually became more difficult. After the preliminary exercises, etudes, simple chaharmezrabs, and a few European pieces were added. Finally, it was time for the Iranian dastgahs and avaz (mostly Mahur and Dashti).</p>\n\n<p>At the beginning, scale exercises in each dastgah, several related etudes, anchoring exercises, then pishdaramad, a few gushehs from each avaz or dastgah, some chaharmezrabs, rengs, and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%aa%d8%b5%d9%86%db%8c%d9%81/\">tasnifs</a> composed by Vaziri were practiced. Polyphonic or duet pieces, such as the Dashti and Bayat-e Tork rengs, were also rehearsed.</p>\n\n<p>For violin playing, Vaziri considered European methods such as Ševčík or Kreutzer necessary for developing finger and bow technique, and he used some of them in his own Violin Method book. In the early days, in addition to Vaziri, some talented students with prior musical background, such as <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%84%d8%ad%d8%b3%d9%86-%d8%b5%d8%a8%d8%a7/\">Abolhasan Saba</a>, also assisted the master in teaching the students.</p>\n\n<p>Ruhollah Khaleqi, Mir Emadi, Arbabi, Ali Mahmudi, Mohammad Hossein and Mostafa Adib, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c-%d9%85%d8%b9%d8%b1%d9%88%d9%81%db%8c/\">Musa Ma’rufi</a>, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ac%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%af-%d9%85%d8%b9%d8%b1%d9%88%d9%81%db%8c/\">Javad Ma’rufi</a>, Hossein Sanjari, Soleyman Sepanlou, Hashmat Sanjari, Abolhasan Saba, Abdolali Vaziri, Parviz Iranpour, Hossein Gol-Golab, Ali Mobasher, and Manuchehr Varseh were among the students of the first period of the Higher School of Music. From later periods, the following can be named: Khadem-e Misagh, Forutan Rad, Mehdi Moftah, Vaziri Tabar, Hossein Ali Mallah, and Mehdi Barkeshli.</p>\n\n<p>After some time, the elementary classes were managed by artists such as Hossein Sanjari, Abolhasan Saba, Manuchehr Varseh, Moez Divan Fekri (one of the pioneers of Iranian theater who turned to cinema around 1330 solar and wrote and directed films such as “Golden Dreams” (1951), “The Shepherd’s Daughter,” and “The Foundling Girl”), Mostafa Adib, and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a>. In addition to training students, Vaziri’s other activities included organizing concerts at the Music Club, recording gramophone records, composing new pieces, and writing music books, which will be discussed in their proper place.</p>\n\n<p>In the summer of 1307 solar, the directorship of the State Music School — which was under the Ministry of Education — was offered to Vaziri. Since its program was originally intended for military music, after Vaziri took charge, the curriculum became more general. Books and treatises on music, organology, orchestration, and Iranian harmony were prepared by Vaziri, a treatise on music theory and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d9%88%d9%86%db%8c/\">harmony</a> by Ruhollah Khaleqi, and a treatise on acoustics by Hossein Gol-Golab.</p>\n\n<p>At the beginning of the 1307–1308 academic year, the aforementioned school was inaugurated under the name “State Music School” with about forty students, and Ismail Zarrin Far was appointed as its administrator. Since we have spoken about the <strong>Higher School of Music</strong> and its activities, it is necessary to mention its founder, who left lasting and highly influential works in Iranian music.</p>', '2024-03-12 21:45:19', 0, '2024-08-30 23:18:20', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
(1578, 'posts', 121, 'en', NULL, 'The concept of piece in Iranian music', NULL, 'This article explores the concept of the “piece” (qateh) in Iranian classical music from the perspective of Arshed Tahmasebi. The term “piece” is a general label for metrical compositions that do not fit into traditional forms such as pishdaramad, reng, chaharmezrab, or tasnif.\n\nHistorically, the term has been used in various contexts within musical literature and compositions. With the emergence of composers such as Ali-Naqi Vaziri, Ruhollah Khaleghi, and Abolhasan Saba, a new type of composition appeared that broke away from conventional structures, leading to the adoption of “piece” as a flexible category name.\n\nThese works are highly diverse in structure and do not follow a single formal pattern. Therefore, “qateh” is not a fixed musical form but rather a broad classification for independent metrical works outside traditional Iranian musical forms.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the concept of <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">\"piece\"</span></strong> in Iranian dastgahi music from the perspective of the prominent Iranian musician and radif master, “Arshad Tahmasbi.” Stay with us for the rest of the article...</p>\n\n<h2>Piece (Qit’eh)</h2>\n\n<p>In our music, the term “piece” is a general and broad title for metric compositions that can belong to any genre, whether <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d9%be%db%8c%d8%b4-%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%a2%d9%85%d8%af/\">pishdaramad</a>, reng, or any other metric form:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Ali-Naqi Vaziri: It is customary to indicate the tempo of the piece in Italian at the beginning of the score.</p>\n\n<p>In this usage, “piece” can be considered synonymous with “melody” or “song”:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Ali-Naqi Vaziri: Finger these following pieces yourself.</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, it can also be applied to metric vocal works:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Ali-Naqi Vaziri: This short theme and the first poem of the Gerili piece were composed for orchestra and require about twenty minutes to perform.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Ruhollah Khaleqi: Apart from <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%aa%d8%b5%d9%86%db%8c%d9%81/\">tasnifs</a> and rengs, these types of zarbi vocal pieces could also be performed by several instruments together when necessary. However, these melodies were short and did not last long. Only the Saqi-nameh and Gerili, because they were accompanied by poetry, would sometimes become longer in the presence of a singer and take the place of a full musical piece.</p>\n\n<p>Thus, we see that the authors of this era, when generally titling works published in notation, sometimes used “piece” and sometimes “melody”:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">- Ten Pieces for Tar (Hossein Alizadeh),</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">- Twenty Pieces for Santur (Parviz Meshkatian),</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">- Twenty-Four Melodies by Arshad Tahmasbi,</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">- Chamber Music Pieces for Santur (Faramarz Payvar),</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">- Eight Melodies for Santur (Faramarz Payvar),</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">- Jalil Shahnaz, Fifteen Pieces for Tar and Setar (Houshang Zarif).</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">We also see that in the titling of some notated collections, the word “piece” is sometimes used in the sense of “item” or “number”:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">- Eighteen Pishdaramad Pieces for Violin (Lotfollah Mofakham Payan),</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">- Thirty Chaharmezrab Pieces for Santur (Faramarz Payvar).</p>\n\n<p>The use of this term is not limited to musicians; other writers have also used “piece” in the meaning of a musical composition:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Morteza Ravandi: In literary works after Islam, such as the Divan of Manuchehri and Nizami’s Khosrow and Shirin, the names of many existing avaz and musical pieces are mentioned, though it is not possible to identify their artistic characteristics; examples include the pieces Yazdan Afrid, Kin-e Siavash, Kin-e Iraj, Takht-e Ardeshir, Bagh-e Shirin, Bagh-e Shahriyar, Takht-e Taqdis, Haft Ganj, Ganj-e Badavard, and the like, each composed to embody a specific theme.</p>\n\n<p>This term also appears in old modal (maqami) music texts, but the authors of the treatises used the word “piece” in such a way that it can also be understood as meaning “section” or “part.” Abd al-Qadir Maraghi wrote:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">As for the nawbat, it consists of four pieces: first qawl, second ghazal, third taraneh, fourth forud-asht […] As for the second type, it is such that the seventeen tones, upon which melodies are based, are gathered in one piece.</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d9%86%d9%82%db%8c-%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Ali-Naqi Vaziri</a>, this capable composer and skillful tar player who studied music in Europe for five years and had a modern mindset in all areas related to this field, gradually presented works to the Iranian music community after returning to Iran in 1301 solar that had a completely different character from the previously common genres. His most famous instrumental works, which he specifically arranged for tar performance, were pieces that could not be classified under titles such as pishdaramad, reng, chaharmezrab, or zarbi.</p>\n\n<p>Usually, the tempo of these types of pieces was faster than the traditional pieces that musicians of that time practiced and performed. His works also contained leaps and technical features that had no precedent in earlier works and genres. We know that the organization of melodic flow in the common genres before Vaziri was based solely on the “melodic” aspects of the works. This meant that players of any instrument could easily perform, for example, any pishdaramad by tar player Darvish Khan or any reng by violinist Mokhtari without any technical concern.</p>\n\n<p>These were pieces that any ney, kamancheh, or santur player could perform, and even singers could hum them using syllables like “lalay lay” and “daday day.” However, performing some of Vaziri’s works exactly as written was not possible for players of instruments other than tar.</p>\n\n<p>One of the special features of Vaziri’s approach was introducing new ideas regarding performance techniques for various instruments — something that had no precedent in Iranian music at the time. These skills are what is technically called “virtuosity” in music, for which there is no precise equivalent in Persian. It can be translated as “extraordinary dexterity and exceptional ability” in playing.</p>\n\n<p>Undoubtedly, such initiatives were influenced by Vaziri’s five-year stay in Europe and his familiarity with the musical culture and capabilities of musicians there. It was also under the influence of these experiences that Vaziri seriously tried, through the creation of new and unconventional works, to separate instrumental music — which until then had little independent meaning or concept — from vocal music and to pave the way for its growth.</p>\n\n<p>In the past century, many musicians have carried out actions that can be described as innovation and pioneering. Darvish Khan himself was an innovative artist in many fields. For example, in tar playing, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b9%d8%a8%d8%af%d8%a7%d9%84%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%b4%d9%87%d9%86%d8%a7%d8%b2%db%8c/\">Abdolhossein Shahnazi</a> and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b2%d9%86%d8%af%da%af%db%8c%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%85%d9%87-%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c-%d8%a7%da%a9%d8%a8%d8%b1-%d8%b4%d9%87%d9%86%d8%a7%d8%b2%db%8c/\">Ali Akbar Shahnazi</a> introduced and applied new styles, and gradually Lotfollah Majd, Jalil Shahnaz, Farhang Sharif, and others each followed this path in their own way. Finally, Mohammad Reza Lotfi also joined this caravan with a different approach:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Parviz Khatibi: In the years 1324 and 1325 solar, concerts were held at the National Music Association, which was founded by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a>. Most music lovers attended these concerts. The main figures of these concerts, besides Khaleqi (the orchestra conductor), were Banan, Mehdi Khaladi, Hossein Tehrani, [Hossein Ali] Vaziri Tabar, and Lotfollah Majd.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">The young and passionate Majd had conquered Tehran with his magical plectrum. After a period when no one paid much attention to the tar, Majd and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ac%d9%84%db%8c%d9%84-%d8%b4%d9%87%d9%86%d8%a7%d8%b2/\">Jalil Shahnaz</a> came to reintroduce this authentic Iranian instrument to the people. Majd’s particular style of playing tar was very different from the styles that were common before him. He had learned to play the tar at home without a teacher.</p>\n\n<p>In santur and kamancheh playing as well, we have always witnessed gradual changes in the styles and performance techniques of these instruments. Essentially, the musicians who created a particular style in different periods based their work on “innovation” and moving against the prevailing norm, because style, in a word, as Shafi’i Kadkani says, means “deviation from the norm.”</p>\n\n<p>In other areas, such as group and orchestral performance, we have already seen what changes have taken place.</p>\n\n<p>In this context, we have even witnessed how instruments such as the robab, oud, and daf — which had no trace in Qajar-era music — were gradually revived and found a place for themselves. However, because all these transformations and changes occurred gradually over years and decades rather than all at once, they never provoked collective resistance or reaction from artists. But this description never applies to Vaziri and his activities, especially regarding the nature of his pieces. His work was not that simple.</p>\n\n<p>Vaziri’s position in the society of that time was as if someone had suddenly thrown him into this arena from another world. To understand and feel the atmosphere of that period, we must, at least for a moment, ignore today’s experiences resulting from extensive global communication, familiarity with various types of music, and exposure to the diverse practices of musicians from Iran and around the world — all made possible by modern communication tools — and suddenly transport ourselves back to the early years of Vaziri’s activity.</p>\n\n<p>In such a situation, it is enough to compare not the radif-based rengs and chaharmezrabs, but even the pishdaramads and rengs of Darvish Khan — who himself was considered an innovative musician with a hand in innovation — with this type of Vaziri’s pieces. Vaziri’s pieces were so different that they were probably perceived as unfamiliar and shocking even by his own devoted students and followers. Many may have even used the public’s ear as an excuse to bring their master’s style closer to the familiar and customary style of the period:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Ruhollah Khaleqi: Some students said that the master’s pieces are a bit heavy for the ears of people who are not familiar with this style. What is the harm in composing some melodies in the usual way? […] And he replied: That is not difficult. Later, he himself composed a set of pishdaramads, tasnifs, and rengs, and these pieces were performed without harmony by a small orchestra at the Music Club after the end of the summer season.</p>\n\n<p>Perhaps because of this distinction in his works, Vaziri was compelled — and rightly so — to choose a specific name for each of his pieces in a pioneering act.</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Bandbaz (Tightrope Walker), Musical Gymnastics, Disheveled Girl, Be Ready, Stolen Kiss, etc.</p>\n\n<p>These types of Vaziri’s pieces could not be named according to the old custom, such as “pishdaramad of such-and-such dastgah” or “reng of such-and-such dastgah,” because they resembled neither pishdaramad, nor reng, nor chaharmezrab, nor even march, waltz, or polka.</p>\n\n<p>Houshang Zarif later added a vocal section to Bandbaz that perfectly matches Vaziri’s work and has for years become an inseparable part of this piece. Hossein Dehlavi also arranged this work under the title “Fantasy for Tar and Orchestra” for several Iranian instruments:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Hossein Dehlavi: The composition of this work, which was still motivated by showing other aspects of Iranian music and making greater use of the capabilities of national instruments, was carried out in the final months of 1343 solar based on the piece “Bandbaz” by Ali-Naqi Vaziri. This piece, which is the first step toward composing a work for “tar and orchestra,” was performed in Tehran in April of the following year.</p>\n\n<p>Here is the beginning of his piece “Disheveled Girl,” one of the most famous works in Iranian music that has been performed many times by musicians of later generations. Among them, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%be%d8%b1%d9%88%db%8c%d8%b2-%d9%85%d8%b4%da%a9%d8%a7%d8%aa%db%8c%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Parviz Meshkatian</a> included it alongside his own works in a group performance in the album “Dastan.” Vaziri composed this Chahargah piece in the “Re” (D) mode. The use of this mode was less common among tar players at the time because performing <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%da%86%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">Chahargah</a> in this mode is technically much more difficult than performing it in the “Do” (C) mode.</p>\n\n<p>In 1335 solar, the first music competition in Iran was held by the Association of Graduates of the Higher Teacher Training College. This association published notated pieces for each instrument and made them available to the contestants. Among them, the “Selected Piece for the Tar Competition” was Vaziri’s “Musical Gymnastics.”</p>\n\n<p>Among tar players of later generations, Houshang Zarif and Keyvan Saket were the most proficient and inclined to perform Vaziri’s “Five Pieces for Tar.” These pieces — Bandbaz, Disheveled Girl, Be Ready, Dashti Etude, and Musical Gymnastics — have always been and still remain part of the core repertoire of tar classes, and every professional player considers themselves obligated to learn them. Although nearly a century has passed since the creation of these works, no tar-specific technical piece has yet achieved the fame of Vaziri’s pieces.</p>\n\n<p>An important point about the works we mentioned from Ali-Naqi Vaziri is that none of them have any structural or <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%af%d8%b1-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">formal</a> similarity to one another. In the chapters on other genres, we saw that, one way or another, a general and common feature can be stated for analyzing the structure of each of those genres. For example, we can at least say where a pishdaramad is placed within a <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a8%d8%b1%d9%86%d8%a7%d9%85%d9%87-%d8%a7%db%8c/\">programmatic music</a> performance. But when explaining this type of Vaziri’s works, the only thing that can be said is that they are neither pishdaramad, nor reng, nor avaz, nor chaharmezrab, nor tasnif, nor zarbi (the zarbi form is divided into two types: <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b6%d8%b1%d8%a8%db%8c-%d8%b3%d8%a7%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9/\">instrumental zarbi</a> and <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b6%d8%b1%d8%a8%db%8c-%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2%db%8c%da%a9/\">vocal zarbi</a>).</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, among musicians, without any pre-established rule or regulation, it has become customary to call pieces that do not fit into the structure of previous genres “qit’eh” (piece).</p>\n\n<p>Up to this point, the “piece” should also be considered one of the genres of music. However, the problem is that the pieces of different composers also have no similarity or homogeneity with the pieces of their colleagues. As we said about Vaziri’s works, this lack of homogeneity may even exist between the various pieces of a single composer. That is, the structure of Vaziri’s “Disheveled Girl” has nothing to do with the structure of Saba’s “In the Cage,” and the structure of this has nothing to do with Payvar’s “Farparak,” and the structure of this has nothing to do with Alizadeh’s “Hejran”; just as the structure of Vaziri’s “Disheveled Girl” has nothing to do with the structure of his own “Be Ready,” and the structure of Alizadeh’s “Hejran” has nothing to do with the structure of his “Raqsan,” and so on.</p>\n\n<p>The fame of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%a7%d9%84%d8%ad%d8%b3%d9%86-%d8%b5%d8%a8%d8%a7/\">Abolhasan Saba</a> in creating new pieces is, if not more than Vaziri’s, at least equal to it. In fact, it must be said that the level of fame this musician — who also had a short life — achieved in Iranian society during the 1320s and 1330s and until the end of his life was such that no other musician of that period reached it. In gaining this popularity and fame, his innate nature and outstanding personality, in addition to his musical abilities, played a very significant role.</p>\n\n<p>Like his teacher, Saba was a multifaceted musician and performer who trained many students, each of whom later became renowned figures in the field of music. All of these students, in their articles and interviews, have strongly emphasized his outstanding personal qualities, such as kindness, simplicity, tolerance, and purity of character. This is the same set of characteristics mentioned in numerous reports about his other teacher, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ba%d9%84%d8%a7%d9%85%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%af%d8%b1%d9%88%db%8c%d8%b4/\">Darvish Khan</a>. In his interactions with all those around him, whether superiors or inferiors, Saba behaved the same way and was very simple, unpretentious, and carefree. Banan recounted this memory to Houshang Ebtehaj:</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Once a month we would go to the house of the Queen Mother, the Shah’s mother, and play music and sing <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2/\">avaz</a>. The Shah would also come to his mother’s house. One day we were all standing, and the Shah was standing too, greeting us. Suddenly the door opened and Abolhasan Khan Saba entered with his violin under his arm and said, “Salam alaykum.”</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Banan says in the recording: I looked and saw he was wearing one red sock and one white sock. It is possible to mistakenly wear black and navy in the dark, but no one mistakes red and white […] Now Saba entered and greeted like the old mustawfis. For Saba, the Shah and such people were not important at all. He was a very progressive and free-spirited person. Suddenly Saba said, “It seems they don’t intend to sit down. I’m tired,” and he went and sat on a chair […] Then the Shah said to Saba: “Master Saba! I heard you smoke a lot of opium.” He replied: “What else should I do, Your Majesty?”</p>\n\n<p>The number of famous teachers and students of Saba is greater than that of any other musician. He remained a music student until the end of his life and was always eager to learn. At the height of his fame, he would follow street dervishes to learn and record the masnavis they sang. He would study old music treatises to understand them, and he always tried to be a student of the best achievements and moral qualities of his teachers both in music and in ethics.</p>\n\n<p>Just as his teacher Vaziri allowed him to perform his “Zard Malijeh” in the middle of the record “Ey Vatan” (one of Vaziri’s works), he too sat beside Faramarz Payvar and accompanied him on violin in the performance of a piece of Shuri that his student had composed.</p>\n\n<p>Perhaps the existence of the only museum in Iran dedicated exclusively to a musician — “Saba House” on Zohir al-Islam Street — is testimony to the special respect Iranian society holds for him.</p>\n\n<p>Unlike his teacher Vaziri, Saba had little inclination to compose pishdaramads, rengs, or especially tasnifs. Most of the works he left behind — which are very famous and well-known — have a new structure and should be placed in the “piece” genre. Also, his three chaharmezrabs — the chaharmezrabs of <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%85%d8%a7%d9%87%d9%88%d8%b1/\">Mahur</a> (based on themes from Darvish Khan), Karavan (Dashti), and Zang-e Shotor (Segah) — are undoubtedly the most famous works in Iranian music in the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%86%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d8%b6%d8%b1%d8%a7%d8%a8/\">chaharmezrab</a> genre.</p>\n\n<p>Most of his works were notated and recorded in the third volume of his violin method during his lifetime and show that, apart from the “piece” genre, he was only inclined to compose chaharmezrabs. We record the list of works from this book here and highlight one of them:</p>\n\n<p>Karavan (Dashti), Chaharmezrab Bayat-e Tork, Chaharmezrab Hejaz, Chaharmezrab Shur, Chaharmezrab <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%a7%d8%b5%d9%81%d9%87%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Esfahan</a>, Exercise in Esfahan’s Oshshaq, To Prison (Shushtari), Dashti Exercise, Samani (Segah), Baharmast (Segah), Chaharmezrab Chahargah, In the Cage (Dashti), Chaharmezrab Mahur, Chupi Dance of Qasemabad Gilan (<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d9%81%d8%b4%d8%a7%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Afshari</a>), Hekayat (Nava), Kuhestani (Oshshaq-e Dashti), Chaharmezrab <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d8%a8%d9%88%d8%b9%d8%b7%d8%a7/\">Abu Ata</a>, Chaharmezrab Mahur, <strong>Two-Beat Piece</strong> (Bayat-e Tork), Reng Bayat-e Tork, In Memory of the Past (Dashti).</p>\n\n<p>We saw that, apart from the chaharmezrabs, exercises, and one reng, all of his pieces that have a new structure have specific names — except for one. It is not clear why Saba did not give a name to this beautiful two-beat piece in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%aa%d8%b1%da%a9/\">Bayat-e Tork</a> and was therefore forced to use the general title “piece” for it.</p>\n\n<p>In this way, the use of this title at the top of a music score was first done by him, because in none of Vaziri’s books (Tar Method, New Tar and Setar Method, Violin Method) is the title “piece” seen at the top of any metric or non-metric section. We know that Vaziri’s methods and Saba’s violin volumes were the first published notated books in Iran. Vaziri published his tar and violin methods between 1300 and 1316 solar, and the publication of the first volume of Saba’s <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d8%af%db%8c%d9%81/\">radif</a> for violin took place in 1323 solar.</p>\n\n<p>An important feature in several of Saba’s works, including the pieces In the Cage, In Memory of the Past, Chupi Dance, Kuhestani, Baharmast, and his Dashti exercise, is their brevity. In these pieces, the composer has used at most two fresh and beautiful themes and, without seeking futile development and expansion of the melody, has concluded the work in accordance with the principle of “speak little and choose your words.” Nevertheless, we see that these types of his pieces have also been well received by musicians, and most of them have been performed many times by various artists.</p>\n\n<p>For example, his “In the Cage,” which is a very short 24-bar piece, has been performed not only by the Payvar group and musicians such as Rahmatollah Badi’i, but also by Hossein Alizadeh in a beautiful group arrangement in the Chavosh 7 album. In truth, this type of Saba’s works can be compared to rubaiyat, in which the poet presents a subtle and profound point in only four lines.</p>\n\n<p>Although Saba did not use traditional forms in composing his pieces, the characteristic of his work — for example, in comparison with Vaziri’s work — is that he organized melodies that have a completely authentic and traditional essence and are profoundly and truly “Iranian.”</p>\n\n<p>Unlike Saba, his friend and colleague Ruhollah Khaleqi was not very active in composing pieces. Instead, he has beautiful works in the tasnif genre, some of which are very well-known. Khaleqi’s most famous instrumental pieces were compositions titled “Rangarang” that were performed in the Golha-ye Rangarang programs and are among the most listenable works in Iranian music.</p>\n\n<p>While discussing Khaleqi, it is necessary to mention an important factor in the popularity of composers’ pieces. Usually, the pieces that have become more famous among the public are those that have become part of the standard repertoire of students and practitioners of the instrument. Such works have generally been produced by a “performer-composer-teacher.” In other words, there have always been intermediaries (the students or semi-professional musicians, whose number has always been far greater than that of professional producer musicians) between the producer and the consumer of music.</p>\n\n<p>After learning a piece from his teacher, a student practices it at home. In every home, there are presumably a spouse, children, and relatives who involuntarily become listeners to that piece. This can be one of the factors in making a piece more widespread and famous. In this regard, the names of the pieces themselves — which are usually beautiful and imaginative names that are easily remembered — can also contribute to this fame: “Disheveled Girl,” “Tightrope Walker,” “Be Ready,” etc. Of course, all these factors are only effective when the piece is also “listenable.”</p>\n\n<p>If we consider this three-fold role (performer – composer – teacher) as an advantage, a creative musician like Khaleqi, compared to Saba and Vaziri, benefited less from this advantage because he did not have the same fame and ability in performance as these two, nor did he establish a long-lasting school for teaching. He was more involved in composition, orchestra conducting, and writing.</p>\n\n<p>In this way, there have been many performers and composers in the decades following Vaziri’s productions who have composed pieces perhaps equal in quality to his and Saba’s works, but our collective memory has not given them the same value as “To Prison” or “Disheveled Girl,” or has even forgotten them, and has only placed the works of the “most prolific” at the top.</p>\n\n<p>Our task here is not to analyze this issue in detail or to introduce overlooked excellent works, but the main purpose is to introduce the <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d9%87%d8%a7%db%8c-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">genres of Iranian music</a>. In this context, we inevitably refer to the more famous pieces and those who have been more active in the fields related to our discussion. This approach does not mean that we do not believe, for example, that Asadollah Malek composed a beautiful piece called “Geryeh-ye Leyli,” or … .</p>\n\n<p>Faramarz Payvar and Hossein Alizadeh are also among the three-fold musicians and have numerous works in the “piece” genre. In the first music competition in Iran, the selected piece for the santur competition was Faramarz Payvar’s “Taraneh.”</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%b9%d9%84%db%8c%d8%b2%d8%a7%d8%af%d9%87/\">Hossein Alizadeh</a> has not been as active in establishing educational classes as Vaziri, Saba, and Payvar, but because of the fame he gained in the field of innovation, many tar players have promoted his works and pieces. In addition to the pieces he composed for tar, he was among the first to perform two relatively long and modern pieces with groups consisting of Iranian instrument players at the beginning of his career and became famous in this field: “Savaran-e Dasht-e Omid” (Riders of the Plain of Hope) and “Hesar.”</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Houshang Ebtehaj: Sometimes coincidences happened that were very valuable … Alizadeh composed a piece that I named “Savaran-e Dasht-e Omid” because of the movement he put in this piece. Alizadeh’s idea was to sit and play the tar part of this piece himself. It is also a very difficult piece. It has a lot of speed and is quite long. At the same time, he had to lead the group and tell them what to do and what not to do. Lotfi said: “Hossein! I’ll play it.”</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; padding-right: 40px;\">Alizadeh never expected that. Lotfi is older than Alizadeh and more senior, and now he would come into the group and sit and play with his own students […] I saw how Alizadeh’s face lit up with joy when Lotfi said he would play it.</p>\n\n<p>Of course, part of Houshang Ebtehaj’s account is not accurate. As I heard from Alizadeh himself (Arshad Tahmasbi), the issue for Alizadeh at that time was not that he had to lead the group, because in rehearsing and performing music of this kind, there is basically no need for the type of conducting we see in Western orchestras, and in all cases, Iranian group composers have always been the performers of their own works as well.</p>\n\n<p>The main point was that at that time Alizadeh was finishing his military service and had therefore been away from practicing his instrument for a long time and his hands were not warm. Therefore, Lotfi volunteered to play in his place. Second, performing this very beautiful piece is not as technically difficult or fast as Ebtehaj described.</p>\n\n<p>Hossein Alizadeh has also composed a large orchestral piece called “Neynava,” which can be considered a kind of “concerto” for the ney. He was also one of the pioneers in composing pieces in irregular meters, among which the piece “Hejran,” dedicated to <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%85%d8%ad%d9%85%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%b6%d8%a7-%d9%84%d8%b7%d9%81%db%8c/\">Mohammad Reza Lotfi</a>, is more famous than his other works and was published with his own duet performance with me in the album “Hamnavayi.” In the piece Hejran, unlike the five-beat or seven-beat pieces that have symmetrical measures and continue uniformly until the end of the piece, Alizadeh has used various rhythms in different sections of the piece according to the plan he had in mind. Therefore, each of his <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%aa%d9%85-%d8%af%d8%b1-%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c/\">themes</a>, based on the number of their sixteenth notes, has different measure divisions.</p>\n\n<p>Alizadeh also has a piece called “Nowruz 62,” composed during the Nowruz of 1362 solar. The measures of this piece also change repeatedly based on the number of eighth notes in each theme. It should be noted that such pieces should not be considered “polyrhythmic” because, for example, this one is built on a single pulse based on the time of one eighth note. Therefore, it can, regardless of the “accents” of the piece, be considered a kind of “one-beat” measure in which each beat is equivalent to one eighth note.</p>\n\n<p>Based on what has been said, the “piece” in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%d9%85%d9%88%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%82%db%8c-%d8%a7%db%8c%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%86/\">Iranian music</a> can only be defined as follows:</p>\n\n<p><strong>“A metric, instrumental, and non-improvised composition that is neither a pishdaramad, nor a reng, nor a chaharmezrab.”</strong></p>\n\n<p>The article you just read under the title “The Concept of Piece in Iranian Music” is taken from the book “The Nature of Iranian Music – An Exploration of Musical Genres” by Arshad Tahmasbi.</p>', '2024-03-15 20:11:43', 0, '2024-08-30 21:28:20', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
INSERT INTO `translations` (`translation_id`, `table_name`, `table_id`, `locale`, `code`, `title`, `brief`, `description`, `subject_1`, `text_1`, `subject_2`, `text_2`, `subject_3`, `text_3`, `content`, `created_at`, `created_by`, `updated_at`, `updated_by`, `approved_at`, `approved_by`, `deleted_at`, `deleted_by`) VALUES
(1579, 'posts', 122, 'en', NULL, 'How is the structure of Iranian programmatic music?', NULL, 'This article examines the structure and historical development of Iranian program music from the late Qajar era to the radio period, based on the perspective of musicologist Arshad Tahmasebi. It explains how early recording limitations of gramophone discs led to the dominance of short musical forms such as solo performances, vocal pieces, tasnif, and reng, while longer forms like pishdaramad were rarely recorded. Over time, especially with the advent of radio, the pishdaramad gained prominence as an introductory piece and became a key structural element alongside vocal and rhythmic sections in musical programs. The article also highlights how radio broadcasts and programs such as “Golha” helped standardize and develop this structure further, while some traditional forms like reng declined in usage and new stylistic approaches emerged.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '<p>In this article, we examine the structure of <strong><span style=\"color: #0000ff;\">Iranian programmatic music</span></strong> from the perspective of the prominent Iranian musician and radif master, “Arshad Tahmasbi.” Stay with us for the rest of the article...</p>\n\n<h2>Iranian Programmatic Music</h2>\n\n<p>With the arrival of record-producing companies in Iran during the late Qajar period, musicians and singers of that time began recording vocal pieces in the form of “solo instrumental performances” and “instrument and voice” (saz-o avaz). These records had limited capacity, making it impossible to record a complete instrumental and vocal program. Therefore, musicians could only record their works as short solo pieces and brief saz-o avaz lasting a few minutes, performing one or two maqams (not a full dastgah), along with single songs such as tasnif, reng, and some local folk tunes. Here are the titles of some records from that era:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Avaz-e Mavarannahr, Avaz by Mirza Seyed Ahmad Khan with tar by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%82%d8%a7-%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86%d9%82%d9%84%db%8c/\">Aqa Hossein-Qoli</a>,</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Azerbaijani with tasnif and reng, Mirza Seyed Ahmad Khan, tar by Mirza Hossein-Qoli,</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Tasnif of Isfahan with reng, ensemble of Nayeb al-Saltaneh,</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Tasnif “Az delam bikhabari” with reng, avaz by Mirza Abolhasan Davud with accompaniment,</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">[...]</p>\n\n<p>Among the 398 records made between 1324 and 1333 AH (1285–1294 solar), the most frequently performed genres were “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2/\">avaz</a>” and “tasnif” (171 cases) and “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%90%d9%86%da%af/\">reng</a>” (57 cases). There is no “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%da%86%d9%87%d8%a7%d8%b1%d9%85%d8%b6%d8%b1%d8%a7%d8%a8/\">chaharmezrab</a>” in these records, and “pishdaramad” appears in only three of them, one of which was recorded twice on different sides:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Pishdaramad in Mahur, Mirza Hossein-Qoli on tar.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Pishdaramad in Abu Ata, kamancheh by Hossein Khan [Esma’ilzadeh].</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Pishdaramad in Mahur, tar by Aqa Mirza Hossein-Qoli. Mahur, Delkash, Haj Mohammad Khan, tar by Aqa Mirza Hossein-Qoli.</p>\n\n<p>Interestingly, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ba%d9%84%d8%a7%d9%85%d8%ad%d8%b3%db%8c%d9%86-%d8%af%d8%b1%d9%88%db%8c%d8%b4/\">Gholam Hossein Darvish Khan</a> — who is known as the inventor of the pishdaramad and composed many pieces in this form — only played tar in 23 of the mentioned records and did not perform any of his own pishdaramads. This is likely due to the long duration of his pishdaramads and the limited capacity of the records of that time. It is unlikely that he composed all his pishdaramads in the last decade of his life, as he passed away in 1305 AH (solar 1286).</p>\n\n<p>In the next generation of records produced by various companies from 1305 to 1318 solar, the statistics of musical genres used in recordings remain roughly the same as in the Qajar period. However, in this era, we witness the emergence of a new genre called “zarbi,” most of which appear in records featuring santur player Habib Samaei:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Gerili, Zarbi-ye Shahnaz 1–2 (with santur by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%ad%d8%a8%db%8c%d8%a8-%d8%b3%d9%85%d8%a7%d8%b9%db%8c/\">Habib Samaei</a>).</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Tasnif, Bayat-e Esfahan, Ruba’i-ye Zarbi 1–2 (with santur by Habib Samaei).</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Zarbi-ye Abu Ata, Hejaz 1–2 (with tar by Qavam).</p>\n\n<p>Nevertheless, it seems that during the same period, the pishdaramad had found a better position in private gatherings and circles of music lovers:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Abolhasan Saba: In private sessions where the audience had a deep understanding of music, they would invite first-rank masters — of whom there were never more than three in any given period. The host, along with a few like-minded and like-spirited friends, would benefit from the presence of these masters, sometimes accompanied by a singer and a percussionist. This meant that the master would begin playing a pishdaramad (which had become common in recent times) in complete silence, accompanied by gentle percussion. Then he would start performing the avaz (daramad) related to the same <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d9%88-%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2/\">dastgah</a> in which the pishdaramad had been played.</p>\n\n<p>Also, in the concerts of this period, the position of the pishdaramad as the opening piece of a complete programmatic music performance was fully established. The concert announcement for Darvish Khan in 1300 solar and Ali Akbar Shahnazi’s concert in 1301 solar clearly show the role of pishdaramad, tasnif, and reng in programmatic music:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">After three years, Darvish’s concert. Do not forget Friday night, 9 Rabi’ al-Thani (corresponding to 17 Azar). Tickets are on sale… If the tickets sell out, you will no longer find a seat in the theater […] The concert program is as follows, in three acts:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Act One: New pishdaramad in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%a2%d9%88%d8%a7%d8%b2-%d8%a7%d9%81%d8%b4%d8%a7%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Afshari</a> – with a new tasnif – new reng.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Act Two: New Chahargah zarbi in question-and-answer style.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Act Three: New pishdaramad in Mahur – new tasnif and new reng.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Shahnazi’s concert, Act One: Pishdaramad of Shur, one of Shahnazi’s innovations, performed as a complete dastgah; a ghazal by the learned poet Vahid Dastgerdi is added to the sweetness of Shahnazi’s enchanting tones in avaz-e Shur, and then the tasnif and reng of Shur conclude this act […] Act Three: The most important dastgah of Eastern music (Chahargah), which has not yet been presented in a concert setting, draws the audience’s attention with a novel pishdaramad, followed by a new <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d8%aa%d8%b5%d9%86%db%8c%d9%81/\">tasnif</a> and a reng arranged in full dastgah form […] Hurry and once again witness the cheerful spirit of Aqa Mirza Hossein-Qoli.</p>\n\n<p>With the establishment of the Radio Organization in Iran, a new arena was created for the activity and presence of musicians. At this time, the time limitations for musical performances were somewhat removed, and public access to musical performances became possible. However, unlike music records, live radio broadcasts did not have the possibility of being preserved and could not be heard again.</p>\n\n<p>The consequence of removing time restrictions on radio was that artists could now include the “<a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%81%d8%b1%d9%85-%d9%be%db%8c%d8%b4-%d8%af%d8%b1%d8%a2%d9%85%d8%af/\">pishdaramad</a>” — which has a longer duration than other forms — in their programs. Moreover, after nearly two decades of the influence that Gholam Hossein Darvish and Rokn al-Din Mokhtari had on this form, the pishdaramad had gained a distinguished position among musical genres, and all composers had turned to creating such pieces. Now let us examine a few examples of the first radio performances:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">The music program to be performed on the inauguration day of the Tehran broadcasting station (4 Ordibehesht 1319) under the supervision of Major Minbashian: 1. Anthem in praise of the Shah; 2. Anthem of the Shah’s affection; 3. Anthem of effort [...]</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li style=\"list-style-type: none;\">\n<ol>\n  <li style=\"list-style-type: none;\">\n<ol>\n  <li>Homayun (pishdaramad, avaz, tasnif, reng) – 15 minutes;</li>\n  <li>Mahur (pishdaramad, avaz, tasnif, reng) – 15 minutes, performed by Iranian musicians.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Weekly radio music program from Saturday 25 Bahman to Monday 27 Bahman 1320 solar.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Saturday 25 Bahman 1320:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Part One: Pishdaramad in <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%af%d8%b3%d8%aa%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87-%d8%b3%d9%87-%da%af%d8%a7%d9%87/\">Segah</a>, avaz by Mr. Adib Khansari with violin by [Mehdi] Khaladi.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Part Two: Choral concert (group singing) featuring Mr. Patmagrian and the choir.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Part Three: Solo piano by Morteza Mahjubi (Dashti), solo santur by Habib Samaei (Nava).</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Sunday 26 Bahman 1320:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Part One: Pishdaramad in Afshari, avaz by Ruhangiz, violin by [Mehdi] Khaladi, tasnif “Naz-e Mehrukhan.”</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Part Two: Pishdaramad in Esfahan, tasnif “Afsaneh-ye Asheq,” tasnif “Yar-e Vafadar.”</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Part Three: Solo piano by Javad Ma’rufi (Mahur), solo violin by [Mehdi] Khaladi (Bayat-e Tork).</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Monday 27 Bahman 1320:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Part One: Pishdaramad in Abu Ata, avaz by <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d9%82%d9%85%d8%b1%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%85%d9%84%d9%88%da%a9-%d9%88%d8%b2%db%8c%d8%b1%db%8c/\">Qamar-ol-Moluk Vaziri</a>, santur by Habib Samaei, tasnif “Bimehri-ye Khazan.”</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Part Two: Tar and avaz by Abdolali Vaziri, Homayun.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 80px; text-align: justify;\">Part Three: Pishdaramad in Chahargah, avaz by Qamar-ol-Moluk Vaziri with piano by Morteza Mahjubi, tasnif “Faribandeh.”</p>\n\n<p>Among the 36 programs recorded in the “History of Music Recording in Iran,” 34 programs are dedicated to <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/category/%D9%85%D9%88%D8%B3%DB%8C%D9%82%DB%8C-%D8%A7%DB%8C%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%86/\">Iranian music</a>. In 22 of them, a “pishdaramad” was performed. Unlike what we saw in the content of the earliest music records, the number of reng performances has decreased, with only 2 rengs performed in these 34 programs.</p>\n\n<p>In 1334 solar, Davud Pirnia took over the supervision of radio music and prepared complete music programs in serial format under the titles “Golha-ye Rangarang” (Colorful Flowers), “Golha-ye Javidan” (Everlasting Flowers), “Golha-ye Sahraei” (Desert Flowers), and “Barg-e Sabz” (Green Leaf). The weekly broadcast of these programs, with the collaboration of famous artists of the time, continued until 1345 solar. Among them, the “Golha-ye Rangarang” series became particularly famous and popular. Interestingly, the woman who narrated and recited the poems in the Golha-ye Rangarang programs gained fame comparable to the artists themselves:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Parviz Khatibi: The Golha program was, in fact, Pirnia’s entire life. He found all his past experiences, along with his lost ideals and dreams, within it. When the sixty-member Golha orchestra began playing in the large studio of Radio Iran, Pirnia would close his eyes and travel to another world. He had learned to play the piano in his youth and, while studying classical music, never forgot authentic Iranian music. Few people have immersed themselves in the vast ocean of Iranian literature as much as Davud Pirnia [...]</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">I first saw Roshanak, or “Sedigheh Rasouli,” in the “Central” studio in Tehran. That day, a group of new announcers had come to take a test. Roshanak was among them. She was a shy and modest girl with a very delicate and sweet voice. By chance, the dubbing directors liked her, and it was arranged for her to start work the following week. But Roshanak never returned to the studio. One day, I ran into her in the hallways of the radio office.</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">With her head lowered and her face turning redder and redder with shyness, she said: “I work with Mr. Pirnia, in the Golha program.” And indeed, how much color and fragrance the “Golha” programs gained from Roshanak’s voice. When she said: “And now, from the flower garden of Iranian literature, several soul-nourishing flowers are presented to you along with the delightful sound of instruments,” thousands of fans of the program would fall silent wherever they were. Roshanak’s final words were always: “May your heart always be happy and your lips always smiling.”</p>\n\n<p>The centerpiece of each Golha program was a “tasnif” composed by musicians such as Morteza Mahjubi, Ali Tajvidi, Mehdi Khaladi, <a href=\"https://sornaz.com/%d8%b1%d9%88%d8%ad-%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%84%d9%87-%d8%ae%d8%a7%d9%84%d9%82%db%8c/\">Ruhollah Khaleqi</a>, and others. These tasnifs were usually performed at the beginning of the program by the “Golha Orchestra.” Most of them were composed in a heavy meter, and their opening instrumental introduction served a similar function to a pishdaramad in these programs. After the tasnif or its introduction, it was time for saz-o avaz, and the program would usually end with a repetition of the tasnif. As a result, reng was generally not performed in these programs:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Habibollah Nasirifar: The number of songs by [Mehdi] Khaladi that have been broadcast on radio so far — all of which have his own unique style — exceeds two hundred. It should also be noted that after he began working at the radio, he discontinued the usual routine of “pishdaramad, avaz, tasnif, reng” and instead composed tasnifs in which, instead of a pishdaramad, the first two lines of poetry were recited (as declamation) with the instrumental melody of the tasnif at the beginning of the program. Then the singer would perform the tasnif, followed by a vocal piece with solo instrument, and the program would conclude with the same tasnif.</p>\n\n<p>Undoubtedly, those familiar with the “Golha-ye Rangarang” programs, when speaking of these programs and their particular style of music, will immediately think of Morteza Mahjubi as the first composer that comes to mind. In 1344 solar, after Mahjubi’s passing, Ruhollah Khaleqi wrote about his tasnifs:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">This artist was among the fortunate ones whose important works have been preserved with the warm voice of Banan and Marziyeh in the Golha program collection, thanks to the enthusiastic supervisor of this enduring program. As far as I have researched, there are twenty-two songs [...] These songs include:</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">Gol-e Bi Vafa, Navay-e Ney, Hasti va Naleh Bi Asar, Man-e Bi Del, Karavan, Az Gham-e Del Naleha Daram, Del-e Ghamdideh, Chun Zolf-e Toam Jana dar Hin-e Parishani, Ashk va Ah, Man az Rooz-e Azal, Ahd Shekan, To va Naz va Fetnegari (poems by Rahe), Che Shabha, Goftam ba To Shabi ra be Sar Aram, Dishab ke To dar Khaneh-ye Ma Amadeh Budi (with poem by Navab Safa), Ahoo-ye Vahshi (poem by Shahr Ashub), Emshab, Omri-st ke dar Kuy-e To (with poem by Moyad Sabeti), To Muro (with poem by Moein Afshar), Ey Eshgh (with poem by Naseh), Eshgh-e Suzan (with poem by Touraj Negahban), and Emshab Cho Ayad ze Dar (with poem by Varzi).</p>\n\n<p style=\"padding-right: 40px; text-align: justify;\">[...] I can confidently say that Mahjubi’s tasnifs, especially several of them, are among those eternal melodies that will remain immortal in our music, and no other tune will replace them. Among them are: Navay-e Ney, Karavan, Man az Rooz-e Azal, and Hasti, of which, in my opinion, “Navay-e Ney” stands at the top.</p>\n\n<p>The article you just read under the title “The Structure of Iranian Programmatic Music” is taken from the book “The Nature of Iranian Music – An Exploration of Musical Genres” by Arshad Tahmasbi.</p>', '2024-03-27 21:04:07', 0, '2024-03-27 21:04:07', 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL),
